/* Psychiatry for Residents: practice runs. Original content. */
window.TPR_SIM={"c-0-1-1":{"setup":"Tuesday 20:40, your second on-call. Ward 3 bleeps: a 27-year-old man on Section 2 for mania has thrown a chair in the day area. Make the scene safer, lead de-escalation with the nurse in charge, and agree a fallback plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Jordan Reid, 27, male, patient on Section 2 (with Priya, the nurse in charge)","open":"Get away from me! You lot locked me up for nothing.","manner":"Loud, pacing, fast speech and easily blocked. Softens quickly once the audience goes, he feels heard and gets real options; flares if crowded or told 'no' with no reason.","free":["His leave was refused at teatime","He says he has a business and deals to close","He hasn't slept properly for days and his head is 'buzzing'","He feels everyone is staring and might jump him"],"ifasked":["He has had about four strong coffees this evening","His legs feel jumpy and he can't sit still since the new tablets","No alcohol or drugs; his brother visited at 16:00","He would like to ring his brother","He took lorazepam 2 mg at 14:00 (nurse)","He would take a tablet if it helps him sleep"],"worries":["Will he lose his business while he is stuck here?","Will staff hold him down and inject him?","Will his consultant hear that he calmed down?"],"rules":["Shouts again if the doctor steps closer or more staff gather","Puts the chair down if asked respectfully and given a reason","Refuses tablets at first; accepts once given a choice and told what it does","Nurse in charge asks for IM to be written up; settles if the doctor explains the plan"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Arrive and set up","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Ade, the on-call doctor. I've got an alarm and it works. Quickly: what's happened, and who knows him best?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: I'm Priya. His leave was refused at teatime and he's been building since. He threw the chair five minutes ago."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Priya. Can your colleague take the other patients to the lounge? You and I stay. I'll do the talking, and the response team waits out of sight.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Yes. Moving them now."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"Has he had any PRN today? What and when?","m":[8]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Lorazepam 2 mg at two o'clock. Nothing since."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"So 2 mg in the last 24 hours. The usual ceiling is 4 mg, so there's room for one more 2 mg dose if he wants it. Any drink or drugs? Has he been off the ward?","m":[8,7]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: No leave yet. His brother visited at four. His new antipsychotic started yesterday."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"Any fever, shaking or unsteadiness? Has anyone checked his sugar?","m":[7]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Obs were fine at six. No sugar done."},{"t":"1:20","band":"First contact","w":"D","s":"Jordan? I'm Dr Ade, one of the doctors. I'm going to stay back here. I can see you're really angry. What's happened?","m":[2,3,4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Get away from me! You lot locked me up for nothing."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"I'm staying right here, not coming any closer. I'm not here to argue about the section. Something's really got to you tonight.","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"They cancelled my leave. I've got a business to run. Deals to close. And I'm stuck in here with this lot."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"That sounds so frustrating, feeling stuck in here while things outside need you.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"And they're all staring at me. They're going to jump me, I know it."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. Nobody's going to rush you. Look, everyone else has gone. It's just me and Priya.","m":[4,1]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"(quieter) Fine. But I'm not sitting down."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"You don't have to. I need one thing from you. Nobody gets hurt tonight, and that includes you. Can you leave the chair where it is?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Alright. I wasn't going to throw it at anyone."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you. That really helps.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:35","band":"His story and a physical check","w":"D","s":"What's the worst bit for you right now?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"I haven't slept for days. My head's buzzing. Everything's turned up to full volume."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. No sleep makes everything louder. Have you had anything tonight, a drink, something your brother brought in?","m":[4,7]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"I don't do drugs. Coffee. Four coffees, maybe."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being straight with me. Since the new tablets, any shaking, or a horrible restless feeling in your legs?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"My legs are jumpy. Can't keep still. But that's just energy."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"It might be, or it might be the tablets, and that can be treated. Would you let a nurse check your temperature and a finger-prick sugar once things are calmer?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"If it gets me out quicker."},{"t":"3:30","band":"Choices","w":"D","s":"I'll be honest. I can't change your leave tonight. Only your consultant can. I'll write down that you asked, and that you calmed things down. That will count tomorrow.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"So what's the point of you, then?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Fair question. I can help with the next hour. You could take a tablet now to take the edge off. You could go to the quiet lounge with a nurse nearby. Or you could phone someone about your work. Which feels best?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"I'm not taking your tablets. They slow me down."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"That's your call. This one is lorazepam, the same as this afternoon. It may make you a bit sleepy, which tonight might be no bad thing.","m":[6,9]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Maybe. Can I ring my brother first?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Yes. Priya can get your phone and you can ring from the quiet lounge. Then you decide about the tablet. So what happens next?","m":[6,10]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"I go to the lounge, ring my brother, maybe take the tablet. And you tell my consultant about the leave. Sorry about the chair."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Thanks for saying sorry.","m":[10,3]},{"t":"4:50","band":"Plan with the team","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge (aside): Doctor, just write him up for IM. He's been like this all shift."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"You've had a hard shift, I can see that. He's stopped throwing and he's talking, so let's give oral a proper try. I'll write the plan now so we're ready if he escalates.","m":[11,4]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: And if he kicks off again?"},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"If he takes 2 mg now, he's had his 4 mg for today. More needs me and the on-call consultant, with closer obs. If he refuses and turns violent, the response team comes in under the trust policy, and I come straight back before any injection.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Okay. What obs?"},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Within eyesight for now, reviewed at handover. NEWS2 after any PRN, plus temperature and glucose when he'll allow. Flag the restless legs to the day team; it could be akathisia.","m":[12,7]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Fine. I'll do the incident form."},{"t":"5:50","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'll write my entry: what happened, what we tried, his choices, the PRN total and the fallback plan. Call me if he escalates, refuses obs or looks unwell. I'll pop back before midnight anyway.","m":[11,12,13]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: That went better than I expected."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"The team did well clearing the room. Is everyone okay after the chair? Can his named nurse debrief him in the next couple of days about what set it off and what helped?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Everyone's fine. I'll put it in his plan."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Jordan, I'm off to write this up. Your consultant will hear about the leave in the morning. Anything else you need from me tonight?","m":[13,9]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Just sleep. Cheers, Doc."}],"marks":[{"c":"Found the nurse in charge, cleared onlookers and agreed one lead voice before engaging him","d":"Content"},{"c":"Kept a safe distance, side-on with a clear exit, and told him he would stay back","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Introduced self by name and role, stayed calm and thanked him for each step down","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Named his anger, fear and exhaustion out loud and validated them (empathy)","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Set a clear, non-threatening limit on violence and asked him to leave the chair","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Offered at least two real choices: oral PRN, quiet space, phone call","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Screened for physical drivers: substances, caffeine, sleep, akathisia, temperature, glucose","d":"Content"},{"c":"Added up PRN in the last 24 hours against the BNF ceiling before offering more","d":"Content"},{"c":"Was honest about leave and the medicine, with no false promises","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checked his understanding of what happens next","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agreed with the nurse what happens if talking fails, oral before IM, per trust policy","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Set observations, a review time and clear reasons to call back (safety net)","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closed with documentation, debrief for patient and staff, and a final check-in with him","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Room cleared and one calm voice first; his feelings named; real choices offered; physical causes and PRN totals checked; fallback plan agreed and written.","borderline":"Calm and kind with him, but PRN totals or physical causes are missed, or the fallback plan stays vague.","fail":"Walks straight in among the audience, argues about the section, threatens or jumps to IM, or never checks what he has already had."}},"c-0-1-2":{"setup":"Thursday 02:00, female acute ward. A 34-year-old woman with schizophrenia has punched a nurse and is banging her head. She refused oral lorazepam ten minutes ago and staff are holding her. Decide on rapid tranquillisation, prescribe safely and set up monitoring.","role":{"who":"Sam Okafor, nurse in charge; the patient is Ms Leanne Price, 34, female, on Section 2 (Rachel is the injured nurse)","open":"Nurse in charge: Thanks for coming. She's being held and she's still fighting. Can you just write up an IM?","manner":"The nurse is tired and wants speed but responds to clear reasons. Leanne screams, then is frightened and paranoid; she sobs and quietens once she is told plainly what will happen.","free":["She punched Rachel and has been banging her head on the wall","She refused oral lorazepam at 01:50","Three staff are holding her on her side on a mattress","The day team say haloperidol 'works best' for her"],"ifasked":["Section 2, admitted Monday (three days ago)","No ECG on file: she refused one on admission","Lorazepam 1 mg orally at 16:00; regular aripiprazole this morning","No leave, so no alcohol or street drugs","Notes record an acute dystonia on haloperidol years ago","A red mark on her forehead from the wall","The emergency bag, oxygen and flumazenil are on the ward"],"worries":["Nurse: why not just give haloperidol?","Nurse: what if one dose doesn't work?","Patient: 'You're poisoning me'","Injured nurse: 'I should have seen it coming'"],"rules":["Nurse pushes for haloperidol until given a clear reason","Patient refuses the tablet and keeps head-banging whatever is said","Patient quietens if spoken to by name and told what will happen","Injured nurse blames herself and relaxes when reassured"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Make the hold safe","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Shah, on call. First, is she safe in the hold? Is she face down, and is anyone's weight on her chest or back?","m":[1,13]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: She's on her side on the mattress. Three of us. No weight on her chest."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Good. Keep her on her side. Who's watching her breathing and colour?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Our healthcare assistant is at her head."},{"t":"0:30","band":"Legal authority","w":"D","s":"What's her legal status, and since when?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Section 2. She came in on Monday."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"So day three. Section 63 covers treatment for her mental disorder without consent, and no T2 or T3 is needed yet. I'll still try for her agreement first.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Fine. Can you just write up the IM?"},{"t":"1:00","band":"Choose the drug","w":"D","s":"I will, but I need a few facts so I don't make this worse. Is there an ECG on file? Any heart history?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: She refused an ECG on admission. Nothing about her heart that I know of."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"What has she had in the last 24 hours, tablets and injections, including the day shift?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Lorazepam 1 mg at four. Her usual aripiprazole this morning. She refused the lorazepam just now."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Any alcohol or drugs? Any bad reaction to antipsychotics, like a neck spasm?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: No leave, so no. Her notes say she had a dystonia on haloperidol years ago."},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: The day team said haloperidol always works best for her, though."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"I hear you, and I know you want this over safely for everyone. NICE asks for an ECG before IM haloperidol, and she's reacted to it before. IM lorazepam 2 mg is safer tonight. That keeps her at 3 mg today, under the 4 mg ceiling. I'll write that reasoning down.","m":[10,4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Okay. That makes sense."},{"t":"2:25","band":"Offer oral once more","w":"D","s":"Leanne, I'm one of the doctors. I can see you're really distressed. We're not going to let you hurt yourself.","m":[5,4]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Patient: Don't you dare stick a needle in me!"},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"I hear you. You don't want an injection. Will you take a tablet instead? It's the same medicine you had this afternoon.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Patient: You're poisoning me. You want me dead."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying, to believe that. Nobody here wants to harm you. It's lorazepam. It helps people feel calmer. The tablet is still your choice.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Patient: Just leave me alone. I want it to stop. (bangs her head on the mattress)"},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Leanne, you're hurting yourself and you can't take the tablet right now, so we're going to give you an injection to help you calm down. It's lorazepam. A nurse will stay with you the whole time.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Patient: (sobbing) I just want it to stop."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"It will. You should start to feel calmer in a few minutes, and we'll keep checking on you.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"3:40","band":"Prescribe and monitor","w":"D","s":"Sam, I've written lorazepam 2 mg IM once now. Flumazenil is on the chart too, and procyclidine IM as needed in case her aripiprazole causes a dystonia.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: When would we give the flumazenil?"},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"If her breathing rate drops below 10 a minute, or you can't rouse her. Give it as charted and call me straight away. It wears off within about an hour, so keep watching afterwards.","m":[7,6]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: And obs?"},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"NEWS2 every 15 minutes for at least an hour, then hourly until she's alert and walking. If she's asleep or refuses, record breathing, colour and responsiveness by sight.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Her forehead's red from the wall."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"Then neuro obs too. If she's drowsier than the lorazepam explains, vomits or has any new weakness, she goes to A&E. I'll examine her head once she's settled.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: And if this doesn't work?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"A second dose would take her over 4 mg, so call me before anything else. I'd speak to the on-call consultant. The day team should get an ECG and bloods tomorrow.","m":[10,7]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Given at 02:12. She's settling."},{"t":"5:10","band":"The nurse who was hurt","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Can I have a word with Rachel?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Injured nurse: I'm alright. I should have seen it coming. I was standing too close."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. That must have been a real shock. You didn't cause this. Let's get your cheek looked at, an incident form done, and occupational health in the morning.","m":[9,4]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Injured nurse: Do I have to report it to the police?"},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"That's your choice, and we'll support you either way. Take a break now; Sam's covering.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:50","band":"Recap and close","w":"D","s":"Sam, let's recap so we agree. Lorazepam 2 mg IM at 02:12. NEWS2 every 15 minutes for an hour. Flumazenil if breathing is under 10. Procyclidine for dystonia. Neuro obs. Call me for any worry. Have I missed anything?","m":[11,7]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: No, that's it. What about tomorrow?"},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Her named nurse should debrief her within the next few days: what she noticed, what helped, and what she'd want next time, written as an advance statement. Please record the hold as a use of force.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Will do."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"I'll tell her consultant at handover, and I'll be back to see her at three. Ring me sooner if anything changes.","m":[12,7]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Thanks, Doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Checked her position in the hold, airway, and that no weight is on her chest or back","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Confirmed legal status and authority to treat (Section 63, within three months) before prescribing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checked ECG, heart history, past reactions, substances and 24-hour benzodiazepine total","d":"Content"},{"c":"Named distress and fear in the patient and staff, and validated it (empathy)","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offered oral medication again and told her plainly what would happen and why","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Prescribed flumazenil and procyclidine at the same time as the IM dose","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Set NEWS2 frequency, a breathing-rate trigger below 10 and who to call","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Recognised possible head injury and set neuro obs and A&E triggers","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Supported the injured nurse: check-up, incident form, occupational health, police her choice","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Chose IM lorazepam to fit ECG status and totals, explained why, and recorded it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checked shared understanding by recapping the plan with the nurse in charge","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Arranged a debrief and advance statement, use-of-force record, handover and review time","d":"Close"},{"c":"Introduced self and stayed calm and respectful under pressure","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Hold made safe, legal authority and ECG checked, totals counted, oral offered, lorazepam chosen with reasons, flumazenil and procyclidine charted, monitoring and debriefs set.","borderline":"Safe drug choice but legal status, 24-hour totals or the breathing-rate trigger are not checked or stated.","fail":"Writes up haloperidol with no ECG, never asks her status, leaves no monitoring plan, or ignores the injured nurse."}},"c-0-1-3":{"setup":"Saturday 16:15, PICU. A 22-year-old man on Section 3 was restrained for twelve minutes after attacking another patient. He is sweaty and breathing fast. Do the post-restraint physical check, order tests, and agree transfer triggers.","role":{"who":"Mr Kyle Mensah, 22, male, patient on Section 3 (with Dawn, the nurse in charge)","open":"Get off me. Nobody's touching me after what they did.","manner":"Hostile and exhausted at first, then frightened as he talks about the hold. Becomes cooperative once his fear is named and every step is explained. The nurse thinks he is 'just tired'.","free":["He felt he couldn't breathe while held","He is hot and his muscles ache","He wants to be left to sleep"],"ifasked":["He was face down for about four minutes with a staff member's hands on his back","He smoked cannabis on leave yesterday","Someone also gave him 'a pill' yesterday; he doesn't know what","He had IM lorazepam 2 mg at 16:00 (nurse)","Last ECG and bloods were two weeks ago and normal (nurse)","He hit his elbow on the floor but not his head","He has drunk nothing since lunch"],"worries":["Is he in trouble? Are the police coming?","Why does he need blood tests?","Will they hold him like that again?"],"rules":["Refuses to be touched until the doctor explains why and asks permission","Discloses the pill only if asked directly about anything other than cannabis","Becomes tearful talking about not being able to breathe; settles if validated","Nurse says he has 'settled' until shown the numbers"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Brief from the nurse","w":"D","s":"Dawn, before I go in: how long was he held, in what position, and was anyone's weight on his back or chest?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Twelve minutes in total. About four of those face down, with hands on his back, then on his side."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"Did he say he couldn't breathe? Did he get any medication?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: He shouted that once. Lorazepam 2 mg IM at four o'clock. He's just tired from fighting. He's settled now."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Maybe, but sweaty and breathing fast after a hold worries me. I want a full set of obs and a proper look now.","m":[8]},{"t":"0:55","band":"Meet him","w":"D","s":"Kyle, I'm Dr Patel, one of the doctors. How are you feeling?","m":[1]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Get off me. Nobody's touching me after what they did."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"I won't touch you unless you say yes. Is it hard to breathe right now? Any pain in your chest?","m":[1,5]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"I couldn't breathe. They were on my back. I thought I was going to die."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. I'm sorry it felt like that. That's exactly why I want to check you're okay.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"(quieter) My chest's alright now. I'm just boiling. My legs ache."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Did you bang your head? Feel sick or dizzy?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Hit my elbow. Not my head."},{"t":"1:55","band":"What he has taken","w":"D","s":"Have you taken anything in the last day or two? Cannabis, alcohol, anything else?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"A bit of weed on leave yesterday."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. Anything other than weed? Tablets, powders, anything someone gave you?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"It wasn't just weed. Someone gave me a pill as well. Don't know what it was."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful, and you're not in trouble for telling me. It helps me keep you safe.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"2:30","band":"Examine","w":"D","s":"Can I check your pulse, breathing, oxygen and temperature now?","m":[1,5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Go on then."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Pulse 118, breathing rate 24, oxygen 96 per cent, blood pressure 142 over 88, temperature 38.2. Alert and talking. That's a NEWS2 of 5.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Five? He looked fine to me."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"He's hot, fast and sweaty after a hold and possibly a stimulant. That can mean muscle breakdown or a dangerous overheating state. We treat it as medical, not as calming down.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Kyle, I'm going to check you from head to toe for injuries. Tell me if anything hurts.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"My elbow. And my shoulder where they grabbed me."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"A graze on your right elbow and some redness on both shoulders. Pupils are big but equal. I'm moving your arms and legs. Any stiffness?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"No. Just aching."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Muscles aren't stiff and no jerking at the ankles. You know where you are and the time. Chest is clear.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:55","band":"Tests and plan","w":"D","s":"I'd like an ECG and some blood tests. One test, CK, shows if muscles have been damaged by the struggle. Is that okay?","m":[7,11]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"Why? I told you I'm fine."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Muscle damage can hurt the kidneys and doesn't always show at first. If we catch it early, fluids usually fix it.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Fine. Get it over with."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Dawn, I'm sending CK, U&E, creatinine, FBC and glucose, plus a urine dip. ECG now. Repeat CK at 24 hours, because it peaks later.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Plenty of oral fluids on a chart, paracetamol 1 g for the fever, and no further IM antipsychotic while he's hot and we don't know his CK.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Okay. When do we worry?"},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"Obs every 15 minutes for at least an hour. Call 999 to A&E if his temperature keeps rising, he gets drowsy or confused, NEWS2 reaches 7, he gets chest pain or his urine goes dark. Call me if anything else changes.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Got it. Fifteen-minute obs, 999 triggers."},{"t":"5:20","band":"Close","w":"P","s":"Am I in trouble? Are the police coming?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"The ward manager will review what happened, and the other patient will be supported. Whether police get involved isn't my decision, but right now my job is your health.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Will they hold me like that again?"},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Face-down holds should be avoided and kept as short as possible. A nurse will go through what happened with you in the next few days, your side included, and what helps you stay calm. Does that sound okay?","m":[12,4]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Can I have a drink?"},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Yes, please drink plenty. So in your words, what's happening now?","m":[11,10]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Blood test, heart trace, drink lots, they check me every fifteen minutes."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Spot on. Dawn, I'll ring his consultant and the on-call consultant, and write it up: hold position, four minutes prone, twelve in total, injuries, findings and plan. Please record the use of force too.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Will do. Thanks, Doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduced self, asked how he felt and gained consent before touching him","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Established hold duration, position and whether weight was on his back or chest","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asked about rapid tranquillisation given and substances, including anything besides cannabis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validated his fear about not being able to breathe (empathy)","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Recorded a full NEWS2 with temperature and stated the score","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Did a head-to-toe injury check plus rigidity, clonus, pupils and orientation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Sent CK, U&E, glucose, FBC, urine dip and did an ECG, with repeat CK at 24 hours","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Challenged 'he's just tired' and explained the danger signs to the nurse","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agreed explicit 999 transfer triggers with the nurse in charge","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Started fluids and paracetamol and avoided further IM antipsychotic while hot","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explained tests in plain words and checked his understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answered his worry about trouble honestly and offered a debrief","d":"Close"},{"c":"Informed the RC and on-call consultant and recorded the use of force in detail","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Hold details, full NEWS2 with temperature, injury and NMS screen, CK with 24-hour repeat and ECG, clear 999 triggers, and a respectful explanation to him.","borderline":"Examines and orders bloods but forgets the repeat CK, the substance history beyond cannabis, or explicit transfer triggers.","fail":"Accepts 'he's settled', skips temperature or CK, gives more IM antipsychotic, or examines without consent or explanation."}},"c-0-1-4":{"setup":"Wednesday 03:00, PICU seclusion suite. A 41-year-old man with bipolar disorder on Section 3 was secluded at 02:10 after punching a window. He is on lithium and has drunk nothing since yesterday evening. Do the first medical review.","role":{"who":"Mr Darren Holt, 41, male, patient on Section 3 (with Tom, the nurse in charge)","open":"Let me out of this box now!","manner":"Shouting at the door at first, then anxious about his shaking hands. Calms over the conversation when spoken to respectfully and given a clear way out. Suspicious of anything staff hand him.","free":["He wants out now","His hands won't stop shaking","He says the nurse provoked him"],"ifasked":["Last drink was about 19:00 yesterday","He hasn't passed urine since the evening","He took his lithium at 22:00","No vomiting or diarrhoea","He feels a bit wobbly but not sick","He is not hearing voices; he felt 'wound up' by noise","No rapid tranquillisation given; he refused oral PRN (nurse)","Right knuckles are sore from the window"],"worries":["How long will he be in here?","What have they given him to make him shake?","Will they take him off lithium if he behaves?"],"rules":["Refuses drinks from staff unless offered a sealed bottle","Shouts threats at first; stops if his anger is acknowledged","Bargains about lithium; accepts an honest answer","Nurse asks the doctor to 'just sign it off'"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Brief from the nurse","w":"D","s":"Tom, thanks. Why was seclusion started, and what was tried first?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: He punched the window and went for a nurse. We tried talking and offered a tablet. He refused."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Any rapid tranquillisation? Obs since 02:10?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Nothing given. He wouldn't let us do obs. Breathing looks normal through the window."},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"When did he last drink, eat and pass urine?","m":[5]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Last drink about seven last night. No urine we know of. He's on lithium, last dose ten o'clock."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"That worries me. Dry plus lithium can push the level up fast. Can you get a sealed bottle of water? I'll come in with you and Ben. You go first and I'll leave first.","m":[5,2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Can't you just sign it off? He's still threatening people."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"I need to see him first. My job is to decide if seclusion is still needed, and if he's physically safe.","m":[9]},{"t":"1:20","band":"Face to face","w":"D","s":"Darren, I'm Dr Lee, one of the doctors. I'm here to check you're okay and see when you can come out.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Let me out of this box now!"},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"I can hear how angry you are. Being shut in a room like this is horrible.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"He wound me up. It's noisy out there all night. I just lost it."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard. I want to get you out as soon as it's safe. Are you hurt anywhere?","m":[4,3]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"My knuckles. And my hands won't stop shaking. What have you given me?"},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"We haven't given you anything tonight. Can you hold your hands out for me?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"(holds them out) See? Shaking."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"It's a fine shake, not a big coarse one. Have you been sick or had diarrhoea? Any slurred speech or feeling wobbly?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Bit wobbly. Not sick."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Can you walk to the door and back for me?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"(walks) There. Happy?"},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Thanks, that's steady. Your speech is clear and you know where you are. When did you last have a drink?","m":[6,5]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Last night. I'm not drinking anything you lot give me."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Fair enough. This is a sealed bottle. You can open it yourself.","m":[5,4]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"(drinks) ...Fine."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Have you passed urine since last night?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"No. Don't need to."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Your lithium can build up when you're dry. I'd like a blood test now for the level and your kidneys. Can I take it here, with Tom beside me?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"I'll stay calm if you take me off the lithium."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"I can't make that deal, and it wouldn't be safe to stop it just like that. I'll hold any more lithium until the result is back, and your team will review the dose in the morning.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"(holds out arm) Go on then."},{"t":"4:00","band":"Decision","w":"D","s":"Done, thanks. What would help you feel calm enough to come out?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"How long am I going to be stuck in here?"},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Honest answer: if you can sit and talk calmly with staff, with no threats, for about half an hour, we can start getting you back to your room. The nurses will check on you every two hours and I'll be back by seven at the latest. Sooner if you're calmer.","m":[10,9]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Half an hour. Okay."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"So what do you need to do to come out?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Sit, stay calm, no threats, half an hour."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"That's it. Keep drinking that water.","m":[11,5]},{"t":"4:55","band":"Plan with the nurse","w":"D","s":"Tom, he was threatening ten minutes ago, so seclusion continues for now. He's calming, so we end it as soon as he meets that goal. We don't wait for the day team.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: What about his fluids?"},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Offer about 200 ml in a sealed bottle every 30 minutes and keep a fluid balance chart. NEWS2 at each nursing review, or by sight if he refuses.","m":[5]},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Lithium level, U&E and creatinine are going now, marked as five hours after his dose. A level above 1.5, vomiting, diarrhoea, worse tremor, unsteadiness or confusion means we end seclusion and he goes to A&E. Call me straight away.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Got it. Do I tell anyone?"},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"I'll ring the on-call consultant now. Next medical review by 07:00. If it runs past eight hours, he needs an independent review.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Okay."},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I'll write it up: reasons it continues, his physical state, fluids, bloods, exit criteria and the next review. Darren, I'll be back. Is there anything else you need now?","m":[13,3]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"A blanket. And can someone ring my sister in the morning?"},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Yes to the blanket. We'll ring your sister in the morning if you're happy for us to. And an advocate can visit if you'd like.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Thanks."}],"marks":[{"c":"Asked the nurse why seclusion started, what was tried, and any RT or obs since","d":"Content"},{"c":"Entered safely with staff (last in, first out) and explained the purpose of the review","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Saw him face to face within the hour and asked about injuries","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validated his anger and distress at being secluded (empathy)","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Recorded fluid intake and urine output and offered a sealed drink","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checked for lithium toxicity: tremor type, GI upset, gait, speech, orientation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Took lithium level, U&E and creatinine, timed from last dose, and held further lithium","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agreed results and signs that end seclusion and trigger A&E transfer","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Decided with reasons whether seclusion continues, and declined to 'just sign it off'","d":"Content"},{"c":"Told him the specific behaviour that ends seclusion and the next review time","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checked he understood what he needs to do to come out","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Informed the on-call consultant and stated the 8-hour independent review rule","d":"Close"},{"c":"Documented the review, set the next medical review by 07:00, offered family contact and advocacy","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Seen within the hour, clear reasons for continuing, dehydration and lithium toxicity checked with bloods and transfer triggers, plain exit criteria and next review time given.","borderline":"Seclusion decision made but lithium risk, fluid balance or exit criteria are handled vaguely.","fail":"Signs it off without seeing him, misses lithium and dehydration, or leaves him in seclusion till morning with no reason or review time."}},"c-0-1-5":{"setup":"Sunday 23:10, adult acute ward. A 19-year-old informal woman admitted after self-harm has been found with a ligature, now cut off. She is on the floor, conscious but drowsy. Lead the first five minutes, decide on A&E, then plan her care.","role":{"who":"Ms Chloe Barnes, 19, female, informal patient (with Jess, the nurse who found her, and an A&E nurse on the phone)","open":"(hoarse) I'm fine. I don't need hospital. It was nothing.","manner":"Drowsy, embarrassed and apologetic, minimising at first. Becomes tearful with guilt, then more open when met kindly. On return she is tired, quieter and willing to plan.","free":["She says she is fine and doesn't want hospital","She feels she has caused everyone trouble","Her throat is sore"],"ifasked":["She took about ten paracetamol at around 21:00, saved from home","Her voice feels different and swallowing hurts","She doesn't remember the nurse coming in","She used a phone charger cable from her bag","What helps: talking to her friend Amy, music, cold water on her face","Warning signs: lying in the dark, scrolling, feeling numb"],"worries":["Are you going to tell my mum?","Why is someone watching me all the time?","Will she get kicked off the ward?"],"rules":["Refuses A&E at first; agrees once told why plainly","Discloses tablets only if asked directly","Cries when she apologises; settles if validated","Nurse who found her blames herself"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"First minute","w":"D","s":"I'm Dr Cole. Chloe, can you hear me? Can you tell me your name?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"(hoarse, eyes half open) Chloe."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Is it hard to breathe or swallow? Is your voice different?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Hurts to swallow. My voice is weird."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"Airway's open, she's talking. Breathing 20, oxygen 97 per cent. Eyes open to voice, a bit confused: GCS 14. Red mark round the neck. Keep her still, please.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Jess, how was she found? Was she hanging, and was she unconscious?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Cable tied to the bathroom door, kneeling, sort of slumped. She didn't respond at first, then came round within a minute."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"And when was she last checked?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Ten minutes before. She was on 15-minute checks."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Please call 999 now. Partial hanging with a blackout needs A&E even if she looks okay. One of you goes with her.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Calling now."},{"t":"1:25","band":"Tablets and consent","w":"D","s":"Chloe, have you taken any tablets or anything else tonight?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"...I took some tablets earlier as well. I'd been saving them."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That's really important. What were they, and when?","m":[4,6]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Paracetamol. About ten. Around nine o'clock."},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"I'm fine. I don't need hospital. It was nothing."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad you're talking to me. Your neck can swell up hours later, and the tablets need a blood test at the right time. That's why I want you checked at the general hospital. Will you go?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"(crying) I've caused everyone so much trouble. I'm so sorry."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"You don't need to be sorry. You were in a lot of pain tonight, and we want to help with that. Going to hospital is part of looking after you.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"...Okay. I'll go."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you. A nurse will stay with you the whole time.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","band":"Phone handover to A&E","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm the on-call psychiatry doctor. Sending you Chloe Barnes, 19, informal, admitted with depression and self-harm.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"A&E nurse: Go ahead."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Partial hanging at about 23:05, phone cable. Brief loss of consciousness, under a minute. GCS 14, hoarse voice, painful swallowing, neck mark. Also about ten paracetamol at 21:00.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"A&E nurse: Okay. Any risk we should know?"},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"High risk of further self-harm, so one-to-one from her escort. She's informal. Section 5(2) doesn't apply in A&E, so if she tries to leave, assess her capacity and call liaison psychiatry. I'll send the ligature details and her drug chart.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"A&E nurse: Got it. Thanks."},{"t":"3:45","band":"The team","w":"D","s":"Jess, please keep the cable and the room exactly as found for the review, and do the incident form.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Nurse: I checked her ten minutes before. I should have stopped this."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"You did your checks, and you found her quickly. That's why she's alive. Take a breather; Sam's covering. We'll have a proper debrief in the next few days.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Thanks. I'm a bit shaky."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"That's a normal reaction. Is everyone else okay? Let's make sure the other patients are settled too.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:30","band":"Back on the ward","w":"P","s":"Later, back from A&E at 04:30. Nurse: Bloods fine, neck scan clear. She's back."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Chloe, I'm glad you're back and safe. How are you feeling?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Tired. Why is someone sitting with me all the time? I can't even go to the toilet!"},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"That must feel really intrusive. For now a nurse will stay within arm's length, because tonight was so serious. The team will review it every day, starting tomorrow morning. We'll reduce it as things feel safer.","m":[10,6]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Are you going to tell my mum?"},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"You're an adult, so that's your choice. It might help to have her support. Shall we think about it together in the morning?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"Maybe. Not tonight."},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"That's fine. Can we write a plan together for when things get that bad? What were the first signs tonight?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Lying in the dark scrolling. Then I just went numb."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"What's helped before, even a little?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Talking to Amy. Music. Cold water on my face."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Let's put that down in your words. And can we check your room together for cables or tablets?","m":[12,10]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Okay. There's a cable in my bag."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. So, to sum up: a nurse stays close tonight, we write your plan in the morning with your named nurse, and you decide about your mum. Does that sound right?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Thanks for not being angry."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Not at all. Get some rest. I'll hand over to your team at nine.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Checked airway, breathing, consciousness (GCS) and neck within the first minute","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asked staff about suspension, loss of consciousness and when she was last checked","d":"Content"},{"c":"Called 999 straight away and arranged a staff escort","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asked directly about tablets or other self-harm tonight, and their timing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explained why A&E is needed despite her feeling fine, and sought her agreement","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Met her guilt and distress with warmth and no judgement (empathy)","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gave A&E a phone handover: status, ligature, blackout, tablets, risk, and plan if she tries to leave","d":"Content"},{"c":"Kept the ligature and room as found and started the incident report","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Supported the nurse who found her and checked on other staff and patients","d":"Close"},{"c":"Agreed an observation level with her and when it will be reviewed","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Answered her question about telling her mum honestly, respecting her choice","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Rebuilt a safety plan in her own words: warning signs, what helps, means removed","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarised the plan, checked her understanding and arranged handover","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"ABC and GCS first, 999 without delay, tablets asked about, clear A&E handover including legal status, staff supported, and a safety plan in her words on return.","borderline":"Sends her to A&E but misses the tablets, the legal-status advice for A&E, or the review time for observations.","fail":"Keeps her on the ward because she 'looks fine', uses a risk score to decide, or forgets the staff and her return plan."}},"c-0-1-6":{"setup":"Friday 21:30, adult acute ward. A 52-year-old man on Section 3 has not returned from one hour of unescorted leave at 18:00. His last relapse involved threats to his neighbour. Grade the risk, start the AWOL process, and tell the right people.","role":{"who":"Ms Carol Dunn, 56, female, sister and nearest relative of Mr Neil Dunn, 52 (with Mo, the nurse in charge, and a police call handler)","open":"Sister: Hello? Is this about Neil? What's happened?","manner":"Anxious and then angry at the ward for 'losing' him. Becomes tearful about coping. Calms and helps once her anger is heard and she is given a clear job.","free":["She is frightened something will happen to him","She is angry he was let out alone","She asks if he will be arrested"],"ifasked":["He rang her about 19:30 from somewhere noisy","He said the neighbour has been 'spying on him again'","He sometimes goes to the Red Lion pub near his flat","She has a key to his flat","He hasn't been drinking much lately, as far as she knows","She can't easily get to him tonight; she lives 40 minutes away"],"worries":["How did you lose him?","Is he going to be arrested?","What if he hurts the neighbour or himself?"],"rules":["Raises her voice at first; settles if her anger is acknowledged","Discloses the phone call only if asked when she last heard from him","Becomes tearful about coping; settles with validation and a plan","Police handler questions the high-risk grade until given specific facts"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Facts from the nurse","w":"D","s":"Mo, what exactly did his Section 17 form allow?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: One hour unescorted, local area, 18:00 to 19:00. Due back at seven."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"How was he before he left? Any hints he wouldn't come back?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Quiet. He mentioned his flat a couple of times. Nothing worrying."},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"Who's tried to reach him?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: We've rung his mobile six times. No answer. Nobody's been to the flat."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"What medicines is he missing tonight?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Clozapine 300 mg at nine."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"So he's missed one dose. If he's off clozapine for more than 48 hours we'd need to restart it slowly. Any alcohol or drugs history?","m":[3,13]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Drinks when unwell. Last relapse he threatened his neighbour."},{"t":"1:10","band":"Grade the risk","w":"D","s":"Then I'd grade this as high risk. He's on Section 3, he's two and a half hours late, he's missing medicine, and his last relapse put a named neighbour at risk. Do you agree?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Yes. I'll start the AWOL form."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Can you get his photo and description ready? I'll ring the police.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:40","band":"The police","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm the on-call psychiatry doctor at Riverside Hospital. I'm reporting a missing detained patient.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Police call handler: Go ahead."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Neil Dunn, 52. Detained under Section 3. Not back from leave due at 19:00. He has schizophrenia and has missed his clozapine. His last relapse involved threats to his neighbour, who we can name. We grade him as high risk.","m":[5,4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Police call handler: Is he really high risk? We're flat out tonight."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"I understand you're stretched. There's a named person at risk, a pattern of threats when he relapses, and no medicine tonight. That's a real risk of serious harm. Please check his flat and his neighbour.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Police call handler: Okay. Description?"},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"White man, about six foot, grey beard, navy coat. I'll email his photo and the AWOL form now. Can I have a reference number?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Police call handler: Reference 2111-0457. We'll send a unit."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Any officer can bring him back to us. Section 18 lets police or our staff retake him.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:05","band":"His sister","w":"D","s":"Hello, is that Carol? I'm one of the doctors at Riverside. I'm calling about Neil. He hasn't come back from his leave, and we're worried about him.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Sister: How did you lose him? You were meant to be looking after him!"},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"I can hear how angry and scared you are, and that's completely understandable. His leave was part of his care plan, but tonight it hasn't gone well. Our focus now is finding him.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Sister: (pause) Sorry. I'm just frightened."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Of course you are. When did you last hear from him?","m":[10,9]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Sister: He rang me about half seven. He said the neighbour's been spying on him again."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that really helps. Did he say where he was, or where he might go?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Sister: It was noisy. Maybe the Red Lion, near his flat."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"I'll pass that and what he said about the neighbour to the police straight away. That's justified because someone could get hurt.","m":[10,5]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Sister: Is he going to be arrested?"},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"No, he's not in trouble. The law lets police or our staff bring him back to hospital. On Section 3 that applies for six months, or until his section would end if that's later.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Sister: (crying) I can't cope if something happens to him."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"This is such a lot to carry. You're not on your own with it. If he calls or turns up, stay calm, encourage him to come back, and ring us. If anyone's in immediate danger, ring 999.","m":[9,11]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Sister: Okay. Should I go round there?"},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Please don't go alone tonight. Let the police check first. Can I give you the ward number?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Sister: Yes. Thank you for ringing."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"I'll update you when we know more. And there's support for you as his carer when this is over.","m":[11,8]},{"t":"5:40","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"Mo, police ref 2111-0457, Red Lion and his flat. I've told them the neighbour is at risk. I'll ring the on-call consultant now. Can you tell the MHA office and do the incident report?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Will do. What happens when he's back?"},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Full review on return: NEWS2, injuries, breath alcohol, urine drug screen if he agrees. Restart clozapine as charted if under 48 hours. Leave on hold until his consultant reviews it, not as a punishment.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: And if he rings the ward?"},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Talk to him kindly and ask him to come back or say where he is. Then tell the police. I'll write the timeline, the risk grade and who's been told.","m":[12,11]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Got it. Thanks."}],"marks":[{"c":"Checked the exact Section 17 terms: unescorted, duration, area and return time","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asked about his mental state before leaving and who has tried to contact him","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asked about missed medicine tonight (clozapine) and substance use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Graded risk as high using the relapse history and the named neighbour","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Reported to police with status, risk, description and photo, and got a reference number","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Held firm on the risk grade with specific facts when police pushed back","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Stated who can retake him under Section 18 and the time limit","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Rang the nearest relative and shared only what helps find him","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validated the sister's anger and distress without blaming anyone (empathy)","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asked the sister about recent contact and places, and acted on her disclosure","d":"Content"},{"c":"Safety-netted the sister and nurse: what to do if he calls, 999 for danger, ward number","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Informed the on-call consultant and MHA office and completed AWOL and incident forms","d":"Close"},{"c":"Planned a return review: physical health, substances, missed clozapine, leave on hold","d":"Plan"}],"global":{"pass":"Leave terms and contacts checked, risk graded high with clear reasons, police report with reference and photo, sister handled kindly, Section 18 explained, consultant and MHA office told, return review planned.","borderline":"Police and family told but risk reasoning is vague, Section 18 limits are wrong, or the return plan is missing.","fail":"Waits until morning, gives police a vague call, blames or brushes off the sister, or ignores the neighbour."}},"c-0-2-1":{"setup":"Monday 22:00, adult acute ward. A 30-year-old informal woman admitted two days ago with depression and suicidal thoughts is at the door with her bag. A nurse used Section 5(4) at 21:50. Assess her and decide: let her go, or Section 5(2).","role":{"who":"Ms Hannah Wright, 30, female, informal patient (with Ruth, the nurse in charge)","open":"You said I could leave whenever I wanted. That was a lie.","manner":"Angry and tearful at the door, says the ward is too noisy. Talks more openly when she feels listened to. Becomes upset but resigned when told about the holding power.","free":["The ward is noisy and she can't think","She feels misled about being able to leave","She wants to go home to her flat"],"ifasked":["Her mood has been 'black' all day; she had a bad phone call with her ex","She thinks about ending her life most of the day","She plans to take the tablets in her kitchen drawer tonight","She has about a month of amitriptyline at home","She lives alone; no one would be there","What has held her back so far: her dog, now with a neighbour","She knows going home could be dangerous but 'doesn't care'"],"worries":["What's a 5(2)? Is she sectioned now?","How long will she be kept?","Can she have a quieter room?"],"rules":["Refuses to sit down at first; agrees if offered a quiet room","Discloses the plan only if asked directly about suicide","Bargains for one night at home; becomes tearful when the doctor says no","Accepts the outcome more calmly if rights are explained clearly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Quick check with the nurse","w":"D","s":"Ruth, when did the 5(4) start?","m":[10]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: 21:50. The H2's done. She's at the door with her bag."},{"t":"0:15","band":"Hear her","w":"D","s":"Hello, Hannah. I'm Dr Ali, one of the doctors. I hear you want to go home. Can we talk for a few minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"You said I could leave whenever I wanted. That was a lie."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"I can see why that feels like a betrayal. I'm sorry it's come to this. I want to understand what's happened tonight.","m":[2,1]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"It's so noisy here, I can't think. I need to get out."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds awful when you already feel so low. Would it help to talk in the quiet room down the corridor?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"...Fine. Five minutes."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you. What made tonight the night you wanted to leave?","m":[1]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"My ex rang. Everything just went black again."},{"t":"1:25","band":"Mood and risk","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry. That sounds painful. How's your mood been today?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Bad. All day."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Sometimes when people feel that low, they think about ending their life. Have you had thoughts like that today?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Most of the day."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Have you thought about how you might do it?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"If I go home, I'm going to finish it. The tablets are in the drawer."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"What tablets are they, and how many?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Amitriptyline. About a month's worth."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"When were you thinking of doing it?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Tonight."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"What's stopped you so far?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"My dog. But the neighbour's got him now."},{"t":"2:35","band":"Home and insight","w":"D","s":"If you went home, who would be there?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"No one. I live on my own."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"What do you think the risks would be if you went home tonight?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"I know it's dangerous. I just don't care any more."},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Just one night at home. I'll come back tomorrow, I promise."},{"t":"3:05","band":"The decision","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much you want to be at home. I'm really worried that you wouldn't be alive tomorrow. So I can't agree to that tonight.","m":[8,2]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"(tearful) So what now? Am I sectioned?"},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"I'm using something called Section 5(2). It means you need to stay on the ward for now. Two doctors and a social worker will see you, probably tomorrow, to decide if you need to be in hospital.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"How long?"},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Up to 72 hours at most, but we want the assessment sooner. It doesn't let us give you treatment you don't agree to. You can appeal later if you're detained, and you can have an advocate.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"This isn't fair."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"I understand it feels that way. It's because your life matters. Can you tell me in your words what's going to happen?","m":[9,2]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"I have to stay. Some people come and decide if I get sectioned. Up to three days."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"That's right. Any questions for me?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Can I have a quieter room? And something to sleep?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"We'll look for a quieter room. I'll check your chart and offer a sleeping tablet tonight if it's safe. And a nurse will spend time with you.","m":[3]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"5:10","band":"Forms and team","w":"D","s":"Ruth, I've completed Form H1. Can you receive it for the managers? It expires 72 hours from the 5(4) start, so Thursday at 21:50.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Received. Thursday 21:50. Rights?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Please give her Section 132 rights tonight, in writing too. Within eyesight for now, reviewed at morning handover. Please check her room with her for anything she could harm herself with.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Will do."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"I'm ringing the on-call consultant now and the AMHP first thing for an assessment tomorrow.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"On-call consultant: Hello?"},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"I've used 5(2) on Hannah Wright, 30, informal, depression. She has a plan to overdose on amitriptyline at home tonight, no one at home, and she wouldn't stay. Could you tell her consultant? I'm asking the AMHP for a morning assessment.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"On-call consultant: Agreed. Well done."},{"t":"6:25","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Hannah, I'll write this up. A nurse will stay nearby, go through your rights, and you'll see the team tomorrow. I'm on call all night if you need me.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thanks for listening, at least."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduced self and listened to why she wants to leave tonight","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validated her anger, low mood and distress at the noise (empathy)","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offered things to make staying more bearable: quiet room, sleep aid, nurse time","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asked directly about mood and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asked about plan, means, timing and what has held her back","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asked who would be at home and what she would do there","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checked her own view of the risks of leaving (insight)","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explained the decision and Section 5(2) plainly: why, how long, treatment, rights","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checked her understanding and invited questions","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Confirmed 5(4) start time, handed H1 to the managers' delegate and stated expiry","d":"Content"},{"c":"Told the on-call consultant and requested an MHA assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agreed observation level and room check for means","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closed with a summary, Section 132 rights and what happens next","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Hears her out, asks directly about plan and means, makes a clear decision with reasons, explains 5(2) kindly, gets the 72-hour clock right from 21:50, and tells the consultant and AMHP.","borderline":"Correct decision but the risk enquiry is thin, the expiry time is wrong, or rights and observations are not addressed.","fail":"Lets her go without assessing risk, uses 5(2) without assessing her, times the 72 hours from the H1, or never informs the consultant."}},"c-0-2-2":{"setup":"Tuesday 21:30, on call. The nurse in charge says a 34-year-old man on Section 3 is refusing his evening olanzapine, saying staff are poisoning him, and asks for IM. Check the legal position, talk with him and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Grace, nurse in charge, then Mr Daniel Mensah, 34, man detained under Section 3","open":"He's calm but adamant. Says we're poisoning him. He's on a Section 3, so can we just give the IM?","manner":"Nurse: tired, wants a quick fix, accepts a clear reason. Daniel: guarded, suspicious and irritable; softens when asked about side effects and offered a real choice.","free":["Refusing evening olanzapine; believes something is being added","Section 3; came in on Section 2; first antipsychotic dose 94 days ago","T3 lists oral antipsychotics within BNF limits only","Calm, no violence or threats tonight"],"ifasked":["Restless legs, cannot sit still since starting olanzapine","Has gained about two stone this admission","Would accept a melt (orodispersible) from a sealed pack","Thinks the tablets are 'for nerves'; does not think he is ill","His brother called the police before admission","Would like an advocate","No PRN given tonight"],"worries":["Can you inject me anyway?","What happens if I keep saying no?","What right do you have to drug me?"],"rules":["Nurse pushes once for IM, then accepts a clear legal reason","Daniel gets angry if told he is ill or the poisoning belief is argued with; settles if validated","Agrees to the melt only if given a real choice and a sealed pack","Thanks the doctor if side effects are taken seriously"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, it's the on-call doctor, returning your bleep about Mr Mensah. First, is he a danger to anyone right now, or refusing calmly?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Calm, but adamant. Says we're poisoning him. He's on a Section 3, so can we just give the IM?"},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I understand, it's been a long shift. Before we decide, can you get his drug chart and any consent form kept with it?","m":[1,3]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Got it. There's a T3."},{"t":"0:35","band":"The paperwork","w":"D","s":"Thanks. When was his first antipsychotic dose this admission, counting any time on Section 2?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Nurse: He came in on a Section 2. First olanzapine was 94 days ago, then he went onto the 3."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"So he's past three months, and the clock started at that first dose, not the Section 3. What exactly does the T3 cover?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Oral antipsychotics, within BNF limits. Nothing about injections."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Then IM isn't covered. Has he had anything for agitation tonight, or made any threats?","m":[1,3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Nothing. He's sitting in his room, just won't take it."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"OK. I'll come and see him now. Please keep his obs as they are.","m":[1]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Fine. I'll tell him you're coming."},{"t":"1:30","band":"His view","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Mensah, I'm Dr Shah, the doctor on call tonight. Is it OK if I sit down and talk with you for a few minutes?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"If you're here to make me take it, don't bother."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"I'm not here to force anything. I'd like to understand what's worrying you about tonight's tablet.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"They're putting something in it. I've seen the nurse turn her back before she hands it over."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening, thinking something's being slipped into your medicine.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"It is. You'd refuse too."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"If I believed that, I'd feel the same. What do you think the tablet is for?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"They say it's for my nerves. I'm not ill. I'm only here because my brother called the police."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"OK. Apart from what might be in it, does the tablet itself cause you any problems?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"I can't sit still. My legs won't stop. And I've put on two stone since I came in. Look at me."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Restlessness and weight gain are real side effects, and they can be treated, or the medicine changed.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"(quietly, close to tears) Nobody's asked me that. I just get told to swallow it."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting, and I'm sorry. Your view matters here.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","band":"Choices","w":"D","s":"Would it help if the nurse brought a sealed pack, and you opened it yourself and checked the name?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Maybe. They could still swap it."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"There's also a melt version, the same medicine, that dissolves on your tongue. Would you consider that?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"I'd take a melt. Not that tablet."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"That's a fair option. I'll check it's allowed on your form and ask pharmacy for it.","m":[3,8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"And if I still say no?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Then nobody will force it on you tonight. One missed dose isn't an emergency, and your paperwork covers tablets, not injections.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"So you can't inject me?"},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"Not tonight, unless something urgent happened, like you were about to seriously hurt someone. That isn't what's happening.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"You've got no right to drug me at all. I'm a prisoner here."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"You're right to ask what gives us the right, and I can see you're angry. Your consultant will go through the rules with you tomorrow.","m":[6,10]},{"t":"4:25","band":"Rights and support","w":"D","s":"Because you're saying no, your consultant will ask an independent doctor, called a SOAD, to review your treatment. They'll talk with you too.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Independent from who?"},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"From this hospital. You can also have an advocate, called an IMHA, and a solicitor. Shall I ask the nurse to refer you to the advocate?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I want someone on my side."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"I'll ask her tonight. And I'll pass on the restlessness and weight so the team look at them tomorrow.","m":[7,10]},{"t":"5:05","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Just so I can check I've explained it clearly, can you tell me what happens next?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"No injection tonight. I can try the melt if it's sealed. An independent doctor comes, and I get an advocate."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Thanks for talking with me. If you feel unsafe tonight, ask the nurses to call me.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"Fine. Thanks for actually listening."},{"t":"5:35","band":"Plan with the nurse","w":"D","s":"He's calm and will consider orodispersible olanzapine from a sealed pack. Please check the T3 covers it and offer it in an hour.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Nurse: And if he refuses again?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Then record the refusal and don't force it. He's past three months and the T3 is oral only, so IM tonight would be unlawful.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Nurse: What if he kicks off later?"},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Call me straight away. Section 62 is only for genuine urgency, like preventing serious violence, and only the least we need, under the rapid tranquillisation policy.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Nurse: OK. Obs stay the same?"},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Yes, unless he gets agitated. Could you also make the IMHA referral and note his side effects?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Will do."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"I'll write up the first-dose date, what the T3 covers, his refusal and reasons, and that nothing was given without consent. I'll ask his RC to request a SOAD tomorrow.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Thanks, that's clear."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thanks for calling early. Bleep me if anything changes.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Checks with the nurse whether anyone is at immediate risk before discussing medication","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Finds the date of the first antipsychotic dose in this detention, including time on Section 2","d":"Content"},{"c":"Reads what the certificate authorises: form type, drug class, route and dose limit","d":"Content"},{"c":"Introduces self to the patient, explains role and asks permission to talk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks open questions about why he is refusing tonight's dose","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his fear and anger without arguing about the poisoning belief","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks directly about side effects such as restlessness and weight gain","d":"Content"},{"c":"Offers a choice he could accept: sealed pack, orodispersible form or a later offer","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains plainly that nobody will force medication tonight, and why","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the SOAD review and offers an IMHA and a solicitor","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks his understanding by asking him to say back the plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tells the nurse IM is unlawful tonight, when Section 62 could apply, and when to call","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Documents the legal position and hands over for the RC to request a SOAD","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Checks first-dose date and T3 wording, declines IM with a legal reason, gives him real choices, offers an IMHA and arranges a SOAD.","borderline":"Gets the law right but argues with the poisoning belief, or misses side effects or the IMHA.","fail":"Agrees IM because he is on Section 3, never reads the certificate, or lectures without asking why he refuses."}},"c-0-2-3":{"setup":"Thursday 10:00, ward round. Your consultant asks you to prepare consent-to-treatment paperwork for a 58-year-old woman on Section 3 with psychotic depression, whose 3-month point is next week. Assess her capacity for medication and for ECT, then agree the forms.","role":{"who":"Mrs Margaret Doyle, 58, detained under Section 3 (her consultant, Dr Patel, speaks at the end)","open":"I don't want anything. Give it to someone who deserves it.","manner":"Slow, quiet, flat, with strong guilt. Becomes tearful when called unwell rather than wicked. Engages a little more if treated gently and not argued with.","free":["Feels she deserves to suffer and to die","Drinking only a few sips a day, not eating","Believes ECT is 'electric shocks' that fry the brain"],"ifasked":["Dizzy on standing, passing very little urine","Knows the tablets are for depression and the voices","Can repeat back side effects and what ECT involves","Wrote an advance refusal of ECT years ago after her mother had it","Her daughter Claire may have it in a folder at home","No LPA or deputy","Thoughts of death, no active plan; would 'just stop eating'"],"worries":["I'm not signing anything.","Why would I need ECT?","Don't let my daughter worry."],"rules":["Says 'treatment is for good people' whenever asked to weigh benefits","Becomes tearful if called unwell; settles if given time","Mentions the written ECT refusal only if asked about advance wishes","Agrees to half a cup of tea if asked kindly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Good morning Mrs Doyle, I'm Dr Shah, one of the ward doctors. Could we talk for a few minutes about your treatment? Can I get you some water first?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"I don't want anything. Give it to someone who deserves it."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I can hear how strongly you feel that, and I'm sorry you're carrying it. I'd still like to sit with you, if that's OK.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"If you must."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"The law asks us to check your views about treatment at this point. There are no trick questions. How have you been eating and drinking?","m":[1,8]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"A few sips. I'm not hungry. I don't deserve it."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Any dizziness, passing less urine, or feeling faint when you stand?","m":[8]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"I'm dizzy when I get up. I hardly go to the toilet."},{"t":"1:05","band":"Medication","w":"D","s":"Thank you. That worries me, and I'll come back to it. You take sertraline and olanzapine. What do you understand they're for?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"The doctor said depression. And the voices."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"That's right. They help lift low mood and quieten the voices that say you're bad. Early on some people feel sick, sleepy, or gain weight. Could you tell me back what I said?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"For low mood and the voices. Might feel sick, sleepy, put on weight."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Exactly. What do you imagine would happen if you stopped them?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Nothing. I'm not ill. I'm wicked, and this is what I deserve. Tablets don't fix wickedness."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"I hear that it feels completely true. What I see is someone very unwell, not wicked.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"(tearful) You don't know what I've done."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Take your time. You don't have to tell me now. Is it OK to carry on?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"2:20","band":"ECT","w":"D","s":"The team is also thinking about ECT. This is a separate decision. What do you know about it?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Electric shocks. You'll fry my brain."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"That fear is very common. It's done under a short general anaesthetic with a muscle relaxant, so you're asleep. Usually twice a week. Some people lose memories around that time, and headaches are common.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Why would I need it?"},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Because it often works well and quickly in severe depression, especially when someone isn't eating or drinking. Can you say back what it involves?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Asleep, twice a week, might forget things. It works for others. Not me. I should suffer."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"So the risks and benefits make sense, but you feel you deserve to suffer, so they don't apply to you?","m":[4,6]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Yes. Treatment is for good people."},{"t":"3:25","band":"Advance wishes","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Have you ever written down wishes about treatment, or given anyone legal power to decide for you, like a lasting power of attorney?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Years ago I wrote that I never wanted ECT. After my mother had it."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"That's really important, thank you. Do you know where it is, or who has a copy?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"My daughter Claire might. It's in a folder at home."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"Would you be OK with me phoning Claire to ask for it, and to explain what's happening with your treatment?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"She can know. Don't let her worry."},{"t":"4:15","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"I'll be gentle with her. Can I ask, are you having thoughts about ending your life?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"I think I should die. I won't do anything. I'll just stop eating."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That's the illness talking, and we'll keep you safe. Staff will stay close by.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"4:40","band":"Explain the next steps","w":"D","s":"Because it's the three-month point, an independent doctor, a SOAD, will visit to review your treatment and talk with you. Your views will be written down.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"I'm not signing anything."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"You don't need to sign anything. You can also have an advocate, an IMHA, to help you have your say. Shall I ask for one?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"If you like."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"For your body: a fluid chart, blood tests today for your kidneys and salts, and pulse and blood pressure checks every four hours. Will you try a cup of tea with the nurse?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Maybe half a cup."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"That would help. So, what's going to happen next?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Bloods, a fluid chart. Another doctor comes. You'll ring Claire about the paper."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"That's it. Thank you for talking with me, Mrs Doyle.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:45","band":"Report to the consultant","w":"D","s":"I assessed each decision separately. She understands and retains information on medication and ECT, but her belief she deserves to suffer stops her weighing either. I think she lacks capacity for both.","m":[4,6,12]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Consultant: So which forms?"},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Not a T2 or T4. A SOAD for a T3 for medication. For ECT, a T6, but not if there's a valid advance refusal. She says she wrote one, and her daughter may have it.","m":[7,12]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Consultant: And if it's valid?"},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Then no T6, and ECT only in a genuine emergency under Section 62. Given her intake, can we request the SOAD today? I've started a fluid chart, NEWS2 and U&E.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Yes. I'll make the request this morning."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'll record both capacity assessments, refer for an IMHA, and update you after I've spoken to Claire.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the purpose and offers comfort such as a drink","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds to her guilt and tears with empathy, without arguing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains medication simply, then checks she understands and retains it","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests whether she can weigh the information, and names the part not met","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains ECT accurately, including anaesthetic, frequency and memory effects","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Assesses capacity for ECT as a separate decision from medication","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about an advance decision, a health and welfare LPA or a deputy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about intake and dehydration; starts fluid chart, NEWS2 and U&E","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts and plans","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains the SOAD visit in plain words and offers an IMHA","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks her consent before involving her daughter","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names the right forms: T3 for medication, T6 for ECT, blocked by a valid advance refusal","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding and closes with clear, recorded next steps","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Two separate capacity assessments naming weighing as the failed part, advance decision checked, correct forms, and physical risk acted on.","borderline":"Gets the capacity conclusion but treats both decisions as one, or misses the advance decision or her poor intake.","fail":"Uses a T2 or T3 for ECT, argues with her guilt, or ignores dehydration and suicide risk."}},"c-0-2-4":{"setup":"Saturday 19:40, on call. A 27-year-old man on Section 3 for mania is back 3 hours late from unescorted leave, smelling of alcohol, and demands overnight leave tomorrow. His mother has rung to refuse home leave. See him, decide tonight's leave, then call her.","role":{"who":"Mr Liam Carter, 27, detained under Section 3; then his mother, Mrs Sue Carter, 54, by phone","open":"Here we go. I was only a bit late.","manner":"Liam: loud, irritable, bargaining; calms when not argued with. Mother: anxious, guilty, tearful when she says no; relieved when her view is respected.","free":["Liam: had 'a couple of beers' and some weed at a friend's","Liam wants overnight leave tomorrow","Mother will not have him home","Mother: last time home he smashed the TV and scared his 15-year-old sister"],"ifasked":["Tripped on a kerb; small bump on the back of his head, mild headache, no vomiting","Actually three or four cans; drinks mostly at weekends, no withdrawal","Had a row with a bouncer, walked off, no police","Will give a urine sample if asked","Mother: no one is at risk at home tonight","Mother: has never been offered carer support"],"worries":["Liam: So am I being punished?","Liam: Why can't I have overnight tomorrow?","Mother: Will you tell him it was me?","Mother: Can you tell me how he is?"],"rules":["Liam denies being drunk unless asked kindly and specifically","Liam raises his voice if told 'no' bluntly; settles if validated","Mother cries when saying no; settles if told it is OK","Mother mentions the TV only if asked what worries her"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Liam, I'm Dr Shah, the doctor on call. I'm glad you're back safely. Can we have a chat in here?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Here we go. I was only a bit late."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Three hours is a while, and staff were worried. First, have you fallen or banged your head today?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Tripped on the kerb. It's nothing."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"Did you hit your head? Any headache, being sick, or blacking out?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Bit of a bump on the back. Not sick. Bit of a headache."},{"t":"0:50","band":"What happened","w":"D","s":"OK, I'll check that in a minute. What did you have to drink or take?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Couple of beers. Three or four cans maybe. And some weed, it helps me sleep."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being straight with me. How much do you usually drink over a week?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Weekends mostly. Not every day."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Any shakes, sweats or seeing things when you stop?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"No. I'm not an alcoholic."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Fair enough. Where did you go, and did anything happen with anyone while you were out?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"My mate's. Had a row with a bouncer. Nothing major."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Did anyone get hurt, or were police involved?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"No. I walked off."},{"t":"1:55","band":"Check him","w":"D","s":"Good. Let me look at your head and eyes, and the nurse will do your obs.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Fine. Hurry up."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"There's a small bump, no cut, and your pupils are equal. The nurse will check you every hour tonight. If you're sick or the headache gets worse, tell her straight away.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"2:30","band":"Leave","w":"P","s":"So, overnight leave tomorrow. I'm owed it."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"I hear how much you want time out. Tonight, because you came back late and have been drinking, you won't be going out again.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"This is a joke! I'm not a kid. I'll be perfect tomorrow, I swear."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"I get that it feels unfair, and being stuck on the ward is frustrating. It isn't a punishment.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Feels like one."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Only your consultant can grant or change leave. I'll write down your side and make sure they review it on Monday.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Monday? So no overnight tomorrow?"},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Not until your consultant has reviewed it. Staying off alcohol and weed here will help your case most.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Fine. Whatever."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Would you give a urine sample for a drug screen? It's your choice.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Yeah, OK."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Can you tell me what happens tonight, so I know I've been clear?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"No leave. Nurse checks my head. Consultant decides on Monday."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll record your late return and what you took, and flag it for your consultant.","m":[8,13]},{"t":"4:15","band":"His mother","w":"D","s":"Mrs Carter, it's Dr Shah, the doctor on call. You rang the ward earlier. Is now a good time?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Mother: Yes. Is he back?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"He's back on the ward. I'm calling to hear your concerns. What's worrying you?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Mother: I can't have him home. Last time he smashed the TV and scared his sister. She's fifteen."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me, that matters for planning. Is anyone at home at risk right now?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Mother: No, not while he's there. (crying) I feel terrible saying no. He's my son."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"That's a really hard thing to say about your son, and it's OK to say it. You know your limits. No for now doesn't mean no forever.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"Mother: Will you tell him it was me?"},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"We'll agree with you first how it's shared. We can say home leave isn't possible right now without naming you. Home leave won't happen without your agreement.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Mother: Thank you. Can you tell me how he is?"},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"I can't share details without his agreement, but I'll ask him. I'm always happy to listen to anything you want to tell us.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Mother: I understand."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Has anyone offered you support for yourself? You can ask the council for a carer's assessment, and there's a carers' group.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Mother: No one's mentioned that."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"I'll ask the ward social worker to call you on Monday about both. Is that OK?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Mother: Yes, please."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"If you're ever worried about safety at home, call 999. Otherwise ring the ward any time. Thank you for talking to me.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and greets him calmly on his return","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about falls and head injury, then checks scalp, pupils and GCS","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks what and how much he drank and used, and about withdrawal","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what happened on leave, including conflict and police contact","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Tells him leave is off tonight and only the RC can change it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates his frustration without arguing or threatening","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks his understanding of tonight's plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Arranges neuro obs, drug screen with consent, and records the late return","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Listens to the mother's concerns and asks about risk at home now","d":"Content"},{"c":"Confirms home leave needs her agreement and keeps his details confidential","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds to the mother's guilt and tears with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers a carer's assessment and carer support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes both talks with clear next steps and a safety net","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Checks his head and intake first, stops leave with the RC named as decider, and supports his mother while keeping confidentiality.","borderline":"Right decision but skips the head injury check, or listens to the mother without offering carer support.","fail":"Grants or promises leave, misses a possible head injury, or breaches confidentiality with the mother."}},"c-0-2-5":{"setup":"Sunday 01:30, on call in A&E majors; the 136 suite is full. Police have brought a 41-year-old man under Section 136, found near a busy road saying he wanted to die. He is drunk with a forehead cut. Make it safe and lawful.","role":{"who":"PC Amy Jones, police officer; then Mr Mark Ellis, 41, detained under Section 136","open":"About 01:20. We've been waiting for the suite, but it's full.","manner":"Officer: polite, keen to leave. Mark: slurred, defensive, then low and tearful; trusts the doctor once treated with respect.","free":["Arrived at A&E at about 01:20","Found by a dual carriageway saying he wanted to die","Drunk, cut forehead from a fall, no weapons, calm with police"],"ifasked":["Took two packets of paracetamol at about 21:00","Wife left in March; lost his job","Was thinking of ending his life by the road","No other self-harm tonight","Brother could support him","Does not want his wife told"],"worries":["Mark: Why am I under arrest?","Mark: Can I go after?","Officer: How long do we need to stay?"],"rules":["Mark tries to leave once; stays if calmly told the law and offered care","Discloses the overdose only if asked directly about tablets","Becomes tearful when asked about loneliness; settles if validated","Officer agrees to leave only if one-to-one staffing and a call-back plan are clear"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Shah, the psychiatry doctor on call. What time did you arrive here with him?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Officer: About 01:20. We waited for the suite, but it's full."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Thanks. So his 24 hours started at 01:20. What happened tonight?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Officer: Found him by the dual carriageway saying he wanted to die. He's had a lot to drink. Cut his head when he fell."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Any weapons, violence, or anything about tablets?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Officer: No weapons. Calm with us. He didn't mention tablets."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"OK. His head comes first. I'll ask the A&E doctor to check his GCS and the wound before any mental health assessment.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Officer: How long do we need to stay?"},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Let me see him first, then we'll agree that with the nurse in charge.","m":[10]},{"t":"1:20","band":"Meet him","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Shah, one of the hospital doctors. Can you tell me your name?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Mark. Why am I under arrest? I haven't done anything."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"You're not under arrest, Mark, and you haven't done anything wrong. The police were worried about your safety. Under Section 136 you stay here until a doctor and a social worker have seen you.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"I'm walking out. You can't keep me here."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"I understand you want to go. The law says you stay until you've been seen, up to 24 hours. Let's sort your head first, then talk.","m":[4,8]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"(tearful) What's the point. Nobody would notice if I was gone."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a really lonely place to be. I'm sorry tonight's been so hard.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"My wife left in March. Then I lost my job. Then tonight."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"That's a lot of loss in a short time. Can I ask, did you take anything tonight, any tablets?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"I took a load of paracetamol before I went out."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me, that changes things. How many, and what time?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Two packets. About nine."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"That's important, and it can be treated. I'll tell the A&E doctor now so they check your blood. Have you hurt yourself any other way?","m":[3,6]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"Were you thinking of ending your life by the road?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"I don't know. I was thinking about it. Then the police came."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. We'll talk properly when you're feeling clearer. For now, someone will stay with you.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:55","band":"Roles and timings","w":"D","s":"He's disclosed about 32 paracetamol at 21:00. A&E are doing GCS, paracetamol and salicylate levels and the head wound first.","m":[3]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"Officer: So when does your assessment happen?"},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"When he can engage properly. He doesn't need to be sober, just coherent. If his condition stops us finishing within 24 hours, the assessing doctor can extend by up to 12 hours. Not for staffing delays.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Officer: Who's doing it?"},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"I'm booking the AMHP and a Section 12 doctor now for when A&E say he's fit, probably early morning.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Officer: And us? We've got jobs waiting."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"I know you're needed. He's calm, the nurse in charge has given him one-to-one, and security know. You can hand over. If he turns violent, our protocol is to call you back.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Officer: That works. I'll leave the 136 form with the nurse."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Please leave a contact number too.","m":[10,13]},{"t":"5:25","band":"Explain to him","w":"D","s":"Mark, here's the plan. A&E treat the paracetamol and your head. Someone stays with you. Then a doctor and a social worker see you together. Can you tell me what happens next?","m":[9,11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Blood test, my head, then two of you talk to me. Can I go after?"},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"That depends on how you are. If you go home, we'll make a safety plan with you, the crisis team will see you within 72 hours, and we'll write to your GP.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"I don't want my wife told."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"We won't contact her without asking you. Is there someone you'd want with you?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"My brother maybe."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"We'll talk about that. If you feel like harming yourself before then, tell the staff straight away.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"OK. Thanks for not treating me like a criminal."},{"t":"6:45","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"You're welcome. I'll come back once your bloods are back. I'm recording the 01:20 start, the overdose, who's with you and the AMHP booking for the day team.","m":[1,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Confirms the arrival time and records when the 24 hours started","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks the police what happened, including weapons, violence and tablets","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Puts the head wound, GCS and overdose care ahead of mental health assessment","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Introduces self and explains Section 136 to him in plain words","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his distress and losses before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks directly about overdose and other self-harm tonight, with amount and time","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal intent behind tonight's events","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains when he can be assessed and when a 12-hour extension is allowed","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Books the AMHP and a Section 12 doctor for when he can engage","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees who stays with him: police, A&E staff or security, with a call-back plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding of what happens next","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Outlines a safety plan, crisis follow-up and GP letter if discharged","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes with clear documentation and handover to the day team","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Records the 01:20 start, treats head and overdose first, books AMHP and Section 12 doctor, and agrees clear roles with police.","borderline":"Safe medically but vague on the clock or extension, or leaves police roles unclear.","fail":"Assesses mental state while ignoring the head injury or missing the overdose, or wrongly extends for staffing delays."}},"c-0-2-6":{"setup":"Wednesday 16:00. Your consultant is off sick. A 23-year-old man on Section 3 with first-episode psychosis has a tribunal at 10:00 tomorrow, and you have known him for 6 weeks. Check the report and rehearse with the covering consultant, then speak to him.","role":{"who":"Dr Anita Patel, covering consultant; then Mr Ryan Hughes, 23, detained under Section 3","open":"Go on.","manner":"Consultant: brisk, testing, supportive. Ryan: wary and defensive at first, then anxious; opens up if he feels he is being treated fairly.","free":["Consultant: report was written by Dr Rao, now off sick","Ryan thinks the doctor will say he is 'mad'","Ryan is nervous about speaking at the hearing"],"ifasked":["Aripiprazole went from 10 mg to 15 mg ten days ago","He has had three escorted ground leaves without problems","Still believes his neighbours watch him through the walls","Has had angry confrontations with the neighbours before","Solicitor is coming at 09:00","Only takes tablets because he has to; feels slowed down"],"worries":["Consultant: Is he dangerous?","Ryan: What happens if they let me go?","Ryan: And if they don't?"],"rules":["Consultant asks one question outside the doctor's knowledge; praises 'I don't know'","Ryan becomes anxious and quiet when talking about speaking; settles if reassured","Ryan admits taking tablets only on the ward if treated honestly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Thanks for making time, Dr Patel. I've read Dr Rao's report and checked it against the notes. Two things are out of date.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Go on."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"It says aripiprazole 10 mg; it went to 15 mg ten days ago. It says no leave; he's had three escorted ground leaves without problems.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Should we just edit the report?"},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"I'd rather not change her report. I'll write a short addendum, signed and dated today, with those changes and how he is now, and send it to the tribunal office and his solicitor.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","band":"Rehearse the evidence","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Good. Let's practise. Nature and degree?"},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"Degree: yesterday he told me his neighbours watch him through the walls, and he feels unsafe going home. It's a first episode, so there's no relapse history. I'd rely mainly on degree.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Consultant: The solicitor will say no incidents for four weeks."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"That's right, and it's real progress. But he still holds the beliefs, and he told me he's unsure he'd take medication at home.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Why can't this be managed in the community?"},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"He hasn't tested overnight leave yet, and his understanding of the illness is limited. Off medication, I think the beliefs could grow, and he's had angry confrontations with the neighbours before.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Is he dangerous?"},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"I wouldn't overstate it. There's been no violence. The risk is mainly to his health, with some risk of conflict with the neighbours.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Consultant: What if they ask about his housing?"},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"I'll say the care coordinator can answer that better. I'll only speak to what I've seen myself.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","band":"Who attends and aftercare","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Who's coming?"},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"You as covering RC, his named nurse Sam, and his care coordinator from early intervention. Can you confirm you're free at ten?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Yes, I'll be there."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"If he's discharged, Section 117 aftercare is ready: early intervention follow-up within 72 hours, CBT for psychosis, and help with his flat and benefits.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Good. Nervous?"},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"A bit. It helps knowing I can say 'I don't know' rather than guess.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Exactly right. Go and see him."},{"t":"3:35","band":"Meet him","w":"D","s":"Hi Ryan, I'm Dr Shah. Have you got a few minutes to talk about tomorrow?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"You're going to tell them I'm mad so I stay locked up."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"I can hear you're worried about that. I'll tell them honestly what I've seen, including how much better you've got. The panel decides, not me.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Who's on the panel?"},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"A judge, a psychiatrist who doesn't work here, and a third member with mental health experience. They'll hear from you, your solicitor and the team.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"(quietly) I don't know what to say in there. I'll mess it up."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"That's very normal, and lots of people feel the same. You only need to say what's true for you.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Have you met your solicitor yet?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"On the phone. She's coming at nine."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"Good. You can ask her anything before it starts, and you can ask for a break at any time.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:10","band":"His view","w":"P","s":"Honestly, I only take the tablets because I have to in here."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. It may come up tomorrow. What would make the medicine feel worth taking at home?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"If I didn't feel so slowed down."},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"That's fair. I'll ask the team to look at the dose and side effects. You can say that tomorrow too.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"What happens if they let me go?"},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"The early intervention team would see you within three days and help with your flat and benefits. That's set up.","m":[7]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"And if they don't?"},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Then you stay for now, and we keep working towards leave and home. You can apply again if the section is renewed.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Right."},{"t":"6:25","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me how tomorrow works, so I know I've explained it OK?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Solicitor at nine, hearing at ten. I say what I think. I can ask for a break."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll see you in the morning. Ask the nurses to call me if you want to talk tonight.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Checks the RC report against the notes and spots what is out of date","d":"Content"},{"c":"Writes a signed, dated addendum instead of altering the original report","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives first-hand evidence on nature and degree with a specific recent example","d":"Content"},{"c":"Answers on risk and need for hospital fairly, without overstating","d":"Content"},{"c":"Defers to the right colleague rather than guessing outside own knowledge","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Confirms who attends: covering RC, named nurse and care coordinator","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Has a Section 117 aftercare plan ready in case he is discharged","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Introduces self to him and explains the hearing and panel simply","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his worry and anxiety about the hearing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks he has a solicitor and knows he can ask for a break","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds honestly to his view on medication and invites his concerns","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks his understanding of how tomorrow works","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with clear next steps and support available tonight","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Addendum for out-of-date facts, short first-hand answers on nature, degree and risk, attendees and Section 117 confirmed, and he is prepared fairly.","borderline":"Evidence is sound but leans on second-hand facts, or he is not prepared for the hearing.","fail":"Edits the RC's report, overstates risk, guesses outside own knowledge, or has no aftercare plan."}},"c-0-2-7":{"setup":"Friday 14:00. A 30-year-old woman on Section 2 for mania started haloperidol 3 days ago. She now has a temperature of 38.2, sweating and stiffness, and refuses bloods and a drug screen. The nurse asks to hold her. Decide what is lawful and act.","role":{"who":"Tom, nurse in charge; then Ms Chloe Bennett, 30, detained under Section 2","open":"Temp 38.2, pulse 118, BP 150 over 95 and it's been up and down. She's sweaty and stiff as a board.","manner":"Nurse: worried, wants to act fast. Chloe: fast speech, suspicious, then frightened and tearful; agrees when she feels in control.","free":["Temp 38.2, pulse 118, BP labile, sweating, rigid","Started haloperidol 3 days ago","Chloe refuses needles and the urine screen"],"ifasked":["More muddled than this morning","Thinks her blood will be used for experiments","Can explain the risk back in her own words","Hates needles but does not want to get sicker","Would accept bloods with a trusted nurse and a hand to hold","Wants to go home"],"worries":["Is it the medicine making me feel this bad?","Nurse: Can't we just hold her under the Section?","Nurse: When does she go to the general hospital?"],"rules":["Nurse asks once to restrain; accepts a clear legal reason","Chloe refuses at first; agrees only if given choices and control","Becomes tearful about people wanting something from her; settles if validated","Never agrees to the urine screen"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Thanks for calling. Before we decide anything, what are her obs?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Temp 38.2, pulse 118, BP 150 over 95 and it's been up and down. She's sweaty and stiff as a board."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Is she confused or drowsier than before?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Nurse: A bit muddled, more than this morning."},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"With haloperidol started three days ago, I'm worried about neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Please hold the haloperidol and any other antipsychotic now.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Done. Can we just hold her and take bloods? She's on a Section 2."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"The section covers treating her mental disorder, not tests for a physical reaction like this. If she lacks capacity for the blood test, the MCA lets us act in her best interests, least restrictively. Let me talk to her first.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Nurse: OK. She's in her room."},{"t":"1:25","band":"Engage her","w":"D","s":"Hello Chloe, I'm Dr Shah, one of the ward doctors. Can I come in and talk for a minute?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"No needles. You're not taking my blood."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"I hear you, and you're allowed to say no. Can I explain why I'm worried, and then you decide?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Quickly."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"You've got a fever and stiff muscles since the new medicine. Rarely, it causes a serious reaction affecting the muscles and kidneys. A blood test tells us if that's happening.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"You'll use my blood for experiments. You're working with them."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening, feeling people might use you.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"(tearful) It is. Everyone wants something from me."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry. The blood goes only to our lab to check your muscles and kidneys. You can watch me label it, and I'll tell you the results myself.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Is it the medicine making me feel this bad?"},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"It might well be. We've stopped it already. That's why the test matters.","m":[2,5]},{"t":"3:10","band":"Capacity","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what's going on?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"The new tablets might be hurting my muscles. You want blood to check."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"That's right. What might happen if we don't check?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Nothing. I'm fine. Maybe I'd get sicker."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"That's possible, and it could become dangerous. How do you weigh that against not wanting a needle?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"I hate needles. But I don't want to get sicker."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Would it help to have a nurse you trust here, numbing cream, and you choose the arm?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Can I squeeze someone's hand?"},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Of course. So is that a yes to one blood test?","m":[5,8]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Fine. One. And I'm not peeing in a pot."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"That's fine, it's your choice and we won't force it. If you change your mind, just tell a nurse.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"I'll do the bloods if you let me go home."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"I understand you want to be home. I can't promise that, but the test helps you get better faster. Let's get through this first.","m":[6]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"So, what happens next?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Blood test with someone holding my hand. Medical doctors check me. You tell me the results."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Thank you, Chloe.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:20","band":"Medical plan","w":"D","s":"She has capacity for this decision and consents to bloods. She refuses the urine screen; please record that and don't push it.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Which bloods?"},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"CK, U&E, creatinine, LFTs, FBC, CRP and glucose, plus an ECG. NEWS2 every hour. I'm calling the medical registrar now about IV fluids and cooling.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Nurse: When does she go to the general hospital?"},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"If her CK is markedly raised, her NEWS2 keeps rising, or she's more confused. I'll agree that with the medical team.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Nurse: And if she gets agitated?"},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"No antipsychotics. Lorazepam 1 to 2 mg if needed. Call me straight away if she gets worse.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Got it."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"I'll record her capacity assessment and consent, send a Yellow Card, complete an incident form and tell her RC.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Thanks."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thanks for flagging it so quickly.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Asks for full obs and features: temperature, pulse, BP, rigidity and confusion","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names NMS as the likely cause and stops haloperidol and other antipsychotics","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains to the nurse that the section does not cover this test, and when the MCA would","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Introduces self and asks permission to talk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the danger in plain words and seeks her agreement first","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her fear without arguing with her beliefs","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers choices that give her control over the blood test","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Assesses capacity for the blood test: understand, retain, weigh, communicate","d":"Content"},{"c":"Accepts and records her refusal of the urine drug screen","d":"Content"},{"c":"Arranges CK, U&E, ECG, hourly NEWS2 and transfer criteria with the medical team","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks her understanding of what happens next","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Records capacity, reports via Yellow Card and incident form, informs the RC","d":"Close"},{"c":"Safety-nets: no antipsychotics, lorazepam if agitated, call if worse","d":"Risk"}],"global":{"pass":"Recognises NMS, stops haloperidol, persuades first, records a decision-specific capacity assessment, and gets urgent bloods and medical input.","borderline":"Right medical plan but gets her agreement by pressure, or is unclear on MHA versus MCA.","fail":"Agrees to hold her under the Section, misses NMS, or carries on antipsychotics."}},"c-0-2-8":{"setup":"Monday 11:00, CMHT office. A CPN rings: a 46-year-old man on a CTO is 10 days late for his depot, has covered his windows, shouted at a neighbour and refused the depot. Agree recall with the RC, explain it and plan the 72 hours.","role":{"who":"Jo Price, CPN; then Mr Andrew Kemp, 46, on a Community Treatment Order (his RC, Dr Patel, speaks briefly by phone)","open":"He's ten days late. Windows covered in foil, very suspicious, and he shouted at his neighbour while I was there.","manner":"CPN: concerned and practical. Andrew: suspicious, angry about the CTO, frightened about his flat; calmer once side effects and his flat are taken seriously.","free":["Depot 10 days overdue; refused it today","Windows covered in foil, suspicious, shouted at neighbour","Says the injection turns him into a zombie"],"ifasked":["Neighbour reports banging on walls at night","Fridge nearly empty","No weapons or threats of self-harm","Sleeps all day and hands shake on the depot","Believes there are cameras in the walls","Would prefer tablets","Will go in Jo's car, not an ambulance"],"worries":["How long will you keep me?","Will they take my flat?","Will they inject me again without talking?"],"rules":["Andrew refuses at first; agrees if side effects and his flat are addressed","Gets angry that the CTO 'was meant to keep me out'; settles if validated","RC agrees recall only if relapse and risk are described, not just the missed depot"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Jo, thanks for calling. Tell me what you saw today, beyond the missed depot.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"CPN: He's ten days late. Windows covered in foil, very suspicious, and he shouted at his neighbour while I was there. Refused the injection and won't come to clinic."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"Any threats, weapons, or risk to himself? Is he eating?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"CPN: No weapons. Neighbour says he bangs on the walls at night. Fridge looked empty."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Thanks. A missed depot alone isn't enough for recall, but this sounds like relapse with risk. I'll ring Dr Patel now.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:55","band":"Agree recall","w":"P","s":"RC: Dr Patel here. Jo's been in touch?"},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Yes. Mr Kemp is ten days late for his depot, suspicious, has covered his windows and is shouting at his neighbour. He needs hospital treatment, and without recall there's a risk to his health and of conflict with the neighbour.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"RC: I agree, both tests are met. I'll sign the recall notice. Can you and Jo serve it by hand?"},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Yes. Handing it to him makes it effective straight away, and we'll record the time. Does his CTO11 cover the depot on recall?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"RC: Yes. Check it again when he arrives."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Will do. We'll go together now.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:55","band":"At his flat","w":"D","s":"Mr Kemp, I'm Dr Shah from the mental health team, and you know Jo. Can we come in for a few minutes?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"I'm not going anywhere. That injection turns me into a zombie."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"I hear that it knocks you out, and that matters. What does it do to you?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"I sleep all day. My hands shake. I can't do anything."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Sleepiness and shaking are side effects we can treat, or we can try something different.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"(shouting) You said the CTO would keep me out of hospital!"},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"You're right, it was meant to keep you well at home, and I'm sorry it's come to this. You'd done well for a long time.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"There are cameras in the walls. If I leave, they'll take my flat."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. Your flat stays yours; a hospital stay doesn't change your tenancy. We'll lock up, and Jo will log your keys.","m":[5,9]},{"t":"3:25","band":"Recall","w":"D","s":"Your doctor has decided you need to come back to hospital for a short time. This is the recall notice, and I'm handing it to you now, at 12:10.","m":[3,7]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Why? I haven't done anything wrong."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"You're not in trouble. You seem frightened, you've had no injection for weeks, and things with your neighbour are getting worse. Hospital is the safest place to get you well.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"How long will you keep me?"},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Up to 72 hours to start with. In that time your doctor decides whether you go home on the CTO or need to stay longer.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"I'll take tablets instead. Just don't make me come in."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"That's worth talking about, and I'll pass it straight to your doctor. But today we still need you to come in.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"(quiet) Fine."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words why we're asking you to come in?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"I stopped the injection, I'm scared, and I keep rowing with next door."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. You can have an advocate and a solicitor. And if the CTO is ended and your section restarts, you'll get a tribunal automatically.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Can Jo drive me?"},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Yes. If you're happy to go with Jo now, that's the calmest way. Would you?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"I'll go with Jo. Not an ambulance."},{"t":"5:30","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"Jo, if he changes his mind, don't force it. Call me, and the AMHP can apply for a Section 135(2) warrant, with police to help bring him in.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"CPN: OK. Do I tell the ward anything?"},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"I'll ring them now. The 72 hours start when he arrives. They'll write the arrival time and deadline on the board and in his notes, and tell the MHA office.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"CPN: What about family?"},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"I'll make sure his nearest relative is told, and he's offered an IMHA when he arrives.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"CPN: Good."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Mr Kemp, thank you for agreeing. Jo will lock up and log your keys. I'll see you on the ward this afternoon.","m":[9,13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Just don't let them give me that injection again without talking to me."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'll tell your doctor that's what matters to you, and they'll talk it through with you first.","m":[6,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Gathers evidence of relapse and risk from the CPN, not just the missed depot","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks with the RC that both recall tests are met","d":"Content"},{"c":"Serves the recall notice by hand and records the time","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Introduces self and explains why the team has come","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his fear and anger about the CTO without arguing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about the side effects behind his refusal and takes them seriously","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains recall and the 72-hour period in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks him to say back the reason for recall","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Addresses practical worries: flat, tenancy and keys","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans safe conveyance, with AMHP, police and Section 135(2) warrant if he refuses","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Arranges for arrival time and the 72-hour deadline to be written where the team will see","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains his rights: IMHA, solicitor and tribunal after revocation; informs nearest relative","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with clear next steps and a promise he can rely on","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Recall based on relapse and risk agreed with the RC, notice served by hand, reason understood by him, safe conveyance, and the 72 hours tracked.","borderline":"Correct recall but vague on serving the notice or the 72-hour clock, or side effects ignored.","fail":"Recalls for the missed depot alone, threatens force first, or loses track of the 72 hours."}},"c-0-2-9":{"setup":"You are the liaison psychiatry doctor on call at 23:40. An 84-year-old woman on a medical ward is trying to leave. Assess her, decide which law applies tonight, advise the nurse in charge and ring her daughter.","role":{"who":"Mrs Kamala Patel, 84, patient on the medical ward (the nurse in charge and her daughter Anjali, by phone, also speak)","open":"I can't sit. I have to go home. Ramesh will be waiting for his tea.","manner":"Polite but restless, clutching her handbag, drifting in and out of focus. Softens with tea and talk of her husband; becomes tearful, then cooperative if not argued with.","free":["Her husband Ramesh needs his tea","She is perfectly well and needs no treatment","She will take the bus home","She has been married fifty-eight years"],"ifasked":["Gives her age and date of birth correctly","Thinks she is at the surgery or community centre","Thinks it is 1998","Loses track of the months after October","Cannot repeat back why she is in hospital","Nurse: new since tonight, clear at six, codeine given, bowels not open, NEWS2 2, temp 37.8","Daughter: only mild name forgetting before; LPA for property and finance only"],"worries":["Ramesh will think she has left him","Why won't they let me out?","Daughter: will she be all right? Should I come now?"],"rules":["Pushes towards the lift if told her husband is dead or argued with; settles if her feelings are validated and she is offered tea","Answers orientation questions but loses the thread after one or two exchanges","Nurse asks directly whether psychiatry can section her","Daughter cries on hearing her mother is looking for her father; settles with a clear explanation"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Shah, psychiatry on call. Thanks for bleeping early. Tell me what's happened since she came in.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: She was settled on the day shift. From about ten she got up, dressed, and now she's at the lift saying her husband needs his tea. He died five years ago."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"Is this new tonight, or has she been muddled before? And what are her obs?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: New, and it comes and goes. She was chatting clearly at six, and her daughter says she's normally sharp. NEWS2 is 2, temp 37.8. No bowels open since admission, and she's had codeine for her hip."},{"t":"0:38","w":"D","s":"That's really useful. Let's not hold her for now. Stay close, keep it calm, and I'll go and sit with her.","m":[2,1]},{"t":"0:46","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Mrs Patel? I'm Dr Shah, one of the doctors. May I sit with you a moment? Would you like a cup of tea?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","s":"I can't sit. I have to go home. Ramesh will be waiting for his tea. He can't cook for himself."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"You've always looked after him. Tell me about Ramesh.","m":[4,1]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"Fifty-eight years married. He likes his dal hot, not warm. Who are you? Why won't they let me out?"},{"t":"1:14","w":"D","s":"I'm a doctor here. Nobody's cross with you. Can you tell me where we are right now?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"This is the community centre. No, the surgery."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"It's the hospital. You came in two days ago. Can I ask a few quick questions to see how your memory is tonight? How old are you, and what's your date of birth?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:36","w":"P","s":"Eighty-four. Twelfth of March, 1942."},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"And what year is it now?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"1998? I don't know. Why does it matter?"},{"t":"1:47","w":"D","s":"That's fine, it's a strange time of night. Can you say the months backwards, starting with December?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"December, November, October... August? I've lost it."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"That's okay, thank you for trying.","m":[3,1]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"(tearful) He'll think I've left him. I've never once not come home."},{"t":"2:07","w":"D","s":"That sounds really upsetting, and it shows how much you care for him. You've been poorly with a water infection. I'll make sure your family know where you are tonight.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:17","band":"Capacity","w":"D","s":"I'll ring your daughter Anjali straight after we talk. The doctors think the water infection and being very dry have made you unwell. You need antibiotics and fluids through the drip. What do you understand about that?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"I'm not ill. I'm perfectly well. I just need to get home."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"If you went home tonight, how would you get there, and what would happen with your treatment?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:41","w":"P","s":"I'd get the bus. I don't need any treatment."},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back what I said has made you poorly?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"Said what? Sorry, I've forgotten. Is Ramesh coming?"},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"No problem. Thank you, Mrs Patel. Shall we have that tea in the day room together while I ring Anjali?","m":[1,4]},{"t":"3:03","band":"Advise the team","w":"D","s":"Her 4AT is 8: two errors on the orientation questions, she couldn't start the months backwards, and this is an acute change that fluctuates. That's delirium.","m":[3,5]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: The registrar asked if you can section her."},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"No. Her confusion comes from a physical illness and she needs medical treatment, so the Mental Health Act isn't the right tool. The Mental Capacity Act is. Likely causes to chase tonight: the infection, constipation or urinary retention, and the codeine. Could the medics do bloods with sodium, a bladder scan and a drug chart review?","m":[8,5]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: I'll tell him. So does she have capacity?"},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"For this one decision, leaving tonight and refusing the drip, no. She can't understand or retain why she's here, and she can't weigh the risks because she believes she must get home to her husband. That's caused by the delirium. I'm writing exactly that in the notes.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: And if she goes for the lift again?"},{"t":"4:01","w":"D","s":"Distract first: walk with her, tea, talk about her family. If she's about to leave and come to harm, you can stop her in her best interests. Least force, shortest time, and record what you did and why.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: We're two down tonight. One-to-one will be hard."},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"I know, it's a tough night. She does need one-to-one for falls and leaving. Could the site manager find you someone? With one-to-one and not free to leave, that's likely a deprivation of liberty. Please complete the urgent DoLS authorisation before I go, and send the standard request to the local authority.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"I'll help with the form if it's new to you. Just so we're agreed, can you tell me the plan back?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:43","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: MCA not MHA. One-to-one, distract first, minimal force if she tries to go, urgent DoLS now, and the medics chase bloods, bladder scan and her meds."},{"t":"4:53","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Call me back if she gets more agitated, falls, needs any sedation, or her NEWS2 rises. Medication only if everything else fails, and the lowest dose.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:03","band":"Daughter by phone","w":"D","s":"Hello, is that Anjali? I'm Dr Shah, a psychiatry doctor at the hospital. Your mum is safe. Sorry to ring so late. Is now okay to talk?","m":[1,10]},{"t":"5:13","w":"P","s":"Daughter: Oh God. Is she all right? What's happened?"},{"t":"5:17","w":"D","s":"She's physically stable, but the infection has made her confused tonight. She's been trying to go home to feed your dad.","m":[5]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Daughter: (crying) Dad died five years ago. She's never been like this. She does all her own shopping."},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"That must be really hard to hear. This is very common with infections in older people, and it usually improves over days as the infection is treated. Were there any memory problems before this admission?","m":[4,10]},{"t":"5:44","w":"P","s":"Daughter: Forgetting names now and then. Nothing like this."},{"t":"5:48","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Does anyone hold a Lasting Power of Attorney for her health and welfare, or has she written any advance wishes?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:56","w":"P","s":"Daughter: My brother and I have property and finance, since last year. Not health."},{"t":"6:02","w":"D","s":"That's useful to know. Could you come in tomorrow morning? A familiar face, her glasses and a family photo really help her get her bearings.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:11","w":"P","s":"Daughter: I'll come at nine. Should I come now?"},{"t":"6:16","w":"D","s":"No need tonight. A nurse will be with her all night, and the ward will ring you if anything changes. Does that make sense?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Daughter: Yes. Thank you for calling."},{"t":"6:28","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Nurse, I've written the 4AT, the capacity assessment for leaving tonight and the MCA plan. Capacity gets reassessed in the morning when she's brighter, and liaison will see her tomorrow. Anything else before I go?","m":[13,12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: No, that's clear. Thanks."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self slowly, uses her name, sits at her level and offers a drink","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gets the nurse's account: onset, fluctuation, NEWS2, new drugs, bowels","d":"Content"},{"c":"Completes the 4AT: alertness, AMT4, months backwards, acute change","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her grief and wish to go home without arguing about her husband","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names delirium and three likely causes for the medical team to chase tonight","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the decision simply and tests understanding, retention and weighing about leaving tonight","d":"Content"},{"c":"States which abilities failed, the evidence and the link to delirium, and documents it","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tells the nurse the MCA, not the MHA, applies and what proportionate force means","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees 1:1 observation and an urgent DoLS authorisation started before leaving","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Phones the daughter: baseline, LPA for health and welfare, asks her to visit tomorrow","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking the nurse and daughter to summarise","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety nets: when to call back, sedation only as last resort, reassess capacity tomorrow","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closes clearly: documentation done and liaison review arranged","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Calm, kind assessment that names delirium, records a decision-specific capacity finding, uses the MCA with 1:1 and urgent DoLS, and involves the daughter.","borderline":"Right framework but capacity is assessed globally, or the DoLS, LPA question or causes are vague.","fail":"Agrees to 'section' her, lets her leave, argues about her husband, or never tests capacity for the specific decision."}},"c-0-2-10":{"setup":"You are a psychiatry trainee on an acute ward at 15:00. A man detained under Section 3 has cellulitis with a fever and refuses transfer for IV antibiotics. Assess his capacity, advise the nurse and agree a safe plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Daniel Mensah, 41, man with schizophrenia detained under Section 3 (the nurse, medical registrar and his brother also speak)","open":"My leg is fine. I told them already.","manner":"Guarded and irritable, convinced the general hospital will poison him. Raises his voice when he feels disbelieved; calms when listened to and offered choices he controls.","free":["His leg is fine, just a bruise","The general hospital will poison him","He will never have a drip"],"ifasked":["He felt shivery last night","No blisters","He can repeat the explanation back accurately","He believes all medicine from that hospital is tampered with","He sees no risk from the leg at all","He trusts a nurse called Joe","He would take sealed tablets from the ward if Joe opens them"],"worries":["Everyone just wants to stick needles in me","You're trying to scare me","Nurse: does his section mean we can make him go?"],"rules":["Becomes louder if the belief is challenged; settles if his fear is acknowledged","Offers to take tablets only after a trusted-nurse option is raised","Dismisses sepsis as what 'they' want you to say","Brother supports oral treatment if asked"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Sam, I'm Dr Ellis. Before I see him, what are his obs now, and can I see the leg?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"Nurse: NEWS2 is 3. Temp 38.2, pulse 104, BP 128 over 80. Left shin's red and hot. He says the hospital will poison him. His section means we can make him go, doesn't it?"},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"Not for this, and I'll explain properly after I've seen him. Can we agree nobody uses force while I assess?","m":[9]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Fine. He's in the quiet room with Joe."},{"t":"0:36","w":"D","s":"Daniel, hi, I'm Dr Ellis, one of the doctors. I've come about your leg. Is it okay if I sit down and we talk for a few minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"My leg is fine. I told them already."},{"t":"0:53","w":"D","s":"You sound fed up with people asking. Could we look at it together? Then you tell me what you think is going on.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"Go on then. It's a bit sore. Hot. That's it."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"Redness halfway up the shin, tight and hot. Any pain much worse than it looks, blisters, or feeling dizzy or shivery?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"Shivery last night. No blisters."},{"t":"1:21","band":"His view","w":"D","s":"What do you think is happening with your leg?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"Bruised it on the bed frame. It'll go down."},{"t":"1:31","w":"D","s":"And what's made you say no to the general hospital?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"They'll poison me. The antibiotics. That place has been in on it since before I came here."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening, feeling people want to hurt you.","m":[7]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"(voice rising) Nobody believes me. Everyone just wants to stick needles in me."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"I can hear how alone that feels. I'm not here to argue or force anything. I want to understand, and tell you honestly what worries me. Is that fair?","m":[7,1]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"2:11","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I think this is cellulitis, an infection in the skin. Your temperature shows it's affecting your whole body. Without antibiotics it can spread into the blood. That's called sepsis, and it can make someone very ill or even kill them.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"You're trying to scare me."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"I don't want to scare you, but you deserve the truth. It usually clears with antibiotics. A drip in the general hospital works fastest. Tablets here might be enough, if the medical doctor agrees it's safe.","m":[4,8]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"Tablets here, maybe."},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back, in your words, what I've said about the leg?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Infection in the skin. Could get in my blood. Sepsis. Antibiotics."},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. And if I asked you again in ten minutes, what would you say?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"Same thing. I'm not stupid."},{"t":"3:12","band":"Weigh it up","w":"D","s":"Not at all. So how are you weighing it up: the risk of sepsis against having antibiotics?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"There's no risk from the leg. The risk is the poison. Everything from that hospital's been tampered with."},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"What do you imagine would happen if you had no antibiotics at all?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Nothing. It's a bruise. You said sepsis, but that's what they want you to say."},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"If tablets came from our ward pharmacy, sealed, and you watched a nurse you trust open the pack, would that feel different?","m":[8,6]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"Maybe. If Joe opens them. Not the drip. Never."},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"So what have you decided?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"No hospital. Tablets from here, if Joe watches me."},{"t":"4:05","band":"Legal position","w":"D","s":"Sam, his Section 3 lets us treat his mental disorder. It doesn't cover cellulitis, so it gives us no power to make him go.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"Nurse: So we just let him refuse?"},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Only if he has capacity. He understands, retains and can tell us a choice. But he can't weigh the risk: he's sure the antibiotics are poison and the leg is harmless, and that comes from his psychotic illness. So for this decision, right now, he lacks capacity, and the Mental Capacity Act applies. I'm recording that, with his own words as evidence.","m":[10,9]},{"t":"4:43","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Does that mean we take him by force?"},{"t":"4:48","w":"D","s":"No. Best interests means least restrictive first, and he's accepting tablets with Joe. I'll check with the medics that's safe.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"4:57","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Hi, it's Dr Ellis on Elm ward. 41-year-old man, left leg cellulitis, temp 38.2, NEWS2 3, no blisters. He lacks capacity to decide about transfer but accepts oral antibiotics here. Are tablets safe for now?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:11","w":"P","s":"Medical registrar: If no penicillin allergy, flucloxacillin 1 gram four times a day. Mark the edge, NEWS2 four-hourly, and transfer if it spreads or NEWS2 hits 5."},{"t":"5:22","w":"D","s":"Thanks. No allergy on his chart. I'll mark the edge now and review him at nine tonight.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"Nurse: His brother Michael visits on Sundays. Should we tell him?"},{"t":"5:36","w":"D","s":"Yes, I'll ring him for his view on what Daniel would want. If there were no one, we'd involve an IMCA for serious treatment.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"Brother: He's always hated hospitals. But he'd want the leg sorted. He trusts Joe. Tablets sounds like a good compromise."},{"t":"5:54","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that really helps. I've written down your view.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","band":"Safety net and close","w":"D","s":"Sam, if NEWS2 reaches 5, the redness passes the line, his systolic drops below 90 or he gets confused, call 999 and me. I'll reassess his capacity at nine and within 24 hours. Can you run that back to me?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Nurse: NEWS2 5, spreading, low BP or confused, 999. You're back at nine."},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"Daniel, here's the plan: tablets from our pharmacy, Joe opens the pack with you, and we keep checking your leg. I'll come back at nine. If it gets worse, we'll talk about hospital again. Okay?","m":[14,8]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","s":"Okay. As long as it's Joe."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"That's a deal. Thanks for talking with me, Daniel.","m":[14,1]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains why here and asks permission to talk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks NEWS2, looks at the leg and asks about sepsis or necrotising features","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores his own understanding and reason for refusing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains cellulitis, treatment options and sepsis risk in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks him to repeat the information back and checks retention","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tests use and weighing of the information and whether he can communicate a choice","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his fear without arguing with or colluding in the belief","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers least restrictive options: oral antibiotics, trusted nurse, sealed pack","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Tells the nurse Section 3 does not cover cellulitis and the MCA applies","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States which ability failed, the evidence and the link to his illness, and records it","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees the plan with the medical doctor: antibiotic, marked margin, NEWS2, review time","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Seeks his brother's views for best interests and mentions an IMCA if no one","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety nets clear transfer triggers and a time to reassess capacity","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closes by summarising the plan with him and keeping the door open","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Tests all four abilities for this one decision, names the failed one with evidence, uses the MCA not the MHA, and agrees a safe least restrictive plan.","borderline":"Reaches a sensible plan but capacity reasoning is vague, or safety netting and family views are thin.","fail":"Relies on Section 3 to force treatment, assumes incapacity from the diagnosis, argues with the delusion, or misses the sepsis risk."}},"c-0-3-1":{"setup":"You are the on-call psychiatry doctor at 21:00. A 27-year-old man has just been admitted informally with a first episode of psychosis. Clerk and examine him, request baseline tests and hand over before 22:00.","role":{"who":"Mr Tom Reid, 27, informal patient with a first episode of psychosis (Nurse Smith, the chaperone, also speaks)","open":"Yeah. I'm knackered. What do you want?","manner":"Tired, wary and short in his answers. Suspicious about why he is being examined and about drug questions; relaxes when given control and plain reasons.","free":["He has barely slept for two weeks","He is exhausted and wants it to be quick","He will not take his top off"],"ifasked":["Headaches at the end of the day, eased by paracetamol, no vomiting","Heart pounds sometimes","Uncle has a thyroid condition","Smokes cannabis daily, more lately to sleep","A few beers at weekends, no other drugs","Has lost some weight and is not eating much","No family history of sudden death"],"worries":["Do you think something's wrong with my brain?","Are you going to tell the police?","What are you doing with my blood?"],"rules":["Agrees to each part only if told why; refuses to undress but accepts examination through clothing","Asks about the police when drug use is raised; answers honestly once reassured","Complains about the bright light during fundoscopy but allows it if acknowledged","Nurse asks for night sedation and what to watch for"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and consent","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Okoye, one of the ward doctors. Are you Tom? Is it okay if I sit down?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I'm knackered. What do you want?"},{"t":"0:13","w":"D","s":"You look exhausted, so I'll be as quick as I can. Everyone admitted here has a physical check. I'd like to check your heart, lungs, tummy, nerves and the back of your eyes. It's to make sure nothing physical is causing how you feel, and that any medicine is safe. Is that okay?","m":[1,5]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"Why? You think there's something wrong with my brain?"},{"t":"0:39","w":"D","s":"Usually we find nothing. But some physical illnesses can cause poor sleep and feeling on edge, and I don't want to miss one. You can say stop at any point.","m":[1,10]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"Fine. Quick."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Would you like anyone else with you in the room? Nurse Smith can stay as a chaperone.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"Whatever. Yeah, she can stay."},{"t":"1:06","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'll note that you agreed, and that Nurse Smith is chaperoning.","m":[2,1]},{"t":"1:12","band":"Focused history","w":"D","s":"Any headaches, fits, blackouts, or problems with your eyesight?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"Headaches sometimes. From not sleeping."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"When do they come on? Worse first thing, or when you cough or bend down? Any sickness with them?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Just end of the day. Paracetamol sorts it. No sickness."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"Any fevers, chest pain, fainting or a racing heart? Anyone in your family die suddenly when young?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"Heart pounds sometimes. Nobody died young. My uncle's got a thyroid thing."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"That's useful, thanks. I ask everyone this: do you use cannabis, other drugs, or alcohol?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"Are you going to tell the police?"},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"No. This is about your health. Cannabis can bring on experiences like yours, so knowing helps us treat you properly.","m":[3,5]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"Weed, every day. Couple of spliffs, more lately to sleep. Nothing else. Few beers at the weekend."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being straight with me. Any weight loss, or feeling hot when others are cold?","m":[4,7]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"Lost a bit. Not eating much."},{"t":"2:32","band":"Examination","w":"D","s":"Okay. Hands first. Pulse 92 and regular. Arms out, fingers spread. A slight tremor, palms not sweaty.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"I'm shaking 'cause I'm tired."},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"Could well be. Blood pressure 128 over 78. Now heart and chest. Can you lift your top, or I can listen through it if you'd rather?","m":[6,5]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"I'm not taking it off. Do it through."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"No problem. Heart sounds normal, no murmur. Deep breaths for me. Chest is clear.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"Told you I'm fine."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Lie back for your tummy, and tell me if anything hurts. Soft, not tender, no enlarged liver.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"Ticklish."},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"Swallow some water while I feel your neck. No lump in the thyroid, and your eyes look normal, no staring or bulging.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"What's that for?"},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"Thyroid problems can cause a racing heart, weight loss and feeling wired, so with your uncle's history it's worth checking.","m":[10,7]},{"t":"3:43","w":"P","s":"Huh. Okay."},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"Now your nerves. Follow my finger. Smile. Screw your eyes shut. Good. Let your arms go floppy: no stiffness. Strength and reflexes normal, toes go down. Walk to the door and back?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"(walks) Like that?"},{"t":"4:02","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Last bit. I'll dim the lights and look at the back of your eyes with a bright light. About a minute. Look at that spot on the wall.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Too bright. Do you have to?"},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"I know it's uncomfortable. It's the one check that shows pressure behind the eyes. Nearly done. Both discs look sharp and healthy.","m":[9,5]},{"t":"4:29","band":"Explain and tests","w":"D","s":"Everything looked normal tonight, which is reassuring. I'd like some bloods, a heart tracing and a urine test, including for drugs. Okay?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"What are you doing with my blood?"},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"It only goes to the hospital lab: thyroid, sugar, salts, liver, cholesterol, a hormone called prolactin and an infection screen. It makes sure any medicine is safe for you. Can you tell me in your words why we're doing these?","m":[11,10]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"To check it's not my body causing it, and the meds are safe."},{"t":"5:06","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Nurse Smith, I'll take the bloods and read the ECG myself before prescribing. Please send the urine when he can; the night doctor chases that. I'll ask the day team to chase bloods at the 9 o'clock ward round.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Okay. What would worry you overnight?"},{"t":"5:27","band":"Red flags","w":"D","s":"A new or worse headache, vomiting, a fit, fever, confusion that comes and goes, or new weakness. Any of those, call the on-call doctor at once. They'd need the medical registrar and probably a CT scan.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Got it. Can you write him something to sleep?"},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"Once I've seen the ECG I'll chart zopiclone 7.5 at night, short-term. No antipsychotic tonight; the consultant will talk options through with him tomorrow.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:57","band":"Handover and close","w":"D","s":"Handover before ten: full examination done with consent, chaperoned, all normal including both fundi. Bloods and ECG done, urine pending. NEWS2 four-hourly, sleep chart, weight and waist in the morning. Anything left goes to the day team. Can you read that back?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:14","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Examination normal, fundi normal, bloods and ECG done, urine pending, four-hourly obs, sleep chart, weight in the morning."},{"t":"6:23","w":"D","s":"Spot on. Tom, thanks for letting me do all that when you're shattered. If you get headaches, feel feverish or anything feels wrong in your body, tell the nurses any time. Any questions?","m":[14,13,5]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","s":"Can I sleep now?"},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Yes. The nurse will bring something to help. Sleep well.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains why a physical check matters and gains consent","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers a chaperone, names them and says the offer will be recorded","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about cannabis, other drugs and alcohol without judgement","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens headache, fits, vision, fever, palpitations and family sudden death","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Acknowledges tiredness and discomfort and adapts the examination","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Examines pulse, blood pressure, heart and lungs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Examines abdomen and thyroid, including tremor and eye signs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Examines cranial nerves, tone, power, reflexes, plantars and gait","d":"Content"},{"c":"Performs fundoscopy on both eyes and states the finding","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains purpose and findings in plain words and checks his understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Requests bloods, ECG and urine drug screen and names who chases each","d":"Plan"},{"c":"States organic red flags and the action if any appear","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Hands over observations, outstanding items and safety net before 22:00","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes warmly, invites questions and tells him how to raise concerns","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Consented, chaperoned examination of every system including fundi, baseline tests with named owners, and a clear handover with red flags.","borderline":"Examination done but fundoscopy, thyroid or neurology is skimped, or the handover lacks who chases what.","fail":"Skips the examination or examines without consent, ignores drug history, or prescribes without baseline checks."}},"c-0-3-2":{"setup":"You are the psychiatry trainee on PICU at 11:00. A woman detained for mania, started on haloperidol three days ago, has a QTc of 510 ms. Her next dose is due at noon. Review the ECG, act on it and tell your consultant.","role":{"who":"Ms Grace Bennett, 46, detained under Section 3 for mania (the ward nurse Kim and the consultant by phone also speak)","open":"My heart's magnificent! I could run a marathon. Did you know I'm opening a restaurant?","manner":"Elated, chatty and distractible, dismissive of any problem. Briefly tearful when her father's collapse comes to mind; settles with reassurance and returns to her plans.","free":["She feels wonderful and is opening a restaurant","She hates the ECG stickers","She wants to go home"],"ifasked":["A little dizzy on standing this morning","No chest pain or breathlessness","Eating little, drinking lots of tea","No vomiting or diarrhoea","Father had a pacemaker and collapsed at home","Nurse: no other QT drugs, lorazepam PRN only, last bloods Friday"],"worries":["Am I going to have a heart attack?","Will I still get something at night?","When can I go home?"],"rules":["Talks over the doctor unless gently redirected","Becomes tearful when heart attack is mentioned; settles if the fear is acknowledged","Refuses a repeat ECG at first; agrees if offered later with a nurse she likes","Consultant asks whether the QT was measured by hand and what would prompt 999"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Kim, I'm Dr Arora. First, let's hold the noon haloperidol until I've reviewed this. Can I see the ECG and her drug chart?","m":[5]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Here. Machine says QTc 510. She's on haloperidol 5 mg twice a day since Sunday. Can she have her dose or not?"},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"Let me check it myself. Right name, today at 11:00. Rate about 78, sinus rhythm, normal axis, PR 160, QRS narrow, no ST changes.","m":[4]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"Nurse: And the QT?"},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"In lead II the QT is about 470 milliseconds, averaged over three beats. RR is 0.77 seconds, so Fridericia gives about 510. The machine's right this time, and it's over 500.","m":[4]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Nurse: So that's bad?"},{"t":"0:54","w":"D","s":"Over 500 means a real risk of a dangerous rhythm. I'm holding haloperidol and writing why on the chart: QTc 510, held pending review. Is she on anything else? Antibiotics, anti-sickness, citalopram, methadone?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Just haloperidol and lorazepam PRN. Last bloods were Friday."},{"t":"1:14","w":"D","s":"Then please send potassium, magnesium and calcium urgently. I'll go and see her.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:21","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Hi Grace, I'm Dr Arora, one of the doctors. Have you got a few minutes? It's about your heart tracing this morning.","m":[1]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"My heart's magnificent! I could run a marathon. Did you know I'm opening a restaurant?"},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"That sounds exciting, tell me after. Have you felt your heart racing or pounding, or felt faint or dizzy?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"No, no, no. Well, a bit dizzy getting up this morning, but that's this awful bed."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"Okay. Any chest pain, or getting out of breath?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"Never. Fit as a fiddle."},{"t":"2:07","w":"D","s":"Are you eating and drinking? Any sickness or diarrhoea?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"I don't need much food. I drink tea. Lots."},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"Has anyone in your family died suddenly, especially when young, or had heart rhythm problems?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"My dad had a pacemaker. He died at eighty. Why all these questions?"},{"t":"2:33","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Your heart tracing shows a change in the timing of its electrics, called a long QT. Haloperidol can cause it. There are usually no symptoms at first, but it can lead to a dangerous rhythm, so we act early.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"(suddenly tearful) Am I going to have a heart attack? My dad collapsed in the kitchen."},{"t":"2:57","w":"D","s":"That must bring back a frightening memory. This isn't a heart attack, and we've caught it early, while you feel well. That's exactly why we check.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"(wiping eyes) Okay. Okay. So what now?"},{"t":"3:14","w":"D","s":"I'm pausing haloperidol. We'll check the salts in your blood and repeat the tracing. The consultant and I will choose a medicine that's kinder to your heart.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"Will I still get something at night? I can't stop my thoughts."},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"Yes. Pausing one medicine doesn't mean stopping treatment. You won't be left without anything.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Good, because everyone here is so slow!"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what we're doing about your heart?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"Stopping the halo stuff, blood test, another sticky pad test, new medicine. No more sticky pads though. I hate them."},{"t":"4:02","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. Fair enough, you've had a lot done. It takes two minutes. Could we do it after lunch with Kim?","m":[9,8,11]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"After lunch. Fine."},{"t":"4:15","band":"Present to consultant","w":"D","s":"Hi Dr Morgan, it's Dr Arora on PICU with an ECG. Situation: Grace Bennett, 46, QTc 510 on haloperidol. Background: Section 3 for mania, haloperidol 5 mg twice daily since Sunday, no cardiac history, no other QT drugs. Assessment: sinus rhythm 78, normal axis, PR and QRS normal, no ST change; I measured QT 470 by hand in lead II, Fridericia QTc about 510. Only some dizziness on standing. Electrolytes sent. Recommendation: I've held haloperidol. Could we agree an alternative today?","m":[10,4]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Good. Did you just read the machine's number?"},{"t":"4:53","w":"D","s":"No, I measured it in lead II over three beats and used Fridericia, because Bazett overcorrects if she's tachycardic.","m":[4]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Fine. Aripiprazole 15 mg in the morning. Repeat the ECG after the electrolytes and again at steady state. What would make you call 999 rather than me?"},{"t":"5:14","w":"D","s":"Chest pain with ST elevation in two neighbouring leads, or new left bundle branch block. That's a STEMI: 999 straight to the heart attack centre for primary PCI, and aspirin 300 mg if there's no contraindication. Also any collapse or torsades.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:32","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Good. Medics or cardiology if the QTc stays over 500 or potassium's low."},{"t":"5:39","w":"D","s":"Will do. I'll send a Yellow Card as well.","m":[13,11]},{"t":"5:45","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"Kim, the plan: haloperidol stopped, aripiprazole 15 mg from tomorrow morning, electrolytes now, repeat ECG this afternoon. Tell me straight away about fainting, palpitations, chest pain or a fall.","m":[11,13]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"Nurse: And if she's wound up tonight? Can she have PRN haloperidol?"},{"t":"6:04","w":"D","s":"No haloperidol at all. Lorazepam as charted. I've crossed it off the PRN side too. Can you tell me the triggers to call me?","m":[5,9,13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Fainting, palpitations, chest pain, falls. And no haloperidol."},{"t":"6:21","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Grace, we're done for now. Kim will do the blood test, and the tracing after lunch. If your heart races or you feel faint, tell staff straight away. Any questions?","m":[14,13]},{"t":"6:34","w":"P","s":"When can I go home to start my restaurant?"},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"That's one for the ward round, and I'll make sure they know you asked. Thanks for your patience, Grace.","m":[14,1]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and engages her warmly despite pressured speech","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about palpitations, dizziness, fainting, chest pain and breathlessness","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about eating, vomiting or diarrhoea, and family sudden death or rhythm problems","d":"Content"},{"c":"Measures QT by hand in lead II and recalculates QTc with Fridericia","d":"Content"},{"c":"Holds the noon haloperidol, including PRN, and writes the reason on the chart","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Requests potassium, magnesium and calcium and checks for other QT drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains long QT and the plan to her in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds to her fear about a heart attack with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking her and the nurse to repeat the plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Presents the ECG systematically to the consultant in SBAR with a clear request","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees an alternative antipsychotic and repeat ECGs","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names the ECG signs of STEMI and the 999 route to primary PCI","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Safety nets: symptoms to report, escalation thresholds and Yellow Card","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closes clearly with her and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Checks the ECG personally, holds haloperidol, sends electrolytes, presents a crisp SBAR, agrees an alternative and knows the STEMI route.","borderline":"Safe decision but the ECG presentation is unstructured, or electrolytes, other QT drugs or safety netting are missed.","fail":"Gives the noon dose, trusts the machine without looking, stops all treatment with no alternative, or cannot recognise a STEMI."}},"c-0-3-3":{"setup":"You are the only doctor on call for a stand-alone mental health unit at 04:00. A man detained under Section 2 has central chest pain and his NEWS2 has jumped to 7. Assess, treat, arrange transfer and hand over.","role":{"who":"Mr Gary Collins, 58, detained under Section 2, smoker with diabetes (staff nurse Ade, the consultant by phone, a paramedic and the nurse in charge also speak)","open":"It's crushing. Right in the middle. I can't get comfy.","manner":"Sweaty, frightened and restless. Plays it down as nerves, then becomes tearful about his daughter. Briefly suspicious about being sent away; accepts transfer when given reasons and a familiar escort.","free":["Crushing central chest pain","He feels sick","He thinks it is just his nerves"],"ifasked":["Pain started at half past three","Spreads down his left arm","Never had heart problems before","Has diabetes and smokes twenty a day","No aspirin allergy, no ulcers or black stools","Has not seen his daughter for months"],"worries":["Am I dying?","You're trying to get rid of me","Will a nurse I know come with me?"],"rules":["Says it is panic until the doctor explains why that cannot be assumed","Becomes tearful about his daughter; settles if offered a call to her","Refuses transfer at first; agrees if told he will come back and a known nurse escorts him","Nurse asks whether to wait for the consultant before calling 999"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and ABCDE","w":"D","s":"Mr Collins, I'm Dr Mehta, the doctor on call. I'm here now and I'm staying with you. Ade, trolley, defib, oxygen and the ECG machine please.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"It's crushing. Right in the middle. I can't get comfy."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Airway's fine, you're talking. Breathing rate 22, sats 93% on air. Ade, oxygen by mask, aim for 94 to 98. Chest sounds clear.","m":[2,7]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Oxygen's on. Pulse 112, BP 98 over 60."},{"t":"0:38","w":"D","s":"He's clammy with cool hands, pulse regular. Alert and talking. Sugar please. No rash, legs not swollen.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Sugar 9.4."},{"t":"0:51","band":"Pain history","w":"D","s":"Gary, show me with your hand where the pain is.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"(fist on chest) Here. Like a band. Since half three."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Does it spread to your arm, jaw or back? Feeling sick or short of breath?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"Down my left arm. Feel sick. Is it my heart?"},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"Ever had this before, or any heart problems?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Never. Diabetes. Twenty a day. It's just my nerves. I get panicky at night."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"It might be nerves, but with diabetes and smoking we can't assume that. Let's do the heart tracing right now.","m":[4,9]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"Nurse: ECG's printing. 04:06."},{"t":"1:48","band":"ECG and 999","w":"D","s":"ST elevation in II, III and aVF, with reciprocal changes in aVL. That's an inferior heart attack. Ade, call 999 now: suspected STEMI, psychiatric unit, no doctor on site who can manage it. Paperwork comes after.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Nurse: What about his section? Shouldn't we wait for the consultant?"},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"No. The emergency comes first. I'll ring the consultant about leave while we wait.","m":[5,10]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"(gripping the bed rail) Am I dying? Tell me straight."},{"t":"2:27","w":"D","s":"This is frightening, and you did the right thing telling the nurses. It looks like your heart. The sooner you get to the heart hospital, the sooner they can open the blocked artery. I'm here with you.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"(tearful) My daughter. I haven't seen her in months."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"That matters a lot. Would you like us to ring her once you're on your way?","m":[8,14]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Please."},{"t":"3:03","band":"Treat","w":"D","s":"Are you allergic to aspirin? Any stomach ulcers or bleeding, or black stools?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"No. No."},{"t":"3:14","w":"D","s":"Chew this aspirin, 300 milligrams. Ade, write down the time please.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:21","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Aspirin given at 04:10."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"No GTN, because his blood pressure's soft with an inferior MI. Defib pads on, monitor on, and repeat the ECG and obs every 15 minutes.","m":[7,13]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"I'm not going to that hospital. You're trying to get rid of me."},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"I can see why it feels like that. You're not being sent away. Your heart needs a procedure we can't do here. A nurse you know goes with you, and you come back here afterwards.","m":[9,8]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"Ade's coming?"},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"If we can spare him, yes. Can you tell me back why you need to go?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"My heart's blocked, and they can fix it there."},{"t":"4:21","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Thanks, Gary. Ade, if he becomes unresponsive, start CPR and use the defib while I'm on the phone.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"4:31","band":"Leave","w":"D","s":"Dr Okafor, it's Dr Mehta on Cedar ward. Gary Collins, Section 2, inferior STEMI on ECG. 999 called at 04:06, aspirin given. I need escorted Section 17 leave to the heart attack centre.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Granted, two nurses. Any risk issues?"},{"t":"4:52","w":"D","s":"Calm and cooperative tonight, no violence this admission. Two nurses with copies of his section papers and the s17 form. I'll record your phone authority at 04:15.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Good. Ring me after the handover."},{"t":"5:11","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Paramedics are here."},{"t":"5:15","band":"Handover","w":"D","s":"Situation: Gary Collins, 58, central chest pain since 03:30, inferior STEMI on ECG at 04:06, NEWS2 7. Background: Section 2 for psychosis; diabetes on metformin; smoker; on olanzapine; no allergies. Assessment: BP 98 over 60, pulse 112, sats 95 on oxygen, sugar 9.4. Aspirin 300 at 04:10, no GTN. Recommendation: heart attack centre for primary PCI. Two nurses escort with his papers; s17 leave granted.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:44","w":"P","s":"Paramedic: Got it. Is he a risk in the back?"},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"He's been calm and cooperative. The nurses know what to do should he try to leave. Here's the ECG and his drug chart.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:02","w":"P","s":"Paramedic: We'll send the ECG ahead to the cath lab now."},{"t":"6:09","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Gary, they'll look after you now. Ade's going with you, and we'll ring your daughter. Anything you want to ask?","m":[14,1]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"Just... thanks, doc."},{"t":"6:24","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Jo, please ring his daughter once he's left, as he agreed. I'll write a timed note, ring the consultant back and complete a Datix. Can you sort cover while two staff are out?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Yes. I'll call the bleep holder for cover."}],"marks":[{"c":"Arrives promptly, introduces self, calls for equipment and stays with him","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Completes a systematic ABCDE with observations and glucose","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes a focused pain history: site, onset, radiation, associated symptoms, history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gets a 12-lead ECG within minutes and identifies inferior STEMI","d":"Content"},{"c":"Has 999 called as suspected STEMI before any MHA paperwork","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks allergy and bleeding, gives aspirin 300 mg chewed and records the time","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives oxygen to target saturations and withholds GTN with low blood pressure","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his fear and distress in plain words","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Addresses his reluctance and anxiety with clear reasons, without dismissing him","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Secures escorted s17 leave from the on-call consultant by phone; papers go with him","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives the paramedics a concise SBAR including timings, MHA status and risk","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks his understanding of why he must go","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety nets while waiting: monitoring, repeat ECG, CPR and defib plan","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closes with next of kin contact, documentation and Datix","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Fast ABCDE and ECG, 999 for STEMI before paperwork, aspirin timed, s17 by phone, and a tight SBAR handover.","borderline":"Right actions but slow or disordered, or the handover misses timings, MHA status or risk.","fail":"Treats it as anxiety, waits for paperwork or the consultant before calling 999, or gives no aspirin without reason."}},"c-0-3-4":{"setup":"You are the on-call psychiatry doctor at 18:30. A man with schizophrenia, whose haloperidol was increased and who had an IM dose yesterday, now has a fever, rigidity and confusion. Assess, act, transfer and speak to his mother.","role":{"who":"Mr Liam Turner, 34, detained under Section 3 with schizophrenia (the ward nurse, his mother Mrs Carol Turner, the consultant by phone and a paramedic also speak)","open":"Hot... hospital? My arms won't... move right.","manner":"Drowsy, slow and frightened, speaking in fragments. His mother arrives worried, turns angry and blaming, then tearful; settles with an honest apology and clear facts.","free":["He feels hot","His arms will not move properly","His muscles hurt"],"ifasked":["He can swallow sips slowly","He agrees to the doctor talking to his mother","Nurse: BP 168/95, was 110/70 an hour ago; urine dark and scanty","Nurse: haloperidol increased two days ago, IM dose yesterday","Mother: he was well on Thursday","Mother: nobody has updated her"],"worries":["Am I dying?","Mother: you've poisoned my son; why did nobody warn us?","Mother: will he get better, and what about his illness?"],"rules":["Patient is too confused for long answers; responds to short, simple questions","Mother shouts when first told it is a medicine reaction; calms if the doctor apologises and explains honestly","Nurse asks about PRN haloperidol and whether this is infection","Mother asks to go with him in the ambulance"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and assess","w":"D","s":"Liam, I'm Dr Hughes, one of the doctors. You're on Ash ward and I'm here to help. Can you tell me where you are?","m":[1,3]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Hot... hospital? My arms won't... move right."},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"You're doing well. Nurse, a full set of obs again please, and has he passed urine today?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Temp 39, pulse 120, BP 168 over 95; it was 110 over 70 an hour ago. Sats 96. Urine's dark, not much of it."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"Airway fine. Chest clear, rate 22. He's drenched in sweat. Let me feel your arms, Liam. Stiff all the way through, like a lead pipe. No clonus at the ankles, reflexes not brisk.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"Hurts. My muscles."},{"t":"0:56","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, we're going to help with that. Can you swallow a sip of water for me?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"(slowly) Yeah."},{"t":"1:08","band":"Recognise and act","w":"D","s":"Fever, lead-pipe rigidity, confusion and a swinging blood pressure, two days after a dose increase and an IM injection. This is neuroleptic malignant syndrome until proven otherwise. Stop all antipsychotics now. I'm writing 'stopped 18:35, suspected NMS' across the chart.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Including his PRN haloperidol? He's been agitated."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"No antipsychotics at all. If he's distressed, lorazepam 1 mg by mouth. I'll chart it.","m":[8]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Okay."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"Please call 999 now: suspected NMS, temperature 39, needs medical admission. I'll take CK, U&E, LFT, FBC, CRP and glucose, and we'll do an ECG.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Calling now."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"Fan on, blankets off, sips of water if he can swallow safely, and obs every 15 minutes.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Ambulance is 20 minutes away. Could it just be an infection? Should we give antibiotics?"},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"Sepsis is possible and they'll check, but there's no source and the rigidity points elsewhere. Serotonin syndrome comes on within hours with clonus and brisk reflexes, and he has neither. Catatonia can look similar, but this followed the haloperidol change. NMS fits best.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:33","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Makes sense."},{"t":"2:37","band":"Patient","w":"D","s":"Liam, your muscles are stiff and your temperature's high. It's a rare reaction to your medicine. We've stopped it, and an ambulance will take you to the main hospital to cool you down and protect your kidneys.","m":[1]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"Am I... dying?"},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. You're unwell, but it's been caught and you're in the right hands. I'll stay until they come.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"My mum's... outside."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Is it okay if I talk to her about what's happening?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Tell her."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Dr Baker, it's Dr Hughes on Ash ward. Liam Turner, Section 3, suspected NMS, antipsychotics stopped, 999 called. I need escorted Section 17 leave.","m":[14]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Granted, one nurse escort. Keep me updated."},{"t":"3:37","band":"Mother","w":"D","s":"Mrs Turner? I'm Dr Hughes. Liam asked me to talk to you. Shall we sit in here?","m":[10,1]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"Mother: What's going on? He was fine on Thursday. Nobody tells me anything!"},{"t":"3:53","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry nobody's updated you. Liam's had a reaction to his medicine called neuroleptic malignant syndrome. It causes a high temperature and stiff muscles. We've stopped the medicine, and an ambulance is taking him to the general hospital.","m":[12,11]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Mother: You've poisoned my son! Why did nobody warn us?"},{"t":"4:16","w":"D","s":"I can hear how scared and angry you are, and you deserve honesty. It's a rare reaction, and it followed the dose increase and the injection yesterday. I'm sorry this has happened. We'll look carefully at his care, and you'll be told what we find.","m":[11,14]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Mother: (crying) Will he get better?"},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Most people recover fully when it's caught early and the medicine is stopped, often within a week or two. At the hospital he'll get fluids and cooling, and they'll watch his kidneys.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Mother: And what about his illness? He can't go without medicine."},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"Once he's fully recovered, his consultant will choose a different one and start low and slow. Can you tell me what you've understood so far?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:13","w":"P","s":"Mother: A reaction to the haloperidol, it's stopped, hospital for fluids, and they'll try something else later."},{"t":"5:22","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. I'm Dr Hughes, and here's the ward number. The medical ward will update you too, and his consultant will ring you tomorrow. If you can't get through, ask for the nurse in charge.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:38","w":"P","s":"Mother: Can I go with him?"},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"You can follow on and meet him there. A nurse will escort him in the ambulance.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:51","w":"P","s":"Paramedic: What have we got?"},{"t":"5:55","band":"Handover","w":"D","s":"Situation: Liam Turner, 34, suspected NMS: temp 39, pulse 120, BP swinging between 168 over 95 and 110 over 70, GCS 13, lead-pipe rigidity. Background: schizophrenia, Section 3. Haloperidol increased two days ago, IM haloperidol yesterday afternoon. Assessment: no clonus, no infection source found. All antipsychotics stopped at 18:35. CK and bloods sent, ECG sinus tachycardia. Recommendation: medical admission for fluids, cooling and CK monitoring. Escort, drug chart, obs chart and section papers go with him.","m":[9,14]},{"t":"6:26","w":"P","s":"Paramedic: Thanks, clear."},{"t":"6:30","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I'll write a timed note, send a Yellow Card and complete a Datix. Ring me if he worsens before they leave.","m":[14,13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Will do."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, keeps him oriented and explains simply what is happening","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Assesses ABCDE, observations, swallow and urine output","d":"Content"},{"c":"Examines for lead-pipe rigidity and clonus and assesses consciousness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Recognises NMS and stops all antipsychotics with the time recorded","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Sends CK, U&E, LFT, FBC, CRP and glucose, and requests an ECG","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges 999 transfer and starts cooling and fluids","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names sepsis, serotonin syndrome and catatonia and why NMS fits best","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Refuses PRN antipsychotic and offers lorazepam for agitation","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives the receiving team an SBAR with the dose timeline and IM haloperidol","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks his consent before speaking to his mother","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds to fear and anger with empathy and an honest apology","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains NMS and prognosis without jargon and checks the mother's understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives a named contact and safety net for updates","d":"Close"},{"c":"Informs the consultant, arranges s17 escort, Yellow Card and incident report","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Spots NMS, stops antipsychotics, sends CK and bloods, gets 999 and s17 sorted, gives a timeline SBAR, and is honest and kind with the mother.","borderline":"Safe emergency care but differentials, the dose timeline or the mother's explanation are thin.","fail":"Gives more haloperidol, treats as sepsis alone and keeps him on the ward, or is defensive or evasive with the mother."}},"c-0-3-5":{"setup":"You are the liaison psychiatry doctor in A&E at 16:00. A woman on sertraline started tramadol three days ago and is now agitated, sweating and jerking. The A&E doctor thinks it is anxiety. Assess her and advise.","role":{"who":"Mrs Helen Price, 52, patient in A&E with depression (the A&E doctor also speaks)","open":"I can't keep still. My legs keep jumping. I'm so embarrassed, I've been in and out of the toilet.","manner":"Restless, sweaty and embarrassed, apologising a lot. Becomes tearful fearing she is going mad; relieved and calmer once it is explained as a drug reaction.","free":["Her legs keep jumping","Repeated diarrhoea","She is embarrassed","GP started tramadol for her back"],"ifasked":["Started tramadol 50 mg, up to four times a day, on Friday","Felt unwell from Saturday night; legs jerking since this morning","Sertraline 100 mg for two years","Took a cough syrup from the chemist; brand unknown","Took one or two extra tramadol on Sunday for pain","Mood fine on sertraline; no suicidal thoughts; has grandchildren"],"worries":["Am I going mad, or is my depression back?","Do I stop both tablets?","Can I take an antidepressant again?"],"rules":["Only mentions the cough syrup if asked about other medicines specifically","Admits the extra tramadol only if asked directly about extra tablets","Becomes tearful about going mad; settles if validated and given a clear explanation","A&E doctor challenges with NMS and anticholinergic toxicity"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"P","s":"A&E doctor: Thanks for coming. 52, on sertraline, started tramadol for her back. Shaky, sweaty, keeps having diarrhoea. I think it's anxiety. Can you see her?"},{"t":"0:13","w":"D","s":"Of course. What are her obs?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"A&E doctor: Temp 38, pulse 110, BP 150 over 90."},{"t":"0:24","w":"D","s":"Mrs Price? I'm Dr Novak, from the hospital's mental health team. The A&E doctor asked me to see you. Is it okay to talk for a few minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"I can't keep still. My legs keep jumping. I'm so embarrassed, I've been in and out of the toilet."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"Please don't be. You feel awful, and none of this is your fault. Let's work out what's going on.","m":[9]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"Thank you."},{"t":"1:01","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"When did you start the tramadol?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"Friday. The GP gave it for my back. Fifty milligrams, up to four times a day."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"And when did you start to feel unwell?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"Saturday night. Sweaty and sick. Then my legs started this morning."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"How long have you been on sertraline, and what dose?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Two years. A hundred milligrams."},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"Anything else at all? St John's wort, migraine tablets, cough or cold remedies, anti-sickness tablets, or drugs like ecstasy or cocaine?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"A cough syrup from the chemist. Nothing else, honestly."},{"t":"1:56","w":"D","s":"Do you know which one? Some contain dextromethorphan, which adds to this.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"My husband's got the bottle. It's a red one. I don't know."},{"t":"2:11","band":"Examine","w":"D","s":"I'd like to check your legs and eyes. Is that okay?","m":[1,4]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"Go ahead."},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"Your knee reflexes are very brisk. I'll bend your foot up quickly. There, it keeps beating: that's inducible clonus. Your legs also jerk on their own. Follow my finger side to side. Your eyes are flicking too: ocular clonus.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:39","w":"P","s":"Is that bad?"},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"It's a clear sign, and it tells us what this is. You've also got a tremor, and you're sweating a lot.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"2:54","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I think this is serotonin syndrome. Sertraline and tramadol both raise a brain chemical called serotonin, and together they've pushed it too high. That causes the sweating, shaking, diarrhoea and jumping legs.","m":[5,10]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I thought I was going mad. I thought my depression was coming back."},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. It isn't your depression and you're not going mad. It's a drug reaction, and most people get better within a few days once the drugs stop.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"So I stop both?"},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"Yes, both stop today, and the medical team will keep you in to watch you. Can you tell me in your words what's happening?","m":[6,10]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"The two tablets together made too much serotonin. Stop them and stay in."},{"t":"3:56","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I ask everyone this. Have you taken more tablets than prescribed, by accident or on purpose?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"An extra one or two tramadol on Sunday. My back was so bad. Not to hurt myself."},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. How's your mood been lately? Any thoughts about ending your life?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"Mood's been okay since the sertraline. No, nothing like that. I've got grandchildren."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"That's good to hear. I'll still come back in the next 24 to 48 hours to talk about your mood and medicines.","m":[11,13]},{"t":"4:42","band":"Advise A&E","w":"D","s":"This is serotonin syndrome. Hunter criteria are met: inducible, spontaneous and ocular clonus, brisk reflexes, tremor and sweating, after tramadol on sertraline. Please stop both, and check the cough syrup.","m":[5,6,3]},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"A&E doctor: Couldn't it be NMS? Or anticholinergic?"},{"t":"5:02","w":"D","s":"NMS builds over days with lead-pipe rigidity and slow, dull reflexes; she has the opposite. Anticholinergic toxicity gives hot, dry skin and no sweating; she's drenched.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"A&E doctor: Fair. What do you want?"},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Admit under the medical team. CK, U&E, LFT, FBC and glucose, cardiac monitoring, and TOXBASE advice. Lorazepam if she's agitated.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:31","w":"P","s":"A&E doctor: I'll refer to the medics and look at TOXBASE now."},{"t":"5:38","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Escalate to critical care if her temperature goes above 39.5, she becomes rigid, or her consciousness drops. Liaison will review her within 48 hours.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"A&E doctor: Got it."},{"t":"5:55","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Mrs Price, the medical team will look after you tonight. If you feel hotter, stiffer or more muddled, or the jerks get worse, tell the nurse straight away. I'll see you in a day or two to plan your antidepressant safely, and I'll write to your GP.","m":[12,13,14]},{"t":"6:16","w":"P","s":"Will I be able to take an antidepressant again?"},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"Very likely, yes, but not alongside tramadol. We'll choose together once you're well, and we'll report this reaction to the national drug safety scheme. Any other questions?","m":[13,14]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you for explaining it."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"You're welcome. Get some rest.","m":[14,9]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and liaison role and asks permission to talk and examine","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Clarifies observations and the timeline of tramadol and symptom onset","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about every other serotonergic drug, remedy and substance","d":"Content"},{"c":"Examines for spontaneous, inducible and ocular clonus, reflexes and tremor","d":"Content"},{"c":"Applies the Hunter criteria and names serotonin syndrome","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Advises stopping both sertraline and tramadol","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Distinguishes it from NMS and anticholinergic toxicity, one sentence each","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees medical admission with CK, U&E and TOXBASE advice","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates her embarrassment and fear","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the diagnosis in plain words and checks her understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks directly about extra tablets, intent and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Safety nets: warning signs and critical care triggers","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Plans liaison review in 24 to 48 hours, antidepressant plan and GP letter","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes clearly and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Finds clonus, names serotonin syndrome by Hunter criteria, stops both drugs, separates it from NMS and anticholinergic toxicity, asks about intent and plans follow-up.","borderline":"Right diagnosis but misses other serotonergic drugs, intent, or the differentials.","fail":"Accepts 'anxiety', discharges her or adds a benzodiazepine alone, or continues sertraline."}},"c-0-3-6":{"setup":"You are the ward resident. It is 16:30 on an acute adult ward. A 58-year-old informal patient on lithium has vomited three times, and this morning's level is 1.6 mmol/L. See her, make her safe and decide whether she needs the medical team.","role":{"who":"Mrs Carol Whitfield, 58, female, informal inpatient with bipolar disorder","open":"Yes, sit down. Sorry, I've got a sick bowl here just in case.","manner":"Polite, washed out and apologetic. Embarrassed and guilty once the ibuprofen comes up. Reluctant about moving hospital, but trusting and cooperative if the doctor explains kindly.","free":["She has been sick three times since lunch","She has loose stools and keeps running to the toilet","She feels 'rotten' and washed out"],"ifasked":["Her hands are shaking more than usual; she spilt her tea","She feels dizzy when she stands and swayed on the way to the toilet","She has kept almost no fluid down today","Her last lithium was at 22:00 last night, given by the nurse; no extra tablets","Blood was taken at about 09:30 this morning","She started ibuprofen on Monday for back pain, at her own request","No water tablets or blood pressure tablets"],"worries":["Do I have to stop lithium? It's the only thing that keeps me well.","Is it damaging my kidneys?","Do I really have to go to another hospital tonight?"],"rules":["Becomes guilty and tearful when ibuprofen is mentioned; settles if told it was not her fault","Walks unsteadily if asked to walk; speech and orientation are normal","Resists transfer at first; agrees if told a nurse will go with her and her bed is kept","Asks what any medical word means if the doctor uses jargon"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mrs Whitfield? I'm Dr Patel, one of the psychiatry doctors. The nurse says you've been poorly this afternoon. Is it OK if I sit with you for a few minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"Yes, sit down. Sorry, I've got a sick bowl here just in case."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Don't apologise at all. Can I just check your full name and date of birth?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"Carol Whitfield. Fourteenth of March, 1968."},{"t":"0:30","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Thank you. How are you feeling right now?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Rotten. I've been sick three times since lunch, and I keep running to the loo."},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"That sounds horrible. Have you managed to keep any drinks down?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Hardly anything. A few sips, and it all comes back up."},{"t":"0:53","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me, that really matters. Any dizziness when you stand?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"0:59","w":"P","s":"A bit. I felt woozy walking to the toilet."},{"t":"1:04","band":"Signs of toxicity","w":"D","s":"Have you noticed your hands shaking more than usual?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"Now you mention it, yes. I spilt my tea at lunch."},{"t":"1:14","w":"D","s":"Could you hold your arms out and spread your fingers? Thank you. That's a bigger, coarser shake than the usual lithium tremor. Can you say 'baby hippopotamus' for me?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"Baby hippopotamus. Did I pass?"},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"You did, your speech is clear. And can you tell me where you are and what day it is?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"Ward 4. Wednesday. I'm not daft, doctor, just sick."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"You're clearly not, and thanks for humouring me. Your obs show a pulse of 96 and blood pressure of 108 over 70, so your NEWS2 score is 3. Could you stand and walk to the window, holding my arm?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Ooh. I'm swaying a bit. I'm not normally like this."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Let's sit you back down. That unsteadiness is important, thank you.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:11","band":"Causes","w":"D","s":"When did you last take your lithium?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Ten o'clock last night, same as always. The nurse gives it to me."},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"Any extra tablets, even by accident? And the nurse tells me your blood was taken at half nine this morning. Does that sound right?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"No extra, never. And yes, straight after breakfast."},{"t":"2:36","w":"D","s":"Good. That's about eleven and a half hours after your dose, close to the usual twelve, so 1.6 is a true high reading.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"Have you taken any other medicines lately? Painkillers, water tablets or blood pressure tablets?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"Only the ibuprofen for my back, since Monday. That was my idea. I asked for it. Have I done this to myself?"},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"You weren't to know. Asking for pain relief was sensible, and catching that clash is our job, not yours.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"I feel such an idiot."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"You're not. Ibuprofen makes the kidneys hold on to lithium, and being sick and dry pushes it up further. Together, that explains today.","m":[5,9,10]},{"t":"3:25","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Can I explain what I think is going on? Your level is 1.6, and we usually aim for 0.6 to 0.8. With the sickness, the shake and the wobble, I think the lithium has built up too high. It's treatable, especially caught early like this.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"Do I have to stop lithium? It's the only thing that keeps me well."},{"t":"3:51","w":"D","s":"I know how much it helps you. This is a pause, not stopping for good. Once things settle, your consultant will restart it safely, probably at a lower dose. We'll keep an eye on your mood meanwhile.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"Is it damaging my kidneys?"},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"That's a fair worry. A high level with sickness can strain the kidneys, which is why I'm checking them now. Caught early, they usually recover.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:21","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"So, the plan. I've asked the nurse to hold your six o'clock lithium and stop the ibuprofen, and I've written the reason on your chart so the night team see it too. Paracetamol is safe for your back instead.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:38","w":"D","s":"I'd like to take bloods now: kidney tests, calcium and a repeat lithium level, plus a heart tracing, an ECG, because a high level can slow the heart. Is that OK?","m":[7,11]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"Yes, go on."},{"t":"4:53","w":"D","s":"I'll take them myself and chase the results by seven.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:59","band":"Medical team","w":"D","s":"One more thing. You've got a coarse shake, you're unsteady, and you can't keep fluids down. Any one of those means the medical team should see you, because you may need a drip and closer kidney checks. I'm going to ring the medical registrar now.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"Another hospital? Tonight? I'd rather stay here. Everyone knows me."},{"t":"5:23","w":"D","s":"I understand, it's unsettling to move when you feel this rough. A nurse from here will go with you, and we'll keep your bed. Are you happy to go if they recommend it?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:37","w":"P","s":"If you think I need it. Yes."},{"t":"5:41","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Just so I know I've been clear, can you tell me in your words what's happening?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"My lithium's too high because of the ibuprofen and being sick. No more tablets tonight, blood tests, and the medical doctors might give me a drip."},{"t":"6:01","band":"Safety net and close","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Meanwhile, take small sips every few minutes. The nurses will do hourly obs and a fluid chart. If you feel muddled, more wobbly, your speech goes odd, you twitch, or you keep vomiting, press your buzzer straight away.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"Will you still be around?"},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"Yes, until nine, and I'll hand you over to the night doctor in person. I'll report the ibuprofen clash on our incident system and tell your GP. Any questions before I make that call?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","s":"No. Just don't forget about me."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"I won't. I'll be back by seven with your results.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms identity and checks she is happy to talk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about vomiting, diarrhoea, fluid intake and dizziness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks for coarse tremor, ataxia, slurred speech and confusion, and states the NEWS2 score","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Confirms the time of the last dose against the time the blood was taken","d":"Content"},{"c":"Identifies ibuprofen and dehydration as the likely causes of the high level","d":"Content"},{"c":"Holds tonight's lithium and the ibuprofen, with the reason written on the chart","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Orders U&E, creatinine, eGFR, calcium, repeat lithium level and ECG, and names who chases them","d":"Plan"},{"c":"States criteria for medical referral and refers her to the acute medical team now","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains the high level and the pause in lithium in plain language","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds with empathy to her guilt and her fears about lithium and her kidneys","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gains consent for tests and transfer, and checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets: names the symptoms to report and the overnight monitoring","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes with handover, incident report, next contact time and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Treats symptoms as well as the number: holds lithium and ibuprofen on the chart, sends bloods and ECG, and refers to medicine on clear criteria.","borderline":"Holds lithium and orders bloods but is vague about referral criteria, the sample timing or who chases results.","fail":"Treats the vomiting with an antiemetic, continues lithium or a reduced dose, or misses the ibuprofen and neurological signs."}},"c-0-3-7":{"setup":"You are the weekend on-call resident. A 34-year-old man on Section 3 has missed three days of clozapine and now agrees to take it. He has a fever of 38.2, pulse 118, constipation and drooling. See him and decide what to do.","role":{"who":"Mr Daniel Okafor, 34, male, detained under Section 3 with treatment-resistant schizophrenia","open":"Yeah. Just give me it. I'm agreeing now, aren't I?","manner":"Impatient and flat at first, sore and shivery. Embarrassed about the drooling. Angry when told no full dose; calms if the reason is explained and the drooling is taken seriously.","free":["His throat is very sore","He wants his clozapine now he has agreed","He is fed up with blood tests"],"ifasked":["He stopped because of the drooling; the pillow is soaked and others notice","Swallowing hurts and he has a sore spot on his gum","He was shivery and hot and cold last night","His chest feels a bit tight and his heart pounds when he lies down","His bowels last opened about five days ago; he feels bloated but has not vomited","He stopped smoking after admission"],"worries":["Why can't I have my normal dose?","Will the voices come back while you build it up?","Can anything be done about the drooling?"],"rules":["Only admits the drooling if asked kindly why he stopped","Gets angry when told he will not get 400 mg; settles if the risk is explained plainly","Refuses bloods at first; agrees if told it is one tube that decides whether restarting is safe"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hi, Mr Okafor? I'm Dr Shah, the doctor on call this weekend. The nurse says you'd like to restart your clozapine. Can we talk about that for a few minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Just give me it. I'm agreeing now, aren't I?"},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"You are, and I'm really glad. Before we do, I need to check a few things, because you're not well today. Is that OK?","m":[1,11]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Fine. Make it quick. My throat's killing me."},{"t":"0:37","band":"Why he stopped","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what made you stop it for those three days?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"The drooling. I wake up and the pillow's soaked. It's disgusting. The others can see it."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. I know that's embarrassing. It's really common with clozapine, and you shouldn't have to just put up with it.","m":[11]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"Nobody's done anything about it before."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"Then let's change that today. I'll come back to it, I promise.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"1:14","band":"Infection","w":"D","s":"Tell me about your throat. Does it hurt to swallow? Any mouth ulcers?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"Swallowing's agony. There's a sore bit on my gum."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"Any shivering or sweats?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Shivery last night. Hot one minute, cold the next."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"That sounds miserable. On clozapine, a sore throat with fever needs a blood test today, because clozapine can occasionally drop the cells that fight infection.","m":[3,11]},{"t":"1:47","band":"Heart","w":"D","s":"Your pulse is fast, 118. Any chest pain, or feeling short of breath?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"My chest feels a bit tight. Not loads."},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"Any pounding or fluttering of your heart, or feeling faint?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"It pounds a bit when I lie down."},{"t":"2:09","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Clozapine can rarely inflame the heart muscle, so I'll want a blood test and a heart tracing to check for that too.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","band":"Bowels","w":"D","s":"When did you last open your bowels?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Dunno. Monday? Five days maybe. I'm used to it."},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"Any tummy pain, bloating or being sick?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"Bit bloated. Not sick."},{"t":"2:37","w":"D","s":"With clozapine, constipation can get serious, because the gut can slow right down. Can I feel your tummy? It's a bit swollen but soft, not tender, and I can hear bowel sounds, which is reassuring.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"One more question. Have you changed how much you smoke since you came in?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"Stopped. Can't be bothered going out with an escort."},{"t":"3:06","w":"D","s":"Well done, that's great for your health. It matters for your dose too: stopping smoking can push clozapine levels up a lot, so pharmacy will factor that in.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Here's where I've got to. I'm not going to give you the 400 milligrams today.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"What? You said you were glad! I've agreed to take it and now you won't give it me. This is a joke."},{"t":"3:38","w":"D","s":"I can see that's really frustrating after you've made the effort. After three days off, your body loses its tolerance. The full dose could drop your blood pressure, make you collapse or cause a fit. Starting low keeps you safe.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:57","w":"P","s":"So how long's that going to take? Will the voices come back?"},{"t":"4:04","w":"D","s":"That's a fair worry. Usually one to two weeks to get back near your dose. If your bloods are fine, we'll start a small dose today, and the nurses will watch how you're feeling.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"4:20","band":"Tests and plan","w":"D","s":"So, the tests. One blood sample now for your white cells, infection markers and a heart marker called troponin, and a heart tracing. Is that OK?","m":[8,12]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"I'm sick of blood tests."},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"I understand, you've had loads. This one can't wait. It's one tube, and it decides whether restarting is safe.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"Go on, then."},{"t":"4:48","w":"D","s":"Thank you. The result goes to the clozapine monitoring service as usual, and we only give clozapine once it's back as green.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:59","w":"D","s":"Then I'll ring the on-call pharmacist and agree a written, day-by-day restart plan. Before any dose, I'll check that clozapine is listed on your treatment form under the Section, the T2 or T3.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"And the drooling?"},{"t":"5:17","w":"D","s":"Sleeping propped up on two pillows helps, and if it's still bad, pharmacy can advise on a medicine called hyoscine. For your bowels, I'll start two laxatives today, senna and docusate.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:32","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Can I check I've made sense? What's the plan, in your words?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:39","w":"P","s":"No big dose. Bloods and the heart thing first. Then start small and go up slowly. Something for the dribbling and the bowels."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"Spot on. If you get chest pain, struggle to breathe, have tummy pain, are sick, or feel hotter and shivery, tell a nurse straight away, day or night.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:04","w":"P","s":"And you'll tell the nurse not to give me the big dose?"},{"t":"6:11","w":"D","s":"I already have, and I've explained why and written it on your chart. She'll start a bowel chart and check your temperature and pulse every four hours.","m":[7,13]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"Alright. Thanks for doing something about the pillow."},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me about it. I'll be back after lunch with the results and the restart plan. Anything else before I go?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. That's it."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains why he is there and checks he is happy to talk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores without judgement why he stopped clozapine","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for neutropenia: sore throat, mouth ulcers, shivers and fever","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for myocarditis: chest pain, breathlessness and palpitations, linked to the pulse of 118","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for constipation and ileus, and examines the abdomen","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about stopping smoking and links it to higher clozapine levels","d":"Content"},{"c":"Declines the full 400 mg restart and explains why to the patient and the nurse","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Orders urgent FBC with neutrophils, CRP, troponin and ECG, and sends the FBC to the monitoring service","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a written re-titration with the on-call pharmacist and checks T2 or T3 lists clozapine","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers practical treatment for the drooling and the constipation","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his embarrassment and frustration","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gains consent for bloods and checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets: chest pain, breathlessness, abdominal pain, vomiting or worse fever reported at once","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes with a summary, next review time and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Withholds the full dose with a clear reason, sends FBC, CRP, troponin and ECG, screens the three dangerous side effects and plans a written re-titration.","borderline":"Refuses the full dose and sends an FBC but misses troponin, the abdominal check, smoking or the T2/T3 check.","fail":"Gives 400 mg after three missed days, calls the fever a cold without an FBC, or ignores five days of constipation."}},"c-0-3-8":{"setup":"You are the night on-call resident. It is 02:40 on an older adult ward. An 81-year-old woman with dementia, on Section 2 and apixaban, was found on the floor with a forehead bruise. Assess her, decide on a CT head and screen for delirium.","role":{"who":"Mrs Edith Marsh, 81, female, detained under Section 2 with Alzheimer's dementia (the same person reads the healthcare assistant's lines)","open":"Where am I? Who are you?","manner":"Bewildered and frightened, wants to get up. Settles with a calm voice and simple words. Becomes anxious at the idea of another hospital, agrees if someone stays with her.","free":["Her head is sore at the front","She wants to get up off the floor","She does not know why she is on the floor"],"ifasked":["She has not been sick","Her neck does not hurt","Her left hip aches a little when pressed","She thinks she is 75 and briefly calls the ward a hotel","She cannot say the months backwards past October","Healthcare assistant: nobody saw the fall; found at about 02:30, awake and talking","Healthcare assistant: no PRN tonight; usually knows she is in hospital and chats, but was muddled last evening looking for her husband"],"worries":["Have I done something wrong?","I don't want to go to another hospital.","Don't frighten my daughter Susan."],"rules":["Becomes tearful and frightened when she cannot answer the 4AT; settles if reassured","Refuses transfer at first; agrees if told a nurse will stay with her","The healthcare assistant wants to lift her back to bed until told to wait"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and make safe","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mrs Marsh. I'm Dr Ahmed, one of the doctors. You've had a fall. Please lie still for a moment while I check you over.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"Where am I? Who are you?"},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"You're safe. You're on the ward in hospital, it's the middle of the night, and I'm a doctor. Can you hear me all right?","m":[1,12]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","s":"I can hear you. I want to get up."},{"t":"0:34","w":"D","s":"I know, the floor's no place to be. Just a minute longer, and then we'll get you comfortable.","m":[2,12]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"Healthcare assistant: We were going to pop her back into bed."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"Not yet, please. Could you bring a pillow and a blanket? I need to check her neck and hips first. She's talking and breathing easily, which is good.","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:04","band":"Head injury","w":"D","s":"Mrs Marsh, do you have pain anywhere? Your head or your neck?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"My head's sore. Here, at the front."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"There's a bruise there. Do you feel sick, or have you been sick?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"No. Not sick."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"I'm going to shine a light in your eyes. Both pupils are equal and react well. Can you squeeze my fingers, and wiggle your toes?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"Like this?"},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Lovely, both sides the same. Can you gently turn your head side to side? Any pain in your neck?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"No, that's all right."},{"t":"1:53","band":"Limbs","w":"D","s":"Now your hips. Any pain when I press here?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"Ow. A little, on that side."},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"Sorry. Both legs are the same length and not turned out. Can you lift each heel off the floor for me?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"There. It's a bit stiff."},{"t":"2:17","band":"From the staff","w":"D","s":"Well done. I don't think anything's broken, but the hospital team will check too. Did anyone see her fall? Was she knocked out at any point?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"Healthcare assistant: No one saw it. I found her at about half two. She was awake and talking when I got to her."},{"t":"2:42","w":"D","s":"Has she had any PRN tonight, like lorazepam? And what's she like normally?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"Healthcare assistant: No PRN. Normally she knows she's in hospital and chats away. Last evening she was muddled, looking for her husband."},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"Thanks, that's really useful. So she's GCS 14 tonight, confused, against her usual 15, and the confusion started before the fall.","m":[3,7]},{"t":"3:12","band":"Delirium screen","w":"D","s":"Mrs Marsh, a few quick questions. How old are you?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"Seventy... something. Seventy-five?"},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you. And where are we right now?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Is it the hotel? No... the hospital."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"That's right. Can you list the months backwards from December, starting with December?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","s":"December, November, October... I've lost it. I'm frightened. Have I done something wrong?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"No, you haven't done anything wrong. You've had a bump and you're a bit muddled. We're going to look after you.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Her 4AT is well above the cut-off, and this is a change from her usual, so I'm treating it as delirium. We'll check bloods for infection, salts and sugar, her urine if she has symptoms, her bladder, bowels and pain, and go through all her medicines.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:18","band":"CT and transfer","w":"D","s":"Mrs Marsh, because you take a blood thinner, apixaban, I'd like the main hospital to scan your head, to check there's no bleeding.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"4:29","w":"P","s":"I don't want to go to another hospital."},{"t":"4:34","w":"D","s":"I understand. It's late and it's frightening. The scan is quick, a nurse from here will stay with you the whole time, and you'll come back to us.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"If someone stays with me. All right."},{"t":"4:53","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Even if she changes her mind, she's confused and on apixaban, so going is in her best interests. Apixaban alone means NICE wants a CT within 8 hours of the fall.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"5:09","w":"D","s":"I'll ring A&E now, and the on-call consultant to authorise section 17 leave with an escort. Please get her section papers, drug chart, ReSPECT form and a note of her usual cognition ready, and I'll write the handover letter.","m":[6,10]},{"t":"5:28","band":"Obs and safety","w":"D","s":"Until she leaves, neuro obs every 30 minutes until her GCS is back to 15. Call me straight away if her GCS drops, she vomits, her headache worsens, she has a fit or any new weakness.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"Healthcare assistant: Every half hour, and call you if anything changes. How do we get her up?"},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"Use the hoist or the flat lifting kit, never under her arms.","m":[2]},{"t":"6:02","w":"P","s":"Don't frighten my Susan, will you?"},{"t":"6:06","band":"Family and close","w":"D","s":"I'll be gentle, I promise. I'll phone your daughter now, before you go, and tell her you're talking and being looked after. I'll complete the incident report and falls form tonight.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:21","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the plan back, so we're both sure?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:27","w":"P","s":"Healthcare assistant: Hoist her to bed, neuro obs every half hour, call you if she drops, papers ready, and a nurse escorts her to A&E."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Thank you for finding her and calling so quickly. I'll be back in ten minutes, once I've spoken to A&E.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, reassures and orients her before examining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Stops her being moved and checks airway, breathing and responsiveness first","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses GCS and pupils, and asks about headache, vomiting and neck pain","d":"Content"},{"c":"Examines hips and limbs for fracture before she is lifted","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks if the fall was witnessed, any loss of consciousness and any sedation given","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names apixaban as the reason for CT head within 8 hours and arranges A&E transfer","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for delirium with the 4AT, compared with her usual baseline","d":"Content"},{"c":"Lists causes of delirium to check: infection, bloods, pain, bladder, bowels, medicines","d":"Content"},{"c":"Sets neuro obs at NICE frequency and states the triggers for urgent recall","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges s17 escorted leave, section papers and a written handover for A&E","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains simply, seeks her agreement and states the best-interests basis","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds kindly to her fear and distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Plans to inform her daughter now and completes the incident and falls reports","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks staff understanding with teach-back and gives a clear return time","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Assesses before moving, recognises apixaban as the CT trigger, screens delirium with the 4AT and arranges a lawful escorted transfer.","borderline":"Sends her for CT but misses the hip check, the 4AT, the s17 leave or the family call.","fail":"Lets staff lift her straight away, misses the anticoagulant, or blames the confusion on dementia and waits for morning."}},"c-0-3-9":{"setup":"You are the resident in a CMHT clinic. First, a 27-year-old man with first-episode psychosis has gained 9 kg on olanzapine and wants to stop. Then a 52-year-old woman on quetiapine has an HbA1c of 51. Agree a plan with each.","role":{"who":"Mr Ryan Kelly, 27, male, first-episode psychosis on olanzapine 15 mg; then Mrs Linda Shaw, 52, female, bipolar depression on quetiapine (one person plays both)","open":"I've put on nine kilos. Nine. None of my clothes fit. I'm stopping the tablets.","manner":"Ryan: angry and ashamed, then engaged once taken seriously. Linda: calm and surprised, frightened about losing quetiapine, reassured by clear explanation.","free":["Ryan: has gained 9 kg in 12 weeks and his clothes no longer fit","Ryan: says he will stop the tablets","Linda: feels completely well"],"ifasked":["Ryan: constantly hungry and eats from the fridge at midnight","Ryan: mostly takeaways and gaming, little exercise","Ryan: smokes ten roll-ups a day and has a couple of beers at weekends","Ryan: his father has type 2 diabetes","Ryan: the voices have mostly gone on olanzapine; he fears them coming back","Linda: no thirst, blurred vision or repeated infections; up once a night to pass urine"],"worries":["Ryan: If I switch, will the weight come off?","Linda: Diabetes? But I feel fine.","Linda: Will I have to come off quetiapine? It's the only thing that's worked."],"rules":["Ryan becomes upset about his appearance early; settles if his distress is validated","Ryan agrees not to stop only if given real options","Linda asks who manages what if the doctor does not say"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hi Ryan, I'm Dr Lewis. Thanks for coming in. Your care coordinator says the weight's been really bothering you. Tell me about it.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"I've put on nine kilos. Nine. None of my clothes fit. I'm stopping the tablets."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"Nine kilos in three months is a lot, and that's the medicine, not you. I'm glad you told us rather than just stopping.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"I look disgusting. I don't even want to go out."},{"t":"0:39","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much this is hurting you. That matters, and we can do something about it today.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Like what? Everyone just says eat less."},{"t":"0:54","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"Let's see what's actually changed. Has your appetite changed since you started?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"I'm starving all the time. I raid the fridge at midnight."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"That's a very common effect of olanzapine. What's a normal day of eating and moving like?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"Takeaways mostly. I don't do much. Gaming."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"Do you smoke, and how much do you drink?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"Ten roll-ups a day. Couple of beers at the weekend."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"Has anyone in your family had diabetes, a heart attack or a stroke?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"My dad's got diabetes."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"Thanks, that's useful. And how have the voices been since you started olanzapine?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"Gone, mostly. That's the annoying thing. It works."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"What worries you about changing it?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"That they come back. But honestly, I'd rather hear voices than be fat."},{"t":"2:09","band":"Not stopping","w":"D","s":"That feeling makes sense. But stopping suddenly makes the voices much more likely to return, sometimes within weeks. Will you give me the chance to change things safely instead?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Yeah. OK. I won't stop. Not yet."},{"t":"2:29","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's a big thing to agree to. There are three main options.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:37","w":"D","s":"One, a proper diet and activity programme with our physical health worker. Two, switching gradually to a medicine with much less weight gain, such as aripiprazole. Three, adding metformin, which can curb appetite and weight. It isn't licensed for this, but it's widely used.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"If I switch, will the weight come off?"},{"t":"3:04","w":"D","s":"Many people lose some, but it isn't guaranteed and takes months. There's a small risk of the voices returning during the swap, so we'd watch closely. Whatever you choose, the diet and activity support helps. What feels right to you?","m":[6,7]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"I think I'd switch. The hunger's the worst bit. And I'll try the health worker."},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"That's a reasonable choice. I'll record it and agree the switch plan with your consultant this week. Could you sum up what we've agreed?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"Keep taking the olanzapine for now, swap slowly to the other one, and see the health worker about food."},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"Exactly. It's your twelve-week check, so today we'll do weight, waist, blood pressure, sugar and cholesterol, and work out your heart risk score, QRISK3. I'll write to your GP this week.","m":[8,11,12]},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"If the voices come back, or you feel like stopping, ring your care coordinator first. Next appointment's in four weeks.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"OK. Cheers, doc."},{"t":"4:26","band":"Second patient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Shaw, I'm Dr Lewis. Thanks for waiting. I have your health check results. First, have you been more thirsty, or passing more urine?","m":[1,9]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"Not really. Maybe up once in the night."},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"Any blurred vision, or infections that keep coming back?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"No. Why? Is something wrong?"},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"One result needs a closer look. It's a blood sugar test called HbA1c. Yours is 51. Diabetes is diagnosed at 48 or above, so you're just over the line.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"Diabetes? But I feel absolutely fine."},{"t":"5:13","w":"D","s":"Most people with early type 2 diabetes feel well. Because you have no symptoms, we repeat the test to confirm before we call it diabetes.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"Will I have to come off quetiapine? It's the only thing that's ever worked."},{"t":"5:34","w":"D","s":"I can hear how worried you are about losing that. It's done a lot for you. Often the best choice is to treat the sugar and keep the quetiapine. We'd only change if you and your consultant felt it was right.","m":[2,7]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"OK. So what happens now?"},{"t":"5:59","w":"D","s":"I'll work out your heart risk score, QRISK3, today, and measure your weight and waist. Your repeat HbA1c and cholesterol are booked with the practice nurse in two weeks. I'll write to your GP this week with the results and the plan.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"So the GP sorts out the diabetes, and you look after the quetiapine?"},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. If you get very thirsty, pass a lot of urine or feel unwell before then, see your GP sooner. Any questions?","m":[8,13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks for explaining it properly."}],"marks":[{"c":"Greets each patient warmly and invites their concerns before giving information","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates each patient's distress without dismissing it","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores appetite, diet, activity, smoking, alcohol and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks how well olanzapine has worked and what he fears about changing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the relapse risk of stopping abruptly and agrees he will not stop","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers three options: lifestyle programme, switch to a lower-risk antipsychotic, metformin","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Uses shared decision making and records his choice","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks the woman about thirst, polyuria, blurred vision and infections","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains HbA1c 51 is above the 48 threshold and plans a confirmatory repeat","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Calculates QRISK3 and books dates for weight, waist, glucose and lipid checks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Commits to writing to each GP within a week, saying who monitors what","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets and closes each consultation with a clear next contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Validates his distress, prevents abrupt stopping, offers three options he chooses from, and confirms the HbA1c with a repeat and a GP letter.","borderline":"Keeps him on treatment but gives only one option, or diagnoses diabetes on one result without a repeat or a letter.","fail":"Dismisses the weight gain, lets him stop suddenly, or leaves the HbA1c to the GP without a plan or letter."}},"c-0-4-1":{"setup":"You covered the acute adult ward this weekend. It is 09:00 on Monday. The consultant, ward manager and nurse in charge are in the morning meeting. Hand over the weekend's self-harm, AWOL and chest pain in about three minutes, then answer questions.","role":{"who":"Dr Rachel Morgan, 50, female, consultant psychiatrist (the same person reads the ward manager's and nurse in charge's lines)","open":"Go ahead. Keep it tight, we've got ward round at ten.","manner":"Busy, brisk and sharp. Challenges decisions and interrupts long stories. Softens and becomes collegial when the resident answers calmly, owns gaps and is clear about what is needed.","free":["Ward round starts at ten","She wants to know what she needs to do today","She expects the highest-risk patient first"],"ifasked":["She will see Amy first this morning","She is happy to review Ben's leave after ward round","She would have liked a phone call about the AWOL on Sunday night","The nurse in charge can call Ben's mother by ten"],"worries":["Why wasn't Amy on closer observations?","Has Ben's family been told about the AWOL?","Who is chasing Colin's repeat obs?"],"rules":["Interrupts if a patient takes more than about a minute: 'What do you need from me?'","Becomes irritated about not being phoned on Sunday; settles if the resident apologises and does not argue","Ward manager asks who is chasing outstanding jobs if no owner is named"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Morning, everyone. I covered the weekend. Three people to highlight, highest risk first: Amy Ford's self-harm, Ben Lowe's AWOL and Colin Hart's chest pain. Everyone else was settled. OK to go?","m":[1,10]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"Go ahead. Keep it tight, we've got ward round at ten."},{"t":"0:24","band":"Amy: self-harm","w":"D","s":"Amy Ford, 23, informal. Situation: on Saturday evening she cut her left forearm. Background: she was on general observations.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"Assessment: the cuts were superficial, cleaned and steri-stripped. Afterwards she was calm, denied ongoing intent and agreed a safety plan. I raised her to 15-minute observations, and her room was searched.","m":[2,4,5]},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"Recommendation: she needs your risk review today, and a decision on her observations and leave.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"Why wasn't she put on closer obs? Fifteen minutes after a cut is brave."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Fair question. She was calm, talking openly and handed over what she'd used. The nurse in charge and I judged 15-minute checks proportionate. I'd value your view this morning.","m":[5,9]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"Fine. I'll see her first. Did she say why she did it?"},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"She said nobody was listening. She's embarrassed now and worried everyone's talking about her, so a gentle review would help.","m":[13]},{"t":"1:44","band":"Ben: AWOL","w":"D","s":"Second, Ben Lowe, 40, on Section 2. Situation: he went absent without leave on Sunday afternoon and police returned him at 22:00.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"Background: he had ground leave. Assessment: he came back calm and unhurt, and said he'd been to a friend's. I couldn't rule out drugs, so I've sent a urine screen. I've suspended his section 17 leave pending your review.","m":[2,4,5]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"Hang on. A detained patient goes AWOL and I hear about it on Monday? I'd have liked a call."},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"You're right, I should have called you. I'm sorry. For any detained patient going AWOL, I'll ring you at the time from now on.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"OK. Thank you for saying so. What do you need from me?"},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Please review his leave and risk today. Police were told at the time, and the Datix is done.","m":[6,8]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Ward manager: Has his family been told?"},{"t":"3:06","w":"D","s":"Not yet, thanks for picking that up. Could the nurse in charge ring his mother this morning?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: I'll call her by ten and record it."},{"t":"3:22","band":"Colin: chest pain","w":"D","s":"Third, Colin Hart, 66, informal. Situation: central chest pain on Sunday afternoon. Background: no cardiac history.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"3:32","w":"D","s":"Assessment: ECG and troponin normal, and the pain was reproduced by pressing on his chest wall, so likely musculoskeletal. Recommendation: repeat obs today, and a medical review if the pain returns.","m":[2,5]},{"t":"3:49","w":"P","s":"Ward manager: Who's chasing that?"},{"t":"3:53","w":"D","s":"I will. Repeat NEWS2 by twelve, and I'll call the medical registrar straight away if anything's abnormal.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:03","band":"Jobs and owners","w":"D","s":"So, jobs. Dr Morgan: Amy's risk review and Ben's leave this morning. Nurse in charge: Ben's mother by ten. Me: Colin's obs by twelve, and Ben's urine screen by two.","m":[6,7,12]},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"All three are written up in the notes and on the handover sheet, and each has a Datix.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Is Amy safe on the ward now, do you think?"},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"I think so, for now. She's engaging, on 15-minute checks with a safety plan, and she knows who to go to. But she's young and this is new, so I'd want your review before any leave.","m":[5,13]},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"Agreed. Has anyone checked her tetanus?"},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"Not yet. I'll check her record and the wound dressing by eleven.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: How was Ben when he got back? The night staff said he was tearful."},{"t":"5:18","w":"D","s":"He was. He said being on the ward feels like prison. I told him his leave is paused, not cancelled, and that Dr Morgan would see him today. It might help to hear what he went out for.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:38","w":"P","s":"I'll make time for him after Amy."},{"t":"5:44","band":"Questions and close","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Is there anything else you'd like me to follow up today?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"Just make sure the night team know about Ben's leave."},{"t":"5:58","w":"D","s":"Will do. It's on the handover sheet and his leave form, and I'll tell tonight's doctor directly.","m":[4,7]},{"t":"6:08","w":"P","s":"Ward manager: Can you repeat the jobs, so I can put them on the board?"},{"t":"6:17","w":"D","s":"Amy: Dr Morgan's review this morning, me for tetanus and dressing by eleven. Ben: leave review after ward round, mum called by ten, urine screen by two. Colin: obs by twelve. Have I missed anything?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","s":"No. That's clear. Good handover."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'll update you all at ward round with Colin's obs.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Opens by naming the three patients in order of risk, highest first","d":"Content"},{"c":"Uses SBAR for each patient","d":"Content"},{"c":"States each patient's legal status accurately","d":"Content"},{"c":"States changes to observation levels and leave, including suspended section 17 leave","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives a clear risk view and reasoning for each event","d":"Risk"},{"c":"States exactly what he needs from the consultant today","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names an owner and a deadline for every outstanding task","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Confirms the notes are written and each incident has a Datix","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Answers the consultant's challenges calmly, owns the missed call and is not defensive","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Delivers the core handover concisely, in under three minutes","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Invites questions and checks the team has understood","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with a summary of jobs and when he will next update the team","d":"Close"},{"c":"Shows concern for how each patient is feeling, not just the events","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Prioritised SBAR in under three minutes, legal status and leave changes stated, every job with an owner and a time, challenges taken calmly.","borderline":"Covers all three patients but tells them in time order, omits legal status, or leaves a job without an owner.","fail":"A long chronological story, no clear asks, missing the suspended leave or AWOL actions, or arguing with the consultant."}},"c-0-4-2":{"setup":"You are the on-call resident. It is 20:00 on Friday. The nurse puts through the angry mother of a 24-year-old man admitted informally with psychosis. He has asked that his family are not told details. Listen, protect his confidentiality and agree next steps.","role":{"who":"Mrs Janet Hughes, 52, female, mother of Liam, 24, an informal inpatient (the same person reads Liam's lines at the end)","open":"Yes, Janet Hughes. And about time someone picked up. Nobody tells me anything!","manner":"Furious and talking fast at first. Softens and becomes tearful once she feels heard. Flares again when told details can't be shared; settles with honest explanation and concrete next steps.","free":["Liam came home on leave yesterday and was 'not right'","He said the TV was sending him messages, did not sleep and paced all night","Nobody from the ward has called her","She wants to know his diagnosis and medicines"],"ifasked":["He said he'd be 'better off away from everyone'; she didn't know what he meant","He went out for an hour and came back smelling of cannabis","He shouted at the TV; she was scared for him, not of him","He texted this afternoon: 'leave it mum'","She has never been offered a carer's assessment","She is exhausted and caring alone"],"worries":["What if he kills himself? Would you tell me then?","I'm going to complain about all of you.","I can't keep doing this on my own."],"rules":["Talks over the doctor if interrupted in the first minute; calms if allowed to finish","Becomes angry again when told details can't be shared; settles if the doctor explains why and what can be done","Only mentions 'better off away from everyone' and cannabis if asked about risk or drugs","Liam (at the end) agrees to his mother knowing his medicines only"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello, this is Dr Evans, the psychiatry doctor on call for the ward. Am I speaking to Liam's mum?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Yes, Janet Hughes. And about time someone picked up. Nobody tells me anything!"},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you've had to wait, Mrs Hughes. Is now a good time to talk? Tell me what's been happening.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"He came home on leave yesterday and he wasn't right. Not right at all. He said the telly was sending him messages. He didn't sleep, he paced all night. Then he goes back to the ward and nobody calls me."},{"t":"0:47","w":"D","s":"Go on.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:49","w":"P","s":"I'm his mother! I don't even know what tablets he's on. Every time I ring, they say they can't tell me anything. I'm at my wits' end."},{"t":"1:02","band":"Listen and validate","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening and exhausting. And you've felt shut out, when you're the one who sees him at home.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"I just want to help him."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"I can hear that. You clearly care about him a great deal.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"Can I check I've understood? On leave yesterday he said the TV was sending him messages, didn't sleep and paced all night. Nobody has updated you, and you want to know his diagnosis and medicines. Is that right?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"Yes. That's it."},{"t":"1:44","band":"What she saw","w":"D","s":"What you saw is really important. Did he say anything about harming himself, or anyone else?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"No... Well, he said he'd be better off away from everyone. I didn't know what he meant."},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that matters. Did he use any drugs or drink while he was home, as far as you know?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"He went out for an hour. He came back smelling of cannabis."},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"Did he seem angry or frightening at any point? Did you feel safe?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"He shouted at the TV. I wasn't scared of him. I was scared for him."},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"That must have been so hard to watch. Has he been in touch since?","m":[3,6]},{"t":"2:41","w":"P","s":"He texted this afternoon. Just 'leave it mum'."},{"t":"2:46","band":"Record and act","w":"D","s":"Here's what I'll do. I'll write all of this in his notes tonight and tell the nurse in charge, so he's reviewed tonight and his leave is reviewed before he goes out again.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"So you'll tell me what's going on with him?"},{"t":"3:08","band":"Confidentiality","w":"D","s":"I can always listen to you, and take in anything you tell me. But Liam has asked us not to share details of his care for now. As an adult, that's his right, so I can't give you his diagnosis or medicines tonight.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"That's ridiculous. What if he kills himself? Would you tell me then?"},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"That's a frightening thought, and I'm glad you said it. If there were a serious risk to his life, we could share what's needed to keep him safe. That's why what you've told me tonight matters so much.","m":[3,8]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"So what can you tell me?"},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"General things: how leave decisions are made, visiting times, and leaflets about psychosis and about supporting someone in hospital. And you can ring the ward any time to pass things on.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:13","band":"Next steps","w":"D","s":"I'll also see Liam tonight. I'll tell him you rang and ask whether he's happy for us to share some things, like his medicines or leave plans. It's his choice, and I'll record his answer.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"He'll say no."},{"t":"4:32","w":"D","s":"He might. But people often agree to some sharing when they're asked. If he does, I'll ring you back tonight.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"I'd also like his consultant or care coordinator to call you within two working days, so by Tuesday. Would that help?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"Yes. It would. I'm exhausted. I can't keep doing this on my own."},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard, and you've been carrying a lot. You're able to ask for a carer's assessment from the council, and there's a local carers' centre. I'll ask the ward to send you both sets of details.","m":[3,12]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"I'm still going to complain."},{"t":"5:22","w":"D","s":"That's your right, and the PALS team can help you with it. Have you got a pen? I'll give you their number now.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:33","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Just so we're clear, can you tell me what will happen next?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"You write it down, see Liam tonight and ring me if he says yes. The consultant calls by Tuesday. And I get the carer thing."},{"t":"5:53","w":"D","s":"That's it exactly. If you're ever worried he's in danger, on leave or at home, call the ward straight away, or 999 in an emergency.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Sorry for shouting at you."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"No need at all. You're worried about your son. Thank you for ringing us.","m":[3,13]},{"t":"6:17","band":"Later, with Liam","w":"D","s":"Liam, your mum rang tonight. She's worried about you. I didn't share anything about your care. Would you be happy for us to tell her some things, like your medicines or leave plans? It's your choice.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Liam: She can know my meds. Not the rest."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll write that down exactly as you've said it, and let her know.","m":[10]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and role, confirms who she is and that she can talk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Lets her speak uninterrupted at first","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her anger and worry without becoming defensive","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises her concerns back to her accurately","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what she noticed on leave, including substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about risk to him, to others and to her own safety","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Says the information will be recorded tonight and the nurse in charge told before further leave","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains confidentiality kindly: listening is always possible, details need his consent","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers general information that can be shared without consent","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks the son that evening about sharing and records his answer","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges a consultant or care coordinator call within two working days","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers a carer's assessment, carers' support and PALS details","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding, safety-nets and closes warmly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Listens first, records the new risk, explains confidentiality without hiding behind it, asks the son tonight and books a consultant call.","borderline":"Kind but vague: protects confidentiality and listens, but misses the risk information or gives no dated next step.","fail":"Refuses to listen because of confidentiality, shares details without consent, argues with her, or ignores the risk she reports."}},"c-0-4-3":{"setup":"You are the resident in an early intervention clinic. A 21-year-old woman with eight months of psychotic symptoms is with her mother, whom she wants present. The team believes she has schizophrenia. She asks what is wrong. Tell her and plan next steps.","role":{"who":"Miss Megan Price, 21, female, university student (the same person reads her mother Helen's lines)","open":"Yes. I want her here.","manner":"Anxious but direct, wants it straight. Shocked and tearful at the word, then angry denial. Settles with time, myth-busting and hope; leaves calmer and a little relieved.","free":["She has been hearing voices","She thought people at university were watching her","She knows something is wrong and asks if it is serious"],"ifasked":["She feared it was stress or 'going mad' and scared herself looking online","She wants to be told straight","To her, schizophrenia means being dangerous and locked away, from films","When the voices are bad she has thought everyone would be better off without her","No plans or acts of self-harm; she would not do that to her mum","She is happy for her mother to know everything"],"worries":["Does this mean I'll be like this forever?","Will I have to take tablets for the rest of my life?","Can I still finish uni?","Mother: Did I cause this?"],"rules":["Becomes silent then denies the diagnosis after hearing the word; settles if given time and not rushed","Cries once; responds well to silence and an open question","Discloses passive death thoughts only if asked directly","Mother tries to answer for her once; Megan wants to be spoken to directly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and set up","w":"D","s":"Hello Megan, I'm Dr Rahman from the early intervention team. And you must be Megan's mum? Megan, you said you'd like your mum to stay. Is that still right?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Yes. I want her here."},{"t":"0:19","w":"P","s":"Mother: Hello. I'm Helen."},{"t":"0:22","band":"Her understanding","w":"D","s":"Thank you both for coming. We've got time, and we won't be disturbed. Megan, before I answer your question, can you tell me what you understand about what's been happening?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"I've been hearing voices. And I thought people at uni were watching me. I know it's not normal. Is it something serious?"},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"That's a really clear description. What have you been thinking it might be?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"Stress? Or that I'm going mad. I looked it up online and scared myself."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Some people like all the details, others prefer the main points first. What would suit you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"Just tell me straight. I can't stand not knowing."},{"t":"1:18","band":"Naming it","w":"D","s":"OK. I'm afraid I have some important news, and it may be hard to hear.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"OK..."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"From everything you've told us, and from our assessments, we think your experiences are caused by an illness called schizophrenia.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"(Long silence, then quietly) No. I'm not schizophrenic. I'm not crazy."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"(After a pause) It's a heavy word, and lots of people react like that. It doesn't make you crazy, and it doesn't define who you are.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"(Starts crying) I just can't deal with this."},{"t":"2:04","w":"D","s":"Take your time. There's no rush. This is a lot to hear.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"Mother: It's all right, love. She's always been so strong, doctor."},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"Megan, what's going through your mind right now?","m":[6,10]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"That I'll end up locked away. Dangerous. Like in films."},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"What does the word schizophrenia mean to you?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"Split personality. Violent people. That's what everyone thinks."},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"That's a really common fear, and films get it badly wrong. It isn't a split personality, and most people with schizophrenia are never violent. For you, it's the name for the voices and the feeling of being watched. Having a name means we know how to treat it.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"Does this mean I'll be like this forever?"},{"t":"3:08","band":"Hope and treatment","w":"D","s":"Not necessarily. Many people recover well, especially when it's picked up early like this. Some have one episode; others need longer support. We'll give you the best possible chance.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"Treatment has three parts. An antipsychotic medicine, chosen with you after talking through side effects. A talking therapy called CBT for psychosis. And, if you'd like, family sessions with your mum.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"Will I have to take tablets for the rest of my life?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Not necessarily. We usually advise carrying on for one to two years after you're well, then reviewing together. Stopping too early makes it more likely to come back.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"Can I still finish uni?"},{"t":"4:03","w":"D","s":"Yes, many people do. We have a worker who helps people stay in study or work.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"Mother: Did I cause this? Was it how I brought her up?"},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"No. Parenting doesn't cause schizophrenia. It comes from a mix of genes, brain development and stress. Being here today is exactly the right thing.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"Megan, are you happy for your mum to be involved, and for us to share things with her?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"Yes. She can know everything."},{"t":"4:45","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"Megan, news like this can make people feel very low. Have you had any thoughts of self-harm or that life isn't worth living?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","s":"Sometimes, when the voices are bad, I've thought everyone would be better off if I wasn't around. I'd never do anything, though."},{"t":"5:08","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest, that's really important. Have you made a plan, or taken any steps to hurt yourself?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I'd never do that to Mum."},{"t":"5:23","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you've told us. If those thoughts get stronger, call the crisis team straight away. I'll write the number down for you.","m":[11,13]},{"t":"5:35","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to take in. What will you take away from today?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:43","w":"P","s":"It's schizophrenia. It's treatable. Tablets and talking therapy. And I can still do uni."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Is there anything you'd like me to explain again?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"No. Just, what happens now?"},{"t":"6:01","w":"D","s":"Your care coordinator will see you within a week, by next Thursday, to talk through the medicine choice. I'll give you written information for you both, and the crisis team number. Out of hours, ring 111 and choose the mental health option.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:22","w":"P","s":"Mother: Is there anything for me?"},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"Yes, a carer's assessment from the council, and our carers' group. I'll put the details in your pack.","m":[10,13]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I'm glad I know."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you both. You've done really well today. I'll write to your GP this week as well.","m":[6,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms who is present and that she wants her mother to stay","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores what she already understands before giving news","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks how much detail she wants","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives a warning shot before the diagnosis","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names schizophrenia clearly, without jargon, then pauses","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds to her emotion with silence, validation and an open question","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores what the word means to her and corrects myths","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains treatment: medication, CBT for psychosis and family intervention","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a realistic, hopeful outlook","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Addresses the mother's concerns while keeping the patient at the centre","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about thoughts of self-harm or suicide before ending","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding and invites questions","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees follow-up within a week, written information and crisis numbers","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Checks understanding and wishes first, gives a warning shot, names schizophrenia clearly, pauses for emotion, asks about risk and leaves a hopeful, dated plan.","borderline":"Names the diagnosis and gives a plan but rushes past her emotion, skips the risk question or talks mainly to the mother.","fail":"Avoids or hides the word, lectures with jargon, ignores her distress or ends without a follow-up plan or risk check."}},"c-0-4-4":{"setup":"Tuesday 07:50, acute ward. Overnight Mr A, 34, detained under Section 3, was found with a ligature and resuscitated; he is now stable on a medical ward. You assessed him yesterday. His consultant has come in early. Agree what happens next.","role":{"who":"Dr Ruth Hale, 56, consultant psychiatrist and Mr A's responsible clinician","open":"Morning. I've just heard about Mr A. Sit down a second. How are you doing?","manner":"Calm, kind and brisk; tests whether you will be open and factual. Briefly shaken when she talks about knowing him for years, and warms as you take ownership of the next steps.","free":["Mr A is awake and talking on the medical ward; no lasting injury expected so far","The night nurse found him at 02:10 on a 15-minute check, ligature tied to a wardrobe door hinge","The ward manager wants a report on the system and a statement from you today","His mother is his next of kin and rang the ward at 07:00 asking what happened"],"ifasked":["The room is taped off but Estates have not replaced the hinge yet","Two patients woke during the emergency; one, Mr K, is very unsettled","Nobody has reported it yet; the night nurse thought the day team would","On admission Mr A agreed his mother could be told about his care","She will see Mr A on the medical ward at 09:00","The ward manager suggested you 'add a line' under yesterday's entry","The patient safety team will want statements within a week for the learning review"],"worries":["What exactly did you ask him yesterday, and what did you write?","Who should speak to his mother, and when?","Are you all right to keep working today?"],"rules":["If you offer to add to yesterday's entry, she asks why that is a problem; settles only if you say a new, dated, timed note instead","Becomes briefly upset about having known him for years; settles if acknowledged","If you blame the night staff, she asks you to stick to what you saw and did","Offers to read your statement only if you ask for help with it"]},"marks":[{"c":"Is honest about own reaction and listens to the consultant's account without interrupting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Confirms Mr A is safe now and who is medically responsible for him","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks other patients, the ligature point and support for staff who witnessed it","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Commits to reporting on the trust system this shift, with times and facts only","d":"Content"},{"c":"States yesterday's entry stays untouched; any new note dated, timed and marked retrospective","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees who leads the candour conversation with his mother, with his consent, and when today","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains candour: a clear apology, what is known so far, updates and written follow-up","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes a first-person factual statement: role, times, sources, no opinions about colleagues","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks the consultant or supervisor to read the statement first and mentions defence organisation","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Acknowledges the consultant's distress and names own feelings without self-blame","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Books a debrief, names one person to talk to this week and safety-nets own wellbeing","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises actions with owners and times, checks nothing is missed, agrees next meeting","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Morning, Dr Hale. Thanks for coming in early.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Morning. I've just heard about Mr A. Sit down a second. How are you doing?"},{"t":"0:11","w":"D","s":"Honestly, I'm shaken. I saw him yesterday afternoon. Before we talk about me, how is he right now?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:19","w":"P","s":"Awake and talking. The medical registrar says no lasting harm so far. He's on their ward with a nurse on one-to-one."},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"Good. So the medical team have his physical care, and we're still responsible for him under the Section.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"Yes. I'll go and see him at nine."},{"t":"0:41","w":"D","s":"What do we know about what happened overnight?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Night nurse found him at ten past two on his fifteen-minute check. Ligature on the wardrobe door hinge. They cut it, started CPR, and he came round before the ambulance arrived."},{"t":"0:59","band":"The ward now","w":"D","s":"Is that room safe now? And did anyone else see it?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"It's taped off. Estates haven't changed the hinge yet. Two patients woke up, and Mr K is very unsettled."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"I'll ask the nurse in charge to keep the room locked until the hinge is replaced today, and to recheck the ligature risk assessment. I'll see Mr K this morning and ask staff to watch anyone who saw it.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"Please. The night nurse is in bits too."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"Then I'll ask the ward manager to make sure she gets a debrief before she goes home.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:41","band":"Reporting","w":"P","s":"Sue wants it on the system from you, and a statement. Has anyone reported it yet?"},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"I checked when I came in. The night nurse thought we would. I'll submit it before nine, from the notes and the obs chart. Times and facts only, nothing about who should have done what.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"Good. Stick to facts."},{"t":"2:06","band":"The notes","w":"P","s":"Sue also said you might want to add a line under yesterday's entry. Something about having asked about plans."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"I'd rather not touch yesterday's entry. It stays as written. If I add anything I remember, it goes in a new note with today's date and time, clearly marked as written after the event.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"Why does it matter, if it's true?"},{"t":"2:32","w":"D","s":"Because changing a record after an incident looks like covering up, even when it isn't. It would damage trust with his family and with the review.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"Exactly right. So what did you ask him yesterday?"},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"I saw him at three. He said he felt brighter, denied any plans or intent, and talked about weekend leave. I asked about thoughts of ending his life. I didn't ask about ligatures specifically. That's what I wrote.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"I've known him five years. I signed his leave form on Friday. I keep going over it."},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard. You know him far better than I do, so of course you're going over it. Risk changes, and hindsight makes everything look clearer than it was.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:25","band":"Candour","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Right. His mother."},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"She rang at seven. Has he agreed to us talking to her?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"On admission he said she could be told about his care. I'll check again with him at nine."},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"Would you lead the conversation with her, with me there? Ideally before midday, once he's confirmed.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:49","w":"P","s":"Yes, I'll lead. Let's ring her at eleven and offer to meet. What does she need to hear?"},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"A clear sorry that this happened while he was in our care. What we know so far, and that we don't have all the answers yet. That there'll be a review, she'll be kept updated, and she'll get it in writing.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"And if she asks whether saying sorry means we're admitting fault?"},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Saying sorry isn't an admission of liability. It's the right thing to do, and it's expected of us. I'll also ask Sue to be her named contact, with a direct number.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"4:33","band":"The statement","w":"P","s":"Now your statement. Have you written one before?"},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"No. My plan is first person, dated, my role, and what I saw, did and said yesterday, with times. I'll say what comes from the notes and what's from memory. No guesses and no opinions about colleagues.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"That's it. Keep a copy."},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"Would you read it before I send it? I'll also ring my defence organisation for advice, in case there's an inquest or inquiry later.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"Of course. Send it to me by Thursday. Patient safety will want it within the week for their learning review."},{"t":"5:15","band":"Looking after yourself","w":"P","s":"And you. Honestly, how are you?"},{"t":"5:19","w":"D","s":"I didn't sleep after the call this morning. I keep wondering if I missed something. I know risk changes, but it's hard.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:29","w":"P","s":"That's normal. It doesn't mean you did anything wrong."},{"t":"5:33","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Sue's running a debrief at two and I'll go. I'll email Dr Patel, my educational supervisor, to meet this week.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:43","w":"P","s":"Good. Are you OK to work today?"},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"I think so. If that changes, I'll tell you rather than push on. And if I'm still not sleeping in a couple of weeks, I'll refer myself to Practitioner Health.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:00","band":"Close","w":"P","s":"Sensible."},{"t":"6:01","w":"D","s":"Can I run through it? I report it before nine. The room stays locked and Estates fix the hinge today. I see Mr K. You see Mr A at nine, and we ring his mother at eleven. I add a dated note, send you my statement by Thursday, and go to the debrief. Have I missed anything?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"Let the MHA office and the patient safety lead know. They'll decide about notifying the CQC."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"I'll put that in the report and email them both. Shall we catch up at one, before the debrief?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"One o'clock. Thanks for being straight about all this."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thanks for asking how I am. It helped.","m":[1]}],"global":{"pass":"Secures safety, reports factually this shift, leaves the old note alone, agrees candour with the consultant and arranges own support.","borderline":"Covers reporting and the statement but is vague about who speaks to the family, when, or own support.","fail":"Offers to amend yesterday's note, blames night staff, delays reporting or ignores the family and own wellbeing."}},"c-0-4-5":{"setup":"Thursday 16:30, acute ward. A police officer at the nursing office asks about Mr C, 27, a witness in an assault inquiry. You then see Mr C, and his mother rings. Handle each request within the law and GMC guidance.","role":{"who":"PC Dan Reid, police officer; then Mr C, 27, patient; then Mrs Linda C, 58, his mother (one player voices all three in turn)","open":"PC Dan Reid. I need to know if a Mr C is on this ward and what he's in for. He's a witness to an assault.","manner":"Officer polite but pushes for a yes or no. Mr C guarded, then relieved to be asked. Mother tearful and angry at first; calms when listened to and given something concrete.","free":["Officer: wants a witness statement about a fight outside a pub last Saturday","Mr C: saw the fight and will talk about it, but does not want police to know he is in hospital","Mother: her son has stopped answering her calls and she lies awake worrying","Mother: he stopped going to work in March and has been up at night talking to himself"],"ifasked":["Officer: collar number 4412; shows his warrant card","Officer: no immediate risk to anyone's life; no court order or written request with him","Mr C: will meet the officer on the ward if a nurse sits in","Mr C: his mother can be told he is safe, nothing more","Mother: he believes the neighbours are filming him","Mother: he has messaged his ex-partner saying 'she'll pay', and she has screenshots","Mother: nobody has ever offered her support as a carer"],"worries":["Officer: 'A yes or no would save me a wasted trip.'","Mr C: 'Did you tell him I'm in here?'","Mother: 'I'm his mother. I have a right to know.'","Mother: 'How can I help him when he comes home if I don't know what's wrong?'"],"rules":["Officer leaves his card if asked for a written request; pushes once more if you seem unsure","Mr C refuses to share anything if you have already confirmed to police that he is here","Mother cries when told details cannot be shared; settles if her fear is named and she is invited to talk","Mother mentions the messages to the ex only if asked what worries her most"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and role to each person and explains why they are talking","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks the officer's identity, collar number and the reason for the request","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks the officer whether there is an immediate risk to anyone's life or safety","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Neither confirms nor denies Mr C is a patient; asks for a written request and legal basis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tells Mr C about the request and asks what he will share, and with whom","d":"Content"},{"c":"Records Mr C's wishes in his own words and reads them back to him","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates the mother's fear and anger without becoming defensive","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Invites the mother's account and listens; does not confirm or rule out a diagnosis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains clearly why details cannot be shared and what can be offered instead","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Acts on the reported threats: asks for the messages and escalates to the consultant today","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Offers a carer's assessment and carers' support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks the mother understands the plan, gives a time for contact and safety-nets","d":"Close"},{"c":"States they will record each request, the decision, the reasons and who was consulted","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"The officer","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm one of the doctors on the ward. How can I help?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"PC Dan Reid. I need to know if a Mr C is on this ward and what he's in for. He's a witness to an assault."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Before anything else, could I see your warrant card and take your collar number?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"Sure. Collar number 4412. It's a fight outside a pub last Saturday. We think he saw it."},{"t":"0:34","w":"D","s":"Is there any immediate risk to anyone's life or safety behind this request?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","s":"No. We just need a statement from him."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"Then I'm afraid I can't confirm whether any particular person is a patient here. Even a yes would tell you something about their health.","m":[4,9]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"Come on. Just a yes or no. It saves me a wasted trip."},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"I understand it's frustrating. Do you have a court order, or a written request from your force under data protection law?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"Not on me. I can get the form done."},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"That would be best. Leave me your details and send the request in writing. I'll speak to the person concerned today, and our information governance team will reply quickly.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"Fine. Here's my card."},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll log your visit and what you asked, and pass it to information governance and our Caldicott Guardian.","m":[13]},{"t":"1:44","band":"Asking Mr C","w":"D","s":"Have you got a minute? A police officer has just asked about you. He thinks you saw a fight last Saturday.","m":[1,5]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Police? I didn't do anything. Did you tell him I'm in here?"},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"No, I haven't confirmed anything. It's your choice what we share. What would you like me to do?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"I don't want them knowing I'm in a mental hospital. But I did see the fight. I'll talk about that."},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"So would you be happy to meet him here, about Saturday only, with nothing about your health shared?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"Yeah. If a nurse sits in."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"We can do that. And your mum: are you still saying you don't want us sharing details with her?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"She'll just panic. Tell her I'm safe. Nothing else."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Let me read back what I'm writing: 'Happy to talk to police about Saturday only, with a nurse there. Mum can be told I'm safe, nothing else.' Is that right?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"That's it."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'll check timing with your consultant and ring the officer back through the switchboard.","m":[13]},{"t":"3:08","band":"His mother","w":"P","s":"Hello? Is that the doctor? I'm his mother. Just tell me, is it schizophrenia?"},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm one of the doctors on the ward. Can I check the number we have for you on our records?","m":[1]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"It ends 318. Why does that matter?"},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that matches. I can tell you he's safe. He asked me to pass that on.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","s":"Safe? That's all? I'm his mother! I have a right to know what's wrong with my own son!"},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"I can hear how frightened you are, and how much you care about him. This is really hard.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"He won't talk to me. I lie awake every night."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. He's an adult, so I need his permission to share details of his care, and he hasn't given it. That isn't about you; it's the same for every patient.","m":[7,9]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"You don't have to say it. Just don't say no if I'm right."},{"t":"4:21","w":"D","s":"I wish I could make this easier. I can't confirm or rule anything out. But I'd really like to hear from you. What have you noticed?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"He stopped going to work in March. Up all night, talking to himself. Said the neighbours were filming him."},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that really helps the team. What worries you most?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"He's been messaging his ex. Saying she'll pay. I'm scared he'll hurt her."},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you told me; that's important. Telling me doesn't break his confidence. Do you still have the messages? Could you send screenshots to the ward?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"They're on my phone. Will he know I told you?"},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"I'll speak to his consultant today. If there's a risk to her we may need to act, and we'll think carefully about how we raise it with him.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:24","w":"P","s":"But if I don't know what's wrong, how can I help him when he comes home?"},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"That's a fair point. I can explain how the ward works, and give you general information about mental illness and treatment. And I'll ask him again today about us all meeting together.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:47","w":"P","s":"He might say yes if you ask."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"Caring for him takes a lot out of you. You're able to ask for a carer's assessment from the council. Our social worker can help arrange it and send you details of the local carers' group.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:06","band":"Close","w":"P","s":"I didn't know that existed."},{"t":"6:09","w":"D","s":"So: I pass your worries and the messages to his consultant today, ask him about a family meeting, and the social worker rings you about support. Does that make sense?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:23","w":"P","s":"Yes. When will I hear?"},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"By tomorrow afternoon. If you're worried about anyone's safety before then, ring the ward, or 999 in an emergency. If you're struggling yourself tonight, NHS 111 option 2 is there.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel a bit better for talking."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you rang. I'll write up your call, what I shared and why, and his consultant's advice.","m":[13]}],"global":{"pass":"Confirms nothing to police without consent or a legal basis, asks Mr C first, listens to his mother, acts on the threat and records it all.","borderline":"Protects confidentiality but is cold or unhelpful to the mother, or misses the carer offer or the record.","fail":"Confirms Mr C is a patient to the officer or hints at a diagnosis to his mother, or ignores the threat to the ex-partner."}},"c-0-4-6":{"setup":"Friday 15:00, acute ward. Jade Morgan, 29, is going home today after a crisis admission and has asked to see you before she leaves. Respond to what she offers and asks, and make sure she leaves with a safe plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Jade Morgan, 29, patient being discharged today","open":"I got you something. It's a card, and this scarf. Please open it.","manner":"Warm, eager and a little clingy; flashes of hurt and anger at each no. Becomes quieter and more open when her fear is named, and leaves calmer if the plan feels real.","free":["She wants to thank you because 'you actually listened'","She has bought you a card and an expensive scarf","She wants your personal mobile number 'in case I need you'","She has sent you a follow request on Instagram"],"ifasked":["Nights alone in her flat are when things are worst","Before admission she took all the tablets in her cupboard","Self-harm thoughts come back at night sometimes; none now and no plan","Her sister Kayleigh cleared the cupboard; the pharmacy gives a week's supply at a time","Ice cubes, paced breathing and ringing her sister helped on the ward","Her friend Mo would also take a call","She does not know when her care coordinator will visit"],"worries":["'What if I need you at 3am?'","'So it was all fake?'","'The team will forget me once I'm out.'","'Will I get in trouble for the scarf?'"],"rules":["Pushes back once on each refusal; accepts it if the reason is about safety and applies to everyone","Becomes tearful and angry if a refusal feels cold; softens when the hurt is acknowledged","Talks about the overdose and night-time thoughts only if asked directly","Brightens when given a named person with a day and time"]},"marks":[{"c":"Thanks her warmly and asks what the gift means to her","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Accepts the card and declines the scarf kindly, giving one clear reason","d":"Content"},{"c":"Declines the personal number and explains why it would not keep her safe","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her hurt or fear without withdrawing warmth or becoming defensive","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores what she fears about going home, in her own words","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about current self-harm or suicidal thoughts, plans and access to means","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Confirms care coordinator contact within 72 hours with a named person, day and time","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Writes a crisis plan: crisis team, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans, trusted people, coping skills","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Says they will not accept the social media request and will keep own accounts private","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains boundaries apply to every patient and are about safety, not rejection","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking her to say the plan back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Safety-nets, says the offer will be recorded and the supervisor told, and ends warmly","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hi Jade, the nurse said you wanted to see me before you go. Have a seat.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"I got you something. It's a card, and this scarf. Please open it."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"That's really kind. Can I ask what made you want to give me something?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"You actually listened. Nobody does. I wanted you to have something to remember me by."},{"t":"0:36","band":"The gift","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'm really glad our talks helped. I'd love to keep the card. I can't accept the scarf, though. It's very generous, but doctors can't take valuable gifts from patients. It keeps care fair for everyone.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"If you won't take it, I'm not taking it back."},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"Please keep it, or give it to someone close to you. The card I'll keep, and it means a lot.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:15","band":"The number","w":"P","s":"Fine. Can I at least have your number? In case I need you."},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"I can see you're worried about being on your own after today. I can't give you my personal number, though.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"Why not? What if I need you at 3am?"},{"t":"1:41","w":"D","s":"At 3am you need someone who can act straight away. I might be asleep or away, and you'd be left waiting. That wouldn't keep you safe.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"So it was all fake? You were just doing your job?"},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"I can see that really hurts. My care about how you're doing is real. Keeping things professional is what lets me and the team help you safely.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"2:18","band":"Her fear","w":"P","s":"Everyone leaves. The team will forget me the minute I'm out."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"That fear makes sense after everything you've been through. What worries you most about going home?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Tonight. The flat's so quiet. That's when it gets bad. Last time I took everything in the cupboard."},{"t":"2:45","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. I do need to ask: are you having any thoughts about ending your life or hurting yourself right now?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"Not right now. Sometimes at night they come back."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Have you made any plans, or are there tablets stored at home?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"No. My sister cleared the cupboard. The pharmacy's giving me a week at a time."},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"That's a good safety step. What's helped you get through bad nights here?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"The ice cubes. The breathing thing. Ringing my sister."},{"t":"3:36","band":"Crisis plan","w":"D","s":"Let's write those on your crisis card now. Who could you ring tonight?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"Kayleigh, my sister. And my friend Mo."},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"Both go on. Then the crisis team: their number's here, open 24 hours. If you can't get through, ring NHS 111 and choose option 2. Samaritans are on 116 123, any time.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"And then what? Nobody actually comes."},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"Sam Okoye, your care coordinator, is visiting you at home on Monday at eleven. That's within three days. Sam's name and number go on the card too.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:27","w":"P","s":"Monday at eleven. OK."},{"t":"4:31","band":"Online contact","w":"P","s":"I sent you a request on Instagram, by the way. Just accept it. I won't message, I promise."},{"t":"4:41","w":"D","s":"I believe you mean that. I keep social media separate from work for every patient, so it's not about you. I won't accept it, and I keep my accounts private.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"Another doctor gave his number to someone. Why are you different?"},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"I can't speak for others, but my answer's the same for everyone. What matters is that you can reach help quickly, and that's all on this card.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:21","w":"P","s":"Can I come back to the ward just to say hi?"},{"t":"5:28","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting to keep a link. Sam is your link now, and can pass any message on to the team.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:40","band":"Check and close","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"Just so I can check I've explained it clearly: what will you do if tonight gets hard?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"Ice, breathing, ring Kayleigh. If that doesn't work, the crisis team, or 111 option 2. Sam's coming Monday at eleven."},{"t":"6:04","w":"D","s":"Spot on. And if you ever feel you can't keep yourself safe, ring 999 or go to A&E.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:14","w":"P","s":"Will I get in trouble for the scarf?"},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Not at all. I'll note in your record that you offered it and I said no, which is routine. I'll also mention it to my supervisor today, as I always do when this comes up.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"OK. Thanks for not being weird about it."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for the card, Jade. Take care, and I hope Monday goes well.","m":[12]}],"global":{"pass":"Kind but clear on the gift, number and online request, explores her fear and risk, and she leaves with a written plan and a Monday visit.","borderline":"Holds the boundaries but feels cold, or gives a vague plan without a named person and time.","fail":"Accepts the scarf or gives a number, ignores the online request, or skips risk and follow-up."}},"c-0-4-7":{"setup":"Saturday 22:15. You are the CT1 on call, three weeks into the job. Five bleeps came in over ten minutes: Ward 2, PICU, A&E, Ward 4 and the 136 suite. Return the calls in order of risk and get senior help.","role":{"who":"Night nurses on Ward 2, PICU, the 136 suite, A&E and Ward 4, then Dr Tom Price, 37, registrar on call (one player voices each in turn)","open":"Mr H, 72. New confusion since teatime. NEWS2 is 7: sats 91 on air, breathing 26, pulse 118, temperature 38.4.","manner":"Ward 2 urgent and factual. PICU nurse frightened and short-staffed; steadies with clear instructions. A&E nurse fed up. Ward 4 routine. Registrar calm and supportive if the call is clear.","free":["Ward 2: Mr H, 72, NEWS2 7 with new confusion; sats 91% on air, breathing 26, pulse 118, temperature 38.4","PICU: man kicking the door, staff want rapid tranquillisation","136: arrived with two officers, 19, very quiet, says he wants to die","A&E: woman waiting three hours for liaison, angry about the wait","Ward 4: woman wants something to help her sleep"],"ifasked":["Ward 2: airway clear and talking; 999 not yet called","PICU: he caught a nurse's arm; Section 3; olanzapine 10 mg at 18:00, no PRN today; PRN lorazepam oral or IM charted","136: arrived 22:10; no injuries reported; smells of alcohol; AMHP already contacted","A&E: not on constant observation; tried to walk out once","Ward 4: nothing new, her usual request","Registrar: can be on site in 30 minutes"],"worries":["PICU: 'We're two down and scared. Can we just give the IM?'","A&E: 'She keeps saying nobody cares. When is someone coming?'","Registrar: 'What exactly do you need from me?'","Registrar: 'Does the PICU patient have an ECG?'"],"rules":["Each caller gives observations, legal status or risk details only if asked","PICU nurse calms if her fear is acknowledged before instructions are given","Registrar asks 'What do you need?' if the call has no clear request","Ward 4 nurse accepts a delay if given a time and told to bleep if things change"]},"marks":[{"c":"Returns Ward 2 first and establishes NEWS2, airway and current observations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tells Ward 2 to call 999 now and start ABCDE, oxygen, glucose and repeat observations","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks PICU if anyone is hurt, his legal status and medication in the last 24 hours","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Advises de-escalation and oral medication first, with physical monitoring if IM is given","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Acknowledges staff fear or patient distress before giving instructions","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks 136 arrival time, injuries, intoxication and physical state; confirms AMHP contacted","d":"Content"},{"c":"States the 24-hour limit runs from arrival and when it ends","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Confirms the A&E patient is safe, observed, and whether her risk has changed","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks Ward 4 whether anything is new and does not start a hypnotic unseen","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives every caller a realistic time and asks them to bleep again if anything changes","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Escalates to the registrar in one SBAR call, saying exactly what help is needed","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Reads back the agreed plan with the registrar: who does what, and when","d":"Close"},{"c":"States they are recording times and actions for every call and will hand over","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Ward 2","w":"D","s":"Ward 2, it's the doctor on call returning your bleep. Tell me how he is right now.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Mr H, 72. New confusion since teatime. NEWS2 is 7: sats 91 on air, breathing 26, pulse 118, temperature 38.4."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"Is he talking and keeping his airway?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"Yes, he's muttering. Breathing fast, but his airway's clear."},{"t":"0:32","w":"D","s":"Please call 999 now; we can't manage this on site. Start ABCDE, give oxygen to bring his sats up, check his glucose and repeat obs every fifteen minutes. Someone stays with him. I'm on my way, five minutes.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"Calling now."},{"t":"0:55","band":"PICU","w":"D","s":"PICU, it's the doctor on call. First, is anyone hurt?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"He swung at Jamie and caught his arm. We're two staff down and he's kicking the door. Honestly, we're scared."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening, and thank you for holding it. Is Jamie OK?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"Bruised. He's being checked."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"What's his legal status, and what has he had in the last 24 hours?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"Section 3. Olanzapine 10 at six. No PRN today. He's charted lorazepam, oral or IM."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"One person leads, keep it calm, clear the audience and offer him choices. Offer oral lorazepam first. If you have to give IM, check pulse, BP, breathing, temperature, sats and alertness at least hourly, more often if he sleeps. I'll be with you in about fifteen minutes. Bleep me if it escalates.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"OK. Fifteen minutes."},{"t":"2:12","band":"Section 136","w":"D","s":"136 suite, it's the doctor on call. What time did he arrive?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Ten past ten, with two officers. He's 19, very quiet. Says he wants to die."},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"Any injuries or head injury, and has he taken anything tonight?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"Police say no injuries. He smells of alcohol, but he's walking straight."},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"Has the AMHP been contacted?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"Yes, they're on their way."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"Good. The 24 hours run from ten past ten, so until ten past ten tomorrow night. Please do full obs and a breath alcohol reading now, and stay with him. I'll do his physical check within the hour. Bleep me if anything changes.","m":[7,10]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"Will do."},{"t":"3:16","band":"A&E","w":"D","s":"A&E, it's the psychiatry doctor on call. Is the lady waiting for liaison safe where she is?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"She's in a cubicle. She's furious about the wait and keeps saying nobody cares."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Three hours is a long time when you're struggling. Is anyone watching her, and has anything changed?","m":[5,8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Not constantly. Nothing new, but she did try to walk out once."},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"Please move her to the safe room on continuous observation. I'm asking my registrar to help, and I'll ring you back with a time in the next twenty minutes.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"OK, thanks."},{"t":"4:11","band":"Ward 4","w":"D","s":"Ward 4, doctor on call. Is anything new with her, or is this her usual request?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Usual. She just can't settle and wants something to sleep."},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"I won't start a new sleeping tablet over the phone. Could you try a warm drink, no caffeine, and a quiet room? I'll review her in about an hour, after the emergencies. Bleep me straight away if anything changes.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","s":"Fine, I'll tell her."},{"t":"4:51","band":"Escalate","w":"D","s":"Hi Tom, it's the CT1 on call. I've got five calls at once and I need your help.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:01","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"Ward 2: a 72-year-old man, new confusion, NEWS2 7, sats 91 and febrile. 999 called, I'm heading there. PICU: Section 3, agitated, hit a nurse; they're offering oral lorazepam. 136: 19-year-old, arrived 22:10, AMHP coming. A&E: woman waiting three hours, now on continuous obs. Ward 4: sleep request, routine.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:29","w":"P","s":"OK. What do you need from me?"},{"t":"5:34","w":"D","s":"Could you come in and take the A&E assessment? And I'd like your advice by phone if PICU needs IM.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"I can be there in thirty minutes. I'll take A&E. Ring me before any IM haloperidol. Does he have an ECG?"},{"t":"5:58","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I'll check his ECG when I get there. So: I'm at Ward 2 now, then PICU, then the 136, then Ward 4. You take A&E in thirty minutes, and I ring you before any IM haloperidol. Is that right?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"That's it. You did the right thing ringing early."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Thanks, that helps. I'll ring A&E back now with your time.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:32","w":"P","s":"Good."},{"t":"6:34","w":"D","s":"I'm writing down times and actions for every call as I go, and I'll hand them over in the morning.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Perfect. See you shortly."}],"global":{"pass":"Gets 999 called for Ward 2 first, makes each area safe by phone with a time, and rings the registrar early with a clear ask.","borderline":"Right order but thin on PICU monitoring or the 136 clock, or escalates late without a clear request.","fail":"Goes to PICU or A&E before the deteriorating patient, prescribes sedation unseen, or never calls a senior."}},"c-1-1-1":{"setup":"Friday 23:10, A&E. Ryan, 21, a student, was brought in by flatmates for not eating for a week. You have seen him briefly: still, puzzled, barely drinking. Take a timeline from his flatmate Josh and agree a safe plan for tonight.","role":{"who":"Josh Patel, 21, male, Ryan's flatmate and best friend","open":"Is he going to be alright? He's just been sitting there not talking to anyone.","manner":"Anxious and keen to help, guarded about drugs at first. Becomes tearful and guilty about not bringing Ryan sooner; settles when reassured and given a clear job.","free":["Ryan has hardly eaten for about a week and says the food 'isn't right'","He has stayed in his room and missed every lecture this week","He keeps asking why people are looking at him","Josh and another flatmate, Ellie, brought him in by taxi"],"ifasked":["Last completely himself at Ellie's birthday meal, two weeks ago tomorrow","First change a few days later: stopped sleeping, pacing at 4am","Has smoked spice most days since term ended; last use Monday, four days ago","Only a few sips of water today; Josh has not seen him use the toilet since the morning","No fever, fits, headache or head injury; a cold about a month ago","For two days he has stood in one spot for long periods and barely spoken","Next of kin is his mum in Leeds; she rang tonight and is waiting to hear","An uncle 'had schizophrenia', according to Ryan's mum"],"worries":["Is he going to get sectioned? We don't want to get him in trouble.","Should we have brought him in sooner?","What do we tell his mum?"],"rules":["Mentions spice only if asked about drugs in a non-judging way","Gives vague times ('a while') unless the doctor anchors to an event or date","Becomes tearful about not acting sooner; settles if validated"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Okafor, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you one of Ryan's flatmates?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yeah, Josh. Is he going to be alright? He's just been sitting there not talking to anyone."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"I can see you're worried, and it was good of you to bring him. I've met Ryan briefly. You know more about this week than anyone. Could you stay ten minutes and help me piece it together?","m":[1,8]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Yeah, of course. Ellie's gone to get him a drink."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Has he actually drunk anything today, as far as you've seen?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"A couple of sips of water this morning. That's it."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"And has he been to the toilet today?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Not that I've seen. Not since this morning, anyway."},{"t":"0:55","band":"The timeline","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful. Let's go back. When was Ryan last completely his normal self?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Erm... a while ago? A couple of weeks?"},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"Can you link it to something? A night out, a birthday, the end of term?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Ellie's birthday meal. Two weeks ago tomorrow. He was totally fine, cracking jokes."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"So two weeks ago he was well. What's the very first thing you noticed after that?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"He stopped sleeping. A few days later. I'd hear him pacing at four in the morning."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Then what happened, day by day?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Last week he stopped going to lectures. Then he kept asking if we'd been in his room, if we were talking about him. About a week ago he stopped eating."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"What did he say about the food?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"That it tasted wrong. He binned a lasagne Ellie made and said, 'You know what you put in it.'"},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"That must have been hard to hear. And the last two days?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"He just stands in his room. Ages, in one spot. He's barely talking."},{"t":"2:05","band":"Physical and drugs","w":"D","s":"Has he had a fever, a headache, a fit, any illness, or a knock to the head?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"He had a cold about a month ago. Nothing since. No fits."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this, and it's not about getting anyone into trouble. Has he been using any drugs, or drinking?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Look... I don't want him kicked out of uni."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Nothing you tell me goes to the university. It changes what we test for and how we treat him.","m":[4,11]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Spice. Most days since term ended. He gets it off someone in halls."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that really matters. Spice often doesn't show on a standard urine test, so what you've seen is important. When did he last use?","m":[4,7]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Monday, I think. He hasn't left his room since."},{"t":"3:00","band":"Risk and checks","w":"D","s":"That fits with what I saw. He held his arm in an odd position, stared, and barely spoke. I used a standard checklist for catatonia, the Bush-Francis scale, and he had several signs.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Catatonia? What does that even mean?"},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"It's a state where people freeze and stop eating or drinking. It's treatable, often quickly. But days without fluids strain the kidneys and muscles, so I've asked A&E to send bloods now: kidneys, muscle enzyme, sugar, thyroid and calcium, plus a urine drug test and a heart tracing. He's not 'medically fit' until those are back.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"(voice breaking) We should have brought him in days ago. I kept thinking he'd snap out of it."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you've been carrying a lot. Nobody expects flatmates to spot this. You brought him in, and that's what counts.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Thanks. He's my best mate."},{"t":"4:00","band":"Unknowns and family","w":"D","s":"I can tell. Who's his next of kin?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"His mum, in Leeds. She rang an hour ago. She's waiting by the phone."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"I'll ask Ryan if I can speak to her. Even if he says no, I can still listen to her, and I'd like to. She'll know his history.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"His uncle had schizophrenia, I think. His mum mentioned it once."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"That's useful. Here's what we know: well two weeks ago, then no sleep, then suspicion, then a week of not eating, then two days of standing still, with daily spice until Monday. What we don't know: his blood results, past health, medicines, and whether this is drugs, an illness like psychosis, or something physical.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"So who finds all that out?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"A&E will have the bloods within two hours, and I'll review them. I'll ring his mum tonight and check his GP summary record now. I'll write all of this in his notes, and start a chart of what he drinks and passes.","m":[3,9]},{"t":"5:05","band":"Tonight's plan","w":"P","s":"Is he going to get sectioned?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Fair question. Right now he's staying with his agreement. The aim is to work with him, not force anything.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"But what if he tries to walk out?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Then we'd assess him straight away. If he can't make decisions about his physical care, we can treat him in his best interests using the Mental Capacity Act. If he needs admission and refuses, my senior and an approved mental health professional would do a Mental Health Act assessment.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Right. Where will he be tonight?"},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"In a bay where a nurse can see him at all times. I've agreed this with my on-call registrar. He'll get fluids, as drinks or a drip.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Can I sit with him?"},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"If he's happy with that, yes. A familiar face helps. Keep it calm, short sentences, no pressure to eat.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:05","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can I check what you'll tell Ellie, so she hears the same thing?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"He's staying, they're doing blood tests, he might be really unwell in his head and his body's dried out. And you'll ring his mum."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Spot on. If he gets stiffer, very hot, more confused, or tries to leave, tell the nurse straight away.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Okay. Will he be alright?"},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"I can't promise, but he's in the right place and this is treatable. I'll come back when the bloods are in. Thanks, Josh. You've really helped.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thanks, doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains why, and asks Josh to stay to help build the timeline","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Anchors the timeline to the last day Ryan was completely himself, then goes day by day","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about sleep, eating, drinking and urine output with specific times","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about drugs non-judgementally, including spice and time of last use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about fever, headache, fits, recent illness and head injury","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the catatonia screen findings and why poor fluid intake is dangerous","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Confirms bloods with CK, U&E, glucose, TFT, calcium, drug screen and ECG before 'medically fit'","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates Josh's guilt and reassures him he did the right thing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises the timeline and names what is unknown, who will find out and when","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains tonight's plan: observed area, senior agreed, legal framework if he tries to leave","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Answers the sectioning worry honestly and in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about next of kin and explains how family will be contacted, respecting consent","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks Josh's understanding and safety-nets: which changes to report at once","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes clearly: thanks him, says what happens next and when","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Builds a dated day-by-day timeline, gets the spice history, links catatonia to bloods before 'medically fit', and gives a safe, lawful plan for tonight.","borderline":"Good rapport and a rough timeline, but vague on dates, unknowns or what happens if he tries to leave.","fail":"Accepts 'medically fit' without bloods, misses catatonia or drug use, or talks about sectioning without a capacity or MHA framework."}},"c-1-1-2":{"setup":"Tuesday 16:30, acute ward. Denise Walker, 48, with schizoaffective disorder, is readmitted informally three weeks after discharge: her seventh admission. The nurse says she is 'always like this'. Clerk her, establishing her baseline, what has changed, and why.","role":{"who":"Ms Denise Walker, 48, female, the patient","open":"Not you lot again. I told the home team I was fine.","manner":"Guarded and irritable, expects to be judged from her notes. Warms up when asked about her life when well. Becomes tearful and frightened about her neighbour; settles when believed.","free":["Lives alone in a council flat","Hears voices every day even when well, but usually quiet","Thinks the home team overreacted","Wants to go home soon"],"ifasked":["When well: cooks, day centre on Tuesdays and Thursdays, sister visits Sundays, sleeps eight hours","Skipped her last paliperidone injection, about seven weeks ago, because of a stone of weight gain","Voices now loud all day, calling her names and talking about her neighbour","Voices say she should 'sort him out'; she has no intent to act","Sleeps three to four hours; stopped cooking and the day centre","Neighbour knocks at night and took £200 using her bank card","Two or three cans of lager most nights since discharge; no drugs","Early signs: stops answering the phone, stops cooking, stays up at night"],"worries":["Will you keep me in for months?","I'm not having the same injection, it made me fat.","Who's going to stop the neighbour?"],"rules":["Says 'I'm fine' until asked a specific question about her own life","Gets angry if told what she is like from the notes; softens if her view is asked first","Becomes tearful about the neighbour; settles if believed and given a clear action"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Denise. I'm Dr Shah, one of the ward doctors. Is it okay if I sit down?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Not you lot again. I told the home team I was fine."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you didn't want to come back in. That's hard. I've read your notes, but I'd rather hear it from you. Have you got twenty minutes?","m":[1,9]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Notes. Everyone reads the notes and decides what's wrong with me."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"That's fair. Notes never tell the whole story. What do people get wrong when they only read the file?","m":[9]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"That I'm always like this. I'm not. I'm normally alright."},{"t":"0:50","band":"Baseline","w":"D","s":"Then that's where I'd like to start. When you're well, what's a normal day like?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Up, cup of tea, cook proper food. Day centre Tuesdays and Thursdays. My sister comes Sundays."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"That's a good routine. How do you sleep when you're well?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Eight hours. In bed by ten."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"And the voices, are they there when you're well?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Always. But quiet. Background. I just ignore them."},{"t":"1:25","band":"What has changed","w":"D","s":"So when you're well, you're cooking, out twice a week, sleeping well, and the voices are quiet. What's different now from when you left three weeks ago?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"I've not been to the day centre. Can't be bothered cooking. I'm up all night."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"How many hours are you sleeping?","m":[3,6]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Three, four. If that."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"And the voices, how have they changed?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Loud. All day. Calling me names. Going on about next door."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. Do they tell you to do things, like hurt yourself or someone else?","m":[4,8]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"They say I should sort him out. I won't. I just turn the telly up."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Have you had any thoughts of self-harm or him?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"No. I just want him to leave me alone."},{"t":"2:35","band":"Why now","w":"D","s":"Tell me about next door.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"(tearful) He knocks at night. He took my card. Two hundred quid gone. I'm scared to open the door."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening, and nobody has the right to take your money. Thank you for telling me. I'll ask our social worker to see you tomorrow about keeping you safe.","m":[7,9]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"You believe me?"},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"I do. It may also help explain why things got worse.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"It's been going on since I got home."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Can I ask about your injection? When did you last have it?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Dunno. Ages ago."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"The depot clinic record says your last paliperidone was seven weeks ago, on the 18th of September. Does that sound right?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Suppose so. I skipped the last one. It's made me put on a stone."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being straight with me. Weight gain is a real side effect, and upsetting. We can look at other options together.","m":[5,9]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"I'm not having the same one."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"That's noted. We'll talk about it properly with the pharmacist. Any drink or drugs since you went home?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"A few cans at night. Two or three. No drugs."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Okay. And money, apart from next door?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Just about managing."},{"t":"4:20","band":"Summary and plan","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've got it. When well: cooking, day centre, your sister, eight hours' sleep, quiet voices. Now: three hours' sleep, loud voices about your neighbour, not cooking, not going out. Is that right?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Yeah. That's it."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"I think three things pushed you back: the missed injection, your neighbour frightening you and taking money, and the cans at night. So we'll review your medicine with the pharmacist, the social worker deals with next door, and we'll talk about the drinking.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"Will you keep me in for months?"},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"I understand that worry. You're here informally, so you have a say. Settling the voices quickly usually means a shorter stay.","m":[11,14]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Okay. What else?"},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Tonight a nurse will check your blood pressure, pulse and weight. Tomorrow, bloods and a heart tracing, because the injection can affect sugar, cholesterol and heart rhythm. Within 24 hours I'll check every medicine with the pharmacist and your GP record.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"More blood tests. I had loads last time."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"I know. Things like salt levels can change and make people feel muddled. It's quick.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:45","band":"Relapse signs and close","w":"D","s":"When you start getting unwell, what's the first thing you or your sister notice?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"I stop answering the phone. Stop cooking. Stay up."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"That's really useful. I'll put those in your care plan so we act sooner next time. I'll invite Mark, your care coordinator, to your first review on Thursday.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Mark's alright. Weekly visits helped before."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Then let's ask for that again. Could you sum up what we've agreed?","m":[13,14]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Medicine review, social worker for next door, bloods tomorrow, Mark on Thursday."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Perfect. If you feel unsafe or the voices get worse, tell the nurses straight away, day or night. I'll see you tomorrow.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thanks, doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and asks her view before quoting the notes","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what a normal day is like when she is well: routine, sleep, voices","d":"Content"},{"c":"Clarifies what has changed since discharge three weeks ago","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores how the voices have changed: volume, content and commands","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes the date of and reason for the missed depot and checks it against the depot record","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about sleep, alcohol, drugs and money","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about problems at home and identifies financial exploitation","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about thoughts of harming herself or others","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her anger and fear with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises baseline and change as two separate statements and checks they are right","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names the reasons for relapse and links each to an action","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains physical checks and medicines reconciliation within 24 hours","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees her early warning signs and invites her care coordinator to the first review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, answers her worries and closes with safety-netting","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Clear 'when well' and 'now' statements in her words, the missed depot confirmed and explained, exploitation found, and each cause linked to an action.","borderline":"Good symptom history but baseline vague, or causes named without actions or physical checks.","fail":"Clerks from the old notes, accepts 'always like this', misses the depot gap, the neighbour or command voices."}},"c-1-1-3":{"setup":"Wednesday 11:00, ward family room. Amit Desai, 34, was admitted two days ago with 'severe depression with psychotic features' and started sertraline. His wife asks to see the doctor. Take a focused collateral history and decide if the diagnosis needs to change.","role":{"who":"Mrs Priya Desai, 33, female, his wife","open":"I need someone to listen. You've put him on antidepressants and nobody asked me what he's actually like.","manner":"Angry and exhausted at first; calms when listened to. Becomes tearful and guilty about the money and the past; steadies with practical next steps.","free":["Amit has hardly slept for two weeks","He has spent their savings on a business idea","He 'was like this once before, at 25'","She thinks the antidepressant is wrong"],"ifasked":["Change began about three weeks ago, after a work review","Sleeps two or three hours but is not tired: 'full of beans'","Talks fast, jumps topics, plans an app 'bigger than Uber'","Spent £18,000 savings and took a £10,000 loan in her name","At 25: three weeks of no sleep and big plans, quit his job, GP said 'stress', no medicine; then months of low mood","His father had 'breakdowns' and took lithium for years","Four or five pints most nights; no drugs she knows of; no steroids; no thyroid problems","Two weeks ago said 'you'd all be better off without me', then laughed; driving very fast; never threatening to her"],"worries":["Is this my fault? I should have told someone years ago.","Could we lose the house?","So is it bipolar?"],"rules":["Stays angry until her concern is acknowledged before any questions","Becomes tearful about the loan; settles with practical steps","Presses for a diagnosis; accepts an honest answer about what can be shared"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mrs Desai? I'm Dr Evans, one of the doctors looking after Amit. Thank you for coming in.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"I need someone to listen. You've put him on antidepressants and nobody asked me what he's actually like."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I can hear how upset you are, and you're right to raise it. What you know could really change his treatment. I've got time now.","m":[1,10]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Okay. Sorry. I've just not slept either."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"A quick word on privacy first. I asked Amit this morning. He's happy for you to know the general plan, but not everything he's told us. I've recorded that. But I can always listen to you, and what you tell me matters.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Fine. As long as someone listens."},{"t":"1:00","band":"The timeline","w":"D","s":"Tell me in your words what you've noticed.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"He's hardly slept for two weeks. He's spent our savings on some app. And he was like this before, at 25."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Let's take those in turn. When did you first notice a change?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"About three weeks ago. The Sunday after his work review. He came home buzzing."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"How many hours is he sleeping now? Does he seem tired?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Two, three hours. And no, he's not tired at all. Full of beans. That's what's weird."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"How has his talking been?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Non-stop. Fast. Jumps from one thing to the next. You can't get a word in."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"And the business idea? How much has he spent?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"An app 'bigger than Uber'. Eighteen thousand. All our savings."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Has there been any other borrowing?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"(tearful) He took out a loan in my name. Ten thousand. We could lose the house."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. Thank you for telling me. Before you leave, we'll talk about protecting your money. I promise.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"I keep thinking it's my fault. I should have told someone years ago."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"It isn't your fault. People rarely see a high as an illness, especially the person having it. You're telling us now, and that's what matters.","m":[10]},{"t":"2:50","band":"The episode at 25","w":"D","s":"Tell me about when he was 25.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Same thing. No sleep, huge plans. He quit his job. About three weeks."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Did he see anyone? Was he given a diagnosis or medicine?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"The GP said stress. No tablets. Then he crashed. Months in bed, really low."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"So a few weeks high, then a long low. Anything like that since?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Not that bad. Ups and downs."},{"t":"3:25","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"Has anyone in his family had depression, bipolar disorder or a hospital stay?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"His dad had 'breakdowns'. He was on lithium for years."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"That's really useful. Has Amit been drinking or using drugs?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Drinking more. Four or five pints most nights. No drugs, not that I know."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Any steroid tablets recently, or thyroid problems?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"4:05","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Has he mentioned not wanting to live, or harming himself?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Two weeks ago he said we'd all be better off without him. Then he laughed. I didn't know what to make of it."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll make sure the team asks him about that every day. And are you safe at home? Has he been angry or threatening?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"No, never. He's just a lot. But he's been driving really fast."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"That's a real concern. While he's this unwell he shouldn't drive. We'll tell him that and record it.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:45","band":"Explain","w":"P","s":"So what is it? Bipolar?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"I can't discuss his diagnosis without his agreement. What I can say is that what you've told me may change how we understand this, and that matters for his medicine.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"The antidepressant?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Yes. I'm speaking to the consultant about it this morning, before his next dose. If Amit agrees, we'd like the three of us to meet.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Finally."},{"t":"5:25","band":"Support and close","w":"D","s":"Now, the money. Our social worker can arrange debt advice. Your bank also has a team for customers in situations like this. It's worth calling them today about the loan and joint accounts.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"I didn't know that existed."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"It does. You're also able to ask for a carer's assessment from the council, to look at support for you. How are you coping?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Not great. My mum's helping with the kids."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"I'm glad she's there. I'll give you our carers' pack before you go.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Can I check what you'll take away from this?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"You'll talk to the consultant about the tablets today. I ring the bank. Social worker for the loan. Carer's assessment."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Here's the ward number. If he talks about harming himself, or you feel unsafe, ring us or 999.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Really."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming in. I'll call you this afternoon, once Amit has agreed what I can share.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges her concern and thanks her for coming","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains confidentiality: will listen fully, shares only with his consent, and states what he agreed","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Builds a dated timeline from the first sign of change","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks how much he sleeps and whether he feels tired","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about speech, plans and spending, and quantifies the money","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the episode at 25: symptoms, length, help sought and what followed","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about family history of mood disorder","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol, drugs, steroids and thyroid problems","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal talk, driving and her own safety at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her distress and guilt with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the picture may change the diagnosis and the consultant will review the antidepressant today","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers a carer's assessment and practical steps to protect their money","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding and gives a contact and safety-net","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes with clear next steps and when she will hear back","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Elicits energised insomnia, overspending, the episode at 25 and lithium in the family, flags possible bipolar and the antidepressant today, and protects the wife.","borderline":"Good timeline but misses the 'not tired' question, family history or practical help with money.","fail":"Refuses to listen on confidentiality grounds, or misses the pointers to mania and leaves the antidepressant unreviewed."}},"c-1-2-1":{"setup":"Thursday 14:00, CMHT clinic. Chloe Bennett, 26, is referred by her GP with mood swings, hearing voices and self-harm. Past letters say 'bipolar?', 'EUPD?' and 'early psychosis?'. Ask the questions that separate these and agree a working formulation with her.","role":{"who":"Ms Chloe Bennett, 26, female, the patient","open":"Everyone gives me a different label. I just want help.","manner":"Wary and frustrated, expecting another label. Opens up when the doctor is curious, not checklist-driven. Becomes tearful disclosing childhood abuse; settles when thanked and not pushed.","free":["Her moods 'swing all over the place'","She sometimes hears a voice","She cuts her arm; last night was deeper than usual","She has been given three different possible diagnoses"],"ifasked":["Mood changes last hours, after arguments or feeling rejected; started around 14","Never four days of high mood with little sleep; only good nights out","A man's voice inside her head, critical, comes with stress; she knows it is her own mind","No thought insertion, withdrawal or feeling controlled","Works part-time in a café, two close friends; relationships intense and end badly","Stepfather abused her as a child; never told anyone; he left when she was 15; no contact","Last night cut after a row with her boyfriend; cleaned it herself; did not want to die","Coping: holding ice, going to her friend Jess's; contacts Jess and her sister"],"worries":["So have I got bipolar or not?","I don't want a personality disorder label. People stop helping you.","Please don't say I'm schizophrenic."],"rules":["Gets irritated by rapid checklist questions; engages with open curiosity","Discloses abuse only if asked gently without needing details; becomes tearful, settles if thanked","Pushes back on 'personality disorder'; accepts a plain-words formulation"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Chloe, I'm Dr Adeyemi, one of the psychiatrists. Thanks for coming. What are you hoping for today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Honestly? Everyone gives me a different label. I just want help."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frustrating. I'd rather understand how things actually go for you than add another label. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Fine. Go on then."},{"t":"0:30","band":"Mood pattern","w":"D","s":"When your mood changes, how long does it last? Minutes, hours, days or weeks?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Hours, mostly. I can be fine in the morning and want to die by teatime."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"What tends to set it off?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Arguments. If someone goes quiet on me. If I think they're leaving."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"When did this pattern start?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Since I was about fourteen."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had at least four days in a row when you felt high or full of energy and needed much less sleep?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Not four days. I have good nights out. That's it."},{"t":"1:20","band":"Voices","w":"D","s":"You mentioned a voice. Where does it seem to come from?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Inside my head. A man's voice. It tells me I'm useless. Please don't say I'm schizophrenic."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Lots of people hear voices, especially under stress or after hard experiences, and most don't have schizophrenia. When does it come?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"When I'm stressed. After a row. I know it's my own mind, really."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Do you ever feel someone is putting thoughts into your head, or taking them out, or that something outside is controlling you?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"2:10","band":"Between crises","w":"D","s":"Between the bad times, how are you? Work, friends?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"I work in a café, part-time. Two good mates. Boyfriends are always intense, then it ends badly."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"How do you see yourself, mostly?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Like there's something wrong with me."},{"t":"2:30","band":"Trauma and safety","w":"D","s":"Have difficult or frightening things happened to you, now or in the past? You don't need to give me details.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"(long pause, tearful) My stepdad. Things happened when I was little. I've never told anyone."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. It wasn't your fault. You don't have to say any more today.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"I'm okay. Keep going."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Can I ask one thing to make sure everyone is safe now? Is he still in your life, or around any children?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"He left when I was fifteen. No idea where he is. Mum's not with him."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Because he could be around other children, I'll speak to our safeguarding lead, sharing only what's needed. If you ever want to report it, we'll support you. Is that okay?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"As long as I don't have to talk to the police today."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"You don't. That's your choice.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:50","band":"Self-harm","w":"D","s":"You said last night was deeper. What happened?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Row with my boyfriend. I cut my arm. Deeper than usual, but I cleaned it."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"At that point, did you want to die?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"No. I wanted the feeling to stop."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Can I look at it before you go, to check it's healing?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Yeah. It's fine, though."},{"t":"4:20","band":"Formulation","w":"P","s":"So have I got bipolar or not?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Honestly, I don't think so. Bipolar usually means highs and lows lasting days or weeks. Yours last hours and follow feeling let down. That started young, perhaps linked to what happened with your stepdad. The voice seems to come with stress, not as a separate illness.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"So it's personality disorder. That's what they always say, and then people stop helping."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"I understand. That label has been used unfairly. Some call this emotional instability, some call it complex trauma. Whatever the name, there are therapies that work well. Does my summary fit how it feels to you?","m":[8,11]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Actually, it does."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"To firm it up, three steps. A mood diary twice a day for a month. An assessment with our psychologist about trauma and managing feelings. And, if you're happy, a chat with someone who knew you as a teenager.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Maybe Mum. Not yet."},{"t":"5:40","band":"Safety plan and close","w":"D","s":"That's fine. Let's write a safety plan now. What tells you a crisis is coming?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"I stop texting back. Go quiet. Then I can't stop crying."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"And two things that help, even a little?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Holding ice. Going round to Jess's."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"Good. Two people you can contact?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Jess, and my sister."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"And if they're not around: NHS 111, option 2, for the crisis team, Samaritans on 116 123, or A&E if you're in danger. I'll write it all down for you.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"What will you take away from today?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"It's probably not bipolar. Diary, psychologist, and my plan."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Your named worker will ring within a week, and I'll see you in two weeks. Now, let's look at that arm.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges frustration with labels and agrees the aim","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks how long mood changes last: minutes, hours, days or weeks","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about triggers, especially rejection or conflict, and age of onset","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about at least four days of high mood with less need for sleep","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores voices: where they seem to come from, when, content and insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about first-rank symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about functioning, self-image and relationships between crises","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about trauma without needing details and responds with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks whether anyone, including children, is at risk now and explains safeguarding","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about last night's self-harm and intent, and offers to check the wound","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains a provisional formulation in plain words and asks if it fits","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees three steps to firm up the diagnosis","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a safety plan: warning signs, two coping steps, two contacts, crisis numbers","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and closes with follow-up arrangements","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Uses time course, triggers, the four-day question and first-rank symptoms to separate the three, handles the trauma disclosure safely, and agrees a formulation and safety plan with her.","borderline":"Covers the symptoms but as a checklist, or gives a formulation without her agreement or a concrete safety plan.","fail":"Picks a label without discriminating questions, misses safeguarding after the disclosure, or ignores last night's self-harm."}},"c-1-2-2":{"setup":"Sunday 03:20, A&E. Laura Kent, 29, with no psychiatric history, is referred as 'acute psychosis, medically cleared'. You have assessed her and spoken to her partner. Now persuade the busy medical registrar to investigate and admit her.","role":{"who":"Dr Ben Hughes, 35, male, medical registrar on call","open":"Medical reg. I've got two minutes. She's the psychosis lady? She's been medically cleared.","manner":"Rushed, tired and sceptical. Pushes back once and shows frustration about his workload. Engages and becomes collaborative once acknowledged and given objective findings.","free":["He has eleven patients waiting and no medical beds","Her first bloods were normal apart from CRP 24","He thinks 'medically cleared' makes her psychiatry's job","He has not seen her himself"],"ifasked":["No blood cultures or CK were sent","No lumbar puncture has been done","MRI is unlikely before Monday","He would start aciclovir with fever and a seizure","His consultant on call is Dr Moss","He is unsure who consents for the LP if she lacks capacity","He welcomes daily liaison psychiatry review on the ward"],"worries":["Couldn't that be a pseudoseizure?","We have no beds.","She's agitated; can you sedate her for us?","Who consents her for the LP? Can't you section her?"],"rules":["Declines once ('sounds psychiatric to me') until given objective findings and told the doctor will escalate","Softens when his workload is acknowledged without the doctor backing down","Agrees to review once specific red flags and tests are named"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, it's Dr Ali, psychiatry registrar. I'm calling about Laura Kent, 29, in cubicle 9. I'd like you to review her for medical admission tonight.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Medical reg. I've got two minutes. She's the psychosis lady? She's been medically cleared."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"I know it's a heavy night, so I'll be quick. I don't think this is a primary psychiatric illness.","m":[1,6]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Findings","w":"D","s":"She drifts in and out. I did a 4AT. She was drowsy, got the place and year wrong, couldn't do months backwards, and her partner describes an acute change. Her score is well above four.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Psychotic patients can be all over the place."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"They can, but they're usually alert. She isn't. And temperature 37.9, heart rate 118, and yesterday a probable seizure. Her partner saw her stare, stiffen, then jerk, and she was confused afterwards.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Could be a pseudoseizure."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Possibly, but look at the whole picture. Ten days of anxiety and no sleep, then strange speech, then a fit, in a 29-year-old with no psychiatric history. That's a classic pattern for encephalitis, including autoimmune.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Her bloods were fine. CRP 24. That's nothing."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Normal first bloods don't rule it out. Were blood cultures, CK, a pregnancy test and a drug screen sent?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Not cultures. Not CK. Don't know about the rest."},{"t":"2:05","band":"Pushback","w":"P","s":"(exasperated) Look, I've got eleven patients and no beds. This sounds psychiatric to me."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"I hear you. That's a brutal night, and I wouldn't be calling if I weren't worried.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Everyone's worried at three in the morning."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Fair. But I remain concerned about delirium or encephalitis. If you can't review her, I'll call Dr Moss now, and I'm letting my own consultant know. I'd much rather we sort it between us.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"...Fine. What exactly do you want?"},{"t":"2:50","band":"The request","w":"D","s":"Admission under medicine. An MRI and an EEG. A lumbar puncture: cells, protein, glucose, HSV PCR and NMDA receptor antibodies, with serum antibodies too. And neurology advice in the morning.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"MRI won't happen before Monday."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Then could we consider IV aciclovir now, without waiting for the LP, until herpes is excluded?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"With fever and a fit, yeah, I'd do that."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"Thanks. And tonight, please: blood cultures, CK, calcium, magnesium, a pregnancy test and a urine drug screen.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Fine. She's pretty agitated, though. Can you sedate her for us?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"I'd avoid antipsychotics. People with encephalitis can react badly: rigidity, or an NMS-type picture. If she needs something for safety, lorazepam 0.5 to 1 milligram orally, with observations.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Okay. Noted."},{"t":"4:05","band":"Capacity and consent","w":"P","s":"Who consents her for the LP? She can't sign anything."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Your team assesses her capacity for each test, as you're doing them. My view is she lacks it right now. She can't hold information for more than a few seconds.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Can't you just section her?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"The Mental Health Act doesn't cover treating a physical illness. If she lacks capacity, it's a best-interests decision under the Mental Capacity Act, and her partner should be consulted.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Right. Where's the partner?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Dan's with her. I've told him in plain words that something physical may be affecting her brain, and the medical team will look into it tonight. He's frightened, but he understands.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Good. Someone from our team will update him on the ward."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you. A named person would really help him. Could that be you tonight?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Yeah, I'll speak to him when I see her."},{"t":"5:30","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"We'll stay involved. Liaison will review her daily on your ward for agitation, delirium care and capacity. I'd suggest enhanced observation if she wanders or pulls at lines.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"That'd help. The ward hates confused patients without support."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"So, to recap: you'll see her within the hour and admit under medicine. Cultures, CK, pregnancy test and drug screen tonight. Aciclovir until HSV is excluded. MRI, EEG and LP with antibodies, and neurology advice. Lorazepam, not antipsychotics, if needed. Is that right?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Yeah. That's fair. I'll be down in half an hour."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Ben. I'll write this in her notes with your name and the time, and hand over to day liaison at nine.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Fine. Cheers."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Bleep me on 4410 if anything changes.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Will do."}],"marks":[{"c":"Identifies self and patient and states a clear request at the start","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives objective evidence of delirium: 4AT result and fluctuating attention","d":"Content"},{"c":"Lists the organic red flags: fever, tachycardia, probable seizure, prodrome, no psychiatric history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Requests admission with named tests: MRI, EEG, lumbar puncture with HSV PCR and NMDA receptor antibodies","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks for cultures, CK, pregnancy test and urine drug screen tonight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Acknowledges the registrar's workload calmly without backing down","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"When declined, states escalation to the medical consultant and own consultant","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Advises avoiding antipsychotics; low-dose lorazepam only if needed for safety","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Raises capacity for tests; treating team assesses; best interests with partner consulted","d":"Plan"},{"c":"States the Mental Health Act cannot be used to treat a physical illness","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Confirms the partner has been told in plain words and asks who will update him","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees ongoing liaison review and observation on the medical ward","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises the agreed plan and checks the registrar agrees","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes clearly: records names and times, gives contact, hands over","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"States objective delirium findings and red flags, names the tests, holds firm with a clear escalation route, and agrees capacity, sedation and liaison roles.","borderline":"Correct concerns but vague requests, or accepts a refusal without escalating.","fail":"Accepts 'medically cleared', suggests antipsychotics or the Mental Health Act for physical care, or argues without objective findings."}},"c-1-2-3":{"setup":"Monday 10:00, ward round. Your consultant asks you to present two patients: Kyle, 30, with schizophrenia, readmitted after cannabis and cocaine use, and Nadia, 38, with depression not improving after two antidepressants. Propose an integrated plan for each.","role":{"who":"Dr Sarah Lin, 52, female, consultant psychiatrist","open":"Go on. The CMHT want to discharge him because it's 'a drugs problem'. Do you agree?","manner":"Brisk and testing, plays devil's advocate. Shows frustration about the 'revolving door' and bed pressure; settles when acknowledged. Warms to evidence and a clear plan.","free":["The CMHT wants to discharge Kyle to drug services","Nadia has had two antidepressants","The ward is short of beds","She wants a plan for each by the end of the round"],"ifasked":["Kyle takes olanzapine 15 mg and his depot is overdue","Kyle's care coordinator, Sam, has known him four years","Nadia had citalopram, then eight weeks of mirtazapine 30 mg","Psychology has a six-week wait for trauma therapy","There is a local IDVA service","She will write to the CMHT manager if asked"],"worries":["Aren't drug services better placed for Kyle?","Shouldn't we just try Nadia on a third antidepressant?","Is Nadia safe to go on leave this weekend?"],"rules":["Challenges any plan that leaves one condition untreated","Voices frustration about readmissions; settles when acknowledged","Accepts a plan that names who does what, and when"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Morning. Two patients today, both with two conditions that feed each other. Shall I start with Kyle?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Go on. The CMHT want to discharge him because it's 'a drugs problem'. Do you agree?"},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Kyle is 30, with schizophrenia, readmitted last week with a relapse after daily cannabis and some cocaine. No, I don't agree with discharge.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Why not? Drug services are the experts on drugs."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Kyle","w":"D","s":"Because the two are tangled. His voices get worse within days of heavy use, cocaine makes him paranoid, and then he misses his depot. Every admission in two years followed heavy use.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Which is exactly why it's a drugs problem."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"It's both. NICE guidance on psychosis with substance misuse is clear: drug use is never a reason to exclude someone from mental health care. We lead, and drug services work alongside us.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"(sighs) I know the guidance. I'm just tired of him bouncing back every six months. And we've no beds."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"I understand. It's frustrating for the team, and for him. But losing his care coordinator would likely make the revolving door spin faster.","m":[11]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Fair. What did he tell you?"},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"I asked what cannabis does for him. He says it calms the voices at night but makes them worse the next day. He uses about three grams of strong skunk a day, around thirty pounds, and cocaine at weekends.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Does he want to stop?"},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Not stop. He'd like to cut down, mainly because of money. That's a real starting point, so I didn't push him further.","m":[4,12]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Physical side?"},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"He smokes twenty cigarettes a day as well. If he stops on the ward, his olanzapine level could rise, so I'll watch for sedation and check a level. Because of the cocaine, an ECG and blood pressure, and a urine drug screen with his consent.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"And the plan?"},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Sam stays as his care coordinator. I'll ask the drug and alcohol service to join his CPA review. Sam starts motivational work, aiming at less, lower-potency cannabis. Drug use goes in his relapse plan as an early warning sign. And I'll advise him about driving and the DVLA.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Good. Write to the CMHT manager and quote NG58."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"I will, today.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:30","band":"Nadia","w":"P","s":"And the second?"},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Nadia is 38, with depression, not improving after citalopram and then eight weeks of mirtazapine 30 milligrams. On Friday she told the night nurse about nightmares of an assault five years ago.","m":[1]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Shouldn't we just try a third antidepressant?"},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"I'd like to know why first. I asked if she relives it. She has flashbacks most days, avoids the area where it happened, is jumpy and sleeps badly. Her PCL-5 score was 58.","m":[7,12]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"That's high."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Well above the usual cut-off of around 33. I think untreated PTSD is keeping her depression going. Another switch won't touch the flashbacks or the avoidance.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Is she safe? Who was it?"},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"Her ex-partner. He still messages her and knows where she lives. She's frightened, but there's been no face-to-face contact for a year.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Has anyone done a DASH?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Not yet. I'll complete it with her today and refer to the IDVA, and to MARAC if it's high risk. She's agreed to block his number.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Treatment?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Trauma-focused CBT or EMDR through psychology. The wait is six weeks, so the ward psychologist can start grounding and stabilisation now. She's scared of talking about it, so she sets the pace.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"Medication?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Mirtazapine is helping her sleep a little. If she wants a medicine aimed at PTSD, sertraline is a NICE option, switched with your advice. I'd avoid benzodiazepines for sleep, and I'll ask about drinking at night.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Reasonable. She's asking for leave this weekend."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"I'd make leave depend on a safety plan that covers him: blocked numbers, a safe address and police contact. If she wants to report him, we'll support her.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:05","band":"Close","w":"P","s":"Alright. Summarise both for me."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Kyle: stays with Sam and the CMHT, drug service joins, motivational work, ECG and olanzapine level, relapse plan. Nadia: DASH and IDVA today, safety plan before leave, stabilisation now, trauma therapy referral, medicine discussed with her. Review both in four weeks.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Good. That's what I wanted."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"I'll document both plans and talk them through with Kyle and Nadia this afternoon.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Thanks. Next patient."}],"marks":[{"c":"Opens each case with a concise summary: age, diagnosis, why admitted","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains how cannabis and cocaine drive his relapses","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States NICE NG58: substance use is no reason to discharge; mental health services lead","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Reports his use found motivationally: amount, cost, potency, readiness to change","d":"Content"},{"c":"Plans physical checks: smoking and olanzapine levels, ECG and BP for cocaine","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives Kyle one integrated plan naming who does what","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Reports PTSD symptoms and her PCL-5 score","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains how untreated PTSD is keeping her depression going","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Reports her current safety from the perpetrator and the actions taken","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives Nadia one integrated plan: trauma therapy, medicine review, who and when","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Acknowledges the consultant's frustration and responds constructively","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Answers challenges calmly with reasons, keeping the patient's view central","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises both plans, confirms agreement and sets a review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Argues from NG58 with a clear mechanism, reports motivational and PCL-5 findings, addresses Nadia's current safety, and gives one plan per patient naming who does what.","borderline":"Right ideas but treats each condition separately, or misses the perpetrator risk or drug-level interaction.","fail":"Agrees to discharge Kyle to drug services, or proposes a third antidepressant without assessing PTSD."}},"c-1-3-1":{"setup":"Tuesday 09:30, ward round. You clerked Lisa Ashton, 44, admitted informally last night after a suicidal crisis following redundancy and separation from her partner. Your consultant wants the key points and your formulation in three minutes, then a plan.","role":{"who":"Dr James Clarke, 58, male, consultant psychiatrist","open":"Right, Lisa Ashton. Key points and your formulation in three minutes, please.","manner":"Busy and direct; interrupts if the presentation drifts. Shows real concern about her suicide risk. Warms to a crisp, honest summary with a clear question.","free":["Wants three minutes, no more","Expects one clear question at the end","Will interrupt with 'go on' or 'quickly'"],"ifasked":["Thinks alcohol may be driving her low mood","Wants AUDIT and withdrawal monitoring","Would start sertraline now if depression is severe and risk is high","Wants her sister involved in leave planning","Wants a medication review within a week of starting"],"worries":["How risky is she right now?","Is it the drink or the depression?","What if she refuses tablets?"],"rules":["Says 'quickly' if the opening runs beyond one sentence","Asks 'so what?' if a formulation factor has no action","Accepts uncertainty if stated clearly with a plan"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Opening line","w":"P","s":"Right, Lisa Ashton. Key points and your formulation in three minutes, please."},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"Lisa Ashton is a 44-year-old woman, admitted informally last night after a suicidal crisis following redundancy and separation from her partner, both three weeks ago.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Diagnosis?"},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"Moderate to severe depressive episode, no psychotic features. Three weeks of low mood, poor sleep, no enjoyment, guilt and hopelessness. One similar episode at 30, which responded to CBT.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"Last night she wrote notes and planned an overdose with saved paracetamol. Her sister found her and called 999. She's glad she was found, but still has thoughts of suicide.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:10","band":"Formulation","w":"P","s":"Formulation. Quickly."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Predisposing: childhood neglect and the past episode, so psychology to work on those patterns. Precipitating: losing her job and her partner in the same week, so social work and a fit note today.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Mm."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Perpetuating: a bottle of wine a night since he left, so AUDIT and a withdrawal chart now. Isolation, so daily nursing time and her sister. Debt, so debt advice and Breathing Space.","m":[4,7]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Breathing Space?"},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Protection from creditors while she's in crisis care, plus 30 days. A debt adviser applies, with evidence from an AMHP.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Good. Protective?"},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Her sister, her dog, and a good past response to therapy. Her sister is our link for leave planning, and she can take the dog.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Risk in words: the main risk is suicide by overdose. It's high now, but reducing with support. Drinking, debt letters and being alone at home make it worse. Her sister, her dog and having a plan make it better.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Her story worries me. Middle-aged, drinking, two big losses, and a plan. That's the group we lose."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"I agree, and I share that worry. That's why I'd keep her on intermittent observations and no leave until there's a written safety plan.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"And tablets at home?"},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Her sister will clear any stockpile before leave, and we'll keep discharge supplies small.","m":[6,9]},{"t":"3:35","band":"Plan and question","w":"P","s":"What does she want?"},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"I asked her. She wants the money sorted and to sleep. She's unsure about antidepressants but willing to talk. She'd like her sister involved.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Plan?"},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"AUDIT and withdrawal scale today, thiamine, and bloods including LFTs and GGT. Social work today for debt, benefits and the dog. Psychology referral for CBT. Safety plan before any leave.","m":[7,9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"And?"},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"My question: do we start an antidepressant now, or wait to see if alcohol is driving the low mood?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Good question. What do you think?"},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"NICE suggests treating the alcohol first, as mood often lifts after three or four weeks without drinking. But her depression looks severe and the risk is high, so I'd lean towards starting sertraline 50 milligrams now.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"I agree. Start it."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"I'll warn her about early nausea and restlessness, and that it takes two to four weeks to help. Because of her risk, she'll be reviewed within a week of starting.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"That was under three minutes. Well done."},{"t":"5:30","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Can I check the outcome? Sertraline now, withdrawal monitoring, social work, psychology, and a safety plan before leave.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Yes. Anything else?"},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"She asked to know what was said, so I'll go through the plan with her straight after the round and record whether she agrees, especially about sertraline.","m":[8,13]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"And if she says no to the tablets?"},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Then I'll record her reasons and we'll offer it again at the review, with more information. Everything else still goes ahead.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"Good. If her mood or drinking gets worse, call me, any time."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Will do. I'll tell the nurse in charge about the observation level and the withdrawal chart now.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Thanks. Next."}],"marks":[{"c":"Opens with one sentence: age, sex, legal status, problem and why now","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives diagnosis and key symptoms briefly, including absence of psychosis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Presents predisposing and precipitating factors, each linked to an action","d":"Content"},{"c":"Presents perpetuating factors, each linked to an action","d":"Content"},{"c":"Presents protective factors and how they are used in the plan","d":"Content"},{"c":"States current risk in words: what, how likely, what worsens, what protects; no score","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Reports alcohol intake and a withdrawal plan","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Reports her own goals and views on treatment","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives a plan linked to the formulation","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Finishes within three minutes with one clear question","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds to the consultant's concern and challenge calmly and with reasons","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains antidepressant start, side effects and review within a week","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks the outcome back and agrees to confirm the plan with her and record her agreement","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Clear one-line opener, a 5P formulation with an action for each factor, risk in words, and a plan with one sharp question, all within three minutes.","borderline":"Accurate content but over time, or a formulation listed without linked actions or without her view.","fail":"Reads out the clerking, gives a risk score instead of words, or ends with no plan or question."}},"c-1-3-2":{"setup":"Wednesday ward round. Mr Hollis, 52, was admitted informally four days ago with low mood and suicidal thoughts after his wife left; he drinks a bottle of vodka daily. Clarify the history with him, then give the consultant a 5P formulation and plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Gary Hollis, 52, male, self-employed plumber, informal inpatient on day 4 of a chlordiazepoxide detox","open":"Go on then. As long as nobody's going to lecture me about drink.","manner":"Guarded and a bit defensive at first, minimises the drinking. Softens once he feels he is not being judged. Tearful and ashamed when his children come up; settles if validated.","free":["His wife left three weeks ago; he is sleeping on his brother's sofa","He drinks about a bottle of vodka a day","He feels a bit shaky first thing; the nurses give him tablets","His boss is keeping his job open"],"ifasked":["Drinking rose to a bottle a day about a year ago, when work was slow and the marriage was rowing; mood slid after that","He was four months dry in 2019 after abnormal liver tests and felt the best he had in years","Two years ago he stopped alone and saw spiders on the wall; no fits","His father drank heavily and died of liver disease at 61","A drink switches his head off; mornings bring shakes and nausea, so he drinks to steady himself","He drives a van for work","Two children, 15 and 12, stay every other weekend; they saw him drunk at Christmas","Suicidal thoughts come late at night after a bottle, about the car in the garage; never when sober; none now"],"worries":["'So which is it, doc? Am I depressed or is it the booze?'","'Just give me antidepressants now and I'll cut down to a few cans.'","'No van, no job.'","'Is social services going to get involved with the kids?'"],"rules":["If he senses a lecture or the word 'alcoholic', he becomes curt and short in his answers","Becomes tearful talking about the children seeing him drunk; settles if the doctor validates his shame","Only reveals the suicidal thoughts if asked directly after he mentions the children","Accepts the delay on antidepressants if given a clear reason and a date to revisit"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Hollis, I'm Dr Shah, one of the psychiatry doctors. Dr Evans has asked me to pull together how we understand what's been happening. Could I ask you a few questions here with the team, then explain our thinking out loud?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Go on then. As long as nobody's going to lecture me about drink."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"No lectures, I promise. How are you this morning? Any shakes, sweats, or seeing things?","m":[1,4]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Bit shaky first thing. The nurse gave me the tablets. No spiders this time."},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"This time? Has that happened before when you stopped?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Two years back. I tried to stop on my own. Saw spiders crawling up the wall. Terrified me. No fits, though."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. It tells us your withdrawal can be severe, so we'll keep the reducing tablets and the vitamin injections going. Any muddled thinking, unsteadiness, or trouble with your eyes?","m":[4,11]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Not that I've noticed. Legs a bit wobbly, but that's the shakes."},{"t":"1:00","band":"Their story","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'd like to get the order of things right. Did the low mood start before the drinking got heavy, or after?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"I'm not an alcoholic. I only drink because she left. Anyone would."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Losing your marriage is a huge blow, and I'm not here to label you. When did it go up to about a bottle a day?","m":[2,11]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Honestly? About a year ago. Work was slow, we were rowing all the time. She left three weeks ago."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"And your mood. Was it low before last year, or did it slide as the drinking climbed?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"It slid. I was all right before. Moody now and then, like anyone."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had a month or more with no alcohol at all?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"2019. Four months dry after a health scare. My liver tests were off."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"How was your mood during those four months?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Best I'd felt in years, to be fair. Sleeping properly, going to the gym. Then a mate's fortieth, and that was that."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"That's really useful. What does a drink do for you in the first hour? And the next morning?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"First hour, it switches my head off. Next morning I'm shaking and sick and I feel like rubbish. So I have one to steady myself."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Is there anyone in your family with drinking or mood problems?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"My dad drank. Died at sixty-one, liver. I swore I'd never be him."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Since your wife left, what's changed with work, money and where you're living?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"I'm on my brother's sofa. Boss is holding the job, but there's the credit card. It's a mess."},{"t":"2:35","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Do you drive, for work or otherwise?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"I've got the van. Haven't driven since I came in."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"And are there any children you look after or see?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Two. Fifteen and twelve. Every other weekend. They saw me drunk at Christmas. (voice breaks) They're better off without me."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"That sounds painful to carry, and thank you for saying it. When you say they're better off without you, have you been thinking of ending your life?","m":[6,11]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Some nights. Late on, after a bottle. I'd think about the car and the garage."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Were you drinking each time those thoughts came?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Always. Sober, I'd never. I couldn't do that to the kids."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"And right now, here on the ward?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Not now. Ashamed, mostly. But I want to see them."},{"t":"3:20","band":"Formulation","w":"D","s":"Wanting to see them matters, and thank you for being so honest. Dr Evans, my formulation in five parts. Mr Hollis is a 52-year-old plumber with alcohol dependence and low mood with suicidal thoughts, three weeks after his separation. Predisposing: his father's alcohol dependence and years of heavy drinking. Precipitating: a year of rising drinking during work and marriage stress, then his wife leaving.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Perpetuating: a daily cycle of drinking to switch off, morning withdrawal relieved by more alcohol, poor sleep, shame, debt and loss of his home. So alcohol is both a trigger and the engine that keeps the low mood going. Protective: his children, his job held open, his brother, and four months dry in 2019 when his mood was good.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"On the key question: the low mood followed the heavy drinking and lifted fully when he was dry. That points to alcohol-induced depression, but an independent depression isn't ruled out, so I'd hold both for now.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"So which is it, doc? Am I depressed or is it the booze?"},{"t":"4:25","band":"Explain and plan","w":"D","s":"Honestly, possibly both, and we can find out. Alcohol on its own drags mood down, and that often lifts within three or four weeks of stopping. If you're still low by then, we treat depression in its own right.","m":[9,12]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Just give me antidepressants now and I'll cut down to a few cans."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"I can see you want to feel better fast. Tablets rarely work while drinking stays heavy, and cutting down after dependence usually slides back. Let's finish the detox first, then decide with a clearer picture.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Fair enough. I don't want to end up like Dad."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"So, three steps. Now: finish the reducing chlordiazepoxide and the Pabrinex vitamin injections, and bloods for your liver and salts. Then at three to four weeks alcohol-free, we repeat a mood questionnaire and sit down properly. That's when we decide on an antidepressant. The alcohol team will also see you this week about staying dry.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Meanwhile we'll ask about those thoughts every day on the ward, and before any leave we'll write a safety plan together.","m":[6,13]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"What about the van? I need it for work."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"While you're dependent on alcohol you mustn't drive, and you need to tell the DVLA yourself. I know that's hard. I'll write down that we've discussed it, and we can help with the form.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Great. No van, no job."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"I hear how worrying that is. Your boss has already held the job, and our social worker can help with work and money while you get well.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"And the kids? Is social services going to get involved?"},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"Because they stay with you, I'll talk it through with our safeguarding lead. That's routine, and it's about making sure you and they are supported.","m":[7]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"That's a lot. Can you tell me in your words what the plan is?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Finish the detox and the vitamin jabs. Stay off it three or four weeks, then you'll see if I still need tablets. No driving. Tell someone if those thoughts come back."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Spot on. And if you feel shaky, sweaty, muddled, or see things, tell a nurse straight away, day or night.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Dr Evans, I'll document the formulation, the suicidal thoughts linked to intoxication, the DVLA advice and the children. Mr Hollis, is there anything you'd change?","m":[7,14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks for not treating me like a drunk."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You're going through a very hard time, and you've been honest with us. We'll see you every day on the round.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the purpose and gains his agreement to talk in front of the team","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes whether low mood began before or after the drinking became heavy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Uses his longest alcohol-free spell, and his mood then, as the test","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past withdrawal, hallucinations, fits and current signs of Wernicke's","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what alcohol does for him, family history and the social impact of the separation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, their link to intoxication, and current intent","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about driving and children; gives DVLA advice and plans a safeguarding discussion","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Presents a 5P formulation in under two minutes, with alcohol as trigger and maintaining driver","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Holds alcohol-induced and independent depression as open possibilities and says how to tell them apart","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a timed plan: detox with thiamine now, mood review at 3–4 weeks dry, then antidepressant decision","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his shame and distress without judgement or labels","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks his understanding and invites his view of the plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets: withdrawal warning signs to report and daily suicide review","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes clearly, stating what will be documented and when he will next be seen","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A tight 5P story with alcohol as trigger and engine, the dry-spell test asked, a dated plan, and risk, DVLA and children recorded.","borderline":"Covers the 5Ps but lists facts without linking them, or misses one of DVLA, children or the intoxication link to suicidal thoughts.","fail":"Labels it 'just the alcohol' or starts an antidepressant now, skips thiamine or suicide risk, or lectures him about drinking."}},"c-1-3-3":{"setup":"Thursday at the CMHT base. Jade, 24, has an EUPD diagnosis and nine A&E visits this year; the team wants to discharge her for not engaging. See her in the quiet room, build a formulation with her, and change the plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Jade Corrie, 24, female, CMHT patient diagnosed with emotionally unstable personality disorder","open":"It's fine. The buzzing's off. Everyone's going to discharge me anyway. That's what you lot always do.","manner":"Flat, wary and very literal; little eye contact at first. Answers vague questions with 'don't know'. Warms when questions are concrete. Becomes tearful when told it isn't her fault.","free":["She hates the waiting room: buzzing lights, people staring; she walks out","She has had four care coordinators this year","She expects to be discharged"],"ifasked":["Letters say 'please attend to discuss your care' and she does not know what that means","At school assemblies were unbearable; one friend; timetable changes caused meltdowns","Supermarkets, strip lights and clothing labels are hard; she wears the same three tops","She takes phrases like 'pop in any time' literally","Never assessed for autism or ADHD","Mum drank; often no food; social workers came and went","Weekly nightmares; sometimes she 'goes blank', loses an hour and finds cuts","Drawing, her sister Leanne and her GP Dr Patel help; passive thoughts some nights, no plan"],"worries":["'Are you saying I'm autistic now? I thought I had BPD.'","'I'm not doing group therapy. I can't cope with groups.'","'Promise you won't discharge me?'"],"rules":["Answers open, abstract questions with 'don't know'; gives detail to specific, concrete ones","If she hears 'non-engagement' or 'attention-seeking', she stands up to leave","Becomes tearful when the doctor says the barriers are not her fault; settles if acknowledged","Agrees to share the crisis plan only if told exactly who will see it"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Jade, I'm Dr Shah, one of the psychiatrists. Thanks for coming in. I've turned the main lights off and it's just us. Is this room okay, or would you like anything changed?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"It's fine. The buzzing's off. Everyone's going to discharge me anyway. That's what you lot always do."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"It sounds like that's happened before, and it must feel like nobody sticks around. I'm not here to discharge you. I want to understand what's getting in the way, so we can change how we work with you.","m":[1,6]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Right. Heard that before."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"That's fair. Judge me on what I do. Can I ask what makes appointments hard to get to?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"The waiting room is unbearable. Buzzing lights, people staring. I just walk out."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"That sounds really overwhelming. Is there anything else that makes it hard?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"The letters. They say 'please attend to discuss your care'. Discuss what? And it's someone different every time. Four care coordinators this year."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"So three things: the noise, letters that don't say what will happen, and new faces each time. Have I got that right?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Yeah. And then they write that I don't engage."},{"t":"1:20","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Can I ask about when you were younger? How did you find school, friendships and changes in routine?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Hated it. Assemblies were too loud. I had one friend. If the timetable changed I'd lose it, and teachers said I was being difficult."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Are there sounds, lights or crowds that are still very hard now?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Supermarkets. Strip lights. Labels in clothes. I wear the same three tops."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Some people say you take things quite literally. Does that fit for you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"If someone says 'pop in any time', I don't know what that means. So I don't go."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Have you ever been assessed for autism or ADHD?","m":[3,9]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"No. Are you saying I'm autistic now? I thought I had BPD."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"I'm not saying that. I'm wondering whether it's worth looking at, because some of what you describe fits, and knowing would change how we help. Both could be true, or neither.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Okay. Nobody's asked before."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Can I ask about home when you were little? Only say what you're comfortable with.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Mum drank. Often there wasn't food. I got myself to school. Social workers came and went."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"That's a lot for a child to carry alone. Do you get flashbacks or nightmares about it now?","m":[4,6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Nightmares most weeks. And sometimes I go blank and lose an hour. I find cuts and don't remember doing them."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. What happens in your body and thoughts just before you hurt yourself?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Too much noise in my head. Usually after something changes or someone lets me down. Then it's like the telly goes off."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"And now? Any thoughts about ending your life?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Not today. Some nights I think everyone would be fine without me. I've not planned anything."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. What has helped, even a little, in the past?","m":[5,10]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Drawing. My sister, Leanne. And Dr Patel, my GP. She explains things properly."},{"t":"3:25","band":"Formulation","w":"D","s":"Can I tell you how I'm starting to understand it? Then you tell me what's wrong.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"My first idea is that a very hard childhood left you with feelings that flood fast, and a mind that's on alert for being let down. My second is that your senses and the way you read language may work differently, which might be autism.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Noisy rooms, vague letters and new faces then pile up. The flood comes, sometimes you go blank and self-harm, and you end up in A&E, which is noisy too. So it isn't that you won't engage. Our way of doing things gets in your way.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"(quietly, tearful) That's the first time anyone's said it's not just me being difficult."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"It isn't you being difficult. You've coped with a lot, on your own, for a long time.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:25","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"So let's change the three things. For the noise: first appointment of the day, straight into this room, no waiting room.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"For the letters: a text and a short written plan saying who, when, and exactly what we'll do. For new faces: one named care coordinator, Sam. If that ever has to change, you'll meet the new person first. I'll put these on your record as reasonable adjustments, so every service can see them.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"And the autism thing?"},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"With your agreement, I'll refer you to the adult autism assessment service and ask them to consider ADHD too. The wait can be long, so these changes start now, Sam will see you every fortnight, and I'll give you details of local autism support.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"I'm not doing group therapy. I can't cope with groups."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"That's okay. When you're ready, therapy can be one-to-one and adapted for noise and pace. Nothing starts without us agreeing it.","m":[6,8]},{"t":"5:30","band":"Crisis plan","w":"D","s":"Let's write your crisis plan now, in your words. What are the first signs for you?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Noise in my head. Not eating. Lights feeling too bright."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"What helps at that point?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Headphones. Drawing. Ringing Leanne."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"And if that's not enough: the crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, or Samaritans on 116 123. Can I send this plan to the A&E liaison team and Dr Patel, so they know to find you a quiet space?","m":[10]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Yes. If they actually read it."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"I'll send it today and ask liaison to put it on their system.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:05","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back the main changes, so I know I've explained it clearly?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Quiet room first thing. Texts, not letters. One person. Autism referral. Crisis plan to A&E and Dr Patel."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Exactly. And if you miss an appointment, Sam will phone you. You won't be discharged for that.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Promise?"},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"That's what I'll write in your notes this afternoon, along with this formulation, and I'll text you a copy. Sam will text you by Monday. If things get bad before then, use the numbers on your plan.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thanks for actually listening."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for explaining it so clearly. That's helped me a lot.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and offers sensory adjustments to the room","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks, without blame, what makes appointments hard and summarises the barriers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores development: school, friendships, routine changes, sensory needs, literal thinking, prior assessment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks sensitively about childhood trauma, nightmares and dissociation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores what precedes self-harm and asks directly about current suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her distress and fear of rejection in plain words","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Shares the formulation as tentative hypotheses (trauma, possible autism, personality), not verdicts","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Replaces 'non-engagement' with three named barriers, each with a specific reasonable adjustment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers neurodevelopmental referral, explains the wait and gives support meanwhile","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Co-produces a crisis plan and agrees sharing it with A&E liaison and her GP","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking her to correct the formulation and repeat the plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States clearly that missed appointments will not lead to discharge","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes with written copy, next contact date and safety-netting","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Builds a tentative trauma and neurodevelopmental formulation with her, turns each barrier into an adjustment, refers for assessment and agrees a shared crisis plan.","borderline":"Mentions autism and trauma but keeps the plan generic, or forgets to share the crisis plan with A&E and the GP.","fail":"Accepts 'non-engagement', calls her manipulative, fixes on one label, or discharges for missed appointments."}},"c-1-4-1":{"setup":"Saturday 23:40 in A&E. Nicola, 34, is medically fit after an intentional overdose; her notes say 'chronic high risk, known to services'. Assess her, separate long-standing risk from what changed today, and make a safety plan with her.","role":{"who":"Ms Nicola Barnes, 34, female, A&E patient after an intentional overdose, known to the CMHT","open":"I've done this a hundred times. Just let me go home.","manner":"Tired, irritable and dismissive at first, expecting to be brushed off. Opens up when asked what was different today. Cries about her dog; settles when the loss is acknowledged.","free":["She has self-harmed many times over ten years","She wants to go home","She feels services only care when she is in A&E"],"ifasked":["An eviction notice came on Wednesday; the new places she can afford won't take dogs","Her dog Bella went to the RSPCA on Friday; Bella was the reason she got up","She drank half a bottle of wine tonight, alone, and took the tablets in about ten minutes","She wanted 'everything to stop'; she did not leave a note or tell anyone","Her sister Kim came round unexpectedly and called the ambulance","She is unsure she is glad she woke up; part of her is relieved Kim found her","She has more tablets at home in the bathroom cabinet","No children or dependants; her care coordinator is Sue; she wants to fight the eviction"],"worries":["'If I tell you how bad it is, you'll section me.'","'You lot only care when I'm in A&E. Nobody rings when I'm drowning.'","'Where am I even supposed to live?'"],"rules":["Gives one-word answers if the doctor reads from the notes or uses 'chronic' to her face","Cries when Bella is mentioned; settles if the doctor names the loss","Becomes guarded if asked for details of the tablets; opens up when asked what she hoped would happen","Agrees to Kim being told if she can choose what is said"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Nicola, I'm Dr Shah, one of the psychiatry doctors. The medical team say you're physically okay now. I'd like to understand what happened tonight, if that's alright.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"I've done this a hundred times. Just let me go home."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I can hear you're exhausted, and you've probably told this story more times than anyone should. I'm not going to go off your old notes. What you tell me stays within the team, unless I'm worried someone's in serious danger, and then I'd tell you first.","m":[1,7]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Fine. Ask your questions."},{"t":"0:45","band":"Tonight","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me about today, from earlier in the day up to the tablets?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Nothing to tell. Woke up, sat on the sofa, opened some wine. Took the tablets around nine."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"What was different about today, compared with other bad days?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"(pause) They took Bella. She was the only reason I got up in the morning."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Bella's your dog? I'm so sorry. That's a huge loss.","m":[3,7]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"(crying) Landlord's given me notice. Nowhere I can afford takes dogs. The RSPCA came yesterday."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Losing your home and Bella in the same week. No wonder tonight felt different. Take your time.","m":[7]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Sorry. I don't usually cry in front of people."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"There's nothing to be sorry for. When did the eviction notice come?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Wednesday. I've been drinking more since."},{"t":"1:50","band":"Intent","w":"D","s":"When you took the tablets, what did you hope would happen?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"I wanted everything to stop. All of it."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Did you plan it, or was it more sudden?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Sudden. Ten minutes, maybe. I didn't write anything. I didn't tell anyone."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"How did you end up here?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Kim, my sister, turned up out of the blue. She rang the ambulance."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"How does it feel now, knowing you're still here?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Honestly? I'm not sure I'm glad I woke up."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. That's a hard thing to say. Is there any part of you that's glad Kim came?","m":[4,7]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Part of me. She looked so scared."},{"t":"2:45","band":"Risk now","w":"D","s":"Right now, are you having thoughts about ending your life?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"If I tell you how bad it is, you'll section me."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"I understand that fear. My aim is for you to go home with a plan you've helped write. Being honest with me helps me get that plan right.","m":[5,12]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"It's there, in the background. I wouldn't do anything tonight. Not with Kim like that."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Are there more tablets at home?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"A few boxes in the bathroom cabinet."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Do you have any children or anyone who depends on you?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"No. Just Bella. Was."},{"t":"3:35","band":"The longer view","w":"D","s":"Over the years, what has kept you safe on the bad nights?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Bella, mostly. Kim. Drawing sometimes. And fighting things, when I've got the energy."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Is there anything you want to fight now?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"The eviction. I think it's dodgy. Sue, my care coordinator, said she'd help once."},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"You lot only care when I'm in A&E. Nobody rings when I'm drowning."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"That sounds lonely, and it's fair feedback. I'll make sure someone contacts you tomorrow, not just next time you're in here.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:15","band":"Formulation","w":"D","s":"Can I tell you how I see it? You've lived with these feelings for years, and that background hasn't changed. What's new is losing your home and Bella in one week, drinking more, and being alone at night. That's why tonight was more dangerous.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Yeah. That's it."},{"t":"4:40","band":"Safety plan","w":"D","s":"Let's write a plan together, in your words, for when the feelings rise again. What are your warning signs?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Sitting in the dark. Opening wine on my own. Not answering Kim."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"What can you do yourself at that point?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"Go out. Draw. Put the telly on loud."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"Who are two people you could be with, or call? And can I let Kim know about tonight and the plan?","m":[9,10]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Kim, and my friend Dee. Yes, tell Kim. Not about the wine."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"And three numbers: the crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, and Samaritans on 116 123. Could Kim take the tablets from the cabinet back to a pharmacy?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"She would. She'd be glad to."},{"t":"5:40","band":"Follow-up","w":"D","s":"The crisis team will contact you within 24 hours. I'll write to your GP on Monday, and ask Sue and a social worker to help with the eviction and housing.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Where am I even supposed to live?"},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"That's exactly what the social worker is for. Losing your home doesn't have to happen overnight, and you won't face it alone.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what's in the plan, so I know it makes sense to you?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"Warning signs, ring Kim or Dee, the numbers. Kim takes the tablets. Crisis team rings tomorrow."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"That's it. If the feelings get stronger before the crisis team call, ring one of those numbers or come back here. You'll keep this copy. Is there anything you'd change?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks. You actually asked about Bella."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"She clearly matters a lot. I'll finish your notes now and let the A&E team know the plan.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and the limits of confidentiality","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes an account of the day leading up to the overdose","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what was different today and identifies the new triggers (eviction, Bella, alcohol)","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores intent, planning, discovery and feelings about surviving, without asking for method detail","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about current suicidal thoughts and access to tablets at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores long-standing pattern and what has kept her safe before","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her loss and frustration without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Separates long-standing risk from today's new triggers in one clear statement","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Co-produces a written safety plan in her words with two named people and three crisis numbers","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks about dependants and gains consent to involve her sister","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Arranges crisis team within 24 hours, GP letter next working day and housing help","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Answers her fear of being sectioned honestly and checks understanding of the plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with safety-netting, a copy of the plan and a chance to add anything","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Asks what changed today, explores intent and current thoughts, separates chronic from acute risk, and leaves her holding a plan she wrote.","borderline":"Takes a good history but the safety plan is generic, or follow-up timings are vague.","fail":"Relies on 'known to services' or a risk score, asks for method detail, or discharges without a co-produced plan or follow-up."}},"c-1-4-2":{"setup":"Friday 16:30 on the ward. Mr Bell, 41, has paranoid schizophrenia and is on Section 3; his first unescorted leave is tomorrow. The risk entry says 'Risk: low. No concerns.' Talk with him and update the risk formulation before the weekend.","role":{"who":"Mr Marcus Bell, 41, male, detained under Section 3 with paranoid schizophrenia","open":"Is this about my leave? Nobody's cancelling it, are they?","manner":"Polite but guarded and keen to get leave. Irritated when the assault is raised, feeling judged. Becomes more open and cooperative once he sees the plan protects his leave.","free":["He wants to see his brother Leon tomorrow","He has been taking his depot injection","He feels much better than when he came in"],"ifasked":["Before admission he stopped his tablets and smoked cannabis daily","He pushed and hit his neighbour in March, believing the neighbour was poisoning his food","He still sometimes wonders if the neighbour tampered with his food, but has no intention of acting","No voices this week; sleeping well","He would like to call at his flat to collect post; it is on the neighbour's street","He thinks one joint on leave would not matter","Early signs: sleeping badly, checking food, staying in his room, asking about the neighbour","He is not angry with anyone now; Leon is the person he trusts most"],"worries":["'Why are you writing about me hurting people? That was ages ago.'","'Can I go to my flat on leave?'","'One joint on leave isn't a big deal, right?'"],"rules":["Bristles if the assault is described as 'violent history'; calms if the doctor explains why it is written","Admits his doubts about the neighbour only if asked gently and specifically","Agrees to stay away from his street if offered a way to get his post","Gives consent for Leon to be told if asked directly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Bell, I'm Dr Shah, the doctor covering this weekend. I'm updating your risk notes before your leave tomorrow, so whoever's on duty knows how to help you. Have you got ten minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Is this about my leave? Nobody's cancelling it, are they?"},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"No. Dr Evans has approved it. Good notes make leave safer and easier to build on. I'd like your help to get them right.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Go on, then."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Before admission","w":"D","s":"What happened in the weeks before you came into hospital?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"I'd stopped the tablets. Smoking weed every day. Not sleeping."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"And the incident with your neighbour?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Why are you writing about me hurting people? That was ages ago."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"I can see it's frustrating to have that follow you. I'm not writing it to judge you. I'm writing it so we know the signs early and can step in long before anything like that could happen again.","m":[7]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Fine. I thought he was putting stuff in my food. I went round and pushed him. Hit him once."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me straight. What was going on for you then?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"I was scared. I really believed it. I hadn't slept for days."},{"t":"1:45","band":"Now","w":"D","s":"How have the voices or worries about people been this week?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"No voices. Sleeping fine."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"And the neighbour? Do you still have any thoughts about him and your food?","m":[3,5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"(pause) Sometimes I wonder. Not like before. I'm not going to do anything."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"I appreciate you being honest. Is there anyone you're angry with at the moment?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"No. I just want to get on."},{"t":"2:20","band":"Leave","w":"D","s":"Where are you planning to go tomorrow, and who will you see?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Leon's, my brother. Then maybe my flat to grab my post."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Your flat's on the same street as the neighbour, isn't it? For this first leave I'd like you to stay away from that street. Could Leon pick up your post?","m":[4,9]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"He could. I suppose that makes sense."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Do you plan to drink or use anything while you're out?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"One joint on leave isn't a big deal, right?"},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"I get that it feels small. But cannabis was there last time you became unwell and worried about the neighbour, and your injection is only three weeks in, so it isn't fully covering you yet. One joint could set things back.","m":[4,7]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Okay. I'll leave it."},{"t":"3:30","band":"Warning signs","w":"D","s":"What would tell Leon you're becoming unwell again?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"I stop sleeping. I check my food. Stay in my room. Leon says I go quiet."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"And asking about the neighbour?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Yeah. That too."},{"t":"3:50","band":"Formulation","w":"D","s":"Here's what I'll write, in plain words. Your main risk is to your neighbour, and it only rose when you were unwell: off medication, using cannabis, and not sleeping.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Warning signs are poor sleep, checking food, isolating, and talking about him. What protects you is your depot, Leon, sleeping well, and your honesty today. Right now you're settled and the risk is much lower than when you came in.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:25","band":"Leave and actions","w":"D","s":"Leave tomorrow is two hours, 2pm to 4pm, to Leon's house, not your street, and back by four. I'll check that matches what Dr Evans wrote on the leave form.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"And if I'm late? Is that it, no more leave?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"If you're a bit late, the nurses will ring you, then Leon. If they can't reach you, they'll call the duty doctor and follow the missing patient policy, which may mean the police help bring you back.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"If you come back smelling of cannabis, or talking about the neighbour, the nurse will call the duty doctor, who'll see you before any more leave. It doesn't mean all leave stops; it means we adjust it.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"Fair enough."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Can I tell Leon your leave times, the ward number, and those warning signs?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Yeah. He'd want to know."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"I'll ring him after we finish. What if he's worried about you on the day?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"He can ring the ward."},{"t":"5:55","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Could you sum up what we've agreed for tomorrow?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Leon's house, two till four, not my street. No weed. Leon gets my post. If I'm late they ring me."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Spot on. Is there anything you want me to add to the notes?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Put that I've been doing well."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"I will. You've been settled for weeks, and that goes in.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"I'll update the electronic risk record, then go through this if-then plan with the nurse in charge and the weekend doctor at handover. Dr Evans will review your leave on Monday.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Okay. Cheers, doc."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Enjoy seeing Leon. If anything's bothering you before you go, ask for me.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and explains honestly why the risk notes are being updated","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores events before admission, including the assault, in a calm, non-judgemental way","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about current psychotic symptoms and beliefs about the neighbour","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks where he will go on leave, who with, and about cannabis or alcohol","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about current anger or intent towards anyone, specifically the neighbour","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Identifies early warning signs with him","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his frustration and explains reasons without blame","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"States the formulation plainly: risk to whom, triggers, warning signs, what protects him, current state","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Links the formulation to tomorrow's leave conditions and checks they match the leave form","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives clear if–then actions for late return, cannabis use and talk of the neighbour","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gains consent to share leave times and warning signs with his brother","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding and invites him to add to the notes","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with record update, handover to nurse in charge and weekend doctor, and review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Names the risk, to whom, triggers and warning signs in plain words, ties them to tomorrow's leave, and leaves an if–then plan the weekend team can act on.","borderline":"Gathers the right history but writes 'low risk' without a story, or misses the link to leave conditions.","fail":"Leaves 'Risk: low' unchanged, cancels leave by reflex, or gives no actions for late return, cannabis or talk of the neighbour."}},"c-1-5-1":{"setup":"Tuesday 11:00 in the CMHT clinic. Hannah, 29, is in her third episode of depression in five years and asks, 'Why does this keep happening? Is something wrong with my brain?' With 15 minutes left, share your formulation and agree next steps.","role":{"who":"Ms Hannah Reid, 29, female, CMHT outpatient with recurrent depression","open":"Why does this keep happening to me? Is something wrong with my brain?","manner":"Anxious, self-critical and articulate. Quick to blame herself. Becomes tearful when she links it to being a perfectionist; settles when reassured it is not her fault. Engaged once she feels heard.","free":["This is her third episode in five years","She started a demanding new job three months ago","She stopped sertraline early last time"],"ifasked":["Her mum died when she was 12","She thinks it might be a chemical imbalance because a friend said so","She works late, cancels friends and then crashes","Last time sertraline helped after about a month; she stopped after three months because she felt better","She does not want medication for life","Some nights she thinks everyone would cope better if she wasn't here; no plans or intent","Her close friend Maya and running used to help"],"worries":["'Am I going to be like this forever?'","'So it's my fault for being a perfectionist.'","'I don't want to be on medication for life.'"],"rules":["If the doctor uses jargon, she asks what it means and looks more anxious","Becomes tearful at the idea it is her fault; settles if reframed as a way of coping","Only discloses passive suicidal thoughts if asked directly","Corrects the doctor's formulation once, adding fear of failure at work"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"That's such an important question, Hannah, and I want to give it a proper answer. We've got about fifteen minutes. Could we spend most of it on that, and save a few minutes for what happens next?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Yes. Please. Is something wrong with my brain? Am I going to be like this forever?"},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"Short answer: no, I don't think anything is broken in your brain. Can I ask what you've been afraid it means?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"That I'm damaged. Like this is just who I am now."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a frightening thing to carry around. It isn't who you are. Depression is something that happens to you, and you've recovered twice before.","m":[3,6]},{"t":"1:00","band":"Her view","w":"D","s":"Before I say how I see it, what do you think is causing it?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Isn't it just a chemical imbalance? My friend said that."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"It's a common idea, but it's too simple. Brain chemistry is part of it, along with stress, life events and the patterns we fall into. That's actually good news, because each part gives us something to work on.","m":[7]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"What do you think sets it off for you?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"I push myself so hard at work, then I just crash. Every time."},{"t":"1:45","band":"Sharing the formulation","w":"D","s":"Can I tell you how I understand it so far, and you correct me?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Some people are more sensitive to stress, partly through genes and partly through early life. Losing your mum at twelve was a huge loss, and I wonder if you learned then to cope by keeping going and not needing anyone.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Then a big pressure comes, like the new job. You work later, see friends less, and stop running. That leaves you drained and alone, your mood drops, and you push even harder. That's the loop that keeps it going.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Does that fit with your experience? What have I got wrong?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Mostly right. You've missed how scared I am of failing. If I'm not the best, I'm nothing."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's an important piece. So fear of failing drives the pushing. I'll add that in.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"(tearful) So it is my fault. For being a perfectionist."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"I can see that's really upsetting, and it isn't your fault. Pushing hard was how you survived a very hard time. It helped you then. It just costs you now, and you can learn other ways.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"(pause) That's kinder than how I think about it."},{"t":"3:30","band":"Linking to treatment","w":"D","s":"Each part of the loop has a treatment step. For the pushing and the fear of failing: talking therapy, CBT, through NHS Talking Therapies. Would you be open to that?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Yes. I think I need that."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"For the pulling away: one plan each week with a friend. Who would that be?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Maya. And I could start running again on Saturdays."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"For the stress sensitivity and the low mood now: sertraline helped last time. Would you consider restarting it?","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"I stopped the tablets early last time. And I don't want to be on medication for life."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"That's your choice, and it's a fair worry. After three episodes, guidance suggests staying on it for at least two years after you're well, because that lowers the chance of another episode. Stopping is then done slowly, together. The side effects are usually mild and settle. It's not necessarily for life.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Two years I can think about. Okay, let's restart it."},{"t":"4:55","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"I need to ask everyone this. When you're this low, do you have thoughts about ending your life?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think everyone would cope better if I wasn't here. I wouldn't do anything."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you made a plan, or taken any steps towards it?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I think of my dad."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"If those thoughts get stronger, I want you to tell someone the same day: Maya, your GP, or the crisis line.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:35","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"We've covered a lot. Can you tell me in your words how we understand it, and what we've agreed?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"I'm more sensitive to stress, maybe from losing Mum. When I'm under pressure I push and pull away, and that keeps the low mood going. So: CBT, see Maya, running, and restart sertraline for two years."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. I'll write this to you in a letter with the diagram, and copy it to your GP.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"That'd help. I forget things when I'm like this."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"I'll see you in two weeks to check how the sertraline is going. If you feel worse, or the thoughts get stronger, ring NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123. Anything else before you go?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. I feel less broken."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You're not broken. I'll see you in two weeks.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Acknowledges her question and agrees how to use the remaining time","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks her own explanation and triggers before sharing the formulation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Answers 'is my brain broken?' directly and explores the fear behind it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Shares a tentative formulation in plain English, no jargon, within three minutes","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Invites correction and builds her addition into the formulation","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her distress and reframes self-blame without dismissing it","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Corrects the 'chemical imbalance' idea gently and accurately","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Links each part of the formulation to a specific treatment step she agrees","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Discusses medication choice and duration honestly, respecting her decision","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and protective reasons","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Uses teach-back to confirm her understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets with crisis numbers and a dated review, and closes clearly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Asks her view first, gives a short tentative formulation she can correct, links it to agreed treatment, checks understanding and asks about suicidal thoughts.","borderline":"Explains well but talks at her, skips teach-back, or leaves the treatment link vague.","fail":"Lectures with jargon, implies it is her fault, agrees 'chemical imbalance', or ends without asking about suicidal thoughts."}},"c-1-5-2":{"setup":"Monday 17:00 at the EIP team base. Ryan, 19, has a first episode of psychosis; his divorced parents ask, 'Did we cause this? Was it the cannabis?' He consents to sharing his diagnosis and plan, not his relationship. Meet them.","role":{"who":"Mrs Claire Doyle, 49, Ryan's mother, with his father Mr Mark Doyle, 51 (divorced when Ryan was 14); the AI plays both","open":"Thanks for seeing us. We just want to know what's going on with him.","manner":"Mother is anxious, guilty and exhausted; father is frustrated and blunt. Both soften when their guilt is named. Father bristles at the consent limits but settles when told he will still be heard.","free":["Ryan dropped out of his first year at university in the spring","He has smoked strong cannabis since 16","They divorced when he was 14"],"ifasked":["Since Christmas he stopped sleeping, stayed in his room and stopped seeing friends","He said the television was sending him messages","Mother checks on him several times a night and hasn't slept properly in weeks","Father thinks Ryan is lazy and wants to take his phone","Ryan's uncle had 'a breakdown' in his twenties","Ryan loves music and used to play guitar; he is close to his younger sister, 15","They know nothing about psychosis except what they read online"],"worries":["'We divorced when he was 14. Did we do this to him?'","'He's 19 and you won't tell us anything? We're his parents!'","'Will he ever be normal again? Go back to uni?'","'He keeps saying his girlfriend controls his thoughts. Is she in danger?'"],"rules":["Father raises his voice when consent limits are first explained; calms when told the team will always listen","Mother becomes tearful asking whether the divorce caused it; settles if guilt is named directly","They raise the girlfriend; accept that details can't be discussed if their concern is clearly taken seriously","Mother admits her own exhaustion only if asked how they are coping"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Shah, one of the doctors in Ryan's team. Thank you both for coming. Could I check who you are, and what you'd most like from today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Thanks for seeing us. I'm Claire, his mum. This is Mark, his dad. We just want to know what's going on with him."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"Ryan has said I can share his diagnosis and his treatment plan with you. There are a few personal things he's asked me to keep private, and I need to respect that.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Father: He's 19 and you won't tell us anything? We're his parents!"},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"I can hear how hard that is, and you clearly care about him. I can tell you a lot today. And I'm always happy to listen to anything you want to tell us. That's never off limits.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Father: Fine. Go on."},{"t":"1:10","band":"Their fears","w":"D","s":"Before I explain, what's worrying each of you most?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"(tearful) We divorced when he was 14. Did we do this to him?"},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you for saying that, Claire. Mark, what about you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Father: Was it the cannabis? He's been smoking skunk since sixteen. I should've stopped him."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you're both carrying a lot of guilt. I want to be clear: you did not cause this. I'll explain why.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:45","band":"What they've noticed","w":"D","s":"First, what have you noticed in him over the last few months?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"He stopped sleeping after Christmas. Dropped out of uni. Stayed in his room. Said the telly was sending him messages."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"That must have been scary. What do you understand about psychosis so far?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Only what I've read online. It's terrifying. Split personality, violence."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"That's a common worry, but psychosis isn't split personality, and most people with it aren't violent. Is there anyone in the family who has had mental health problems?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Father: My brother had a breakdown in his twenties. He's fine now."},{"t":"2:35","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Ryan's diagnosis is a first episode of psychosis. It means his mind has had trouble telling what's real from what isn't, which is why the messages felt real to him.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"We think some people's brains are more sensitive to stress, partly through genes. On top of that, things like heavy cannabis, lack of sleep and big changes, like starting university, can tip them over.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"A divorce is stressful for any family, but it doesn't cause psychosis. Lots of children of divorced parents never get unwell.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"On cannabis, you're right to ask. Strong cannabis, used daily from a young age, does raise the risk. It's one piece of the picture, not the whole cause, and stopping it gives him the best chance.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Father: So we should've stopped him."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Most parents can't stop a teenager doing something he's set on. What matters now is helping him cut down, and you can do that.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:50","band":"Prognosis","w":"P","s":"Will he ever be normal again? Go back to uni?"},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Many young people recover well, especially with early help and a supportive family, and lots return to study or work. Some have another episode, and a few need longer support. I can't promise which, but the signs are hopeful.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:15","band":"Listening","w":"P","s":"He keeps saying his girlfriend controls his thoughts. Is she in danger?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"That's an important worry, and thank you for telling me. Ryan has asked me not to discuss his relationship, so I can't go into it. But I've heard you, I'll pass it to his team today, and we'll take it seriously.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Okay. As long as someone knows."},{"t":"4:45","band":"Their coping","w":"D","s":"How are you both coping?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"(pause) I haven't slept in weeks. I keep checking on him at night."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. You matter too. You have a right to a carer's assessment through the council, to look at support for you. Would you like me to refer you?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Yes. I didn't know that was a thing."},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Father: Sometimes I think he's just lazy. I want to take his phone off him."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"I understand the frustration. Losing motivation can be part of the illness, not laziness. Our family sessions look at how to help without arguments.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:35","band":"Plan and support","w":"D","s":"We offer family intervention: around ten sessions with you and Ryan, if he agrees. It helps everyone understand the illness, spot early signs and reduce stress at home.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Father: I'd come to that."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Here's the team's number for office hours. At night or at weekends, ring the crisis line or NHS 111 option 2. If he's ever in immediate danger, call 999.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:05","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"That's a lot. Is there anything you'd like me to explain differently, or say back to me?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"That we didn't cause it. Stress, his brain and the cannabis together. He can get better. Family sessions, my carer's assessment, and the number if things go wrong."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. I'll record what we discussed, refer you for the carer's assessment, and ask Ryan about the family sessions this week.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel like I can breathe a bit."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You've both done a lot for him already. We'll be in touch this week.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks who is present and what they want from the meeting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the limits of Ryan's consent clearly at the start","d":"Content"},{"c":"Invites both parents to state their fears before explaining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what they have noticed, their understanding of psychosis and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names their guilt directly and says clearly they did not cause it","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains psychosis and the stress–vulnerability model in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains cannabis risk honestly without blaming the parents","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives a balanced, hopeful prognosis without false certainty","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Listens to information outside consent, discloses nothing private, and acts on the safety concern","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks how they are coping and offers a Care Act carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers family intervention and gives in-hours and out-of-hours crisis contacts","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks their understanding and invites questions","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with a clear summary of actions and timing","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Lets both parents voice fears, lifts the guilt, explains stress–vulnerability and cannabis plainly within consent, and offers family intervention, carer's assessment and crisis contacts.","borderline":"Explains well but skips the carers' own needs, or is vague about consent limits.","fail":"Breaches consent, refuses to listen citing confidentiality, blames the family or genes, or promises full recovery."}},"c-1-5-3":{"setup":"Wednesday 14:00, discharge planning meeting on the ward. Angela, 47, has schizoaffective disorder and has been on Section 3 for ten weeks. Her sister, care coordinator, nurse, OT and consultant are present. Present the formulation and plan in two minutes, then agree it.","role":{"who":"Ms Angela Reeve, 47, female, detained under Section 3 with schizoaffective disorder (the AI also voices her sister Joanne, 50, when cued)","open":"Go ahead. As long as I get a say.","manner":"Wary and a little prickly, sensitive to being discussed. Becomes warmer once addressed directly. Upset about her job; settles when that is taken seriously. Sister is supportive but stretched.","free":["She stopped her old medication because of weight gain","She wants her job back","She enjoys her art group"],"ifasked":["She lost her office job three months before admission, just after stopping medication","The new medication has caused less weight gain so far","Early signs: poor sleep, stopping tablets, not answering the phone, staying in","She worries she will lose her flat if things fall apart","She would prefer clinic to home treatment visits","Sister works full time and cannot phone every day","She is happy for her sister to have a copy of the crisis plan"],"worries":["'Weight gain's why I stopped. If it comes back, I'm stopping again.'","'You're all talking about me like I'm not here.'","'Can I skip the home treatment visits if I come to clinic?'","'If I go home and it falls apart, will I lose the flat?'"],"rules":["If the doctor addresses the team instead of her, she says 'You're all talking about me like I'm not here'","Pushes to skip home treatment visits; accepts a short period with a clear end","Becomes tearful about her job; settles when an employment referral is offered","Sister says she cannot be the daily contact; accepts a role if it is realistic"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Angela, before I start, is it okay if I summarise how we understand things so far? Please stop me if I get anything wrong.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Go ahead. As long as I get a say."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"You'll have the last word, I promise. I'll keep it to two minutes.","m":[1,7]},{"t":"0:25","band":"Two-minute summary","w":"D","s":"Angela is 47 and has schizoaffective disorder. She's been on Section 3 for ten weeks. She came in after stopping her medication because of weight gain, at a stressful time when she'd also lost her job.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"What kept things going was that loss of routine, poor sleep, and pulling away from people, so nobody noticed early.","m":[3,6]},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Her strengths are her sister Joanne, her art group, and her real wish to get back to work. She's done well on a new medication with less weight gain.","m":[4,6]},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"When she's unwell, the main risks are self-neglect and being taken advantage of. Her early signs are poor sleep, stopping tablets, and not answering the phone.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"The plan is discharge next Thursday, home treatment follow-up within 72 hours, and help with work and weight.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:40","band":"Her view","w":"D","s":"Angela, have I got that right? What would you like everyone to know?","m":[7]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Mostly. But you're all talking about me like I'm not here."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"You're right, and I'm sorry. This is your plan, so I'll talk to you. What would you change?","m":[12]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Weight gain's why I stopped. If it comes back, I'm stopping again."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"That's completely understandable, and it matters. We'll weigh you monthly at the physical health clinic, check your bloods, and you can ring us if the scales creep up, rather than stopping on your own.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's fair."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"How have you found cooking and managing money on the ward?","m":[10]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Fine. I did a shop with the OT on Monday and cooked a curry."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Named people","w":"D","s":"Ruth, as your care coordinator, has weekly visits ready, and the home treatment team will see you for the first two weeks. How does that sound?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Can I skip the home treatment visits if I come to clinic? I hate people in my flat."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Let's agree a short run of visits, then review them with you at the end of the first week. The first contact within 72 hours is important, because that's when people are most at risk after leaving hospital.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Fine. A week."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Thanks. And our OT says you're managing cooking and money well. She'll refer you to the employment specialist service.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I just want my job back. Nobody will hire me now."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard, losing work you cared about. The employment specialists help people back into work while they're still recovering. You won't be doing it alone.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:10","band":"Section 117 and home","w":"P","s":"If I go home and it falls apart, will I lose the flat?"},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Because you've been on Section 3, you're entitled to Section 117 aftercare. The NHS and the council must jointly provide the aftercare you need, free of charge, until they agree it's no longer needed. The ward social worker will check your tenancy and benefits before you leave.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Free? Okay. Good."},{"t":"4:50","band":"Crisis plan","w":"D","s":"Joanne, what did you notice before Angela became unwell?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Sister: She stopped answering. I'll help, but I work full time. I can't be the contact every day."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. You don't need to be. Ruth and the home treatment team will be the daily contacts. Could you be the person who rings if Angela goes quiet? And we'll offer you a carer's assessment.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Sister: Yes, I can do that. But what do I do if she stops answering?"},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Ring Ruth in office hours. Out of hours, ring the crisis line on the plan. If you think she's in immediate danger, call 999.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Sister: Okay. That's clear."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Angela, may Joanne have a copy of your crisis plan? It lists your early signs, Ruth's number, the crisis line and NHS 111 option 2.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. She should have it."},{"t":"6:10","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"So: discharge next Thursday with two weeks' medication. Home treatment within 72 hours, then review the visits after a week. Ruth weekly. Monthly weight checks. Employment referral. Section 117 aftercare. Crisis plan to you, Joanne and your GP.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Angela, does that match what you want? Is there anything you'd like to add?","m":[7,13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Write down that I'm going back to work. That's my goal."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"It's going at the top. I'll send your GP a summary within 24 hours, and we'll review everything at the next care review. Thank you, everyone.","m":[7,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Asks the patient's permission before summarising and promises her the last word","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"States who she is and why now, concisely","d":"Content"},{"c":"Includes what has kept the illness going","d":"Content"},{"c":"Includes her strengths and goals","d":"Content"},{"c":"States risks and early relapse signs in plain terms","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Delivers the formulation and plan within two minutes, structured and jargon-free","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Invites her view, gives her the last word and builds her goal into the plan","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds to her weight concern and visit concern with concrete plans","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains Section 117 aftercare accurately: joint NHS and council duty, free of charge","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names who does what and when, including 72-hour follow-up","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a crisis plan held by patient and sister, adjusts to sister's limits, offers carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Acknowledges and validates her feelings when she feels talked about or loses hope","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises the agreed plan clearly and closes the meeting","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A crisp two-minute formulation, spoken to her, with strengths, risks and early signs, ending in named people, dates, Section 117 and a crisis plan she and her sister hold.","borderline":"Covers the content but runs long, talks to the team over her, or forgets Section 117 or dates.","fail":"Rambles without a clear message, ignores her views on weight or work, omits aftercare or a relapse plan."}},"c-2-1-1":{"setup":"It is Saturday 23:40 in A&E. Kieran, 19, was brought in by his flatmate after three weeks of little sleep, daily cannabis and fears of being filmed. Take a history and mental state, screen for organic causes, assess risk and agree a safe plan.","role":{"who":"Kieran Doyle, 19, male, the patient (left university in September)","open":"Can we turn that light off? I don't like it.","manner":"Quiet, watchful, glances at the ceiling light. Guarded at first, warms when not argued with. Tearful when talking about sleep; frightened about being locked up.","free":["Has barely slept for three weeks","Believes the neighbours upstairs film him through the smoke alarm","Smokes cannabis every day","Flatmate Danny brought him in"],"ifasked":["Hears the neighbours talking about him through the ceiling ('He's up again')","Feels his thoughts leak out so others know them","Skunk, five or six joints a day, up from weekends since leaving university","Cocaine once at a party a month ago; drinks at weekends; never used spice","Oriented; no headaches, fits, head injury or fevers","Mum's cousin had schizophrenia","Passive wish not to wake up; no plan, his mum keeps him going","Bought a knife last week to keep by the bed for protection"],"worries":["Are you going to lock me up?","No blood tests: you'll put something in me","It's only weed, why does it matter?"],"rules":["Argues if the filming belief is challenged directly; opens up if the doctor stays curious","Refuses bloods until each test is explained and he can watch","Mentions the knife only if asked about protecting himself or confronting the neighbours","Becomes tearful about not sleeping; settles if validated"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains role and time, and makes the room comfortable for him","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Opens with an open question and lets him tell his story in his own words","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the filming belief: onset, meaning, conviction and other explanations, without arguing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for hallucinations, thought interference and passivity with non-leading questions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks orientation and asks about head injury, fits, fever and headache to exclude delirium","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes a non-judgemental substance history: cannabis type, amount, recent change, other drugs, alcohol","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for depression and mania, and asks about family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicide, harm to others, weapons and acting on beliefs","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his fear and exhaustion with empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains each blood, urine and ECG test with a reason and gains consent","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains possible psychosis in plain words and offers early intervention referral tonight with hope","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a plan: home with flatmate, knife removed, crisis team within 24 hours, no driving, cut down cannabis","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking him to repeat the plan","d":"Close"},{"c":"Gives clear safety netting (return to A&E, 111 option 2) and closes with next steps","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Calm, curious history that finds the voices, thought broadcasting, cannabis pattern and the knife, excludes delirium, and ends with an agreed EIP plan.","borderline":"Good psychopathology and plan, but the organic screen is thin or the knife and suicide questions come late or vaguely.","fail":"Argues with the belief, labels it drug-induced and sends him to his GP, or never asks about weapons, suicide or organic causes."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Patel, one of the psychiatry doctors. Is it Kieran? The A&E team asked me to see you. I've got about twenty minutes. Is it OK to talk here?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Can we turn that light off? I don't like it."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Of course. There, is that better? Tell me in your words what's been happening these last few weeks.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:25","band":"His story","w":"P","s":"I haven't slept properly in about three weeks. The people upstairs have been filming me. Through the smoke alarm. There's a little red light, and it blinks when I move."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening, feeling watched in your own home.","m":[9]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"It is. I can't even go to the toilet without thinking they're watching. Nobody believes me. Danny thinks I've lost it."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"I'm not here to argue with you. Help me understand. When did you first notice it?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Couple of weeks back. I heard them laughing through the ceiling right after I got out of the shower. Then I saw the red light."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"What do you think they want?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"To put it online. Make money off me. And they know what I'm thinking, sometimes."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"How do they know what you're thinking?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"It's like my thoughts leak out. When I walk past people, they look at me like they heard."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Do you ever hear voices or sounds when no one else is around?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"I hear them talking about me through the ceiling. 'He's up again. He's in the kitchen.'"},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Do they speak to you, or talk about you? Do they ever tell you to do things?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"About me. They've never told me to do anything."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Do you ever feel something is putting thoughts into your head, or controlling how you move?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"No. Not like that."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"How sure are you about the filming, out of ten? Could there be any other explanation?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Ten. Well, nine. Danny says it's just a normal smoke alarm. But why would it blink when I move?"},{"t":"2:25","band":"Physical and substances","w":"D","s":"Fair question. I want to make sure nothing physical is going on too. What's today's date, and where we are?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Saturday. The seventh? This is A&E at the General."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Spot on. Any headaches, fits, a bang on the head, fevers, or being physically unwell lately?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"No. Just knackered."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Now cannabis. I'm not judging; lots of people smoke. What do you use, how much, and has it changed?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Skunk. It was weekends, but since I dropped out of uni in September it's five or six joints a day."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Anything else? Spice, coke, ket, pills? And alcohol?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Bit of coke at a party a month ago. I drink at weekends. Never touched spice."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Low and hopeless, or the opposite, really high with racing thoughts? And is there any family history of mental health problems?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Low, because of all this. Not high, I'm wrecked. My mum's cousin had schizophrenia, I think."},{"t":"3:35","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"With all this happening, have you had any thoughts about ending your life, or harming yourself?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think it'd be easier not to wake up. But I wouldn't do anything. I wouldn't do that to my mum."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Have you thought about confronting the neighbours, or done anything to protect yourself from them?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"I bought a knife. Just to keep by the bed, in case they come down."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you told me. Have you thought of using it, or gone up there?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"No. I just want them to leave me alone. I just want to sleep."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"You sound exhausted and scared, and that's a lot to carry. You did the right thing coming in.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Are you going to lock me up?"},{"t":"4:35","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"That's not my plan. I think lack of sleep and strong cannabis may have put your brain under real strain. That can make ordinary things, like a blinking light, feel threatening. It's called psychosis, and it's treatable.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"So I'm mental."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"No. It's a health problem, and young people recover from it. There's a team just for people your age, called early intervention. I'd like to refer you tonight.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Fine. But no blood tests. You'll put something in me."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"That's understandable if you feel watched. The bloods check thyroid, infection, sugar, salts and liver, which can all cause experiences like this. A urine test shows what's in your system, and an ECG checks your heart. You can watch every step.","m":[10,9]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"OK. If I can watch."},{"t":"5:30","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Would you go home with Danny tonight if he takes the knife? And can I tell him the plan?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Yeah, he can have it. Tell him. He's been good. I don't want to sleep in my room though."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Could you sleep in Danny's room? The crisis team will ring you by tomorrow afternoon, and the early intervention team will see you soon after. Please don't drive for now. If this is psychosis, the DVLA needs to know.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Right. And the weed?"},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Cutting down really helps, and the team will support you. Even less tonight is a start.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Bloods, then home with Danny and he keeps the knife. Crisis people ring tomorrow. No driving. Less weed."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If you feel unsafe, think about going after the neighbours, or feel like ending your life, come straight back here or call 111 and choose option 2. Danny will have the number too. I'll speak to him now.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"OK. Thanks for not thinking I'm mad."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thanks for talking so openly, Kieran. The nurse will do your bloods next, and I'll see you before you go.","m":[14]}]},"c-2-1-2":{"setup":"It is Tuesday 10:15 on the acute ward. Aisha, 34, was admitted overnight on section 2 after police found her shouting at traffic. Before the 11:30 ward round, do a full mental state examination and describe exactly what she is experiencing.","role":{"who":"Ms Aisha Grant, 34, female, the patient, detained on section 2","open":"I've already told the police. Why does everybody keep asking?","manner":"Irritable and suspicious at first, then talkative once she feels heard. Speech drifts between ideas. Angry about being detained; calmer when her frustration is named.","free":["The cars are being controlled and she is the only one who sees it","She knew it when a traffic light turned red last Monday","She is not ill and wants to leave"],"ifasked":["Two voices, a man and a woman, discuss her as 'she' with a running commentary","The voices sound outside, from the ceiling vent","The woman says 'she has to warn them'","Yesterday she stepped into the road to stop a bus","Her thoughts 'go out like radio' so the cars know where she is","No thought echo, insertion, withdrawal or passivity","Would go straight back to Hill Street if allowed out","No thoughts of suicide or harming others"],"worries":["Why are you asking me these weird questions?","If I say they read my thoughts you'll keep me longer","Let me out"],"rules":["Drifts into loosely linked sentences when explaining who controls the cars","Clams up if asked leading yes/no questions in a row","Gets angry if told she is ill; settles if her frustration is acknowledged","Mentions the bus only if asked what she does when the voices speak"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and timing, and normalises unusual questions","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Opens with an open question about recent events before any closed questions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits both parts of the delusional perception: the traffic light and its sudden meaning","d":"Content"},{"c":"Rates conviction out of ten and gently tests other explanations without arguing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Clarifies voices: number, second or third person, commentary, location, content","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks plainly about thought echo, insertion, withdrawal and broadcast","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about passivity of actions, feelings and movements","d":"Content"},{"c":"Reads back and records her exact words, including the thought disorder","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what she does in response to the voices, including any commands","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about suicide, harm to others and what she would do if she left","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her distress and anger at being detained","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Assesses insight and willingness to accept any help","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains section 2 rights: Tribunal within 14 days and an advocate","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Summarises back, tells her the risk goes to the nurse in charge, and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Open then precise questions that capture the delusional perception, third-person commentary, thought broadcast and the bus incident in her words, with risk passed on.","borderline":"Most symptoms found, but described with labels instead of quotes, or the response to voices is not explored.","fail":"Rapid yes/no checklist, arguing about the cars, or missing that she stepped into the road."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Okafor, one of the ward doctors. Is it Aisha? I'd like about twenty minutes to understand what's been happening, before the team meets at half eleven. Is now OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"I've already told the police. Why does everybody keep asking?"},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I know it feels repetitive. The night doctor didn't write much, and I'd rather hear it from you. What do you think has been happening lately?","m":[2,11]},{"t":"0:30","band":"Beliefs","w":"P","s":"The cars are being controlled. Someone is running them. I'm the only one who sees it."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Tell me more. How did you first realise?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Last Monday. I was at the crossing on Hill Street and the light went from amber to red. And I just knew. That's when I understood they were steering everything."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"So the light changed, and in that moment you knew what it meant?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Exactly. It wasn't a normal red. It was a message."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Who do you think is behind it?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"The council. Red is the colour of the post, and the postman knows every route, so the routes are all mapped, the maps were in my dad's car when we went to Wales."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"I'd like to write down your exact words so the team hears it as you said it. 'Red is the colour of the post, and the postman knows every route.' Have I got that right?","m":[8]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Yes. Write it down. Someone should."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"How sure are you about the cars, out of ten?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Ten. A hundred."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Is there any other way of seeing it? Could the lights just be on a timer?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"You'd say that. You're not the one they're signalling."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"That's fair, I can't see it from where you're standing. It sounds lonely, being the only one who notices.","m":[11]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"It is. My sister thinks I'm making it up."},{"t":"2:25","band":"Voices","w":"D","s":"Some people under this much pressure hear voices or sounds when nobody's there. Has anything like that happened to you?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"They talk about me. All day."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me exactly what they say?","m":[5,8]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"'She's walking now. She's sitting now. She's eating the toast.' Like a commentary."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"So they say 'she', not 'you'? Is it one voice or more, and where do they seem to come from?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Two. A man and a woman, talking to each other about me. From the ceiling vent, clear as you."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting to live with. Do they ever tell you to do things? What do you do then?","m":[9,11]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"The woman says 'she has to warn them'. So I warn the drivers. Yesterday I went into the road to stop a bus. It had to brake."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"That could have been very dangerous for you. Do they ever tell you to hurt yourself or anyone else?","m":[9,10]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"No. Never that. Just warn them."},{"t":"3:45","band":"Thoughts and control","w":"D","s":"Some of my next questions may sound odd. I ask everyone, so nothing gets missed. Do you ever hear your own thoughts spoken out loud?","m":[1,6]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"No. Why would I?"},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Do you feel thoughts are being put into your mind, or taken out of it?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"No. They're mine."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Can other people know what you're thinking, without you saying it?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Yes. When I think, it goes out into the street. My thoughts go out like radio. That's how the cars find me."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"'Like radio.' I'll write that down. Do you ever feel something outside you controls your body, your actions or your feelings?","m":[8,7]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"No. I'm in charge of me. It's the cars that are controlled."},{"t":"4:40","band":"Risk and insight","w":"D","s":"If you left the ward now, what would you do? And have you had any thoughts about ending your life, or harming anyone?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Go back to Hill Street. People could die if nobody warns them. I'd never hurt anyone. I'm trying to save people."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"Could any of this be linked to your health, like stress or not sleeping?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"I'm not ill. I was just shouting. People shout. Let me out."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Being kept here when you don't feel ill must be really frustrating. I'm not dismissing that.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"So why am I locked up?"},{"t":"5:25","band":"Rights","w":"D","s":"You're on section 2, which lets us assess you for up to 28 days. You can appeal to a Tribunal in the first 14 days, and an independent advocate can help. The nurses will go through your rights again today.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"If I say they read my thoughts, you'll keep me longer."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"I understand that worry. What you've told me helps us pick treatment that gets you out sooner. Would you accept help with sleep, or with the voices?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Sleep, maybe. Not the voices. They're real."},{"t":"6:05","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've got it right. Since the light changed, you're certain the cars are controlled. Two voices comment on you, your thoughts go out like radio, and you went into the road to warn a bus. Fair?","m":[14,8]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Yes. That's it."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Because of the bus, I'll tell the nurse in charge now so staff can keep you safe. I'll take this to the team at half eleven and come back with the plan.","m":[14,10]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Fine. Just tell them I'm not crazy."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'll tell them exactly what you told me, in your words. Thanks for talking with me, Aisha.","m":[14]}]},"c-2-1-3":{"setup":"It is Wednesday 14:00 in the ward family room. Liam, 28, was admitted informally two days ago with persecutory beliefs. His brother Ryan, 31, is upset. Liam agrees you can talk but does not want his drug use mentioned. Take a collateral history.","role":{"who":"Mr Ryan Walsh, 31, male, the patient's older brother","open":"Before you start, nobody tells us anything! We found out he was here from his landlord.","manner":"Angry and on edge at first, then guilty and worn out. Softens quickly once his frustration is acknowledged and he feels listened to.","free":["Found out about the admission from the landlord","Liam has 'not been right' for months","He is exhausted from watching over him"],"ifasked":["Before: funny, sociable, five-a-side every week, team leader at a warehouse","About a year ago stopped football, said the lads talked about him","Lost his job in March, said his boss was spying; stopped answering calls from April","Not eating, flat neglected, up all night, taped over plug sockets","Uncle Mick had schizophrenia","Last Thursday Liam said he'd stab the man next door if he kept 'beaming things' at him","Only ordinary kitchen knives; Liam has barely left the flat","Liam once said he was 'tired of it all'"],"worries":["Is he on drugs? Just tell me straight.","How long is he in for?","Will Liam know I told you about the neighbour?"],"rules":["Pushes once for drug information; accepts a calm, non-defensive answer","Volunteers that friends say Liam smokes a lot of weed if asked what he has noticed","Mentions the threat only when asked about anything frightening or about safety","Voice breaks when describing sleeping on Liam's sofa; settles if validated"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms who he is, and acknowledges his frustration","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains what Liam agreed can be shared, simply and without being defensive","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about premorbid personality and functioning","d":"Content"},{"c":"Builds a dated timeline: withdrawal, job loss, sleep, eating and self-care","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about previous episodes and family history of mental illness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Receives drug information neutrally without confirming or denying anything","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about threats, named targets and access to weapons","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about suicide, self-harm and exploitation","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his guilt and exhaustion with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives general information about psychosis and informal admission","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the threat will be recorded and discussed with the consultant today","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers a carer's assessment, carer information and a family meeting with Liam's consent","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, gives a contact and safety net, and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Calms the brother, gets premorbid history, a dated decline, the uncle and the threat to the neighbour, keeps the drug use private, and offers carer support.","borderline":"Good rapport and history, but weapons and targets are not asked directly or carer support is vague.","fail":"Confirms the drug use, refuses to talk because of confidentiality, or never asks about threats."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Shah, one of the doctors looking after Liam. You must be his brother, Ryan? Thanks for coming in.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Yeah. And before you start, nobody tells us anything! We found out he was here from his landlord."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That must have been awful, finding out like that. I'm sorry. I'd like to put that right today.","m":[1,9]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Two days. Two days and no phone call."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"I understand. Liam has agreed that I can speak with you about how he's doing. A few things he's asked to keep private, and I have to respect that. But I can listen to anything you want to tell me, and I'd really value it.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Fine. So what's wrong with him?"},{"t":"1:00","band":"Before and timeline","w":"D","s":"He came in feeling that people are against him, and we're still assessing. You know him best. What was Liam like before all this?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Brilliant. Funny, sociable, five-a-side every week. Team leader at the warehouse."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"When did you first notice something was different?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"About a year ago. He stopped coming to football. Said the lads were talking about him."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"What came next?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"He lost his job in March. Said his boss was spying on him. Then from about April he stopped answering the phone."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"And recently? Sleep, eating, looking after himself?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"He looks like he hasn't eaten in weeks. Flat's a tip. He's up all night. He'd taped over the plug sockets."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Has anything like this happened before? And is there any family history of mental health problems?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Never him. Our uncle Mick had schizophrenia. Mum never talked about it. Is that what this is?"},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"It's too early to say, but that's really useful, and I'll make sure the team knows.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Is he on drugs? Just tell me straight."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"I can see why you'd want to know. Some things he's asked to keep between him and us, and I need to respect that. But I'm happy to hear anything you've noticed.","m":[6,2]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"His mates say he's been smoking a lot of weed. I found bags in the flat."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. That's helpful.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:00","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Has Liam said or done anything that frightened you, or worried you about anyone's safety?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Last week he said he'd stab the bloke next door if he kept beaming things at him."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; you've done the right thing. When did he say this?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Thursday. I was round his. He was shouting at the wall."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"Does he have any knives or weapons? Has he gone near the neighbour since?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Kitchen knives, normal stuff. He's barely left the flat."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Has he talked about harming himself or ending his life? Is anyone taking advantage of him, money or people at the flat?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"He said once he was tired of it all. That's it. Nobody goes round except me."},{"t":"4:05","band":"His feelings","w":"P","s":"I should've seen it coming. I told him to snap out of it."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Lots of families say the same. It's very hard to spot from the inside. You're here now, and what you've told me is exactly what we need.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"I've been sleeping on his sofa to watch him. I can't keep doing this."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting, and you clearly care about him a lot. You have a right to support for yourself too.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:40","band":"Explain","w":"P","s":"So what's the plan? How long is he in for?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"He's here informally, so he's agreed to stay while we assess and treat. I can't give a date yet. Usually we offer medicine, talking therapy and support for the family, and many people improve well.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"And what happens about the neighbour?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"I'll record it in his notes and discuss it with my consultant and the team today. We'll decide together whether anyone else needs to know. That's our job now, not yours.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Will he know I told you?"},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"We may need to talk with him about it, and I'll be careful how. Keeping everyone safe comes first.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:40","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"For you, there's a carer's assessment through the council, looking at what support you need. Can I refer you today? I'll also give you our carers' pack and Rethink Mental Illness details.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Please."},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"And with Liam's agreement, I'd like to invite you to a family meeting to go through the plan together.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"He'll say yes. He trusts me."},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can I check what you're taking away from today?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"You'll tell the consultant about the neighbour. I get a carer's assessment, the pack, and a family meeting if Liam agrees. And I won't hear everything."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"That's right. Here's the ward number; I'm your contact today. If you're worried about him or anyone's safety, ring us, or 999 in an emergency.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Thanks. That's more than anyone's told me."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming, Ryan. Please try to get some proper sleep tonight.","m":[13,9]}]},"c-2-1-4":{"setup":"It is Thursday 11:00. Darren, 41, has schizophrenia, has missed three depots and hasn't left his flat for two weeks. You visit with Sam, his care coordinator. He opens the door a crack. Engage him, assess mental state and risk, and decide next steps.","role":{"who":"Mr Darren Price, 41, male, the patient, talking through a half-open door","open":"Go away. I don't need you. I'm fine in here.","manner":"Whispering, suspicious, keeps glancing at the walls. Hostile if pushed; slowly talks more when given space and not argued with. Low and flat about food and money.","free":["Wants them to go away","Neighbours beam radiation through the walls","The foil on the windows keeps it out"],"ifasked":["Whispers through the sockets call him dirty and tell him not to trust anyone","Sleeps about two hours a night","Has not eaten properly for days; no money left","Electricity meter ran out two days ago","The knife by the door is 'for them, if they come through'","Sometimes thinks he'd be better off dead; no plan; would not leave his mum alone","Stopped the depot because his legs went stiff and he believes it poisons him","No visitors; nobody else uses the flat"],"worries":["Are you here to section me?","You're going to stab me with that needle again","I'm not going to hospital"],"rules":["Shuts the door if anyone moves towards entering","Explains the knife only if asked calmly about it","Accepts food help once he feels listened to","Gets quieter and more open when his fear is validated"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and Sam, explains the visit and asks permission to talk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Respects his refusal of entry without forcing, arguing or threatening","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Notices the knife by the door and asks about it calmly","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks open questions about the foil and his fears without challenging the beliefs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses sleep, food, money and utilities","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks briefly about voices and what they tell him","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts and plans","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about intent to harm the neighbours or anyone else","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks for exploitation: visitors, money taken, others using the flat","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores why he stopped the depot and offers to review side effects","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his fear and distress with empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Is honest about next steps: consultant today, AMHP and doctors, legal route if no access","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers practical help today: food, benefits, meter, mother updated with consent","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises, agrees when they return, gives a crisis number and leaves safely","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Patient doorstep engagement that finds the radiation beliefs, voices, starvation, the knife and passive death wish, and leaves with honest same-day escalation and food help.","borderline":"Good engagement, but risk to others or the knife is not explored, or escalation is vague.","fail":"Tries to enter, argues about the radiation, threatens sectioning, or leaves with no plan."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and engage","w":"D","s":"Hello, Darren. I'm Dr Mensah from the mental health team, and you know Sam. We've been worried because we haven't seen you. Can we talk for a few minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Go away. I don't need you. I'm fine in here."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"OK. We won't come in without your say. Can we just talk here at the door? I'll stay right here.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Keep your voice down. They're listening."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Sure, I'll keep it quiet. Who's listening?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Next door. Both sides. They're beaming radiation through the walls."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. I noticed the foil on the windows. Is that to do with it?","m":[4,11]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"The foil keeps it out. Mostly. Since the summer."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"You've worked hard to protect yourself. What does the radiation do to you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Burns my skin at night. Messes with my head. I can't think straight."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Do you hear them, or anyone, talking when nobody's there?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"They whisper through the sockets. Say I'm dirty. Tell me not to trust anyone. Like you."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"That must be awful to hear. Trust takes time, I get that. How are you sleeping?","m":[11,5]},{"t":"1:55","band":"Self-care","w":"P","s":"I don't. Couple of hours."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"And eating? When did you last have a proper meal?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"I haven't eaten in days. There's no money left. Dry cereal."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; that worries me. Is your electricity and gas still on?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Meter ran out. Two days ago."},{"t":"2:25","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Darren, I can see a knife by the door. Can you tell me about that?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"That's for them. If they come through."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about using it on the neighbours, or going round there?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"No. Only if they come in. I'm not stupid. I stay in."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Has anyone else been coming here? Anyone staying, borrowing money, or using the flat?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"No one comes. I don't open the door. Except to you, apparently."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"When things are this hard, some people think about ending their life. Have you had thoughts like that?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think I'd be better off dead than living like this. But I won't. Mum would be on her own."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to live with. Have you made a plan, or taken any steps to hurt yourself?","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"3:50","band":"Medication","w":"D","s":"You've missed your last few injections. What made it hard to come?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"You're going to stab me with that needle again. It poisons me. My legs went stiff."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry it felt like that. We're not giving anything today. Stiff legs is a side effect we can fix, with a lower dose or a different medicine.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Are you here to section me?"},{"t":"4:30","band":"Next steps","w":"D","s":"We're here because we care how you are. I'll be honest: I'm worried. You're not eating or sleeping, and the knife worries me. I'll speak to my consultant today.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"And then what?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"We may ask a mental health social worker and another doctor to see you, to decide what help you need. If we can't see you, there is a legal way to make sure you're seen. I'd much rather you choose to talk with us.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"I'm not going to hospital."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"I hear that. Talking with us gives you the biggest say. Can we get you some food today? Sam can bring a food parcel and sort an urgent benefits check and the meter.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Food would be good."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"Would you let us tell your mum we've seen you? She's been worried. And could the knife go in a drawer for now?","m":[13,3]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Tell her I'm alright. Maybe the drawer. If they don't come."},{"t":"6:00","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've got this right: no food, the meter's out, the radiation and whispers are worse, and you'd use the knife if they came in. Fair?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Yeah. That's about it."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Sam will be back this afternoon with food. If you feel unsafe, or like hurting yourself, ring the crisis number on this card, or 999. We'll come back today, maybe with the people I mentioned.","m":[14,12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Leave the card. Then go."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"We're going now. Thanks for talking to us, Darren. See you this afternoon.","m":[14]}]},"c-2-1-5":{"setup":"It is Friday 15:30 on the acute ward. Chloe, 26, with schizophrenia, is back on section 3 eight months after discharge, having been well on oral aripiprazole. Explore with her why she relapsed and agree a relapse prevention plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Chloe Bennett, 26, female, the patient, detained on section 3","open":"I know, I stopped the tablets. It's my fault I'm back.","manner":"Ashamed and self-blaming at first, then relieved when not judged. Flares up about her care coordinator leaving; warm once she feels heard.","free":["Stopped her tablets","Things were going fine after discharge","Blames herself for coming back"],"ifasked":["Stopped aripiprazole in April, three months before readmission","Aripiprazole made her pace all night, 'like ants in my legs'","Slight weight gain; no sedation, sexual or period problems","First signs: poor sleep, then feeling people at work talked about her, staying in","Mum noticed she went quiet and stopped replying; a friend noticed odd late-night texts","Boyfriend of five years left in May","Cannabis went from weekends to daily after the break-up","Care coordinator left and nobody replaced her for two months; one bad night in June thinking of an overdose, rang her mum instead"],"worries":["I'm not having a depot. That's for people who are forced.","How will I know it's starting again next time?","Will anyone actually follow me up this time?"],"rules":["Only names the restlessness if asked about side effects directly","Gets angry about the care coordinator gap; settles if the doctor agrees and apologises","Refuses the depot if pushed; considers it if offered as her choice","Becomes more open when not blamed"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and frames the talk as understanding, not blame","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Opens with an open question about what led to readmission","d":"Content"},{"c":"Maps a timeline: when tablets stopped, first changes, what others noticed","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about restlessness, weight, sedation and sexual side effects","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores cannabis use and its change without judgement","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about stressors: relationships, money, housing, work","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about contact with the community team and gaps in care","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her guilt, loss and anger with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains akathisia simply and that it can be treated","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers options including a long-acting injection, leaving the choice with her","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Identifies her early warning signs and agrees a written relapse prevention plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees dual-diagnosis referral, named care coordinator and contact within 72 hours under s117","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts now and since stopping","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back, safety-nets and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Non-blaming exploration that finds akathisia, the break-up, daily cannabis and the service gap, then a plan she owns with early warning signs and real follow-up.","borderline":"Finds adherence and cannabis but misses akathisia or the care gap, or the plan is the doctor's not hers.","fail":"Blames her for stopping, pushes a depot, or simply restarts the same tablets."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Chloe, I'm Dr Hughes, one of the ward doctors. I'd like to understand how you ended up coming back in, so we can plan a better discharge. This isn't about blame. Is that OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"I know, I stopped the tablets. It's my fault I'm back."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"Lots of people stop at some point, and there's usually a good reason. What do you think led up to it?","m":[8,2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"It was all going fine after I left. Then it wasn't."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Timeline","w":"D","s":"Let's go back a bit. When did you stop the aripiprazole?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"About April. So three months before I came in."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"What made the tablets hard to take?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"They made me pace all night. I couldn't sit still. So I stopped."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"That sounds horrible. Was it an inner restlessness, like you had to keep moving?","m":[4,8]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Like ants in my legs. I'd walk round the flat at three in the morning."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Did they cause anything else? Weight gain, sleepiness, problems with sex or periods?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Bit of weight. Not loads. Nothing else."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"That restlessness has a name, akathisia. It's a real side effect, not you being difficult, and it can be treated.","m":[9]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Nobody told me that. I thought I was going mad again."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you went through that on your own. After you stopped, what changed first? And what did others notice?","m":[8,3]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Not sleeping. Then I felt people at work were talking about me. Mum said I went quiet and stayed in my room. My friend said I sent weird texts at night."},{"t":"2:20","band":"Other pressures","w":"D","s":"Was anything else going on then? Money, housing, work, relationships?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"My boyfriend left in May. Five years. That's when it got bad."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"That's a big loss, on top of everything else.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Yeah. It was."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Lots of people use cannabis or alcohol to cope. Were you using anything, and did it change?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Cannabis helps me. It's the only thing that calms me down. After he left, it went from weekends to every day."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"I can see why, especially after the break-up. It also seems to bring the voices back quicker. Would you see a worker who helps people cut down, without judging?","m":[5,12]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Maybe. If they don't lecture me."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Who were you seeing from the community team?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"My care coordinator left and nobody replaced her. Two months! I rang once and nobody called back."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"You're right, and that gap shouldn't have happened. I'm sorry.","m":[8,7]},{"t":"3:40","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"How are you in yourself now? Any thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life, now or over the summer?","m":[13]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Not now. I had a bad night in June. I thought about taking all my tablets. I rang my mum instead."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad you rang her. That's a strength we can build into the plan.","m":[13,8]},{"t":"4:10","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"For the restlessness, we could lower the dose, add a medicine called propranolol, or think about a different antipsychotic.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"And the depot? I'm not having one. That's for people who are forced."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"I hear that. An injection isn't a punishment, and nobody is deciding it for you today. Some people choose it so they don't have to remember tablets. There's a monthly aripiprazole one, and the dose can be lowered if you feel restless.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"I'll think about it. I'm not saying yes."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"That's fine. It's your decision. I'll give you a leaflet.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:00","band":"Relapse plan","w":"P","s":"How will I know it's starting again next time?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"That's the heart of this. Your early signs sound like poor sleep, feeling talked about, staying in your room and texting at night. Is that right?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"And not replying to Mum. She always notices that first."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Good, we'll add that. Let's write those down with your named nurse, what you'll do and who to call, and share it with your mum if you'd like.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Yeah. She should have it."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Before you leave, you'll have a named care coordinator and someone will see you within three days of discharge. Your aftercare is free under section 117.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Promise?"},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"I'll write it into the discharge plan and check it's booked.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Sort the restless thing. Think about the injection. See someone about weed. Write my warning signs down. Proper care coordinator, seen within three days."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"That's it. If those signs come back, or you feel like hurting yourself, tell your nurse now, or after discharge ring your care coordinator or the crisis line.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"OK. Thanks for not lecturing me."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being so open, Chloe. We'll pick this up at ward round on Monday.","m":[14]}]},"c-2-1-6":{"setup":"It is Monday 16:00 in the early intervention clinic. Jake, 23, has had psychotic symptoms for eight months and the team has diagnosed schizophrenia. He and his mother, 50, have come to hear the diagnosis. Explain it and answer their questions.","role":{"who":"Mrs Karen Ellis, 50, female, his mother; her son Jake, 23, also speaks (one person voices both, each line starting with the speaker's name)","open":"Mother: Is this our fault, and will his younger sister get it too?","manner":"Mother anxious and quick to blame herself, tearful about his childhood, then relieved. Jake quiet and guarded, sinks when he hears the word, brightens when talk turns to work and friends.","free":["Mother: is this our fault?","Mother: will his younger sister get it?","Jake: voices and feeling watched since about March"],"ifasked":["They thought it was stress from his job","A nurse mentioned 'psychosis'; Jake looked up schizophrenia online","They fear 'split personality' and violence from news stories","Jake wants to get back to work and see his friends","Jake had suicidal thoughts a few weeks ago, none now","Jake drives","His sister is 17"],"worries":["Mother: does this mean he's dangerous?","Jake: will I have to take tablets for life?","Jake: so my life's over?"],"rules":["Mother denies the diagnosis once ('just stress'); accepts it if linked to his symptoms","Mother becomes tearful about his childhood; settles if her guilt is addressed directly","Jake withdraws if given jargon; engages if asked what matters to him","Both can repeat the key points back if the explanation was clear"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and checks Jake is happy for his mother to be present","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what they already understand and what they want to know today","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores what worries them about the word schizophrenia","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives the diagnosis clearly, linked to his own symptoms, in small chunks","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains causes as a mix of genes, brain development and stress or cannabis, without jargon","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tells the mother clearly it is not her fault, with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives balanced genetic risk for the sister in rough numbers with protective advice","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Corrects the violence and split-personality myths accurately","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Outlines treatment: medication, CBT for psychosis, family sessions, work support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives an honest, hopeful outlook naming what is in his favour","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates Jake's distress and asks what matters to him","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts and gives a crisis route","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back from both","d":"Close"},{"c":"Covers DVLA, written information, carer support and a follow-up date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Starts with their understanding, names schizophrenia plainly, lifts the mother's guilt, gives fair sibling risk and real hope, and checks they can repeat it.","borderline":"Accurate content but delivered as a lecture, or teach-back and safety questions are missed.","fail":"Jargon, false reassurance or a bleak prognosis, wrong genetic figures, or ignoring the mother's guilt."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Rahman from the early intervention team. Jake, are you happy for your mum to be here while we talk?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Jake: Yeah. I want her here."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Before I say anything, what do you both understand about what's been happening?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:25","band":"Their view","w":"P","s":"Jake: I've been hearing voices and thinking people were watching me. Since about March. Mum thought it was my job."},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Mother: He was working all hours. And I need to ask: is this our fault, and will his younger sister get it too?"},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"Those are really important questions, and I'll answer both. First, has anyone mentioned a word or a diagnosis?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Jake: The nurse said 'psychosis'. I looked up schizophrenia online. I hope it's not that."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"What worries you about that word?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Jake: Split personality. Dangerous. That guy in the news."},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Mother: Does it mean he's dangerous? You read about it in the papers."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"That's a very common fear, and the news makes it worse. Schizophrenia isn't a split personality. Most people with it are never violent, and they're more likely to be victims of crime.","m":[8,3]},{"t":"1:35","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"The team's diagnosis is schizophrenia. I know that's a hard word to hear.","m":[4,11]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Mother: He's not schizophrenic. He was just stressed from his job."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Stress definitely played a part. We use the word because the voices and the feeling of being watched have lasted eight months. Naming it means we can offer treatment that works.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Mother: So what actually is it?"},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Put simply, the brain's way of filtering and making sense of things gets disrupted. So ordinary sounds can become voices, and ordinary things feel threatening. Does that fit, Jake?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Jake: Yeah. Like everything was about me."},{"t":"2:25","band":"Causes and guilt","w":"D","s":"Now your first question. No, it isn't your fault. Parenting doesn't cause schizophrenia.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Mother: I keep thinking I missed something. When he was little..."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"I can see how much you've been carrying that. It comes from a mix of genes, how the brain develops, and stresses like cannabis or big life pressures. Nothing you did caused it. You being here is one of the best things for his recovery.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Mother: Thank you. I needed to hear that."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"About his sister. Her risk is higher than average, but most brothers and sisters never get it. Roughly one in ten, compared with about one in a hundred for everyone else.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Mother: One in ten still sounds a lot."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"It's natural to worry. Put the other way, about nine in ten stay well. Avoiding cannabis and getting help early if her sleep or mood change both help.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","band":"Treatment and outlook","w":"P","s":"Jake: Will I have to take tablets for the rest of my life?"},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Not necessarily. We usually advise at least a year or two once you're well, then review together. You'll be part of every decision.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Jake: I don't want just pills."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"There's more. A talking therapy called CBT for psychosis, family sessions for you, your mum and your sister, and a work specialist to help you back into a job.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Jake: So my life's over. No job, no girlfriend, nothing."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"I can hear how heavy this feels. It isn't over. Many people with this diagnosis work, have relationships and live full lives. What matters most to you?","m":[11,10]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Jake: Getting back to work. Seeing my mates."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"Good goals. You've got things on your side: help came early, you were doing well before, and your family's behind you. Some people have one episode and recover well; others have ups and downs. Sticking with treatment improves the odds.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:50","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"Jake, news like this can leave people feeling hopeless, even thinking about ending their life. Have you had thoughts like that?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Jake: A few weeks ago, a bit. Not now. Not since the voices got quieter."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. If those thoughts come back, ring the team's duty number, or 111 and choose option 2, day or night.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"Mother: Does he need to tell anyone? Work?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Work only if he chooses, and he may be entitled to adjustments. Jake, as you drive, you do need to tell the DVLA. They usually review once you've been well and stable for at least three months.","m":[14]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Jake: OK. I'll do it this week."},{"t":"5:50","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I've given you a lot. Mrs Ellis, how would you explain this to your daughter tonight?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Mother: It's an illness in how the brain makes sense of things. Nobody's fault. Her risk is a bit higher, but most siblings are fine, and she should keep off cannabis."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. Jake, what's the plan in your words?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"Jake: Keep taking the medicine, start the therapy, tell the DVLA, see the work person."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Here's a leaflet on schizophrenia and one for carers, and Mrs Ellis, you can have a carer's assessment. Let's meet again in two weeks for any new questions.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Mother: Thank you. This is more hopeful than I expected."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"There's real reason for hope. See you both in two weeks.","m":[14]}]},"c-2-1-7":{"setup":"It is Wednesday 10:30 on the acute ward. Priya, 30, has schizophrenia and has improved on olanzapine but gained 8 kg in two months. She has relapsed twice after stopping tablets. Discuss antipsychotic options with her, including a long-acting injection.","role":{"who":"Ms Priya Nair, 30, female, the patient, an informal inpatient","open":"It's helped. The voices have gone. But I've put on 8 kilos. I hate how I look. I'm stopping it.","manner":"Upset and determined to stop at first, tearful about her body. Wary of injections. Becomes engaged and practical once her priorities are put first.","free":["Olanzapine has stopped the voices","Gained 8 kg in two months and hates how she looks","Wants to stop olanzapine"],"ifasked":["Relapsed twice after stopping tablets suddenly","Sleeps until 11 on olanzapine and feels like a zombie","Periods are normal","She and her partner plan to try for a baby next year","Has a contraceptive implant fitted last year","Fears an injection means being controlled or a 'problem patient', dislikes needles"],"worries":["Can I just take olanzapine when I feel bad?","Will the weight come off if I switch?","How often is the injection, and does it hurt?","What if I say no?"],"rules":["Threatens to stop olanzapine until the doctor acknowledges the weight gain","Mentions baby plans only when asked about periods, sex or what matters to her","Bristles if the injection is pushed; asks practical questions if told it is her choice","Agrees to try oral aripiprazole but will not decide on the injection today"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and asks an open question about her experience of olanzapine","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Advises against stopping suddenly, linked to her past relapses","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what matters most to her in a medicine before comparing options","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what happened when she stopped tablets before","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about contraception and pregnancy plans","d":"Content"},{"c":"Compares two or three antipsychotics on weight, prolactin, sedation and movement effects in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the injection: how often, where, oral overlap and side effects","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Makes clear the injection is her choice and she can change her mind","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explores her worries about injections, including feeling controlled","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Agrees a cross-taper plan and what to report during the switch","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Sets a dated metabolic monitoring schedule with dietitian support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Acknowledges her distress about weight with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives a leaflet, a decision date, and records the shared decision","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Puts her priorities first, compares options fairly, explains the injection honestly as a choice, and agrees a safe switch with monitoring and contraception covered.","borderline":"Sound pharmacology but led by the doctor, or pregnancy plans and monitoring are missed.","fail":"Pushes a depot, lets her stop olanzapine abruptly, or ignores pregnancy plans when choosing a drug."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Priya, I'm Dr Evans, one of the ward doctors. Your consultant asked me to talk through your medication with you. What has it been like taking olanzapine?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"It's helped. The voices have gone. But I've put on 8 kilos. I hate how I look. I'm stopping it."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"That's a big change in two months, and it's completely reasonable to be upset.","m":[13]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"None of my clothes fit. I can't look in the mirror."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard, just when you're feeling better in yourself. What happened the last times you stopped tablets?","m":[13,4]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"I ended up back in here. Twice. Both times I just stopped."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"So stopping suddenly is when things go wrong. Please don't stop today. Let's find something kinder on weight instead.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Can I just take olanzapine when I feel bad?"},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"I see why that's tempting. It works by preventing symptoms, so on and off is when people relapse. The aim is a medicine you can live with every day.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:30","band":"What matters","w":"D","s":"What matters most to you in a medicine? Weight, energy, periods, sex life, not having to remember tablets?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Weight, first. And not feeling like a zombie. I sleep till eleven on this."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Anything about periods or sex drive?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Periods are fine. Actually, we're thinking about trying for a baby next year."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; that really matters. Are you using contraception now?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"The implant. Put in last year."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Good. Let's choose a medicine with a baby in mind, and plan it with us and your GP before you try. Keep the implant until then.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"Can I compare a few options? Olanzapine works well, but it often causes weight gain and sleepiness, as you've found.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Tell me about it."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Aripiprazole causes much less weight gain, isn't usually sedating, and doesn't raise prolactin, a hormone that can affect periods and fertility. Its main downside is feeling restless early on.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"And the others?"},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Risperidone or paliperidone sit in the middle for weight, but can raise prolactin, so periods may stop and fertility drop. They can cause stiffness too. With a baby planned, I'd lean away from those.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Will the weight come off if I switch?"},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Often some does, especially with diet and activity support, but not always all of it. Aripiprazole gives you the best chance.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:30","band":"Injection","w":"D","s":"You mentioned remembering tablets. Have you heard of a long-acting injection?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"An injection? That feels like you're controlling me."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"I understand why it might feel like that. What worries you about it?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Like I'm a problem patient. And needles. And being stuck on it."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"It's your choice; we wouldn't give it unless you agree. Some people like being free of daily tablets. And you can change your mind and stop or switch later.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"How often would I need it, and does it hurt?"},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"With aripiprazole, once a month, into the arm or buttock, like a vaccine. It may be sore for a day or two, and we'd swap sides. For two weeks after the first one you'd still take tablets while the level builds.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Does it have side effects?"},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"The same as the tablets: maybe restlessness, poor sleep or nausea. We'd only start it once you're settled on the tablets, so we know you tolerate it.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:55","band":"Plan","w":"P","s":"OK. I'll try the aripiprazole tablets. I'm not sure about the injection yet."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"That sounds sensible. We'd start aripiprazole 10 mg each morning and lower the olanzapine step by step over two to four weeks. The pharmacist will write the steps down.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"What if I feel weird during the switch?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Tell the nurses straight away, especially restlessness, poor sleep, or the voices coming back. We can slow it down.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Will you keep checking my weight?"},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"Yes. Weekly for six weeks, then weight, blood pressure, sugar and cholesterol at twelve weeks, and yearly after that. The dietitian can see you this week too.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:55","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Start aripiprazole, come off olanzapine slowly, tell you if I'm restless. Weekly weigh-ins. Keep the implant. Maybe the injection, monthly, my choice."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"That's it exactly. Here's a leaflet on the injection. Have a think and let me know by Friday.","m":[12,14]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"And if I say no?"},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Then we carry on with tablets, and that's fine. I'll record what we discussed, that you understood the options, and that the decision is yours.","m":[8,14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"OK. Thanks. I feel like I actually get a say."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You do. I'll see you Friday, Priya.","m":[14]}]},"c-2-1-8":{"setup":"You are the ward doctor on an acute adult ward. A nurse bleeps you about Jade, 24, on Section 3, who started haloperidol 10 days ago and cannot keep still. Assess and examine her movements, explain the hormone symptoms, and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Jade Lewis, 24, female inpatient on Section 3 with schizophrenia","open":"I can't sit. I've tried. I just want to jump out of my skin.","manner":"Pacing, restless and tearful. Irritable that staff call her agitated. Settles and engages once the feeling is named as a side effect and she is involved in choices.","free":["Restlessness began a few days after haloperidol started","She cannot sit through meals and barely sleeps","Periods have stopped and milk is leaking from her breasts","Nurses have given her extra doses for 'agitation'"],"ifasked":["Restlessness is worst when sitting or lying, mainly in her legs","Neck twisted on day two and settled after an injection","Mild hand tremor","Last night she thought she would rather be dead, but has no plan or intent","No thoughts of harming others","Last period about seven weeks ago; no sex for months","No headaches or visual change","No fever, sweating, confusion or muscle pain; no asthma"],"worries":["Is something seriously wrong because she is leaking milk?","Will her periods come back? She wants children one day","Why do nurses keep giving her more of the same drug?"],"rules":["Cannot sit still for more than a minute; stands up during the talk","Says she will refuse haloperidol; softens if offered a real say in the plan","Becomes tearful about fertility; settles if the doctor acknowledges it","Answers risk questions honestly if asked kindly and directly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, are you Jade? I'm Dr Patel, one of the ward doctors. The nurses said you're feeling awful. Please don't feel you have to sit. We can walk and talk, or stand, whatever's easier.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"I can't sit. I've tried. I just want to jump out of my skin."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That sounds horrible. Tell me what it feels like inside.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"0:25","band":"Their story","w":"P","s":"Like there's an engine in me that won't switch off. My legs have to move. If I sit at dinner I have to get up after a minute."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"When did this start?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"About a week ago. A few days after they started the new tablets."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"That's haloperidol, started ten days ago. Is it worse when you sit or lie down?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Sitting is the worst. Lying in bed at night, my legs are going the whole time. I've barely slept."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Any stiffness, shaking hands, or your neck or eyes pulling to one side?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"My hands shake a bit. My neck went funny on day two, but the nurse gave me something and it stopped."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Okay. Any movements of your mouth, tongue or face you can't control?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"No, I don't think so."},{"t":"1:25","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Some people tell me this feeling is so bad they think about hurting themselves, or they snap at people. Has anything like that crossed your mind?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Honestly? Last night I thought I'd rather be dead than feel like this. And the nurses keep saying I'm agitated and giving me more of the same stuff."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad you told me. That sounds unbearable. Have you had any thought of acting on it, or a plan?","m":[4,11]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"No. I wouldn't. I just want it to stop. I'm not going to hurt anyone either."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Thank you. You're right to question the extra doses. I think this is the medicine causing it, not your illness, so more of the same would make it worse. Any fever, sweating, feeling confused or muscles aching?","m":[6,8]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"2:25","band":"Examine","w":"D","s":"Good. Your temperature and pulse this afternoon were normal too. Could I look at how you move? Would you sit down for a moment, then stand still for two minutes while I watch?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"I'll try. See, I'm already shifting about."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"I can see you rocking and crossing your legs. Now just stand still if you can.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"I can't. My feet keep going."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"You're shifting from foot to foot, and you've told me how strong it feels inside. That fits moderate to marked akathisia on the scale we use. Can I check your arms for stiffness and watch you walk?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Fine. My hands are shaking now, look."},{"t":"3:15","band":"Hormones","w":"D","s":"There's a mild tremor and a little stiffness at the wrist, no rigidity, and your walk is fine. That's my baseline for tomorrow. You also mentioned your periods. When was your last one?","m":[3,6,7]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"About seven weeks ago. And there's milk coming from my breasts. Why am I leaking milk? Is something seriously wrong with me?"},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"That must be frightening. Could you be pregnant at the moment?","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"I don't think so. I've not been with anyone for months."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"We'll still do a test to be sure. Any headaches, or trouble with your vision?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"4:00","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Okay. Let me explain what I think is happening. The restlessness has a name, akathisia. It's a common side effect of haloperidol. It isn't you getting more unwell, and we can treat it.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"So I'm not going mad?"},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"No. The milk and missed periods are most likely from a hormone called prolactin, which this medicine pushes up. Today I'll send prolactin, a pregnancy test, thyroid, kidney tests and a muscle enzyme test.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:30","band":"Plan","w":"P","s":"I'm not taking that tablet again. Not tonight, not ever."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"I understand why. Let's not push on with something that's hurting you. You're on a section, but your views really matter in which medicine we choose. Can we plan it together?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Okay. But will I ever get my periods back? I want kids one day."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"In most women periods come back once prolactin falls. I'm glad you told me about wanting children; it matters to the choice. One option is to lower haloperidol, another is to switch to aripiprazole, which is gentler on both problems. I'll discuss it with your consultant tomorrow.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"The gentler one sounds better."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"I'll put that forward. For tonight, a tablet called propranolol often eases this restlessness. Do you have asthma?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"No, never."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Then I'll start a small dose, ten milligrams, and the nurses will check your pulse and blood pressure before each one. Just so I can check I've explained it clearly, can you tell me what the plan is?","m":[10,12]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"The jumpy feeling is the tablets, not me. Blood tests and a pregnancy test. The new tablet tonight, and maybe changing to the gentler one."},{"t":"5:55","band":"Safety and close","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll ask the nurses not to give extra haloperidol for restlessness, and someone will check on you more often tonight. If the feeling gets too much, or you have thoughts of hurting yourself, tell them straight away.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"What if I get a temperature or go stiff?"},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Tell a nurse at once. That's rare but needs checking quickly, and I'm here overnight.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thanks for actually listening."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me how bad it's been. So: it's a side effect, we're treating it tonight, bloods today, and your consultant will review the medicine on tomorrow's ward round. Anything else you'd like to ask?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. I'll try the propranolol."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks her name and lets her stand or walk while talking","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits inner restlessness, its onset after haloperidol and that sitting makes it worse","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about stiffness, tremor, neck or eye spasms and face or tongue movements","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about urges to harm herself or others because of the restlessness","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Watches her seated and standing for two minutes and rates it on the Barnes scale","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for NMS: fever, confusion, sweating, muscle pain; checks observations and rigidity","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks last period, pregnancy chance, breast discharge, headaches and vision changes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names akathisia as a treatable medicine side effect, not her illness worsening","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains raised prolactin and requests prolactin, pregnancy test, TFTs, U&E and CK","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a dose cut or aripiprazole switch, and propranolol after checking for asthma","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates her distress, her refusal and her worry about having children","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking her to explain the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets: no PRN antipsychotic for restlessness, higher observation, report fever or stiffness","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes clearly: summary, when she will be reviewed, invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Names akathisia, rates it with a structured look, asks about suicide and NMS, excludes pregnancy and agrees a shared drug plan with clear safety advice.","borderline":"Recognises akathisia and prolactin but misses the suicide question, the pregnancy test or a clear instruction about PRN antipsychotics.","fail":"Treats restlessness as agitation, offers more antipsychotic or procyclidine, or ignores amenorrhoea and galactorrhoea."}},"c-2-1-9":{"setup":"You are a doctor in a CMHT clinic. Michael, 32, has schizophrenia and still hears voices after olanzapine and risperidone. His consultant asks you to discuss clozapine with him and his mother and agree where it could safely be started.","role":{"who":"Mr Michael Brown, 32, outpatient with schizophrenia; his mother Mrs Angela Brown, 60, is with him","open":"Yeah, she knows everything anyway.","manner":"Michael is wary and blunt about needles and risks; warms up when asked what he wants. Angela is tired and sad, becomes hopeful and practical once included.","free":["Voices are with him all day despite two medicines taken properly","He has read clozapine can kill you","He wants to sleep and go back to the gym","He does not want to go into hospital"],"ifasked":["Smokes about twenty cigarettes a day","No heart problems, fits or diabetes","Opens his bowels about every other day","Lives with his mother, who is home most days","He does not drive; the clinic is a bus ride away","Willing to have weekly bloods if the reason is explained"],"worries":["Can clozapine kill me?","Weekly needles for months?","Can I still smoke?","What if I forget doses?"],"rules":["Angela becomes tearful about years of suffering; settles if her effort is recognised","Michael pushes back on blood tests; accepts them once the colour system is explained","Asks to think it over at the end; accepts follow-up from his care coordinator"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Shah, one of the doctors in the team. You must be Michael, and you're his mum?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Yes, I'm Angela. I've come to support him."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming. Michael, Dr Evans asked me to talk about a medicine called clozapine. Are you happy for your mum to stay and hear about your health?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Yeah, she knows everything anyway."},{"t":"0:30","band":"Their view","w":"D","s":"Great. Before I say anything, what have you heard about clozapine?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"I've read it can kill you. Why would I take that?"},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"It's right to ask that, and it's a scary thing to read. I'll be honest about the risks in a minute. First, if the voices got quieter, what would you most like to change?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Sleep properly. Maybe go back to the gym. They go on at me all day."},{"t":"1:10","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. You've taken olanzapine and risperidone properly, each for a good while, and the voices haven't settled. When two medicines haven't worked, clozapine is the one most likely to help.","m":[3,10]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"How likely, though?"},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Somewhere between a third and two-thirds of people in your position improve clearly, sometimes a lot. We give it a fair trial of a few months to know.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"I've watched him suffer for years. Nothing works."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"That's clearly been really hard for you, Angela. Clozapine is different from what he's had, and your help will matter a lot. Michael, can I check a few things first?","m":[10]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Do you have any heart problems, fits, diabetes, or trouble with your bowels?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"No. I go about every other day."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"And do you smoke?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"About twenty a day."},{"t":"2:20","band":"Risks and monitoring","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Now the risks. Rarely, clozapine lowers the infection-fighting white cells. So we do a blood test weekly for eighteen weeks, then every two weeks until a year, then monthly.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Weekly needles for months? I'm not a pin cushion."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"It is a lot, I agree. The results come back green, amber or red. Green means carry on. Amber means we test more often. Red means we stop clozapine straight away to keep you safe.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Okay. What else?"},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"In the first two months, it can rarely inflame the heart. Warning signs are chest pain, breathlessness, a racing heart or fever. It also slows the gut, and severe constipation can be dangerous. And at higher doses it can cause fits.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Constipation? That's serious?"},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"It can be, so we'll start a laxative from day one and ask about your bowels every visit. Common nuisances are sleepiness, drooling at night and weight gain. We can help with all of those.","m":[5,8]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Can I still have a smoke?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Yes, but tell us before you cut down or stop, even switching to vaping. Smoke speeds up how fast clozapine is cleared, so stopping can push levels up quickly and we'd lower the dose.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"What if I forget a couple of days?"},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"If you miss more than two days, don't restart your usual dose. Call us, because we need to start again low and build up.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:20","band":"Where to start","w":"P","s":"Can I start it at home? I don't want to go on a ward."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"I hear that. Some people start at home. It means a nurse visiting every day for two or three weeks to check pulse, blood pressure and temperature before your dose, plus weekly bloods. Who's at home with you?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Me. I'm there most days and I don't drive, but the clinic is a bus ride."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"That really helps. You're physically well and you'd have your mum. I think a home start is safe, with daily home treatment visits. If anything worries us, we'd bring you in. How does that sound?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Better than hospital, yeah."},{"t":"5:15","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Good. Before we start, we'd do bloods, an ECG, weight and a heart blood test, and register you with the clozapine monitoring service. Just so I know I've been clear, Michael, what are the two rules about smoking and missed doses?","m":[9,11]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Tell you before I stop smoking. If I miss more than two days, ring you, don't just restart."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Angela, what would make you ring us the same day?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Fever or sore throat. Chest pain or breathless. And if he hasn't been to the toilet for a few days."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"Spot on. Three days without a bowel movement, ring us. At night or at weekends, call the crisis line or 111. If he has chest pain or can't breathe, call 999.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Okay. I feel a bit better about it."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"So, clozapine is the best next step, started at home with daily checks and weekly bloods. I'll give you a booklet and a red-flag card, and talk to Dr Evans today about baseline tests. What questions do you have?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Can I think about it over the weekend?"},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Of course. Your care coordinator will call you on Monday, and you can ring sooner with any questions.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks he is happy for his mother to stay and hear his details","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what he knows about clozapine and what he hopes would change","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains clozapine is offered after two failed antipsychotics, with honest odds of benefit","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains low white cells, the blood test schedule and what green, amber and red mean","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names myocarditis, severe constipation and seizures with the warning signs of each","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about heart problems, fits, diabetes, bowel habit and smoking","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives the smoking rule and the 48-hour missed-dose rule","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Mentions common effects: sleepiness, drooling, weight gain, and how they are managed","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees home or ward start with daily pulse, BP and temperature checks arranged","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his fear and his mother's exhaustion","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking him or his mother to repeat key rules","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets: same-day contact for fever, sore throat, chest pain, breathlessness, no bowels in 3 days","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes with a summary, written information, next steps and a chance for questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gives an honest reason and odds for clozapine, covers blood monitoring, myocarditis, constipation, smoking and missed doses, and agrees a safe start with teach-back.","borderline":"Explains blood monitoring well but misses constipation or smoking, or agrees a home start without daily observations.","fail":"Talks only about neutropenia, lectures without checking concerns, or ignores the mother and the setting for titration."}},"c-2-1-10":{"setup":"You are a doctor at the Early Intervention in Psychosis team. Ryan, 21, has first-episode schizophrenia; his voices are controlled on aripiprazole but he has withdrawn. His father rang angry. Assess Ryan, then discuss psychosocial treatment with them both.","role":{"who":"Mr Ryan Clarke, 21, male EIP patient; his father Mr Gary Clarke, 52, joins later","open":"Whatever. He's the one who's angry.","manner":"Ryan is flat, monosyllabic and guarded, opening up when asked about music. Gary arrives angry and blaming; softens when listened to and when someone asks about him.","free":["Stays in his room most of the day","Stopped his music technology course","Voices are much quieter","Father says the team is doing nothing"],"ifasked":["Gets up around midday","Mood is flat; sometimes thinks 'what's the point' but has no intent and names his mum as a reason not to","Slightly sleepy in the mornings; no stiffness or restlessness","Quiet voices now and then, not distressing","Avoids people from college because they saw him unwell","No cannabis since his admission","Would consider a part-time return to college"],"worries":["Is he just lazy?","What is the team actually doing?","Will he ever be like he was?"],"rules":["Ryan refuses therapy at first; agrees to try one session if it is goal-focused","Ryan asks that his 'what's the point' comment is kept private","Gary becomes quieter and tearful when asked about his own needs","Gary keeps pushing until a concrete plan is offered"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Ryan, I'm Dr Okoro, one of the doctors in the EIP team. Thanks for coming in. Your dad's here too. I'd like to talk with you on your own first, then we can decide together what to share with him. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Whatever. He's the one who's angry."},{"t":"0:25","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"Fair enough. This is about you, though. What does a normal day look like for you now?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"I get up about midday. Stay in my room. Phone, telly. That's it."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"And before you became unwell, what did you enjoy?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Football. College, I was doing music tech. I used to be good at stuff. Now I'm nothing."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful. Losing things you were good at is a big loss. How's your mood been, most days?","m":[3,11]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Flat. Not sad exactly. I just can't be bothered with anything."},{"t":"1:10","band":"Rule out other causes","w":"D","s":"Do you ever feel hopeless, or think about ending your life?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think, what's the point. But I wouldn't do anything. I wouldn't do that to my mum."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being open. If those thoughts ever get stronger, I'd want you to tell us. Does the aripiprazole make you sleepy, stiff, or unable to keep still?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Bit sleepy in the morning. Nothing else."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"And the voices. Are they still there, even quietly?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Now and then, quiet. They don't bother me much."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Is staying in partly to avoid something, like people or places?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Kind of. I don't want to see people from college. They saw me when I was ill."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"That makes sense. Do you use cannabis or anything else at the moment?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"No. Not since hospital."},{"t":"2:25","band":"Explain and options","w":"D","s":"Well done, that really helps recovery. Ryan, the voices are much better. What's left sounds like what we call negative symptoms: low energy, low drive, pulling back. They're part of the illness and they can improve.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"So it's not just me being lazy?"},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"No, it's not laziness. Two things help. One is CBT for psychosis, at least sixteen sessions with one of our psychologists, working on goals you choose.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"I don't want to talk to some therapist. What's the point?"},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Fair enough to feel that. It's not just going over the past. It's practical, like getting back to college. Would you try one session and decide after?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Maybe. If it's about college."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"It can be. We also have an employment and education worker who could help you plan a gradual return to your music course. Would that be a goal for the next three months?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Maybe part-time."},{"t":"3:45","band":"With his father","w":"D","s":"Part-time music tech from next term. I'll refer you to both today. Before your dad joins, what are you happy for me to tell him?","m":[6,10]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"The plan's fine. Don't tell him what I said about what's the point."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Okay. I won't share that. If I ever thought you were at risk, I'd talk to you first. Let's bring him in.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"You lot are doing nothing. He just rots in his room."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Mr Clarke, I can hear how frustrated and worried you are. Watching your son pull away must be really hard. Ryan's agreed I can share the plan.","m":[11,6]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Go on then. Is he just being lazy? Should I push him harder?"},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"It isn't laziness. The voices have settled, but the illness also affects energy and drive. We call those negative symptoms. Pushing hard often backfires. Small regular steps that Ryan chooses work better.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"So what are you actually doing about it?"},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"Ryan's agreed to try CBT for psychosis, and we'll help him plan a return to college. I'd also like to offer family sessions, at least ten over a few months, with Ryan there. They help families cope and reduce relapse.","m":[8,9,10]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Family sessions. Right. I'd do that."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Caring for someone is tiring too. I'd like to offer you a carer's assessment through the local council, and details of our carers' group.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Nobody's ever asked about me before."},{"t":"5:45","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Then I'm glad we have. Ryan, can you tell your dad what we've agreed?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Therapy, try one session. Help to go back to college part-time. And family sessions."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"Spot on. Mr Clarke, does that make sense?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Yeah. It's more than I knew about."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"If either of you is worried about Ryan's safety or a relapse, ring the team, or the crisis line out of hours. I'll write the plan down for you both.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"When do we hear about it?"},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Your care coordinator will ring this week with dates, and I'll see Ryan in four weeks to see how it's going. Any other questions?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks, doc."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and sees the son alone first, explaining why","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores his typical day, lost interests and what he used to enjoy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, hopelessness and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about sedation, stiffness and restlessness from aripiprazole","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks for residual voices, avoidance and cannabis or other drug use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees with the son what may be shared with his father","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains negative symptoms to the father in plain words, not laziness","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers CBT for psychosis, at least 16 sessions, with a named referral","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers family intervention, at least 10 sessions, including the son","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees one concrete goal with the son, such as supported return to college","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Acknowledges the father's anger and the son's sense of loss","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers the father a carer's assessment and carer support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks both understand the plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives crisis contacts and a clear next appointment","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Rules out depression, side effects, voices and cannabis, keeps Ryan's confidence, explains negative symptoms, and offers CBTp, family intervention, a concrete goal and carer support.","borderline":"Offers the right therapies but skips secondary causes or does not agree what may be shared with the father.","fail":"Labels it laziness or only changes medication, shares Ryan's private comments, or argues with the father."}},"c-2-1-11":{"setup":"You are the ward doctor on an acute adult ward after the morning ward round. Sophie, 28, informal, relapsed with schizophrenia after daily cannabis use and has nightmares and startle after an assault. Assess the trauma symptoms and cannabis use and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Sophie Turner, 28, female informal inpatient with schizophrenia","open":"It's fine with her here.","manner":"Quiet, tense and watchful, arms folded. Relaxes when told she need not give details. Defensive about cannabis until asked what it does for her.","free":["Has nightmares most nights","Terrified when male staff check on her at night","Was assaulted three years ago and does not want to describe it","Smokes cannabis to sleep"],"ifasked":["Flashbacks during the day","Avoids that part of town and going out at night","Always on edge and jumpy","Voices are mostly separate but louder when scared","Two or three joints of skunk daily, about £60 a week from benefits","Stopped once for a week: poor sleep and irritability, then restarted","The man lives on her estate; no threats but she sees him","Fleeting thoughts of self-harm after nightmares, never acted"],"worries":["Will therapy make me relive it?","I don't want the police involved","Maybe it was my fault"],"rules":["Shuts down if asked for details of the assault","Blames herself if asked about the perpetrator; settles if firmly told it was not her fault","Becomes defensive if cannabis is criticised; opens up if its purpose is explored","Agrees to referrals if she keeps control"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Sophie, I'm Dr Williams, one of the ward doctors. Thanks for staying after the ward round. Nurse Amira is here with us. Is that okay, or would you prefer just one of us?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"It's fine with her here."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"You mentioned something frightening happened three years ago. You don't need to tell me any details. I'd just like to know how it affects you now.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"I've never told anyone about it properly. I don't want to go into it."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Trauma symptoms","w":"D","s":"Thank you for trusting us with what you have. We won't go into it. Do you get memories or nightmares that feel like it's happening again?","m":[2,9]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Most nights. I wake up soaked. Sometimes in the day it just flashes in."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. Do you avoid places, people or anything that reminds you of it?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"I don't go near that part of town. I don't go out at night."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"And do you feel on edge or jumpy?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"All the time. When men come in at night to check on me, I think it's happening again."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying, and it makes complete sense after what you went through. How's your sleep apart from the nightmares?","m":[3,9]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Terrible. I can't drop off unless I've had a smoke."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Okay. Before we get to that, the voices. Are they linked to what happened, or separate?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Separate mostly. They call me names. But they're louder when I'm scared."},{"t":"2:10","band":"Cannabis","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Now cannabis. I'm not here to judge, I just want to understand. How much do you use, and what type?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Skunk. Two or three joints every day. More at night."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"How much does that cost you a week?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"About sixty quid. I'm on benefits, so it's most of my money."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"And what does it do for you?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Weed is the only thing that lets me sleep. It stops the nightmares."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"I can see why you'd use it then. Have you tried cutting down? What happens when you stop?","m":[6,9]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"I stopped for a week once. Couldn't sleep, really snappy. I went back to it."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"That's common in the first week. The difficulty is, strong cannabis seems to bring the voices back. That's what happened before you came in, isn't it?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Maybe."},{"t":"3:25","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"Can I ask about your safety now? Is the person who hurt you still around, or in contact?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"He lives on the same estate. I see him sometimes. Maybe it was my fault. I was out of it that night."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"It was not your fault. Nobody is to blame for being assaulted, whatever state they were in. He is responsible. Has he threatened you since?","m":[7,9]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"No. He just looks at me."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"That must be frightening. Have you had thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Sometimes after the nightmares. I've never done anything."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. If those thoughts get stronger, will you tell a nurse?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"4:30","band":"Explain and plan","w":"D","s":"Here's what I think. What you describe sounds like post-traumatic stress. It's treatable, even with the voices. And cannabis helps you sleep tonight but seems to bring the voices back.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Will therapy make me relive it all?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Trauma therapy goes at your pace and you stay in control. It starts with feeling safer and coping skills. It works for people with psychosis too. Can I refer you to our psychologist?","m":[10,12]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Okay. If I can stop it."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"You can stop any time. On the ward, I'll put in your care plan that women do your night checks where possible, and staff knock and say who they are.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"That would help. A lot."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"I'd also like you to meet our dual diagnosis worker about cannabis, and the nurses can help with a sleep plan. There's also a centre for people who've been sexually assaulted, if you want it.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"I don't want the police involved."},{"t":"5:50","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's your decision, and I respect it. You can change your mind. The centre and Rape Crisis can support you without reporting. Could you sum up what we've agreed?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Women at night. Psychology for the nightmares. The drugs worker. And the support numbers."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"That's it. You're here informally, so if you feel like leaving or using, or the thoughts of hurting yourself get stronger, please talk to a nurse first.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"I'll see you again at Tuesday's ward round to check how the nights are going. Anything you'd like to ask?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks for not making me say it."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, offers a female nurse present and says she need not give details","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about nightmares and flashbacks that feel like it is happening again","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about avoidance, feeling on edge, startle and sleep","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks whether the voices are linked to the trauma","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes a non-judgemental cannabis history: amount, strength, cost, route and what it does","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about attempts to cut down and withdrawal, and links use to relapse","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks for ongoing contact or threat from the perpetrator","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about self-harm and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her fear and counters self-blame","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains PTSD is treatable alongside psychosis and how cannabis both helps and harms","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees trauma-informed ward measures: female night checks, knock and announce","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees psychology and dual diagnosis referrals and offers reporting and support options","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and agreement with the plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets and closes with a review time","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Screens PTSD without asking for the story, takes a functional cannabis history, checks current safety and suicide risk, and agrees trauma-informed care and referrals.","borderline":"Covers PTSD and cannabis but misses ongoing risk from the perpetrator or practical ward changes.","fail":"Asks for details of the assault, lectures about cannabis, or treats it as psychosis alone."}},"c-2-1-12":{"setup":"You are the on-call psychiatry doctor in A&E at night. Mr Hughes, 35, with paranoid schizophrenia, stopped his depot two months ago and says he will deal with his neighbour tonight. Assess the risk, decide what to do now, then phone your consultant.","role":{"who":"Mr Lee Hughes, 35, man with paranoid schizophrenia, in A&E; the AI also voices the on-call consultant on the phone at the end","open":"Whatever. I need to get home and sort this out.","manner":"Tense, guarded, angry and convinced. Becomes less hostile if his fear is acknowledged; turns irritable when told he cannot go home, but stays seated if spoken to calmly.","free":["His neighbour is poisoning his water; he tastes metal","He wants to go home to 'sort this out'","He stopped his depot because it made him feel like a zombie"],"ifasked":["Plans to hurt the neighbour tonight when he returns from the pub","Has kitchen knives at home","Conviction for assault eight years ago when unwell","Neighbour lives with his wife; no children; he lives alone","Two cans of beer tonight; no drugs","No thoughts of self-harm","Eating little","Would not act if he moved away or the neighbour were arrested; does not want prison again"],"worries":["Are you locking me up while he gets away with it?","Are you going to tell the police?","I don't want the injection again"],"rules":["Raises his voice if challenged about the belief; settles if fear is validated","Bargains to go home with a promise; accepts staying if reasons are given calmly","Agrees to an oral tablet but refuses an injection","As consultant: agrees MHA assessment and police disclosure if given a clear summary"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Hughes? I'm Dr Ahmed, the psychiatry doctor on call. I'll sit here by the door, if that's okay. There's a security officer just outside, which is routine here at night.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Whatever. I need to get home and sort this out."},{"t":"0:20","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"I want to understand what's happening. The nurse said you're worried about your neighbour.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"He's poisoning my water. I can taste it. Nobody believes me."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. How long has this been going on?","m":[2,9]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Three weeks. Metal taste. He's got a key to the water meter. I've been drinking bottled."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"How sure are you that it's him?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Hundred percent. I've seen him looking at my window."},{"t":"1:00","band":"Risk to others","w":"D","s":"You told the nurse you'd have to deal with him tonight. What did you mean by that?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"I'll sort him out myself. Somebody has to."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"When you say sort him out, are you thinking of hurting him?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"If I have to. He's trying to kill me."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about how, or when?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Tonight. When he comes back from the pub."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being straight with me. Do you have any weapons, or anything you'd use?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"I've got knives at home. Kitchen ones."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Have you ever hurt anyone before?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Got done for assault eight years ago. A bloke in a pub. I was ill then too."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Who else lives near you? Any children at his house or yours?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"He's got a wife. No kids. I live on my own."},{"t":"2:15","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"Okay. Have you been drinking or using any drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Couple of cans tonight. No drugs."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"And your injection. The team said you stopped it two months ago. What happened?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"I'm not having the injection again. It made me a zombie."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Feeling like that is a fair reason to stop, and we can look at a lower dose or something different. With all this going on, have you had thoughts of hurting yourself?","m":[6,7,9]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"No. I'm not the one who should die."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Any physical problems tonight? Headaches, feeling ill, not eating?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Not eating much. I'm fine."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Is there anything that would stop you going after him?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"If he got arrested. Or if I moved. I don't want to go to prison again."},{"t":"3:20","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"That matters. I'm worried someone could get badly hurt tonight, including you. I think you need to stay in hospital while we help with these fears.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"So you're locking me up and he gets away with it?"},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"I get why it feels unfair. It isn't a punishment. I'm going to ask my consultant and a social worker to see you tonight, to decide whether you need to come into hospital, if needed under the Mental Health Act.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Let me go home and I promise I won't do anything."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"I appreciate you saying that. But you've told me you planned to act tonight and you have knives at home. I can't safely agree to that.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Are you going to tell the police?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"I may need to share limited information to keep your neighbour safe. I'll only share what's needed, and I'll tell you if I do.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Great. Brilliant."},{"t":"4:40","band":"Immediate plan","w":"D","s":"While we wait, a nurse will stay with you, and security will be nearby. You look exhausted. I can offer a tablet to help you feel calmer. Would you take it?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Maybe. If it's not the injection."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"It's a tablet, lorazepam. If you feel angrier or want to leave, tell the nurse, and I'll come straight back. Can you tell me what happens next?","m":[12,14]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Someone else comes to see me. I stay here. You might tell the police."},{"t":"5:20","band":"Escalate","w":"D","s":"That's right. Thank you for talking to me. I'm going to ring my consultant now.","m":[14]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"[On-call consultant, by phone] Go ahead."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Dr Ahmed, liaison. Mr Hughes, 35, paranoid schizophrenia, off his depot two months. He believes his neighbour is poisoning his water. He plans to hurt him tonight when he gets home, has kitchen knives, and has a past assault conviction.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"[Consultant] Is he trying to leave?"},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"Not yet. He's on continuous observation with security close by and has accepted oral lorazepam. I think he needs a Mental Health Act assessment tonight, and I'd like to call the duty AMHP.","m":[13,12]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"[Consultant] Agreed. I'll come in as the second doctor. What about the neighbour?"},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"I think we should tell the police: his name, the neighbour's address and the threat, nothing more. I've told him we might. Can we agree that together?","m":[13,11]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"[Consultant] Yes, agreed. Document what you shared and why. If he tries to leave before we assess, use common law and call security."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Will do. I'll call the AMHP and the police now and write it all up.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self calmly, sits near the exit with security close by","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the poisoning belief and how certain he is","d":"Content"},{"c":"Clarifies what 'deal with him' means: intent, plan and timing","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about weapons and access to them","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about past violence, convictions and who else lives nearby","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol, drugs and why he stopped his depot","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks thoughts of self-harm and asks about physical health","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks what would stop him acting","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his fear and anger without agreeing with the belief","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains honestly why he should stay and that colleagues will assess him","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tells him limited information may go to police to keep someone safe","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Sets an A&E safety plan: observation, security, offer of oral medication","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives the consultant a clear SBAR, requests MHA assessment via AMHP and agrees police disclosure","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes with the patient: next steps, checks understanding and what to do if distress rises","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gets intent, plan, timing, weapons and past violence, stays calm and honest, sets immediate safety measures and gives a clear SBAR leading to MHA assessment and police disclosure.","borderline":"Identifies high risk but is vague on weapons or timing, or does not address protecting the neighbour.","fail":"Lets him go home, argues about the delusion, plans to use Section 5(2) in A&E, or misses the named victim."}},"c-2-1-13":{"setup":"You are the duty doctor in a CMHT clinic. Mr Shaw, 47, with delusional disorder, believes his wife is unfaithful; she reports he pushed her last week. They have children aged 9 and 12. Assess him, see his wife alone, and plan for safety.","role":{"who":"Mr Darren Shaw, 47, man with delusional disorder; his wife Mrs Kerry Shaw, 44, is seen alone later","open":"Terrible. My wife's having an affair and everyone's acting like I'm the problem.","manner":"Darren is aggrieved, intense and convinced, quick to feel judged; calmer if not challenged. Kerry is frightened, apologetic and fears losing the children; steadier once believed.","free":["Darren is sure his wife is having an affair","He has 'proof': marks on underwear and deleted texts","Kerry is frightened and it is getting worse"],"ifasked":["Checks her phone, bag and washing daily; waits outside her work","Pushed her against a wall last week after she laughed","Would 'batter' the man if he found him; no weapons","Six or seven pints most nights","A previous partner left because of his jealousy; denies hitting her","The children hear arguments; his son told him to stop shouting","He said 'if I can't have you no one will' (Kerry)","Kerry's sister can take her and the children (Kerry)"],"worries":["Darren: I don't need tablets, she needs to tell the truth","Darren: are you involving social services?","Kerry: will they take my children?","Kerry: maybe it is my fault for working late"],"rules":["Darren minimises the push and becomes angry if told what his wife said","Darren admits more if asked openly and without accusation","Kerry blames herself; settles when clearly told it is not her fault","Kerry agrees to referrals once told the aim is support"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Shaw, I'm Dr Mensah, the duty doctor today. Thanks for coming in. How have things been at home lately?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","band":"His story","w":"P","s":"Terrible. My wife's having an affair and everyone's acting like I'm the problem."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a really painful place to be. Tell me what makes you think she's being unfaithful.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"I've got proof. Marks on her underwear, texts deleted. Don't tell me I'm mad."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"I'm not calling you mad. I can see how sure you feel and how much it torments you. How much of the day do you spend thinking about it?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"All of it. I can't work. I lie awake going over it."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Do you check her phone, or her things, to look for evidence?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Every day. Her phone, her bag, her washing. She hides it well."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"Have you ever followed her, or asked her about it again and again?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"I wait outside her work sometimes. And yeah, I ask her. She won't admit it."},{"t":"1:25","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When you ask her, how do those conversations go? Has it ever got physical?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Last week I pushed her against the wall. I barely touched her, she's exaggerating."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Whatever's happening, pushing her is something we need to talk about. How did it come to that?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"She laughed at me. I saw red."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Have you ever thought about hurting her more seriously, or the man you think she's seeing?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"If I find out who he is, he's finished."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"When you say finished, what do you picture doing?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"I don't know. Battering him."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Do you have any weapons, or anything you'd use?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"No. I'm not like that."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"How much are you drinking, and do you use any drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Six or seven pints most nights. It's the only thing that calms me."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Has anything like this happened with a past partner?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"My ex said I was jealous. We split up. I never hit her."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"And your two children. How are they with all this?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"They hear us arguing. My son told me to stop shouting at Mum."},{"t":"3:00","band":"Explain to him","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. These thoughts are taking over your days and hurting you all. I'd like to offer treatment, a medicine and talking support, to make them less consuming.","m":[9,8]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"I don't need tablets. I need her to tell the truth."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"I understand you feel the problem is her. Treatment can still help your sleep and how much this fills your head. Because the children are hearing arguments and there's been a push, we also have a duty to make sure they're safe.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"You're going to involve social services?"},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"We'll work with them to support your family. I'll discuss your treatment with my consultant today and see you again within a week. Please cut down the drinking. I'll talk with your wife separately as we do with all families.","m":[9,14]},{"t":"4:05","band":"With his wife","w":"P","s":"[Wife, Mrs Kerry Shaw, seen alone] Thank you for seeing me. I don't know what to do."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you for calling us. He hasn't been told what you said. Some questions I ask everyone: are you frightened of him?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Yes. It's got worse every month. He checks everything. Last week he said if he can't have me no one will."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Has he ever tried to strangle or choke you, or threatened to kill you or the children?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Not choked. But that comment scared me. I think he meant it."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you. What you describe is domestic abuse, and it's not your fault.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"Maybe if I hadn't worked late he wouldn't think this."},{"t":"5:05","band":"Safety plan","w":"D","s":"None of this is your fault. His beliefs come from an illness, and his behaviour is his responsibility. You deserve to feel safe. From what you've told me, I judge the risk as high.","m":[11,10]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"If social services come, they'll take my kids."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"That's a common fear. Their aim is to help you keep the children safe. I'll refer to the children's safeguarding hub today, a specialist domestic abuse adviser for you, and a multi-agency meeting called MARAC.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Okay. What do I do tonight?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Is there somewhere you and the children could stay?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"My sister's. She's said we can."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"That would be safer while we start his treatment. Here's the National Domestic Abuse Helpline number, open day and night. If you're ever in danger, call 999. Can you tell me the plan back?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Kids and me to my sister's. Ring 999 if he turns up angry. Someone will call me about support."},{"t":"6:25","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's right. The adviser will ring you within a day or two, and I'll update his GP. He'll be reviewed within a week.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I didn't think anyone would believe me."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I believe you. You did the right thing coming in.","m":[11,14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and opens with open questions about the relationship, not the wife's report","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the jealous belief: evidence, conviction and time spent on it","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about checking her phone, belongings, following and interrogating","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about physical violence, including the push, without quoting his wife","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about thoughts of harming his wife or the supposed rival, and weapons","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol, drugs and violence to past partners","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks how the children are affected and whether they witness arguments","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his distress without agreeing the affair is real","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers treatment and explains the duty to keep the children safe","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Sees the wife alone and asks key DASH questions","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Names it as domestic abuse and tells the wife it is not her fault","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains same-day MASH referral, IDVA referral and MARAC","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-plans with the wife: where to go, helpline, 999, and checks she understands","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes clearly with both: next review and GP informed","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Explores jealousy and violence without exposing the wife, sees her alone, judges high risk, refers to MASH, IDVA and MARAC, and safety-plans for tonight.","borderline":"Assesses him well but misses alcohol, the children or the separate conversation with the wife.","fail":"Confronts him with the wife's account, sees them together about the abuse, or does not act on the children's safety."}},"c-2-2-1":{"setup":"You are a doctor in a CMHT new patient clinic. Ms Patel, 39, has had low mood for three months. Her partner wants to speak with you alone afterwards. Take a depression history and mental state, assess risk, and get collateral appropriately.","role":{"who":"Ms Nisha Patel, 39, woman referred with low mood; her partner Mr Tom Reid, 42, speaks to you after","open":"Fine. I'm just tired. Work's been mad. He's overreacting.","manner":"Nisha is guarded and minimising, little eye contact, slow speech; opens up when not rushed. Tom is anxious and protective, relieved to be heard.","free":["Feels tired and blames work","Low and flat since the summer","Lost interest in running and the children's swimming"],"ifasked":["Wakes at 4 a.m.; lost half a stone","Poor concentration; off work two weeks","No past highs, no psychotic symptoms","A glass of wine some nights; no drugs","Feels she is a terrible mother and they would be better off without her","Fleeting suicidal thoughts; first denies plans","Children aged 8 and 11","Wrote letters to the children and bought paracetamol (admits after the partner's account)"],"worries":["Nisha: will you tell him everything?","Nisha: I don't want tablets","Tom: what do I do, she won't talk to me"],"rules":["Nisha denies plans until the doctor gently raises the partner's account","Nisha asks that her suicidal thoughts are not shared with Tom","Tom discloses the letters and paracetamol when asked what worries him","Nisha becomes tearful when the children are mentioned; settles if validated"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Ms Patel? I'm Dr Morgan, one of the doctors here. I'd like to talk with you on your own first, then your partner can join if you're happy. What we discuss stays between us unless I'm worried about your safety.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Fine. I'm just tired. Work's been mad. He's overreacting."},{"t":"0:25","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Work stress is real and wears people down. How have you been in yourself over the last few months?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Low. Flat. Since about the summer."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Is there anything you still get pleasure from?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Not really. I used to love running and the kids' swimming. Now it's all effort."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"How are your sleep and appetite?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"I wake at four every morning. I've lost about half a stone without trying."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"And your energy and concentration?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"No energy. I read the same email three times. I've been off work two weeks."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Have there been times you felt very high or full of energy, and barely needed to sleep?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"No, never."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Have you heard or seen things others didn't, or felt people were against you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"How much do you drink, and any drugs?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"A glass of wine some nights. Nothing else."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"How do you see yourself these days?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"I'm a terrible mum. They'd be better off without me."},{"t":"2:10","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you feel like that. Depression makes people believe things like this. How do you see the future?","m":[5,10]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"I don't really see one."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Have you had thoughts about ending your life?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Sometimes. Fleeting. I wouldn't do anything. The kids."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you made a plan, or taken any steps to prepare?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"The children are clearly important. How old are they?","m":[6,12]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Eight and eleven."},{"t":"2:55","band":"Consent","w":"D","s":"Your partner would like a word. Are you happy for me to listen to him and share the plan? Is there anything you'd rather I kept private?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Are you going to tell him everything I've said?"},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"No, not without your agreement. I'll listen to him and tell him the plan.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Fine. Just don't mention the thoughts."},{"t":"3:30","band":"Partner's view","w":"D","s":"[Partner, alone] Thanks for waiting, Mr Reid. She's agreed I can share the plan. I'd really like to hear what you've noticed.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"She's been giving her things away. And I found letters to the kids in her drawer."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's really important and I'm glad you told me. Anything else? Any tablets she might have saved up?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"There's a lot of paracetamol in the bathroom she bought last week. I don't know what to do anymore."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"It's hard watching someone you love struggle. You've done the right thing. I need to speak with her again now.","m":[10,9]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Okay. I'll wait out here."},{"t":"4:30","band":"Revisit risk","w":"D","s":"[With her again] I've spoken with your partner. He's worried, and he told me about letters to the children. I'm not cross. I just want to understand.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"I wrote them a few weeks ago. In case. I bought the tablets too."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. That sounds like a very dark place to be in. Do you feel you might act on it?","m":[9,10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"I don't know. Some days I'm scared I will."},{"t":"5:10","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"What you're describing is depression, and it's very treatable. Options are medicine like sertraline, talking therapy, or both. Because of what you've told me, I'd like the home treatment team to visit you daily for now.","m":[11,9]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"I don't want tablets. I'm not that kind of person."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"That's fine to feel. Talking therapy is a real choice too, and we can decide together this week. Today, could your partner take the paracetamol to the pharmacy?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Yes. Okay."},{"t":"5:55","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Let's write a safety plan together: warning signs, what helps, who to call. The children will be with your partner. Can you tell me the plan in your words?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Tablets go. The home team comes daily. If it gets bad, I call the crisis line or 999."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"That's right. Out of hours, NHS 111 has a mental health option too. Your partner will have the numbers. The home treatment team will visit tomorrow morning, and I'll see you in one week.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I didn't think I'd say all that."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, sees her alone first and explains confidentiality","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about low mood, enjoyment, energy and duration","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about sleep, appetite, concentration and work","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for mania, psychosis, alcohol and drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores guilt, hopelessness and how she sees the future","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans, preparation and reasons to live","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees with her what may and may not be shared with her partner","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks the partner what changes he has noticed and anything that worried him","d":"Content"},{"c":"Acts on the partner's account by revisiting risk with her","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her distress and the partner's worry","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains depression and treatment choices in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a written safety plan, means safety and asks about the children at home","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding of the plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with crisis contacts and a next contact date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Covers depressive, bipolar and psychotic features, asks directly about suicide, agrees what to share, uses the partner's account to revise risk and agrees a safety plan with follow-up.","borderline":"Good history but does not return to her after the collateral, or misses the children or means safety.","fail":"Shares her disclosures without consent, refuses to hear the partner, or accepts her denial of plans despite clear preparation."}},"c-2-2-2":{"setup":"You are the ward doctor on Tuesday at 16:40. Mrs Doyle, 58, came in informally this morning and is barely drinking. Take a focused history and mental state, judge urgency, then agree tonight's plan with the nurse in charge, who joins you.","role":{"who":"Mrs Margaret Doyle, 58, female, informal inpatient (the nurse in charge, Sam, joins for the last minute)","open":"(quietly, not looking up) There's no point you sitting there.","manner":"Slowed, flat and quiet, with long pauses before answering. Softens a little and holds eye contact when the doctor stays patient and does not argue with her beliefs.","free":["Feels 'black' inside since the summer","Believes her insides have rotted and there is nothing to feed","Believes she deserves to be punished","Wakes at 4 am and lies there"],"ifasked":["Only a sip of water this morning, to take a tablet","Has not passed urine since the morning","A woman's voice at night calls her dirty and wicked and says she does not deserve to drink","Thought about walking into the river before she came in; did not go","Sees not drinking as a way it will 'be over'","One episode of depression in her forties, treated by her GP with tablets; never high","Takes amlodipine only; no alcohol or drugs"],"worries":["Am I going mad?","Don't let them give me electric shocks","Tom would be better off without me"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when asked about her husband and children; settles if validated","If the doctor argues that her insides are fine, goes silent for the next two questions","Agrees only to hold a cup with a nurse beside her, not to promise to drink"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Doyle. I'm Dr Patel, one of the psychiatry doctors. The nurses are worried about you, so I've come to see how you are. Is it alright if I sit here?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"(long pause) If you want. There's no point."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll go slowly, and you take as long as you need.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"How have you been feeling in your spirits over the last few weeks?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Black. Everything's black. Since the summer."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"Since the summer. What's happened to your sleep and your appetite?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"I wake at four and lie there. I don't want food. There's no reason to eat."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"No reason to eat. Can you tell me more about that?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"My insides have rotted. There's nothing in there to feed. It would just sit there."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening to live with. How sure are you that it's happening?","m":[3,9]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"I know it. I can smell it on myself."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"I believe it feels completely real. I see it a bit differently. I think a very severe depression can make the body feel like that.","m":[3,10]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"It's what I deserve anyway."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"What do you feel you deserve?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Punishment. I've done wicked things. I let Tom down. The children."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"That guilt sounds very heavy to carry. Do you ever hear voices when nobody else is around?","m":[4,9]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"A woman. At night. She says I'm dirty and wicked and I should rot."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"That must be awful to hear. Does she tell you to do anything?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"She says I don't deserve water."},{"t":"2:10","band":"Physical risk","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. When did you last have a drink?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"A sip this morning. For my tablet."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"And when did you last pass water?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"This morning, I think. Not since."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Which tablets do you take? And any alcohol or other drugs?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Just the blood pressure one. Amlodipine. I've never been a drinker."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Have you felt this low before? Or ever the opposite: very high, full of energy, hardly sleeping?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Once, in my forties. The GP gave me tablets. Never high. Not me."},{"t":"2:55","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When things feel this black, some people feel life isn't worth living. Have you had thoughts like that?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"(starts to cry) Tom would be better off. Everyone would."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry you're carrying that. I think the illness is telling you that, and it can be treated. Have you thought of ending your life?","m":[6,9]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"If I stop drinking, it'll be over. That's what I deserve."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"Have you thought of any other way, or done anything to prepare?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"The river. Before I came in. I didn't go."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. That helps me keep you safe.","m":[6,9]},{"t":"3:45","band":"Capacity","w":"D","s":"Can I explain something? Without fluids, your kidneys could stop working within a few days. Could you tell me back what I said?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"My kidneys. But they're rotten already. Water can't help a dead thing."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"So if you don't drink, what do you think will happen?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Nothing. I'm dead inside. It makes no difference."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"I understand. I think the depression is making it very hard to weigh this up right now. That isn't your fault.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Am I going mad?"},{"t":"4:25","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"No. You have a severe depression that has changed how you see your body and yourself. It's serious, but it usually responds well to treatment.","m":[10,9]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Don't let them give me electric shocks."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"Nobody will do anything without talking it through with you and Tom first. Tonight it's blood tests, a heart tracing, and a nurse to sit with you. Would you try a few sips with her?","m":[10,13]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"I'll hold the cup. I'm not promising."},{"t":"5:05","band":"Plan with the nurse","w":"P","s":"(nurse in charge joins) It's Sam. What do you need from us tonight?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"She has psychotic depression: nihilistic and guilty delusions, and a voice telling her not to drink. Sips only since morning, no urine since then. She thought about the river before admission.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"NEWS2 at four was 2. Heart rate 104."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Please start 1:1 observation, a fluid chart and NEWS2 four-hourly. I'll take bloods now: U&E, FBC, LFTs, glucose, calcium, TFTs, CK, phosphate and magnesium, plus an ECG.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"And if she still won't drink?"},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Bleep me if her NEWS2 rises, she's passed no urine by midnight, or she drinks nothing. Medics would review her for IV fluids. I don't think she has capacity to refuse fluids now, and I'll record why.","m":[11,8]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"She's informal. What if she goes for the door?"},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Use your 5(4) if needed and bleep me straight away; I can use a 5(2). I'm ringing the consultant now about a Mental Health Act assessment if she keeps refusing.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"I'll suggest an antidepressant plus an antipsychotic, and an urgent ECT referral, because this could become life-threatening. I'll update her husband tonight.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"So 1:1, fluid chart, obs, bloods, and call you on those triggers."},{"t":"6:35","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Mrs Doyle, Sam will sit with you now, and I'll come back after the bloods.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and purpose, sits at her level and allows long pauses","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about mood, sleep and appetite and how long they have changed","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits the nihilistic belief and its conviction without arguing or colluding","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores guilt, deserving punishment, and voices with their content","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about last drink and urine output and treats them as urgent","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, methods and preparation, including fluid refusal","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens past episodes, high mood, alcohol and current medicines","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests her capacity to refuse fluids, specific to that decision, and records it","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her distress and guilt and offers hope that this is treatable","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains severe depression and the physical danger in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees 1:1 observation, fluid chart, NEWS2, named bloods, ECG and medical triggers with the nurse","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Escalates to the consultant tonight: combined drug treatment, urgent ECT referral, MHA if she refuses or leaves","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks the next steps are understood and closes with when they will return","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gets the delusions, voices, intake and suicide risk calmly, sees this as an emergency, and leaves the nurse with a clear plan for tonight and a consultant call.","borderline":"Good history, but the plan is vague on fluids, bloods and triggers, or capacity is mentioned without being tested.","fail":"Argues with the delusions, misses the fluid refusal or suicide risk, or leaves it until tomorrow's ward round."}},"c-2-2-3":{"setup":"Thursday, 11:00, acute adult ward. Mr Lewis, 47, is informal on day 5 and has refused sertraline for three days. The nurse asks you to see him. Find out why, assess his capacity for this decision, and agree a safe plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Gareth Lewis, 47, male, informal inpatient","open":"If you're here about the tablets, I've already said no.","manner":"Guarded and irritable, arms folded. Opens up once he feels listened to rather than pushed. Flat and quiet when he talks about losing his job.","free":["Lost his job as a warehouse manager two months ago","Says tablets 'just numb you'","Wants to be left alone in his room"],"ifasked":["Took fluoxetine two years ago and felt 'wrapped in cotton wool', stopped it himself after three months","Still has thoughts most days that he would be better off dead","Thought about taking an overdose before admission; no plan on the ward","Has thought about walking off the ward 'just to get some air', not to harm himself","His wife Karen and two teenage children visit; the children are the reason he keeps going","Would consider a tablet that does not flatten him, and would talk to someone 'if it's practical'","Does not believe he is being punished or that he is bad"],"worries":["Can you force me to take them?","If I take one, can I go home this weekend?","Talking won't bring my job back"],"rules":["If told he will be sectioned for refusing, closes down and stops answering","Softens if his reason (feeling numb) is taken seriously","Answers capacity questions clearly and sensibly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Lewis, I'm Dr Okafor, one of the ward doctors. The nurses said you've stopped your sertraline. I'm not here to push it on you. I'd like to understand why. Is now okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"If you're here about the tablets, I've already said no."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"Fair enough. I'll keep it short. Can you help me understand what worries you about them?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"They just numb you. I'd rather feel something, even if it's rubbish."},{"t":"0:45","band":"His reasons","w":"D","s":"Numb. Tell me more about that.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Two years ago they put me on fluoxetine. I felt wrapped in cotton wool. Couldn't cry, couldn't laugh. I stopped it after three months."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"That sounds horrible. Emotional blunting is a real side effect, and wanting to feel things makes sense.","m":[3,9]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"So why does everyone keep pushing them at me?"},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Because people are worried about you. Is there anything else behind saying no? Some people feel they don't deserve help.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"No. It's not that. I just don't want to be a zombie."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Thanks. What's your own sense of what's going on for you at the moment?","m":[4,7]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"I'm depressed. Since the job went, it's all gone grey."},{"t":"1:55","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Losing your job is a huge blow. I'm sorry.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Twenty years there. Gone in a ten-minute meeting."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"When things feel this grey, do you have thoughts about ending your life?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Most days I think they'd be better off without me."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you made a plan, or taken any steps about it?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Before I came in, I thought about tablets. Not here. There's nothing to do it with."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Is saying no to the sertraline part of giving up on yourself?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"No. If something didn't flatten me, I'd take it."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"What keeps you going right now?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"The kids. They're fifteen and seventeen."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about leaving the ward?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Sometimes. Just to get some air. Not to do anything."},{"t":"3:15","band":"Legal position","w":"P","s":"Can you force me to take them?"},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"No. You're here informally, and you can say no to treatment. I'd rather find something you're willing to try.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"If your safety became seriously at risk, for example you wanted to leave and we were very worried, we'd talk about other options honestly. Not because of a tablet.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's fair."},{"t":"3:55","band":"Capacity","w":"D","s":"Can I check I've explained things well? What do you understand about why the team suggested sertraline?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"It's meant to lift the depression. Takes a few weeks."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"And what might happen with no treatment at all?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"I stay like this. Or get worse. Maybe longer in here."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"So how are you weighing that up?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Being numb again feels worse than waiting. But I'd try something different."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"That's clear, and you've explained your reasons well. I think you have capacity for this decision, and I'll record that.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:50","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"There are other options. Mirtazapine is less likely to blunt feelings. It helps sleep but can increase appetite and weight.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"I'm not sleeping, so that might actually help."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"There's also talking therapy. Our psychologist does behavioural activation: small practical steps to get going again.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Therapy's a waste of time. Talking won't bring my job back."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"No, it won't. But it can help with the hopelessness, and our welfare adviser can help with money and work.","m":[11,9]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"If I take one, can I go home this weekend?"},{"t":"5:40","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'd like to get you home too. Let's base leave on how you're feeling and staying safe, not on one tablet.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"So: I'll talk to the consultant about mirtazapine 15 mg at night, and ask the psychologist to see you. For now, nurses will check on you every hour.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"If those dark thoughts get stronger, or you feel like leaving, please tell a nurse straight away. Could you tell me back what we've agreed?","m":[13,12]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Talk to the consultant about the new tablet, see the psychologist, tell a nurse if it gets bad."},{"t":"6:35","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Spot on. The consultant will see you at tomorrow's ward round. Thanks for talking with me.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose without pressure and asks permission","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Uses open questions to find his real reason for refusing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores past antidepressant experience and the emotional blunting","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks whether hopelessness, guilt or psychotic beliefs drive the refusal","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts, plans and whether refusal is linked to wanting to die","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about protective factors and any wish to leave the ward","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks he understands the purpose of treatment and the risk of no treatment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests weighing and communication, concludes he has capacity, and records it","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his job loss, frustration and side-effect concerns","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains accurately that an informal patient with capacity can refuse, without threats","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers at least two alternatives, including a different drug and psychological therapy","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees observation level, review triggers and consultant review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets, checks understanding with teach-back and closes clearly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Finds the blunting, tests capacity properly, assesses suicide risk, stays honest about the law and leaves him with real choices and a safety net.","borderline":"Gets the reason and offers alternatives, but capacity is assumed rather than tested, or risk is covered too briefly.","fail":"Pushes the tablet, threatens sectioning, or misses that he still has daily suicidal thoughts."}},"c-2-2-4":{"setup":"Wednesday, 14:20, CMHT clinic. Ms Bennett, 34, has taken paroxetine 30 mg for 8 months and wants to stop today because of sexual side effects. Review her side effects, explain discontinuation symptoms, and agree a safe plan to switch or stop.","role":{"who":"Ms Laura Bennett, 34, female, CMHT outpatient","open":"I'll be honest, I want to stop the paroxetine. Today.","manner":"Determined and a bit impatient at first. Embarrassed and quieter when talking about sex. Relaxes and becomes collaborative once her concern is taken seriously.","free":["Mood much better than last year","Sexual side effects since starting paroxetine","Missed two days last month while away and felt awful"],"ifasked":["Low desire and unable to reach orgasm; it is straining things with her partner, Dan","On the second missed day: dizzy, tearful, 'electric shocks' in her head; settled within a day of restarting","Mood good for about five months; no suicidal thoughts","Early warning signs: stops replying to friends, sleeps late, cries over small things","On the combined pill; no plans for pregnancy for a couple of years","Was thinking of buying St John's wort online to replace the paroxetine","Takes ibuprofen occasionally for period pain; no other medicines"],"worries":["Am I addicted?","Will the depression come back?","Will mirtazapine make me put on weight?"],"rules":["Becomes embarrassed if sexual questions are blunt; opens up if asked gently with a reason","Pushes back once with 'can't I just take it every other day?'","Agrees to a switch if she knows she can slow it down"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Laura, I'm Dr Hughes, one of the psychiatry doctors. I know you've come in wanting to stop your paroxetine. Can we talk through why, and the options?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"I'll be honest, I want to stop it. Today."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I understand, and you're entitled to stop. I'd like to make sure we do it in a way that doesn't make you feel ill. What's bothering you most?","m":[1,8]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"The side effects. It's... personal."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Side effects","w":"D","s":"Lots of people find this hard to talk about. Sometimes these tablets affect sex. Is that part of it?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Yes. I've got no interest, and when I do, nothing happens. I can't finish."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That's a common side effect and a really good reason to change. How is it affecting things?","m":[2,9]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"It's wrecking things with Dan. He thinks it's him. I feel like I'm broken."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard for both of you. You're not broken; this is the medicine.","m":[9]},{"t":"1:30","band":"Missed doses","w":"D","s":"Tell me what happened when you missed your tablets last month.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"I was away and forgot them. By the second day I was dizzy and teary, and I had these electric shocks in my head. It was terrifying."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"How quickly did it settle once you restarted?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Within a day."},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Am I addicted to it?"},{"t":"2:05","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"No. You don't crave it or need more. Your body has adapted to it, so stopping suddenly causes withdrawal, or discontinuation, symptoms.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Paroxetine is one of the worst for this, because it leaves the body very quickly.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Can't I just take it every other day?"},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"I wouldn't. Skipping days is exactly what caused the shocks. Smaller daily doses work much better.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","band":"Mood and safety","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been over the last few months?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Honestly good. Since about the spring. Five months maybe."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"That's great. Any thoughts of self-harm or that life isn't worth living?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Not for ages."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"If the depression started coming back, what would you notice first?","m":[4,7]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"I stop texting friends back. I sleep late. I cry at adverts."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"That's helpful. Withdrawal starts within days, feels physical, dizziness and shocks, and settles if you take a dose. Relapse creeps in over weeks, like the signs you just described.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Will it come back if I stop?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"It might. Stopping within about six months of getting well carries a higher risk. Switching to another medicine keeps you protected.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Can I check a few things first? Are you on contraception, and are you planning a pregnancy?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"I'm on the pill. No babies for a couple of years."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Any other medicines, including things from the chemist or online?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Ibuprofen sometimes. And I was going to order St John's wort to replace this."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you mentioned it. Please don't take it alongside these medicines; together they can cause a dangerous reaction.","m":[5,12]},{"t":"4:40","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"So, the options. We could lower the dose, switch to mirtazapine, which affects sex much less, or taper slowly and stop with a relapse plan.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Mirtazapine. Does that make you put on weight?"},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"It can increase appetite and some people gain weight. It also helps sleep. Vortioxetine is another option if weight worries you.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"I'll try mirtazapine. As long as I can stop if it's awful."},{"t":"5:20","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Of course. So, paroxetine 20 mg and mirtazapine 15 mg at night for one to two weeks. Then paroxetine 10 mg with mirtazapine 30 mg. Then stop the paroxetine.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"I'll write the steps down. If you get dizziness or shocks, stay on that step longer or go back one. You set the pace.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"I'll write to your GP this week with the dose steps and who prescribes. I'll ring you in one to two weeks.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"If your mood drops or dark thoughts come back, call the crisis line or NHS 111. Can you tell me back the plan?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Cut to 20 and start mirtazapine at night, then 10 and 30, then stop. Go slower if I get the shocks. No St John's wort."},{"t":"6:25","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Perfect. And if things aren't better sexually in a few weeks, let us know. Any questions before you go?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks for not just telling me to put up with it."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, sets the agenda and acknowledges her right to stop","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks gently about the type and impact of sexual side effects","d":"Content"},{"c":"Clarifies the missed-dose symptoms, their timing and response to restarting","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks current mood, early warning signs and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks contraception, pregnancy plans and other medicines, including St John's wort","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains discontinuation symptoms as not addiction, and why paroxetine is worst","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains how withdrawal differs from relapse","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers options (lower dose, switch, slow taper) with pros and cons","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her embarrassment and the strain on her relationship","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Agrees a written cross-taper with dose steps she can slow down","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Commits to a GP letter this week stating dose steps and who prescribes","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets: phone review, crisis line or NHS 111, and going back a step","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Takes the sexual side effects seriously, explains withdrawal versus relapse clearly and agrees a written, flexible switch with GP communication and a safety net.","borderline":"Safe plan, but sexual function or pregnancy and interactions are only briefly touched, or the plan is not written down.","fail":"Agrees to stop today or every other day, dismisses the side effects, or misses St John's wort and pregnancy questions."}},"c-2-2-5":{"setup":"Friday, 10:00, CMHT clinic. Mr Carter, 52, has had depression for 14 months despite sertraline, venlafaxine and 12 sessions of CBT. Your consultant suggests lithium. Check why treatment hasn't worked, explain the options, and explain lithium safely.","role":{"who":"Mr David Carter, 52, male, CMHT outpatient","open":"The consultant mentioned lithium. I looked it up. Isn't that for bipolar?","manner":"Tired, slowed and sceptical; says he has 'tried everything'. Becomes engaged and asks practical questions once he feels the options are explained honestly.","free":["Depressed for over a year","Tried two antidepressants and CBT","Read online that lithium is for bipolar","Worried about blood tests"],"ifasked":["Sertraline 150 mg for about 10 weeks; took it daily","Venlafaxine: missed many doses in the first month because of nausea; then took 225 mg daily for about three months","Never had high spells or needed little sleep; no unusual beliefs or voices","Drinks about 4 pints at weekends; no drugs","Takes ramipril for blood pressure and ibuprofen for back pain most days","Off sick from his job as a bus depot manager; daughter moved away last year","Sometimes thinks he'd be better off not waking up; no plans","GP thyroid and kidney tests were normal last month"],"worries":["Are you saying I'm bipolar?","Why all the blood tests?","Do I have to stop the ibuprofen for my back?"],"rules":["Admits missing venlafaxine doses only if asked without judgement","Says 'I'm just broken' early; engages if validated and given hope","Chooses lithium if the other options are also explained fairly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Carter, I'm Dr Shah, working with Dr Evans. She mentioned lithium, and I'd like us to go through your options together. What do you already know?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"I looked it up. Isn't lithium for bipolar? I've tried everything anyway. I'm just broken."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"I can hear how worn down you are after so long. You're not broken. Depression that hasn't lifted is common, and there are treatments we haven't tried yet.","m":[11]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Okay. Go on then."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Past treatment","w":"D","s":"First, can I check how the earlier treatments went? How long were you on sertraline, and at what dose?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"About ten weeks. 150 milligrams by the end."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"And the venlafaxine?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"225 a day. About four months."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"Lots of people miss tablets, especially when they feel sick. Were there times you missed doses?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Honestly, the first month I missed loads. It made me feel sick. After that I took it every day."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. So about three months at a full dose, taken properly. That's a fair trial.","m":[3,2]},{"t":"1:35","band":"Other causes","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had spells of very high mood or energy, needing little sleep? Or heard or believed unusual things?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I'm the opposite."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"How much do you drink over a week? Any other drugs?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Four pints at the weekend. Nothing else."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"What medicines do you take for your physical health?","m":[5,10]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Ramipril for my blood pressure. Ibuprofen most days for my back."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"That's important; I'll come back to it. What's going on at work and at home?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Off sick from the bus depot. My daughter moved to Leeds last year. House is quiet."},{"t":"2:35","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Does it ever get so bad you think about ending your life?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Some mornings I think it'd be easier not to wake up. I wouldn't do anything. My daughter."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being open. If that changes, I want you to tell us. It also matters for which treatment is safest.","m":[6,9]},{"t":"3:05","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"There are several options. Lithium added to your venlafaxine. Or an antipsychotic like aripiprazole or quetiapine in a low dose. Or adding mirtazapine. Or a different talking therapy.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"And if things became very severe, ECT is very effective.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Are you saying I'm bipolar?"},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"No, it's a fair worry. Lithium in smaller amounts is often used to help an antidepressant work. Your history doesn't suggest bipolar.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Why all the blood tests then?"},{"t":"3:45","band":"Lithium","w":"D","s":"The useful level in the blood is narrow. Too low does nothing; too high can make you ill. We also check kidneys and thyroid, because lithium can affect them.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"How often?"},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Weekly at first, until the dose is right, then three-monthly through the first year. Kidney and thyroid tests every six months.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"I take ibuprofen for my back. Do I have to stop?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Yes, it's best to stop. Ibuprofen-type painkillers, blood pressure tablets like ramipril, and water tablets can all push lithium up. Paracetamol is safer.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"Your GP and I will keep ramipril going but check levels more closely.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"The most important bit: if you get vomiting, diarrhoea, a coarse shake, unsteadiness or confusion, stop taking the lithium and get a blood level the same day.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"Keep your fluids up too, especially in hot weather or if you're unwell.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"That's a lot to remember."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"It is. You'll get a lithium pack: a booklet, a record book and an alert card.","m":[12,11]},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Because lithium is dangerous in overdose, we'll give a limited supply at first. Is that okay?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"Fine. How long before I know if it works?"},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Usually within a few weeks of reaching the right level. We'll judge it at four to six weeks.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:45","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Would you like to try lithium, or would you rather think about the other options?","m":[7,13]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"I'll give the lithium a go."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"Okay. Today: kidney and thyroid bloods, calcium, weight and an ECG. If those are fine, lithium at night, and your first level a week later, twelve hours after the dose.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Your GP will share care, and I'll see you in four weeks. Can you tell me the warning signs?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Being sick, diarrhoea, shaky, wobbly, confused. Stop it and get a blood test. No ibuprofen."},{"t":"6:35","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. Any other questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"No. It's the first time in ages I've felt there's a next step."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, asks what he knows and agrees a shared decision","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks dose and duration of each antidepressant trial","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about adherence without judgement and judges if trials were adequate","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for past high mood and psychotic features","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol, physical medicines and ongoing stressors","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts and limits lithium supply because of overdose risk","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Offers at least three options, including lithium, antipsychotic augmentation, mirtazapine and ECT","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why lithium is used and how blood level monitoring works","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Teaches toxicity signs and sick-day rules, including fluids","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Names interacting drugs: NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors and diuretics","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his exhaustion and gives realistic hope","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Arranges baseline tests, first level timing, lithium pack and GP shared care","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back and agrees a review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Checks the past trials and diagnosis before labelling resistance, offers several options fairly, and explains lithium monitoring, interactions and toxicity in plain words.","borderline":"Explains lithium well but skips adherence, bipolar screening or interacting drugs.","fail":"Pushes lithium without baseline tests or toxicity advice, or misses the ibuprofen and ramipril interaction."}},"c-2-2-6":{"setup":"Monday, 15:00, ward interview room. Mrs Marsh, 66, has severe recurrent depression, is eating poorly and has not improved on two antidepressants. The team suggests ECT. Explain ECT and rTMS, answer her concerns, and check her understanding.","role":{"who":"Mrs Eileen Marsh, 66, female, informal inpatient","open":"They want to give me electric shocks. I've seen what that does in films.","manner":"Frightened and tearful, slow to answer. Becomes calmer and asks sensible questions when the doctor explains honestly and does not rush her.","free":["Frightened by ECT scenes in films","Has heard of 'the magnet treatment' and thinks it sounds gentler","Feels too old and tired for all this"],"ifasked":["Had a general anaesthetic for a knee replacement in 2019 with no problems","High blood pressure on amlodipine; no heart or lung disease","Has a partial upper denture","Takes lorazepam at night for sleep","Has eaten very little for two weeks; lost about half a stone","Sometimes wishes she would not wake up; no plans or actions","Would like her daughter Claire there for the next talk","Does crosswords and worries about forgetting her grandchildren's names"],"worries":["Will it wipe my memory?","Wouldn't the magnet one be better?","What if I start and want to stop?","Can I try one more tablet first?"],"rules":["Wears a hearing aid; speak clearly and face her","Becomes tearful at 'too old for all this'; settles if validated","Will not agree to sign anything today; agrees to the next steps if she is given time"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Marsh, I'm Dr Ahmed, one of the ward doctors. I'm here to talk about the treatments the team mentioned. Can you hear me well? Are your glasses handy for the leaflet later?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Yes, my hearing aid's in. They want to give me electric shocks. I've seen what that does in films."},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. Before I explain anything, what have you heard about ECT?","m":[2,9]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"They strap you down and you're screaming. And people come out like zombies."},{"t":"0:45","band":"Her beliefs","w":"D","s":"What worries you most about it?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"My memory. I do the crossword every day. I don't want to forget my grandchildren's names."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"That makes complete sense. Thank you for telling me. Can I explain how it's actually done now, and then talk about the magnet treatment too?","m":[2,9]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"1:15","band":"Explain ECT","w":"D","s":"The team suggested it because you've had two medicines without improvement, and you're hardly eating. That worries us.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"ECT is given under a short general anaesthetic. You're fully asleep, with a muscle relaxant, so your body barely moves. Nobody straps you down.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"A small electric current causes a brief seizure in the brain, which helps lift severe depression. It's usually twice weekly, often for six to twelve sessions.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"So I wouldn't feel it?"},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"No. Afterwards some people have a headache, aching muscles or feel muddled for an hour or two.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"And my memory?"},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Memory problems are the main side effect. Many people forget events around the weeks of treatment. Most memory comes back within weeks, but a few people have lasting gaps.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"We'd check your memory before the course and as it goes on, and adjust or stop if there were problems.","m":[6,13]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Wouldn't the magnet one be better? It sounds gentler."},{"t":"2:40","band":"rTMS","w":"D","s":"rTMS is gentler. You're awake, a magnetic coil sits on your head, and it's daily on weekdays for four to six weeks. Side effects are usually a headache or scalp discomfort.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"But for depression as severe as yours, especially when you're not eating, it works less well than ECT. It's also hard to get on the NHS.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Can I try one more tablet first?"},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"That's a fair question. Another tablet might take six weeks or more, and you're not eating now. ECT often helps within a couple of weeks.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I'm too old for all this. Just let me be."},{"t":"3:40","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you feel so worn down. ECT often works very well in older people. When you say let me be, do you ever feel you'd rather not be alive?","m":[9,14]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I hope I don't wake up. I wouldn't do anything."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. I'll make sure the nurses know, so they can check in with you.","m":[14]},{"t":"4:10","band":"Fitness","w":"D","s":"Can I ask about your health? Any problems with anaesthetics, your heart or lungs?","m":[3]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Knee replacement in 2019, no trouble. Blood pressure tablets."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Any loose teeth or dentures? And what do you take at night?","m":[3]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"A partial plate at the top. Lorazepam to sleep."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Lorazepam can make treatment less effective, so the team would review it.","m":[3]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"What if I start and want to stop?"},{"t":"4:55","band":"Consent","w":"D","s":"You can stop at any time, even after signing, before any session. It wouldn't affect the rest of your care.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"And there's no rush to decide today. Can you tell me in your words what ECT involves?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"I'm asleep. A small current gives a short fit in the brain. Twice a week, maybe six to twelve times."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Exactly. And the main risks?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"My memory, mostly for the time around it. It usually comes back."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"And how does it compare with the magnet one for you?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Gentler, but not as good when you're this bad. And hard to get."},{"t":"5:55","band":"Next steps","w":"D","s":"You've understood that really well. What would help you decide?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"I'd like Claire here. And to see where it's done."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Let's do that. I'll give you a leaflet and arrange a visit to the ECT suite with Claire this week.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Meanwhile we'll book a check with the anaesthetist, an ECG, blood tests, and a memory test to compare later.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:30","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Then the consultant will meet you and Claire. You can ask me or the nurses anything before then.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Thank you, Doctor. It's less frightening than I thought."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks hearing and glasses, explains purpose","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what she has heard about ECT and what worries her most","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks anaesthetic history, heart and lungs, teeth and medicines","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains why ECT is suggested now, linking it to poor eating and failed medicines","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains ECT: anaesthetic, relaxant, brief seizure, twice weekly, 6–12 sessions","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives honest information on memory and other side effects without minimising","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains rTMS accurately, including lower effect in severe depression and limited NHS access","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Discusses the alternative of another medicine and how long it would take","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her fear and low mood","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"States consent is voluntary, needs no rush, and can be withdrawn before any session","d":"Content"},{"c":"Uses teach-back to check understanding, risks and her weighing of options","d":"Content"},{"c":"Arranges leaflet, ECT suite visit with family, pre-assessment and baseline memory test","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes with a clear next step and who she can ask","d":"Close"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts when she says 'let me be' and informs nursing staff","d":"Risk"}],"global":{"pass":"Starts from her fears, explains both treatments honestly in plain words, confirms understanding by teach-back and gives her time with clear next steps.","borderline":"Accurate information, but delivered as a lecture, or memory risk and the right to withdraw are only briefly mentioned.","fail":"Minimises memory risk, says rTMS is just as good, pressures her to sign today, or misses her passive death wish."}},"c-2-2-7":{"setup":"Thursday, 13:30, cardiology ward. You are the liaison psychiatry doctor. Mr Brennan, 61, is six days after a heart attack treated with stents, tearful, sleeping badly and refusing cardiac rehab. Assess his mood and risk, rule out other causes, and advise the cardiology team.","role":{"who":"Mr Keith Brennan, 61, male, cardiology inpatient (the cardiology registrar joins for the last minute)","open":"Another doctor. Anyone would be fed up in here, you know. I'm not depressed.","manner":"Defensive and gruff at first, then tearful when talking about his wife and his work. Warms up when his fear is named and he is not lectured.","free":["Fed up and tearful since the heart attack","Sleeping badly on the ward","Does not see the point of rehab"],"ifasked":["Mood fine before the heart attack; low since about two days after","Lost interest in everything, even the football; poor appetite","Frightened that exercise will set off another heart attack","Some nights thinks it would be easier not to wake up; no plans","Has a drawer of old co-codamol at home from a back injury","Drank about 20 units a week; smoked 20 a day until admission","Self-employed plumber, worried about money","Lives with his wife Linda, who is 'holding it together'"],"worries":["Will antidepressants strain my heart?","Is there any point to rehab? My heart's knackered","I just want to go home"],"rules":["Fully oriented with good attention on cognitive testing","Says 'I brought this on myself' once; softens if not blamed","Accepts the plan if it is linked to getting back to work"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Brennan, I'm Dr Morris from the liaison psychiatry team. We see people on the medical wards when things are hard emotionally. Is now a good time, and are you okay to talk here?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Another doctor. Anyone would be fed up in here, you know. I'm not depressed."},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"You're right, hospital is grim. The team said you've been tearful and not sleeping. Can we look at it together?","m":[1,7]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Go on then."},{"t":"0:45","band":"Confusion screen","w":"D","s":"First, a few quick questions I ask everyone. How old are you, and what's your date of birth?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Sixty-one. Fourth of March, sixty-three."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Where are we, and what year is it?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"The Royal, cardiology ward. 2026."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Can you list the months backwards from December, starting with December?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"December, November, October, September, August, July, June... May, April, March, February, January."},{"t":"1:25","band":"His mood","w":"D","s":"Perfect, thank you. How have you been feeling since the heart attack?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Low. I cry at nothing. I lie awake all night."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Were you low before this happened?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"No. I was fine. This started a couple of days after."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Can you still enjoy anything? How's your appetite?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Nothing. Not even the football. I'm picking at the food."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"What did you drink and smoke before you came in?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Twenty a day. A few pints most nights, maybe twenty units a week. I brought this on myself."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Lots of people feel that after a heart attack. Blaming yourself won't help your recovery. The next step will.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:35","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"You told a nurse there's no point anymore. What did you mean?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"(tearful) Some nights I think it'd be easier if I didn't wake up. Linda wouldn't have to look after me."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful. Thank you for telling me. Have you thought of ending your life, or planned anything?","m":[5,7]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"No. I wouldn't do that to her."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Do you have tablets stored at home?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"A drawer of old co-codamol from my back."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Could Linda take those to the pharmacy? Just while things are hard.","m":[5,11]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Fair enough."},{"t":"3:35","band":"Rehab","w":"D","s":"Why don't you want cardiac rehab?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Is there any point? My heart's knackered. What if I go on a treadmill and it happens again?"},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"That's a real fear. Your cardiologists say your heart is recovering, and rehab is supervised by people who know your heart. That's what gets you back to work.","m":[6,10]},{"t":"4:10","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I think you have a depression that started after the heart attack. It's common, it's treatable, and treating it helps heart recovery.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Will antidepressants strain my heart?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Good question. Sertraline has been studied in people after heart attacks and is safe for most. I'd suggest 50 mg once a day.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"With your blood thinners there's a small extra bleeding risk, so we'd add a stomach-protection tablet and check your salt levels in a few weeks.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"Rehab also has a psychological support part, and talking therapy can follow at home.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"I just want to go home."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"I understand. Going home is fine when it's safe. If you feel worse, ring your GP, NHS 111 or the Samaritans on 116 123. Okay?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Okay. I'll give rehab a go."},{"t":"5:30","band":"Advise the team","w":"P","s":"(cardiology registrar arrives) Hi, I'm Dr Lee. What do you think?"},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"He has a moderate depressive episode starting after the MI. No delirium on the 4AT. Passive death wishes, no plan; old co-codamol at home, his wife will remove it.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"I'd suggest sertraline 50 mg once daily. With dual antiplatelets please add a PPI, lansoprazole rather than omeprazole with clopidogrel; avoid tricyclics; check his QTc and sodium at two to four weeks.","m":[9,12]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"He's on aspirin and ticagrelor. What about rehab?"},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Please refer him, with its psychological support. Ring us if he asks to self-discharge.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"6:25","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I'll write this in the notes today, with advice for the GP: review within two weeks of discharge. Mr Brennan, is that clear?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Tablet, rehab, Linda clears the drawer. Yeah, that's clear."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and the liaison role, checks timing and privacy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks orientation and attention briefly to exclude delirium","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits core depressive symptoms: mood, interest, sleep and appetite","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes onset after the MI and asks about prior mood, alcohol and smoking","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the 'no point' comment, suicidal thoughts, plans and stored tablets","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores fear of another heart attack as a reason for refusing rehab","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his feelings and self-blame without judging","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains depression after MI as common, treatable and linked to heart recovery","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Recommends sertraline 50 mg with bleeding risk, PPI choice and other cardiac cautions","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Links mood treatment to cardiac rehab and its psychological support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees means restriction and crisis contacts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Speaks directly to the cardiology team with diagnosis, risk, drug advice and self-discharge plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Commits to a written advice note with GP follow-up and checks understanding","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Excludes delirium, assesses mood and suicide risk properly, recommends a cardiac-safe antidepressant with the bleeding issue addressed, and gives the cardiology team a clear plan.","borderline":"Sound mood assessment but vague drug advice, or no direct communication with the cardiology team.","fail":"Calls it a normal reaction, suggests a tricyclic or citalopram without thought, or misses the passive death wish and stored tablets."}},"c-2-2-8":{"setup":"Tuesday, 11:30, CMHT clinic. Mr Hughes, 68, lost his wife suddenly four months ago. His GP says he cries daily, has lost weight and hears her calling his name. Decide if this is grief or depression, assess risk, and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Dennis Hughes, 68, male, CMHT outpatient (his daughter Sarah joins at the end with his consent)","open":"The GP made me come. I don't know what you can do. She's gone.","manner":"Quiet and tearful, with long pauses. Eases when allowed to talk about Jean. Becomes guarded when asked about drinking, then honest if not judged.","free":["Wife Jean died suddenly at home four months ago, after 41 years of marriage","Cries every day","Hears Jean call his name at night"],"ifasked":["Wakes at 4 am every day; lost about a stone","Low all day, not only in waves; no pleasure even with his grandchildren","Feels useless and a burden to Sarah","Hears Jean only as he drifts off; knows she is not there; no other voices or odd beliefs","Drinks three or four large whiskies most nights since the funeral","Wants to be with Jean; has looked at her old tablets in the bathroom; no plan","Blames himself for not noticing her headaches","Grandchildren and Sarah are what keep him going"],"worries":["Am I going mad?","I don't need pills. Grief is grief","Is Dad going to be alright? (daughter)"],"rules":["Cries when talking about the day Jean died; settles if given time and silence","Becomes defensive if alcohol is raised bluntly","Agrees to Sarah joining only if asked first"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Hughes, I'm Dr Clarke. Your GP told me your wife died suddenly four months ago. I'm so sorry.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"The GP made me come. I don't know what you can do. She's gone."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Would you tell me about her? What was her name?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Jean. Forty-one years. She just collapsed in the kitchen. A bleed in the brain, they said."},{"t":"0:35","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"Forty-one years with Jean. And then so suddenly.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"(cries, long pause) I don't know who I am without her."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"(waits) That's a huge part of your life. It's no surprise you feel lost. Take your time.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"She'd had headaches. I should have noticed. I could have saved her."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Lots of people go over and over what they could have done. From what you say, it came out of the blue.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:20","band":"Grief or depression","w":"D","s":"Do the hard feelings come in waves, with reminders of Jean, or is the low mood there all the time?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"All the time. From when I wake up."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Is there anything you still get pleasure from, even a little? The grandchildren?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Nothing. I go through the motions when they visit."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about yourself as a person at the moment?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Useless. A burden on Sarah."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"How are you sleeping and eating? And managing at home?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Awake at four every morning. I've lost a stone. Sarah brings meals. I don't eat them."},{"t":"2:20","band":"The voice","w":"D","s":"Your GP said you hear Jean call your name. What's that like?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"At night, as I'm dropping off. Am I going mad?"},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"No, you're not. Many bereaved people hear or sense the person they've lost, especially when falling asleep. When it happens, do you know she's not there?","m":[9,6]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Yes. I know. I just want it to be her."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Any other voices? Or feeling that people are against you, or things have special meaning?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"3:10","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Lots of people drink more after a loss. How much are you having at the moment?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"(pause) A whisky or two at night. Maybe three or four. Large ones."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Every night?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Most. Since the funeral. It helps me sleep."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Do you ever feel that life's not worth living?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"I'd like to be with her. I don't see the point of carrying on."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you told me. When you say be with her, have you thought of ending your life?","m":[7,2]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"I've looked at her old tablets in the bathroom. I haven't taken any."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Have you made any plans? And what stops you?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"No plans. Sarah and the grandkids. I couldn't do it to them."},{"t":"4:25","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Can I tell you what I think? Hearing Jean, the tears, going over that day, all of that is grief.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"But low mood all day, no pleasure, feeling useless, waking early and losing weight suggest a depression on top. That makes grieving harder, and it's treatable.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"I don't need pills. Grief is grief."},{"t":"4:55","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Grief is natural, and I won't try to take it away. Treating the depression can help you grieve. A tablet called mirtazapine helps sleep and appetite. Talking support helps too.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"I'd also suggest Cruse Bereavement Support and cutting down the whisky, because it deepens low mood. Your GP will do blood tests for the weight loss.","m":[10,8]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"I'll try. For Sarah."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Would it be okay to bring Sarah in and share the plan with her?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Yes. She worries."},{"t":"5:40","band":"With his daughter","w":"P","s":"(Sarah joins) Is Dad going to be alright?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"With support, most people improve. Your dad is grieving, and he has a depression we can treat. He's agreed to try a tablet and bereavement support.","m":[11,10]},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Could you take your mum's old tablets to the pharmacy? If he talks about ending his life, call the crisis line or 999. Samaritans are on 116 123 any time.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"Of course. Today."},{"t":"6:20","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I'll see you in two weeks, sooner if things get worse. Mr Hughes, what will you do if the dark thoughts get stronger?","m":[13,12]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Tell Sarah, or ring that number."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Thank you both for coming.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Offers condolences, uses Jean's name and lets him tell the story","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Allows silence and validates tears, longing and guilt","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks whether the pain comes in waves with reminders or is constant","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about enjoyment, self-worth and guilt beyond the loss","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about sleep, appetite, weight and coping at home","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the voice and his insight, and screens for other psychotic symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about wish to join Jean, suicidal thoughts, plans, protective factors and access to tablets","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol quantity and pattern without judgement, and advises cutting down","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains grief versus depression in plain words, and that the voice is part of grief","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers bereavement support and depression treatment, with mirtazapine explained","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks his consent before sharing the plan with his daughter","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a safety plan: remove tablets, crisis line, 999 and Samaritans","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges review in two weeks, checks understanding and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gives space for grief, separates grief from depression with clear features, explains the voice calmly, and agrees a safety plan, treatment and family support with consent.","borderline":"Kind and safe, but grief and depression are not clearly separated, or alcohol and access to tablets are missed.","fail":"Calls the voice psychosis, dismisses it all as normal grief, or misses his wish to join Jean."}},"c-2-2-9":{"setup":"Tuesday 16:30, CMHT duty room. Daniel, 34, was sent urgently by his GP after saying he 'can't see the point any more'. His sister waits outside. Assess his suicidal thinking, agree a safety plan with him and arrange follow-up.","role":{"who":"Mr Daniel Hughes, 34, man referred urgently by his GP","open":"I don't really know why I'm here. The GP made a big deal of it.","manner":"Flat, embarrassed and quick to play things down. Opens up if he feels listened to rather than ticked off a list. Tearful about his partner; guarded about his sister until he controls what she hears.","free":["Lost his warehouse supervisor job of eight years two weeks ago","Partner Leah moved out last week","Lies awake until 4am and is barely eating","Drinks 'a few cans' at night to switch his head off"],"ifasked":["Thoughts of ending his life most nights, lasting an hour or two, worst after drinking","Has looked up paracetamol doses online and counted three boxes at home on Sunday","No note, has not given things away","Took an overdose at 19 after a breakup; one night in A&E, no follow-up","Drinks six to eight cans a night; no drugs","Behind on rent and avoiding his bank app","No rope, no firearms, no psychotic or manic symptoms","Kept going by his nephew Alfie, 6, and his sister Claire; walking the dog and his friend Kyle help"],"worries":["Are you going to section me if I tell you everything?","Don't tell my sister everything; she'll tell our mum","What's the point of a plan? It won't get my job back"],"rules":["Says 'I wouldn't actually do anything' unless asked gently and directly","Becomes tearful about Leah; settles if validated without being rushed","Agrees to his sister's involvement only if he decides what she hears","Gives short closed answers if questions come as a checklist"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Daniel, I'm Dr Patel, one of the psychiatry doctors. Thanks for coming in. Your GP was worried and asked us to see you. Is it OK if we talk on our own first, and decide later whether your sister joins us?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"I suppose. I don't really know why I'm here. The GP made a big deal of it."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"That's fine. What you tell me stays within the team. The only time I'd share without asking is if I thought your life or someone else's was in serious danger, and I'd try to talk to you first. Is that OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Yeah. OK."},{"t":"0:40","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"Sure. Tell me what's been happening over the last few weeks.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Lost my job two weeks ago. Warehouse supervisor, eight years. Then Leah moved out last week. So, yeah."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"That's two huge losses in a very short time. How have you been getting through the days?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Not great. I lie awake till four. I don't really eat. I just sit there."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"What goes through your mind when you're lying awake?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"That I've wrecked everything. She said I'd stopped trying. Sorry. I'm a grown man crying in a mental health office. This is pathetic."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"It isn't pathetic at all. You've lost your job and your partner in a fortnight. Anyone would be struggling, and coming in today took strength. Take your time.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Thanks. It's just a lot."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Has anything else been piling up? Money, the flat, anything legal?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Rent's due and I'm already behind. I can't even open the bank app."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"And how much have you been drinking lately? Anything else, like cannabis or other drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Six cans a night. Eight, maybe. It's the only thing that switches my head off. No drugs."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"I can see why it feels like it helps. Have you ever heard voices, felt people were against you, or had spells of being really high?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that. Just this."},{"t":"2:10","band":"Suicidal thinking","w":"D","s":"When things feel this bad, some people start to feel life isn't worth living. Has that happened to you?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Sometimes. I said that to the GP, but I wouldn't actually do anything."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you told the GP. People often play it down once they're in front of a doctor. When you can't see the point, do you have thoughts about ending your life?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Most nights, an hour or two at a time. Worse when I've had a drink."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about how you might do it?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Tablets. I've looked up how many paracetamol it would take. I've got a few boxes at home."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. That matters, and it tells me how bad it's got. Have you done anything else to prepare, like a note or sorting out your affairs? Anything else at home, like rope or a gun?","m":[3,5]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"No note. No rope or anything. I counted the boxes on Sunday. That's it."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Have you ever acted on thoughts like this before?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"At nineteen, after a breakup. Took some tablets. A&E kept me in a night. Are you going to section me if I tell you everything?"},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"No. Telling me how you feel doesn't mean you'll be sectioned. My aim is to keep you safe at home with extra support, so I'd rather know the whole picture.","m":[3]},{"t":"3:40","band":"Shared formulation","w":"D","s":"What has stopped you acting on these thoughts so far?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"My nephew, Alfie. He's six. And Claire, my sister. It'd destroy her."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"And what makes the worst moments worse?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Being on my own in the flat, late. The drink. Seeing Leah's stuff still there."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've got this right. Losing your job and Leah has left you ashamed and hopeless. The thoughts come most nights, worst late, alone, after drinking, with tablets in the flat. Alfie and Claire keep you going. Does that fit?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Yeah. That's it."},{"t":"4:20","band":"Safety plan","w":"D","s":"I'd like us to write a plan now, in your words, for those worst hours. You take a copy home.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"What's the point of a plan? Nothing on it gets my job back."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"You're right, it won't fix the job. It's for getting through the bad hours. What's helped, even a little, on a bad night?","m":[3,9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Walking the dog. Ringing my mate Kyle, but I haven't."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"So: warning signs are lying awake after midnight and reaching for another can. Then walk the dog. Then ring Kyle or Claire. Then the crisis line, or Samaritans on 116 123, any time. If you're about to act, 999. And cutting down the drink, with the alcohol service number I'll give you.","m":[6,9]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"OK. I can do that."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"The tablets worry me most. Would you let Claire take the boxes away tonight?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Don't tell her everything, though. She'll tell our mum and make it worse."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"That's your choice, and I'll respect it. Could she hear just the warning signs, who to call, and that the tablets need to go? Nothing about the details you've told me.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Yeah. That's fine."},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"I'd like the home treatment team to contact you within 24 hours. They can visit daily for a while. I'll also write to your GP today. Is that OK?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Better than sitting in the flat."},{"t":"5:50","band":"Involve his sister","w":"D","s":"Claire, Daniel's agreed I can share his safety plan with you. Here's a copy. Watch for him sitting up late drinking or going quiet. If you're worried, ring the crisis line. If you think he's in immediate danger, call 999.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"(Sister) Thank you. I'll take the tablets tonight. Should I stay with him?"},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"If you can stay tonight, that would really help, if Daniel's happy with that.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:20","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Daniel, in your words, what will you do if it gets bad tonight?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Walk the dog. Ring Kyle or Claire. Then Samaritans or the crisis line. 999 if I'm going to do something."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Spot on. If the home treatment team haven't called by tomorrow afternoon, ring this number. You did well today, and you're not on your own with this.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Thanks. I mean it."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and the limits of confidentiality, and asks about involving his sister","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Opens with an open question and lets him tell his story before direct questions","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds to shame, fear and hopelessness with specific validating statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks stepwise and directly: life not worth living, suicidal thoughts, frequency, strength and method","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about preparations, rehearsal, past attempts and access to tablets, ligatures or firearms","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens alcohol, drugs, debt and psychotic or manic symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores what has kept him alive and what would make tonight worse","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises a shared safety formulation in his words and checks he agrees","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Writes a safety plan with him: warning signs, coping, named people, crisis line, Samaritans 116 123, 999","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees removal of the stockpiled tablets tonight","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gains consent, agrees what his sister hears, and briefs her on warning signs and 999","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges home treatment team contact within 24 hours and a same-day GP letter, without a risk category","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back, safety-nets for no contact and closes warmly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Asks directly about suicide in steps, builds a formulation with him, and leaves him with a written plan, tablets removed, sister briefed and HTT within 24 hours.","borderline":"Covers thoughts and plan but misses means, alcohol or past attempt, or gives a vague plan without named people and numbers.","fail":"Checklist questioning, labelling him 'low risk', no means restriction, nothing in writing, or sharing with his sister without consent."}},"c-2-2-10":{"setup":"Saturday 02:15, A&E. Chloe, 22, took an overdose of her prescribed tablets and paracetamol after a row with her boyfriend. She is on an acetylcysteine infusion and wants to go home. Check her medical status, assess intent and risk, and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Miss Chloe Barnes, 22, A&E patient after an overdose","open":"I feel completely fine. Take this drip out, I want to go home.","manner":"Irritable and dismissive at first, then embarrassed. Softens when her wish to leave is acknowledged. Becomes quiet and frightened when asked about her boyfriend; relieved once she is believed.","free":["Took 'everything in the cupboard' after a row with her boyfriend Jake","Feels fine and hates being in hospital","It was 'stupid'; she just wanted to sleep and not deal with him"],"ifasked":["Took 24 paracetamol and about ten sertraline 50 mg at once around 21:30","Locked the bathroom door, then got scared and rang her friend Megan, who called the ambulance","No planning, no note, no final acts; 'didn't care either way' at the time","Relieved now; no current thoughts of ending her life","Cut her arms at 16; low since the summer; GP started sertraline","Wine at weekends, occasional cannabis","Jake checks her phone, holds her bank card, has pushed her and once grabbed her neck","No children; lives with Jake; can stay at her mum's"],"worries":["Please don't tell Jake I'm here","My mum's going to be so ashamed of me","Will the police get involved?"],"rules":["Agrees to stay once the liver risk is explained clearly and her frustration is acknowledged","Only discloses abuse if asked privately and without judgement","Gets angry if asked the same questions in a rushed way; settles with an apology and a slower pace"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Check with the team","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Okafor, the liaison psychiatry doctor. Before I see Chloe, what did she take, and when?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"(A&E doctor) Twenty-four paracetamol and about ten sertraline 50 mg, all at once around half nine. Her four-hour level was over the treatment line, so the infusion started just before two."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Has TOXBASE been checked, and what's the medical plan?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"(A&E doctor) Yes. ECG's normal. She needs the full course, then repeat INR, ALT, creatinine and paracetamol level. She keeps saying she'll walk out."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'll see her now. If she tries to leave before her capacity is assessed, please ask nursing and security to keep her safe and bleep me.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:45","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Chloe, I'm Dr Okafor, one of the psychiatry doctors. Is it OK to talk here? Is anyone with you?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"No one. I feel completely fine. Take this drip out, I want to go home."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"I can hear you really want to go, and A&E at two in the morning is the last place anyone wants to be. Can I explain why the drip matters first?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Fine. Quickly."},{"t":"1:15","band":"Explain and capacity","w":"D","s":"Paracetamol can damage the liver even when you feel well. The damage often doesn't show for a day or two, and it can kill. The drip protects your liver if it runs to the end. Does that make sense?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"But I feel fine."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"That's the tricky part. Can you tell me in your words what could happen if you left now?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"My liver could pack in. I could get really ill, or die, even though I feel OK."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"That's right. Weighing that up against wanting to go home, what do you want to do?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"I don't want to die from it. I just hate it here. I'll stay till it finishes."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Just so I know it's stuck, what's the drip for again?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"To stop my liver getting damaged."},{"t":"2:15","band":"Intent","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what happened tonight, starting from earlier in the day?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Me and Jake had a row. He'd been on at me all day. I went in the bathroom and took everything in the cupboard."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"What did you hope would happen when you took them?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"It was stupid. I just wanted to sleep and not deal with him for a while."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you were desperate to get away from how you felt. At the time, did any part of you think you might not wake up?","m":[3,7]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Maybe. I didn't really care either way."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Had you planned it before today? Bought tablets, written anything, said goodbye to anyone?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"No. It was in the moment. No note."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Did you lock the door, or try to make sure no one found you?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"I locked the bathroom. Then I got scared and rang my friend Megan. She called the ambulance."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"How does it feel now, knowing you're still here?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Relieved, I think. Embarrassed."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"Are you having any thoughts about ending your life now?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"No. Not now."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Has anything like this happened before? And how's your mood been over the last few months?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"I cut my arms at sixteen. Mood's been rubbish since the summer. The GP put me on sertraline."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"How much are you drinking, and do you use any drugs?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Wine at weekends. Bit of weed sometimes. Not tonight."},{"t":"4:00","band":"Home and safety","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this in private. How are things with Jake? Do you ever feel scared of him, or that he controls what you do?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"He checks my phone. He's got my bank card. He said if I left he'd tell everyone I'm mental."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That's controlling behaviour. It isn't your fault, and it's against the law. Has he ever hurt you physically?","m":[3,9]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"He's pushed me. Once he grabbed my neck. Please don't tell him I'm here. He'll go mad."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"I won't tell him anything without your agreement, and I'll ask staff not to give him information. Are you safe to go back to the flat?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Not really. I could go to my mum's. She'll be so ashamed of me, though."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"Lots of people worry about that. Most families are mainly relieved and want to help. I can help you work out what to say. Are there any children at home, or anyone you look after?","m":[3,10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"No kids. Just me and him. And the cat."},{"t":"5:10","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"So here's what I suggest. The drip finishes, then the medical team checks your bloods and decides when you're fit. In the morning we finish this assessment properly and write a safety plan together.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"And then I can go?"},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"If your bloods are fine and you feel safe, yes, to your mum's. I'd also like to refer you to a domestic abuse adviser, an IDVA, and raise a safeguarding concern. Is that OK?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Will the police get involved?"},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Not unless you want them to, or someone's in immediate danger. Because he grabbed your neck, I may need to share some information to keep you safe, but I'd always talk to you first.","m":[9,11]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"OK. I get it."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"I'll write to your GP today and ask for smaller prescriptions for now. And the National Domestic Abuse Helpline is a free, any time: 0808 2000 247.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Before I go, can you tell me what we've agreed?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Stay till the drip's done. Bloods. Talk to you in the morning. The abuse adviser. Go to Mum's, not Jake's."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"That's it. If you feel like leaving, or the thoughts come back tonight, tell the nurse and they'll call me. You did the right thing ringing Megan.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Thanks. Sorry I was rude earlier."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"No need at all. Try to get some rest.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Confirms with A&E the drugs, amounts, timing, level, TOXBASE use and the medical plan","d":"Content"},{"c":"Introduces self, checks privacy and opens with an open question about the night","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges her wish to leave and validates distress, shame and fear","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains in plain words that paracetamol liver damage can be delayed and fatal despite feeling well","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tests capacity to refuse the infusion: understand, retain, use and weigh, communicate","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about planning, concealment, notes and final acts before the overdose","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks what she expected to happen, help-seeking, feelings now and current suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about past self-harm, recent mood, alcohol and drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks privately about coercive control and violence, including strangulation, and safety at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Identifies any children or dependants","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees plan: finish treatment, medical team clears, full assessment, IDVA, safeguarding, GP letter","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, safety-nets for leaving early or new thoughts, and closes clearly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Keeps her on treatment with her agreement after a clear capacity check, explores intent fully, uncovers coercive control privately and agrees a safeguarding-led plan.","borderline":"Keeps her safe but judges intent on tablet count, or asks about the boyfriend without checking violence, children or a safe place to go.","fail":"Lets her leave, declares her medically fit, uses Section 5(2) in A&E, or misses domestic abuse."}},"c-2-3-1":{"setup":"Thursday 21:00, acute ward. Kayleigh, 29, has just arrived on Section 2 after police found her singing in the street at 3am. Do a focused mental state assessment for mania, then present it to the nurse in charge with risks and investigations.","role":{"who":"Ms Kayleigh Morgan, 29, newly admitted patient on Section 2","open":"Doctor! Great, finally. I don't need a doctor. I've been chosen. I'm going to fix the whole NHS by Christmas.","manner":"Bright, loud and fast, jumping between topics and rhyming. Flirtatious and irritable when pressed. A brief tearful moment when asked about low mood, then bounces back.","free":["Feels 'electric', best ever","Has been chosen to fix the NHS by Christmas","Bus numbers are a code from the universe","Has written a 40-page plan"],"ifasked":["About two hours' sleep in the whole week","God talks to her through the radio and calls her 'the healer'","Cried for an hour on Tuesday out of nowhere","Briefly thought she could jump off a bridge and fly; no thoughts now","Shoved a man who told her to shut up","Spent £4,000 savings on paints and a flight to New York","Prosecco 'to celebrate'; denies drugs","Last period about six weeks ago; no contraception"],"worries":["You can't keep me here. I'll sue all of you","Are you going to drug me?","When can I get back to my painting?"],"rules":["Threatens to walk out if questions drag; stays if the doctor agrees to keep it short","Gets distracted by objects and sounds; follows a calm redirect","Becomes flirtatious once; accepts a polite, firm boundary"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Kayleigh, I'm Dr Shah, the doctor on tonight. You've had a busy few days. Can I ask some questions for a few minutes and check how your body's doing? We can stop and carry on tomorrow.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Doctor! Great, finally. I don't need a doctor. I've been chosen. I'm going to fix the whole NHS by Christmas."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a big mission. Tell me how you came to know you'd been chosen.","m":[5]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Bus numbers. Seven, eleven, they line up. It's a code from the universe. Is that your real watch? I love that colour."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"It's a lot to notice at once. Let's come back to you. How's your mood been, in a word?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Electric. Best I've ever felt. Never better, never ever, clever clever."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"And sleep? How many hours have you had this week?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Who needs sleep? Two hours, maybe. I've been writing my plan. Forty pages."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Do your thoughts feel faster than usual?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Like a motorway with no speed limit. You're boring me. Stop with the questions or I'm walking out."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Fair enough. It must be frustrating being brought here when you feel this good. I'll keep it short. A few more, then you can rest.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Fine. Five minutes."},{"t":"1:35","band":"Beliefs and perception","w":"D","s":"Do you hear voices or see things other people don't?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"God talks to me through the radio. Only good things. He says I'm the healer."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Is anyone trying to harm you or stop your mission?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"The managers won't like it. Let them try."},{"t":"2:00","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When people feel this high, they sometimes get moments of feeling very low or desperate too. Has that happened?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Tuesday I cried for an hour. Out of nowhere. Then I was fine again."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. When you cried, did you have any thoughts about ending your life or hurting yourself?","m":[3,6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"For a second I thought I could jump off the bridge and fly. But I wouldn't. Not now."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Any thoughts like that now, or plans?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"No. I've got too much to do."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Have you had any thoughts of hurting anyone else, or got into any fights?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Only if they get in my way. I shoved a man who told me to shut up. Joke."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"And money. What have you spent recently?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"All my savings. Four grand. Paints and a flight to New York. Tom's furious. You're gorgeous, doctor. Come and sit closer."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"I'll stay where I am, thanks. I'm here to check how you are.","m":[2,7]},{"t":"3:20","band":"Body and brain","w":"D","s":"Have you had any alcohol, cannabis, cocaine or other drugs this week?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"A couple of proseccos. Celebrating. No drugs. I don't need them."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"Could you be pregnant at the moment? When was your last period?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"No idea. Six weeks? I'm not on anything."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Then before any medicine, we'll do a pregnancy test. Would you give a urine sample? It also checks for drugs.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Whatever. Fine."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me where we are and what day it is?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Hospital. Psych ward. Thursday night. I'm not stupid."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Can you take seven from a hundred, and keep going?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Ninety-three, eighty-six, seventy... look, a bird! Blue birds mean luck."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Do you think anything's wrong with your health right now?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Nothing. I've never been healthier."},{"t":"4:25","band":"Rights","w":"D","s":"You're on Section 2 of the Mental Health Act. We can keep you here for up to 28 days to assess you. You can appeal to a tribunal in the first 14 days and have an advocate, an IMHA, to help.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"You can't keep me here. I know my rights. I'll sue all of you."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"You do have rights, and that's why I'm telling you. The nurse will give you a leaflet and we'll go over it again tomorrow. Would you have some food and water now?","m":[10,13]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Toast. With jam. Then I'm painting."},{"t":"5:10","band":"Present to nurse in charge","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Mark. Kayleigh, 29, on Section 2. Bright clothes, glitter, dishevelled. Overactive, distractible. Speech pressured with clang: 'never better, never ever, clever clever'. Mood elevated, but she cried for an hour on Tuesday, so possible mixed features.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"(Nurse in charge) Any psychosis?"},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Grandiose delusion: 'I've been chosen to fix the NHS by Christmas.' Voices: 'God talks to me through the radio.' Fleeting thought of jumping off a bridge to fly, none now. Shoved a stranger. Orientated, poor attention, no insight.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"(Nurse in charge) What do you want from us tonight?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Intermittent obs every 15 minutes, low-stimulus room, and watch her around other patients. NEWS2 four-hourly, food, fluid and sleep chart. Urine pregnancy test and drug screen tonight. Bloods first thing: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, calcium, glucose, lipids, prolactin. ECG before any antipsychotic.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"(Nurse in charge) PRN?"},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Nothing until the pregnancy result is back, unless it's an emergency. Then lorazepam 1 mg as needed. Consultant review on the morning ward round. Call me if she becomes aggressive or talks about harming herself.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"(Nurse in charge) Got it. And her rights?"},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Given verbally just now, with the IMHA. Please give her the leaflet and repeat it tomorrow. Anything you'd like me to clarify?","m":[10,13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"(Nurse in charge) No, that's clear. Thanks."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, keeps it brief and offers to stop and continue tomorrow","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Stays calm, redirects distraction and holds a polite boundary on disinhibition","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her frustration and distress with empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about mood, sleep, energy and speed of thought","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores grandiose beliefs, persecutory ideas and hallucinations, capturing her exact words","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about low mood, suicidal thoughts and current plans to detect a mixed state","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about harm to others, spending and vulnerability","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens alcohol and drugs, asks last period and arranges pregnancy test and drug screen","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks orientation, attention and insight to screen for delirium","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains Section 2 rights: 28 days, tribunal within 14 days, IMHA, leaflet","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Presents the MSE domain by domain, quoting her words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Hands over observation level, physical monitoring, investigations, PRN caution and morning review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers food and fluids, checks the nurse's understanding and closes clearly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A calm, short interview that covers every MSE domain with quotes, finds the mixed features, screens body causes and pregnancy, gives rights and hands over a clear plan.","borderline":"Covers mania well but misses suicide in the mixed state, pregnancy or orientation, or presents labels without her words.","fail":"Argues with her beliefs, insists on a long clerking, gives no rights, or plans medication without pregnancy status or ECG."}},"c-2-3-2":{"setup":"Sunday 14:10, acute ward. Mark, 41, admitted informally with hypomania three days ago, is at the door with his car keys to buy a second car. He has not slept. Assess whether he can leave, act lawfully, and deal with driving and money.","role":{"who":"Mr Mark Ellis, 41, informal inpatient with hypomania","open":"Make it quick, doc. I've got a dealer waiting at four.","manner":"Loud, fast and charming, then angry when told he cannot leave. Bargains once he sees he is being held. Calms when listened to and given clear reasons.","free":["Going to buy a second car today for a 'sure-fire' courier business","This deal will make him a millionaire if he buys today","Knows informal patients can leave"],"ifasked":["Car costs £42,000 from savings and a loan approved on his phone this morning","Has slept about an hour a night","Had a few beers before admission; nothing since","Drove himself in on Thursday","Brother Steve usually helps with money","Has never told the DVLA about his illness"],"worries":["You can't stop me, I'm voluntary","Are you going to drug me?","Will the DVLA take my licence forever?"],"rules":["Refuses to stay voluntarily however well it is put","Gets angry when told about the holding power; settles if his anger is acknowledged and the reason is not 'the car'","Bargains to phone the dealer; agrees to involve his brother if he can do the talking"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mark, I'm Dr Ahmed, the doctor on call. The nurse said you're heading out. Can we talk for a few minutes in the quiet room, just here?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Make it quick, doc. I've got a dealer waiting at four."},{"t":"0:20","band":"His plans","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Tell me what your plans are today.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Buying a second car. Range Rover. My courier business is sure-fire. This deal will make me a millionaire. If I don't buy today, I lose it."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"I can see how exciting it feels. How much is it, and where's the money coming from?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Forty-two grand. Savings and a loan. Approved on my phone this morning."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"How have you been sleeping since you came in?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Don't need it. An hour a night. My brain's on fire, in a good way."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Any alcohol or drugs?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Nothing in here. A few beers before I came in."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"What do you think could happen if you left now?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"I'd be rich. What else?"},{"t":"1:30","band":"Ask him to stay","w":"D","s":"I'm worried you're not well enough to leave safely today. You haven't slept, and big decisions made like this often look different later. Would you stay on the ward voluntarily while we sort this out?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"I'm here voluntarily. You can't stop me leaving. That's the law."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"You're right that informal patients can usually leave. There is a legal power for when someone's too unwell. Before anything like that, what would you lose by waiting one week?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"The deal! No. I'm going. Move."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"So you're telling me you won't stay. I'll write that down, in your words.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Write what you like."},{"t":"2:25","band":"Holding power","w":"D","s":"Then I'm using Section 5(2) of the Mental Health Act. It means you stay on the ward for up to 72 hours while you're properly assessed.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"You're locking me up for buying a car? This is ridiculous."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"I hear how angry you are, and I'm sorry it's happening on a day you feel great. It isn't about the car. You're unwell, not sleeping, and could come to real harm with money and driving.","m":[4,6]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"So what happens now?"},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"I complete a form, the H1, and it goes to the Mental Health Act office now. The 72 hours start then. I'm also calling the AMHP service today to arrange the full assessment. This hold can't be renewed.","m":[6,8]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"And you'll drug me?"},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"No. This power doesn't let us treat you without your agreement. I'd like to offer something to help you sleep, but that's your choice.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Maybe. If it actually works."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Here's a leaflet with your rights. There's no tribunal for this short hold, but you can ask for an advocate, and the nurse will go through it with you.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Fine. Whatever."},{"t":"4:00","band":"Driving","w":"D","s":"Have you been driving recently?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"Drove in on Thursday. I'm a brilliant driver. Better than ever. My reactions are amazing."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Feeling sharper is part of the illness, but judgement gets affected. While you're unwell with mania, you mustn't drive, and you need to tell the DVLA yourself.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Will the DVLA take my licence forever?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"No. Most people get it back. Once you've been well and stable, usually about three months, you can reapply with a report from your doctor. Telling them keeps you legal and insured.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"And if I don't tell them?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"If you kept driving against advice, I might have to tell them. I'd always tell you first. For now, can the ward keep your keys in the safe?","m":[9,10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Fine. Take them. I'll stay if you let me phone the dealer and put a deposit down."},{"t":"5:15","band":"Money","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you'll stay. I'd ask you not to commit to big purchases until you're sleeping again. Could your bank cards go in the safe too?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"My cards? Seriously?"},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Just for a few days. Who helps you with money? Could we call your brother together and ask your bank about a spending block?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Steve. He'll lecture me. Fine, call him, but I'm doing the talking."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Deal. Thank you. That protects your money while you're unwell, and it's still your decision.","m":[4,11]},{"t":"6:00","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what's happening now, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"I'm stuck here up to three days. Someone comes to assess me. No driving, tell the DVLA. Keys and cards in the safe. Call Steve."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. I'll ring the on-call consultant and the AMHP service now, and the nurse will stay close. Ask for me any time if you want to talk again.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"And the sleeping tablet?"},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"I'll write up zopiclone 7.5 mg for tonight. It's there if you want it.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and moves the conversation to a quiet space in a calm tone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores his plans, the amount and source of the money","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about sleep, alcohol and drugs, and his view of the risks","d":"Content"},{"c":"Acknowledges his anger and frustration and keeps the reason about illness, not the car","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks him to stay voluntarily and records his answer in his words","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains Section 5(2): up to 72 hours, assessment, no renewal, no treatment power","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives his rights with a leaflet and access to an advocate","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States Form H1 goes to the MHA office now, consultant informed, AMHP assessment requested today","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises no driving, his own duty to tell the DVLA, relicensing, and telling him first before any disclosure","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees his car keys are kept in the ward safe","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees cards in the safe and a call with his brother to the bank, with consent","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, offers sleep support and closes clearly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Negotiates first, then applies 5(2) lawfully with H1 and same-day AMHP request, gives rights, and secures driving and money with his agreement.","borderline":"Holds him correctly but gives thin explanations, or forgets DVLA, keys or money.","fail":"Locks the door without a legal power, lets him leave with his keys, thinks 5(2) allows treatment, or waits until the end of the 72 hours to call the AMHP."}},"c-2-3-3":{"setup":"Monday 10:00, ward round. Priya, 36, with bipolar disorder, is on Section 2 with mania. She takes sertraline and no mood stabiliser. Her husband asks what you will do and how long it will take. Agree a treatment plan with them both.","role":{"who":"Mr Raj Shah, 38, her husband (Mrs Priya Shah, 36, is present)","open":"Doctor, what will you do to treat her, and how long will it take?","manner":"Tired, anxious and direct. Close to tears about the children, then focused and practical once he has a plan. Priya is restless, says little and insists she is not ill.","free":["Police brought her in; she hasn't slept for days","Their children, 4 and 7, keep asking where Mum is","She has been on sertraline from the GP for months"],"ifasked":["Priya has a coil","His mother, 70, is helping with childcare","Her cousin takes valproate and he wonders if she should too","He is worried about weight gain because she hates it","He is exhausted and has taken time off work"],"worries":["How long until she's home?","What about a mood stabiliser like valproate?","What if she gets agitated on the ward?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful about the children; settles if validated","Pushes for a discharge date; accepts 'weeks' if a weekly update is promised","Priya agrees to try the tablet if it is framed around sleep"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Evans, one of the ward doctors. Priya, your husband has questions about treatment. Are you fine with me talking to you both and share what we know?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"(Priya) He can stay. Not that I'm ill."},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Doctor, what will you do to treat her, and how long will it take?"},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"That's the right question. Before I answer, what have you both understood so far, and what worries you most?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"The police brought her in. She hasn't slept for days. The kids keep asking where Mum is. I just want her back."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to carry, looking after the children and seeing Priya like this. It's completely understandable to feel overwhelmed.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Sorry. It's been a long week."},{"t":"1:15","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Priya's mood is very high and her thinking is very fast. We call that mania. It's part of her bipolar disorder.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"But she's on sertraline. Isn't that meant to help?"},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"It helps depression, but in bipolar disorder an antidepressant can push mood too high. So I'd like to stop the sertraline today.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"(Priya) I've been on it for months."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"You might feel a bit dizzy or irritable for a few days after stopping. That usually settles within a week, and we'll keep an eye on it.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","band":"Treatment","w":"D","s":"I'd recommend a medicine called olanzapine. It calms mania and helps sleep. Ten milligrams at bedtime to start, adjusted as needed.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"What are the side effects?"},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Drowsiness, feeling hungrier and weight gain are the common ones. Some people feel dizzy when they stand. We'll weigh her weekly and give diet advice early.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"(Priya) I'm not getting fat. I don't need tablets."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"I hear that. You haven't slept properly for days, and this should help. Can you tell me what you understand about the tablet and why I'm suggesting it?","m":[3,7]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"(Priya) To make me sleep and slow me down. I'll get hungry. I'll try it if it helps me sleep."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's a clear choice. You're on Section 2, so treatment can be given, but I'd much rather we agree it together, and I'll record that you did.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"What about a mood stabiliser? Her cousin's on valproate."},{"t":"3:35","band":"Safety checks","w":"D","s":"Before I prescribe anything, Priya, could you be pregnant at the moment? Are you using contraception?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"(Priya) I've got the coil."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Good. We'll still do a pregnancy test today. Valproate isn't a first choice for anyone under 55. It can seriously harm an unborn baby, and two specialists must agree nothing else is suitable before it's started.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"So what instead, long term?"},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Once she's settled, we'll talk about lithium. It's very good at preventing future episodes, but it needs regular blood tests.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:25","band":"Monitoring","w":"D","s":"Before starting, we'll check weight, blood pressure, blood sugar, cholesterol and an ECG. Then weight weekly for six weeks, and those bloods again at 12 weeks.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"And what if she gets agitated in here?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"The nurses can offer lorazepam if she's very agitated, with a clear daily maximum, and we review it every day.","m":[6]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"And how long until she's home?"},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"I can't give you an exact date, and I don't want to promise one that slips. Most people improve over a few weeks. We'll review her every week and update you.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Weeks. OK. The kids, though..."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Would some help with the children be useful? Our social worker can call you, and you're able to ask for a carer's assessment from the council.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Yes, please. My mum's helping, but she's seventy."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"I'll ask the social worker to ring you tomorrow.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:55","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can I check I've explained it well? What will you tell your mum tonight?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Sertraline stops. Olanzapine at night, might make her hungry. Pregnancy test. No valproate. A few weeks, weekly updates. Then lithium."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Priya, does that match what you understood?","m":[7,12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"(Priya) Yes. Can I sleep now?"},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Of course. I'll write to your GP so the sertraline isn't restarted. Raj, here's the ward number. Ring any time.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Thank you. That's the first time this week I know what's happening."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and gets her consent before discussing her care with her husband","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what they understand and what worries them before explaining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates the husband's distress about the children and the week","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains mania as part of bipolar disorder in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why sertraline is stopped and warns of discontinuation symptoms","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Proposes one oral antipsychotic with dose, timing, side effects and a capped PRN plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding of treatment, records consent and explains the Section 2 position","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks pregnancy status and contraception and arranges a pregnancy test","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains why valproate is not a first choice under 55 (MHRA rules)","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Sets out baseline and weekly-to-12-week physical monitoring","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a realistic time frame with weekly review and long-term lithium plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers carer and childcare support, checks understanding by teach-back and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gets consent, stops sertraline, agrees olanzapine with clear side effects and monitoring, checks pregnancy, rules out valproate and gives an honest time frame.","borderline":"Right drug plan but misses pregnancy, monitoring or the valproate rules, or talks to the husband without checking consent.","fail":"Continues the antidepressant, starts valproate casually, promises a fixed date, or ignores the patient's own view."}},"c-2-3-4":{"setup":"Wednesday 11:30, CMHT clinic. Steve, 47, with bipolar I disorder, stable on lithium for three years, comes with his wife after six weeks of low mood. His GP suggested citalopram. Assess his depression and risk, and agree a safe treatment plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Steven Clarke, 47, outpatient with bipolar I disorder","open":"Rubbish, to be honest. Six weeks now. I can't face work.","manner":"Slowed, quiet and self-critical. Tearful when he talks about letting his family down; brightens slightly once there is a plan. His wife Helen is worried and supportive.","free":["Low for six weeks, off work","Sleeping 12 hours and still exhausted","GP suggested citalopram"],"ifasked":["Lost interest in his allotment; eating more, mostly biscuits; can't follow TV","Some mornings wishes he hadn't woken up; no plan or preparation","Spare box of lithium in the kitchen","Missing lithium about three nights a week lately","Last level eight months ago; feels cold all the time","No racing thoughts, irritability or high spells","Drives his daughter Sophie to college"],"worries":["Should I start the citalopram?","Can I still drive?","What about work?"],"rules":["Tearful about letting the family down; settles if validated","Admits missed lithium only if asked without blame","Discloses suicidal thoughts only when asked directly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Clarke, I'm Dr Lewis. Thank you both for coming. Are you happy for your wife to stay while we talk?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Yeah, she knows everything. Call me Steve."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Steve. Tell me how things have been.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Rubbish, to be honest. Six weeks now. I can't face work. I sleep twelve hours and still feel knackered."},{"t":"0:35","band":"His symptoms","w":"D","s":"What about enjoying things, appetite and concentration?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Nothing. I used to love the allotment. Haven't been. Eating loads, mostly biscuits. Can't follow the telly."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard, especially after three good years.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"That's the worst bit. I thought I'd beaten it. I feel like I've let everyone down."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"You haven't let anyone down. This is the illness coming back, not a failing, and coming in was the right thing.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When people feel this low, they sometimes feel life isn't worth living. Have you felt like that?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Some mornings I wish I just hadn't woken up."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Have you had thoughts about ending your life?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"I've thought about it. Not seriously."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Have you made a plan, or taken any steps to prepare?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"No. I couldn't do that to Helen and our Sophie."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Are there any tablets stored at home, including spare lithium?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"There's a spare box of lithium in the kitchen."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Would you be OK with Helen looking after the spare box for now?","m":[4,10]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Yeah, that's fine."},{"t":"2:15","band":"Mixed features","w":"D","s":"Any times recently when your mood felt high, your thoughts raced, or you felt wired and irritable?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"No. The opposite."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Helen, have you noticed anything like that?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"(Wife) No. He's just been flat. Not himself at all."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Lithium","w":"D","s":"How have you been getting on with your lithium? Lots of people miss doses when they're low.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"I've missed a few. Maybe three nights a week lately."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being honest. When was your last level, and your thyroid check?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Level was eight months ago. Can't remember the thyroid. I feel cold all the time."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"That's useful. Feeling cold and tired can mean an underactive thyroid, which lithium can cause, and that can drive low mood. I'd like a lithium level 12 hours after your night dose, kidney tests, thyroid and calcium. Tomorrow morning?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"OK. Before my cup of tea."},{"t":"3:35","band":"Treatment","w":"P","s":"My GP mentioned citalopram. Should I start that?"},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"In bipolar disorder, an antidepressant on its own can push your mood too high, into mania, or make it swing faster. So we avoid it alone.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"So what then?"},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"There are a few options. I'd suggest quetiapine alongside your lithium, taken at bedtime. Start at 50 mg and build up to 300 mg by day four.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Side effects?"},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Sleepiness and dizziness on standing at first, and some weight gain. Other options are olanzapine with fluoxetine, or lamotrigine, which is slower to build up. What do you think?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Quetiapine. I'm sleeping loads anyway."},{"t":"4:35","band":"Safety and practical","w":"D","s":"Let's write a short safety plan. What are your warning signs, and who would you turn to?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Staying in bed all day. I'd tell Helen, or ring my brother."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Good. Then the crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, or Samaritans on 116 123. And 999 if you feel you might act.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Can I still drive? I take Sophie to college."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Not for now. With poor concentration and these thoughts, you shouldn't drive, and you need to tell the DVLA. Most people drive again once they've been well for a while.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"And work?"},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"I'll give you a fit note today. When you're ready, with your consent, I can write to occupational health about a phased return.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"Helen, please watch for him getting more hopeless or talking about wishing you weren't here, or his mood swinging high. Here's our duty number.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"(Wife) Thank you. I've been scared to leave him on his own."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"That's understandable. You can ask the council for a carer's assessment, and Bipolar UK run groups for families.","m":[3,12]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"And once you've a bit more energy, I'll refer you for psychological therapy for bipolar depression. Steve, can you tell me the plan in your words?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Bloods tomorrow. Lithium every night. Quetiapine. No citalopram. No driving. Safety plan."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Spot on. I'll write to your GP to hold the citalopram, and see you within two weeks. Ring us sooner if things get worse.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks consent for his wife to stay and opens with an open question","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores mood, interest, sleep, appetite, concentration and functioning","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his distress and guilt with specific empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans, preparations and access to tablets","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks for mixed or hypomanic features, including from his wife","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks lithium adherence, last level and symptoms of hypothyroidism","d":"Content"},{"c":"Orders a 12-hour lithium level, U&Es, eGFR, thyroid tests and calcium","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains why citalopram alone risks switching to mania or rapid cycling","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a NICE option with dose, titration and side effects, and names alternatives","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a written safety plan with means safety and crisis numbers","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives DVLA and work advice, including a fit note","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives his wife warning signs, a contact number and carer support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers psychological therapy, uses teach-back, books review within 2 weeks and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Asks about suicide directly, checks lithium and thyroid, steers away from an SSRI alone, agrees quetiapine and a safety plan, and covers driving, work and his wife.","borderline":"Sound drug plan but misses adherence, thyroid or mixed features, or gives no written safety plan.","fail":"Starts citalopram alone, never asks about suicide, or misses the lithium level and thyroid."}},"c-2-3-5":{"setup":"Friday 15:00, ward family room. Laura, 31, recovering from her second manic episode, starts lithium soon and asks why she needs constant blood tests and whether it is dangerous. Her mother is with her, by agreement. Explain lithium and agree a safe start.","role":{"who":"Ms Laura Bennett, 31, inpatient recovering from mania","open":"Why do I need blood tests all the time? And is lithium dangerous?","manner":"Bright, engaged and a little anxious. Overwhelmed partway through by the amount of information; calmer once told it is all written down. Her mother Anne asks practical questions.","free":["Wants her mother present","A friend told her lithium 'wrecks your kidneys' and is 'poison'","Never wants to be admitted again"],"ifasked":["Has a contraceptive implant; not planning pregnancy yet","Takes ibuprofen for period pain","Goes to the gym most days","Not on any other regular medicines","No kidney or heart problems she knows of"],"worries":["Is lithium dangerous?","What about my kidneys?","What if I get sick or it's hot?"],"rules":["Becomes overwhelmed halfway; settles if told she doesn't need to remember everything","Repeats warning signs correctly if asked","Mother asks what to do if Laura is too confused to tell anyone"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Laura, I'm Dr Morgan. And you must be Laura's mum. Laura, are you happy for your mum to stay while we talk about lithium?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Yes, I want her here. Why do I need blood tests all the time? And is lithium dangerous?"},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"Both good questions. What have you heard about it so far?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"My friend said it wrecks your kidneys, and it's poison if you take too much. I'm scared, honestly."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"It makes sense to be scared when you've heard that. Lithium needs care, and I'll be straight with you about the risks.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","band":"Why lithium","w":"D","s":"Lithium is one of the best medicines for preventing future highs and lows. It also lowers the risk of suicide in bipolar disorder.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"After this time, I never want to be back here."},{"t":"1:10","band":"Levels","w":"D","s":"The tricky part is the dose. Too little doesn't work, too much is toxic. So we aim for a blood level between 0.6 and 0.8.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"So that's what the blood tests are for."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Exactly. You take it once at bedtime, and the test is the next morning, 12 hours later. It's Priadel 400 mg, and always the same brand, because brands aren't interchangeable.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"OK. How often are the tests?"},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Before you start: kidneys, thyroid, calcium, blood count and weight. Then a level one week after starting and a week after each change of dose, until levels are stable. Then three-monthly for the first year, with kidneys and thyroid every six months.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"That's a lot."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"It is at first. It gets easier. You'll have a record book to keep track of every result.","m":[5,11]},{"t":"2:25","band":"Side effects","w":"D","s":"Common early effects are a fine shake in your hands, thirst, passing more urine, a metallic taste and mild sickness. These often settle.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"And the kidney thing?"},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Over years, lithium can affect the kidneys and thyroid. That's why we check them, so any change is caught early.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:55","band":"Toxicity","w":"D","s":"Now the most important part. Signs the level is too high: vomiting, diarrhoea, a coarse shake, unsteadiness, confusion or slurred speech. If any happen, stop taking the lithium and get urgent help. Ring 111 or go to A&E and say you're on lithium.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"(Mother) What if she's too confused to tell anyone?"},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"That's where you come in. If she seems confused, unsteady or very sick, call 111 or take her to A&E and tell them she's on lithium.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"And if you have a stomach bug or can't keep fluids down, stop it and ring us or your GP. That's the sick-day rule.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"What about summer? I'm at the gym most days."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Drink steadily through the day, more in heat or exercise, and avoid crash diets or suddenly cutting salt. Dehydration pushes the level up.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:05","band":"Other medicines","w":"D","s":"Some medicines raise lithium levels. Avoid anti-inflammatory painkillers like ibuprofen and naproxen; paracetamol is fine. Some blood pressure tablets, ACE inhibitors and ARBs, and thiazide water tablets do the same. Always tell a pharmacist or doctor you're on lithium.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"I take ibuprofen for period pain."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Swap to paracetamol. If a doctor ever has to start one of those medicines, your level should be rechecked within a week.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:40","band":"Pregnancy and stopping","w":"D","s":"Are you using contraception, or planning a pregnancy?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"I've got the implant. Nothing planned."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Good. Lithium can affect a baby's heart in early pregnancy, so if you plan one, talk to us first. And never stop it suddenly, because that can bring mania back.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Sorry, this is a lot. I'm feeling a bit overwhelmed."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"That's completely normal. You don't need to remember it all now. It's all in writing, and we'll go over it again.","m":[2]},{"t":"5:25","band":"Paperwork and GP","w":"D","s":"Here's your lithium pack: the leaflet, an alert card and the record book. I'll send a shared care plan to your GP and ask them to flag the painkiller warning on your record.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"(Mother) Where should we keep it?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Somewhere you both know, and bring the book to every blood test.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:55","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the warning signs and what you'd do?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Being sick, diarrhoea, a bad shake, wobbly, confused or slurring. Stop it and ring 111 or go to A&E, and say lithium."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Perfect. And the sick-day rule?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"If I can't keep fluids down, stop it and ring you or the GP."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Baseline bloods today, first dose tonight, level in one week. We'll see you within 72 hours of going home. Any other questions?","m":[5,12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. I feel much better about it now. Thanks."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms she wants her mother present, and asks what she knows","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her fear and feeling overwhelmed","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains why lithium is used, including relapse prevention and lower suicide risk","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the 0.6-0.8 target and the 12-hour post-dose level in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees baseline bloods, day 7 level, weekly after changes, then 3- and 6-monthly checks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Describes common side effects and the long-term kidney and thyroid risk honestly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Teaches toxicity signs and what to do, including for her mother","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains the sick-day rule and steady fluid intake","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Lists NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, ARBs and thiazides as interacting drugs","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Covers same-brand prescribing, pregnancy planning and never stopping suddenly","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives the lithium pack and record book and sends a shared care plan to the GP","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Uses teach-back for warning signs and sick-day rule and closes clearly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Explains lithium honestly in plain words, sets out levels and monitoring, teaches toxicity signs and interactions, and confirms by teach-back with written materials.","borderline":"Accurate but lecturing, or misses interactions, the sick-day rule or pregnancy.","fail":"Wrong level timing, no toxicity advice, no plan for who monitors, or dismisses her fears."}},"c-2-4-1":{"setup":"It is 02:15 in A&E. Leah Turner, 28, is here for the third time in six weeks with chest tightness and a racing heart. Tests are normal. Take a panic and agoraphobia history and agree a plan for discharge.","role":{"who":"Ms Leah Turner, 28, woman, patient in A&E","open":"I don't know why they've sent a psychiatrist. It's my heart.","manner":"Tired, embarrassed and defensive at first, sure it is her heart. Softens and becomes tearful when her fear is taken seriously; relieved once the panic cycle makes sense.","free":["Tonight's attack started in bed: pounding heart, tight chest, tingling hands","She thought she was dying","This is her third visit in six weeks","She can no longer go to the supermarket or take the bus alone"],"ifasked":["The attack peaked in about five minutes and eased after about 20","The first attack came out of the blue in a supermarket","She checks her pulse and worries about the next attack all day","Her partner Sam does the school run; she feels safer when he is with her","Three or four coffees a day; no drugs","Half a bottle of wine most nights, more at weekends, to settle herself","No weight change, tremor or palpitations when calm; no chest pain on exertion","Low and fed up at times, but no thoughts of ending her life"],"worries":["Have they missed something wrong with my heart?","Am I wasting A&E's time?","Can't you just give me something to calm me down?","I feel like a useless mum"],"rules":["Insists it is her heart until the doctor explains the body's alarm in plain words","Becomes tearful about her children; settles if her fear is validated","Only mentions the wine if asked directly about alcohol","Asks for diazepam; accepts therapy if told clearly why tablets are not the answer"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Patel, one of the psychiatry doctors. I look at how stress and the body affect each other. Are you comfortable in here? It's quieter than the cubicle.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"It's fine. I'm Leah. Honestly, I don't know why they've sent a psychiatrist. It's my heart."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"That's fair to wonder. You've had three frightening visits in six weeks, so the team asked me to help. I won't rush you, and we'll make a plan before you go home. Is that OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"OK. I just want to know what's wrong with me."},{"t":"0:38","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Tell me about tonight's attack, from the very first sign.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"I was lying in bed. My heart just started thumping. Then my chest went tight, I couldn't get a breath, and my hands were tingling."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"How quickly did it build to its worst, and how long before it eased?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"Really fast. Five minutes, maybe. Then it eased after twenty or so. By the time the ambulance came I was shaky but better."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"At its worst, what did you think was going to happen?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"I thought I was dying. Honestly. I thought I'd have a heart attack and the kids would find me."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. Thinking you might die with your children in the house is a dreadful feeling. It makes sense that you called for help.","m":[8]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I keep saying sorry to everyone. I feel like I'm wasting A&E's time."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"You're not wasting anyone's time. You were frightened and you came for help. Do the attacks come out of the blue, or only in certain places?","m":[8,3]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"Both, now. The first one was in Tesco, out of nowhere. Tonight I was in bed."},{"t":"1:53","w":"D","s":"Between attacks, how much do you worry about the next one?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"All the time. I check my pulse. I keep thinking it's going to happen again."},{"t":"2:04","w":"D","s":"Have you started avoiding places because of that?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"The supermarket. The bus. Sam does the school run now. I can't go out on my own."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"Do you feel safer if someone is with you?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"Yes. If Sam's there I can just about manage. I feel like a useless mum."},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard, and it isn't your fault. Avoiding places protects you in the short term, but it makes your world smaller. We can work on getting it back.","m":[8,4]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"(nods) Thanks. It is hard."},{"t":"2:39","band":"Physical causes","w":"D","s":"I've read your notes. Your ECG, the heart blood test and your sugar are all normal tonight. Do you know if they checked your thyroid?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"They took loads of blood. I don't know what for."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"I'll check that with the A&E doctor before you go. When you're calm, do you notice a racing heart, shaking, weight loss, feeling hot, or chest pain on the stairs?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"No. My weight's the same, and no pain on stairs. It's only when the attacks come."},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"How much coffee or energy drinks do you have in a day? And alcohol, or any other drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Three or four coffees. No drugs. And a few glasses of wine most nights. It's the only thing that settles me."},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Wine calms you at first, but as it wears off it can set off attacks, often at night. Roughly how much is a few?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Half a bottle. Maybe more at weekends."},{"t":"3:43","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When people feel this trapped, they can get very low. How's your mood been, and have you had any thoughts about ending your life?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"Fed up, sometimes. But no. Never. I've got my kids."},{"t":"3:58","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Can I explain what I think is happening? These are panic attacks. Your symptoms are real. It's the body's alarm going off when there's no danger.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"But it feels like my heart. How can it not be my heart?"},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"Adrenaline makes your heart race and your breathing speed up. You notice it and think, 'I'm dying.' That fear makes more adrenaline, so it spirals. Let me draw it as a circle.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:27","w":"P","s":"So the fear makes it worse?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Then you avoid places in case it happens, and your world shrinks. The attack always peaks and passes. Nobody dies from a panic attack.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"That actually makes sense. That's what happened in Tesco."},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"To check I've explained it well, could you tell me in your words how the circle works?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"My heart races, I think it's a heart attack, I panic more, so it gets worse. Then I stay at home."},{"t":"5:00","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Now, what would help you most?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:04","w":"P","s":"Can't you just give me something to calm me down when it starts?"},{"t":"5:09","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting fast relief. Tablets like diazepam help briefly, but they're addictive and make panic worse in the long run, so we don't use them. A talking therapy called CBT works best and lasts.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:24","w":"P","s":"And if I need tablets?"},{"t":"5:26","w":"D","s":"Your GP can start a daily tablet called sertraline at a low dose. It can make you a bit jittery for the first week or two, then that settles.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:38","w":"P","s":"I'd rather try the therapy first."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"Good plan. You can refer yourself to NHS Talking Therapies. I'll write down the website and number. I'll also write to your GP today about the thyroid test, the therapy and cutting down the wine.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Will they see me quickly?"},{"t":"5:57","w":"D","s":"Waits vary, so I'll give you a leaflet on slow breathing and self-help to start now. Could Sam help with short trips out, rather than doing everything for you?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"Yeah. He'd like that, actually."},{"t":"6:11","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"If you get chest pain when you exert yourself, you faint, or the pain feels different from your attacks, call 999 or come back. That isn't panic and needs checking.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:23","w":"P","s":"And if it's a normal attack?"},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"Slow your breathing, stay where you are and let it pass. To sum up: these are panic attacks, your heart is healthy tonight, therapy is the main treatment, and your GP gets a letter today. Any questions?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"No. I feel a lot better knowing what it is."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. I'll check that thyroid result now, then you can go home with Sam.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the liaison role plainly and checks she is comfortable","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes a timeline of the last attack: first sign, time to peak, duration and feared outcome","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks whether attacks are unexpected and how much she worries about the next one","d":"Content"},{"c":"Maps avoided places and whether she needs a companion to go out","d":"Content"},{"c":"Confirms ECG, troponin, glucose and thyroid test, and asks about symptoms when calm and on exertion","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about caffeine, alcohol and drugs, and quantifies alcohol without judgement","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about mood and suicidal thoughts and records the answer","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her fear and distress in her own terms","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains panic as a false alarm and draws the panic cycle in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks her to explain the panic cycle back to check understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Declines diazepam with a clear reason and offers CBT, with an SSRI as an option","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives NHS Talking Therapies self-referral details, self-help and a same-day GP letter","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets: chest pain on exertion, fainting or different pain need urgent review","d":"Close"},{"c":"Summarises, invites questions and closes with a clear next step","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Clear attack timeline, physical causes confirmed, suicide asked, panic cycle explained and taught back, and she leaves with self-referral details and a safety-net.","borderline":"Good history but the explanation is rushed, or the thyroid test, alcohol or the safety-net is missed.","fail":"Tells her it is 'just anxiety', prescribes diazepam, skips the suicide question or sends her home with no plan."}},"c-2-4-2":{"setup":"You are in a CMHT outpatient clinic. Ryan Clarke, 22, a university student, was referred with 'GAD, not responding to sertraline 50 mg'. His mother has come too. Clarify GAD versus social anxiety and agree a treatment plan with him.","role":{"who":"Mr Ryan Clarke, 22, man, university student, with his mother Mrs Karen Clarke","open":"(Mother) He won't tell you properly. He's always been shy. Let me explain.","manner":"Ryan is quiet, blushes, avoids eye contact and gives short answers while his mother talks over him. Opens up when seen alone and treated with respect.","free":["He has missed most seminars this term and fears failing the year","He takes sertraline 50 mg and says it has not worked","His face goes red and his mind goes blank when he has to speak"],"ifasked":["He has taken sertraline daily for about five weeks; mild nausea at first","His worry is mainly about people judging him, not about many everyday things","It started around year eight at school and worsened at university","He rehearses sentences, sits at the back and avoids eye contact","He drinks three or four cans before going out, about twice a week; his mother does not know","No drugs","Low mood; sometimes thinks everyone would be better off without him; no plans; his mother and sister and his degree hold him back"],"worries":["Can I try something stronger, like pregabalin?","Will therapy make me stand up and give a speech?","Is this going to stop me finishing my degree?"],"rules":["Mother answers for him unless the doctor politely redirects her","Only discloses drinking and dark thoughts when seen alone","Asks for pregabalin; accepts a higher sertraline dose and CBT if the reasons are clear","Asks that his mother is not told about the drinking or the dark thoughts"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Ryan? I'm Dr Okafor, one of the psychiatrists. Thanks for coming in. And you must be Ryan's mum?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"(Mother) Yes, Karen. He won't tell you properly. He's always been shy. Let me explain."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Karen. Your view really helps, and I'll come to you. First I'd like to hear from Ryan in his own words. Ryan, would you like your mum to stay for now?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"(quietly, looking down) She can stay. For now."},{"t":"0:36","w":"D","s":"OK. Later I'll offer you some time on your own, which I do with everyone. We've got about forty minutes. Is that alright?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Fine."},{"t":"0:48","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"Your GP says you're on sertraline. How much, for how long, and do you take it every day? Any side effects?","m":[9]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"Fifty milligrams, every day, about five weeks. Felt a bit sick at first. It hasn't really done anything."},{"t":"1:06","w":"D","s":"Thanks. On a typical day, what worries you most?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"Uni. Seminars, mainly. Having to speak."},{"t":"1:14","w":"D","s":"Is the worry about lots of things, like money, health or family? Or is it mainly about being with other people?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"Mainly people. I don't really worry about other stuff. Just people looking at me."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"What do you fear will happen in a seminar?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"That I'll say something stupid. My face goes red, my hands shake, my mind goes blank. (pause) They all think I'm an idiot."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"That sounds horrible, Ryan. Lots of people feel exactly that, and it takes courage to say it out loud.","m":[10]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"I've stopped going. I'm going to fail the year."},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"How long have you felt like this? Was it there at school?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"(Mother) He was like this at school. He wouldn't even answer the register."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Karen. Ryan, is that how you remember it?","m":[7,1]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Around year eight. It got worse when I moved away for uni."},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"Karen, would you mind waiting outside for a few minutes? I'll bring you back in at the end.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"(Mother) Of course. (leaves)"},{"t":"2:31","band":"Coping and risk","w":"D","s":"When you do have to face people, what do you do to get through it?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:39","w":"P","s":"I rehearse what I'll say, over and over. I sit at the back. I don't look at anyone."},{"t":"2:47","w":"D","s":"Anything else that helps, like a drink beforehand?","m":[6,8]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"(hesitates) I have a few drinks before I go out. Otherwise I can't face anyone. Mum doesn't know."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That's really common. How much, and how often? Anything else, like drugs?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"Three or four cans, maybe twice a week. No drugs."},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"Alcohol helps for the evening, but the anxiety is often worse the next day. How's your mood been?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"Pretty low. I feel like a failure."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Have you had felt that life isn't worth living, or thoughts about ending your life?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think everyone'd be better off without me. I wouldn't do anything. I've never planned anything."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you've told me. Feeling that low is hard. What stops you acting on those thoughts?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"My mum. My little sister. And I do want to finish my degree."},{"t":"3:54","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"From what you've told me, the main problem is social anxiety: a strong fear of being judged by others. It's different from GAD, which is worry about lots of everyday things.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"So it's not GAD?"},{"t":"4:11","w":"D","s":"Not mainly. Rehearsing, sitting at the back and drinking first feel safe. But they stop you finding out that people judge far less than you fear.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"The sertraline didn't work, though. Could I try something stronger, like pregabalin?"},{"t":"4:29","w":"D","s":"I understand you want something that works. Fifty milligrams for five weeks isn't a full trial. Pregabalin isn't the first choice here, and it can be habit-forming.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"So what do I do instead?"},{"t":"4:45","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'd suggest raising sertraline to 100 milligrams and reviewing in about four weeks. The main treatment is a one-to-one CBT made for social anxiety.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"Will they make me stand up and give a speech?"},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"No. You and the therapist plan small steps together, starting with things you feel you could manage. You stay in control of the pace.","m":[12,10]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"OK. And uni?"},{"t":"5:14","w":"D","s":"With your consent, I'll write to student support about extensions and seminar adjustments. Would that be OK?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"Yeah. That would really help."},{"t":"5:24","w":"D","s":"Just to check I've been clear, can you tell me the plan in your words?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:32","w":"P","s":"Higher dose of sertraline, CBT for social anxiety, a letter to uni. And cut down the drinking before going out."},{"t":"5:41","w":"D","s":"Spot on. What would you like me to tell your mum?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:47","w":"P","s":"The plan. Not the drinking or the dark thoughts. I'll tell her myself."},{"t":"5:53","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's your choice, and I'll respect it unless I was worried about your safety. If you ever feel unsafe, call NHS 111 and choose the mental health option, or Samaritans on 116 123.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"OK. (Mother returns) (Mother) So what's going on? I thought it was just shyness."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Ryan described things really well. It's social anxiety, and it's very treatable. The best help is to encourage him to go to therapy and let him answer for himself.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:29","w":"P","s":"(Mother) I can do that. I just want him to finish his degree."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"So do we. I'll write to the GP and see Ryan again in four weeks. Any questions, either of you?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks. It's the first time it's actually made sense."}],"marks":[{"c":"Greets him first and asks him how he wants his mother involved","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Politely redirects his mother and sees him alone for part of the appointment","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Opens with a question about what worries him on a typical day","d":"Content"},{"c":"Separates fear of judgement from general worry with a targeted question","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores feared outcome and physical symptoms in social situations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Identifies at least two safety behaviours","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about onset and course, including school years","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol, drugs, mood and suicidal thoughts, and what holds him back","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks sertraline dose, duration, adherence and side effects","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his shame and low mood without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains social anxiety, safety behaviours, the short SSRI trial and why not pregabalin","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees CBT for social anxiety, sertraline increase and a student support letter with consent","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding and agrees what is shared with his mother","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets with crisis numbers and closes with follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Speaks to him first, sees him alone, separates social anxiety from GAD, finds safety behaviours and risk, and agrees CBT, a fair SSRI trial and student support.","borderline":"Right diagnosis, but the mother dominates, alcohol or suicide is missed, or the plan is vague.","fail":"Lets the mother answer throughout, labels it GAD without exploring, or switches to pregabalin."}},"c-2-4-3":{"setup":"You are in the perinatal mental health clinic. Hannah Reid, 32, has a 4-month-old daughter and checks the house for hours a day. Her health visitor referred her. Elicit obsessions and compulsions, assess severity and risk, and explain ERP.","role":{"who":"Mrs Hannah Reid, 32, woman, mother of 4-month-old Isla","open":"Before anything... are you going to take my baby away?","manner":"Exhausted, tearful and frightened of being judged. Hesitant to describe the thoughts. Visibly relieved when told honestly that OCD thoughts alone are not a safeguarding matter.","free":["She checks the oven, plugs and door locks over and over","She no longer bathes Isla alone","She is terrified social services will be called","Isla is with her mother in the waiting room and is feeding well"],"ifasked":["She gets pictures of Isla drowning in the bath and of the house catching fire","The thoughts are her own; she hates them and pushes them away","She has never wanted them to happen or felt any urge to act","Checking takes three to four hours a day and sometimes delays feeds","Her partner Tom reassures her and checks the oven for her","Distress is 9 out of 10","Mood is low and tired but she still enjoys Isla; no suicidal thoughts","No voices and no sense that thoughts come from outside; she is breastfeeding"],"worries":["Are you going to take my baby away?","What kind of mother thinks things like that?","Will tablets get into my breast milk?","I can't just stop checking"],"rules":["Only describes the thoughts after being told nothing will shock the doctor","Calls herself disgusting; settles if the doctor validates without false promises","Asks for a promise that she won't hurt Isla; accepts the reason reassurance is not given","Chooses a small first ERP step if offered a choice"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Hannah. I'm Dr Shah from the perinatal mental health team. Thanks for coming in.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Before anything... are you going to take my baby away?"},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"I can hear how frightened you are. No, that's not why you're here. Unwanted thoughts are very common after a baby, and my job is to help you get well and look after her.","m":[1,9]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I've been dreading this all week."},{"t":"0:32","w":"D","s":"That must have been an awful week. Where's your baby now?","m":[9,2]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"With my mum in the waiting room. Isla's fine. She's feeding well."},{"t":"0:43","w":"D","s":"Good. What you tell me stays in your care team. I'd only share more widely if someone was at real risk, and I'd talk to you first.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"0:57","band":"Obsessions","w":"D","s":"Many new parents get thoughts or pictures that pop into their mind and upset them. Does that happen to you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"All the time."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what they're like? Nothing you say will shock me.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"(long pause) I get pictures of her drowning in the bath. Or the house on fire with her in it."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. Do they feel like your own thoughts, even though you don't want them?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"They're mine, I know they are. But I hate them. I try to push them away."},{"t":"1:41","w":"D","s":"Have you ever wanted them to happen, or felt an urge to act on them?","m":[7]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"No! God, no. That's the worst thing I can imagine. That's why I'm so scared."},{"t":"1:56","band":"Compulsions and impact","w":"D","s":"That fear tells me a lot. What do you do when the thoughts come?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"I check. The oven, the plugs, the door locks. Over and over till it feels right."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"What do you imagine would happen if you didn't check?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"The house would burn down and it'd be my fault."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"How many hours a day does the checking take?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Three, four hours. Sometimes I'm late with her feeds because I'm still checking."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"And bathing her?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"I don't bathe her on my own anymore. I wait for Tom. (crying) What kind of mother thinks things like that? I'm disgusting."},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"You're not disgusting. These thoughts horrify you because you love her. OCD tends to attack whatever matters most.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:52","w":"P","s":"Really?"},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"Really. Do you ask Tom to tell you things are safe?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"Constantly. He checks the oven for me too. He's exhausted."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"From 0 to 10, how distressing is all this?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"Nine. I'm shattered."},{"t":"3:09","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Have you ever felt thoughts were put into your head from outside, or heard voices others can't?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"No. It's just me, my own head."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been since Isla was born? Can you still enjoy her?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"Low and tired. But I love being with her when I'm not stuck in it."},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"Have you had felt that life isn't worth living, or thoughts of hurting yourself?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"No. I just want this to stop."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'm not worried you'll hurt Isla, and I'll write down why. Thoughts you hate and fight aren't a reason to involve social services. I'd only think again if they ever felt acceptable, or you wanted to act on them.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"(breathes out) I thought you'd call them the minute I told you."},{"t":"4:10","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"What you have is OCD. The thoughts are obsessions. The checking and avoiding are compulsions: they bring relief for a moment, then the fear comes back.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"Just tell me she's safe. Promise me I won't hurt her."},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"I understand why you want me to say it. But reassurance works like checking: it helps for a moment, then the doubt grows. What I can say is that this is OCD, and it's treatable.","m":[10,9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"So every time Tom checks for me, it makes it worse?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"In the long run, yes. The treatment is exposure and response prevention, or ERP. With a therapist, you face the fear step by step without checking, and the anxiety falls on its own.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"I can't just stop checking. I can't."},{"t":"5:09","w":"D","s":"We won't ask you to stop everything at once. You pick one small step, like checking something once and walking away. You notice the anxiety rise, then fall.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:23","w":"P","s":"Maybe the plugs. That's less scary than the oven."},{"t":"5:27","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"That's a great first step. Our psychologist will see you weekly and build up from there. Tom can learn to stop checking for you, too.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Will tablets get into my breast milk?"},{"t":"5:43","w":"D","s":"Good question. Sertraline is one of the most used options when breastfeeding, as very little reaches the milk. You wouldn't need to stop feeding. Your health visitor would check Isla's feeding and weight.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:59","w":"P","s":"I'd rather start with the therapy. But I'll think about the tablet."},{"t":"6:05","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's fine. We can start it whenever you decide. Could you sum up what we've agreed, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[12,14]},{"t":"6:16","w":"P","s":"It's OCD. You're not worried about Isla. Therapy with ERP, starting with the plugs. Tom stops reassuring me. Maybe sertraline."},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"Exactly. With your OK, I'll update your health visitor and GP. If your mood drops, the thoughts change, or you feel unsafe, call our team or NHS 111, option 2.","m":[14,2]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"OK. Thank you. I feel like I can breathe."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'll see you in two weeks, and the psychologist will be in touch before then.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and answers her fear about the baby first, honestly and calmly","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks who is caring for the baby and explains confidentiality and its limits","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Normalises intrusive thoughts and asks about their actual content without judgement","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes the thoughts are her own, unwanted and resisted","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits checking, avoidance, reassurance-seeking and the feared consequence","d":"Content"},{"c":"Measures hours per day, distress and impact on caring for the baby","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about urges to act, intent and psychotic features","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens mood, postnatal depression and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her guilt and fear without giving repeated reassurance","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains OCD and how checking and reassurance keep it going","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains ERP in plain words and agrees a small first step with her","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Discusses sertraline and breastfeeding accurately","d":"Plan"},{"c":"States honestly that OCD thoughts alone need no safeguarding referral, and when that would change","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding, safety-nets and closes with follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gets the content of the thoughts calmly, rates severity, rules out intent and psychosis, explains ERP with a first step, and is honest about safeguarding.","borderline":"Good rapport but avoids asking what the thoughts are, or explains ERP vaguely, or gives repeated reassurance.","fail":"Makes a child protection referral for OCD thoughts, misses risk screening, or never asks what the thoughts are."}},"c-2-4-4":{"setup":"You are in a CMHT assessment clinic. Jordan Ellis, 19, wants a GP letter for NHS rhinoplasty; the GP sees no abnormality. He wears a cap and mask and has left college. Assess for BDD and risk, and respond to his surgery request.","role":{"who":"Mr Jordan Ellis, 19, man, patient","open":"I don't know why I'm here. I asked my GP for a letter for surgery.","manner":"Guarded, keeps cap and mask on, irritable when he senses he is being dismissed. Opens up when not argued with; low and quiet when talking about loneliness.","free":["He believes his nose is crooked with a big bump","He wants an NHS surgery letter","He left college in March because people stared"],"ifasked":["Six to seven hours a day thinking about his nose","Checks mirrors, car windows and phone camera; compares side photos with people online","Never goes out without cap and mask","Conviction 100%, wavers slightly when asked if it might look less bad to others","Low mood, poor sleep, sees nobody; sometimes thinks he would be better off dead; no plans; would not do that to his mum","Has watched videos on breaking and resetting his own nose; has looked at clinics in Turkey but has no money","Some worry about his skin; no eating or weight concerns; no other compulsions; was shy before but not like now"],"worries":["Don't tell me you can't see it","So you think I'm mad?","Will you write the letter?","Will tablets change how my nose looks to me?"],"rules":["Challenges the doctor to look at his nose; becomes angry if told it looks fine","Only mentions the DIY videos if asked about fixing it himself or going abroad","Bargains for the letter; accepts the refusal if it is explained without arguing about his nose","Becomes quiet and sad about relationships; discloses suicidal thoughts if asked gently"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Jordan, I'm Dr Ahmed, one of the psychiatrists. Keep your cap and mask on if that's more comfortable.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"I will. (pause) I don't know why I'm here. I asked my GP for a letter for surgery."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Your GP asked me to see you because you're clearly very distressed about your nose, and it's taking over your life. I'd like to understand. Tell me what you notice about it.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"It's crooked. There's a bump. It's massive from the side. Just look at it. Don't tell me you can't see it."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"I won't argue about how it looks. I can see how much distress it causes you, and that's what I want to help with.","m":[9,2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"1:01","band":"His concern","w":"D","s":"How much of your day goes on thinking about it?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"Most of it. Six, seven hours."},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"Do you check it in mirrors, take photos, or compare it with other people?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"Mirrors, car windows, my phone. I take side photos and compare them with people online."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"And do you try to hide it?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"Cap and mask, always. I won't go out without them. I left college in March. Everyone stared."},{"t":"1:37","w":"D","s":"What do you think people notice?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"They look at it. They're disgusted."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"How sure are you, from 0 to 100, that it looks the way you describe?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"A hundred. It's not in my head."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Is there any chance it looks less bad to others than it does to you?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"No. Well... my mum says it's fine. But she has to say that."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"What would surgery change for you?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"Everything. I could go back to college. Get a girlfriend. Have a life."},{"t":"2:20","band":"Mood and risk","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"Rubbish. I don't sleep. I don't see anyone. Nobody will ever want to be with me looking like this."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"That sounds lonely and really painful. Have those feelings ever led to thoughts about ending your life?","m":[9,7]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"(quiet) Yeah. Sometimes I think I'd be better off dead than looking like this."},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you made a plan, or taken any steps to hurt yourself?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"No plans. I wouldn't do that to my mum."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"Some people try to fix it themselves, or look at surgery abroad. Have you?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"(pause) I've watched videos on how to break and reset your own nose. And I've looked at clinics in Turkey."},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you told me. Breaking it yourself could badly injure you. Have you tried, or got anything ready?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"No. I just watched. I haven't got the money for Turkey yet."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"OK. Do you worry about other parts of your body, your weight or your muscles?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"My skin a bit. Not my weight. I eat normally."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Any other things you feel you have to do, like washing or checking things around the house?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"No. Just the nose stuff."},{"t":"4:02","w":"D","s":"And before all this, were you anxious around people?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"A bit shy. Not like now."},{"t":"4:10","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Can I tell you what I think? This sounds like body dysmorphic disorder, or BDD. The brain locks onto one feature and magnifies it.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"So you think I'm mad? That it's all in my head?"},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"No. BDD is real and common. It isn't vanity. Your distress is real, and it responds to treatment.","m":[10,9]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"So will you write the letter?"},{"t":"4:41","w":"D","s":"I won't write a surgery letter. Most people with BDD stay unhappy after surgery, or the worry moves to another part. Surgery isn't a treatment for BDD.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"If you write it, I'll do whatever therapy you want afterwards."},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"I understand how much you want this. Treating the preoccupation first gives you the best chance of feeling better. Private surgery is your choice, but my advice is not now.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:17","w":"P","s":"(sighs) What's the treatment, then?"},{"t":"5:20","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"A specific CBT for BDD that includes ERP. You'd work on cutting down the checking and hiding, a step at a time.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:31","w":"P","s":"Will tablets change how my nose looks to me?"},{"t":"5:36","w":"D","s":"Not overnight. Fluoxetine can ease the pull to check and the distress, usually over 8 to 12 weeks. As you're under 25, I'd see you within a week of starting, in case it makes you agitated.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"OK. I'll try it."},{"t":"5:57","w":"D","s":"Let's write a safety plan together. What helps when the thoughts get dark?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:04","w":"P","s":"Gaming with my mates online. Talking to Mum."},{"t":"6:08","w":"D","s":"Good. If you feel unsafe, call NHS 111 and choose option 2, or Samaritans on 116 123. And please don't try anything on your nose yourself.","m":[13,8]},{"t":"6:22","w":"P","s":"I won't."},{"t":"6:23","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:29","w":"P","s":"It's BDD. No letter for now. CBT and fluoxetine. Call 111 if it gets bad."},{"t":"6:37","w":"D","s":"That's it. I'll write to your GP today and see you next week. Any questions?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. It's weird. You're the first one who didn't just tell me my nose is fine."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the referral plainly and lets him keep cap and mask on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores his concern and what surgery would change, without arguing or reassuring","d":"Content"},{"c":"Measures time preoccupied, mirror checking, photos and comparing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about camouflage, avoidance and impact on college and social life","d":"Content"},{"c":"Rates strength of conviction and gently tests insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for depression, OCD, social anxiety and eating or muscle concerns","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts and plans","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about DIY procedures or surgery abroad and access to means","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his distress without confirming or debating the defect","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains BDD as a real, common, treatable condition","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains honestly why surgery is unlikely to help and declines the letter","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees CBT with ERP for BDD and fluoxetine with early review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a written safety plan with crisis numbers","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, summarises and closes with follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Explores the preoccupation without arguing, asks directly about suicide and DIY surgery, declines the letter with reasons, and agrees CBT, an SSRI and a safety plan.","borderline":"Good history but the DIY or overseas question is missed, or surgery is declined bluntly without explanation.","fail":"Tells him his nose is fine, writes the surgery letter, or misses suicide risk."}},"c-2-4-5":{"setup":"You are in a CMHT clinic. Amira Hassan, 29, a refugee from Sudan, has nightmares, poor sleep and anger. A telephone interpreter is on the line; her husband offers to interpret instead. Take a trauma-informed PTSD history and agree a safe plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Amira Hassan, 29, woman, patient (speaks through a telephone interpreter; husband Mr Khalid Hassan is present at first)","open":"(Husband) I speak perfect English. I'll translate. The phone is a waste of time.","manner":"Quiet and wary at first, glancing at the door. Becomes tearful when the trauma is touched on; steadies with grounding. Warmer and more open once she feels in control.","free":["She has nightmares and hardly sleeps","She feels angry and shouts at her children","She is worried what she says will reach the Home Office"],"ifasked":["There was fighting in her town; people were killed, including her brother (she will not give detail)","Flashbacks when she hears loud bangs; nightmares four or five nights a week; about three hours' sleep","Avoids the news and the community centre","Checks the door at night; jumpy and irritable","Feels cut off, sometimes numb, and guilty that she survived","No alcohol or drugs","Sometimes wishes she would not wake up; no plans; her children keep her going","Feels safe with her husband; family of four live in one hotel room; children aged 6 and 3, the youngest wets the bed"],"worries":["Will this hurt my asylum case?","Is something wrong with my mind? People at home would say I am crazy","I don't want to talk about what happened"],"rules":["Husband offers to interpret; leaves politely if the reason is explained respectfully","Prefers a female interpreter if asked","Becomes tearful if asked about the event; settles with grounding and permission to stop","Agrees for her husband to hear the plan but not about her wish not to wake up"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Hassan, thank you for coming with your wife. We use a professional interpreter for every patient, so nothing gets lost and you don't have to carry that.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"(Husband) I speak perfect English. I'll translate. The phone is a waste of time."},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"I understand, and thank you. It's our rule for everyone. Could you wait outside for now? I'll invite you back at the end.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"(Husband) (pause) Fine. I'll be outside."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"(to the interpreter on speaker) Thank you. Can I check you speak Sudanese Arabic, and that you'll interpret everything word for word?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:49","w":"P","s":"(Interpreter) Yes, I'm Sudanese. Yes, word for word."},{"t":"0:53","w":"D","s":"(to Mrs Hassan) I'm Dr Evans. Are you comfortable with this interpreter, or would you prefer someone else?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"She is fine. A woman is better for me."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"Everything you say stays within your health care. Is anything worrying you before we start?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Will what I say go to the Home Office? Will it hurt my asylum case?"},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"No. I don't share anything with the Home Office unless you ask me to. You also don't need to tell me what happened in detail today. If it gets too much, raise your hand and we'll stop.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"OK. Thank you."},{"t":"1:44","band":"Her symptoms","w":"D","s":"Without going into details, can you tell me what kind of thing happened?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"There was fighting in our town. Soldiers came. People were killed. My brother... (stops)"},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"That's enough. You don't need to say more. I'm very sorry.","m":[10,4]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"(crying) I'm sorry."},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"Take your time. You're safe here. Can you feel your feet on the floor?","m":[10]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"(pause) Yes. I'm OK."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Do memories or pictures of it come back when you don't want them?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"If I hear a loud bang, I am back there. And every night I hear the shooting. I wake up screaming."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting and terrifying. How often do the nightmares come, and how much do you sleep?","m":[10,5,8]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"Four or five nights a week. I sleep maybe three hours."},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"Do you avoid things that remind you, like the news or certain people?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"I don't watch the news. I don't go to the community centre. Too many people talk about home."},{"t":"3:04","w":"D","s":"Do you feel on guard, jumpy, or quick to get angry?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Yes. I check the door at night. I shout at my children for nothing. I'm not the mother I was."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Feeling on edge and irritable is a common part of trauma. It doesn't make you a bad mother.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"I feel bad all the time. Like it's my fault I lived."},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"Do you feel cut off from people, or find your feelings hard to control?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"Cut off, yes. Even from my husband. Sometimes I feel nothing at all."},{"t":"3:50","band":"Risk and safety","w":"D","s":"Do you use alcohol, or anything else, to cope?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"Do you ever think about ending your life, or make plans to?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I wish I didn't wake up. But no plans. I won't do anything. My children need me."},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"Are you safe at home? Does anyone at home hurt you or frighten you?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:19","w":"P","s":"No. My husband is a good man. He is worried. He wants to help."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"And the children: how are they, and where do you live?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"Six and three. They are well, but the little one wets the bed. We are four people in one hotel room. (pause) Doctor, is something wrong with my mind? In my country, people would say I am crazy."},{"t":"4:52","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"No. This is PTSD. It's the mind's normal reaction to terrible events. Your brain is still on alert, as if the danger is happening now.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"Will it go away?"},{"t":"5:08","w":"D","s":"Many people recover with the right therapy. That's trauma-focused CBT, or a therapy called EMDR. Both are done with a trained interpreter, and you go at your pace.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"I don't want to talk about what happened. I can't."},{"t":"5:28","w":"D","s":"That's OK. You decide when. The therapist builds trust first. Meanwhile, I'll show you a grounding exercise for the flashbacks.","m":[11,10]},{"t":"5:38","w":"P","s":"OK. I can try that."},{"t":"5:41","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'll refer you to our trauma team and say clearly that you need a female Sudanese Arabic interpreter.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Thank you."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"I'll also link you with a refugee support charity and write to the housing officer about your room.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:01","w":"P","s":"That would help us a lot."},{"t":"6:04","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed, so I know I've been clear?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:11","w":"P","s":"It's PTSD, not crazy. Therapy with a woman interpreter. Help with the house."},{"t":"6:18","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If the wish not to wake up gets stronger, call NHS 111 and choose option 2, and ask for an Arabic interpreter. I'll write it down for you.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:33","w":"P","s":"OK. Thank you."},{"t":"6:34","w":"D","s":"Would you like your husband to hear the plan?","m":[3,13]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"Yes, he can come in. But not about the dying thoughts."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Of course. I'll write to your GP and see you again in four weeks.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Thanks the husband, explains professional interpreting for everyone, and sees her alone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks the interpreter's dialect and asks her preference, speaking to her directly","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains confidentiality, including the Home Office worry; says no trauma detail is needed; agrees a stop sign","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what kind of event happened without asking for detail","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about re-experiencing: flashbacks and nightmares","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about avoidance and sense of current threat: hypervigilance, startle, anger","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about complex PTSD features: self-worth, feeling cut off, emotional control","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about sleep, alcohol, suicidal thoughts and plans","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about safety at home, domestic abuse, the children and housing","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates distress and uses grounding when she is overwhelmed","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains PTSD and trauma-focused CBT or EMDR in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Refers to the trauma team stating interpreter language and gender, plus refugee and housing support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, gives crisis access with interpreter, and closes with follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Uses the professional interpreter, sees her alone, covers all PTSD clusters without digging for detail, checks risk and safety, and makes an interpreter-aware referral.","borderline":"Covers symptoms but misses complex PTSD features, home safety or housing, or the explanation is rushed.","fail":"Lets the husband interpret, asks for detailed trauma accounts, or misses suicide risk."}},"c-2-4-6":{"setup":"You are on the acute medical unit. Gary Holt, 46, was admitted with abdominal pain; last month's CT and colonoscopy were normal and he wants another scan. Agree a plan with the medical consultant, then assess for health anxiety and explain it.","role":{"who":"Mr Gary Holt, 46, man, inpatient (the medical consultant, Dr Morgan, speaks first)","open":"Psychiatry? So you think I'm making it up? The pain is real.","manner":"Defensive and frustrated, quick to feel dismissed. Grief shows when he talks about his father. Calms when the pain is accepted as real and the plan is consistent.","free":["He has abdominal pain and thinks it is bowel cancer","He wants a repeat scan","He feels every doctor tells him something different"],"ifasked":["His father died of bowel cancer at 50, missed at first; Gary was 20","The worry took off last year when a friend became ill","Hours each night searching symptoms online","Checks his stools daily, prods his abdomen, asks his wife for reassurance","Relief after normal tests lasts a few days to a week","Avoids hospital programmes and cancer adverts","Wakes at 4am worrying and has lost interest in things; no suicidal thoughts","No weight loss, no bleeding, eating normally"],"worries":["So you think I'm making it up?","Just one more scan and I'll stop worrying","What if I really get cancer and you ignore me?"],"rules":["Becomes angry if told nothing is wrong; settles when the pain is accepted as real","Becomes tearful about his father; opens up if this is acknowledged","Bargains for one more scan; accepts the refusal if asked how long the last relief lasted","Accepts the plan if red flags are written down"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Agree with the team","w":"D","s":"Dr Morgan, before I see him, can we agree one plan so we give him the same message?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"(Consultant) Please. Bloods are normal. CT and colonoscopy last month were clear. Nothing new. I'm not planning another scan."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"Thanks. So no repeat imaging unless something new appears, discharge to the GP with planned reviews, and I'll suggest CBT. Agreed?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"(Consultant) Agreed. I'll tell him the same."},{"t":"0:37","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Holt. I'm Dr Lewis from the psychiatry team. The doctors asked me to see you because worry about health can be exhausting. Is now a good time?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"Psychiatry? So you think I'm making it up? The pain is real."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"I believe your pain is real. I'm not here to say otherwise. I'd like to understand what it's been like for you.","m":[8]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"OK. Fine."},{"t":"1:13","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"What do you think is causing the pain?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"Bowel cancer. I know the scan was clear, but they can miss things."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"What worries you most about that?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"My dad died of it at fifty. They missed it with him too. (voice breaks) I was twenty."},{"t":"1:38","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. That's a huge loss, and it explains a lot of your fear.","m":[8,6]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"I'm forty-six. I keep counting."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"When did the worry really take off?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Last year, when a mate got ill. Since then, it's constant."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"How much of your day goes on thinking about your health?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"Most of it. I spend hours every night on Google checking symptoms."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"Do you check your body, or ask others for reassurance?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"I check my stools every day. I prod my belly. I ask my wife all the time. She's had enough."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"When a test comes back normal, how long does the relief last?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"A few days. A week at most. Then I think, what if they missed it?"},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Do you avoid anything, like programmes about illness?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"I can't watch hospital programmes. I turn off cancer adverts."},{"t":"2:55","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"How have your mood and sleep been?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"Bad. I wake at four, worrying. I've lost interest in things."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Have you had felt that life isn't worth living, or thoughts of ending it?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"No. I'm scared of dying. That's the whole point."},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"That makes sense. Have you had any weight loss or bleeding?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"No. Eating fine. Weight's the same. No bleeding."},{"t":"3:28","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Can I share what I think is going on? I think you have health anxiety. The pain is real, and the worry makes it louder.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:43","w":"P","s":"So it's all in my head?"},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"No. Worry affects the gut, and the gut sends real signals. Checking and searching ease the worry for a bit, then it comes back stronger. It's a cycle.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"Just one more scan and I'll stop worrying. I promise."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"I understand. After the last scan, how long did the relief last?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"...A few days."},{"t":"4:16","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Another scan gives a few days' peace and more radiation. Dr Morgan and I agree it wouldn't help, so we won't repeat it unless something new appears.","m":[10,1]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"(angry) Every doctor says something different. I don't trust any of you."},{"t":"4:38","w":"D","s":"That's frustrating, and I understand why trust is hard after your dad. That's why we've agreed one plan, and your GP will be your main doctor for this.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"4:54","w":"P","s":"What if I really do get cancer one day and you all ignore me?"},{"t":"5:02","w":"D","s":"A fair worry. Health anxiety doesn't stop you being checked. Bleeding from the bottom, weight loss or yellow skin will be seen properly. That goes in your plan.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"OK. That's fair."},{"t":"5:20","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"The treatment is CBT for health anxiety. You can refer yourself to NHS Talking Therapies.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"What would that involve?"},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Cutting down the Googling and checking, step by step, and learning to live with some doubt. Your GP will see you at set times, not only when you're worried.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"My GP will hate seeing me every month."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"Planned visits usually mean fewer emergency ones. I'll mention your mood too. If it doesn't lift, a tablet like sertraline could help.","m":[12,7]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"OK. I can try."},{"t":"6:06","w":"D","s":"Would talking to someone about your dad help as well?","m":[6]},{"t":"6:11","w":"P","s":"Maybe. I've never really talked about it."},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"No more scans unless something new. CBT. Regular GP visits. And if there's bleeding or weight loss, I get seen."},{"t":"6:33","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. I'll put this in the discharge letter to your GP today, and Dr Morgan will say the same. Any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. It's the first time anyone's said it straight."}],"marks":[{"c":"Agrees one plan with the medical consultant before seeing the patient","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Introduces self honestly and explains why the team asked for the review","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks his own explanation of the pain and his main fear","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores time spent, body checking, online searching, reassurance-seeking and avoidance","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks how long relief lasts after normal tests","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores triggers, including his father's death, and offers bereavement support","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, sleep and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his pain as real and acknowledges his grief and frustration","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the reassurance cycle without implying the pain is imaginary","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains honestly why a repeat scan will not help, using his own experience","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks current red flags and agrees which new symptoms would justify tests","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees CBT for health anxiety and a named GP with planned reviews","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and closes, with the plan shared with the GP","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Agrees one team plan first, accepts the pain as real, maps checking and reassurance, explains the cycle, refuses the scan kindly and records red flags.","borderline":"Right diagnosis but the scan refusal sounds dismissive, or the trigger, mood or red flags are missed.","fail":"Orders another scan, tells him it is all in his head, or gives a different message from the medical team."}},"c-2-4-7":{"setup":"You are in the CMHT duty clinic. Samuel Adeyemi, 34, avoids bank cards and the NHS App, believing bar codes carry 666. His GP fears psychosis. His pastor is with him. Decide if this is an overvalued idea, shared belief or delusion, and plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Samuel Adeyemi, 34, man, bus mechanic, with his pastor, Pastor Daniel Okoro","open":"My GP thinks I'm crazy. Are you going to put me on medication?","manner":"Calm, polite and articulate, but wary of being labelled. Relaxes when his faith is respected; warm and grateful by the end.","free":["He believes bar codes carry 666, the mark of the beast","He avoids bank cards, the NHS App and anything with a bar code","He is worried about being labelled mentally ill"],"ifasked":["He learned it from a visiting preacher about five years ago, then read about it","Many in his church share the belief; some think he takes it too far","Conviction about 95 out of 100; accepts some bar codes may be harmless","It takes up little of his day; pays cash","Has not collected his blood pressure tablets because of the bar code","No messages meant for him, no voices, no sense of being persecuted or of thoughts being interfered with","Mood and sleep good; no thoughts of harming himself or others","Pastor confirms the belief is common in the church and Samuel has not changed"],"worries":["Are you going to put me on medication?","Will I be sectioned?","How can I get my medicine if everything has a bar code?"],"rules":["Lets the pastor stay if asked; answers for himself","Considers doubt when asked gently, but not if argued with","Becomes guarded if his faith is dismissed","Agrees to practical solutions readily"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Good morning, Mr Adeyemi. I'm Dr Clarke, one of the psychiatrists. And you are?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"(Pastor) Pastor Okoro, from his church."},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"Welcome. Mr Adeyemi, would you like Pastor Okoro to stay? What we discuss is confidential, and I'll only ask him things with your agreement.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"He can stay. (calmly) My GP thinks I'm crazy. Are you going to put me on medication?"},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"No. I'm not here to judge your faith. I'd like to understand your beliefs and how they affect your life. Then we'll decide together if anything is needed.","m":[9,1]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","s":"OK. That's fair."},{"t":"0:56","band":"The belief","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me about bar codes and why they matter to you?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"It's in Revelation. The mark of the beast, 666. It's in every bar code. That's just a fact."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Where did you first come across this?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:19","w":"P","s":"At church, about five years ago. A visiting preacher. Then I read about it myself."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"Do others in your church believe the same?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"Many do. Not everyone. Some think I take it too far."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"How sure are you, from 0 to 100?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Ninety-five. Only God knows everything."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"Is there any chance some bar codes are harmless?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:53","w":"P","s":"(thinks) Maybe some. On groceries, perhaps. But I don't want to risk my soul."},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"That's helpful. How much of your day does it use up up?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"Not much. I just avoid the cards and the app. I pay cash."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"How has it affected your work, shopping or health care?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"Work is fine. I'm a bus mechanic. Some shops won't take cash. And I didn't collect my blood pressure tablets because of the bar code."},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"That sounds frustrating. We can sort that out.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"I hope so."},{"t":"2:44","band":"Other experiences","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this. Have you noticed anything else strange, like messages meant just for you on TV, or voices others can't hear?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"Any sense that people are against you, or that your thoughts are being interfered with?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"No. People at work tease me a bit. That's all."},{"t":"3:16","w":"D","s":"How are your mood and sleep?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Good. I sleep well. I'm happy, apart from this GP business."},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of self-harm or anyone else, because of this?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"(surprised) No. Never. I'm a peaceful man."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I have to ask everyone.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:42","w":"P","s":"I understand."},{"t":"3:43","band":"Collateral","w":"D","s":"Pastor Okoro, with Mr Adeyemi's agreement, is his view usual in your church?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"(Pastor) Several of our members believe the same. He's devout, but not unusual for us."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"Has anything about him changed recently: how he talks, sleeps, or gets on with others?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"(Pastor) No. Same man. Still at choir, still working."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Has he ever said the beast was after him personally?","m":[8,6]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","s":"(Pastor) Never. He talks about it as a warning for everyone, not just him."},{"t":"4:29","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you both. Mr Adeyemi, you're calm and clear, your mood is good, and I haven't found any other unusual experiences.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"So you don't think I'm mentally ill?"},{"t":"4:46","w":"D","s":"No, from today I don't. Your belief comes from your faith, and others share it. You hold it strongly, but you can consider other views. That isn't a sign of illness.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"(smiles) Thank God. I was worried about being sectioned."},{"t":"5:11","w":"D","s":"That isn't going to happen. Religious beliefs alone are never a reason for that.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"5:19","band":"Plan","w":"P","s":"How can I get my medicine if everything has a bar code?"},{"t":"5:26","w":"D","s":"Let's solve that. I'll ask your GP for paper prescriptions and phone appointments instead of the app.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:37","w":"P","s":"That would really help. The tablets matter."},{"t":"5:41","w":"D","s":"Is there anyone at church who could help with the practical side?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","s":"(Pastor) I can go to the pharmacy with him."},{"t":"5:54","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Mr Adeyemi, could you get your blood pressure tablets this week?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:02","w":"P","s":"Yes. If it's paper, I'll go tomorrow."},{"t":"6:06","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"If anything changes, like messages meant for you, voices, or your sleep and mood dropping, please see your GP or call our duty team. I'll write the number down.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"(Pastor) I'll keep an eye out too."},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:32","w":"P","s":"No medication. Paper prescriptions. Come back if something new or strange happens."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll write to your GP today. Any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you for listening, doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, respects his faith and agrees the pastor's role and confidentiality","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the content and origin of the belief, including church teaching","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes whether others in his church share the belief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Rates conviction and gently tests whether other explanations are possible","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks how much time it takes and its impact on work, money and health care","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for self-reference, hallucinations, persecution and thought interference","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, sleep and risk to self and others","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Obtains collateral from the pastor with consent, including recent change","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his fear of being labelled and respects his faith throughout","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises mental state findings and explains the conclusion: shared overvalued belief, not illness","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees practical adjustments: paper prescriptions, phone booking and help collecting tablets","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives clear signs for re-referral and the duty number","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks understanding and closes with a GP letter","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Respects his faith, tests conviction gently, screens for other psychotic features, gets collateral, concludes overvalued shared belief and solves the practical problems.","borderline":"Reaches the right conclusion but skips collateral or the screen for other symptoms, or gives no re-referral signs.","fail":"Labels the belief a delusion, starts an antipsychotic, argues about religion, or discharges with no advice."}},"c-2-5-1":{"setup":"A&E, Thursday 23:40. Kayleigh Morgan, 27, with EUPD, has cut her forearm after a row with her partner. Third visit this week; the wound is closed and she wants admission. Assess her, agree a safe plan and decide about admission.","role":{"who":"Ms Kayleigh Morgan, 27, woman with EUPD attending A&E","open":"Whatever. You're going to send me home anyway. That's what you lot always do.","manner":"Guarded and irritable at first, expecting rejection. Softens and becomes tearful when listened to. Calm and engaged once she is part of the plan.","free":["She argued with her partner Jordan, who said he was leaving","She cut her forearm in the bathroom to make the feelings stop","She wants to be admitted for one night","Staff have called her a 'frequent flyer'"],"ifasked":["No tablets or other ingestion tonight","Two glasses of wine tonight; drinks a bottle of wine 3-4 nights a week; no drugs","Passive wish not to wake up; no plan, no preparations, no intent","Nothing new this time: all three visits followed rows with Jordan","No children; lives with Jordan and a cat","Jordan shouts; she hesitates and denies physical harm","Her mum lives 10 minutes away and will take her in","Ice, music and talking to Mum have helped before; care coordinator is Ruth"],"worries":["'You'll just send me home like last time.'","'Will the crisis team actually ring?'","'Ruth will think I've failed.'"],"rules":["Gets angry if the doctor sounds rushed or uses labels; settles if this is acknowledged","Becomes tearful when asked what is different this time","Pushes for admission once; accepts the plan if her reasons for wanting it are taken seriously","Also plays the A&E nurse in charge in the final handover"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Kayleigh, I'm Dr Shah, one of the psychiatry doctors. I've read a bit about this week, but I'd rather hear it from you. Is it OK to talk in here?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Whatever. You're going to send me home anyway. That's what you lot always do."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you've felt brushed off before, and I'm sorry. I'm not rushing tonight. Can you tell me what happened this evening?","m":[1,8]},{"t":"0:30","band":"Tonight's story","w":"P","s":"Me and Jordan had a row. He said he was going out and not coming back. I couldn't breathe. I went in the bathroom and cut."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening, thinking he might leave. What was in your mind just before you cut?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"That everyone leaves. That I'm too much. Cutting makes the noise stop for a bit."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"So cutting quietens the feelings for a while. How bad do you feel right now, if ten is the worst it's been?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"About a seven. It was a ten at home."},{"t":"1:10","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Have you taken any tablets tonight, or anything else that could harm you?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"No tablets. I had a couple of glasses of wine before the row."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"How much do you drink in a usual week? And any drugs?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Wine three or four nights a week, maybe a bottle. No drugs, not for years."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being straight with me. I need to ask this directly. Have you had thoughts about ending your life tonight?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I wish I just wouldn't wake up. But I don't want to kill myself. I wanted it to stop."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Have you made a plan, or taken any steps to get ready, like writing a note or saving up tablets?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"This is your third visit this week. Is anything different tonight compared with Monday and Tuesday?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Not really. It's always after a fight with him. I'm just so tired of being like this."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting, carrying this every night. It makes sense that you'd want somewhere to feel safe.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Everyone thinks I'm attention-seeking. I heard the nurse call me a frequent flyer. (starts crying)"},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you heard that. It shouldn't have been said, and I'll raise it with the staff. Coming here tells me you were in a lot of pain.","m":[8,1]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"(wipes eyes) OK."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Who's at home tonight? Do you look after any children, or anyone else?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"No kids. Just me, Jordan and the cat."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this. Has Jordan, or anyone, ever hurt you, pushed you or made you feel scared at home?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"(pause) He shouts. He's never... no. Not really."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"OK. If that ever changes, you can tell me or your care coordinator. I'll put the domestic abuse helpline on your plan too, just in case. Is that alright?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:20","band":"Admission","w":"P","s":"Fine. Can I just stay in tonight? One night."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"What are you hoping a night on the ward would give you?","m":[2]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"To feel safe. To not be on my own with my head. And to sleep."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Those are fair things to want. Can I be honest? For many people with your diagnosis, unplanned stays make the urges stronger, not weaker. I'm not saying no to help. I'm saying let's find what will really get you through tonight.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"So you are sending me home."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"Not back to the same row, no. Where would feel safest tonight?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:05","band":"Safety plan","w":"P","s":"My mum's, I suppose. She always says I can go there. She's ten minutes away."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Could she come and get you?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Probably. She'll be up."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Good. What's helped on nights like this before?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Holding ice. Music in my headphones. Talking to Mum. Ruth, my care coordinator, taught me the ice one."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Let's write those down together: your warning signs, the ice, music, your mum, and Ruth in the daytime. What would you do if the urge got strong at three in the morning?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Wake Mum up. Or ring someone."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Yes. Here's the 24-hour crisis line, and you can ring NHS 111 and choose option 2 for mental health. I'll also book the crisis team to phone you tomorrow by twelve. Would that be OK?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"Will they actually ring? Last time nobody did."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"I'll book it myself and write the time on your plan. If they haven't rung by twelve, call the crisis line and say I booked it.","m":[11,13]},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Could you also give the wine a miss tonight and tomorrow? It tends to make nights like this worse.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"I won't drink at Mum's anyway."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Can I check I've explained it clearly? Tell me in your words what happens tonight and tomorrow.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Mum picks me up. Ice and music. If it gets bad I ring the crisis line or 111. The crisis team rings by twelve."},{"t":"5:45","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Spot on. I'll email Ruth tonight and write to your GP. Is that OK with you?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Do you have to tell Ruth? She'll think I've failed."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"She won't. She'll want to help sooner, and you won't have to tell the story twice.","m":[8,13]},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"If you cut again, if the wound gets red or hot, or if you feel you can't keep safe, come back or ring 999. You're never wasting our time.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Thanks. You actually listened."},{"t":"6:20","band":"Tell the nurse in charge","w":"D","s":"Full assessment done. Her risk is the same as her usual pattern, she's going to her mum's with a written plan, and the crisis team will ring by midday. Could you let me know if she tries to leave before her mum arrives? And could we drop 'frequent flyer'? She heard it.","m":[13,1]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"(Nurse in charge) Fair point, I'll have a word with the team. I'll keep an eye on her until her mum comes."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self warmly, makes time and avoids labels such as 'frequent flyer'","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores tonight's trigger, her feelings before cutting and what she hopes admission will give","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what is different this time compared with the earlier visits this week","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans, preparations and intent","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks for ingestion tonight and asks about alcohol and drugs","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about children or other dependants at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks sensitively about domestic abuse and offers a route for help","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her distress and responds to anger with empathy, not defensiveness","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains briefly why an open-ended admission is unlikely to help, without sounding rejecting","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Builds a safety plan with her: where she sleeps, coping strategies, contacts, alcohol","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Books a timed crisis team call and gives the 24/7 crisis line and NHS 111 option 2","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking her to repeat the plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets, and informs the nurse in charge, care coordinator and GP before she leaves","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A full, kind assessment covering trigger, intent, change, children, abuse and substances, ending in a timed, written plan she agrees with.","borderline":"Covers risk but misses abuse or children, or the plan lacks named times and contacts.","fail":"Cuts the assessment short because of repeat visits, promises admission to end the talk, or sends her home with no plan or handover."}},"c-2-5-2":{"setup":"CMHT clinic, Tuesday 14:00. Chloe Bennett, 23, with EUPD and repeated self-harm, has been offered DBT. Her mother is with her. She wants a tablet instead. Explain DBT, answer the medication question and check she wants to go ahead.","role":{"who":"Ms Chloe Bennett, 23, woman with EUPD (her mother, Mrs Karen Bennett, 52, is with her)","open":"She can stay. Just not the stuff about my ex.","manner":"Sceptical and a bit flat, arms folded. Briefly tearful about feeling broken. Interested once DBT sounds practical; anxious about the group and the wait.","free":["She wants a tablet that stops her feelings","Counselling made her cry every week","Her emotions go from nought to a hundred after one text","Her mum can stay, but not to discuss her ex"],"ifasked":["Thinks DBT is 'a group thing, like AA for feelings'","Sertraline from the GP made her feel like a zombie","Last self-harm 10 days ago, cutting her thighs","No current thoughts of ending her life","Copes by texting her friend Mia or going for a walk","Has never been told how long DBT lasts"],"worries":["'Why can't I just have a tablet?'","'How long is the wait?'","'What if I hate the group?'"],"rules":["Becomes tearful about 'being the broken one'; settles if validated","Pushes for medication twice; accepts an honest answer with reasons","Agrees to referral only if structure and the wait are explained clearly","Also plays her mother, who feels she has failed her and wants a role"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Shah, one of the psychiatry doctors. Chloe, thanks for coming in. And you must be Chloe's mum?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"(Mother) Karen. Hi."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Chloe, is it OK if your mum stays while we talk? And is there anything you'd rather I didn't go into in front of her?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"She can stay. Just not the stuff about my ex."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"Of course. That stays between us. Karen, I'll mainly be talking with Chloe, but you're welcome to ask things too.","m":[2,1]},{"t":"0:45","band":"What she knows and wants","w":"D","s":"Chloe, what have you been told about DBT so far?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"That it's a group thing. Like AA for feelings. Honestly, why therapy? I want a tablet that stops these feelings."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"That makes sense. What would that tablet do for you, if it existed?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Stop me going from nought to a hundred. One text and I'm wrecked for the whole day."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Have you tried therapy or medication before? How did it go?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Counselling. It just made me cry every week. And sertraline from the GP made me feel like a zombie."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"That sounds really disheartening, trying things that didn't help.","m":[8]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"(voice cracks) I'm sick of being the broken one. Everyone's tried to fix me."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry. You're not broken. You've been dealing with very strong feelings without the right tools. That's what DBT is for.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:00","band":"Explain DBT","w":"D","s":"DBT is different from counselling. It's practical skills training for people whose feelings come on huge and fast. Can I explain how it works?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"There are four parts. You see your own therapist once a week. You go to a skills group once a week, about two hours. You can phone for coaching between sessions when things get hard. And it usually lasts about a year.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"A year? Every week?"},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Yes, it's a big commitment. People often find the first few months the hardest, then it gets easier as the skills start working.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"The group teaches four sets of skills. Noticing what's happening inside you. Getting through a crisis without making it worse. Calming the storm of feelings. And asking for what you need from people.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"So like homework?"},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Each day you fill in a diary card of urges and the skills you used, and your therapist goes through it with you.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","band":"The tablet question","w":"P","s":"So why can't I just have the tablet as well?"},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Honest answer: no medicine treats EUPD itself. Tablets can't teach the skills, and they can add side effects or overdose risk.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Medication does have a place. If you developed depression, we'd treat that on its own. In a crisis, something to help sleep for a few nights at most.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"(Mother) We just want her to be OK. I feel like I've failed her."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"Karen, you clearly care a lot, and being here today matters. You haven't failed her.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:05","band":"Risk and safety plan","w":"D","s":"Chloe, can I check how safe you've been lately? When did you last hurt yourself?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Ten days ago. I cut my thighs. Not since."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts about ending your life at the moment?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"No. Not now."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"What do you do when the feelings get too strong?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Text my friend Mia. Go for a walk. Sometimes it doesn't work."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"Let's add those to your safety plan, with the crisis line and NHS 111 option 2 for when they don't work.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:55","band":"The wait","w":"P","s":"How long's the wait?"},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"I can't promise a date. Waits vary, and I'll ask the DBT team for an estimate. Your care coordinator will see you every week until it starts.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"What if I hate the group?"},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"That worry is really common. You'll meet the team first and can ask them anything. If DBT isn't right, there are other structured therapies like MBT.","m":[11,8]},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Karen, some DBT services run family sessions, so you could learn the skills too. You can also ask for a carer's assessment through social care.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"(Mother) I'd like that."},{"t":"5:45","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Chloe, just so I can check I've explained it clearly, how would you describe DBT to Mia?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"A year of skills stuff. Group, my own therapist, and I can ring someone. No magic tablet."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"That's it exactly. Do you want me to go ahead with the referral?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I'll give it a go."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Great. Here's a leaflet about DBT, and I'll write to your GP today. No new tablets, and we'll see you again in four weeks.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"If you self-harm more, or start thinking about ending your life, ring the crisis line or 111 straight away. Don't wait for the appointment.","m":[10,14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"OK. Thanks."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and greets both, speaking to the patient first","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks her consent for her mother to stay and what may be shared","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Finds out what she knows about DBT and what she hopes a tablet would do","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about previous therapy and medication experience","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains weekly individual therapy, weekly skills group, phone coaching and about 12 months","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes the four skills areas and diary cards in plain words, without jargon","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Says honestly that no medicine treats EUPD itself and names where medication helps","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates the distress of both patient and mother","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks recent self-harm and current suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees a safety plan with crisis line and NHS 111 option 2","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Is honest about the wait and arranges weekly contact; mentions an alternative therapy","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers the mother a role and a carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and confirms she wants the referral","d":"Close"},{"c":"Gives written information, a GP letter, a review date and safety-net advice","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Clear plain-English account of DBT, an honest answer on medication, consent respected, risk checked, and she chooses to go ahead.","borderline":"Explains DBT but misses one part, or forgets to check consent about her mother or recent self-harm.","fail":"Lectures with jargon, prescribes to end the argument, talks over her to her mother, or promises a wait time."}},"c-2-5-3":{"setup":"Acute ward, Wednesday 10:30. Natalie Price, 31, informal with EUPD, calls you the only doctor who understands her. Staff are divided and she has complained about a healthcare assistant. See her, then speak to the nurse in charge.","role":{"who":"Ms Natalie Price, 31, informal inpatient with EUPD, day 21 of admission","open":"Better now you're here. You're the only doctor who actually understands me.","manner":"Warm and flattering at first, then angry and tearful when told information is shared. Calms when taken seriously and given clear goals.","free":["The doctor is 'the only one who understands' her","Night staff 'hate' her and make her wait","She has written a complaint about a healthcare assistant","She wants the doctor to keep her comments secret"],"ifasked":["At 2am the HCA, Gary, refused PRN and pulled her arm off the office door","It left a red mark that has gone","She hoped Gary would be told off and nights would be fair","No self-harm for 48 hours; no suicidal thoughts","Her goals: sleep, return to her flat, start therapy","Her care coordinator is Leanne"],"worries":["'Promise you won't tell the others.'","'Nobody believes me.'","'Will anything happen about my complaint?'"],"rules":["Becomes angry and tearful when told information is shared; settles if validated and given a reason","Tests the doctor once more for special treatment if boundaries waver","Also plays the nurse in charge, Bola, who is stressed and feels caught between shifts"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Natalie, thanks for waiting. I've got some time now. How are you doing today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Better now you're here. You're the only doctor who actually understands me."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"I'm glad our talks help. Lots of people on the team want things to go well for you. What's been the hardest part of this week?","m":[3,2]},{"t":"0:25","band":"Her week","w":"P","s":"Nights. The night staff hate me. They make me wait for everything."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"That sounds upsetting. Can you tell me what happened at night?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"I asked for my PRN at two in the morning. Gary, the healthcare assistant, said no and pulled my arm off the office door. It hurt."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Did it leave a mark, or any injury?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"A bit of a red mark. It's gone now."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"Is that what you wrote the complaint about?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Yes. But promise you won't tell the others what I said about the night staff."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"I can't keep it secret, Natalie, because we all look after you together. That way you get the same answer whoever is on shift. I will make sure your complaint is looked at properly.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"(angry) So you're like the rest of them. Nobody believes me. I'm always the problem."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"I can hear how hurt and angry you are. I do take this seriously. Feeling disbelieved is awful.","m":[7]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I just want someone on my side."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"I am on your side, and so is the team. Sharing this is how I help you. What did you hope would happen when you wrote the complaint?","m":[4,7]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"That Gary gets told off. And that nights are fair."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Because Gary touched you, I'll pass your complaint to the ward manager and the safeguarding lead today. They'll look into it, and complaining won't change your care.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Really? Today?"},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Today. You can also contact PALS, and I'll give you their details.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:40","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Can I check how you've been in yourself? Any self-harm, or thoughts about ending your life?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Not for two days. No, I don't want to die. I just get wound up at night."},{"t":"2:55","band":"Goals","w":"D","s":"That's good to hear. What do you want to get out of the rest of this admission?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Sleep properly. Get back to my flat. Start that therapy they talked about."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Those are clear goals. Shall we aim for discharge in about two weeks, and invite your care coordinator to plan it with us?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Two weeks. OK. Can it be Leanne? She's my care coordinator."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"Yes, I'll ask Leanne to come to a planning meeting. I'll also see that every shift follows the same plan for PRN and leave, so nights feel fair.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Just to check: what's happening with your complaint, and what happens next?","m":[14]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"It goes to the manager and safeguarding today. And Leanne comes to plan going home."},{"t":"3:55","band":"With the nurse in charge","w":"D","s":"Bola, have you got five minutes? I'd like to talk about Natalie.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"(Nurse in charge) If it's about this morning, I know. Two of my nurses were shouting in the office."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"I think we're seeing splitting. She's describing some of us as all good and others as all bad, and the team has started to divide the same way. It's no one's fault, but it's affecting her care.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"(Nurse in charge) Honestly, the night staff feel blamed, and the day staff think nights are punishing her. I'm stuck in the middle."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"That sounds hard for you and the team. It's a stressful pattern to work in.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Could we have a short team meeting today, say at two? I'd like one written plan for leave, observations and PRN that every shift uses, and a named nurse with a set time with her each day.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"(Nurse in charge) We can do two o'clock. What about the complaint about Gary?"},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"She says he pulled her arm off the office door last night. It's an allegation about staff, so I'll pass it to the ward manager and the safeguarding lead today and record it in her notes.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"(Nurse in charge) Fine. Gary's not on till tonight."},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Can we also write what we see in the notes, not words like 'manipulative'? She can read them, and it helps the next shift more.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"(Nurse in charge) Agreed. Some of the notes this week weren't great."},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"And could we book reflective practice with psychology this week? People need space to talk about how this feels.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"(Nurse in charge) They'll welcome that."},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So: meeting at two, one written plan, a named nurse, complaint to the manager and safeguarding today, and a discharge planning meeting with Leanne. Does that work for you?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"(Nurse in charge) Yes. I'll print the plan for the night staff."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Bola. If she asks to leave or her risk changes before then, please bleep me straight away. I'll update the consultant now.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"(Nurse in charge) Will do."}],"marks":[{"c":"Greets her warmly, gives time and asks an open question about her week","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores what has happened at night in her own words","d":"Content"},{"c":"Responds warmly to idealisation without accepting the 'only one' role","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains kindly that information is shared within the team, and why","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Takes the complaint seriously: gets details, injury, and explains the route and PALS","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks recent self-harm and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her anger and distress without becoming defensive","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Agrees admission goals and an expected discharge date with her and her care coordinator","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names splitting to the nurse in charge in neutral, non-blaming language","d":"Content"},{"c":"Proposes a same-day team meeting and one written plan for leave, observations and PRN","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Passes the allegation to the ward manager and safeguarding lead today and records it","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Acknowledges staff feelings and arranges reflective practice","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks the team to document observed behaviour, not labels","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, summarises next steps and safety-nets if she asks to leave","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Stays warm with her while holding team boundaries, escalates the allegation today, and leaves the team with one written plan.","borderline":"Handles the patient well but leaves the team plan vague, or delays the complaint.","fail":"Accepts the 'special doctor' role or keeps secrets, dismisses the complaint, or blames the patient as manipulative."}},"c-2-6-1":{"setup":"A&E, Saturday 19:15. Daniel Hughes, 38, was brought by police after punching a neighbour's door. His wife says he changed after a severe head injury 18 months ago. Take a history from her, assess risk and plan assessment.","role":{"who":"Mrs Sarah Hughes, 36, his wife","open":"Sarah, please. Yes. I'm just shaking.","manner":"Shaken and tired, keen to be understood. Becomes tearful about missing her husband and fear at home; relieved when the change is explained.","free":["He shouted at neighbours and punched their door tonight","He 'is not the same man' since the crash 18 months ago","He was a calm, careful accountant","He now loses his temper fast and swears at strangers"],"ifasked":["Unconscious at the scene; four days in intensive care; no memory for about three weeks","Only a CT at the time; missed neuropsychology appointment","Spent about £8,000 on gadgets and online; still holds the bank cards","Sexual comments to her friend; no insight","Brief blank staring spells; daily headaches; four cans at weekends","Threw a plate; grabbed her wrist once; she is sometimes scared","Two children, aged 9 and 6, at her mother's tonight; they hide when he shouts","Once said the family would be better off without him; still drives the children"],"worries":["'Is he just being horrible?'","'Will they take the children?'","'Can he get better?'"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when asked about safety at home; settles if validated","Only mentions the wrist grab and the 'better off without him' comment if asked directly","Asks about children's services if a referral is mentioned"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mrs Hughes? I'm Dr Shah, one of the psychiatry doctors. Daniel is with the police officer in the next bay. Is it OK if I talk with you first, here in private?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Sarah, please. Yes. I'm just shaking."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That's understandable after tonight. Take your time. What happened this evening?","m":[2,11]},{"t":"0:25","band":"Tonight and before","w":"P","s":"The neighbours had music on. He went round, shouting and swearing, and punched their door. They called the police."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"Has anything like this happened before?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Not with neighbours. At home, yes. He's not the same man since the crash."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"Tell me what Daniel was like before the accident.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Calm. Patient. An accountant, really careful with money. He'd never raise his voice with the kids."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"And what's changed since?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"He flies off the handle in seconds. Swears at strangers. He's spent about eight thousand pounds on gadgets and online stuff."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Have you noticed changes in how he talks to people, or anything sexual that's out of character?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"He made comments to my friend about her body. In front of me. He used to be so shy."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Does he see these changes himself?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"No. He says I'm the one who's changed."},{"t":"1:40","band":"The injury","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me about the crash? Was he knocked out?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Unconscious at the scene. He was in intensive care for four days."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"What's the first thing he remembers after the crash?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Waking up on the rehab ward, about three weeks later. He doesn't remember any of the hospital before that."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"So he lost about three weeks of memory. That tells me it was a very severe injury. Has he had any scans since he left hospital?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Only the CT at the time. He missed his neuropsychology appointment."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Has he had any fits, blank spells or bad headaches?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"A couple of times he's gone blank for a few seconds, staring. Headaches most days."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Is he drinking more, or using any drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Four cans at weekends. Two tonight. No drugs that I know of."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"I need to ask about safety at home. Has Daniel ever hurt you, or frightened you?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"He's thrown a plate. He grabbed my wrist once, hard. (tearful) I'm scared of him sometimes. I miss my husband."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"That sounds incredibly hard. You've lost the man you knew, and you're still caring for him. Feeling scared and sad at the same time makes complete sense.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"I feel guilty even saying it."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"You don't need to. Telling me helps us help both of you. Do you have children?","m":[8,11]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Two. Ella's nine and Sam's six. They're at my mum's tonight. They've seen him shouting."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Has he ever hurt or threatened the children?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"No. But they hide when he shouts."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"How has his mood been? Has he talked about not wanting to live?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Once, after a row, he said we'd be better off without him. He laughed it off."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Is he still driving?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Yes, he drives the kids to school. Nobody told him not to."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"And who looks after the money now?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"He still has the bank cards. I've had to hide the savings book."},{"t":"4:25","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Sarah, what you're describing fits a change caused by the injury, especially to the front of the brain, which controls temper, judgement and how we behave with others. It isn't him choosing to be like this, and it's not a personality disorder.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"So it's not just him being horrible?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"No. The part of the brain that puts the brakes on has been damaged. He can talk well, but acting wisely is harder. There are specialist services for this, and for you.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:05","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'd like him to have an MRI scan, a tracing for the blank spells, and detailed testing of his thinking by a neuropsychologist. I'll ask his GP to refer him to the brain injury team.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Because the children have seen this, I'll make a referral to children's services. That's about support, not blame. And I'd like to go through a safety checklist with you tonight.","m":[8,13]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Will they take the kids?"},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"That's very unlikely. The aim is to support the family. You've done the right thing by keeping them safe tonight.","m":[11,13]},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"He shouldn't drive for now, and he needs to tell the DVLA. I'll tell him myself and write it down. We should also look at whether he can manage money safely.","m":[10,13]},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"And there's Headway, the brain injury charity, and a carer's assessment for you through social care.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can I check that made sense? What will you tell your mum about tonight?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"That it's the brain injury, not him being cruel. He needs a scan and specialists. And I get support too."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If you ever feel unsafe, ring 999. I'll talk with Daniel now, and the police liaison team will know about his injury.","m":[14,7]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Nobody's explained it like that before."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and speaks to her in private","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what happened tonight and about previous incidents","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gets a clear picture of his personality before the injury","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks for specific changes: temper, spending, social and sexual behaviour, insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Estimates severity from loss of consciousness and post-traumatic amnesia","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about seizures, headaches, alcohol and drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about violence towards her and her safety","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about children at home and their safety; explains the referral","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about his mood and thoughts of not wanting to live","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about driving and money, and gives driving advice","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her fear, grief and guilt","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the brain-behaviour link in plain words; not his choice, not a personality disorder","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Plans MRI, EEG, neuropsychology, brain injury referral, Headway and a carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and safety-nets before seeing him","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A clear before-and-after picture, injury severity, full risk to wife, children, self and road, and a plain explanation with specific referrals.","borderline":"Good history but misses PTA, driving or the children.","fail":"Labels it a personality disorder, ignores the wife's safety or the children, or plans only sedation."}},"c-2-6-2":{"setup":"A&E resus, Monday 15:20. Leah Fraser, 26, with known temporal lobe epilepsy on lamotrigine, has had four long attacks today. The A&E registrar calls them 'pseudoseizures'. Assess the attacks, keep her safe and explain the plan without blame.","role":{"who":"Ms Leah Fraser, 26, woman with temporal lobe epilepsy","open":"Wiped out. Embarrassed. They think I'm faking, don't they?","manner":"Drowsy, embarrassed and defensive at first. Quietly tearful when afraid of losing her tablets. Relieved and engaged when told the attacks are real.","free":["She feels staff think she is faking","Today's attacks go on and on; she can sometimes hear people","Her partner Jay filmed one on his phone"],"ifasked":["Usual seizures: rising stomach feeling, smell of burning, lost minute, lip smacking, eyes open","Takes lamotrigine daily; took it this morning","Started the combined pill about three months ago","Sleeping badly; some wine at weekends","Lost her job last month; her father is in hospital","Something happened when she was a teenager; she won't discuss it today","Recently got her driving licence back"],"worries":["'They think I'm faking.'","'Will you stop my tablets?'","'Is it all in my head?'"],"rules":["Becomes defensive if 'pseudo' or 'not real' is used","Becomes tearful about stopping her tablets; settles if reassured","Declines to discuss past trauma today; is grateful if not pushed","Also plays the A&E registrar at the start and end"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Hear from the A&E team","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Shah from liaison psychiatry. Before I see her, can you describe the attacks you've seen?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"(A&E registrar) Four today, ten to fifteen minutes each. Eyes screwed shut, head going side to side, it comes and goes. She cries afterwards. Classic pseudoseizures."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"Did anyone try to open her eyes? Any tongue biting, incontinence or drop in sats?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"(A&E registrar) She resisted eye opening. No tongue biting. Sats stayed fine."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"What has she had so far, and how is she now?","m":[14]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"(A&E registrar) Buccal midazolam from the paramedics and 2 mg lorazepam here. Obs are normal now, she's drowsy but talking."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Could we call them dissociative seizures rather than pseudoseizures? She has known epilepsy too, so I'd like to be careful. Is there any video?","m":[8,3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"(A&E registrar) Her partner filmed one, I think."},{"t":"1:15","band":"Open with her","w":"D","s":"Hello Leah, I'm Dr Shah. I'm one of the doctors who looks at how the brain and stress work together. How are you feeling now?","m":[1]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Wiped out. Embarrassed. They think I'm faking, don't they?"},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"I don't think you're faking. These attacks are real, and I want to understand them properly. Is that OK?","m":[7,9]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Yes. OK."},{"t":"1:45","band":"Her seizures","w":"D","s":"Tell me about your usual epilepsy seizures. What happens?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"I get a rising feeling in my stomach and smell burning. Then I lose a minute. Jay says I smack my lips."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Do your eyes stay open in those?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Jay says they're open, staring."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"And today's attacks, are they the same?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"No. They go on and on. I can sometimes hear people but I can't answer."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Jay filmed one. Would you be OK with me watching it?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Yeah, it's on his phone."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Thanks. (watches) I can see your eyes are shut tight and your head moves side to side. It goes on for about twelve minutes.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:45","band":"Medicines and triggers","w":"D","s":"Have you been taking your lamotrigine as usual?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Every day. I took it this morning."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Have you started any new medicines, including contraception?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"The combined pill, about three months ago."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"That's useful. The combined pill can lower lamotrigine levels, so I'll flag that to neurology. How's your sleep, and any alcohol?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Barely sleeping. A bit of wine at weekends."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Has anything been stressful lately?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"I lost my job last month. And my dad's in hospital."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"That's a lot at once. Sometimes difficult things from the past matter too. Has anything happened to you that still troubles you? You don't have to go into detail.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"(quiet) Something when I was a teenager. I don't want to talk about it now."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"That's completely fine. Thank you for telling me. We can come back to it whenever you're ready.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I'm scared they'll stop my tablets and I'll have proper fits again."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"I understand. We won't stop your tablets. That fear makes complete sense after everything today.","m":[7,10]},{"t":"4:20","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Can I explain what I think might be going on? Some of today's attacks look like dissociative seizures. That's when the brain switches off for a while under a lot of strain. They're real and common, and they're not your fault.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"So it's in my head?"},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"It's in your brain, like a computer crashing rather than a broken part. It's treatable, usually with a specific talking therapy. And some people have both kinds of seizure, so we need to be sure.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"The best test is a video EEG, where you stay in hospital with a camera and brain tracing until an attack happens. I'll ask neurology to arrange it.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Until neurology have seen you, please don't drive, and you should tell the DVLA. I'll write that in your plan.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"I only just got my licence back. (sighs) OK."},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"I know that's hard. Can you tell me what you've taken in, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Some of today might not be epilepsy, it's real, I keep my tablets, a video test, and no driving."},{"t":"5:55","band":"Agree the plan with A&E","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Leah. I'll talk to the A&E doctor now.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"Some features look dissociative, but with known epilepsy I'd keep to the seizure protocol. Please continue lamotrigine and avoid more benzodiazepine unless an attack is clearly epileptic and lasts five minutes or more.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"(A&E registrar) Fair. I'd rather not give more anyway. Neuro?"},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Yes, I'll refer for video EEG and mention the combined pill. I'll write an attack plan for her and Jay, a GP letter, and written driving advice.","m":[11,12,13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"(A&E registrar) Great. Sorry about the pseudoseizure thing."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"No problem. If she has an attack with open eyes, lip smacking or a slow recovery, treat it as epileptic and call me.","m":[14,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self to her, checks how she feels, and engages without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gets an eyewitness account: duration, eyes, movements, tongue biting, recovery","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks for and watches a phone video of an attack, with consent","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about her usual epileptic seizures and how today's differ","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks adherence, interacting medicines, sleep and alcohol","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about stressors and gently about past trauma, without pushing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her fear of not being believed and of losing her tablets","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Challenges 'pseudoseizures' respectfully and uses non-blaming language","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains dissociative seizures as real, common, treatable and possibly coexisting with epilepsy","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees to continue lamotrigine and avoid further benzodiazepine unless clearly epileptic","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges neurology review for video EEG","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises no driving and to tell the DVLA, in writing","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding and closes with a written attack plan","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks physical safety after sedation and safety-nets for epileptic features","d":"Risk"}],"global":{"pass":"Careful features-based history with video, both diagnoses kept open, lamotrigine continued, no extra sedation, and a warm, blame-free explanation.","borderline":"Right conclusion but thin on eyewitness detail, or forgets driving advice.","fail":"Calls the attacks 'pseudo' or 'not real', stops antiepileptics, or misses the chance of coexisting epilepsy."}},"c-2-6-3":{"setup":"Acute medical ward, Friday 11:00. Hannah Clarke, 34, has been mute and motionless for two days, eyes shut, not eating or drinking. Her brother died last month. The medical team writes 'likely functional'. Assess her and agree safe care.","role":{"who":"Dr Aisha Rahman, 33, medical registrar (with Mrs Linda Clarke, 61, the patient's mother)","open":"Sure. Honestly, we think it's functional. Bloods are normal, CT's normal. We need the bed, so we're hoping you'll take over.","manner":"Busy and keen to transfer care, a bit defensive about gaps. Becomes engaged once concerns are explained clearly. The mother is frightened and grieving.","free":["Bloods and CT head are normal","They think it is functional","They need the bed and want psychiatry to take over","She is on IV fluids since yesterday"],"ifasked":["Fluid balance chart is patchy; urea slightly raised; no CK done","No VTE prophylaxis: marked 'mobile' on admission","Unclear whether pressure area turns are happening","No EEG, MRI or LP yet","Mother: flu-like illness three weeks ago, then said people were watching her","Mother: no previous episodes; takes the pill only; no drugs","Mother: brother Michael, 38, collapsed and died at the gym last month"],"worries":["'Can you take her over?'","'She can't stay on a medical ward forever.'","Mother: 'I can't lose another child.'"],"rules":["Pushes for transfer twice; accepts shared care if the reasons are clear","Mother cries when her son is mentioned; settles if validated","Mother only mentions the flu-like illness if asked about recent illness","Hannah stays mute, with eyes closed, resisting eye opening"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"At the bedside","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Shah from liaison psychiatry. Thanks for the referral. Can we go through things at the bedside together?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Sure. Honestly, we think it's functional. Bloods are normal, CT's normal. We need the bed, so we're hoping you'll take over."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"Hello Hannah. I'm Dr Shah, one of the psychiatry doctors. You don't need to speak. I'm going to ask some questions and then check how your body is doing.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"How is she for fluids? Is there a fluid balance chart, and are U&Es and CK done?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"IV fluids since yesterday. The chart's patchy. Urea is a bit up. No CK."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Could we get a CK today and keep the chart strict? She's not eating, so she'll need a dietitian. If there's no intake by day three, we should plan NG feeding.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"OK, fair."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Is she on VTE prophylaxis, and who's checking her pressure areas?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"She was marked as mobile on admission, so no enoxaparin. Turns, I'm not sure."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"She's been immobile for two days, so I'd start enoxaparin unless there's a reason not to, and two-hourly turns with a Waterlow score.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","band":"Catatonia screen","w":"D","s":"Hannah, I'm going to gently lift your arm now. You're safe.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"(lifts arm) Her arm is staying up in the air. And there's resistance when I move it, that grows the more I push.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Is that significant?"},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Hannah, I'm going to try to open your eyes for a moment. (tries) She's resisting. Mutism, staring, posturing and resistance are all items on the Bush-Francis catatonia scale. She scores on several.","m":[2,5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"I'd like a lorazepam challenge: 1 to 2 mg IV, re-score after ten to thirty minutes, with sats monitored and flumazenil on hand.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"And if she improves?"},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Then it supports catatonia, and regular lorazepam usually helps. Please hold off antipsychotics until catatonia and NMS are excluded.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"2:50","band":"Organic work-up","w":"D","s":"I agree a psychological cause is possible with her bereavement. But non-convulsive status can look exactly like this, so she needs an EEG. And an MRI, as CT can miss things.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"We can request those."},{"t":"3:10","band":"Her mother","w":"D","s":"Mrs Clarke, I'm Dr Shah, the psychiatry doctor. Is it alright if I ask you a few things about Hannah?","m":[1,9]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"(Mother) Linda. Yes, anything."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Has anything like this happened before?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"(Mother) Never. She's a teacher. She's always coped."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Was she unwell at all in the weeks before? Fever, headaches, or acting oddly?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"(Mother) She had a flu-like thing about three weeks ago. Then she said people were watching her. That's not like her."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"That's really important, thank you. Does she take any medication, alcohol or drugs?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"(Mother) Just the pill. A glass of wine sometimes. No drugs."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry about Michael. How has Hannah been since he died?","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"(Mother) (crying) He collapsed at the gym. Thirty-eight. And now Hannah's like this. I can't lose another child."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. That's a terrible amount of loss and fear for one family. We're taking Hannah's care very seriously.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:35","band":"Best interests","w":"D","s":"Right now Hannah can't tell us what she wants, so we have to make decisions in her best interests. That means fluids, feeding if needed, and tests. What would she want, do you think?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"(Mother) Everything done. She'd hate to be left like this."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll record that we consulted you. Is there anyone else we should speak to?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"(Mother) Her dad. And her partner, Tom."},{"t":"5:15","band":"Agree shared care","w":"D","s":"Given the flu-like illness and new paranoia, I'm worried about encephalitis. Could we discuss an LP with neurology, including anti-NMDA receptor antibodies?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Encephalitis? I hadn't thought of that."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"It often starts with psychiatric symptoms in young women. It's easily missed.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Fine. But she can't stay on medicine forever if it's psychiatric."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"I understand the bed pressure. Until the work-up is done and she's physically safe, she needs to stay medical. Liaison will review her every day.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Daily? OK. Anything else?"},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"She's under continuous care and not free to leave, so please consider an urgent DoLS authorisation. And if she gets a fever, odd mouth movements or unstable obs, call neurology the same day.","m":[11,13]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"Got it."},{"t":"6:25","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So: CK and fluid chart, enoxaparin and turns, lorazepam challenge, EEG, MRI, LP discussion, dietitian, best interests note, and daily review. Have I missed anything?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"No, that's clear. I'll update the team."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Hannah, the team are going to keep caring for you here and do some more tests. I'll come back tomorrow. Your mum is right here.","m":[2,1]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self to the team, the patient and her mother, speaking to the patient directly","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains each step to the patient before touching her","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks hydration, fluid balance, U&E, CK and nutrition, with a feeding plan","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks VTE prophylaxis and pressure area care","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for catatonia at the bedside using Bush-Francis items","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks for EEG to exclude non-convulsive status, and MRI","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about recent illness and proposes LP with encephalitis antibodies","d":"Content"},{"c":"Proposes a monitored lorazepam challenge with flumazenil available","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Takes a collateral history from her mother: past episodes, substances, recent stress","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates the mother's grief and fear","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains best interests to her mother, consults her and records it; considers DoLS","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees shared care: stays medical, daily liaison review, no antipsychotics yet","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets: fever, orofacial movements or unstable obs mean same-day neurology","d":"Close"},{"c":"Summarises the plan and checks the registrar agrees","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Physical safety first, catatonia screened, organic causes pursued, MCA used properly, and a respectful approach to the mute patient.","borderline":"Good psychiatric view but misses VTE, CK or the encephalitis clue.","fail":"Accepts 'functional' and takes over without work-up, misses catatonia, or ignores the patient in the bed."}},"c-2-6-4":{"setup":"Memory clinic, Thursday 10:00. Graham Wilson, 59, has become rude, craves sweets and lost his job for inappropriate remarks. His memory seems fine. His wife is with him. Do a frontal lobe examination, then report to your consultant.","role":{"who":"Mr Graham Wilson, 59, former sales manager (his wife, Mrs Janet Wilson, 57, is with him)","open":"Fine by me. She's the one with the problem, if you ask me.","manner":"Cheerful, blunt and unconcerned, eating sweets. Gets irritable with 'childish' tasks. His wife is tired and becomes tearful.","free":["His memory is 'perfect'","His wife is the one with the problem","He thinks the tasks are childish"],"ifasked":["Letter F: six words with repeats and a proper name","Animals: about eleven, with one repeat","Luria: gets stuck on fist-edge","Go/no-go: taps on every tap","Concrete answers: 'both in the fruit bowl'; stone 'rolls down the hill'","Racehorse 'about 200 miles an hour'","Recalls all three words","Still drives; has no lasting power of attorney"],"worries":["'So what's wrong with me?'","'I've been driving forty years.'","Wife: 'He used to be so kind.'"],"rules":["Agrees to testing if told it is not about memory and he can stop","Gets irritable once; continues if reassured","Wife becomes tearful when findings are explained; settles if validated","Also plays the consultant, Dr Brooks, who joins at the end"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and consent","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Wilson, I'm Dr Shah. Dr Brooks will join us shortly. Your GP asked us to look at some changes your wife has noticed. Is it alright that she's here?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Fine by me. She's the one with the problem, if you ask me. (unwraps a sweet)"},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"I'd like to do some short tasks that test planning, word finding and self-control. These are different from memory tests. They take about fifteen minutes. Is that OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Puzzles? My memory's perfect."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"You may well be right about memory. These look at a different part of the brain, the front part. You can stop any time. Shall we start?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Go on then."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"First, please remember three words for me: apple, table, penny. I'll ask you for them later.","m":[8]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Apple, table, penny. Easy."},{"t":"1:15","band":"Fluency","w":"D","s":"In one minute, tell me as many words as you can starting with F. No names of people or places. Go.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Fish, fork, fun, Frank, fish, fall... fat. Fish. That's it."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Now as many animals as you can in one minute.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Dog, cat, horse, cow, sheep, pig, lion, tiger, dog... bird, mouse, rat. Done."},{"t":"1:45","band":"Sequencing and inhibition","w":"D","s":"Watch my hand: fist, edge, palm. Fist, edge, palm. Now you copy me, then keep going on your own.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"(copies, then repeats) Fist, edge, fist, edge, fist, edge..."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Good try. Let's do it once more together. (repeats demonstration)","m":[3]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"(gets stuck again on fist, edge) This is childish."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"I know some of these feel silly. They're useful because they're so simple. You're doing fine.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Next one. When I tap once, you tap twice. When I tap twice, you don't tap at all.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"(taps along to every tap) Like that?"},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you. That's the end of that one.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:45","band":"Abstraction and estimates","w":"D","s":"How are an apple and a banana alike?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"They're both in the fruit bowl."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"What does 'a rolling stone gathers no moss' mean?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"A stone rolls down the hill, so moss can't grow on it."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"How fast does a racehorse gallop?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"About two hundred miles an hour, I reckon."},{"t":"3:15","band":"Memory and reflexes","w":"D","s":"Earlier I gave you three words to remember. Can you tell me them?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Apple, table, penny. Told you my memory's fine."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"It is, that was perfect. Last bit: I'll check some reflexes. I'm going to stroke your palm and touch your lip. Is that alright?","m":[8,7]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"If you must."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"(strokes palm) Your hand is gripping my fingers by itself. (taps lip) And your lips pout a little. Thank you.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:55","band":"Explain findings","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Your memory was excellent. Some tasks were harder, especially switching between actions, holding back, and finding words. That can happen when the front of the brain isn't working as well.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"So what's wrong with me?"},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"I don't know yet. It could be several things, including a treatable cause. We'd like a brain scan, blood tests and more detailed thinking tests before we say anything firm.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"(Wife) He used to be so kind. Now he says awful things and doesn't even notice. (tearful)"},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Mrs Wilson, that sounds really painful, watching the person you know change. It's not something he's choosing.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:50","band":"Practical advice","w":"D","s":"There are a few practical things. Mr Wilson, I'd advise you not to drive until we know more, and to tell the DVLA. I'll write that down.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"I've been driving forty years."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"I know it's a big thing to ask. It's about keeping you and others safe while we find out.","m":[11,9]},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"It's also worth setting up a lasting power of attorney for money and health now, while you can choose who helps. And Mrs Wilson, you can ask social care for a carer's assessment.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Can I check what you'll both take away from today?","m":[14]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"(Wife) A scan, blood tests, more testing. No driving. Sort out power of attorney."},{"t":"5:50","band":"Report to the consultant","w":"D","s":"Dr Brooks, Mr Wilson is 59 with two years of disinhibition, sweet craving and job loss. Letter fluency was reduced with repeats and a rule break. Category fluency was low.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"He perseverated on the Luria sequence, failed go/no-go, and gave concrete answers and a poor estimate. Grasp and pout reflexes were present. Recall was perfect.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"I'm thinking of behavioural-variant frontotemporal dementia, but we need to exclude a frontal tumour or vascular cause first. I'd suggest MRI, a dementia blood screen including B12, folate, calcium and thyroid, and neuropsychology.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"(Consultant) Good summary. Agreed. Let's see them together."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"We'll see you both with the results within four weeks. If his behaviour puts anyone at risk before then, call us or the GP.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the purpose of the tests and gains consent","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Tests letter then category fluency for one minute each, noting repeats and rule breaks","d":"Content"},{"c":"Demonstrates and tests the Luria fist-edge-palm sequence","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests go/no-go with clear instructions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests abstraction with a similarity and a proverb","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests cognitive estimates","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks grasp and pout reflexes after explaining and getting consent","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks memory recall to contrast with frontal findings","d":"Content"},{"c":"Responds to his irritation and his wife's distress with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains findings in plain words without a premature diagnosis","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Advises on driving, lasting power of attorney and a carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives the consultant a structured summary with a differential including a structural lesion","d":"Content"},{"c":"Recommends MRI brain, a dementia blood screen and neuropsychology","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, gives a follow-up time and safety-nets","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"At least six frontal tests done smoothly with consent, clear plain explanation, practical advice, and a structured report naming a structural cause.","borderline":"Tests done but reflexes or estimates missed, or the report lacks a differential.","fail":"Does only memory tests, gives a firm dementia diagnosis without imaging, or forgets driving and safety."}},"c-2-6-5":{"setup":"You are the CMHT doctor in Wednesday clinic. Ryan, 29, a delivery driver, was referred by his GP for ADHD and wants a prescription today. Take a focused ADHD history, explain how diagnosis is made and agree a safe next step.","role":{"who":"Mr Ryan Doyle, 29, male, delivery driver","open":"Look, I'll be honest, I've waited months for this. I just want to get sorted with some tablets today.","manner":"Restless, fidgety, talks fast and jumps topics. Pushy about medication at first. Softens once he feels believed, and becomes quietly emotional about being called lazy all his life.","free":["Lost three jobs for lateness and missed deadlines","Loses keys and phone, forgets orders, starts things and never finishes","A friend's Elvanse 'made everything quiet' in his head","Girlfriend says he never listens and bills go unpaid"],"ifasked":["At primary school he was always out of his seat and sent out of class; reports said 'needs to concentrate'","His mum was called in every term and still has his school reports","Uses cocaine once or twice a month and has taken his friend's tablets a few times","Drinks a few pints at weekends","Clipped a wing mirror last month while distracted by the satnav","No chest pain, palpitations or blackouts; an uncle had a heart attack in his sixties","Mood a bit low over the jobs, no suicidal thoughts, never had high spells with little need for sleep"],"worries":["Why can't I just have what my mate takes?","How much longer will I have to wait?","Will work find out?","Can I still drive?"],"rules":["Pushes for a prescription twice; accepts the answer if the reason is explained without lecturing","Becomes angry about the wait, then admits he feels like a failure; settles if validated","Only mentions cocaine if asked directly about drugs","Agrees to the doctor contacting his mum if the reason is explained"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, is it Ryan? I'm Dr Shah, one of the psychiatry doctors. Thanks for coming in.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Look, I'll be honest, I've waited months for this. I just want to get sorted with some tablets today."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I hear that, and thanks for being upfront. I won't be starting medication today, because ADHD needs a careful assessment first. But I'm taking this seriously, and you'll leave with a clear next step. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Suppose so. My mate's on Elvanse. I tried one and my head went quiet for the first time ever."},{"t":"0:45","band":"Their story","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a real relief. We'll come back to that. Tell me about a normal day. Where do things go wrong?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"I'm late for everything. I lose my phone, my keys. At work I forget drops or go off on a tangent. I've lost three jobs for lateness and missing deadlines."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Three jobs. That's a lot. What about at home?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"My girlfriend says I never listen. Bills don't get paid. I start stuff and never finish it. She's close to leaving, honestly."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"So it's hitting work, home and your relationship.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Everywhere, yeah."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"And inside, do you feel restless?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"All the time. Like a motor. I can't sit through a film."},{"t":"1:45","band":"Childhood","w":"D","s":"Let's go back further. What were you like at primary school, before about eleven?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Nightmare. Always out of my seat. Teachers said I was bright but lazy."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Were you sent out of class, or did you struggle to finish work?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Sent out loads. Never finished anything. Mum got called in every term."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"It would really help to hear what your mum remembers, and see any old reports. Only with your permission. Would that be okay?","m":[10]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Why my mum? I'm twenty-nine."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Fair question. ADHD has to start in childhood, and most of us don't remember those years well. Her view makes the assessment much stronger.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Fine. She's probably still got my reports in the loft."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Mood, substances, heart","w":"D","s":"Great, thank you. How's your mood been lately?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Bit down, with the jobs. Not depressed, I don't think."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had spells of days needing very little sleep, feeling on top of the world, or overspending?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"No. I sleep badly, but I'm knackered, not buzzing."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of self-harm or that life isn't worth living?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Thanks. What about alcohol, cannabis or other drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Few pints at the weekend. And a bit of coke now and then. Helps me focus, weirdly."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being straight with me. How often, roughly? And how many times have you had your mate's tablets?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Coke once or twice a month. His tablets maybe four or five times."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"That's helpful. Any chest pain, palpitations or blackouts? Anyone in the family die suddenly when young?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"No. My uncle had a heart attack, but he was in his sixties."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Okay. You drive for work. Any accidents or near misses?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Clipped a wing mirror last month. Got distracted by the satnav."},{"t":"4:05","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thanks. From what you've told me, it's reasonable to look into ADHD properly. The specialist team does a structured interview about now and childhood, and speaks to someone like your mum. That's how a diagnosis is made.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"But the tablet worked. Doesn't that prove it?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"I can see why it feels that way. But stimulants sharpen most people's focus, ADHD or not. And using someone else's is illegal and can strain the heart, especially with cocaine. Please don't take them again.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"So how long now? I've already waited four months to see you. It's a joke."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"That's really frustrating, and I'm sorry. I'll be honest, the local wait is long. In England you can ask for an approved provider under Right to Choose, which is sometimes quicker. I'll put that in my letter.","m":[9,13]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"It's just, I'm sick of feeling like a failure. Everyone's always said I'm lazy."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting, carrying that for years. Coming here for answers isn't lazy at all.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"Cheers. Nobody's said that before."},{"t":"5:30","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Here's the plan. I'll refer you to the adult ADHD service and write to your GP, asking them not to start stimulants before you're seen. Meanwhile, could we work on cutting the coke down? I'll give you the drug service number.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Yeah, I'll try. What about driving?"},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"Only drive when you feel alert, take breaks, and set the satnav before you move off. If your concentration ever affects safe driving, you must tell the DVLA.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Will work find out about all this?"},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Only if you choose to tell them. If you're diagnosed, you can ask for reasonable adjustments, and Access to Work can help.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:25","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what happens next, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"You refer me, you talk to my mum, no more of my mate's pills, cut the coke, drive careful."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Spot on. If your mood drops or you feel unsafe, see your GP or call NHS 111 and choose the mental health option. You'll get a copy of my letter. Thanks for being so open, Ryan.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and sets the agenda early: full assessment, no same-day prescription, request not dismissed","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Uses open questions to explore current inattention, restlessness and impulsivity with real examples","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes symptoms before age 12 using school examples","d":"Content"},{"c":"Confirms impairment in at least two settings, such as work and home","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for low mood, bipolar features and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol, drugs and use of someone else's stimulants, including frequency","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about chest pain, palpitations, blackouts and family sudden death","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about driving incidents and gives clear driving and DVLA advice","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Acknowledges his frustration and sense of failure with genuine empathy, without defensiveness","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gains consent to speak to his mother for childhood history and reports","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains how diagnosis is made and why a stimulant response proves nothing","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Advises him not to use other people's medication, giving legal and cardiac reasons","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees referral, GP letter, Right to Choose option and help to cut down cocaine","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back, safety-nets for low mood and closes warmly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Sets the no-prescription agenda kindly, nails childhood onset and two settings, screens mood, drugs and heart, and leaves a clear referral plan.","borderline":"Good symptom history but thin on childhood, cardiac or driving questions, or the plan is vague about what happens next.","fail":"Prescribes or promises stimulants, treats the friend's tablet as proof, or misses substance use and bipolar screening."}},"c-2-7-1":{"setup":"You are the doctor in the community eating disorder clinic on Thursday morning. Chloe, 19, has just been diagnosed with anorexia nervosa (BMI 15.8). She asks if she will get better. Her mother waits outside. Explain the illness, outlook and plan.","role":{"who":"Miss Chloe Barnes, 19, female, first-year university student","open":"Will I actually get better? My mum read online that people die from this.","manner":"Polite, anxious, small voice. Minimises her weight at first. Becomes tearful when talking about dying and about letting her mum down; steadier once she has honest numbers and a plan.","free":["Lost weight over nine months since starting university","Mum read online that people die of anorexia","Worried about having to leave her course"],"ifasked":["Runs for an hour most days, even when exhausted","Never makes herself sick and has not used laxatives","Gets dizzy when she stands up quickly; no fainting or chest pain","Feels cold all the time","Mood has been low for a couple of months; no thoughts of ending her life","Close to her mum; has one good friend at university","Would like her mum to know the diagnosis and plan, but not her weight"],"worries":["Am I going to die?","Will I have to drop out of uni?","Will I have to gain loads of weight?","Please don't tell my mum everything"],"rules":["Asks the 'will I die' question again if the answer is vague","Cries when asked about her fears; settles if validated without false reassurance","Says 'I'm not even that thin' once; accepts gentle reasoning linked to her body","Agrees to her mum coming in once she controls what is shared"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Chloe, I'm Dr Patel, one of the doctors here. Thanks for coming back in today. Is it okay if we talk about your diagnosis and what happens next?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Yes. Will I actually get better? My mum read online that people die from this."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"That's a really important question, and I'll answer it honestly. First, can I ask what you already understand about anorexia, and what worries you most?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"I know it's about not eating. But I'm not even that thin. Girls on my course are smaller than me."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"It can be very hard to see from the inside. And what scares you most?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Dying. And letting my mum down. She's already so stressed."},{"t":"1:10","band":"Their picture","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. May I ask a few quick questions first, to make sure my answer fits you? Do you ever make yourself sick, or use laxatives?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"No, never."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"And exercise?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"I run most days. About an hour. Even when I'm really tired."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Any dizziness, fainting, chest pain, or your heart racing?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"I get dizzy if I stand up fast. Nothing else. I'm always cold."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Have you had any thoughts about ending your life?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Low for a couple of months. But no, never anything like that."},{"t":"2:05","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thanks. So, anorexia is an illness where fear of gaining weight drives you to eat much less than your body needs. Your BMI is 15.8, and the dizziness and feeling cold are your body showing the strain.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"So am I going to die?"},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"It's a serious illness, and I won't pretend otherwise. It has a higher death rate than most mental illnesses, mainly from heart problems. But most people who get treatment early improve, and many recover fully.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"What does many mean?"},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Long-term, roughly half of people recover completely, about a third improve a lot, and about one in five stay unwell. Those numbers include people who never got help.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"I don't want to be the one in five."},{"t":"3:20","band":"Outlook","w":"D","s":"Several things put you in a good position. You're young, you've been ill for under a year, you're starting treatment now, and you have a mum who clearly cares.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"What makes it worse, then?"},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Your weight being this low, the low mood, and over-exercising make it harder. Making yourself sick or a long illness would too. Those are exactly what treatment works on.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Sorry. It's just a lot. I feel like I've ruined everything."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Take your time. It's a lot to hear, and it makes sense you're upset. You haven't ruined anything. You've come for help, and that's the most important step.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Okay. What actually happens now?"},{"t":"4:25","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"You'll see a therapist here for an early-intervention programme for young people. It's usually CBT for eating disorders, which helps with the fear around food and weight.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Will I have to gain loads of weight?"},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"Weight is part of recovery, because a starved brain makes the anorexia thoughts louder. But we'll go at a pace you can manage, with support.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"And uni?"},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"Many students carry on during treatment, with help from student services. Your GP will also check your weight, pulse, blood pressure and bloods regularly to keep your body safe.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:20","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"Get help the same day, through your GP, 111 or A&E, if you faint, get chest pain, your heart races or feels very slow, or you feel confused or very cold.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's scary, but okay."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Your mum's outside. What would you like her to know?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"The diagnosis and the plan. Not my weight. She'll freak out."},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"That's your choice, and I'll respect it. If your life were ever in danger, I'd need to involve others, but I'd tell you first. Shall I bring her in for the last few minutes?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Yes, please."},{"t":"6:20","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Before I do, can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"It's serious but I've got a good chance. Therapy here, GP checks, and same-day help if I faint or get chest pain."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. I'll see you in two weeks, same time. Here's the Beat helpline and the crisis number: 111, option 2. You did really well today.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and asks permission to discuss the diagnosis and future","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what she already knows and what she fears before giving numbers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about vomiting, laxatives and over-exercise","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about dizziness, fainting, chest pain and palpitations","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about mood and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains anorexia in plain words linked to her BMI and symptoms","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers 'will I die' honestly: serious illness, with rough recovery figures","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names at least two good prognostic signs that apply to her","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names at least two poor prognostic signs, framed as targets for treatment","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her distress and guilt without false reassurance","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names a NICE-recommended therapy and regular GP physical monitoring","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives clear red flags that need same-day medical help","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees what she consents to share with her mother before inviting her in","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, books review and gives Beat and crisis details","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Finds out her fears first, gives honest figures with prognostic signs that fit her, names therapy and monitoring, and respects her wishes about her mother.","borderline":"Honest and kind but prognostic signs are generic, or red flags and follow-up are thin.","fail":"Falsely reassures or frightens her, lectures without checking her understanding, or shares details with her mother without consent."}},"c-2-7-2":{"setup":"You are the on-call psychiatry doctor on Friday at 17:30. The nurse in charge bleeps about Megan, 24, admitted informally with anorexia (BMI 13.2), who has barely eaten for two weeks. The dietitian has gone home. Assess refeeding risk and leave a safe weekend plan.","role":{"who":"Kemi Adeyemi, 45, female, nurse in charge (briefly also the on-call medical registrar)","open":"Hi, thanks for coming up. The dietitian's gone home. Can we just start her on full meals tonight? She needs feeding.","manner":"Experienced and practical, under pressure with a short-staffed ward. Wants a clear answer. Becomes stressed when the patient threatens to leave; calms with a concrete plan.","free":["Megan, 24, informal admission this afternoon, BMI 13.2","Has eaten very little for two weeks","Dietitian has gone home until Monday","Only two qualified nurses on tonight"],"ifasked":["Obs: pulse 46, BP 88/56 lying and 80/50 standing with dizziness, temperature 35.9, sats 98%, glucose 3.8","No bloods or ECG done since arrival","Thiamine not prescribed","Admits vomiting after eating and using laxatives some days","Struggled to stand from squatting without using her hands","No chest pain, no ankle swelling","Megan says she will leave if made to eat a full plate"],"worries":["Can she just have full meals tonight?","How often do we do bloods over the weekend?","What do we do if she refuses or tries to leave?","When do I call the medical team?"],"rules":["Pushes once more for full meals; accepts the plan if the reason is clear","Gets visibly stressed about staffing and Megan's threat to leave; settles if acknowledged","Plays the medical registrar briefly when the doctor phones; agrees the plan if thresholds are specific","Reads the plan back if asked"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Okoro, the on-call psychiatry doctor. Thanks for bleeping me. I know you want her eating tonight, and I agree she needs food. Can you tell me about her first?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Megan, 24, came in informally this afternoon. BMI 13.2. She's barely eaten for two weeks. The dietitian's gone. Can we just start full meals?"},{"t":"0:35","band":"Assess","w":"D","s":"Let's get her eating safely tonight. What are her latest obs, including lying and standing BP and blood sugar?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Pulse 46. BP 88 over 56 lying, 80 over 50 standing, and she felt dizzy. Temp 35.9. Sats 98. Sugar 3.8."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Has she had any bloods or an ECG since she arrived?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Not yet. We've been busy settling her."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"Do we know if she's been vomiting, or using laxatives or water tablets?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"She told me she's sick after eating, and takes laxatives some days."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Any chest pain, fainting, palpitations or ankle swelling?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"No chest pain, no swelling. Just the dizziness."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Could you ask her to stand from a squat without using her hands?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"I did earlier. She couldn't. Had to push up on the bed."},{"t":"1:45","band":"Decide","w":"D","s":"Okay. She's at high risk of refeeding syndrome: BMI under 16, almost nothing eaten for over ten days, plus purging, which can drop her potassium.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"So you're saying don't feed her?"},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"No. Not feeding her is dangerous too. But a full menu straight away can drop her phosphate and potassium and strain her heart. She should start tonight on the refeeding meal plan in the protocol.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Fine. What do you need?"},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Bloods now, before her first meal: U&E, phosphate, magnesium, calcium, glucose, FBC and LFTs. And a 12-lead ECG for rate and QTc.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"I can do the ECG. Will you take the bloods?"},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Yes, I'll do them now. I'll also prescribe thiamine 100 mg three times a day, vitamin B compound strong one tablet three times a day, and a multivitamin daily, starting before she eats, for ten days.","m":[5,7]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Okay. Honestly, it's two of us tonight and she's in her room crying, saying she'll leave if we make her eat a full plate."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to manage with two of you, and you're doing the right things. Please tell her she won't be given a full plate. We'll start small and a nurse will sit with her.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Thanks. That helps. And if she still wants to go?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Call me. I'll assess her capacity and, if the risk is serious, whether we need a Mental Health Act assessment. She's not to be held without that.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"Okay. Is she safe to stay here, or should she go to the medical ward?"},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Her pulse, blood pressure and squat test are worrying but not red. I'll discuss her with the medical registrar now.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:25","band":"Medical registrar","w":"P","s":"Medical registrar: Hi, it's the med reg. What's the story?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Megan, 24, anorexia, BMI 13.2, two weeks of minimal intake, purging. Pulse 46, standing drop with dizziness, fails the squat test. Bloods and ECG going now, thiamine prescribed. Can we agree replacement and transfer criteria?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Medical registrar: Fine. Replace phosphate, potassium and magnesium as per protocol and keep feeding. Call us if pulse under 40, chest pain, confusion or glucose under 3, and we'll review for transfer."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll call you with tonight's results either way.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"So what's the weekend plan?"},{"t":"5:20","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Bloods daily for the first week, including phosphate. Obs four times a day, the squat test daily, and capillary glucose before meals. Fluid chart too.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"And when do I call you?"},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Call me and the medical team straight away if her pulse goes under 40, glucose under 3, she gets chest pain, swelling, confusion, or her phosphate drops sharply.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"If phosphate's low tomorrow, do we stop feeding?"},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"No. Keep feeding and replace it. Stopping causes low sugar and more weight loss. The dietitian and eating disorder team will review her Monday.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you read the plan back to me, so we're sure we've got the same one?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"Refeeding meal plan tonight, not full meals. Bloods and ECG now, vitamins before food. Daily bloods, obs four times a day. Call if pulse under 40, sugar under 3, chest pain, swelling or confusion."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll write it in the notes now, take the bloods, then come and meet Megan. Thanks, Kemi.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges the nurse's concern and agrees she needs food","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Obtains full obs including lying and standing BP and glucose, and checks for bloods and ECG","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about purging, laxatives, chest pain, fainting and swelling","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names high refeeding risk using at least two criteria","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Orders U&E, phosphate, magnesium, calcium, glucose, FBC, LFT and an ECG before the first meal","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks risk features such as the squat test and decides on ward versus medical transfer","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Prescribes thiamine, vitamin B compound strong and a multivitamin before feeding","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains starting cautiously without withholding food, and replacing rather than stopping","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Sets weekend monitoring: daily bloods, obs four times a day, squat test and capillary glucose","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives the nurse specific escalation thresholds","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees electrolyte replacement and transfer criteria with the medical registrar","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans for refusal or leaving: support at meals, capacity, MHA only if needed","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Shows empathy for the nurse's pressure and the patient's fear","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks for a read-back of the plan, documents it and says what happens next","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Calls the risk clearly, orders bloods, ECG and thiamine before food, starts cautious feeding, and leaves specific weekend thresholds agreed with the medical team.","borderline":"Safe tests and vitamins but vague monitoring or escalation thresholds, or no agreed plan with the medical registrar.","fail":"Agrees full meals without checks, withholds food until Monday, or forgets thiamine and phosphate."}},"c-2-7-3":{"setup":"You are the psychiatry doctor in a GP liaison clinic on Tuesday morning. Jade, 23, has told her GP she binges and makes herself sick. Her weight is normal. Take a focused history, assess physical and mental risk, and explain treatment options.","role":{"who":"Miss Jade Turner, 23, female, works in retail","open":"This is so embarrassing. Is there just a tablet I can take to stop it?","manner":"Ashamed, avoids eye contact, speaks quietly. Opens up when not judged. Becomes tearful about never having told anyone; relieved to hear it is common and treatable.","free":["Binges several times a week and then makes herself sick","Has never told anyone before the GP","Wants a tablet to stop it"],"ifasked":["Binges four or five evenings a week: a loaf of toast, cereal, chocolate, in about half an hour; feels she cannot stop","Vomits after every binge","Takes four senna tablets two or three times a week","Skips breakfast and lunch on bad days","Does not have diabetes and takes no regular medicines","Teeth have become sensitive; brushes straight after vomiting","Occasional palpitations and leg cramps; no fainting; mood flat, no self-harm or suicidal thoughts; drinks heavily some Saturdays","Her self-worth depends on her shape"],"worries":["Is there a tablet?","If I stop being sick, I'll put on loads of weight","My weight's normal, so it can't be serious?"],"rules":["Cries when thanked for being honest; settles if validated","Asks about tablets again if the explanation is rushed","Only mentions laxatives and teeth if asked directly","Agrees to bloods and an ECG once she understands why"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Jade, I'm Dr Ahmed, a psychiatry doctor who works with your GP. What we talk about stays between us and your care team, unless I'm worried about your safety.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Okay. This is so embarrassing. Is there just a tablet I can take to stop it?"},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"We'll definitely talk about treatment. First, thank you for telling your GP. That takes courage.","m":[1,8]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"I've never told anyone. Not even my mum."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"That must have been a lot to carry on your own. What you're describing is common, and it's an illness, not a weakness.","m":[8]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Thanks. I just feel disgusting."},{"t":"1:10","band":"Their story","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you feel that way. Can you tell me what happens during a binge?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"I eat loads, really fast. A whole loaf as toast, cereal, chocolate. About half an hour."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"How often? And do you feel you can stop once you've started?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Four or five evenings a week. And no, I can't stop. It's like autopilot."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"What do you do afterwards to make up for it?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"I make myself sick. Every time."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Anything else, like laxatives, water tablets, fasting or exercise?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Senna. Four tablets, two or three times a week. And I skip meals on bad days."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Do you have diabetes or take insulin?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about your weight and shape? How much does it affect how you see yourself?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"It's everything. If I feel fat, the whole day's ruined."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Have you noticed palpitations, dizziness, cramps or fainting?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"My heart flutters sometimes. Leg cramps at night. Never fainted."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"And your teeth?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"They've gone really sensitive. I brush straight after, to get rid of the taste."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"How's your mood? Any thoughts of self-harm or of ending your life?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Flat, mostly. But no, I've never hurt myself."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"Do you drink or use drugs?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"I drink too much some Saturdays. No drugs."},{"t":"3:40","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being so honest. What you've described is bulimia nervosa. It's common and very treatable.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"But my weight's normal. It can't be that serious, can it?"},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"I understand why it seems that way. But being sick and using laxatives can lower your potassium, which can upset your heart rhythm at any weight. The fluttering could be a sign.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Oh. That's scary."},{"t":"4:25","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"That's why I'd like a blood test and a heart tracing today. If your potassium is very low, under 2.5, or the tracing is abnormal, you'd be seen at hospital today.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Okay. And the tablet?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Tablets on their own don't work well for bulimia. The first step is guided self-help: a workbook with short sessions with a therapist. If that isn't enough, you'd have CBT for eating disorders, at least twenty sessions.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"If I stop being sick, I'll put on loads of weight."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"That's a really common fear. Vomiting doesn't remove most of what you eat, and regular meals usually reduce binges. Most people's weight stays fairly steady. If your mood stays low, medication can help alongside therapy.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Okay. What about my teeth?"},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"Rinse with water or a fluoride mouthwash after being sick, and don't brush for about an hour. Please book a dentist too. And don't stop the senna suddenly; we'll cut it down together.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed, so I know I've been clear?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Bloods and heart test today. Guided self-help first. Rinse, don't brush. See a dentist."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Here's the Beat helpline number. If you faint, get chest pain, your heart races, or you feel unsafe, call 999 or go to A&E. I'll see you with your results.","m":[12,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains confidentiality and its limits, and thanks her for disclosing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Clarifies binge frequency, size, speed and loss of control","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about all compensatory behaviours: vomiting, laxatives, diuretics, fasting, exercise","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about diabetes and insulin omission","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores weight and shape concern and its effect on self-worth","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about palpitations, dizziness, cramps, fainting and dental damage","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about mood, self-harm, suicidal thoughts, alcohol and drugs","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Responds to her shame with warmth and normalising, without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names bulimia nervosa and explains why a normal weight can still be dangerous","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains guided self-help first, then CBT-ED, and that tablets alone are not recommended","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Arranges potassium and ECG today and states which result means same-day hospital review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives dental advice, laxative advice and the Beat helpline","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back and safety-nets with clear urgent signs","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Takes a non-judgemental, precise history of binges and purging, screens physical and mental risk, and explains stepped therapy with same-day bloods and ECG.","borderline":"Good history but misses laxatives, insulin or teeth, or the treatment explanation is muddled.","fail":"Offers a tablet as the answer, ignores potassium and ECG because her weight is normal, or responds to her shame in a judgemental way."}},"c-2-8-1":{"setup":"You are the CMHT doctor in Monday clinic. Aisha, 31, with bipolar I disorder stable on lithium for four years, wants to try for a baby. Her sister has just started valproate. Counsel her about lithium in pregnancy and the valproate rules.","role":{"who":"Mrs Aisha Rahman, 31, female, primary school teacher","open":"My partner and I want to try for a baby. Do I have to stop my lithium?","manner":"Thoughtful, prepared, a little anxious. Has read online about heart defects. Becomes tearful about feeling selfish; steadier once given numbers and a plan she shares in.","free":["She and her partner, Sam, want to start trying soon","Has read that lithium causes heart defects","Her sister, 27, was recently started on valproate by another hospital"],"ifasked":["Diagnosed at 23 after a manic episode with psychosis; admitted for six weeks","Stopped lithium herself at 26 and was manic within three months, admitted again","Stable on lithium for four years since; last level, kidney and thyroid tests normal","Still taking the combined pill; not pregnant","Does not smoke; rarely drinks","Sam notices early signs: less sleep and talking fast","Most important to her: a healthy baby and staying well for it"],"worries":["Will lithium harm my baby?","Isn't it selfish to stay on medication?","Is my sister's valproate safe?","Can I breastfeed?"],"rules":["Tearful when she says she feels selfish; settles if validated","Asks directly about her sister's treatment; accepts a general answer","Will only accept 'it's small' if given actual numbers","Keen to bring Sam to the next appointment"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Aisha, I'm Dr Evans. Good to see you. What would you like to talk about today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"My partner and I want to try for a baby. Do I have to stop my lithium?"},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming to talk before trying. That's exactly the right time. You don't necessarily have to stop. Can I ask what you already know?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"I read lithium causes heart defects. That's really all I know."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"Okay, we'll go through that properly. What matters most to you in this decision?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"A healthy baby. And being well enough to look after it."},{"t":"1:05","band":"Her history","w":"D","s":"Both really important. Can you remind me how unwell you've been in the past?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"I was manic with psychosis at 23. Six weeks in hospital."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"And has lithium ever been stopped?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"I stopped it myself at 26. I was manic again within three months and back in hospital."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"That's very helpful to know. Are you using contraception now, and could you be pregnant at the moment?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"I'm on the pill. Definitely not pregnant."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Good. Please keep taking it until we've agreed a plan together.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Okay. So does lithium harm the baby?"},{"t":"2:10","band":"Risks both ways","w":"D","s":"Lithium slightly raises the chance of a heart problem in the baby. Without lithium, about 1 baby in 100 has one. With lithium, it's roughly 2 in 100. So most babies are fine.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Two in a hundred. That's still a risk."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"It is. We'd offer a detailed scan of the baby's heart in pregnancy. But we also have to weigh what happens if you stop.","m":[5,9]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Isn't it selfish, though? Taking it while I'm pregnant?"},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Not at all. I can hear how much you already care about this baby. Staying well is good for the baby too.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Sorry. I just want to do the right thing."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"You are doing the right thing, by asking. Given that you relapsed quickly off lithium, stopping carries a high risk of becoming unwell, especially after birth, when there's a risk of postpartum psychosis.","m":[6,10]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"So I should stay on it?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"It's your decision, and we'll make it together. Whatever happens, please never stop lithium suddenly. If you did decide to stop, it would be slowly, over at least four weeks.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"What if I stay on it in pregnancy?"},{"t":"4:15","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"We'd check your level every four weeks, then weekly from 36 weeks, and within 24 hours of the birth, because levels change a lot. You'd deliver in hospital and we'd plan your dose for after the birth.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"And breastfeeding?"},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Lithium passes into breast milk, so it needs careful planning. The perinatal team will talk it through. Please start folic acid, 400 micrograms daily, once you start trying.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"Can I ask about my sister? She's 27 and just started valproate."},{"t":"5:05","band":"Sister","w":"D","s":"I can't comment on her care. In general, UK rules say valproate shouldn't be started in anyone under 55 unless two specialists agree there's no alternative.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Why so strict?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"In pregnancy, about 1 in 10 babies has a birth defect, and up to 4 in 10 have developmental problems. Women who could get pregnant need reliable contraception and yearly review. She should ask her own doctor, and not stop suddenly.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Okay. I'll tell her to ask."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"I'll refer you to the perinatal mental health team for planning. Would you like Sam there too?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Yes. He's the one who spots my early signs."},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what you're taking away today?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Keep the pill and lithium for now, don't stop suddenly. Heart risk about 2 in 100. Perinatal team, with Sam. Folic acid when we try."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Spot on. If Sam notices early signs, like less sleep or racing thoughts, call us or the crisis line the same day. If you ever think you're pregnant, tell us straight away.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, welcomes her planning ahead, and sets a shared agenda","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what she knows about lithium in pregnancy and what matters most to her","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes relapse history, including admissions and what happened when lithium stopped","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks current contraception and whether she could be pregnant","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives cardiac malformation risk in absolute numbers, about 1 to 2 in 100","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the relapse risk of stopping, including postpartum psychosis","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Advises never stopping lithium suddenly, with tapering over at least four weeks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises continuing contraception until a plan is agreed, and folic acid when trying","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Outlines monitoring: levels every 4 weeks, weekly from 36 weeks, within 24 hours of birth, fetal heart scan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her guilt and fear without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains valproate rules in general terms without discussing her sister's care","d":"Content"},{"c":"Refers to the perinatal team and offers to involve her partner","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking her to summarise","d":"Close"},{"c":"Safety-nets with early warning signs and what to do if pregnant","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Balances absolute fetal risk against her clear relapse risk, gives the monitoring schedule, keeps contraception in place, and handles the sister question without breaching confidentiality.","borderline":"Accurate but one-sided, or numbers given as relative risk, or the monitoring schedule is incomplete.","fail":"Tells her to stop lithium, gives no numbers, or comments on her sister's treatment directly."}},"c-2-8-2":{"setup":"You are the doctor at the community drug and alcohol service on Wednesday. Kayleigh, 28, on methadone and off heroin for six months, is 14 weeks pregnant. She wants her dose cut fast. Explain methadone in pregnancy, address her fears and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Kayleigh Morris, 28, female, on methadone maintenance","open":"I want my methadone cut, fast. I don't want my baby born addicted, and I don't want social services taking it.","manner":"Determined, guarded, braced to be judged. Defensive about social services. Breaks down when talking about losing the baby; becomes calmer and more open once she feels on the same side.","free":["14 weeks pregnant, booked with a midwife","On methadone 60 mg daily, supervised at the chemist","Off heroin for six months","Terrified the baby will be taken"],"ifasked":["No heroin, crack or benzodiazepines in the last month; the odd drink before she knew she was pregnant, none since","Some evening cravings, no clear withdrawal","Has not yet met the specialist substance misuse midwife","Lives with her son, 6; ex-partner not involved; no one hurting or pressuring her","Keeps methadone in a kitchen cupboard","Had naloxone once but lost it; thinks she doesn't need it now"],"worries":["Will my baby be born addicted?","Will social services take my baby?","Do I need naloxone if I'm clean?","Can I breastfeed?"],"rules":["Pushes once more for a fast reduction; accepts a slow option if offered","Cries when talking about the baby being taken; settles if validated and given honest information","Mentions evening cravings only if asked about withdrawal or cravings","Resists naloxone at first; accepts it when the child-safety reason is given"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Kayleigh, I'm Dr Hughes. Thanks for coming in. How are you doing?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"I want my methadone cut, fast. I don't want my baby born addicted, and I don't want social services taking it."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"It's clear how much you care about this baby. Six months off heroin is a big achievement. Let's talk it through properly. What worries you most?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"That my baby's born rattling. And that they'll say I'm a bad mum."},{"t":"0:50","band":"Where things are","w":"D","s":"Those are big worries. Can I check where things are first? Have you used heroin, crack, benzos or alcohol in the last month?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing. A few drinks before I knew I was pregnant, none since."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Well done. On your 60 mg, do you get any withdrawal or cravings?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Bit of craving in the evenings. Not bad."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. That can happen more as pregnancy goes on. Who lives at home with you?","m":[9]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Me and my son. He's six."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Is anyone hurting you or pressuring you, at home or elsewhere?","m":[9]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"No. His dad's not around."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Where do you keep your methadone?","m":[10]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Kitchen cupboard."},{"t":"2:00","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Okay, we'll come back to that. About cutting fast: I understand why you want to. But a quick cut can put your baby into withdrawal in the womb, which can cause distress or early labour.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"So I'm stuck on it?"},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Not stuck. A fast cut also makes relapse more likely, and using again after a drop is when overdoses happen. A steady dose is the safest thing for both of you right now.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"I still want to come off."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"That's a fair goal. If you still want to reduce, we can do it slowly, in small steps, with close checks. We'd stop if you started struggling.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Will the baby be addicted, though?"},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"I'll be honest. Your baby may have some withdrawal after birth, like being unsettled or feeding poorly. The hospital watches for it and treats it. It doesn't cause lasting harm.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"And social services? Everyone says they'll take it."},{"t":"3:45","band":"Social care","w":"D","s":"Being on methadone isn't, by itself, a reason to take a baby away. A social worker may be involved before the birth, to see what support you need.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"I can't lose another thing. I've worked so hard."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"I can see how frightening this is, after everything you've done. You're stable, you're honest, and you're asking the right questions. That's what we'll tell people.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"You'll be on my side?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Yes. If anyone needs to be involved, we'll talk to you first. No surprises.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thank you."},{"t":"4:40","band":"Safety at home","w":"D","s":"Now, the methadone in the cupboard. Even a small amount can kill a child. Please keep it in a locked box. We can give you one today.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I didn't think about him getting into it."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"Do you have naloxone at home?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"I lost it. I don't need it now, I'm clean."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad you're clean. Think of naloxone like a fire extinguisher. It could also save your son if he swallowed methadone. I'll show you how to use it.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Fine. Fair enough."},{"t":"5:40","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'll also arrange joint care with the specialist substance misuse midwife and your obstetrician, and a birth plan so the baby team know what to watch for.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Can I breastfeed?"},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"Usually yes, if you're stable and not using anything else, and it can help the baby. Your midwife will check your blood tests.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed, so I'm sure I've been clear?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Stay on 60 for now, slow cut later if I want. Locked box, naloxone. Meet the specialist midwife. Social services isn't automatic."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If evening cravings get worse, tell us. Your dose may need adjusting, and that's normal in pregnancy. I'll see you in four weeks, sooner if you need.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and acknowledges her motivation and achievement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what worries her most before explaining","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks recent use of heroin, crack, benzodiazepines, alcohol and current withdrawal or cravings","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains why a steady dose is safer than a rapid cut: fetal withdrawal and overdose risk","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers a slow, monitored reduction later rather than refusing outright","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Describes neonatal withdrawal honestly and that it is treatable","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her fear of losing the baby with genuine empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains openly that methadone alone is not a reason for removal, and that social care may be involved","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about other children at home and domestic abuse","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Advises locked storage out of children's reach","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Supplies naloxone and explains its use, including for a child","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Arranges joint care with the specialist substance misuse midwife and a birth plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, safety-nets for cravings and agrees review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Explains clearly why a steady dose is safest, is honest about neonatal withdrawal and social care, covers storage and naloxone, and links her to the specialist midwife.","borderline":"Correct advice but defensive or rushed on social care, or misses storage, naloxone or her son.","fail":"Agrees a rapid detox, falsely promises social care won't be involved, or ignores child safety at home."}},"c-2-8-3":{"setup":"You visit Hannah, 32, at home with the crisis team. Her baby is 7 weeks old and she told her health visitor she feels 'a terrible mother'. Her partner is home. Take a postnatal depression history, assess risk to mother and baby, explain treatment.","role":{"who":"Mrs Hannah Clarke, 32, female, on maternity leave (her partner Tom, 34, joins at the end)","open":"I don't really know why you're here. I'm just tired. Every mum's tired.","manner":"Flat, tearful, guarded at first, worried about being judged. Opens up when not rushed. Breaks down when disclosing frightening thoughts about the baby; relieved when they are explained.","free":["Baby Isla is 7 weeks old","Can't sleep even when Isla sleeps","Feels a terrible mother","Tom is supportive but back at work"],"ifasked":["Low and tearful most days for about five weeks; nothing feels enjoyable","Eating very little","Feels distant from Isla, 'like looking after someone else's baby'","Has unwanted images of dropping Isla on the stairs; horrified by them and avoids carrying her on the stairs","Has thought everyone would be better off without her, but no plans and would not act because of Isla and Tom","No hearing voices, no confusion, no strange beliefs","No previous mental illness; her aunt had 'a breakdown' after a baby, no diagnosis known","Breastfeeding; no one at home is hurting her"],"worries":["Will they take my baby?","Can I take tablets while breastfeeding?","I don't want to see a psychiatrist, I just need sleep"],"rules":["Only discloses the images about Isla if asked directly and kindly","Cries after disclosing; settles if told this is common and that her horror is reassuring","Asks whether the baby will be taken; needs a clear honest answer","Agrees to Tom joining for the plan"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Hannah, I'm Dr Mensah from the crisis team. Your health visitor was worried and asked us to visit. We're here to help, not to judge. Would you like to talk on your own first?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Yes, okay. I don't really know why you're here. I'm just tired. Every mum's tired."},{"t":"0:35","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"They are. But it sounds like more than that. How have you been feeling since Isla was born?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Low. Crying most days. For about five weeks now."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"Are you able to enjoy anything, even small things?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Not really. Nothing feels like anything."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"How's your sleep when Isla's asleep? And your appetite?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"I just lie there. I'm barely eating."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about Isla?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Distant. Like I'm looking after someone else's baby. I'm a terrible mother."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry you feel that. Depression makes caring mums feel terrible. The fact you're worried tells me how much you care.","m":[10]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"She deserves better."},{"t":"1:40","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Sometimes mums have frightening thoughts about harm coming to their baby. Has anything like that happened?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"I get pictures of dropping her on the stairs. I'd never do it. I'm a monster."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That took real courage. Those thoughts are common with postnatal depression, and the fact they horrify you is reassuring. Have you ever felt you might act on them?","m":[5,6,10]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I hold the bannister, or Tom carries her."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"So you're keeping her safe. Have you had thoughts that you'd be better off dead, or of ending your life?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think everyone would be better off without me."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about how, or made any plans?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"No. I couldn't do that to Isla or Tom."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Have you heard voices, felt confused, or that things aren't real, or had strange ideas?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Have there been any mental health problems in the past, like bipolar disorder? Or anyone in the family after a baby?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"No. My aunt had a breakdown after her baby, but I don't know what it was."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"That's useful, thanks. Who helps you? And is anyone at home hurting or frightening you?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Tom's lovely, but he's back at work. No, nobody hurts me."},{"t":"4:00","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"What you're describing sounds like postnatal depression. It's common, it's treatable, and it doesn't make you a bad mother.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Will they take Isla if I admit how bad it is?"},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"That's a really common fear. Getting help is what keeps families together. Most mums with depression get better at home with their baby.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Can I take tablets? I'm breastfeeding."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Yes. Sertraline is often used, as very little gets into breast milk. There's also talking therapy, and a specialist perinatal team who work with new mums.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"I don't want a psychiatrist. I just need sleep."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"Sleep really matters, and they can help with that too. Would you meet them once?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Okay. Once. Can Tom come in now?"},{"t":"5:20","band":"Safety plan","w":"D","s":"Of course. Hi Tom. Hannah's unwell with depression, not failing. Can I go through a plan with you both?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"Partner: Yes, please. What can I do?"},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Could you take some night feeds so Hannah can sleep? I'll refer urgently to the perinatal team and the health visitor will visit more often.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Partner: Yes. What should I watch for?"},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"Call 999 straight away if Hannah seems confused, isn't sleeping at all, says strange things, or talks about harming herself or Isla. I'll write this down with the crisis number for you both.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Hannah, can you tell me what we've agreed?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Tom does night feeds, I see the perinatal team, think about sertraline. Call 999 if I get confused or feel unsafe."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"That's it. We'll see you again within a week, sooner if needed. You've been really brave today.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains why here, and offers to talk alone first","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about mood, enjoyment, sleep when the baby sleeps, and appetite","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about bonding and feelings towards the baby","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and what stops her acting","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly and kindly about thoughts of harm to the baby","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Distinguishes unwanted intrusive thoughts from intent to act","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for postpartum psychosis: voices, confusion, unreality, odd beliefs","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about previous bipolar disorder or postpartum illness and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about support and domestic abuse","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates guilt and distress without false reassurance","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains diagnosis and options: therapy, sertraline with breastfeeding, perinatal team","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a written safety plan with her and partner, including when to call 999","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, arranges review within a week and closes warmly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Asks directly and kindly about suicide and harm to the baby, separates intrusive thoughts from intent, rules out psychosis, and agrees a written plan with both parents.","borderline":"Good rapport but one key risk question is vague, or psychosis and bipolar history are not covered.","fail":"Avoids asking about harm to the baby, treats intrusive thoughts as intent, or leaves without a safety plan."}},"c-2-8-4":{"setup":"Saturday 02:30, postnatal ward. Hannah Okafor, 29, had her first baby five days ago, has barely slept and says he has been swapped. Her husband David is with her. Assess her now, decide the level of care and keep mother and baby safe.","role":{"who":"Mrs Hannah Okafor, 29, first-time mother on day 5 after birth (her husband, Mr David Okafor, 31, is at the bedside)","open":"Who are you? Did the midwife send you? I'm fine, honestly. I've never felt better.","manner":"Fast, distractible and perplexed, swinging between elation and suspicion. Becomes tearful and frightened if she thinks the baby will be taken; settles a little with a calm, slow voice. David is anxious and guilty.","free":["Has slept about two hours since Thursday and feels 'like electricity'","Thoughts are racing; she has been sorting the baby's clothes by colour","Believes the baby was swapped in the nursery and sends her signals through his eyes","First baby, normal vaginal delivery, breastfeeding"],"ifasked":["Hears a quiet woman's voice near the cot saying the baby isn't hers; it gives no commands","Denies any thought or urge to harm the baby: 'I'd never hurt a baby, even if he isn't mine'","Once tonight thought it 'would be better if I wasn't here'; no plan, no intent","No previous mental illness (David)","Her maternal aunt has bipolar disorder and was admitted several times (David)","Asked twice if it was Wednesday and has not eaten today (David)","No fever, headache or fits; no alcohol or drugs; takes only paracetamol and iron"],"worries":["Are you going to take my baby away?","I'm not ill. Every new mum is tired.","(David) Is this the baby blues? Will she go back to normal?","(David) I should have noticed. Is this my fault?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful if she thinks the baby will be removed; calms if told the aim is to keep them together","Answers honestly about harm to the baby and herself only if asked plainly and kindly","Agrees to stay tonight if David can stay and she can see the baby","David gives the family history only if asked directly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Shah, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Hannah? And you must be David. Congratulations on your baby.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Who sent you? Did the midwife say something? I'm fine, honestly. I've never felt better."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"The midwife asked me to come because you've had very little sleep. I'd like to hear how things have been. Is it okay if we talk for a few minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"Okay. But quickly. There's so much to do. I've been sorting all his clothes by colour."},{"t":"0:34","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"How much sleep have you managed since the birth?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"Maybe two hours since Thursday. I don't need it. I feel amazing, like electricity."},{"t":"0:44","w":"D","s":"Are your thoughts coming faster than usual?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"So fast. I start one thing and three more arrive. David says I'm talking too much."},{"t":"0:54","w":"D","s":"The midwife said you feel the baby isn't yours. Can you tell me more about that?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"They switched him in the nursery. Look at his eyes. He's sending me signals. My real baby's somewhere else."},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening and confusing. How sure are you about it?","m":[3,8]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"Completely sure. Nobody believes me. (starts crying) Why won't anyone listen?"},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"I can see how upsetting this is, and I am listening. You're exhausted and scared, and you've been carrying this on your own tonight.","m":[8]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"(quieter) I just want to know he's safe."},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"Have you been hearing voices or seeing things other people don't seem to notice?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"There's a quiet voice. A woman's voice, near the cot. It says he isn't mine."},{"t":"1:44","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. When you feel he isn't yours, have you had any thoughts of hurting him, or that you need to do something to him?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"No. I'd never hurt a baby, even if he isn't mine. I just want my real one back."},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"Has the voice ever told you to do anything to him, or to yourself?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"No. It just tells me things. It doesn't tell me to do stuff."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"When people feel this overwhelmed, they sometimes have thoughts of ending their life. Have you had any thoughts like that?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"Once tonight I thought maybe it would be better if I wasn't here. Then it went. I wouldn't do anything."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"Have you made any plan, or thought about how?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"2:37","band":"Background and physical","w":"D","s":"David, can I ask you a few things too? Has Hannah ever had problems with her mood before?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"(David) Never. She's the calm one. This came on yesterday, out of nowhere."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Has anyone in her family had bipolar disorder, or been unwell after having a baby?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"(David) Her aunt on her mum's side has bipolar. She's been in hospital a few times."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful. Has she had a fever, headache or fits, or seemed muddled about the day or where she is?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"(David) No fever. She asked me twice if it was Wednesday. And she hasn't eaten today."},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"Her temperature is 37.1 and her blood pressure is 128 over 80, which is reassuring. I'm still sending bloods and a urine test tonight, to check for infection, thyroid, salts, sugar and drugs, because these can all cause confusion after a birth.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"(David) Is this the baby blues? I should have noticed. I let her go two nights without sleep."},{"t":"3:42","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"This came on very fast, and you called for help, which was exactly right. It isn't your fault. And it isn't the baby blues, which is much milder.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"(David) Then what is it?"},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"I think Hannah has postpartum psychosis. It's an illness that can start in the days after birth, with little sleep, racing thoughts and beliefs that feel completely real. It's serious, but it's treatable, and most women recover fully.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"I'm not ill. Every new mum is tired. Are you going to take my baby away?"},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"Your baby is safe, and our aim is to keep you together. There are special units where mums and babies are admitted together, called Mother and Baby Units.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I don't want to go anywhere. I want to go home."},{"t":"4:33","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I understand you want to be at home with him. Right now I'm worried about you, and you need proper rest and specialist care. Could you stay here tonight while we sort that out?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"If David stays. And I keep seeing the baby."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Yes, David can stay. Tonight a nurse will be with you all the time, and a named midwife will look after your baby close by, where you can see him. That's to help you rest.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:04","w":"P","s":"(David) So she shouldn't be on her own with him?"},{"t":"5:08","w":"D","s":"Not for now, until the specialist team has seen her. Please stay close, and let the staff help with feeds and nappies.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:17","w":"P","s":"(David) What happens next tonight?"},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"I'm phoning the perinatal team now so they can see Hannah in the next few hours, and asking the bed manager for a Mother and Baby Unit bed. I'll tell my consultant, the midwife in charge and the hospital safeguarding lead, so everyone works together.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:37","w":"P","s":"(David) And if she tries to leave?"},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"If she wants to leave and we're worried she can't make that choice safely, my consultant and a specialist social worker may assess her under the Mental Health Act. We'd only do that to keep her and the baby safe.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:56","w":"P","s":"(David) Okay. I just want her back."},{"t":"5:59","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"David, so I can check I've explained it clearly, can you tell me what you understand is happening tonight?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:07","w":"P","s":"(David) It's postpartum psychosis, it's treatable, a nurse stays with her, the baby's with the staff nearby, and you're finding a mother and baby bed."},{"t":"6:16","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. If she seems more confused, talks about harming herself or the baby, or tries to leave with him, tell the nurse straight away. Here's the ward number and a leaflet from Action on Postpartum Psychosis.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:31","w":"P","s":"Will I get better?"},{"t":"6:33","w":"D","s":"Yes, Hannah. With treatment, most women get completely well. I'll come back once I've spoken to the team, and a senior doctor will see you first thing in the morning.","m":[9,14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Okay. I'll stay tonight, if David stays."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks names, congratulates and speaks calmly at her pace","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about sleep, energy and racing thoughts since the birth","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the 'swapped' belief and asks about voices or other perceptual changes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks plainly about thoughts or commands to harm the baby","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts and plans","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks the husband about past mood illness and family history of bipolar disorder","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks obs, asks about fever and confusion, and sends a physical screen for organic causes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her fear of losing the baby and the husband's guilt","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names postpartum psychosis, says it is treatable, and distinguishes it from baby blues","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Puts 1:1 nursing in place with the baby cared for in sight of a named staff member","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Calls perinatal team and bed manager for a Mother and Baby Unit bed; tells consultant and safeguarding lead","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks the husband's understanding by asking him to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets: warning signs, who to tell, a number, and what happens if she tries to leave","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes with what happens next and when they will next see a doctor","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Treats it as an emergency, asks plainly about harm to baby and self, screens for organic causes, and secures 1:1, urgent perinatal review and an MBU bed with the family on board.","borderline":"Safe plan, but harm questions are vague, the organic screen is thin, or the explanation is rushed and full of jargon.","fail":"Calls it baby blues, avoids asking about harm to the baby, leaves her alone with the baby, or gives advice without arranging specialist review tonight."}},"c-2-8-5":{"setup":"Wednesday 14:00, perinatal clinic. Laura Bennett, 33, 26 weeks pregnant on quetiapine, has just been told she has gestational diabetes and wants to stop the tablet. Then see Chloe Turner, 27, whose baby is 6 weeks old and who fears she'll hurt her.","role":{"who":"Mrs Laura Bennett, 33, 26 weeks pregnant, bipolar disorder on quetiapine; then Ms Chloe Turner, 27, mother of a 6-week-old girl","open":"I've just been told I've got diabetes. Is it that tablet? Because if it is, I'm stopping it today.","manner":"Laura is angry and frightened, softening once her complaint is taken seriously and her choice respected. Chloe is ashamed, quiet and tearful; visibly relieved when told her thoughts fit OCD.","free":["Laura: glucose tolerance test this morning showed gestational diabetes","Laura: has taken quetiapine for three years and has been well on it","Chloe: wrote 'please help, I keep thinking I'll hurt my baby' on the form","Chloe: keeps picturing dropping her baby down the stairs"],"ifasked":["Laura: four years ago she stopped lithium and was manic within a week, needing a six-week admission","Laura: diabetes team suggested diet and home glucose checks, possibly metformin","Laura: nobody warned her quetiapine could raise blood sugar","Chloe: the thoughts horrify her and she has never wanted to act on them","Chloe: no longer carries the baby upstairs, lets her partner do bath time, checks the stair gate many times","Chloe: no voices; the thoughts feel like her own","Chloe: mood a bit low, sleep broken by feeds, no thoughts of ending her life","Chloe: breastfeeding"],"worries":["Laura: Why wasn't I warned?","Laura: Can't I stop it until the baby's born?","Chloe: Am I a monster?","Chloe: Will the health visitor take her off me? Can I take anything while breastfeeding?"],"rules":["Laura gets sharper if told she must take the tablet; calms when her right to choose is respected and the risks are laid out","Laura agrees to continue once the diabetes plan is concrete","Chloe cries when she asks if she is a monster; settles if validated and told such thoughts are common","Chloe mentions avoidance and checking only if asked what she does after the thought"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Bennett, I'm Dr Shah from the perinatal team. I can see you've had hard news this morning. What's worrying you most?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"I've got gestational diabetes. Is it that tablet? If it is, I'm stopping it today."},{"t":"0:18","band":"Explain the link","w":"D","s":"That's a fair question, and I'll be straight with you. Quetiapine can raise blood sugar, so it may well have played a part. It isn't something you've done wrong.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Nobody told me that. Why wasn't I warned?"},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you weren't told clearly. You had every right to know. Can I ask a bit about your illness, so we can work out what's safest?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"0:48","band":"Her history","w":"D","s":"Before quetiapine, how unwell did you get, and how quickly?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"Four years ago I stopped lithium and within a week I was manic. Not sleeping, spending money. I was in hospital six weeks."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"That sounds a frightening time. What have the diabetes team suggested so far?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Diet, checking my sugars at home, maybe metformin if that's not enough."},{"t":"1:16","band":"Weigh it up","w":"D","s":"So here's the balance. If you stop quetiapine suddenly, there's a real chance the bipolar comes back, and the first weeks after the birth carry the highest risk of all. Diabetes in pregnancy is something the diabetes team treat every day, and most babies are born healthy.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Can't I just stop it until the baby's born and restart afterwards?"},{"t":"1:41","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting to protect the baby. A relapse late in pregnancy or just after birth can be serious for you both, and switching tablets now would expose the baby to two medicines. It's your decision, and I'll support you whichever way you go.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"So what would you do?"},{"t":"2:03","band":"Agree a plan","w":"D","s":"My advice is to keep taking it for now, let the diabetes clinic manage your sugars, and have my consultant review the dose with you. We'd also write a plan for after the birth, so you're seen in the first week.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"(sighs) Okay. If the diabetes people are on it, I'll keep going."},{"t":"2:27","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll put that in a letter to you, the diabetes clinic, your midwife and your GP, so everyone has the same plan. Can you tell me what we've agreed, so I know I've been clear?","m":[5,12]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"Keep taking it, the diabetes clinic checks my sugars, your consultant looks at the dose, and there's a plan for after the baby."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll see you in two weeks. If your mood goes up or down, you're sleeping much less, or you're tempted to stop the tablet, ring us sooner.","m":[13]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Alright. Thanks for being honest."},{"t":"3:08","band":"Second patient: her thoughts","w":"D","s":"Hello Ms Turner, I'm Dr Shah. Thank you for writing what you did on the form; that took courage. Many new parents have frightening thoughts they don't want. Can you tell me what yours are like?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"(quietly) I keep picturing dropping her down the stairs. It just comes into my head."},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"How do you feel when that picture comes?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Sick. Horrified. I'd never want that. (starts crying) Am I a monster?"},{"t":"3:41","w":"D","s":"No, you're not. These thoughts are upsetting precisely because they go against everything you want for her. You've been carrying this alone, and I'm glad you told me.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"I thought you'd say I was dangerous."},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"What do you do after the thought comes? Some parents check things, avoid certain jobs, or ask for reassurance.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"I don't carry her upstairs any more. My partner does bath time. I check the stair gate over and over."},{"t":"4:16","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Have you ever felt the thought was coming from outside you, or heard a voice telling you to do it?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"No. It's my own thought. I just hate it."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Have you ever felt that acting on it would be right, or wanted to?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"Never. Not for a second."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been, and your sleep?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:44","w":"P","s":"A bit low. Sleep's broken with feeds, but I get back off."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Have you had any thoughts about ending your life?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"No. I want to be here for her."},{"t":"4:59","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"What you're describing sounds like OCD after birth. Unwanted thoughts like these are common, and mothers who have them don't act on them. The checking and avoiding make sense, but they keep the fear going.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:14","w":"P","s":"If I tell the health visitor, will they take her off me?"},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"No. Thoughts that horrify you are not a reason for that. Sharing them helps us get you the right treatment quickly.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"So what's the treatment?"},{"t":"5:33","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"The main one is a talking therapy called CBT with exposure and response prevention. Step by step, you'd get back to carrying and bathing her without the checking. I'll refer you with priority because you've just had a baby.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:49","w":"P","s":"I'm breastfeeding. Can I take anything?"},{"t":"5:53","w":"D","s":"Yes. Sertraline is widely used in breastfeeding and very little reaches the baby. We'd start at 50 milligrams a day; it takes a few weeks to help, and mild sickness or feeling edgy at first is common.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"I'd like to try both."},{"t":"6:12","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Good. Can you tell me in your words what we think is going on?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"It's OCD. The thoughts are horrible but they don't mean I'll do it. Therapy, and maybe sertraline."},{"t":"6:27","w":"D","s":"That's it exactly. I'll see you in two weeks. If the thoughts start to feel right, or come as a voice, or your mood drops a lot, contact us the same day, or ring 111 and choose option 2 out of hours.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel lighter already."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and asks what worries her most before giving information","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Says honestly that quetiapine can raise blood sugar, without blame, and accepts the complaint","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about past relapses and how fast she becomes unwell off medication","d":"Content"},{"c":"Weighs relapse risk, highest after birth, against metabolic risk in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Respects her choice and agrees a written plan: continue, diabetes monitoring, postnatal relapse plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Uses a normalising open question to invite the new mother to describe her thoughts","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks how the thought feels and about checking, avoidance and reassurance-seeking","d":"Content"},{"c":"Excludes psychosis: thoughts from outside, voices, any wish to act","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about mood, sleep and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her shame and explains intrusive thoughts as OCD without dismissing them","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers CBT with exposure and response prevention and sertraline as compatible with breastfeeding","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks each woman's understanding in her own words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Books a 2-week review with each and says what should prompt earlier contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Honest about the glucose link, weighs relapse against metabolic risk, agrees a written plan; then distinguishes OCD from psychosis and offers ERP and sertraline, with reviews for both.","borderline":"Plans are safe but the relapse risk is poorly explained or the OCD assessment misses avoidance or psychotic screening.","fail":"Agrees to stop quetiapine abruptly, or treats the mother's intrusive thoughts as intent and refers to children's social care."}},"c-2-8-6":{"setup":"Thursday 11:00, CMHT duty room. Sarah, a community midwife, phones from a home visit about Kayla Doyle, 24, 34 weeks pregnant. She asks if a safeguarding referral is over the top. Assess the risk, decide, and keep Kayla engaged.","role":{"who":"Ms Kayla Doyle, 24, 34 weeks pregnant, known to the CMHT with EUPD (Sarah Jones, community midwife, and Mark, the trust safeguarding lead, also speak)","open":"(Midwife) Hi, it's Sarah, community midwife. I'm at Kayla Doyle's flat. I'm wondering if a safeguarding referral is over the top.","manner":"Kayla is guarded, then angry when the referral is raised, then tearful and frightened about losing the baby; settles when treated honestly. Sarah is caring but worried about losing Kayla's trust.","free":["(Midwife) Kayla has missed three antenatal appointments","(Midwife) Kayla told her she used cocaine recently","(Midwife) her partner Jordan, 26, has a history of violence","Kayla: she badly wants this baby and has the cot ready"],"ifasked":["Used cocaine at a party two weeks ago, 'just a bit'; drinks little","Jordan gets rough when drinking; pushed her into a wall last month; 'never touched my bump'","Jordan is at work; she is safe to talk now","Jordan takes the car, which is partly why she misses appointments","First baby; her own mother is supportive and lives nearby","Sees her care coordinator fortnightly and likes her","Thinks about cutting during rows but has not for months; no thoughts of harming the baby","Jordan checks her phone"],"worries":["So you're reporting me?","They'll take her the minute she's born.","Why didn't anyone help me before?","(Midwife) I don't want to wreck my relationship with her."],"rules":["Kayla talks about Jordan only if asked kindly and after checking she is safe to talk","Gets angry when the referral is raised; settles if told honestly why and that the CMHT is staying involved","Agrees reluctantly if she hears it is about support before the birth","Sarah accepts the referral once the threshold reasons are clear"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Sarah, I'm Dr Shah, the duty doctor. Thanks for calling. What specifically is worrying you about Kayla?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"(Midwife) She's missed three antenatal appointments, she told me she used cocaine recently, and her partner Jordan has a history of violence. She's lovely with me, though, and I don't want to wreck that."},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"That's a fair worry. Have you seen anything today, like injuries, or Jordan being controlling?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","s":"(Midwife) He's at work. She flinched when his car pulled up last week, that's all."},{"t":"0:36","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Would Kayla be willing to speak to me? I'd rather she heard my thinking directly.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"(Midwife) I'll ask. (pause) She says okay. Here she is."},{"t":"0:48","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Hi Kayla, I'm Dr Shah, one of the doctors at the CMHT. Sarah asked me to have a chat. Is anyone else there with you, and is it okay to talk?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"Just Sarah. Jordan's at work. What's this about?"},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"Sarah's worried about how things are for you and the baby, and I'd like to understand. How are you feeling about the baby coming?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"I want her more than anything. I've got the cot and everything."},{"t":"1:21","w":"D","s":"That's lovely to hear. Is she your first, and who'll be around to support you after the birth?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"First one. Jordan, and my mum. She's round the corner. She's good."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Sarah mentioned some cocaine. How much are you using at the moment, and what about drink or other drugs?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"I only had a bit at a party, two weeks ago. It's not a big deal. I barely drink."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Even occasional cocaine can affect the placenta and the baby's growth. Would you meet the drug service, so they can help you stop?","m":[4,10]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"Maybe. I don't use much."},{"t":"2:07","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this. Does anyone at home hurt you, frighten you or control what you do?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"(pause) He gets rough when he's had a drink. He pushed me into the wall last month. But he's never touched my bump."},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. It isn't your fault, and you and the baby deserve to be safe. Has it got worse since you've been pregnant?","m":[5,9]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"A bit. He takes the car so I can't get to my appointments."},{"t":"2:42","w":"D","s":"Have you had any thoughts of self-harm or the baby?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"Never the baby. When we row I think about cutting, but I haven't for months. Nothing now."},{"t":"2:55","band":"Explain the decision","w":"D","s":"Kayla, I want to be honest with you. With the cocaine, Jordan hurting you and the missed check-ups together, I'm worried. I'm going to ask children's services to do an assessment before the birth, so support is ready when she arrives.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"So you're reporting me. I knew I couldn't trust you lot."},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"I can hear how angry and let down you feel. I'm telling you because I want to be straight with you, not go behind your back. This is about help, for you and for her.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"(crying) They'll take her off me the minute she's born."},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"I know that's the fear, and it's a big one. Most pre-birth assessments end in a support plan, not removal. And you've got real strengths: you want her, your mum's close, and you keep seeing your care coordinator.","m":[7,9]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"Why didn't anyone help me before it got this bad?"},{"t":"3:57","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"That's a fair question, and I'm sorry. This is how we make sure help is there now. I'm not going anywhere, and I'll ask your care coordinator to see you every week until after the birth.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Okay. I don't like it, but okay."},{"t":"4:16","w":"D","s":"There's also an IDVA, a specialist who supports women whose partners hurt them. Can I refer you? And there's a free 24-hour helpline, 0808 2000 247. Is it safe for you to keep that number?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Yes, refer me. Put the number in Sarah's notes, not my phone. He checks it."},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"Of course. If you're ever in danger, ring 999. Can you tell me what's going to happen now, so I know I've explained it properly?","m":[13]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"You're telling children's services so they can help before she's born, I'll see the drug people and the IDVA, and my coordinator comes every week."},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. Thank you, Kayla. Could you pass me back to Sarah?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:04","w":"P","s":"(Midwife) Hi. So it isn't over the top?"},{"t":"5:08","band":"Agree who acts","w":"D","s":"No. Substance use, violence at home and missed care together meet the threshold. Kayla's agreed, reluctantly. I'll ring our safeguarding lead now and call you straight back.","m":[7,12]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"(Safeguarding lead) Safeguarding, Mark speaking."},{"t":"5:23","w":"D","s":"Hi Mark, Dr Shah on CMHT duty. Kayla Doyle, 24, 34 weeks pregnant, EUPD, recent cocaine, partner pushed her last month, three missed antenatal appointments. She knows and has agreed. I'm proposing a referral to children's social care for a pre-birth assessment today.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"(Safeguarding lead) Agreed, that meets threshold. Phone the front door today and follow up in writing. Has she had a DASH done?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Not yet. Her care coordinator will do it this week alongside the IDVA referral, and if it scores high we'll refer to MARAC.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"5:59","w":"P","s":"(Safeguarding lead) Good. Make sure the midwife, GP and health visitor are copied in."},{"t":"6:06","w":"D","s":"Sarah, I've spoken to Mark. I'll phone children's services now and send the written referral by half twelve, copying you, the GP and the health visitor. Could you refer her to the substance misuse midwife, and I'll do the drug service?","m":[12,10]},{"t":"6:23","w":"P","s":"(Midwife) Yes, I'll do that today. That's much clearer, thanks."},{"t":"6:28","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I'll record what we discussed, who I spoke to and when, why I'm sharing, and that Kayla was told and agreed. The helpline number stays out of anything sent home.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"(Midwife) Perfect. Shall I tell her you'll be in touch?"},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Yes, please. I'll ring her tomorrow afternoon to see how she's doing. Thanks, Sarah.","m":[11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Asks the midwife for specific concerns and what she has observed","d":"Content"},{"c":"Introduces self to the woman and checks it is safe and private to talk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about feelings about the baby, support at home and other children","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about drug and alcohol use without judgement","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about abuse at home, including physical harm and escalation","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about thoughts of harming herself or the baby","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Weighs risks against protective features and states the threshold is met","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tells her honestly about the referral, why, and that it aims at support","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her anger and fear without backing away from the referral","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers drug service and IDVA referrals and the domestic abuse helpline in a safe format","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Commits to continued CMHT support with weekly contact","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees with the safeguarding lead and midwife who refers today and who is copied in","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding and gives emergency advice","d":"Close"},{"c":"States what will be recorded: rationale, names, times and what was shared","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Weighs risk and protective features, judges the threshold met, tells Kayla honestly, keeps her engaged, and agrees with the lead and midwife who refers today, with drug and IDVA support.","borderline":"Right decision, but misses asking about the partner alone, or leaves who refers and who is told unclear.","fail":"Waits until after the birth, refers secretly without good reason, or treats her diagnosis alone as the reason for referral."}},"c-2-8-7":{"setup":"Tuesday 10:30, CMHT clinic. Jess Carter, 34, is referred as '?bipolar' for a week of mood swings before each period. Later you see Helen Morris, 46, with recurrent depression, whose mood has dipped with night sweats and irregular periods. Assess both and agree plans.","role":{"who":"Ms Jess Carter, 34, referred for 'mood swings, ?bipolar'; then Mrs Helen Morris, 46, with recurrent depression","open":"I don't know. One week a month I'm a different person. Then it just lifts.","manner":"Jess is frustrated and defensive, fearing she won't be believed; warms when validated. Helen is tired and worried she is 'going mad'; relieved by a clear explanation.","free":["Jess: irritable, tearful and out of control for about a week before each period","Jess: well again a day or two after bleeding starts","Helen: mood has dipped again with poor sleep","Helen: night sweats and irregular periods"],"ifasked":["Jess: never had high mood, little need for sleep or racing ideas","Jess: pattern for years, worse since her youngest was born; two warnings at work; shouts at the children","Jess: in that week thinks everyone would be better off without her; no plan, never self-harmed","Helen: daytime hot flushes and word-finding problems at work","Helen: low mood similar to past episodes but flatter, less hopeless","Helen: takes sertraline 100 mg daily without missing doses","Helen: fleeting thoughts of death on bad nights, no plan; never hypomanic"],"worries":["Jess: So I'm not bipolar? Everyone thinks I'm making excuses.","Jess: What's the treatment?","Helen: Don't I need a blood test for menopause?","Helen: Is HRT safe for me?"],"rules":["Jess becomes tearful when describing shouting at her children; settles if validated","Jess reveals suicidal thoughts only if asked about the premenstrual week specifically","Helen gives menopausal symptoms only if asked directly","Both accept the plan once it is explained in plain words"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Ms Carter, I'm Dr Shah. Your GP wondered about bipolar disorder. What do you think is going on?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"I don't know. One week a month I'm a different person. I snap at everyone, cry at nothing, feel out of control. Then it just lifts."},{"t":"0:23","band":"Cycle history","w":"D","s":"When in your cycle does it start, and when does it go?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"About a week before my period. A day or two after I start bleeding, I'm me again."},{"t":"0:39","w":"D","s":"And in the week after your period, how's your mood?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Honestly, fine. Good, even."},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"Has it followed that pattern for a while?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","s":"Years. It's got worse since my youngest was born."},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had days of very high mood, needing hardly any sleep, or racing ideas, at any time of the month?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I sleep well apart from that week."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"How much does that week affect work, your relationship and your children?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"I've had two warnings at work. I shout at the kids and then feel awful. (tearful) My partner calls it my monster week."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard, and it's clearly not who you want to be. This isn't a flaw in your character.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Everyone thinks I'm making excuses."},{"t":"1:49","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"In that week, have you had any thoughts about ending your life?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think everyone would be better off without me. I'd never do anything. It's gone once my period starts."},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"Have you ever harmed yourself, or made a plan to?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"No, never."},{"t":"2:14","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"What you describe sounds like premenstrual dysphoric disorder, or PMDD, rather than bipolar disorder. It's a real condition, linked to how the brain reacts to normal hormone changes, and it's treatable.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"So I'm not bipolar?"},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"Bipolar episodes don't follow your calendar like this. To be sure, I'd like you to rate your mood every day for two cycles on this diary. I'll see you in about eight weeks to go through it.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"And treatment?"},{"t":"2:53","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"If the diary confirms it, the options are an SSRI antidepressant, daily or just in the second half of your cycle, or a particular contraceptive pill, plus CBT. I'll write to your GP with these, and ask them to check your thyroid and blood count.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Okay. That makes sense."},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what you'll do over the next two months?","m":[12]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Fill in the diary every day for two cycles, then come back and go through it."},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"Perfect. If those thoughts get stronger, or you start to plan anything, ring the crisis line, or 111 and choose option 2. The numbers are on this card.","m":[13]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel believed for once."},{"t":"3:53","band":"Second patient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Morris, I'm Dr Shah. How have things been since your last review?","m":[1]},{"t":"4:01","w":"P","s":"My mood's dipped again. I'm not sleeping, I wake up drenched in sweat, and my periods are all over the place."},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"Have you had hot flushes in the day, or problems with memory or concentration?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:19","w":"P","s":"Flushes, yes. And I lose words at work. I thought I was going mad."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"That must be frightening. You're not going mad. Is this low mood like your previous depressions, or different?","m":[6,9]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"Similar, but flatter. More tired. Less hopeless than last time."},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"Are you taking your antidepressant every day, and at what dose?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:49","w":"P","s":"Sertraline, 100 milligrams. I haven't missed any."},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had a spell of very high mood, or needing little sleep but feeling full of energy?","m":[3]},{"t":"5:04","w":"P","s":"No, never."},{"t":"5:06","w":"D","s":"Have you had any thoughts about ending your life?","m":[5]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"Fleeting, on bad nights. Nothing like before. No plans."},{"t":"5:17","band":"Explain and plan","w":"D","s":"I think two things are happening. Your depression may be coming back, and perimenopause is probably adding to it, with the night sweats breaking your sleep.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"Perimenopause? Don't I need a blood test for that?"},{"t":"5:36","w":"D","s":"Not over 45 with symptoms like yours; your GP can diagnose it from what you've told me. I'd like to review your sertraline with you, and ask your GP to discuss HRT, which can help sleep and sweats and may help mood.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:56","w":"P","s":"Is HRT safe for me?"},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"For most women it is, but your GP will go through your history first. In the letter I'll also ask them to check your thyroid and blood count, and I'll refer you for CBT.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:17","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"6:19","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you sum up the plan for me?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"Review the sertraline, talk to my GP about HRT, bloods, and some therapy."},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll see you in four weeks. If your mood drops further or those thoughts get stronger, ring the crisis line or 111, option 2, straight away.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you. That's a relief."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and asks for her own view of the problem","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes a cycle history: onset, offset and mood in the week after the period","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens both women for past hypomania not linked to the cycle","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about impact on work, relationships and children","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks each woman about suicidal thoughts, including in the premenstrual week","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates distress and the fear of being dismissed","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains suspected PMDD versus bipolar plainly and gives a two-cycle daily diary with review date","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks the 46-year-old about periods, flushes, night sweats, sleep and concentration","d":"Content"},{"c":"Compares with past depressions and checks antidepressant dose and adherence","d":"Content"},{"c":"Links the mood dip to perimenopause and depression; agrees antidepressant review and HRT discussion","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes the GP letter: PMDD options, HRT discussion, TFT, FBC and CBT","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks each woman's understanding in her own words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives both women crisis contacts and a review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Takes a clear cycle history, excludes hypomania, asks about suicide, uses a two-cycle diary rather than diagnosing from recall, and gives a NICE-consistent perimenopause and GP plan.","borderline":"Reaches the right impressions but misses the hypomania screen, the luteal-week suicide question, or a clear GP plan.","fail":"Labels cyclical mood as bipolar, ignores perimenopause in the 46-year-old, or suggests valproate."}},"c-2-8-8":{"setup":"Friday 15:45, CMHT clinic. Claire Walsh, 38, with depression, is alone for a few minutes while her husband parks. She has just told you he checks her phone, controls the money and calls her useless. Respond safely and agree a safety plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Claire Walsh, 38, under the CMHT for depression; mother of two children aged 7 and 10","open":"Please don't say anything to him. He'll be up in ten minutes.","manner":"Anxious, quiet and watchful of the door. Tearful when the word 'abuse' is used; grows steadier when believed and not pushed to leave. Frightened when social care is mentioned.","free":["Husband Martin, 42, checks her phone every night","He controls all the money; she gets twenty pounds a week","He calls her useless, including in front of the children","She has stopped seeing friends"],"ifasked":["No hitting, pushing, choking or strangling; no threats to kill her or himself","It has got worse since she became unwell","He has never threatened the children or pets","The 10-year-old covers her ears; the 7-year-old asked why Daddy shouts","He tracks her phone and she must say where she goes","Sometimes frightened of him; mostly 'feels small'","Passive thoughts of not waking up; no plan; the children keep her going","Safe contact: mobile, text first, mornings only while he is at work"],"worries":["Will he find out?","Do I have to leave him?","Will social workers turn up at the house?","What do I tell him if he asks what we talked about?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when the behaviour is named as abuse; settles if told it is not her fault","Shuts down if pressed to leave him","Gives her safe contact details only if asked how to reach her safely","If the doctor mentions the disclosure in front of the husband, she denies everything"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. It's just us in here and nobody can hear. Is it okay to keep talking while Martin's parking?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Yes, but he'll be up in ten minutes. Please don't say anything to him."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I won't. If he comes in, I'll switch to talking about your tablets. Can you tell me a bit more?","m":[1,13]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"He checks my phone every night. He has all the money; I get twenty pounds a week. He calls me useless in front of the kids. I've stopped seeing my friends because it's not worth the row."},{"t":"0:49","band":"Name it","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting and frightening to live with. What you're describing is abuse, even though he hasn't hit you. It's called coercive control, and it is not your fault.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"(tearful) Abuse? I thought abuse meant hitting. I thought I was just rubbish at keeping him happy."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"You're not. A lot of women tell me they felt exactly that. Thank you for trusting me with it.","m":[1,8]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"I've never said it out loud before."},{"t":"1:27","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"I'd like to ask some safety questions I ask everyone in this situation. Has he ever hurt you physically, or pushed you?","m":[3,5]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"No. He's never hit me."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"Has he ever put his hands round your neck, choked or strangled you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"Has he ever threatened to kill you, or himself?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"No. He says I'd never cope without him."},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"Is it getting worse, or happening more often?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"Worse since I got ill. He says nobody else would put up with me."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Has he ever threatened the children, or any pets?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"No. He's never touched the kids."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Are the children seeing or hearing this?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"Yes. My ten-year-old covers her ears. My little one asked why Daddy shouts at me."},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"Does he track where you go, or stop you seeing family?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"He tracks my phone. I can't go out without saying where."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Are you frightened of him?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"Sometimes. Mostly I just feel small."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Those questions are from a standard checklist called DASH. You've said yes to nine. That's medium risk today, not the highest level, but serious enough that you deserve specialist support.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"What kind of support?"},{"t":"3:17","band":"Safety plan","w":"D","s":"There's a worker called an IDVA, an independent domestic violence adviser, who helps with safety, money and housing options. Would you like me to refer you?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"Will he find out?"},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"Not from us. What's a safe way for us to contact you?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"My mobile. But text first, and only in the mornings, when he's at work."},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"Okay. And we won't send any letters home about this without checking with you first.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:57","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Yes, refer me."},{"t":"4:01","w":"D","s":"There's also a free 24-hour helpline, 0808 2000 247. Is it safe to save that, or shall I write it on an appointment card?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"Write it on a card. He checks my contacts."},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"Of course. If you're ever in danger, ring 999. If you can't speak, press 55 when they answer, and they'll know you need the police.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:31","w":"P","s":"I didn't know that."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Have you had any thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:44","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think it'd be easier not to wake up. I wouldn't do anything. The kids need me."},{"t":"4:53","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. I'll keep checking that with you. Do you feel you have to do anything today?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"5:04","w":"P","s":"Do I have to leave him? I can't, not now."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"No. You don't have to decide anything today. My job is to help you be as safe as possible, whatever you choose.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:21","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"5:23","band":"The children","w":"D","s":"Because the children are hearing this, I need to talk to our safeguarding lead about them. It's about support for the family, not taking them away, and I'll tell you what they advise.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"(frightened) Will social workers turn up at the house?"},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"That's a really understandable fear. I'm getting advice first, and I'll speak to you before anything happens, by text in the morning.","m":[11,8]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"He'll ask what we talked about."},{"t":"6:01","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Tell him we went through your sleep and your medication, and I'll talk about the same thing if he comes in. Can you tell me what we've agreed?","m":[13,14]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"You'll refer me to the IDVA, text in the mornings, no letters, the helpline's on a card, and 999 then 55 if I can't talk."},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"That's right. I'll see you on your own in two weeks. I'll record what you've told me, the score, and why I'm speaking to the safeguarding lead, in a protected part of your notes.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel like someone finally heard me."}],"marks":[{"c":"Thanks her, believes her, and confirms privacy while she is alone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names the behaviour as abuse and says it is not her fault","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about physical violence, strangulation and threats to kill","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about escalation and threats to the children, pets or himself","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Works through the DASH checklist and explains what the score means","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks what the children see and hear","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her fear and respects her choices without pressing her to leave","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Agrees a safe contact method and no letters home without her consent","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers IDVA referral and the 24-hour helpline in a safe format","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains honestly that the children will be discussed with the safeguarding lead","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives emergency advice, including the silent 999 call","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans with her what to say if her husband returns","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, books a review seen alone, and says what will be recorded","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Believes her and names the abuse, asks the high-risk questions with DASH, agrees safe contact and IDVA referral, and handles the children honestly, all while keeping the disclosure from the husband.","borderline":"Kind and safe, but skips strangulation or escalation questions, or leaves contact and the children's plan vague.","fail":"Raises the disclosure with the husband present, assumes no violence means no abuse, sends letters home, or forgets the children."}},"c-2-9-1":{"setup":"Monday 13:30, CMHT clinic. A 26-year-old patient, registered as male and known for depression, asks to be called Sophie, is buying hormones online and wants you to 'sign her off' for a gender clinic. Assess respectfully and explain the referral pathway.","role":{"who":"Sophie Reid, 26, trans woman (registered as male), under the CMHT for depression","open":"Sophie. She and her. My records still say my old name.","manner":"Guarded and braced for judgement at first; warms quickly when her name and pronouns are used. Tearful about waits and harassment; anxious she'll be told to stop hormones.","free":["Has known she is a girl since about age six; came out to friends last year","Wants her records changed to Sophie, she/her","Is buying oestradiol online","Wants to be 'signed off' for the gender clinic"],"ifasked":["Mood low; some days can't get out of bed; dreads the bus","Was shouted at on the bus last month; people stare","Some nights thinks she'd be better off not waking up; no plan, never self-harmed","Protective: friends, her flatmate who checks on her, work, her mother","Oestradiol tablets 4 mg a day from a website; no anti-androgen","No blood tests or checks since starting","Does not smoke","Sertraline has helped a bit"],"worries":["Are you going to tell me to stop the hormones?","Will you sign me off for the gender clinic?","How long will it take?","Please don't use my old name in letters."],"rules":["Becomes guarded if misgendered or her identity is questioned","Becomes tearful talking about the waits; settles if validated","Gives hormone details openly once reassured she won't be told off","Agrees to bloods once safety is explained without judgement"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Shah. Before we start, what name and pronouns would you like me to use?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Sophie. She and her. My records still say my old name."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Sophie. Would you like me to update your record today?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"Yes please. Every letter with that name feels like a slap."},{"t":"0:34","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"I'll do it today. Can you tell me about your gender, and how you've felt over time?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Since I was about six I've known I'm a girl. I hid it for years. I came out to my friends last year. I'm sure."},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"Thank you for sharing that. How's your mood been lately?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"Low. Some days I can't get out of bed. Work's okay, but I dread the bus."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"What happens on the bus?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"People stare. Someone shouted at me last month. And the clinic waits are years. I feel stuck."},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting and frightening. Waiting years for care you're sure you need, while dealing with that, would wear anyone down.","m":[8]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"(tearful) It does. Some nights I wonder what the point is."},{"t":"1:55","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me, Sophie. When you feel like that, do you have thoughts about ending your life?","m":[1,5]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think I'd be better off not waking up. I haven't planned anything."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"Have you ever harmed yourself, or made a plan?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"No. My friends keep me going, and I'd never do that to my mum."},{"t":"2:32","w":"D","s":"What else helps you hold on?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"Work, honestly. And my flatmate. She checks on me."},{"t":"2:44","band":"Hormone safety","w":"D","s":"You mentioned hormones. Which ones are you taking, where are they from, and what dose?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"Oestradiol tablets from a website. Four milligrams a day. I know it's not ideal."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being open. Are you taking anything to lower testosterone, and have you had any blood tests?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"Nothing else. No blood tests. I'm scared you'll tell me to stop."},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"I understand why you started, with waits this long, and I'm not going to tell you to stop suddenly. My concern is safety: oestrogen can raise the risk of blood clots, more so in smokers, so I'd like your GP to check your bloods and blood pressure.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"I don't smoke. What bloods?"},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"Hormone levels, liver tests, cholesterol and a blood count. Please don't raise the dose or stop suddenly without advice.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"Okay. So will you sign me off for the gender clinic?"},{"t":"4:14","band":"Explain the pathway","w":"D","s":"There's nothing for me to sign off. Your GP usually makes the referral to an NHS adult gender dysphoria clinic, and your depression doesn't stop that. We treat it alongside.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"How long will it take?"},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"I'll be honest: the waits are long, often several years. I'll ask your GP to refer you now so you're on the list, and we'll support your mental health while you wait.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"Years. (sighs) At least it's started."},{"t":"5:00","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"For your mood, I'd suggest carrying on with sertraline, and I'll refer you for CBT. How does that sound?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:13","w":"P","s":"Fine. Sertraline has helped a bit."},{"t":"5:17","w":"D","s":"In my letter to your GP I'll ask for the referral and the bloods. Is it okay to mention the hormones, and is there anything you'd rather I left out?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Mention the hormones. Just don't use my old name."},{"t":"5:42","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I won't; the letter will say Sophie, she/her. Can you tell me what we've agreed, so I know I've been clear?","m":[1,11,12]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Records updated, the GP refers me and checks bloods, sertraline and CBT, and I don't mess with the dose."},{"t":"6:07","w":"D","s":"That's it. If those thoughts get stronger or you start making plans, ring the crisis line or 111 and choose option 2, or go to A&E. Let's write that plan down now, with your flatmate's number on it.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Okay. That helps."},{"t":"6:33","w":"D","s":"I'll see you in four weeks and check how the referral is going. Anything else you'd like to ask?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you for calling me Sophie."}],"marks":[{"c":"Asks for her chosen name and pronouns and uses them throughout","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers to update the record and gets her consent","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks openly about her gender experience without questioning it","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about mood and the impact of waiting and harassment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and what keeps her safe","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks which hormones, dose, source and any monitoring","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains hormone safety without judgement and asks the GP for bloods","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her frustration about waits and her reasons for self-sourcing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the GP referral route to an NHS adult gender clinic, realistic waits, and that depression is no barrier","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Continues depression treatment and offers CBT","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees what goes in the GP letter","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding in her own words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a written crisis plan and a 4-week review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Uses her name and pronouns throughout, assesses mood and suicide risk, addresses hormone safety without judgement, and explains the GP referral route and waits honestly.","borderline":"Respectful and safe, but vague about the referral route, hormone monitoring or the crisis plan.","fail":"Misgenders her, treats her identity as a symptom, delays referral on mental health grounds, or skips the suicide assessment."}},"c-3-1-1":{"setup":"Memory clinic, Tuesday 10:30. Mr Frank Doyle, 74, a retired bus driver, has 18 months of forgetfulness. The usual nurse is off sick. Do his ACE-III, explain it fairly, then give the consultant a brief summary before the 11:30 review.","role":{"who":"Mr Frank Doyle, 74, man, retired bus driver (daughter Karen waits outside)","open":"Morning, doctor. Our Karen's dragged me here. She thinks I'm going daft.","manner":"Cheerful and a little defensive at first. Bristles at easy questions, then gets embarrassed when recall fails. Settles and becomes warm if reassured that there is no pass or fail.","free":["Karen thinks his memory has been slipping for about a year and a half","He feels fine in himself and sleeps well","He used to know every stop on the 42 route","He burnt a pan last month"],"ifasked":["Wears two hearing aids, with new batteries today, and reading glasses","English is his first language; born in Leeds","Left school at 15; no dyslexia","No sudden change, illness or fall before the memory problems","Drives the church minibus every Sunday","Has not got lost anywhere","Happy for results to be shared with Karen"],"worries":["Does the doctor think he is stupid?","Has he got dementia?","Will the DVLA be told?"],"rules":["If asked the date in a talking-down way, says 'Do you think I'm stupid?'; settles if told everyone gets the same questions","Cannot recall the address; becomes embarrassed and says he is failing; settles if the doctor normalises it","Tells the doctor about the minibus only if asked about driving","Daughter tries to come in at the start; leaves if given a kind reason and a later chance to talk"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Good morning. I'm Dr Patel, one of the doctors in the memory service. Are you Mr Frank Doyle? Which name should I use for you?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Frank's fine. Our Karen's dragged me here. She thinks I'm going daft."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Frank. Your GP asked us to look at your memory. I'd like to ask some questions that test memory and thinking. It takes about twenty minutes. Is that all right?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"(Daughter, at the door) Can I sit in? He gets flustered. I can help him answer."},{"t":"0:39","w":"D","s":"That's kind, Karen. The questions only work if your dad answers on his own. I'd really value your view straight after, because what you see at home matters just as much.","m":[4]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"(Daughter) Fair enough. I'll be in the waiting room."},{"t":"0:58","band":"Before testing","w":"D","s":"Before we start, a few practical things. Are your hearing aids in, and can you hear me clearly?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"Both in. New batteries this morning."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Good. Do you need glasses for reading?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"These ones, yes. They're on."},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"Is English your first language? And how old were you when you left school?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"English. Born in Leeds. Left school at fifteen and went on the buses later."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Any trouble with reading or spelling, like dyslexia? I'll note all this on the form.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"No, never."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been lately? Have you felt low, or been sleeping badly?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"No, I'm all right in myself. Sleep like a log."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"Did the memory change come on slowly, or suddenly, perhaps after an illness or a fall?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Slowly. Karen says it's been going on a year and a half."},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Some questions will feel very easy and some are meant to be hard. Please don't worry if you can't answer. There's no pass or fail.","m":[1,6]},{"t":"2:18","band":"Testing","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the day, the date, the month and the year?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"I know what day it is. Do you think I'm stupid?"},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Not at all. I ask everyone exactly the same questions, so it's fair. Take your time.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"Tuesday. November. Twenty twenty-six. The date I'm not sure. I don't get the paper now."},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'm going to give you a name and address. Please repeat it after me, and I'll ask you for it again later. We'll go through it three times so it has a chance to stick.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Got it. I think."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"Now tell me as many words as you can that start with the letter P in one minute. Not names of people or places. Go.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"Pen, pot, pig. Paper, post. Pint. Er... park. Pudding."},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Please copy these drawings as carefully as you can. Then draw a clock with all the numbers, with the hands showing ten past five.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Easy enough. There you are."},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"Lovely. Earlier I gave you a name and address. Can you tell me what it was?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"Oh. Harry... something. Something Road? I can't remember. This is embarrassing. I'm going to fail, aren't I?"},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"That one is hard for most people; it's meant to be. There's no pass or fail, honestly. It just shows us which bits of memory need more help.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:01","w":"P","s":"Sorry. I used to know every stop on the 42 route. Every one."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a real loss, and it's understandable to feel frustrated. Let me read out some choices and you pick the right ones.","m":[7,6]},{"t":"4:19","w":"P","s":"That one. And that one. I'm guessing now."},{"t":"4:23","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"That's fine. We're finished. How do you feel that went?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"Not great. The address threw me. But I'm fine at home. Karen's just fussing."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"It's good she cares. Other people sometimes notice changes first. Your attention, drawing and language were good. Remembering new information was harder.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"So have I got dementia then?"},{"t":"4:48","w":"D","s":"I can't tell that from one test. The consultant will put it together with your history, blood tests and scan, and talk it through with you at half eleven.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"To check I've explained it well, can you tell me in your words what happens next?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"The test isn't the answer on its own. The boss looks at everything and sees me at half eleven."},{"t":"5:18","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. A couple of safety questions. Are you still driving?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:24","w":"P","s":"Every Sunday. I drive the church minibus. Will you tell the DVLA?"},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Not from today's test. If a diagnosis that affects driving is made, you'd need to tell the DVLA yourself, and we'd help you. The consultant will talk it through today.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:43","w":"P","s":"Fair enough. Never had a scratch."},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"Good. Have you got lost anywhere, or had trouble with the cooker?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"Never lost. I burnt a pan last month, mind."},{"t":"5:57","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Is it all right if I share today's results with Karen and ask what she's noticed?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:07","w":"P","s":"Yes. She'll get it out of me anyway."},{"t":"6:11","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Frank. Please wait with Karen, and the consultant will see you both at half eleven. If you're worried before then, ask at the desk and I'll come.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"Thanks, doctor. You were very patient with me."},{"t":"6:28","band":"Handover","w":"D","s":"(To consultant) Mr Doyle, 74. ACE-III version A, 79 out of 100. Attention 17 of 18, memory 13 of 26, fluency 9 of 14, language 24 of 26, visuospatial 16 of 16.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:42","w":"P","s":"(Consultant) Any caveats?"},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Hearing aids and glasses in, quiet room, English first language, left school at 15. Mood screen negative, gradual onset, no signs of delirium. The profile looks amnestic, but it isn't a diagnosis on its own. He drives the church minibus, and his daughter is ready for collateral.","m":[13,2,11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the purpose kindly and gains consent before testing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks hearing aids, glasses, first language, schooling and dyslexia, and records them","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, sleep and sudden onset or recent illness (delirium) before testing","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Keeps the daughter out during testing kindly and offers her a say afterwards","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives the address for registration and tests delayed recall only after the intervening tasks","d":"Content"},{"c":"Uses standard wording and order without hints or coaching","d":"Content"},{"c":"Responds to irritation and embarrassment with empathy and says there is no pass or fail","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks how he felt it went, checking insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the domain findings plainly and that the test alone is not a diagnosis","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks his understanding of what happens next","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about driving, getting lost and cooking, and explains DVLA without judging from the score","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks his permission before sharing results with his daughter","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives the consultant version, total, domain scores, caveats and pattern in under a minute","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes clearly: thanks him, states the next step today and how to get help","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Fair, standard testing after sensory and mood checks, kind handling of embarrassment, no diagnosis from the score, and a crisp domain summary to the consultant.","borderline":"Testing completed but caveats or the mood check are thin, or the summary gives only a total.","fail":"Lets the daughter prompt, tests recall too early, ignores hearing or schooling, or tells him he has dementia from the score."}},"c-3-1-2":{"setup":"Older adults ward, Thursday 14:00. Mrs Margaret Ellis, 68, admitted two days ago with confusion, cannot dress, ignores the left of her plate and gets lost. Examine her parietal and temporal lobe function, interpret it, and act on it before the ward round.","role":{"who":"Mrs Margaret Ellis, 68, woman, inpatient (nurse in charge present at the start and end)","open":"If you must. There's nothing wrong with me. I'm perfectly fine.","manner":"Polite but brisk, sure she is well. Mildly offended by simple tasks. Frightened when she admits getting lost; calmer and trusting once the doctor explains and promises to come back.","free":["She feels perfectly well","She keeps getting lost on the way to the toilet","Her sister had a stroke","Her son Paul visits"],"ifasked":["Right-handed","Right-left, finger naming, sums and writing are correct","Marks the line well to the right and crosses out lines only on the right","Reports only the right hand when both are touched","Cannot put on her cardigan; the clock numbers are crowded on the right","Denies any problem with her left arm","Recalls only part of the address; misses movement in the lower left field","(Nurse) Fine on Sunday; problems since Monday; one near-fall last night; NEWS2 1"],"worries":["Why draw a clock? She is not a child","Is she losing her mind?","Is it a stroke like her sister's?"],"rules":["Denies any left-sided problem throughout; never accepts it even when shown","Becomes tearful about getting lost; settles if her fear is named and she is told she will be kept safe","Answers right-side tasks quickly and well","(Nurse) Asks for sedation for agitation; accepts a clear alternative plan"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"(To nurse in charge) Before I see her, when did all this start?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"(Nurse) Her son says she was fine on Sunday. Since Monday she can't dress and she ignores her left side. NEWS2 is 1."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"So about three days. Any falls, or new weakness?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"(Nurse) A near-fall last night going to the toilet. She keeps turning right."},{"t":"0:32","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mrs Ellis. I'm Dr Okafor, one of the ward doctors. I'd like to do some short tasks with your hands, eyes and a pen. They show how different parts of the brain are working. Is that all right?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"If you must. There's nothing wrong with me. I'm perfectly fine."},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you feel well in yourself. These help us look after you properly. Tell me if you get tired.","m":[1,9]},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"Which hand do you write with?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"My right. Always have."},{"t":"1:16","band":"Dominant side","w":"D","s":"Please show me your right hand. Now put your right hand on your left ear.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"There."},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"Close your eyes. Which finger am I touching? And now?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"Index finger. Now the little one."},{"t":"1:37","w":"D","s":"What is seven times six? And a hundred take away seven?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"Forty-two. Ninety-three."},{"t":"1:47","w":"D","s":"Please write a sentence about the weather.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"'It is raining again.' There."},{"t":"1:55","band":"Non-dominant side","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Can you mark the middle of this line for me?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"There. Middle."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Now cross out every line on this page.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"All done. Easy."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"Keep your eyes closed. I'll touch one hand or both. Tell me which.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"Right. Right. Right again."},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"Please copy these two shapes, then draw a clock showing ten past eleven.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"Why do you want me to draw a clock? I'm not a child."},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"Fair point, it does seem simple. Drawing shows how parts of the brain work together. Please do your best.","m":[6,9]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"There. The numbers got a bit squashed."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Could you put your cardigan on for me?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"This sleeve's all wrong. Where's the other one? I can't do it."},{"t":"3:06","w":"D","s":"Is there anything wrong with your left arm?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"No. It's fine. Why does everyone keep asking that?"},{"t":"3:17","band":"Temporal","w":"D","s":"Some memory and eye checks now. Remember this name and address for me. Then take this paper in your right hand, fold it in half and put it on the table.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"Like that?"},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Perfect. What's this called? And this part?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"A pen. A watch. That's the buckle."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"Look at my nose and say 'now' when you see my finger move.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"Now. Now. I didn't see that one."},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"Who is in the photo by your bed? And what was the name and address?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"That's my son, Paul. The address... Jim something. Bedford? I keep getting lost on the way to the loo. I'm frightened I'm losing my mind."},{"t":"4:17","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"That must be really frightening. You're not losing your mind. Something seems to be affecting how the right side of your brain handles space, especially on your left.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"Is it a stroke? My sister had one."},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"It might be, so I'm asking the medical doctors to see you today and arranging a brain scan. Can you tell me what I've said, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[10,12]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"Something in my brain, and a scan today."},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"Exactly. The nurses will help you to the toilet so you're safe.","m":[10,13]},{"t":"5:05","band":"Escalate","w":"D","s":"(On the phone to the medical registrar) 68-year-old right-handed woman on our ward. Over three days: left neglect, dressing and constructional apraxia, and she denies any left-sided problem. Sums, writing and finger naming are intact. I'm worried about a right parietal stroke or a mass. Could you review today and support an urgent CT head?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:31","w":"P","s":"(Registrar) Onset over three days, so not a thrombolysis call. I'll see her by four. Request the CT and send bloods and an ECG."},{"t":"5:44","w":"D","s":"Will do. I'll document the timings and findings now.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:49","w":"P","s":"(Nurse) She was agitated last night. Can we give her something to settle her?"},{"t":"5:57","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Let's hold sedation until we know the cause. Approach her from the right, and keep her bed, call bell and food on her right. Turn the plate halfway through meals.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:12","w":"P","s":"(Nurse) Falls plan as well?"},{"t":"6:16","w":"D","s":"Yes. Falls precautions, help to the toilet and four-hourly NEWS2. Call me straight away if she gets drowsier, weaker or her obs change.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:28","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Mrs Ellis. A medical doctor will see you this afternoon, then the scan. I'll ring Paul. Press the buzzer on your right if you need anything, and I'll come back with the result.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you, doctor. Do come back and tell me."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the tasks simply and gains verbal consent","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks handedness before any testing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes onset, time course and falls from the nurse before testing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests Gerstmann features: right-left, finger naming, calculation and writing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests neglect with line bisection, cancellation and tactile extinction","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests constructional and dressing apraxia with shapes, clock and cardigan","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about the left arm to check for anosognosia","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests temporal lobe: recall, naming, three-stage command, visual fields and face recognition","d":"Content"},{"c":"Responds to her fear and irritation with empathy and simple reassurance","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains findings to her in plain words and checks understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names findings and localises to right parietal lobe; treats new onset as an emergency","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Escalates to the medical registrar for same-day review and urgent CT head, and documents","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a nursing plan: right-sided approach, food and bell, falls care, NEWS2, no sedation","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes clearly: next steps, family update, how to call for help","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Handedness first, systematic dominant, non-dominant and temporal testing, correct right parietal localisation, and same-day medical escalation with a neglect-aware nursing plan.","borderline":"Finds the neglect but tests unsystematically, or escalates without a clear nursing plan.","fail":"No handedness, tests memory only, misses that new signs over days mean stroke, or sedates the confusion."}},"c-3-1-3":{"setup":"Memory service, Wednesday 11:00. Mr Ron Ashworth, 79, widowed, says his memory is fine. His son Mark, 50, is in the family room and has agreed to talk. Take a collateral history to help decide whether this is dementia, and identify risks.","role":{"who":"Mr Mark Ashworth, 50, man, son of the patient","open":"Hello. Dad says there's nothing wrong, but I'm worried sick, to be honest.","manner":"Keen to help but tired and guilty. Opens up with specific questions. Gets upset about the scam and his own exhaustion; steadies and becomes practical when listened to.","free":["Dad repeats the same question several times in one visit","Changes started about three years ago, slowly","Mark now pays his bills","He visits every day and has his own family"],"ifasked":["Gradual, no sudden drops; first sign was missing a grandchild's birthday","Lost twice in town; once police brought him home","Loses keys; has missed tablets; takes a bladder tablet from the GP","Struggles for words sometimes; personality much the same, a bit quieter","No hallucinations, no falls, no bad days and good days, no acting out dreams","Not low in mood; drinks a couple of pints at weekends","Drives every day; a new scrape on the car door","A caller posing as his bank took £3,000; burnt a pan once; no LPA"],"worries":["Will Dad know he spoke to the doctor behind his back?","Should he have done something sooner?","Is it Alzheimer's?","He is exhausted"],"rules":["Gives the scam only if asked about money or scams; tearful about it","Defends Dad's driving at first; admits the scrape if asked about incidents","Settles if guilt and tiredness are validated","Becomes guarded if told a diagnosis today"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Shah, one of the doctors in the memory team. You must be Mark. Thanks for waiting.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Hello. Dad says there's nothing wrong, but I'm worried sick, to be honest."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Your dad has agreed to us talking. People with memory problems often don't notice them, so your view really matters. It'll take about fifteen minutes. Is that all right?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"Will he know I've spoken to you behind his back?"},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"He agreed to it, and I'll be open with him that we spoke. Anything you'd rather keep private, tell me and we'll talk about it first.","m":[1,10]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","s":"Okay. That helps."},{"t":"0:58","band":"Their story","w":"D","s":"When did you first notice a change, and what was the very first thing?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"About three years ago. He missed his grandson's birthday. Never done that."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"Has it come on gradually, or suddenly, or in steps with sudden drops?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"Gradually. Just slowly worse."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"What does his memory look like day to day?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"He asks the same question three or four times a visit. Loses his keys every day."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Has he got lost, even somewhere familiar? Any trouble finding words?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"Twice in town. Once the police brought him home. And he loses words now and then."},{"t":"1:56","w":"D","s":"How does he manage money, medicines and the phone?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"I pay his bills now. He's missed tablets. He takes a bladder one from the GP too."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Does he still cook and look after himself?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"Simple stuff. Beans on toast. He keeps himself clean."},{"t":"2:23","w":"D","s":"Has his personality changed? Is he more irritable, rude or doing things out of character?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"Not really. Quieter, maybe. Still Dad."},{"t":"2:36","band":"Pattern","w":"D","s":"Does he see things that aren't there, have falls, or have very good and very bad days? Does he shout or kick in his sleep?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"No, none of that."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"Has he seemed low, or stopped enjoying things? How much does he drink?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"Not low. A couple of pints at the weekend, that's all."},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"Has anything changed quickly in the last few weeks, like after an infection?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"No. Same slow slide. I should have done something sooner. I thought it was old age."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Changes like this creep up, and lots of families think the same. You've brought him now, and that's what matters.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:36","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Does he still drive? Anything that's worried you, like scrapes or getting lost?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"He drives every day. Taking his keys would kill him. There's a new scrape on the door, mind."},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"I understand how much it means to him. If he's diagnosed with dementia, he must tell the DVLA, and we'll help with that conversation.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:07","w":"D","s":"Has anyone tried to take advantage of him, like scam calls or people asking for money?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"Someone rang pretending to be his bank. He gave them his card details. Lost three thousand pounds."},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"That's really upsetting, and I'm sorry. Has the bank been told? I'd like to raise a safeguarding concern today so he's better protected.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"Bank knows. Yes, please do that."},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"Any fires or near misses at home, like pans or the gas?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"He burnt a pan once. Smoke everywhere."},{"t":"4:55","band":"You","w":"D","s":"How are you coping with all this?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"I'm round there every day. I've got my own family. I'm exhausted."},{"t":"5:07","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to carry, and it's understandable to feel worn out. You're able to ask for a carer's assessment from the council. Can I refer you? Is there a Lasting Power of Attorney?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:24","w":"P","s":"Yes, please. And no, no LPA."},{"t":"5:29","band":"Summary","w":"D","s":"It's worth thinking about while he can still make one. Let me check I have this right: three years of slow decline, repeating questions, getting lost, missed tablets, no hallucinations or falls. Risks are driving, the scam and the pan. Anything I've missed?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:51","w":"P","s":"No, that's it. Is it Alzheimer's?"},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"It's too early to say. We'll put your account with his memory tests, bloods and scan, and the consultant will explain the result to you both. I'll also ask the GP to review his bladder tablet, as some can affect memory.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:17","w":"P","s":"Right. That's fair."},{"t":"6:20","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I'll raise the safeguarding concern today and refer you for the carer's assessment. If he gets lost again or something worries you, ring the memory team. Out of hours, call 111, or 999 in an emergency.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"Thanks, doctor. It helps to say it out loud."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Mark. What you've told me will help us a great deal. I'll go and see your dad now.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms father's consent and explains why the son's view matters","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes the first change, onset and course: gradual, sudden or step-wise","d":"Content"},{"c":"Covers memory, orientation and language with concrete examples","d":"Content"},{"c":"Covers daily function: money, medicines, cooking and self-care","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about personality and behaviour change (frontal features)","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for Lewy body features: hallucinations, falls, fluctuation, acting out dreams","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for depression, alcohol and recent sudden change suggesting delirium","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about driving incidents and explains the DVLA duty if diagnosed","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about scams, money and fire, and acts on the scam with safeguarding","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Responds to worry, guilt and exhaustion with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks how he is coping, offers a carer's assessment and asks about LPA","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises the history back and checks it is accurate","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains next steps without a premature diagnosis and flags the medicine review","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes clearly with actions, a safety net and thanks","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Structured onset-to-function history with pattern questions, every key risk covered, the scam acted on, and the son's own strain addressed.","borderline":"Good memory history but thin on function, pattern features or carer needs.","fail":"Memory-only history, misses money, scams or driving, ignores the son's exhaustion, or names Alzheimer's today."}},"c-3-2-1":{"setup":"Memory clinic, Friday 15:00. Mrs Patricia Wells, 72, and her daughter Claire, 45, are back for results. The consultant has diagnosed mixed Alzheimer's and vascular dementia (ACE-III 78, mainly memory; MRI hippocampal atrophy, mild small vessel change). Explain the diagnosis and next steps.","role":{"who":"Mrs Patricia Wells, 72, woman, patient (daughter Claire, 45, present)","open":"Hello, doctor. I'm hoping you're going to tell me it's just old age.","manner":"Chatty and hopeful, then shocked and briefly denying. Tearful and talks of being a burden; steadies when listened to and wants practical answers. Claire is calm and asks clear questions.","free":["She came because Claire noticed she was forgetting things","She forgets names and appointments","She still drives to the shops and church","She lives alone"],"ifasked":["She was worried it might be Alzheimer's, like her mother","She likes the main points, not lots of detail","Happy for Claire to hear everything","Claire now helps with bills; Patricia has missed bills and repeats shopping","No hallucinations, no stiffness, no falls","Has had fleeting thoughts that she would rather not be a burden, but no wish to die and no plans","Has high blood pressure; takes amlodipine"],"worries":["Is it just old age?","Will she stop knowing her own daughter?","Will she have to give up her car?","What is the difference from her friend's MCI? (Claire)"],"rules":["Denies at first when told 'dementia'; accepts gently if told some forgetting is normal but her results show more","Becomes tearful about being a burden; answers honestly if asked directly about suicidal thoughts","Reacts badly to jargon and asks what it means","Claire asks about MCI if the doctor does not explain it"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mrs Wells. I'm Dr Ahmed, one of the doctors who works with Dr Clarke. Which name should I use for you?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"Pat, please. This is my daughter, Claire. I'm hoping you'll tell me it's just old age."},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming, both of you. Pat, is it all right for me to talk about your results with Claire here?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Yes, I want her here. She remembers things better than me."},{"t":"0:42","band":"What they know","w":"D","s":"What's your understanding of why you came to the memory clinic?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:49","w":"P","s":"Claire thought I was forgetting things. Names, appointments. Paying bills."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Before I explain, what were you worried it might be?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"Alzheimer's. My mother had it."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Some people like lots of detail, others just the main points. What would suit you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"The main points. Claire can ask the rest."},{"t":"1:22","band":"Diagnosis","w":"D","s":"I'm afraid the results do show something more than normal ageing. Is it all right to go on?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"The tests show you have a condition called dementia. That means memory and thinking have changed enough to affect daily life, like the bills and shopping.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"Dementia? No. I just forget names. Everyone my age does."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"It's a lot to take in, and some forgetting is normal with age. Your tests show changes beyond that.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"There are two causes working together. Alzheimer's disease, which affects memory first; your scan shows shrinkage in the memory area. And some changes in the small blood vessels of the brain. We call this mixed dementia.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"(Daughter) What's the difference from MCI? Mum's friend has that."},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"In mild cognitive impairment, memory is affected but daily life mostly isn't. Your mum's memory now affects shopping and bills, so it's dementia.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"(Daughter) And the doctor mentioned Lewy body. Is that ruled out?"},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"We thought about it. Lewy body dementia usually brings seeing things that aren't there, stiffness or falls. Pat has none of those, so it doesn't fit.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"Will I end up not knowing my own daughter?"},{"t":"3:14","band":"Feelings","w":"D","s":"That's a natural fear. Dementia usually changes slowly, over years. Many people stay at home and know their family for a long time.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"I don't want to be a burden on Claire. What's the point?"},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, this is so hard to hear. When you say 'what's the point', have you had thoughts of wishing you weren't here, or of ending your life?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"No. Not like that. I'd never do anything. I just don't want to be a nuisance."},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being open. If those thoughts ever come, please tell Claire, your GP or us straight away.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:10","band":"Treatment and support","w":"D","s":"There's no cure at present. But a tablet called donepezil may help memory for a while. We'd check your pulse first, then start low at night.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Will it stop it getting worse?"},{"t":"4:29","w":"D","s":"It doesn't stop the disease, but it may slow the symptoms for a time. For the blood vessel part, your GP will look after your blood pressure, cholesterol and sugar.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"I'm not giving up my car, mind."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Many people with early dementia keep driving for a while. You'll need to tell the DVLA and your insurer. They decide, sometimes after a driving assessment.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"(Daughter) What other help is there?"},{"t":"5:09","w":"D","s":"A memory nurse and dementia adviser, Alzheimer's Society groups, and Cognitive Stimulation Therapy. It's also worth setting up a Lasting Power of Attorney while you can make those choices. Claire, you can have a carer's assessment.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:29","w":"P","s":"That's a lot. My head's spinning."},{"t":"5:33","band":"Check","w":"D","s":"That's understandable. Can you tell me in your words what I've said, so I know I've been clear?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:44","w":"P","s":"It's dementia, Alzheimer's and blood vessels. A tablet might help. I tell the DVLA. And sort out the power of attorney."},{"t":"5:56","w":"D","s":"That's spot on. Claire, anything you'd add?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:01","w":"P","s":"(Daughter) No. Thank you for being straight with us."},{"t":"6:07","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I'll give you leaflets today and send a plain letter to you and your GP. The memory nurse will see you in four weeks.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"And if I've more questions before then?"},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Ring the memory nurse number on the leaflet. If you feel very low or unsafe, call your GP, 111, or 999.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:37","w":"P","s":"Thank you, doctor. It's not what I wanted, but at least I know."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You've done really well today. Take care, both of you.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and asks consent to discuss results with the daughter present","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks what she understands and what she was worried about","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks how much detail she wants","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives a warning shot, then says 'dementia' clearly and defines it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains mixed Alzheimer's and vascular causes in plain words, linked to her results","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why this is not mild cognitive impairment or Lewy body dementia","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Pauses and responds to shock, fear and denial with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts after the diagnosis and safety-nets","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains donepezil honestly (no cure, pulse check) and vascular risk control","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains telling the DVLA and insurer without removing her licence","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Covers LPA, post-diagnostic support, CST and the daughter's carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back and invites the daughter's questions","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with written information, a letter, follow-up date and who to call","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Names dementia clearly and kindly, explains mixed causes and the MCI and Lewy body differences, asks about suicidal thoughts, and leaves a concrete plan including DVLA and LPA.","borderline":"Clear diagnosis but rushed past emotion, or misses one of DVLA, LPA or follow-up.","fail":"Avoids the word dementia, uses jargon, promises a cure, talks only to the daughter, or misses risk after the diagnosis."}},"c-3-2-2":{"setup":"CMHT older adults office, Monday 16:30. Mrs Linda Marsh, 61, arrives in tears without an appointment. Her husband Graham, 63, a former accountant, has changed over two years. Take a collateral history for possible frontotemporal dementia, assess risk and support her.","role":{"who":"Mrs Linda Marsh, 61, woman, wife of the patient","open":"I'm sorry to turn up like this. I don't know where else to go. He's a different man.","manner":"Tearful, apologetic and exhausted at first. Becomes clearer and more focused once she feels heard. Ashamed when admitting she shouts at him; relieved by practical steps.","free":["Over two years he has become rude to neighbours","He laughed when their grandson was in hospital","He eats sweets constantly","The GP thought it was depression"],"ifasked":["Gambles online; has lost about £20,000","Made a crude remark to a young woman at the supermarket till","Sits all day unless prompted; no sadness; insists on lunch at exactly 12","Can no longer do their tax return, though he was an accountant; memory for events is fairly good","Speech normal; no weakness, twitching, choking or slurred speech","His father 'went odd' in his fifties","Still drives, faster and careless; no one else exploiting him","He has never hurt her; he shouts; she feels safe at home"],"worries":["Could it still be depression?","Is it her fault for shouting at him?","Will her children get it?","How can she stop the money going?"],"rules":["Cries early; settles if given time and her distress is named","Gives the gambling figure only if asked about money","Admits shouting at him with guilt; settles if told it is understandable","Mentions his father only if asked about family history"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Come and sit down. I'm Dr Mensah, one of the doctors here. There are tissues there, and I'll get you some water. Take your time.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"I'm sorry to turn up like this. I don't know where else to go. He's a different man."},{"t":"0:24","w":"D","s":"Don't apologise. You're clearly very worried, and I'm glad you came. I have time now.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Can you even talk to me without him here?"},{"t":"0:39","w":"D","s":"Yes. I can listen to anything you want to tell me. I just can't share his details without his agreement. Who's your husband?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"Graham Marsh. He's sixty-three. He used to be an accountant."},{"t":"0:58","band":"Their story","w":"D","s":"You said he's a different man. Can you give me some examples?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"He's rude to the neighbours. And when our grandson was in hospital, he laughed. I don't recognise him."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"That must have been awful to see. Losing his warmth like that is one of the hardest changes for a family.","m":[1,4]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"(Crying) It was. I didn't know what to say to my daughter."},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"When did you first notice a change? Did it come before any memory problems?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:42","w":"P","s":"About two years ago. His memory's not bad, actually. It's his manner."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"Does he say or do things in public that embarrass you, with strangers for instance?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"He said something crude to a young girl at the till. Out loud."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Has his eating changed?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"Sweets. Constantly. He eats a whole bag in one go."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Has he become less motivated, or rigid about routines?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"He sits all day unless I push him. And lunch has to be at twelve exactly."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Does he seem sad? And how is he with planning, like bills or the tax return?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:39","w":"P","s":"Not sad. Just blank. He can't do the tax return now, and he was an accountant."},{"t":"2:48","band":"Pattern","w":"D","s":"Has his speech changed, like finding words or understanding them? Any weakness, muscle twitching, choking or slurred speech?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"No, he talks normally. No choking."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"Has anyone in his family had dementia, motor neurone disease or mental illness?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"His father went strange in his fifties. Will my children get this?"},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"Some types do run in families. If that's so here, a genetics clinic can talk your children through it later. First we need to find out what's happening to Graham.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"3:33","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"How are your finances now?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","s":"He's lost twenty thousand pounds gambling online. I can't stop him."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"That's frightening, and it isn't your fault. Has anyone else been asking him for money, online or in person?","m":[9,1]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"Not that I know of. It's just the betting sites."},{"t":"4:01","w":"D","s":"Does he still drive? Any worries there?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"He drives much faster now. He doesn't seem bothered."},{"t":"4:11","w":"D","s":"Until he's assessed, it's safer that he doesn't drive. Apart from the remark at the till, any other behaviour like that, towards anyone?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"No, just comments. Strangers, mostly."},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this. Has he ever frightened you or hurt you? Do you feel safe at home?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"He shouts, but he's never laid a finger on me. I'm the one shouting, if I'm honest. I thought he was doing it on purpose."},{"t":"4:51","w":"D","s":"Anyone would react like that to such changes. If this is an illness, he isn't choosing it. You deserve support too.","m":[11,1]},{"t":"5:03","w":"P","s":"The GP said it's depression. Could it still be that?"},{"t":"5:09","band":"Explain and plan","w":"D","s":"It's a fair thought. But loss of warmth, crude remarks, craving sweets and poor planning with good memory suggest the front of the brain may be affected. That's called frontotemporal dementia. We need a specialist assessment to be sure.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:29","w":"P","s":"Dementia. Oh God. What happens now?"},{"t":"5:33","w":"D","s":"I'll ask for an urgent young-onset dementia assessment, with a brain scan and detailed thinking tests, and tell my consultant today.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"For money, ask the bank today about gambling blocks and your joint account. A Lasting Power of Attorney is possible if he still has capacity; if not, the Court of Protection can appoint someone.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"I'll refer you for a carer's assessment, and Rare Dementia Support can help. Can you tell me the main steps, so I know I've been clear?","m":[13,14]},{"t":"6:17","w":"P","s":"Bank today, no driving, you refer him urgently, and I get some help for me."},{"t":"6:25","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll write to the GP and see you again within two weeks. If he becomes aggressive or you feel unsafe, call 999. Otherwise, ring this team.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel like someone's finally listening."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming in. You've done the right thing.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Offers privacy, time and tissues, and validates her distress before probing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains she can be heard without breaching his confidentiality","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks for concrete examples and the timeline relative to memory change","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits disinhibition and loss of empathy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits apathy, rigid routines and sweet craving","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits executive change with relatively preserved memory","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about language change and motor neurone disease symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about family history of dementia, MND or mental illness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses financial loss and exploitation","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses driving and sexual disinhibition, and advises no driving until assessed","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about her safety at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains possible frontotemporal dementia simply, and why not just depression","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees urgent young-onset referral, money protection, LPA or deputyship and carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and closes with follow-up and a safety net","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Unhurried, validating start, the core bvFTD features with MND and family history, a full risk sweep including her safety, and concrete money and referral steps.","borderline":"Good features but rushed emotion, or misses her safety or money protection.","fail":"Accepts depression, takes a memory-only history, ignores her safety or the gambling, or dismisses her without a plan."}},"c-3-2-3":{"setup":"Memory clinic, Wednesday 13:30. Mr Arthur Bell, 77, was diagnosed with mild to moderate Alzheimer's last month. He fainted once this year and takes bisoprolol for atrial fibrillation. He and his wife Jean want to know about medication. Discuss options and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Arthur Bell, 77, man, patient (wife Jean, 74, present)","open":"Afternoon, doctor. Jean's read up on these memory tablets. I'm not so sure.","manner":"Friendly but wary of more pills. Warms up when listened to. Jean is anxious and eager for the strongest treatment; calms when the reasons for caution are explained.","free":["He takes six tablets already","Jean read that tablets can bring memory back","He forgets what he went upstairs for"],"ifasked":["Hopes to keep doing the crossword and his allotment","Fainted in the kitchen in March; checked by the GP, not the heart clinic","Takes bisoprolol, apixaban, amlodipine, atorvastatin, omeprazole and a bladder tablet (oxybutynin)","No ulcers, asthma, breathing problems or seizures","Jean fills a weekly pill box","Sleeps well; no bad dreams now","Pulse today 58"],"worries":["Will these bring my memory back?","Could they make him faint again? (Jean)","Can he have the strongest dose straight away? (Jean)","What happens when it stops working?"],"rules":["Says he doesn't want more pills; agrees if his choice is respected and his list may be cut","Jean pushes for the top dose; accepts starting low if told why","Mentions the bladder tablet only if asked about the full medicine list"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Good afternoon. I'm Dr Rossi, one of the memory clinic doctors. Mr Bell, and is this Mrs Bell?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Arthur and Jean. Jean's read up on these memory tablets. I'm not so sure."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming. Today I'd like to talk about medicines for Alzheimer's, so you can decide. Is that what you'd hoped?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"(Wife) Yes. We want to do something."},{"t":"0:41","band":"Hopes","w":"D","s":"What have you heard about these medicines?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:46","w":"P","s":"(Wife) That they can bring his memory back."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"And Arthur, what would you hope a medicine might do for you?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"Keep me doing the crossword. And the allotment."},{"t":"1:07","band":"Benefits","w":"D","s":"Those are good goals. I wish the tablets could bring memory back, but they can't cure Alzheimer's. In some people they help memory and daily life for a while, and slow the decline.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"So it's not a cure then."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"No. It's fair to try one if it's safe, and to stop if it doesn't help.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:43","band":"Side effects","w":"D","s":"The main group are called cholinesterase inhibitors. Donepezil is the usual first choice. The common side effects are feeling sick, loose stools, poor appetite, vivid dreams and muscle cramps.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"(Wife) He fainted in the kitchen in March. Could these make that happen again?"},{"t":"2:11","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"That's an important question. Tell me about the faint. Was he checked?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"(Wife) The GP saw him. Blood tests. Nobody looked at his heart."},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"These tablets can slow the heart, especially with bisoprolol. Your pulse today is 58, which is on the slow side. So before starting, I'd like your GP to do a heart tracing, and get heart specialist advice if it shows a problem.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"I see. Better safe."},{"t":"2:57","w":"D","s":"Any stomach ulcers, asthma or breathing problems, or seizures?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"No, none of those."},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"Can you talk me through all your tablets? Jean, have you got the list?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"(Wife) Bisoprolol, apixaban, amlodipine, atorvastatin, omeprazole. And a bladder one. Oxybutynin."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Oxybutynin can worsen memory and works against these tablets. I'll ask the GP to review it.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","s":"I don't want more pills. I already take six."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"That's understandable, and it's your choice. We may be able to cut one, and you can stop the new one if it doesn't suit you.","m":[9,7]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Well, if one goes, I'll try it."},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"(Wife) Can we just get the strongest dose straight away?"},{"t":"4:13","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting the most benefit. Starting low lets his body adjust and reduces sickness and heart effects. Then we increase.","m":[9,11]},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"So: heart tracing first. If it's safe, donepezil five milligrams at night for four weeks, then ten if he's tolerating it. If the dreams are bad, take it in the morning.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"What happens when it stops working?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"We don't stop it simply because the illness moves on. Later, another medicine called memantine can be added, or used if donepezil doesn't suit.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:06","band":"Check","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what we've agreed, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"Heart test first. Then a small tablet at night, going up after a month. It might help for a while. The bladder pill gets looked at."},{"t":"5:34","w":"D","s":"Spot on. Jean, could you keep a short diary of side effects and how he manages day to day?","m":[10,12]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"(Wife) I can do that."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"The memory nurse will see you four to six weeks after starting, to check pulse, weight and side effects. Once he's settled, the GP takes over prescribing under shared care. I'll give you a leaflet too.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:13","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"If you faint, feel dizzy, your pulse feels very slow, or your stools turn black, stop the tablet and get help that day. In an emergency, call 999.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Righto. That's clearer than the internet."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you both. I'll write to the GP today and you'll get a copy.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"(Wife) Thank you, doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks who is present and agrees the purpose","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what they know and hope the medicine will do","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains honestly that it is not a cure but may help for a time","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains common side effects in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explores the faint and links bradycardia risk to bisoprolol; asks for ECG first","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about peptic ulcer, asthma or COPD, and seizures","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Reviews the full medicine list and acts on the anticholinergic","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains when memantine is used and that progression is not a reason to stop","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds to reluctance and pressure with empathy, respecting his choice","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees start dose, titration and timing after the heart check","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges 4–6 week review, shared care, written information and a diary","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets: fainting, dizziness, slow pulse, black stools","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes clearly with thanks and the GP letter","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Honest benefits, plain side effects, the faint and bisoprolol caught with ECG first, oxybutynin flagged, memantine placed, and a dated titration and review plan.","borderline":"Good explanation but misses the cardiac link or the anticholinergic.","fail":"Promises a cure, starts the drug without a heart check, or gives no review or safety net."}},"c-3-2-4":{"setup":"Saturday 22:00, on call for older adults. A care home rings about Mrs Edna Price, 81, with dementia with Lewy bodies, seeing children in the garden and shouting at night. The out-of-hours GP suggested haloperidol. Advise the senior carer safely.","role":{"who":"Sam Turner, 38, woman, senior carer on night shift (resident Mrs Edna Price, 81)","open":"Hi, it's Sam at Oakfield care home. Edna's seeing children in the garden and screaming. The GP said haloperidol. Can you okay it?","manner":"Rushed and stressed; wants a quick yes. Frustrated when told no; settles and becomes cooperative once given a clear plan and thanked for her effort.","free":["Edna is seeing children in the garden and is frightened they will fall in the pond","The other residents are awake","They are two staff short"],"ifasked":["Has seen children on and off for months, but tonight she is frightened","Sleepier and more muddled today; drinking less","Bowels last opened four days ago; urine smells strong","Temperature 37.8, pulse 92, BP 128/74; not breathless","Seems stiffer and slower this week; nearly fell yesterday","Kicks and shouts in her sleep for years","No new medicines; calms with radio music and her daughter's voice"],"worries":["Why won't you just approve haloperidol?","Should we tell her the children aren't real?","What if she gets worse tonight?"],"rules":["Pushes once more for haloperidol; accepts if the risk is explained clearly","Gives the bowel, urine and temperature details only if asked","Becomes tearful about staffing; calms if acknowledged","Reads back the plan if asked"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, this is Dr Lewis, the older adults doctor on call. Who am I speaking to, and who is the call about?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"It's Sam, senior carer at Oakfield. Edna Price, eighty-one. Lewy body dementia. She's seeing children in the garden and screaming. The GP said haloperidol. Can you okay it?"},{"t":"0:33","w":"D","s":"Thanks for calling, Sam. First, is Edna safe right now? Is anyone with her?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"A carer's with her in her room. She's not hurting herself."},{"t":"0:51","band":"No haloperidol","w":"D","s":"Good. Please don't give haloperidol. People with Lewy body dementia can react very badly to it, with severe stiffness, falls and even death.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"The GP said it'd settle her. Why won't you just approve it?"},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"I know you need help tonight. The risk with this condition is serious, so let's find the cause and calm her another way.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"1:30","band":"Her symptoms","w":"D","s":"What exactly is she seeing, and is she frightened?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"Children by the pond. She's scared they'll fall in."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"Is this new, or has she seen them before?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"On and off for months. But she's never been this upset."},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"Does she act out her dreams, shouting or kicking in her sleep?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"For years. Her daughter told us."},{"t":"2:13","band":"Delirium check","w":"D","s":"Is she more sleepy or muddled than usual today? Eating and drinking?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"Sleepier, yes. Drinking less."},{"t":"2:26","w":"D","s":"When did she last open her bowels and pass urine? Any pain?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"Bowels four days ago. Urine smells strong. She keeps rubbing her tummy."},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"Could you do her observations now?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"Temp 37.8, pulse 92, blood pressure 128 over 74. She's breathing fine."},{"t":"2:57","w":"D","s":"Any new medicines lately?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"No changes."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Has she been stiffer or slower, or had any falls?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"Stiffer this week. She nearly fell yesterday."},{"t":"3:19","w":"P","s":"She's screaming, the others are awake, and we're two staff short. I'm on my own with the meds."},{"t":"3:31","band":"Calm her","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard, Sam, and you're doing the right thing by calling.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Could one person sit with her in a quiet room with the light on? Close the curtains so the garden is dark, and play the radio music she likes.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"Should we tell her the children aren't real?"},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Arguing usually upsets her more. Tell her she's safe and the garden is quiet, then gently distract her. Her daughter's voice on the phone may help.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:22","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"With a temperature, smelly urine and four days without a poo, I'm worried about an infection or constipation. Please ask the out-of-hours GP to see her tonight to check her urine, bloods and tummy.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:44","w":"P","s":"Okay. I'll ring them now."},{"t":"4:48","w":"D","s":"If she becomes very drowsy, breathless, can't be woken, or falls and hurts herself, call 999. If she's unsafe and nothing works, call me back.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"I'll ask the older adults team to see her on Monday. They'll check her pulse and may start a memory medicine, which often eases hallucinations in Lewy body dementia.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:23","w":"D","s":"They'll also check for parkinsonism: finger taps for slowing, wrist stiffness, tremor, walking and turning, balance, and lying and standing blood pressure.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:38","w":"D","s":"Please put an alert on her care home records: no haloperidol or similar drugs. I'll do the same with the GP and hospital. Could you let her daughter know tonight or first thing?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:59","w":"P","s":"Yes, I'll ring her daughter first thing."},{"t":"6:05","band":"Check","w":"D","s":"Can you read the plan back to me, so I know we're agreed?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:14","w":"P","s":"No haloperidol. Lights on, someone with her, no arguing. GP tonight for urine and tummy. 999 if she's really unwell. Your team on Monday. Alert on her file."},{"t":"6:32","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. I'll write this call up now.","m":[13,14]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"Thanks. Sorry I was short with you."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"No need at all. You've looked after her well. Call me any time tonight.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms caller and resident, and checks immediate safety","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Says clearly not to give haloperidol and explains the sensitivity risk","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Clarifies the hallucinations: content, distress, duration and acting out dreams","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for delirium: alertness, fluids, bowels, urine, pain, observations and new medicines","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about stiffness, slowness and falls","d":"Content"},{"c":"Acknowledges the carer's stress and the urgency with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives practical non-drug strategies: company, lighting, reassurance, distraction","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges out-of-hours GP review tonight for physical causes","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets: when to call 999 and when to call back","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Arranges next-working-day specialist review and AChE inhibitor consideration","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Describes the parkinsonism check for the review, including postural BP","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks for a sensitivity alert on all records and for the daughter to be told","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with a read-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes clearly, thanks the carer and documents the call","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A clear no to haloperidol with reasons, a full delirium screen, practical calming steps, GP review tonight, Monday review with parkinsonism check, and records alerted.","borderline":"Says no to haloperidol but thin on delirium checks or follow-up.","fail":"Approves haloperidol or another typical antipsychotic, misses physical causes, or gives no plan or safety net."}},"c-3-3-1":{"setup":"It is 03:00 on the orthopaedic ward. Mr Arthur Bell, 84, two days after hip fracture surgery, is pulling out his cannula and shouting. The surgical FY1 is busy. Assess him for delirium, look for causes and agree tonight's plan with his nurse.","role":{"who":"Mr Arthur Bell, 84, the patient (Staff Nurse Grace Okafor, at the bedside, answers ward questions)","open":"Who are you? Get these people away from me. They're keeping me prisoner.","manner":"Frightened and angry at first, picking at his sheets. Settles when someone sits at eye level and speaks slowly; drifts back to the 'men in the corner' when asked hard questions.","free":["He wants to go home to his wife, Joan","There are men in the corner watching him","His hip is burning","He is not having another needle"],"ifasked":["Age 84, born 3 May 1942 (correct); thinks he is in a barracks or prison and that it is 1987","Cannot start the months backwards","Keeps needing to pass urine but little comes; no bowel movement since before surgery","Nurse: temperature 37.9, pulse 104, saturations 93% on air, glucose 6.8, NEWS2 3","Nurse: oxycodone four-hourly and cyclizine; also on tamsulosin and amlodipine; 400 ml negative fluid balance","Nurse: chatting normally yesterday morning; worse each evening","Wife reports two pints on Sundays only; no Parkinson's or memory diagnosis"],"worries":["Nurse: 'The FY1 says section him or give him something.'","Nurse: 'Can't he just have lorazepam so the bay can sleep?'","Nurse: 'What do I do if he goes for the rails again?'"],"rules":["Shouts and pulls away if approached from behind or above","Calms if his fear is named and he is told where he is","Refuses the cannula; accepts a tummy check and a drink"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Bell. I'm one of the doctors on tonight. I'm going to sit down here so you can see me properly.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Who are you? Get these people away from me. They're keeping me prisoner."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. You're in hospital, Mr Bell, after your hip operation. It's the middle of the night, and you're safe.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Hospital? Then who are those men in the corner? Can't you see them?"},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"I can't see anyone there, but I believe they look real to you. Grace, could we turn the side light up, and has he got his glasses and hearing aids?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Light's on. His glasses were in the locker, and here's his hearing aid."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Is that better, Mr Bell? Can you hear me all right now?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Better. I still don't know who you are."},{"t":"0:55","band":"Safety first","w":"D","s":"I'm a doctor, here to help. Grace, before I go on, is anyone hurt, and what are his latest observations?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Nobody's hurt. NEWS2 is 3. Temperature 37.9, pulse 104, sats 93% on air. Sugar's 6.8."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"That temperature and those sats matter. Could we give some oxygen to bring him into his target range, and repeat his obs in an hour?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Nurse: I'll do that now."},{"t":"1:20","band":"4AT","w":"D","s":"Mr Bell, a few quick questions. How old are you, and what's your date of birth?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Eighty-four. Third of May, 1942."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Spot on. And where are we right now? What year is it?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Some sort of barracks. Or a prison. It's 1987, isn't it? Why are you testing me?"},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Sorry, I know it's a lot. It helps me see how the operation is affecting you. Last one: can you say the months backwards, starting at December?","m":[4,2]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"December, then... look, he's moved again. What's he doing over there?"},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Grace, has he changed over the last day, and does it come and go?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Completely. Yesterday morning he was talking about the cricket. He's fine in the day, then this starts in the evening."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"So his 4AT is 8 at 03:10: alert now, two orientation errors, couldn't start the months, and a clear acute change that fluctuates. That's delirium until proven otherwise.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:20","band":"Causes","w":"D","s":"Mr Bell, are you in any pain? Does your tummy feel full, or do you need a wee?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"My hip's burning. And I keep needing to go, but nothing comes."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"That sounds miserable, and we can help with it. Grace, when did he last pass urine and open his bowels, and how's his fluid balance?","m":[6,2]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Small amounts only, he's 400 ml down, and no bowels since before surgery. He's on oxycodone four-hourly and cyclizine. Tamsulosin and amlodipine at home."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Tamsulosin makes me think of his prostate. Could you bladder scan him now? Retention, constipation and opioids together can cause all of this. Do we know if he drinks?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Nurse: His wife said two pints at the club on Sundays, nothing more."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Good. Any Parkinson's, Lewy body dementia or memory problems in the notes?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Nothing. His wife says he was sharp as a tack last week."},{"t":"3:15","band":"Medication","w":"P","s":"Nurse: The FY1 said section him or give him something. Can't he have lorazepam? The whole bay's been awake."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"It's been a really hard night for you all. Lorazepam usually makes delirium worse and he'd be likelier to fall, especially with the oxycodone. And sectioning isn't the right tool: this is a physical illness, so we treat him under the Mental Capacity Act.","m":[9,2]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Nurse: So what do we do if he goes for the rails again?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Non-drug steps first. Bed at its lowest with a crash mat, a side room if one's free, glasses and hearing aid in, a clock in view, and one nurse with him as much as possible. Could you ring Joan? She's welcome to come and sit with him, even tonight.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"Nurse: She said ring any time. I'll call her."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"If he's still very distressed or a danger to himself after all that, I'll write haloperidol 500 micrograms by mouth as required, repeat after two hours if needed, no more than 5 mg in 24 hours. He needs an ECG for his QTc, and it stops within a week.","m":[10,7]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Nurse: So nothing right now?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Nothing now. He settles when someone sits with him, and the likely causes are fixable. Treating those is what will calm him.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:40","band":"Capacity","w":"D","s":"Mr Bell, the nurses may need to put a drip back in for fluids and antibiotics. What do you think?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"No. I've had enough needles. I'm going home to Joan."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Fair enough, you've had plenty. Can you tell me why a drip might help you?","m":[11,2]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"What drip? I've told you, there are men watching me."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"Grace, he can't take in or weigh up the information right now, so he lacks capacity for treatment decisions tonight. We'll care for him in his best interests using the Mental Capacity Act, the least restrictive way. Try drinks first; the FY1 can decide if the cannula is really needed.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Will you write that down? Night decisions always get questioned."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Yes. I'll document the 4AT with the time, the capacity assessment, the best interests reasons and the haloperidol plan.","m":[11,14]},{"t":"5:35","band":"Handover","w":"D","s":"I'll bleep the FY1 now and ask for a bladder scan result, a bowel chart, U&E with sodium, calcium, CRP, FBC, cultures because of the fever, a chest and wound check, and an opioid review: regular paracetamol, less oxycodone, and stop the cyclizine.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Nurse: She's stuck with a sick patient. What if she can't come for an hour?"},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"Then call the surgical registrar; new delirium with a fever shouldn't wait. If his sats drop, his NEWS2 rises, he gets drowsy or he hurts himself, call the medical team straight away and bleep me again.","m":[13,3]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Okay. Will psychiatry see him again?"},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Yes, the liaison team will see him later today. Can you run the plan back to me so I know we've got the same picture?","m":[13,14]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Bladder scan, obs and oxygen, glasses and clock, one nurse, ring Joan, haloperidol only if he's still dangerous after that, and call the registrar if he gets worse."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. Thank you, you've handled a hard night really well.","m":[13,14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Mr Bell, Grace is staying with you, and Joan's coming in. You're safe here.","m":[14,2]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Joan's coming? Good. Tell her to bring my radio."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self calmly at eye level and checks glasses and hearing aids are in","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names his fear or validates distress before reorienting him","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks immediate safety, NEWS2, oxygen saturations and glucose with the nurse","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Completes all four 4AT items and records the total with the time","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes acute change and fluctuation from the nurse's account","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about pain, urine, bowels, fluid balance, alcohol and the drug chart","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks for Parkinson's or Lewy body dementia before any antipsychotic","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Sets four non-drug measures: aids, light and clock, one nurse, wife invited","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains why lorazepam and the Mental Health Act are not right tonight","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Decides on haloperidol with dose, 24-hour ceiling, ECG and stop date","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Records lack of capacity for treatment tonight and care in best interests using the MCA","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Hands the surgical team a named cause search, including bladder scan and opioid review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks the nurse's understanding and gives clear escalation triggers","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes with documentation, liaison follow-up and reassurance to the patient","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Completes the 4AT, calls it delirium, sets a named cause search and non-drug plan, and keeps haloperidol as a dosed, time-limited last resort under the MCA.","borderline":"Recognises delirium but the cause search is vague, or haloperidol is written without a ceiling, ECG or stop date.","fail":"Reaches for lorazepam or the Mental Health Act, skips the 4AT, or never looks for a physical cause."}},"c-3-3-2":{"setup":"Mrs Margaret Price, 79, was admitted two days ago with a urine infection and new confusion. Her daughter Helen, 52, is upset in the relatives' room. Take a collateral history to separate delirium from dementia and explain the plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Helen Price, 52, teacher, the patient's daughter","open":"Are you the psychiatrist? Why has my mum suddenly gone mad? She didn't even know me this morning.","manner":"Anxious, talks fast and wants a straight answer. Guilty about living far away. Softens and becomes thoughtful once she feels listened to.","free":["Her mother lives alone in a bungalow since her father died five years ago","She spoke to her on Sunday and she sounded normal","The ward has said she 'can't go home like this'","Her mother called her by her aunt's name this morning"],"ifasked":["A neighbour found her in her nightdress in the garden on Tuesday morning","Before this she shopped, cooked, washed and drove to town herself, and walked without a stick","For about a year she has repeated stories on the phone and missed a gas bill","She is muddled and sleepy in the mornings and worse in the evenings; saw children in the room last night","Fell in the kitchen on Monday; no head injury","GP started oxybutynin for her bladder three weeks ago; a sherry at Christmas only","Was confused for three days after her hip replacement in 2019","Property and financial LPA only, made after her father died; no health and welfare LPA"],"worries":["'So is it dementia or not?'","'Is she going to end up in a home?'","'I should have noticed sooner.'"],"rules":["Interrupts if the doctor uses jargon; settles with plain words","Becomes tearful when asked how she is coping; recovers if validated","Only mentions the year of memory slips if asked about the past year"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello, are you Helen? I'm one of the psychiatry doctors working with the medical team. Thanks for waiting. Is it all right if we talk about your mum?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Yes. Why has she suddenly gone mad? She didn't even know me this morning."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That must have been a real shock. She hasn't gone mad, and I want to understand what's happening. You know her best, so what you tell me really helps.","m":[2,1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Okay. Sorry. I just drove up from Leicester and she called me by my aunt's name."},{"t":"0:45","band":"Onset","w":"D","s":"No need to apologise. When did you last know she was her usual self?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Sunday. I ring her every Sunday. She was telling me about her neighbour's cat. Completely normal."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"And when did someone first notice a change?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Tuesday morning. Her neighbour found her in the garden in her nightdress, talking nonsense. She rang the ambulance."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"So she was well on Sunday, and it came on by Tuesday. That's over a day or two, which is really useful to know.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","band":"Baseline","w":"D","s":"What was she managing before this? Shopping, cooking, washing, money, tablets?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"All of it. She shops, cooks, pays her bills, does her own pill box. She lives alone since Dad died."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Does she drive, and how does she get about?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"She drives into town twice a week. Walks fine, no stick."},{"t":"1:50","band":"Course","w":"D","s":"Over the last day or two, does the confusion come and go? Is she sleepier, or very restless?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"The nurses say she's sleepy in the mornings and up all night. Last night she said there were children in the room."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Has she had any falls, new tablets, or anything to drink lately?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"She fell in the kitchen on Monday, didn't hit her head. The GP gave her something for her bladder a few weeks ago. She only drinks sherry at Christmas."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Do you know what the bladder tablet is called?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Oxy-something. Oxybutynin?"},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's important; it can add to confusion. Has anything like this happened before, say after an operation?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Actually yes. After her hip in 2019 she was muddled for about three days."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Longer view","w":"D","s":"Thinking about the past year, before this illness, have you noticed any slow changes in her memory?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Honestly? She's been repeating herself on the phone for about a year. And she missed a gas bill. I didn't want to say."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; you were right to. It doesn't change her treatment now, but it means we should check her memory properly once she's well. Has she got lost, or left the cooker on?","m":[7,2]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"3:30","band":"Support","w":"D","s":"Does she have a Lasting Power of Attorney, and is there anyone at home with her?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"We did a financial one after Dad died, not a health one. She's on her own. The neighbour pops in."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"And how are you managing with all of this?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Not great. I'm forty miles away, I've got a full-time job. I should have noticed. I only ring on Sundays."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to carry. Delirium comes on over hours to days, so most families couldn't have spotted it sooner. You're here now, and you've given us exactly what we needed. You can also ask the council for a carer's assessment for yourself.","m":[10,2]},{"t":"4:25","band":"Explain","w":"P","s":"So is it dementia or not? The ward says she can't go home."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"I understand you want a straight answer. This looks like delirium: sudden confusion caused by her infection. It usually improves as the infection clears, though it can take some weeks. While she's this unwell, a memory test can't tell us whether there's dementia underneath.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"So when will you know?"},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"Once she's recovered, we'll test her memory again. Given the slips you've noticed, if it's still not right, her GP can refer her to the memory service.","m":[9,11]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Is she going to end up in a home?"},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"That's a big worry. It's far too early to decide; most people improve once the infection is treated. Any decision about where she lives comes later, with her involved.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"5:30","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Here's what I'll suggest to her consultant: keep treating the infection, stop the oxybutynin, keep her drinking and her bowels moving, and recheck her thinking before she goes home. Could you fill in a short questionnaire about how she's changed over the years?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Yes, of course."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Two safety points. Please make sure she doesn't drive until her memory's been rechecked. And if you notice scams, missed meals or gas left on once she's home, tell her GP.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"She'll hate not driving, but okay."},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what you'll tell your brother tonight?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"That it's delirium from the infection, it should get better, and they'll check her memory again once she's well. And no driving."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. I'll speak to her consultant now and write in her notes. The ward can reach me if you have more questions tomorrow.","m":[14,12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel a bit less panicky."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. Go and sit with her; a familiar face helps her settle.","m":[14,2]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and role, and confirms who she is before starting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges her shock or guilt and validates it","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Fixes the onset to a date or event","d":"Content"},{"c":"Records her baseline: self-care, money, medicines, driving and mobility","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about fluctuation, drowsiness or restlessness, and hallucinations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about falls, alcohol, new medicines and previous episodes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores gradual memory decline over the past year","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about a Lasting Power of Attorney and who lives with her","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains delirium plainly and why dementia cannot be diagnosed today","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks how the daughter is coping and mentions a carer's assessment","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives a plan: treat infection, stop anticholinergic, recheck cognition after recovery, memory service if needed","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks the daughter's understanding in her own words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets driving and risks at home after discharge","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closes clearly with feedback to the consultant and how to reach the team","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Anchors onset in days, records a clear baseline and the year of memory slips, explains delirium without labelling dementia, and gives a recovery-then-retest plan.","borderline":"Gets the timeline but misses medicines, LPA or the daughter's own needs, or explains in jargon.","fail":"Diagnoses dementia or care home placement today, refuses to talk to her, or never pins down the onset."}},"c-3-4-1":{"setup":"You are on the older adult CMHT duty desk. The manager of Willow Court care home phones about Mrs Edith Rowe, 86, with Alzheimer's disease, who has hit staff during care and says her purse was stolen. Assess by phone and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Denise Walsh, 54, manager of Willow Court residential care home","open":"Hello, it's Denise from Willow Court. I need someone to prescribe something for Edith Rowe. My staff can't cope.","manner":"Busy and frustrated, protective of her staff, pushes for a tablet. Becomes cooperative and practical once she feels her team's strain is heard and help is coming.","free":["For two weeks Edith has hit staff during personal care","She refuses to wash and says carers stole her purse","Two carers have been bruised","The owners may give notice if this continues"],"ifasked":["Monday 07:00 and Saturday 07:15: hit out when night agency staff did her wash; Thursday 19:30 at bath time","She calms when her regular carer Shirley sings to her","Winces when rolled; arthritic knee; bowels not opened for five days","GP started co-codamol for her knee three weeks ago","Hearing aid battery flat for a week; no cough, urine smell or falls","Purse not found; she has hidden it in her pillowcase before; £40 may be missing; not yet logged","DoLS authorised until March; daughter Carol visits weekly"],"worries":["'We need something to calm her down or we'll have to give notice.'","'Why can't she just have a tablet?'","'What if she hurts someone before you visit?'"],"rules":["Pushes back once on medication; accepts if risks are explained plainly and a visit is promised","Gives the agency staff detail only if asked who was doing the care","Sounds defensive if theft is raised; settles if told it is routine to check"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello Denise, I'm one of the doctors on the older adult team's duty desk. Can I check I've got the right person: Edith Rowe, 86, with Alzheimer's?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"That's her. I need someone to prescribe something. My staff can't cope."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"It sounds like your team are under real strain. I want to help keep Edith settled and keep her placement. Can you tell me what's been happening?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Two weeks of it. Hitting out at washing time, refusing to wash, and she says we've stolen her purse. Two carers are bruised. The owners are talking about notice."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"That's hard for everyone. Is anyone in danger right now, and is she hitting out at other residents?","m":[8]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"No, just staff, and only during care. She's sat in the lounge now, perfectly calm."},{"t":"1:15","band":"ABC","w":"D","s":"Let's take the last three times. When was it, what were staff doing, and who was with her?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Monday about seven in the morning, then Saturday about quarter past seven. Both her morning wash. Then Thursday evening at bath time."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Who did the morning washes?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Agency staff off the night shift, now you mention it. A man on Saturday."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"And what happened just before, and what calmed her afterwards?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"They'd woken her to wash. She lashed out, they backed off. She calms when Shirley, her regular carer, sings to her."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful. Unfamiliar faces waking her early sounds like a big trigger.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:15","band":"Causes","w":"D","s":"Is she in any pain? Does she wince when she's moved?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"She does grimace when we roll her. She's got a bad knee."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"When did she last open her bowels, and any urine smell, cough, falls or new bruises?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Let me look... five days ago. No smell, no cough, no falls."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Has the GP changed any medicines recently?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Co-codamol for her knee, about three weeks ago."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"That fits: co-codamol can constipate, and constipation is very uncomfortable. Is she sleepier or more muddled than usual, and are her glasses and hearing aid working?","m":[6,4]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Same as ever, mostly. Oh, her hearing aid battery's been flat for a week."},{"t":"3:15","band":"Theft and safeguards","w":"D","s":"About the purse: has it been found, and is anything actually missing?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Not found. She's hidden it in her pillowcase before. Her daughter thinks there was forty pounds in it."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Please log it today, search her room with her, and tell her daughter. If money's still missing, raise a safeguarding concern. It's routine; we just can't assume it's the dementia.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Fine. It's not been logged yet. I'll do it."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"And is her DoLS authorisation in date?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Yes, until March."},{"t":"4:00","band":"Plan","w":"P","s":"So can you prescribe something? Otherwise I don't know what we'll do."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"I hear how worried you are. Calming drugs like antipsychotics raise the risk of stroke and death in dementia, so we only use one if she's severely distressed or dangerous and these steps have failed, and then for no more than six weeks, with review.","m":[10,2]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"So what do I tell my staff today?"},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Three things. Only familiar staff do her personal care, no agency. Let her wake naturally and wash her later in the morning, with Shirley singing. And change her hearing aid battery today.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"We can do that."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"Also please start an ABC chart for two weeks: time, what was happening before, what she did, and what helped. Score her pain at each wash.","m":[9,3]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Okay. Who's checking her physically?"},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"I'll ask the GP to see her within 48 hours: her tummy for constipation, chest, urine only if she has symptoms, and her co-codamol, probably swapping to regular paracetamol. Our care home team will visit this week.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:40","band":"Close","w":"P","s":"What if she really hurts someone before then?"},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Staff should step back and try again later; she's safe in a chair. If she's suddenly much more confused, feverish, drowsy, or someone's hurt, call the GP or 111 the same day, or 999 in an emergency, and ring our duty line.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Right."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back what you'll do today, so I know I've been clear?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"Regular staff only, wash her later with Shirley, new battery, ABC charts, log the purse, and the GP's coming."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll ring her daughter to explain, and I'll send you the plan in writing this afternoon. Thank you, Denise, your staff are doing well.","m":[14,12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thanks. That's more helpful than I expected."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and confirms the caller and resident details","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges staff strain and the threat of notice with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes an ABC account of recent incidents: time, task, carer, trigger, what calmed her","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about pain, constipation, urine, chest, falls and bruises","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about recent medication changes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks for delirium signs and that hearing aid and glasses work","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks whether the purse is found and the theft claim logged and looked into","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks current danger to staff and residents and DoLS status","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives three specific non-drug changes to start today and an ABC chart","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains antipsychotic risks plainly and when one would be used","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Arranges GP review within 48 hours and a team visit this week","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks the manager's understanding of the plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets: what to do and who to call if things worsen","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes clearly: family informed, plan in writing, next contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gets a clear ABC picture, finds likely physical drivers, gives concrete non-drug steps, explains why no antipsychotic today, and takes the theft claim seriously.","borderline":"Sensible advice but no ABC detail, no GP review timescale, or theft dismissed as a symptom.","fail":"Prescribes an antipsychotic over the phone, or treats it as 'just behaviour' and ends the call without a plan."}},"c-3-4-2":{"setup":"Home visit with the older adult CMHT. Mr Frank Dawson, 77, with vascular dementia, has twice left home at night; police found him two miles away. His wife Pat is exhausted; their son Mark is present. Assess and agree a safe plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Pat Dawson, 74, his wife (Frank, 77, and their son Mark, 46, also speak)","open":"Thank you for coming. I'm at the end of my rope. I just want to lock the doors at night.","manner":"Tired, tearful at times, loyal to Frank. Frank is cheerful and proud; Mark is anxious and wants a quick fix. All three warm up when Frank is spoken to directly.","free":["Frank has gone out at night twice this month","Last week police found him two miles away in his pyjamas","She hasn't slept properly in weeks","Mark wants him 'sectioned' to keep him safe"],"ifasked":["Frank: 'I was going to the depot. Early shift.' He drove buses for 40 years, starting at 5 am","He goes out between 2 and 3 am; the bathroom door is next to the front door","He gets up two or three times a night to pass urine and dozes in his chair all afternoon","He takes his water tablet with his tea","Knee pain at night; no new tablets apart from that","He still has his car keys; he tried to drive last month before Pat hid them","Pat has back pain, no help and no breaks; has not heard of the Herbert Protocol"],"worries":["Pat: 'Can I lock the front door at night?'","Mark: 'Can't you just section him? At least he'd be safe.'","Pat: 'I feel terrible even thinking about a care home.'"],"rules":["Frank bristles if talked about as if he is not there; engages when asked about his work","Frank resists a 'tag' but agrees to try a watch for a month if it is framed as keeping his walks","Pat becomes tearful about sleep; settles when offered practical help"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm one of the doctors from the memory and older people's team. Mr Dawson, is it all right if we have a chat about the nights?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Frank: Call me Frank. Nights? Nothing wrong with my nights."},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Pat: I'm at the end of my rope. I just want to lock the doors."},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you both. Frank, I hear you went out the other night. Where were you going?","m":[2,1]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Frank: To the depot. Early shift. They can't run the 5 o'clock without me."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"It sounds like that job really mattered to you. What did you drive?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Frank: Buses. Forty years. Never late once."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Forty years, that's quite something. So when you wake up, it feels like time for work.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:05","band":"Pattern","w":"D","s":"Pat, what time does he go out, and what happens just before?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Pat: Two or three in the morning. He gets up for the toilet and I hear the front door. Last week the police found him by the ring road in his pyjamas."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"That must have been terrifying. Where's the bathroom?","m":[4,3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Pat: Right next to the front door. Same colour door."},{"t":"1:40","band":"Triggers","w":"D","s":"How often is he up to pass urine at night, and does he sleep in the day?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Pat: Two or three times a night. And he dozes in his chair all afternoon."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"When does he take his water tablet?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Pat: With his tea, about five."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"That will have him up at night. Frank, any pain at night?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Frank: Knees. Old bus-driver knees."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Any new tablets, or has he seemed more muddled than usual lately?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Pat: No new tablets. About the same, maybe a bit worse in the evenings."},{"t":"2:30","band":"Risks","w":"D","s":"Does Frank still have car keys?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Pat: He tried to drive last month. I hid them."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Good thinking. Frank, I'd advise you not to drive now, and we'll need to tell the DVLA together. Pat, does he use the cooker?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Pat: No, I do all the cooking. He doesn't go near it."},{"t":"2:55","band":"Carer","w":"D","s":"Pat, how are you managing?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Pat: I haven't slept properly in weeks. I lie there listening for the door. My back's gone. I feel awful even thinking about a care home."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"That sounds utterly exhausting, and it's natural to have those thoughts; it doesn't mean you love him less. You need help now, not later. I'll refer you for a carer's assessment, and we'll ask about a regular break.","m":[3,11]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Pat: A break would be... yes. Please."},{"t":"3:40","band":"Options","w":"P","s":"Pat: But can I lock the front door at night?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"I can see why you'd want to. Locking him in is a fire risk, and it could unlawfully restrict him. A door sensor that wakes you when it opens is safer, and you'd sleep better too.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"Mark: Can't you just section him? At least he'd be safe."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Mark, I can see you're scared for him. Sectioning is for mental illness that needs hospital treatment; it wouldn't stop the walking and would take him away from home.","m":[9,3]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Mark: So what does stop it?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Fixing the reasons. Move the water tablet to the morning; I'll check with the GP. Regular painkillers for his knees. A night light and a toilet sign on the bathroom door. And a walk and something to do in the day, so he's not dozing all afternoon.","m":[12,6]},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"Frank, there's also a small watch with a tracker, so Pat knows you're safe and you can keep going out for walks. Would you try it?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Frank: I'm not wearing a tag like a criminal."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Nobody wants to feel watched. It looks like an ordinary watch. Would you try it for a month?","m":[10,2]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Frank: A month. Then we'll see."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Pat, there's also the Herbert Protocol: a form with Frank's photo and favourite places, so the police can find him quickly. Social care can arrange the door sensor. If Frank can't agree to something on the day, we'll record a best interests decision.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:55","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can I check we're agreed? Pat, could you tell me the plan?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Pat: Water tablet in the morning, painkillers, night light and toilet sign, door sensor, the watch, that police form, and help for me."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If he goes out at night again and isn't found quickly, call 999 and give them the form. If he's suddenly more confused or unwell, call the GP that day.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Mark: And if Mum can't cope?"},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Ring our duty line and we'll come sooner. I'll review you all in four weeks. Frank, thanks for talking to me.","m":[14,1]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Frank: Any time. Tell the depot I'll be in Monday."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and involves Frank directly, not just talking about him","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks Frank where he was going and why","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates the wife's exhaustion and the son's fear","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes the pattern: timing, frequency, what happens just before, the police episode","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores triggers: toilet, diuretic timing, daytime sleep, evening restlessness, old work routine","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about pain, new medicines and increased confusion","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks driving and car keys and other home risks","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Advises against locking him in, explaining fire risk and lawfulness","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains in plain words why the Mental Health Act does not apply","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees door sensor, GPS device and Herbert Protocol, with consent or best interests","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Refers the wife for a carer's assessment and respite","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Suggests daytime activity and an evening toilet routine","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks the family's understanding of the plan","d":"Close"},{"c":"Safety-nets and sets a review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Talks to Frank, finds the reasons for night walking, agrees least restrictive safety measures and gets the wife real support, with a clear answer on sectioning and locks.","borderline":"Safe plan but talks over Frank, or misses the diuretic, driving or carer's assessment.","fail":"Endorses locking him in, offers sedation or an MHA assessment, or ignores the exhausted carer."}},"c-3-4-3":{"setup":"Joint home visit with a CMHT nurse to Mrs Irene Hughes, 72, alone in a council flat. The fire service found her hallway blocked. She is polite but reluctant at the door. Engage her, assess hoarding, capacity and risk, and agree a first step.","role":{"who":"Mrs Irene Hughes, 72, retired school librarian, living alone","open":"Good morning. I don't need any help, thank you. Everything here is useful.","manner":"Polite but guarded at the door, suspicious of the council. Warms up when she feels her things are respected; tearful when talking about her late husband.","free":["The newspapers are for articles she means to cut out","She knows where everything is","Neighbours complain about her; she thinks they want her out","She will not have anything thrown away"],"ifasked":["Collecting began after her husband Ken died three years ago and has got worse this year","Throwing things away feels like losing a bit of Ken","Hob covered with boxes; she uses the microwave; sleeps in her armchair because the bed is covered","Uses a two-bar electric fire next to the newspapers; does not smoke; took the smoke alarm battery out because it beeped","Low since Ken died, poor sleep, still enjoys the radio and her library books; no thoughts of ending her life","Sometimes misses letters among the papers; no getting lost; no voices or feeling watched","Hallway would take her 'a minute or two' to get out of in a fire; she can explain why that is dangerous"],"worries":["'The council sent you, didn't they? They want me out.'","'Are you going to put me in a home?'","'You'll throw everything out.'"],"rules":["Refuses entry if the clutter is criticised; lets them in if they ask about her life first","Becomes tearful about Ken; settles when invited to talk about him","Bargains: will clear the hall if the rest of the flat is left alone"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"At the door","w":"D","s":"Good morning, Mrs Hughes. I'm one of the doctors from the older people's mental health team, and this is Sam, a nurse. Social care asked us to visit after the fire service came. Is now an all right time?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"I don't need any help, thank you. Everything here is useful."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"That's fair. We're not here to take anything away. I'd just like to hear how life is for you here. Could we come in for ten minutes, or would another day suit you better?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"The council sent you, didn't they? They want me out of this flat."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"I can see why you'd be suspicious. We're from the health team, not housing. Helping you stay safely in your own home is what we want too.","m":[2,13]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Well. Ten minutes. Mind the bags."},{"t":"1:10","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Thank you for letting us in. Tell me about the newspapers. What makes them important?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"There are articles I mean to cut out. You never know when you'll need something."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"How do you feel when you think about throwing some away?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Sick. Panicky. Like I'm losing something I can't get back."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"When did the collecting start?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"After Ken died. Three years ago. Worse this year. If I throw it away, it's like losing a bit of him."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"It sounds like these things keep you close to him. We won't rush anything. What was Ken like?","m":[10,4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Kind. Funny. He did the crossword every day. Sorry, I don't usually cry."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Please don't apologise. It's clear how much you loved him.","m":[10]},{"t":"2:15","band":"Living space","w":"D","s":"Can you use the kitchen, bathroom and bed the way you'd like?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"I use the microwave; the hob has boxes on it. I sleep in my chair. The bed's got Ken's things on it."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Sam and I use a set of pictures to rate how full rooms are. The hallway is about a 6 out of 9, and this room about a 5. Does that sound fair?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"I suppose. I know where everything is."},{"t":"3:00","band":"Fire safety","w":"D","s":"If there were a fire, how would you get out?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Down the hall. Takes a minute or two with the bags."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Do you smoke or use candles, and how do you keep warm?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"I don't smoke. That electric fire there."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"It's quite close to the papers. Is your smoke alarm working?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"I took the battery out. It kept beeping."},{"t":"3:30","band":"Her health","w":"D","s":"How have you been in yourself since Ken died: your mood, sleep, enjoying things?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Low. I don't sleep well. I still like the radio and my library books."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"When people feel low, sometimes life feels not worth living. Have you had thoughts like that, or of ending your life?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"No. Ken would be furious. I just get lonely."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Any trouble with memory, like losing track of bills? And do you ever hear voices, or feel people mean you harm?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Letters get lost in the papers. No voices. The neighbours are nosy, that's all."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Do you ever feel you have to check or clean things over and over, or that things are contaminated?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"4:35","band":"Capacity","w":"D","s":"So I've understood: why might the hallway matter in a fire?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Because I couldn't get out quickly, and the papers would burn. I'm not daft."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"You're clearly not. You understand the risk and can weigh it up. So it's your decision whether to clear the hallway. I'd like you to make it with us.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"I'll clear the hall if you leave the rest of the flat alone."},{"t":"5:10","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"That's a really good start, thank you. Could Sam help you make a clear path to the front door by next Friday? And can the fire service come and fit new smoke alarms?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"Are you going to put me in a home?"},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"No. We want the opposite: you safe in your own home. With your agreement, I'd like to share this with your social worker and the fire service, so they can help. I'll ask your GP to check your bloods and we'll talk about help for your mood.","m":[13,12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"As long as nobody comes in throwing things out."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Nobody will clear anything without your say. We'll go at your pace.","m":[13,10]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Could you tell me what we've agreed, so I know I've been clear?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"I clear the hall by Friday with Sam, the fire people fit an alarm, and you tell the social worker. The rest stays."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"That's it. Sam will come next Monday at ten. Please keep the fire away from the papers until then. Thank you for letting us in.","m":[14,7]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Monday at ten. I'll put the kettle on."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and nurse, explains the reason for the visit honestly and asks permission","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Respects her wish and earns entry without arguing about the clutter","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores what the items mean and how discarding feels","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes onset and course of collecting and its link to bereavement","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks whether she can use her kitchen, bathroom and bed","d":"Content"},{"c":"Rates the clutter using the Clutter Image Rating","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks fire risk: exit route, cooker, heaters, smoking and smoke alarm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens mood, including asking directly about suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for memory problems, psychosis and OCD","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her grief or fear with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Makes a decision-specific capacity judgement about clearing the hallway","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees one concrete first step with a date and a fire service visit","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains the multi-agency plan honestly and seeks consent to share","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks her understanding and closes with the next visit date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gets invited in by respecting her, finds the grief behind the hoarding, checks fire risk and mood, makes a specific capacity judgement and agrees a dated first step.","borderline":"Engages well but misses the smoke alarm or heater, the mood screen, or a clear capacity statement.","fail":"Argues about the clutter or threatens clearance, gets no entry or agreement, or confuses an unwise choice with lack of capacity."}},"c-3-5-1":{"setup":"Older adult CMHT clinic. Mr George Fenwick, 78, widowed two years, is referred by his GP with poor sleep, weight loss and 'not seeing the point'. His daughter Susan is waiting outside. Assess depression and suicide risk and agree a safety plan.","role":{"who":"Mr George Fenwick, 78, retired engineer and widower (daughter Susan, 50, joins at the end)","open":"I don't really know why I'm here. The GP insisted. I'm just old, that's all.","manner":"Courteous, flat and slow, minimises. Opens up about his wife and his plans when asked kindly and directly. Accepts help once he feels respected rather than managed.","free":["His wife Margaret died two years ago","He sleeps badly and has lost weight","He gave up driving two months ago after his eyesight test","He has prostate cancer 'that they're watching'"],"ifasked":["Low most days for six months; enjoys nothing, even his garden; wakes at 4 am","Lost about 6 kg; no energy","Feels he should have spotted Margaret's illness sooner","Thinks daily that he'd be better off with Margaret; has thought of taking her leftover morphine tablets, still in the bathroom cabinet","Has given his tools to his grandson, updated his will last month, and written a letter to Susan","No guns; no previous self-harm; whisky up from one to three large glasses a night","What keeps him going: his granddaughter's wedding in May, and his dog Bess"],"worries":["'What's the point of treatment at my age, with cancer?'","'Don't tell my daughter, she's got enough on.'","Susan: 'Should I take his tablets away?'"],"rules":["Only mentions the morphine and the letter if asked directly about plans and preparations","Becomes tearful about Margaret; steadies if his grief is acknowledged","Agrees to Susan being told if he can choose what is shared"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Fenwick, I'm one of the doctors in the older people's team. Thank you for coming. How have you been feeling in your spirits lately?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"I don't really know why I'm here. The GP insisted. I'm just old, that's all."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Getting older brings real losses, and you've had big ones. I'd like to understand how things are for you. Is there anything you still look forward to or enjoy?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Not really. I used to love the garden. Now I can't be bothered. Haven't for months."},{"t":"0:45","band":"Depression","w":"D","s":"How long have you felt this low? Most days?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Most days. Six months, maybe. Worse since I gave up the car."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"How are your sleep, appetite and energy?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Awake at four every morning. Not hungry. I've lost nearly a stone. No energy at all."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Losing the car must have been a real blow. And Margaret, two years ago. How are you with that?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"I should have seen she was ill. I was an engineer, I notice things. I didn't notice her."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"That's a heavy thing to carry for two years. Guilt like that is very common in grief. It's clear you loved her very much.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Forty-nine years. Sorry."},{"t":"1:45","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Take your time. When people feel this low, sometimes they feel life isn't worth living. Have you felt like that?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Most days I think I'd be better off with Margaret."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you had thoughts about ending your life?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"...Yes. At four in the morning."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about how you might do it?","m":[5,7]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Margaret's tablets. The morphine. They're still in the bathroom cabinet."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Have you done anything to prepare, like counting them out, or a trial run?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"No. I look at them, that's all."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Your daughter mentioned you've been giving things away. Can you tell me about that?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"My tools went to my grandson. I've updated my will. And I've written Susan a letter. So she won't have anything to deal with."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"When people put their affairs in order like that, sometimes they're getting ready to die. Is that what's been happening?","m":[6,5]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"I suppose it is. I hadn't put it like that."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Are there any other tablets stored up, any guns, or anything else at home you've thought of using?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"No guns. Just my own prescriptions."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Have you ever harmed yourself before? And how much are you drinking?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Never. Three large whiskies a night now. It used to be one."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"What's kept you going so far?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"My granddaughter's wedding in May. And Bess, my dog. Who'd walk her?"},{"t":"3:40","band":"Explain","w":"P","s":"But what's the point of treatment? I'm 78 and I've got cancer."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"I can hear how hopeless it feels. This is depression, and it makes the future look darker than it is. Treatment works as well at 78 as at 40, and you deserve to enjoy that wedding.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:05","band":"Safety plan","w":"D","s":"Let's make a plan together for the bad moments. What tends to warn you things are getting worse?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Waking at four and thinking about the cabinet."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Then what could you do at four that helps?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Put the radio on. Make tea. Let Bess out."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Good. And who could you ring?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Susan. And my friend Ron from bowls."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"Let's write those down, with Samaritans on 116 123 and NHS 111, option 2, for mental health, any time. Would you let Susan take Margaret's tablets back to the pharmacy today?","m":[10,7]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Don't tell her everything. She's got enough on."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"I respect you wanting to protect her. She's already worried and rang us. What would you be comfortable with me sharing?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"That I'm low and need help. Not the letter. Not yet."},{"t":"5:15","band":"Treatment","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'd suggest sertraline 50 mg each morning, two weeks' supply at a time. It can lower sodium at your age, so we'll check a blood test now and in two to three weeks. I'll see you again within a week, and less whisky will help it work.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"All right. I'll try."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"I'm also going to talk to my consultant before you leave, as your risk is high right now. The crisis team may ring you tonight.","m":[14]},{"t":"5:55","band":"Daughter","w":"P","s":"Susan: Is he all right? Should I take his tablets away? I don't want him to feel like a child."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Your dad is very low and needs treatment. He's agreed you can help. Could you take Margaret's old tablets back to the pharmacy today, ring him every day for the next two weeks, and call 111 option 2 if you're worried?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Susan: Yes. Of course. Dad, why didn't you say?"},{"t":"6:30","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Mr Fenwick, what will you do if four in the morning gets bad?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Radio, tea, Bess. Ring Susan or Ron. Or that 111 number."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll see you next week, and I'll write to your GP today. Thanks for being so open with me.","m":[14,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and uses open questions to build rapport","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about core symptoms: low mood, loss of interest and duration","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about sleep, appetite, weight and energy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores bereavement, guilt and loss of driving with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plan, method and preparation","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about final acts: giving things away, will and letters","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks access to means: stockpiled tablets, firearms and other methods","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about past self-harm, alcohol and what keeps him going","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains depression as treatable at his age, with realistic hope","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Writes a safety plan: warning signs, coping steps, two contacts, Samaritans and NHS 111 option 2","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names an antidepressant with sodium checks, small supply and review within a week","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gets his agreement to involve his daughter and gives her three tasks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding of the safety plan","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes with senior discussion today, review date and GP letter","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Asks plainly about suicide, uncovers method and final acts, restricts means, writes a personal safety plan, starts safe treatment and involves his daughter with consent.","borderline":"Covers depression well but asks about suicide vaguely, or misses final acts or the tablets at home.","fail":"Accepts 'just old', never asks directly about suicide or means, or sends him home without a safety plan or senior input."}},"c-3-5-2":{"setup":"It is 22:00 in A&E. Mrs Valerie Moss, 71, with no psychiatric history, has not slept for three nights and has spent thousands online. Her husband Derek is with her. The A&E doctor wants her 'admitted to psych'. Assess her and decide the next steps.","role":{"who":"Mrs Valerie Moss, 71, retired florist (husband Derek, 74, also speaks)","open":"Hello, darling! Isn't this exciting? I've never felt so alive. Why does everyone want me in hospital?","manner":"Bright, fast, charming and easily distracted; irritable if her spending is challenged. Derek is frightened and quiet, then relieved to be asked about the steroids.","free":["She hasn't needed sleep for three nights","She is buying a seaside cottage to open a flower café","She has spent about £8,000 online on furniture and jewellery","She feels wonderful and doesn't need a doctor"],"ifasked":["Derek: the GP started prednisolone 40 mg for a flare of her COPD two weeks ago","Derek: after their son was born she 'couldn't get out of bed for months'","Derek: her mother 'had nerves' and was in hospital once","She drove to a 24-hour supermarket at 3 am","No headaches, falls, weakness or speech changes; one glass of wine at weekends","Oriented to date and place; says the hospital staff are 'spies sent by the bank'","Agrees to blood tests and, after some persuasion, a scan; reluctant about any ward"],"worries":["'My money is my business.'","Derek: 'Is she losing her mind?'","'I'm not having a scan. There's nothing wrong with my head.'"],"rules":["Talks over the doctor; responds to warm, brief, firm redirection","Becomes irritable if called ill; cooperates if her energy is acknowledged","Derek only mentions the steroids when asked about new medicines"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Moss, I'm one of the psychiatry doctors. And you must be Derek. Can we have a chat about the last few days?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Hello, darling! Isn't this exciting? I've never felt so alive. Why does everyone want me in hospital?"},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"You're clearly full of energy, and it's good you feel well. I'd just like to understand what's been happening. How much have you been sleeping?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Sleeping? Who needs it! Three nights, not a wink, and I'm not even tired. I've got plans."},{"t":"0:45","band":"Mania","w":"D","s":"Tell me about your plans.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"A seaside cottage. A flower café. I've bought the furniture already, and some jewellery to celebrate. I've got a gift for flowers, everyone says so."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Your thoughts sound very quick at the moment. When did all this start?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Last week? The week before? Time flies when you're brilliant."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Derek, how much has she spent, and over how long?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Derek: About eight thousand pounds since Wednesday. She's never like this. Is she losing her mind?"},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"This must be frightening to see. It looks like mania, a very high mood. When it starts after 70, there's often a physical cause, so that's where we start.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"1:40","band":"History","w":"D","s":"Derek, has she ever had a very low or high spell before?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Derek: After our son was born, she couldn't get out of bed for months. Nobody called it anything."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Anything similar in the family?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Derek: Her mother had nerves. She was in hospital once."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Any new tablets in the last few weeks?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Derek: The GP put her on steroids for her chest. Prednisolone, forty milligrams, two weeks ago."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's really important. Steroids can trigger mania. They mustn't be stopped suddenly, so I'll ask the medical team to review the dose. Any alcohol or other drugs?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"A glass of wine on Saturdays! I'm not a junkie, darling."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Any headaches, falls, weakness or trouble with your speech?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"None. I'm fitter than ever."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"Mrs Moss, do you feel anyone is against you, or do you have special powers?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"The nurses here are spies from the bank. They want to stop my cottage."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds unsettling. Derek, has she been driving, or doing anything risky?","m":[4,13]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Derek: She drove to the supermarket at three in the morning."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Mrs Moss, with no sleep, driving isn't safe right now, so please don't drive. With this kind of illness, the law says you must tell the DVLA.","m":[13]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"My money is my business. And my car."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"It is your money, and you've every right to it. Derek's worried because this is so unlike you. Could we pause big purchases for a few days while we check you're well?","m":[8,12]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"A few days. Then the cottage."},{"t":"3:45","band":"Bedside checks","w":"D","s":"What's today's date, and where we are?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Saturday the seventh of November, and we're in A&E, obviously."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Spot on. Can you squeeze my fingers, hold your arms out, close your eyes, and walk to the curtain for me?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"There! Like a ballerina."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Strength, drift and walking look normal, and you're well oriented.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:15","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Because this is new at 71, I'd like bloods, a urine test, a heart tracing and a brain scan tonight, to rule out an infection, a thyroid problem, steroid effects or a change in the brain.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"I'm not having a scan. There's nothing wrong with my head."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"You know yourself best. The scan is quick and painless, and if it's clear, that's one less worry for Derek. Will you let us?","m":[9,8]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Oh, fine. For Derek."},{"t":"4:55","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Derek, tonight could you pause her cards through the bank's app, ask for a fraud flag, and keep the car keys? You can cancel recent orders tomorrow.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Derek: Yes. I'll do it now."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Mrs Moss, I'd like you to stay in hospital tonight, not go home. Will you stay while the tests come back?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"One night. Then I'm off to the coast."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Thank you. She's agreed to stay informally. If she tries to leave and the risk is high, we'd assess her capacity and ask an AMHP for a Mental Health Act assessment. I'll ring the on-call consultant now.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Derek: So she's not going to the psych ward tonight?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Not until the physical tests are back. I'll ask the A&E team for FBC, U&E, liver, calcium, glucose, thyroid, B12, folate, CRP, urine culture, an ECG and a CT head. Then we decide the right ward.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Derek, can you tell me what happens next, so I know I've been clear?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"Derek: Tests and a scan tonight, she stays, the steroids get looked at, I freeze the cards and keep the keys."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If she becomes drowsy, confused or tries to leave, tell the nurse straight away. I'll come back once the results are in.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Will it be long? I've got calls to make!"},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"A few hours. Try to rest. Derek's staying with you.","m":[14,1]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, includes the husband and stays warm and non-judgemental","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Confirms mania: sleep, speech, grandiosity, spending and duration","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past depressions and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about psychotic symptoms and risky behaviour","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Reviews medicines, including steroids, and alcohol or drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about headache, falls, weakness and speech change","d":"Content"},{"c":"Does a brief cognitive screen and bedside neurological check","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates the husband's fear or her anger","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains why a physical cause must be excluded at 71","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Requests the organic screen tonight: bloods, urine, ECG and CT head","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Decides setting and legal route and informs the on-call consultant","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees with the husband how to protect her money tonight","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises no driving and that she must tell the DVLA","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding and closes with safety-netting and next contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Confirms mania, finds the steroid trigger, insists on an organic work-up including CT head, sets a lawful setting and protects her money and safety tonight.","borderline":"Recognises mania but the organic screen is incomplete, or the legal route and money plan are vague.","fail":"Sends her straight to a psychiatric ward without physical work-up, misses the steroids, or discharges her home."}},"c-3-5-3":{"setup":"You are visiting Mrs Whitlock, 78, at home with the older adults CMHT. She has rung the police four times about her neighbours and has taped her vents shut. Assess her symptoms, look for an organic cause, assess risk and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Edna Whitlock, 78, the patient, a retired school secretary who lives alone","open":"Sit where you like. Speak up, dear, my ears aren't what they were.","manner":"Polite, well dressed and guarded; insists she is not mad. Brightens once she can hear properly. Tearful when she feels disbelieved; warms if validated, but resists tablets.","free":["The neighbours pump gas through the bedroom wall at night and come in to move her things","It started about six months ago, after a new couple moved in next door","She has rung the police four times and they did nothing","She has taped the air vents shut to keep the gas out"],"ifasked":["She smells something sweet and sickly in the bedroom and wakes with headaches","At night in bed she hears the neighbours laughing about her through the wall","She uses the gas fire every evening","She has thought about giving them a piece of her mind but has never gone round","She forgot one GP appointment; no falls, no visual hallucinations, no shaking","She stopped wearing her hearing aids a year ago because they whistled","Her son lives in Canada and she sees almost nobody","Sleep is poor but she has no thoughts of ending her life"],"worries":["You think I'm making it up, don't you?","If I take the tape off, their gas will get in","I'm not taking pills that turn me into a zombie","Will you tell the police to make them stop?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful and says nobody believes her if the doctor argues or doubts her; settles if her fear is validated","Only mentions the voices and the smell if asked directly","Agrees to the gas check, bloods and hearing referral if framed around sleep, stress and safety","Will not agree to medicine today but will think about it if told it is a tiny dose she can stop"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Whitlock. I'm Dr Patel, one of the doctors from the older people's mental health team. Your GP asked me to call. May I sit here, facing the window, so you can see my face?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Sit where you like. Speak up, dear, my ears aren't what they were."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Of course. I've brought a small amplifier. If you pop these headphones on, I'll speak into this. Can you hear me clearly now?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Oh. Yes, that's much better. I haven't heard that well in ages."},{"t":"0:35","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Good. Tell me if I go too fast. Your GP said things have been very hard with the neighbours. Can you tell me what's been happening?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"They pump gas through the wall at night. And they come in when I'm asleep and move my things. I'm not mad, Doctor. I just want it to stop."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening, and exhausting, night after night. I'm not here to argue with you. I want to understand it properly. When did it start?","m":[9,2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"About six months ago. After the new couple moved in next door."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"How do you know it's gas? What tells you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"I can smell it. Sweet and sickly. And I wake up with headaches."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Is that smell anywhere else? Outside, on your clothes, or in your food?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Only in here. Mostly the bedroom."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"How sure are you that it's the neighbours? Completely sure, or could there be another explanation?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Who else would it be? Forty years I've lived here. Never any trouble till they came."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Has anything else made you think it's them?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"I hear them. Through the wall. Laughing about me. 'She's still up.'"},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"That must be horrible, lying there listening. Thank you for telling me. Does it happen when it's quiet, with the TV off? Do you ever hear voices when you're out?","m":[9,4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Only at night, in bed. Never when I'm out."},{"t":"2:05","band":"Organic and mood screen","w":"D","s":"Do you ever see things that worry you? People or animals in the room that then vanish?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"No. I'm not seeing things."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"How's your memory? Do you lose things, or forget appointments? Can you tell me today's date?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"I don't lose things, they move them. It's Tuesday, the fourteenth. Well, I missed the GP once. Everyone does."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Are there days you feel muddled, then clearer again? Any falls, stiffness or shaking? Any new medicines or alcohol?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"No falls. I walk to the shop every day. Just my blood pressure tablet, and a sherry at Christmas."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been through all this? Have you felt low, or that life isn't worth living?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"I'm worn out. I don't sleep. But no, I've never thought of ending it."},{"t":"2:55","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"What have you done to protect yourself?","m":[3,8]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Rung the police four times. Useless. And I taped the vents so the gas can't get in."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"You blocked them to keep yourself safe, I understand that. Do you use that gas fire?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Every evening. It's November, I'm not freezing."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll come back to that. Have you thought about doing anything to the neighbours, going round or getting your own back?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"I've thought about giving them a piece of my mind. But I'm 78. I wouldn't hurt anybody. I don't even open the door to them."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"And are you eating and drinking normally? Some people worry their food has been tampered with.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"I eat. I buy it myself. Tea all day."},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Why am I the one seeing a doctor? You think I'm making it up, don't you? Nobody believes me. My son's in Canada. I've nobody."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"I don't think you're making anything up. Something very distressing is happening, and you've coped alone for months. That's a lot to carry. Seeing me doesn't mean you're to blame.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Well. You're the first one who's listened properly."},{"t":"4:10","band":"Explain and plan","w":"D","s":"Can I tell you what worries me? A gas fire with the vents taped can build up carbon monoxide. You can't smell it, and it causes morning headaches. Could a Gas Safe engineer check it, and fit a carbon monoxide alarm?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"But if I take the tape off, their gas gets in."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"I understand that fear. Until the engineer comes, could you leave the gas fire off and use an electric heater? The fire service can also do a free safety visit.","m":[8,13]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"I suppose. As long as he's not from the council."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Next, I'd like your GP to do blood and urine tests. Thyroid, vitamins or infection can make sleep and stress much worse. And your hearing: getting aids that work may help you feel more in control at night. Shall I ask for an audiology referral?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Blood tests I don't mind. The hearing aids whistled, that's why I stopped."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"They can be refitted. Lastly, a small tablet at night could help you sleep and ease the fear. A tiny dose, far lower than younger people take. We'd check a heart tracing first, watch for stiffness or dizziness, and stop if you don't like it.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"I'm not taking pills. They'll turn me into a zombie."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"That's a fair worry. At this dose most people don't feel drugged. You needn't decide today. Would you think about it?","m":[9,11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"I'll think about it. I'm not promising."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"That's fine. Just to check I've explained things clearly, what will you do about the gas fire before the engineer comes?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Leave it off. Use the electric heater. Let the gas man look."},{"t":"5:55","band":"Safety net and close","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If you get a headache, sickness or dizziness indoors, open a window, go outside and ring 999. And if you ever feel you must confront the neighbours, ring our team first. Here's the number, written large.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Good, I can read that."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"So: a gas check and alarm, bloods and urine, a hearing referral, and you'll think about the tablet. Next time I'll do a memory test with the amplifier. I'll write to your GP this week and see you within a week. Next Tuesday?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Tuesday's fine. Will you tell the police to make them stop?"},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"I can't control what the police do, and I wish I could make it stop for you. What I can do is help with sleep, stress and hearing. See you Tuesday.","m":[9,14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you, Doctor. Mind the step on your way out."}],"marks":[{"c":"Faces her in good light, uses an amplifier and checks she can hear","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Invites her story with open questions and neither argues with nor endorses the belief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Maps conviction, evidence and what she has done because of the belief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about voices and smells, and when they occur","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens memory, orientation, fluctuation, visual hallucinations, falls, parkinsonism, medicines and alcohol","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about low mood and thoughts that life is not worth living","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about plans or past action against the neighbours","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Identifies the carbon monoxide risk from taped vents and checks eating and drinking","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her fear, isolation and frustration without colluding","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Frames gas check, bloods and hearing referral around her safety, sleep and stress","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers very low-dose medicine with ECG, side-effect checks and freedom to stop","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks she has understood the gas advice in her own words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets: carbon monoxide symptoms, 999, and team number before confronting neighbours","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises the plan, mentions GP letter, and books review within a week","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Hears her out with aids in place, screens organic causes, mood and risk, spots the gas danger and agrees at least one acceptable step.","borderline":"Covers the belief and risk but misses smells, cognition or the gas fire, or offers medicine without framing it around her goals.","fail":"Argues the belief is false, ignores her deafness, misses the carbon monoxide risk, or jumps to detention."}},"c-3-5-4":{"setup":"You are in the memory clinic with Mr Clarke, 72, and his wife. He has 9 months of worry, door checking and forgetfulness, and takes diazepam most days. Separate anxiety, depression and early dementia, and agree a safe plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Raymond Clarke, 72, the patient, retired bus engineer; his wife Mrs Pat Clarke, 70, joins halfway","open":"Fine by me. Pat can wait outside a minute.","manner":"Restless, fidgeting, speaks quickly; fears he has his father's Alzheimer's. Clings to the diazepam at first; relaxes when the plan is slow. Pat is tired, tearful and wants a clear answer.","free":["He forgets what Pat tells him within minutes","His father had Alzheimer's","The worry started about nine months ago after a heart scare that turned out to be nothing","He checks the doors four or five times before bed"],"ifasked":["He worries about money, Pat's health and burglars, and fears something bad will be his fault","Sleep is poor, he has stopped going to bowls and feels low some days; no suicidal thoughts","Diazepam from the out-of-hours doctor four weeks ago: 2 mg tablets, one or two most days, sometimes 5 mg at night","He feels woozy in the mornings and nearly fell on the stairs a fortnight ago","About 6 units a week, and some nights a whisky with the tablet","He still drives to the shops and takes Pat to hospital","Pat: he has never got lost and still manages the bills"],"worries":["This is how it started with my father, isn't it?","The diazepam is the only thing that helps; you're not taking it away","I'm too old for talking therapy","Pat: why not just do a brain scan?"],"rules":["Mentions the whisky only if alcohol is asked about","Becomes defensive if the diazepam is stopped suddenly; agrees to a slow taper","Pat becomes tearful about her own exhaustion; settles if acknowledged","Both accept the plan if the doctor is honest about uncertainty"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Good morning, Mr Clarke, Mrs Clarke. I'm Dr Okafor, a psychiatrist in the memory clinic. I'd like to start with you on your own, Mr Clarke, then hear from your wife with your permission. Is that all right?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Fine by me. Pat can wait outside a minute."},{"t":"0:20","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"Thank you. What worries you most at the moment?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"My memory. Pat tells me something and five minutes later it's gone. My dad had Alzheimer's. This is how it started with him, isn't it?"},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"That's an understandable fear after what you saw with your dad. I'll be honest about what we find. Can I ask about the worry first? When did it start?","m":[8,2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Last winter. I had a heart scare, turned out to be nothing. Since then I can't switch off."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"What sort of things do you worry about?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Everything. Money, Pat's health, burglars. I check the doors four or five times before bed. Then lie awake checking them in my head."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. What goes through your mind when you check?","m":[8,2]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"That if I don't, something bad will happen and it'll be my fault."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"How's your sleep and mood? Have you lost interest in things you used to enjoy?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Sleep's terrible. I've stopped going to bowls. Can't concentrate. I feel low some days."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"When you feel low, have you ever felt that life isn't worth living, or thought of ending it?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I've got Pat and the grandchildren."},{"t":"1:45","band":"Diazepam, alcohol and safety","w":"D","s":"Thank you, I ask everyone that. Now the diazepam. Who prescribed it, and how much do you take?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"The out-of-hours doctor, last month, after a bad night. Two milligram tablets, one or two most days. Sometimes a five at night."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"So 2 to 5 milligrams most days for about four weeks. Does it make you drowsy or unsteady?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Woozy in the mornings. I nearly went down the stairs a fortnight ago. Caught the rail."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"How much alcohol do you drink over a week?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"A few pints at the weekend. Some nights I have a whisky with the tablet to help me sleep."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; that matters. Alcohol with diazepam worsens balance, memory and breathing. Are you still driving?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Yes. To the shops, and Pat to the hospital."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Please don't drive if you feel drowsy on diazepam; that's an offence even on prescription. Can I ask your wife in now?","m":[6,13]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"2:55","band":"Her account","w":"D","s":"Mrs Clarke, thank you for waiting. With Raymond's agreement, I'd like your view. What have you noticed?","m":[1,7]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Wife: He's not himself. Forgets what I've said, asks the same thing twice. And the checking. It's wearing us both out."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Has he got lost anywhere, or had trouble with money, cooking or the TV remote?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Wife: No. He still does the bills, better than me. Never got lost. And he's been dozier since that tablet."},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Wife: I've not slept properly in months either. I just want to know if it's dementia."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you've both been carrying a lot, and you've kept everything going. That's hard.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:45","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"The memory test this morning scored 82 out of 100. Most points went on attention and recall. That's what worry does: a busy mind doesn't store new things well. Diazepam does the same. His daily skills are intact. So one score today isn't a diagnosis.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"So it might not be Alzheimer's?"},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"It might not, and I can't rule it out yet either. The fairest test is to treat the anxiety, bring the diazepam down, and repeat the memory test in about six months. If it improves, that points away from dementia.","m":[9,14]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Wife: Why not just do a brain scan and tell us?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting a clear answer. A scan may help later if things decline, but it can't tell us how much is worry. Treating first gives a clearer picture.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:40","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"For the diazepam, I won't stop it suddenly; that can be dangerous. I'll write to your GP with a plan to cut it by small steps every two weeks, one prescriber, short scripts.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"The diazepam is the only thing that helps. You're not taking it away."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"I can see it gives you relief, and nothing goes overnight. We go at a pace you agree, while other treatment starts working. Does that sound fair?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"If it's slow, all right."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"For the anxiety, I'd suggest sertraline, 25 mg once a day from Monday, then 50 after a week. You might feel a bit jittery or sick at first; it settles. It takes four to six weeks. Your GP will check bloods, including your salt level.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"And this talking business? I'm too old for therapy."},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Lots of people think that, but CBT works well at your age, often better than tablets. You can refer yourself to NHS Talking Therapies online this week.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"I'll give it a go."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what you'll do over the next two weeks?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Start the new tablet Monday. Cut the diazepam slowly with the GP. Ring the talking people. No whisky with the tablet. No driving if I'm woozy."},{"t":"6:10","band":"Safety net and close","w":"D","s":"Spot on. If you feel much worse, have thoughts of self-harm or have a fall, ring your GP or 111, or 999 in an emergency. Mrs Clarke, the council can do a carer's assessment for you too.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Wife: I didn't know I could ask for that."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"So: bloods with the GP, sertraline from Monday, a written diazepam taper, talking therapy, and a repeat memory test in six months. I'll write to your GP within two weeks with a copy to you. Any questions?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. That's clearer than I expected. Thank you, Doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and gains consent to see him alone and to hear from his wife","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores worry content, onset, checking and what drives it","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for depression: sleep, interest, concentration, mood","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Records the diazepam fully: prescriber, dose, frequency, duration and effects","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol, falls and driving","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gets his wife's account of memory and daily function: getting lost, money, cooking","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his fear of dementia and his wife's exhaustion","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains honestly why one score is not a diagnosis and how anxiety and diazepam lower it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a slow written diazepam taper with the GP, not a sudden stop","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers sertraline with start date and side effects, plus NHS Talking Therapies","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking him to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets: no driving if drowsy, worsening mood, self-harm thoughts or falls","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes with bloods, repeat memory test in six months, GP letter and carer's assessment","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Takes both accounts, records the diazepam and alcohol fully, explains uncertainty honestly and agrees a slow taper, SSRI, therapy and retest.","borderline":"Covers anxiety and memory but misses alcohol, falls or driving, or leaves the taper vague.","fail":"Labels dementia on one score, ignores or suddenly stops the diazepam, or speaks only to the wife."}},"c-3-6-1":{"setup":"You are the liaison psychiatrist on a medical ward. Mrs Hughes, 84, with moderate Alzheimer's, wants to go home to her cat; the team favour a care home and found unpaid bills. Assess her capacity for each decision and advise the nurse in charge.","role":{"who":"Mrs Dorothy Hughes, 84, the patient, widow; the nurse in charge, Sister Bell, joins at the end","open":"I've got them in. If this is about a home, I'm going home to my cat.","manner":"Bright, chatty and determined, but loses track of detail. Defensive about the fall. Becomes tearful and feels ganged up on; softens when her wishes are heard.","free":["She is going home to her cat, Smudge, whom the neighbour is feeding","She came in with a water infection","She has lived in her house for fifty years and her husband died there","The boy next door does her shopping with her bank card"],"ifasked":["She says she 'sat down' on the floor; she lay there some hours in the dark","She cannot say how she would get help after a night-time fall","She does not know her weekly pension and guesses about a hundred pounds","The neighbour's boy knows her PIN and she never checks receipts","Bills go in a drawer because 'they make mistakes'","She would lend five thousand pounds to anyone who needed it","She signed 'something at the solicitor's' years ago; her son Michael knows"],"worries":["Everyone's ganging up on me and wants rid of me","So you're putting me in a home","Who will look after Smudge?"],"rules":["Repeats the options correctly but always answers fall questions with 'I'd get up'","Becomes tearful when she feels outnumbered; settles when asked what she wants everyone to know","Nurse in charge asks practical questions about consent, IMCA, money and DoLS"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Good morning, Mrs Hughes. I'm Dr Ahmed, a psychiatrist. The team asked me to talk with you about going home. Have you got your hearing aid and glasses? Is this quiet room all right?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"I've got them in. If this is about a home, I'm going home to my cat."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Your cat clearly matters a great deal, and I want to hear about home. There are no right or wrong answers. What's your cat called?","m":[1,6]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Smudge. Next door's feeding him. He'll think I've left him."},{"t":"0:35","band":"Check for delirium","w":"D","s":"Before we start, can you tell me where we are, and what month it is?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"The hospital. The General. It's October. No, November. Cold, anyway."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"Good. Could you say the months backwards for me, from December?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"December, November, October, September, August, July, June."},{"t":"0:55","band":"Where to live","w":"D","s":"Well done. Can you tell me why you came into hospital?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"A water infection. And I sat down on the floor. I never fell."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"Okay. The ward says you were on the floor some hours before your neighbour found you. Does that fit with what you remember?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Maybe. It was dark. My legs went."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Let me explain the two options. One: home, with carers four times a day to help you wash, dress and eat, and a pendant alarm to call for help. At night you'd be alone. Two: a care home, with staff there day and night. Is that clear so far?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Carers four times a day, or a home. I'm not daft."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Not at all. What would be good and bad about each, for you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"At home I've got Smudge and my things. In a home they'd make me eat at set times and I'd never see my cat. Carers are strangers, but I'd put up with them."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"That's very clear. What might happen if you went home and fell again at night?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"I wouldn't fall. I'm careful."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Suppose you did, at two in the morning, and the carers weren't due till eight. What would you do?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"I'd get up. I always get up."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Last time you couldn't get up for hours. How would you get help if your legs went again?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"I'd manage. I don't need an alarm. I'd just get up."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Can you remind me what the two options were?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Home with some carers, or a home. Everyone's ganging up on me. My son, the nurses, now you. You all want rid of me."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening, feeling outnumbered. Nobody wants rid of you. My job is to make sure your wishes are heard properly. What would you most want everyone to know?","m":[9,6]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"That I've lived in that house fifty years. My husband died there. I want to die there, with Smudge on the bed."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll write that down in your words, so everyone reads it.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:15","band":"Managing money","w":"D","s":"Now a separate question, about money. Who pays your bills at home?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"I do. Well, the boy next door helps. I give him my card for the shopping."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"That's kind of him. Does he know your PIN? Does he bring back change and receipts?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"He knows the number. He's a good lad. I don't bother with receipts."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Roughly how much is your pension each week? And if a gas bill for two hundred pounds came, what would you do?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Oh, a hundred pounds? I'd put the bill in the drawer. They make mistakes."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"If someone asked to borrow five thousand pounds, what would you think?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"If they needed it, I'd help. You can't take it with you."},{"t":"4:05","band":"Tell her","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being so patient. You understand the options and you've told me clearly that home and Smudge matter most. I'm less sure how you'd stay safe after a night fall, and money is hard to keep track of. So we'll all meet, with you and Michael, and your wishes will be central.","m":[10,6]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"So you're putting me in a home."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"No decision's been made. Going home with more help will be looked at seriously. Did you ever sign a power of attorney, giving Michael a say?","m":[10,12]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"I signed something at the solicitor's years ago. Ask Michael. And tell them about Smudge."},{"t":"4:45","band":"Advise the team","w":"D","s":"Sister, my findings. She's not delirious today: oriented, months backwards intact, infection treated, aids in. For where to live, she understands and communicates a choice but can't use or weigh a night-time fall, because of her Alzheimer's. So she lacks capacity for that decision today.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Nurse: And money?"},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"Separate assessment. She can't state her income, files bills unopened and would lend five thousand pounds. Again caused by the dementia, so she lacks capacity for her finances. Two decisions, two records, with her words about home quoted.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Her son wants a care home. Can't he just sign?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Only with a registered health and welfare LPA. Please ask him and check the Office of the Public Guardian register. Otherwise it's a best interests decision. Refer to social work for a Care Act assessment and book a meeting with her, Michael, OT and us. Home with carers and an alarm is the less restrictive option.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Do we need an advocate?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"An IMCA is needed for a long-term move if there's no family or friend appropriate to consult. Michael can be consulted, so not now. For money, without a financial LPA, he can apply to the Court of Protection for deputyship; I'll do the COP3. For benefits only, DWP appointeeship.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Anything else?"},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"The neighbour has her card and PIN and bills are unpaid, so please ask social work to raise a safeguarding concern today. If she goes to a care home and isn't free to leave, the home applies for DoLS. If she changes, call me to reassess. Can you read back the actions?","m":[13,14]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Check the LPA, social work referral, best interests meeting, safeguarding concern today. You'll do the COP3 if needed."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"That's it. I'll write both assessments in the notes now and review her on Friday.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Thanks. I'll ring Michael this afternoon."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces purpose, checks hearing aid and glasses, uses a quiet room","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Excludes delirium with orientation, an attention test and ward information, and states this","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains both residence options and their risks in plain words before testing","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tests understanding and retention of the residence information","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests using and weighing with a concrete night-time fall scenario","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits and records her own words about home, her husband and her cat","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Assesses finances separately: income, handling a bill, a lending scenario","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores who holds her card and PIN and the risk of exploitation","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her distress at feeling outnumbered","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Tells her the outcome honestly and that her wishes stay central","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives a separate conclusion for each decision with the causal link to dementia","d":"Content"},{"c":"Advises on LPA check, best interests meeting, IMCA test, deputyship or appointeeship","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets: safeguarding concern today, DoLS if care home, reassess if she changes","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks the nurse's understanding, agrees documentation and a review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Excludes delirium, supports her, runs two separate functional tests with a causal link, quotes her wishes and gives the team the correct legal steps.","borderline":"Reaches sensible conclusions but merges the two decisions, skips the delirium check or gives vague legal advice.","fail":"Concludes incapacity from the diagnosis, lets the son consent without a valid LPA, or misses the possible financial abuse."}},"c-3-6-2":{"setup":"In the memory clinic you have just told Mr Dawson, 76, he has Alzheimer's disease. His daughter is with him. As he stands, he says he'll drive himself home. Explain the effect on driving, his duty to tell the DVLA, and what you will do.","role":{"who":"Mr Frank Dawson, 76, the patient, retired joiner and widower; his daughter Karen, 48, is with him","open":"Driving? I'll drive myself home, I've been driving for fifty-five years.","manner":"Proud and brisk, then angry when driving is raised. Bargains, then becomes sad about losing freedom. Accepts advice when treated with respect. Karen is protective and worried.","free":["He has driven for fifty-five years without an accident","He intends to drive himself home today","The car takes him to the club on Tuesdays, shopping and church"],"ifasked":["Last month he went the wrong way round a roundabout after new lane markings","Karen: he got lost coming back from the garden centre and rang her from a lay-by","He holds only a car licence, no lorry or bus entitlement","His wife died two years ago and the club is his main contact","His friend Bill from the club lives nearby and drives","He did not know dementia has to be reported to the DVLA"],"worries":["You can't stop me; you're taking my licence away","What if I only drive to the shops?","Will I get my licence back?","Karen: can't you just leave it out? It'll break him"],"rules":["Denies problems until Karen mentions the roundabout, then calls it a one-off","Becomes angry if told he has lost his licence; calms when told the DVLA decides","Voice cracks about losing friends; settles if the loss is acknowledged","Agrees Karen drives him home if asked directly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Stop and open","w":"D","s":"Mr Dawson, before you go, could you sit back down for a few minutes? There's something important about driving we need to talk through.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Driving? I'll drive myself home, I've been driving for fifty-five years."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That's a long time, and I can see it matters. Today's news is a lot to take in as well. How have you found driving lately?","m":[8,2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Fine. Never had an accident."},{"t":"0:35","band":"Driving history","w":"D","s":"Has anything happened on the road that worried you or anyone else? Near misses, scrapes, getting lost?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Daughter: Dad, the roundabout. Last month."},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"That was a one-off. Everyone makes mistakes."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what happened?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"New lane markings. I went the wrong way round for a bit. Nobody hit me."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"That must have been unnerving. I'm not judging your driving. Have there been other times you've lost your way?","m":[8,2]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Daughter: He got lost coming back from the garden centre. Rang me from a lay-by."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Do you hold a lorry or bus licence, or just a car licence?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Just the car."},{"t":"1:35","band":"The advice","w":"D","s":"Mr Dawson, I need to be straight with you. With today's diagnosis and the roundabout, I don't think you should drive home today.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Fifty-five years and never had an accident. You can't stop me. You're taking my licence away."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"I'm not taking your licence. The DVLA decides that, not me. But the law says anyone with dementia must tell them, and until they decide, my advice is not to drive at all.","m":[4,5,3]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Tell them? Then what?"},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"You tell them online, or on a form called CG1. They'll ask me and your GP for reports, and sometimes arrange a driving assessment. Some people with early Alzheimer's keep a licence with yearly reviews. Others are told to stop.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"And if I don't tell them?"},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"You could be fined up to a thousand pounds, and your insurance may not cover you in a crash.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Nobody needs to know."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"I have to be honest about my part. If you kept driving without telling them, I'd have to inform the DVLA's medical adviser myself, for your safety and others'. I'd tell you before, and write to you after. I'd much rather you did it yourself.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Daughter: Can't you just leave it out? It'll break him."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"I can see you're protecting him. I can't leave it out; his safety and other people's depend on it. I'll help him through it, and he keeps the chance to tell them himself.","m":[8,7]},{"t":"3:35","band":"Loss and alternatives","w":"P","s":"Without the car I'm stuck. Since my wife went, the club's all I've got. I'll never see my friends."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Losing the car feels like losing your freedom, and coming on top of today's news, that's a real loss. What does the car let you do in a normal week?","m":[8,13]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Club on Tuesdays. Shopping. Church on Sunday."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"Let's plan how you keep doing those. The council does an older person's bus pass, there's community transport that collects from home, and lifts. Who at the club lives near you?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Bill. He drives. I suppose he'd take me. What if I only drive to the shops? It's two miles."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"I understand; it's your independence. Short trips still mean junctions and roundabouts. Until the DVLA decides, please don't drive at all. Could Karen help with a weekly shop?","m":[3,9]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Daughter: I can do Saturdays."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"And if the DVLA asks for a practical check, there are accredited driving assessment centres. It's the fairest test there is.","m":[9,5]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Will I get my licence back?"},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"I can't promise. The DVLA decides after reports and perhaps an assessment. Telling them yourself puts you in the strongest position.","m":[5]},{"t":"5:10","band":"Today and close","w":"D","s":"How will you get home today? Karen, could you drive your dad?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Daughter: Yes. I'll take him in his car and get a taxi back for mine."},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Fine. Give her the keys then."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you. That's a hard thing to do. Can I check I've been clear? What happens about driving now?","m":[8,11]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"I don't drive. I tell the DVLA, online or that form. They decide. If I keep driving, you tell them."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. Karen, could you help him with the CG1 this week?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Daughter: We'll do it at the weekend."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"Losing driving can make people low. If you feel down or worried, ring the memory clinic or your GP. I'll also link you with an Alzheimer's Society adviser.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"I suppose that's something."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"I'll record today's advice and the date in your notes and in the letter to you and your GP. I'll phone in two weeks to check the DVLA has been told and see how you're managing. All right?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"I don't like it. But all right."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you for hearing me out, Mr Dawson. Speak in two weeks.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Come on then, Karen. You're driving."}],"marks":[{"c":"Stops him kindly before he leaves and makes time to talk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about recent driving, near misses, getting lost and licence type","d":"Content"},{"c":"States plainly that he should not drive home today or until the DVLA decides","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains that dementia must be notified to the DVLA, online or by form CG1","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the DVLA decides, with possible outcomes including review or driving assessment","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States the consequences of not notifying: fine and invalid insurance","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Says honestly that he will inform the DVLA if he keeps driving, telling him before and after","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Responds to anger, denial and loss with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers alternatives: bus pass, community transport, lifts, driving assessment centre","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees how he gets home today","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding of the plan in his own words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Records advice and date, plans a 2-week call and safety-nets low mood","d":"Close"},{"c":"Explores what driving means to him and the effect on mood and contact","d":"Content"}],"global":{"pass":"Stops him driving home, explains his DVLA duty and that the DVLA decides, states the doctor's role, records it and plans follow-up with alternatives.","borderline":"Gives correct advice but misses the plan for today, the follow-up call, or what the doctor will do if he keeps driving.","fail":"Lets him drive home, says the doctor removes his licence, gives wrong DVLA rules, or agrees to keep it secret."}},"c-3-6-3":{"setup":"On a home visit you find Mrs Ellis, 81, with vascular dementia, in a cold flat with an empty fridge. Her carer says her grandson, 26, holds her bank card and PIN. Assess the concern, keep her safe and make a safeguarding referral.","role":{"who":"Mrs Margaret Ellis, 81, the patient, widow; later the duty social worker by phone","open":"Course, love. Jo's a treasure.","manner":"Warm, a little vague, loyal to her grandson and quick to excuse him. Grows quiet and tearful when asked about his temper. Reluctant about social workers but agrees if she is not blamed.","free":["Her grandson Liam moved in during the summer after losing his flat","Liam has her bank card; 'he needs it more than me'","The heating is off and she is wearing a cardigan","Jo the carer makes her toast"],"ifasked":["The heating has been off one or two weeks","She wrote her PIN down for Liam","Liam says there is nothing left at the end of the week","He shouts and bangs doors if she asks where the money has gone; he has not hit her","Sometimes she is frightened","She does not know her pension amount","She wants Liam to stay, but wants heating and her own money for the hairdresser"],"worries":["If you get him in trouble he'll leave and I'll have nobody","I don't want social workers poking around","Will the police get involved?","I'm such a burden on everyone"],"rules":["Minimises at first; discloses the shouting only if asked how Liam reacts","Becomes tearful about being alone; settles if her love for Liam is acknowledged","Agrees to a social worker visit if told it will go at her pace","The duty social worker asks about immediate risk and LPA, and gives reference SG-4417"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Ellis, it's Dr Shah from the memory team. Could we chat on our own for a few minutes? Jo, would you mind putting the kettle on in the kitchen?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Course, love. Jo's a treasure."},{"t":"0:20","band":"Basic needs","w":"D","s":"Thank you. It's chilly in here. Is the heating on?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"No. It's broken, or the money's run out on it. I've got my cardigan."},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"How long has it been off?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"A week. Two maybe."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"And food? The fridge looked quite empty. When did you last have a hot meal?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Jo makes me toast. Liam was meant to do a shop."},{"t":"0:55","band":"Money and the grandson","w":"D","s":"Liam's your grandson? Tell me about him.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"He moved in in the summer. Lost his flat. He's a good boy. He keeps me company."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"How are you managing for money? Who looks after your bank card and pension?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Liam has my card. He needs it more than me. He's had a hard time."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Does he know your PIN? Does he bring back shopping or change?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"I wrote the number down for him. He says there's nothing left at the end of the week."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Has anyone asked for money, or taken money you didn't want to give?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"He asks. I say yes. He gets cross if I ask where it's gone."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"What happens when he gets cross?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"He shouts. Bangs the door. He's never hit me."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Do you feel safe at home? Does he ever make you frightened?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Sometimes. But if you get him in trouble he'll leave, and I'll have nobody."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"That's a frightening thought; he's company, and you love him. You haven't done anything wrong. Our aim isn't to punish anyone. It's to make sure you're warm, fed and have what you need.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"I'm such a burden on everyone."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"You're not a burden. You've every right to a warm home and proper meals.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","band":"Capacity for this decision","w":"D","s":"Can I ask a bit more about the money? Roughly how much is your pension each week?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"I don't know. Liam knows."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"If the money goes on Liam and there's none for heating or food, what happens to you?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"I'd be cold, I suppose. But he needs it."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Knowing that, would you still want him to have your card?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"I don't want to upset him."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"What would you like to happen?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"I want him to stay. But I want my heating back. And a bit of my own money for the hairdresser."},{"t":"3:40","band":"Explain and agree","w":"D","s":"That's very clear, thank you. I'm worried you're at risk, so I do need to ask social services to help. It's called a safeguarding referral. I can't keep it secret, but I won't go to Liam about it.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"I don't want social workers poking around."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"I hear you, and I'd feel the same. They'll talk with you first and go at your pace, with your wishes at the centre. Would that be all right?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"If they're nice about it. Will the police get involved?"},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Not necessarily. A social worker looks at it with you first. If a crime is suspected, the police may be told, but your wishes count and we'll keep you informed.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"For tonight, I'd like to get the heating checked, some food in, and an extra carer visit this evening. Is that all right?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"That would be lovely."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what's going to happen next, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"Someone sorts the heating and food. A social worker visits. You won't tell Liam I said anything."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"That's right. If you ever feel unsafe, ring 999. Jo has our team number too.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:15","band":"Referral call","w":"D","s":"Hello, Dr Shah, older adults CMHT. I'm raising a safeguarding concern about possible financial abuse. 81-year-old woman, vascular dementia. Grandson, 26, holds her card and PIN; heating off up to two weeks, little food, he shouts when she asks. She can't weigh giving him her money and is frightened. Please start a Section 42 enquiry today.","m":[10,6]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Social worker: Thanks. Any immediate danger tonight?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"No violence reported. She wants him to stay. I've asked for an urgent heating check and an extra carer visit tonight. Can you arrange emergency food?","m":[9]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Social worker: I'll get a food delivery today and meals from tomorrow. Does anyone hold an LPA?"},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Not known. I'll search the Office of the Public Guardian register today; if he's an attorney, we report it to them. Could your team contact her bank's vulnerable customer team about the card?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Social worker: Yes, we'll do that. Your reference is SG-4417."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"SG-4417, thank you. I'll tell my consultant and our safeguarding lead, record her words, and visit within a week. Please invite me to the strategy meeting.","m":[10,13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Social worker: Will do. I'll call her tomorrow."}],"marks":[{"c":"Asks to speak to her alone and introduces self gently","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks open questions about her grandson, her card, PIN and pension","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about heating, food and hot meals","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about pressure, anger, fear and feeling safe","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her love for her grandson and fear of being alone, without blame","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Assesses capacity for the specific decision about giving him her money","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what she wants to happen and keeps her wishes central","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains honestly that a safeguarding referral is needed, without promising secrecy","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Arranges heating, food and extra carer support for tonight","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a clear SBAR referral for a Section 42 enquiry and records the reference","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees OPG register check, bank contact and the police role","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding of what happens next","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets, informs consultant and safeguarding lead, and plans review within a week","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Sees her alone, records her words, assesses decision-specific capacity and coercion, meets tonight's needs and raises a same-day safeguarding concern with reference.","borderline":"Raises the concern but misses capacity, her wishes, or the plan for food and heating tonight.","fail":"Confronts the grandson, promises secrecy, accepts her minimising without action, or delays the referral."}},"c-3-6-4":{"setup":"In the ward family room, Mrs Price, 77, wife of Albert, 79, who has Alzheimer's and needs help with all care, says she can't cope at home. Explain what her LPA allows, how the care home decision is made, and start advance care planning.","role":{"who":"Mrs Irene Price, 77, his wife and attorney","open":"I have his power of attorney, so can I just decide he goes into a home? And what happens if he gets pneumonia?","manner":"Composed and practical at first, then tearful with guilt about a promise. Exhausted. Grows calmer and more engaged once she understands her role.","free":["She holds his power of attorney and wants to know if she can decide on a care home","She is worried about what happens if he gets pneumonia","She is 77 and can no longer lift him"],"ifasked":["They made both LPAs at a solicitor's five years ago and they were registered; copies are at home","She does not remember whether the life-sustaining treatment section was completed","He made her promise never to put him in a home","He loved chapel, his garden and brass bands, and hated fuss","He hated hospital and was very confused after his hip operation","He never wrote an advance decision","His brother wants him kept at home"],"worries":["I promised I'd never put him in a home","Will they just let him die if he gets pneumonia?","Can I insist he's resuscitated?","Who pays for the care home?"],"rules":["Cries when the promise comes up; settles if her love and exhaustion are acknowledged","Asks about resuscitation after the pneumonia discussion","Accepts limits on her powers if reasons are explained kindly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mrs Price, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the ward doctors. Thanks for waiting. I gather you have some important questions about Albert. What would help most to cover?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"I have his power of attorney, so can I just decide he goes into a home? And what happens if he gets pneumonia?"},{"t":"0:25","band":"The LPA","w":"D","s":"Both really important. Can I start with the power of attorney? There are two types: one for money and property, one for health and welfare. Which do you have?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Both, I think. We did them at the solicitor's five years ago."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"Were they registered at the Office of the Public Guardian? They can't be used until they are.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Yes, the solicitor sent them off. I've copies at home."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Could you bring in the health and welfare one, or give us the online access code? We'll keep a copy in his notes.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"I can bring it tomorrow."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you. There's a section about life-sustaining treatment. Do you remember whether it gives you power to make those decisions?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"I don't remember. Does it matter?"},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"It matters if we talk about pneumonia. Without that wording, you can't refuse life-sustaining treatment for him, so we'll check it.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","band":"Her role","w":"D","s":"If it's registered, then yes, the care home decision is yours, because Albert can no longer weigh it up himself. The law asks you to decide in his best interests, thinking about what he'd have wanted, not just what's easiest.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Of course I want what's best for him."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"I know you do. What did he say, when he was well, about care homes or hospitals?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"He made me promise I'd never put him in a home. And now I'm going to."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"That promise came from love, and you've cared for him for years. Making sure he's safe and well looked after is keeping faith with him too.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"I'm 77. I can't lift him any more. I'm so tired."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"You've given so much, and you're exhausted. Saying you can't manage alone isn't failing him.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"What mattered most to Albert? Routines, faith, people, music?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Chapel on Sundays. His garden. Brass bands. And he hated fuss."},{"t":"2:55","band":"How the decision is made","w":"D","s":"That helps us find the right place. We'll hold a best interests meeting with you, the social worker, his nurse, the OT and the consultant. We'll share his care needs and record his wishes. You decide with all that in front of you.","m":[7,5]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"His brother says we should keep him at home. Does he get a say?"},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"His brother's views matter and will be heard. As attorney, the decision is yours. If there were serious disagreement, the Court of Protection could decide.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"And who pays for it?"},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"We'll complete an NHS Continuing Healthcare checklist. If his needs are mainly health needs, the NHS pays. Otherwise social care does a financial assessment. And I'd like the council to do a carer's assessment for you.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:55","band":"Planning ahead","w":"D","s":"Can we talk about pneumonia? It's easier to think about now than in a crisis. Did Albert ever write down any treatment he'd refuse?","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"That's called an advance decision: a signed, witnessed document refusing a treatment. It's separate from your power of attorney. As he hasn't made one, his past wishes and your role as attorney guide us.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"If he gets pneumonia, will they just let him die?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Nobody stops caring. As dementia advances, chest infections become more likely. Some people do better treated in the home with antibiotics and comfort first; others go to hospital. What do you think he'd want?","m":[9,5]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"He hated hospital. After his hip he was so confused. He'd want to be comfortable, at peace."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful. We'll write a plan with the consultant, covering hospital transfer, antibiotics and resuscitation, and share it with his GP and the care home.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Can I insist he's resuscitated whatever happens?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting to do everything. CPR is a medical decision; if it wouldn't work, doctors won't offer it, and an attorney can't demand it. But your views and his wishes shape every decision, and we'll explain our reasons.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"I see. I wouldn't want him hurt for nothing."},{"t":"5:35","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"I've given you a lot. Can you tell me in your words what happens next?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"I bring the LPA in. There's a meeting and I decide about the home, thinking of what he'd want. And we plan for if he gets ill."},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. One more thing: in a care home he may not be free to leave, so the home may apply for a Deprivation of Liberty Safeguard. It's a legal protection for him.","m":[12,11]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Lots of paperwork."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"There is, and the social worker will help. Admiral Nurses and Alzheimer's Society carer groups can support you too. If you feel overwhelmed before the meeting, ring the ward any time. I'll book the meeting for next week and confirm the date tomorrow.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Thank you for being so kind. I feel less alone with it."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"You're not alone with it. See you tomorrow, Mrs Price.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Tomorrow, then. I'll bring the papers."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and asks what she most needs to cover","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks which LPA she holds, whether registered, and arranges to see it or check the register","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks whether the LPA covers life-sustaining treatment and why it matters","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains she decides only because he lacks capacity, and must act in his best interests","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Elicits his past wishes, values and beliefs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her guilt about the promise and her exhaustion","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the best interests meeting, others' views and Court of Protection if disputed","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about an advance decision and explains how it differs from an LPA","d":"Content"},{"c":"Starts advance care planning sensitively: hospital, antibiotics, comfort, CPR, shared plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains an attorney cannot demand treatment such as CPR","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Covers funding, CHC checklist, carer's assessment and DoLS","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding in her own words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with support, a contact if overwhelmed, and a meeting date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Confirms a registered health and welfare LPA, explains her best interests role and limits, records his wishes, and starts advance care planning with carer support.","borderline":"Explains the LPA correctly but skips the life-sustaining treatment check, his past wishes or the carer's assessment.","fail":"Tells her she can decide whatever she wants, or that she can demand CPR, or never checks the LPA type and registration."}},"c-4-1-1":{"setup":"You are the psychiatry doctor in the psychological therapies assessment clinic. Leah Morgan, 34, has been referred by her GP for 'psychotherapy' after two antidepressants and guided self-help. Assess her suitability and agree which therapy, if any, to recommend.","role":{"who":"Ms Leah Morgan, 34, woman referred by her GP for psychotherapy","open":"I just want someone to fix me. I keep ending up in the same kind of relationships.","manner":"Polite and a little flat at first, then tearful when talking about being left. Warms up if she feels understood; tests the doctor near the end with 'you'll reject me too'.","free":["Her last relationship ended in March; he called her cold and left","Has had two antidepressants; sertraline 'took the edge off'","Six sessions of guided self-help were worksheets that 'never touched the relationship stuff'","Works in admin; her manager is flexible about appointments"],"ifasked":["Her mother left when she was eight; she learned to keep everyone happy","In each relationship she puts the partner first and drops friends, then feels used, goes quiet, and he leaves","After the break-up she wished she would not wake up; no such thoughts or plans now, never self-harmed","Drinks a glass or two of wine three nights a week; no drugs","Has never heard voices or had highs","Copes by running and ringing her sister; lives with her cat"],"worries":["Which therapy is the best one? Just tell me and I'll do it.","What if I start digging into all this and fall apart?","Can't you just see me yourself?","I suppose you'll reject me too, like everyone else."],"rules":["Becomes tearful when asked what she tells herself after a break-up; settles if validated","Mentions her mother leaving only if asked where the feeling comes from","If promised a start date, later asks for it in writing","If told she has had enough therapy, goes quiet and says 'I knew it'"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Shah, one of the psychiatry doctors in the therapies team. Are you Leah?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. Leah's fine."},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Leah. Today is an assessment, not therapy itself. I'll ask about you, and at the end we'll agree a plan together. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Okay. I just want someone to fix me, honestly."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"One thing first. What you tell me stays within your care team and your GP. The only exception is if I thought you or someone else was at serious risk, and then I'd talk to you first. Does that make sense?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's fine."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"You said you want someone to fix you. That sounds like you've been carrying a lot. What made you ask for help now?","m":[8]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"My last relationship ended in March. Same as all the others. I keep ending up in the same place, and I'm tired of it."},{"t":"1:05","band":"Her goals","w":"D","s":"If therapy worked, what would be different at the end?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"I'd feel less low, I suppose."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"And beyond your mood, what would you want to change?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"I'd stop picking people who leave. Or stop being the one who gets left."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"So the goal is to break that pattern, not just feel better for a while. Have I got that right?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Yes. Exactly that."},{"t":"1:30","band":"The pattern","w":"D","s":"Can we walk through the last relationship, from how it started to how it ended?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"It was great at first. I did everything for him. Cooked, sorted his flat, dropped my friends to fit round him."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"And then what happened?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"I started to feel used. I'd go quiet and sulk. Then he said I was cold, and he left. They always say that."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"When it ends, what do you tell yourself about why?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"(tearful) That I'm too much. Or not enough. That there's something wrong with me and everyone sees it in the end."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful, and lonely. Thank you for telling me. It makes sense you want it to stop.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Sorry. I didn't think I'd cry."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"No need to apologise. It shows how much this matters. Where do you think that feeling first comes from?","m":[8,3]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Mum left when I was eight. Dad did his best, but I learned to keep everyone happy."},{"t":"2:35","band":"What has helped","w":"D","s":"You've had two antidepressants and six sessions of guided self-help. What helped, and what didn't?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Sertraline took the edge off. The self-help was worksheets about thoughts. Fine, but it never touched the relationship stuff."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"That's really useful. It tells me we need something aimed at the pattern, not only the mood.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:00","band":"Safety and suitability","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this. When things are at their worst, do you have thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"After he left I wished I wouldn't wake up. Not now. I've never done anything. I've got my sister, and my cat."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being straight with me. Any thoughts like that today, or any plans?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"How much do you drink in a usual week? And any drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"A glass or two of wine, three nights a week. No drugs."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Have you ever heard voices others can't hear, or had days of feeling very high and full of energy?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"No, never."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Is anything going on that could get in the way of weekly sessions? Work, housing, anything legal?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"No. My manager's flexible."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Therapy can stir things up for a while. When feelings get big, what do you do, and who do you turn to?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"I go for a run, or ring my sister. But what if I start digging into all this and fall apart?"},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"That's a wise question. Before you start, we'll have a plan for hard days between sessions. Your sister and your running are part of that.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:25","band":"Formulation and options","w":"D","s":"Can I tell you how I'm making sense of it? Then you tell me if it fits?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"It looks like three steps. You fear being left, so you put yourself last to keep people close. Then you feel used and pull away. They leave, and it proves the fear. Does that ring true?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"That's... yes. That's every single time."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Because the problem is a pattern with people, I'd recommend cognitive analytic therapy, or CAT. It maps patterns like this one, usually over sixteen to twenty-four weekly sessions.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"So that's the best one? Can't you just see me yourself? I don't want to start again with a stranger."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"I understand; telling your story again is tiring. The alternative is psychodynamic therapy, which looks more at how the past shapes now. The team decides who sees you, so I can't promise a therapist or a date. I'll write it all down so you don't start from scratch.","m":[10,8]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"So I'll just be on a list. I suppose you'll reject me too, like everyone else."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"I can hear how strong that fear is. It's the very pattern we just mapped. You're not being turned away; I'm recommending you for therapy.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"5:50","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"To check I've explained it well, can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"You'll suggest CAT to the team, for the pattern. Maybe psychodynamic instead. And there's a wait."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Exactly. The wait is usually a few months. I'll write to your GP and send you a copy. If things get worse while you wait, see your GP, call NHS 111 and choose the mental health option, or ring Samaritans on 116 123.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Okay. That actually helps."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"I'll take this to the team meeting next week and write to you after. Before you go, we'll write a short safety plan together. Any questions?","m":[13,12]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you. I feel like someone actually listened."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. You did the hard part today. Let's do that safety plan now.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains it is an assessment not therapy, and gives confidentiality limits early","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gets her goal for therapy as a change in pattern, not just relief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Walks through the latest relationship from start to end to find the repeating pattern","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what helped and what did not with antidepressants and guided self-help","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and past self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for alcohol or drug use, psychosis, mania and a current crisis","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks how she copes with strong feelings and who supports her","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her distress and fear of rejection without judging the wish to be 'fixed'","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Shares a three-step pattern formulation in plain words and checks she recognises it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Recommends CAT with a reason and names psychodynamic therapy as alternative, promising no date","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking her to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives the likely wait, a GP letter copied to her, and crisis contacts","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes with the team meeting, written follow-up, a safety plan and a chance for questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Finds the pattern, checks suitability and risk, shares a formulation she recognises and recommends CAT without promising a date.","borderline":"Sound history and risk check, but the formulation is vague or the choice of therapy is not explained.","fail":"Takes a full history and runs out of time, uses jargon, skips risk, or promises a therapy or start date."}},"c-4-1-2":{"setup":"You are a psychiatry trainee ending the 12th of 16 supervised therapy sessions with Daniel Price, 41. He hands you an expensive watch and says he has sent you a friend request. Respond, address his dependency, and keep the relationship safe.","role":{"who":"Mr Daniel Price, 41, man in session 12 of 16 of supervised therapy","open":"This is for you. And I've sent you a friend request, so we can keep in touch after. You're the only person who understands me. I don't know what I'll do when this stops.","manner":"Eager and proud of the gift, then hurt and sharp when it is declined. Softens and becomes tearful if his hurt is named; ends embarrassed but relieved.","free":["The watch cost him a lot; he wants to be remembered and to give something back","Found the doctor on Facebook and sent a friend request","Only four sessions are left and he dreads the end","Feels the doctor is the only person who understands him"],"ifasked":["His father walked out when he was nine; 'everyone leaves in the end'","No thoughts of suicide or self-harm, now or before; dreads empty evenings","His sister Claire keeps inviting him round and he says he is busy","Works as a warehouse supervisor; the team relies on him","Goes to church on Sundays"],"worries":["Please take it. I'll be really hurt if you don't.","So I'm just another case to you?","Is it against the rules? Will you get in trouble?","You're going to write down that I tried to give you a present?"],"rules":["Becomes angry if the refusal sounds like a rule being quoted; settles if his hurt is acknowledged","Mentions his father only if the doctor wonders about past endings or losses","If the watch is accepted, he asks for the doctor's phone number next","Answers the suicide question honestly only if asked directly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Respond to the gift","w":"D","s":"Daniel, thank you. I can see a lot of thought went into this. Can we sit back down for a few minutes and talk about it?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"It's just a thank-you. Open it. It's a proper one, not a cheap one."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I'm really touched that you wanted to show what these sessions have meant. I'm not going to open it, though. I can't accept a gift this valuable.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Why not? I'll be really hurt if you don't. It cost me a lot."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Because taking something like this would change what we have here. This works because it's clear what it is. The thank-you itself, I'm keeping.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"So I'm just another case to you. Real people accept presents."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"It hurts to feel pushed away, especially now, so near the end. You're not just a case. These sessions have mattered to me too.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"(quiet) Doesn't feel like it."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what it felt like when I said no?","m":[6,4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Like being knocked back. Same as always."},{"t":"1:25","band":"What it means","w":"D","s":"What made you want to give it to me today, at session twelve?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Because there's only four left. I wanted you to remember me. I wanted to give something back."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Wanting to be remembered, and to give back. That makes sense. You mentioned a friend request too.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Yeah, I found you on Facebook. So we can keep in touch after. Just now and then."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting to keep a link. I won't accept it, and I can't keep in touch personally once we finish. Online contact blurs what this is, and it could show people you're my patient.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Is it against the rules or something? Will you get in trouble?"},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"There is guidance, yes. But the main reason is you. If this turned into a friendship, the ending would hurt more, not less.","m":[3,5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"It's going to hurt anyway."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Tell me more about that.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"You're the only person who gets me. When this stops I'll have nothing."},{"t":"2:45","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. When you say you'll have nothing, do thoughts of self-harm or wishing you weren't here come into it?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Not like that. I just get this empty feeling. Like falling."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Have you ever had thoughts about ending your life, now or in the past? Any plans?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I just dread the empty evenings."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"What keeps you going on the hard days?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"My sister, Claire. And work. I'm a supervisor; the lads rely on me. And church on Sundays."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Those are real supports. Is there anyone you could see a bit more of as we finish?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Claire keeps asking me round. I always say I'm busy."},{"t":"3:40","band":"Link to the ending","w":"D","s":"Can I share a thought? Earlier in our work you told me your dad walked out when you were nine. I wonder if our ending is touching that old hurt.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"(tearful) Everyone leaves in the end. That's just how it goes."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"That's a painful thing to have learned so young. No wonder this ending feels so big. I'd like it to feel different from that one.","m":[6,5]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"How can it be different? It's still ending."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Because we plan it together, it doesn't just stop. You get to say goodbye properly, and you leave with what you've learned.","m":[5,9]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"What would that look like?"},{"t":"4:30","band":"Plan the ending","w":"D","s":"In the last four sessions we review what's changed, talk openly about the loss, and write a blueprint of what helped. You keep a copy.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"A blueprint. Like a plan for when it gets bad?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Yes. Warning signs, what works, and who to call. We'll also find a peer support group you could start before we finish, and maybe say yes to Claire once a week.","m":[9,8]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Okay. I could do that."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"If things get bad between sessions, ring the crisis team, call NHS 111 and choose the mental health option, or ring Samaritans on 116 123. I'll write them down for you.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"And the watch?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Please take it home. I'll be open with you: I talk about my sessions in supervision, and I'll talk about today. I'll also note in your record that you offered a gift and I declined it.","m":[11,2]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"You're going to write down that I tried to give you a present?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Yes, plainly and kindly. It's part of the work, not a black mark. And I'll let your care coordinator know the ending is worrying you.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Fine. I suppose that's fair."},{"t":"6:05","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"How are you feeling about what we've talked about?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Embarrassed. But better than I thought I'd feel."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed, so I know I've been clear?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"No watch, no Facebook. Four sessions on the ending, a blueprint, and those numbers if I'm bad."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"That's it exactly. Thank you, Daniel. It took courage to talk about this. I'll see you next Friday at half four.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"See you Friday."}],"marks":[{"c":"Thanks him warmly for the thought behind the gift before declining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Declines the watch without opening it, giving a plain reason","d":"Content"},{"c":"Declines the friend request and explains why personal contact is not possible","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores what the gift means to him and why he gave it today","d":"Content"},{"c":"Links the gift and request to his fear of the ending and earlier losses","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates his hurt and anger at the refusal without becoming defensive","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts and plans after 'I'll have nothing'","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about his supports and what keeps him going","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees to use the last four sessions for a planned ending and written blueprint","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives crisis contacts for between sessions","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Tells him openly the session will go to supervision and be recorded today","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks how he feels and asks him to say back what was agreed","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes warmly and confirms the next session","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Declines both warmly with a reason, explores the meaning, asks about suicide directly and leaves a planned ending, supervision and a record.","borderline":"Declines kindly and plans the ending, but misses the meaning of the gift or asks about risk vaguely.","fail":"Accepts the gift, refuses coldly as a rules issue, misses the risk in 'I'll have nothing', or keeps it secret from supervision."}},"c-4-1-3":{"setup":"You are a psychiatry trainee in session 7 of weekly CBT with Chloe Bennett, 22, who has recurrent depression. Twenty minutes in, she shows you fresh cuts on her forearm. Respond, assess risk and needs, and agree what will be shared and with whom.","role":{"who":"Ms Chloe Bennett, 22, woman with recurrent depression in weekly CBT","open":"I did this last night after a row with Josh. Please don't tell anyone. If you tell my care coordinator, I'll stop coming.","manner":"Anxious, ashamed and guarded about sharing. Tearful when she feels she has 'ruined everything'. Relaxes once she is involved in deciding what gets said.","free":["Cut her left forearm last night after an argument with her partner, Josh","Cleaned the cuts herself; they stopped bleeding last night","Fears her care coordinator, Priya, will think she is getting worse","Does not want this shared with anyone"],"ifasked":["Josh had been drinking, got in her face and threw her phone at the wall","He has pushed her once before; mostly shouts when drunk","Did not want to die; wanted 'the noise in my head to stop'; no suicidal thoughts now","Took no tablets, alcohol or drugs; no other self-harm; first time in about a year","No children at home; Josh is staying at his brother's tonight","Her mum, her job and her friend Ellie keep her going"],"worries":["Will they section me or take me off therapy?","Then why does she need to know at all?","What would you even tell her?"],"rules":["Discloses the push only if asked whether he has ever hurt her or made her afraid","Becomes tearful when the care coordinator is mentioned; settles if told she will be involved","Threatens to leave if told it will be shared without her say","Asks for Josh's push not to be passed on yet"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Respond","w":"D","s":"Thank you for showing me, Chloe. That must have been hard. Let's put today's plan to one side; this matters more.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Please don't tell anyone. If you tell my care coordinator, I'll stop coming."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I can hear how scared you are about that. I won't do anything behind your back. Can I check you're okay physically first?","m":[7,1]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"0:30","band":"Wounds","w":"D","s":"May I look? Are any of them deep, gaping or still bleeding? Any redness, heat or pus?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"No. They stopped last night. I cleaned them. They're just scratches really."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"They look shallow and clean, so you don't need A&E. I'd like the practice nurse to dress them today. Was the blade clean?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"It was a new one. And I had my tetanus jabs, I think."},{"t":"1:05","band":"What happened","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me briefly what happened before you hurt yourself?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Me and Josh had a row. He'd been drinking. He got in my face and threw my phone at the wall. I went in the bathroom and just did it."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. None of that is your fault.","m":[7]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"It's stupid. I'm an adult. I shouldn't be doing this."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"It isn't stupid. Self-harm is often a way of surviving a feeling that's too big in the moment. What were you hoping would happen?","m":[7,3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"I just wanted the noise in my head to stop."},{"t":"1:55","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Did any part of you want to die last night?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"No. I wasn't trying to kill myself."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Thank you. And right now, any thoughts about ending your life?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"No. I feel flat, but no."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Did you take anything, last night or since? Tablets, drink or drugs?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing. I hardly drink."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Have you hurt yourself in any other way?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"No, just this. First time in about a year."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"What's stopped you acting on these feelings before?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"My mum. And work. I don't want to let them down."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Safety at home","w":"D","s":"You said Josh got in your face. Has he ever hurt you, or made you afraid?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"(quiet) He pushed me once. Mostly it's shouting when he's had a drink."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. Do you feel safe going home tonight? Are there any children living with you?","m":[6,7]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"No kids. He's at his brother's tonight. I'll be okay."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"There's specialist help for this, and you stay in charge of what happens. The National Domestic Abuse Helpline is a free, day and night, on 0808 2000 247. I can also refer you to a local advocate if you want.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Maybe. Not today. Can I keep the number?"},{"t":"3:35","band":"Sharing","w":"D","s":"Of course. I'll put it on your plan. What is it that worries you most about your care coordinator knowing?","m":[12,8]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"That Priya will think I'm getting worse. That I'll get sectioned, or kicked off therapy."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"That's a real fear. Nothing today suggests hospital, and therapy carries on. Sharing this is about more support, not punishment.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"Then why does she need to know at all?"},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"When we started, we agreed I'd share it if I was worried about your safety. Priya is part of your care team. If things get hard between sessions, she shouldn't be working in the dark.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I feel like I've ruined everything."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"You haven't. You came, you told me, and you showed me. That's the opposite of ruining things.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Okay. What would you even tell her?"},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"Just the essentials: what happened, that you're safe now, and our plan. Is there anything you'd want me not to mention?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Don't tell her about Josh pushing me. Not yet."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"Okay. I'll say there was an argument at home, and you'll tell her more when you're ready. Would you like to be here when I ring her, straight after we finish?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I'd rather hear what you say."},{"t":"5:20","band":"Safety plan","w":"D","s":"Let's write a safety plan now. What are your warning signs that things are building?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"I go quiet, and I scroll on my phone all night."},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"When the urge comes, what could you do instead? Some people use cold water, ice, or a walk.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Cold water, maybe. And ring my mum."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Who are two people you could contact?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Mum, and my friend Ellie."},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"Good. Then the crisis team number, NHS 111 and the mental health option, and Samaritans on 116 123. This copy is yours.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:05","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So I know I've been clear, can you tell me what happens next?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"We ring Priya together. Nurse for my arm today. Same time next week."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll also talk it through with my supervisor this week. If you can't make next week, I'll ring you; I won't just close your case. How are you feeling now?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Lighter, weirdly. Thanks for not freaking out."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you for trusting me. Let's make that call together now.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Thanks her, stays calm and pauses the CBT agenda for safety","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks the cuts for depth, bleeding and infection and arranges same-day wound care","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks briefly what happened before the self-harm, without graphic detail about method","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about intent to die then, suicidal thoughts now and what has stopped her before","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about tablets, alcohol, drugs and any other self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks if she feels safe with her partner and whether children live at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her shame and fear without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores what she fears would happen if her care coordinator knew","d":"Content"},{"c":"Reminds her of the agreed confidentiality limits and why the care team needs to know","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees with her what is shared, by whom and when, today","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Completes a written safety plan with two named contacts and crisis numbers","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers domestic abuse support, including the national helpline","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding and closes with follow-up and supervision","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Checks wounds, substances, intent and safety at home, then agrees with her what the care coordinator is told and writes a safety plan.","borderline":"Safe risk check and plan, but sharing is announced rather than agreed, or domestic abuse is not explored.","fail":"Promises secrecy, sends her to A&E without assessing, uses a risk score, or never asks about substances or her partner."}},"c-4-2-1":{"setup":"You are the psychiatry doctor in a CMHT outpatient clinic. Ryan Clarke, 29, has a moderate depressive episode and has been offered CBT, which he doubts. He has ten minutes before his bus. Explain CBT and help him decide whether to start.","role":{"who":"Mr Ryan Clarke, 29, man with a moderate depressive episode","open":"My GP says I need CBT. Isn't that just positive thinking? My problems are real. I lost my job and my girlfriend left.","manner":"Sceptical and a bit defensive, watching the clock. Opens up when his losses are taken seriously; briefly tearful about the break-up; ends interested.","free":["Lost his job and his girlfriend left","Thinks CBT means being told to look on the bright side","Has a bus to catch in ten minutes"],"ifasked":["Thinks 'I'm a failure', 'nothing ever works out for me' and 'I'll never get another job'","Has stopped applying for jobs, stays in, sleeps in the day","Stopped seeing friends and playing five-a-side","Sometimes thinks it would be easier not to wake up; no plans; his mum would be devastated","Does not want tablets yet"],"worries":["Am I just going to be told it's all in my head?","Homework? Really?","What if it gets bad while I'm waiting?"],"rules":["Pushes back if the doctor sounds like CBT will make his problems go away","Gets briefly tearful when the break-up is linked to 'proof' he is a failure; settles if validated","Engages more if the doctor uses his own words and examples","Chooses CBT if offered a real choice; refuses if pushed towards medication"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hi Ryan, I'm Dr Okafor, one of the psychiatry doctors. I know you've got ten minutes before your bus, so I'll keep it tight. Can we use it on what CBT is and whether it's for you?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Fine. My GP says I need CBT. Isn't that just positive thinking? My problems are real. I lost my job and my girlfriend left."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"They are real, and they're big losses. I'm sorry. What have you heard about CBT so far?","m":[8,2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"That they tell you to look on the bright side. Smile and it'll go away."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"And what worries you most about trying it?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"That I'll sit there being told it's all in my head."},{"t":"1:00","band":"What CBT is","w":"D","s":"Fair worry. CBT isn't positive thinking. It doesn't pretend the job and the break-up didn't happen. It looks at how depression changes the way you see yourself and what you do, which can keep you stuck.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Go on then."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Let's use your own example. When you think about losing your job, what goes through your mind about yourself?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"That I'm useless. A failure. Nothing ever works out for me."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"And how does that make you feel?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Rubbish. Heavy. Like there's no point."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"And what do you do then?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Stay in. I've stopped applying. What's the point, I'll never get another job. I don't even play five-a-side any more."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Can I draw that? 'I'm a failure' leads to feeling low. So you stop applying and stay in. No job comes, and that seems to prove the thought. What would you add here?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Sleeping all day, I suppose. Then I feel worse."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Exactly. It goes round in a circle, and CBT helps you break it at any point.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:30","band":"Thinking traps","w":"D","s":"Depression also sets thinking traps. Calling yourself a failure is labelling: one event becomes the whole of who you are.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"But I did get sacked."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"You did, and that's a fact. 'I'm a failure' is a verdict on all of you. 'Nothing ever works out' is overgeneralising: one bad run becomes forever.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I say that a lot."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"And 'I'll never get another job' is catastrophising, jumping straight to the worst outcome. In CBT you test thoughts like that against the evidence.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"(eyes welling) It's hard to see any evidence the other way. She left too. Feels like proof."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful, two losses so close together. It makes sense you feel this low. You don't have to believe things will change yet.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Sorry. Didn't mean to get like that."},{"t":"3:45","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"No need to be sorry. When it's that heavy, do you have felt that life isn't worth living?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think it'd be easier not to wake up. I wouldn't do anything. My mum would be destroyed."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you made any plans, or thought about how?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"No. Never got that far."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Your mum clearly matters, and that's important. If those thoughts get stronger, tell someone straight away. I'll give you numbers before you go.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Okay. So what's CBT actually like?"},{"t":"4:30","band":"What it involves","w":"D","s":"Usually sixteen to twenty weekly sessions, about an hour each. You and the therapist work as a team. There are small tasks between sessions, and some people feel a bit worse at first.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Homework. Brilliant."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Small steps. Often the first is behavioural activation: planning one thing a day you used to enjoy, like five-a-side, and rating your mood. Doing a bit more often lifts mood before the thoughts change.","m":[6]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"I could probably manage a kickabout."},{"t":"5:10","band":"Choice","w":"D","s":"You have options: CBT, behavioural activation on its own, an antidepressant, a mix, or waiting and reviewing. What feels right to you?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"I don't want tablets yet. I'll give CBT a go."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Good. Before you go, could you talk me through the circle back in your words?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"I think I'm a failure, feel rubbish, stay in, nothing changes, so I believe it more. CBT breaks that."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Spot on. And the thinking traps?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Labelling, overgeneralising, and the catastrophe one."},{"t":"5:55","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll record that you've chosen CBT. You can self-refer to NHS Talking Therapies online today, and I'll write to your GP within a week. Meanwhile, one planned activity a day.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"And if it gets bad while I'm waiting?"},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"See your GP, call NHS 111 and choose the mental health option, or ring Samaritans on 116 123, any time. The crisis team number is on this sheet.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Okay. Got it."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Any last questions before your bus?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"No. It's not what I thought. It actually makes sense."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. Go and catch that bus. We'll see you back here once you've started.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and agrees a short agenda that respects his bus time","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what he has heard about CBT and what worries him before explaining","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees his problems are real and corrects the 'positive thinking' myth in one sentence","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Draws his own thought-feeling-action cycle from the job loss, with him filling gaps","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names three thinking traps, each tied to words he used today","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains behavioural activation as a practical first step","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives format: about 16-20 weekly hour sessions, homework, teamwork, possible early dip","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates his losses and distress without rushing on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts, plans and protective reasons","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Offers CBT, behavioural activation, antidepressant, combination or waiting, and lets him choose","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking him to explain the cycle and traps back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Records his choice and gives referral route, GP letter and crisis contacts","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes on time, inviting final questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Uses his own example to draw the cycle and name three traps, corrects the myth, screens risk and records his free choice.","borderline":"Accurate explanation of CBT, but generic, with no personal cycle, or risk and alternatives are rushed.","fail":"Lectures in jargon, says CBT is positive thinking, ignores his real losses, or skips the risk check."}},"c-4-2-2":{"setup":"You are the psychiatry doctor in a general adult outpatient clinic. Amira Hassan, 26, has feared dogs all her life and recently turned down a job because of it. Explain systematic desensitisation and graded exposure, and agree her first step.","role":{"who":"Ms Amira Hassan, 26, woman with a lifelong fear of dogs","open":"My friend said you can make me face my fear. How does that even work? I'm scared just thinking about it.","manner":"Nervous and a little embarrassed, laughs it off at first. Becomes tearful about the lost job. Grows calmer and more confident once she sees she controls the pace.","free":["Avoids parks and walks a long way round to work","Turned down a job because the office allows dogs","Is scared even thinking about facing dogs"],"ifasked":["A dog chased her when she was seven","Fears a dog will jump up and bite her","Heart races, hands shake and she feels sick when she sees a dog; no fainting","Has never fainted at blood or needles","No panic attacks out of the blue; mood is fine; no thoughts of self-harm","Ratings: photo 30, video 45, dog across the street 70, small dog on a lead 85, stroking a dog 95","Her friend Sara has a calm, small dog"],"worries":["Are you going to make me touch a dog today?","What if I panic and can't stop?","Can't I just have a tablet for it?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when talking about the job she turned down; settles if validated","Gets more anxious if exposure is described as sudden or forced","Gives her 0-100 ratings only if asked step by step","Asks about tablets if no plan is offered for bad days"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello Amira, I'm Dr Patel, one of the psychiatry doctors. I can see even talking about this is hard. Thank you for coming.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"My friend said you can make me face my fear. How does that even work? I'm scared just thinking about it."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"Nobody will make you do anything. Today is about understanding the fear and how treatment works. You'll decide the pace. Is that okay?","m":[1,9]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"(laughs nervously) Okay. Sorry, it's silly."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Her fear","w":"D","s":"It isn't silly. Can you tell me when the fear started?","m":[10,2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"When I was seven. A big dog chased me in the park. I still remember it."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"What do you fear will happen if you meet a dog now?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"That it'll jump up and bite me."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"And what happens in your body when you see one?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"My heart races, my hands shake, I feel sick. I just want to get away."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"How is it affecting your life day to day?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"I avoid parks. I walk the long way to work. And I turned down a job last month because dogs are allowed in the office."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"That's a big thing to give up.","m":[10,3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"(tearful) It was the job I wanted. I felt pathetic."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"You're not pathetic. This fear has been with you since you were a child, and you've coped the best way you knew. It makes sense that losing that job hurts.","m":[10]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Thanks. I just want it to stop running my life."},{"t":"2:05","band":"Checks","w":"D","s":"A couple of checks. Have you ever fainted at the sight of blood or needles?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"No, never."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Do you get panic attacks out of the blue, away from dogs? And how's your mood been?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"No panic attacks. My mood's fine, just fed up with this."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of self-harm or that life isn't worth living?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"2:40","band":"How fear works","w":"D","s":"Can we think about the last time you saw a dog?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Last week. One came round the corner. I crossed the road and nearly got hit by a bike."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"And once you'd crossed?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Relief. Massive relief."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"That relief is the trap. Your brain learns 'dogs are dangerous, and I only just escaped'. So the alarm stays loud, and next time you avoid again. Fear, avoid, relief, and round it goes.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"So avoiding makes it worse?"},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"In the long run, yes. Treatment does the opposite, in small steps. It's called graded exposure, or systematic desensitisation. You face a small version of the fear, stay with it, and the fear comes down on its own.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"What if I panic and can't stop?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Anxiety always peaks and then falls if you stay. First you learn a way to calm your body. Shall we try one now?","m":[7,6]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Breathe in through your nose for four, and out slowly for six. Let your shoulders drop. Again. How was that?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Bit calmer actually. My shoulders were up round my ears."},{"t":"4:15","band":"The ladder","w":"D","s":"Great. Practise that twice a day this week. Next, we build a ladder. How anxious would a photo of a dog make you, from 0 to 100?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"About 30."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"A short video of a dog?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"45."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"A dog across the street? A small dog on a lead nearby? Stroking a dog?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"70 across the street. 85 for a small dog close by. Stroking one, 95."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"That's your first five rungs. You start at the bottom, with photos, and only move up when a step feels manageable. No dogs today.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"Good, because I was dreading that."},{"t":"5:05","band":"First step","w":"D","s":"The first step: look at dog photos for twenty to thirty minutes a day, until your anxiety drops to about half. Write your rating before, at the peak and after in this diary.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Every day?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Daily practice is what makes it work. If a step goes badly, drop back one rung, don't stop. Later, your friend's calm dog might help, planned with the therapist.","m":[9,12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Sara has a little spaniel. Can't I just take a tablet instead, though?"},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Tablets like diazepam aren't recommended for this; they can stop the brain learning the fear is safe. Exposure works better. I'll refer you to NHS Talking Therapies, and you can self-refer too.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Okay. That makes sense."},{"t":"6:05","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words how this works?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Avoiding keeps the fear going. I go up the ladder slowly, starting with photos every day, and the fear comes down if I stay with it."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. I'll write to your GP, and we'll review in six weeks. Any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"No. I actually feel like I could do the first step."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"I think you can. Start with the breathing tonight.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and acknowledges her fear before explaining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores onset, feared outcome and body symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores avoidance and its impact on work and daily life","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for blood-injury fainting, panic attacks, low mood and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Draws the fear-avoidance-relief loop using a recent dog encounter","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains graded exposure and systematic desensitisation in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Teaches slow breathing and has her practise it once in the room","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Builds the first five ladder rungs with a 0-100 rating for each","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Stresses she controls the pace and that daily practice makes it work","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her fear and her upset about the lost job","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Agrees the first exposure task, how often, and a diary of ratings","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises against sedatives, plans for setbacks, and gives the referral route","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back and confirms review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Explains the fear loop with her own example, practises relaxation, builds a rated ladder and agrees a daily first step.","borderline":"Clear theory, but the ladder is not rated, or no concrete first task and practice plan is agreed.","fail":"Explains theory only, suggests flooding or tablets, forgets to check for fainting, or leaves no homework."}},"c-4-2-3":{"setup":"You are the psychiatry doctor in a CMHT outpatient clinic. Jake Turner, 19, has contamination OCD and washes for four hours a day; his mother asks what ERP is. Explain ERP to both, including how family can help, and agree next steps.","role":{"who":"Mrs Karen Turner, 48, his mother, with her son Jake Turner, 19, who has OCD","open":"The doctor said he needs ERP. What is it, and how can I help? I clean the house for him every day so he doesn't get upset.","manner":"Mother: caring, worried and quick to speak for Jake; defensive then tearful when her cleaning is discussed. Jake: quiet, embarrassed, answers well when spoken to directly; scared of not washing.","free":["Mother cleans the house for him every day","Jake washes and cleans for about four hours a day","His hands are red and cracked","The GP said he needs ERP"],"ifasked":["Jake fears he will carry germs home and make his family ill","Relief after washing lasts minutes; the urge comes back","He asks his mother many times a day if things are clean","Low mood for a few months; denies self-harm or suicidal thoughts","Has missed college days because of washing","Ratings: touching the sofa 40, a door handle 60, the bin lid 90","He knows the fears are excessive but cannot stop"],"worries":["Mother: So I've been making it worse?","Jake: What if I don't wash and someone gets ill?","Mother: Should I just stop cleaning for him?","Jake: Do I have to take tablets?"],"rules":["Mother answers for Jake unless the doctor speaks to him directly","Mother becomes tearful if accommodation is framed as her fault; settles if her care is recognised","Jake becomes anxious if told to stop all washing at once; engages with small steps","Jake agrees only to changes he has helped choose"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Ahmed, one of the psychiatry doctors. Jake, is it okay with you if we talk about this together with your mum here?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"Yeah. That's fine."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you. And Mrs Turner, thank you for coming. I'll explain ERP and how you can help, but first I'd like to hear from Jake.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"The doctor said he needs ERP. What is it? I clean the house for him every day so he doesn't get upset."},{"t":"0:40","band":"His OCD","w":"D","s":"We'll come to that, I promise. Jake, when you touch something you think is dirty, what goes through your mind?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"That I'll carry germs home and make Mum or my sister ill. Really ill."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. What do you do then?","m":[12,3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"Wash. Over and over. About four hours a day, with the cleaning."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Does the relief last?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"A few minutes. Then the feeling comes back."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"How does it affect your day?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"I've missed college. I'm late for everything."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Your hands look really sore. Do they crack or bleed?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"They bleed sometimes. It stings when I wash."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Let's get a fragrance-free emollient and a soap substitute from your GP; use it after every wash. The GP can check for infection too.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:45","band":"Mood and risk","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Any thoughts of self-harm or that life isn't worth living?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"Low for a few months. Fed up. But no, nothing like that."},{"t":"2:00","band":"The family","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Jake. And do you ask Mum to check things or reassure you?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"Loads. Is this clean, did you wipe that."},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"And I do the cleaning so he doesn't have to. Bleach every morning."},{"t":"2:15","band":"Explain ERP","w":"D","s":"Let me draw how OCD works. Something triggers it, like touching the bin. The thought comes: 'I'll make Mum ill'. Anxiety shoots up. You wash, and it drops. So your brain learns washing kept everyone safe.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"And next time it's worse."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Exactly. The relief is the hook. ERP stands for exposure and response prevention. With a therapist, you face a small fear, like touching a door handle, and then don't wash. The anxiety rises, then falls by itself.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"But what if I don't wash and someone gets ill?"},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"That fear makes total sense, and it feels very real. ERP is how your brain finds out, safely and slowly, that the danger is far smaller than OCD says. You'd never jump straight to the hardest thing.","m":[12,8]},{"t":"3:20","band":"The ladder","w":"D","s":"So let's start a ladder. From 0 to 100, how anxious would you be touching the sofa and not washing? A door handle? The bin lid?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"Sofa 40. Door handle 60. Bin lid 90."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Could you start with the sofa: touch it and wait thirty minutes before washing, once a day, and note your anxiety at the start, peak and end?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"Thirty minutes... okay. I can try that."},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"So I've been making it worse? Everything I've done?"},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"You've done it out of love, and that's completely understandable. Over time, cleaning for him and reassuring can feed the OCD. The answer isn't to stop everything at once.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"(tearful) I just couldn't bear seeing him so upset."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Of course. You've been carrying a lot too. Could we agree one small change, with Jake? Say, answering 'Is it clean?' once, then saying, 'I know this is hard. I believe you can manage it.'","m":[10]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"I could do that. Jake?"},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"Yeah. Once is okay. Not stopping everything though."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"Agreed. Just one change this week, and you'll plan the next steps together with the therapist. Mrs Turner, you can also ask for a carer's assessment, and OCD-UK has support for families.","m":[10,13]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"Do I have to take tablets?"},{"t":"5:05","band":"Medication option","w":"D","s":"No, it's your choice. Medication helps many people with OCD. An SSRI such as sertraline starts at 50 mg in the morning and can go up slowly. It can take up to twelve weeks to work.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"Any side effects?"},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Sometimes nausea, sleep changes or sexual side effects. At your age we see you after one week, as a few young people feel more agitated or have suicidal thoughts early on. You can think about it.","m":[11,6]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"I'll think about it. I'd rather try the ERP first."},{"t":"5:50","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's fine. Can you both tell me in your words what you're each doing this week?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"Touch the sofa, wait half an hour, write my numbers down. Cream on my hands."},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Answer once, then the line about believing in him."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll refer you for CBT with ERP and write to your GP, with a copy to you. If things get worse, call NHS 111 and choose the mental health option, or Samaritans on 116 123. We'll review in four weeks.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Thank you. That's the first time it's made sense."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"You've both done really well today. See you in four weeks.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self to both and gets Jake's consent before discussing him with his mother","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks Jake directly what he fears when he touches something dirty","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about time spent washing, how long relief lasts, and impact on college","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about reassurance seeking and the family cleaning for him","d":"Content"},{"c":"Notices his cracked skin and arranges emollient and GP review","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about low mood and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Draws the trigger-thought-anxiety-wash-relief cycle using his handwashing","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains ERP including response prevention, with a concrete example","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Builds first ladder rungs with 0-100 ratings and agrees one task without washing","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his mother's care and agrees one gradual change with Jake's agreement","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Discusses an SSRI: starting dose, up to 12 weeks to work, one-week review","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates Jake's fear and distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks both understand, gives carer support, crisis numbers and review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Keeps Jake at the centre, explains ERP with response prevention using his washing, and agrees one task for him and one gradual change for his mother.","borderline":"Clear ERP explanation, but no rated ladder, or the family change is vague or not agreed with Jake.","fail":"Talks over Jake to his mother, tells the family to stop all reassurance at once, omits response prevention, or misses skin and mood."}},"c-4-3-1":{"setup":"You are in the CMHT psychotherapy clinic. Claire, 34, has moderate depression since her mother died and a rift with her sister; sertraline has not helped. Assess her suitability for interpersonal therapy, explain it, and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Claire Dawson, 34, female, patient","open":"Hi. Claire's fine. I'm not really sure what to expect, to be honest.","manner":"Flat, polite and tired at first. Becomes tearful and relieved when her grief is taken seriously; prickly about her sister; warmer and more hopeful once the therapy is explained.","free":["Feels flat, sleeps badly and enjoys nothing for about six months","Mum died last September; she coped at first with all the sorting out","Sertraline 'hasn't done much'","She and her sister fell out in May while clearing Mum's house"],"ifasked":["She did all the caring through hospital and hospice; her sister barely visited","Evenings are worst; she still has Mum's number in her phone","Has not visited the grave since the funeral and turned Mum's photo face down","No contact with her sister since June; did not reply to a birthday card","Sometimes thinks she 'wouldn't mind not waking up'; no plans; Tom and work keep her going","Takes sertraline 50 mg but misses about two doses a week","A glass of wine at weekends; no drugs; no voices or paranoid ideas","No children; lives with partner Tom"],"worries":["If this therapy is better, why didn't anyone offer it before?","Will I have to make up with my sister?","Do I have to stop the sertraline?","How long is the wait?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when describing caring for Mum; settles if the doctor validates rather than moves on","Gets defensive if pushed to reconcile with her sister","Only mentions passive death wishes if asked directly and kindly","Agrees readily to weekly sessions if offered after 4 pm"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Patel, one of the psychiatry doctors. Thanks for coming in. Your GP asked us to see if a talking therapy called interpersonal therapy might help. I'll ask how things have been, check you're safe, then talk it through. We've got about forty minutes. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Hi. Claire's fine. I'm not really sure what to expect, to be honest."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"That's fine. What you tell me stays within the team caring for you, unless I'm worried about your safety or someone else's. Can you tell me in your words what's been happening over the last six months?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"I just feel flat all the time. I'm not sleeping, I don't enjoy anything. I drag myself to work, then sit on the sofa. The tablets haven't done much."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. When did you first notice your mood going down?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Around May, I think. Mum died last September, but I coped at first. There was so much to sort out."},{"t":"1:00","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"So you lost your mum in September, kept going through the funeral and the paperwork, and then around May things slid. Was anything else going on in May?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"That's when my sister and I fell out. We were clearing Mum's house. She turned up once in two weeks and then wanted Mum's ring."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Was that the start of it, or had things been building?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Building. She hardly visited when Mum was ill. I did the hospital, the hospice, everything. (voice breaks) And now everyone says I should be over it."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. You cared for your mum through all of that, and then felt let down again by your sister. There's no deadline on grief, and being told to move on can make it lonelier.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"(tearful) Thank you. Nobody's said that."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Take your time. What are the hardest moments now, when you think about your mum?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Evenings. I keep thinking I should ring her. Her number's still in my phone."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Do you find you're longing for her most of the day, or steering away from things that remind you of her?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"I haven't been to the grave since the funeral. I turned her photo face down. I can't look at it."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That longing and avoiding can be signs of grief getting stuck, and therapy can help with that. How are things with your sister now?","m":[5,3]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"We haven't spoken since June. She sent a birthday card. I didn't reply."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Who else is important to you, and who do you feel close to?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"My partner, Tom. He's patient, but I think he's run out of ideas. I've stopped seeing friends."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Screening","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone a few routine questions. Have you been hearing voices or seeing things others don't, or felt people were against you?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"How much are you drinking, and do you use any drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Just a glass of wine at weekends. No drugs. I'm too tired to drink, honestly."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"And how are you getting on with the sertraline? Doses can be easy to miss.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Fifty milligrams. I probably forget it twice a week."},{"t":"3:20","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When people feel this low, some of them think life isn't worth living. Has that happened to you?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think I wouldn't mind not waking up. But I wouldn't do anything."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Have you made any plans, or thought about how?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"No. I couldn't do that to Tom. And work keeps me going, a bit."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Those are important reasons. Are there any children living with you?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"No, it's just me and Tom."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Let's write a short plan for bad nights: your warning signs, what helps, Tom, then the numbers. The crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, and Samaritans on 116 123, any time. Would you use them?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"I think so. I'd probably text Tom first."},{"t":"4:05","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Then Tom goes first on the plan. From what you've said, your low mood started after losing your mum and got worse with the fallout with your sister. That's exactly what interpersonal therapy is designed for.","m":[8,3,9]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"If it's that good, why didn't anyone offer it before?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"That's a fair question. No therapy suits everyone, and services often start with what's most available. Your story points clearly to loss and a painful relationship, which is where this therapy focuses.","m":[4,9]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"So what actually happens?"},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"About sixteen weekly sessions with one therapist. The first few map the people in your life and agree what to work on: likely your grief for your mum and things with your sister. The middle sessions work on those, and the last few plan the ending so it isn't a shock.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"Do I have to make up with her? Because I'm not ready."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"No. It won't push you to make up. It helps you work out what you want from that relationship and how to say it.","m":[9,4]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Okay. And the tablets, do I stop them?"},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"No, keep taking them. Therapy and an antidepressant together often work better than either alone. I'll ask your GP to look at the dose, and at ways to help you remember it.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:25","band":"Check understanding","w":"P","s":"Right. That makes sense."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"I've given you a lot. Could you tell me in your words what you'd be signing up for?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Weekly sessions, about sixteen, talking about Mum and my sister. And I keep the sertraline going."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. Would weekly sessions fit around work?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"If it's after four, yes. I want to try."},{"t":"5:50","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Good. Before you go, please fill in a short mood questionnaire called the PHQ-9. We'll repeat it in therapy so we can see change. I'll take your case to our allocation meeting this week.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"How long's the wait?"},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"I'll let you know after the meeting. If it's long, we'll check in, and Talking Therapies is an option meanwhile. Cruse Bereavement Support may help too.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"Okay. That feels less hopeless than I expected."},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. So: we'll offer interpersonal therapy on your grief and your sister, you keep the sertraline, and your GP reviews it. I'll write to your GP within a week and copy you in. If those thoughts get stronger before therapy starts, use your plan straight away. Any questions?","m":[13,8]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"No, I think that's it. Thank you for listening."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Claire. Take care, and we'll be in touch soon.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the purpose, time available and confidentiality","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Uses open questions to draw out her story and her key relationships","d":"Content"},{"c":"Links onset of low mood to her mother's death and the rift with her sister on a timeline","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her grief and hurt with specific empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Screens for prolonged grief: persistent longing and avoidance of reminders","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for psychotic symptoms and alcohol or drug use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans, reasons for living and children at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees a written safety plan with partner and crisis numbers","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains IPT in plain words: about 16 weekly sessions, phases, grief and dispute focus","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks sertraline adherence, keeps it going and asks GP to review dose","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking her to explain the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees baseline PHQ-9, allocation and interim support while waiting","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes with a clear summary, GP letter copied to her, and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Warm, focused assessment that links onset to loss and the sister rift, screens risk, explains IPT clearly alongside sertraline, and agrees a safe plan.","borderline":"Suitability reached and IPT explained, but grief or psychosis screening thin, or understanding not checked.","fail":"Turns into a full history and runs out of time, skips suicide questions, uses jargon, or implies IPT replaces medication."}},"c-4-3-2":{"setup":"You are in a CMHT therapy room, session 8 of supportive psychodynamic therapy with Leah, 27. She arrives late, angry, and asks for your mobile. Respond safely, then briefly bring the session to your supervisor.","role":{"who":"Ms Leah Morgan, 27, female, patient (later Dr Anne Fraser, consultant psychotherapist, supervisor)","open":"I'm late. Whatever. You're just like all the doctors. You don't really care.","manner":"Sharp and testing at first, arms folded. Softens and becomes tearful when the doctor owns a real lapse and stays curious. Brief flare at any interpretation, then thoughtful.","free":["Feels the doctor doesn't care and is 'paid to sit there'","Wants the doctor's mobile 'just for emergencies'","Weekends are the worst; nobody rings"],"ifasked":["The doctor was ten minutes late last week and gave no reason","She nearly left the waiting room last week","Sits by her phone every weekend","Sometimes wants to 'disappear' but not die; no plans","Last cut herself in the summer; no self-harm this week","Would text her friend Jade if things got bad","Has spoken before about waiting by the window for her busy mum"],"worries":["If you cared you'd give me your number","Don't psychoanalyse me","Are you going to tell your supervisor I kicked off?"],"rules":["Escalates if the doctor is defensive or explains her feelings away","Softens and becomes tearful when the lateness is owned with a simple apology","Accepts a link to her mum only if offered tentatively and she is asked if it fits","Supervisor asks how the doctor felt and what they make of it"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Leah, come in. I'm glad you made it today.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"I'm late. Whatever. You're just like all the doctors. You don't really care."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"That's important to say, and I'd much rather hear it than have you not come. Can you tell me more about what's left you feeling I don't care?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"You're paid to sit there. Ten minutes after I leave, you've forgotten me."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you're feeling really let down, and on your own with it.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Well."},{"t":"0:45","band":"What happened","w":"D","s":"Did something in our last session, or since, upset you?","m":[4,2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"You were late last week. Ten minutes. You didn't even say why."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"You're right, I was late, and I'm sorry. That matters, especially when this time is meant to be yours.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Oh. I didn't think you'd say that."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"What was it like, sitting in the waiting room not knowing?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Like I wasn't worth turning up for. I nearly left."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"That sounds painful. Thank you for coming back today.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","band":"Her request","w":"P","s":"If you actually cared, you'd give me your mobile. Just for emergencies."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Can I ask what having my number would mean to you?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"That someone's there. The weekends are the worst. Nobody rings."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"So the gaps between our sessions feel very empty.","m":[3,5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"(tearful) Every week. I just sit by my phone."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"That sounds really lonely. I'm glad you can tell me.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:05","band":"Making sense","w":"D","s":"Can I wonder something out loud? Tell me if it doesn't fit.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"You've told me about waiting by the window for your mum, who was often busy and didn't come. I wonder if waiting for me last week, and for the phone at weekends, brings back some of that feeling.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Don't psychoanalyse me."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Fair enough, I don't want to explain your feelings away. Does any part of it ring true?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"(pause) Maybe. It felt the same. Like I was a nuisance."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"You're not a nuisance. Those feelings make sense, and this is a good place to look at them.","m":[3]},{"t":"3:05","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When the weekends are bad, have you had thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life this week?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I just want to disappear. I haven't done anything."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"When you say disappear, do you mean wanting to die?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Not die. Just switch off. I haven't cut since the summer."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"Have you made any plans, or felt you might act on it?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"No. I wouldn't."},{"t":"3:40","band":"Boundary and safety","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I can't give you my own number; that stays private for everyone I see. But I want you to have help at any hour: the crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, or Samaritans on 116 123. Shall we put them in your phone now?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"So that's a no, then."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"It's a no to my mobile, not a no to you. I'll be here next Thursday at half four, same room, every week until we finish.","m":[7,8,3]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Okay. Are you going to tell your supervisor I kicked off?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"I talk about everyone I see in supervision; it's how I think about helping you best. It stays within the team, and it isn't because you've done anything wrong.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"4:50","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Before we stop, what will you do if the weekend gets bad?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Text Jade. If it's really bad, ring the crisis line. Then come Thursday."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"That's it. Today you told me you felt I didn't care, partly because I was late, and we noticed how waiting stirs up old feelings. I'm glad you said it. See you Thursday at half four.","m":[12,8]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"See you Thursday."},{"t":"5:25","band":"Supervision","w":"D","s":"Anne, can I bring Leah from this afternoon? Session eight. She came late, said I didn't care, and asked for my mobile. I'd been late last week, and I apologised.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"(Supervisor) How did you feel in the room?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Irritated at first, then a strong pull to rescue her: give extra time, or my number. I held the boundary and gave crisis numbers. No self-harm this week and no plans.","m":[13,9]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"(Supervisor) Good that you noticed both. What do you make of them?"},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"The irritation may be how others end up reacting to her, and the rescue pull matches her wish for someone who always turns up. I'd like help using that, not acting on it.","m":[13,6]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"(Supervisor) Agreed. Bring your process notes on Monday, and let's plan the Christmas break early."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"I will, and I'll let her care coordinator know about the break.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Greets her warmly and does not criticise her lateness","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds to the accusation with curiosity rather than defence","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her feelings of being let down and alone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about and owns a real lapse, with a simple apology","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores what having the doctor's number would mean to her","d":"Content"},{"c":"Offers one tentative link to earlier relationships in plain words and checks fit","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Declines the personal number clearly while staying warm","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives crisis numbers and confirms the next session date and time","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks directly about self-harm, suicidal thoughts and plans this week","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains supervision as routine and confidential within the team","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks she can state her plan for a bad weekend","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Ends on time with a brief, non-blaming summary","d":"Close"},{"c":"Names irritation and rescue urge to the supervisor and agrees how to use them","d":"Plan"}],"global":{"pass":"Stays calm and curious, owns the real lapse, offers one tentative link, holds the boundary warmly, checks risk and takes the countertransference to supervision.","borderline":"Boundary held and risk checked, but defensive moments, an over-firm interpretation, or supervision only mentioned in passing.","fail":"Takes the accusation personally, interprets early in jargon, ignores the real lateness, gives in on the number, or forgets risk."}},"c-4-3-3":{"setup":"You are at the EIP team base. The parents of Daniel, 21, who has schizophrenia and is home after his second relapse this year, ask about family therapy. Daniel has agreed you can talk with them. Explain family intervention and agree how to offer it.","role":{"who":"Mrs Karen Hughes, 52, his mother, and Mr Mark Hughes, 55, his father","open":"Yes. We're at the end of our tether, honestly.","manner":"Mother anxious and exhausted, tearful about her sleep. Father frustrated and blunt, calling Daniel lazy, but softens when his worry is named and the illness is explained.","free":["Daniel has been in hospital twice this year and is now home","He lies in bed until 2 pm, smokes in the garden, then goes back to bed","Father thinks he is lazy","Mother checks on him every hour at night"],"ifasked":["Daniel said they could come but did not want to attend","Mother has not slept properly in months","Before the last relapse he stopped his tablets and said the neighbours were watching him","Mother fears he will go missing or hurt himself","Father tells him to get up and calls him lazy most days","Nobody has offered them a carer's assessment","They would come to sessions; unsure Daniel would"],"worries":["Is this saying we caused his illness?","What happens in these sessions; do we just sit and argue?","Why won't anyone tell us anything?","Will Daniel even come?"],"rules":["Father bristles if he feels blamed; softens when his worry is acknowledged","Mother becomes tearful about her sleep; settles when her own needs are taken seriously","Both agree to one change at home if it is concrete and framed kindly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr and Mrs Hughes, thank you for coming in. I'm Dr Patel, one of the doctors in Daniel's early intervention team. I understand you wanted to ask about family therapy.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"(Mother) Yes. We're at the end of our tether, honestly."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you came. Before we start, Daniel told his care coordinator he's fine with me talking to you about his diagnosis and care plan. Is that your understanding too?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"(Father) He said we could come. He didn't want to come himself."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"That's fine. So I can talk about his diagnosis and plan, but not what he's said privately in his sessions. And I can always listen to you.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"(Mother) That's fair."},{"t":"0:55","band":"Their story","w":"D","s":"What's your understanding of what's been happening with Daniel?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"(Mother) They said schizophrenia. He's been in hospital twice this year. Now he's home and he just isn't Daniel."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"What's a typical day like at the moment?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"(Father) He lies in bed till two, smokes in the garden, goes back to bed. He's lazy. I was working at his age."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"It's really hard watching your son in bed all day, and I can hear how worried you are underneath that.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"(Father) Course I'm worried. But someone has to push him."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"And how do the nights go?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"(Mother) I check on him every hour. I haven't slept properly in months. (starts to cry) I don't know how much longer I can do this."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"You're carrying a huge amount, and your health matters too. Thank you for telling me. I'll make sure we talk about support for you before we finish.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"(Mother) Sorry. It's just been a lot."},{"t":"2:10","band":"Risk and warning signs","w":"D","s":"There's nothing to be sorry for. When you check on him, what are you most afraid of?","m":[4,9]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"(Mother) That he'll go missing again, or hurt himself. Last time he stopped his tablets and said the neighbours were watching him."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"So stopping his tablets and feeling watched were early signs last time. That's really useful to know.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:35","band":"Explain the illness","w":"D","s":"Can I explain something about the illness that may help with the lying in bed?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"(Father) Go on."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Schizophrenia has two sides. The obvious one is voices and suspicious thoughts. The quieter side drains drive, energy and feeling. Some medicines also make people sleepy. So it looks like laziness, but it isn't a choice.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"(Father) So I just leave him there?"},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Not exactly. Pushing hard usually backfires, but small, steady steps help. I'll also ask the team to check whether his medicine is making him too drowsy.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"(Mother) Are you saying we caused this?"},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"No. Families don't cause schizophrenia. But how things feel at home can affect how often it comes back, and that's good news, because it means you can help.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:35","band":"Family intervention","w":"P","s":"(Father) So what is this family therapy? We all sit in a circle and argue?"},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"No. It's called family intervention. A trained worker meets you, ideally with Daniel, at least ten times over three months to a year. You learn about the illness, spot early warning signs and solve problems together. It lowers the chance of relapse and supports you too.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"(Mother) Daniel might not come."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"We'll invite him and keep inviting him. If he says no, the worker can still support you.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Can I gently reflect two things I've heard? Mark, you tell Daniel he's lazy. Karen, you check every hour. Both come from love, but criticism and constant checking can both raise his stress.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"(Father) I don't know what else to say to him."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"Instead of 'get up', could you ask him to join you for one thing, like a walk to the shop at four? Calm and low-key.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"(Father) I can try that."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"And Karen, once we've written down his warning signs, could you check once before bed instead of every hour?","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"(Mother) That frightens me. But I'd sleep."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"It makes sense that it frightens you. The warning-sign plan is what makes it safer.","m":[4,9]},{"t":"5:05","band":"Support for carers","w":"D","s":"You're each able to ask for a carer's assessment from adult social care. It looks at your needs and can lead to breaks or practical help. Shall I request that today?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"(Mother) Yes, please. Nobody's ever mentioned that."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"I'll also give you details of Rethink Mental Illness carer groups.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:30","band":"Check understanding","w":"D","s":"That's a lot. Can you tell me in your words what family intervention is?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"(Father) Someone meets us with Daniel, about ten times, helps us understand it and stop winding each other up."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"That's it exactly. If you see those warning signs, or you're worried about his safety, ring the team's duty number in office hours, the crisis line out of hours, or NHS 111 option 2. In an emergency, 999.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"(Mother) Can you write those down?"},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Yes, now. And with Daniel's agreement, we'll share his relapse plan with you.","m":[12,9]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So: Daniel's tiredness is mostly the illness, not laziness. I'll refer you for family intervention, request carer's assessments for you both, and ask the team to review his medication. We'll go over it at his next care plan review.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"(Father) Thank you. That helps more than I expected."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you both for coming. Any other questions before you go?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"(Mother) No. Thank you, doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and purpose to both parents","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Confirms what Daniel has agreed can be shared before discussing his care","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores their understanding and a typical day and night at home","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates both parents' worry and the mother's exhaustion","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains negative symptoms and sedation, reframing 'lazy' as illness","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States families do not cause schizophrenia but home stress affects relapse","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes family intervention: at least 10 sessions over 3 to 12 months, with Daniel","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names criticism and over-checking kindly and agrees one alternative each","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Identifies early warning signs and links them to a shared relapse plan","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Offers carer's assessments and carer support groups","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking them to explain family intervention back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives duty, crisis and emergency contacts as a safety net","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closes with summary, referrals and follow-up at care plan review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Checks consent first, reframes 'lazy' as illness without blame, explains family intervention accurately, agrees changes at home and arranges carer support.","borderline":"Explains family intervention but skips consent, carer's assessment or a concrete change at home.","fail":"Breaches confidentiality, blames the family, promises a cure, or ignores the parents' own needs."}},"c-4-3-4":{"setup":"You are at the CMHT allocation meeting. Suggest therapies for three new referrals: a woman of 23 with EUPD and repeated self-harm, a man of 41 with PTSD after a crash, and a woman of 50 with chronic primary pain. Justify each to the team.","role":{"who":"Dr Helen Okafor, 52, female, consultant chairing the meeting, with Jo (care coordinator), Sam (psychologist) and Chris (team manager)","open":"Go ahead. We've got about seven minutes for these.","manner":"Busy but fair. The care coordinator is anxious about the young woman's safety; the team manager pushes for speed; the consultant warms to clear reasoning.","free":["Three referrals: woman 23 with EUPD and self-harm, man 41 with PTSD four months after a car crash, woman 50 with chronic primary pain and low mood","The DBT waiting list is about nine months"],"ifasked":["The young woman has had five A&E visits for cutting in three months","She dislikes groups but is desperate for help","The man has flashbacks, nightmares and avoidance; he will not drive","He drinks four or five pints most nights to sleep","He has no suicidal thoughts and is angry about the insurance","The woman with pain saw the pain clinic once two years ago and has stopped seeing her grandchildren","She may hear therapy as 'it's all in your head'"],"worries":["Why not put them all on the general CBT list?","She might not survive a nine-month wait","He doesn't want to relive the crash","Who is sending which referral?"],"rules":["Care coordinator becomes visibly worried about the nine-month wait; settles when interim support is offered","Team manager challenges the specialist referrals once; accepts a respectful, reasoned reply","Consultant asks 'Next?' if the doctor lingers"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Thanks, everyone. I've read the three new referrals. I suggest we take each in turn: what they need, the best therapy, what happens while they wait, and who does what. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"(Consultant) Go ahead. We've got about seven minutes for these."},{"t":"0:20","band":"Woman, 23","w":"D","s":"First, the 23-year-old woman with emotionally unstable personality disorder and repeated self-harm. Jo, what does she want help with most?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"(Care coordinator) The cutting. Five A&E visits in three months. She says her feelings go from nothing to a hundred."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"Has she managed groups before, and could she commit to around a year?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"(Care coordinator) She hates groups, but she's desperate."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Then I'd suggest DBT. NICE advises considering it for women with this diagnosis when reducing repeated self-harm is the priority. It runs about a year: weekly individual therapy, a skills group, and phone coaching in a crisis.","m":[3,8]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"(Care coordinator) The DBT wait is nine months. Honestly, I'm scared she won't still be here by then."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"That's a real fear, and you've been carrying it between visits. She shouldn't wait with nothing. Meanwhile I'd suggest structured clinical management here: fixed appointments with you, a written safety plan and crisis numbers, rather than ad hoc crisis contacts.","m":[9,4,11]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"(Care coordinator) That would help. What if she won't do DBT?"},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Mentalisation-based treatment is a good alternative. It helps people make sense of their own and others' feelings. We'll ask which she prefers. And no medication for the disorder itself; at most a short sedative course in a crisis, no more than a week.","m":[4,10,8]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"(Consultant) Agreed. Next?"},{"t":"2:15","band":"Man, 41","w":"D","s":"The 41-year-old man, four months after the car crash. Sam, are there flashbacks, nightmares and avoidance?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"(Psychologist) All three. He won't drive and can't go past the junction."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Is he using alcohol to cope?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"(Psychologist) The GP says four or five pints most nights to get to sleep."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"That matters. We'd want an AUDIT and the drinking coming down before trauma processing, or it could make things worse. Any self-harm or suicidal thoughts?","m":[5,11]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"(Psychologist) GP says no. He's mainly angry about the insurance."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Then trauma-focused CBT is first line, about eight to twelve sessions. EMDR is the NICE alternative for trauma like this: he recalls the crash in small steps while following eye movements or taps, so the memory feels less raw.","m":[6,8]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"(Psychologist) He told the GP he doesn't want to relive it."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Understandable; avoidance is how he's coped. We'd explain it goes at his pace, with coping skills first. Talking Therapies could also take him, and he can self-refer.","m":[6,10]},{"t":"3:40","band":"Woman, 50","w":"D","s":"Third, the 50-year-old woman with chronic primary pain and low mood. Has the pain service seen her?","m":[2]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"(Team manager) Once, two years ago. She says, 'I just have to live with it.'"},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"What does she want: the pain gone, or to live better despite it?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"(Team manager) Both, probably. She's stopped seeing her grandchildren."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Then ACT fits. NICE recommends ACT or CBT for chronic primary pain. ACT doesn't promise to remove the pain; it helps her get back to what she values, like her grandchildren, despite it.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"(Team manager) She might hear that as 'it's all in your head'."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Good point. We'd say plainly that the pain is real. I'd add a PHQ-9 for her mood, and NICE advises against starting opioids, gabapentinoids or benzodiazepines for this pain.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:45","band":"Challenge","w":"P","s":"(Team manager) Couldn't we just put all three on the general CBT list? It's shorter."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"I understand the pressure on our lists. But each has a specific option general CBT would miss, and the young woman could deteriorate on a generic wait. Could we check the specialist waits before deciding?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"(Team manager) Fair. I'll get the numbers."},{"t":"5:15","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"So, who does what? Jo, could you discuss DBT or MBT with her, update her safety plan, and send the personality disorder pathway referral?","m":[12,10]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"(Care coordinator) Yes, this week."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"I'll review the man: AUDIT, his choice between trauma-focused CBT and EMDR, and his work and insurance stress. Then I'll refer him to the trauma service.","m":[12,5,10]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"(Psychologist) And the pain referral?"},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Sam, could you check she's willing and refer her to the pain clinic for ACT? Please flag any interpreter or access needs on all three.","m":[12,10]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"(Psychologist) Will do."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"Each care plan needs the crisis line and NHS 111 option 2. I'll record the decisions in the minutes and write to all three GPs.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"(Consultant) Good."},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"To check we agree: DBT, with MBT as an option and structured support meanwhile; trauma-focused CBT or EMDR once his drinking is addressed; and ACT through the pain service. Does anyone see it differently?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"(Consultant) That's clear and well reasoned. Let's review the waits next week."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'll add it to next week's agenda.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Sets a clear structure for taking each referral in turn","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks the team targeted questions about each person's goals and history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Recommends DBT for the young woman because reducing self-harm is the priority","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names MBT as an alternative and structured clinical management while waiting","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol and stabilises before trauma processing for the PTSD referral","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Recommends trauma-focused CBT or EMDR and describes EMDR in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Recommends ACT for chronic primary pain and frames it without dismissing the pain","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Grounds each recommendation in NICE guidance, including prescribing cautions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Acknowledges colleagues' worries and pressures respectfully before responding","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Ensures each patient's preference and access needs are sought","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Secures safety plans and crisis contacts, especially for the self-harm referral","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees who sends each referral, minutes the decisions and plans GP letters","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises the decisions and checks the team agrees","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Matches each person to a first-line NICE therapy with a clear reason, names alternatives and interim support, stabilises before trauma work, and assigns actions.","borderline":"Sensible matches but thin on waits, alcohol, preference or who does what.","fail":"One therapy for all, unexplained acronyms, trauma work before stabilisation, or no interim safety plan for the self-harm referral."}},"c-4-4-1":{"setup":"You are in a CMHT therapy room with Gareth, 38, your CBT patient with depression. He missed two sessions, says you looked bored last time, has lost his job, and his PHQ-9 is higher. Four sessions remain before you rotate. Repair, review and plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Gareth Lewis, 38, male, patient","open":"Yeah. Didn't see much point, to be honest. Last time you looked bored.","manner":"Guarded and terse at first. Surprised and softer after a genuine apology. Tearful about the job. Flares briefly at news of the rotation, then settles when reassured he will not be dropped.","free":["Felt the doctor looked bored and kept checking the clock","Didn't see much point coming back","Lost his warehouse job three weeks ago"],"ifasked":["Missed sessions mainly because he couldn't face anyone after the job loss","Would rather text than phone","Some nights thinks everyone would be better off without him; no plans or intent","His daughter stays at weekends and keeps him going","Drinking about four cans most nights since the job loss","Hasn't claimed Universal Credit; rent due in two weeks","Hasn't told his mum about the job","Wants less paperwork, more talking"],"worries":["Now you're leaving too; what was the point?","Will I get someone else, or is this it?","I don't want tablets that make me a zombie"],"rules":["Stays guarded if the doctor defends themselves; opens up after a plain apology","Becomes tearful about the job; settles if validated","Only discloses death wishes if asked directly","Agrees to talk to the GP about medication if not pressured"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and repair","w":"D","s":"Gareth, come in. It's good to see you. I'm glad you came today.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Didn't see much point, to be honest. Last time you looked bored."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me straight. That can't have been easy to say.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"You kept looking at the clock."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"If I looked distracted, that's on me, and I'm sorry. You deserve my full attention in here.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Right. I wasn't expecting that."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"What would make these sessions feel more worth coming to?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Fewer homework sheets, maybe. More actually talking about what's going on."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"That's helpful. We can do that.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","band":"Missed sessions","w":"D","s":"You missed the last two sessions. I'm not annoyed; I'd like to understand what made it hard to come, or to call.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Partly the bored thing. But mainly, I lost my job three weeks ago. I couldn't face anyone."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry, Gareth. That's a huge blow on top of feeling low.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"(voice cracks) Eight years in that warehouse. They let six of us go on a Friday. I haven't even told my mum."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Losing something you'd built for eight years, and carrying it on your own. That sounds really hard.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Yeah."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"If coming in feels too much again, could you text the team, or let us call you? We'd rather hear from you than worry.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"I can text. Phone calls are hard."},{"t":"1:55","band":"Deterioration and risk","w":"D","s":"You filled in the mood questionnaire in the waiting room. When we started, you scored 14. Today it's 19, which tells me things have got harder.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Doesn't surprise me."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"It's not a failure; it tells us the plan needs to change. You ticked the question about being better off dead. Can you tell me about that?","m":[6,7]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Some nights I think everyone would be better off without me. I'm not going to do anything."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Have you thought about how you might end your life, or made any plans?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"No. I've got my daughter at weekends. I wouldn't do that to her."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"She clearly matters a lot to you. Have you been drinking more since the job went?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"A few more cans than usual. Four, maybe, most nights."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"That can make low moods and those night-time thoughts worse. Let's update your safety plan now: warning signs, what helps, who you'd tell, and the numbers. The crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, and Samaritans on 116 123.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Fine. I'd tell my mate Dean."},{"t":"3:30","band":"Ending and handover","w":"D","s":"Good, Dean goes first. There's something I need to be open about. I'm moving to a new job in four weeks, so we have four sessions left.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"And now you're leaving too. What was the point?"},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"It's fair to feel let down; the timing is mine, not yours. You won't just be dropped. I'll take this to my supervisor this week and ask for therapy to continue with a colleague. I'll tell you the answer next week.","m":[5,9,10]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Okay. As long as I'm not just cut off."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"You won't be. For our last four sessions, how about we focus on what you said: talking through the job loss, small daily steps, and a plan to stay well afterwards?","m":[9,3]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"What do you mean by a plan?"},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"A one-page plan you keep: what keeps the low mood going, what's helped, early warning signs, and who to contact. A copy goes to your GP. We can start it today.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Warning signs. Staying in bed, not answering my phone. Like the last two weeks."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"Exactly that. Let's write those down first.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:05","band":"Medication and practical help","w":"D","s":"Given the higher score, I'd also like your GP to review medication with you. How do you feel about that?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"I'm not taking pills. I don't want to be a zombie."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"That's a common worry. Most modern antidepressants don't do that. It's your choice; I'd just like the GP to talk it through so you can decide with the facts.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"I'll talk to him. No promises."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"That's all I'm asking. On the job, have you claimed Universal Credit yet? Jobcentre Plus can help, and Citizens Advice can help with rent or debts.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Not yet. Rent's due in two weeks."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"Could you ring Citizens Advice this week? Let's make that your first step.","m":[13,11]},{"t":"6:05","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Let's check we're on the same page. What are you taking away today?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Text if I can't come. Use the safety plan. Ring Citizens Advice. See the GP about tablets. And you'll find out who I see after you."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. If those thoughts get stronger, use the numbers straight away. Same time next Monday?","m":[14,8]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Yeah, next Monday. Thanks for, you know, saying sorry."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming back and telling me. See you Monday.","m":[14,1]}],"marks":[{"c":"Welcomes him back warmly and thanks him for raising the problem","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Owns the 'bored' impression with a plain apology, without defending","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what would make sessions more useful and builds it into the plan","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores reasons for missed sessions without blame and agrees a contact route","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his distress about the job loss and the ending","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Compares today's PHQ-9 with baseline and explains what the rise means","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans, protective reasons and alcohol","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Updates the safety plan with a named person and crisis numbers","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Names the rotation openly and agrees how to use the last four sessions","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Commits to supervision and a named handover for continued therapy","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Starts a relapse prevention blueprint with his early warning signs","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers a GP medication review while respecting his choice","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Signposts Jobcentre Plus, Universal Credit and Citizens Advice","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding and closes with the next session date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Repairs the rupture non-defensively, finds why he stopped coming, asks directly about suicide, and leaves him with a clear ending, handover and blueprint.","borderline":"Rupture repaired and risk asked, but the ending, handover or medication review left vague.","fail":"Blames him for missed sessions, gets defensive, ignores the higher score or suicide risk, or leaves the rotation ending unplanned."}},"c-5-1-1":{"setup":"You are in the CAMHS neurodevelopmental clinic. Jayden, 8, was referred after repeated school letters about leaving his seat and shouting out. His mother has come alone. Take a collateral history, explain the likely diagnosis and next steps, including what medicine would involve.","role":{"who":"Mrs Kerry Walsh, 34, female, Jayden's mother","open":"Yes, we're both his parents. Honestly, school think he's naughty and my husband thinks it's my parenting. I just want to know what's wrong.","manner":"Tired and defensive, expecting blame. Softens and becomes tearful when the blame is lifted. Sceptical about medicine until the checks are explained; hopeful by the end.","free":["Mornings are chaos: he needs telling five times and loses his things","School says he leaves his seat, shouts out and loses work","Her husband blames her parenting","He can play his console for hours without moving"],"ifasked":["Nursery said he never sat for story time","Normal pregnancy and birth; walked at 1, talking by 2","Eyes checked at school; hearing never tested","Slow to settle at night; no snoring, no staring spells","No heart problems or fainting; no sudden deaths in the family; her father takes blood pressure tablets","She was 'exactly like him' at school and still loses her keys daily","Lots of shouting and arguing at home about him; nobody hits him","Ran into the road after a ball last month; asked to leave football club"],"worries":["Is this my fault?","Can't you give him something today so school stop phoning?","Will tablets turn him into a zombie or stunt his growth?","Is this the diagnosis, then?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful repeating that Jayden calls himself 'the bad boy'; settles if validated","Gets defensive if parenting questions sound blaming","Mentions her own difficulties only if family history is asked","Accepts waiting for medicine if school support starts now"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mrs Walsh? I'm Dr Ahmed, one of the doctors in the CAMHS team. Thanks for coming in about Jayden. We've got about forty minutes. Can I check, do you and his dad both have parental responsibility?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Yes, we're both his parents. Honestly, school think he's naughty and my husband thinks it's my parenting. I just want to know what's wrong."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to carry, feeling blamed from both sides. You've done the right thing coming. I'll ask about home, school, his early years and his health, then tell you what I think. Is that okay?","m":[2,1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Yes. Fine."},{"t":"0:35","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Talk me through a typical morning getting him ready for school.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"It's chaos. I tell him five times to put his shoes on and he's off doing something else. He loses his jumper, his reading book. We're late most days."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"And when he's sitting down, say at a meal or doing homework?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"He can't stay on his chair. Up and down, kneeling, under the table. Homework's a battle. Ten minutes and he's done."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Does he blurt things out, or struggle to wait his turn?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"All the time. He interrupts me on the phone, pushes in at the park. He ran into the road after a ball last month."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"That must have been frightening. Is it the same at school and at clubs, or mostly at home?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Worse at school, if anything. He leaves his seat, shouts out, loses his work. And he got asked to leave football because he wouldn't wait in line."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"When did you first notice he was more on the go than other children?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Nursery. They said he never sat for story time. But he'll sit on his console for three hours. How can it be attention?"},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"That's a really common puzzle. Games reward him every few seconds, so they'd hold almost anyone. What counts is the boring tasks, like homework or getting dressed, and that's where he struggles.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:55","band":"Development and health","w":"D","s":"How was the pregnancy and birth, and when did he walk and talk?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Born on time, normal birth. Walked at a year, talking by two. Nobody worried."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Has he had his hearing and eyesight checked? How does he sleep? Any snoring, or staring spells where he switches off for a few seconds?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Eyes were done at school. Never his hearing. He takes ages to settle, but no snoring. No staring that I've seen."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"How does he get on with other children? Any worries about his speech, or needing things done the same way?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"He's got friends, he's just too rough sometimes. He'll chat to anyone. He's not fussy about routines."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Has he ever fainted, or had heart problems or chest pain when running? And is there anyone in the family with a heart condition, or who died suddenly when young?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that. My dad's on blood pressure tablets, that's all."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Does anyone in the family have similar difficulties?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"(laughs, then quiet) Honestly? Me. I was exactly like him at school. I still lose my keys every day."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; that's really useful. ADHD often runs in families. If it affects you now, your GP can refer you for your own assessment.","m":[6,2]},{"t":"3:05","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"I ask every family this. Has anything stressful happened at home? Does he get smacked when things get heated?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"We shout. I'm not proud of it. My husband and I argue about him a lot. But nobody hits him."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Lots of parents shout when they're worn out. Does Jayden seem frightened of anyone, at home or at school?","m":[7,2]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"No. He just gets told off all day. Last week he said he's the bad boy in his class. (tearful) That broke my heart."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"That's so hard to hear from your own child. It tells me he needs people to notice what he's good at, not just the telling off. We can work on that together.","m":[2]},{"t":"3:50","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"From what you and school describe, this looks like ADHD. It's a difference in how the brain develops, which makes attention, sitting still and waiting harder. It's mostly inherited. It isn't caused by your parenting, and he isn't naughty.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"So it's not my fault? My husband keeps saying I'm too soft."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"It's not your fault. Parenting skills can help a child with ADHD, but they don't cause it. I'm happy to see your husband too, so he hears it directly.","m":[8,2]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"He'd need to hear it from you. So is that the diagnosis, then?"},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Not quite yet. To confirm it we need the full assessment: questionnaires from you and his teacher, ideally an observation in class, and time with Jayden himself.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Can't you just give him something today so school stop phoning me?"},{"t":"4:35","band":"Medication","w":"D","s":"I understand how worn down the calls make you. Medicine isn't safe to start before we're sure, or before his checks. If we do use it, the usual first choice is methylphenidate, taken in the morning with breakfast.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"I've read they turn kids into zombies and stunt their growth."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"It's right to ask. At the right dose children should seem more like themselves, not flat. Before starting we measure his height, weight, pulse and blood pressure and plot them on charts. We start low, and check them after every change of dose, and six-monthly.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"And if it does affect his growth?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Appetite can drop and growth can slow a little. That's why we give it after breakfast and weigh him regularly. We can lower the dose or pause it. You can talk to us about stopping at any time.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:25","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"So here's what I suggest for now. I'll send rating scales to you and his teacher. I'll write to school asking for support straight away: sitting near the teacher, short instructions, movement breaks. He doesn't need a diagnosis for that.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"That would help. They just send him out."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"I'll also refer you to a parent programme for ADHD, and your husband's welcome too. I'll ask the GP to arrange a hearing test. Then we'll see you both with Jayden in four weeks.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Four weeks. Okay."},{"t":"5:55","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"I've given you a lot. Could you tell me in your words what you'll say to your husband tonight?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"That it's probably ADHD, it's his brain, not my parenting. School fill in forms, we go on a course, he has a hearing test, and if he needs tablets they check his heart and growth first."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. I'll give you an NHS leaflet on ADHD. Keep a close eye near roads. If you're worried about his safety, or you're struggling yourself, call the clinic or your GP, or 111 out of hours.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel like someone's finally listening."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. I'll write to you, the GP and school this week. Any other questions before you go?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No, I think that's everything."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Mrs Walsh. See you and Jayden in four weeks.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, role and time, and confirms who has parental responsibility","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges her worry and the blame she feels with specific empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Describes inattention, hyperactivity and impulsivity using everyday examples, not labels","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes onset before 12 and impairment at home, school and clubs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens development, social communication, hearing, vision, sleep and absences","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks personal and family cardiac history, sudden deaths and family history of similar difficulties","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks sensitively about stress at home, physical punishment and whether he is frightened of anyone","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains ADHD in plain words as brain development, not a parenting failure","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains medication: methylphenidate, baseline height, weight, pulse, BP, and ongoing monitoring","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a plan: rating scales, school adjustments, parent programme, hearing test, review with Jayden","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking her to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives written information and safety-nets, including road safety and who to call","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes with a summary, letters to GP and school, and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gathers a multi-setting picture with onset, screens development, cardiac history and safeguarding, lifts the blame, and explains a staged plan with correct pre-medication checks.","borderline":"Good history and kind explanation, but misses cardiac history or safeguarding, or the medication monitoring is vague.","fail":"Accepts single-setting information, implies poor parenting, offers medicine today, or skips heart and growth checks."}},"c-5-1-2":{"setup":"You are in a community CAMHS clinic. Amara, 6, was referred by her school SENCO for playing alone, intense meltdowns when routines change and lining up toys. Her father has come. Take a developmental history, then explain autism and what happens next.","role":{"who":"Mr Daniel Okafor, 38, male, Amara's father","open":"Hi. Look, her older brother was exactly the same and he grew out of it. So is she autistic or not? And will it ruin her life?","manner":"Direct and impatient, wanting a yes or no. Becomes tearful about coping alone. Relaxes once blame is lifted and practical help is offered; engaged by the end.","free":["Brother Jordan, 10, was 'the same' and is 'fine now'","Amara lines up her toy animals and screams if they are moved","Meltdowns of up to half an hour when routines change","Separated from Amara's mother when Amara was two"],"ifasked":["First words late, around two; pointed to ask for things but not to show","Did not always turn to her name, yet hears a crisp packet from another room","No loss of words or skills","Covers ears at hand dryers, cuts labels out, eats about six foods; growing well","Hearing never formally tested; no fits; bed at ten, up at five","Copies other children at school, holds it together, then falls apart at home","Ran off down the road during a meltdown last week","Mother thinks the separation caused it; no one has hurt Amara"],"worries":["Will she ever have friends, a job, a normal life?","Did the separation cause it?","Someone online said a gluten-free diet cured their son","What happens while we wait?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when describing coping alone; settles if validated","Pushes back if told Jordan 'must be autistic too'","Raises the diet only once autism is explained","Warms up when school support is offered without a diagnosis"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Okafor? I'm Dr Lewis, one of the CAMHS doctors. Thanks for coming in about Amara. We've got about forty minutes.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Hi. Look, her older brother was exactly the same and he grew out of it. So is she autistic or not? And will it ruin her life?"},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Those are the two big questions, and it's natural to want answers now. I'll answer them honestly today. First I need to get to know Amara. Who does she live with?","m":[2,1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Mostly me and her brother Jordan. Her mum and I split when she was two. She has her every other weekend. We both have parental responsibility."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll ask about when she was little, how she plays and how she is now, then explain what I think.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:55","band":"Early years","w":"D","s":"When did she say her first words? And did she point to show you things, like a plane in the sky?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Words were late, about two. She'd point to get a biscuit, but not just to show me something."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"When you called her name as a toddler, would she look round?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Not always. We wondered if she was deaf. But she'd hear a crisp packet from the next room."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Has she ever lost skills or words she used to have?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"No. Slow, but never backwards."},{"t":"1:35","band":"How she is now","w":"D","s":"Tell me how she plays. Does she do pretend play, like feeding a doll or playing shops?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Not really. She lines her animals up in the same order. If Jordan moves one, she screams."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"What happens when routines change?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Meltdowns. On the floor, half an hour. Last week the bus was different and she ran off down the road."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"That must have been terrifying. Is she safe near roads when she's upset?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Not really. I hold her wrist the whole time. I'm doing this on my own most days. (voice cracks) I'm exhausted."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Coping with meltdowns every day, mostly alone, is genuinely hard. You're able to ask for a carer's assessment from the council, and I'll give you the details today.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"I didn't know that. Thanks."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"How does she get on with other children at school?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Plays on her own. The teacher says she copies the others but doesn't join in. She holds it together at school, then falls apart at home."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Does she react strongly to noise, clothes, food textures or smells?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Hand dryers, she covers her ears. Cuts the labels out of everything. Eats about six foods."},{"t":"3:00","band":"Health and family","w":"D","s":"Has she ever had a proper hearing test? Any fits, staring spells, or problems with sleep?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Never had her hearing done. No fits. Sleep's terrible: up till ten, awake at five."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Is she growing well on those six foods?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"GP says she's fine on the chart."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Does anyone in the family have similar difficulties?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Jordan, like I said. He's ten. Quiet, loves trains, doesn't have many mates. But he's fine."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"I'm glad he's doing well. Autism often runs in families, so we can think about Jordan another time if you'd like.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"How were things for Amara around the separation? Has anything frightening happened to her, or has anyone hurt her?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"No one's hurt her. It was messy, we shouted. Her mum thinks the split caused all this."},{"t":"3:55","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"It's natural to wonder. Autism isn't caused by separation or parenting. It's about how the brain develops from very early on. Can I tell you what I think so far?","m":[8,2]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Late talking, not pointing to share, lining things up, distress with change and noise: that pattern fits autism. I can't confirm it today. That needs our autism team's full assessment.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"So what is it, exactly?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Autism is a lifelong difference in how someone communicates, relates to others and experiences things like noise or change. It isn't an illness, and it isn't anyone's fault. Does that make sense so far?","m":[8,12]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"I think so. But will she ever have friends, a job, a normal life?"},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"That's what every parent asks. Many autistic people have friends, careers and families. What helps most is people understanding how she thinks, and support now makes a real difference.","m":[8,2]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Someone online said a gluten-free diet and supplements cured their son."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting to try anything. Good research hasn't shown diets or supplements change autism, and cutting foods could harm her growth when she only eats six. The National Autistic Society website is a trusted place to read.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:20","band":"Next steps","w":"D","s":"Here's what happens next. I'll put her on our autism assessment pathway. The team will talk with you, watch her play, and get information from school. It should start within three months, and they'll contact you.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"And meanwhile? Three months is a long time."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"School can help now; she doesn't need a diagnosis. I'll write to the SENCO suggesting a visual timetable, warning before changes, and a quiet space. And I'll ask the GP to arrange a hearing test.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"That's more than I expected."},{"t":"5:55","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Just to check I've been clear, what will you tell her mum about today?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"That it might be autism, it's how her brain works, not the split. There's an assessment, school can help now, she gets a hearing test. And no diet stuff."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Spot on. If she loses any words or skills, or has a fit or staring spell, see the GP straight away. Keep holding her hand near roads. I'll send you a written summary, copied to the GP and school.","m":[13,7]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thank you. I feel less on my own."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. Any other questions before you go?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Maybe her sleep."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Good thought. Start with a sleep diary and a calm, fixed bedtime routine. If that doesn't work, the team can look at other options. Thanks for coming, Mr Okafor.","m":[13,6]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, purpose and time, and clarifies who she lives with and parental responsibility","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his worry and exhaustion with specific empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about first words, pointing to share and response to name","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about pretend play, friendships, routines and repetitive behaviour","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about sensory responses and any loss of skills","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens hearing, seizures, sleep, feeding and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about early adversity, harm and wandering risk during meltdowns","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains autism as a lifelong difference, without blame or doom","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Corrects the diet myth kindly and names one trusted information source","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the assessment pathway and that school support does not need a diagnosis","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees concrete steps: pathway referral, SENCO letter, hearing test, carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding in chunks and asks him to say it back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with a written summary, safety-netting and an invitation for questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Takes a structured developmental history with regression, sensory, health and safety screens, explains autism without blame or doom, and gives concrete steps that start now.","borderline":"Covers social communication and explains kindly, but misses hearing, regression or safety, or gives no clear next steps.","fail":"Diagnoses or dismisses autism on the spot, blames the separation, agrees with the diet, or leaves the father with no help while waiting."}},"c-5-1-3":{"setup":"On the adolescent inpatient unit, Ethan, 16, autistic since 7, was admitted informally after 3 months of withdrawal and talking to himself. He says the TV talks about him. Assess for new psychotic symptoms and explain your reasoning to the nurse and his mother.","role":{"who":"Ethan Clarke, 16, male, patient (with his mother, Mrs Lisa Clarke, and the ward nurse)","open":"Lower. Fifteen minutes. Okay.","manner":"Ethan is literal, flat and brief, tiring quickly. His mother is protective and suspicious of medicine, then frightened and tearful, and finally relieved to be listened to.","free":["Ethan: says Doctor Who lines aloud and always has","Ethan: the newsreader 'says' his name by how she looks at the camera","Mother: 'This is just his autism'","Nurse: eating very little; stands by the window for twenty minutes or more"],"ifasked":["Ethan: a man's voice since the summer, from the ceiling corner, through his ears; he tells it to stop","Ethan: certainty about the TV is ten out of ten; no thought insertion or control","Ethan: tired, not sad; no wish to die; no thoughts of harming anyone; no drugs","Ethan: his body 'gets stuck' and then goes","Ethan: understands why he is on the ward and wants to stay","Mother: a year ago at school daily, top in computing, ate with the family","Mother: stopped eating with them in July; answering back like an argument since about August; freezing only in the last two weeks","Mother: her brother had schizophrenia; no head injury or recent illness"],"worries":["Mother: 'You lot want to drug him'","Mother: can someone be autistic and have schizophrenia too?","Mother: will tablets make him a different person?","Nurse: should we hold the antipsychotic?"],"rules":["Ethan stops after about fifteen minutes; agrees to continue tomorrow if given a choice","Ethan answers only literal, concrete questions; open or abstract ones get 'don't know'","Mother becomes tearful about missing something; settles if reassured","Mother softens once her knowledge of his baseline is valued"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Handover","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Okoro, the psychiatry trainee. Before I see Ethan, what's changed and what's worrying you most today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"He says the TV is talking about him. He's eating very little, half a sandwich today. And he stands by the window for ages, not moving."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"How long does he stand there, and does he respond when you speak to him?","m":[1,7]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Twenty minutes, sometimes more. He answers, but slowly. He's drinking okay. Mum's here and she's not happy about tablets."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"Thanks, that's really helpful. Could you start a food and fluid chart? I'll see Ethan first, then sit with Mum.","m":[1,7]},{"t":"0:40","band":"With Ethan","w":"D","s":"Hi Ethan. I'm Dr Okoro. I'd like to ask you some questions for about fifteen minutes. You can say pass, or ask for a break. Would you like the light lower?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Lower. Fifteen minutes. Okay."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you. When you are on your own, do you talk out loud?","m":[4,2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. I say lines from Doctor Who. I always do that."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"That sounds like something you've done for a long time. Do you ever hear a voice talking when no one is in the room?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Since the summer. A man's voice. He says my name. He says I'm being filmed."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Where does the voice come from: inside your head, or outside, through your ears?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"The corner of the ceiling. Through my ears. I tell him to stop."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"That must be frightening. Tell me about the TV.","m":[9,5]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"The newsreader says my name. Not out loud. It's the way she looks at the camera. It means they know about me."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"How sure are you that she means you, from zero to ten?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Ten."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Does anyone put thoughts in your head, or control your body?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"No. Just watching."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Do you feel sad, or that you don't want to be alive?","m":[6,8]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Tired. Not sad. I don't want to die."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Have you had thoughts of self-harm or hurting anyone, like the people filming?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"No. I want them to stop."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Have you used cannabis, vapes or any other drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"No. I don't like smoke."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Sometimes you stand very still by the window. What is that like?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"My body gets stuck. Then it goes."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you. You are staying here voluntarily. Why are you on the ward, and what would happen if you went home now?","m":[12]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"To make the voice stop. At home it's louder. I want to stay. Too many questions now."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"That's fine. You've done really well. Would you rather I came back tomorrow at the same time, or write the rest on paper?","m":[2,9]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Paper. Tomorrow."},{"t":"3:00","band":"Baseline with Mother","w":"D","s":"Mrs Clarke, thank you for waiting. You know Ethan better than anyone, so I need your help to understand his usual self.","m":[9,3]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"He's always talked to himself. This is just his autism. You lot want to drug him."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"I hear that, and I'm not deciding anything about medicine today. What was he like a year ago: school, interests, eating, sleep?","m":[9,3]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"At school every day, top in computing. Ate with us, loved his Doctor Who box sets. Quiet, but content."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"When did you first notice a change, and what came first?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"July, when school broke up. He stopped eating with us. Then I heard him answering back in his room. I thought it was his scripts."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Is answering back, as if replying to someone, new? Or has he always done that?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"(pauses) No. The scripts are lines. This is like an argument. That's new, since about August."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Has he ever frozen, stopped talking, or held odd postures before?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Never. Only these last two weeks. (tearful) I'm frightened I've missed something."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"You haven't. You noticed he'd changed and got him help. It's hard to tell apart, even for us. Is there any family history of psychosis, bipolar or epilepsy? Any head injury or recent illness?","m":[9,6]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"My brother had schizophrenia. No head injuries. He hasn't been ill."},{"t":"4:30","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Here's my thinking. His self-talk and literal style are his autism, and they haven't changed. What's new since summer is a voice from outside, answering it back, and believing the TV is about him. Those suggest psychosis.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Can someone be autistic and have schizophrenia too?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Yes, both can happen. Autism doesn't protect against psychosis, which is why we compare him with his own usual self. I'm also worried the freezing and poor eating could be catatonia, which is serious but treatable.","m":[10,7]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"If he takes tablets, will he become a different person?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"That's a fair worry. The aim is to calm the frightening experiences, not change who he is. Autistic young people are often sensitive to side effects, so if we use one we'd start very low and go slowly.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:25","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Before any medicine, we'll do bloods, a urine drug screen and a heart tracing. I'll do a catatonia rating today and tell the consultant. Then the team meets with you this week to decide together.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Should we hold the antipsychotic the registrar mentioned?"},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Yes, please hold it until the consultant has seen the catatonia rating and bloods. Keep the food and fluid chart and a daily weight. Has his care, education and treatment review been held?","m":[11,7,12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"It's booked for Friday."},{"t":"6:00","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Good. Mrs Clarke, Ethan told me he understands why he's here and chooses to stay. At 16 that decision is his. Could you tell me what you understand of the plan?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"You think the voices are new, maybe psychosis on top of his autism. Tests first, a catatonia check, no tablets till the team meets, and tiny doses if he needs them."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If he stops drinking, freezes for longer, or talks about harming himself, tell the nurses straight away. I'll see Ethan tomorrow at the same time. Any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"No. Thank you for listening to me."}],"marks":[{"c":"Gets a focused handover from the nurse covering intake, stillness and current concerns","d":"Content"},{"c":"Adapts communication for Ethan: literal questions, choices, sensory adjustments, breaks","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes his baseline from his mother and dates what is new","d":"Content"},{"c":"Separates scripting from hallucinations with concrete questions about source and response","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits delusions of reference, conviction and passivity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, drugs, physical causes and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for catatonia: freezing, slowing, intake, and asks for a food and fluid chart","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about thoughts of harm to self and others","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates Ethan's distress and his mother's knowledge and fear with specific statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains reasoning to mother: new change from baseline; autism and psychosis can coexist","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees bloods, drug screen, ECG, catatonia rating, holding the antipsychotic, low-and-slow plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his capacity for informal admission and that a C(E)TR is arranged","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks the mother's understanding and closes with safety-netting and review time","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Uses his baseline to show what is new, separates scripting from hallucinations with literal questions, screens catatonia and organic causes, and explains a cautious plan the mother accepts.","borderline":"Identifies psychotic symptoms but misses catatonia, drugs or baseline dating, or explains without engaging the mother.","fail":"Attributes everything to autism or labels schizophrenia without baseline, uses abstract questions, or starts an antipsychotic before catatonia and tests are checked."}},"c-5-2-1":{"setup":"It is 22:40 in the paediatric area of A&E. Chloe, 15, took paracetamol this evening after messages from classmates online. Her mother is on her way. Check her treatment is safe, assess risk and reasons, and handle her request that her parents aren't told.","role":{"who":"Chloe Baker, 15, female, patient (with her mother, Mrs Baker, and the A&E nurse)","open":"I'll talk to you, but don't tell my parents. They'll go mad.","manner":"Guarded and embarrassed, then tearful about the group chat. Opens up once confidentiality is explained honestly. Mother arrives angry and frightened, calming when given a role.","free":["Classmates made a group chat mocking her","She took her mum's paracetamol from the kitchen","She feels fine and wants to go home","Her friend Amy brought her in"],"ifasked":["About twelve 500 mg tablets at six, four more around seven","Unplanned; no note; wanted to sleep and not wake up to it","Got scared and messaged Amy; relieved to be alive now","Cut her arm a few times since September; nobody knows","Low mood, poor sleep, hates school","No requests for images, threats or meeting from anyone online","Feels safe at home; Mum shouts but nothing more","No thoughts of harming the girls involved"],"worries":["Will you promise not to tell my mum?","Can I just go home?","Mum will take my phone and I'll have nobody","Am I going to be sectioned?"],"rules":["Will not discuss the overdose until confidentiality limits are explained","Cries when describing the group chat; settles if validated","Agrees to Mum knowing about the bullying, not the cutting, if asked her view","Mother calms if given something useful to do"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Medical safety","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Shah from CAMHS. Before I see Chloe, what time did she take the tablets, and where are the bloods up to?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"She says about twelve at six, then four more around seven. Paracetamol 500. Level, LFTs, INR, U&E and a gas went just now."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"So it's staggered. Has acetylcysteine been started?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Yes, the registrar started it at half ten by weight, as TOXBASE advises for staggered doses. She'll go to the children's ward."},{"t":"0:35","band":"Open and confidentiality","w":"D","s":"Hi Chloe, I'm Dr Shah. I'm one of the doctors who helps young people when things get really hard. Is it okay if I sit down?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"I'll talk to you, but don't tell my parents. They'll go mad."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"I can hear you're worried about how they'll react. Most of what you tell me stays private. If I'm worried about your safety I may need to share some things, but I'll tell you first and we'll decide how together.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"If I tell you everything, will you promise not to tell my mum?"},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"I can't promise that, and I don't want to trick you. Your mum already knows you're here. What we can decide together is what else she's told.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"(sighs) Fine."},{"t":"1:25","band":"Her treatment","w":"D","s":"Do you know why you've got the drip?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"To protect my liver. I feel fine though. Can I go home?"},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you feel okay. Paracetamol can damage the liver quietly for a day or two before you feel ill. What might happen if you stopped the drip now?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"My liver could get damaged. I could die. Okay, I'll stay."},{"t":"1:50","band":"What happened","w":"D","s":"You've understood that really clearly. Can you tell me what happened today?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Some girls in my class made a group chat about me. They posted a photo of me eating and everyone was laughing. (crying) I just wanted it to stop."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"That sounds humiliating and lonely. No wonder you wanted it to stop.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"When you took the tablets, did you want to die, or to escape the feeling for a while? Either answer is okay.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"I don't know. I just wanted to sleep and not wake up to it."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Had you planned it, or was it in the moment? Did you write a note or tell anyone?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"In the moment. They were Mum's, in the kitchen. Then I got scared and messaged Amy. She brought me here."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad you messaged her. How do you feel now about being alive?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Relieved, I think. Embarrassed."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Have you hurt yourself before, or felt like this before?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"I've cut my arm a few times since September. Not deep. Nobody knows."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. How's your mood been, day to day?","m":[5,8]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Low. I don't sleep much. I hate school."},{"t":"3:15","band":"Online and home","w":"D","s":"Has anyone online asked you for photos, threatened you, or asked to meet up?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that. Just the laughing and the names."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"And at home, do you feel safe? Has anyone hurt you?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"No. Mum shouts, but she's not like that. She'll just be upset."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Some people feel really angry with whoever did this. Have you had thoughts of hurting any of those girls?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"No. I just want them to leave me alone."},{"t":"3:45","band":"What Mum hears","w":"D","s":"Let's plan what Mum hears. She needs to know about the overdose and how we'll keep you safe. What about the bullying and the cutting?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"She can know about the bullying. Not the cutting. Not yet."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Would you like to tell her about the bullying, or shall I, with you there?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"You. I'll be there."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Okay. Tonight the cutting stays between us, and your CAMHS worker can help you tell her when you're ready. If your safety changes, I'd need to tell her, and I'd tell you first.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Why didn't you tell me? Who are these girls? I'm going straight to the school."},{"t":"4:30","band":"With Mother","w":"D","s":"Mrs Baker, I'm Dr Shah. Of course you're upset and want to protect her. Chloe took paracetamol this evening. She's on treatment and medically safe right now. She's had a hard time with some classmates online.","m":[10,8]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"(shaky) I had no idea. I should have known."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"This is a frightening night for you. What Chloe needs most is to see you're calm and listening. Let's agree together how school is told, so she doesn't feel things are done behind her back.","m":[10,8]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Mum'll take my phone and then I'll have nobody."},{"t":"5:10","band":"Safety plan","w":"D","s":"Your friends online matter. What if you mute the group, save the messages, and keep the people who are kind? Would that work for you both?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Okay. As long as she shows me if it starts again."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Before Chloe comes home, please lock away or remove all medicines, including paracetamol. Could you do that?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Yes. Tonight."},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Chloe, if those feelings come back, who could you go to?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Mum, I suppose. Or Amy. Or text Shout?"},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Yes: Shout on 85258, or Childline on 0800 1111. Tonight you'll stay on the children's ward to finish treatment. A CAMHS worker will see you before you go home, and you'll leave with a dated appointment.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Am I going to get sectioned?"},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"No, that isn't what's happening. You're here to finish treatment and make a plan. With your okay, we'll let school's safeguarding lead know about the group chat.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you both tell me the plan, so I know I've been clear?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Finish the drip, children's ward tonight, see CAMHS before home, lock the medicines away, school told about the group chat."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If Chloe feels unsafe after she's home, call 111 and choose the mental health option, or 999 in an emergency. I'll write to your GP tomorrow. Any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks for not making it worse."}],"marks":[{"c":"Confirms with A&E the ingestion timing, bloods sent and acetylcysteine started as TOXBASE advises","d":"Content"},{"c":"Introduces self and explains confidentiality and its limits before she discloses","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks her understanding of treatment to judge Gillick competence for that decision","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the overdose before, during and after: planning, intent, note, help-seeking","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about current intent, previous self-harm and mood","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about online harm, image requests, threats and safety at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about thoughts of harming the classmates involved","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates distress with specific empathic statements for Chloe and her mother","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Agrees with Chloe exactly what her mother will be told, and by whom","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Tells the mother the essentials calmly and proportionately, and gives her a supporting role","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a joint safety plan: medicines locked away, phone plan, who to contact","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges paediatric admission, dated CAMHS follow-up and school contact with consent","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks both understand the plan and closes with crisis numbers and GP letter","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Confirms paracetamol treatment first, sets honest confidentiality limits, covers intent, self-harm, bullying and safeguarding, and agrees with Chloe what her mother hears before a joint safety plan.","borderline":"Risk covered and treatment checked, but confidentiality handled late, or the mother told without Chloe's input.","fail":"Promises total secrecy or tells the mother everything, skips the medical check, or allows discharge before treatment is complete."}},"c-5-2-2":{"setup":"In an urgent CAMHS slot, Liam, 14, has stopped school, stays in his room and covered his window with foil over 6 weeks. He says he's watched through his phone. Take a history and explain an initial plan to him and his father.","role":{"who":"Liam Hughes, 14, male, patient (with his father, Mr Mark Hughes, 42)","open":"There's nothing wrong with me. It's the phone.","manner":"Liam is guarded, frightened and flat, more open when seen alone. His father is anxious, guilty and keen for answers; calmer once home treatment is explained.","free":["Liam: 'they' watch him through the phone camera","Liam: the foil keeps them out","Father: asks if this is schizophrenia and if Liam needs hospital","Father: off school for 6 weeks, staying in his room"],"ifasked":["Liam: 'they' can hear his thoughts; they come out of his head","Liam: two men's voices calling him a grass","Liam: smokes cannabis with older lads who give it free","Liam: asked once to drop off a bag; refused","Liam: sometimes thinks it would be easier not to be here; no plans, no self-harm","Liam: no thoughts of harming others","Father: change began in March: dropped football, up all night, grades fell; foil a month ago","Father: no head injury or fits; his own mother had bipolar disorder"],"worries":["Father: is this schizophrenia?","Father: will he be locked up, is he dangerous?","Father: will medicine turn him into a zombie?","Liam: do I have to stop smoking?"],"rules":["Liam mentions cannabis and older lads only when seen alone","Liam rejects any suggestion he is ill, but accepts help with sleep and safety","Father blames himself; settles if reassured","Liam asks the doctor not to tell the police about the older lads"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Liam, hello Mr Hughes. I'm Dr Evans, a doctor in the CAMHS team. We've got about forty minutes. I'd like to talk with you both, then have some time with Liam on his own. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"Fine. Is this schizophrenia? Will he need to go to hospital?"},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Those are really important questions, and I'll answer them before you leave. First I need to understand what's been happening. Liam, I'll keep what you tell me private, unless I'm worried someone isn't safe.","m":[1,8]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"There's nothing wrong with me. It's the phone."},{"t":"0:40","band":"His experiences","w":"D","s":"Okay. Tell me about the phone.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"They're watching me through it. The camera. That's why the foil's on the window."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"Who do you think they are?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Don't know. People from the estate. They know what I'm thinking."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"How do they know what you're thinking? Do your thoughts get out of your head, or get put in?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"They just come out. Everyone can hear them sometimes."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Do you hear voices when nobody's there?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Sometimes. Two men talking about me. Saying I'm a grass."},{"t":"1:15","band":"Timeline","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. Mr Hughes, when did you first notice a change in Liam?","m":[8,2]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"Maybe March. He stopped football, stayed up all night gaming. His grades dropped. Six weeks ago he stopped going to school. The foil went up about a month ago."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"So from March, a slow drift from friends and sleep. Six weeks ago, school stopped. A month ago, the foil and the phone. Is that right?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"Yes. I thought he was just a moody teenager. I should have seen it."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Early psychosis looks a lot like normal teenage withdrawal; most parents miss it at first. You noticed the foil and brought him in, and that's what helps. Could you give Liam and me ten minutes?","m":[8,1]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"Sure."},{"t":"2:10","band":"Liam alone","w":"D","s":"Liam, lots of young people try cannabis or other things. Have you?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"I smoke a bit with the older lads. Helps me sleep. They don't charge me."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being honest. Strong cannabis can make these experiences worse. I'm a bit worried about why older lads give it for free. Have they asked you to do anything, like carry things?","m":[4,7]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"They asked me to drop a bag off once. I didn't. That's why they're watching me."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"That sounds scary. Your safety matters, so I'll need to talk with our safeguarding lead about those lads. I'll explain anything before it happens.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Don't tell the police."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"I can't promise what happens next, but nothing will be done without explaining it to you first.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"With all this going on, have you had thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think it'd be easier if I wasn't here. I wouldn't do anything."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you made any plans, or hurt yourself?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about hurting anyone, like the people watching you?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"No. I just stay in my room. If I leave my room, they'll get me."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. You're safe in this room right now.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:40","band":"Health and family","w":"D","s":"Mr Hughes, thanks for waiting. Has Liam had any head injury, fits or recent illness? Anyone in the family with psychosis, bipolar or serious mental illness?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"No head injury. Never had a fit. My mum had bipolar. She was in hospital twice."},{"t":"4:00","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I'll be honest with you both. It's too early to call this schizophrenia. It looks like a first episode of psychosis: the brain starts making sense of things in a way that isn't quite right, like hearing voices or feeling watched. It's treatable.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"I'm not ill. There's nothing wrong with my head."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"I'm not asking you to agree it's an illness. You've been frightened and hardly sleeping for weeks. Would you let us help you feel safer and sleep better, even if we disagree about why?","m":[9,8]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Maybe. If I can sleep."},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"Is he going to be locked up? Is he dangerous?"},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"I can see how scared you are. Most young people with psychosis are treated at home by the early intervention team. People with psychosis are far more often frightened than dangerous. Hospital is only if home can't keep him safe.","m":[10,8]},{"t":"5:00","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'm referring Liam today to the early intervention team. They aim to start treatment within two weeks. A care coordinator will visit at home, with talking therapy for Liam and family sessions for you.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"Will the medicine turn him into a zombie?"},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"That's a common fear. If we use medicine, we choose it with you both, start low and adjust if he feels slowed. First we'll check weight, blood pressure, bloods and a drug screen, and keep checking them.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Do I have to stop smoking?"},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"Cutting down would really help the voices. The team can link you with a young people's drug service.","m":[11,4]},{"t":"5:45","band":"Crisis plan","w":"D","s":"Here's a written crisis plan. If things get worse, call the CAMHS crisis number on this card, or 111 and choose the mental health option. If anyone's in immediate danger, call 999.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"Should I do anything at home?"},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Lock away medicines and sharp things for now. Tell us straight away if he stops eating or drinking, or talks about ending his life.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you both tell me what you've taken from today?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"It might be psychosis. A team will come to the house. Smoke less."},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"Not schizophrenia yet. Treated at home, tests first, and the crisis numbers."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"That's right. I'll write to you and the GP today, and I'll see you both again in one week. Any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"No. Thank you. That's less scary than I thought."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self to both, explains time and confidentiality, and sees Liam alone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Builds a dated timeline from first change: sleep, school, withdrawal, foil","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits persecutory beliefs, hallucinations and thought interference with open then specific questions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about cannabis and other drugs, and where they come from","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about head injury, seizures, recent illness and family history of psychosis or bipolar","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about thoughts of self-harm, suicide and harming others","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Recognises free drugs and errands from older peers as possible exploitation and plans safeguarding","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates fear and guilt with specific empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains first-episode psychosis in plain words without labelling schizophrenia","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers the hospital question honestly: home treatment with EIP, hospital only if unsafe","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees urgent EIP referral, physical baseline and shared medication choice with monitoring","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a written crisis plan with numbers and home safety advice","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks both understand and closes with a summary, letter and review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Builds a dated timeline, elicits psychotic symptoms, screens drugs, organic causes and risk including exploitation, avoids the schizophrenia label, and agrees same-day EIP referral with a crisis plan.","borderline":"Clear symptoms and plan, but misses exploitation, family history or seeing Liam alone, or the crisis plan is vague.","fail":"Labels schizophrenia, never asks about drugs or self-harm, promises no hospital, or gives no urgent referral."}},"c-5-2-3":{"setup":"You're on call for CAMHS on Friday evening. The paediatric registrar bleeps you about Ruby, 16, admitted after 4 nights without sleep, singing, trying to leave to 'start a business' and messaging strangers online. Assess her, keep her safe tonight and explain the plan.","role":{"who":"Ruby Shah, 16, female, patient (with her mother, Mrs Priya Shah, 45, and the paediatric registrar)","open":"Sure, sit, sit! I've got about a thousand ideas, you'll love this. Vegan candles. I'll be a millionaire by Christmas.","manner":"Ruby is fast, cheerful and overfamiliar, turning irritable when limits are set. Her mother is frightened and guilty, tearful about the antidepressant, then calmer with a clear plan.","free":["Ruby: hasn't slept since Monday and feels amazing","Ruby: starting a candle business; has quit school","Ruby: has met 'an amazing guy' online","Mother: Ruby still takes the antidepressant from last year"],"ifasked":["Ruby: thoughts 'like fireworks'; everyone is too slow","Ruby: bought lots of wax online with Mum's card","Ruby: the man is 24 and plans to collect her tomorrow; she sent him a photo in her bra","Ruby: vodka on Saturday; no drugs; 'probably not' pregnant","Ruby: no voices; 'meant for big things'","Mother: change started last Wednesday; never like this before","Mother: depressed last year; fluoxetine started by GP and CAMHS","Mother: her brother has bipolar disorder"],"worries":["Ruby: you can't keep me here, I'm 16","Mother: did I cause this by pushing for the antidepressant?","Mother: does this mean bipolar for life?","Mother: can't I just say she has to stay?"],"rules":["Ruby talks over the doctor unless questions are short","Ruby becomes irritable when told to stay; agrees to wait if validated","Ruby only mentions the photo if asked directly about images","Mother becomes tearful about the antidepressant; settles if reassured"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Handover","w":"D","s":"Hi, it's Dr Novak, CAMHS on call. What's going on with Ruby?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","who":"Registrar","s":"Sixteen, no sleep for four nights, singing, tried to leave twice to start a business. Obs are normal, no fever. She's still on fluoxetine from last year."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Have bloods gone? Any drug screen or pregnancy test? And who's with her now?","m":[1,7]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","who":"Registrar","s":"Bloods sent. No urine yet. A healthcare assistant's with her, and Mum's here."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"Could you ask the nurse in charge for one-to-one nursing until I've seen her? I'm on my way.","m":[9]},{"t":"0:40","band":"With Ruby","w":"D","s":"Hi Ruby, I'm Dr Novak, one of the psychiatry doctors. Can I sit with you for a bit?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Sure, sit, sit! I've got about a thousand ideas, you'll love this. Vegan candles. I'll be a millionaire by Christmas."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"You sound full of ideas. When did you last sleep?","m":[3,2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Don't need it! Not since Monday. I feel amazing. Better than ever."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Your thoughts seem to be going really fast. Is that how it feels inside?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Like fireworks. Everyone's too slow. You're too slow, no offence."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Have you been spending money, or doing things you wouldn't usually?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Bought loads of wax online with Mum's card. And I've quit school. Don't need it."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Who have you been messaging online?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Loads of people. I've met an amazing guy. He's 24. He's picking me up tomorrow."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you told me. A 24-year-old arranging to collect a 16-year-old he's only met online worries me. Has he asked you for photos?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Some. Just selfies. Well, one in my bra. It's fine, he loves me."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being honest. I'd like to look at the messages with you later, to check you're safe.","m":[5,9]},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Have you taken any drugs or drunk alcohol this week? Could you be pregnant at the moment?","m":[6,7]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"No drugs. Bit of vodka Saturday. Pregnant? No way. Well, probably not."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Do you hear voices, or feel you have special powers or a mission?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"No voices. I just know I'm meant for big things."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Capacity","w":"D","s":"The doctors want you to stay tonight so you can rest and have some tests. What do you think about that?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"I'm 16. You can't keep me here. I'm leaving."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"You're right that your views count a lot at 16. What might happen if you left tonight?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Nothing! I'd get my candles and meet Jay. Sleep's for losers."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Does four nights with no sleep worry you at all?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Nope. Why would it?"},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"I can hear how frustrating all these questions are. I'm worried you can't weigh up the risks right now. Please stay while I speak to your mum and the consultant.","m":[10,8]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"(sighs) Fine. Ten minutes. Then I'm off."},{"t":"3:30","band":"Mother's view","w":"D","s":"Mrs Shah, thanks for waiting. When did you first notice a change in Ruby?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Last Wednesday she stopped sleeping and talked non-stop. Before that she was fine. Last year she was very depressed, and fluoxetine was started. She still takes it."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Has she ever been like this before? Any family history of bipolar disorder?","m":[4]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Never like this. My brother has bipolar. (tearful) I pushed for the antidepressant. Did I cause this?"},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"You were trying to help her when she was very low. Antidepressants can sometimes trigger mania in people already prone to it. No one could have known that beforehand.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"4:20","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I think Ruby may be having a manic episode. Mood, energy and thinking speed up too far, and judgement is affected. It's an illness, and it's treatable.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Does this mean she has bipolar for life?"},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"It's too early to say. A manic episode after depression raises that possibility, so we'll follow her closely. Many young people do well with treatment.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:50","band":"Tonight's plan","w":"D","s":"Tonight a nurse will be with her all the time. Before any medicine we'll do a pregnancy test, a urine drug screen and an ECG. I'll discuss stopping the fluoxetine with the consultant.","m":[7,9,11]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"She keeps saying she'll walk out. Can't I just say she has to stay?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"At 16, parents can't agree to her being kept somewhere against her will. I don't think she has capacity right now to decide to leave, and I'll record that. If she tries to go, the doctor in charge can use the Mental Health Act to hold her for up to 72 hours for assessment.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Oh. And the man online?"},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"We'll take that very seriously. I'm making a safeguarding referral tonight, and the police will be informed. With her agreement, the nurses will look after her phone.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:55","band":"Escalate","w":"D","s":"I'm ringing the on-call CAMHS consultant now to agree all this, and they'll review Ruby in the morning.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Thank you. What do I do now?"},{"t":"6:05","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Stay with her if you can, and keep things calm and quiet. Can you tell me the plan in your words?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"She stays tonight with a nurse, has tests, the antidepressant's reviewed, the consultant sees her tomorrow, and you're reporting the man."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If she tries to leave, the nurses will call me straight away. Any questions?","m":[13,8]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"No. Thank you."},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Stop following me! I don't need a babysitter."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"I know it's annoying having someone with you. You've had no sleep and things feel very fast. As soon as you're calmer, we'll step it down. The consultant will see you in the morning.","m":[10,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Gets a handover covering medicines, physical observations, tests sent and supervision","d":"Content"},{"c":"Introduces self calmly and keeps questions short to work with pressured speech","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits core manic features: sleep, racing thoughts, grandiosity, spending, risk-taking","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gets dated onset and collateral from mother, including past depression and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks who she has messaged and whether anyone asked for images or to meet","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens drugs, alcohol and psychotic symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Arranges pregnancy test, urine drug screen and ECG before any medicine","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Assesses her capacity to leave and states the lawful authority to hold her if needed","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Sets 1:1 nursing and safeguarding actions for the online contact","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates her frustration and her mother's guilt with specific empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains possible mania and the antidepressant review in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Escalates to the on-call CAMHS consultant and agrees next-morning review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks the mother's understanding and closes with a clear summary","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Elicits mania with collateral, spots online exploitation, gets a pregnancy test before medicine, documents capacity and lawful authority, and escalates with a clear overnight plan.","borderline":"Recognises mania and keeps her safe, but misses the image question, pregnancy test or the antidepressant review.","fail":"Relies on the mother's consent to detain her, misses the grooming risk, starts medicine before tests, or lets her leave."}},"c-5-3-1":{"setup":"You are in the CAMHS clinic. Lily, 10, has missed five weeks of school and has never spoken there in three years; a fine has been mentioned. Assess with her mother, explain what is happening, and agree a first plan with school.","role":{"who":"Mrs Sarah Turner, 38, Lily's mother (Lily Turner, 10, is with her and mostly nods, points or draws)","open":"Yes. Honestly, I'm at the end of my rope. She hasn't been in school for five weeks, and now they're threatening us with a fine.","manner":"Tired, worried and a little defensive about school. Tearful when her own anxiety comes up; relieved and practical once the plan is concrete. Lily stays close to her mum and answers only by nodding, drawing or whispering to her.","free":["Lily has missed school for five weeks; tummy aches and crying at the door every morning","The pain settles by mid-morning once she knows she is staying home","She chats freely at home but has never spoken to her teacher or classmates in three years","The school attendance officer has written about a possible fine"],"ifasked":["No tummy aches at weekends or in the summer holidays","She talks to Mum, Dad, Nan and her cousin; silent in shops and at school; whispered to one nursery worker","Words came on time; only the newborn hearing test; English is the only language at home","Plays pretend for hours with her cousin; no rigid routines or noise sensitivity","Best friend moved away at Easter and a new teacher started in September, when things got worse","Happy and eating well at home; no self-harm; nobody is hurting anyone at home; money is tight; Dad works away in the week","Mother was a very shy child and still panics in crowds"],"worries":["Is she making the tummy aches up, as the school seems to think?","Can you sign her off until she's ready?","What do we do about the fine?","Have I passed my anxiety on to her?"],"rules":["Lily never speaks to the doctor; she nods, shakes her head, draws, or whispers to Mum","Lily freezes and hides her face if asked a direct spoken question; relaxes if offered nodding or drawing","Mother becomes tearful about her own anxiety; settles if the doctor validates rather than moves on","Mother asks for a sick note and accepts a graded return once the avoidance cycle is explained"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Lily, I'm Dr Shah. I'm a doctor who helps children with worries. You don't have to talk to me at all today. You can nod, shake your head, point or draw. Is that OK?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","who":"Lily","s":"(nods, holding Mum's sleeve)"},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Brilliant, thank you. Here are some pens and paper, and you can sit right next to Mum. Mrs Turner, thanks for coming. I'll hear what's been happening, and keep checking in with Lily as we go. Is that alright?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Yes. Honestly, I'm at the end of my rope. She hasn't been in school for five weeks, and now they're threatening us with a fine."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to carry, and that letter must have felt like a blow on top of everything. We'll come back to the fine, I promise. Can you walk me through a school morning?","m":[8,3]},{"t":"1:00","band":"Her story","w":"P","s":"She wakes up fine. Then as soon as we start getting dressed it's tummy ache, she cries, and at the door she just clings to me. Some days she's sick."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"When do the tummy aches stop?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Pretty much as soon as I say she can stay home. By ten she's fine."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"And at weekends, or in the holidays?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Never. The summer was lovely. That's why school think she's putting it on."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Lily, one for a nod or a shake. Is school the thing your tummy worries about most?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","who":"Lily","s":"(nods, then draws a classroom full of faces)"},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's a really helpful drawing. Lots of faces. Does it feel like everyone's looking at you?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"(after Lily whispers to her) She says everyone will stare at her."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling Mum, Lily. Lots of children feel exactly like that, and nobody here is going to make you talk.","m":[8,2]},{"t":"2:00","band":"Where she speaks","w":"D","s":"Mrs Turner, you said she's never spoken at school. Who does she talk to, and where?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"At home she never stops. Me, her dad, her nan, her cousin. But at school, not one word to the teacher or the other kids. Three years now. In shops she goes silent too."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Did she ever talk at nursery? And did her words come on time as a toddler?","m":[4,6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"She whispered to one nursery worker. Her words came on time, I think. Nobody's ever said there was a problem."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Has her hearing been tested since she was a baby? And is English the only language at home?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Just the newborn test. And yes, only English."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"How does she play and get on with friends at home? Does she need things done a set way, or get upset by noise or change?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"She plays pretend with her cousin for hours. She's not fussy about routines. She's just shy, like I was."},{"t":"3:00","band":"Triggers and safety","w":"D","s":"Has anything changed at school: unkindness, a new teacher, a friend leaving?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Her best friend moved away at Easter. And they changed teacher in September. That's when it got really bad."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"That makes sense. And at home, anything worrying: illness, money, arguments, anyone hurting anyone?","m":[5,7]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that. Money's tight, that's all. Her dad works away in the week."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Lily, nod or shake. Has anyone at school, or anywhere, been unkind to you or hurt you?","m":[5,7]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","who":"Lily","s":"(shakes her head firmly)"},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Mrs Turner, has she seemed sad most of the time, gone off her food or lost weight, or said she wants to hurt herself?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"No. She's happy at home. She eats fine."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"And how are you in yourself? You said you were shy too.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"(eyes filling) I still panic in crowds. I keep thinking I've passed this on to her."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"That's a heavy thought to carry. Worry does run in families, but it also means you understand her better than anyone. It's worth seeing your GP for yourself too; when parents get help, children often improve faster.","m":[8,7]},{"t":"4:05","band":"Explain","w":"P","s":"So is she making the tummy aches up? That's what school seem to think."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"No, the pain is real. Worry makes the body release stress chemicals that cause tummy pain and sickness. That's why it's worst on school mornings and gone by ten.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Right. So what is it, then?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Two linked things. She's very anxious about school, and her voice gets stuck there. That's called selective mutism. It's an anxiety problem, not stubbornness or rudeness.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Can't you just sign her off until she's ready?"},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting to protect her. The trouble is, every day at home makes the worry bigger, so 'ready' never quite comes. So rather than a sick note, I'll write a letter supporting a small, planned return.","m":[9,8]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"OK. What does small mean?"},{"t":"5:00","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Starting this week: the school gate with one key adult, then a short morning in a quiet room. Speaking comes later, in tiny steps. Can I share this with the school SENCO and your GP, and call the SENCO now?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's fine."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you, I'll note your consent. (on the phone) Hello, it's Dr Shah from CAMHS. Mrs Turner has agreed I can talk to you about Lily. She has anxiety with selective mutism. Could a key adult meet her at the gate from Thursday, with a quiet base and a short morning?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","who":"SENCO","s":"Yes. Mrs Okoro, her teaching assistant, could do that. Thursday's fine. What about in class?"},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Please no direct questions in front of others for now. Let her nod or use cards, and don't make a fuss when she does speak. Could the attendance lead hold off any penalty? I'll send a letter today.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","who":"SENCO","s":"Of course. I'll set up an attendance support plan and put it in writing."},{"t":"5:55","band":"Check understanding","w":"D","s":"Mrs Turner, just so I can check I've explained it clearly, can you tell me the plan in your words?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Gate on Thursday with Mrs Okoro, short mornings, nobody pushes her to talk. Hearing test through the GP. And you're writing to school about the fine."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll also ask for a speech and language check, and refer you both to the Mental Health Support Team for step-by-step work together. Lily, is meeting Mrs Okoro at the gate OK as a first step?","m":[6,12,2]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","who":"Lily","s":"(nods slowly and draws a small door)"},{"t":"6:25","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's a great drawing. Mrs Turner, if she becomes low, stops eating, loses weight or talks about hurting herself, call us or your GP sooner. Otherwise I'll see you both in four weeks. Any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you. I feel like someone's finally listening."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You've done the hard bit by coming. The letter goes today. See you both in four weeks.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Greets the child first, at her level, and says she does not have to talk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers nodding, pointing or drawing and gets the child's own view on mornings and school","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Clarifies the morning pattern: timing of tummy aches, and weekends and holidays","d":"Content"},{"c":"Maps where and with whom she speaks, and since when","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores school and home triggers, including unkindness, changes and anyone hurting her","d":"Content"},{"c":"Rules out hearing loss, language delay, autism and EAL; requests hearing and speech and language checks","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, eating, self-harm and safeguarding, and asks about the mother's own anxiety","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates the mother's and child's distress with specific empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains real pain from worry, selective mutism and the avoidance cycle in plain words; no sick note","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gets and records the mother's consent to share with school and GP","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees by phone with the SENCO a return within days, key adult, quiet base and no pressure to speak","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks the mother's understanding by asking her to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets for low mood, weight loss or self-harm and books a 4-week review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Engages the child without pressure, separates anxiety from truancy, rules out hearing and language causes, and agrees a graded return with the SENCO within days.","borderline":"Sound history and explanation, but hearing and language checks missed, the school plan is vague, or the child is sidelined.","fail":"Presses the child to speak, writes a sick note, misses safeguarding or mood questions, or leaves without a school plan."}},"c-5-3-2":{"setup":"You are in the CAMHS clinic on Thursday morning. Jayden, 7, was excluded last week for kicking a teaching assistant. His exhausted mother, 29, asks for a tablet to calm him. Assess the behaviour, screen for other causes and explain parent training.","role":{"who":"Ms Kelly Price, 29, Jayden's mother (Jayden Price, 7, plays in the room)","open":"Tough doesn't cover it. He's been excluded, he breaks everything, he won't listen. Is there a tablet that'll calm him down?","manner":"Exhausted and close to tears, braced to be judged. Defensive if she feels blamed; opens up when the doctor is warm and practical. Relieved by the end.","free":["Daily 'meltdowns' for about a year: screaming, throwing and kicking","Fixed-term exclusion last week for kicking a teaching assistant","He ignores instructions, especially when asked to stop screen time","She wants a tablet to calm him down"],"ifasked":["She often gives in to end a meltdown, such as handing back the tablet","Better at her mother's, who is firm; worse at school","Calm at bedtime stories and when baking with her","Restless since nursery: cannot sit through tea, out of his seat, shouts out in class","Speech a bit unclear, reading behind, glue ear at 4 never re-tested","Born at 35 weeks; talking a little late","His father threw things and shouted before they split two years ago; Jayden saw it; no contact now, non-molestation order in place","She cries most nights and sleeps badly; no thoughts of self-harm"],"worries":["Can he have a tablet to calm him?","Are you saying this is my fault?","I don't want to sit in a room with other parents judging me","The school have given up on him"],"rules":["Becomes tearful and says 'I'm a bad mum' if the doctor talks about her giving in; settles if reassured without blame","Discloses past domestic abuse only if asked directly while Jayden is busy","Agrees to the parenting group once childcare and timing are solved","Jayden chats about his Lego and ignores the adult conversation"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the children's mental health doctors. You must be Jayden. There's Lego and pens here; you can play while I chat to Mum. Is that OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","who":"Jayden","s":"(grabs the Lego box) Can I build a truck?"},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"A truck sounds great. Ms Price, thanks for coming. The GP said things have been really tough lately.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Tough doesn't cover it. He's been excluded, he breaks everything, he won't listen. Is there a tablet that'll calm him down?"},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you've been coping with an awful lot on your own. I will come to medicines, I promise. First, can you tell me about a typical bad day?","m":[1,9]},{"t":"0:40","band":"His story","w":"P","s":"This morning. I said shoes on, he screamed, threw his cereal bowl and kicked the door. Every morning's a fight."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"What was happening just before you asked about shoes?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"He was on the tablet. I said turn it off, he ignored me, I said it again louder."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"And afterwards, once it blew over, what happened?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"I end up putting his shoes on for him, just to get out the door. Or I give him the tablet back in the car to stop the screaming."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"That's what most tired parents would do. It can mean the meltdown sometimes gets him what he wants, which we can work on together. Is it the same at school and with other adults?","m":[2,3,9]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"School's worse. He kicked the teaching assistant when she took him out of PE. At my mum's he's better. She's very firm."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"That's really useful. What works at home, even a little?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Bedtime's OK when I read to him. And he's lovely when it's just us baking."},{"t":"1:45","band":"Other causes","w":"D","s":"Those are real strengths to build on. How's his attention: sitting still, waiting his turn, finishing things?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Terrible. He can't sit through tea. His teacher says he's always out of his seat and shouting out."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Has that been there since he was little, and everywhere, even at your mum's?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Since nursery. Even at Mum's he's on the go."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"How are his talking, reading and friendships? Does noise upset him, or does he need things done a set way?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"His speech is a bit unclear; strangers struggle. Reading's behind. He has friends but falls out. Noise doesn't bother him."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Has his hearing been checked? Lots of ear infections?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"He had glue ear at four. Never got re-tested."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"And the pregnancy and birth, any problems? Did he walk and talk on time?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"He came at 35 weeks. Walking was fine. Talking was a bit late."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Jayden, that truck is amazing. Could you build it a garage at the table over there?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","who":"Jayden","s":"OK! A massive one."},{"t":"3:00","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"(quietly) I ask every parent this. Is anyone at home ever frightened, or has anyone hurt you or Jayden?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"(pauses) His dad. We split two years ago. He used to throw things and shout. Jayden saw a lot of it. (voice cracks) I feel awful about that."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Leaving took courage, and none of that was your fault. Is he in contact now, and do you both feel safe at home?","m":[7,9]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"No contact. There's a non-molestation order. It's just me and Jayden now. We're safe."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. What Jayden saw may still feed his anger, so it goes into his plan. If anything changes, the National Domestic Abuse Helpline is a free, day and night, on 0808 2000 247. Is it safe for you to keep that number?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's fine."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"How are your own mood and sleep?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Rubbish. I cry most nights after he's in bed. I'm not going to do anything stupid. I'm just knackered."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting, and I'm glad you said it. Please see your GP about your own mood this week. I'll also ask the early help team about some practical support.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:15","band":"Explain","w":"P","s":"So, the tablet. Can he have something?"},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"I understand why you're asking. There isn't a tablet that fixes behaviour. Calming medicines mainly make children drowsy, with side effects like weight gain, and the meltdowns come back.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"So it's my fault, then. I'm a bad mum."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"No. Some children are much harder to parent than others, and you've done it alone after a really hard time. The best help is a parenting group that gives you extra tools for children like him.","m":[9,11]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"What does that actually involve?"},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"About ten to twelve parents meet once a week for ten to sixteen weeks, around two hours each time. You learn play, praise, clear instructions and calm consequences, then practise at home. Your mum could come too.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"I'm not sitting in a room being judged."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Lots of parents worry about that. Most say meeting others in the same boat was the best bit. What would make it hard to get there: times, childcare, transport?","m":[12,9]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"The school run. And I've no one to have him in the evenings."},{"t":"5:30","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Then I'll ask for a daytime group with a crèche. With your consent, I'll also send you and his teacher short questionnaires about behaviour and attention, to check for ADHD.","m":[12,4]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Yes, send them."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"I'll ask the GP for a hearing test and a speech and language assessment, and write to school asking the SENCO to plan support for him. Is that OK?","m":[5,12]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Please. They've just given up on him."},{"t":"6:00","band":"Check understanding","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"No tablets. A daytime parenting group with a crèche. Forms for me and school. Hearing test. GP for me."},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Spot on. I'll see you both in six to eight weeks, once the forms are back. If you ever feel unsafe or can't cope, call us or your GP sooner. Any questions?","m":[13,8]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you. I thought you'd just tell me off."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"You came asking for help, and that was exactly right. Jayden, can I see that garage before you go?","m":[13,1]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","who":"Jayden","s":"It's got a door that opens!"}],"marks":[{"c":"Greets mother and child, gives the boy an activity and acknowledges the mother's strain without blame","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes a before-during-after account of a typical meltdown","d":"Content"},{"c":"Compares behaviour at home, school and with other adults, and asks what already works","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens attention, restlessness and impulsivity across settings for ADHD","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens speech, hearing, reading, friendships and sensory or routine needs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about pregnancy, birth and early milestones","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about violence at home with the boy occupied, checks current safety and gives the helpline","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about the mother's mood and sleep and directs her to GP and early help","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates the mother's distress and guilt with specific empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains why there is no tablet for behaviour: sedation, side effects, no lasting change","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the parenting group: weekly for 10 to 16 weeks, about 2 hours, what is learned","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Solves practical barriers and, with consent, sends questionnaires to parent and school","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and closes with hearing, speech and school actions and a review in 6 to 8 weeks","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Warm, non-blaming assessment with a before-during-after history, screening for ADHD, language and abuse, and a clear, practical referral to a parenting group.","borderline":"Parent training explained, but screening for other causes or domestic abuse is thin, or barriers to attending are not solved.","fail":"Prescribes for behaviour, blames the mother, never asks about violence at home, or discharges without a plan."}},"c-5-3-3":{"setup":"It is Friday 16:45 in the eating disorders clinic. Chloe, 14, fainted at school today; she is about 72% of median BMI with a pulse of 42. Assess medical risk, decide tonight's setting, and explain family therapy to her and her parents.","role":{"who":"Chloe Bennett, 14, female, patient (with her parents, Mrs Rachel Bennett and Mr Mark Bennett)","open":"I'm fine. I only fainted because it was boiling in the hall.","manner":"Guarded and minimising, then frightened and angry about hospital. Reluctantly agrees once her fears are heard. Mother anxious and guilty; father practical, pushing to take her home.","free":["Fainted standing up in assembly today; felt her heart 'fluttery' first","She does not think she is thin","Parents want to take her home for the weekend"],"ifasked":["Did not hit her head; no chest pain","Ate an apple and soup yesterday; only about a litre of water today","Runs every morning and does sit-ups at night; no vomiting, laxatives or diet pills","Periods stopped in July","Scratched her arm once in the summer; no current thoughts of self-harm or suicide","Parents: she cuts up and hides food and leaves the table early","No bowel symptoms, thirst, heat intolerance or past illness","Mother has dieted on and off for years"],"worries":["Can we take her home and bring her back on Monday?","They'll make me eat loads and I'll get fat","Did I give her this? (mother)","What is family therapy? Are we on trial? (father)"],"rules":["Chloe refuses hospital at first; agrees reluctantly if her fear is acknowledged and she is involved","Mother becomes tearful about her own dieting; settles if reassured without blame","Father pushes to go home until the specific risks are explained","Chloe asks if her mum can stay on the ward"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Chloe, I'm Dr Lewis, one of the eating disorder doctors. Mr and Mrs Bennett, hello. First I need to check Chloe is safe after today's faint, then we'll talk about tonight. Chloe, is it OK to start with you?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"I'm fine. I only fainted because it was boiling in the hall."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"OK. Tell me what happened. Did you bang your head, get chest pain, or feel your heart racing?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"I stood up in assembly and everything went black. I didn't hit my head. My heart felt fluttery just before."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Nurse, could we have lying and standing pulse and blood pressure, a temperature, a finger-prick sugar and a 12-lead ECG now, please?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Lying pulse 42, BP 88 over 50. Standing, it went up to 66 and she felt dizzy. Temperature 35.6. Sugar's 4.1. ECG's next."},{"t":"1:00","band":"Medical risk","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Chloe, your heart is beating very slowly, it jumps a lot when you stand, and you're cold. Your weight is about 72% of what's typical for your age and height. Together, that worries me.","m":[3,9]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"I'm not even thin. Everyone in my class is smaller than me."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"I hear that you don't feel thin, and that's one of the cruel things this illness does. But your heart and your faint tell me your body is struggling, whatever your size.","m":[8,3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"(quietly) OK."},{"t":"1:35","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"What did you eat and drink yesterday and today?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"An apple yesterday, and some soup. Today just water. About a litre."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Do you ever make yourself sick, take laxatives or diet pills, or exercise to burn calories?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"No sick or pills. I run every morning. And sit-ups at night."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"And have your periods stopped?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Since July."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest; this is a lot to talk about. When things feel like this, do you ever think of hurting yourself, or that you'd be better off dead?","m":[6,8]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"I don't want to die. I just want everyone to leave me alone. I scratched my arm once in the summer."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Any thoughts of hurting yourself now?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"No. Not now."},{"t":"2:35","band":"Parents","w":"D","s":"Mrs Bennett, Mr Bennett, what have you noticed at mealtimes?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"She cuts everything up and hides it. She's gone from the table before we've sat down. We've tried everything."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Any other illness: bowel problems, being very thirsty, weeing a lot, feeling hot and shaky?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"Nothing. She's never been ill."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"That helps. We'll still send bloods for other causes and for safety: salts, phosphate, magnesium, sugar, liver, blood count, thyroid and coeliac.","m":[2,7]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"(tearful) I've been on diets her whole life. Did I give her this?"},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you for saying that. No parent causes an eating disorder alone; genes and temperament play a big part. What we do know is that parents are the most powerful part of getting better.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"3:30","band":"Decision","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"So can we take her home for the weekend? We'll make sure she eats, and bring her back Monday."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"I can tell how much you want to care for her yourselves. But the faint, a pulse of 42 and her weight together put her at high risk tonight. She could collapse or get a dangerous heart rhythm. She needs to be on the children's ward tonight.","m":[9,3,8]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"No. I'm not going to hospital. You can't make me."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"I know this isn't what you wanted. I can't agree to you going home tonight, because your heart is in danger. You and your parents will be part of every decision. What worries you most about the ward?","m":[9,13,8]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"They'll make me eat loads and I'll get fat."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"That sounds really scary. The aim is to make your heart safe, not to make you fat. A dietitian plans the meals carefully and builds them up step by step.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Do I even get a choice?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Your views really matter, and I'd much rather we agree. Your parents can consent for you too. If you refused something essential, my consultant would talk with you all about what the law allows. I hope it won't come to that.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"(long pause) Fine. But I'm not happy about it."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"That's honest, and fair. Thank you.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:45","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"When you start eating again, salts like phosphate can drop suddenly. That's called refeeding syndrome. So the ward gives vitamins, checks your blood every day for the first days, and adjusts rather than cuts the food.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"And after hospital? What then?"},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"The main treatment is family therapy for anorexia. For a while you two take charge of meals at home, with our team coaching you, then hand control back to Chloe as she recovers. It's about eighteen to twenty sessions over a year.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"So we're on trial?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Not at all. It's the best-proven treatment for teenagers, and it works because it puts you on Chloe's side against the illness.","m":[10,8]},{"t":"5:35","band":"Handover","w":"D","s":"(on the phone) Hi, it's Dr Lewis from the eating disorder team. 14-year-old girl, probable anorexia, 72% median BMI, faint today, pulse 42, postural rise, temperature 35.6. High risk on MEED. Bloods sent. Can you admit her for stabilisation, with a dietitian refeeding plan and daily phosphate, magnesium and potassium?","m":[12,11]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","who":"Paediatric registrar","s":"Yes, send her up. What's the QTc?"},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"QTc 440, sinus bradycardia, I'll send the ECG up. I'm her eating disorder link; we'll review her daily from tomorrow, and my consultant knows.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Chloe, Mr and Mrs Bennett, can you tell me in your words what happens now?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"She goes to the children's ward tonight. They feed her slowly and check her blood every day. Then family therapy with your team."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Exactly. On the ward, if she gets breathless, swollen ankles or confused, tell the nurses straight away. I'll see you all tomorrow. Any questions?","m":[13,11]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Can Mum stay with me?"},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Yes, a parent can stay. I'll walk up with you now.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains safety comes first, and speaks to the girl directly","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Requests lying and standing pulse and BP, temperature, glucose, ECG and full bloods","d":"Content"},{"c":"Uses percentage median BMI and MEED high-risk markers rather than raw BMI","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about the faint: head injury, chest pain and palpitations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about food and fluid intake, vomiting, laxatives, exercise and periods","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about self-harm and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks parents about mealtimes and other illness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates the girl's fear and the parents' guilt with specific empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Decides she cannot go home tonight and explains the specific risks in plain words","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains family therapy for anorexia: parents lead meals, about a year, no blame","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Ensures a dietitian refeeding plan with daily phosphate, magnesium and potassium","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Hands over clearly to the paediatric registrar and names the eating disorder link","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Addresses consent and refusal, checks understanding and closes with next steps","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Recognises high MEED risk from pulse, faint and %mBMI, secures paediatric admission with refeeding monitoring, and explains family therapy warmly to all three.","borderline":"Admits her safely, but omits key observations or bloods, the refeeding plan, or the self-harm question.","fail":"Relies on BMI alone, lets her go home for the weekend, or blames the parents."}},"c-5-4-1":{"setup":"It is Saturday 22:15 in A&E. Jade, 15, cut her arms tonight. With her mother out of the room, she says her stepfather 'comes into my room at night' and begs you not to tell. Respond, explain confidentiality, and start safeguarding tonight.","role":{"who":"Jade Hughes, 15, female, patient","open":"Please don't tell anyone. He'll kill me. And Mum will hate me.","manner":"Frightened, tearful and ashamed; angry when told the secret cannot be kept. Calmer once she knows she will not go home tonight and will be told each step. Mother returns confused and upset.","free":["Her stepfather 'comes into my room at night'","She is terrified he will hurt her if he finds out","She thinks her mum will hate her","She cut her arms at home tonight"],"ifasked":["It happens when Mum works nights; he sits on the bed and touches her; since the summer","He is at home now with her brother Liam, aged 8","She does not know whether Liam has been harmed","Her friend Abby knows; Mum and school do not","She cut to 'feel something else', not to die; sometimes wants to disappear","No plan to harm herself tonight as long as she does not go home"],"worries":["Will I have to tell the police everything?","Is Liam going to be taken away?","Can Mum stay, but not be told yet?","What if I say I made it up?"],"rules":["Refuses to give further detail and becomes distressed if pushed with closed or leading questions","Cries when told the secret can't be kept; settles if the doctor explains kindly who will be told and why","Asks for Mum but does not want her told yet","Mother demands to take her home and is upset at not being told why"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Receive the disclosure","w":"D","s":"Jade, thank you for telling me. You've done the right thing. I'm listening, and I'm not cross with you.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Please don't tell anyone. He'll kill me. And Mum will hate me."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"I can hear how frightened you are. I need to be honest with you straight away: I can't keep this a secret, because it's about your safety. I'll only tell people whose job is to protect you, and I'll tell you each step before it happens.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"(crying) Then I wish I hadn't said anything."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"I understand why you feel that. Telling was brave, and none of this is your fault. You're not in any trouble.","m":[4]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"(wipes her eyes) OK."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"You said he comes into your room at night. Tell me a bit more about that, in your words?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"When Mum's on nights. He sits on my bed and touches me. Since the summer. I don't want to say any more."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"You don't need to say any more tonight. I'm going to write down the words you've used, exactly as you said them, and the time.","m":[2,12]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Will I have to tell the police all of it?"},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Not tonight, and not to me. Specially trained officers talk with young people later, in a calm room, at your pace, with someone you trust nearby.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:40","band":"Safety now","w":"D","s":"Is he here in the hospital now?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"No. He's at home with my brother. Mum brought me in."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"How old is your brother? Have you ever worried about him?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Liam's eight. I don't think so. But I don't know."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll make sure the people helping you think about Liam's safety too.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Is Liam going to be taken away?"},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"I can't promise what will happen, but their job is to keep you both safe, and they'll explain each step. Does anyone else know: Mum, a teacher, a friend?","m":[6,3]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"My friend Abby. Not Mum."},{"t":"2:25","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"How are you feeling right now? Are you thinking of hurting yourself again, or of ending your life?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I just want to disappear. But I wasn't trying to die tonight. I cut so I'd feel something else."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being so honest. Do you have any thoughts or plans to hurt yourself tonight, here?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"No. Not if I'm not going home."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"You won't be going home tonight. You'll stay on the children's ward, with nurses there all night, and he won't be allowed to visit. What would help you feel safer?","m":[10,13]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Can Mum stay? But please don't tell her yet."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"That's a fair thing to ask. A social worker will help decide how and when Mum is told, with support for her too. For now I won't share any details with her. I'm going to make some calls, then come straight back.","m":[11,3]},{"t":"3:25","band":"Calls","w":"D","s":"(on the phone) Hello, I'm the CAMHS doctor in A&E. A 15-year-old girl has told me her stepfather comes into her room at night and touches her, since the summer. She self-harmed tonight. He's at home with her eight-year-old brother. Mum is here and doesn't know. I'm admitting her to the children's ward. Can we have an urgent strategy discussion?","m":[8,6]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","who":"Out-of-hours social worker","s":"Thank you. Your reference is 4471. We'll set up a strategy discussion tonight with police, including the brother. Please report it to police too, and don't give Mum details yet."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"Will do. I'll send the written referral in the morning. I'll tell the ward the stepfather mustn't visit.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","who":"Out-of-hours social worker","s":"Yes. No contact from him on the ward tonight."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"(on the phone) Hello, I'm reporting a disclosure of sexual abuse of a 15-year-old girl by her stepfather. Children's social care are aware, reference 4471. She's safe on the children's ward.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","who":"Police call handler","s":"Thank you, Doctor. Our log number is 1188. A child protection officer will join the strategy discussion."},{"t":"4:35","band":"Mother returns","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"What's going on? Why can't I take her home? I'm her mother."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"I understand this is confusing and worrying. Jade is going to stay on the children's ward tonight so we can look after her properly. A social worker will talk with you as soon as possible to explain more.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Is it the cutting? Is she being sectioned?"},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"No, she's not being sectioned. I know it's hard not having answers yet. Please stay, and the ward staff will look after you both.","m":[11,4]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"(upset) Fine. I'll sit with her."},{"t":"5:15","band":"Escalate and record","w":"D","s":"(on the phone) Dr Okafor, it's the CAMHS registrar. A 15-year-old has disclosed sexual abuse by her stepfather. Social care reference 4471, police log 1188. The paediatric consultant has agreed admission; stepfather not to visit. I'll inform the safeguarding team in the morning.","m":[12,10]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","who":"CAMHS consultant","s":"Good. Write her exact words with times, and every call you've made. I'll come in first thing."},{"t":"5:40","band":"Back with Jade","w":"D","s":"Jade, I've spoken to the social worker and the police. A social worker will see you tomorrow, and you'll go up to the ward soon. Your stepfather won't be allowed onto the ward.","m":[13,10]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Did you tell Mum?"},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"No. Mum knows you're staying on the ward, nothing else. The social worker will help with that, and you'll know before it happens.","m":[13,11]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"What if I say I made it up?"},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Lots of young people feel like taking it back when they're scared. People will keep helping you either way. Can you tell me what happens next, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[13,4]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"I go to the ward. A social worker comes tomorrow. He can't come in."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"That's right. A nurse will stay close tonight, and I'll come and see you on the ward before I finish.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"OK. Thank you for listening to me."}],"marks":[{"c":"Stays calm, thanks her and uses open prompts in her own words","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Avoids leading or detailed questioning and explains that trained officers will talk later","d":"Content"},{"c":"Does not promise secrecy; explains kindly why, with whom, and that she will be told each step","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her fear and guilt and says clearly it is not her fault","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks where the stepfather is now and her immediate safety","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about other children at home and raises the brother with social care","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about self-harm and suicidal thoughts tonight","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Phones out-of-hours children's social care with a clear handover and logs the reference","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Reports to police the same night and logs the reference","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges paediatric admission as a place of safety with no visits from the stepfather","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Responds to the mother kindly without disclosing details before social care advise","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Records her exact words and times, and informs both consultants and the safeguarding team","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains next steps to her, checks understanding and closes with support tonight","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Receives the disclosure calmly, never promises secrecy, checks safety and siblings, and calls social care and police tonight with a paediatric admission.","borderline":"Safeguarding started, but questioning is leading, the sibling or self-harm risk is missed, or references and verbatim notes are not recorded.","fail":"Promises confidentiality, interrogates her, tells the mother or stepfather, or sends her home."}},"c-5-4-2":{"setup":"You are in the CAMHS clinic. Sam, 15, is still depressed after 8 weeks of CBT and wants to start fluoxetine. His mother agrees; his separated father has phoned to refuse. Assess his competence, clarify parental responsibility and agree a safe plan.","role":{"who":"Sam Fletcher, 15, male, patient (mother, Mrs Fletcher, waits outside; father, Mr Fletcher, by phone)","open":"Yeah. I want to try it. My dad's kicking off about it though.","manner":"Quiet, flat but thoughtful; clear about what he wants. Briefly hopeless about 'being broken'. Father angry and protective on the phone, softening when his worries are taken seriously.","free":["Eight weeks of CBT and still very low","Wants to try the medicine; knows it is an antidepressant","Dad is against it; Mum says it is his choice"],"ifasked":["Knows it is one tablet daily and takes a few weeks to work","Names nausea, headaches and poor sleep as side effects","Knows the alternative is carrying on with CBT alone and that he may stay the same or get worse; missing school","Sometimes thinks it would be easier not to be here; no plans or acts; his little sister keeps him going","Happy for Dad to know he wants it and the plan, not the suicidal thoughts unless he is unsafe","Mother: parents were married when he was born; separated three years; no court orders"],"worries":["Will I have to take it forever? Will I get hooked?","I don't want Mum and Dad fighting about me","Father: these pills make kids suicidal","Father: I have parental responsibility, so I decide and I'm entitled to know everything"],"rules":["Sam answers clearly if asked open questions; says 'maybe I'm just broken' and settles if validated","Discloses passive suicidal thoughts only if asked directly","Father refuses at first; agrees to attend the next appointment if his fear is acknowledged and the law explained plainly","Father insists his objection is recorded"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Sam, I'm Dr Achebe. Thanks for seeing me on your own first. We're here to talk about fluoxetine, the medicine the team suggested. What you say stays between us unless I'm worried about your safety. OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I want to try it. My dad's kicking off about it though."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"We'll come to your dad. First, in your words, why are we suggesting this medicine?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Because I've done CBT for eight weeks and I'm still really low. It's an antidepressant. It's meant to lift your mood."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Understanding","w":"D","s":"That's right. How would you take it, and how soon might it help?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"One a day? The nurse said it takes a few weeks."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"Yes, often two to four weeks or more. We'd start at 10 mg in the morning and maybe go up to 20 after a week. What side effects might happen?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Feeling sick. Headaches. Not sleeping."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"Good. There's one more important one. In the first few weeks, a small number of young people get more suicidal thoughts, or feel restless. What would you do if that happened?","m":[3,6]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Tell Mum. Or ring the crisis number."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Exactly. What other options are there, and what might happen if you don't take it?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Keep doing CBT and wait. But I might stay like this, or get worse. I'm already missing school."},{"t":"1:45","band":"Weighing up","w":"D","s":"How are you weighing it up? What matters most to you?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"I just want to feel normal again. If it doesn't help, I can stop. And I'm keeping the CBT."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"And is anyone pushing you either way?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Mum says it's my choice. Dad says it's poison. (sighs) I've done CBT for weeks and nothing's changed. Maybe I'm just broken."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"You're not broken. You've stuck at therapy when it was hard. Some people need medicine as well, to lift their mood enough for the therapy to work.","m":[12]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Thanks."},{"t":"2:30","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"How low does it get? Any thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life at the moment?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think it'd be easier not to be here. I've never done anything. I wouldn't, because of my little sister."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That must be hard to carry. Have you made any plans, or thought about how?","m":[6,12]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Let's write a safety plan: your warning signs, what helps, Mum, then the CAMHS duty line, NHS 111's mental health option, or 999. Mum would keep the tablets. OK?","m":[6,11]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Yeah, that's fine."},{"t":"3:15","band":"Parents","w":"D","s":"Sam, you've understood what this medicine is for, its side effects, the other options and the risks of not having it, and you've made the choice yourself. That means you can consent to this. What would you like me to share with your dad?","m":[5,8,10]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"That I want it, and the plan. Not the stuff about wishing you weren't here. Unless you have to."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"That's fair. I'd only share that if I thought you were unsafe, and I'd tell you first.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"(to his mother) Mrs Fletcher, thanks for waiting. A quick check: were you and Sam's dad married when he was born? Any court orders?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Yes, we were married. We split three years ago. No court orders. He sees Sam every other weekend."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"So you both have parental responsibility. Sam is competent to decide this, and he consents, so that stands. But I'd really like his dad on board, so I'll ring him now.","m":[7,10]},{"t":"4:10","band":"Father","w":"D","s":"(on the phone) Mr Fletcher, it's Dr Achebe from CAMHS. Thanks for calling earlier. I'd like to hear your worries.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"I'm his father. I refuse permission. I'm not having my son on mind drugs. I've read they make kids suicidal."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much you care about him, and that worry is reasonable. In the first weeks a small number of young people do get more suicidal thoughts. That's why we'd start low and see him every week.","m":[9,3]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"But I've got parental responsibility. Doesn't that mean I decide?"},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"You do have parental responsibility, and your views matter. But under 16, if a young person fully understands a decision, the law lets him consent himself. Sam does understand it, and he wants to try.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"And I'm entitled to know everything he says."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"Because he's competent, he has a right to confidentiality too. He's happy for me to share the plan, and I'd always tell you if I thought he was unsafe. Could you come to his next appointment?","m":[8,9]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"(pause) Fine. I'll come. But I want it on record I'm not happy."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"I'll record that, and that we spoke today. Thank you for hearing me out.","m":[9,13]},{"t":"5:30","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Sam, your dad's coming next time. Here's the plan: fluoxetine 10 mg each morning, maybe 20 after a week, CBT carries on, and someone sees or calls you every week for the first four weeks.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Do I have to take it forever? Will I get hooked?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"It isn't addictive. If it helps, we'd keep it going for about six months after you feel well, then reduce it slowly together. Don't stop it suddenly.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"6:05","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the plan back, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Ten milligrams in the morning. Weekly calls. Tell Mum or ring the crisis line if I feel worse. Keep going with CBT."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll write up how you made this decision, and agree it with my consultant before the first prescription today. If you feel worse, use your safety plan straight away. Any questions?","m":[13,6]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"No. Thank you for ringing his dad."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Of course. See you next week, Sam.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, sees him alone and explains confidentiality and its limits","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks his understanding of purpose, dose and time to work","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks he understands side effects, including early suicidal thoughts","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks he understands alternatives and the risk of no treatment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks he weighs the options and chooses freely without pressure","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts and plans, and agrees a written safety plan","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Confirms who holds parental responsibility and any court orders","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees with him what can be shared with his father","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Phones the father the same day, hears and validates his worries, and offers a meeting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains correctly that a competent under-16's consent stands despite one parent objecting","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees fluoxetine 10 mg then 20 mg with ongoing CBT and weekly contact for 4 weeks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his distress with a specific empathic statement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Documents the competence assessment and consultant agreement, checks understanding and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Assesses Gillick competence step by step for fluoxetine, clarifies parental responsibility, handles the father respectfully and agrees a monitored plan.","borderline":"Competence assessed, but early suicidality, confidentiality with the father or weekly monitoring is missed.","fail":"Lets the father's objection decide, uses Fraser or the MCA wrongly, or prescribes without risk checks and monitoring."}},"c-5-4-3":{"setup":"You are in the CAMHS clinic on Monday. Amira, 17, has ADHD and recurrent depression and takes methylphenidate from CAMHS. She turns 18 in four months; the adult team says she 'may not meet threshold'. Plan a safe transition with her and her father.","role":{"who":"Ms Amira Hassan, 17, female, patient (with her father, Mr Yusuf Hassan)","open":"My tablets, mainly. And seeing Jo, my nurse. But I'm scared I'll just get dropped on my birthday and lose my medication.","manner":"Anxious but articulate; brightens once she sees a concrete plan. Father frustrated with services and protective; settles when listened to and given clear steps.","free":["Fears losing care and medication at 18","Methylphenidate helps her focus; Jo, her CAMHS nurse, has helped most","Starting university in September","Father angry that the adult team says she is 'not ill enough'"],"ifasked":["Mood is OK at the moment, better than last year","Cut herself at 15; no self-harm or suicidal thoughts since","Early warning signs: stops replying to messages, sleeps all day, stops tablets","Takes methylphenidate modified-release 36 mg each morning","Going to Leeds, living in halls; had an EHC plan at school that has ended; manages day to day","Learning to drive","Happy for her father to know appointments and the plan, not everything she says"],"worries":["Will I be dropped and lose my tablets on my birthday?","If I get low at uni, no one will notice","Does ADHD matter for driving?","Father: you can't shut me out because she's 18"],"rules":["Becomes more anxious if the plan sounds vague; settles with dates and named people","Father challenges information sharing; calms if told he will always be listened to","Asks about driving only after uni is discussed","Agrees to share appointments and the plan with her father"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Amira, Mr Hassan. I'm Dr Clarke. You turn 18 in four months, so today is about planning your move to adult services. Amira, it's your plan, so I'll mostly ask you. What's helped most in CAMHS?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"My tablets, mainly. And seeing Jo, my nurse. But I'm scared I'll just get dropped on my birthday and lose my medication."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"I understand why it feels like that, especially after the adult team's letter. We won't discharge you until someone else has taken over. What else worries you about turning 18?","m":[2,1]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Starting again with new people. And I'm going to uni in September. If I get low there, no one will notice."},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"And the adult team say she's not ill enough. So she has to get worse first?"},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"That's a fair frustration, Mr Hassan. I'll ask them for their decision in writing and discuss it with my consultant. If they still say no, I'll ask for a meeting of everyone involved.","m":[11,2]},{"t":"1:15","band":"How she is now","w":"D","s":"Amira, how's your mood at the moment?","m":[8]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Alright, actually. Better than last year."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Any self-harm or thoughts about ending your life lately? I know you cut yourself when you were fifteen.","m":[8]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"No. Not since then. I just worry it'll come back when things get stressful."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"That's an important worry. What are your early warning signs that you're getting low?","m":[12,2]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"I stop replying to messages. I sleep all day. I stop taking my tablets."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Really useful. Those go into a written relapse plan, with who to call at uni. And your medicine is still methylphenidate, 36 mg each morning?","m":[12,6]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Yes, the long-acting one. It really helps me focus."},{"t":"2:10","band":"Information sharing","w":"D","s":"Something changes at 18. Your dad won't automatically be told about your care. What would you like us to share with him?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Appointments and the plan, yeah. Not every single thing I say."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"That's your choice. I'll record it, and you can change it any time.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"Hang on. I'm her dad. You can't shut me out just because she's 18."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"You're clearly very important to her, and she wants you involved; that's now in her notes. If you're ever worried about her safety, call us. We can always listen, even when we can't share.","m":[3,2]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"(sighs) OK. I just don't want her falling through the cracks."},{"t":"2:55","band":"Life plans","w":"D","s":"That's exactly what we're here to stop. Amira, tell me about uni. Where, and where will you live?","m":[9,2]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Leeds, in halls. I've got a firm offer."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Did you have an EHC plan at school, or do you need help with day-to-day things like cooking, money or getting up?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"I had one at school, it's finished. I manage fine day to day. Just forgetful."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Then an adult social care assessment probably isn't needed. The uni disability service is worth contacting; they can arrange extra time, and you may get Disabled Students' Allowance. Telling them is your choice.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"OK. And I'm learning to drive. Does the ADHD matter?"},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Good question. You must tell the DVLA if your ADHD or mood affects your ability to drive safely. Taken as prescribed, the medicine often helps concentration. Never drive if you feel drowsy or unwell, and we'll check the current DVLA guidance together.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Right."},{"t":"4:00","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Here's the plan. Jo will be your named transition worker from today. This week I'll refer you to the adult ADHD service and the adult mental health team, with a full summary, your risk history and your dose.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"But the ADHD waiting list is like two years."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"It's long. That's why I'm asking your GP to take over prescribing now under a shared care agreement, with checks of pulse, blood pressure and weight every six months. CAMHS keeps prescribing until the GP confirms.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"What if the GP says no?"},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Then CAMHS carries on prescribing and I take it to my consultant. She won't be left without a prescription.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"OK. That's actually reassuring."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"We'll also hold a joint meeting in about two months, with you, Jo and someone from the adult team, so you meet them before you move over.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Can it be before September? I'll be away."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Yes, we'll set it for July. Register with a GP in Leeds in your first week, and I'll give you a summary letter to take.","m":[7,4]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"5:25","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"If you get low again or think of harming yourself, use your relapse plan. Before 18, ring our CAMHS crisis line. After that, the adult crisis line or NHS 111 option 2, or 999 in an emergency. It'll all be written down.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Got it."},{"t":"5:45","band":"Check understanding","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what happens next?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Jo's my worker. You refer me to adult services and ask the GP to take over my tablets. Meeting in July. Register with a GP in Leeds. And Dad gets told about appointments."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. I'll put this in a written plan with dates, copied to you, your GP and the adult teams.","m":[13,4]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","who":"Father","s":"Thank you. That's the first time anyone's explained it properly."},{"t":"6:20","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. I'll see you in six weeks to check the replies have come back. Amira, any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"No. I feel a lot better about it."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Good. Planning early is the hard part, and you've done it. See you in six weeks.","m":[13,1]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, treats her as the main decision-maker and asks what helps and what worries her","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her fear of being dropped and her father's frustration with specific statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains information sharing at 18 and records her consent about her father","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names a transition worker and sets a written plan with dates, starting now","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Refers to adult ADHD and adult mental health services with summary, risk history and dose","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges GP shared care with monitoring so methylphenidate continues without a gap","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges a joint meeting with the adult team before transfer","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Reviews current mood, past self-harm and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about university, living plans, EHC plan and day-to-day care needs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Covers driving and her duty to tell the DVLA","d":"Content"},{"c":"Responds to the threshold refusal: decision in writing, consultant, professionals meeting","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a written relapse plan with warning signs and adult crisis contacts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding and closes with a review date and questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Puts her at the centre, secures medication continuity via shared care, refers early with a named worker and joint meeting, and handles consent at 18 correctly.","borderline":"Referrals made, but medication handover, consent to share or the relapse plan is vague.","fail":"Discharges into a gap, shares freely with the father after 18 without consent, or ignores risk and practical adult issues."}},"c-6-1-1":{"setup":"Medium secure admission ward, Wednesday 2 pm. Your consultant wants your plan for a structured violence risk assessment on Mr Reid, 31, transferred from prison under Sections 47/49, ready for Friday's CPA review. Talk it through and agree the plan.","role":{"who":"Dr Helen Marsh, 52, female, consultant forensic psychiatrist and his RC; later joined by Dr Sam Kerr, ward psychologist","open":"Come in. So, Mr Reid. Friday's CPA is coming fast. Where would you start?","manner":"Brisk and testing, pushing once for a simple score. Becomes candid and a little upset about a past assault on a nurse; warms when the plan has names and dates.","free":["Mr Reid, 31, paranoid schizophrenia, transferred from prison two weeks ago under Sections 47/49","Serving a sentence for wounding his neighbour","Calm on the ward and says he has 'done his time'","The team want a draft formulation for Friday's CPA review"],"ifasked":["At the offence he had stopped his depot about two months earlier and was smoking cannabis daily","He believed the neighbour was poisoning his water","Two assaults on staff between ages 19 and 24, both when unwell","Prison notes mention a fight on the wing last year","His mother is supportive and willing to talk by phone","MAPPA level not yet confirmed with the offender manager","The ward psychologist is trained in HCR-20 V3 and free on Thursday morning"],"worries":["Can't we just give the tribunal a score?","Will the records arrive before Friday?","The nurses are uneasy after a previous transferred patient assaulted a nurse on leave","Who owns each part of the plan?"],"rules":["Pushes once for a total score; accepts structured judgement if it is explained clearly","Becomes upset about the past assault on a nurse; settles if acknowledged","Expects names and dates, not 'we'll get round to it'","Calls the psychologist in when co-rating is raised"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Dr Marsh. Thanks for making time to go through Mr Reid's risk assessment.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Come in. So, Mr Reid. Friday's CPA is coming fast. Where would you start?"},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"First, what it's for. Is it mainly to support leave applications to the Ministry of Justice, or the tribunal as well? And are we aiming for a draft by Friday, then a full version later?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Both, eventually. Leave in a few months, and the tribunal will want it. So you'll tot up the HCR-20 and tell me if he's high risk?"},{"t":"0:40","band":"Approach","w":"D","s":"Not quite. HCR-20 version 3 is structured professional judgement, not a score. We rate each factor for whether it's present and whether it's relevant to him, then build a formulation, scenarios and a plan. There's no cut-off.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"The tribunal likes numbers, though."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"They do, but a total would mislead them. What helps is a clear summary: low, moderate or high priority, the scenarios we're worried about, and what we're doing about each one.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Fair enough. What will you base it on? He's very calm on the ward."},{"t":"1:20","band":"Sources","w":"D","s":"That's exactly why I won't rely on interview alone. I'd request five things: his prison healthcare notes, probation's OASys reports, the CPS papers and witness statements, his full list of previous convictions, and collateral from his mother, if he agrees.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Friday is two days away. When will you have those?"},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"I'll email prison healthcare and his offender manager today for the notes and OASys, and chase them by phone tomorrow at ten. The CPS papers and convictions list I'll request today through his solicitor and the police, and chase on Thursday. I'll ring his mother on Thursday afternoon.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"And if the CPS papers don't arrive in time?"},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Then Friday's version is clearly labelled a draft, with a list of what's outstanding. Nothing gets finalised without them.","m":[12]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"What do you know so far?"},{"t":"2:15","band":"Formulation","w":"D","s":"He wounded his neighbour with a knife. At the time he'd stopped his depot for about two months, was smoking cannabis every day, and believed the neighbour was poisoning his water. Before that, two assaults on staff in his early twenties, both when he was unwell.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"So how would you sum that up for the team?"},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Draft formulation: his serious violence has happened when he's psychotic, off his depot and using cannabis, and it's been driven by fear of a persecutor he felt he had to defend himself against.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Good. Clinical and risk management items?"},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Clinically, partial insight and some residual paranoia, but no current violent thoughts. Looking ahead, the big factors are stopping treatment, cannabis on leave, unstable housing and losing support. Protective factors are his mother, who's close to him, and that he took his depot reliably in prison.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Scenarios?"},{"t":"3:20","band":"Scenarios","w":"D","s":"Three. Repeat: he stops his depot, uses cannabis, becomes paranoid about a neighbour and attacks to defend himself. Warning signs: a missed or late depot, and accusing people of tampering with his food or drink.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"And the worse case?"},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Escalation: the same relapse, but he carries a weapon and targets staff he sees as part of a plot. Warning signs: carrying objects for protection, and withdrawing to his room and refusing to talk.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Best case?"},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"He stays on his depot and off cannabis, and moves to supported housing. Signs it's working: clean drug screens and keeping his psychology sessions.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"That escalation scenario is what worries me. Two years ago a transferred patient here attacked a nurse on her first escorted leave. I signed that leave. The nurses haven't forgotten, and nor have I."},{"t":"4:20","band":"Support","w":"D","s":"That sounds like it's stayed with you, and with the team. It makes sense they're uneasy. That's partly why I want these warning signs in his care plan, so every nurse knows what to look for and who to call.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Yes. Thank you. Let's make sure this one is solid."},{"t":"4:40","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"So, each plan item with an owner and a date. The depot continues, and his named nurse checks the depot card at every dose, reviewed at the weekly ward round. Urine drug screen after every leave by the ward nurses, reviewed monthly.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"And the psychology and social side?"},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"Psychology starts substance misuse work within two weeks. The social worker confirms his MAPPA level with the offender manager by next Wednesday. I'll add the warning signs to his care plan by Friday. The whole HCR-20 is reviewed in six months, or sooner after any incident.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"He'll want to see what we write."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"He should. I'll go through the draft with him before Friday, show the evidence for each point and record his own view alongside it.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Who rates it with you? You shouldn't do it alone."},{"t":"5:40","band":"Co-rating","w":"D","s":"Agreed. I'd like to co-rate it with Dr Kerr. Is she around?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"She's next door. Sam, have you got a minute?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","who":"Psychologist","s":"Hi. Is this Mr Reid's HCR-20?"},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"Yes. Could we co-rate it on Thursday, so the draft is ready for Friday's CPA? I'll bring whatever records have arrived.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","who":"Psychologist","s":"Thursday at ten works, after the community meeting."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Great, Thursday at ten in the psychology room. I'll send the invite now.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"And the Ministry of Justice?"},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Nothing goes to them until the plan is agreed. Then you'd apply for leave to the Mental Health Casework Section, with this assessment attached.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Good. Anything else?"},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Just to check I've covered what you need: purpose is leave and the tribunal; five sources requested with chase dates; the formulation; three scenarios with warning signs; owners and review dates; co-rating Thursday at ten. Does that match?","m":[11,13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"That's exactly it. Well done."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'll circulate the draft by Thursday evening.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Opens courteously and clarifies the purpose, the decision it informs and the deadline","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"States HCR-20 V3 is structured professional judgement with no total score or cut-off","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names at least five record sources, including family collateral, with a date to chase each","d":"Content"},{"c":"Covers historical, clinical, risk management and protective factors with examples","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives a formulation linking the wounding to psychosis, stopping the depot and cannabis","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Sets out repeat, escalation and best-case scenarios, each with two warning signs","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Acknowledges the consultant's and team's worry with a specific empathic statement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives every plan item a named owner and a review date","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Involves the patient in the draft and links leave to Ministry of Justice consent","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a co-rating date with the psychologist before Friday's CPA","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks the summary meets the consultant's needs","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety nets: draft labelled provisional, gaps listed, early review after any incident","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closes with a clear summary and when the draft will be sent","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Treats HCR-20 as structured judgement, gathers collateral with dates, formulates, writes three scenarios with warning signs and ties each to an owned, dated plan.","borderline":"Correct approach and sources, but scenarios lack warning signs or the plan has no owners or dates.","fail":"Adds up a score, relies on today's calm interview, or gives no link between the assessment and a management plan."}},"c-6-1-2":{"setup":"Prison healthcare centre, Thursday 10:30. Mr Carter, 26, on remand for assault, has refused food for two days, shouts at his cell wall and punched his cellmate. Assess his mental state and risk, decide on transfer, and agree a plan with the wing manager.","role":{"who":"Mr Liam Carter, 26, male, remand prisoner; later Mr Dave Price, healthcare wing manager","open":"Who are you? Another one of them?","manner":"Suspicious and guarded at the hatch, frightened rather than hostile. Opens up once he hears the doctor is NHS, not prison, and his fear is taken seriously.","free":["He won't eat because 'they' are putting something in the food","Voices talk about him through the wall at night","His cellmate 'was working with them'","He wants to be left alone"],"ifasked":["The voices say officers and the cellmate are poisoning him and will kill him first","He hit the cellmate because the voices said the cellmate would strike first","Only a little tap water; no food for two days","Smoked spice twice in his first week on remand, none for ten days","Thinks it would be easier to be dead; no plan, no past self-harm","No plan to hurt anyone, but will 'defend himself' if anyone enters his cell","Would accept sealed water and packet food","Never been in a psychiatric hospital"],"worries":["Will you tell the officers what I say?","Will hospital wreck my court case?","Why can't you just inject me here?"],"rules":["Stays guarded until the doctor makes clear they are NHS, not prison","Voice cracks when describing sleepless nights; settles if validated","Refuses servery food but accepts sealed items","Becomes agitated if told the food is definitely safe"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Carter? I'm Dr Ahmed, a doctor from the NHS mental health team. Is it okay if I talk with you through the hatch for a few minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Who are you? Another one of them?"},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"No. I work for the health service, not the prison. Officers are nearby for everyone's safety. What you tell me stays with the health team, unless I'm worried someone could get hurt. Then I'd share only what's needed, and I'd tell you.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Fine. Make it quick."},{"t":"0:40","band":"His experiences","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Officers say you haven't eaten for two days. What's been happening?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"I'm not eating their food. They're putting stuff in it."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Who's putting it in, and what do you think it is?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"The officers. Poison. I can taste it. The voices told me."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the voices. When do you hear them, and what do they say?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"At night, through the wall. Two men. They say I'm next. They're going to finish me."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Do they ever tell you to do things?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"They said he'd do me first. My cellmate."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Is that what happened last week, with your cellmate?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"He was working with them. I had to get in first."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"So you hit him because you believed he was about to kill you. That must have been terrifying, feeling hunted like that, day and night.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Every night. I can't sleep. I just lie there waiting for it. (voice cracks) I'm so tired."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"I'm really sorry. Nobody should have to feel that scared. I want to help you feel safer.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Nobody believes me."},{"t":"2:10","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"I believe you're frightened, and I'm taking it seriously. Are the voices saying anything now about anyone here?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Not now. But if anyone comes in my cell, I'll defend myself."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being straight with me. Is there anyone you're planning to hurt?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"No. Just leave me alone."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"With all this going on, have you had thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think it'd be easier to be dead. I wouldn't do anything."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Have you made any plans, or hurt yourself, in here or before?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Have you been using anything in here, like spice or tablets?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Spice, twice, my first week. Nothing for ten days."},{"t":"3:10","band":"Physical","w":"D","s":"Thanks. How much have you had to drink today?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"A bit of tap water."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"If I get you sealed bottled water and packet food, would you take those?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"If it's sealed. Yeah."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Good. I'd also like the GP to check your blood pressure and do a blood test today, to make sure your body is coping. Would you let them?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"If a nurse comes as well."},{"t":"3:45","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I'll make sure of that. I think your mind is under huge strain, and the voices and fears may be part of an illness. We can't treat it properly here. I want to get you a hospital bed.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Hospital? That'll wreck my court case."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"It won't decide your case. Your solicitor and the court will be told you're getting treatment. Meanwhile, would you try a tablet to help you sleep and settle the voices?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Why can't you just inject me here and sort it?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"The law doesn't allow treatment without consent in prison. So here it's your choice, and full treatment happens in hospital. Could you sum up for me what happens next?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Sealed food, the GP checks me, and you try to get me to hospital."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll come back tomorrow.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:50","band":"Plan with prison staff","w":"D","s":"Mr Price, thanks for waiting. He's psychotic. He believes the food is poisoned and that people are about to kill him. That's why he hit his cellmate.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","who":"Wing manager","s":"So what do you need from us?"},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"Keep him in a single cell on healthcare, no sharing. Check him every fifteen minutes for now, and review his ACCT today, because he's had thoughts of being dead.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","who":"Wing manager","s":"We can do that. He kicked off when two of my officers went in yesterday."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Always go in with two officers, explain calmly before you open the door, and don't argue about the food. Please start a food and fluid chart and offer sealed water and packet food.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","who":"Wing manager","s":"Fine. And medically?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"I'm asking the GP to see him today for obs, U&E and CK. If he looks dry or confused, or his kidney results are off, he goes to A&E.","m":[6,10]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","who":"Wing manager","s":"How long till he's moved?"},{"t":"6:05","band":"Transfer","w":"D","s":"I'm sending the Section 48 referral to the medium secure unit this afternoon, and the second doctor's assessment is booked for Monday. Then the Ministry of Justice issues a warrant. I'll chase if it drifts towards the 28-day standard.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","who":"Wing manager","s":"Right. Who do I call if he gets worse overnight?"},{"t":"6:30","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"The healthcare on-call, and 999 if he collapses or anyone's hurt. I'll write this up now and I'm back tomorrow at ten. Any questions?","m":[10,12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","who":"Wing manager","s":"No, that's clear."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Mr Price.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self as NHS, not prison staff, and explains limits of confidentiality","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits voices and persecutory beliefs and links them to food refusal and the assault","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his fear with specific empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks directly about current thoughts of harming others, including named people","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicide, self-harm and spice use","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Addresses fluids: offers sealed food and arranges same-day obs, U&E and CK via the GP","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains illness, need for hospital and no enforced treatment in prison, in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks his understanding of what happens next","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees with staff: single cell, observation frequency, food and fluid chart, two-officer approach","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety nets: clear triggers for A&E and who to call overnight","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Starts transfer: Section 48 referral today and second assessment booked","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes with documentation, review time and an invitation for questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Builds trust, links psychosis to the food refusal and assault, screens suicide and spice, gets physical checks today, starts Section 48 transfer and leaves staff a clear plan.","borderline":"Psychosis recognised and transfer started, but physical risk or spice is missed, or the staff plan is vague.","fail":"Labels it behavioural, suggests enforced treatment in prison, or ignores two days of food and fluid refusal."}},"c-6-1-3":{"setup":"Low secure rehabilitation ward, Monday 9:15 handover. Mr Doyle, 38, on Section 37/41, came back late from unescorted leave, missed psychology twice and was seen passing items. Review risk with the nurse in charge, decide today's leave and agree a security plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Grace Adeyemi, 45, female, nurse in charge; also Mr Kevin Doyle, 38, the patient, and Dr Shah, his RC, by phone","open":"Morning. I wanted you to hear about Kevin Doyle before he goes out on leave at eleven.","manner":"Calm and experienced, protective of a patient she has known for years. Upset at first about stopping leave before his tribunal; reassured once the plan is fair, open and clear.","free":["He was back 50 minutes late from ground leave on Saturday","Seen passing something to another patient on Sunday","Missed two psychology sessions","Unescorted ground leave is due at 11 today"],"ifasked":["A small wrapped package; not found; the other patient refused a search","Giggly with red eyes on return; said he'd lost track of time","Urine drug screen offered on Saturday; he declined","His mother visited last week and left upset; staff don't know why","The Ministry of Justice agreed unescorted ground leave three months ago","No incident report has been done yet"],"worries":["His tribunal is in six weeks; are we setting him back?","I don't want to wreck his trust in us","Who tells the Ministry of Justice?"],"rules":["Becomes upset ('I've worked with him four years') when suspension is raised; settles if validated","Accepts a pause once told it is not a punishment and is reviewable","The patient is angry about losing leave, then admits a debt only if asked privately and gently about pressure","The RC agrees a proportionate pause and asks who is telling the Ministry of Justice"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Morning, Grace. Thanks for flagging Kevin. Can we go through what's happened before his eleven o'clock leave?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Morning. I wanted you to hear about Kevin Doyle before he goes out on leave at eleven."},{"t":"0:20","band":"The facts","w":"D","s":"What exactly was seen being passed, and was it found?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"A small wrapped package, to Jamie, on Sunday. Not found. Jamie refused a search."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"And on Saturday, how late was he, and how did he seem when he got back?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Fifty minutes. Giggly, red eyes. Said he'd lost track of time."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"Was a urine drug screen done?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Offered. He said no."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Thanks, that's clear. Anything else changed for him lately?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"His mum visited last week and left upset. And he's missed psychology twice."},{"t":"1:10","band":"Weigh it up","w":"D","s":"Together, that's a cluster: late, possibly intoxicated, passing something, and pulling away. Those are warning signs in his HCR-20 plan. I think his unescorted leave should pause until Thursday's MDT.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"I've worked with him four years. He's never been this close to a tribunal. If we stop his leave, I'm scared we lose him."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much you care about him, and the trust you've built really matters. A pause isn't a punishment. It protects him and the progress he's made.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Okay. Put like that, yes."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"For a restricted patient it's the RC's call. I'll ring Dr Shah now.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Dr Shah, it's Dr Lee on Willow. Kevin Doyle was fifty minutes late from ground leave on Saturday, looked intoxicated and declined a drug screen. On Sunday he passed a package to a peer, and he's missed psychology twice. I suggest suspending unescorted leave until Thursday's MDT, with escorted leave if he's settled.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","who":"RC","s":"Agreed. Suspend unescorted, escorted can continue. Has the Ministry of Justice been told?"},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Not yet. Shall I draft the notification for you to send today?","m":[9]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","who":"RC","s":"Yes please. Get it to me by four."},{"t":"3:00","band":"Security plan","w":"D","s":"Grace, Dr Shah agrees. Three security steps. Physical: a room search this morning, with his consent if possible, by you and one other nurse.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Fine. We'll do it after handover."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Procedural: a urine screen including spice, offered today and after every leave for a month. Escorted leave only, two staff, until Thursday. The ward nurses own that.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"And relational?"},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"His named nurse, Tom, spends time with him today to find out what's going on, including his mum's visit. And psychology rebooks him this week.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Tom's on till eight. I'll ask him."},{"t":"3:55","band":"Talking with him","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Can we see Kevin together now? I'd like to tell him myself.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"What's this? I'm due out at eleven."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Kevin, I'm Dr Lee. We're worried about Saturday and a couple of other things, so Dr Shah has paused your unescorted leave until Thursday's meeting. Escorted leave can carry on.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"So I'm late once and you take my leave? That's a joke."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"I understand it feels unfair. It isn't a punishment. On Thursday we look at it again, and what helps is a drug screen, going to psychology, and talking with Tom.","m":[4,6]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"This'll wreck my tribunal."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"One setback handled well can count for you. Can I ask you something privately? Is anyone pressuring you, here or outside, to carry things or do things?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"(long pause) I owe someone. Money. I didn't know what else to do."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; I know that wasn't easy to say. We can keep you safe without naming you. Can we talk it through with Tom today?","m":[4,7]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"Yeah. Okay."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"So, in your words, what's happening with your leave, and what gets it back?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"Escorted only till Thursday. Do the test, go to psychology, talk to Tom."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Spot on. Thanks, Kevin.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:50","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Grace, he's in debt to someone, maybe Jamie. I'll raise a safeguarding concern, and please let the security lead know. Can you log it on Datix before the shift ends? I'll send the Ministry of Justice draft to Dr Shah by four.","m":[7,9]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"I'll do the Datix before two."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"If he refuses the search, seems paranoid or tries to leave, bleep me on 412 and tell Dr Shah. I'll write this up now, and we review it all at Thursday's MDT. Anything I've missed?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. That feels fair to him and safe for us."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Grace. Speak later.","m":[11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Opens with the nurse in charge and sets the aim before the planned leave","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gathers four facts: item passed, lateness, state on return and drug screen result","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees a proportionate leave decision with the RC for a restricted patient","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates the nurse's and patient's feelings with specific empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names one physical, one procedural and one relational security action, each with an owner","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains the pause to him as non-punitive, with clear steps to restore leave","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks him privately and directly about pressure or exploitation, and acts on the answer","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks his understanding of the leave decision","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Ensures the Ministry of Justice is told and Datix is logged before the shift ends","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety nets: clear triggers for calling the doctor and RC","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closes with a summary, documentation and the MDT review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gets the facts first, agrees a proportionate pause with the RC, sets physical, procedural and relational actions, asks about exploitation and makes sure the Ministry of Justice and Datix are done.","borderline":"Sensible leave decision, but security actions lack owners, or exploitation and the Ministry of Justice are missed.","fail":"Carries on as normal before the tribunal, acts punitively, or changes leave without the RC."}},"c-6-2-1":{"setup":"CMHT clinic, Tuesday 4 pm. Mr Bennett, 44, is convinced his wife is having an affair; he checks her phone, follows her and drinks heavily. Assess him and the risk, then see his wife, 40, alone. They have children aged 8 and 11.","role":{"who":"Mr Mark Bennett, 44, male, patient; then his wife, Mrs Sarah Bennett, 40, seen alone","open":"I don't know why I'm here. She's the one who should be seeing someone.","manner":"He is tense, aggrieved and certain, softening when his distress is acknowledged and guarded about violence. She is frightened and self-blaming, and relieved to be heard.","free":["He is sure she has been seeing a colleague for six months","Checks her phone every night and follows her to work","Can't sleep or concentrate: 'it's eating me alive'","Drinks to calm down"],"ifasked":["Checks her underwear; 'stains' are his proof; accepts no other explanation","About 70 units a week; heavy drinking for two years before the jealousy began","Shoved her into a door frame three weeks ago and punched a wall","Told her 'one way or another she'll admit it'","Hunting knife in the shed; no firearms","Has thought 'better if neither of us were here'; no plan","(Wife) Never choked; frightened of him; the 11-year-old has started bedwetting again"],"worries":["Whose side are you on?","(Wife) Is he going to hurt me?","(Wife) Is this my fault?","(Wife) Will social services take the children?"],"rules":["He gets angry if told the affair isn't real; settles if the doctor stays curious","Admits the shove only if asked directly and calmly","She cries about the children; settles when told it is not her fault","She agrees to the DASH questions once she understands why"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Bennett, I'm Dr Okoye, one of the psychiatry doctors. I'll talk with you first, and your wife has asked to see me separately afterwards. What you tell me is confidential, unless I'm worried someone is at serious risk.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"I don't know why I'm here. She's the one who should be seeing someone."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you're under real strain. Tell me what's been happening.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"She's having an affair. Six months. A bloke from work. I check her phone every night. I follow her to work. It's eating me alive."},{"t":"0:45","band":"His belief","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. What makes you so sure?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"The messages. And her underwear. I check it. There are stains. That's proof."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Might there be another explanation for what you've found?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"No. None. Don't you start."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"I'm not taking sides, I'm trying to understand. If she showed you every message and there was nothing, would that change your mind?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"She'd have deleted them. I know what I know."},{"t":"1:20","band":"Alcohol","w":"D","s":"How much do you drink in a typical week?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Lager most nights. Vodka at weekends. Seventy units, maybe."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Which came first, the heavy drinking or the worries about her?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"The drinking. A couple of years. This started in the spring."},{"t":"1:40","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When you confront her, what happens? Have you ever pushed, hit or threatened her?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"(pause) I shoved her into the door frame. Three weeks ago. Punched the wall."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Have you said anything to frighten her into confessing?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"I said one way or another she'll admit it."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"What did you mean by one way or another? Have you had thoughts of hurting her, or the man?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"I'd never kill her. I've thought about smashing his face in."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Are there any weapons at home?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"A hunting knife in the shed. No guns."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"With all this pain, have you thought it would be better if you weren't here, or if neither of you were?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Sometimes. Better if neither of us were. I wouldn't do anything."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"That sounds like an awful place to be, and I'm glad you told me. Would you let the knife go to your brother's for now, and see the alcohol team?","m":[5,9]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"If it gets her off my back. Yeah."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"I'm worried about safety at home, so I'll share some concerns with your wife and other services. I'll tell you what I share.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Whatever."},{"t":"3:20","band":"Escalate","w":"D","s":"Dr Shah, quick one. Forty-four-year-old man, fixed jealous belief that looks delusional, seventy units a week, shoved his wife, said she'll admit it one way or another, a knife at home, and thoughts that neither of them should be here. Children aged eight and eleven.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","who":"Consultant","s":"That's high risk. Children's social care referral today. Tell her what she needs to stay safe, not his whole history."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Agreed. I'll do a DASH with her, and refer to MARAC if it's high.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:00","band":"With his wife","w":"D","s":"Mrs Bennett, thanks for waiting. I can't share everything your husband told me, but I am worried about your safety. Do you feel safe at home?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","who":"Wife","s":"Is he going to hurt me?"},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"I can't predict that for sure, and I'm taking it seriously. Intense jealousy like this can be part of an illness, and drink makes it worse. Can I go through some safety questions with you? It's a checklist called DASH.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","who":"Wife","s":"Okay."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Has he ever hurt you, or tried to choke or strangle you? Are you frightened of what he might do?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","who":"Wife","s":"He shoved me. He's never choked me. I'm frightened, yes. Maybe it's my fault. I went for drinks with work once."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"This is not your fault. Nothing you did justifies being checked or followed.","m":[5]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","who":"Wife","s":"(crying) The kids hear all of it. Our eldest has started wetting the bed again."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"That must be so hard, holding it together for them. You're doing the right thing by talking to me today.","m":[5]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","who":"Wife","s":"Will social services take them?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"The aim is to support you and keep them safe, not to take them away. I'm making a referral to children's social care today, and I'll tell you what happens next.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","who":"Wife","s":"Okay."},{"t":"5:45","band":"Safety plan","w":"D","s":"Your answers put you at high risk, so I'm referring you to MARAC, a meeting of police, health and support services. Here's the National Domestic Abuse Helpline: 0808 2000 247, free, day and night. If he threatens you, call 999.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","who":"Wife","s":"Should I take the kids to my mum's?"},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"That's a sensible option for now if you can do it safely. We'll also offer him treatment, starting with the alcohol team.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","who":"Wife","s":"Thank you."},{"t":"6:25","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"To check I've been clear: what would you do if he became threatening tonight?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","who":"Wife","s":"Call 999, then the helpline. Go to Mum's."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll write to your GP and his, and someone from our team will call you tomorrow. Any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","who":"Wife","s":"No. Thank you for listening."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, plans separate interviews and states limits of confidentiality","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Tests how fixed the belief is and his evidence, without arguing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Records weekly alcohol units and whether drinking came before the jealousy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about violence, threats, weapons, homicidal thoughts and suicide","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates both partners' distress with specific empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Is honest with each partner about what will and will not be shared","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Discusses with the consultant and agrees disclosure and same-day safeguarding","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Completes DASH questions with the wife and identifies high risk","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Plans children's social care referral, alcohol team, weapon removal and separation option","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains morbid jealousy to the wife plainly, without disclosing his full history","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives the helpline number and clear 999 advice","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks the wife's understanding of her safety plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with follow-up, letters and an invitation for questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Tests the belief without arguing, asks about violence, weapons and homicide-suicide, links alcohol, agrees disclosure with the consultant and gives the wife DASH, helpline and a safeguarding referral.","borderline":"Good history from him, but suicide or weapons missed, or the wife gets reassurance without a concrete safety plan.","fail":"Argues with the belief, forgets the children, or tells the wife nothing or far too much."}},"c-6-2-2":{"setup":"A&E liaison room, Friday 11 am. Police found Ms Finch, 35, outside a TV presenter's home for the fourth time this month; she has sent him over 100 letters. Assess her beliefs and the risk to others and herself, and agree a plan with police.","role":{"who":"Ms Laura Finch, 35, female, patient; also PC James Hill, the officer who brought her","open":"I don't need a doctor. I need to get back before he finishes filming.","manner":"Bright, polite and certain, warm when talking about him. Irritable about his girlfriend. Tearful about being alone, and more open once she is not argued with.","free":["He sends her messages through his broadcasts","They will marry this summer","She has written over 100 letters","She goes to his house 'to make it easier for him'"],"ifasked":["Began eight months ago when he 'smiled at her' on air; blue ties are their sign","Four visits this month, daily emails to the studio, comments on all his posts; sent a watch and a birthday cake","His girlfriend is 'keeping him from me'; wants to confront her face to face","Shouted at a studio security guard","If he rejected her 'there'd be no point'; overdose in 2019 after a similar belief about her GP","Admitted for six weeks in 2019, diagnosed delusional disorder","Sleeps five hours; normal energy; no voices or passivity; no alcohol or drugs","Police warning letter last month, which she blames on the girlfriend"],"worries":["Are you going to tell the police what I said?","I'm not taking pills to stop loving someone","Can I go home today?"],"rules":["Becomes irritable if told he doesn't love her; settles if the doctor stays curious","Becomes tearful when rejection is raised; settles if validated","Mentions the 2019 overdose only if asked directly about ending her life","The officer wants to know what he can share and what happens next"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"PC Hill, before I see her, is she under arrest or on a Section 136?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","who":"PC","s":"Neither. She agreed to come in. The presenter's family are frightened. We'll need your view."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Rahman, a mental health doctor. The officer asked us to see you. What you say stays with the health team, unless I'm concerned someone could get hurt. Is it okay to talk?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"I don't need a doctor. I need to get back before he finishes filming."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"I won't keep you longer than I need to. Can you tell me about you and him? How did it start?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Eight months ago. He smiled at me during the weather. Then he started wearing blue ties for me. That's our sign."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"What does he want you to do when he sends a sign?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Go to him. We're getting married this summer."},{"t":"1:15","band":"Contact","w":"D","s":"How do you get in touch with him?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Letters. Over a hundred. I email the studio every day. I comment on all his posts."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Have you sent him anything else, or been to see him?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"A watch. A birthday cake. And I go to his house to make it easier for him."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"How many times have you been to his house?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Four times this month."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Has anyone asked you to stop?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"The police sent a letter. His girlfriend made them."},{"t":"2:00","band":"Risk to others","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about his girlfriend?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"She's the problem. She's keeping him from me. Someone should make her see sense."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"When you say make her see sense, what do you mean? Have you thought about harming her?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Not hurt her. Just tell her straight. Face to face."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Have you ever felt angry with him, or with anyone else who's got in the way?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Never with him. The security man at the studio, yes. I shouted at him."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Risk to self","w":"D","s":"What would happen if he told you clearly he didn't want contact?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"(tearful) He wouldn't. If he doesn't marry me, there's no point to anything. I've got no one else."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds very lonely and painful. Thank you for telling me. When you say no point, have you thought of ending your life?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Not now. I took tablets in 2019. When my GP stopped seeing me."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Did you have similar feelings about your GP back then?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Yes. They said it was delusional disorder. I was in hospital six weeks."},{"t":"3:30","band":"Screen","w":"D","s":"Have you been sleeping, and has your energy or thinking felt sped up lately?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"About five hours. Normal energy. I'm not high, if that's what you mean."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Do you ever hear voices when nobody's there, or feel someone is controlling your thoughts?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Any alcohol or drugs?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"4:00","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I can see how much he means to you. I'm worried this is causing you a lot of stress and could get you into serious trouble. I think it's the same illness as in 2019, and treatment helped then.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"I'm not taking pills to stop loving someone."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"That's not what they're for. They help with the stress and the strong thoughts. I'd like a team to see you this afternoon about whether a hospital stay would help.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Are you going to tell the police what I said?"},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"Some of it, yes, because I'm worried someone could be harmed. I'll share only what's needed, and I'll tell you what I've said.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"5:00","band":"Plan with police","w":"D","s":"PC Hill, she believes he loves her, most likely a delusional disorder, as in 2019. She's angry with his partner and wants to confront her face to face. The risk of the stalking continuing is high.","m":[7,9]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","who":"PC","s":"What can I pass on?"},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"That there's a risk to the presenter and his partner, and that she's being assessed under the Mental Health Act. Not her diagnosis or history. I'm sharing in the public interest to stop serious harm, under GMC guidance, and I'm recording that.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","who":"PC","s":"We're looking at a Stalking Protection Order."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"That makes sense. I've asked the AMHP for a Mental Health Act assessment this afternoon. If she's admitted, she's followed up within 72 hours of discharge. If not, the CMHT gives her a care coordinator, who sees her within 72 hours.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","who":"PC","s":"And if she walks out before the AMHP arrives?"},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Please tell the nurse in charge and bleep me straight away. I'll write up what was shared and why.","m":[11,13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Laura, I've told the officer there's a risk and that you're being assessed. Can you tell me what happens next?","m":[9,12]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"A team comes to see if I need hospital. And you told him there's a risk."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"That's right. If you feel like harming yourself while you wait, tell a nurse straight away. I'll come back before the team arrives.","m":[11,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Clarifies her legal status with police and explains limits of confidentiality","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the belief with open questions, without arguing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Records the full contact pattern: letters, visits, online contact and gifts","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about anger at the presenter, his partner and blockers, and past fixations","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicide and past self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her loneliness and distress with a specific empathic statement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Screens for mania, schizophrenia and substances and states a likely diagnosis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains concerns and treatment in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees with police what is shared, with reason and legal basis, and tells her","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans a Mental Health Act assessment and follow-up within 72 hours","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety nets for her leaving or self-harming while waiting","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks her understanding of what happens next","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with documentation of what was shared and a clear next step","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Explores the belief without arguing, maps contact and third-party risk, asks about suicide, screens for mania and psychosis, and agrees lawful, minimal sharing and an MHA assessment.","borderline":"Belief and contact explored, but the partner or self-harm risk is not asked, or information-sharing is vague.","fail":"Argues that he doesn't love her, discharges because she is calm, or shares everything with police without reason."}},"c-6-2-3":{"setup":"Police custody suite, Wednesday 1 pm. Mr Holt, 29, was arrested for exposing himself to two teenage girls at a bus stop; he seems low and says it is 'the third time'. Assess his mental state and risk, then advise the custody sergeant.","role":{"who":"Mr Ryan Holt, 29, male, delivery driver detained in custody; later Sergeant Lewis, custody sergeant","open":"Are you another police officer?","manner":"Ashamed, quiet, eyes down. Defensive about the first incident, then honest. Tearful about his parents; more open once he feels he is not being judged.","free":["Arrested at a bus stop this morning","Says this is the third time","Delivery driver, lives with his parents","Feels 'disgusting'"],"ifasked":["First time 18 months ago in a park (a woman jogging); second four months ago on a train platform (a woman in her twenties)","Urge builds over days when stressed; aroused during, ashamed after; masturbated the second time","Today the girls were about 14; says age 'didn't matter' but it is getting more frequent","Fantasies about exposing only; denies touching, approaching or following anyone","Denies sexual interest in children or indecent images","Low for two months, poor sleep; since arrest thought of stepping in front of a lorry; no attempt; his mum stops him","Two pints at weekends; no drugs, voices or head injury","Understood the caution; a solicitor is booked"],"worries":["Will you tell the police everything I say?","Will I go on a register? Will I lose my job?","Will my parents find out?"],"rules":["Claims the first incident was an accident; becomes honest if not challenged harshly","Tearful about his parents; settles if validated","Gives the lorry method only if asked directly","The sergeant wants a clear answer on fitness for interview and an observation level"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Holt. Is it okay if I come in and sit down?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Are you another police officer?"},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"No. I'm Dr Evans, a doctor with the liaison and diversion team. Custody asked me to see how you're doing. One important thing first: what you tell me isn't fully private. I may share a summary with the police, your solicitor and the court, especially if someone is at risk. Is that okay?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"I suppose. I've got nothing left to hide."},{"t":"0:45","band":"What happened","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what happened this morning?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"I was at the bus stop. I exposed myself. Two girls. I'm disgusting. My parents will never look at me again."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"I can hear how ashamed you feel. You're being honest with me, and that matters. There is help for this.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"(tearful) My mum's going to find out. It'll break her."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"That's a really painful thing to carry. We'll come back to your parents. You told the officers this was the third time. Can you tell me about the first?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"That was an accident. I was just adjusting myself."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Okay. What was going on for you just before it?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Honestly? I'd had an urge for days. Stress at work. In a park, eighteen months ago. A woman jogging."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"And the second time?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Four months ago, on a train platform. A woman, twenties maybe. I touched myself that time."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"How did you feel before, during and after?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"It builds and builds. During, excited. Aroused, I suppose. After, sick with myself."},{"t":"2:25","band":"Escalation","w":"D","s":"The girls today were young. Does the age of the person watching matter to you?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"No. They were just there. But it's happening more often. That scares me."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Have you ever touched, approached or followed anyone sexually without their consent?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Never. Never."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Do you have sexual thoughts about children, or have you looked at images of children?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"No. Absolutely not."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Do you have fantasies about exposing, or about other sexual acts that worry you?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Just about exposing. Nothing else."},{"t":"3:15","band":"Mental state","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been, before today?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Low for a couple of months. Not sleeping."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Any drink or drugs, head injuries, or experiences like hearing voices?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"A couple of pints at weekends. Nothing else."},{"t":"3:35","band":"Suicide risk","w":"D","s":"People in your situation often have very dark thoughts. Since the arrest, have you thought of ending your life?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Honestly, I'd rather not be here than go through this."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you thought about how you might do it?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Stepping in front of a lorry. Just a thought. I haven't done anything."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Is there anything that would stop you?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"My mum. I couldn't do that to her."},{"t":"4:20","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"That matters. Here's what I think. You've been low, and you have a pattern of urges that's hard to control. It's treatable, and I'll refer you to a specialist team.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Will I go on a register? Will I lose my job?"},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"I can't answer legal questions, but your solicitor can. My job is your health and keeping you safe. Can you tell me back what I've said?","m":[8,9]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"I'm low, there's help for the urges, and you're referring me."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll come back before you're released.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:10","band":"Advise custody","w":"D","s":"Sergeant Lewis, he understands the caution and the questions, and his answers should be reliable, so he's fit for interview. He doesn't need an appropriate adult.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","who":"Sergeant","s":"Good. Anything else?"},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"He's low and ashamed and has had suicidal thoughts since the arrest, with a method in mind. Please keep him on constant observation for now, and review that before release.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","who":"Sergeant","s":"He'll probably be bailed tonight."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Then check he goes home to his parents or somewhere safe, not alone. He gets a written safety plan with NHS 111, option 2, and Samaritans. I'll give you all this in a written note now.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","who":"Sergeant","s":"And the girls?"},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Your team leads safeguarding for them. I'll refer him to the community forensic service, and tell his GP with his consent.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","who":"Sergeant","s":"If he gets worse before you're back?"},{"t":"6:30","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Call the custody nurse and me straight away, and an ambulance if he harms himself. I'll review him again before release. Any questions?","m":[12,14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","who":"Sergeant","s":"No, that's clear."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Sergeant.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and role as separate from the police","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains limits of confidentiality before asking about earlier incidents","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Records the pattern of all three incidents: urges, triggers, arousal and feelings after","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his shame and distress with specific empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about victim age, escalation, contact offences, other paraphilias and interest in children","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens mood, substances, psychosis and organic causes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, method and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains the problem and available help plainly, without giving legal advice","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks his understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Advises custody on fitness for interview and need for an appropriate adult","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Recommends a clear observation level with review before release","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives a written safe release plan and safety net","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Refers to forensic services and addresses safeguarding and GP contact","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes with a review before release and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Explains confidentiality first, maps all three incidents and escalation, asks about children and contact offences, finds post-arrest suicide risk and gives custody clear written advice.","borderline":"Pattern and mood covered, but escalation or interest in children not asked, or advice to custody is vague.","fail":"Says he is not mentally ill and nothing is needed, misses suicide risk, or gives legal or charging advice."}},"c-6-2-4":{"setup":"Thursday 11:00, CMHT clinic. A GP has urgently referred Adam Kerr, 29, with low mood; he told the GP he has sexual thoughts about children and wants help. Take a sensitive history, assess risk to children and to him, and agree a safe plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Adam Kerr, 29, male, warehouse worker, patient","open":"I have never told anyone this properly.","manner":"Ashamed, quiet, eyes on the floor. Braces for disgust. Opens up if the doctor stays calm and plain-spoken; tearful when he calls himself a monster; panics about his sister finding out, then settles with honesty.","free":["Has had sexual thoughts about young girls since about 14 and hates them","Mood low for months; barely sleeping since the GP appointment","Afraid he will be reported to the police","Lives alone; works in a warehouse with adults only"],"ifasked":["Thoughts are about girls of about 8 or 9, before puberty; also attracted to adult women, had a two-year relationship","Thoughts most days; worse at night, when drinking or alone all weekend","Viewed sexual images of children online for a few months two years ago; deleted them; none since, though tempted when drunk","Has never touched a child or contacted a child online","Niece Ellie, 6, visits most Saturdays and sometimes stays over when his sister works nights; sometimes alone with her","Has had a passing thought about Ellie once or twice and left the room","Drinks six to eight cans on a Saturday","Passive thoughts that he'd be better off not here; no plan; his mum is the reason he wouldn't"],"worries":["Will you call the police?","Will my sister find out?","Can this be cured, or will I always be like this?","Will Stop It Now report me?"],"rules":["Only admits the images if asked directly and calmly; clams up if the doctor sounds shocked","Becomes tearful when he calls himself a monster; settles if told he is not and that asking for help was right","Mentions Ellie only when asked about family","Agrees to every part of the safety plan once told the referral is to protect Ellie and him"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Ahmed, one of the psychiatry doctors. Thanks for coming in. Your GP told us a little of what you said. There's no rush.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"(looking at the floor) I have never told anyone this properly."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming. That took courage. Before you tell me more, one thing. What you say stays within your care team, unless I think a child or anyone else may be at risk. Then I'd have to share it, and I'd tell you first if I safely could. Is that okay?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"So you'd call the police?"},{"t":"0:38","w":"D","s":"Having thoughts is not a crime, and you came for help. I wouldn't call the police over thoughts. If a child might be at risk, I'd speak to our safeguarding lead, who decides who else needs to know. Does that make sense?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","s":"I suppose. I've come this far."},{"t":"0:57","band":"Mood and safety","w":"D","s":"Before the details, how have you been since you saw your GP?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"Bad. I've barely slept. I keep thinking, what have I done, telling someone."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"Have you had any thoughts about ending your life since then?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think it'd be better if I wasn't here. Everyone would be better off."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you told me. Have you thought about how you'd do it, or made any plans?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"No. My mum... I couldn't do that to her. It's more a feeling."},{"t":"1:32","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll come back to that before you go. Can you tell me about the thoughts you told your GP about? Use whatever words you like.","m":[1]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"I get sexual thoughts about children. Girls. I feel sick saying it."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"Thank you for saying it plainly. That helps me help you. How old were you when you first noticed them?","m":[1,4]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"Fourteen, fifteen. I thought it would go when I got older. It didn't."},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"What ages are the girls you have these thoughts about?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"Young. Before puberty. Eight, nine. (pause) I can't believe I'm saying this."},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"You're doing well. Are you also attracted to adults?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"Women, yes. I was with someone for two years. It didn't last."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"How often do the thoughts come, and what makes them worse?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"Most days. Worse at night, if I've had a drink, or when I'm on my own all weekend."},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"How much do you drink over a week?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"Saturdays mostly. Six, eight cans."},{"t":"2:41","w":"D","s":"And how do you feel when the thoughts come?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Disgusted. I'm a monster. I hate myself. (crying)"},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"You're not a monster. You didn't choose these thoughts, you hate them, and you've come for help. Take a moment.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"(wiping eyes) Sorry. Nobody's ever said that."},{"t":"3:01","band":"Images and contact","w":"D","s":"No need to be sorry. Now I need to ask some direct questions. I ask everyone in your situation. Have you ever looked at sexual images of children, online or anywhere else?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"(long pause) Yes. Two years ago, for a few months. I deleted it all. Nothing since, I swear."},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Have you felt the urge to look since?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"When I'm drunk. I close the laptop and go to bed."},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"Have you ever touched a child, or acted on the thoughts any other way, like messaging a child online?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"No. Never. That's what frightens me, that I might."},{"t":"3:43","band":"Access to children","w":"D","s":"Do you live with, work with or look after any children? Think about work, neighbours, family and babysitting.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"Work's all adults. I live on my own."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"And in the family?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:57","w":"P","s":"My sister's girl, Ellie. She's six. They come most Saturdays, and she stays over when my sister's on nights."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That's important. Are you ever alone with her? Have the thoughts ever been about her?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"Sometimes, when she stays. Once or twice a thought came. I went in the other room. Nothing happened."},{"t":"4:21","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Leaving the room was the right thing to do. Let me tell you what I think. This is a recognised condition. The interest itself isn't a crime, and many people with it never offend. Because Ellie stays with you, I need to speak to our safeguarding lead today. I'm telling you now, as I promised.","m":[9,10,2]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"(panicking) So my sister finds out. Social workers at her door. Everyone will know."},{"t":"4:47","w":"D","s":"I can see how frightening that is. I'll share only what's needed to keep Ellie safe, and the lead decides whether children's social care need to know. Keeping Ellie safe protects you too.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"Will I always be like this? Can it be cured?"},{"t":"5:05","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"The interest often stays, but people learn to manage it and live safely. I'll refer you to a specialist service that does exactly that. I'd also treat your depression with sertraline, which may also quieten the thoughts.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"5:19","w":"P","s":"I'll try anything."},{"t":"5:21","w":"D","s":"Let's write a safety plan now. First: no time alone with Ellie or any child, and no overnight stays, until the specialists have seen you. Second: cut the weekend drinking. Third: if urges get strong, leave and call Stop It Now, a confidential helpline, on 0808 1000 900. For very low days, Samaritans on 116 123 or NHS 111 option 2. Can you agree to that?","m":[11,3]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I'll tell my sister I'm working Saturdays. Will they report me, the helpline?"},{"t":"5:52","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"They're there for people in exactly your position, and they'll explain their limits at the start, like I did. Can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","m":[11,13]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"No being alone with Ellie or any kids. Less drink. Ring the helpline if it gets bad. Tablets and a referral. And you're telling the safeguarding person today."},{"t":"6:14","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. I'll write to your GP and record what I share and why. How do you feel about your safety tonight?","m":[13,12,3]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"Better than when I came in. I'm going to Mum's for tea."},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"Good. If thoughts of not being here get stronger, or you start planning, call 111 option 2 or go to A&E. I'll ring you after I've spoken to the safeguarding lead and see you within a week. Thank you for trusting me.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Really."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, thanks him for coming, and stays calm, plain and non-judgemental throughout","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains limits of confidentiality before any details, and returns to them when sharing is needed","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts and plans early, and checks again before he leaves","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Records onset, age and sex of children, and whether he is also attracted to adults","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks frequency, distress, triggers and alcohol use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about viewing sexual images of children, including when last and urges since","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about any contact with a child, including online","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about every child he has access to: work, home, family, babysitting, overnight stays","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates shame and self-hatred without minimising or colluding","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the interest is not a crime, help works, and why the safeguarding lead is told today","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a safety plan: no time alone with children, less alcohol, Stop It Now, crisis numbers","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans specialist referral, sertraline for depression, and a GP letter recording what was shared","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding by asking him to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with review within a week, a call-back, and when to seek urgent help","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Sets confidentiality limits first, takes a calm, direct history including images, contact and every child, checks suicide risk twice, and leaves with same-day safeguarding, a safety plan and referral.","borderline":"Good rapport and sexual history but misses overnight stays or images, or leaves the safeguarding step vague.","fail":"Shows shock, promises full secrecy, calls the police over thoughts alone, or never asks about access to children or suicide."}},"c-6-2-5":{"setup":"Saturday 02:30, police custody suite. Ryan Price, 26, is under arrest for setting fire to bins beside his ex-partner's block of flats; nobody was hurt. Assess the fire, motive, risk and mental state, then advise the custody sergeant.","role":{"who":"Mr Ryan Price, 26, male, arrested in custody (Sergeant Morris, custody sergeant, also speaks)","open":"Whatever. I just want to go home.","manner":"Slurred, sullen and defensive at first. Flares with anger about his ex; tearful and flat when asked about mood. Cooperates once he knows the doctor is not police. The sergeant is busy but practical.","free":["Drank cans and vodka at a mate's tonight","His ex-partner Jade posted a photo with a new partner","Walked to her flats, set the paper in a bin alight with his lighter","Wants to go home"],"ifasked":["Watched the fire for a minute, then ran; did not call anyone or warn residents","Knew people were asleep in the flats","Wanted Jade 'to feel what I felt'; says he meant to scare her, not hurt her","Set bins alight at school aged 14, and a bin by the shops in 2023 after a row","Mood low since the break-up in the summer; drinking every night, not eating","Had extra help at school for special educational needs; reads poorly","Thinks he'd be better off dead; no plan or act; no voices or paranoid ideas"],"worries":["Am I going to prison?","Are you telling them I'm mad?","(Sergeant) Is he mentally ill, and is he safe to interview?","(Sergeant) Constant obs? We're short tonight."],"rules":["Says 'I just lit it' and nothing more unless taken step by step","Only admits previous fires if asked directly","Becomes tearful when asked about mood; settles if the doctor names his pain","Sergeant agrees to every recommendation if given a clear reason"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Okoye, psychiatry doctor with the liaison team. Before I see him, has he any burns or breathing problems from smoke, and has the custody nurse checked him?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","who":"Custody sergeant","s":"Nurse saw him at midnight. No burns, chest clear. He's had a fair bit to drink. Is he mentally ill, and is he safe to interview? That's what I need."},{"t":"0:28","w":"D","s":"Understood. I'll see him now and come back with clear advice. Does he have a solicitor?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","who":"Custody sergeant","s":"Duty solicitor's booked for the morning."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Hello, Ryan? I'm Dr Okoye, a psychiatry doctor. I'm not police. I'm here to check your health. What I find may be shared with the police and court so they understand how you are. Is that okay?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"(slurred) Whatever. I just want to go home."},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"I get that. Are you hurt anywhere, or feeling unwell?","m":[1]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"Head's banging. That's the drink."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"How much have you had tonight?","m":[8]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"Few cans, then vodka at my mate's. Ten cans' worth? Dunno."},{"t":"1:20","band":"The fire","w":"D","s":"Can you take me through tonight, step by step, from earlier on?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"I was at my mate's. Jade put a picture up with her new bloke. I kept looking at it."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"What happened then?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"I walked round to hers. Her flats. I was going to talk to her."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"And when you got there?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"Lights off. She wouldn't answer. The bins were by the door. I had my lighter. I just lit the paper in the top one."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"What did you do once it caught?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Watched it a minute. Then it went up and I legged it."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"Did you call anyone, or knock on doors to warn people?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"No. (pause) I didn't think. I just ran."},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"Did you know people were asleep in the flats?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I know how it sounds."},{"t":"2:30","band":"Motive","w":"D","s":"When you lit it, what did you want to happen?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"(angry) I wanted her to feel what I felt. She ruined my life. She took everything."},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"It sounds like the break-up has really hurt you. I need to understand, though. Did you want to hurt Jade, or scare her?","m":[10,6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Scare her. Not hurt her. (quieter) I don't know any more."},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"Have you set fires before, even small ones, as a kid or an adult?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"Bins at school when I was fourteen. And one by the shops a couple of years back, after another row."},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being honest. Does fire excite you, or is it more when you're angry?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"When I'm wound up. I'm not into fire or anything."},{"t":"3:31","band":"Screening","w":"D","s":"Some questions I ask everyone. Have you heard voices, or felt that people were plotting against you?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"No. I'm not mad."},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been lately?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Rubbish since she left in the summer. Not eating. Drinking every night."},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"Did you get extra help at school with reading or learning?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"Had a helper. Special needs, they said. I can't read well."},{"t":"4:04","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"That's useful to know. When you're feeling this low, have you thought of ending your life?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I don't care what happens to me any more. Sometimes I think I'd be better off dead."},{"t":"4:21","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a lot of pain, and I'm glad you told me. Have you thought how, or hurt yourself, tonight or before?","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"No. Just thoughts. In here it's worse. They took my laces and that."},{"t":"4:38","w":"D","s":"Okay. I'll make sure someone checks on you a lot tonight.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:44","w":"P","s":"Am I going to prison for this? Are you telling them I'm mad?"},{"t":"4:51","w":"D","s":"Your solicitor can answer about prison. I'll tell them you're not unwell in your mind, but you're low, you've been drinking, and you need support. You should have someone with you in interview to help you follow it, and not be interviewed till you're sober. I'll refer you to the mental health team and an alcohol worker too.","m":[14,11]},{"t":"5:17","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Okay."},{"t":"5:19","band":"Advise custody","w":"D","s":"Sergeant, he isn't psychotic and understands what's happening. He's intoxicated, so not fit for interview until sober and reassessed. He had special needs support and reads poorly, so he needs an appropriate adult. He's low with suicidal thoughts in custody, so I advise constant observation tonight.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","who":"Custody sergeant","s":"Constant? We're short tonight. Is that really needed?"},{"t":"5:44","w":"D","s":"For now, yes. He's drunk, low, and just told me he'd be better off dead. The custody nurse can review it when he's sober.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:56","w":"P","who":"Custody sergeant","s":"Fair enough. I'll sort it."},{"t":"5:59","w":"D","s":"One more thing. The fire was aimed at his ex-partner, Jade, at her flats. He wanted her scared, and he's done this before after rows. I think she's at risk. Can that go to the investigating officer for her safety, and can I have a reference number?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","who":"Custody sergeant","s":"I'll flag it to the OIC and the domestic abuse team and put an intel log on. Here's the reference."},{"t":"6:30","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Thanks. So we're agreed, what will you put in place?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","who":"Custody sergeant","s":"No interview till he's sober and reassessed, appropriate adult, constant obs, and the risk to the ex passed on."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll write that advice in the custody record now, send a report to the solicitor and court by morning, and hand over to the morning liaison team. Call me if he gets worse.","m":[13,14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Checks with custody for burns, smoke inhalation, intoxication and healthcare review before seeing him","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains role as health, not police, and that findings may be shared with police and court","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks for a solicitor and asks about learning difficulties or extra support at school","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes him step by step through the hours before and the lighting of the fire","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what he did once the fire caught: stayed, fled, warned anyone","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores motive directly, including what he wanted his ex-partner to feel","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about previous fire-setting and interest in fire","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for psychosis, mood and alcohol or drug use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, self-harm and means in custody","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his hurt and distress without condoning the fire","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Advises sergeant clearly: not fit for interview until sober, appropriate adult, constant observation","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Passes risk to the ex-partner to police and obtains a reference number","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks the sergeant's understanding and records advice in writing","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tells him what happens next, then arranges court report, referral and morning handover","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Goes through before, during and after the fire, names the revenge motive and past fires, screens mental state and suicide, and gives the sergeant clear advice plus a logged risk to the ex-partner.","borderline":"Sound mental state screen and advice, but misses what he did after lighting it or the learning needs, or doesn't pass on risk to Jade.","fail":"Says 'not mentally ill' after a brief chat, skips motive and suicide risk, or gives no advice on interview fitness."}},"c-6-2-6":{"setup":"Friday 23:40, A&E majors. Jamie Walsh, 32, fell during arrest and has neck pain. He is refusing a CT scan and collar and wants to go back to his cell. Assess his capacity to refuse and advise the emergency doctor.","role":{"who":"Mr Jamie Walsh, 32, male, brought from police custody (Dr Lee, emergency doctor, also speaks)","open":"I'm fine. I just want to go back to my cell. Get this thing off my neck.","manner":"Loud, irritable and slurring, keeps trying to lift his head. Softens when offered nicotine and taken seriously; briefly tearful about his son, then digs in. The emergency doctor is busy and wants a clear answer.","free":["Wants to leave and go back to his cell","Desperate for a cigarette","Hates the staring and the police at the curtain","Says his neck is 'just sore'"],"ifasked":["Drank several pints and some vodka today","Hit his face on the pavement; head 'a bit fuzzy'; doesn't think he blacked out","Says he has been told his neck 'might be broken'","Cannot recall the paralysis risk a minute after it is explained","Says 'nothing happens to me' when asked about risks","Has a young son he sees on Sundays","Does not want his mum told he is in hospital"],"worries":["Can you make me have it?","Can I have a cigarette?","(Emergency doctor) Can I just scan him, or do we section him?","(Emergency doctor) The police have offered to hold him down."],"rules":["Repeats the risk back only if it is put very simply, and loses it again within a minute","Accepts a nicotine patch but refuses the scan whatever else is offered","Becomes briefly tearful when his son is mentioned, then angry","Emergency doctor accepts the plan if told clearly who decides what"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Rahman from liaison psychiatry. What exactly do you need him to decide, and how urgent is it?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","who":"Emergency doctor","s":"CT of his cervical spine, and keeping the collar on until it's done. He went face first during the arrest, hands cuffed. Midline tenderness. If there's a fracture and he thrashes about, he could be paralysed. I want it within the hour."},{"t":"0:26","w":"D","s":"Any tingling or weakness? And what about head injury, drink, drugs? Has his sugar been checked?","m":[2,1]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","who":"Emergency doctor","s":"No neuro signs so far. Graze on his forehead, GCS 14. Smells of alcohol, slurring. Glucose 6.1."},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Can the nurse keep his head still while we talk, and could the officers step back to the curtain if it's safe?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","who":"Emergency doctor","s":"Nurse is with him. I'll ask the officers."},{"t":"0:56","band":"His view","w":"D","s":"Hi, Jamie? I'm Dr Rahman, a doctor from the mental health team, not the police. Keep your head still for me. Can we talk for a few minutes?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"I'm fine. I just want to go back to my cell. Get this thing off my neck."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"I hear you. A&E on a Friday night is grim. What's making you want to leave so much?","m":[4,11]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"Everyone staring. Coppers everywhere. And I'm gagging for a fag. I've been here hours."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Have you had anything to drink or taken anything today?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Few pints. Bit of vodka. So what."},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"When you fell, did you bang your head? Black out, been sick, any fits?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"Hit my face. Don't think I blacked out. Head's a bit fuzzy."},{"t":"1:52","band":"Testing capacity","w":"D","s":"What have the doctors told you about your neck?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"That it might be broken. It isn't. It's just sore."},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"It might just be bruising. The worry is that if a bone is broken and you move, it can damage the spinal cord. That can mean paralysis, not moving your arms or legs, for life. The scan takes a few minutes and tells us for sure. The collar keeps you safe till then.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"Paralysed? Don't be daft. I'm walking about, aren't I?"},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"Some people with a broken neck can still walk at first. That's why the scan matters. What do you think could happen if it's broken and missed?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:39","w":"P","s":"Nothing. I'm hard as nails. Nothing happens to me."},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"Can I make this easier? You can't smoke here, but I can get you a nicotine patch now. Would that help?","m":[10]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Go on then. Can you make me have the scan, though?"},{"t":"2:59","w":"D","s":"If you understand the risks and still say no, you can refuse. That's your right. I need to check you can weigh it up. Can you tell me what I said about your neck a minute ago?","m":[7,9]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Er... it's sore. And you're getting me a patch."},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"And what could happen if a broken neck is missed?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"Dunno. You'd need an operation? Can I go now?"},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"That's okay. It's hard to take things in after a drink. Let me put it simply. A missed broken neck can cause paralysis. That means you can't move your arms or legs. Can you say that back?","m":[5,7,10]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Broken neck, can't move my legs. Right."},{"t":"3:48","w":"D","s":"Good. What are the good and bad things about the scan, for you?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Bad thing, I'm stuck here. Good thing, nothing. It won't happen to me."},{"t":"4:01","w":"D","s":"What would it mean for you if you couldn't walk?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"(pause, then quiet) I've got a little lad. I see him Sundays. (angry) Doesn't matter. It won't happen."},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"Your son clearly matters a lot. That's exactly why they want to check. Would it help to move to a quieter bay, or ring someone you trust?","m":[11,10]},{"t":"4:26","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I don't want my mum knowing I'm in here. Just leave me alone. I'm not having it."},{"t":"4:34","w":"D","s":"That's okay, no calls. You've been clear you don't want it. One last thing: what do you remember now about the risk to your neck?","m":[9,7]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"That it's sore. And I'm getting a patch."},{"t":"4:49","band":"Opinion","w":"D","s":"Dr Lee, he's clear he doesn't want the scan, so he can communicate a choice. He understands it's about his neck. But he can't retain the paralysis risk for more than a minute, and he can't weigh it; he keeps saying nothing can happen to him. The impairment is alcohol, maybe with a minor head injury. In my opinion he lacks capacity for this decision right now.","m":[12,2,9]},{"t":"5:16","w":"P","who":"Emergency doctor","s":"So can I just scan him? Or do we section him?"},{"t":"5:22","w":"D","s":"Not the Mental Health Act. That treats mental disorder, not a neck. This is the Mental Capacity Act. You're the treating doctor, so you make the best-interests decision. I'd go least restrictive: the patch, a quiet bay, the nurse talking him through it. Restraint only if needed and proportionate.","m":[12,10]},{"t":"5:42","w":"P","who":"Emergency doctor","s":"The police have offered to hold him down."},{"t":"5:46","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"They can stay for safety, but shouldn't restrain unless your team asks for clinical reasons. They can't consent for him either.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","who":"Emergency doctor","s":"And if he sobers up and still says no?"},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"Then he's reassessed. Capacity can come back within hours. If he has it and still refuses, record what he was told, that he understood, and give safety-netting advice. I'll reassess at 02:00, sooner if he's clearly sober. Can you run the plan back to me?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","who":"Emergency doctor","s":"Lacks capacity now, I decide best interests, least restrictive, police don't restrain unless we ask, you're back at two."},{"t":"6:27","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll write my opinion now: the decision, what I explained, his answer to each part of the test, and why. Jamie, the doctors will look after your neck. The patch is coming, and we'll move you somewhere quieter. I'll be back later.","m":[13,11]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Whatever. Just get the patch."}],"marks":[{"c":"Clarifies with the emergency doctor the exact decision, risks of refusing, and urgency","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks for impairment: alcohol, drugs, head injury, delirium and blood glucose","d":"Content"},{"c":"Introduces self as a doctor, not police, and keeps his neck still while talking","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores his reasons for refusing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains simply the risk of a missed fracture and what the scan involves","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tests understanding by asking what the doctors are worried about","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests retention by asking him to repeat the information after a gap","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests weighing by asking him to set out pros and cons of the scan for him","d":"Content"},{"c":"Notes he can communicate a clear choice","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tries at least two practical supports: nicotine replacement, quiet bay, phone call, simpler explanation","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his frustration and fear without arguing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives a clear opinion: lacks capacity now from intoxication; MCA best interests, not MHA","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees reassessment time and police role, checks the doctor's understanding, documents each step","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Pins down the decision, names the impairment, tests all four parts with support attempts, gives a clear MCA opinion that rejects the MHA, and agrees reassessment and the police role.","borderline":"Correct conclusion but tests only understanding, or gives no practical support or reassessment time.","fail":"Calls him capable because he can talk, treats an unwise refusal as incapacity, or reaches for the Mental Health Act."}},"c-6-3-1":{"setup":"Tuesday 10:00, medium secure unit ward round. Marcus Bell, 38, on a section 37/41 for four years and stable on clozapine, asks when he can go to the shops alone. Explain restricted leave, assess his readiness, and start the process.","role":{"who":"Mr Marcus Bell, 38, male, restricted patient on s37/41 with paranoid schizophrenia","open":"When can I go to the shops on my own? Everyone else gets leave.","manner":"Frustrated and a bit sharp at first, comparing himself with others. Becomes quiet and tearful talking about the man he killed. Engaged and relieved once the process is laid out clearly.","free":["Six months of escorted community leave with no problems","No voices or paranoia for two years; sleeping well","Feels it is unfair that other patients go out alone","Attends the cooking group"],"ifasked":["Was psychotic, off medication and using cannabis heavily when he killed a stranger he believed was following him","Thinks about the man's family every day; sometimes feels he doesn't deserve leave","Early warning signs: poor sleep, thinking people on the bus are watching him, snapping at staff","Would tell his named nurse rather than hide it, unlike last time","No cannabis or alcohol now; all screens clean; has practised refusing drugs","Sometimes asks for his night clozapine later because it sedates him; drooling and some constipation","Smokes about ten roll-ups a day","On edge in busy places, but not paranoid"],"worries":["Why am I treated like a criminal?","Does the victim's family get a say?","How long will it take?","What if I see someone from back then?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when asked about the offence; settles if the doctor acknowledges the weight of it","Pushes back if leave is refused without an explanation; calms when told the law requires the Ministry's consent","Mentions the side effects only if asked about clozapine","Names warning signs readily if asked; this is his strength"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Marcus, thanks for raising this. You've clearly thought about it. I'd like to explain how leave works for you, ask some questions, and agree what we do next. Okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"When can I go to the shops on my own? Everyone else gets leave."},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"It's a fair question. Six months of escorted leave without a single problem is real progress.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"So why am I still treated like a criminal? Others on my ward go out alone."},{"t":"0:40","band":"How leave works","w":"D","s":"I can see why that feels unfair. The difference is your order. The court added a restriction order, section 41, so the Ministry of Justice must agree any leave outside the hospital. Your consultant applies, but can't grant it alone. That's the law, not a judgement on you.","m":[2,1]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"So it's out of your hands."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"Partly. But we can make the strongest case possible, and much of that comes from you. Can I ask a few questions?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"1:19","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"1:21","band":"Readiness","w":"D","s":"How's your mind been lately? Any voices, or feeling people are against you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"Nothing. Not for two years. I sleep well. I'm doing the cooking group."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"And out on escorted leave, how do you feel around strangers?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:42","w":"P","s":"A bit on edge when it's busy. Nerves. Not like before."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"Can we talk about what happened four years ago? How do you understand it now?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"I was ill. I'd stopped my tablets, I was smoking loads of weed. I thought he was following me to kill me. (quiet) He was just walking home."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"That's a very hard thing to say. Take your time.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"(voice breaks) He had a family. I think about them every day. Some days I don't think I deserve to go out at all."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Carrying that shows you understand what happened and what it cost. That understanding is part of why you're safer now. It doesn't mean you can't move forward.","m":[8,4]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"My psychologist says that."},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"If you started to become unwell again, what would you notice first?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"Not sleeping. Thinking people on the bus are looking at me. Snapping at staff."},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"And what would you do?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Tell my named nurse. Not hide it like last time."},{"t":"3:11","w":"D","s":"That's exactly what the Ministry will want to see. What about cannabis or drink now?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:19","w":"P","s":"Nothing. My screens are clean. I don't want to go back there."},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"And if someone offered you a joint while you were out?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"Say no and come back. I've practised that with the psychologist."},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"How are you getting on with clozapine? Any doses missed?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"I take it. I ask for the night one later sometimes because it knocks me out. Drooling at night. Bit bunged up too."},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. Constipation on clozapine matters, so I'll make sure you get a laxative and we'll ask about it every week. Do you smoke?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Ten roll-ups a day. Why?"},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"Smoking changes clozapine levels. If you smoke more or less while out, your level can shift a lot. Tell us if that changes.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:26","w":"P","s":"Never knew that."},{"t":"4:28","band":"Explain the steps","w":"D","s":"Here's the process. First, the team updates your risk assessment, the HCR-20. We ask the Victim Liaison Officer for the family's views, and the MAPPA coordinator. Then your consultant sends the application to the Ministry's Mental Health Casework Section.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"His family? They get a say?"},{"t":"4:52","w":"D","s":"They can ask for conditions, such as areas you must keep away from. The Ministry decides.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"That's fair. What if I see someone from back then?"},{"t":"5:06","w":"D","s":"Good thing to think about. What would you do?","m":[5]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"Walk away. Come back. Tell staff."},{"t":"5:16","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Good. That goes in your plan. When leave is agreed, the plan sets out what it's for, the times, the area, places to avoid, a drug screen when you're back, and what happens if you're late: staff follow the missing-patient policy, police and the Ministry are told, and leave can stop.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"How long will all this take?"},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Usually some weeks after the application goes in, sometimes longer. I can't promise a date. Meanwhile, keep up the escorted leave, the clean screens and your relapse plan with your nurse. It all goes in the report.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"Weeks. Better than nothing."},{"t":"6:06","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what happens next?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:13","w":"P","s":"Risk thing updated. Victim liaison and MAPPA asked. Consultant sends it to the Ministry. Few weeks. Meanwhile I keep doing escorted leave and stay clean."},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"Spot on. I'll talk to your consultant after ward round so we can start this week, and write the plan in your notes. Your named nurse will keep you updated, and we'll review it at next week's ward round.","m":[13,9]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thanks. First time anyone's told me how it actually works."}],"marks":[{"c":"Acknowledges his progress and frustration, and says what the conversation will cover","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains plainly that the Ministry of Justice must agree any leave under a restriction order","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about current mental state, including voices and suspicious thoughts","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores his understanding of the offence and its link to illness, drugs and stopping medication","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks him to name early warning signs and what he would do","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about cannabis, alcohol and how he'd handle drugs offered on leave","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks clozapine adherence, side effects including constipation, and smoking","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his guilt about the victim without rushing past it","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Sets out the steps: updated HCR-20, victim liaison, MAPPA views, RC application to MHCS","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a realistic timeline and what he can do now to help his case","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Describes the leave plan: purpose, times, area, exclusion zone, testing, late return","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding by asking him to explain the process back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with next steps, who updates him, and when","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Explains Ministry consent plainly, tests insight, warning signs, drugs and clozapine, lays out HCR-20, victim liaison, MAPPA and application, and agrees a realistic timeline and leave plan.","borderline":"Explains the law but assesses readiness thinly, or misses clozapine side effects, smoking or the victim's views.","fail":"Promises leave the RC cannot grant, or refuses without explaining why, and skips risk and insight."}},"c-6-3-2":{"setup":"Wednesday 14:00, prison healthcare centre. Paul Grant, 44, is on remand for GBH; his solicitor wants a report on fitness to plead, and staff say he believes the judge is in a plot. Assess fitness to plead, then advise the solicitor, including MAPPA.","role":{"who":"Mr Paul Grant, 44, male, remand prisoner (Ms Patel, his defence solicitor, speaks by phone)","open":"Why should I talk to you? You're working for the judge.","manner":"Guarded and suspicious, watching what is written. Speaks fast about the plot. Briefly tearful about not trusting anyone. The solicitor is brisk and wants a yes or no.","free":["The judge, police and now his solicitor are in a plot against him","They keep a file on him about what he knows of the council","Charged with GBH after hitting a man with a bottle outside a shop","Doesn't want to go to hospital"],"ifasked":["Hears a man's voice at night telling him not to trust his solicitor","Believes his solicitor signals to the judge with a ring","Admitted to hospital in 2015 with schizophrenia; stopped tablets","Knows what guilty and not guilty mean and that guilty means sentence","Would see no point challenging jurors: 'they'd all be planted'","Will not tell his solicitor anything or take her advice","Would use the witness box to expose the plot","No thoughts of self-harm; would 'defend himself' if attacked; would tell a trusted officer"],"worries":["You're working for the judge.","I'm not going to any hospital.","(Solicitor) Is he fit or not? I need a yes or no.","(Solicitor) Will MAPPA mean he's watched for life?"],"rules":["Continues only if told who asked for the report and that he need not answer everything","Answers questions on the charge and plea correctly; delusions surface on instructing counsel and jurors","Becomes tearful when the doctor names how lonely this must be","Solicitor accepts the opinion if the abilities, not the diagnosis, are explained"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Grant? I'm Dr Mensah, a psychiatry doctor. Your solicitor, Ms Patel, asked me to see you and write a report for the court about your health and how you understand the trial. What you tell me isn't private. It goes to her and may be read in court.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"Why should I talk to you? You're working for the judge."},{"t":"0:28","w":"D","s":"I've been asked by your solicitor, not the judge. I'll write what I find honestly. You don't have to answer every question, but it helps me give a fair picture. Are you happy to carry on?","m":[2,1]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"For now. I'm watching what you write."},{"t":"0:48","band":"Mental state","w":"D","s":"That's fair. Staff say you've been worried about something. What's been happening?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"The judge is in on it. They all are. Police, judge, now my solicitor. They've had a file on me for years."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. What do you think they want?","m":[8,3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"To put me away so I can't tell people what I know about the council."},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"How do you know your solicitor is part of it?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"She smiled at the prosecutor at my bail hearing. She wears the same ring as the judge. Signals."},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"Do you hear voices or see things others don't?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"A man's voice at night. Says don't trust her. She'll sell me out."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"Have you had help for your mental health before?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"Hospital in 2015. Schizophrenia, they said. I stopped the tablets. Didn't need them."},{"t":"1:56","band":"Fitness to plead","w":"D","s":"Now about the case. What are you charged with?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"GBH. Hit a bloke outside the shop with a bottle. He was one of them, following me."},{"t":"2:09","w":"D","s":"What does the prosecution say happened?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"That I went for him for no reason. That's their story."},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"If you're asked guilty or not guilty, what's the difference?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Guilty means you did it and get sentenced. Not guilty means there's a trial. But they've decided anyway."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"If you pleaded guilty, what might happen?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"Sentenced. Prison, probably."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"A jury is twelve people. If you thought one knew you or was unfair, what could you do?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"They'd all be planted. No point."},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"In court, witnesses give evidence. Could you follow it, and tell your solicitor if someone says something wrong?","m":[6,7]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"Why would I tell her? She'd pass it straight to the judge."},{"t":"3:07","w":"D","s":"So could you give her your side and take her advice?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"No chance. I'll do it myself."},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"And if you were questioned in the witness box?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"I'd tell them everything. The council, the files. Make them listen."},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"It sounds exhausting, feeling everyone's against you.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"(tearful) It is. I can't sleep. I don't trust anyone. Not even my sister now."},{"t":"3:40","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"That sounds very lonely. Have you had thoughts of self-harm or anyone in here?","m":[8,9]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"Not myself. I'd defend myself if they came for me. No one has."},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"If you felt in danger in here, what would you do?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Stay in my cell. Tell the officer. The old one, not the new one."},{"t":"4:07","w":"D","s":"Okay. I think treatment could help you sleep and think more clearly, so you can deal with the case your way. Would you consider hospital?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:19","w":"P","s":"I'm not going to any hospital. I'll handle my own case."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting to stay in control. Let's talk again tomorrow. I'll tell your solicitor what I've found.","m":[8,1]},{"t":"4:34","band":"Advise the solicitor","w":"D","s":"Ms Patel, it's Dr Mensah. I've seen Mr Grant. He has a relapse of schizophrenia: voices, and fixed beliefs that you, the judge and the police are plotting against him.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","who":"Solicitor","s":"So is he fit or not? I need a yes or no."},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"Right now, in my opinion, he's unfit. He understands the charge and what a plea means. But because of his delusions he can't instruct you, couldn't sensibly challenge a juror, and couldn't follow or give evidence rationally. Failing one ability can be enough.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:13","w":"P","who":"Solicitor","s":"He's got schizophrenia. Doesn't that make him unfit anyway?"},{"t":"5:18","w":"D","s":"No. It's his abilities now, not the label. Many people with schizophrenia are fit to plead. His are affected by these particular beliefs.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:29","w":"P","who":"Solicitor","s":"So what now?"},{"t":"5:31","w":"D","s":"He needs hospital treatment. I'll recommend assessment for transfer under section 48. Fitness can return within weeks, so it should be reviewed then. A finding of unfitness also needs two doctors, one section 12 approved.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:47","w":"P","who":"Solicitor","s":"His family keep asking about MAPPA. Will he be watched for life?"},{"t":"5:54","w":"D","s":"No. MAPPA is how police, probation and prisons manage risk; health and others have a duty to cooperate. If he gets a hospital order, or twelve months or more in custody, for this, he'd come under it as a violent offender. The level depends on risk and can come down.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:16","w":"P","who":"Solicitor","s":"Okay. When can I have the report?"},{"t":"6:20","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"If you send his past psychiatric records and the case papers, within two weeks. Can you tell me what you'll take from this call?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:32","w":"P","who":"Solicitor","s":"Unfit now because of the delusions, not the diagnosis. Hospital transfer, then review. Two doctors. MAPPA only after a hospital order or a long sentence, and not for life."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"That's right. My consultant will review and sign the report with me. I'll send it securely.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Explains who asked for the report and that it is not confidential and goes to court","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Tells him he need not answer everything and checks he will continue","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores voices and persecutory beliefs about judge and solicitor, and past history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests understanding of the charge and the prosecution case","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests understanding of plea and its consequences","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests ability to challenge a juror and follow the proceedings","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests ability to instruct his solicitor and give evidence","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his fear and isolation without agreeing with the delusions","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about risk to self and others in prison","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives the solicitor a clear opinion linking unfitness to delusions, not diagnosis alone","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Recommends treatment, s48 transfer, two medical opinions and review of fitness","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains MAPPA correctly: police, probation, prisons lead; others cooperate; levels can fall","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks the solicitor's understanding and agrees papers, deadline and consultant sign-off","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"States non-confidentiality first, tests each Pritchard ability with a direct question, ties unfitness to delusions, recommends s48 transfer and review, and explains MAPPA accurately.","borderline":"Correct opinion but tests only some abilities, or explains MAPPA vaguely.","fail":"Calls him unfit because of the diagnosis, skips the confidentiality warning, or gives the solicitor no clear opinion."}},"c-6-3-3":{"setup":"Monday 16:30, CMHT duty clinic. Tom Reid, 36, with paranoid schizophrenia, stopped his depot two months ago and says he will stab his neighbour if 'poisoned again'. He refuses consent to share. Assess the threat, decide on disclosure and act.","role":{"who":"Mr Tom Reid, 36, male, CMHT patient (a police call handler speaks by phone)","open":"My neighbour's poisoning my water. I can taste it. Metal.","manner":"Tense, frightened and certain. Flares with anger when told information will be shared; softens when his fear is taken seriously. Bargains with the depot. The police call handler is calm and procedural.","free":["Neighbour above, Mr Davies at number 14, is poisoning his water through the pipes","Hears him drilling and talking through the wall at night","Says: 'If he does it again, I'll stab him. I know where he lives.'","Refuses to let anyone be told"],"ifasked":["Stopped his depot two months ago; thinks it may be poison too","Plans to knock and stab him at the door 'next time I taste it'","Keeps a kitchen knife by his front door; no weapon on him now","Hit a man in the street in 2019 while unwell; got a caution","Smokes cannabis every day","His mum and fear of prison have stopped him so far","No thoughts of harming himself"],"worries":["You can't tell anyone, that's my private business.","So you're on his side?","If I take the depot again, will you leave it?","Are they arresting me?"],"rules":["Mentions the knife only if asked directly about weapons at home","Becomes tearful saying nobody protects him; calms if his fear is validated without agreeing with it","Refuses consent whatever is offered, but stays in clinic if told honestly what will happen","Call handler asks whether anyone is in immediate danger before anything else"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Reid. I'm Dr Wilson, one of the duty psychiatry doctors. Thanks for coming in. What's been happening?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"My neighbour's poisoning my water. I can taste it. Metal. I've been sick twice this week."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. Before we go on: what you tell me stays within your care team, unless I'm worried someone could be seriously hurt. Is that okay?","m":[1,6]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"Fine. As long as you believe me."},{"t":"0:36","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"I can see how real this feels. How do you think he's doing it?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"Through the pipes. He's above me, number 14. Mr Davies. I hear him drilling at night. He talks about me through the wall."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"What does he say?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"That I'm scum. That he'll finish me off. Every night."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"When did you last have your depot?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"Two months back. I stopped. It's probably poison too."},{"t":"1:14","band":"The threat","w":"D","s":"You said something to the nurse about what you'd do if it carried on. Can you tell me?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"If he does it again, I'll stab him. I know where he lives. Simple."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about when, or how?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Next time I taste it. Go up and knock. When he opens the door."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"Do you have a knife or any weapon on you now, or at home?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Not on me. Kitchen knife at home. I keep it by the door now. In case."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That's important. Have you ever hurt anyone before?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"2019. Hit a bloke in the street. I was ill then. Got a caution."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"Do you use cannabis or drink?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"Weed, every day. Helps me sleep through the drilling."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"What's stopped you going up there so far?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"My mum. She'd be gutted. And prison. But I can't live like this. (voice cracks) Nobody's protecting me."},{"t":"2:36","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying: not feeling safe in your own home, and nobody listening. I want to help you feel safe.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"(calmer) Nobody's said that before."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Have you had any thoughts of hurting yourself?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"No. I'm not the one who should die."},{"t":"3:00","band":"Consent and disclosure","w":"D","s":"Tom, I'm worried. You've said you'd stab your neighbour, there's a knife by your door, and you're unwell. I'd like your agreement to tell the police, so everyone's kept safe, including you. Will you agree?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"No. You can't tell anyone. That's my private business."},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"I understand, and most of what you tell me is private. But if someone could be seriously hurt, I can't keep it to myself. I'll share only what's needed: your name, what you said, his address, and that you're unwell. Not your whole history.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:43","w":"P","s":"(angry) So you're on his side? Calling the police on me?"},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"I'm not on his side. I'm on the side of nobody getting hurt, including you. If you hurt him, your life changes forever. I want to stop that.","m":[6,8]},{"t":"4:03","w":"P","s":"If I take the depot again, will you leave it?"},{"t":"4:09","w":"D","s":"Restarting the depot is a really good step and I want to arrange it. But because of what you've said, I still need to share this. I'd also like a team to see you today about hospital, where you'd feel safer.","m":[11,8]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"Hospital? I'm not mad. Fine. I'll wait. Ten minutes."},{"t":"4:33","band":"Act","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Wilson, a psychiatry doctor at the CMHT. I'm sharing information to protect someone. A patient has made a specific threat to stab a named neighbour at a known address. My consultant agrees I should call.","m":[10,9]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","who":"Police","s":"Is anyone in immediate danger right now?"},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"He's with us in clinic. He keeps a kitchen knife by his front door. He has psychosis, stopped treatment two months ago, and believes the neighbour is poisoning him. The neighbour is Mr Davies, flat 14, the flat above his.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","who":"Police","s":"Your patient's name and age?"},{"t":"5:19","w":"D","s":"Tom Reid, 36. An approved mental health professional is arranging a Mental Health Act assessment today. If he leaves before then, I'll ring 999.","m":[10,9]},{"t":"5:31","w":"P","who":"Police","s":"Understood. We'll assess the risk and decide on any contact with the neighbour. Your log number is 1604."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Log 1604. Please keep his health details to those who need them.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:47","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Tom, I've told the police what you said, so everyone stays safe. I shared only what I said I would. A mental health team is coming to see you today about hospital.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"(quiet) Are they arresting me?"},{"t":"6:06","w":"D","s":"I can't say what the police will do, but they know you're unwell and that we're helping. Can you tell me what happens next?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:18","w":"P","s":"Police know. A team's coming. Maybe hospital. Start the jab again."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"That's right. I'll record what you said in your words, why I shared it, who I told and when, and the log number. If you're not admitted, the crisis team will follow you up.","m":[13,11]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"Okay. Just make the drilling stop."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"We'll work on that together. I'll stay with you until the team arrives.","m":[6]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, listens to his fears, and sets confidentiality limits early","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the poisoning belief, voices and stopping the depot","d":"Content"},{"c":"Clarifies the threat: named person, intent, plan, timing and trigger","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about weapons now and at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about past violence, cannabis, self-harm and what has stopped him so far","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his fear without agreeing with the delusion","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Seeks his consent to share before deciding to disclose","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tells him honestly what will be shared, with whom and why","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Involves consultant and arranges same-day MHA assessment via the AMHP service","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Shares minimum necessary with police and records the log number","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers treatment: restarting depot, hospital, crisis team follow-up","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding of what happens next","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Records the threat in his words, the reason for disclosure, who was told and when","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Maps the threat, weapon, past violence and protective factors, seeks consent, then discloses the minimum to police with a log number, arranges same-day MHA assessment, and tells him honestly.","borderline":"Right decision but misses the knife at home, or doesn't seek consent first or record the log number.","fail":"Keeps the threat confidential, warns the neighbour directly, shares his whole history, or lets him leave with no assessment."}},"c-7-1-1":{"setup":"Tuesday 21:30, acute adult ward. Dean Walker, 48, has just arrived from A&E. He drinks a litre of vodka a day, last drank 12 hours ago and had a withdrawal fit last year. Assess his risk, start treatment and agree inpatient detox.","role":{"who":"Mr Dean Walker, 48, van driver, alcohol dependent, just admitted from A&E (the nurse in charge, Sam, also speaks)","open":"Yeah, that's me. Can you just give me something? I've been sat in A&E all day.","manner":"Sweaty, shaky and irritable at first, wanting tablets not questions. Softens once his fear of another fit is taken seriously. Defensive about his daughter until reassured the aim is support.","free":["Drinks about a litre of vodka a day, sometimes cans on top","Last drink about 9:30 this morning","Shakes, sweats and nausea every morning until his first drink","Had a fit in his kitchen on day 2 of stopping alone last year"],"ifasked":["Never had a supervised detox","Uses street diazepam now and then, none for a week","Has barely eaten for weeks","No falls or head injuries","Never seen insects or been very confused after stopping","Drives a van for work","Daughter, 11, has been getting herself to school; she is with his sister Karen tonight"],"worries":["Am I going to have another fit?","Can't I just take a few tablets and go home?","Will social services take my daughter?","If I can't drive, I lose my job."],"rules":["Irritable until told the questions will only take two minutes and lead straight to treatment","Becomes tearful remembering his daughter finding him after the fit; settles if validated","Mentions his daughter only if asked who lives with him or about children","Agrees to stay once told plainly why a past fit makes home detox unsafe"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Hughes, one of the psychiatry doctors on call. Are you Dean?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Yeah, that's me. Can you just give me something? I've been sat in A&E all day."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"A long wait, feeling like this, is rough. Two minutes of answers lets me get the dose right, then I'll chart it straight away. Okay?","m":[1,6]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"Fine. Go on."},{"t":"0:26","band":"Drinking and withdrawal","w":"D","s":"When was your last drink, as close as you can say?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"About half nine this morning. Finished the bottle before the ambulance came."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"So about twelve hours ago. How much do you drink on a normal day?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"A litre of vodka. Few cans on top sometimes."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"That's about forty units a day, plus the cans. What happens in the morning before your first drink?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"Shakes, sweats, feel sick. I keep a bottle by the bed to stop it."},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"Has stopping ever given you a fit, or made you see or hear things that weren't there?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Last year. Tried to stop on my own and on the second day I had a fit in the kitchen. Woke up in an ambulance."},{"t":"1:21","w":"D","s":"Have you ever been really confused, or seen things like insects, after stopping? And have you had a detox with a hospital or alcohol team?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"No, just the fit. That was enough. Never had a proper detox, just that time at home."},{"t":"1:39","band":"Safety checks","w":"D","s":"Do you take anything else? Sleeping tablets, diazepam off the street, other drugs?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Bit of street diazepam when I can't get a drink. Nothing this week."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"Any falls or knocks to the head lately? And how's your eating been?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"No falls. Eating, hardly anything. Toast now and then."},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Your pulse is fast at 104; the rest of your obs are fine. I'll check your sugar with a finger prick, then look you over head to toe. Is that alright?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"2:17","w":"D","s":"Sugar is 5.8, normal. No bumps on your head, no temperature, chest clear, no yellow eyes. Your eye movements and walking are normal too, so no sign of the brain vitamin problem I look for.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"Am I going to have another fit? I can feel it building."},{"t":"2:37","band":"Children at home","w":"D","s":"That fear makes complete sense after last year. You've come to the right place.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"(eyes filling) I thought I was going to die on that kitchen floor. My daughter found me."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"That must have been terrifying, for both of you. Thank you for telling me. How old is she, and who's looking after her tonight?","m":[6,9]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"Eleven. My sister, Karen, has her. Lately she's been doing her own breakfasts and getting herself to school, while I'm like this."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. It's clear you care about her. Can she stay with Karen while you're here?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:19","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Will social services take her off me?"},{"t":"3:23","w":"D","s":"This is about support, not taking her away. I'll let the ward social worker know so you both get help, and I'll keep you involved in anything that's shared.","m":[6,9]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"3:36","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Here's where we are. Your body depends on alcohol and you're twelve hours in. Fits usually come in the next day or two, and because you had one last year, you're at high risk of another.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"Just give me a few tablets and let me go home. I've done it before."},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"I hear you want to be home. But last time at home ended in a fit. Here, the nurses spot trouble early and treat it straight away. That's why anyone who's had a fit is advised to detox in hospital. It's about a week. Will you stay?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:17","w":"P","s":"A week. (pause) Alright. I don't want another one."},{"t":"4:21","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'm charting chlordiazepoxide. It calms the withdrawal and protects against fits, and we'll lower the dose slowly over the week.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:31","w":"P","s":"The nurse said something about vitamin injections. Can't I have tablets?"},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"Heavy drinking and poor eating use up vitamin B1, and tablets barely get absorbed now. A drip called Pabrinex protects your brain and memory. It goes in before any sugary drink or drip, because sugar without it can harm the brain.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"Fine. Whatever keeps me right."},{"t":"4:56","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Do you drive?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"Van, for work. Why?"},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"With alcohol dependence and a fit last year, you must not drive, and you're legally required to tell the DVLA. I'll give you that in writing and note it in your records.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:14","w":"P","s":"That's my job gone."},{"t":"5:17","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, that's a real blow. We'll help you think it through once you're feeling better. It doesn't all need sorting tonight.","m":[6]},{"t":"5:26","band":"Handover","w":"D","s":"Sam, can I hand over? Dean is high risk because of a past withdrawal fit. Please start CIWA-Ar now and repeat it every one to two hours tonight, with NEWS2.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:39","w":"P","who":"Nurse in charge","s":"Okay. When do you want a call?"},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"Bleep me if CIWA-Ar goes over 15, he becomes confused, has a fit, or NEWS2 reaches 5. Extra chlordiazepoxide is charted if CIWA-Ar is 10 or more. Pabrinex first, before any glucose.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:56","w":"P","who":"Nurse in charge","s":"Got it. Anything for a fit?"},{"t":"5:59","w":"D","s":"Lorazepam is on the chart, and call the medical registrar for any seizure. I've sent bloods including magnesium and phosphate. Falls precautions too, please.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:09","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Dean, so I can check I've explained it clearly, can you tell me what's happening tonight?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:16","w":"P","s":"I stay about a week. Tablets for the shakes, vitamin drip first. No driving, tell the DVLA. Social worker talks to me about my girl."},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. If you feel worse, feel muddled, or start seeing or hearing odd things, press the buzzer straight away. I'll check on you in the night, and the team will review you in the morning.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"Thanks, doc. Sorry for being short earlier."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"No need at all. You did the right thing coming in. Try to get some rest.","m":[1,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks his name and explains why the questions come before medicine","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Pins down the time of the last drink and daily amount in units","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past fits, DTs or hallucinations on stopping, and past detoxes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about street benzodiazepines, other drugs, falls, head injury and eating","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks capillary glucose and NEWS2 and looks for head injury, infection and Wernicke's signs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his fear, frustration and worries without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains he is high risk because of the past fit and why inpatient detox is safer","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains chlordiazepoxide and Pabrinex in plain words, with thiamine before any glucose","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about children at home, who cares for his daughter tonight, and involves the social worker","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Advises him to stop driving and tell the DVLA, and gives this in writing","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Hands over CIWA-Ar every 1–2 hours, NEWS2 and clear bleep triggers to the nurse in charge","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks him to say the plan back to check understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets warning signs to report and says when he will next be reviewed","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Pins down timing, amount and past fit, rules out mimics, explains high risk, starts chlordiazepoxide and Pabrinex first, agrees inpatient stay, covers children and DVLA, and hands over clear triggers.","borderline":"Safe prescribing and admission, but misses children at home, DVLA advice or specific CIWA-Ar bleep triggers.","fail":"Sends him home or delays treatment, gives glucose before thiamine, skips glucose and head-injury checks, or never asks about children."}},"c-7-1-2":{"setup":"Thursday 02:15, acute adult ward. Tony Marsh, 55, admitted two days ago with depression and heavy drinking, is sweating, disorientated and seeing insects. He tried to climb out of the window. Assess him, treat him safely and arrange medical transfer.","role":{"who":"Mr Tony Marsh, 55, inpatient with depression and heavy drinking (Kim, the healthcare assistant, and the medical registrar on the phone also speak)","open":"Get them off the walls! Can't you see them? They're everywhere!","manner":"Terrified, sweating and distractible, drifting in and out of attention. Suspicious of medicine but calmer with a soft voice and a familiar staff member. Kim is steady but rattled; the registrar is busy and resistant.","free":["Sees black beetles coming out of the plug socket and onto his arms","Believes men put them there and want him dead","Wants to get home to his mum","(Kim) He tried to climb out of the window 20 minutes ago"],"ifasked":["Thinks it is Sunday morning and that he is at the depot or his mum's","Loses track counting backwards from 20","Denies a fall or head injury","(Kim) Last drink Monday afternoon, about 60 hours ago, per his wife","(Kim) No fits tonight; pulse 128, temperature 38.1","(Kim) Capillary glucose 5.2","Cannot say what the tablet is for"],"worries":["You're trying to poison me.","I need to get home, my mum's waiting.","(Kim) Should we just give haloperidol?","(Registrar) Can't you manage him on your ward? We're full."],"rules":["Refuses medicine unless Kim gives it","Becomes more agitated if touched without warning; settles with calm explanation","Attention fluctuates: answers one question clearly, then loses the thread","The registrar accepts once given vital signs, risk and no overnight medical cover"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Kim, I'm Dr Okoye, the psychiatry doctor on call. Quick update before I go in: what's happened?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","who":"Healthcare assistant","s":"Tony's been shouting about insects for an hour. Sweating buckets. Twenty minutes ago he tried to climb out the window. We got him down."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Is he hurt? Any fits tonight? Do we know when he last drank?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","who":"Healthcare assistant","s":"Not hurt that we can see. No fits. His wife said Monday afternoon, so about sixty hours. Pulse 128, temp 38.1."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Please stay with me. Can someone clear the chair and anything sharp from the room, lock the window and dim the lights?","m":[8]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","who":"Healthcare assistant","s":"On it."},{"t":"0:50","band":"Mental state","w":"D","s":"Tony? Hello, I'm Dr Okoye, one of the doctors. You're in hospital, and you're safe.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"Get them off the walls! Can't you see them? They're everywhere!"},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"I can see how frightened you are. Tell me what you're seeing.","m":[4,6]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Beetles. Black ones. Coming out the plug socket. They're on my arms!"},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"That sounds horrible. Is anyone trying to harm you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"The men put them there. They want me dead. I've got to get out."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me where you are right now?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"The depot. I'm on shift. (looks around) No. My mum's. I need to get home, she's waiting."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"What day is it, and what time do you think it is?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"Sunday. Morning."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"It's Thursday night, about quarter past two, and you're on the ward. Can you count backwards from twenty for me?","m":[1,3]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Twenty, nineteen, eighteen. (stares at the wall) What was I doing?"},{"t":"2:05","band":"Physical checks","w":"D","s":"That's okay. Have you got pain anywhere? Did you fall or bang your head?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"My head's fine. Get away from my head!"},{"t":"2:17","w":"D","s":"Alright, I'll keep my hands where you can see them. Can I shine a light in your eyes and check your sugar with a finger prick?","m":[1,5]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"Quick, then."},{"t":"2:32","w":"D","s":"Pupils equal and reacting, no cuts or bumps on his scalp. Kim, what's the sugar?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:39","w":"P","who":"Healthcare assistant","s":"5.2."},{"t":"2:41","band":"Treat","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Tony, the things you're seeing come from your body coming off alcohol. It's frightening, but we can treat it. I'd like to give you a tablet to calm it.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"I'm not taking anything. You're trying to poison me!"},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"I understand you don't trust us right now. It's to stop the shaking and the bugs. Would you take it from Kim?","m":[6,7]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Only if she gives it."},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"Kim, oral lorazepam 2 milligrams now, please. I'll chart it.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:19","w":"P","who":"Healthcare assistant","s":"Should we not just give haloperidol? That's what we normally use."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Good thought, but this is alcohol withdrawal. Lorazepam treats the cause, and haloperidol on its own can bring on fits. I'll add it only if he's still very disturbed after this.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","who":"Healthcare assistant","s":"Okay. He's taken it."},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'm charting Pabrinex at the treatment dose, two pairs three times a day over 30 minutes, and taking bloods, including cultures, magnesium and clotting.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:54","band":"Capacity and safety","w":"D","s":"Tony, can you tell me what the tablet Kim just gave you is for?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:01","w":"P","s":"What tablet? (whispers) The men are coming."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Kim, he can't take in or weigh what I'm telling him, so he lacks capacity for this decision. We'll treat him in his best interests using the Mental Capacity Act, and I'll record that.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","who":"Healthcare assistant","s":"And if he goes for the window again?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"1:1 at arm's length, please. Hold him only if he's about to hurt himself, as gently and briefly as possible. I'll write that into the plan.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:37","band":"SBAR handover","w":"D","s":"Hello, medical registrar? I'm Dr Okoye, psychiatry core trainee at the inpatient unit. I need a medical bed for a man with delirium tremens.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:49","w":"P","who":"Medical registrar","s":"Go on."},{"t":"4:51","w":"D","s":"Situation: Tony Marsh, 55, delirious, seeing insects, pulse 128, temperature 38.1. Background: admitted two days ago with depression and heavy drinking, last drink about 60 hours ago. He tried to climb out of a window.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:07","w":"P","who":"Medical registrar","s":"What have you done?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Assessment: delirium tremens, possibly with sepsis and dehydration. Lorazepam 2 mg given, Pabrinex charted, glucose 5.2, no head injury seen. Request: transfer tonight, please.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:21","w":"P","who":"Medical registrar","s":"We're full. Can't you manage him on your ward?"},{"t":"5:26","w":"D","s":"I know you're stretched. But he's febrile, tachycardic and delirious, and we have no overnight medical cover here. DTs can kill. He needs a medical bed tonight.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:39","w":"P","who":"Medical registrar","s":"Fair enough. Send him to the medical assessment unit within the hour."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you. If he fits or his NEWS2 climbs before then, we'll call 999.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:52","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Kim, he's going to the medical unit with a 1:1 escort. NEWS2 every 30 minutes until then. Call me for a fit, drowsiness or breathing under 10.","m":[8,13]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","who":"Healthcare assistant","s":"Okay. What about his wife?"},{"t":"6:08","w":"D","s":"I'll ring her now as next of kin. I'll also tell the on-call consultant and complete a Datix for the window. Can you read the plan back to me, so we're both clear?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:23","w":"P","who":"Healthcare assistant","s":"1:1, half-hourly NEWS2, escort to the medical unit, call you for a fit or drowsiness, 999 if he crashes."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"Perfect, thank you. Tony, Kim's staying with you, and we're taking you to a ward that can look after your body.","m":[1,6,13]},{"t":"6:42","w":"P","s":"(quieter) Kim's staying?"},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"She is. I'll come back and see you before you go.","m":[1,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self calmly, uses his name and tells him he is in hospital and safe","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gets a rapid update from staff: last drink, fits, vital signs and the window attempt","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests orientation and attention and notes fluctuating confusion","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores visual hallucinations and persecutory fear","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks glucose, temperature and looks for head injury","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his fear and gives a simple explanation without arguing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives lorazepam first line and treatment-dose Pabrinex; declines haloperidol alone","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Puts him on 1:1 observation in a low-stimulus room with hazards removed and window locked","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses capacity, acts in best interests using the MCA, and records it","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Names delirium tremens and gives the medical registrar a structured SBAR handover","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Holds firm on the need for a medical bed tonight when challenged","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Completes a Datix and informs the on-call consultant and next of kin","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks staff can repeat the plan and escalation triggers, and reassures him before leaving","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Recognises DTs from fluctuating confusion, hallucinations and autonomic signs, gives lorazepam and Pabrinex, checks glucose and head injury, sets 1:1 under the MCA, and secures transfer with a clear SBAR.","borderline":"Right treatment and transfer, but no capacity record, no Datix, or a vague handover the registrar can push back on.","fail":"Calls it psychosis and gives haloperidol alone, waits for the day team, misses fever or head injury, or keeps him on the ward."}},"c-7-1-3":{"setup":"Wednesday 14:00, A&E liaison. Lee Barker, 42, a heavy drinker for 15 years who cut down three days ago, hears men outside his flat threatening to kill him. A&E say he is medically fine and orientated. Assess him and plan management.","role":{"who":"Mr Lee Barker, 42, long-term heavy drinker, walked into A&E (the liaison consultant also speaks on the phone)","open":"There's men outside my flat. Every night. Calling me a paedophile, saying they'll kill me. I'm not one.","manner":"Frightened, exhausted and angry at the accusation; fully alert and orientated. Defensive if told it is 'just the drink'. Relieved and calmer once believed and given a plan.","free":["Hears two or three men outside his window and in the stairwell","They call him a paedophile and say they will 'do him tonight'","Has not slept for two nights","Has seen a white van outside, but the men are gone when he looks"],"ifasked":["Voices talk about him, not to him, and laugh","Voices began Sunday; he cut down on Friday when money ran out","Usually drinks two bottles of wine and four cans of strong lager a day","Had similar voices two years ago after stopping; they went within a couple of weeks","Keeps a hammer by the door to use if they come in; no named target, no plan to look for them","Thought last night he would be 'better off dead'; no plan; his sister's children stop him","Cannabis months ago, nothing now; no past psychiatric history; father drank heavily"],"worries":["This has nothing to do with drink. I've seen their van.","Does this mean I've got schizophrenia?","I don't want tablets that make me a zombie.","If I stop drinking, will they go away?"],"rules":["Gets angry when the accusation is mentioned; settles if his anger is validated","Mentions the hammer only if asked directly about protecting himself","Becomes quietly tearful when asked about suicide; answers honestly if asked kindly","Agrees to informal admission once told he will not be labelled with schizophrenia today"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Rahman, one of the psychiatry doctors here in A&E. Is it Lee? I hear you've had a frightening few days. Can you tell me what's been happening?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"There's men outside my flat. Every night. Calling me a paedophile, saying they'll kill me. I'm not one."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying, and being called that would upset anyone.","m":[9]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"I haven't slept for two nights. I didn't know where else to go."},{"t":"0:31","band":"The voices","w":"D","s":"You did the right thing coming here. Where do the voices seem to be coming from?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"Outside. Under the window, in the stairwell. Two or three blokes."},{"t":"0:43","w":"D","s":"Do they speak to you, or talk about you?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"About me. 'He's a nonce, we'll do him tonight.' Like I'm not there. Then they laugh. I've seen a white van, but when I look out, they've gone."},{"t":"0:59","band":"Timeline","w":"D","s":"When did the voices start, and when did you cut down on the drink?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"They started Sunday night. I cut down Friday, ran out of money. Went down to a few cans."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"So they started two days after you cut down. Have you heard voices like this before, either when drinking steadily or after stopping?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"Once, two years ago, after I stopped for a bit. They went after a couple of weeks."},{"t":"1:30","band":"Drinking and drugs","w":"D","s":"How much do you usually drink over a day?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Two bottles of wine and four cans of strong lager. Fifteen years, give or take."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"That's roughly forty units a day. Any shakes or sweats now? Seeing things that aren't there, like insects?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Bit of a tremor in the mornings. Nothing like insects. Just the voices."},{"t":"1:56","w":"D","s":"Do you use anything else, like cannabis, cocaine or spice? And have you or anyone in your family ever been treated for a mental illness?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"Bit of weed, months ago. Nothing now. No mental illness. My dad drank, that's it."},{"t":"2:13","band":"Clear head","w":"D","s":"I'd like to check your head is clear. What day is it, where are we, and can you say the months backwards from December?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"Wednesday. A&E at the General. December, November, October, September, August, July, June, May, April, March, February, January."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Spot on. You're alert and fully orientated, which tells me a lot about what this is.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:38","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"I also need to ask about safety. Have you thought about protecting yourself if those men come?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Damn right. I'll sort them out myself if I have to."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Being threatened like that would make anyone furious. When you say sort them out, what have you thought about doing?","m":[7,9]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"I've got a hammer by the door. If they come in, I'll use it."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Have you thought about going to find them, or anyone in particular?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"No. I don't know who they are. I just want to be left alone."},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"Have the voices told you to hurt yourself or anyone else?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"No. They just say what they'll do to me."},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"With all this going on, have you had thoughts about ending your life?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"(quietly, tearful) Last night I thought I'd be better off dead. Then they couldn't get me."},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting and lonely. Thank you for telling me. Have you made a plan, or taken any steps?","m":[8,9]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"No. I wouldn't. My sister's kids. I couldn't do that to them."},{"t":"3:56","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Here's what I think is happening. After years of heavy drinking, cutting down can make the brain produce voices like this. It's called alcoholic hallucinosis. I believe the voices feel real and frightening.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"This has nothing to do with drink. I've seen their van."},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"I'm not saying you imagined everything. But the voices started just after you cut down, and the same happened two years ago. That pattern is common. Can we treat it and see?","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:27","w":"P","s":"Does this mean I've got schizophrenia now?"},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"Not necessarily. These voices usually settle within days or weeks off alcohol. If they don't, we'll look again. No one's labelling you today.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"So what do you want to do?"},{"t":"4:45","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'd like you to come into hospital as a voluntary patient. Stopping on your own after fifteen years isn't safe, so we'll manage the withdrawal with medicine and give vitamin B1 injections to protect your brain.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"I don't want tablets that make you a zombie."},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"Fair worry. I'd suggest a low dose at night, short term, to quieten the voices and help you sleep. We'll review after two to four weeks off alcohol and stop it if they've gone.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"Alright. I'll stay. I just want to sleep."},{"t":"5:22","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"I come in voluntary. Medicine for the withdrawal, vitamin jabs, a low tablet at night, and you check in a few weeks if the voices have gone."},{"t":"5:39","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If the voices tell you to do something, or the thoughts of dying come back, tell the nurses straight away, day or night.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:50","band":"Present","w":"D","s":"Hi, it's Dr Rahman in liaison. One-minute summary on a 42-year-old man: likely alcoholic hallucinosis. Fifteen years of heavy drinking, cut down Friday, threatening third-person voices since Sunday, clear consciousness, similar episode two years ago.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:04","w":"P","who":"Consultant","s":"Risk?"},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Hammer by his door for self-defence, but no named target and no plan to seek anyone out. Fleeting suicidal thoughts last night, no plan, protective factor his nieces and nephews. Raised, but manageable in hospital.","m":[7,13]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","who":"Consultant","s":"Plan?"},{"t":"6:21","w":"D","s":"Informal admission, chlordiazepoxide guided by CIWA-Ar, Pabrinex, low-dose olanzapine at night, review at two to four weeks. If he tries to leave while this unwell, an MHA assessment.","m":[11,13]},{"t":"6:33","w":"P","who":"Consultant","s":"Agreed. Bloods and a urine drug screen too, please."},{"t":"6:37","w":"D","s":"Will do. Lee, the bed's being arranged. You're safe here, and I'll see you before you go up.","m":[1,12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thanks. First time someone's actually listened."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains role and invites his account openly","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the voices: content, where they come from, and second or third person","d":"Content"},{"c":"Maps when the voices started against when he cut down, and any past episodes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests orientation and attention to confirm clear consciousness; asks about tremor and visual hallucinations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes a drinking history in units and asks about cannabis, cocaine and spice","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past psychiatric history and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about weapons, plans to confront the men and any named target","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his fear, anger and distress without colluding or dismissing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains alcoholic hallucinosis in plain words and avoids a schizophrenia label today","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees informal admission with assisted withdrawal, thiamine, short antipsychotic and a 2–4 week review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and safety-nets for voices urging action or suicidal thoughts","d":"Close"},{"c":"Gives the consultant a clear risk formulation and plan, including the MHA fallback","d":"Plan"}],"global":{"pass":"Confirms clear consciousness, ties onset to cutting down, asks plainly about weapons, targets and suicide, explains hallucinosis without labelling, and agrees informal admission with a set review.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis and plan, but misses the weapon question or gives no review date for the voices.","fail":"Calls it DTs or schizophrenia, skips weapons and suicide risk, or discharges him as 'just alcohol'."}},"c-7-1-4":{"setup":"Friday 11:00, acute adult ward. Pat Hughes, 60, alcohol dependent and admitted with low mood, has been unsteady, drowsy and muddled since yesterday, with sweet-smelling breath. Examine her, say what you find and escalate.","role":{"who":"Mrs Pat Hughes, 60, inpatient with low mood and long-term alcohol dependence (Grace, her nurse, and the consultant also speak)","open":"Tired. So tired. I just want to sleep.","manner":"Drowsy, slow and a little irritable; reluctant about the tummy check. Becomes tearful and self-blaming when the liver is mentioned, then calmer and grateful once reassured.","free":["Feels very tired and wants to sleep","Feels 'like I'm on a boat' when sitting up","Came in 'for my nerves'"],"ifasked":["Slipped by the bed yesterday; did not hit her head","Bowels not opened for four days; no black or bloody stools","Has not vomited blood","Trousers tight; Grace says her eyes look yellow","No double vision","(Grace) Has had diazepam as needed all week","(Grace) Capillary glucose 4.9, temperature 37.6"],"worries":["Why are you looking at my walking? I came in for my nerves.","I don't want you looking at my stomach.","Can't you just give me something to sleep?","I've done this to myself, haven't I?"],"rules":["Agrees to the tummy check once a chaperone is offered and she is told she can stop","Becomes tearful when the liver is mentioned; settles if validated without blame","Walks wide-based and cannot heel-toe; finger-nose is near normal; hands flap when held out","Grace offers more diazepam unless told to hold it"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and consent","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Lewis, one of the ward doctors. Are you Pat? The nurses say you've been unsteady and a bit muddled since yesterday. How are you feeling?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"Tired. So tired. I just want to sleep."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"I'd like to check your balance, your coordination and your tummy, to find out why. Is that alright? Grace can stay with us as a chaperone.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Why are you looking at my walking? I came in for my nerves."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"Fair question. Alcohol can affect the balance part of the brain, and the liver. Both can cause this, and these checks tell us which.","m":[9]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Go on then. Grace can stay."},{"t":"0:52","band":"Quick history","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Have you had any falls, or bumped your head?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"Slipped by the bed yesterday. Didn't hit my head."},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"When did your bowels last open? Any black or bloody stools?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"Not for four days, I think. Not black."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"Have you vomited any blood?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"1:18","band":"Cerebellar examination","w":"D","s":"Let's start with your walking. Could you walk to the door and back, then heel to toe along this line? Grace will be right beside you.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"(walks with feet wide apart, lurching at the turn) I can't do the heel-to-toe. I'll fall."},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"That's fine, stop there. Can you sit on the edge of the bed with your arms folded?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"(sways) Whoa. I feel like I'm on a boat."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Now touch my finger, then your nose, as quickly as you can. And the other hand.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"(a little slow but accurate) Like that?"},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Now run your heel down your shin, knee to ankle. Then the other leg.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"(heel wobbles off the shin) It keeps slipping."},{"t":"2:17","w":"D","s":"Fine. Tap the back then the palm of one hand on the other, as fast as you can. Now swap.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"(slightly clumsy) I'm all fingers and thumbs today."},{"t":"2:32","w":"D","s":"Keep your head still and watch my finger with just your eyes, and say if you see double.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"(eyes jerk slightly looking to each side) No double."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Can you say 'British constitution' for me?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"British consti... tution. (slightly slurred)"},{"t":"2:53","band":"Liver signs","w":"D","s":"Now hold your arms straight out, wrists bent back like stopping traffic, and keep them there for half a minute.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"(hands flap irregularly) My hands keep dropping."},{"t":"3:07","w":"D","s":"Thank you, rest them. Next I'll look at your eyes, hands and skin, then gently press your tummy. Still okay?","m":[1,7]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"I don't want you looking at my stomach."},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"That's okay to say. I'd like to check for fluid, because it changes your treatment. Grace is here, and we can stop at any point. Would that be alright?","m":[1,8]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"Alright. Be quick."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"Your eyes are yellow, there are small red spider marks on your chest, and your palms are red. Your tummy is swollen and dull at the sides, which means fluid. Your breath has a sweet smell too.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I've done this to myself, haven't I? My liver's gone."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you're feeling that. Your liver is struggling, but this drowsiness often gets better with treatment. Today is about getting you the right care, not blame.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"4:14","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Here's what I think. Your liver isn't clearing toxins well, and they're making you drowsy and muddled. Something often sets that off, like constipation or an infection. The medical team can treat it today.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:29","w":"P","s":"Can't you just give me something to sleep? I'll be fine tomorrow."},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"Sleeping tablets could make this much worse, because your liver can't clear them. Can you tell me in your words what's going on?","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","s":"My liver's not working right, it's making me muddled, and no sleeping tablets."},{"t":"4:54","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"She's had diazepam as needed all week for withdrawal. Shall I give her another?"},{"t":"5:01","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Thanks for checking, Grace. Please hold all diazepam and any other sedatives. Her drowsiness and flap look like liver-related confusion, and more sedative could tip her over. I'll mark the chart.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Okay. Anything else?"},{"t":"5:18","w":"D","s":"I'm charting Pabrinex, two pairs three times a day, as I can't rule out Wernicke's. Can you check her sugar and temperature? I'll take bloods: FBC, U&E, liver, INR, glucose, CRP and ammonia.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:33","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Sugar 4.9. Temp 37.6."},{"t":"5:36","band":"Escalate","w":"D","s":"Dr Ahmed, it's Dr Lewis about Pat Hughes. In one line: wide-based gait and truncal ataxia, plus asterixis, jaundice, spider naevi and ascites, so likely hepatic encephalopathy on top of alcohol-related cerebellar damage.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:51","w":"P","who":"Consultant","s":"Agree. What have you done?"},{"t":"5:54","w":"D","s":"Held sedatives, charted Pabrinex, sent bloods including INR and glucose. No bowels for four days, a likely trigger. I'd like same-day medical review.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","who":"Consultant","s":"Ring the medical registrar now. If she gets drowsier, she goes to A&E."},{"t":"6:12","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Grace, NEWS2 at least four-hourly, bed low, and stay close when she walks. Bleep me if she's drowsier, NEWS2 rises, or she vomits blood or passes black stools.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Got it."},{"t":"6:27","w":"D","s":"Pat, a doctor from the medical team will see you today. If you feel more muddled or sick, tell Grace straight away. I'll come back once they've seen you.","m":[9,13]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"Thank you, doctor. Sorry for being difficult."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You weren't difficult at all. Rest now.","m":[1]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the purpose, gains consent and offers a chaperone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about falls, head injury, bowels, black stools and vomiting blood","d":"Content"},{"c":"Examines gait, heel-toe walking and sitting balance for truncal ataxia","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests finger-nose, heel-shin and rapid alternating movements on both sides","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks eye movements for nystagmus and speech for dysarthria","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests for asterixis with arms out and wrists extended for about 30 seconds","d":"Content"},{"c":"Looks for jaundice, spider naevi, palmar erythema, ascites and fetor","d":"Content"},{"c":"Responds to her reluctance and self-blame without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains findings in plain words and checks her understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Holds all sedatives and explains why to the nurse","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Charts Pabrinex and sends bloods including INR and glucose","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Presents findings in one clear sentence to the consultant and arranges same-day medical review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Sets NEWS2, falls precautions and clear escalation triggers before leaving","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Consents and chaperones, completes a structured cerebellar check, finds asterixis and liver signs, holds sedatives, gives Pabrinex, sends INR and glucose, and escalates in one clear sentence for same-day review.","borderline":"Good examination, but misses asterixis or the trigger, or escalates without a clear summary.","fail":"Gives more benzodiazepine, misses the drop in conscious level, skips consent or chaperone, or files it as chronic ataxia."}},"c-7-1-5":{"setup":"Monday 15:00, acute adult ward. Rachel Cole, 39, has finished a 7-day alcohol detox and asks if a tablet can stop her relapsing again. Her LFTs are mildly raised and kidneys normal. Help her choose a medicine and arrange follow-up.","role":{"who":"Ms Rachel Cole, 39, inpatient finishing a 7-day alcohol detox, mother of a 12-year-old son","open":"Yes please. I've done this three times and always relapse within a month. I've let everyone down.","manner":"Motivated but ashamed and doubtful. Tearful about her son. Keen on 'the one that makes you sick'. Brightens when given choice and told a slip will not end her care.","free":["Three previous detoxes, each followed by relapse within a month","Cravings hit in the evenings once her son is in bed, or after a bad day at work","'One glass' becomes a bottle, then two"],"ifasked":["Goal is to stop completely","Takes co-codamol most days for back pain","Not pregnant; happy to do a test","No heart problems or psychosis","Partner works away and cannot supervise tablets","Weighs over 60 kg","Last time she lapsed, she hid it and stopped attending"],"worries":["Which one is best? Just tell me.","Will these tablets damage my liver more?","AA didn't work. I don't want to talk about God in a church hall.","Won't you give up on me if I drink?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful talking about her son; settles if validated","Mentions co-codamol only if asked about painkillers","Pushes for disulfiram until supervision is explained","Commits to calling the team after a slip once told she will not be discharged for it"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Rachel, I'm Dr Evans, one of the ward doctors. Well done on finishing the detox. You asked about tablets to help you stay off alcohol. Shall we go through that?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Yes please. I've done this three times and always relapse within a month. I've let everyone down."},{"t":"0:23","w":"D","s":"It takes real strength to come back a fourth time. Most people need more than one go before it sticks.","m":[6]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"(tearful) My son's twelve. He's stopped believing me when I say this is the last time."},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful, and it's clear how much he matters to you. Let's see what we can do differently this time.","m":[6]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"0:54","band":"Her pattern","w":"D","s":"What usually happens in the days before you drink again?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Cravings in the evening once he's in bed. Or a bad day at work, and I tell myself one glass."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"And one glass becomes?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"A bottle. Then two. Then I'm back where I started."},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"Is your goal to stop completely, or to cut down?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"Stop. Cutting down never works for me."},{"t":"1:28","band":"Safety checks","w":"D","s":"That makes sense. A few checks, because they change which tablet is safe. Do you take painkillers like codeine, tramadol or co-codamol?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"Co-codamol most days, for my back. Is that a problem?"},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; it matters, and I'll come back to it. Could you be pregnant at the moment?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"No. But I'll do a test if you need one."},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"Yes please, we check before starting any of these. Any heart problems, or have you ever had psychosis?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"Your blood tests show your liver is a bit strained, which is common after heavy drinking. Your kidneys are normal. Both matter here.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Will these tablets mess up my liver even more?"},{"t":"2:29","band":"The options","w":"D","s":"Sensible worry. There are three options. The first, acamprosate, eases cravings. It's cleared by the kidneys, not the liver, so it suits you. The main side effect is loose stools.","m":[5,7]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"And the others?"},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"Naltrexone takes the pleasure out of a drink, so a slip is less likely to become a binge. But it blocks codeine, so with your co-codamol it could put you into withdrawal and stop your pain relief working.","m":[4,7]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"What about the one that makes you sick if you drink? That's what I need."},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"Disulfiram. Even a little alcohol, in mouthwash or sauces, causes flushing, vomiting and a racing heart. Someone needs to watch you take it daily, with regular liver tests, as it can rarely harm the liver.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"Nobody's going to watch me take a tablet every day. My partner works away."},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"Then it's not the best fit right now. Which matters more to you: the evening cravings, or a slip turning into a binge?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"The cravings. That's what gets me every time."},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"Then acamprosate sounds like a good match. How do you feel about it?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"Yes. Let's do that."},{"t":"4:02","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"You're over 60 kilos, so it's two tablets three times a day, starting today. We'd continue for up to six months, then review.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"Who gives me it after I leave?"},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"We'll supply the first month. Your GP takes over after that, and I'll write to them today with the dose, how long, and a kidney check.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"4:32","w":"D","s":"Tablets work best with support. I've booked you with the community alcohol team a week on Thursday.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"AA didn't work for me. I don't want to sit in a church hall talking about God."},{"t":"4:49","w":"D","s":"That's fine. SMART Recovery is practical and not religious, and the alcohol team does one-to-one work. Which sounds more like you?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"SMART, maybe. I'll give it a go."},{"t":"5:04","band":"Lapses","w":"D","s":"What would you do if you had a drink after you leave?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Honestly? Hide it. Stop going to appointments. That's what I did last time."},{"t":"5:17","w":"D","s":"A lot of people do. This time, a slip means you call the team, not drop out. We won't discharge you for it, and you keep taking the tablets unless the drinking gets heavy again.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"5:34","w":"P","s":"Really? I thought you'd give up on me."},{"t":"5:38","w":"D","s":"No. A slip tells us what to work on. Can we agree you'll ring them if it happens?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:47","w":"P","s":"Okay. I'll ring."},{"t":"5:50","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed, so I know I've been clear?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"Acamprosate, two tablets three times a day for up to six months. GP after the first month. Alcohol team a week on Thursday, try SMART, and if I slip I ring them."},{"t":"6:12","w":"D","s":"Perfect. If cravings get overwhelming, or you feel low or unsafe, call the crisis line, or 111 and choose option 2. The numbers will be on your discharge letter.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:26","w":"P","s":"Thank you."},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"One more thing: because of the alcohol dependence, you need to tell the DVLA. I'll put that in writing too. Any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"No. I feel like I've got a proper plan this time."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You do. The ward will call you within a week of going home. Good luck, Rachel.","m":[1,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges the completed detox and agrees the agenda","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores her relapse pattern and triggers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks whether her goal is abstinence or cutting down","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about opioid painkillers, pregnancy, heart problems and psychosis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Links her LFTs and kidney function to the choice of medicine","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her guilt and hopelessness about past relapses","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains acamprosate, naltrexone and disulfiram in plain words, with side effects","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Shares the decision by asking what matters most to her","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees acamprosate dose, start date, duration and GP prescribing after the first month","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Books community alcohol team within 2 weeks and offers SMART Recovery or AA","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a lapse means calling the team, not dropping out","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks her to summarise the plan to check understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets with crisis contacts and DVLA advice, and closes with follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Checks goal, opioids, pregnancy and blood results, explains all three medicines clearly, agrees acamprosate with dose, duration and GP handover, books community follow-up, and normalises lapses.","borderline":"Right medicine, but misses the opioid or pregnancy check, or leaves follow-up and lapse plan vague.","fail":"Starts naltrexone with co-codamol, gives unsupervised disulfiram, or discharges with no medicine or community plan."}},"c-7-2-1":{"setup":"Wednesday 10:30, acute adult ward. Jamie Reid, 33, detained under section 2 for mania, says he uses heroin daily and is aching, yawning and runny-nosed. He wants methadone or he'll leave. Assess him and explain the treatment options.","role":{"who":"Mr Jamie Reid, 33, detained under section 2 for a manic episode, daily heroin user (Ali, the ward nurse, also speaks)","open":"Give me methadone now or I'm walking out. I'll get gear on the street.","manner":"Restless, irritable and fast-talking, rattling with withdrawal. Feels disbelieved. Calms when treated as truthful and given a timescale; honest about risks once trust builds.","free":["Used heroin daily for five years","Last used about 10 pm last night","Aching, yawning, runny nose, stomach cramps","Wants methadone or 'something' now"],"ifasked":["Mostly smokes, sometimes injects; has shared needles a couple of times; never tested","Spends about forty pounds a day, three or four times daily","Street diazepam when he can get it; crack at weekends; little alcohol","Overdosed once last year; a friend gave naloxone spray","Left the community drug service in the summer; used the chemist on the High Street","Two daughters, 4 and 7, live with their mother; he has them at weekends and never uses around them","Wants to be stable on a script, not come off"],"worries":["You think I'm lying about how much I use?","Will social services take my kids?","Can I just have diazepam to take the edge off?","How long do I have to wait?"],"rules":["Threatens to leave until given a clear timescale and symptom relief","Gets angry if he feels disbelieved; settles when told everyone gets the same checks","Mentions children only if asked; anxious about social services until reassured","Chooses buprenorphine once both options are explained"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Jamie, I'm Dr Clarke, one of the ward doctors. The nurses say you're in withdrawal. I want to help. Can we talk for a few minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"Give me methadone now or I'm walking out. I'll get gear on the street."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I hear you. You're aching and you want it to stop. I want that too, and the quickest way is to get a few facts straight first.","m":[1,7]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Fine. Quick."},{"t":"0:34","band":"Drug history","w":"D","s":"When did you last use, and do you smoke it or inject?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","s":"Last night, about ten, before they brought me in. Smoke it mostly. Inject sometimes."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"How much do you use, and how often?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"Every day for five years. Forty quid a day, three or four goes."},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"When you inject, do you ever share needles or other kit?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Couple of times. Never been tested."},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest; that's really useful. We can sort testing while you're here.","m":[3,7]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"Whatever."},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"Do you use anything else? Benzos, alcohol, crack, pregabalin?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"Street Valium when I can get it. Bit of crack at weekends. Not much drink."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Have you ever overdosed, or been given naloxone?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Once, last year. Mate gave me the spray. Scared me, to be honest."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"That must have been frightening. Are you on a script from a drug service now, and which pharmacy?","m":[5,7]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"I was with the drug service in town. Dropped out in the summer. The chemist on the High Street."},{"t":"2:00","band":"Children","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Do you have children, or anyone who depends on you?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"If I tell you, will social services take my kids?"},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"That's a real worry for lots of parents. Using drugs doesn't mean your children get taken. My job is to make sure they're safe and you get help.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Two girls, four and seven. They live with their mum. I have them weekends."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"Do you keep anything at your place they could get hold of?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"No. I never use when they're there."},{"t":"2:41","w":"D","s":"Good. What do you want from treatment: stable on a script, or coming off completely?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"Stable. I can't do cold turkey again."},{"t":"2:53","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Okay. First, the nurse will score your withdrawal with a checklist called COWS, and we'll test your urine. I'll ring the drug service and your chemist to check you're not on a script we don't know about.","m":[5,8]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"You think I'm lying? I'm rattling, look at me."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"I believe you, and I can see you're in withdrawal. Everyone gets the same checks, because street heroin varies so much that a guessed dose can kill. Checking protects you.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:29","w":"P","s":"So what do I get? Methadone or Subutex?"},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"Both stop withdrawal and cravings. Methadone is a daily liquid. It works very well, but it builds up slowly and is riskier in overdose, so we start low.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"And the other one?"},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"Buprenorphine is a tablet under your tongue. It's safer if you mix drugs like Valium. But you need to be properly in withdrawal before the first dose, or it makes you feel worse.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"I'm in withdrawal now, and I do take Valium. The buprenorphine, then."},{"t":"4:11","w":"D","s":"That sounds sensible. I'll agree the starting dose with the addiction team this morning, and we'll build it up over a few days.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","s":"How long's that? I'm dying here."},{"t":"4:25","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"A few hours, not days. Meanwhile I'll give you medicine for the cramps, diarrhoea and sickness.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"Can I just have some diazepam to take the edge off?"},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"I understand you want relief now. Mixing diazepam with opioids is one of the main causes of overdose, so I won't add that.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"(sighs) And what if I just go?"},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"You're on a section for your mood, so the team would have to bring you back. But I'd rather you stayed because the plan works. Your tolerance drops fast here, and street gear after a few days could kill you.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Okay. I'll stay if I get the bupe today."},{"t":"5:18","w":"D","s":"If your score and checks are as I expect, yes. While you're here, can we test your blood for hepatitis B, C and HIV, and start the hepatitis B vaccine?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:31","w":"P","s":"Go on then."},{"t":"5:34","w":"D","s":"And before any leave, you'll get a naloxone kit and a reminder about tolerance.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:41","band":"Handover","w":"D","s":"Ali, please do a COWS score now and every four hours, and send a urine drug screen. No opioid until I've spoken to the drug service and chemist.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:54","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Okay. What about his symptoms?"},{"t":"5:57","w":"D","s":"Loperamide and metoclopramide as needed; I'll chart them. Naloxone's on the chart. If he's drowsy, has pinpoint pupils or breathing under 10, give it and call 2222.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Got it."},{"t":"6:12","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Jamie, can you tell me what happens next, so I know I've been clear?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"Nurse scores me, wee test, you ring the drug team and the chemist, then bupe today. Pills for the runs meanwhile. Blood tests, and naloxone before leave."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"Spot on. If you feel much worse, or the urge to leave gets strong, tell Ali and she'll call me. I'll be back within two hours with an update.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Two hours. Okay. Cheers, doc."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges his withdrawal and agrees to talk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what he uses, how much, how often, route and treatment goal","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about injecting, sharing equipment and past blood-borne virus testing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about benzodiazepines, alcohol, crack, pregabalin and past overdose","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about a current script, drug service and dispensing pharmacy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about children and dependants, and safe storage at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his distress and fear of being disbelieved","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains COWS, urine drug screen and calls to drug service and pharmacy before any opioid","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains methadone and buprenorphine in plain words and agrees a choice with the addiction team","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers symptom relief and declines to add benzodiazepines, with reasons","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers BBV tests, hepatitis B vaccine and take-home naloxone with a tolerance warning","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Addresses his threat to leave honestly, without using the section as a threat","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Hands over COWS frequency, naloxone and escalation triggers to the nurse","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding and closes with a clear timescale","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Takes a full drug history including injecting, sedatives and overdose, confirms with COWS, urine screen and calls before prescribing, explains both options, offers BBV tests and naloxone, and keeps him engaged.","borderline":"Safe and kind, but misses children, sharing equipment or naloxone, or gives no clear timescale.","fail":"Prescribes a large dose on his word, adds benzodiazepines, refuses all treatment, or uses the section as a threat."}},"c-7-2-2":{"setup":"Saturday 23:40, acute adult ward, on call. Darren, 38, informal, says he missed four days of methadone and wants his usual dose. Later the nurse bleeps: Kelly, 29, back from leave, is barely rousable. Keep both safe.","role":{"who":"Mr Darren Hughes, 38, male, informal patient on methadone (later: Ms Kelly Shaw, 29, female patient back from leave, and Grace, nurse in charge)","open":"Doc, I'm rattling here. I just need my methadone. Eighty mil, same as always.","manner":"Darren is sweaty, restless and irritable, then bargains; settles once believed and offered real relief. Kelly is groggy, then defensive and frightened after naloxone. Grace is brisk and capable.","free":["Darren: on methadone 80 mg daily for two years from a community pharmacy","Darren: missed the last four days because he could not get to the pharmacy before admission","Darren: cramps, sweats, runny nose, cannot settle","Kelly: returned from three days' leave this evening"],"ifasked":["Darren: pick-up is supervised daily at Boots on the high street","Darren: smoked a little heroin on Wednesday, nothing since; no tablets, a couple of cans","Darren: no heart medicines; was once told his ECG was 'a bit long'","Darren: has no naloxone kit at home","Kelly: RR 6, sats 84% on air, GCS 8, pinpoint pupils (Grace)","Kelly: took 'a bit of gear and a couple of pregabalin' at about 21:30","Kelly: did not mean to die; just wanted to sleep","Grace: her bag was not searched on return"],"worries":["Darren: Why won't you just give me my dose?","Darren: Just give me half, then. Forty.","Kelly: I'm fine now. I'm not going to A&E.","Kelly: Am I going to get kicked off the ward?"],"rules":["Darren threatens to 'go out and score' if refused; agrees to stay if believed and given symptom relief","Kelly becomes tearful and ashamed when asked what she took; settles if not judged","Kelly accepts transfer once told plainly the drug can outlast the injection","Grace gives the room search findings only if asked"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, Darren? I'm Dr Okoye, the doctor on call tonight. The nurses said you're struggling. Can I sit down?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","who":"Darren","s":"Doc, I'm rattling here. I just need my methadone. Eighty mil, same as always."},{"t":"0:17","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"You look really uncomfortable, and I believe you're on methadone. Let me get a few details so I can help properly. Which pharmacy do you use, and is it supervised?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","who":"Darren","s":"Boots on the high street. Supervised, every day. Two years now."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"When did you last have a dose?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","who":"Darren","s":"Tuesday. I couldn't get there before I came in. So four days."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"And since then, any heroin, tablets or alcohol?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","who":"Darren","s":"Bit of brown on Wednesday. A couple of cans. Nothing since I got here."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being straight with me. Do you take anything for your heart, or has anyone mentioned your ECG?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","who":"Darren","s":"Someone said it was a bit long once. Nothing else."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"What are you feeling right now? Cramps, sweats, runny nose, aches?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","who":"Darren","s":"All of it. My guts are killing me. Why won't you just give me my dose?"},{"t":"1:29","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you feel this rough. Here's my worry. After four days off, your body has lost some of its tolerance. Eighty tonight could slow your breathing enough to kill you, especially with your ECG.","m":[9,3]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","who":"Darren","s":"Just give me half, then. Forty. That's safe, isn't it?"},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"I can see you're meeting me halfway. But without confirming your dose, I can't call any amount safe tonight. Withdrawal is horrible, but it won't kill you. Overdose can.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","who":"Darren","s":"Fine. I'll go out and score, then."},{"t":"2:09","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"That's your choice, but I'd really rather you didn't. Using tonight, with lower tolerance, is when people die. I'll chart something for the cramps, the sickness and the diarrhoea now, and help you sleep.","m":[13,4]},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"First thing tomorrow I'll ask the day team to ring Boots to confirm your dose and last pick-up, and ask them to hold your prescription while you're here. Then the addiction team restarts you safely, with an ECG. Will you give me tonight?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","who":"Darren","s":"Yeah. Alright. If you sort the cramps."},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"Could you sum up what we've agreed?","m":[12]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","who":"Darren","s":"No meth tonight, tablets for the rattle, you ring Boots, then I restart tomorrow."},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If you feel much worse, or think about using, tell the nurse straight away and she'll bleep me.","m":[13]},{"t":"3:10","band":"One hour later: the bleep","w":"P","who":"Nurse in charge","s":"(bleep) Doctor, it's Grace on Ward 3. Kelly's just back from leave. I can't wake her properly and her breathing's really slow."},{"t":"3:21","band":"Emergency","w":"D","s":"I'm coming now. Pull the emergency call, bring the trolley, oxygen and naloxone, and ring 999 if she doesn't respond.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"Kelly, can you hear me? Open your eyes. Grace, what are her obs?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","who":"Nurse in charge","s":"Resps 6, sats 84 on air, GCS about 8. Pinpoint pupils."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"Airway open, head tilt, high-flow oxygen, and bag her if her breathing drops further. This looks like opioid toxicity. Naloxone 400 micrograms IM now, please, and write down the time.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","who":"Nurse in charge","s":"Given at 00:52."},{"t":"4:01","w":"D","s":"Thanks. If there's no change in two to three minutes, we repeat. We're aiming for her breathing, not wide awake. Check her glucose too, please.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","who":"Nurse in charge","s":"Second dose at 00:55. Resps 14 now, sats 95. She's stirring."},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"Good. Kelly, I'm a doctor. You're on the ward. Did you take anything while you were out?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:27","w":"P","who":"Kelly","s":"(groggy, tearful) Just a bit of gear and a couple of pregabalin. Same as always. Am I in trouble?"},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. You're not in trouble. You must be scared and embarrassed right now, and that's okay. My job is keeping you safe.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:49","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Did you mean to hurt yourself tonight, or was it to take the edge off?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","who":"Kelly","s":"Just wanted to sleep. I wasn't trying to die."},{"t":"5:02","w":"D","s":"After days on the ward your body can't take what it used to, and pregabalin slows breathing too. That's why it hit you so hard.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:14","w":"P","who":"Kelly","s":"I'm fine now. I'm not going to A&E. Leave me alone."},{"t":"5:20","band":"Transfer","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you're awake. But the injection wears off within an hour, and what you took lasts much longer. You could stop breathing again. You need watching for at least six hours, in A&E.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:36","w":"P","who":"Kelly","s":"(quietly) Okay. Will I get kicked off the ward?"},{"t":"5:41","w":"D","s":"No. We'll talk about leave and safety when you're back, but you won't be punished for being honest. Grace, please ring 999 for transfer, and send the times of both naloxone doses with her.","m":[9,8]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","who":"Nurse in charge","s":"Will do. Anything else?"},{"t":"6:00","band":"Safety and close","w":"D","s":"Yes. Please search her room and bag under ward policy with a second nurse, and I'll do the Datix and ring the on-call consultant.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:12","w":"D","s":"Both Kelly and Darren need a take-home naloxone kit and training before any leave or discharge. I'll add that to their plans.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:23","w":"P","who":"Nurse in charge","s":"Found two more pregabalin in her washbag. Nothing else."},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"Thanks, I'll note that. So, handover: Kelly to A&E for at least six hours. Darren gets symptom relief, no methadone tonight, Boots and the addiction team in the morning. I'll review him at eight before I go.","m":[14,5]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","who":"Nurse in charge","s":"Got it. I'll let him know you'll see him at eight."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges his withdrawal distress and says he believes him","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks his dose, pharmacy, supervision, last dose, other drug use and heart history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Declines unconfirmed methadone and explains lost tolerance after four missed days in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Assesses withdrawal symptoms and charts symptom relief","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans to phone the pharmacy to confirm dose and last collection and suspend the community script","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Goes to the drowsy patient first, calls for help and the trolley, and runs ABCDE","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives naloxone 400 micrograms IM, repeats to effect and asks for each dose time recorded","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Calls 999 for transfer and explains at least 6 hours' monitoring because naloxone wears off","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates distress, fear and shame without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what she took, including pregabalin, and whether she meant to harm herself","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Arranges Datix, a ward-policy search, consultant update and take-home naloxone for both","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking a patient to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets: warns against using tonight and says who to tell if symptoms worsen","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes with a clear handover and when each patient will next be reviewed","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Treats the overdose first with ABCDE, timed naloxone and 999 transfer for prolonged monitoring, and safely withholds unconfirmed methadone with kind explanation and symptom relief.","borderline":"Both patients kept safe, but monitoring time, pharmacy check, Datix or naloxone kits left vague.","fail":"Gives an unconfirmed methadone dose, stops monitoring once she wakes, or delays going to the drowsy patient."}},"c-7-2-3":{"setup":"Wednesday 14:00, CMHT clinic. Linda, 54, has taken diazepam and lorazepam from her GP for nine years since her husband died, and now buys extra online. Her GP wants advice. Assess her dependence and agree a safe reduction plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Linda Marsh, 54, female, patient referred by her GP","open":"Before you start, please don't take them off me. I can't do that again.","manner":"Tense, guarded and frightened at first. Ashamed and tearful about the online tablets and her husband. Relaxes and becomes engaged once she feels in control of the pace.","free":["Started diazepam after her husband Mike died nine years ago; lorazepam added later for 'bad nights'","Tried to stop on her own three years ago and 'thought she was dying'","Frightened of being cut off","Works part-time in a school office"],"ifasked":["Diazepam 5 mg three times a day and lorazepam 1 mg at night, both from the GP","Buys 'blue' diazepam online; takes one extra most days when she runs short","Last stop: shaking, no sleep, felt unreal; no fits, no confusion, no hallucinations","About 6 units of alcohol a week; no painkillers, pregabalin or gabapentin","Low at times but no thoughts of ending her life; her daughter and grandson keep her going","Drives to work every day","Breathing exercises helped a little in the past"],"worries":["Will I have to stop everything at once?","Why switch to diazepam? The lorazepam works better.","Am I going to lose my licence?","What if I can't cope with a step?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when talking about the online tablets or Mike; settles if not blamed","Only mentions the online tablets if asked about every source","Engages fully once told she can set the pace and pause","Asks about driving only if the doctor raises it"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mrs Marsh? I'm Dr Ahmed, one of the psychiatrists. Thanks for coming in. Your GP asked us to help with your tablets.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Before you start, please don't take them off me. I can't do that again."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I hear you. Nobody is going to stop them suddenly. Today is about understanding what you take, and then making a plan together, at a pace you choose. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Okay. Linda's fine."},{"t":"0:38","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Linda. Can you tell me every tablet you take in a normal day, and where each one comes from?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:49","w":"P","s":"Diazepam, five milligrams, three times a day. And lorazepam, one at night. The GP does both."},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"Is that everything, including anything you get elsewhere?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"(pause, tearful) I buy some online. Blue ones. When I run short, which is most days. I feel like a junkie saying it."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; I know that wasn't easy to say. Lots of people end up there after years on prescribed tablets. It doesn't make you a bad person.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"I just never meant for any of this. It started when Mike died."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry about Mike. Losing your husband, and then carrying this for nine years, is a lot. Those online tablets worry me, though, because you can't know what's in them.","m":[6,2]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"I know. Some are stronger than others."},{"t":"1:56","band":"Risks","w":"D","s":"What happens when you miss a dose or run out?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"I shake, I can't sleep, my heart races. Three years ago I just stopped, and I thought I was dying."},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. Did you ever have a fit, get confused, or see things that weren't there?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"No. Just felt unreal, like behind glass. Lasted about a week, then I started again."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"How much do you drink over a week? And any painkillers, pregabalin or gabapentin?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"A glass of wine on Fridays and Saturdays. Maybe six units. No painkillers."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Have you ever had thoughts about ending your life?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"Low at times. But no. My daughter and my grandson keep me going."},{"t":"3:00","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you have them. Here's what I think is happening. Your body has got used to these tablets. When levels drop, it reacts. That's dependence, and it's very common after long use.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"So I'm an addict."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"I'd say your body is dependent, and that came from years of prescribing. It isn't a failing. Lorazepam wears off in hours, so you get mini-withdrawals. Diazepam lasts much longer, which makes steps smoother.","m":[7,6]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","s":"But the lorazepam works better for me."},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"It feels stronger because it's fast in, fast out. That's exactly what makes it harder to come off.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:51","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Your one milligram of lorazepam is roughly ten of diazepam. With your fifteen, that's about twenty-five a day. I'll have our pharmacist check that against the BNF before anything changes.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"And then what? You cut it in half?"},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"No. We switch to diazepam only, hold steady for two weeks, then cut about a tenth every two to four weeks. You help choose the pace. If a step is too hard, we pause there, not go back. It will be written down for you.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"That's slower than I thought. That I could maybe do."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"To make it safe, I'd ask the GP to give weekly prescriptions from one doctor, and you'd stop the online ones. Could you agree to that?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"Yes. I want to stop buying them. I hate it."},{"t":"4:59","w":"D","s":"I'll also refer you to NHS Talking Therapies for CBT for anxiety and sleep, so you have other tools as the dose comes down. You can self-refer too.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:13","w":"P","s":"Okay. Do I still need to drive? I drive to work."},{"t":"5:20","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"Good question. Don't drive if you feel drowsy, especially after a step. Because you've been taking more than prescribed, the DVLA guidance says you should tell them. I'll put that advice in the letter to your GP.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:38","w":"P","s":"(upset) Will I lose my licence?"},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"I understand that's worrying. The DVLA decides, and being in treatment counts in your favour. I'm happy to help with any forms.","m":[6,12]},{"t":"5:54","w":"P","s":"Alright. Thank you."},{"t":"5:57","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the plan in your words, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:06","w":"P","s":"Switch to diazepam, check the dose, hold two weeks, then small cuts I choose. Weekly scripts, no online. Talking therapy. Careful with driving."},{"t":"6:18","w":"D","s":"Perfect. If withdrawal gets bad, call your GP or our team, and don't buy more. If you feel in crisis, ring 111 and choose option 2.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:31","w":"P","s":"And I'll see you again?"},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Yes, in four weeks. I'll write to your GP today with the full schedule. You've done really well today.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel a bit less scared."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and reassures early that tablets will not be stopped suddenly","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Lists each benzodiazepine, dose, timing and source, including online supply","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past withdrawal, fits, confusion and hallucinations","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol, opioids and gabapentinoids","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about mood and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her shame and grief without blame","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains dependence and why one long-acting diazepam is easier to reduce, in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Converts to one total diazepam dose, to be checked with a pharmacist against the BNF","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees a slow, patient-led, written schedule with pauses rather than reversals","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges weekly dispensing from one prescriber and agrees to stop online buying","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Refers to NHS Talking Therapies for CBT for anxiety and insomnia","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives driving and DVLA advice and says it will be recorded in the GP letter","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking her to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets with who to call if withdrawal worsens, crisis number and review in 4 weeks","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Takes a full benzodiazepine history including online supply and seizure risk, explains dependence without blame, and agrees a slow, written, patient-led diazepam taper with one prescriber, CBT and driving advice.","borderline":"Safe and kind, but conversion, dispensing arrangements or DVLA advice left vague.","fail":"Stops or halves tablets abruptly, misses online buying or seizure risk, or lectures her."}},"c-7-2-4":{"setup":"Sunday 03:10, A&E. Jamie, 31, is sweating, shaking and paranoid. His friend says he used GBL every hour or two for months and ran out last evening. A&E has referred him as 'acute psychosis'. Assess him and advise the A&E registrar.","role":{"who":"Mr Jamie Cole, 31, male, A&E patient (with his friend Ollie, 30, and Dr Hayes, A&E registrar)","open":"Who's that? Shut the curtain! They're outside, I can hear them.","manner":"Jamie is terrified, sweating and distractible, briefly calmer with a slow, quiet voice. Ollie is worried and guilty. The registrar is busy and keen to transfer him to the psychiatric ward.","free":["Jamie: hears men outside the curtain coming to hurt him","Ollie: GBL 'every hour or two, day and night' for months","Ollie: he ran out and stopped yesterday evening","Registrar: 'He's psychotic, can't he go to your ward?'"],"ifasked":["Ollie: last dose about 8 pm, roughly seven hours ago","Ollie: crystal meth at parties at weekends, last used Friday night; a few drinks, no benzodiazepines","Ollie: no fits, never this bad before; no past mental illness","Registrar: HR 128, BP 165/100, temperature 38.1, GCS 14, glucose 6.2","Jamie: thinks he is 'at a party', does not know the day","Ollie: Jamie goes to chemsex parties and sometimes attends a sexual health clinic","Jamie: agrees Ollie can be told what is happening"],"worries":["Ollie: Should we have brought him sooner?","Registrar: Can't you just take him to your ward?","Jamie: Are you going to lock me up?","Ollie: Is he going to be okay?"],"rules":["Jamie tries to get off the trolley if voices are raised; settles with calm reassurance","Ollie mentions crystal meth only if asked about other drugs","Ollie becomes tearful and guilty when told how serious it is; settles if validated","Registrar accepts medical admission once given the dosing history and obs"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Jamie. My name's Dr Mensah, I'm one of the doctors. You're in hospital, and you're safe. Can we turn the lights down a bit and keep it quiet in here?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","who":"Jamie","s":"Who's that? Shut the curtain! They're outside, I can hear them."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. Nobody here is going to hurt you. I'm going to stay with you for a few minutes. Can you tell me where you are?","m":[6,5]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","who":"Jamie","s":"A party? No. I don't know. Why am I shaking?"},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"Do you know what day it is?","m":[5]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","who":"Jamie","s":"Friday? I dunno."},{"t":"0:57","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Jamie. Do you see or hear things others can't?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","who":"Jamie","s":"Men. Behind the curtain. They want to hurt me."},{"t":"1:11","band":"Obs and history","w":"D","s":"Dr Hayes, what are his obs, and how have they changed?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","who":"A&E registrar","s":"Pulse 128, BP 165 over 100, temp 38.1, GCS 14. Pulse was 110 an hour ago. Glucose 6.2. He's psychotic. Can't he just go to your ward?"},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"Let me just get the history first. Ollie, is it? When exactly did Jamie last take GBL?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","who":"Friend","s":"About eight last night. He ran out. Before that, every hour or two, day and night, for months."},{"t":"1:56","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful. Has he used anything else, like alcohol, crystal meth, mephedrone or benzodiazepines?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","who":"Friend","s":"A few drinks. And crystal at parties, at the weekends. Last time Friday. Should I have said?"},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that matters. Has he ever had a fit, or been this unwell when he stopped before?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","who":"Friend","s":"No fits. Never like this. (voice cracking) We thought he'd sleep it off. Should we have brought him sooner?"},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"You brought him in, and that was the right thing. Most people don't know how fast GBL withdrawal turns serious.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:51","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Dr Hayes, I don't think this is a primary psychosis. He dosed round the clock, stopped seven hours ago, and now he's hot, fast, hypertensive and disoriented. That's GBL withdrawal with delirium, and it's a medical emergency.","m":[5,7]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","who":"A&E registrar","s":"So what do you want us to do?"},{"t":"3:18","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Early, high-dose benzodiazepines, titrated to symptoms. Please check TOXBASE and ring the Poisons service if needed. He needs close monitoring and a critical care review. Baclofen only on specialist advice.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","who":"A&E registrar","s":"And haloperidol for the paranoia?"},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"I'd avoid antipsychotics first line. They don't treat withdrawal, they lower the seizure threshold and worsen temperature. A psychiatric ward can't manage this, and the MHA isn't the right tool.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:57","w":"P","who":"A&E registrar","s":"Fair. But he keeps trying to get off the trolley."},{"t":"4:04","w":"D","s":"Right now he can't understand or weigh up what's happening, so he lacks capacity for this decision. Treat him in his best interests using the Mental Capacity Act. Keep any holding brief and proportionate, and record it.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:25","band":"Consent and context","w":"D","s":"Jamie, are you okay with me talking to Ollie about what's going on?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","who":"Jamie","s":"Yeah. Ollie's fine."},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Ollie, does Jamie go to chemsex parties, or see a sexual health clinic?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","who":"Friend","s":"Sometimes, yeah. He goes to the clinic now and then."},{"t":"4:53","w":"D","s":"Thanks. When he's clearer, the team will offer HIV and STI tests, and PEP if there's been a risk in the last 72 hours. If anything happened that wasn't wanted, we'll make sure he gets support.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:14","w":"P","who":"Friend","s":"Is he going to be okay?"},{"t":"5:18","w":"D","s":"With the right treatment, most people recover well. Here's a leaflet on GBL and the local drug service, for you and your friends.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:32","w":"P","who":"Jamie","s":"(suddenly) Are you going to lock me up?"},{"t":"5:38","w":"D","s":"No, Jamie. Your body is reacting to stopping GBL suddenly. It's a medical problem, and we have medicine that works. We're keeping you safe.","m":[1,8]},{"t":"5:52","band":"Safety and close","w":"D","s":"Dr Hayes, can you tell me back what you'll do, so we're aligned?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:01","w":"P","who":"A&E registrar","s":"Medical admission, big benzo doses to effect, TOXBASE, critical care outreach, no haloperidol, MCA if needed."},{"t":"6:11","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Call critical care straight away if he has a fit, his temperature rises, or his GCS drops. Those are signs it's getting worse.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","who":"A&E registrar","s":"Got it."},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"I'll write my assessment now, refer him to addiction liaison for the morning, and stay involved. Bleep me if you're worried, and I'll review him after the next dose.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","who":"A&E registrar","s":"Thanks. That helps."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, lowers stimulation, orients him and reassures him he is safe","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks pulse, BP, temperature, GCS and glucose and notes the trend","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks the friend for exact time of last dose and how often he dosed","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about co-used drugs, alcohol and any past fits","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Tests orientation and hallucinations and names withdrawal delirium, not primary psychosis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates fear and guilt without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Tells registrar it is an emergency: early high-dose benzodiazepines, TOXBASE, close monitoring, critical care review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises against antipsychotics first line and against psychiatric admission or the MHA","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Assesses capacity, treats under MCA best interests and records any restraint","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks his permission before sharing with friend and gives harm reduction information","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Covers chemsex, sexual health testing, PEP and possible safeguarding","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks the registrar's understanding by asking for the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets with clear escalation triggers: seizure, rising temperature, falling GCS","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes with addiction liaison referral, staying involved and documentation","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gets the timing and frequency of GBL use, recognises withdrawal delirium as a medical emergency, steers early high-dose benzodiazepines and monitoring, avoids antipsychotics and the MHA, and uses the MCA.","borderline":"Recognises withdrawal but is vague on urgency, benzodiazepine dosing, capacity or chemsex follow-up.","fail":"Accepts the 'psychosis' label, advises an antipsychotic or psychiatric admission, or misses the dosing history."}},"c-7-2-5":{"setup":"Friday 22:15, Section 136 suite. Police brought Connor, 24, after he ran in traffic. He is sweating and restless with a clenched jaw. Next door, Chloe, 19, mentions daily ketamine and pain passing urine. Manage both safely.","role":{"who":"Mr Connor Price, 24, male, detained under s136 (with a suite nurse; PC Davies, police officer; Sam, AMHP, by phone; and Ms Chloe Reid, 19, female, in the next room)","open":"Get off me! I've done nothing wrong. Let me out of here!","manner":"Connor is hot, agitated and fearful, then cooperative with calm handling. PC Davies is protective and suggests holding him down. Chloe is guarded and defensive, then frightened and more open.","free":["Connor: 'Had a couple of lines and a pill at a party'","Connor: feels his heart pounding","PC Davies: he was running between cars shouting","Chloe: uses ketamine most nights; it hurts to pass urine"],"ifasked":["Connor: cocaine and MDMA from about 20:00; drank a lot of water","Connor: no chest pain or headache; takes no antidepressants","Obs: temperature 39.0, pulse 140, BP 170/105, NEWS2 high","PC Davies: handcuffed about five minutes in the van, not face down; arrived 21:50","Chloe: needs more than before to get the same effect","Chloe: goes to the toilet constantly, saw blood once last week, gets tummy cramps","Chloe: low mood, no current thoughts of harming herself"],"worries":["PC Davies: Can't we just hold him face down until he settles?","AMHP: Can we finish the MHA assessment first?","Chloe: Will my bladder be damaged forever?","Chloe: I'm not going to a drug service."],"rules":["Connor calms if spoken to slowly and offered water; escalates if crowded","PC Davies stops pushing restraint once told it can kill","Chloe tears up when asked about blood in her urine; settles if validated","Chloe agrees to the GP first, and the drug service once it is presented as her choice"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"P","who":"Connor","s":"Get off me! I've done nothing wrong. Let me out of here!"},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Connor, I'm Dr Rahman, one of the doctors. I'm here to help, not to judge. Can we have just one officer in here and dim the lights? Would you like some water?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","who":"Connor","s":"(pacing) Yeah. My heart's going mental."},{"t":"0:29","band":"Assess","w":"D","s":"Let's check you over. Nurse, can I have his temperature, pulse and blood pressure, and the NEWS2? Connor, any chest pain, headache or trouble breathing?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","who":"Connor","s":"No pain. Just hot. Really hot."},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Temp 39.0, pulse 140, BP 170 over 105. NEWS2 is high."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Connor, what have you taken tonight, and when? I'm asking so we can treat you safely.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","who":"Connor","s":"Couple of lines and a pill at a party. About eight."},{"t":"1:12","w":"D","s":"Have you been drinking lots of water? And do you take any antidepressants?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","who":"Connor","s":"Loads of water. No meds."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"PC Davies, did you see any packets? And was he restrained at all?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","who":"Police officer","s":"Cuffed in the van, five minutes maybe, sitting up. Can't we just hold him face down if he kicks off?"},{"t":"1:43","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I know you want everyone safe. But face-down holds on someone this hot and agitated can cause sudden death. Please avoid prone restraint.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:56","w":"D","s":"He's got a temperature of 39 and a pulse of 140 after cocaine and MDMA. That's stimulant toxicity, a medical emergency. He needs A&E now.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","who":"Police officer","s":"What about the Mental Health Act? He was running in traffic."},{"t":"2:17","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"The 136 still applies. We can transfer him to A&E under section 136(3), with you and a nurse as escort. I'll ring A&E and ask for cooling, IV fluids, an ECG, CK and kidney bloods, and benzodiazepines first line, guided by TOXBASE. No antipsychotics without senior advice.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","who":"AMHP","s":"(phone) Sam, AMHP. Can we finish the MHA assessment first? I've got the doctors lined up."},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"I'd like to, but he's medically unwell. He arrived at 21:50, so the 24-hour clock runs from then. If his condition delays the assessment, the doctor responsible can extend by up to 12 hours, and we'll record why.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","who":"AMHP","s":"Okay. Let me know when he's fit."},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"Will do. His psychosis may settle once he's treated. Connor, we're taking you to A&E to cool you down and calm your heart. You're not in trouble.","m":[6,1]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","who":"Connor","s":"Fine. Whatever. Just make it stop."},{"t":"3:37","band":"Chloe: next room","w":"D","s":"Hi, Chloe? I'm Dr Rahman. Sorry about the noise next door. You mentioned ketamine earlier. Can I ask a bit more?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:49","w":"P","who":"Chloe","s":"It's not a big deal. Ketamine's not addictive. I just do a bit most nights."},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. Do you find you need more now for the same effect?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","who":"Chloe","s":"I suppose. More than last year."},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"You said it hurts when you pass urine. Are you going more often, any blood, or tummy pain?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","who":"Chloe","s":"I'm on the toilet all the time. There was blood last week. And cramps. (tearful) I've been too scared to tell anyone."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening, and I'm glad you've told me now. Ketamine can irritate and damage the bladder.","m":[11,9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","who":"Chloe","s":"Will my bladder be damaged forever?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"That's a fair worry. If you stop or cut down early, the bladder often recovers. If it carries on, the damage can last. That's why it's worth checking now.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:06","band":"Mood and risk","w":"D","s":"You came in because of low mood. How is it now? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","who":"Chloe","s":"Low. But no, nothing like that."},{"t":"5:20","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'd like your GP to check your urine and kidneys, and refer you to urology if needed. And the local drug service could help you cut down. How does that sound?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:38","w":"P","who":"Chloe","s":"I'm not going to a drug service. I'm not a junkie."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"You're right, you're not. They see students and young professionals all the time, and it's confidential. Could we start with your GP, and you decide about the rest?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","who":"Chloe","s":"Okay. GP first. Maybe the other one."},{"t":"6:05","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what you'll do next, so I know I've been clear?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:14","w":"P","who":"Chloe","s":"See the GP for my wee and kidneys. Think about the drug service. Try to cut down."},{"t":"6:24","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If you get bad tummy pain, can't pass urine, or see a lot of blood, go to A&E. And if your mood dips or you feel unsafe, call 111 and choose option 2.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:42","w":"P","who":"Chloe","s":"Okay. Thank you."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'll write to your GP tonight with your consent. Connor is on his way to A&E, and I'll record the times and the reason. The team will review you both in the morning.","m":[14,7]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self calmly, lowers stimulation and offers water","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks temperature, pulse, BP and NEWS2, and asks about chest pain","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what he took and when, plus water intake and antidepressants","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names stimulant toxicity as a medical emergency to staff","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks police about restraint and advises against prone holds","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Arranges A&E transfer under s136(3) for cooling, fluids, ECG, CK and benzodiazepines first line","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Tells the AMHP, records detention time, and states the 24-hour limit and 12-hour extension","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks the woman about ketamine frequency and tolerance without judgement","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about bladder pain, frequency, blood in urine and abdominal pain","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens her mood and thoughts of self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her fear and defensiveness","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Arranges GP urine and kidney checks and drug service referral with her consent","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding by asking her to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets with warning signs and closes with clear next steps","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Checks obs, names stimulant toxicity as a medical emergency, transfers under s136(3) with benzodiazepines first line and no prone restraint, knows the time limits, and gives the ketamine user a kind bladder and drug service plan.","borderline":"Safe transfer, but vague on s136 time limits, restraint risk or ketamine bladder follow-up.","fail":"Treats it as psychosis in the suite, gives an antipsychotic, allows prone restraint, or ignores her bladder symptoms."}},"c-7-3-1":{"setup":"Tuesday 10:30, ward round then CMHT clinic. You see three people briefly: Liam, 22, psychosis and daily strong cannabis; Karen, 47, depression and a bottle of wine nightly; Josh, 26, ADHD and weekend cocaine. Agree a joint plan with each.","role":{"who":"Mr Liam Carter, 22, male inpatient; Mrs Karen Doyle, 47, female CMHT patient; Mr Josh Bennett, 26, male CMHT patient (with Priya, dual diagnosis worker)","open":"(Liam) Here we go. You're going to tell me to stop smoking weed.","manner":"Liam is wary and defensive, then curious once not lectured. Karen is guarded and ashamed, then tearful and relieved. Josh is irritable and feels judged, then pragmatic once he sees he will be treated.","free":["Liam: smokes strong 'skunk' every day; it calms him","Karen: drinks a bottle of wine each night to sleep","Josh: methylphenidate helped him at work; wants it back","Josh: uses cocaine some weekends"],"ifasked":["Liam: both admissions followed heavy use; voices louder when he smokes more","Liam: does not drive; lives with his mum","Karen: shaky and sweaty in the mornings; sometimes a drink before work","Karen: no fits or DTs; low mood came first after a divorce, then the drinking grew","Karen: thoughts she'd 'be better off not here' when drunk; no plan; her son keeps her going","Karen: drives most days","Josh: cocaine about two weekends a month, never sold or shared tablets","Josh: drives to work; no chest pain"],"worries":["Liam: Isn't cannabis natural? How can it make me ill?","Karen: If I stop drinking I won't sleep at all.","Josh: So because I do a bit of coke, I don't get my meds?","Josh: Give me the old methylphenidate and I'll stop the coke."],"rules":["Liam shuts down if lectured; engages if asked what he makes of the link","Karen becomes tearful when asked about thoughts of not being here; settles if validated","Josh calls it discrimination unless the doctor makes clear his ADHD will be treated","Each agrees one small goal if it is framed as their choice"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Liam: ward round","w":"D","s":"Morning, Liam. I'm Dr Clarke. I'd like to hear how things are, and talk about what happens after you leave. Is now okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","who":"Liam","s":"Here we go. You're going to tell me to stop smoking weed."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"I'm not here to lecture you. I'm curious what cannabis does for you, the good and the not so good.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","who":"Liam","s":"It calms me down. Helps me sleep. Everyone smokes."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"That makes sense. And the downsides?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:39","w":"P","who":"Liam","s":"Isn't it natural? How can it make me ill?"},{"t":"0:44","w":"D","s":"Fair question. Today's skunk is much stronger, and THC can bring on voices and paranoia. What do you notice about the voices when you smoke more?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","who":"Liam","s":"(pause) They get louder. Both times I came in, I'd been smoking loads."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"That's a really honest thing to notice. What would cutting down even a bit look like for you?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","who":"Liam","s":"Maybe not in the day. Just at night. And not the strong stuff."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"That's a real goal, and it's yours. Priya, our dual diagnosis worker, can help with it after discharge, and you'll stay on your medication. Do you drive?","m":[3,11]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","who":"Liam","s":"No licence. Mum drives me."},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"Okay. If you ever apply, you'd need to tell the DVLA about the psychosis. I'll note that in your GP letter. Can you tell me the plan back?","m":[11,13]},{"t":"1:53","w":"P","who":"Liam","s":"Smoke only at night, weaker stuff, see Priya, keep taking my tablets."},{"t":"2:00","band":"Karen: CMHT clinic","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mrs Doyle? I'm Dr Clarke. Thanks for coming. Your GP mentioned low mood, and some drinking. Is that okay to talk about?","m":[1]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","who":"Karen","s":"Karen. A bottle of wine isn't drinking. I'm not an alcoholic."},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"I'm not here to put a label on you. A bottle is about ten units, most of the weekly guide, every night. Do you get shaky or sweaty in the mornings?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","who":"Karen","s":"Some mornings. Sometimes I have a glass before work, to steady myself."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. Have you ever had a seizure or seen things, when you've stopped?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","who":"Karen","s":"No. I've never really stopped."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"Which came first, the low mood or the drinking?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","who":"Karen","s":"The mood. After the divorce. Then the wine crept up."},{"t":"3:04","w":"D","s":"When you've been drinking, have you had thoughts about ending your life?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","who":"Karen","s":"(tearful) Sometimes I think everyone would be better off without me. I wouldn't do anything. My son."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. It sounds like you've been carrying a lot on your own. I'm glad your son keeps you going.","m":[4,6]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","who":"Karen","s":"If I stop drinking I won't sleep at all."},{"t":"3:38","band":"Karen: plan","w":"D","s":"Lots of people feel that. Alcohol helps you drop off but spoils deep sleep and deepens low mood. Mood often lifts after three or four weeks without it, and then we'd review your antidepressant.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Stopping suddenly could be risky, so we'd plan it. I'll do an AUDIT and bloods: liver tests, blood count and kidneys. You'd start thiamine. With no fits, the community alcohol team could manage your detox at home.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","who":"Karen","s":"At home? That's a relief. Okay."},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"And please don't drive while drinking heavily. Dependence needs reporting to the DVLA; I'll put that advice in your GP letter.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","who":"Karen","s":"Okay. I'll take the bus for now."},{"t":"4:33","band":"Josh: CMHT clinic","w":"D","s":"Hi, Josh. I'm Dr Clarke. You'd like to restart methylphenidate. Tell me how it helped before.","m":[1,9]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","who":"Josh","s":"Massively. I could actually finish things at work. Now I'm all over the place."},{"t":"4:49","w":"D","s":"How often do you use cocaine now? And have you ever sold or shared your tablets?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","who":"Josh","s":"Couple of weekends a month. Never sold them. So because I do a bit of coke, I don't get my meds? That's discrimination."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"I understand why it feels unfair. I do want to treat your ADHD. Cocaine and a stimulant together strain your heart, so I'll choose carefully.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"5:23","w":"P","who":"Josh","s":"Give me the old methylphenidate and I'll stop the coke."},{"t":"5:28","band":"Josh: plan","w":"D","s":"I appreciate that. I'd suggest lisdexamfetamine, once a day and harder to misuse, or atomoxetine, which isn't a stimulant. First, pulse, blood pressure and an ECG. Then weekly supplies, urine tests and shared care with your GP.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:47","w":"P","who":"Josh","s":"Weekly? Fine, if I actually get treated."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"You will. You drive, so never drive after using, and drug misuse may need reporting to the DVLA. That'll be in your letter. Could you aim for no cocaine before your heart checks?","m":[11,3]},{"t":"6:08","w":"P","who":"Josh","s":"Yeah. I can do that."},{"t":"6:11","band":"Joint plan and close","w":"D","s":"Priya, can we write joint plans for all three today? Nobody waits to be 'clean' first.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","who":"Dual diagnosis worker","s":"Yes. I'll see Liam before discharge, link Karen with the alcohol team, and join Josh's review."},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"Great. Each plan will have substance goals, crisis contacts including 111 option 2, and the DVLA advice in each GP letter. Liam gets his 72-hour follow-up after discharge.","m":[12,14,11]},{"t":"6:43","w":"P","who":"Dual diagnosis worker","s":"And reviews?"},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Karen in two weeks with bloods, Josh after his ECG, Liam at his CPA review. If anyone slips, we increase contact, not discharge.","m":[14,12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self to each patient and sets a non-judgemental tone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores what cannabis does for Liam and his view of the link with voices","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees one realistic cannabis goal with Liam using motivational language, not ultimatums","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates feelings and avoids lecturing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks Karen about units, morning shakes, morning drinking and past fits","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, including when drinking","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Plans withdrawal with AUDIT, bloods, thiamine and a decision on where to detox","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains the alcohol and mood link and antidepressant review after 3–4 weeks abstinent","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks Josh about cocaine frequency, diversion and past benefit from medication","d":"Content"},{"c":"Chooses a low-misuse ADHD option with pulse, BP, ECG, small supplies and GP shared care","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives DVLA advice to each and says it goes in the GP letter","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees joint care plans with the dual diagnosis worker, excluding nobody","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking a patient to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets with crisis contacts and closes with review dates","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Agrees a realistic goal with each patient using motivational language, plans a safe alcohol withdrawal with thiamine, picks a low-misuse ADHD option with heart checks, and writes joint plans with the dual diagnosis worker, DVLA advice and crisis contacts.","borderline":"Non-judgemental and safe, but one plan is thin, such as no withdrawal setting, cardiovascular checks or DVLA advice.","fail":"Tells anyone to stop using before treatment, misses alcohol withdrawal risk or suicide risk, or restarts immediate-release methylphenidate with a large supply."}},"c-8-1-1":{"setup":"You are on the CLDT duty phone on Tuesday afternoon. The manager of a supported living house rings about Ben, 34, who has severe learning disability and autism and does not speak. Take a focused history, find likely causes and agree a safe plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Lorraine Price, 50s, female, manager of Ben's supported living house (Ben Carter, 34, non-verbal, severe learning disability and autism)","open":"It's Lorraine Price, manager at Elm Road. It's about Ben. He's been hitting himself for over a week and we need his risperidone putting up today.","manner":"Brisk, stretched and a little defensive at first, pushing for a quick medicine fix. Becomes upset about staff sickness and losing the placement; settles and works with you once her team's strain is acknowledged.","free":["Ten days of head hitting, pushing staff and refusing food; two staff hurt","Usually calm, enjoys his music and a daily walk","Takes risperidone 1 mg twice daily and sodium valproate for epilepsy","He is quieter now, in his room, rocking"],"ifasked":["He hits the right side of his head by his ear and has been holding his jaw","Spits out crunchy food; dentist visit over a year ago","Last proper bowel motion five days ago; only smears in his pad since; tummy looks bigger","His lactulose ran out about two weeks ago and was not re-ordered","Wet the bed once last week; no seizures seen","A loud new housemate moved in three weeks ago; his day centre is shut for refurbishment","ABC charts show incidents at mealtimes and during personal care","His sister is his main family contact"],"worries":["Just put the risperidone up, my staff are getting hurt","He won't let a GP near him; last time he bit the nurse","If he hurts someone again the placement may end","Will you still look at his risperidone?"],"rules":["Insists 'he goes most days' until asked to check the bowel chart","Becomes tearful about staff sickness and losing him; settles if validated and offered practical support","Mentions the lactulose only when asked about medicine changes","Accepts no risperidone increase if given a clear reason and a plan for the next 48 hours"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, this is Dr Shah, the psychiatry trainee on duty for the learning disability team. Who am I speaking to?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"It's Lorraine Price, manager at Elm Road. It's about Ben. He's been hitting himself for over a week and we need his risperidone putting up today."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Thanks for ringing, Lorraine. I'll help today. First, is Ben safe right now? Any cut or swelling on his head, vomiting, fever, unusual sleepiness or trouble breathing?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","s":"There's a red mark on his right temple, no cut. He hasn't been sick, his temperature was normal this morning, and he's awake in his room, calmer."},{"t":"0:39","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Who knows him best, and do you have his health action plan and behaviour support plan to hand?","m":[1,3]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"I've managed him four years. His key worker Tasha is on today. I've got his folder here."},{"t":"0:54","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"Great. Tell me what's different compared with Ben at his best, and when it started.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"Ten days ago. Normally he's chilled, likes his music and his walk. Now he's hitting his head, pushing staff and barely eating. Two of my staff have been hurt."},{"t":"1:12","w":"D","s":"That's a big change, and staff getting hurt is serious. When someone can't tell us something's wrong, behaviour is often how they show it. Can we go through pain, bowels, fits, medicines and his surroundings in turn?","m":[8,3]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"We've tried everything. Honestly, just put the risperidone up."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"I hear you. Give me five minutes, because if he's in pain, more risperidone only quietens the signal. Where does he hit himself? Always the same spot?","m":[8,4]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"Mostly the right side of his head. By his ear."},{"t":"1:45","band":"Pain and bowels","w":"D","s":"That's useful. Is he pulling at that ear, rubbing his jaw, or chewing on one side?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"Now you say it, he's been holding his jaw. And he spits out anything crunchy."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"When did he last see a dentist, and when was his annual health check?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"Dentist, over a year, I think. The health check was in the spring."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Okay. Now his bowels. When did he last open them, and what was it like?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"He goes most days, so it can't be that."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Could you check his chart while I'm on? Going most days doesn't rule it out. Small hard bits or soiling can leak round a blockage.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"(pause) Right. Last proper one was Thursday. Since then just smears in his pad. Tasha says his tummy looks bigger."},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"Thank you for checking, that matters a lot. Is he being sick, or holding or punching his tummy?","m":[5,2]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"No sick. He has punched his tummy a couple of times."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Fits, medicines, surroundings","w":"D","s":"And fits. Any seizures, staring spells, sudden falls, wet beds or very sleepy days?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"No fits that we've seen. He wet the bed once last week, which isn't like him."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"Worth noting. Has any medicine been started, stopped or changed, including antibiotics or anything bought over the counter?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Nothing new. Oh, his lactulose ran out about two weeks ago. It never got re-ordered."},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"That could fit with his bowels. Is he pacing or unable to sit still, or has he been shaky or muddled?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Pacing a bit. Not shaky or muddled."},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"And around him. Any new staff or housemates, or changes to his routine or day service?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"A new housemate three weeks ago. Loud, music at night. And his day centre's shut for refurbishment."},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"So poorer sleep and no favourite outing. What do the ABC charts show happens before each incident?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:49","w":"P","s":"Mostly mealtimes and when staff do personal care. We've not looked at them closely."},{"t":"3:55","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"Any injuries you can't explain, or is he frightened of anyone in particular?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:01","w":"P","s":"No. The head mark is from hitting himself. Staff saw it."},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"Thank you. If that ever changes, it needs a safeguarding referral the same day.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"(voice cracks) Sorry. My night staff are shattered and two are off sick. If he hurts someone again the placement might end. He's been with us for years."},{"t":"4:23","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard on you and the team, and it's clear you care about him. Let's protect his home together. I'll tell his care manager about staffing today and ask our behaviour specialist to visit this week.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"Thank you. That does help."},{"t":"4:40","band":"Explain and plan","w":"D","s":"Here's my thinking. The likeliest cause is pain: a blocked bowel since the lactulose ran out, and maybe a tooth or ear on the right. More risperidone could hide that and add side effects, so I won't increase it today.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"So what do we do instead?"},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"I'll ring his GP now for a review within 48 hours: tummy, ears, mouth, urine and bloods, including his valproate level. I'll ask for the first slot, a quiet room and Tasha with him, or a home visit.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"He won't let a GP near him. Last time he bit the nurse."},{"t":"5:18","w":"D","s":"Good to know. Our nurse can prepare him with pictures first. Any checks he can't agree to will be decided in his best interests, with you and his family involved.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"His sister's his main family. I'll give you her number."},{"t":"5:34","w":"D","s":"Thank you. From today, can Tasha keep a daily bowel chart with stool type, an ABC chart for every incident and the DisDAT pain chart? Who will check them each day?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"Tasha will do them, and I'll check them every evening."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Please re-order his lactulose today, keep familiar staff on his shifts, and follow the behaviour plan steps when he starts hitting.","m":[11,10]},{"t":"5:59","w":"P","s":"Okay. Anything else we should watch for?"},{"t":"6:03","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Yes. If he vomits, his tummy goes hard and swollen, he gets a fever, becomes very drowsy or has a seizure, call 999. Can you tell me those back?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:14","w":"P","s":"Sick, hard swollen tummy, fever, very sleepy, or a fit. Then 999."},{"t":"6:19","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If things escalate before the GP sees him, ring our duty line on this number.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:26","w":"P","s":"Will you still look at his risperidone at some point?"},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"Yes. I'll review him in two weeks with the results and charts, and check whether he still needs it at all. I'll email you and the GP a written plan today.","m":[14,9]},{"t":"6:43","w":"P","s":"That's clearer. Thanks, doctor."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Lorraine. You've been really helpful. Speak soon.","m":[14,1]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms the caller's role and finds who knows him best and his plans","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks immediate safety: head injury, vomiting, fever, drowsiness, breathing","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Establishes his usual best and the onset and pattern of the change","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for pain, linking where he hits to ear, tooth or jaw, and dental history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about last bowel motion, stool type, overflow soiling and a swollen tummy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens seizures, medicine changes and side effects such as akathisia","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about changes in housemates, staff, routine and what ABC charts show","d":"Content"},{"c":"Acknowledges staff strain and validates the manager's distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Declines a risperidone increase and gives the reason in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Arranges GP review within 48 hours with reasonable adjustments named","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Starts bowel, ABC and DisDAT charts with a named person responsible","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Considers safeguarding and best interests for tests he cannot agree to","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives red-flag safety-netting with 999 and checks it with teach-back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Agrees a 2-week review including a STOMP check and closes clearly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Treats the change as communication, finds constipation and likely dental pain, declines more risperidone, and leaves a timed, adjusted, safety-netted plan.","borderline":"Covers most causes but misses the bowel chart or medicine change, or gives a vague plan without named responsibilities.","fail":"Increases risperidone or advises PRN sedation, misses constipation or pain, or gives no red-flag advice."}},"c-8-1-2":{"setup":"You are in the learning disability outpatient clinic. Chloe, 27, has mild learning disability and has taken risperidone for six years. She is settled but has gained 14 kg. Explain the plan to reduce it slowly, check she understands, and agree next steps.","role":{"who":"Miss Chloe Evans, 27, female, patient (her key worker, Sam, is with her)","open":"Hiya. Sam said you want to talk about my tablet. Am I in trouble?","manner":"Friendly, eager to please and says 'yes' quickly. Gets worried that stopping will make her angry again; relaxes and joins in when the doctor talks to her directly and uses pictures.","free":["Takes 'the white tablet' every night","Likes her flat, her swimming and her cat","Has got 'bigger' and her clothes don't fit","Feels sleepy in the mornings"],"ifasked":["Has not had a period for about a year","Sometimes milk comes out of her breasts; she was too embarrassed to say","Started the tablet after a hard move six years ago when she 'got cross a lot'","Has not been cross or hit anyone for over three years","Happy for Sam to know the plan and help her","Prefers pictures and short words"],"worries":["If I stop the tablet, will I get angry again?","Will I lose the weight?","Do I have to stop it all at once?"],"rules":["Says 'yes, okay' to closed questions without understanding; only shows real understanding with teach-back","Becomes anxious and quiet if adults talk over her; brightens when addressed directly","Mentions periods and breast milk only if asked kindly and directly","Sam wants to give the old dose on bad days until told why not"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Chloe, I'm Dr Ahmed, one of the doctors. You're not in trouble at all. You're doing really well. Is it okay if I talk to you, and then Sam can help?","m":[1,8]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"Hiya. Sam said you want to talk about my tablet. Am I in trouble?"},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"No trouble. I promise. Is it okay if Sam stays and hears about your tablet, so Sam can help you at home?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"Yeah, Sam can stay."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you. How do you like people to explain things? Some people like pictures.","m":[1,5]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Pictures. And not big words."},{"t":"0:52","band":"Her view","w":"D","s":"Okay, pictures and small words. Tell me about your tablet. What do you know about it?","m":[3,5]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"It's the white one. I take it at night. Since I moved here."},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"That's right. Why did you start it, do you remember?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"I got cross a lot when I moved. I didn't like the new house."},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"And how are you now?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"Good. I like my flat. I go swimming. I've got a cat called Biscuit."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"That sounds lovely. How does the tablet make your body feel?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:42","w":"P","s":"Sleepy in the morning. And I've got bigger. My jeans don't fit."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Some women on this tablet find their periods change. Are your periods coming?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"(quiet) No. Not for ages."},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"Okay. Sometimes milk comes from the breasts too. Has that happened?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"(looks down) Yes. I didn't tell anyone. It's embarrassing."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you told me. It's not your fault, and lots of people get that on this tablet. Taking less may help it stop.","m":[8,6]},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"2:30","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I've got three things to tell you. Thing one. You're doing really well now.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Thing two. The tablet can make you put on weight and feel sleepy, and stop your periods.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"Like my jeans."},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"Like your jeans. Thing three. We want to take a little bit of tablet away, very slowly, like this picture. One small step, then wait.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"Yes. Yes, okay, doctor."},{"t":"3:09","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Chloe. I want to check I explained it well. Can you tell me, in your words, what will happen with your tablet?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:21","w":"P","s":"Um. I stop the tablet?"},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Nearly. Look at the picture. We take a little bit away, then we wait. Not all at once. Can you show me on the steps?","m":[7,5]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"(points) A little bit less. Then wait. Then a bit less again."},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"Perfect, that's exactly it.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:49","w":"P","s":"If I take less, will I get angry again?"},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"That's a really good worry to tell me. You haven't been cross for years. If you feel cross or upset, you tell Sam and we slow down. You're in charge with us.","m":[8,6]},{"t":"4:11","w":"P","s":"Okay. Will I lose weight?"},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Some people do, slowly. Swimming helps too. We'll weigh you every time you come, so you can see.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Okay. When does it start?"},{"t":"4:29","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Let's choose together. Next Monday you start a smaller tablet at night, from two to one and a half. Then you come back to see me in five weeks. Is that okay?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"Monday. Smaller tablet. Five weeks."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Brilliant. Sam, may I give you a few tips? Chloe, is that okay?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"4:59","w":"D","s":"Sam, some bad days in the first two weeks after a step can be her body getting used to less, not the old problems coming back. Please don't go back to the old dose yourselves.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:17","w":"P","who":"Sam","s":"If she has a really bad day, can't we just give her the old dose?"},{"t":"5:26","w":"D","s":"Please write it down and ring us instead. We'll decide together, usually the same day. Could you note her sleep, mood and any upsets each week?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","who":"Sam","s":"Yes, I can do that."},{"t":"5:43","w":"D","s":"Chloe, today the nurse will check your weight and blood pressure. Then a blood test at your GP next week, for sugar, fat and a hormone called prolactin. We'll check again in three months.","m":[11,5]},{"t":"6:01","w":"P","s":"A needle? I'm okay with needles."},{"t":"6:05","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Good. Here's your plan with pictures. It has my name and our phone number. If you feel upset or worried, you or Sam ring this number.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"Can I put it on my fridge?"},{"t":"6:24","w":"D","s":"Great idea. So what will you do if you feel upset after Monday?","m":[12,7]},{"t":"6:32","w":"P","s":"Tell Sam. And ring the number."},{"t":"6:36","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. Any questions for me, Chloe?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks, doctor. I'll tell Biscuit."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You do that. You did really well today. See you in five weeks.","m":[13,8]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, speaks directly to Chloe and asks how she likes things explained","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks Chloe's permission before sharing her plan with the key worker","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what she knows about the tablet, why it started and how she is now","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly and kindly about weight, sleepiness, periods and breast milk","d":"Content"},{"c":"Uses short sentences, everyday words and pictures, one idea at a time","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why: she is well, side effects matter, so reduce slowly and watch","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back, not 'do you understand?', and corrects gently","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds warmly to embarrassment and fear, and praises her","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Agrees the first dose step and the review date with Chloe herself","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Tells the key worker that withdrawal is not relapse and not to change the dose","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges weight, BP and bloods including prolactin with dates","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives an easy read plan with a named contact and what to do if upset","d":"Close"},{"c":"Invites questions and closes warmly with the next appointment","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Talks to Chloe, not about her, explains in three simple ideas with pictures, uses teach-back, and agrees a dated plan with consent before involving Sam.","borderline":"Kind and clear but relies on 'yes' answers, or agrees the plan mainly with Sam.","fail":"Talks over Chloe to her key worker, uses jargon like 'taper', or misses side effects and safety-netting."}},"c-8-1-3":{"setup":"You are the liaison psychiatry doctor in A&E on Saturday afternoon. Jamie, 22, autistic with mild learning disability, is here with his mother after barricading his room. Assess whether this is psychosis, manage risk and plan the least restrictive care.","role":{"who":"Mr Jamie Walsh, 22, male, patient (his mother, Mrs Carol Walsh, 49, is with him)","open":"Too loud. Too loud. Make it stop.","manner":"Hands over ears, rocking, frightened and literal. Settles in a quiet room and answers short, clear questions. His mother is worried, protective and at first puts it down to his autism.","free":["Neighbours have put 'wires' in his walls to listen to him","He cannot go back to his room","The department is too loud and bright"],"ifasked":["Hears a man's voice through the wall saying he is stupid, for about a month","Smokes cannabis with a lad from college most days for two months","Has a craft knife in his room for model making; no plan to hurt anyone","Not eating much and not showering; sleeping badly","No thoughts of killing himself","Mother: stopped college six weeks ago, talks to himself, lost interest in his trains","Mother: no freezing, no stiffness, still drinking, no recent illness or new medicines"],"worries":["I'm not taking tablets. They'll poison me.","Mother: Is he going to be locked up on a ward?","Mother: Isn't this just his autism getting worse?"],"rules":["Becomes more distressed with noise, two people talking or vague questions; calms with one quiet speaker","Answers literally; misunderstands figures of speech","Admits cannabis only if asked without judgement","Mother accepts it is a change once the doctor compares it with his usual self"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Jamie. I'm Dr Reid, a doctor. It's too loud here. Shall we go to a quiet room next door?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Too loud. Too loud. Make it stop."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Okay. Come with me and Mum. (in the side room) I'll dim the light and shut the door. Only I will talk. If you want a break, say 'stop' or hold up your hand.","m":[1,9]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"(rocking slows) Okay. Stop means stop."},{"t":"0:36","w":"D","s":"Yes. Stop means stop. I'll ask short questions. Is that okay?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"0:45","band":"His experience","w":"D","s":"Mum said you were scared last night. What happened?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"The wires are in the walls. The neighbours put them. They're listening. I can't go back there."},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"That sounds really scary. You're safe in this room now. When did you first notice the wires?","m":[9,3]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"When it got cold. Before Bonfire Night."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"Thank you. How sure are you that the wires are there, from nought to ten?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"Ten. I know they are."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Do you hear anything from the walls, like voices, when nobody is in the room?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"A man. He says I'm stupid. Through the wall."},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"That must be horrible to hear. How often does he talk?","m":[4,9]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Every night. Lots."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Jamie, is it okay if I ask Mum some questions now?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"1:58","band":"Baseline and change","w":"D","s":"Mrs Walsh, what's Jamie like at his usual best, and what's changed?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"He's always been odd about things. Honestly, I think it's just his autism getting worse."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"You know him best, so that matters. What does he normally do with his days?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"College three days, trains, his model railway. He talks to himself a bit when he's planning. But since about six weeks ago he's stopped college. He doesn't touch his trains. And he talks back to someone."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"So six weeks ago, around late September, he stopped college, lost interest in trains and started answering someone. Fear of the neighbours and barricading are new too?","m":[5,10]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Yes. He's never done anything like that."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"Has he been freezing in one position, going silent, or stopping eating or drinking?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"He's eating less and not washing. But he drinks, and he doesn't freeze."},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"Any recent illness, fever, head injury, new medicines or confusion about the time or place?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"3:25","band":"Physical and substances","w":"D","s":"Thank you. The nurse has done his observations and blood sugar, and they're normal. I'll also ask for a urine test and some bloods. Jamie, do you ever take drugs or drink? You won't be in trouble.","m":[7,6]},{"t":"3:43","w":"P","s":"I smoke weed with a lad at college. It stops my thinking."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being honest. I'm not cross. How much, and how often?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:57","w":"P","s":"Most days. Since the summer."},{"t":"4:01","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Cannabis can make frightening thoughts and voices stronger. We can talk about that later. Jamie, have you thought about hurting the neighbours?","m":[6,8]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"No. I just want them to stop."},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"Do you have anything at home you could hurt someone with?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"My craft knife. For models."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Mrs Walsh, could you put the knife somewhere safe tonight? Jamie, have you thought about hurting yourself or dying?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"No. I want to be safe."},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"He'll put the knife away if I ask. He trusts me."},{"t":"4:48","band":"Explain and plan","w":"D","s":"I'm a bit worried about Jamie's eating and washing too. I think he's become unwell with an illness called psychosis. It's a change from his usual self, not just autism, and it can be treated.","m":[10,8]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Is he going to be locked up on a ward?"},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"Not if we can avoid it. I'd like the home treatment team to visit every day, the same one or two people. And I'll ask his learning disability team for an urgent meeting of everyone who knows him. A ward only if home isn't safe.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:34","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"(crying) I just want my boy back."},{"t":"5:38","w":"D","s":"I can see how frightening this has been for you. You did the right thing bringing him in. We'll do this with you.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"I'm not taking tablets. They'll poison me."},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. You don't have to decide tonight. If we do use a tablet, it'd be a very small dose and Mum can check it with you.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Maybe. Not tonight."},{"t":"6:13","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's fine. Mrs Walsh, if he's in danger, or tries to hurt someone, ring 999. For other worries, ring the crisis line, or 111 option 2. The home team will visit tomorrow.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:29","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"999 if danger. Crisis line or 111 if I'm worried."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Jamie, can you tell me what happens tomorrow?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"People come to my house. To help."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"That's right. The learning disability team will also see you on Monday. You did really well today, Jamie. Thank you both.","m":[14,11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Reduces noise and light, uses one speaker, and agrees a stop signal early","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Uses short, literal questions and checks Jamie is okay to continue","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the persecutory belief: content, onset and conviction","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about hearing voices and how often","d":"Content"},{"c":"Uses mother's account to compare with his baseline and fixes an onset date","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about cannabis and other substances without judgement","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens catatonia, delirium and physical illness, with obs, glucose and urine test","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses risk to neighbours, weapons, self-harm and self-neglect","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates fear and distress in both Jamie and his mother","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains in plain words why this is a treatable illness, not just autism","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees home treatment and asks the CLDT for an urgent C(E)TR, ward only if unsafe","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Responds to fear of tablets without pressure: low dose, no decision tonight","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets with 999, crisis line and 111 option 2","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks Jamie understands what happens next and closes warmly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Adjusts the setting at once, separates new psychosis from his autistic baseline with a clear onset, screens physical causes and risk, and plans home treatment with a C(E)TR.","borderline":"Finds psychosis but misses catatonia or cannabis, or plans without the CLDT and C(E)TR.","fail":"Interviews in the noisy bay, calls it 'just autism', or admits him without considering less restrictive options."}},"c-8-2-1":{"setup":"You are in a joint CMHT and CLDT clinic. Kelly, 31, has mild learning disability and three months of low mood since her father died. You think sertraline may help. Assess her capacity to decide about starting it, and act on the result.","role":{"who":"Miss Kelly Brown, 31, female, patient (her support worker, Jo, is in the waiting room)","open":"Yes. Yes, okay.","manner":"Quiet, polite and keen to please, saying 'yes' to everything at first. Tearful and guilty about her dad. Opens up and gives her own views once questions are open and she is told there is no right answer.","free":["Feels sad and cries most days since Dad died in the summer","Not sleeping well; wakes very early","Lives in her own flat with support from Jo three days a week"],"ifasked":["Has stopped going to her bowling club and eats less","Sometimes thinks she 'wants to be with Dad'; no plans or self-harm; her cat and her sister keep her going","Would rather talk to someone than take tablets at first","Worries a tablet will 'change' her","Her friend took a tablet that made her feel sick","Happy for Jo to know about the tablet and help her remember it"],"worries":["I should be over it. Dad would be cross I'm still crying.","Will the tablet change me?","Do I have to take it forever?"],"rules":["Answers 'yes' to closed questions whatever is asked","Becomes tearful and guilty about still crying; settles when validated","Discloses passive wish to be with Dad only if asked directly and gently","Can explain the choice in her own words once pictures and open questions are used"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Kelly, I'm Dr Okafor. We've talked a bit about how sad you've been. Now I want to talk about whether a tablet might help. It's your choice, and there are no wrong answers. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"Yes. Yes, okay."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Can you tell me in your words how things have been since your dad died?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","s":"Sad. I cry every day. I wake up really early and can't get back to sleep."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"What else has changed for you?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"I don't go to bowling any more. I don't feel hungry."},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard. When did your dad die?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"In the summer. (tearful) I should be over it. Dad would be cross I'm still crying."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Missing your dad this much shows how much you loved him. Crying isn't wrong. I think an illness called depression has come on top of the sadness, and we can help with that.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"(wipes eyes) Okay. Thank you."},{"t":"1:18","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"Kelly, sometimes when people feel this sad, they think about dying, or about hurting themselves. Has that happened to you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think I want to be with my dad."},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me, that's really important. Do you mean you've thought about dying?","m":[4,3]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"Sort of. At night. But I wouldn't do anything."},{"t":"1:47","w":"D","s":"Have you made any plans, or hurt yourself?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"No. I've got Smudge, my cat. And my sister."},{"t":"1:56","w":"D","s":"I'm glad they're there. If those thoughts get stronger, I want you to tell someone straight away. We'll write down who.","m":[4,13]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"2:08","band":"Information","w":"D","s":"Now, three choices. Look at these pictures. One: a tablet called sertraline. It can make the sad feelings smaller over a few weeks.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"Two: talking help, with someone who helps people after someone dies. Three: no treatment for now, and we keep seeing you.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"I don't want tablets. I want to talk to someone."},{"t":"2:37","w":"D","s":"That's a fair choice, and talking help is good. You can have both, too. Can I tell you a bit more about the tablet first?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"Yes."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"For the first week or two, some people get headaches or feel a bit sick. That usually goes away. It doesn't change who you are.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"My friend had a tablet that made her sick."},{"t":"3:08","band":"Testing the decision","w":"D","s":"Some people do feel sick at first. If it's bad, we can change it. What do you think the tablet is for? Say it any way you like.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:21","w":"P","s":"For the sad feelings. To make them smaller."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"That's right. And how quickly does it work?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Not straight away. Weeks."},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"Spot on. What might happen in the first week?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"Feel sick. Or a headache. But it goes."},{"t":"3:43","w":"D","s":"Good. Let's look at the pictures of your flat for a minute. (after a short pause) Now, can you remember the three choices?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"Tablet. Talking to someone. Or nothing and keep coming."},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"Brilliant. What would be good about the tablet for you, and what would be bad?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"Good is I might stop crying and go back to bowling. Bad is I might feel sick. And I don't want to take it forever."},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"You don't have to take it forever. If it helps, people usually keep taking it about six months after they feel better. Then we lower it slowly together.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"Will it change me? Make me a different person?"},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"No, you'll still be Kelly. It can lift the sad feelings a bit. So, what would you like to do?","m":[9,8]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","s":"I'll try the tablet. And talk to someone too."},{"t":"4:52","band":"Decision and plan","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Kelly. I think you can make this decision yourself. You told me the tablet is 'to make the sad feelings smaller', and you weighed feeling sick against going back to bowling. I'll write that down.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"Is that good?"},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"Very good. You start on Monday morning, one small tablet with breakfast, 25 mg. After a week, it goes up to 50 mg. I'll also ask for the bereavement talking help.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"Monday. With my cornflakes."},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"Perfect. If you feel very sick, or more upset, or the dying thoughts get stronger, ring the number on this card. A nurse will phone you in two weeks to see how you are.","m":[11,13]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"A nurse rings in two weeks."},{"t":"5:49","w":"D","s":"Yes. Is it okay if I tell Jo about the tablet, so she can help you remember it?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:58","w":"P","s":"Yes. Jo's nice."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"I'll bring Jo in. (Jo joins) Kelly has decided to try sertraline from Monday. Could you help her remember it each morning, and tell us if she feels sick, more upset, or talks about not wanting to live?","m":[12,11]},{"t":"6:18","w":"P","who":"Jo","s":"Of course. I'll put a note on her fridge."},{"t":"6:23","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Kelly, what will you do if those dying thoughts get stronger?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Tell Jo, or ring the number on my card. Or 999."},{"t":"6:36","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll see you again in four weeks. You did really well today.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:42","w":"P","s":"Thank you, doctor."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Kelly. Take care of Smudge.","m":[14,3]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, presumes capacity and makes clear the choice is hers","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks open questions about mood, sleep, appetite and loss of interest","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her grief and guilt warmly","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks directly about thoughts of death, plans and self-harm, and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives decision-specific information on sertraline, talking help and no treatment with pictures","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tests understanding with open questions, not yes/no","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests retention after a short gap","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests her weighing of pros and cons in her own words","d":"Content"},{"c":"Confirms she can communicate a clear choice","d":"Content"},{"c":"States the capacity outcome for this decision, quoting her own words","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees start date, dose steps, side effect advice and a 2-week check","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks consent before sharing with the support worker, then involves her","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Safety-nets with a crisis card and checks she knows what to do","d":"Close"},{"c":"Agrees follow-up and closes warmly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Presumes capacity, gives balanced simple information, tests all four abilities with open questions, records her own words, and agrees a safe, dated plan.","borderline":"Reaches the right outcome but tests only understanding, or accepts some 'yes' answers as evidence.","fail":"Treats 'yes' as consent, assumes incapacity because of learning disability, asks the support worker to consent, or skips suicide risk."}},"c-8-2-2":{"setup":"You are the liaison psychiatry doctor on a surgical ward. Paul, 45, has moderate learning disability and painful gallstones. He keeps saying 'no hospital', and his support worker answers for him. Assess his capacity for the operation and advise the team.","role":{"who":"Mr Paul Ward, 45, male, patient (his support worker, Dean, is with him; a surgical registrar speaks first)","open":"No hospital. No hospital.","manner":"Anxious, guarded and repetitive, with short phrases. Calms when spoken to slowly and allowed time. Dean is caring but jumps in to answer; he steps back when asked respectfully.","free":["Doesn't want to stay in hospital","His tummy hurts after food","Wants to go home to his flat"],"ifasked":["Years ago he was held down for a blood test and it hurt","Thinks 'tablets' will stop the pain for good","Knows the pain is in his tummy and that the doctors want to 'cut'","Cannot say what might happen if he doesn't have the operation","His mum is in a care home with dementia; no other family","Likes Dean to be with him and likes his music on headphones","Had morphine four hours ago; pain now 3 out of 10"],"worries":["Needles hurt. They held me down.","Can I have tablets instead?","Dean: Can't his mum just sign the form?","Surgeon: He's said no, so we'll discharge him, yes?"],"rules":["Repeats 'no hospital' if rushed or if Dean answers for him","Discloses the restraint memory only if asked what scares him","Becomes calmer and more talkative once his fear is acknowledged","Dean says 'he never understands these things' until asked to prompt instead"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Before seeing him","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Malik from liaison psychiatry. Before I see Paul, can you tell me exactly what you're proposing, the main risks, and what happens if he doesn't have it?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","who":"Surgeon","s":"Laparoscopic cholecystectomy, keyhole gallbladder removal, on this admission. Risks are bleeding, infection, bile leak, a small chance of open surgery. Without it, more attacks, and a risk of pancreatitis or cholecystitis."},{"t":"0:31","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Is there any other option, and how urgent is it?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","who":"Surgeon","s":"Painkillers only treat the attacks. It's not an emergency today, but we'd like it within days. He's had morphine this morning."},{"t":"0:49","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Paul, I'm Dr Malik. I help people make choices about their care. Is it okay to talk for a few minutes? How's your tummy now?","m":[2,9]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"No hospital. No hospital."},{"t":"1:06","w":"D","s":"Okay. I hear you. I'm not here to make you do anything. I just want to hear what you think.","m":[2,5]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","who":"Dean","s":"He never understands these things. I'll just answer for him."},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Dean. You know him well and that's a big help. I need to hear Paul's answers in his words first. Could you let him answer, even if he takes a while, and then tell me what helps him understand?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","who":"Dean","s":"Oh. Right, sorry. Yes, of course."},{"t":"1:47","w":"D","s":"Paul, how bad is the pain right now, from nought to ten?","m":[9]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Three. Tummy's sore."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"And do you know where you are today, and what day it is?","m":[9]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Hospital. Friday. Dean came in his car."},{"t":"2:09","band":"His view","w":"D","s":"Thank you. What scares you about hospital?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Needles hurt. They held me down."},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. I'm sorry that happened. When was that?","m":[5,4]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Long time ago. Blood test. Lots of people."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"It makes sense you don't want to be here. Is that why you say no to the operation?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Yeah. No needles. No holding."},{"t":"2:44","band":"Information","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. Let me show you some pictures. This is your tummy. These little stones are making the pain. The doctors want to take out this bag with the stones, using tiny cuts. You'd be asleep.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"Asleep. Okay."},{"t":"3:06","w":"D","s":"If you don't have it, the pain will keep coming back, and you might get very poorly. What do you think the operation is for?","m":[6,7]},{"t":"3:19","w":"P","s":"Cut my tummy. For the pain."},{"t":"3:23","w":"D","s":"That's right. What's making the pain?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"Don't know. Tummy."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"Let's look again. (points) What are these?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Stones."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"Good. What might happen if you don't have the operation?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:43","w":"P","s":"Can I have tablets instead? I'll take the tablets."},{"t":"3:48","w":"D","s":"Tablets help the pain for a little while, but they don't take the stones away. What would happen with just tablets?","m":[8,6]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Tablets fix it. No hospital."},{"t":"4:03","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Paul. You've told me really clearly you don't want it, and that you're scared of needles and being held.","m":[8,5]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"4:16","band":"Advice","w":"D","s":"Dean, can I check with you: any family we should speak to?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","who":"Dean","s":"His mum's in a care home with dementia. No one else. Can't she just sign the form?"},{"t":"4:33","w":"D","s":"No one can sign for an adult, even a parent. If Paul can't decide, the surgeon decides in his best interests, after hearing from people who know him. With no family able to help, we need an independent advocate, called an IMCA.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"4:54","w":"P","who":"Dean","s":"Right. I didn't know that."},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"Today he can say what he wants clearly, but he can't yet understand or weigh that tablets won't remove the stones. Fear and the morphine may be getting in the way, so I'll try again tomorrow.","m":[8,9,10]},{"t":"5:16","w":"P","who":"Surgeon","s":"He's said no, so we'll discharge him, yes?"},{"t":"5:21","w":"D","s":"Not yet. He seems to lack capacity for this decision today, but it's mostly fear, and we can help that. The decision is yours, as his surgeon. I'll write that, plus an IMCA referral and a best interests meeting on Tuesday.","m":[13,11]},{"t":"5:42","w":"P","who":"Surgeon","s":"Okay. What would make it easier for him?"},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"Numbing cream an hour before any needle, first on the list, Dean with him into the anaesthetic room, headphones with his music, and the learning disability nurse showing him theatre beforehand.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","who":"Dean","s":"I can come in with him. He'd like that."},{"t":"6:08","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Dean. Any restraint would have to be planned and proportionate, and if it's more than minimal we'd involve the trust's legal team. Paul, can I tell you the plan?","m":[12,10]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"No needles today?"},{"t":"6:27","w":"D","s":"No needles today. The nurse will show you some pictures, and I'll come back tomorrow to talk again. Dean can stay with you. What happens tomorrow?","m":[14,12]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"You come back. Talk. Dean stays."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"That's right. Thank you, Paul. You did really well telling me what scares you.","m":[14,5]}],"marks":[{"c":"Clarifies the exact decision, risks, alternatives and urgency with the surgeon first","d":"Content"},{"c":"Introduces self to Paul, speaks to him directly and gives him time","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Respectfully asks the support worker to let Paul answer and to prompt instead","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores why he refuses and finds the past restraint trauma","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his fear and acknowledges his clear wish","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives simple, decision-specific information with pictures, including what happens without surgery","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tests understanding and retention with open questions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests weighing, including the tablets alternative, and his ability to communicate","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks pain, orientation and recent opioids that may affect the assessment","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains that no one consents for an adult and the surgeon decides in best interests","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Identifies there is no family to consult and that an IMCA is needed","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names at least three reasonable adjustments and the limits on restraint","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises in writing: outcome, decision-maker, IMCA and a dated best interests meeting; no discharge","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Tells Paul the plan simply, checks he understands and closes warmly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Hears Paul's own answers, finds fear behind the refusal, tests all four abilities, and gives the surgeons clear written advice on decision-maker, IMCA, adjustments and a best interests meeting.","borderline":"Reaches a reasonable outcome but lets Dean answer, misses the IMCA, or gives vague adjustments.","fail":"Accepts 'no' as a capacitous refusal without testing, lets the support worker or mother consent, or agrees to discharge him."}},"c-8-2-3":{"setup":"You are in the CLDT clinic. The mother of Amy, 19, who has moderate learning disability, asks to see you alone. Amy has a new boyfriend at her day centre, and her mother wants her sterilised. Respond, explain the law and agree a safe plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Sandra Cole, 51, female, Amy's mother (Amy, 19, moderate learning disability, is not in the room)","open":"Thanks for seeing me on my own. I'll get straight to it. I want Amy sterilised. I can't cope with a pregnancy on top of everything.","manner":"Exhausted, tearful and determined at first, then angry when told she cannot decide. Softens into guilt and relief once her strain is heard and real help is offered.","free":["Amy has started 'seeing' a man called Lee at her day centre","She is Amy's only carer since her husband left four years ago","She works part time and barely sleeps"],"ifasked":["Lee is about 30 and works as a volunteer at the day centre","Amy came home with a love bite and said Lee told her it was 'their secret'","Amy says she loves Lee and wants to marry him","Amy has never had sex education adapted for her","Sandra feels low, cries most days, sleeps 4 hours; no thoughts of harming herself or Amy","Has never had a carer's assessment"],"worries":["I'm her mother. Why can't I decide?","Can she just have the injection without knowing what it's for?","Am I a terrible mother for even asking?","What's a carer's assessment? Will they judge me?"],"rules":["Becomes angry if the law is stated before her exhaustion is acknowledged","Mentions the 'secret' and that Lee is a volunteer only if asked about the relationship","Becomes tearful when asked how she herself is coping; settles if validated","Accepts the plan once she hears practical support for herself and Amy"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Cole, I'm Dr Hassan. Thanks for coming in. You wanted to talk on your own, which is fine. What's on your mind?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Thanks for seeing me on my own. I'll get straight to it. I want Amy sterilised. I can't cope with a pregnancy on top of everything."},{"t":"0:24","w":"D","s":"That's a big thing to ask, and I can tell it hasn't come from nowhere. You look exhausted. What's been happening?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"She's got a boyfriend at the day centre. I'm on my own with her. If she gets pregnant, I'll end up raising the baby. I can't do this any more."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you're carrying an awful lot by yourself. I really want to help. How long have you been looking after Amy on your own?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"Four years, since her dad left. I work part time. I barely sleep."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"That's a huge amount. Before we talk options, I need to be straight with you about the law, because it matters. Is that okay?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"1:19","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"1:22","band":"The law","w":"D","s":"Amy is an adult now. In England no one, not even a parent, can agree to an operation like sterilisation for another adult. It would need Amy's own agreement, or a decision by the Court of Protection, and the court looks for less permanent options first.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"I'm her mother. I know what's best. Why can't I decide?"},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"You know her better than anyone, and your view really counts. But the law protects adults from permanent decisions made by others. Amy's own wishes come first, and we check whether she can decide each thing for herself.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"(sighs) It just feels like nobody's on my side."},{"t":"2:11","band":"The relationship","w":"D","s":"I'm on your side and Amy's. Can you tell me about the boyfriend?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"Lee. He's about thirty. He volunteers there."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"A volunteer, so staff at the day centre. How did you find out?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"She came home with a love bite. She said Lee told her it was their secret."},{"t":"2:37","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. I do need to ask directly: do you think Lee might be taking advantage of her, or that anything has happened she didn't want?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"That's what frightens me. She says she loves him and wants to marry him. But a secret?"},{"t":"2:59","w":"D","s":"You're right to be worried. A volunteer telling her to keep it secret is a concern. I'll tell the trust safeguarding lead today, and they'll contact the local authority and the day centre manager.","m":[5,10]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Will the police be involved?"},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"If Amy tells us something that's a crime, yes. First, I'd like our learning disability nurse and me to see Amy alone this week, gently, to hear how she feels and whether she's safe.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Then can she just have the injection without knowing what it's for?"},{"t":"3:41","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd want that. But giving her contraception without telling her wouldn't be lawful or fair to her. If Amy can understand it, it's her choice. If she can't, it's a best interests decision with you involved.","m":[8,7]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"So what can actually be done?"},{"t":"4:03","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"Two things. Our nurse can run easy read sessions on relationships, consent and keeping safe, about six to eight sessions. And her GP or the sexual health clinic can talk to her about contraception, like an implant that lasts three years.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"She's never had anything like that explained to her properly."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"Then it could really help her. We'll also check whether she can decide about sex and about contraception, each one separately.","m":[9,7]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"(tearful) Am I a terrible mother for even asking?"},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"No. You asked because you love her and you're frightened and worn out. That's not terrible at all. You did the right thing coming to talk.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"(crying) Thank you. I haven't said any of this to anyone."},{"t":"5:01","band":"Mum's own needs","w":"D","s":"Can I ask how you are in yourself? You said you barely sleep.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"I cry most days. Four hours' sleep, maybe."},{"t":"5:13","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard. Have you had any thoughts of self-harm or of things being too much to go on?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:23","w":"P","s":"No. Never. Amy needs me."},{"t":"5:26","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. I'd like you to see your GP about your mood. And I'll refer you for a carer's assessment from adult social care.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:38","w":"P","s":"What's a carer's assessment? Will they judge me?"},{"t":"5:43","w":"D","s":"No. It's your right as a carer. It looks at what you need, like breaks, help at home or support groups. It's about helping you, not judging you.","m":[11,9]},{"t":"5:56","w":"P","s":"Okay. I'd like that."},{"t":"5:59","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've been clear. What will happen next?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"You'll see Amy on her own this week. You'll tell safeguarding about Lee. She gets the sessions and can talk about contraception. And I see my GP and get the assessment."},{"t":"6:19","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. And no permanent operation unless Amy agrees, or a court decides.","m":[13,6]},{"t":"6:27","w":"P","s":"I understand. I just want her safe."},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"So do I. Let's meet again in two weeks. Here's our duty number if anything worries you before then.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Thank you, doctor. I feel a bit lighter."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for trusting me with this. Take care of yourself too.","m":[13,12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, agrees to talk alone and invites her concerns openly","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges her exhaustion before stating the law","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores what lies behind the request: her fears, isolation and coping","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about the relationship and Amy's own wishes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about coercion, abuse or exploitation","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains plainly that no parent can consent for an adult and a court would be needed","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains that Amy's capacity is assessed decision by decision and her wishes come first","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Declines covert contraception clearly and kindly, with the reason","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers two less restrictive options and names who provides them","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges to see Amy alone and informs safeguarding the same day","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about the mother's mood and risk, and arranges GP review and carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates her guilt without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding, safety-nets and agrees a 2-week review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Holds the mother's distress, explains the law clearly without coldness, picks up the safeguarding concern, and agrees less restrictive options and support for both.","borderline":"Explains the law correctly but misses the exploitation concern or the mother's own needs.","fail":"Agrees to pursue sterilisation or covert contraception, lectures on the law without empathy, or misses safeguarding."}},"c-8-3-1":{"setup":"You are in the CLDT clinic. Susan, 52, has Down syndrome and lives with her older sister Margaret. Over a year she has become forgetful, got lost and had a first seizure. Take a history from Margaret, outline the assessment and explain next steps.","role":{"who":"Mrs Margaret Hill, 60, female, Susan's sister and carer (Susan Hill, 52, Down syndrome, is with her)","open":"Thanks for seeing us. Is this dementia? Just tell me straight.","manner":"Direct, anxious and a little impatient for an answer. Guilty that she missed the signs. Becomes tearful about the future; settles when listened to and given a clear plan. Susan is cheerful and chatty but vague.","free":["Susan has got more forgetful over about a year","Got lost in town last month, a route she has walked for 20 years","Stopped going to her craft group","Had a seizure two months ago and was seen in A&E"],"ifasked":["Susan used to manage her own bus pass, make tea and do simple shopping; best was about two years ago","Video of Susan at her niece's wedding three years ago shows her chatty and dancing","Now repeats questions, loses things, struggles with buttons","More irritable in the evenings and sleeps in the day; snores loudly","Has not had her thyroid checked or a hearing or sight test for over two years","Susan's best friend at the craft group moved away in the spring","The seizure was a sudden jerk on waking then shaking; no medicine started yet","Margaret has arthritis and worries about who will care for Susan"],"worries":["How long has she got?","I should have noticed sooner.","Is there a tablet for it?","Who'll look after her when I can't?"],"rules":["Wants a yes or no about dementia; accepts 'likely but not yet certain' if the reason is explained","Becomes tearful about having called Susan lazy; settles if validated","Susan says 'I don't want a blood test' unless it is explained simply and she is given a choice","Mentions the snoring and the friend moving away only if asked"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Clarke, one of the learning disability doctors. Hello Susan. Is it okay if I talk with you and Margaret together today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","who":"Susan","s":"Hello! Yes. I like your badge."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Susan. Margaret, I know you have questions. I'd like to understand what's changed first, then I'll answer them. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Thanks for seeing us. Is this dementia? Just tell me straight."},{"t":"0:31","band":"Her best","w":"D","s":"I'll be honest with you as soon as I've got the full picture. Can you tell me what Susan was like at her best?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"About two years ago she'd catch the bus with her own pass, make tea, do a bit of shopping. She was at her craft group twice a week."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Have you got anything that shows her then, like reports or videos?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"Her day centre reports. And a video of her dancing at our niece's wedding three years ago. Chatty as anything."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"That's really useful, please bring them. When did you first notice a change?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"Maybe a year ago. She started asking the same things over and over."},{"t":"1:23","band":"What has changed","w":"D","s":"What else has changed since then?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"She loses things. She can't do her buttons. Last month she got lost in town on a walk she's done for twenty years."},{"t":"1:38","w":"D","s":"That must have been frightening. Has her personality changed at all?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"More snappy in the evenings. And she's stopped her craft group."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"Do you know why she stopped going?","m":[3,5]},{"t":"1:53","w":"P","s":"She said it was boring. Her best friend there moved away in the spring."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"That's important, she may be missing her friend. Is Susan sleeping well? Any loud snoring or pauses in her breathing?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"She snores like a train. And she naps in the day now."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"When did she last have her thyroid checked, and her hearing and eyesight?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"Not for over two years, I don't think."},{"t":"2:27","w":"D","s":"Okay. Has she been low or tearful, or started any new medicines?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:33","w":"P","s":"No new tablets. Not tearful, just quieter."},{"t":"2:37","band":"The seizure","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the seizure two months ago.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"She jerked as she woke up, then went stiff and shook. A&E said see the GP. Nothing's happened since."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Any smaller jerks in the mornings since, or dropping things?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"Now you mention it, she drops her cup some mornings."},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I should have noticed sooner. I thought she was being lazy. I told her off about her buttons."},{"t":"3:11","w":"D","s":"Please don't be hard on yourself. These changes come on slowly and they're easy to miss. You noticed and brought her in. That's exactly right.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"(nods) Thank you."},{"t":"3:25","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Here's my honest view. People with Down syndrome are more likely to get Alzheimer's disease, and younger. The memory loss, getting lost and new seizure make me worried it's starting.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"So it is dementia."},{"t":"3:41","w":"D","s":"It's likely, but not yet certain. Thyroid problems, poor hearing or eyesight, sleep apnoea from the snoring, and low mood after losing her friend can all look similar, and they can be treated. So we check those first.","m":[7,5]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"How long has she got?"},{"t":"4:01","w":"D","s":"That's a natural question. It varies a lot, often years, and I can't say yet. Let's get the right diagnosis first.","m":[7,6]},{"t":"4:11","w":"P","s":"What happens now?"},{"t":"4:14","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Bloods at the GP: thyroid, blood count, B12, folate, calcium and sugar. A hearing test and an eye test. A sleep study if needed. And our psychologist will do a memory assessment to compare over time.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","who":"Susan","s":"I don't want a blood test."},{"t":"4:33","w":"D","s":"That's okay, Susan. The blood test helps us check if a tablet can make you feel better. The nurse can use magic cream so it hurts less, and Margaret can hold your hand. You can think about it. What do you think?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","who":"Susan","s":"Magic cream. And Margaret holds my hand. Okay."},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Susan. I'll also refer her to the epilepsy team for the seizure and those morning jerks, which can happen with Alzheimer's in Down syndrome.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"Is there a tablet for the memory?"},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"If it is Alzheimer's, there are tablets that can help for a while. We'd check her heart first.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:21","w":"P","s":"I'm sixty with bad knees. Who'll look after her when I can't?"},{"t":"5:27","w":"D","s":"That's a big worry, and we'll plan for it. Social care can assess Susan's needs and do a carer's assessment for you. And while Susan can still decide, we can help her choose who speaks for her later, a Lasting Power of Attorney, and plan her wishes.","m":[12,11]},{"t":"5:47","w":"P","s":"She's always said she wants me to sort things for her."},{"t":"5:53","w":"D","s":"Then let's talk to Susan about that next time, with easy read information.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"And until then?"},{"t":"6:02","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"If she has another seizure lasting over five minutes, or doesn't wake properly, call 999. If she coughs or chokes with food, or gets lost again, let us know straight away.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:16","w":"P","s":"999 for a long fit. Ring you about choking or getting lost."},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Can you tell me why we're not saying it's dementia yet?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:28","w":"P","s":"Because her thyroid, ears, eyes or sleep might be making it look worse, and those can be fixed."},{"t":"6:37","w":"D","s":"That's right. I'll see you both in six weeks with the results. Thank you, Margaret. Bye Susan.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","who":"Susan","s":"Bye! I'll bring my crafts."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, includes Susan and agrees how the appointment will run","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Builds a picture of her best, using reports or videos, and dates the decline","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores memory, getting lost, lost skills, personality and interests","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes a clear history of the seizure and asks about morning jerks","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens treatable mimics: thyroid, hearing, sight, sleep apnoea, low mood, medicines, life events","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates the sister's guilt and worry","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains in plain words why Alzheimer's is likely but not yet diagnosed","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Outlines bloods, hearing and sight tests and a baseline memory assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges an epilepsy review for the new seizure","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Responds to Susan's refusal respectfully, with simple information and a choice","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Raises Lasting Power of Attorney and advance care planning while Susan can decide","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Addresses future care with needs and carer's assessments","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets for prolonged seizure, choking and getting lost","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back and agrees a 6-week review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Builds a clear baseline and timeline, screens treatable mimics, explains 'likely but not yet diagnosed', arranges epilepsy review and raises LPA while Susan can still decide.","borderline":"Takes a good history but skips hearing, sight or thyroid, or misses advance planning.","fail":"Diagnoses dementia on the spot without excluding mimics, ignores the seizure, or talks only about Susan without including her."}},"c-8-3-2":{"setup":"Thursday 14:30, CLDT clinic. Mr Imran Shah, 58, moderate intellectual disability, has declined over 9 months and now sees children at night. Haloperidol started two days ago; he is stiff and drowsy. Assess him with his support worker and agree a safe plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Grace Oduya, 40s, female, his support worker for four years (Mr Imran Shah, 58, also speaks in short phrases)","open":"Grace, I'm his support worker. I've known Imran nearly four years. I'm really worried. He's not been right since Tuesday.","manner":"Warm, organised and anxious; feels guilty she asked for the tablet. Tearful when she says it is like losing him; steadies once validated. Mr Shah is drowsy, quiet and frightened about the children.","free":["Haloperidol 1.5 mg started on Tuesday after he said children were in his bedroom","Since then he is stiff, sleeps in his chair and is barely drinking","Over nine months he has become slower and needs help with buttons","She has brought his hospital passport and medicines list"],"ifasked":["A year ago he made his own toast and tea, chose his clothes and did jigsaws","Some days he stares and is muddled, then is sharp and joking the next morning; it can change within an hour","He also sees a cat on the sofa (there is no cat), for about four months","Three falls since summer; shuffles with small steps; right hand shakes at rest","Night staff hear him shouting and thrashing in his sleep; he fell out of bed in March","Not hot or sweaty; urine dip clear last month; bowels normal for him; glasses not checked for two years","No earlier memory or dementia assessment","His sister Farah visits on Sundays"],"worries":["The GP said it would calm him. Shouldn't he stay on it?","When he sees the children, do we argue with him?","Will he get better?","Was it my fault for asking for the tablet?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful about 'losing him'; settles if the doctor validates rather than moves on","Mentions sleep thrashing and the cat only if asked directly","Mr Shah answers only short, simple questions put to him by name","Mr Shah cannot repeat back what the tablet plan is a minute later"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Shah. I'm Dr Ade. I'm a doctor. Thank you for coming today.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","who":"Mr Shah","s":"Hello."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"And you're from his home? What's your name, and how long have you known him?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Grace, I'm his support worker. I've known Imran nearly four years. I'm really worried. He's not been right since Tuesday."},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Grace. I'll talk to Imran first, then to you. Imran, how are you feeling today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","who":"Mr Shah","s":"Tired. Legs stiff. Sleepy."},{"t":"0:32","band":"Safe right now","w":"D","s":"Stiff and sleepy. That sounds horrible. Can I gently feel your arm? Thank you. Grace, he's stiff on both sides. Has he been hot or sweaty?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"No, not hot. But he's barely drinking. Half a cup at breakfast. And he nearly fell at the door."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"Is he much sleepier than usual? Can you wake him easily?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"He sleeps in his chair most of the day since the tablet. You can wake him, he just drifts off again. Haloperidol, 1.5 milligrams, started Tuesday."},{"t":"1:06","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Our nurse has just checked him. Temperature 37.1, pulse 84, blood pressure normal. He's stiff, but he's not hot and he's talking to us. So I don't see signs of NMS, a rare dangerous reaction, right now. We'll keep watching.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:22","w":"P","s":"Oh, thank goodness."},{"t":"1:24","band":"Then and now","w":"D","s":"Before all this, a year ago, what was Imran like on his best day?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"He made his own toast and tea, chose his clothes, did his jigsaws. Knew everyone's name."},{"t":"1:37","w":"D","s":"And what has changed over the nine months?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"Slower. Needs help with buttons. Got lost finding his bedroom twice. He's stopped the jigsaws."},{"t":"1:47","w":"D","s":"Are there good days and bad days? How fast does it change?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:53","w":"P","s":"That's the odd bit. Some days he's staring and muddled, not himself. Next morning he's sharp and joking with me. It can change in an hour."},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"Imran, Grace says you see children in your room. Can you tell me about them?","m":[1,5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","who":"Mr Shah","s":"Small children. At night. By the window."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"Do they scare you?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","who":"Mr Shah","s":"Yes. Don't like them."},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. Thank you for telling me. I believe you see them, and we'll help you feel safe.","m":[8,5]},{"t":"2:26","w":"D","s":"Grace, does he see anything else, like animals or people in the day?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"Now you mention it, a cat on the sofa. We haven't got a cat. That's been about four months."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Has he fallen? Is his walking different? Any shaking?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"Three falls since the summer. He shuffles now, little steps. His right hand shakes when it's resting."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"Does he shout, kick or thrash in his sleep, as if acting out a dream?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"Night staff say he shouts and thrashes. He fell out of bed in March. We thought it was nightmares."},{"t":"3:06","band":"Other causes","w":"D","s":"Some things can look like this and are treatable. Any fever, cough, pain passing water? Bowels? Pain anywhere? Eyesight or hearing changes?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Urine dip at the GP last month was clear. Bowels are normal for him. His glasses haven't been checked for two years. No pain we know of."},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"Any other new medicines? And has he ever had a memory check, so we can compare?","m":[7,3]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"Only his usual blood pressure tablet. No memory check. Honestly, I feel like we're losing him. And I'm the one who asked the GP for something."},{"t":"3:43","w":"D","s":"That's hard to watch. You asked for help because he was frightened, and that was right. Some of what you see is the tablet, and that part should ease.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Sorry."},{"t":"3:56","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Putting it together: good and bad days, seeing children and a cat, shuffling, acting out dreams. That pattern fits Lewy body dementia, a type of dementia. People with it can be very sensitive to medicines like haloperidol. Even a small dose can cause stiffness, sleepiness and falls.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"The GP said it'd calm him. Shouldn't he stay on it?"},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"I can see why it was tried. In this pattern it can be dangerous, so the safest thing is to stop it today. I'll ring the GP myself. It isn't a firm diagnosis yet; we still check for infection and other causes.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:36","band":"Decide and plan","w":"D","s":"Imran, the tablet is making you stiff and sleepy. I think we should stop it. Is that OK?","m":[1,11]},{"t":"4:44","w":"P","who":"Mr Shah","s":"No more tablets. They make me bad."},{"t":"4:47","w":"D","s":"You're right. We'll stop it. Can you tell me what we just said about the tablet?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:54","w":"P","who":"Mr Shah","s":"Which tablet?"},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Imran. Grace, he agrees, but today he can't hold on to the information, so I'm deciding in his best interests. Who else should be involved?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:07","w":"P","s":"His sister Farah. She visits on Sundays."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"I'll ring Farah today and invite her to the meeting. So: no more haloperidol from now. I'll phone the GP today for a check and bloods, including a muscle test called CK, and a urine test. I'll put an alert on his record and hospital passport: avoid antipsychotics. And we'll book a memory assessment, plus eyes and hearing checks.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:32","w":"P","s":"So when he sees the children, do we argue with him?"},{"t":"5:37","w":"D","s":"No arguing, and no playing along. Stay calm, switch the light on, sit with him. A night light may help. Please keep a daily chart: good or bad day, sleep, falls, what he sees.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:51","band":"Safety net","w":"D","s":"Call 999 if he gets very stiff, hot or sweaty, more confused, or stops drinking. Keep offering drinks today.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:59","w":"D","s":"So I can check I've explained it clearly, can you tell me what you'll watch for tonight?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"No haloperidol. If he's hot, really stiff, confused or not drinking, 999. Chart every day, and a night light."},{"t":"6:13","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Spot on. I'll see you both here in two weeks with the chart. Our consultant will be there, and Farah is invited. Any questions?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:23","w":"P","s":"Will he get better?"},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"The stiffness should ease over a few days without the tablet. If it is Lewy body dementia, there are medicines that can help with the seeing things, and we'll plan support to keep him as well as he can be.","m":[9,14]},{"t":"6:41","w":"D","s":"Imran, we're stopping the tablet. See you soon.","m":[1,14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","who":"Mr Shah","s":"Thank you, doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and speaks to Mr Shah first, by name, in short simple sentences","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks immediate safety: temperature, rigidity, drinking, alertness; rules NMS in or out","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Establishes his own baseline a year ago and the decline since","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about fluctuation between good and bad days","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks gently about visual hallucinations and whether they frighten him","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about falls, shuffling, tremor and acting out dreams","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for delirium and physical causes: infection, bowels, pain, sight, hearing, drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates the patient's fear and the support worker's distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains possible Lewy body dementia and antipsychotic sensitivity in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Stops haloperidol today, phones the GP for bloods including CK, adds an avoid-antipsychotics alert","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Assesses capacity for the medicine decision and involves his sister in best interests","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives clear 999 triggers: very stiff, hot, newly confused or not drinking","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks the support worker's understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with a 2-week review date, daily chart and chance to ask questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Speaks to him first, checks for NMS, takes a baseline-anchored history of DLB features, stops haloperidol today and gives clear 999 triggers.","borderline":"Safe and kind but thin on fluctuation or sleep behaviour, or vague about the GP call, alert and review date.","fail":"Talks only to the carer, blames the learning disability, misses NMS, or switches to another antipsychotic."}},"c-8-3-3":{"setup":"Tuesday 11:00, CLDT duty clinic. Amira, 24, severe intellectual disability and epilepsy, has hit out, screamed and slept badly for 6 weeks. Her mother is with her. Link the behaviour to her epilepsy, look for physical causes, and set up her annual health check.","role":{"who":"Mrs Samira Hassan, 52, female, Amira's mother","open":"Samira. Thank you for seeing us. I'm worried sick. Six weeks of hitting and screaming, and nobody's looked at her properly.","manner":"Frustrated and protective at first; angry about the missed health checks. Shaken and tearful when told about sudden death in epilepsy; relieved and warmer once she feels heard and has a plan.","free":["Six weeks of hitting out, screaming and poor sleep","The home wants a 'calming' tablet","No annual health check for two years despite her phoning","Lamotrigine was increased recently"],"ifasked":["Amira uses a few Makaton signs, sounds and leads you by the hand; hums when happy","She keeps holding the side of her face and refuses toast and apples","Seizures up from 2 to 5 a month, mostly at night","Worst the morning after a night seizure; also screams at mealtimes","Her favourite key worker left in August; lamotrigine went up about four weeks ago","Last dentist about three years ago; periods regular with cramps; bruise on shin from a seizure fall","Night staff don't really know her seizure plan; not sure the midazolam is in date","Someone at the home asked about using midazolam when she is upset"],"worries":["Nobody listens to me.","The GP wants to give her something to calm her. Will that fix it?","Could she die from a seizure at night?","Can they give her the midazolam when she's kicking off?"],"rules":["Becomes angry about the missed checks; calms if the doctor agrees it is too long and acts","Becomes tearful when SUDEP is raised; settles if the doctor is honest and gives practical steps","Mentions jaw holding only when asked how Amira shows pain","Discloses the night staff gap only when asked about the rescue plan"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Amira. I'm Dr Ross, a doctor. Mum is here with you.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Ross from the learning disability team. You must be Amira's mum. What should I call you?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Samira. Thank you for seeing us. I'm worried sick. Six weeks of hitting and screaming, and nobody's looked at her properly."},{"t":"0:23","w":"D","s":"Six weeks is a long time to watch that. I want to understand it properly. First, how does Amira communicate? Words, signs, pictures?","m":[1,8]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"A few Makaton signs. Mostly sounds, and she pulls you to what she wants. She hums when she's happy."},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"And how does she show you she's in pain or unhappy?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"Screams. Bangs her head. And lately she keeps holding the side of her face."},{"t":"0:54","w":"D","s":"That's really useful. I can see her holding her jaw now. Is she eating?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Picks at things. Won't touch toast or apples any more. Only yoghurt and soup."},{"t":"1:07","band":"Seizures and behaviour","w":"D","s":"Let's look at the seizure diary together. How many seizures this month, compared with before?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"She usually has two a month. Last month it was five. Mostly at night."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"When does the hitting happen: before a seizure, after one, or not linked?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"Staff say it's worst the morning after a night seizure. She's muddled and lashes out. But she screams at mealtimes on other days too."},{"t":"1:37","w":"D","s":"And before a seizure, is she grumpy? During one, any lip smacking or picking at clothes?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"Sometimes grumpy the evening before. I don't know about during."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"What changed six weeks ago? Medicines, staff, routine, her room?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Her favourite key worker left in August. And the lamotrigine went up about four weeks ago because of the fits."},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"So the behaviour started before the dose went up. That helps. Since the increase, any rash, dizziness, double vision or wobbly walking?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"No rash. Maybe a bit more wobbly. I'm not sure."},{"t":"2:17","band":"Pain and health","w":"D","s":"Let me check other sources of pain. Any ear pulling? How are her bowels? Periods? Any falls or injuries?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"Staff don't chart her bowels. Periods are regular, she gets cramps. She's got a bruise on her shin from falling in a fit last week."},{"t":"2:37","w":"D","s":"When did she last see a dentist?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Three years, maybe. She won't open her mouth for them. And two years with no health check. I keep phoning. Nobody listens to me."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"You're right to be angry. Two years is far too long, and you've kept pushing for her. I'll write to the GP today asking for her annual health check and a health action plan, and copy you in.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Finally."},{"t":"3:09","band":"Rescue plan and safety","w":"D","s":"Does she have a rescue plan for long seizures, with buccal midazolam? Is it in date?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"Yes, midazolam. I don't know if it's in date. Honestly, the night staff don't really know her plan."},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me, that matters. I'll ask the epilepsy nurse to review the plan, train the night team this week, and check the midazolam is in date and within reach.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"Someone at the home asked if they could give her the midazolam when she's kicking off."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"No. It's only for seizures, exactly as her plan says. Using it for distress isn't safe. Calming her will have its own plan.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"There's something about night seizures I'd like to talk about. Is that OK?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:01","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"4:03","w":"D","s":"Rarely, people with epilepsy die suddenly, often during or after a seizure at night. It's called SUDEP. It's uncommon, but night seizures and missed doses make it more likely.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Oh God. Nobody's ever told me that. She's on her own in her room at night."},{"t":"4:23","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, that's a lot to hear. It's frightening. We can lower the risk: doses on time every day, and better night monitoring, like a bed seizure alarm. The epilepsy nurse will look at that with you and the home.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"4:39","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Here's what I think. Amira can't tell us in words, so her behaviour is how she tells us something's wrong. Holding her jaw and avoiding hard food make me think of tooth pain. More night seizures can also leave her muddled and cross the next morning.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"The GP wants to give her something to calm her. Will that fix it?"},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"It might make her sleepy, but it won't fix why she's upset, and it could hide pain. So no calming tablet for now. Let's find the cause first.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:16","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Amira can't decide about tests herself today, so we decide in her best interests, with you and the staff. Does that feel right?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"Yes. I want to be involved."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"So: a special care dentist this week. My letter to the GP asks for her health check, a health action plan, a check of ears, tummy, bowels and urine, and to confirm she's on the learning disability register. Staff keep a seizure diary and a behaviour chart for two weeks, with seizure times on it.","m":[11,3]},{"t":"5:52","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what happens next, so I know I've been clear?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Dentist this week. You write to the GP for her health check. The epilepsy nurse trains the night staff, checks the midazolam and looks at an alarm. Charts for two weeks. No calming tablet."},{"t":"6:14","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If a seizure goes on longer than her plan says, give the midazolam as written and call 999 if it doesn't stop. If she gets a rash on the lamotrigine, she needs a doctor the same day.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Right. 999 if it doesn't stop. Rash, same day."},{"t":"6:34","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I'll see you both in three weeks with the charts. Any questions before you go?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"No. It finally feels like someone's listening."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Bye Amira. Thank you for letting me see you today.","m":[1,14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Greets Amira directly and asks how she communicates","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks how Amira shows pain or distress, and notices jaw holding","d":"Content"},{"c":"Times the behaviour against seizures: before, during, after or unrelated","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what changed six weeks ago: staff, routine, medicines","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens pain sources: teeth, ears, bowels, periods and injuries","d":"Content"},{"c":"Reviews the lamotrigine increase and asks about rash, dizziness and unsteadiness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks the rescue midazolam plan, staff training, and refuses its use for distress","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates the mother's anger and fear without defensiveness","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains behaviour as communication and why no calming tablet yet","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Discusses SUDEP honestly and plans night monitoring","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Applies best interests and requests the annual health check and action plan in writing","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks the mother's understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets: 999 if seizure continues after midazolam; same-day review for rash","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closes with a review date and clear roles","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Includes Amira, maps behaviour to seizures, finds likely dental pain, fixes the rescue plan, discusses SUDEP and requests the health check in writing.","borderline":"Finds pain causes but misses the rescue plan, SUDEP or the written health check request.","fail":"Prescribes a calming drug without a physical search, agrees midazolam for behaviour, or dismisses the mother."}},"c-8-4-1":{"setup":"Wednesday 10:00, CLDT clinic. Jake, 32, moderate intellectual disability and autism, has taken risperidone 2 mg for 6 years and gained 14 kg. He hit two staff this month; his key worker and deputy manager want the dose raised. Respond and agree a safer plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Dana Price, 30s, female, Jake's key worker (Mr Leon Burke, deputy manager, and Jake, 32, also speak)","open":"Dana, his key worker. This is Leon, the deputy manager.","manner":"Dana is calm and observant. Leon is tired and pressured, pushing for a dose increase; frustrated, then relieved when injuries are taken seriously and a crisis plan is offered. Jake speaks in short phrases.","free":["Jake hit two staff this month: a bruised arm and a scratched face","Staff want the risperidone put up","He paces, bites his sleeve and gets loud before he hits"],"ifasked":["Last incident: Tuesday 10 am in the kitchen, after an agency worker said there was no centre","Afterwards he goes to his room, gets his iPad and calms; both incidents were mornings","His day centre closed in the summer; half the team left; many agency staff","Sleeps well; sometimes constipated; dentist in spring; not low","Risperidone started for behaviour when he moved in; never psychotic; never reviewed","No recent bloods, blood pressure or ECG; always hungry","No LPA or deputy; his mother visits weekly"],"worries":["Staff are getting hurt; we need something now.","So you're doing nothing?","When do we talk about the dose?","(Jake) Can I go back to the centre?"],"rules":["Leon becomes frustrated if medicine is refused before injuries are acknowledged; settles if validated","Dana gives the trigger detail only when asked what happened just before","Jake answers short questions put to him directly","Jake can say the tablet makes him calm and fat but cannot weigh up taking less"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Jake. I'm Dr Nwosu. Thanks for coming. Is it OK if I talk with you and your staff today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"OK. Home after."},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"Yes, home after. And who's with you today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"Dana, his key worker. This is Leon, the deputy manager."},{"t":"0:23","w":"D","s":"Thanks. How does Jake like to communicate? Anything that helps?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"Short sentences. Pictures on his iPad. Don't stand too close."},{"t":"0:34","band":"Safety first","w":"D","s":"I hear two staff were hurt this month. Is everyone OK? Is it safe at home right now?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","who":"Manager","s":"One bruised arm, one scratched face. Both back at work. But the team's rattled. We want the risperidone put up."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"That's serious, and I'm sorry staff were hurt. Before we talk about medicine, let's agree what you do this week if he gets upset. What are his early signs?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"He paces, bites his sleeve, gets loud."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Then give him space, lower your voices, and offer his iPad or a walk. No crowding him. If someone is at risk, step away and call our duty line in hours, or the crisis line out of hours. I'll write it down for you today.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","who":"Manager","s":"OK. That's more than we had."},{"t":"1:34","band":"Understand the behaviour","w":"D","s":"Jake, is anything making you cross at home?","m":[1,4]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"No centre. Bored. New people."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"No centre, and new people. Thank you, Jake. That really helps.","m":[4,1]},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"Dana, tell me about the last time. Where was it, who was there, what happened just before?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"Tuesday, about ten. In the kitchen. A new agency worker told him there's no centre today. He threw his cup, then hit her arm."},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"And just after?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"We took him to his room and he calmed with his iPad. Both times were mornings, now I think about it."},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"What's changed in the last few months?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","who":"Manager","s":"His day centre shut in the summer. Half the team left. Lots of agency staff now."},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"Any pain, teeth, constipation, sleep problems? Is he low or worried?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Sleeps fine. Constipated sometimes. Dentist was in spring. Not low, just restless and bored."},{"t":"2:47","band":"The medicine","w":"D","s":"Why was risperidone started six years ago? Any voices or odd beliefs ever?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","who":"Manager","s":"For behaviour when he moved in. Never anything like that. Nobody's reviewed it."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"He's put on 14 kilos in two years. Has anyone checked his bloods, blood pressure or heart tracing?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"Not that I know. He's always hungry."},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","who":"Manager","s":"Look, with respect, my staff are getting hurt and I can't keep them. We need something now."},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"I hear that. Your team is frightened and short-staffed, and you're holding it together. That's tough. I want to help them and Jake.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:33","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"There's a national programme called STOMP: stopping over-medication of people with a learning disability or autism. Tablets like risperidone are often given for behaviour without an illness, and they cause weight gain, diabetes risk and sleepiness.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"A higher dose would likely make Jake sleepier and heavier, but it won't fix the reason: no centre and lots of new faces. So I won't increase it today. I also won't stop it suddenly; that can cause rebound agitation and odd movements.","m":[9,8]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","who":"Manager","s":"So you're doing nothing?"},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"Not nothing. We go after what's causing it, and the crisis plan protects staff meanwhile.","m":[9,3]},{"t":"4:20","band":"Decide together","w":"D","s":"Jake, your tablet. What's it for?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"Makes me calm. Makes me fat."},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"What would happen if you took less?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"Don't know."},{"t":"4:34","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Jake. He understands some of it but can't weigh it up today, so this is a best interests decision. Does anyone hold power of attorney or a deputyship for his health?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:49","w":"P","s":"No. His mum's involved. She visits every week."},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"Then we'll invite Jake and his mum to a best interests meeting in four to six weeks, with our pharmacist.","m":[10,14]},{"t":"5:03","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Meanwhile I'll ask the GP for his weight, waist, blood pressure, a sugar test called HbA1c, cholesterol, prolactin and an ECG. And I'm referring him to our positive behaviour support team, to work out his triggers and write a plan.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"Do we fill anything in?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Yes, a behaviour chart for four weeks: what happened before, what he did, what happened after. And I'll ask his care coordinator about a new day activity. That's probably the big one.","m":[4,11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","who":"Manager","s":"And when do we talk about the dose?"},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"At six weeks we review together. If things are settled, we plan a slow reduction, small steps every month or two, with you watching closely.","m":[9,14]},{"t":"5:57","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the plan back, so I know I've been clear?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"Crisis plan now. Chart for four weeks. GP bloods and ECG. Behaviour support referral. Ask about a day activity. Best interests meeting with his mum, review at six weeks. Dose stays at 2 mg."},{"t":"6:19","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Log every incident, and if anyone is badly hurt or in danger, call 999 and use your safeguarding process.","m":[3]},{"t":"6:29","w":"P","who":"Manager","s":"OK. I can sell that to the team."},{"t":"6:34","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Jake, your tablet stays the same for now, and we'll find you things to do. See you in six weeks.","m":[1,14]},{"t":"6:43","w":"P","who":"Jake","s":"Centre?"},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"We'll ask about a new one. Thanks, Jake. Bye for now.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Greets Jake first, speaks to him directly and adapts communication","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges the staff injuries and asks whether everyone is safe now","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees an interim crisis plan before discussing medicine","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Takes a functional history: setting, trigger and what happens after","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what has changed: staff, day service, routine","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for pain, constipation, sleep and low mood","d":"Content"},{"c":"Reviews the original indication, lack of review and weight gain","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains STOMP and side effects in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Declines the increase without stopping risperidone abruptly, and gives reasons","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Assesses capacity, checks LPA or deputy, and plans a best interests meeting","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Requests weight, BP, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin and ECG, and refers for PBS","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates staff frustration and fear","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with a 6-week review and best interests meeting date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Includes Jake, acknowledges injuries with a crisis plan first, takes a functional history, explains STOMP, declines the increase without abrupt stopping and sets monitoring, PBS and review.","borderline":"Declines the increase sensibly but gives no crisis plan, or misses metabolic checks or the best interests process.","fail":"Increases the dose to please staff, stops it abruptly, or talks only about Jake rather than to him."}},"c-8-4-2":{"setup":"Friday 15:00, home visit with the CLDT nurse: Leah, 19, severe intellectual disability and autism, has bitten her hand and thrown things most afternoons since leaving school in July. Her father asks why, and what goes on the charts. Explain and start a plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Paul Carter, 52, male, Leah's father (Mrs Julie Carter, her mother, also speaks)","open":"She'll stay as long as the music's on. So, why does she do it? And what do we write on these charts?","manner":"Paul is direct and practical, then worn out and self-blaming. Julie is quieter and observant. Both relax and engage once they feel they are not being judged and are shown how.","free":["Biting her hand and throwing things most afternoons for four months","She left school in July and has no day service","Mornings are usually fine"],"ifasked":["Episodes start about 3 pm in the living room and last about ten minutes","Loud TV or the neighbour's drill sometimes come just before","Afterwards they take her upstairs with ear defenders or a tablet and she calms","At school, afternoons were swimming and the sensory room","Heavy periods with cramps; last dentist two years ago","Paul has stopped work; no respite or short breaks","Yesterday at 3:10 pm: Paul on the phone, TV on, Leah pacing; bit her right hand twice and threw the remote"],"worries":["Why does she do it?","What if we don't know what came before?","Should we hold her hands to stop her?","What about us? We can't keep doing this."],"rules":["Paul becomes tearful and self-blaming when asked how they are coping; settles if reassured without blame","Julie links episodes to periods only if asked","Both lose interest if jargon is used without explanation","Agree readily to the chart once they have filled one entry together"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Leah. I'm Dr Mensah. I like your music. I'm here to talk with Mum and Dad. You can stay or go to your room.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"She'll stay as long as the music's on. So, why does she do it? And what do we write on these charts?"},{"t":"0:24","w":"D","s":"Good questions, and we'll get to both. I'm one of the doctors in the learning disability team. What do you know about the charts already, and what would help most today?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:39","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Nothing really. The nurse left them with us. We just want the afternoons to be bearable."},{"t":"0:47","band":"Their story","w":"D","s":"Tell me about a bad afternoon. When does it start, and where is she?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"About three o'clock. Living room usually. She bites the side of her hand and throws whatever's near. Ten minutes, sometimes more."},{"t":"1:06","w":"D","s":"What's happening just before?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Often nothing much. She's wandering. Sometimes the telly's loud, or the neighbour's drilling."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"And straight after, what do you do?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"We take her upstairs with her ear defenders and she calms. Or we give her the tablet to watch."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"And mornings?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Mornings she's fine. Breakfast, music, a long bath."},{"t":"1:37","w":"D","s":"What changed four months ago?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"She left school in July. No day service yet. At school, afternoons were swimming and the sensory room."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"So her afternoons went from busy and familiar to empty. That's a big change.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:58","band":"Pain and health","w":"D","s":"May I look at her hand? Some grazes, no redness or swelling. Any signs of toothache, ear pulling, constipation? How are her periods?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Heavy periods. She gets cramps. Not sure about teeth. Last dentist was two years ago."},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"Do the bad weeks line up with her period?","m":[5,3]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"I never thought of that. Maybe they do."},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"Honestly, we're on our knees. I've stopped work. We wonder if we've done something wrong."},{"t":"2:36","w":"D","s":"You haven't. You lost everything school gave you overnight. Anyone would be worn out. And you're already doing things that help, like the ear defenders.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Thank you. Nobody's said that."},{"t":"2:52","band":"Explain the chart","w":"D","s":"So, why she does it. Behaviour is usually Leah's way of telling us something: she's bored, it's too noisy, or something hurts. The chart helps us find out which. It has three parts: A, B and C.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"A is what happened just before. B is exactly what she did. C is what happened just after, including what you did. Add the time and the place.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"What if I don't know what came before?"},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"Write 'nothing obvious'. That's useful too. It doesn't need to be perfect.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Let's fill one in together, from yesterday. What time was it?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:42","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Ten past three."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"Where, and what was going on just before?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:49","w":"P","s":"Living room. I was on the phone, telly on, she was pacing."},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"That's A. Now B, exactly what she did.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:01","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Bit her right hand twice, threw the remote."},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"And C?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"I hung up and took her upstairs. She calmed with her headphones in about ten minutes."},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"That's a full entry. You're already good at this.","m":[7,9]},{"t":"4:22","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Please fill it in every time, within an hour while it's fresh, for two weeks, up to four if we need more. And a quick tick on the time-of-day sheet.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Should we hold her hands to stop the biting?"},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"Please don't. Holding her can make her more upset and someone may get hurt. Give her space, move things she might throw, keep it calm and quiet. No calming tablet either; we look for the cause first.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"OK. And what about us? We can't keep doing this."},{"t":"5:06","w":"D","s":"You're able to ask for a carer's assessment from the council. Our social worker will arrange it, and look at short breaks and a day service place. Her school plan may also carry on to 25 if she goes into education or training.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"That would be a lifeline."},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"I'll also ask the GP for a physical check, including ears, bowels and periods, and a dentist visit.","m":[5]},{"t":"5:38","band":"Safety net","w":"D","s":"Keep her hand clean and covered. If it gets red, swollen or oozy, or she's feverish, see the GP that day. If things ever feel unsafe, ring our duty line, or the crisis line out of hours.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:56","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me how you'll use the chart, so I know I've explained it clearly?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Every time, within the hour. What came before, what she did, what came after, time and place. Two weeks. Don't worry if it's messy."},{"t":"6:17","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Exactly. The nurse and I will come back in two weeks. We'll look at the pattern and write a simple traffic-light plan: green for what keeps her calm, amber for early signs, red for what to do safely.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Two weeks. OK."},{"t":"6:38","w":"D","s":"Any questions before we go?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"No, that's clear. Thank you."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Bye Leah. Thanks for letting us visit.","m":[1,14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Greets Leah first and includes her; introduces self to the parents","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what the parents already know and want from the visit","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes a functional history: when, where, what comes before and after","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what changed when she left school","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks her hand and screens for pain: teeth, ears, bowels, periods","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains ABC in plain words without jargon","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Fills in one real chart entry with the parents","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees chart duration of 2 to 4 weeks, filled within the hour","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates the parents' exhaustion without blame","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers a carer's assessment, short breaks and a day service link","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises against restraint and sedation; least restrictive first","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets hand infection signs and crisis contacts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with a 2-week review to build a green-amber-red plan","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Includes Leah, takes a functional history, explains ABC plainly with a real worked entry, checks pain, supports the carers and sets a 2-week review.","borderline":"Explains the chart clearly but misses pain causes or carer support, or gives no clear review date.","fail":"Uses jargon without explanation, blames the parents, or suggests restraint or sedation first."}},"c-g-01":{"setup":"Sunday 16:20, on call at a standalone mental health unit. Mrs Margaret Doyle, 61, had a panic attack and still has chest tightness. Take a focused history, examine her heart, and decide on ambulance, same-day medical review or reassurance.","role":{"who":"Mrs Margaret Doyle, 61, woman, informal inpatient with depression and panic disorder (Staff Nurse Jo Hill, the ward nurse, is present)","open":"Fourteenth of March, 1965. Are you the heart doctor?","manner":"Anxious, a little defensive, expecting to be dismissed. Settles when the doctor explains each step and takes the pain seriously; tearful when she feels finally believed.","free":["Central chest tightness, like a band, started about 40 minutes ago during a panic attack while sitting","Feels nobody believes her about her heart","Smokes 20 a day","Takes amlodipine for blood pressure and citalopram"],"ifasked":["Pain does not spread to arm, neck, jaw or back and is not worse on breathing","No nausea, sweating or faintness; felt shaky","Father had a heart attack at 58","No diabetes, known cholesterol problem or past clots","Has had the same tightness on the stairs at home for a month and told no one","Pain is easing but still present"],"worries":["Please don't just tell me it's nerves again.","If the tracing is normal, does that mean my heart is fine?","Do I need to go to hospital?"],"rules":["Discloses the pain on the stairs only if asked about effort or exertion","Becomes tearful if the doctor seems to label it panic early; settles if validated","Accepts the plan and staying on the ward if the doctor is honest about what is and is not excluded","Nurse gives the obs and ECG when asked"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mrs Doyle? I'm Dr Ade, the doctor on call this afternoon. Can I check your date of birth?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Fourteenth of March, 1965. Are you the heart doctor?"},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"I'm one of the psychiatry doctors, and a medical doctor first. The nurse told me about your chest. I'd like to ask a few questions and examine your heart. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"Yes. Please. Nobody believes me about my heart."},{"t":"0:28","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening, feeling you're not believed. I'm taking this seriously, and I'll tell you exactly what I find.","m":[8]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"(sighs) Thank you. It's still tight, right here."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Staff Nurse Hill will stay with us as a chaperone, if that's alright. Jo, did you manage the obs and the ECG?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:49","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Pulse 92, BP 138 over 84, sats 98% on air, temp 36.8, resp rate 18. NEWS2 is zero. Here's the ECG."},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Sinus rhythm at 90, no ST changes, and the QTc is 438, which is fine on her citalopram. Please keep it in the notes to compare.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:08","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Mrs Doyle, can you point with one finger to where it hurts? Is it there now?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"(points to the middle of her chest) Here. Like a band. It's eased a bit, but it's still there."},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"When did it start, and what were you doing?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"About forty minutes ago. I was sitting on my bed and the panic came. My heart was pounding. Then this tightness stayed."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"Does it spread to your arm, neck, jaw or back? Is it worse when you breathe in? Did you feel sick, sweaty or faint?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"No, it stays in the middle, and breathing doesn't change it. No sweating. Just shaky, like with the panic."},{"t":"1:53","w":"D","s":"Any heart problems, diabetes, high cholesterol, or a clot in your leg or lung? Anyone in the family with a heart attack before sixty?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"Only blood pressure, that's the amlodipine. My dad had a heart attack at fifty-eight. And I smoke twenty a day."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"Has your chest ever felt like this when you've been walking or on the stairs?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"(pause) Actually, yes. Going up the stairs at home, this last month. I didn't tell anyone. I thought they'd say it was nerves."},{"t":"2:26","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That's really important, and I'm glad you did.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:32","band":"Examine","w":"D","s":"I'll start with your hands. I'll feel how warm they are and press on a nail for a few seconds.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Warm hands, capillary refill under 2 seconds, tar staining on the right fingers, no clubbing.)"},{"t":"2:47","w":"D","s":"Now your pulse at both wrists. Just let your arms relax.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:52","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Radial pulse 92, regular, no delay between the two sides.)"},{"t":"2:57","w":"D","s":"I'll check your blood pressure in this arm, then the other. It squeezes tightly for a moment.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"(Finding: BP 138/84 right arm, 136/82 left arm.)"},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"Now I'll look at your eyes, then your neck. Turn your head a little to the left and rest back on the pillow.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"(Finding: No pallor. JVP not raised. Carotid pulses normal.)"},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"I'd like to feel your chest and listen to your heart; Jo will help keep you covered. Breathe normally. Now roll a little onto your left side. Now sit forward, breathe right out and hold it.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Apex in the 5th intercostal space, mid-clavicular line. Heart sounds I and II, no murmur, no rub.)"},{"t":"3:43","w":"D","s":"Deep breaths in and out through your mouth while I listen to the bottom of your lungs.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Chest clear, no crackles at the bases.)"},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"Last bit: your ankles and both calves. I'll press gently. Tell me if anything is sore.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"No, that's fine. My feet are just cold."},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"(Finding: No ankle or sacral oedema. Both calves soft, non-tender and the same size.)"},{"t":"4:10","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Your tracing, pulse, blood pressure and heart sounds are normal. The blood pressure is the same in both arms, so a tear in the main artery is very unlikely. Your oxygen and calves are normal, so a clot on the lung is unlikely too.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"So it's panic. Like everyone says."},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"Not yet. A normal tracing doesn't rule out a heart attack, and the pain on the stairs worries me. I'm not calling it panic until we've checked properly.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I've been so scared it's my heart and nobody would look."},{"t":"4:48","w":"D","s":"Of course you have. Your dad, the smoking, now this pain. Anyone would be frightened, and you were right to speak up.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:56","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Here's the plan. I'm taking a blood test called troponin, which shows heart strain, and it'll be repeated in a few hours. I'm also asking the medical team to see you today.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"Do I need to go to hospital?"},{"t":"5:13","w":"D","s":"Not right now, because your tracing and obs are normal and the pain is easing. If the pain comes back strongly or the tracing changes, we'd call an ambulance and give you an aspirin, 300 mg, to chew.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:27","w":"P","s":"What do I do if it comes back?"},{"t":"5:31","w":"D","s":"Tell a nurse straight away, day or night. They'll do another tracing and call me. Jo, four-hourly obs please. Repeat the ECG and call me if pain returns or NEWS2 is five or more; with ECG changes, dial 999.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Will do. Should I hold her PRN lorazepam?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Yes, not for chest pain, so we don't mask anything. I'll add cholesterol and sugar to the bloods, and ask the medical team about a heart clinic for the pain on the stairs.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"Mrs Doyle, can you tell me in your words what happens next, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:11","w":"P","s":"My heart looks normal so far, but you're doing a blood test, a medical doctor's coming, and I tell the nurse if it comes back."},{"t":"6:21","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. And once your heart is cleared, we can look at the panic together, because it causes real chest sensations.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"I'd like that. And I suppose I should think about the cigarettes."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"That would be great. I'll write you up for nicotine patches today. Any other questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you for actually listening."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You're welcome. I'll come back when the troponin is back, and I'm writing everything in your notes now.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks identity, explains purpose, gains consent and offers a chaperone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Confirms full obs with NEWS2 and reads the 12-lead ECG aloud, including QTc","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes a focused pain history: site, onset, character, radiation, associated symptoms and effort","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about cardiac risks: smoking, diabetes, cholesterol, clots and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Examines hands, pulse and BP in both arms, explaining each step","d":"Content"},{"c":"Examines JVP, apex, heart sounds sitting forward and lung bases","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks ankles, sacrum and both calves for oedema and DVT","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her fear of not being believed and her distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"States ACS, dissection and PE likelihood with supporting findings, without calling it panic yet","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Arranges troponin and same-day medical review; states ambulance and aspirin 300 mg triggers","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding by asking her to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets: report recurrence at once, repeat ECG, obs frequency and 999 triggers","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closes with questions, smoking support and when the doctor will return","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Uses obs and ECG, examines in a clear order including both arms and calves, keeps ACS open, arranges troponin and medical review, and tells her honestly what was found.","borderline":"Safe plan, but the examination misses a key part (both-arm BP, JVP or calves) or the explanation is rushed.","fail":"Labels it panic or gives PRN lorazepam before excluding cardiac causes, skips the ECG or troponin, or examines in silence."}},"c-g-02":{"setup":"Wednesday 18:10, on call, male acute ward. Daniel Mensah, 36, three weeks into clozapine, has a fever and green sputum; NEWS2 is 5. Examine his chest, grade severity, and agree bloods, antibiotic, tonight's clozapine and transfer triggers.","role":{"who":"Mr Daniel Mensah, 36, man, inpatient with treatment-resistant schizophrenia on clozapine 200 mg a day (Sister Grant, nurse in charge, is present)","open":"Third of June, ninety. I feel rough, doc.","manner":"Tired, feverish and cooperative but weary of blood tests. Becomes anxious and quiet when he thinks clozapine may be stopped; settles when reassured it will likely continue.","free":["Cough with green sputum since this morning, no blood","Feels rough and hot","Clozapine is the first medicine that has worked for him"],"ifasked":["Sharp right-sided low chest pain on deep breathing","No sore throat or mouth ulcers","Heart feels fast; no central chest pain; can lie flat","Pillow soaked with saliva most mornings; coughed on tea yesterday","Stopped smoking on admission a week ago"],"worries":["Is it the clozapine? Will you take me off it?","Why more blood? I had some on Monday.","Why a heart tracing for a cough?","Can't I just have paracetamol and sleep it off?"],"rules":["Mentions stopping smoking only if asked about smoking","Agrees to bloods once told why the neutrophil count matters tonight","Nurse in charge reads out the ECG and agrees the plan when asked directly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, are you Daniel Mensah? I'm Dr Ade, the doctor on call. Can you tell me your date of birth?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"Third of June, ninety. I feel rough, doc."},{"t":"0:13","w":"D","s":"Sorry you feel rough. The nurse says you've a temperature and a cough. I'd like to ask some questions and examine your chest. Sister Grant, the nurse in charge, will stay with us. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"Yeah, fine. Whatever helps."},{"t":"0:32","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"When did the cough start, and what are you coughing up?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"This morning. Green stuff. No blood."},{"t":"0:41","w":"D","s":"Any pain when you breathe in? Any sore throat or ulcers in your mouth?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"Sharp pain here, on the right, low down, when I take a big breath. Throat's fine."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Any pain in the middle of your chest, a racing heart, or breathlessness lying flat?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"My heart's going fast, but I think that's the fever. I can lie flat okay."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"Have you been dribbling more at night, or choked on food or drink? And are you still smoking?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"The pillow's soaked most mornings, and I coughed on my tea yesterday. I stopped smoking when I came in. Haven't had one for a week."},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"Well done. That's useful, because stopping smoking pushes clozapine levels up. I'll check your level tonight.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"Is it the clozapine? Will you take me off it? It's the first thing that's worked."},{"t":"1:41","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much it's helping, and I don't want to lose that. Most likely this is a chest infection. Let me examine you, then we'll decide together.","m":[11]},{"t":"1:53","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Okay. I just don't want to go back to how I was."},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"That makes complete sense. We'll do everything we can to keep you on it.","m":[11]},{"t":"2:06","band":"Examine","w":"D","s":"I'll start with your hands, then watch your breathing for a bit. Sit back at an angle for me.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Warm hands, no clubbing, cyanosis or flap. Pulse 108, regular. Respiratory rate 22.)"},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Stick your tongue out. Now I'll feel your neck for glands and check your windpipe is in the middle. Slight pressure here.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"That's a bit uncomfortable."},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"(Finding: No central cyanosis. No lymph nodes. Trachea central.)"},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"I'll put my hands on your chest. Big breath in. Now I'll tap down your chest, side to side.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Expansion reduced at the right lower chest. Dull to percussion at the right base.)"},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"Say \"ninety-nine\" each time I move my hand. Then breathe in and out through your mouth while I listen.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"Ninety-nine. Ninety-nine. Ninety-nine."},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Bronchial breathing and coarse crackles at the right base, with increased vocal resonance. Left side clear.)"},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"Lean forward and hug yourself for me. I'll do the same on your back.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"(coughs) Sorry. It hurts on the right when I cough."},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Back: dull, bronchial breathing and crackles in the right lower zone. Heart sounds normal, no rub. No ankle swelling.)"},{"t":"3:33","band":"Explain and grade","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Daniel. The bottom part of your right lung sounds infected. That's pneumonia in the right lower lobe.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"Pneumonia? Is that serious?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Here's how I'm judging it. Your warning score, NEWS2, is five, so you need a doctor tonight. Your pneumonia score, CRB-65, is zero: not confused, breathing under 30, blood pressure fine, under 65. So it's low severity, and we can treat it here for now.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"Okay. So why does the nurse want more blood? I had some on Monday."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"I know, you've had lots. This one's different. Clozapine can lower your infection-fighting cells, called neutrophils. With a fever I must check them tonight, because if they're low, an infection can get serious fast.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:24","w":"D","s":"I'm also checking for inflammation, a heart blood test called troponin, and your clozapine level. And a heart tracing now.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"Why my heart? It's my chest that's bad."},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"Good question. In the first weeks, clozapine can rarely inflame the heart, and it can look like flu. The tracing and the blood test rule that out.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","who":"Nurse in charge","s":"ECG's done. Sinus rhythm 106, QTc 432."},{"t":"4:52","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Sinus tachycardia, no ST changes, normal QTc. That fits the fever.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:58","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Sister Grant, can we agree tonight's clozapine? I'd like to hold tonight's dose until the FBC and troponin are back, and ask the pharmacist about restarting in the morning.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","who":"Nurse in charge","s":"Fine. I'll hold it and note it on the chart. Which antibiotic?"},{"t":"5:16","w":"D","s":"Amoxicillin 500 mg three times a day, first dose once we know the neutrophils. Not clarithromycin, because it can raise clozapine levels.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Can't I just have paracetamol and sleep it off?"},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"You can have paracetamol for the fever, one gram now. But your chest is infected, so you need the antibiotic too. Then sleep, and the nurses will check on you.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"Sister, hourly obs overnight please. If NEWS2 reaches seven, sats fall below 92%, he's confused, or the neutrophils come back low, he goes to A&E with an escort, and call me.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","who":"Nurse in charge","s":"Got it. I'll brief the night team."},{"t":"5:59","w":"D","s":"Daniel, can you tell me what we're doing tonight, so I know I've explained it clearly?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:06","w":"P","s":"Blood tests for my infection cells and my heart, no clozapine tonight, an antibiotic, and they keep checking me."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Spot on. If your breathing gets worse, you get chest pain, or a sore throat or mouth ulcers, tell the nurse straight away.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"And I'll get the clozapine back?"},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"Most likely, yes. The pharmacist will advise on restarting it safely. I know how much it matters to you.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:37","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Any other questions?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks, doc. Sorry for the fuss."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Not a fuss at all. I'll check the results around nine and come back to tell you. I'm writing it all in your notes now.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks identity, explains purpose, gains consent with chaperone present","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about cough, sputum, pleuritic pain, sore throat and mouth ulcers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about chest pain, palpitations and orthopnoea to screen for myocarditis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about hypersalivation, choking and smoking change (aspiration and toxicity)","d":"Content"},{"c":"Examines hands, respiratory rate, face, neck and trachea, explaining each step","d":"Content"},{"c":"Examines chest front and back: expansion, percussion, vocal resonance, auscultation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names right lower lobe pneumonia from the findings","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States NEWS2 and CRB-65 scores and what they mean","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Requests FBC with neutrophils, CRP, troponin, clozapine level and ECG, and interprets the ECG","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees to hold tonight's clozapine with the nurse in charge and chooses amoxicillin, avoiding clarithromycin","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Responds with empathy to his fear of losing clozapine","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains why tonight's blood test matters and checks his understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Sets hourly obs and transfer triggers, and safety-nets on new symptoms","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closes with questions and when results will be fed back","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Systematic front-and-back chest examination, scores severity, links fever on clozapine to neutropenia, myocarditis and toxicity, and agrees the dose, antibiotic and transfer triggers.","borderline":"Treats the pneumonia safely but misses one clozapine danger, the back of the chest, or a clear transfer trigger.","fail":"Gives clarithromycin with clozapine unchanged, skips the FBC, or stops clozapine permanently without reason."}},"c-g-03":{"setup":"Friday 11:30, rehabilitation ward. Gary Pickering, 47, on clozapine 450 mg and hyoscine, has not opened his bowels for 12 days and vomited twice. Examine his abdomen and decide on surgical transfer, clozapine and a plan for his return.","role":{"who":"Mr Gary Pickering, 47, man, rehabilitation inpatient on clozapine for six years (Nurse Patel is chaperone)","open":"Ninth of August, seventy-nine.","manner":"Uncomfortable and dismissive at first, keen to avoid hospital. Becomes scared about surgery and resistant about A&E waits; agrees when reassured he will not go alone.","free":["No bowel movement for about 12 days","Vomited twice this morning","Tummy feels swollen and sore"],"ifasked":["No wind since Wednesday","Vomit was greenish, not foul-smelling","Pain comes in waves, all over","No watery leakage; no past abdominal operations","Stopped his laxatives because they made him rush to the toilet","Drinks six cans of strong lager most days on leave, more at weekends"],"worries":["Are they going to cut me open?","I waited 12 hours last time. I'm not going.","Won't I go downhill without the clozapine?","Why does clozapine do this?"],"rules":["Admits stopping laxatives only if asked directly","Refuses A&E at first; agrees if the doctor phones ahead and a nurse goes with him","Consents to rectal examination if it is explained and he is told he can say no"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Gary? I'm Dr Ade, one of the ward doctors. Can I check your date of birth?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Ninth of August, seventy-nine."},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"Thanks. The nurse says your bowels haven't moved for a while and you've been sick. I'd like to ask some questions and examine your tummy, with Nurse Patel here as chaperone. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"Yeah. It's just constipation. I always get it. Give me something and I'll be fine."},{"t":"0:34","w":"D","s":"You've been very patient with it. Let me check properly first, because on clozapine it can be more than ordinary constipation.","m":[7]},{"t":"0:43","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"When did you last open your bowels? Are you passing wind? And what did you bring up this morning?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"About twelve days. No wind since Wednesday, I think. The sick was greenish, twice. Didn't smell bad."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Where's the pain? Is it constant, or does it come in waves?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"All over. Comes in waves, then eases off."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Any watery leakage from your back passage? Ever had an operation on your tummy?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"No leaking. Never had an operation."},{"t":"1:21","w":"D","s":"Are you taking the laxatives the nurses give you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"(pause) I stopped them. They made me run to the toilet."},{"t":"1:31","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being honest. That's common, and it's not your fault. We'll find something that suits you better.","m":[7]},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"And how much do you drink when you're on leave? Any yellow eyes or swollen legs?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"Six cans of the strong stuff most days I'm out. More at weekends. Eyes and legs are fine."},{"t":"1:55","band":"Examine","w":"D","s":"I'll lie you flat with one pillow and uncover your tummy, keeping your lower half covered. First your hands. Arms out, and bend your wrists back like stopping traffic.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"(Finding: No flapping tremor. No palmar erythema, Dupuytren's, leukonychia or clubbing.)"},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"Look up for me. I'll check your eyes and chest.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"(Finding: No jaundice or pallor. No spider naevi or gynaecomastia. Dry tongue.)"},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Now your tummy. Tell me if anything hurts, and I'll watch your face. Gentle first, then a bit deeper.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:33","w":"P","s":"Ow. It's sore all over, but not sharp."},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Distended, no scars. Mild generalised tenderness, no guarding or rebound. No masses. Liver and spleen not palpable.)"},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"Now I'll tap on your tummy, then listen for a while.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Tympanic throughout, no shifting dullness. High-pitched tinkling bowel sounds.)"},{"t":"2:57","w":"D","s":"I need to check your groins for a hernia. I'll uncover you briefly. Give a cough for me.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"(coughs) Nothing there, is there?"},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Hernial orifices clear.)"},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"The last part would be a rectal examination, to feel for hard stool low down. It's quick, with Nurse Patel here, and you can say no.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"If it helps. Go on, then."},{"t":"3:29","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Rectal examination with chaperone: empty rectum, no blood.)"},{"t":"3:34","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Gary. Your tummy is swollen and hollow when I tap, the bowel sounds are high and tinkly, you've been sick, you're not passing wind and the back passage is empty. That points to a blockage or a gut that's stopped moving, which clozapine can cause. Your liver signs are clear.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Are they going to cut me open?"},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"That's a scary thought. Most people with this get better with rest for the bowel, a drip and close watching. The surgeons decide after a scan.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:11","w":"D","s":"But a surgeon needs to see you today. I'll phone the surgical team, send a letter so you don't have to repeat everything, and a nurse will go with you to A&E.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Last time I waited twelve hours there. I'm not going."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"That sounds miserable, and I'm sorry. This time I'm phoning ahead and you won't be on your own. Waiting here could make this much more dangerous.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"(sighs) Alright. If someone's with me."},{"t":"4:46","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Until the surgeons see you: nothing to eat or drink, not even tea, and no laxatives or enemas, because they could harm a blocked bowel. I'm also holding your clozapine and the hyoscine for drooling today, because both slow the gut.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:03","w":"P","s":"Won't I go downhill without the clozapine?"},{"t":"5:08","w":"D","s":"We'll restart it with the pharmacist once your bowel is safe. If it's off more than two days, we build it up slowly, and the ward will watch your mood.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:21","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Shall I take bloods before he goes?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Yes please: FBC, U&E, liver tests, CRP, amylase, a venous gas for lactate and a clozapine level. I'll tell his consultant.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:34","w":"D","s":"Gary, when you're back, you'll have a stool softener twice a day and a laxative at night, the hyoscine stops for good, and we'll ask about your bowels every week.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","s":"As long as it doesn't send me running."},{"t":"5:52","w":"D","s":"We'll adjust it until it's comfortable. And we'll talk about cutting down the drink, because that affects your liver and gut.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:01","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what's happening now?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:07","w":"P","s":"No food, no laxatives, no clozapine, and I'm going to the hospital today with a nurse to see the surgeons."},{"t":"6:16","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. If the pain becomes sharp or constant, or you're sick again before you leave, tell Nurse Patel straight away. Any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:26","w":"P","s":"Why does clozapine do this?"},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"It slows the muscles in the gut wall. It's common and can be serious, which is why we'll check with you every week.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"Fair enough. Thanks, doc."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You're welcome. I'm phoning the surgical registrar now, and I'll bring the letter before you go.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks identity, gains consent and has a chaperone present","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about bowels, flatus, vomit, pain pattern, overflow and previous surgery","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks non-judgementally about laxative use and alcohol","d":"Content"},{"c":"Looks for liver disease in hands, eyes and chest, including flapping tremor","d":"Content"},{"c":"Palpates watching his face, percusses and listens for bowel sounds","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks hernial orifices and offers rectal examination with chaperone","d":"Content"},{"c":"Responds with empathy to his denial, fear and reluctance","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names obstruction or ileus and lists the signs that support it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Stops food, drink, laxatives and enemas, and holds clozapine and hyoscine","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges same-day surgical review with a phone call, written summary, bloods and escort","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Describes the bowel prevention plan for his return","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding and explains why clozapine slows the gut","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets on worsening pain or vomiting and closes with next steps","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Treats it as a possible emergency, completes a full abdominal examination including hernias, liver signs and a rectal offer, stops oral intake and laxatives, holds clozapine and hyoscine, and sends him to surgeons today with an escort.","borderline":"Correct transfer, but misses liver signs or hernias, or gives no plan for his return.","fail":"Gives an enema or stimulant laxative, continues clozapine, or delays surgical review to tomorrow."}},"c-g-04":{"setup":"Tuesday 10:00, female acute ward. Susan Clarke, 52, on lithium for nine years, says her neck looks fatter and she feels cold and slow. Take a brief thyroid history, examine her thyroid, and plan tests, treatment and monitoring.","role":{"who":"Mrs Susan Clarke, 52, woman, inpatient with bipolar disorder on lithium carbonate 800 mg at night (Nurse Ahmed is chaperone)","open":"Second of May, 1974.","manner":"Polite and low in energy. Becomes annoyed that monitoring lapsed and frightened about cancer; settles with an apology and honest findings. Tearful about feeling useless.","free":["Neck looks fatter in photos for about six months","Gained 4 kg, feels cold and slow","Lithium has kept her well for years"],"ifasked":["No neck pain, voice change, swallowing or breathing problems","Dry skin; feels the cold more than others","Low mood feels heavier and slower than her usual depressions","Aunt had a lump in her neck","Hands have always shaken a little"],"worries":["Nobody told me lithium could do this. Why wasn't it checked?","Is it cancer?","Do I have to stop lithium?","Can I eat seaweed or take a supplement instead?"],"rules":["Raises cancer fear after the history; calms when the findings are explained","Becomes annoyed about the overdue test; settles if the doctor apologises plainly","Accepts treatment if told lithium can continue"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Good morning, Mrs Clarke. I'm Dr Ade, from the ward team. Can I check your date of birth?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Second of May, 1974."},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"Thanks. On the ward round you said your neck looks fatter. I'd like to ask about it and examine your neck, with Nurse Ahmed as chaperone. Is that alright?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Yes, fine. It's probably nothing."},{"t":"0:29","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"When did you first notice it? Is it getting bigger?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"About six months ago, in photos. My daughter said it too. I think it's grown a bit."},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"Any neck pain, change in your voice, or trouble swallowing or breathing?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:49","w":"P","s":"No pain. Voice is the same. Swallowing's fine."},{"t":"0:53","w":"D","s":"Do you feel the cold more than others? Any change in your skin, hair or bowels?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"I'm always cold now. Skin's dry, I've put on four kilos, and I'm slow. Everything's slow."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"Does this low mood feel different from your usual depressions?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"Heavier. Slower. Like wading through mud."},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"That's helpful. I've checked your record: your last thyroid test was fourteen months ago. On lithium it should be every six months.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"Nobody told me lithium could do this. Why wasn't it checked?"},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"You're right to be annoyed. The test was overdue, and I'm sorry. I'll send it today and make sure the dates are written down for you.","m":[7]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"(calmer) Thank you. My aunt had a lump in her neck. I keep thinking it's cancer."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"I can understand that worry, especially after your aunt. Let me examine you, and I'll tell you honestly what I find.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:00","band":"Examine","w":"D","s":"First I'll just look, with your chin up a little.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Smooth, symmetrical swelling in the lower front of the neck. No scars or redness.)"},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"Take a sip of water, hold it in your mouth, and swallow.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"(swallows) Did it move?"},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"It rose as you swallowed. Now stick your tongue out for me.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"(Finding: The swelling rises with swallowing but not with tongue protrusion.)"},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"I'll stand behind you and feel your neck with my fingertips. Chin down slightly. Tell me if anything's tender.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"Not tender. It feels odd, having someone behind you."},{"t":"2:47","w":"D","s":"Sorry, nearly done. One more swallow. Now I'm feeling for glands, and above your collarbones.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Diffuse, smooth, soft goitre in both lobes. No nodules, not tender. No cervical or supraclavicular nodes.)"},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"I'll tap on your breastbone, then listen over your neck with the stethoscope.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"(Finding: No retrosternal dullness. No bruit.)"},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"Now your hands. Hold them out straight, fingers apart, and I'll feel your pulse.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:19","w":"P","s":"They always shake a bit. That's the lithium, isn't it?"},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Fine postural tremor. Cool, dry hands. Pulse 58, regular.)"},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"It can be, yes. Now follow my finger with your eyes, without moving your head.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"(Finding: No lid retraction, lid lag or eye protrusion. Eye movements full.)"},{"t":"3:41","w":"D","s":"Last, your ankle reflexes. Kneel on the chair for me, and I'll tap the back of each heel.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Ankle jerks present but slow to relax on both sides.)"},{"t":"3:55","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you. The swelling is your thyroid. It's smooth and even, with no lumps or glands. With your slow pulse, dry skin, slow reflexes and feeling cold, I think your thyroid is underactive. Lithium commonly causes that.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:11","w":"P","s":"So not cancer?"},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"It doesn't look like it. Cancer is more often a single hard lump. The blood test will confirm what's going on.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"Do I have to stop lithium? It's kept me out of hospital for years."},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"No. An underactive thyroid is treated with a daily tablet, levothyroxine, and you carry on with lithium. Stopping lithium would risk a relapse.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I've felt so useless. I thought I was just getting worse."},{"t":"4:47","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. Part of that heaviness may be your thyroid, which is treatable. It's not you failing.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:55","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Today I'm sending TSH and free T4 for the thyroid, calcium, and U&E and eGFR for your kidneys. Lithium can raise calcium too. And a lithium level now, which is about twelve hours after last night's dose.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:11","w":"P","s":"And then?"},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"If it's underactive, you'll start levothyroxine each morning before breakfast. We recheck TSH every three months until it's steady, so first in early February, then yearly.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:27","w":"D","s":"Your lithium level, kidneys, thyroid and calcium will then be checked every six months, next in May. I'll write the dates on a lithium record for you and in the letter to your GP.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"Couldn't I just eat seaweed or take a supplement instead?"},{"t":"5:46","w":"D","s":"I can see why you'd want something natural. Extra iodine won't fix this and can make it worse. The tablet replaces exactly what your thyroid isn't making.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:58","w":"D","s":"What will you take away from today, so I know I've been clear?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"My thyroid's probably underactive because of the lithium. I keep the lithium, maybe start a thyroid tablet, and have bloods every three and six months."},{"t":"6:16","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's it exactly. If you notice a hard lump, hoarseness, trouble swallowing, or fast growth, tell us and we'd scan it. And with lithium, sickness, diarrhoea, a coarse shake or unsteadiness means a level that day.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:32","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thank you. I feel a bit better knowing."},{"t":"6:36","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. Anything else you'd like to ask? I'll come and see you tomorrow when the results are back.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No, that's everything."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks identity, gains consent and offers a chaperone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about onset and growth of swelling, pain, voice, swallowing and breathing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about hypothyroid symptoms and checks the date of the last thyroid test","d":"Content"},{"c":"Inspects, then watches the swelling with swallowing and tongue protrusion","d":"Content"},{"c":"Palpates from behind with a swallow, feels nodes, percusses the sternum and listens for a bruit","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks pulse, tremor, eye signs and ankle reflexes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Responds with empathy to her anger, fear of cancer and distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Requests TSH, free T4, calcium, U&E, eGFR and a 12-hour lithium level","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains likely lithium-induced hypothyroidism and that lithium continues","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives a monitoring plan with dates for TSH and lithium bloods, shared with the GP","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding by asking her to say it back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets on lump red flags and lithium toxicity signs","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closes with questions and when results will be discussed","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Examines the thyroid in the right order with systemic signs, sends the right bloods, explains lithium-induced hypothyroidism and keeps lithium, with dated monitoring.","borderline":"Sound examination but misses retrosternal percussion, bruit or reflexes, or gives monitoring without dates.","fail":"Stops lithium, leaves it to the GP after discharge, or examines without swallow and palpation from behind."}},"c-g-05":{"setup":"Monday 14:00, acute medical ward, liaison referral. Laura Bennett, 31, lost vision in her left eye over a morning; the referral says 'likely functional'. Check her eyes fully, decide if this is an eye or brain emergency, then screen mood and risk.","role":{"who":"Ms Laura Bennett, 31, woman, medical inpatient with left visual loss","open":"They think I'm making it up, don't they? Why else would they send a psychiatrist?","manner":"Guarded and hurt at first, then cooperative once examined properly. Frightened about a tumour; tearful about her losses; relieved when told the signs are real.","free":["Left eye went blurry then dim over a morning two days ago","Feels nobody believes her","Lost her job and her relationship recently"],"ifasked":["The loss is mostly in the centre; edges are clearer","Aches when moving the left eye","Red looks washed out in the left eye","No flashes, floaters or headache","Legs went numb for a fortnight a few years ago and recovered","Fleeting thoughts of 'what's the point'; no plans; her sister and her cat keep her going"],"worries":["So it is all in my head?","Is it a brain tumour?","Will my sight come back?"],"rules":["Mentions the past numb legs only if asked about previous neurological symptoms","Becomes tearful when asked about her mood; answers risk questions honestly if asked kindly","Settles if the doctor states the findings are physical"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Laura? I'm Dr Ade from the liaison psychiatry team. Is it okay if I sit down?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"They think I'm making it up, don't they? Why else would they send a psychiatrist?"},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"That must feel awful. I'm not here because anyone thinks you're pretending. I'd like to check your eyes carefully myself and tell you honestly what I find. Then we can talk about how you're doing. Is that okay?","m":[8]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"(pause) Fine. At least someone's looking."},{"t":"0:36","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Tell me exactly what happened to your sight.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Two days ago I woke up and my left eye was blurry. Over the morning it went dim, like a dirty window."},{"t":"0:51","w":"D","s":"Is it blurred everywhere, or a dark patch or a curtain over part of it?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"Mostly the middle. I can see round the edges."},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"Does it hurt to move that eye? Are colours washed out, especially red? Any flashing lights, floaters or headache?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"It aches when I look sideways. Red looks sort of pink; I thought I was imagining it. No flashes or headache."},{"t":"1:21","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had numbness, weakness, tingling or eye problems before, even if they went away?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"A few years ago my legs went numb for about a fortnight. It went, so I never told anyone."},{"t":"1:37","w":"D","s":"Thank you. That's important, and I'll pass it on.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:42","band":"Examine","w":"D","s":"Let's check your sight. Cover your right eye and read the lowest line you can. Then try again through this card with tiny holes.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:53","w":"P","s":"Only the big letters at the top. The holes don't help."},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"Now look at this red pen top with each eye in turn. Is it the same red?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"With the left it's paler. Washed out."},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Left acuity 6/36, no better with pinhole; right 6/6. Red desaturation in the left eye.)"},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"Now your pupils. Look at the far wall. I'll shine a light in one eye, then swing it to the other, back and forth.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Both pupils react directly and consensually. On the swinging light test the left pupil dilates when the light reaches it: left RAPD.)"},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Cover your right eye and look at my nose. Say when you see my finger come in. Is any part of my face missing?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"Your nose is fuzzy. Like a smudge in the middle."},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Central scotoma in the left eye, peripheral field full. Right field normal.)"},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"Follow my finger in an H shape. Any pain or double vision?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"Ouch, it aches on the left when I look across. No double."},{"t":"3:14","w":"D","s":"Last, I'll look at the back of each eye with a light. I'll come quite close. Keep looking at that spot on the wall.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Fundi: discs pink with sharp margins in both eyes. No haemorrhages or exudates. Maculae normal.)"},{"t":"3:33","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Laura. I've found real physical signs. Your left eye sees less, red looks washed out, and your left pupil reacts in a way called an RAPD. Nobody can produce that on purpose. It means the nerve behind that eye isn't working properly.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"So I'm not making it up?"},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"No. With the pain on moving the eye, this looks most like inflammation of the eye nerve, called optic neuritis. The back of the eye often looks normal with it.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Is it a brain tumour?"},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"I can hear how scared you are. What I've found points to inflammation, which often improves over weeks. A scan will help us be sure.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"I'll recommend that you see the eye doctors and neurologists today, and have an MRI of your brain and eye sockets. I'll tell them about your legs too.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"Today? Okay. Will my sight come back?"},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"Many people recover much of their sight, but the specialists will tell you more. Any treatment is their decision.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:51","band":"Mood and risk","w":"D","s":"How have you been in yourself? The referral mentions your job and a breakup.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"(tearful) First my job, then him, now this. I can't cope with any more."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"That's a huge amount at once. It makes sense you feel overwhelmed.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:11","w":"D","s":"How low has your mood been? Have you had felt that life isn't worth living?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I've thought, what's the point. Only for a second. I'd never do anything."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Have you made any plans, or thought about how?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"5:34","w":"D","s":"What keeps you going?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:38","w":"P","s":"My sister. She's coming tonight. And my cat. My neighbour's feeding her."},{"t":"5:44","w":"D","s":"Those matter. If those thoughts get stronger, tell the nurses, and after you go home you can call NHS 111 and choose option 2.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:55","band":"Safety and close","w":"D","s":"One more thing. Please don't drive until the specialists have checked your sight. I'll write that in your notes.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:04","w":"P","s":"I wasn't planning to. I can barely see."},{"t":"6:09","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what you understand is happening next?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:14","w":"P","s":"Eye doctors and a neurologist today, an MRI, and no driving. And it's probably my eye nerve, not stress."},{"t":"6:23","w":"D","s":"That's right. The stress is real and we'll help with it, but it hasn't caused this.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel like someone finally believes me."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. I'll speak to your medical registrar now and come back this week to see how you're getting on. Any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. Just, thanks."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and gains consent, addressing why psychiatry is seeing her","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes a visual history: onset, pattern, pain on eye movement, colour and flashes or floaters","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past neurological symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks acuity with pinhole and colour vision in each eye","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks pupils with the swinging light test, then fields and eye movements","d":"Content"},{"c":"Examines both fundi and describes the discs","d":"Content"},{"c":"States that an RAPD is present and that it means optic nerve disease, not functional loss","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her anger, fear and distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Recommends same-day ophthalmology and neurology review and MRI brain and orbits","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Screens mood and suicide risk and asks what keeps her going","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Advises her not to drive until her vision is assessed","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks her understanding by asking her to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives a crisis contact, plans follow-up and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Examines both eyes fully, names the RAPD as organic, recommends same-day specialist review and MRI, screens mood and risk separately, and advises no driving.","borderline":"Finds the signs but gives a vague recommendation, or forgets driving advice or risk.","fail":"Accepts the functional label without checking pupils and fundi, or lets the life stresses drive the diagnosis."}},"c-g-06":{"setup":"Thursday 15:00, male acute ward. Kieran Walsh, 27, admitted with depression, has three weeks of tingling feet and fingers and stumbles in the dark. Screen red flags, examine his limbs fully, name the pattern, and plan bloods, treatment and referral.","role":{"who":"Mr Kieran Walsh, 27, man, inpatient with depression (Nurse Kowalski is present)","open":"Sure. They reckon it's my anxiety.","manner":"Easy-going and a bit dismissive about the gas, then frightened by the Romberg test and the idea of a wheelchair. Opens up once he feels not judged.","free":["Tingling started in the feet three weeks ago, then the fingertips","Keeps stumbling, worse in the dark","Staff think it's anxiety"],"ifasked":["Bounces off the walls at night; nearly fell in the shower","No back pain, bladder or bowel change, or saddle numbness","Used large nitrous oxide canisters most weekends for a year","Every day by the end; last use the night before admission; it helped his mood","Eats little: toast, energy drinks, pizza"],"worries":["Am I going to end up in a wheelchair?","Why do you want me to close my eyes and stand still?","I hate needles. Can't I take a tablet?"],"rules":["Says 'it's just laughing gas' at first; gives the true amount if not judged","Sways and nearly falls on closing his eyes","Agrees to injections once told why they work better"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, Kieran? I'm Dr Ade, one of the ward doctors. The nurse says you've had tingling and some stumbling. Can we talk about it, then I'll check the nerves in your arms and legs?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Sure. They reckon it's my anxiety."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Let's find out properly. Nurse Kowalski will be with us, and I'll explain each step as I go.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"Where's the tingling, and where did it start?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"My feet first, about three weeks ago. Then my fingertips."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"Is your balance worse in the dark or with your eyes shut? Any falls?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","s":"Way worse. Going to the toilet at night I bounce off the walls. I nearly fell in the shower."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"Any back or neck pain? Any change with your bladder or bowels, or numbness between your legs or around your bottom?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:59","w":"P","s":"No back pain. Bladder and bowels normal. Nothing down there."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this, and I'm not judging. Do you use any drugs, including nitrous oxide?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"Yeah, the big canisters. Most weekends for a year. It's just laughing gas. Everyone does it."},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"Lots of people think it's harmless. How much by the end, and when was the last time?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"(pause) Every day by the end. Last time was the night before I came in. It was the only thing that helped my mood."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being straight with me. That helps us understand both your mood and your nerves. What do you eat on a normal day?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"Not much. Toast, energy drinks. Pizza sometimes."},{"t":"1:50","band":"Examine standing","w":"D","s":"Can you walk to the door and back? Then heel to toe, like on a tightrope.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Unsteady, broad-based gait; unable to walk heel to toe.)"},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"Now stand with your feet together, eyes open. I'm right beside you, arms ready. Now close your eyes.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Whoa! (sways) I'm going!"},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Steady with eyes open; sways and steps out with eyes closed. Romberg positive.)"},{"t":"2:20","band":"Examine legs","w":"D","s":"I've got you. Sit on the bed. That test checks the nerves that tell your brain where your feet are. Now I'll roll your legs and lift your knees to check the tone. Just go floppy.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Tone normal. No wasting or fasciculation. No ankle clonus.)"},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Now push against me. Lift your leg up, push down, bend your knee, straighten it, feet up to your nose, and down.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"My hips feel weak. The rest's okay."},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Power 5/5 except hip flexion 4/5 on both sides.)"},{"t":"2:59","w":"D","s":"Now reflexes, at your knees then your ankles. Clench your teeth for me while I tap.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Knee jerks brisk. Ankle jerks absent even with reinforcement. Plantars up-going on both sides.)"},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"Run your heel down your shin. Now this tuning fork on your big toe. Tell me when you feel buzzing.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:21","w":"P","s":"I feel it touching, but no buzzing. Buzzing now, at the knee."},{"t":"3:27","w":"D","s":"Eyes closed. I'm moving your big toe. Is it up or down?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"Up? No idea, honestly."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"I'll touch you with cotton wool from your toes upwards, then your trunk. Tell me where it feels normal.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"It's faint, then normal about here, mid-shin. My tummy and chest feel normal."},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Heel-shin mildly unsteady. Vibration absent to the knees. Joint position impaired at the toes. Light touch reduced to mid-shin. No sensory level on the trunk.)"},{"t":"4:02","band":"Examine arms","w":"D","s":"Now your arms, same order. Hold them out, palms up, eyes closed. Push and pull against me. Then reflexes, finger to nose, and the tuning fork on your fingers.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"The buzzing's weaker in my fingers."},{"t":"4:18","w":"P","s":"(Finding: No drift. Tone, power and reflexes normal. Finger-nose normal. Vibration reduced at the fingertips.)"},{"t":"4:25","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Kieran. This isn't anxiety. The nerves in your spinal cord that carry position and vibration aren't working properly, and you have brisk knees but absent ankle reflexes. That pattern fits low vitamin B12 from heavy nitrous oxide use. It's called subacute combined degeneration.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:43","w":"P","s":"Am I going to end up in a wheelchair?"},{"t":"4:47","w":"D","s":"That's a frightening thought, and I'm glad you asked. Many people improve a lot with B12 injections and stopping the gas, especially when it's caught early, like yours.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"(quiet) I thought I'd wrecked myself for good."},{"t":"5:03","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you've been carrying that alone. We've caught it in time to treat it, and we start today.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:12","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'll take bloods now: B12, folate, a full blood count, and homocysteine, which shows if B12 is working. Then I'd like to start B12 injections today, without waiting for results.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"I hate needles. Can't I take a tablet?"},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"I understand. When nerves are affected, injections work more reliably. It's a small needle in the arm, every other day at first, and the nurse can use numbing cream.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"Alright. If it helps my legs."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"I'm also referring you urgently to the neurologists this week, and asking for an MRI of your spine.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:53","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what you've understood so far?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"The gas has messed up my B12 and my spinal cord. Injections, bloods, a scan and the neurologists. And no more gas."},{"t":"6:07","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Until you're steadier, use the shower chair and ask staff to help at night. If you get back pain, numbness between your legs, trouble passing urine or new weakness, tell the nurses straight away. That needs an urgent scan.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:23","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Would you see the drug and alcohol team before you go home? They're good with this, and not judgemental.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:32","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I think I need help to stop."},{"t":"6:36","w":"D","s":"That's a big step. I'll set it up. Any questions? I'll write this up and see you on tomorrow's ward round.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks for taking it seriously."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, gains consent, has a chaperone and explains the examination","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about onset, spread, darkness and falls","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for back pain, bladder or bowel change and saddle numbness","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Takes a non-judgemental nitrous oxide and diet history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests gait, heel-to-toe and Romberg while standing close","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests leg tone, power and reflexes with reinforcement, comparing sides","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests heel-shin, vibration, joint position and light touch, checking for a trunk level","d":"Content"},{"c":"Examines the upper limbs in the same fixed order","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names dorsal column loss with mixed UMN and LMN signs, likely B12 deficiency from nitrous oxide","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds with empathy to his fear","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Sends B12, folate, FBC and homocysteine and starts IM hydroxocobalamin today","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Makes an urgent neurology referral with MRI spine","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and safety-nets on bladder, back pain, weakness and falls","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closes with a drug service offer and next review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Screens red flags, examines gait, Romberg and all four limbs in order, names subacute combined degeneration, starts IM B12 and refers urgently with MRI.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis but the examination skips Romberg, sensation levels or the arms, or the referral is not urgent.","fail":"Labels it anxiety, gives oral B12 or folate alone, or misses cauda equina and cord compression questions."}},"c-g-07":{"setup":"Wednesday 11:00, female acute ward. Amira Hassan, 23, admitted with 'dissociative episodes', had a spell this morning that a nurse saw. Take a history from her and the nurse, explain possible focal epilepsy, and arrange tests, referral and safety and driving advice.","role":{"who":"Ms Amira Hassan, 23, woman, inpatient admitted with anxiety (Nurse Okafor, who saw the episode, is present)","open":"Yes, that's fine. I don't remember much of it anyway.","manner":"Cooperative and worried she is 'going mad'. Becomes tearful and guilty when she admits a spell while driving; relieved when it is explained as a brain problem.","free":["Spells start with a rising feeling in her stomach and déjà vu","Cannot remember the middle of the spells","Drives to work"],"ifasked":["About two a week for four months, more lately","Woken with a sore tongue once or twice; never found on the floor","Fit with a fever at age two; no family epilepsy; no head injury","A couple of glasses of wine at weekends; no drugs","Had a spell at the wheel last month and pulled over just in time","(Nurse) Eyes open, unresponsive to name and touch, lip smacking and picking, about a minute, muddled ten minutes"],"worries":["How can it be epilepsy? I've never had a proper fit.","So I can't drive? I'll lose my job.","Will I need tablets?","I'm not sure about being filmed."],"rules":["Discloses the spell while driving only if asked directly about driving","The nurse answers each witness question precisely when asked","Declines filming at first; open to it later if not pressured"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Amira. I'm Dr Ade, one of the ward doctors. I'd like to talk about the spell you had this morning. Nurse Okafor saw it. Is it alright if she joins us and tells me what she saw?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's fine. I don't remember much of it anyway."},{"t":"0:23","band":"Witness account","w":"D","s":"Nurse Okafor, can you take me through it from the very start?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"About quarter past nine she was sitting in the lounge. She went still and stared ahead."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"Were her eyes open or closed?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Wide open. Staring."},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"Did she respond to her name, or to touch?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"No. I said her name twice and touched her arm. Nothing."},{"t":"0:56","w":"D","s":"What movements did you see?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"She smacked her lips and picked at her sleeve with her right hand. The same movement, over and over."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"How long did it last, and what was she like afterwards?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"About a minute. Then muddled for ten minutes; she asked where she was. No colour change, and she didn't fall."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"That's a really clear account, thank you. Could you write it in the notes too?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:33","band":"Her account","w":"D","s":"Amira, what's the very first thing you notice when a spell starts?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"A rising feeling in my stomach, like going over a hill. And a feeling I've been here before, this exact moment."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"And then? Do you know what's happening around you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Not really. I come back and people are looking at me. I can't remember the middle."},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"How often do they happen, and since when?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"About twice a week, for four months. More lately."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"Any at night? Ever woken with a bitten tongue, a wet bed, or on the floor? Any injuries?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"I've woken with a sore tongue once or twice. Never on the floor."},{"t":"2:27","w":"D","s":"Any head injury, meningitis, or fits as a child? Anyone in the family with epilepsy?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"Mum says I had a fit with a fever when I was two. No family epilepsy. Never hit my head."},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"Alcohol or drugs?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"A couple of glasses of wine at weekends. No drugs."},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"Do you drive? Have you had a spell while driving?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"(pause) I drive to work. I had one at the wheel last month. I pulled over just in time."},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; I know that wasn't easy to say.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I could have killed someone. I've been terrified to tell anyone."},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"You were frightened, and you've told me now, which is the right thing. Let's keep you and everyone else safe.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:29","band":"Checks","w":"D","s":"Your ECG from this morning is normal, with a normal QTc. Your only medicine is sertraline, and nothing on your chart lowers the threshold for fits. I'd like to check your nerves quickly: eyes, face, arms and legs.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"(Finding: Neurological examination normal. No tongue bite or injuries today.)"},{"t":"3:56","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I think these spells may be a type of epilepsy called focal seizures. They start in one small part of the brain, often the temporal lobe. The stomach feeling and déjà vu are the start of the seizure; the staring and lip smacking come next.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"How can it be epilepsy? I've never had a proper fit."},{"t":"4:22","w":"D","s":"Good question. Most people picture falling and shaking, but some seizures only change your awareness for a minute. Panic builds over minutes, you stay aware, and you're not muddled after. Yours fit seizures better.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"So I'm not going mad? I thought it was all in my head."},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"No, you're not. These spells come from the brain's electrical activity, not your imagination. We'll keep helping with your anxiety too.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:54","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'm referring you to the first seizure clinic, to be seen within two weeks. They'll arrange an MRI brain scan and a brain wave test called an EEG. I'll also send some blood tests.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Will I need tablets?"},{"t":"5:14","w":"D","s":"That's for the specialist. Many people take one medicine and become seizure-free.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:21","w":"D","s":"Until you've been seen, you mustn't drive, and the law says you must tell the DVLA. I'll write in your notes that I've advised you today.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:33","w":"P","s":"I'll lose my job."},{"t":"5:37","w":"D","s":"That's a real blow, and I'm sorry. I can write to your employer. Many people drive again once seizures are controlled.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"Some safety things: showers, not baths. No swimming or heights on your own. If a spell lasts five minutes, the nurses will give emergency treatment and call 999. We'll keep a chart of the spells, and with your agreement, a short video helps the specialist.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:07","w":"P","s":"I'm not sure about a video. Can I think about it?"},{"t":"6:13","w":"D","s":"Of course. It's your choice, and careful notes work too.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:19","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed, so I know I've been clear?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"It might be epilepsy. A specialist in two weeks, a scan and a brain test. No driving, tell the DVLA, showers not baths."},{"t":"6:36","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Any questions for me?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Will you help me with the DVLA form?"},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Yes. We'll do it together this afternoon. I'm writing everything up now, and I'll see you after lunch.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and gains her agreement to involve the nurse","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes a witness account: onset, eyes, responsiveness, movements, duration and recovery","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about the warning, awareness and memory of the spells","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about frequency, night-time spells, tongue biting, injuries and driving","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about febrile fits, head injury, family history, alcohol and drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Reviews the ECG and drug chart and does a neurological examination","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains possible focal epilepsy in plain words, contrasting it with panic","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds with empathy to her guilt, fear and loss","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Refers to the first seizure clinic with MRI and EEG requested","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises no driving and informing the DVLA, and records it","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives bath, swimming and height safety advice and the five-minute rule","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks her understanding by asking her to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with questions, practical help and next contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gets a structured witness and personal account, recognises focal seizures, does an ECG and examination, refers with MRI and EEG, and gives clear driving and safety advice.","borderline":"Right diagnosis, but the witness account is thin or the driving and safety advice is vague.","fail":"Calls it dissociation or panic without a proper account, increases PRN benzodiazepines, or forgets driving advice."}},"c-g-08":{"setup":"Tuesday 13:30, acute medical ward, liaison referral. Chloe Turner, 20, fainted in a lecture: BMI 14, HR 44, potassium 2.9. She wants to go home tomorrow. Take an eating disorder history, grade her medical risk, and advise the medical team.","role":{"who":"Ms Chloe Turner, 20, woman, university student and medical inpatient (Dr Rao, medical registrar, joins at the end)","open":"I'm fine. I just need to eat more. I can go home tomorrow.","manner":"Brittle, minimising and keen to leave. Becomes quiet and ashamed when disclosing vomiting, angry when told she should stay, then tearful. Responds to calm honesty.","free":["Fainted in a lecture","Says she just needs to eat more and wants to go home tomorrow","Says coursework made her forget to eat"],"ifasked":["Black coffee, an apple and some soup a day since February","Weighed 52 kg before; now 39 kg","Makes herself sick after most meals; nobody knows","Walks two hours a day and gets anxious if she doesn't","Sees fat on her stomach; terrified of 'ballooning'","No period for six months","Sometimes thinks it would be easier not to wake up; no plans"],"worries":["I'll eat everything if you let me go home tomorrow.","You can't keep me here. I'm an adult.","Can my mum come?"],"rules":["Discloses vomiting only if asked directly and kindly","Fails the SUSS sit-up and squat","Can repeat the risks back but insists they don't apply to her","The medical registrar asks for clear recommendations"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, Chloe? I'm Dr Ade from the liaison psychiatry team. I work with the medical doctors here, and they asked me to see how you're doing. Is now okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"I'm fine. I just need to eat more. I can go home tomorrow."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"I want to understand what's been going on, then I'll be honest with you about what I think. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"(shrugs) Okay."},{"t":"0:30","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Can you take me through what you eat and drink on a typical day?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"Black coffee in the morning. An apple at lunch. Maybe some soup at night. I just got busy with coursework."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"How long has it been like that? What did you weigh before?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Since about February. I was fifty-two kilos."},{"t":"0:54","w":"D","s":"So thirteen kilos in about nine months. That's a lot, and fast.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"I didn't really notice."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Some people in this situation make themselves sick, or use laxatives or diet pills. Do you do any of those?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"(long pause) I make myself sick after most meals. Nobody knows."},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. It explains your low potassium, which affects your heart.","m":[8]},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"How much exercise do you do in a day?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"I walk about two hours. Every day. I get anxious if I don't."},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about your body and your weight right now?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"I know the number's low. But in the mirror I still see fat on my stomach. If I eat what they give me here, I'll balloon."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"That fear sounds really strong. Lots of people with this illness feel exactly that.","m":[8]},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"When was your last period?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"About six months ago."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"When things are this hard, some people have thoughts of ending their life. Have you?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think it'd be easier if I didn't wake up. I wouldn't do anything. No plans."},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Who knows about this? Who's supporting you?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"My mum knows I've lost weight. Nobody at uni."},{"t":"2:28","band":"Physical risk","w":"D","s":"I've looked at your results. Your heart rate is 44. Your blood pressure is 86 over 54 lying and 74 over 48 standing. Your temperature is 35.4, potassium 2.9 and phosphate 0.8, and the QTc on your heart tracing is 462.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"My heart's always been slow. I used to run."},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"Can I check your muscle strength with a quick test? Lie flat, and sit up without using your hands.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"(struggles) I can't. I have to use my hands."},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"Now squat down and stand up, again without hands. I'm right here.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"(wobbles and grabs the bed) I can't get up."},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"(Finding: SUSS sit-up score 1, squat score 1. Dizzy on standing.)"},{"t":"3:16","band":"Explain and capacity","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I need to be honest. Your heart is very slow, your blood pressure falls when you stand, your salts are low and your muscles are weak, including your heart muscle. Together these mean a high risk of your heart stopping.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"My heart is fine. I'll eat everything if you let me go home tomorrow."},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"I'm glad part of you wants to eat. The trouble is, the first days of eating carry their own risks. Your salts can drop fast and affect your heart, so it needs daily checks here.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what I've said about the risks?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"You think my heart could stop. And eating again can mess up my salts."},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"That's right. How does that apply to you?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"It doesn't. That's for really ill people. I just need to go home."},{"t":"4:16","w":"D","s":"If you went home and carried on eating as you have been, what might happen?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"Nothing. I've been fine for months."},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Chloe. You've understood and remembered the facts. But I don't think you can weigh the risk to your heart right now, because the illness is telling you it doesn't apply to you. So I don't think you can decide to leave today.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:43","w":"P","s":"(angry) You can't keep me here. I'm an adult."},{"t":"4:48","w":"D","s":"You're right that adults make their own choices, and I respect that. My job is to check whether this illness is making the decision for you. Right now, I'm worried it is.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:01","w":"P","s":"(crying) I don't want to be here."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"I know. This is really hard, and you won't be on your own with it.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:11","band":"Advise the team","w":"D","s":"Dr Rao, here's my advice. This is severe anorexia nervosa with purging, and on MEED she's high risk: BMI 14, HR 44, a postural drop with dizziness, temperature 35.4, potassium 2.9, a failed SUSS and QTc 462.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","who":"Medical registrar","s":"Okay. What do you recommend?"},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Refeeding with the dietitian today, not a very low start. Thiamine and vitamin B compound strong before feeding. Replace potassium. Phosphate, potassium, magnesium and glucose daily for a week, with cardiac monitoring. Supervised meals, and a nurse with her for an hour after each meal.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:48","w":"D","s":"She lacks capacity to decide to leave today. If she tries, call me and a senior at once. In the short term the MCA lets you keep her safe in her best interests; beyond that, I'll arrange a Mental Health Act assessment.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","who":"Medical registrar","s":"Understood. Will the eating disorder team see her?"},{"t":"6:09","w":"D","s":"Yes, tomorrow. I'll phone them today and write all of this in the notes.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Chloe, here's what happens next. You'll stay here, eat with support, and have blood tests every day. The eating disorder team will see you tomorrow, and I'll come back too.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:27","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Can my mum come?"},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"Yes, and with your say-so we'll keep her updated. If the thoughts of not waking up get stronger, please tell the nurses. Any questions?","m":[5,14]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"No. I just want it to be over."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I understand. I'll see you tomorrow.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the liaison role and gains consent","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about typical intake and weight change over time","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about vomiting, laxatives and exercise","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores body image, fear of weight gain and periods","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts, plans and support","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Reviews rate of loss, HR, lying and standing BP, temperature, QTc and electrolytes","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Performs and scores the SUSS test","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Responds with empathy to her fear, shame and anger","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the medical danger honestly and checks her understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Assesses capacity to refuse admission, focusing on weighing the information","d":"Risk"},{"c":"States high medical risk using MEED markers to the medical team","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises refeeding with dietitian, thiamine, daily bloods, cardiac monitoring and supervised meals","d":"Plan"},{"c":"States the legal route if she tries to leave: MCA short term, MHA assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes honestly with next steps, family involvement and eating disorder team review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Takes a full history including purging and suicide, grades risk with MEED including SUSS, assesses capacity, and gives specific refeeding, monitoring and legal advice.","borderline":"Safe advice, but misses purging or SUSS, or the capacity assessment is vague.","fail":"Agrees to discharge, recommends very low-calorie feeding only, or ignores capacity and the legal route."}},"c-g-09":{"setup":"Thursday 14:00, older adult ward. Ronald Pike, 74, is ready for discharge, but his wife has a court order stopping him returning home and he doesn't know yet. Tell him, assess his needs and wishes, and agree a safe discharge plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Ronald Pike, 74, man, older adult inpatient with depression now improved (Mrs Fry, ward social worker, is present)","open":"About time. I'm feeling much better. Sleeping like a log.","manner":"Cheerful at first, then stunned, tearful and defensive at the news. Minimises drinking until gently challenged. Firm against a care home; softens when offered his own front door.","free":["Feels much better and is sleeping well","Says there was a row at home and the police came","Wants to be near his son Paul"],"ifasked":["Jean did all the cooking, money and tablets; he has never cooked","Can wash and dress himself","Four or five pints at the club and whisky at home most days","Does not remember much of the night of the assault","Believes Jean has been unfaithful for years; no plan to contact her","Open to a flat with staff on site; would choose Paul for power of attorney"],"worries":["That's my house. I paid for it.","I'm not going in a home.","If I stop drinking, can I go home?"],"rules":["Goes silent then tearful at the news; settles if given time and not judged","Gives the true drinking amount only after a gentle second question","Agrees to referrals if his preference is heard","The social worker agrees to actions when asked directly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Good afternoon, Mr Pike. I'm Dr Ade, one of the ward doctors, and this is Mrs Fry, the ward social worker. Is it alright if we talk about plans for leaving hospital?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"About time. I'm feeling much better. Sleeping like a log."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad. What's your understanding of why you came into hospital?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"I was low. Couldn't sleep. There was a row at home and the police came. It got out of hand."},{"t":"0:33","band":"The news","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'm afraid I have some difficult news. Your wife has got a court order, which means you can't go back to the house for now.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"(silence) She wouldn't do that. That's my house. I paid for it."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"I can see this is a huge shock, and I'm sorry to be the one telling you. Take your time.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"(long pause, tearful) Forty-eight years. And she does this."},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"Forty-eight years is a long time. It makes sense to feel hurt.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"I never laid a finger on her before. Never."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"I hear you. I'm not here to judge what happened. The court has decided you can't go back for now, so I'd like us to plan where you can be settled and safe.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:27","band":"His needs","w":"D","s":"Before you came in, who did the cooking, shopping, bills and tablets?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"Jean did all that. I've never cooked a meal in my life."},{"t":"1:38","w":"D","s":"Could you manage those yourself? Which would be hardest?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:42","w":"P","s":"I can wash and dress, no problem. But money, tablets, cooking? I'd starve."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"That's an honest worry. The OT found you're safe with a microwave, which is a start, and we can arrange help with the rest.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"Can I ask about drinking? How much were you having in a normal week?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"A few pints. Like anyone."},{"t":"2:09","w":"D","s":"Your family say it's been heavier for years. What would a normal day look like?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"(pause) Four or five pints at the club. Whisky at home. Maybe more than I let on."},{"t":"2:23","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being honest. Do you remember the night the police came?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"Not really. Bits. I didn't mean to hurt her. I don't even remember."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"It sounds like that weighs on you. Not remembering is something we should look into, along with the drinking.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about Jean at the moment? Have you had worries about her?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"She's been carrying on behind my back for years. I know it."},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"That sounds painful. How long have you felt that? Have you thought about going round or contacting her?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"Years. But no. I'm not daft. I'll keep away."},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"Good. Going near her would break the order and get you into serious trouble.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:15","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"Living alone straight away might be hard, as Jean did the meals, money and tablets. There are three main options. A flat of your own with carers visiting. An extra care flat, with your own front door and staff on site. Or a care home with your own room.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"I'm not going in a home. That's where you go to die."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"I hear that. A care home isn't the only option. If the right flat isn't ready in time, a short stay in one could bridge the gap, but only as a stop-gap.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"What would matter most to you in a new place?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"Being near our Paul, my son. And my own front door. Staff on site doesn't sound bad, if they leave me be."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"So an extra care flat near Paul is your first choice. Have I got that right?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Yes. That's it."},{"t":"4:19","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Mrs Fry, could you arrange a Care Act assessment of his care needs and a housing referral to the council? Mr Pike, we need your consent for the housing referral. Is that okay?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","who":"Social worker","s":"I'll start both today. With your agreement, I'll also look for a short-term care home place in case the flat isn't ready."},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"Fine. As long as it's short."},{"t":"4:46","w":"D","s":"I'd also like the alcohol team to see you before you leave, to help you stay off it. And in a few weeks, once you've been off drink longer, we'll repeat the memory test.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"If I stop drinking, can I go home?"},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"Stopping would be good for you, and we'll help. But the order isn't up to us. You can get legal advice about it, and Mrs Fry can tell you how.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:17","w":"D","s":"One more thing, so there are no surprises. Because of the order, we'll tell the police and Jean's support worker your discharge date and where you'll live. That's part of keeping everyone safe.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:31","w":"P","s":"(sighs) I suppose I've no say in that."},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"It's something we have to do, but I wanted you to hear it from me.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"While you're well, it may help to think about a power of attorney for your money, someone you trust. Mrs Fry can explain.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"Paul, maybe."},{"t":"5:56","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what you've understood from today?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"I can't go home, Jean's got an order. You're finding me a flat with staff near Paul, maybe a home for a bit first. The drink people, another memory test. And the police get told."},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. So Mrs Fry starts the care assessment and housing referral today, I'll refer you to the alcohol team and book the memory test, and with your permission we'll meet with Paul next week to agree the plan.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:31","w":"P","s":"Yes, ask Paul. Thank you for being straight with me."},{"t":"6:36","w":"D","s":"Thank you for listening, on a really hard day. The nurses are here if you want to talk later. Any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. Not now."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and colleague and asks his understanding of the admission","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Tells him clearly and early about the court order","d":"Content"},{"c":"Allows silence and responds to shock and guilt with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about cooking, money, medicines and self-care","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about drinking amount and memory of the incident","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about feelings towards his wife, jealous beliefs and any intent to contact her","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Offers at least three realistic accommodation options","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Elicits and confirms his preference","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees Care Act assessment and housing referral with consent, and raises power of attorney","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees alcohol support and repeat cognitive testing","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains that discharge details will be shared with police and his wife's support worker","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks his understanding by asking him to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with who does what and the next meeting","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Breaks the news clearly with time for his reaction, assesses daily living and drinking, offers realistic options, agrees referrals and is open about sharing information to protect his wife.","borderline":"Plan is safe but options are narrow, his wishes are not sought, or safeguarding is not explained to him.","fail":"Avoids telling him about the order, plans a return home, discharges to a hostel, or omits safeguarding."}},"c-p-01":{"setup":"Tuesday afternoon, community mental health clinic. Mrs Priya Shah, 32, has recurrent depression and has been well for a year on sertraline 100 mg. She wants to try for a baby. Review her, explain the options and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Priya Shah, 32, female, patient","open":"Thanks for seeing me. I've been reading online and I'm scared. I think I should come off the sertraline before we start trying.","manner":"Anxious and well-read; asks pointed questions. Tearful when recalling 2023. Relaxes when given honest numbers and when her choice is respected.","free":["Well for over a year: working, sleeping, enjoying things","Read that SSRIs cause heart defects and newborn withdrawal","She and her husband Dev want to start trying in three months","Wants to do everything right for the baby"],"ifasked":["Stopped sertraline in 2022 after six months and relapsed within three months","2023 episode: off work four months, thought Dev would be better off without her, no attempt","No suicidal thoughts for over a year","Not taking folic acid or any other medicine","Last period two weeks ago; on the combined pill","Drinks two glasses of wine at weekends; never smoked","BMI 24 at the GP last month; no diabetes or epilepsy","Her mother had postnatal depression"],"worries":["Will the baby be born 'addicted'?","Will sertraline cause a birth defect?","Can she breastfeed on it?","What if she gets pregnant before the next appointment?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful talking about 2023; settles if validated","If told sertraline is 'completely safe', asks why the internet says otherwise","Relaxes noticeably once given absolute numbers","Wants to talk to Dev before a final decision; accepts if that is respected"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms purpose and asks what she has read or fears","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her worry about the baby before giving information","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks current mood and asks directly about suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Establishes episode history, severity and outcome of stopping sertraline before","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks pregnancy status, contraception, other medicines, alcohol, smoking and folic acid risk","d":"Content"},{"c":"States sertraline is not linked to a clear rise in birth defects and suits breastfeeding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains neonatal adaptation, PPHN and bleeding risk in plain words with absolute numbers","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains risks of relapse and untreated depression for mother and baby","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Advises folic acid 400 micrograms daily now until 12 weeks, with reason for that dose","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises not stopping suddenly if pregnant and gives warning signs and crisis contacts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back and respects her right to decide","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Summarises the plan, GP letter, perinatal advice, leaflet and review date","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Shah, I'm Dr Adeyemi, one of the psychiatry doctors. I understand you'd like to talk about pregnancy. What's on your mind?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Thanks for seeing me. I've been reading online and I'm scared. I think I should come off the sertraline before we start trying."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That's a really natural worry. Wanting to protect a baby before it even exists says a lot about you. What have you read?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"That it causes heart defects. And babies come out withdrawing, like they're addicted. I couldn't live with that."},{"t":"0:38","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. I'll go through what's actually known, honestly, with real numbers. First, can I check how you are at the moment?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Honestly, good. The best I've been in years. Working, sleeping, enjoying things."},{"t":"0:54","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad. Any low days lately, or felt that life isn't worth living?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"No. Not for over a year."},{"t":"1:05","band":"Her history","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me about the times you've been unwell before?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Twice. 2022 wasn't too bad. 2023 was awful. I couldn't get out of bed. I thought Dev would be better off without me."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a really dark time, and I'm sorry you went through it. Thank you for telling me. Did you ever act on those thoughts?","m":[2,3,4]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"No. I was off work four months. The sertraline and the therapy got me out of it."},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"Have you ever stopped the sertraline before?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"In 2022. I felt better after six months, so I stopped. Three months later I was low again."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"That's important. It tells us your depression can come back fairly quickly without it.","m":[4,8]},{"t":"1:58","band":"Health now","w":"D","s":"Could you be pregnant at the moment?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"No. My period was two weeks ago and I'm on the pill until we start trying."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Are you taking any other medicines, supplements or herbal remedies? Any folic acid?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"Nothing. Should I be?"},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"Yes, we'll come to that. Do you drink or smoke?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"A couple of glasses of wine at weekends. Never smoked."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Any diabetes or epilepsy, and do you know your weight or BMI?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"No to both. The GP said my BMI was 24 last month."},{"t":"2:42","band":"The facts","w":"D","s":"Thank you. So here's the honest picture. Sertraline is one of the most studied antidepressants in pregnancy. Large studies haven't shown a clear rise in major birth defects, including heart defects.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"But the article said there was a link."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Some early studies raised it. Bigger studies that allowed for the depression itself didn't confirm it. No medicine is proven completely safe, but this is reassuring.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"And the withdrawal thing?"},{"t":"3:14","w":"D","s":"If you take it late in pregnancy, some babies are jittery, sleepy or slow to feed for the first few days. It's usually mild and settles on its own. The midwives just watch the baby more closely after birth.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:29","w":"P","s":"OK. That's less scary than addicted."},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"There's also a rare lung condition in newborns called PPHN. Normally about 2 in 1000 babies get it. With sertraline late in pregnancy it's about 3 in 1000. So 997 in 1000 don't.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"Right. That's not what I imagined."},{"t":"3:51","w":"D","s":"And taking it in the month before birth slightly raises the chance of heavier bleeding after delivery. The maternity team just need to know so they're prepared.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"So why not stop and be completely safe?"},{"t":"4:07","w":"D","s":"Fair question. Stopping isn't risk-free either. Last time you were unwell within three months. Depression in pregnancy can affect eating, sleep and antenatal care, is linked with early birth and smaller babies, and makes depression after birth more likely.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"I don't want to be like 2023 with a newborn."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"No. You've worked hard to get well, and I want to protect that as well as the baby.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"Can I breastfeed on it?"},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"Yes. Very little gets into breast milk, and it's one of the antidepressants we prefer for breastfeeding.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:47","band":"The plan","w":"D","s":"Folic acid now: 400 micrograms every day, from today until you're 12 weeks pregnant. Any pharmacy sells it. With your BMI and health, you don't need the higher 5 milligram dose.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"I'll get some today."},{"t":"5:03","w":"D","s":"And if your period's late or a test is positive, please don't stop sertraline suddenly. Keep taking it, book with the midwife, tell your GP, and ring us so we can review you.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:16","w":"P","s":"What if I start feeling low in the pregnancy?"},{"t":"5:21","w":"D","s":"Tell us early: sleep changing, losing interest, pulling away from people. Ring your GP or us; out of hours, NHS 111 and choose the mental health option. If you ever feel unsafe, call 999 or go to A&E.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:37","w":"D","s":"Just so I can check I've explained it clearly, could you tell me in your words what we've covered?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"No clear link with defects. The baby might be jittery at first. Small risks with the lungs and bleeding. Stopping could make me ill again. Folic acid now."},{"t":"5:57","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. What are you leaning towards?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:01","w":"P","s":"Staying on it. But I want to talk to Dev first."},{"t":"6:07","w":"D","s":"That's sensible. It's your decision, and it's fine to take time. Here's a leaflet on sertraline in pregnancy to share with him.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:17","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So: sertraline 100 milligrams for now, folic acid from today, and no sudden stopping if you get pregnant. I'll write to your GP with a copy to you, ask the perinatal team for advice because 2023 was severe, and see you in three months, sooner if you're pregnant.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel so much better about it."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. Any other questions before you go?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. That's everything."}],"global":{"pass":"Validates her fear, establishes high relapse risk, gives honest absolute risks, starts folic acid and records her informed choice with a plan for a positive test.","borderline":"Accurate facts but thin relapse history, or little check of understanding and no written follow-up.","fail":"Advises stopping sertraline or calls it 'completely safe', omits folic acid, or gives no plan if she becomes pregnant."}},"c-p-02":{"setup":"Thursday morning, joint antenatal clinic. Miss Leah Carter, 30, is 12 weeks pregnant with her first baby. She has bipolar I disorder and is well on quetiapine 300 mg. Her partner Tom is with her. Start her perinatal plan.","role":{"who":"Miss Leah Carter, 30, female, patient (her partner Tom, 33, speaks once if invited)","open":"The midwife said I needed to see you. Is it true I could get ill after the birth?","manner":"Bright but guarded; worried she will be judged or the baby taken. Upset recalling her 2021 admission. Warms when she sees the plan keeps her in control.","free":["Well for three years; working as a teaching assistant","Found out she was pregnant at 6 weeks; planned pregnancy","Wonders if she should stop quetiapine for the baby","Partner Tom is supportive"],"ifasked":["Still takes quetiapine 300 mg every night; missed two doses in early pregnancy with sickness","2021: first signs were sleeping 3 hours, buying expensive things, irritability; later believed she had a special mission","Six weeks in hospital in 2021, on a section for the first two weeks","Maternal aunt has bipolar disorder","Mood and sleep fine now; no suicidal thoughts","Hopes to breastfeed","BMI 27 at booking","Tom can take two weeks' paternity leave; her mum lives 20 minutes away"],"worries":["Will they take the baby away if she gets ill?","Should she stop quetiapine for the baby's sake?","Can she breastfeed on quetiapine?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful about 2021 and being sectioned; settles if validated","Bristles at being told what to do; engages when offered choices","Asks about the baby being taken; reassured only by an honest answer about MBUs","Tom speaks once if asked what he would notice first"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and role, and asks what she knows and wants from today","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her fear about becoming ill and about losing the baby","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks current mood, sleep, psychotic symptoms and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Confirms quetiapine adherence and her early warning signs from 2021","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about family history of bipolar disorder or postpartum psychosis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains postpartum psychosis risk as about 1 in 5, highest in first 2 weeks","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains quetiapine in pregnancy: no clear birth defect rise, glucose screening, newborn observation","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Advises continuing quetiapine, explaining why stopping raises relapse risk","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Describes the written perinatal plan and who receives it by 32 weeks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a birth and postnatal plan that protects sleep, including night feeds","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives her and her partner warning signs and same-day crisis contacts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding, summarises referral, glucose test and next review","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Miss Carter, I'm Dr Osei from the perinatal mental health team. We work with women who've had mental health problems, to plan for pregnancy and after. Congratulations. What would you like from today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:16","w":"P","s":"The midwife said I needed to see you. Is it true I could get ill after the birth?"},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"I'll answer that honestly in a moment. Can I first ask what you've heard?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"That women with bipolar go mad after having babies. And then social services get involved."},{"t":"0:41","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a frightening thing to carry into your first pregnancy. I'm sorry. Let's sort fact from fear together.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:51","band":"How she is","w":"D","s":"How have you been since you found out you were pregnant? Mood, sleep, energy?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:59","w":"P","s":"Honestly fine. A bit sick, tired, but happy."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Any times feeling very high or racing, or low and hopeless? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"And anything unusual, like hearing things or beliefs others find strange?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"No. Not since 2021."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"Are you still taking the quetiapine every night?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"Mostly. I missed two nights when I was being sick. And I've wondered whether I should stop it for the baby."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being honest. We'll talk about that. Can you tell me what happened in 2021?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"It started with not sleeping. Three hours a night, but I felt amazing. Spending loads. Then I thought I had a mission. I was in hospital six weeks. On a section at first."},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening, especially being sectioned. Thank you for telling me. Those first signs, the sleep and the spending, are really useful for planning.","m":[2,4]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"Tom noticed before I did."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"Has anyone in your family had bipolar disorder, or become unwell after having a baby?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"My aunt has bipolar."},{"t":"2:34","band":"The risk","w":"D","s":"Thank you. So, your question. Yes, the weeks after birth are a higher-risk time. About 1 in 5 women with bipolar disorder become seriously unwell, usually in the first two weeks. Your aunt's illness nudges that up a little.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"One in five. So I could end up back in hospital."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"It's possible. That also means most women stay well. And because we know now, at 12 weeks, we can plan to lower your risk.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","band":"The medicine","w":"D","s":"About stopping quetiapine: I understand wanting the baby on nothing. Quetiapine hasn't been linked with a clear rise in birth defects. Stopping it makes relapse much more likely, in pregnancy or after the birth.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"So it's safer to keep taking it?"},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"For you, yes, I'd advise continuing 300 milligrams at night. Two things to watch: it can raise blood sugar, so the midwife will book a glucose test at 24 to 28 weeks. And the baby may be a bit unsettled or jittery after birth, so the paediatric team will keep an eye.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"Can I breastfeed on it?"},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"Generally yes. Small amounts pass into milk; we'd watch that the baby isn't too sleepy and is feeding well.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"OK. That's better than I thought."},{"t":"4:11","band":"The plan","w":"D","s":"The main tool is a written perinatal plan. It sets out your early signs, your medicines, your birth plan and who sees you after the birth. It's finished by 32 weeks and goes to you, the midwife, the obstetrician, labour ward, your GP and health visitor.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"So everyone knows before I even get there."},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"Exactly. For the birth, sleep is your biggest trigger. So early pain relief, a side room afterwards, Tom staying, and someone else doing one night feed so you get a five or six hour block.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"I don't want special treatment."},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"That's fair, and the plan is yours to shape. It's just that sleep did matter last time.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"I suppose it did."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"After the birth, our team sees you before you go home and regularly for the first two weeks.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:19","w":"P","s":"And if I do get ill? Will they take her?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Having bipolar disorder doesn't mean that. If you needed hospital, there are Mother and Baby Units where you stay together. I'll name the nearest one in your plan.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"5:39","w":"D","s":"Tom, what would you notice first if Leah was becoming unwell?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"(Partner) Her sleeping less and talking really fast."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. If you see that, ring our team the same day. Out of hours, the crisis team or NHS 111, mental health option. If she's unsafe, 999.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:04","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back the main points, so I know I've been clear?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:12","w":"P","s":"One in five risk, mostly the first two weeks. Keep taking quetiapine. Sugar test at 24 to 28 weeks. A written plan by 32 weeks, and protect my sleep."},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'm referring you to our team today, the midwife will book the glucose test, and I'll see you at 20 weeks to start writing the plan together. How does that sound?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"Better. Much better than I expected."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad. Ring us any time before then if anything changes.","m":[11,12]}],"global":{"pass":"Validates her fears, confirms adherence and early signs, gives the 1 in 5 risk honestly, keeps quetiapine and sets out a shared written plan that protects sleep.","borderline":"Correct advice but vague on who holds the plan, or no warning signs given to the partner.","fail":"Advises stopping quetiapine, leaves planning until after birth, or gives false reassurance about the postpartum risk."}},"c-p-03":{"setup":"Wednesday afternoon, perinatal mental health clinic. Mrs Sarah Doyle, 34, is 24 weeks pregnant with her second baby and has missed two antenatal appointments after a traumatic first birth. Assess her fear and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Sarah Doyle, 34, female, patient","open":"I nearly didn't come in. I'm only here because the midwife rang me twice.","manner":"Tense and defensive at first, expecting to be talked out of a caesarean. Cries describing theatre. Softens and engages once she feels believed and hears she has a real choice.","free":["First birth three years ago: 30-hour labour, forceps, third-degree tear, heavy bleeding","Felt nobody told her what was happening","Panics at the hospital entrance","Wants a caesarean and expects to be refused"],"ifasked":["Nightmares two or three times a week; flashbacks to theatre lights","Avoids her son's swimming lessons because the pool smells like the hospital","Jumpy, always on edge, sleeps about 5 hours","Mood a bit low but enjoys time with her son","'Wishing she wasn't pregnant' is fear; no thoughts of ending the pregnancy, self-harm or suicide","Thought she was going to die and leave her son without a mother","Nobody has ever gone through the first birth notes with her","Husband Mark is supportive but 'doesn't get it'"],"worries":["Will they refuse her a caesarean?","Is a caesarean safer for her?","Will she be judged for missing appointments?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful describing theatre; settles if validated, not hurried","If told second births are easier, becomes angry and quiet","Answers risk questions openly if asked gently and directly","Relaxes once told her choice will be respected"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges how hard it was to come, and agrees the purpose","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Lets her tell the first birth story with open questions, without correcting","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her fear and distress without minimising","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the worst moment and what she believed would happen","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for PTSD: re-experiencing, avoidance and sense of threat","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood and asks directly about thoughts of ending the pregnancy, self-harm or suicide","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks what she wants for this birth and what would help her feel safer","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains both birth options fairly, without pushing either","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains her right to a planned caesarean under NICE guidance","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Refers for fear-of-childbirth therapy, obstetric review and birth reflections","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Builds control and consent into the birth plan and rebooks missed checks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises, checks understanding, gives crisis contacts and a review date","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Doyle, I'm Dr Hassan from the perinatal mental health team. Thank you for coming in. I know that wasn't easy.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"I nearly didn't come in. I'm only here because the midwife rang me twice."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you did. I'd like to understand what's happening for you and plan this birth with you, not for you. Is that OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"As long as you're not going to tell me the second one's easier."},{"t":"0:39","w":"D","s":"I'm not. I'd like to hear what happened first. Can you tell me about your first birth, in your words?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:50","band":"First birth","w":"P","s":"I was in labour thirty hours. Then suddenly the room filled with people. Nobody told me anything. They used forceps. I tore badly and bled a lot."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. What happened next?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"They rushed me to theatre. Those lights. I thought I was going to die and leave him without a mum. (She cries.)"},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Take your time. That was a frightening experience, and being left without information made it worse. What you're feeling now makes complete sense.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"Everyone says I should be grateful we're both fine."},{"t":"1:38","w":"D","s":"You can be grateful and still be hurt by it. Both are true. What was the very worst moment for you?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"Lying there bleeding, hearing someone say 'we're losing her'."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me that. That belief, that you were going to die, often stays with people.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"2:04","band":"Trauma now","w":"D","s":"Do memories of it come back when you don't want them? Nightmares, flashbacks?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"Nightmares two or three times a week. Any bright light, I'm back in theatre."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Is there anything you avoid now because of it?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"The hospital. And my son's swimming lessons. The pool smells like the hospital."},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"That's really important. Do you feel jumpy or on edge, like something bad is about to happen?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"All the time. I jump at everything. I sleep about five hours."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Mood and risk","w":"D","s":"How's your mood? Do you still enjoy things?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"A bit low. But I love time with my son."},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"You told the midwife part of you wishes you weren't pregnant. What goes through your mind with that?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"I'm just so scared of the birth. I want this baby. I just don't want to give birth."},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"Have you had any thoughts of ending the pregnancy, or of harming yourself or ending your life?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I'd never leave my son."},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"Thank you for answering so openly. It sounds like the wish is about fear, not about the baby.","m":[3,6]},{"t":"3:46","band":"Her wishes","w":"D","s":"When you picture this birth, what do you want, and what would help you feel safer?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"A caesarean. Planned. Knowing when, knowing who. But they'll say no."},{"t":"4:01","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"Let me be clear about your options. One is a planned caesarean, usually at 39 weeks. It avoids labour, but recovery is longer and it affects future pregnancies. The other is a vaginal birth with a plan built around you: a named consultant, early epidural, and nothing done without your consent.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"And if I still want the caesarean?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Then you can have one. NICE guidance says that if you still want a caesarean after support and a proper discussion, you should be offered one. The obstetrician gives you your own figures, and the choice is yours.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"Really? Nobody's said that."},{"t":"4:52","w":"D","s":"Really. Getting help with the fear matters either way, for this birth and for afterwards.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"5:00","band":"The plan","w":"D","s":"So, three things. I'll refer you to the maternal mental health service: a psychologist who specialises in fear of birth and trauma. I'll ask your consultant obstetrician to see you within two weeks. And a senior midwife can go through your first birth notes with you, so you know what happened and why.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"I'd like that. I still don't know why it all happened."},{"t":"5:31","w":"D","s":"Whatever you choose, we'll write control into the plan: one named midwife, being told what's happening, consent before each examination, and a word you can use to stop and talk.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"That's what I needed last time."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"And the checks you missed matter for you and the baby. Could the midwife see you at the community clinic this week instead of the hospital?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:04","w":"P","s":"Yes. That I can do."},{"t":"6:08","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what you're taking away from today?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"I'm not mad, I've got trauma symptoms. I can ask for a caesarean. Therapy, the consultant and the notes."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If things get worse before then, call our team; out of hours, NHS 111 and choose the mental health option. I'll see you again in four weeks.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel listened to for the first time."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad. You've done the hardest part by coming in.","m":[3,12]}],"global":{"pass":"Hears the first birth fully, screens PTSD, mood and risk, explains both options and her right to a caesarean, and refers to therapy and obstetrics with control built in.","borderline":"Kind and accurate but misses the risk question, or plans without asking what she wants.","fail":"Tries to talk her out of a caesarean, minimises the first birth, or misses PTSD and risk."}},"c-p-04":{"setup":"Monday late morning, postnatal ward. Mrs Amira Hussain, 28, has bipolar II disorder and had a baby girl yesterday. She takes lamotrigine 200 mg and sertraline 100 mg. Staff told her she can't breastfeed. Explain her choices and the plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Amira Hussain, 28, female, patient","open":"Are you the one who's going to tell me properly? Because someone told me I can't feed my own baby.","manner":"Upset and angry, then guilty and tearful about 'poisoning' the baby. Calms and becomes practical once the facts are clear and her choice is respected.","free":["Baby girl Noor, born yesterday at 39 weeks, healthy","Told by a healthcare assistant she can't breastfeed on her tablets","Has not fed Noor since; midwife gave formula","Really wanted to breastfeed"],"ifasked":["Took both tablets this morning; no missed doses","Lamotrigine dose stayed at 200 mg all pregnancy","Slept about 3 hours since the birth","No elation or racing thoughts so far; mood tearful but OK; no thoughts of self-harm","Last hypomania two years ago started with poor sleep","Husband Karim can do a night feed","Has thought about stopping lamotrigine to feed"],"worries":["Is she harming the baby?","What should she watch for?","Can she stop lamotrigine while breastfeeding?","Will she bond if she can't breastfeed?"],"rules":["Angry for the first minute; calms if the doctor apologises and does not defend the advice","Tearful and guilty about 'poisoning' the baby; settles if validated","Asks to stop lamotrigine; accepts the reason once the rash risk and relapse risk are explained","Mentions poor sleep only if asked"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges her distress and apologises for the wrong advice","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what she was told and what she hoped for feeding","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks medicines taken, missed doses and any dose change in pregnancy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks her sleep, mood and early signs of relapse since the birth","d":"Risk"},{"c":"States both medicines are generally compatible with breastfeeding for a healthy term baby","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains sertraline's very low exposure and lamotrigine's higher exposure needing monitoring","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers breastfeeding, mixed feeding and formula as real choices without pressure","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names the baby's warning signs and who checks feeding and weight","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises continuing both drugs and explains lamotrigine re-titration if stopped","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a night-feed plan with her partner to protect her sleep","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates her guilt and fears about harming or bonding with the baby","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding, summarises, arranges feeding support and follow-up","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Hussain, I'm Dr Begum from the perinatal mental health team. Congratulations on Noor. The midwife said you've been given some upsetting advice.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Are you the one who's going to tell me properly? Because someone told me I can't feed my own baby."},{"t":"0:26","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. That must have been awful on her first day. What exactly were you told?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"That my tablets go into the milk and I'd hurt her. So she's had formula. I really wanted to breastfeed."},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll be clear straight away: that advice wasn't right. Both your tablets are generally considered compatible with breastfeeding for a healthy baby like Noor. I'll raise what happened with the ward manager.","m":[1,5]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"So why did she say it?"},{"t":"1:14","w":"D","s":"It's a common misunderstanding. A lot of people assume any medicine rules it out. I'm sorry it happened to you.","m":[1]},{"t":"1:26","band":"Her medicines","w":"D","s":"Can I check a few things? Did you take both tablets this morning?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Yes, lamotrigine 200 and sertraline 100."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Any missed since the birth? And did the lamotrigine dose change in pregnancy?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"No missed doses. It stayed at 200 the whole time."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"Good, so no need to adjust it now. How have you been sleeping?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"About three hours since she was born. I'm scared I'll go high."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"That's a really sensible worry. Any racing thoughts, feeling unusually energetic, or very low?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"No, just teary and tired. And no dark thoughts, before you ask."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"Thank you. We'll come back to sleep, because it matters for you.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:40","band":"The facts","w":"P","s":"But they do go into the milk, don't they? I'm poisoning her."},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"I can hear how guilty you feel, and you're not poisoning her. You're keeping yourself well so you can care for her. Small amounts do reach the milk, and that's why we keep an eye on her.","m":[11,6]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"How much?"},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"Sertraline hardly at all; in most babies it can't even be measured. Lamotrigine more, and babies clear it slowly. For a healthy, full-term baby, breastfeeding is usually fine, but we watch her closely.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"Could I stop the lamotrigine while I'm feeding? Just to be safe?"},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd want to. The next few months are when relapse is most likely. And if lamotrigine stops for about a week, it has to be built up again from a low dose because of a risk of serious rash.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"I didn't know that."},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"So please keep taking both every day, whatever you choose for feeding.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:15","band":"Choices","w":"D","s":"You've got three real choices. Breastfeeding fully, mixing breast and bottle, or formula. All are safe with your medicines, and the choice is yours.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:29","w":"P","s":"If I didn't breastfeed, would I still bond with her?"},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"Yes. Bonding comes from holding her, talking to her, responding to her. However you feed her. It's clear how much you love her already.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"I want to breastfeed. But I need sleep."},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"Then let's do both. Could someone at home give one bottle of expressed milk at night, so you get a five or six hour stretch?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"My husband Karim has already offered."},{"t":"5:17","band":"Watching Noor","w":"D","s":"Here's what to watch for in Noor: unusually sleepy, not waking for feeds, not gaining weight, pauses in her breathing or colour change, or a rash. Any of those, tell the midwife or health visitor that day.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:37","w":"P","s":"Who checks her weight?"},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"The midwife checks feeding daily and weighs her around day five. The health visitor takes over at about two weeks. I'll write to them and the GP with this plan.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:59","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back the main points, so I'm sure I've been clear?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:08","w":"P","s":"I can breastfeed. Keep taking both. Watch if she's sleepy or not feeding, or a rash. Karim does a night feed."},{"t":"6:21","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. I'll ask the infant feeding lead to come and help with a feed now. If your sleep slips or you feel yourself going high, ring our team that day.","m":[12,4]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel like a mum again."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You've been her mum all along. I'll see you on day seven.","m":[11,12]}],"global":{"pass":"Apologises, corrects the advice, gives honest drug facts, offers real choices, names the baby's warning signs and who checks them, and protects her sleep.","borderline":"Correct facts but no plan for monitoring the baby, or forgets her sleep and relapse risk.","fail":"Advises stopping lamotrigine or formula 'to be safe', or leaves the misinformation uncorrected."}},"c-p-05":{"setup":"Friday afternoon, community perinatal clinic. Mrs Grace Whitfield, 33, is 5 weeks after her first baby's birth. She told the health visitor she has pictures of stabbing him and has hidden the knives. Assess the thoughts and the risk, and explain.","role":{"who":"Mrs Grace Whitfield, 33, female, patient","open":"I nearly didn't come. I thought if I said it out loud you'd ring social services.","manner":"Ashamed and frightened; avoids eye contact; sobs at first. Relieved and calmer once told the thoughts are common and not dangerous; then asks for repeated reassurance.","free":["Baby son Leo, 5 weeks old, first baby, breastfeeding","Sudden pictures of stabbing Leo with a kitchen knife","Has hidden every knife in the house","Terrified Leo will be taken away"],"ifasked":["Thoughts started at about 2 weeks; many times a day","Horrified by them; knows they are her own thoughts; has never wanted to act","Won't bath Leo alone; checks his breathing several times a night","Asks partner Ben many times a day if she is a bad mother","No voices, no strange beliefs about Leo; he is definitely her baby","Mood a bit low, still enjoys walks with Leo; sleeps when he sleeps","No thoughts of harming herself or ending her life","Had a spell of checking and washing at university that passed on its own"],"worries":["Will Leo be taken away?","How do you know I won't do it?","Can I take tablets while breastfeeding?"],"rules":["Sobs when first describing the thoughts; settles if validated without hurry","If the doctor reacts with alarm, shuts down and minimises","Asks for reassurance a second time; settles if the doctor explains the reassurance trap kindly","Answers psychosis and risk questions openly if asked plainly"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, sets a non-judgemental tone and explains confidentiality honestly","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her shame and fear before probing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits the thoughts in detail: content, onset, frequency","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes the thoughts are unwanted, resisted and recognised as her own","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about avoidance, checking and reassurance-seeking","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about intent, urges and ever acting on the thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for psychotic features: beliefs about the baby, voices, perplexity","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens mood, sleep and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains OCD-type thoughts and that they do not raise the risk of harm","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States the safeguarding decision and its reason clearly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers CBT with ERP and sertraline, noting breastfeeding compatibility","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives signs needing same-day contact, summarises, checks understanding and arranges review","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Whitfield, I'm Dr Mensah from the perinatal mental health team. Thank you for coming. I know it took courage.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"I nearly didn't come. I thought if I said it out loud you'd ring social services."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That fear makes complete sense. I'll be honest with you: I would only share things if I believed Leo was at risk. Lots of mums tell us about thoughts like this, and most of the time that isn't the case.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Okay. (She starts to cry.)"},{"t":"0:44","w":"D","s":"Take your time. There's no rush, and nothing you say will shock me.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:52","band":"The thoughts","w":"P","s":"I get pictures. Of stabbing him. With a kitchen knife. Out of nowhere."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That must be horrible to live with. When did they start, and how often do they come?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"About two weeks after he was born. Now it's many times a day."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"How do you feel when one comes?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"Sick. Horrified. I love him so much."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Do they feel like your own thoughts, or as if they're put there from outside?","m":[4,7]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"They're mine. That's what makes it so awful."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"Do you try to push them away?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"All the time. It just makes them worse."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"What do you do to keep him safe from them?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"I've hidden every knife. I won't bath him unless Ben's there. I check he's breathing three or four times a night. And I keep asking Ben if I'm a bad mum."},{"t":"2:17","w":"D","s":"That all makes sense as ways to feel safer. I'll come back to them. Now I need to ask something plainly. Have you ever wanted to act on these thoughts, or come close to doing so?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I'd die first."},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"Any urge at all, even for a moment?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"No. It's the opposite. I'm scared of the thought."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Other experiences","w":"D","s":"Some mums after birth feel their baby isn't really theirs, or that something needs to be done to him. Has anything like that happened?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"No. He's definitely mine. He's perfect."},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"Have you been hearing voices or seeing things others don't?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"How's your mood? Sleep? Any thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"A bit low. I sleep when he sleeps. I still like our walks. No, nothing like that."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Has anything like this happened before, in any form?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"At uni I went through a phase of checking and washing. It passed."},{"t":"3:49","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you. What you're describing is called intrusive thoughts, part of a condition called OCD. It's common after birth. The thoughts stick because they're the opposite of what you want. Thoughts like this don't make it more likely you'll harm him.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"How do you know I won't do it?"},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"Because of how you respond to them. You hate them, fight them and protect him. That's the pattern of OCD. I'd worry if you wanted to act, felt the thoughts were right, or heard voices. None of that is happening.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"So you're not going to call social services?"},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"No. From what you've told me, Leo is safe with you, and I'll write that reasoning in your notes.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Can you just say again that I'm not dangerous?"},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"I understand the need. Reassurance helps for a minute, then the doubt comes back. That's how OCD keeps going. Hiding knives works the same way. I've said it clearly once; treatment helps you cope without needing it.","m":[9,2]},{"t":"5:14","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"The main treatment is a type of talking therapy called CBT with exposure and response prevention. Step by step, you'd reduce the hiding and checking. I'll refer you to our psychologist this week.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:29","w":"P","s":"And tablets? I'm breastfeeding."},{"t":"5:33","w":"D","s":"Sertraline is the one we usually choose for breastfeeding. Very little reaches the baby. We'd start at 50 milligrams in the morning. It can make you a bit sick or jittery for the first week or two. It's your choice, with or without therapy.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:54","w":"P","s":"I'll think about it."},{"t":"5:58","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's fine. Please contact us the same day if the thoughts change: if you feel an urge to act, if they start to feel right, if you hear voices, or you stop sleeping. Out of hours, NHS 111 and choose the mental health option.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:19","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what you're taking away from today?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:26","w":"P","s":"They're OCD thoughts. They don't mean I'll do it. Therapy, maybe sertraline. Call if they change."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Exactly. With your agreement I'll update your health visitor and GP, and I'll see you in two weeks.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel like I can breathe."}],"global":{"pass":"Elicits ego-dystonic, resisted thoughts with avoidance, excludes intent and psychosis, screens mood and suicide, explains OCD safely and plans CBT with ERP.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis but thin psychosis screen, or repeated reassurance instead of explanation.","fail":"Makes a safeguarding referral on content alone, misses psychotic features or suicide risk, or reassures and discharges without treatment."}},"c-p-06":{"setup":"Wednesday morning, maternal mental health service clinic. Ms Chloe Bennett, 27, had an emergency caesarean under general anaesthetic 3 months ago; her daughter was in NICU. She has nightmares and avoids the GP surgery. Assess and explain the treatment.","role":{"who":"Ms Chloe Bennett, 27, female, patient","open":"My GP said I should come. I don't really know what you can do. It's not like you can change what happened.","manner":"Flat and guarded, then tearful describing waking alone. Ashamed of not feeling like a mum. Opens up and becomes hopeful once she hears it has a name and a treatment.","free":["Emergency caesarean under general anaesthetic 3 months ago","Daughter Isla was in NICU for 4 days","Has nightmares about the birth","Missed Isla's 8-week vaccinations because she couldn't go into the surgery"],"ifasked":["Worst moment: waking alone, nobody telling her if Isla was alive","Nightmares four times a week; flashbacks when she hears a siren or smells hand gel","Avoids the hospital road, the GP surgery and talking about the birth","Checks Isla constantly; jumps at noises; sleeps 4 to 5 hours","Mood low; guilt that her 'body failed'; still enjoys some time with her sister","No thoughts of suicide; no thoughts of harming Isla","'Doesn't feel like her mum': feels Isla bonded with her partner Jamie first","Stopped breastfeeding at 6 weeks"],"worries":["Will talking about it make it worse?","Will she be reported for not feeling like Isla's mum?","Will she have to go back into the hospital?"],"rules":["Tearful describing waking alone; settles if validated and given time","If asked for every detail, becomes overwhelmed and goes quiet","Discloses 'not feeling like her mum' only if asked gently and told it is common","Becomes hopeful when told the condition has a name and responds to treatment"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and lets her set the pace","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits the birth story and worst moment without demanding every detail","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her distress and guilt without minimising","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about re-experiencing: nightmares, flashbacks, triggers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about avoidance and its impact, including the missed vaccinations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about sense of threat: hypervigilance, startle, sleep","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood and asks directly about suicide and thoughts of harming the baby","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores bonding gently and normalises it","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives the diagnosis of PTSD after birth in plain words, without blame","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains trauma-focused CBT or EMDR: what happens, how many sessions, who delivers it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees birth reflections, parent-infant support and the vaccination plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises, checks understanding, gives crisis contacts and a review date","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Ms Bennett, I'm Dr Fraser from the maternal mental health service. Thank you for coming. Today I want to understand what's been going on since Isla's birth, and talk about what can help.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"My GP said I should come. I don't really know what you can do. It's not like you can change what happened."},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"You're right, I can't change it. But I may be able to change how it affects you now. We'll go at your pace, and you can stop at any point.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"0:47","band":"The birth","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what happened, only as much as feels OK today?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Labour was fine, then her heart rate dropped. Suddenly everyone was running. They put me to sleep. I woke up and she was gone."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. What was the worst moment for you?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"Waking up alone. Nobody told me if she was alive. For an hour. (She cries.)"},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. Waking up alone, not knowing, is one of the hardest things a mother can go through. Take your time.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Everyone says I should be grateful she's fine."},{"t":"1:41","w":"D","s":"You can be grateful and still be hurt by what happened. Both are true.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:49","band":"Symptoms","w":"D","s":"Does it come back when you don't want it to? Dreams, or feeling it's happening again?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"Nightmares four times a week. If I smell hand gel or hear a siren, I'm back there."},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"Is there anything you avoid now because it reminds you?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"The hospital road. Talking about it. And I couldn't go into the GP surgery. Isla missed her jabs."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard, and it tells me how strong the fear is. We'll sort the jabs together.","m":[3,5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"I feel like a terrible mum."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"You're not. Avoiding reminders is part of trauma, not a sign of how much you care. Do you feel on edge, as if something bad is about to happen?","m":[3,6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"All the time. I check her constantly. I jump at every noise. I sleep maybe four or five hours."},{"t":"3:05","band":"Mood and risk","w":"D","s":"How's your mood? Do you blame yourself for anything?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"Low. I think my body failed her."},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"Nothing you did caused her heart rate to drop. That guilt is part of the trauma. Have you had thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?","m":[3,7]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"No. I'd never leave her."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of harming Isla?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"No. God, no."},{"t":"3:43","band":"Bonding","w":"D","s":"Your GP said you told her you don't feel like her mum. Can you tell me about that? It's something many mums feel after a birth like yours.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"She was with Jamie first. In NICU. I feel like she's his. I'm scared to say that."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"Thank you for trusting me with it. After being separated and so frightened, that feeling is common. It usually gets better as the trauma is treated, and we can help directly too.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:24","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"What you're describing has a name: post-traumatic stress disorder, after a traumatic birth. The memory hasn't been filed away as past, so your body reacts as if it's still happening. It isn't weakness, and it's very treatable.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"So I'm not going mad."},{"t":"4:47","w":"D","s":"No, you're not. The main treatment is a talking therapy focused on the trauma. Either trauma-focused CBT, or EMDR, which uses eye movements while you hold the memory in mind.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"Won't talking about it make it worse?"},{"t":"5:08","w":"D","s":"It's a fair worry. You go back to it in a planned, safe way, at your pace. That's what makes the memory lose its power. Usually 8 to 12 weekly sessions with one of our psychologists, here, not at the hospital.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"Do I need tablets?"},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"They're not the first choice. If you'd prefer them, or your mood drops further, sertraline is an option, and we can talk about it.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:45","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Three other things. A birth reflections appointment, where a senior doctor goes through your notes and explains why things happened. Some parent and baby sessions to help you and Isla. And I'll ask your health visitor to give Isla's jabs at home.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"At home? That would help so much."},{"t":"6:11","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what you're taking away from today?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:17","w":"P","s":"It's PTSD from the birth. It's treatable. Therapy here, a notes appointment, help with Isla, jabs at home."},{"t":"6:27","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. If things get worse, or you have thoughts of self-harm or Isla, ring us the same day; out of hours, NHS 111, mental health option. I'll see you in four weeks.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel like someone finally gets it."}],"global":{"pass":"Takes the trauma history at her pace, covers all three PTSD clusters, screens mood and risk, normalises bonding, and explains trauma-focused therapy with a practical plan.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis but misses the risk questions or the bonding concern, or gives a vague treatment description.","fail":"Pushes for every detail, offers tablets first, treats bonding difficulty as a safeguarding issue, or gives no plan."}},"c-p-07":{"setup":"Wednesday 16:30, mother and baby unit. Hannah Lewis, 32, was admitted under Section 2 two days ago with postpartum psychosis, 11 days after her first baby. She has consented to sharing. Her husband wants to talk. Explain the illness and plan with him.","role":{"who":"Mr Tom Lewis, 34, Hannah's husband","open":"Thanks for seeing me. Nobody's really told me what's going on. Is she going to be OK?","manner":"Exhausted, polite and frightened; slightly defensive about the Section. Breaks down briefly when he talks about not spotting it; steadies when reassured it is not his fault, and becomes practical.","free":["She stopped sleeping about four days after Alfie was born, then talked non-stop","On the last night she said Alfie was 'not really ours' and was sending her signals","He has hardly slept for a week and is back at work on Monday","He wants to know whether she will ever be 'herself' again"],"ifasked":["Hannah's mother was in hospital for 'a breakdown' a few weeks after Hannah was born; nobody talks about it","His sister is staying with him and helping","He feels low and keeps replaying the night she was admitted; no thoughts of harming himself","He is drinking two or three beers a night to get to sleep","They had hoped to have a second child in a couple of years","He did not know he could visit outside set times or help with Alfie on the unit"],"worries":["Why is she on a Section if she's not dangerous?","Could she hurt Alfie?","Can we ever have another baby?","Is this my fault for not getting help sooner?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when talking about the night of admission; settles if his guilt is named and lifted","If told the future risk is low, he later says 'so we'll be fine next time?' and relaxes completely (a sign of false reassurance)","Only mentions Hannah's mother if asked about family history","Accepts help for himself only if it is framed as helping Hannah and Alfie"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Hannah's consent to share, and asks how he is before explaining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Finds out what he already knows and what he fears most","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names postpartum psychosis and explains it in plain words, without jargon","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States clearly that it is not caused by him or Hannah","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why the Section is in place and his rights as nearest relative","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives an honest outlook: full recovery for most, weeks to settle, months to recover, low mood possible","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives the future-pregnancy risk as about 1 in 2 and how planning lowers it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about family history of mental illness after childbirth","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees concrete ways he can help on the unit and tells him what changes to report","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks about his sleep, mood, alcohol and thoughts of self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his distress and guilt before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers carer's assessment, partner peer support and a family meeting with the consultant","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding and summarises the plan with a named contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"He leaves understanding the illness, the outlook and the 1-in-2 future risk, free of blame, with clear jobs and support for himself.","borderline":"Accurate explanation but rushed; little attention to his own state, or recurrence risk given vaguely.","fail":"False reassurance about future pregnancies, jargon-heavy lecture, or ignoring his distress and own risk."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Lewis. I'm Dr Rahman, one of the doctors on the unit. Hannah has said she's happy for us to talk about her care with you. Before anything else, how are you holding up?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Thanks for seeing me. Nobody's really told me what's going on. Is she going to be OK?"},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"I'll answer that properly, I promise. Can I first ask what you've been told so far, and what's worrying you most?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Just that she's got psychosis and she's on a Section. And I keep thinking she might hurt Alfie. Or herself."},{"t":"0:38","w":"D","s":"Those are frightening things to carry, especially on no sleep. Let's go through them one at a time.","m":[11]},{"t":"0:45","band":"Their story","w":"D","s":"What did you notice in the days before she came in?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"She stopped sleeping about four days after he was born. Then she talked non-stop. The last night she said Alfie wasn't really ours, that he was sending her signals."},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"I thought she was just excited. I should have seen it. I should have rung someone days earlier."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much that's weighing on you. It came on fast, and the early signs look a lot like new-baby excitement. You did notice, and you got her help. That's why she's here.","m":[11,4]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"Yeah. OK. Sorry."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"No need to be sorry. Take your time.","m":[11]},{"t":"1:30","band":"Explain the illness","w":"D","s":"What Hannah has is called postpartum psychosis. It's a serious illness that can start in the first days or weeks after a birth. It affects about one or two women in every thousand.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"Her mood and thinking have been thrown off course, so she believes things that aren't true, like the idea about Alfie. It's an illness of the brain, a bit like a very high fever of the mind.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"But why her? She's never had anything like this."},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"Often there's no single reason. The big hormone changes and loss of sleep after birth play a part, and some families are more prone. It isn't caused by anything you or Hannah did.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"Can I ask, has anyone in Hannah's family had a serious mood illness, or been unwell after having a baby?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"Actually, her mum was in hospital for a breakdown after Hannah was born. Nobody ever talks about it."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful, thank you. It makes this illness more likely, and it'll help us plan. I'll make sure the consultant knows.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:39","band":"The Section","w":"P","s":"She's not dangerous, though. Why has she been sectioned?"},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"I understand it feels wrong. She's very unwell and can't see that she is, so the Section lets us treat her safely. Section 2 lasts up to 28 days and is reviewed all the time.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:57","w":"D","s":"As her husband, you're her nearest relative. That gives you rights: to be kept informed, and to ask for her discharge in writing. The team would talk with you first if that ever came up.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"OK. I didn't know that."},{"t":"3:14","band":"Outlook","w":"P","s":"So will she be back to normal? Will she be Hannah again?"},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Most women make a full recovery. With medicine, sleep and care here, the worst usually settles over a few weeks. Getting fully back to herself often takes months.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"Many women feel low or anxious after the high phase passes. That's part of recovery, not going backwards, so please don't be alarmed if you see it.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"And Alfie? Is he safe with her?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"He's cared for here with staff around them all the time, so he's safe. As she gets better, those risks fall. If she says anything that worries you, tell the nurse straight away.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:58","band":"Future pregnancy","w":"P","s":"We'd talked about having another one. Is that off the table now?"},{"t":"4:04","w":"D","s":"Many couples do have more children. I'll be honest with you: after one episode, there's about a one in two chance it could happen again after a later birth.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"One in two. God. That's a lot."},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"It is a lot, and it's hard to hear today. The good news is planning makes a real difference. Seeing the perinatal team before trying, specialist care in pregnancy, protected sleep, and sometimes medicine straight after the birth.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"4:34","w":"D","s":"So it's not a no. It's a plan made together, well before.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:39","band":"How he can help","w":"P","s":"What can I actually do? I feel useless."},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"You're far from useless. Three things help most. Short, calm visits, any time, not just set hours. Helping her care for Alfie here, like bath time with the nursery nurse. And telling us about any change you notice.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"If she says something odd, don't argue with it. Just be calm, and gently move on.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"I can do that. I didn't know I could do bath time."},{"t":"5:10","band":"Him","w":"D","s":"And you. How are you sleeping, and how's your mood?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Barely sleeping. I keep replaying that night. I've been having a couple of beers to get off."},{"t":"5:22","w":"D","s":"That's understandable after a shock like this. The beer may get you to sleep but it wakes you later. Any thoughts of self-harm or of wishing you weren't here?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that. I just need to get through it."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. Would you see your GP this week about sleep and time off? You're back at work Monday, and that sounds too soon.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"5:49","w":"D","s":"I'll also ask our social worker to arrange a carer's assessment, and give you details for Action on Postpartum Psychosis. They have partners who've been exactly where you are.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:01","w":"P","s":"That would help, actually."},{"t":"6:04","w":"D","s":"And our consultant would like to meet you both on Friday at two, to talk about treatment and that future plan.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:12","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Just so I can check I've explained it clearly, what will you tell your sister tonight?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"That it's postpartum psychosis, it's not our fault, most women get completely better but it takes months. And if we have another baby, we plan it with the team first."},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Here's a leaflet, and the nurse in charge's number, any time, day or night. Is there anything I haven't answered?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you. That's the first time it's made sense."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me about her mum. Go and see them both, and I'll see you on Friday.","m":[13,1]}]},"c-p-08":{"setup":"Thursday 11:00, home visit. Amira Haddad, 29, is six weeks after her first baby and severely depressed despite a week of crisis team visits. Your consultant has found an MBU bed. Recheck risk, explain the admission and agree it with her.","role":{"who":"Mrs Amira Haddad, 29, mother of six-week-old Lina","open":"The crisis nurse said you'd come. I know I'm not getting better. I'm sorry.","manner":"Slow, quiet and tearful; apologises a lot. Becomes anxious and briefly resistant when the unit is mentioned; settles when her fears are answered one by one and she feels it is her choice.","free":["She sleeps about two hours a night even when Lina sleeps","She has stopped eating properly and has lost weight","She feels Lina would be better off with someone else, like her mother-in-law","She feels like a failure as a mother"],"ifasked":["Last week, crossing the road, she thought about stepping in front of a bus; she stepped back","She sometimes wishes she would not wake up; no plan today, and she would not leave Lina","She has no thoughts of harming Lina","She has not heard voices; she does not think Lina is ill or damaged","She is breastfeeding and worried about her milk","Karim is at work and does not know how bad it is"],"worries":["Social services will take Lina away","A proper mum wouldn't need to go to hospital","Can Karim visit, and how far away is it?","What happens if I say no?"],"rules":["Cries when talking about Lina being better off without her; settles if the doctor names it as the illness","Initially says no to admission; agrees only after the social services worry and Karim's visits are answered","Asks 'what if I say no?' and only trusts the doctor if answered honestly"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and asks how she has been since the crisis team started","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and the recent bus incident","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about thoughts of harming the baby","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for psychotic features such as voices or beliefs about the baby","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her guilt and distress, naming them as part of the illness","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains why admission is recommended now: worsening despite home treatment, and risk","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes the MBU concretely: baby stays, night support, feeding support, visiting","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers the fear about social services honestly and accurately","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers 'what if I say no' honestly, including the possibility of the MHA","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees medication change and physical checks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees practical arrangements: time, transport, packing, and telling her husband","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and confirms her consent freely given","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Risk rechecked including to the baby, admission explained concretely, fears answered honestly, and a freely agreed practical plan.","borderline":"Admission agreed but risk to the baby not asked, or MBU explained vaguely.","fail":"No risk check, coercive or misleading reassurance, or avoiding the social services and 'what if I say no' questions."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Amira. I'm Dr Rahman from the perinatal team. Thank you for letting us in. How have the last few days been since the crisis team started coming?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"The crisis nurse said you'd come. I know I'm not getting better. I'm sorry."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"You don't need to apologise. You're unwell, and it's our job to help. Tell me what's been hardest.","m":[5]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"I can't sleep, even when she sleeps. Two hours maybe. I don't eat. I just feel she'd be better off with someone else. Her grandmother is better with her than me."},{"t":"0:44","w":"D","s":"That sounds such a painful thought to live with. It's very common in depression after a baby, and it's the illness talking, not the truth about you.","m":[5]},{"t":"0:57","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When you feel like that, do you ever have thoughts about ending your life, or of not waking up?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I wish I wouldn't wake up. Last week, crossing the road, I thought about the bus. Then I stepped back."},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That must have been frightening. Have you had thoughts like that since, or made any plans?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"No plans. I wouldn't leave Lina. But the thoughts come."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"And have you had any thoughts of harming Lina, even thoughts that scare you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I just feel I'm not good for her."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"Have you heard voices, or felt that Lina is ill or damaged in some way?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"1:58","band":"Explain why","w":"D","s":"Amira, I'm going to be honest with you. The team has come twice a day for a week, and you're feeling worse, not better. With the thoughts about the bus, I'm worried about your safety.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"I think you and Lina need more help than we can give at home. I'd like you both to go to a mother and baby unit.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"A hospital? No. I can't. They'll say I'm a bad mother."},{"t":"2:32","w":"D","s":"I can see that's a big fear. Can I tell you what it's actually like, and then you tell me what worries you?","m":[5,7]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"2:46","band":"What the unit is","w":"D","s":"It's a small ward just for mums and babies. Lina sleeps in a cot beside your bed. There are nurses and nursery nurses there day and night.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:59","w":"D","s":"At night, if you express milk, they can do a feed so you can sleep. Sleep is one of the things that will help you get better.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"Can I keep breastfeeding?"},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Yes. They'll help you with feeding, and the pharmacist checks every medicine is fine for Lina.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:23","w":"P","s":"Where is it?"},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"It's about fifty miles away. Karim can visit every evening, and weekends are flexible. The social worker can help with his travel costs.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:37","band":"Fears","w":"P","s":"If I go in, social services will be involved. They'll take her off me."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd worry about that. Going to the unit is about getting you well with Lina beside you. It isn't a referral to social services. We tell the health visitor and GP, because they look after you both.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:03","w":"P","s":"And a proper mum wouldn't need to go to hospital."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"Depression after a baby is an illness. It's not a test of being a good mum. You've let us help, and that's what a good mum does.","m":[5]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","s":"What if I say no?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"It's your decision, and I'd listen to why. I'll be honest, though. I'm worried enough that if things got worse, we might have to think about the Mental Health Act. I'd much rather we decide this together, today.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"You'd section me?"},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"Only if things got much worse and you couldn't stay safe. That's not where we are. Right now, I'm asking you.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:56","band":"Plan","w":"P","s":"How long would I be there?"},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"It varies. Often a few weeks. We'd review it with you every week, and you'd go home when you and the team feel ready.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:11","w":"D","s":"We'll also increase your sertraline from 50 to 100 milligrams, starting today. It's fine with breastfeeding. And they'll do some blood tests, including thyroid and iron, because they can drag your mood down too.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:27","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"Do you think you could go today, with Lina?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Can Karim know first? He doesn't know how bad it's been."},{"t":"5:41","w":"D","s":"Of course. Would you like to ring him now, or shall I speak to him with you?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Can you? I don't think I can say it."},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"I'll do it with you here. Then Lucy, the nurse, can drive you both at three. Your mother-in-law can help pack: Lina's things, your clothes, chargers, and anything that comforts you.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:09","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Before we ring him, can you tell me in your words what's going to happen?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:18","w":"P","s":"Lina and I go to the unit today. They help at night so I can sleep. It's not social services. Karim can come in the evenings."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. And is this something you're agreeing to, not something you feel pushed into?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:38","w":"P","s":"I'm scared. But yes. I want to feel like her mum again."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"That's a brave decision, and I think it's the right one. Until you go, Lucy will stay with you. Let's ring Karim.","m":[12,5]}]},"c-p-09":{"setup":"Tuesday 14:00, CMHT assessment clinic. Kwame Mensah, 35, is referred by his GP with low mood since his daughter was born three months ago. Take a history, assess risk to himself and the baby, and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Kwame Mensah, 35, father of three-month-old Ama","open":"Thanks. I'm probably wasting your time. All new dads are knackered, aren't they?","manner":"Polite, guarded and self-critical; plays things down at first. Opens up when not judged, and becomes tearful when talking about shouting at the baby. Relieved and practical once he hears a plan.","free":["Ama was born by emergency caesarean section after a long labour","He is tired all the time and irritable at work and home","He has been staying late at work rather than going home","He feels guilty because Efua had the operation, not him"],"ifasked":["Mood low most days for about two months; nothing feels enjoyable, including football","He lies awake when Ama is asleep and wakes at 4 a.m.","Four cans of 4.5% lager most nights","He had depression at 20; sertraline helped","He has images of the birth, the alarm and the theatre, that pop into his head","Once at 3 a.m. he shouted at Ama, put her in her cot and walked out for five minutes","Sometimes thinks the family would be better off without him; no plans; his faith and Ama stop him","Efua seems fine and is sleeping better than him"],"worries":["Will the health visitor think I'm a danger to Ama?","I don't want tablets","Will my work find out?"],"rules":["Says 'I'm fine, just tired' unless asked specific questions","Only discloses the shouting episode if asked about crying or anger in a non-judgemental way","Becomes tearful about the shouting; settles if the safe action is praised","Accepts sertraline if reminded it helped before and given a review date"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and responds to 'wasting your time' with reassurance","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes low mood, anhedonia and duration","d":"Content"},{"c":"Separates depressive sleep problems from baby-related waking","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about the birth and screens for intrusive memories","d":"Content"},{"c":"Quantifies alcohol in units and asks about past depression and treatment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about anger and coping with crying, and about thoughts of harming the baby","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts, plans and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his guilt and distress and praises safe coping","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about the partner's mental health","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the diagnosis of depression in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees sertraline with dose, side effects and review, and Talking Therapies referral","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees an alcohol goal, explains information sharing with the health visitor, and gives a crisis plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and summarises next steps with dates","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Clear diagnosis of depression, risk to self and baby assessed without judgement, and a dated plan he can repeat back.","borderline":"Depression identified but alcohol not quantified, or baby risk asked awkwardly or not at all.","fail":"Dismissed as tiredness, no risk enquiry, or a judgemental reaction to his anger that shuts down disclosure."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Mensah. I'm Dr Rahman, one of the psychiatry doctors. Your GP asked us to see you. Is Kwame OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Kwame's fine. Thanks. I'm probably wasting your time. All new dads are knackered, aren't they?"},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"You're not wasting my time at all. Being tired with a baby is normal, but your GP was worried about more than that. What made you go?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Efua made me. She says I'm snappy all the time. I stay late at work so I don't have to go home. That's not like me."},{"t":"0:44","band":"Their story","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been since Ama was born?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Low, I suppose. Most days for a couple of months. Nothing's fun. I used to love football. I don't even watch it now."},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"When Ama's asleep, are you able to sleep?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"No. That's the stupid thing. She sleeps and I lie there. And I wake at four, every day."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"That's an important difference. Waking because a baby cries is normal; lying awake when she's asleep, and waking early, often points to depression.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"How was the birth for you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"Horrible. The alarm went off, everyone ran, they took her to theatre. I sat in a corridor thinking they'd both die."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. Do pictures of that day come back when you don't want them to?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Sometimes. The alarm, mostly. But Efua had the operation, not me. I've got no right to feel like this."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Her recovery matters, and so do you. Partners can be badly shaken by an emergency birth too. Feeling low doesn't take anything away from her.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:12","band":"Drinking and past","w":"D","s":"How much are you drinking in a typical week?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"Few cans at night. Four, maybe. It helps me switch off."},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"Four cans of normal-strength lager every night is about fifty units a week, more than three times the low-risk limit. Has anything like this happened before?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"At uni. I was on sertraline for a year. It worked."},{"t":"2:43","band":"Risk to the baby","w":"D","s":"Lots of parents tell me that when a baby cries at 3 a.m. and won't settle, they feel real anger. How is that for you?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"Once I shouted at her. Really shouted. Then I put her in her cot and walked out. What kind of dad does that?"},{"t":"3:07","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; I know that took courage. You put her down safely and walked away. That's exactly the right thing to do.","m":[8,6]},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had thoughts of harming her, or worried that you might?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I'd never touch her. I just hated myself."},{"t":"3:32","band":"Risk to self","w":"D","s":"When you feel that low, do you have thoughts of self-harm or that the family would be better off without you?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:43","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think they'd be better off without me. I haven't thought about doing anything."},{"t":"3:51","w":"D","s":"What stops you?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"Ama. And my faith. I'd never leave her."},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"Those are strong reasons. If those thoughts got stronger, or you started to think of a way, I'd need you to tell someone straight away.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"How is Efua in her mood?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"She's good, honestly. Better than me."},{"t":"4:20","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Kwame, what you're describing sounds like depression: low mood, losing interest, poor sleep and guilt for two months. It happens to about one in ten dads after a baby. It's treatable.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"So I'm not just weak."},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"Not at all. It's an illness, and you've come for help. That's the opposite of weak.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:47","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'd suggest a few things. First, talking therapy through NHS Talking Therapies. You can refer yourself online today.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"Second, sertraline again, as it helped before. Fifty milligrams in the morning. The first two weeks can bring a bit of nausea or jitteriness; it usually settles.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"I don't really want tablets."},{"t":"5:13","w":"D","s":"That's fair, and therapy alone is an option. You've pushed through for two months, though. Could we try it for six weeks and review together?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"OK. Six weeks."},{"t":"5:28","w":"D","s":"Third, the drinking. It wakes you at four and drags your mood down. Could we aim for no drinking on work nights to start?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"I can try that."},{"t":"5:44","w":"D","s":"I'd also like to let your health visitor know you're getting help, with your agreement. It's so she can support you both. It's not because I think Ama is unsafe.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:58","w":"P","s":"As long as it's not social services."},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"It isn't. If I ever had that worry, I'd tell you first. If you feel unsafe, ring 111 and choose the mental health option, or Samaritans on 116 123.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:17","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"Sertraline in the morning, refer myself for therapy today, no beers on work nights, and you'll tell the health visitor."},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Your GP will see you in two weeks. Is there anything I haven't covered?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. I feel better for saying it out loud."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you did. Go home to Efua and Ama. You'll get through this.","m":[13]}]},"c-p-10":{"setup":"Monday 10:30, maternal mental health clinic. Sophie Turner, 31, gave birth to her son Leo, stillborn at 38 weeks, six weeks ago. Her bereavement midwife asks whether she is depressed. Assess grief, depression and risk, and agree support.","role":{"who":"Ms Sophie Turner, 31, Leo's mother","open":"The midwife thinks I'm depressed. I don't know what I am. I just miss him.","manner":"Quiet, tearful and warm; brightens when talking about Leo. Becomes angry and sharp about being sent home with reduced movements; calms if the doctor listens without defending the hospital.","free":["She cries every day, in waves, especially when she sees prams or the nursery door","She held Leo and has photos and his footprints","She feels her body failed him","The post-mortem results are not back yet"],"ifasked":["She enjoyed a walk with her sister last week and laughed once, then felt guilty","She sleeps 5 to 6 hours and is eating, though less","She feels she is still a good person and a good partner","She sees the silent delivery room in her head three or four times a week and has nightmares weekly","She avoids the nursery and the road to the hospital","She reported reduced movements two days before and was sent home after a normal trace","Sometimes she wishes she could be with Leo; no thoughts of ending her life, no plans","Ben is struggling too but 'won't talk'"],"worries":["Am I depressed? Should I be on tablets?","Everyone expects me to be better by now","Will anyone listen about the reduced movements?"],"rules":["Softens noticeably if the doctor uses Leo's name","Gets angry about being sent home; settles if listened to and given the review route, escalates if the doctor defends staff","Only mentions the wish to be with Leo if asked sensitively about such thoughts"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, offers condolences and uses Leo's name","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Invites her to talk about Leo and what has happened since","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about moments of enjoyment and relief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about self-worth, sleep and appetite to separate grief from depression","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for intrusions, nightmares and avoidance","d":"Content"},{"c":"Listens to anger about care without defending, and explains the PMRT and PALS","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks sensitively about a wish to be with Leo, then active suicidal thoughts, plans and intent","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about protective factors and support, including her partner","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her distress and guilt","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains that this sounds like grief, not depression, and why no medication now","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees support: bereavement midwife, Sands, optional therapy, support for her partner","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a crisis plan, signs to return sooner and a review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Warm, unhurried, uses Leo's name, separates grief from depression on evidence, clarifies risk and agrees support with a review.","borderline":"Kind but misses the trauma screen or does not clarify the wish to be with Leo.","fail":"Labels grief as depression and starts medication, defends the hospital, or omits risk."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Sophie. I'm Dr Rahman. I'm so sorry about Leo. Thank you for coming in today.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"The midwife thinks I'm depressed. I don't know what I am. I just miss him."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"Of course you miss him. I'd like to understand how you're doing, so we get the right support around you. Would you tell me about Leo?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"He was perfect. Dark hair like Ben. I held him for hours. We've got photos and his footprints."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"He sounds beautiful. I'm glad you had that time with him.","m":[1,9]},{"t":"0:43","band":"Their story","w":"D","s":"How have the weeks since been for you?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"I cry every day. It comes in waves. Prams, the nursery door. Everyone wants me to be getting better. It's only been six weeks."},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"Six weeks is no time at all. There's no timetable for losing Leo. I'm not here to hurry you.","m":[9]},{"t":"1:09","band":"Grief or depression","w":"D","s":"Are there moments, even small ones, when the pain eases, or you can enjoy something?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"I went for a walk with my sister last week. I laughed at something. Then I felt awful for laughing."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"That happens to a lot of parents. Laughing doesn't mean you love him any less.","m":[3,9]},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about yourself as a person?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"I'm still me, I think. A good partner. But my body failed him. That's what I can't get past."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much you're blaming yourself. We don't know the cause yet, and the results will be gone through with you. How are you sleeping and eating?","m":[9,4]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"Five or six hours. I eat, just less."},{"t":"2:06","band":"Trauma","w":"D","s":"Do pictures of the birth come back when you don't want them to, or in dreams?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"The silence. When he was born and nobody said anything. I see that three or four times a week. Nightmares too."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"That sounds very distressing. Do you find yourself avoiding things that remind you?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"I don't go in the nursery. I drive the long way so I don't pass the hospital."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Anger about care","w":"P","s":"And the thing is, I told them two days before he wasn't moving as much. They did a trace and sent me home."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"That sounds incredibly painful to carry. Thank you for telling me.","m":[9,6]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"Nobody's said if that mattered. Nobody's listened."},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"I'm listening. I can't tell you whether it changed things, and I won't guess. The hospital reviews every stillbirth, and parents are invited to send their questions. Your bereavement midwife can help you send yours.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"If you want to raise it as a concern now, PALS can help too. I'll give you their details.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"OK. I want them to answer that."},{"t":"3:31","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Some parents tell me that they wish they could be with their baby. Have you had thoughts like that?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"Sometimes. At night. I just want to be with him."},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have those thoughts ever turned into thinking about ending your life, or making any plans?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"No. No plans. I'd never do that to Ben, or my mum. And I want to try again one day."},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"Those are really important. Have you harmed yourself at all, or been drinking more?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"No. A glass of wine at the weekend."},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"Who's supporting you? And how is Ben?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"My mum, my sister. Ben's struggling but he won't talk."},{"t":"4:28","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Sophie, from what you've told me, this sounds like grief. It's deep and painful, and it's normal after losing Leo. You still have moments of relief, you feel like yourself, and you're sleeping and eating.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"That tells me you're not depressed right now. The pictures and nightmares are trauma symptoms, which are common after a birth like yours.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"So no tablets?"},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"Tablets don't treat grief. If it turned into depression, or the trauma symptoms don't ease, we'd talk about treatment then.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:07","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'd suggest keeping your weekly contact with the bereavement midwife, and looking at Sands. They have a helpline and local groups for parents, and Ben too.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:19","w":"D","s":"If you'd like to talk to a counsellor now, I can refer you. If the pictures and nightmares are still there in a couple of months, trauma-focused therapy works well.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:33","w":"P","s":"Maybe later. Could Ben get help?"},{"t":"5:37","w":"D","s":"Yes. He can come with you, or see someone on his own. Sands supports dads as well.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:46","w":"D","s":"If the wish to be with Leo ever becomes a thought of ending your life, ring 111 and choose the mental health option, or Samaritans on 116 123, and let us know.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"The results meeting and Leo's due date may be hard days. Let's plan who'll be with you then.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"My mum. She's said she'll come."},{"t":"6:13","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I'd like to see you again in four weeks. What will you take away from today?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"That this is grief, not me going mad. Send my questions through the midwife. Sands for me and Ben. Ring if it gets darker."},{"t":"6:33","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Is there anything I haven't asked that you wanted to say?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Just that you said his name. Thank you."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Leo matters. I'll see you in four weeks.","m":[1]}]},"c-p-11":{"setup":"Friday 14:00, perinatal clinic. Chloe Barnes, 19, is 30 weeks pregnant, with EUPD and cannabis and cocaine use. A pre-birth child protection conference is in three weeks and the social worker wants your report. Assess risks and talk honestly with her.","role":{"who":"Miss Chloe Barnes, 19, pregnant at 30 weeks","open":"I know why I'm here. You're all building a case to take my baby.","manner":"Angry, defensive and quick to feel judged; swears once. Softens when her fear is named and when strengths are mentioned. Becomes tearful and has an urge to self-harm when outcomes are discussed; steadies with calm, practical support.","free":["She grew up in foster care from age 9","She has cut down cannabis from 'all day' to a couple of joints at night","She has been to every midwife appointment","She is terrified the baby will be taken at birth"],"ifasked":["Last cocaine three weeks ago, at a party","Last self-harm two months ago, superficial cuts; urges weekly; she uses ice and calls Jade, her personal adviser","Fleeting thoughts of being better off dead when stressed; no plan; the baby stops her","Ryan pushed her into a wall four weeks ago and checks her phone; she has not told anyone","Smokes 10 cigarettes a day; no alcohol","She does not know she can see the reports or bring someone to the conference"],"worries":["If I do everything you say, will I keep her?","What will you write about me?","I'm not going to that conference"],"rules":["Becomes more hostile if the doctor is defensive or lists her problems first","Only discloses Ryan's behaviour if seen alone and asked directly but gently","Asks for a promise she will keep the baby; trusts the doctor more if refused honestly","Says she wants to cut when outcomes are explained; settles with a calm, practical response"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms she is alone, and validates her fear and anger before explaining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what she understands and what she wants the conference to know","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the pre-birth assessment and conference in plain words, including her right to attend and bring support","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains possible outcomes honestly and refuses to promise one","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tells her what the report will say, including strengths, and offers to show it first","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses substance use specifically: cannabis, cocaine, alcohol and tobacco","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses self-harm and suicidal thoughts, with coping and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about domestic abuse directly and responds safely to disclosure","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Responds calmly to the urge to self-harm and agrees immediate coping steps","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains what will be shared with the social worker and why","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees concrete actions she controls: drug service, screens, skills work, IDVA","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, gives crisis contacts and arranges review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Honest, calm and non-judgemental; explains the process and outcomes without promises, assesses all risks including domestic abuse, and agrees concrete steps.","borderline":"Process explained and risks assessed, but strengths missed or domestic abuse not explored.","fail":"Promises an outcome, becomes defensive or punitive, or ignores self-harm or domestic abuse."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, Chloe. I'm Dr Rahman from the perinatal team. Is it just you today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I know why I'm here. You're all building a case to take my baby."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"I can hear how frightened you are. I'm not here to build a case. I'm here to help you, and to be honest with you, including about what I write.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"That's what they all say."},{"t":"0:34","w":"D","s":"That's fair, given what you've been through. Can you tell me what you've been told is happening?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"Social worker came. She said there's a conference. Basically a meeting to decide if I'm a fit mum."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"And what would you want that meeting to know about you?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"That I've cut down. That I go to every midwife appointment. That I'm not my mum."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"Those things matter, and I'll make sure they're heard.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:12","band":"Explain the process","w":"D","s":"Can I explain how it works? The social worker is doing a pre-birth assessment: what's going well, what worries people, and what help you need.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"The conference brings together you, the social worker, your midwife, me and others. You can bring someone, like Jade, your personal adviser. You'll see the reports before the day.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"I didn't know I could see them."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"You can. And you'll see mine before I send it.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"If I do everything you say, will I keep her?"},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"I wish I could promise that. I can't, and I won't lie to you. The meeting could decide on no plan, on support as a child in need, or a child protection plan.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"If they're very worried, they could look at a plan for after the birth, like a foster placement where you and the baby live together. If that happens, you'd get a solicitor.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"So they could take her. I knew it."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"It's possible, and I know that's terrifying. It's not decided. What you do in the next three weeks really counts.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:41","w":"P","s":"I want to cut right now. I can't do this."},{"t":"2:47","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me rather than hiding it. Let's stay with this for a minute. What helped last time the urge was this strong?","m":[9]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"Ice. Ringing Jade."},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"Shall we ring Jade together after we finish here? And there's a cold water bottle by the sink; hold it for a bit.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"OK. Yeah."},{"t":"3:16","band":"Risks","w":"D","s":"When did you last hurt yourself?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Two months ago. Just my arm. I get urges most weeks."},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"Have you had thoughts about ending your life?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"Sometimes, when it's bad. No plan. She stops me."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"She's a strong reason. Tell me about drugs and drink now: cannabis, cocaine, alcohol, cigarettes.","m":[7,6]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Couple of joints at night. Coke, three weeks ago at a party. No drink. Ten fags."},{"t":"3:53","w":"D","s":"That's a real drop from all day. That's going in my report as a strength.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"4:01","band":"Domestic abuse","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this in pregnancy. Has Ryan ever hurt you, frightened you or controlled what you do?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:11","w":"P","s":"He pushed me once. Into a wall. He was stressed. And he checks my phone."},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; that was brave. Being pushed isn't OK, whatever the stress. Can I ask a few more questions, so we can keep you and the baby safe?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"Fine. But don't make it worse."},{"t":"4:38","w":"D","s":"I'll go at your pace. I'd like to refer you to an IDVA, an advocate who helps with safety. Is that OK?","m":[8,11]},{"t":"4:49","w":"P","s":"Yeah, OK."},{"t":"4:51","band":"Sharing and the report","w":"D","s":"I do need to tell the social worker about Ryan, because it affects your safety and the baby's. I'll tell you exactly what I say, and you can help me word it.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"Will they hold it against me?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Being hurt by someone isn't your fault. Telling us, and accepting help, counts in your favour.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:19","w":"D","s":"My report will say your mental health history, the self-harm and drugs, and Ryan. It'll also say you've cut down, you've been to every appointment, and you're honest with me.","m":[5]},{"t":"5:33","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Three things you control before the conference. See the substance misuse midwife next week and do weekly drug tests.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:43","w":"D","s":"Join the skills group with our psychologist on Thursdays, for managing urges. And meet the IDVA.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:51","w":"P","s":"I can do that. I'm still not sure about going to the conference."},{"t":"5:59","w":"D","s":"If you're there, they hear your side from you. Jade can come with you. Would that help?","m":[3]},{"t":"6:07","w":"P","s":"Maybe. If Jade comes."},{"t":"6:11","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"You'll show me the report. Drug tests every week. The group on Thursdays. Some advocate for Ryan stuff. And ring Jade now."},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If things get bad, ring us in the day, or 111, mental health option, at night. 999 if you're in danger. I'll see you next Friday.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"You actually told me the truth. Nobody does that."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'll keep doing it. Let's ring Jade.","m":[12]}]},"c-p-12":{"setup":"Wednesday 09:30, perinatal clinic. Mei Chen, 27, is 28 weeks pregnant with panic attacks and constant worry about the baby. She has written that she wants no medication. Assess her and agree a plan led by psychological therapy.","role":{"who":"Ms Mei Chen, 27, 28 weeks pregnant with her first baby","open":"Before we start, I've written it on the form. I don't want any tablets. Please don't push me.","manner":"Tense, articulate and guarded about medicines; relaxes visibly once her choice is respected. Becomes tearful when describing fear of the baby dying in an attack; calms with clear explanation.","free":["Panic attacks three or four times a week for six weeks","She worries all day that something is wrong with the baby","She has stopped taking the bus and shopping alone","She does not want medicines because she has read they harm babies"],"ifasked":["Attacks: heart pounding, breathless, tingling hands, sure she will die; peak in about ten minutes","No breathlessness at rest, no chest pain, no swollen leg; palpitations only during attacks","She checks the baby's movements many times a day","She is terrified of labour since a friend had a traumatic birth","Had anxiety at university; CBT helped","Mood not low; sleeping six hours; no thoughts of self-harm or of harming the baby","Drinks four cups of coffee a day"],"worries":["Will the panic hurt the baby?","How do you know it's not my heart?","How long will I wait for therapy?"],"rules":["Stays guarded until the doctor explicitly accepts her no-medicines choice","If medication is pushed, she says she will not come back","Becomes tearful when describing thinking the baby will die with her; settles when reassured with facts","Mentions fear of childbirth only if asked how she feels about the birth"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and accepts her choice about medicines early","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes a panic history: symptoms, duration, frequency and triggers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about worry about the baby, checking, avoidance and fear of childbirth","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks physical red-flag questions for PE and cardiac causes and checks pulse and sats","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about past anxiety and what helped, and about caffeine","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses mood, self-harm and thoughts of harm to the baby","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her fear and distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains panic in plain words and reassures honestly that it does not harm the baby","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Recommends CBT as first-line therapy and explains timescales","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees self-help, caffeine reduction and blood tests","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Involves the midwife about fear of birth and keeps medicine as an option she can revisit","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives safety netting, including physical red flags, and a review date","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking her to summarise","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Respects her choice from the start, makes a confident diagnosis, excludes physical causes and risk, and agrees a clear therapy-led plan.","borderline":"Good plan but physical causes or fear of childbirth missed, or her choice acknowledged late.","fail":"Pushes medication or prescribes a benzodiazepine, or attributes breathlessness to panic without asking about PE."},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mei. I'm Dr Rahman from the perinatal team. Your midwife asked me to see you about the panic attacks.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Before we start, I've written it on the form. I don't want any tablets. Please don't push me."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me straight away. I won't push you. It's your choice, and the best first treatment for this is a talking therapy anyway.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Oh. OK. Good."},{"t":"0:38","band":"The attacks","w":"D","s":"Could you walk me through the last attack, from the start?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"I was in the supermarket. My heart started pounding. I couldn't breathe, my hands tingled. I was sure I was going to die."},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"How long did it last, and how often do they happen?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"It's worst after about ten minutes, then it fades. Three or four a week for six weeks."},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"And every time I think the baby will die with me."},{"t":"1:21","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying, to think that every time. Thank you for telling me.","m":[7]},{"t":"1:29","band":"Worry and avoidance","w":"D","s":"Between attacks, what do you worry about?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"The baby. All day. I check the movements constantly."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Are there places you've stopped going?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"The bus. Shopping on my own. I feel pathetic."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"You're not pathetic. Avoiding is what everyone does with panic. The trouble is that it keeps the fear going.","m":[7,3]},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about the birth itself?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"Terrified. My friend had an awful birth last year. She nearly died."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"No wonder you're frightened after seeing that. I'll make sure we address it.","m":[7,3]},{"t":"2:22","band":"Physical causes","w":"P","s":"How do you know it's not my heart? Or the pregnancy?"},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"Good question. Pregnancy changes the body, so I need to check. Do you get breathless when you're resting, or have chest pain, or a swollen, painful leg?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"No. Only during the attacks."},{"t":"2:47","w":"D","s":"And palpitations outside the attacks?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:52","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"I'll check your pulse and oxygen now, and I'd like blood tests for anaemia and thyroid, which can cause similar feelings.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Your pulse is 84 and regular, and your oxygen is 99 percent. That's reassuring.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:14","band":"Background and risk","w":"D","s":"Have you felt like this before?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"At university. I had CBT. It really helped."},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"That's really useful to know. How much coffee or tea do you drink?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"Four coffees a day. I'm so tired."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Any times you've felt so low you've had thoughts of hurting yourself?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"No. I'm anxious, not sad. I'd never hurt myself."},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"And any thoughts of harming the baby, even frightening ones?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"No. I just want her to be safe."},{"t":"4:04","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Mei, this sounds like panic disorder, with a lot of worry about the baby. In panic, your body sounds an alarm when there's no danger. Your heart races, you breathe fast, and that feels scary, so the alarm gets louder.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Attacks are frightening, but they pass and they don't harm you or the baby. Everyday stress doesn't damage her either.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"Really? I've been so scared it was hurting her."},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"Really. And getting help now is good for both of you.","m":[8,7]},{"t":"4:49","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"CBT for panic is the first-line treatment, and it worked for you before. I'll refer you to NHS Talking Therapies on the pregnancy priority pathway. You should be assessed within a couple of weeks.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"And until then?"},{"t":"5:09","w":"D","s":"A slow breathing technique: in for four, out for six. A self-help guide for panic. And cutting coffee to one a day, which will help the jitteriness.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:24","w":"D","s":"Taking the bus with your partner, short trips first, is a good start too.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"I'll let your midwife know about your fear of the birth, so you can talk through your options. There's a specialist service if the fear stays strong.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:46","w":"D","s":"If the therapy isn't enough, we can talk about medicine again, with honest facts. You'd always decide.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:56","w":"P","s":"That feels fair."},{"t":"5:59","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"If you get breathless at rest, chest pain, a swollen leg, or a bad headache with blurred vision, get seen the same day. That isn't panic.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:13","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:18","w":"P","s":"It's panic, not my heart. It won't hurt the baby. CBT referral, breathing, one coffee, blood tests. Tell the midwife about the birth. No tablets."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"Exactly that. I'll see you in two weeks. Anything else on your mind?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"No. I feel lighter. Thank you for listening."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You've done the hard part by coming. See you in two weeks.","m":[12]}]},"c-s-01":{"setup":"Saturday late morning, the emergency department. Jade Kowalski, 22, has been medically cleared after her fourth overdose this year. Take a history to reach a diagnosis, assess risk briefly, and explain what you think is going on.","role":{"who":"Ms Jade Kowalski, 22, female, patient","open":"Seriously? Another doctor. I've told everyone already. I just want to go home and feed my cats.","manner":"Irritable and guarded at first; engages once given a reason and treated with respect. Tearful when abandonment comes up. Warms noticeably if the diagnosis is explained without judgement.","free":["Row with boyfriend Callum by phone this morning: he accused her of flirting","Drank wine and took 16 paracetamol, then rang 999 about 40 minutes later","Fed up with hospital and wants to go home to her cats, Biscuit and Pepper","Feels she is always in rows and doesn't know why"],"ifasked":["Wanted to die at the time; no wish to die now","Mood switches within minutes, since about age 14; frequent anger; feels empty","Self-image changes with whoever she is with; dropped out of nursing, hairdressing and university","Relationships start perfect and turn sour within weeks; terrified of being left","Spent a whole Universal Credit payment in an afternoon; caution for kicking in a bus shelter","Wine 3 to 4 nights a week, about a bottle each time; cannabis at weekends, MDMA sometimes","Cuts her forearms with a razor about weekly to release pressure","Hit and pushed by, and hit, her previous boyfriend; Callum only shouts"],"worries":["Is she 'a psycho'?","Will she be kept in?","Can she have a tablet to stop the mood swings?"],"rules":["Refuses to engage if the doctor launches into questions without explaining why","Becomes tearful and says 'everyone leaves' if asked about the future; settles if validated","Was in foster care from 11; declines to discuss further, and this should be respected","If the diagnosis is explained kindly, says it's a relief to be listened to"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains why the conversation matters, and gains her agreement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her frustration and distress before probing further","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Clarifies today's overdose: trigger, amount, intent, alcohol and rescue","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores speed and triggers of mood shifts, anger and emptiness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about self-image and how stable it is","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores relationship pattern and fear of being left","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about impulsive acts, alcohol and drugs, and repeated self-harm","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for depressive episode and hypomania, using duration to separate them","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for voices and paranoia and clarifies stress-linked experiences","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses current intent, future risk and violence in relationships","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains the working diagnosis plainly, without blame, with hope about therapy","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks her understanding and responds to her reaction","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees crisis plan, means removal, referral and crisis contacts, then closes","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Jade, I'm Dr Iqbal, one of the psychiatry doctors. I know the medical team say you're fit to go. Is it OK if I sit down?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Seriously? Another doctor. I've told everyone already. I just want to go home and feed my cats."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"That's fair. You've had a long morning and you've repeated yourself a lot; I'm sorry. I'm asking because this is the fourth overdose this year, and I'd like to work out what keeps happening so the help actually fits. About fifteen minutes, then we plan getting you home. OK?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Fine. Go on then."},{"t":"0:35","band":"What happened today","w":"D","s":"Can you take me through this morning, from the start?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Callum rang. Said I was flirting with his mate at the pub. I wasn't. He screamed, I screamed back, told him to get lost. Then I had wine and took the paracetamol."},{"t":"0:54","w":"D","s":"How many tablets, and what did you want to happen?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"Sixteen. In that moment I wanted it all to stop. I wanted to die."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"That sounds like an unbearable moment. What happened next?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"About forty minutes later I panicked. Who'd feed Biscuit and Pepper? So I rang 999."},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"And now, how do you feel about being alive? Any thoughts about ending your life?","m":[10]},{"t":"1:22","w":"P","s":"Glad, I suppose. I don't want to die now. I just want to go home."},{"t":"1:28","band":"Emotions","w":"D","s":"When your mood changes, how fast does it happen, and how long does it last?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"Like a switch. Fine at lunch, want to die by two because a mate cancelled. Gone by tea time."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"How long has it been like that? And what about anger, or a feeling of being empty inside?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Since I was about fourteen. I lose it all the time, then hate myself. And empty, yeah. Like a hollow. That's partly why I drink."},{"t":"2:01","band":"Who she is","w":"D","s":"How do you see yourself, as a person?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Depends who I'm with. I change my clothes, my music, everything. Mostly I think I'm worthless."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"That sounds lonely and exhausting. Do your plans for the future change a lot too?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"Nursing, then hair, then uni. Dropped out of all of them."},{"t":"2:24","band":"Relationships","w":"D","s":"Tell me about your relationships. How do they usually go?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"They start amazing. He's perfect, then a few weeks in he's an idiot. I can't stand him and I can't stand him leaving."},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"What happens when you think someone might leave you?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"I feel sick. Panicky. I've begged. I've turned up at someone's house at three in the morning."},{"t":"2:51","band":"Impulses and self-harm","w":"D","s":"Do you ever act on impulse in ways you regret? Can you give me an example?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"Spent my whole Universal Credit in an afternoon. Got a caution for kicking in a bus shelter."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"How much are you drinking, and do you use any drugs?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"Bottle of wine three or four nights a week. Weed at weekends, MDMA sometimes. Never needles."},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"Apart from overdoses, do you hurt yourself in other ways?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"I cut my arms with a razor. Weekly, maybe. It lets the pressure out."},{"t":"3:29","band":"What it isn't","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being straight with me. I want to check a couple of other things. Have you had two weeks or more of feeling low every single day, whatever was going on, with your sleep and appetite off?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:42","w":"P","s":"Not really. If things are good, I'm fine."},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"And spells lasting days of feeling high, full of energy, needing hardly any sleep but not feeling tired?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"No. My ups last an hour, not days."},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"Have you ever heard voices when nobody's there, or felt people were against you?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"When I'm really stressed I think people are talking about me. I know they're not. And sometimes I go numb, like I'm watching myself."},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"Does that pass when things calm down? And do you ever hear voices coming from outside your head?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:18","w":"P","s":"It passes. No voices. Just my own thoughts telling me I'm useless."},{"t":"4:24","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"If things go wrong with Callum again, what do you think you'd do? And have rows ever turned physical?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"Probably the same. Everyone leaves. My mum left. I was in foster care from eleven. I don't want to talk about that. My ex and I used to hit each other. Callum just shouts."},{"t":"4:47","w":"D","s":"That's OK, we don't need to go into it today. Thank you for telling me. It makes sense that being left hurts so much.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"4:58","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Can I share what I think is going on? Quick mood swings, intense relationships, fear of being left, acting on impulse, cutting to cope, feeling empty, all since your teens. That fits a pattern called emotionally unstable personality disorder. Some people call it borderline.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:16","w":"P","s":"So I'm a psycho. Great."},{"t":"5:19","w":"D","s":"No. It's not about being bad or mad. Your emotions fire up faster and stronger than most people's and take longer to settle, often after a hard childhood. It's common, and it's treatable.","m":[11,2]},{"t":"5:31","w":"P","s":"Treatable how? Tablets?"},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Mainly talking therapy, like DBT, which teaches skills for strong feelings and relationships. There isn't a tablet for this itself, and lots of people improve a great deal. It isn't depression or bipolar, and you're not losing your mind. What do you make of that?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:51","w":"P","s":"Sort of a relief. Someone's actually listened."},{"t":"5:56","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what we've said?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"My emotions go from nought to a hundred, it's a known thing, and therapy helps."},{"t":"6:06","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"Exactly. You can go home today with a plan. I'll write to your GP and refer you to the community team for therapy. Let's make a crisis plan now, and get the rest of the paracetamol out of the flat.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:18","w":"P","s":"My mate Shannon's picking me up. She can take them."},{"t":"6:23","w":"D","s":"Good. If it gets bad: ring Shannon, the crisis line, or NHS 111 and choose the mental health option; 999 if you're in danger. Anything you want to ask before I go?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks for not treating me like an attention-seeker."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"You deserve to be taken seriously. Take care, Jade.","m":[13]}],"global":{"pass":"Earns her trust, gathers the full pattern with examples, rules out mood episodes and psychosis, covers risk, and explains the diagnosis kindly with a crisis plan.","borderline":"Covers most features but as a checklist without examples, or a thin explanation and no means removal.","fail":"Dismissive or jargon-laden, diagnoses bipolar from 'mood swings', or omits current suicidal intent."}},"c-s-11":{"setup":"Thursday morning, rehabilitation physical health clinic. Mr Martin Hale, 47, is well on clozapine. His annual results show metabolic syndrome. Explain the results, check his risk factors and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Martin Hale, 47, male, patient","open":"I read in the paper that people like me die 20 years early. Twenty years. And now you've got results. You're going to stop my clozapine, aren't you?","manner":"Anxious and a little defensive at first; protective of clozapine. Relaxes and becomes keen once told clozapine stays. Wary of more tablets.","free":["Read that people with schizophrenia die 20 years early","Clozapine is the only drug that ever stopped the voices","Plans to stop smoking on Monday, cold turkey","Hungry all the time"],"ifasked":["Smokes 30 a day","Father had a stroke at 70","Turned down BP tablets from the GP","No alcohol or drugs","Eats crisps and microwave meals; cannot cook","Little exercise; can't afford a gym","No thirst or frequent urine"],"worries":["Will you stop my clozapine?","Why did nobody tell me about dying early?","I don't want lots more tablets"],"rules":["Stays guarded until told clearly that clozapine continues","Agrees to metformin and statin if each is explained; hesitates over BP tablets","Only mentions cold-turkey quitting plan as good news, not as a question"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Hale, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the doctors in the rehab team. Your yearly blood results are back. Before we look at them, what's on your mind today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"I read in the paper that people like me die 20 years early. Twenty years. And now you've got results. You're going to stop my clozapine, aren't you?"},{"t":"0:26","w":"D","s":"That's a frightening thing to read, and I can see it's shaken you. Let me say straight away: I've no plans to stop your clozapine.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"Really? Because it's the only thing that's ever worked. Before it, the voices never stopped."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"I know. Fifteen years well is a big deal, and we want to keep it that way. Today is about looking after your body alongside it. Is that okay?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"Okay. But why did nobody tell me about dying early?"},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry nobody talked it through. You deserved that. The gap is real, but a lot of it comes from things we can change. Can I ask about a few of those?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"1:18","band":"His risk factors","w":"D","s":"Do you smoke?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"About thirty a day. But I'm stopping Monday. Cold turkey. I decided after the article."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"That's a brilliant decision. We'll come back to how to do it safely. Is there any heart attack, stroke or diabetes in your family?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"My dad had a stroke at 70. Grandad too, I think."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"Thanks. The GP's mentioned your blood pressure before, I think. What happened?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Said it was high. Offered tablets. I said no, I feel fine."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Fair enough. Do you drink, or use anything else?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"No. Not for years."},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"And food: what did you eat yesterday?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Cereal, crisps, a microwave lasagne. I'm hungry all the time on this stuff. And I can't cook."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"That hunger is real; clozapine does that to many people. How much do you move in a day?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"Not much. I'm tired. The gym costs money."},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"Have you been very thirsty lately, or passing more urine, or getting up at night to go?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"2:34","band":"The results","w":"D","s":"Good. So, your results. Four things have shown up together: your waist, your blood pressure, your sugar and your blood fats.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Is that bad?"},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"Together they're called metabolic syndrome. It means your body is struggling to handle sugar and fat. Your sugar is in the prediabetes range: not diabetes, but heading that way.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"Is it the clozapine?"},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Most likely it's the main cause, with smoking and sitting a lot adding to it. Left alone, it raises the chance of diabetes, heart attack and stroke. That's a big part of the gap you read about.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"So it does have to go."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"No. You've tried other medicines and they didn't work. Switching could make you unwell again, so it's the very last resort. We treat each problem around the clozapine.","m":[3,11]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"Okay. Okay. What do I do, then?"},{"t":"3:42","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Start with smoking. It's the best thing you can do for your heart. But there's a catch: tobacco smoke keeps your clozapine level down.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"What do you mean?"},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"If you stop suddenly, your clozapine level can rise within days. You might get very sleepy, drool more, or get jerks. So let's move the date to two weeks, check your level before, then weekly for a month, and adjust the dose with you.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"I never knew that. Can I use patches?"},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"Yes. Patches don't affect the level. The stop-smoking service can set you up with a patch and a spray. Now food: a dietitian and the Healthier You programme work far better than going it alone. Would you try them?","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"If someone shows me what to buy. Not salad every day."},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"Fair. Your key worker could help with shopping and cooking twice a week. And a walking group is free. Which one change could you start this week?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Swap the crisps for fruit, maybe. And the walking group."},{"t":"4:59","w":"D","s":"Great. Two tablets could help as well. Metformin lowers sugar and can take the edge off hunger. One tablet with breakfast at first; some people get an upset tummy that settles. We check your kidneys before and once a year.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:19","w":"P","s":"And the other?"},{"t":"5:21","w":"D","s":"Atorvastatin, 20 mg at night. It cuts the chance of a heart attack or stroke. We check your liver and blood fats before and at three months. Tell us about any unexplained muscle aches.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:37","w":"P","s":"I said no to blood pressure pills before."},{"t":"5:41","w":"D","s":"I understand. I'll ask your GP to check your pressure at home for a week, so the decision rests on proper readings. Then you can decide.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"That's fair."},{"t":"5:55","band":"Safety and close","w":"D","s":"One safety point. Rarely, clozapine makes sugar shoot up fast. If you're very thirsty, passing lots of urine, vomiting or very drowsy, ring the GP or 111 that day, or 999 if you're very unwell.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:11","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thirsty, weeing, being sick."},{"t":"6:14","w":"D","s":"Exactly. So I can check I've explained it clearly, can you tell me the plan in your words?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:22","w":"P","s":"Keep the clozapine. Stop smoking in two weeks with blood tests. Metformin and the statin. Dietitian and walking. GP does my blood pressure."},{"t":"6:34","w":"D","s":"Spot on. I'll write it down for you and your key worker, send it to your GP, and see you in four weeks. What else is worrying you?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Nothing. I feel better than when I came in, actually."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and asks what he knows and what worries him before giving results","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his shock and fear before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"States clearly and early that clozapine will continue","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about smoking, family history, blood pressure, alcohol, diet and activity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about thirst, passing urine and other symptoms of high sugar","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the results as metabolic syndrome and prediabetes in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the health risks sensitively, linking them to the life expectancy gap","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Identifies the smoking and clozapine interaction and plans levels and dose review","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees specific lifestyle steps and refers to a dietitian or structured programme","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers metformin and atorvastatin with dose, side effects and blood checks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks the GP to confirm and treat blood pressure, and explains switching is a last resort","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives warning signs of very high blood sugar and what to do","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with a written plan, GP letter and review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Reassures early that clozapine stays, takes a full risk history, explains metabolic syndrome plainly, makes the smoking quit safe and agrees metformin, statin and GP actions.","borderline":"Correct plan but numbers read out without meaning, or the smoking interaction mentioned without a level and dose plan.","fail":"Proposes switching clozapine first, misses the smoking interaction, or hands everything to the GP without a plan."}},"c-s-12":{"setup":"Monday afternoon, CMHT clinic. Mr Adam Clarke, 35, has paranoid schizophrenia and is well on olanzapine 10 mg, but has gained 12 kg. Discuss his weight and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Adam Clarke, 35, male, patient","open":"I'm well now, I'll give it that. But I've put on twelve kilos again. I can't keep getting bigger.","manner":"Polite, motivated, a little embarrassed. Brightens when told it is not his fault. Reluctant about groups until offered choices.","free":["Well for three months on olanzapine 10 mg","Gained 12 kg since restarting","Wants to keep taking olanzapine","Stopped it two years ago because of weight and relapsed"],"ifasked":["Tried risperidone, quetiapine, aripiprazole, amisulpride, haloperidol: all less effective","70 kg before first olanzapine, 88 kg now","Always hungry; eats crisps and chocolate; bigger portions","Sleeps ten hours","Walks 3 km three times a week","Breathless on stairs; partner says he snores","Vapes; few beers at weekends","Father had a heart attack at 65"],"worries":["Is it the tablet or is it me?","Should I switch?","I don't want to go to a group"],"rules":["Embarrassed about groups; agrees if validated and given an alternative","Mentions snoring only if asked about sleep or snoring","Asks about switching after the explanation"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Clarke, I'm Dr Patel, one of the doctors in the team. I hear you wanted to talk about your weight. What's been happening?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"I'm well now, I'll give it that. But I've put on twelve kilos again. I can't keep getting bigger."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frustrating, especially after all the work you've put into getting well. I'm glad you've raised it. First, how have you been in yourself?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Good. No voices for three months. I'm back doing a bit at my brother's shop."},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"That's great to hear. Any worries about people, or feeling watched?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"No. That's all gone."},{"t":"0:51","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"Can you remind me what happened before you went back on the olanzapine?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"I stopped it two years ago because of the weight. A year later the voices came back and I ended up in hospital."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"That must have been hard. Which other medicines have you tried over the years?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Risperidone, quetiapine, then aripiprazole, amisulpride, haloperidol. None of them worked like olanzapine."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful to know. When did the weight start going on?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"Every time I start olanzapine. I was 70 kilos before I ever took it. I'm 88 now."},{"t":"1:38","w":"D","s":"So it comes on with the olanzapine each time. What's your appetite like?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Starving. All the time. I eat crisps and chocolate even when I try not to. My portions are bigger too."},{"t":"1:54","w":"D","s":"How much do you sleep, and how active are you?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"Ten hours a night. I walk about three kilometres, three times a week."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Well done on the walking. Have you tried any diets or groups?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"No. I'd be embarrassed. Sitting in a room being weighed."},{"t":"2:15","band":"His health","w":"D","s":"A lot of people feel that way. We'll find something that suits you. Any breathlessness, chest pain or sore joints?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"I get puffed out on the stairs. No chest pain."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Does anyone say you snore, or do you feel sleepy in the day?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"My girlfriend says I snore like a train now. Yeah, I'm tired a lot."},{"t":"2:42","w":"D","s":"Thanks; I'll come back to that. Do you smoke, vape or drink?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"I vape. Stopped cigarettes years ago. A few beers at the weekend."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"And has anyone in the family had heart problems or diabetes?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"Dad had a heart attack at 65. He had a stent."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"Your sugar and cholesterol tests at twelve weeks were fine. I'd like to repeat them today and check your waist and blood pressure.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Okay. Is it the tablet or is it me?"},{"t":"3:18","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Mostly the tablet. Weight gain is one of the commonest side effects of olanzapine. It turns your appetite up, so you feel hungry even when you've eaten enough.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"So it's not just me being greedy."},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"Not at all. But the weight does matter. It raises the chance of diabetes and heart disease, and with your dad's history, it's worth acting now.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:49","w":"P","s":"Should I switch to something else?"},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"I wouldn't. You've tried five others and none worked as well, and stopping olanzapine led to hospital. A switch could mean another relapse. Let's tackle the weight around it.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"Okay. What can I actually do?"},{"t":"4:11","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"A few things that fight the hunger. Three regular meals, no grazing in between. More protein and high-fibre foods, which keep you full longer. Smaller plates. And keep the crisps and chocolate out of the house.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"The last one's hard. But okay."},{"t":"4:33","w":"D","s":"Keep the walking and build up to thirty minutes most days. And honestly, people lose more weight in a structured programme than on their own. It covers food, exercise and the feelings behind eating.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"I don't know about a group."},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"The trust's healthy-living group is small and friendly. If that feels too much, the free NHS Weight Loss Plan app works at home. Which would you try?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"5:07","w":"P","s":"I'll try the group. Maybe the app as well."},{"t":"5:11","w":"D","s":"Great. Which two changes could you start this week?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:16","w":"P","s":"No snacks after eight. And walk every day."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Perfect. If you're still gaining in three months, there are tablets. Metformin helps people lose about three kilos on average. It can upset the stomach at first, and your GP checks your kidneys before starting.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:39","w":"P","s":"And the other one?"},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"Adding a small dose of aripiprazole helps some people lose about two kilos. It can cause restlessness or poor sleep. Neither is magic; they work best with the changes we've agreed.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:58","w":"P","s":"I'd try metformin first, if it comes to it."},{"t":"6:02","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's a sensible choice. About the snoring: it could be sleep apnoea. I'll ask your GP to look into it. Can you tell me the plan in your words, so I know I've been clear?","m":[6,12]},{"t":"6:18","w":"P","s":"Keep the olanzapine. No snacks after eight, walk every day, the group. Bloods today. Metformin if I'm still going up."},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"Exactly. We'll weigh you monthly and see you in three months. Please don't stop the olanzapine without talking to us. I'll write to your GP and send you a copy. Any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks for not just telling me to eat less."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and asks an open question about his concerns","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his frustration and embarrassment about his weight","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes a brief illness history, including current symptoms and last relapse","d":"Content"},{"c":"Links the weight gain in time to olanzapine and asks how other antipsychotics worked","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about appetite, diet, sleep, activity and past attempts to lose weight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for health effects such as breathlessness, chest pain and snoring, and checks monitoring","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about smoking or vaping, alcohol and family history of heart disease or diabetes","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains weight gain as a common olanzapine effect and its health risks, sensitively","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives specific diet and activity advice","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Recommends a structured weight programme and says why it works better","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains why a switch is not advised and discusses metformin and aripiprazole add-on honestly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees monitoring, follow-up and not stopping olanzapine, and closes with a written plan","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Links the gain to olanzapine, screens health and risk factors, explains without blame, avoids a switch, gives specific advice and a programme, and offers add-ons honestly with follow-up.","borderline":"Sound advice but thin history of previous medicines, or generic lifestyle advice without a programme or follow-up.","fail":"Advises switching or cutting olanzapine, gives no specific plan, or misses the physical health screen."}},"c-s-14":{"setup":"Saturday morning, acute psychiatric ward. Ryan Daley, 24, was moved to the medical unit overnight with neuroleptic malignant syndrome. His mother has arrived, angry. Ryan consents to full sharing. Explain what has happened and what comes next.","role":{"who":"Mrs Carol Daley, 51, female, Ryan's mother","open":"Finally. I got a phone call at three in the morning saying 'a side effect' and that's it. He came here to get better and now he's on a medical ward!","manner":"Frightened and angry; raises her voice at first. Calms when the doctor apologises, is honest and answers her questions directly.","free":["Phoned at 3 am and told only 'a side effect'","Told 'confidentiality' when she asked","Knows Ryan needs medication; it lets him work and keep friends"],"ifasked":["Noticed he was confused all week and told staff","Ryan had haloperidol before without problems","His father lives separately; she will update him","Never told NMS was a risk"],"worries":["What happened to him?","Is he going to die?","Can he take antipsychotics again?","Who will keep me updated?"],"rules":["Stays angry if the doctor is defensive or uses jargon","Asks directly whether the dose was raised too fast","Settles once honest outlook and a named contact are given"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and settle","w":"D","s":"Mrs Daley? I'm Dr Osei, one of the doctors on Ryan's ward. Would you come into the quiet room so we can talk properly? Ryan has agreed we can share everything with you.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:16","w":"P","s":"Finally. I got a phone call at three in the morning saying 'a side effect' and that's it. He came here to get better and now he's on a medical ward!"},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"That sounds awful, and I'm so sorry. Nobody should get a call like that and be left in the dark. What have you been told so far?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"Nothing! Every time I asked, they said confidentiality. What have you done to him?"},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"You have every right to answers, and I'll give you all of them. What's worrying you most right now?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"Is he going to die? Can he ever take tablets again? And who do I even ring?"},{"t":"1:05","band":"What happened","w":"D","s":"Those are exactly the right questions, and I'll answer each one. First, what happened.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"Ryan's had a rare reaction to his antipsychotic medicine, haloperidol. It's called neuroleptic malignant syndrome, or NMS.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"I've never heard of it. Nobody said it was a risk."},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"It's uncommon, and we can't predict who'll get it. Last night his muscles went very stiff, his temperature went up, his pulse was racing and he became confused.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"He was confused all week. I said so."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry. That must make this even harder. A blood test showed his muscles were under strain, which fits with NMS.","m":[3,5]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"So why did it happen? Did you put the dose up too fast?"},{"t":"2:07","w":"D","s":"It's a fair question. A quick increase in dose is one known risk factor. Being dehydrated and very agitated add to it. We're reviewing exactly how his doses were given, and you'll hear what we find.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Right. At least you're not pretending."},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"The medical team also checked for other causes, like an infection, and so far those tests are clear.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"Why couldn't you treat him here?"},{"t":"2:41","band":"Treatment","w":"D","s":"NMS can be dangerous, so he needs the medical ward: fluids through a drip, cooling and close checks we can't do safely here. Moving him quickly was the right thing.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"What are they actually doing?"},{"t":"2:59","w":"D","s":"The haloperidol was stopped straight away. He's on a drip, being cooled, and having a medicine called lorazepam to ease the stiffness and calm him.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"Anything else?"},{"t":"3:16","w":"D","s":"They're watching his kidneys closely, because strained muscles can affect them. They're also giving a small injection to prevent clots. If he were sicker, there are extra medicines they could add.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"Is he going to die? Just tell me."},{"t":"3:37","band":"Outlook","w":"D","s":"I can see how terrifying this is. I'll be honest: NMS can be life-threatening, which is why he moved fast. But I rang the medical ward this morning.","m":[3,8,10]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"And?"},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"His temperature's coming down, the muscle blood test is falling, his kidneys are fine, and he's more alert. Most people recover fully within one to two weeks.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"Oh, thank God. I thought you were going to say the worst."},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"That's completely understandable after the night you've had.","m":[3]},{"t":"4:19","w":"P","s":"But then what? He was hearing voices telling him to hurt people. He can't stay off tablets."},{"t":"4:28","band":"Restarting safely","w":"D","s":"You're right; he'll need treatment again. But not until he's fully recovered, at least two weeks after.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"And the same tablet?"},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"No. We'd choose a different medicine, start at a very low dose and increase it slowly. We'd check his temperature, pulse and blood pressure several times a day, and his blood tests twice a week.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"And if it starts again?"},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"We'd spot it early and stop the medicine straight away. Most people can take an antipsychotic again safely this way. You can be part of that decision.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"What about his mind while he's over there?"},{"t":"5:19","w":"D","s":"He's still under Section 2, and one of our nurses is with him. Our liaison team will see him every day and can give calming medicine if he's distressed.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:33","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"5:36","band":"Updates and close","w":"D","s":"Now, who you ring. His named nurse, Jo Price, will call you at four every afternoon. Here's the ward number for any time, day or night.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:49","w":"P","s":"Every day?"},{"t":"5:52","w":"D","s":"Every day. And I've booked you a meeting with his consultant on Monday at two. If you want to raise concerns formally, this leaflet explains PALS. It won't affect his care.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:08","w":"P","s":"I might. But this has helped."},{"t":"6:11","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. When you speak to Ryan's dad tonight, what will you tell him? It helps me know I've explained clearly.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"A rare reaction to the tablet, it's being treated, he should get better in a week or two, different tablet later, slowly. And a call at four."},{"t":"6:34","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. What other questions do you have before you go and see him?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"None for now. Can I go over there now?"},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Yes. I'll ring ahead so the medical ward expects you.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Ryan's consent and offers a private space","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what she has been told and what worries her most before explaining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges her anger and apologises for poor communication without being defensive","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains NMS as a rare, unpredictable reaction to antipsychotics in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes the features: stiffness, fever, fast pulse and confusion, with the muscle blood test","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names likely contributing factors honestly, including the quick dose increase","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Mentions other causes checked, such as infection","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains why NMS is serious and why he was moved to the medical unit","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains the treatment: drug stopped, fluids, cooling, calming medicine and kidney monitoring","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives an honest outlook with today's progress and usual recovery time","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains when and how an antipsychotic will be restarted safely","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains how his mental health will be cared for meanwhile","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a named contact, daily update time, consultant meeting and PALS information","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding and closes by inviting remaining questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Apologises and listens first, explains NMS plainly and honestly, gives treatment and a realistic outlook, sets out a safe restart plan and agrees named daily updates.","borderline":"Accurate explanation but defensive or jargon-heavy, or the future medication plan and update arrangements are vague.","fail":"Hides behind confidentiality, blames others, gives false reassurance or omits how she will be kept updated."}},"c-s-22":{"setup":"Community clinic, Wednesday morning. Irene Walsh, 74, with schizophrenia, had her quetiapine increased five weeks ago. Today's ECG shows QTc 520 ms; her potassium and calcium are low. Explain the ECG, check risk factors and agree a plan with her.","role":{"who":"Mrs Irene Walsh, 74, outpatient with schizophrenia","open":"Fine, love. Never better. The nurse said you wanted a word about my heart tracing?","manner":"Cheerful and chatty, sure she is well. Shaken when told it is abnormal and tearful about losing her tablet. Calms and cooperates once her fear is heard and a restart plan is offered.","free":["Feels completely well","Has never had heart trouble","Quetiapine went up five weeks ago because the voices came back"],"ifasked":["No palpitations, dizziness, faints, chest pain or breathlessness","Parents lived into their eighties; no sudden deaths in the family","Finished clarithromycin from the GP three days ago","Uses a dosette box; no extra doses","Takes indapamide and metformin","Daughter Karen is in the waiting room and can drive"],"worries":["Is it dangerous?","Why that tablet and not the others?","Can't I just go home?"],"rules":["Asks what QTc means if jargon is used","Becomes tearful about the voices returning; settles if validated","Resists going to hospital at first; agrees if the reason is clear and Karen can drive"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Walsh, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the doctors in clinic. Thanks for waiting. How are you feeling today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Fine, love. Never better. The nurse said you wanted a word about my heart tracing?"},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"I do. Has anyone talked to you before about heart tracings, or ECGs, and why we do them?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Only that it's routine with my tablets."},{"t":"0:29","band":"Tell her it's abnormal","w":"D","s":"That's right. I've looked at today's tracing, and I'm afraid it isn't normal. Can I show you what I've found?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"Oh. Go on, then."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Read the tracing","w":"D","s":"I go through it in order. Your heart rate is 75 and the rhythm is steady and normal. The size and direction of the beats are normal too, with no sign of strain or damage.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"So what's wrong with it?"},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"This gap here, from the start of the beat to the end of this wave, is called the QT. It's about 465 milliseconds. Corrected for your heart rate, it comes to 520. Eight months ago it was 446.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"I don't know what any of that means, doctor."},{"t":"1:22","band":"Explain in plain words","w":"D","s":"Sorry, let me put it simply. After each beat, the heart has to recharge before the next one. Yours is taking longer than it should to recharge.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Is that dangerous?"},{"t":"1:37","w":"D","s":"It can be. When the recharge is this slow, there's a risk of a sudden fast, chaotic rhythm that can cause fainting, or very rarely make the heart stop. It hasn't happened, and acting today makes it much less likely.","m":[9]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"(hand to her chest) Today? I feel perfectly well."},{"t":"2:00","band":"Symptoms and history","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you feel well, and that's a good sign. Have you had any fluttering or racing in your chest, dizzy spells, blackouts, chest pain or breathlessness?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Any heart problems in the past? And has anyone in your family died suddenly when young, or had a heart rhythm problem?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Never had heart trouble. Mum and Dad lived to their eighties."},{"t":"2:31","band":"Drugs and doses","w":"D","s":"Now your tablets. You're on quetiapine 600 at night since the increase five weeks ago, indapamide for blood pressure and metformin. Anything else? From the GP, the chemist, or a shop?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Just them. Oh, and antibiotics for my chest, from the GP. I finished them on Saturday."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"Do you know which antibiotic? And could you have taken an extra quetiapine by accident, say forgetting you'd had one?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"Clarithromycin, I think. And no, I use my dosette box. It's always right."},{"t":"3:09","band":"Why it has happened","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful. Several things add together. Quetiapine slows the recharge, more so at higher doses. Clarithromycin does too. And your blood test shows low potassium and low calcium, which make it worse.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Why are those low?"},{"t":"3:27","w":"D","s":"Indapamide, your water tablet, often lowers potassium. Being a woman and over seventy also tends to make the QT longer. We'll check magnesium today too, as low magnesium matters as well.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"So it might not be the quetiapine at all?"},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"It's a mixture, but the quetiapine increase is likely the biggest part, because the tracing was nearly normal on 400.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:55","band":"Her fear","w":"P","s":"(tearful) Please don't take it off me. Last year the voices came back and I couldn't leave the house for weeks."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening, and I can hear how much you don't want to go back there. Keeping you well matters to me too. I'm not taking you off treatment for good. We need to make it safe first.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"What does that mean, then?"},{"t":"4:26","band":"The plan today","w":"D","s":"Two things today. First, please don't take tonight's quetiapine. I've spoken to Dr Rahman, your consultant, and she agrees. Missing a few doses is very unlikely to bring the voices back.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"And the second?"},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"I'd like you seen at the hospital's same-day unit this afternoon. I've phoned the medical doctor there and they're expecting you. They'll repeat the tracing, monitor your heart and top up your potassium.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"Can't I just have a tablet and go home?"},{"t":"5:03","w":"D","s":"I understand you'd rather go home. At 520, the advice is to be checked and monitored the same day. Is Karen still in the waiting room? She could drive you straight there.","m":[9,11,10]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"She is. All right, if it's that important."},{"t":"5:22","band":"Follow-up","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Once your levels are back to normal, Dr Rahman will see you this week. If the tracing has settled, the plan is to restart quetiapine at your old dose of 400, and repeat the tracing a week later.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"And if it's still long?"},{"t":"5:43","w":"D","s":"Then we'd talk about switching to a tablet like aripiprazole, which barely affects the heart rhythm. I'll ask your GP to review the water tablet, and Sue, your nurse, will visit weekly to check the voices are staying away.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:02","w":"P","s":"Sue's lovely. All right."},{"t":"6:04","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"I've given you a lot. Could you tell me in your words what's happening today?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:12","w":"P","s":"No quetiapine tonight. Karen takes me to the hospital now for another tracing and potassium. Then Dr Rahman this week."},{"t":"6:21","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. If at any point you feel your heart racing, feel faint or black out, call 999 straight away. Have you any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:33","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you for explaining it properly."},{"t":"6:36","w":"D","s":"You're welcome. I'll write to your GP today, and I'll phone you tomorrow to see how you got on.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Bye, love."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces, asks how she feels and what she knows about ECGs","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Tells her clearly the ECG is abnormal before explaining","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Reads the ECG in order and states the QT, rate and recalculated QTc","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the slow recharge in plain words without jargon","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about palpitations, dizziness, blackouts, chest pain and breathlessness","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about previous heart disease and family history of sudden death","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks all medicines including recent antibiotics, and accidental extra doses","d":"Content"},{"c":"Links low potassium and calcium, diuretic, age and sex to the QTc, and plans magnesium","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the risk of a dangerous rhythm honestly, with clear urgency","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds to her fear of relapse with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Holds quetiapine with the consultant's agreement and arranges same-day medical assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees restart dose, fallback drug, repeat ECG, GP letter and CPN relapse checks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back and gives a 999 safety net","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Confirms and explains QTc 520 plainly, finds the drug and electrolyte causes, holds quetiapine, arranges same-day medical review and agrees a restart plan she accepts.","borderline":"Safe plan but jargon-heavy, or misses the antibiotic or electrolytes, or brushes past her fear of relapse.","fail":"Treats 520 ms as routine, switches to a high-risk antipsychotic, or never checks symptoms."}},"c-s-23":{"setup":"Community mental health clinic, Monday late morning. Dorothy Kemp, 74, with schizophrenia and diabetes, attends for a physical health check. Her ECG shows ST elevation. Check her symptoms, explain the finding and arrange what she needs.","role":{"who":"Mrs Dorothy Kemp, 74, outpatient with schizophrenia, diabetes and hypertension (the clinic nurse Femi and later a paramedic also speak)","open":"Bit of indigestion, doctor, that's all. I took two Rennies before I came. I didn't want to miss my appointment.","manner":"Polite and downplaying at first. Frightened and tearful when told it is a heart attack. Calms when the doctor stays with her and explains what will happen.","free":["Has 'indigestion' today, worse than usual","Took two antacids","Came by bus and has shopping in the car park"],"ifasked":["Central burning heaviness since about 8 am","Spreads into the left arm","Feels clammy and a little sick","No previous heart problems","No allergies; no ulcers or bleeding","Husband Alan is at home; number in her bag"],"worries":["Am I going to die?","Can't I just go home and rest?","Is it my schizophrenia tablets?"],"rules":["Asks what a STEMI is if jargon is used in front of her","Becomes tearful when told it is a heart attack; settles if validated and the doctor stays","Wants to go home until told clearly why she cannot"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Kemp, I'm Dr Patel. I've just looked at your heart tracing. Before anything else, how are you feeling right now?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Bit of indigestion, doctor, that's all. I took two Rennies before I came. I didn't want to miss my appointment."},{"t":"0:20","band":"Focused symptoms","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming. Show me where the indigestion is. When did it start, and what does it feel like?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","s":"Here, in the middle. Burning, like a weight on it. Since about eight this morning. Worse than usual."},{"t":"0:38","w":"D","s":"Does it spread anywhere, to your arm, neck or jaw? Do you feel sweaty, sick or short of breath?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"A bit into my left arm. And I've been clammy. I thought it was the bus."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had this kind of pain when you walk, or any heart trouble before?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"Never. My GP always says my sugar and blood pressure might cause a heart attack one day."},{"t":"1:10","band":"Recognise and act","w":"D","s":"(to the nurse) Femi, this ECG shows ST elevation in V1 to V4 and lead I, with reciprocal depression in II, III and aVF, rate 108. It's an anterior STEMI. Please call 999 now, then bring the emergency bag and the defibrillator.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Calling now. What do I tell them?"},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"Say: 74-year-old woman, central chest pain since 8 am, ECG shows anterior ST elevation, suspected STEMI, alert, in clinic. We need an emergency ambulance.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"(alarmed) Doctor, what's going on? What's a STEMI?"},{"t":"1:49","band":"Explain simply","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, that was a lot of medical words. Mrs Kemp, I'm worried that your pain isn't indigestion. The tracing shows you're having a heart attack right now.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"A heart attack? But I'm talking to you. I feel all right, mostly."},{"t":"2:09","w":"D","s":"One of the blood vessels feeding your heart has become blocked, so part of the heart muscle isn't getting enough blood. That's what's causing the pain. People can still talk and walk during a heart attack.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"Oh God. Am I going to die?"},{"t":"2:29","band":"Her fear","w":"D","s":"That's a frightening thing to hear, and it's completely natural to be scared. You're in the right place, the ambulance is coming, and the treatment works best when it's this early. I'm staying right here with you.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"(holding the doctor's hand) All right. All right."},{"t":"2:50","band":"First treatment","w":"D","s":"I want to give you an aspirin to chew, which helps thin the blood. Are you allergic to aspirin, or have you had a stomach ulcer or bleeding?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"No allergies. No ulcers."},{"t":"3:06","w":"D","s":"Then please chew this 300 milligram tablet now. Stay sitting back; don't get up. Femi will check your blood pressure, pulse and oxygen while we wait, and the defibrillator is just here as a precaution.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"Nurse: BP 146 over 88, pulse 108, sats 96 percent on air. Ambulance on its way, about ten minutes."},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Your oxygen's fine, so you don't need extra. The ambulance crew will give you something stronger for the pain.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:41","band":"Urgency","w":"P","s":"Can't I just go home and rest? My Alan will be worrying. I've got the shopping in the car."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"I understand you want to be home with Alan. But you mustn't go home or drive. You need to go straight to a specialist heart hospital, where they can open the blocked artery, usually within the next hour or two.","m":[5]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"Open it how?"},{"t":"4:11","w":"D","s":"Usually through a thin tube passed up from your wrist to the heart, while you're awake. They put a small mesh tube called a stent in the artery to keep it open. Every minute saved protects more heart muscle.","m":[5,4]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Is it my tablets? Did my schizophrenia tablets do this?"},{"t":"4:34","band":"Her question","w":"D","s":"That's a fair question. Mainly it's the diabetes, blood pressure and cholesterol, which have been high. Some antipsychotics, including quetiapine, can raise blood fats and sugar over the years, which adds to it.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Should I stop them, then?"},{"t":"4:52","w":"D","s":"No, please keep taking them unless the heart team say otherwise. They'll start you on tablets to protect your heart, including one for cholesterol, and we'll look at your quetiapine together once you're home.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:09","band":"Check understanding","w":"D","s":"I know this is a lot. Can you tell me what you understand is happening now?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:16","w":"P","s":"I'm having a heart attack, the ambulance is taking me to the heart hospital to unblock it, and I'm not to go home."},{"t":"5:27","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. Is it all right if I phone Alan to let him know and ask him to meet you there?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:37","w":"P","s":"Yes please. His number's in my bag. Tell him not to panic."},{"t":"5:43","band":"Handover","w":"D","s":"I will. Femi, could you print a copy of the ECG and her medication list: quetiapine MR 400, metformin, amlodipine, and last week's bloods. I'll write a handover note including her schizophrenia and the aspirin given at 11:52.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:01","w":"P","s":"Nurse: Printing now. Paramedics are at the door."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"(to the paramedic) Dorothy Kemp, 74. Central chest pain since 8 am into the left arm, sweaty. ECG: anterior STEMI, rate 108. Aspirin 300 mg chewed at 11:52. Obs stable. Diabetes, hypertension, schizophrenia on quetiapine. No allergies. Here's the ECG and the list.","m":[11,6]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Paramedic: Thanks, doc, great handover. We'll pre-alert the cath lab."},{"t":"6:30","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Mrs Kemp, you're in very good hands. I'll call Alan now, and I'll let your GP and Dr Hayes know this afternoon. I'll ring the ward tomorrow to see how you are.","m":[13,9]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you for not letting me go home, doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces and asks how she feels right now","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Recognises anterior STEMI from territorial ST elevation with reciprocal depression","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes a brief focused pain history: site, character, onset, radiation, sweating, cardiac history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains a heart attack in plain words without jargon","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why she must not go home or drive and why speed matters","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Has 999 called with a clear 'suspected STEMI' message","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks allergy and bleeding risk before giving aspirin 300 mg chewed","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Keeps her seated, monitors observations, has a defibrillator at hand and uses oxygen only if needed","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Responds to her fear with empathy and stays with her","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Answers her question about antipsychotics honestly and advises not to stop them","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Hands over ECG, medicines, aspirin time and history to paramedics","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding with teach-back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Informs husband, GP and consultant and closes with follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Recognises STEMI quickly, gets 999 called, gives aspirin safely, explains plainly and kindly, and hands over well.","borderline":"Right diagnosis and transfer, but slow to call, jargon-heavy, or forgets the aspirin allergy check or handover details.","fail":"Misses the STEMI, repeats the ECG or sends her by taxi or home."}},"c-s-24":{"setup":"Community psychosis team, Thursday morning. Your consultant hands you the annual ECG of a 45-year-old man on clozapine and asks you to present it systematically and explain what matters for clozapine. She will ask questions.","role":{"who":"Dr Fiona Gallagher, 58, consultant psychiatrist, community psychosis team","open":"Right, Dr Ali. Kwame Asante's annual ECG. Talk me through it as if I can't see it. I'll interrupt with questions.","manner":"Brisk and testing but fair. Hurries the trainee if they linger. Softens and becomes reflective when talking about a patient she lost; warms to honesty about limits.","free":["The patient is on clozapine 400 mg a day for three years","She wants a system, then the clozapine angle","She will interrupt with questions"],"ifasked":["Last year's ECG had a QTc of 405 ms","He has no cardiac symptoms","No dose change this year","The trust protocol checks troponin and CRP weekly for the first four weeks","He takes no other QT-prolonging drugs"],"worries":["Why bother with calibration?","Is Bazett good enough here?","How would you tell myocarditis from an infarct or pericarditis?","Once he's past the first months, are we safe?"],"rules":["Hurries the trainee along if they spend too long on one step","Asks about QT if the trainee forgets it","Mentions losing a patient to myocarditis; responds warmly if acknowledged","Rewards 'I'd check the protocol' over a guessed number"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Set up","w":"P","s":"Right, Dr Ali. Kwame Asante's annual ECG. Talk me through it as if I can't see it. I'll interrupt with questions."},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"Happy to. Just to check, do you want my full system out loud, then what matters for clozapine?","m":[14]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"Exactly that. Go."},{"t":"0:20","band":"Details first","w":"D","s":"This is Kwame Asante, 45, recorded today at 10:14 for his annual physical health check on clozapine 400 mg a day. Standard calibration: 25 millimetres a second, 10 millimetres per millivolt.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Why bother with calibration?"},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"If the paper speed or gain is changed, every interval and voltage I read is wrong. It takes two seconds to check.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:47","band":"Rate and rhythm","w":"D","s":"Rate: there are 14 QRS complexes on the 10-second strip, so 84 a minute. That fits the R-to-R gap of just under 4 large squares.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:59","w":"P","s":"And the rhythm?"},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"Regular. There's an upright P wave before every QRS, a QRS after every P, and the PR interval is constant. So sinus rhythm.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:12","band":"Axis","w":"D","s":"Axis: lead I is positive and aVF is positive, with similar size, so about plus 45 degrees. Normal.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"What would make you worry about the axis?"},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"Right axis deviation can come with tricyclic toxicity or a pulmonary embolus; left with an old inferior infarct or a conduction block. Neither here.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:36","band":"Intervals","w":"D","s":"PR is 3 small squares, 120 milliseconds, at the lower end of normal, so no heart block and no short PR. QRS is 2 small squares, 80 milliseconds, so narrow.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Fine. ST and T waves?"},{"t":"1:52","band":"ST and T","w":"D","s":"I've checked every lead against the baseline between the T and the next P. The ST segments are flat and isoelectric throughout. T waves are upright except aVR, which is normal.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:07","band":"QT and QTc","w":"P","s":"QT?"},{"t":"2:09","w":"D","s":"From the start of the Q to the end of the T is about 8 and a half small squares, 340 milliseconds. The RR is 0.71 seconds. By Bazett, 340 divided by 0.85 gives a QTc of about 400.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"Is Bazett good enough at 84?"},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Bazett overcorrects as the rate climbs, and clozapine patients are often tachycardic. Fridericia, using the cube root, gives about 380 here. Either way it's well under 450.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:43","band":"Summary","w":"D","s":"So in one line: sinus rhythm at 84, normal axis and intervals, no ST or T changes, QTc about 400. A normal ECG, and the same as last year's.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"Good. Now, before someone starts clozapine, what are you looking for?"},{"t":"3:02","band":"Before clozapine","w":"D","s":"Anything that clozapine could make worse: an existing arrhythmia or heart block, a long QTc, signs of old or current ischaemia, and left ventricular hypertrophy. Plus baseline troponin and CRP, so we can compare later.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"Why does an existing arrhythmia matter so much?"},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"Clozapine commonly causes a fast heart rate and can cause low blood pressure, so a heart that's already irregular may not cope. Those patients need cardiology advice before we start.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:36","band":"Myocarditis","w":"P","s":"Talk to me about myocarditis."},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"It's inflammation of the heart muscle. It's most likely in the first two months, mostly the first four weeks. Look for fever, a persistent fast pulse, chest pain, breathlessness or a flu-like illness.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"And on the ECG?"},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"Often just a sinus tachycardia with non-specific ST and T wave changes. That's why most UK protocols check troponin and CRP weekly for the first four weeks. A clearly raised troponin or CRP means stop clozapine and get urgent medical review.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:16","band":"Telling them apart","w":"P","s":"How would you tell myocarditis from an infarct, or from pericarditis?"},{"t":"4:21","w":"D","s":"An infarct gives ST elevation in one territory, with reciprocal depression opposite. Pericarditis gives widespread, saddle-shaped elevation with PR depression and no reciprocal change. Myocarditis is usually patchier and non-specific.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"(quietly) I lost a man to clozapine myocarditis in my first year as a consultant. His ECG looked almost normal."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry. That must stay with you, and it explains why you check so carefully. It's a good reminder that a near-normal tracing doesn't rule it out, which is why the bloods and his symptoms matter so much.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:03","band":"When to repeat","w":"P","s":"It does. So once he's past the first months, are we safe?"},{"t":"5:08","w":"D","s":"Not completely. Cardiomyopathy can appear at any time, so new breathlessness, ankle swelling or reduced exercise tolerance needs an ECG, BNP and an echo. And his metabolic risk raises his heart attack risk over the years.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"When would you repeat his ECG, then?"},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"At baseline, after dose increases, at least yearly with his physical health check, and at once with any chest pain, palpitations, fainting, breathlessness or a resting pulse that stays high. I'll admit I'd want to check our trust protocol for the exact titration schedule.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Honest answer. And clozapine's effect on the QT?"},{"t":"5:53","w":"D","s":"Low compared with haloperidol or quetiapine, but it adds up with other drugs like citalopram or macrolides, so I check his full drug list each time.","m":[7,12]},{"t":"6:06","band":"Close","w":"P","s":"Good. Anything else doctors should know about ECGs?"},{"t":"6:09","w":"D","s":"Always compare with the last one, and if I'm unsure, I ask the medical team rather than file it. Could you give me feedback on what I could tighten?","m":[14,13]},{"t":"6:23","w":"P","s":"Your system was solid. Next time, name the leads for each territory as you go. Let's file this with a note in his record."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Will do. I'll document the ECG as normal, QTc 400, compared with last year, and book next year's in his annual review.","m":[14,8]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Perfect."}],"marks":[{"c":"States patient details, indication and calibration before reading","d":"Content"},{"c":"Calculates the rate by counting complexes or the 300 rule","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes sinus rhythm: P before every QRS, regular, constant PR","d":"Content"},{"c":"Determines the axis using leads I and aVF","d":"Content"},{"c":"Measures PR and QRS against normal limits","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks ST segments against the baseline and T waves in every lead","d":"Content"},{"c":"Measures QT and calculates QTc, naming when Bazett misleads","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives a one-line summary and compares with the previous ECG","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names pre-clozapine checks: arrhythmia, QTc, ischaemia, LVH, baseline troponin and CRP","d":"Content"},{"c":"Describes myocarditis timing, symptoms, ECG and blood monitoring, and when to stop clozapine","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Distinguishes infarct, pericarditis and myocarditis on the ECG","d":"Content"},{"c":"States when to repeat the ECG and which symptoms need urgent action or an echo","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Acknowledges the consultant's experience with empathy and admits uncertainty safely","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Confirms what is wanted, asks for feedback and closes with documentation","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Presents the ECG in a fixed, measured order, summarises and compares, and explains clozapine's cardiac risks and monitoring accurately.","borderline":"Reads the ECG correctly but out of order or without measurements, or is vague on myocarditis.","fail":"Reads out the machine report, misses the QT, or cannot say what to look for on clozapine."}},"c-s-25":{"setup":"Memory service, Friday afternoon. Margaret Ellison, 78, has just been diagnosed with vascular dementia. Her son, who doubts the diagnosis, has asked to see you; she has consented. Explain the diagnosis and answer his questions about management.","role":{"who":"Mr Paul Ellison, 50, her son, a hospital porter","open":"Paul, please. Dad rang and said 'she's got dementia'. I saw her three months ago and she was fine. She still drives, she goes to her club. I just don't buy it.","manner":"Sceptical and a little defensive at first. Becomes quiet and guilty when the vascular causes are explained. Engaged and practical once his doubts are answered with evidence.","free":["Shocked by the diagnosis","Thinks it's a water infection, from his work as a porter","His mother still drives and goes to her club"],"ifasked":["Mum has been grumpy and forgetful lately on the phone","She has always been poor at taking her blood pressure tablets","Dad is 80 and struggling","He has two teenage children","Mum would hate losing her car","He has not had his own blood pressure checked for years"],"worries":["Did you test for infection?","Can she have donepezil?","Will I get it, or my kids?","What about her driving?"],"rules":["Pushes the infection theory until the test results are explained","Becomes guilty about her tablets; settles if validated","Asks what terms mean if jargon is used","Accepts the driving advice if her feelings are acknowledged"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Ellison, I'm Dr Nair from the memory team. Thank you for coming in. Your mum has said she's fine with me talking to you about her results. What have you heard so far?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:16","w":"P","s":"Paul, please. Dad rang and said 'she's got dementia'. I saw her three months ago and she was fine. She still drives, she goes to her club. I just don't buy it."},{"t":"0:31","w":"D","s":"It sounds like it came as a real shock, especially when she seemed so well to you. What worries you most about it?","m":[12,2]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","s":"Honestly? I think it's an infection. I'm a porter at the General; I see old people confused with water infections all the time."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"That's a really sensible thought, and we checked for exactly that. Can I explain how we reached the diagnosis, a bit at a time, and you stop me whenever you want?","m":[2,13]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"1:08","band":"What dementia means","w":"D","s":"First, the word. Dementia isn't one disease. It's an umbrella term for brain changes that steadily affect memory, thinking and daily life. Alzheimer's is one type. There are others.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"I thought dementia was Alzheimer's."},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"A lot of people do. Your mum has a different type, called vascular dementia. Before I explain that, does the umbrella idea make sense?","m":[3,13]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Umbrella, different types."},{"t":"1:36","band":"How we know","w":"D","s":"Your dad noticed changes over several months: irritability, forgetting appointments, and getting lost in the street three weeks ago. That slow build-up is different from an infection, which usually comes on over hours or days and makes people drowsy or muddled from one hour to the next.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"But did you actually test for infection?"},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Yes. Her urine test was clear, and her blood tests were all normal: blood count, kidneys, liver, thyroid, vitamins, sugar and markers of infection. So infection and other physical causes have been ruled out.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"Okay. What about depression? She's been grumpy."},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"Good question. She did a mood questionnaire and scored low, so depression isn't behind this. On the memory test, she scored 68 out of 100. A score under 82 usually means a real problem with thinking.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"68. Right. (pause) And the scan?"},{"t":"2:38","band":"Vascular dementia","w":"D","s":"The MRI scan shows a lot of damage to the tiny blood vessels deep in the brain, plus the old stroke from two years ago. Over time, poor blood flow damages the brain cells that do the thinking. That's vascular dementia.","m":[6,4]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"So it's because of the stroke?"},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Partly. The stroke, her diabetes, high blood pressure and cholesterol have all affected the blood vessels. Does that fit with what you know about her health?","m":[6,13]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"(quiet) It does. Mum's always been terrible at taking her blood pressure tablets. I should have nagged her more."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Lots of children feel that, and it shows how much you care. But this builds up over many years, from many causes. You didn't cause it, and you couldn't have stopped it alone.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Thanks. What happens now, then? Does it get worse?"},{"t":"3:39","band":"What to expect","w":"D","s":"It usually progresses, but often in steps: stable for a while, then a drop, sometimes after another small stroke. Some people stay at a level for a long time. Everyone's pace is different.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"Can you stop the steps?"},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"We can lower the chance of them. Keeping her blood pressure, sugar and cholesterol well controlled, taking her blood thinner, staying active and not smoking all protect the vessels.","m":[7,9]},{"t":"4:10","band":"Tablets","w":"P","s":"My mate's dad got tablets for memory. Donepezil? Can she have them?"},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Those tablets help in Alzheimer's and some other types, but research shows they don't help in vascular dementia alone, and they can cause side effects. So the most useful medicines for her are the ones protecting her blood vessels. I'll write to her GP to tighten those up.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"Fair enough. Will I get it? And my kids?"},{"t":"4:41","band":"Inheritance","w":"D","s":"Vascular dementia isn't usually passed down directly. What can run in families is diabetes, blood pressure and cholesterol. If you keep those in check, you lower your own risk. Your GP can do an NHS Health Check.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"Right, I'll book one. What about her driving? She'll go mad if you take her car."},{"t":"5:05","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"I know her independence matters hugely to her. By law, she has to tell the DVLA about the diagnosis. Because she got lost recently, our advice is that she stops driving until the DVLA decides. We'll explain that to her too.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"5:24","w":"P","s":"She won't like it. And the wandering, should we worry?"},{"t":"5:28","w":"D","s":"It's worth planning for. Some families use a GPS tracker, and the police have a form called the Herbert Protocol, with her photo and places she might go, that speeds up a search.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:43","band":"Support","w":"P","s":"What help is there? Dad's eighty. He's struggling already."},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"Quite a bit. We'll offer her group cognitive stimulation therapy, a dementia adviser from the Alzheimer's Society, and your dad a carer's assessment from the council. It's also a good time for her to set up a Lasting Power of Attorney while she can.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:07","w":"P","s":"That's a lot to take in."},{"t":"6:10","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"It is. Can you tell me in your words what you'll tell your dad tonight?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:17","w":"P","s":"It's vascular dementia, from her blood vessels and the stroke, not an infection. No memory tablets, but her blood pressure and sugar matter. She stops driving and tells the DVLA. And there's help for Dad."},{"t":"6:33","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Here's a leaflet on vascular dementia and our team's number. We'll see your mum again in six weeks, and you're welcome to come.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"I will. Thank you for not rushing it."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces, confirms the mother's consent to share and asks what he knows","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores his concerns, including his infection theory, before explaining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains dementia as an umbrella term in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains how history, bloods, urine, HADS, ACE-III and MRI support the diagnosis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Addresses infection and delirium directly with the timing and normal tests","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains vascular dementia and links it to her stroke, diabetes, BP and cholesterol","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes the likely stepwise course and that risk-factor control can slow it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers inheritance accurately and advises on his own risk","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains why cholinesterase inhibitors are not used and what treatment helps instead","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Raises the DVLA duty, advice to stop driving, and wandering safety","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Offers CST, a dementia adviser, a carer's assessment and LPA","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his shock, guilt and concern for his mother's independence","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Chunks information and checks understanding, including teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with a leaflet, contact number and review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Answers his doubts with evidence, explains vascular dementia and its course clearly, gets tablets and inheritance right, raises driving and links real support.","borderline":"Accurate but lecturing, or misses driving or the infection question, or checks understanding only at the end.","fail":"Offers donepezil, dismisses his concerns, or uses jargon throughout without checking."}},"c-s-26":{"setup":"Wednesday afternoon, older adult ward. Mr Kofi Mensah, 75, has an abnormal DaT scan. He agreed you can speak to his daughter, who he lives with. Explain the diagnosis and its management to her.","role":{"who":"Ms Abena Mensah, 46, female, his daughter","open":"Thank you. I've been waiting all week. Nobody tells me anything properly.","manner":"Tired, protective and a little frustrated at first. Tearful at the prognosis. Settles and becomes practical once she feels heard and the plan is clear.","free":["Good and bad days; some afternoons he doesn't know her","Sees little dogs and children in the corner","Swung his stick at 'dogs', broke a glass door, nearly hit her 6-year-old","Wants him home: she promised her late mother"],"ifasked":["Her mother said he fought in his sleep years ago","Two falls in the summer; slower, shuffles a little","Went rigid and could hardly swallow for two days after a medicine on the medical ward","Constipated for months","Memory changes came before the falls","She has two sisters who live away and rely on her for news"],"worries":["Is it Parkinson's?","Can he come home?","How long has he got?","Why can't you give something to stop the hallucinations?"],"rules":["If given a long stream of facts without pauses, says she's lost and asks you to slow down","Becomes tearful at the prognosis; settles if acknowledged","Pushes for a drug to stop the hallucinations until the danger is explained clearly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Rahman, one of the doctors on the ward. Thank you for coming in. Your dad agreed yesterday that I could talk to you about his results.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I've been waiting all week. Nobody tells me anything properly."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frustrating, especially when you're the one passing everything on to the family. Before I explain, what have you been told so far?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"Just that the scan 'supports Lewy body'. I've never heard of it. Is it Parkinson's? And can he come home?"},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"Those are exactly the right questions, and I'll answer both. First, can I check a few things you've noticed at home, so I can link it all together?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Of course."},{"t":"0:50","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"Over the past months, have there been good days and bad days, when he's sharp one hour and muddled or drowsy the next?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Yes! Mornings he's himself, doing the crossword. By lunchtime he sometimes doesn't know who I am. Then the next day he's fine again."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"And what does he see that isn't there?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"Dogs. Little dogs, and sometimes children, sitting in the corner. He talks about them as if they're real."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"How does he react to them?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"Mostly he's fine. But in March he thought the dogs were going for him and swung his stick. He smashed the glass door and nearly caught my youngest. She's six."},{"t":"1:38","w":"D","s":"That must have been frightening for all of you. I'm glad nobody was hurt. We'll come back to keeping the children safe, I promise.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I haven't slept properly since."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"Does he ever shout, punch or kick in his sleep, as if he's acting out a dream?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Funny you ask. Mum used to say he fought in his sleep. That was years ago, before any of this."},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"And has he fallen, or seemed stiffer or slower on his feet?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"Two falls in the summer. He's slower, yes. Shuffles a bit."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"One more thing. On the medical ward, did anyone mention how he reacted to a medicine for agitation?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"They gave him something one night and he went stiff as a board. He could hardly swallow for two days."},{"t":"2:36","band":"The diagnosis","w":"D","s":"Thank you; that's all really helpful. I'm afraid what I have to say isn't good news. Is it all right to go on?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"Yes. Just tell me straight."},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"The scan, together with everything you've just told me, shows that your dad has a type of dementia called dementia with Lewy bodies.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"Dementia. Right."},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"I'll give you a moment. It's a lot to hear.","m":[2]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"Go on. What are Lewy bodies?"},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Tiny clumps of a protein that build up inside brain cells and stop them working properly. They affect thinking, alertness, what people see, sleep and movement.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:21","w":"P","s":"So is it Parkinson's? He doesn't shake."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"It's a close cousin. The same protein is involved, but here it affects thinking first. Many people never get a tremor; the slowness and falls are the movement part.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"And it explains everything you've seen: the good and bad days, the dogs and children, fighting in his sleep, and the falls.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"So the scan proved it?"},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"The scan looks at dopamine, a chemical messenger in the brain's movement centres. His shows less than normal. Together with his symptoms, that makes the diagnosis very likely.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:01","w":"P","s":"I thought he was just getting old. I should have pushed the GP sooner."},{"t":"4:07","w":"D","s":"You noticed, and you got him help. The good days in between make this very hard to spot, even for doctors.","m":[2]},{"t":"4:16","band":"What happens next","w":"P","s":"Will he get better?"},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry to say there's no cure. It usually gets worse over the years: thinking, movement and the hallucinations. The pace varies a lot. It does shorten life, but many people live several years, and good care makes a real difference.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I promised my mum I'd look after him."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"You're clearly doing that already. We'll help you keep that promise for as long as it's safe for him and for your family.","m":[2]},{"t":"4:50","band":"Treatment","w":"D","s":"There is treatment that helps. A tablet called rivastigmine often improves alertness and reduces the hallucinations. We check his pulse and heart tracing first, because it can slow the heart. Sickness is the commonest side effect, so we start low and build up.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:07","w":"P","s":"Can't you give him something to stop the dogs altogether?"},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd want that. The usual medicines for hallucinations, called antipsychotics, are dangerous in this illness. They can make people very stiff, drowsy, or seriously ill. That's what happened on the medical ward.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"So that's why. Nobody told me."},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"We'll put a warning on his drug chart, his hospital record and his GP file today. Please tell any doctor or paramedic who sees him too.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:40","band":"Home and safety","w":"P","s":"Can he still come home?"},{"t":"5:43","w":"D","s":"That's the plan we're working towards. Our occupational therapist will visit the house first: rugs, glass, lighting. Our physio will look at his walking and his stick.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"When he sees the dogs, stay calm, ask what he sees, turn on a brighter light and reassure him. Don't argue. And for now, please don't leave the children alone with him when he's frightened or muddled.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"That makes sense. I can do that."},{"t":"6:13","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"It's a lot to take in. So I can check I've explained it clearly, what will you tell your sisters tonight?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:22","w":"P","s":"It's Lewy body dementia, it'll get worse slowly, he can try rivastigmine, no antipsychotics ever, and the OT will check the house."},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. Let's meet with your sisters next Wednesday at two. Here's a leaflet, and I'll ask our social worker about a carer's assessment for you. What else would you like to ask?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Nothing for now. Thank you for being straight with me."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduced self, confirmed his consent to share, and asked what she already knew","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledged her distress and guilt, and paused after bad news","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Clarified fluctuation, hallucinations, sleep behaviour and falls in her own words","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asked about previous antipsychotic exposure and his reaction","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gave a warning shot and named dementia with Lewy bodies plainly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Linked each core feature to symptoms she had described","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explained the DaT scan result without jargon","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gave an honest course and prognosis without false hope","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explained rivastigmine: purpose, pulse check, main side effects","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explained antipsychotic sensitivity and agreed to flag it on all records","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agreed falls and home-safety steps, including protecting the grandchildren","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checked understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closed with written information, carer support and a family meeting date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Names DLB early, ties it to her stories, warns clearly about antipsychotics and leaves a safe plan for home.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis and drugs, but generic, with little linking to his symptoms or no check of understanding.","fail":"Vague or wrong diagnosis, no antipsychotic warning, false reassurance, or a jargon-heavy lecture without pauses."}},"c-s-27":{"setup":"Thursday morning, memory clinic. Mr Gerald Whitcombe, 78, has memory loss, falls and sees his late brother. MoCA 18/30. Examine him for parkinsonism, measure lying and standing BP, and relate your findings to Lewy body dementia.","role":{"who":"Mr Gerald Whitcombe, 78, male, patient","open":"Gerald's fine. Was the test bad? I got flustered.","manner":"Polite, a little embarrassed and anxious after the memory test. Reluctant to walk. Warms up with clear explanations and reassurance.","free":["Right hand clumsy; can't write properly","Falls: sometimes woozy getting up, once went backwards","Sees his late brother in the garden"],"ifasked":["Wife says he punches the pillow at night, for years","Constipated a lot","Light-headed on standing","No pain today"],"worries":["Was the memory test bad?","Have I got Parkinson's?","Will I fall during the walk?"],"rules":["Shows a slight right rest tremor that settles when he moves","Right wrist catches and ratchets when moved, more when the other hand moves","Finger taps shrink and slow; writing gets small","Takes three steps back on the pull test; dizzy on standing"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Consent","w":"D","s":"Good morning, Mr Whitcombe. I'm Dr Fernandes, one of the memory clinic doctors. Thank you for doing the memory test. Can I check how you'd like to be called?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Gerald's fine. Was the test bad? I got flustered."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"Lots of people do, Gerald; it's a stressful thing. The consultant will go through it all with you. Now I'd like to examine how your muscles and movements work, and check your blood pressure lying down and then standing. About ten minutes. Is that all right?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"If it helps. Does June need to come in?"},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"She's welcome to, or a nurse can join us. Your choice.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"Let her stay outside. She worries."},{"t":"1:00","band":"Symptoms","w":"D","s":"Before I start: any shaking, stiffness, or slowness, say doing buttons or writing?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"My right hand's gone clumsy. I can't write properly."},{"t":"1:14","w":"D","s":"And the falls: did you trip, go dizzy, or just go over?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"Sometimes woozy getting up. Once I just went backwards in the kitchen."},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"Do you act out your dreams? Has June said you shout or kick in your sleep?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"She moved into the spare room. Says I punch the pillow. Years now."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's really useful. Any constipation or trouble swallowing? And is anything painful today?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Bunged up a lot. Nothing hurts."},{"t":"1:59","band":"Watch and lying BP","w":"D","s":"Please lie on the couch for five minutes. I'll take your blood pressure now, and again when you stand. Rest your hands on your tummy and relax.","m":[4,9]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"Righto."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"I'm watching your face and hands while you rest. Your blood pressure lying is 148 over 82.","m":[4,9]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Is that all right?"},{"t":"2:29","band":"Tone","w":"D","s":"It's fine for now. Next I'll move your wrist and elbow. Let them go floppy, as if they're not yours.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Feels odd. Why are you twisting it?"},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Sorry, I'm feeling for stiffness. Now keep opening and closing your left hand while I move the right.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"Like this?"},{"t":"2:57","w":"D","s":"Perfect. And now the other side.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:02","band":"Slowness","w":"D","s":"Tap your first finger and thumb together, as big and as fast as you can, ten times. Then the other hand.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"They're getting smaller. My right's useless."},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"You're doing well. Please write your name and address on this paper.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Look at that. I used to make furniture. Now my writing's tiny."},{"t":"3:32","w":"D","s":"That must be hard after a life working with your hands. Smaller writing is one of the signs I'm looking for, and there's help for it.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"3:46","band":"Standing BP","w":"D","s":"Now stand up slowly. I'll check your pressure at one minute and three minutes. Tell me if you feel light-headed.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:57","w":"P","s":"Bit swimmy, doctor."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"Hold the chair. At one minute it's 130 over 76; at three minutes it's 122 over 74. That's a big drop, and it may explain some falls.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:14","band":"Gait and balance","w":"P","s":"I don't want to walk. I'll only fall again."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"That's a sensible worry after three falls. I'll walk right beside you; hold my arm to start. Walk to the door, turn, and come back.","m":[2,7]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"There. Slow, I know."},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Now I'll stand behind you and give a quick tug on your shoulders. Take a step back if you need to. I'll catch you, I promise.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","s":"Whoa. Thanks for catching me."},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"That's what I needed to see. Two quick things: follow my finger up and down, then side to side. And I'll check the reflexes in your feet.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"5:13","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Gerald. Your muscles are stiffer and slower on the right, your balance is unsteady, and your blood pressure drops when you stand. Both help explain the falls.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"Have I got Parkinson's, then?"},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"I can see you're worried. It looks like a condition related to Parkinson's. Together with your memory and seeing your brother, it may be one called Lewy body disease. The consultant and I will talk it through with you and June today.","m":[2,10,11]},{"t":"5:53","w":"P","s":"Not just old age, then."},{"t":"5:57","w":"D","s":"No. Can you tell me in your words what I found, so I know I've explained it clearly?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:08","w":"P","s":"Stiff and slow on the right, wobbly, and my blood pressure drops when I stand."},{"t":"6:16","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Until then, stand up slowly and sit on the bed edge first. I'll suggest the GP looks at your blood pressure tablets, and we'll refer you to the falls clinic. And no one should give you antipsychotic or anti-sickness medicines like metoclopramide without checking with us. Any questions?","m":[11,13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you for catching me."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Present to consultant: bradykinesia with decrement, right more than left cogwheel rigidity, poor arm swing, positive pull test, and a 26 mmHg postural drop. With hallucinations and fluctuation, I think probable dementia with Lewy bodies.","m":[11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduced self, explained the examination and gained consent, offering a chaperone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responded with empathy to anxiety, embarrassment and fear of falling","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asked briefly about tremor, stiffness, falls, dream enactment and autonomic symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Observed face and rest tremor before touching","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested tone at both wrists, using activation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested bradykinesia for decrement and checked handwriting","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assessed gait, turning and postural instability safely","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screened for mimics: vertical gaze and plantar responses","d":"Content"},{"c":"Measured lying BP after rest and standing BP at 1 and 3 minutes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explained the findings in plain words and linked them to falls","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Related findings to probable DLB and named safety actions (BP drugs, falls clinic, antipsychotics)","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checked understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closed with next steps and invited questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Fluent, gentle examination covering tremor, tone, bradykinesia with decrement, gait, pull test and proper postural BP, linked to probable DLB.","borderline":"Most signs examined but no decrement, no activation, or a rushed standing BP; weak link to DLB.","fail":"Unsafe pull test, no consent, no postural BP, or concludes 'not parkinsonian' because there is no tremor."}},"c-s-28":{"setup":"Monday morning, residential care home. Mr Leonard Fairbairn, 84, has Alzheimer's dementia and was started on risperidone for aggression. His brother is angry and thinks he's oversedated. Explain BPSD, the medicine and the plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Ronald Fairbairn, 80, male, his brother","open":"You're the doctor? You've drugged my brother into a zombie. I read about these pills in the paper. Who agreed to this?","manner":"Angry and standing at first; refuses to sit until he feels heard. Breaks down when his brother's late wife is mentioned. Becomes cooperative and practical once his concerns are agreed with.","free":["Leonard is drowsy, dribbling and not eating","Read that antipsychotics kill people with dementia","Nobody asked him before the medicine was started"],"ifasked":["Leonard has arthritis in his knee and never complains","He hates being washed","Calls for his wife Margaret every night; she died in March","Loved big band music; danced with Margaret every Saturday","No Lasting Power of Attorney","Has boxes of family photographs"],"worries":["Is he just being kept quiet?","Will the dose go up?","What will you do instead of pills?","Who gave permission?"],"rules":["Stays angry if the doctor is defensive or blames others","Calms quickly if the doctor agrees the risks are real","Cries when Margaret is mentioned; settles if validated"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Defuse","w":"P","s":"You're the doctor? You've drugged my brother into a zombie. I read about these pills in the paper. Who agreed to this?"},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"Mr Fairbairn, I'm Dr Mensah from the older people's mental health team. I can see you're very upset. Please come into the quiet room so I can hear this properly.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"Fine. But I want answers."},{"t":"0:32","w":"D","s":"You'll get them. Tell me what you've seen.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"He's half asleep, dribbling, not eating. He used to be sharp as a tack. You lot just want him quiet."},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"So you're angry because Leonard seems sedated, he's not eating, and nobody asked you about the medicine. Is that right?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Yes. Exactly that."},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"You're right to question it. These medicines have real risks in dementia, and they should never be used just to keep someone quiet.","m":[2,5]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"Well. At least someone admits it."},{"t":"1:20","band":"Understanding BPSD","w":"D","s":"Can I explain what's been going on? In dementia, changes like hitting out, pacing or calling out at night are very common. We call them behavioural and psychological symptoms.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:36","w":"P","s":"So he's just getting worse?"},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Partly the illness, yes. But usually it's a sign something's bothering him that he can't put into words: pain, constipation, an infection, being frightened during washing, or grief.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"He does hate being washed."},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"That's helpful. Most of the hitting has been during washing. Does he have pain anywhere, that you know of?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"His knee. Arthritis. He'd never complain, though. Never did."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"Does he still talk about his wife?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"(voice breaking) He calls for Margaret every night. She died in March. He never got over it."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. That's heartbreaking for him and for you. Grief can come out as agitation in dementia, and no tablet fixes grief. We need to work with it.","m":[1,4]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Sorry for shouting."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Risperidone","w":"D","s":"No need to apologise. Can I explain the risperidone? It was started three weeks ago because he'd hit two carers and pushed a resident, and people were getting hurt.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"But why a drug?"},{"t":"3:09","w":"D","s":"It's the only medicine licensed for that, and only for up to six weeks. It can cause sleepiness, stiffness, falls and swallowing problems. It also raises the risk of stroke and death, roughly one extra death in every hundred people treated over three months.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"So the paper was right."},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"It was. That's why I'm not going to increase it, even though staff have asked.","m":[5,9]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"Good. Will you stop it?"},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"I'd like to halve it from tomorrow, and aim to stop it completely by the end of November, unless he becomes a serious danger again. Stopping suddenly can bring the hitting straight back.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:06","band":"Non-drug plan","w":"P","s":"So what will you do instead?"},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Today I'll examine him, check his bowels and his knee, and do blood tests. He'll have regular paracetamol for his pain, and something for constipation.","m":[4,6]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"Don't you test the urine?"},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"Not with a dipstick at his age; it often shows germs that aren't causing trouble. We'll send urine to the lab only if he has symptoms or a fever.","m":[4]},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"Staff will also keep a chart of what happens before and after each outburst, so we can spot the triggers. What did Leonard love?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"Big band. Glenn Miller. He and Margaret danced every Saturday."},{"t":"5:03","w":"D","s":"That's lovely. We'll ask carers to play his music during washing, keep the same carers where they can, and put a photo of Margaret in his room. Could you bring some photographs?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"I've got boxes of them."},{"t":"5:24","band":"Who decides","w":"D","s":"One more question. Do you hold a Lasting Power of Attorney for his health?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:32","w":"P","s":"No. Should I?"},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Then decisions about his treatment are made in his best interests, and your views are a key part of that. I'll record what you've told me today.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"That's all I wanted."},{"t":"5:53","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Could you tell me back what we've agreed, so we're both clear?","m":[8]},{"t":"6:01","w":"P","s":"No increase. You check his knee and his bowels. Music during washing. Halve the tablet, stop by the end of the month."},{"t":"6:13","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If he gets more drowsy, stiff or has trouble swallowing, staff will call us the same day. I'll see you here in two weeks. Can we book that now?","m":[9,10]},{"t":"6:29","w":"P","s":"Yes, please."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"And if you're unhappy with anything, tell me, the care home manager, or our PALS team. You won't be seen as difficult. Any other questions?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you for being straight with me."}],"marks":[{"c":"Moved to a private space and let him express his anger without interrupting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Reflected his concerns back and agreed with what was valid","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explained BPSD in plain words as a sign of distress or unmet need","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explored causes: pain, constipation, infection, washing triggers and grief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explained why risperidone was used and its risks, including stroke and death, honestly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Described a specific, personalised non-drug plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checked LPA status and explained best-interests decision-making","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checked understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agreed no increase, a taper and a stop date","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-netted side effects and set a review date with him","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closed by explaining how to raise concerns and inviting questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Calms him by listening and honesty, explains BPSD and the real risks, and leaves a personalised plan with a stop date.","borderline":"Accurate facts but defensive or rushed; weak search for causes or no stop date.","fail":"Argues, underplays the risks, increases the dose without looking for a cause, or ignores the grief."}},"c-s-29":{"setup":"Tuesday afternoon, memory clinic. Mrs Margaret Holloway, 68, has mild cognitive impairment. She has consented to you speaking with her husband while she is at another appointment. Explain the diagnosis, results and plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Peter Holloway, 70, male, her husband","open":"Yes, she's at the eye clinic. She asked me to come. To be honest, I haven't slept.","manner":"Anxious and braced for bad news; asks direct questions. Relieved when told it isn't dementia. Guilty when admitting he's been short with her.","free":["Margaret's mother had Alzheimer's","Margaret is terrified it's the same","She forgets names and the odd appointment"],"ifasked":["She runs the house, the accounts and drives without problems","No getting lost or burnt pans","She says 'pardon' a lot","She doesn't exercise much but would like a walking group","He has been short with her when she forgets"],"worries":["Is it Alzheimer's?","Will it turn into Alzheimer's?","Is there a tablet?","Should the children be tested?","Can she keep driving?"],"rules":["Asks 'how many?' if told 'some people' get worse, and wants a straight number","Becomes quiet and guilty after hearing the odds; settles if reassured","Pushes for a tablet once"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Mr Holloway? I'm Dr Osei from the memory clinic. Thank you for coming. Margaret signed a form saying I can talk to you about her results.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Yes, she's at the eye clinic. She asked me to come. To be honest, I haven't slept."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a worrying few days. What did Margaret ask you to find out, and what's worrying you most?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"Whether it's Alzheimer's. Her mum had it. Ended up not knowing any of us. Margaret's terrified."},{"t":"0:47","w":"D","s":"That's completely understandable after what you both went through with her mum. I'll be straight with you.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"Please."},{"t":"0:59","band":"Her function","w":"D","s":"First, how is she managing day to day: money, cooking, appointments, driving?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"She does everything. Runs the house, does the accounts. She's forgotten a couple of appointments and names. She never used to."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"Anything like getting lost, burning pans, or problems with the bills?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"1:31","band":"The diagnosis","w":"D","s":"Then here's the good news first: this is not dementia, and it is not Alzheimer's.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"(exhales) Oh, thank God."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"Margaret has what we call mild cognitive impairment. Her memory is a bit lower than we'd expect for her age, but not enough to affect her daily life. That's the line between this and dementia.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"If it's not dementia, why is it called impairment?"},{"t":"2:09","w":"D","s":"Fair point; it's a clumsy name. It just means the tests pick up a small change.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:19","band":"Results","w":"P","s":"What did the tests show, then?"},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"The memory test scored 81 out of 100. The usual cut-off is in the low to high 80s, so it's just below: real, but mild. Memory and finding words were the weaker parts.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:42","w":"D","s":"The other tests were all reassuring. Her mood questionnaire was normal, so it isn't depression. The bloods ruled out thyroid or vitamin problems. And the scan showed no stroke, tumour or bleed.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"That's a relief, I think."},{"t":"3:04","band":"The odds","w":"P","s":"But will it turn into Alzheimer's?"},{"t":"3:09","w":"D","s":"Honestly, we can't tell for certain. Some people with this stay the same for years, some get better, and some go on to develop dementia.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:23","w":"P","s":"How many?"},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"In memory clinics like ours, roughly one in ten people a year. So in any one year, most don't. Her mum's history raises the chance a little, but it doesn't decide it.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"(quietly) I've been short with her when she forgets things. I feel awful."},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"That's a very human reaction, and it shows how worried you've been. Now you both know what's going on, it may feel easier.","m":[2]},{"t":"4:05","band":"What helps","w":"P","s":"Is there a tablet she can take?"},{"t":"4:11","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting to do something. The dementia tablets don't help at this stage, and they cause side effects, so national guidance says not to use them.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:26","w":"P","s":"So we just wait?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"No, there's plenty she can do. Walking or exercise most days, about two and a half hours a week. A hearing test. Keeping her blood pressure well controlled; I'll ask the GP to check it. And staying socially busy.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"She'd love an excuse to join the walking group. And she does say 'pardon' a lot."},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"Then both are perfect places to start. A shared calendar and phone reminders help with the appointments too.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:11","w":"P","s":"What about our children? Should they get tested?"},{"t":"5:17","w":"D","s":"For Alzheimer's that starts later in life, like her mum's, gene tests don't give useful answers and aren't recommended. Healthy habits matter more.","m":[6]},{"t":"5:31","w":"P","s":"And driving?"},{"t":"5:34","w":"D","s":"Her driving isn't affected, so she can carry on. If you ever notice getting lost, near misses or new dents, tell us or the GP straight away.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:49","band":"Follow-up and close","w":"D","s":"We'll repeat the memory test in a year, or sooner if anything changes in how she copes. And I'd like to see Margaret myself when she's back today, so she hears this from me.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"6:07","w":"P","s":"She'd like that."},{"t":"6:11","w":"D","s":"So I know I've explained it clearly, what will you tell her first?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"That it's not Alzheimer's. It's mild. Walking group, hearing test, blood pressure, and back in a year."},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. It's also a good time for you both to think about Lasting Powers of Attorney; this leaflet explains them. What other questions do you have?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"None. I feel I can breathe again. Thank you."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduced self and confirmed her consent to share","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explored his agenda and fears, and validated his distress and guilt","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checked her daily function before explaining","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explained MCI clearly as not dementia, using the function line","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explained the ACE-III, HADS, bloods and CT and what each ruled out","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gave honest progression odds in natural frequencies, including family history","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explained why no medication is offered","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gave specific, personalised risk-reduction advice","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-netted signs of decline, including driving","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Arranged review in 12 months and to see Margaret herself","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checked understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closed with written information and invited questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Says 'not dementia' early, explains the tests and odds honestly, gives practical risk reduction and arranges follow-up and to see her.","borderline":"Accurate but vague on numbers or results, or no plan to see the patient herself.","fail":"Calls it Alzheimer's, offers anti-dementia drugs, or dismisses it as normal ageing with no follow-up."}},"c-s-30":{"setup":"Friday morning, medical ward. Mrs Dorothy Ashworth, 82, is medically fit for discharge after urosepsis and pneumonia. She refuses the twice-daily carers the OT recommends. Assess her capacity to refuse carers, tell her the outcome and plan safely.","role":{"who":"Mrs Dorothy Ashworth, 82, female, patient","open":"Another doctor? I've told everyone. I don't need strangers washing me.","manner":"Sharp, proud and irritated at first; feels unheard. Softens when the doctor makes clear she won't be forced. Thoughtful and articulate when asked open questions.","free":["Wants to go home; fears catching another infection","Has lived alone for thirty years","Neighbours, the Patels, help with shopping and offer meals"],"ifasked":["Has fallen at home before; rolls over and crawls to the phone","Dizzy on standing first thing in the morning","Lonely since her husband Arthur died; not depressed","Would strip wash at the sink, sitting","Would accept an OT visit and an alarm","Would like a weekly phone call"],"worries":["Will I be kept in hospital?","Are you saying I'm not all there?","Will strangers take over my routine?"],"rules":["Becomes curt if asked closed or repeated questions","Opens up if the doctor says early that she won't be forced","Accepts OT, physio and alarm, but not carers"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Good morning, Mrs Ashworth. I'm Dr Nwosu, one of the psychiatry doctors. The team asked me to talk with you about having carers when you go home. Is now a good time?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:16","w":"P","s":"Another doctor? I've told everyone. I don't need strangers washing me."},{"t":"0:23","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you've had to keep saying it. That must be tiring. I'm not here to make you have carers. I want to understand your decision and make sure it's properly recorded.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:39","w":"P","s":"Well. Go on, then."},{"t":"0:43","w":"D","s":"Have you got your glasses on, and can you hear me all right?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"Yes, I can see you. Just about."},{"t":"0:56","band":"The information","w":"D","s":"The team suggested carers twice a day, morning and evening, to help with washing, dressing and meals for a while.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"That's because you fell on the ward, your walking is slower after being so poorly, and your eyesight isn't at its best until the cataract operation.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"I know all that. I want my own house."},{"t":"1:26","band":"Understand","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what the carers would do, and why they were suggested?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:36","w":"P","s":"Help me wash and dress, and do my meals. Because I fell and I'm a bit wobbly."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"That's right. And what do you think has changed since you came in?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:53","w":"P","s":"I'm weaker. That pneumonia nearly finished me."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. I'm glad you're through it.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:04","band":"Weigh it up","w":"D","s":"What do you see as the good and bad sides of having carers?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"Good: someone checks on me. Bad: strangers in my house at set times, telling me when to get up. I've lived alone thirty years."},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"And what might happen if you go home without them?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"I could fall again. I know that. I'm not daft."},{"t":"2:37","w":"D","s":"Not at all. How would you manage washing and meals in the first week?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Strip wash at the sink, sitting down. The Patels next door have offered meals, and they do my shopping already."},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"And if you fell at night, what would you do?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"I've fallen before. I roll over and crawl to the phone. I'd rather risk that than stay here and catch something else."},{"t":"3:14","w":"D","s":"So for you, being independent at home matters more than the risk of a fall, and you've thought about how to manage. Is that fair?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"Exactly that."},{"t":"3:30","band":"Falls and mood","w":"D","s":"Do you get dizzy when you stand up?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"A bit. First thing in the morning."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Any low spells, or losing interest in things?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"No, I'm not down. It does get lonely since Arthur died."},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Lots of people feel that way living alone. Would you like someone to visit or phone each week, through a befriending service?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"Maybe a phone call. I'd like that."},{"t":"4:13","band":"Retain and conclude","w":"D","s":"Just so I know I explained it well, can you remind me what the carers would do and why?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"Wash, dress, meals, twice a day, because of the fall and being wobbly. So are you saying I'm not all there?"},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"Quite the opposite. You've understood and remembered the information, weighed it up and told me clearly. You have the capacity to make this decision, and it's yours to make, even if some people would choose differently.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Finally someone's listened."},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"I'll tell the team and write it in your notes.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:04","band":"Safety plan","w":"D","s":"Can I suggest some things that might make home safer, without daily carers?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:11","w":"P","s":"Such as?"},{"t":"5:14","w":"D","s":"An occupational therapist could visit at home and fit things like a stool for the sink and grab rails. A physio could help your strength and balance. And a pendant alarm, so if you fall you press a button rather than crawl.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"The alarm's a good idea. And the OT. Not carers. Not yet."},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"That's fine. Before you go we'll check your blood pressure lying down and then standing, as your tablet might be causing the dizziness. The GP will see you in two weeks.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"5:57","w":"D","s":"If things get harder, you can ask for carers at any time; here's the number. And if you fall and can't get up, are hurt, or feel muddled again, press the alarm or ring 999.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"I can manage that."},{"t":"6:18","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:23","w":"P","s":"Home without carers. The OT, the physio, an alarm, blood pressure check, GP in two weeks. And I can change my mind."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Anything else you'd like to ask me?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"Only when can I go?"},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'll speak to the discharge team this morning. Thank you, Mrs Ashworth.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduced self and named the specific decision","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledged her frustration and feelings; made clear she would not be forced","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Supported decision-making (glasses, hearing) and gave the relevant information plainly","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested understanding with open questions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested using and weighing with risk scenarios and her values","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screened for postural dizziness, mood and isolation","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Tested retention by asking her to repeat it later","d":"Content"},{"c":"Told her the conclusion clearly and that the decision is hers","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offered a safety plan: OT, physio, pendant alarm, befriending","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranged postural BP, GP review, and safety-netted falls and confusion","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checked understanding of the plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closed by inviting questions and next steps on discharge","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Names the decision, tests all four abilities with open questions, concludes capacity clearly and agrees a safety plan she accepts.","borderline":"Correct conclusion but patchy testing of weighing or no safety plan.","fail":"Treats an unwise choice as incapacity, assesses the wrong decision, or walks away without reducing risk."}},"c-s-31":{"setup":"Tuesday 14:00, community clinic. Liam Doyle, 34, has paranoid schizophrenia and is five months behind with his rent; a notice seeking possession has arrived. His housing officer asks if he can manage his money. Assess his capacity, tell him the outcome and agree support.","role":{"who":"Mr Liam Doyle, 34, man with paranoid schizophrenia, under the community team","open":"Is this about the rent? Because I'm not ill, if that's what they think.","manner":"Guarded and a little embarrassed; defensive if he feels judged. Relaxes once told the starting point is that he can decide, then becomes practical and keen to sort it.","free":["Diagnosed at 22; last admission five years ago","Takes quetiapine every night; it makes him sleep late","His mum managed his money until 18 months ago","He 'kept meaning to' pay the rent"],"ifasked":["Income about £1,140 a month from Universal Credit, including housing costs","Rent £520 a month; gas, electric and phone about £150","Owes about £2,600","Uses a banking app and can do simple sums","A friend borrowed £60 in the spring and paid it back","No gambling or drugs; two or three cans at weekends","Knows he could be taken to court and evicted","Would go to a hostel rather than back to his mum's"],"worries":["Are you going to take my money off me?","Will my mum have to be told?","Can I still get evicted?"],"rules":["Bristles if asked about his illness before the purpose is explained; settles once told capacity is presumed","Answers money questions accurately when asked plainly","Accepts rent paid direct if it is offered as his choice; refuses if told he must","Says he feels 'like an idiot' about the arrears; settles if this is met kindly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Liam. I'm Dr Mensah, one of the doctors in the team. Thanks for coming in.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Is this about the rent? Because I'm not ill, if that's what they think."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"It is about the rent. I can hear you're fed up with people assuming things, and that's fair. Your housing officer asked whether you can manage your own money. That one question is all we're looking at today.","m":[1,10]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"And if you say I can't?"},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"The law says I start by assuming you can. I'll ask some questions, and at the end I'll tell you honestly what I think. Is that OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"Go on, then."},{"t":"0:51","band":"His mental state","w":"D","s":"How have you been in yourself lately? Any voices, or feeling watched or followed?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"No. Not for years. I know my early signs."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"Good. Do you feel the housing association, or anyone else, is against you over money?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"No. They just want what they're owed. Fair enough, really."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"And are you still taking your quetiapine?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:19","w":"P","s":"Every night. It knocks me out till ten, mind."},{"t":"1:24","band":"Understanding","w":"D","s":"Let's look at the money itself. What comes in each month, and from where?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"Universal Credit. About eleven hundred and forty, with the housing bit."},{"t":"1:37","w":"D","s":"And what has to go out before anything else?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:42","w":"P","s":"Rent's five-twenty. Gas, electric and phone, maybe a hundred and fifty. Then food."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"How much do you owe the housing association now, and how did it build up?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"About two thousand six hundred. Mum used to do it all. When I took over I kept meaning to pay. The letters come while I'm asleep. I feel like an idiot."},{"t":"2:09","w":"D","s":"That sounds hard to talk about, and you've been really honest. It happens to a lot of people. Can I check a couple of practical things?","m":[10]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"If you paid for a meal deal costing four eighty-five with a ten-pound note, what change would you get?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:33","w":"P","s":"Five fifteen."},{"t":"2:37","w":"D","s":"Has anyone been borrowing money from you, or is any going on betting, drink or drugs?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"A mate stayed in the spring, borrowed sixty quid. He paid it back. A few cans at the weekend. That's it. The money's mostly just sat in my account."},{"t":"2:58","band":"Weighing it up","w":"D","s":"If the rent isn't paid, where could that lead?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"They could take me to court. Evict me. Then it's a hostel, probably. I'm not going back to Mum's."},{"t":"3:11","w":"D","s":"So a lot rides on this. What's good about handling your money yourself?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"It's mine. I'm thirty-four. I make my own choices."},{"t":"3:23","w":"D","s":"And the downside?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"If I slip up, there's no one to spot it. That's how this happened."},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"There are a few ways to do it: you pay the rent yourself, Universal Credit pays it straight to them, or a support worker helps you. How do they compare for you?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"Straight to them means I can't forget. But I don't want someone else holding my money. Are you going to take it off me?"},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"I can hear that really worries you. No. If you can make this decision, it stays your money and your choice.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"Right. Then rent paid direct. The rest I'll do myself."},{"t":"4:13","band":"Retain and decide","w":"D","s":"Before I tell you what I think, can you tell me back what you owe and what could happen?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"Two thousand six hundred. I could get evicted."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"So, what's your decision about running your money?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:31","w":"P","s":"I keep doing it myself, but the rent goes straight to the housing association."},{"t":"4:38","w":"D","s":"If I asked you again next week, would you say the same?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I've thought about it now."},{"t":"4:48","band":"The outcome","w":"D","s":"Here's what I've found. You understood your money, remembered it, weighed up both sides and told me your choice clearly. So you can make your own decisions about your money. The rent slipped because of organisation, not your illness.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"So I'm not mad, then. Can I still get evicted, though?"},{"t":"5:11","band":"Support he chooses","w":"D","s":"You're not. The risk is real until the arrears are sorted, so let's act this week. Shall we ask Universal Credit, through your online journal, to pay the rent direct?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:24","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Can someone help me do it?"},{"t":"5:28","w":"D","s":"Your care coordinator can sit with you this afternoon. A debt adviser at Citizens Advice can also agree a repayment plan for the arrears. Would you see them?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"If it stops the court thing. Not my mum, though. Don't ring her."},{"t":"5:48","w":"D","s":"That's your right; I won't contact her without your agreement. Would a support worker for a few weeks help to set up direct debits for the bills?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Maybe. Yeah, alright."},{"t":"6:03","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Your housing officer asked the question. Can I tell them the outcome and this plan, and nothing about your health?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:13","w":"P","s":"That's fine."},{"t":"6:16","w":"D","s":"To check I've been clear, which two things are you doing in the next few days?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"Rent paid direct, and see Citizens Advice."},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"Exactly. And if the paranoid feelings ever come back, tell your care coordinator early, and we'd look at the money together again. I'll see you in a month to check it's working.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:42","w":"P","s":"Fair enough. That wasn't as bad as I thought."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduced self, named the single decision being assessed and presumed capacity","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checked for psychotic symptoms or paranoid ideas linked to money or the landlord","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested understanding of income, outgoings and the arrears figure","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checked basic money skills and asked about exploitation, gambling or substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explored the consequences of not paying, including eviction","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asked him to weigh managing alone against the options for help","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checked retention by asking him to repeat the key facts","d":"Content"},{"c":"Obtained a clear decision and checked it was consistent","d":"Content"},{"c":"Told him the outcome plainly, separating disorganisation from incapacity","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responded with empathy to his defensiveness, shame and fear of losing control","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Agreed support he chose: rent paid direct, debt advice, support worker","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Sought consent to share, checked understanding, gave a relapse safety net and review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Tests all four abilities against his real figures, finds capacity, separates disorganisation from illness and agrees support he chooses.","borderline":"Reaches the right outcome but tests the abilities thinly or offers little practical help.","fail":"Treats schizophrenia or the arrears as proof of incapacity, skips the mental state, or imposes help without consent."}},"c-s-32":{"setup":"Thursday 11:30, urgent home visit. Mrs Edna Pryce, 78, widowed ten months ago, has had only sips of tea for three days and says her 'insides are rotten'. Take a history and mental state, assess risk including intake, and decide the urgency.","role":{"who":"Mrs Edna Pryce, 78, woman, widow living alone","open":"You needn't have come. There's nothing anyone can do for me now.","manner":"Slowed and quiet, with long pauses and little eye contact. Tearful about Gerald; frightened and more fixed if the belief is challenged. Softens and agrees to hospital when it is linked to Susan and to coming home.","free":["Husband Gerald died ten months ago of stomach cancer; married 52 years","Everything feels grey; nothing matters","Not eating because there is no point"],"ifasked":["Certain her insides are rotting and she is dying; she smells it on her breath","Only sips of tea for three days, when Susan insists","Wakes at 3 am; mornings worst; clothes hang off her","Dizzy on standing this morning; passed a little dark urine","Feels guilty and a burden on Susan","Thinks she'd be better off dead; no plan; Susan manages her tablets","One depression in her thirties, treated by her GP","Takes mirtazapine at night; helped a little at first"],"worries":["Is it cancer, like Gerald?","Will I have to leave my house?","Don't frighten Susan"],"rules":["Gives one-word answers to long or complex questions; says more to short, simple ones","Becomes more fixed and frightened if the doctor argues with the belief; calmer if it is acknowledged","Gives the full certainty and the smell only if asked gently","Cries when Gerald is mentioned; settles if validated"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mrs Pryce? I'm Dr Khan, from the older people's mental health team. Your GP asked me to call in. May I sit down here, next to you?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"You needn't have come. There's nothing anyone can do for me now."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"It sounds as if things feel very bleak. I'd like to understand. I'll keep my questions short. Is that alright?","m":[1,10]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"If you want."},{"t":"0:34","band":"Mood","w":"D","s":"I was so sorry to hear about Gerald. How have you been since he died?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"Fifty-two years. Then nothing. Everything's gone grey. (She starts to cry.) I'm sorry. I don't usually do this."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"Please don't apologise. Fifty-two years is a lifetime together. Of course it hurts.","m":[10]},{"t":"0:59","w":"P","s":"Nobody understands."},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"Take your time. Does anything still give you some pleasure, even small things?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"No. Not the garden, not the telly. Nothing."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Is any time of day worse than the rest?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Mornings. I can't face them."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"How are you sleeping? And your appetite, weight and concentration?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"I wake at three and just lie there. My clothes hang off me. My head's all fog."},{"t":"1:39","band":"The belief","w":"D","s":"Susan mentioned you're worried about your insides. Can you tell me about that?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"(Long pause.) They're rotten. Going bad inside me."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. What makes you think so?","m":[4,10]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"I can smell it. On my breath. I'm dying, doctor."},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"If 100 is completely certain, how certain are you?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"A hundred. I know it."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"Could anything else explain how your body feels, like being so low that everything feels wrong?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"No. Don't tell me it's my nerves."},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"I won't argue with you; I can see it feels completely real. Is it why you've stopped eating?","m":[10,4]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"What's the point of putting food into something rotten?"},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"Do you feel you've done something wrong, or that you deserve this? Or worry there's no money left?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"I've let Susan down. I'm a burden. Money's fine, Gerald saw to that."},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"Do you hear voices when no one's there, or see things others can't?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"No. Just the smell."},{"t":"3:07","band":"Thinking","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me today's date, and then the months in reverse, starting with December?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"It's November. Thursday? December, November, October... September... August."},{"t":"3:20","band":"Intake and physical risk","w":"D","s":"Thank you. What have you had to eat and drink since Monday?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"Sips of tea when Susan makes me. Nothing else."},{"t":"3:32","w":"D","s":"When did you last pass water? Any dizziness or falls?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"This morning. Not much, and dark. I went dizzy getting up and held on to the bed."},{"t":"3:47","w":"D","s":"May I check your pulse and blood pressure, lying and then standing?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"If you must."},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"Your pulse is fast and your blood pressure drops when you stand. That tells me your body is very short of fluid.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"It doesn't matter."},{"t":"4:12","band":"Suicide risk","w":"D","s":"It matters to me. When you feel this low, do you think you'd be better off dead?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","s":"Every day. Better off with Gerald."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Have you thought of ending your life, or made any plans? Saved any tablets?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"No. I wouldn't do that to Susan. She sorts my tablets."},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"Have you ever felt like this before? And are you taking the mirtazapine your GP started?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"Once, in my thirties. Tablets from the doctor helped. I take the new one at night. It helped at first, not now."},{"t":"4:59","band":"Explain and decide","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. I think you have a severe depression. It can make people feel their body is dying when it isn't, and it can be treated.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:14","w":"P","s":"Is it cancer, like Gerald?"},{"t":"5:17","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd fear that after watching him. We'll do blood tests to check your body properly. What I can see is an illness we can treat.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:31","w":"P","s":"Will I have to leave my house?"},{"t":"5:36","w":"D","s":"For a while, yes. You've had almost nothing to drink for three days, and that's dangerous. You need hospital today, first for fluids and tests, then our ward to treat the depression.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"I want to stay in my own bed."},{"t":"5:57","w":"D","s":"I know home matters. The aim is to get you strong enough to come back here. Would you go, for Susan's sake and yours?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"If Susan thinks so. I'm so tired."},{"t":"6:13","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can I speak to Susan now, tell her what we've agreed and arrange it? And can you tell me what's happening next?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"Hospital today. For water and tests. Don't frighten her."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"I'll be gentle with her. Susan will stay with you until you go. Could you try a few sips of water with me now?","m":[12,6]},{"t":"6:42","w":"P","s":"I'll try."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduced self, explained the visit and used short, simple questions to suit her slowing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explored onset after bereavement, low mood, anhedonia and diurnal variation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asked about sleep, appetite, weight and concentration","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explored the rotten-insides belief: content, evidence, certainty and link to not eating","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screened for guilt, poverty ideas and hallucinations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Quantified food and fluid intake, urine and dizziness, and checked pulse and postural BP","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screened for delirium with orientation and an attention test","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asked directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and stored tablets","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asked about past depression and current mirtazapine","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validated her grief and fear without arguing with or agreeing to the belief","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explained severe depression and the need for same-day hospital care in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checked understanding, involved her daughter with consent and kept her safe until transfer","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Elicits severe depression with a fixed nihilistic delusion, quantifies intake and suicide risk, and arranges same-day hospital care kindly.","borderline":"Gets the diagnosis but asks about fluids late or vaguely, or leaves the urgency unclear.","fail":"Calls it grief, argues with the belief, never asks about drinking or suicide, or plans routine follow-up."}},"c-s-33":{"setup":"Monday 15:00, older adult ward. Mrs Iris Bennett, 75, right-handed, has vascular dementia. Nurses report trouble dressing, filling in her menu card and handling change. Your consultant asks you to test her parietal lobe function and say what the findings mean.","role":{"who":"Mrs Iris Bennett, 75, woman, inpatient with vascular dementia","open":"Are you the one with my scan results? Nobody tells me anything.","manner":"Chatty and cooperative but sensitive about failing; tearful when the sums go wrong; embarrassed about dressing. Settles quickly when validated and told some tests are meant to be hard.","free":["Right-handed; wears glasses","Retired bookkeeper at the Co-op","Has not been told her scan result"],"ifasked":["No weakness or numbness","Cannot find the toilet without the sign","Knows her memory is poor","Recognises her grandson Ryan in the photo","Finds writing letters hard now","Nurses help her with the menu card"],"worries":["What did my scan show?","Am I going mad?","Will I get my numbers back?"],"rules":["Fails sums and writing; uses her finger as a toothbrush; gets stuck with the inside-out cardigan; draws pentagons apart and a flat cube","Completes fields, finger naming, left-right, letter sequence, all touch tests and face recognition correctly","Becomes tearful after failing serial 7s; carries on if validated","Asks about the scan; accepts if told who will explain and when"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and consent","w":"D","s":"Mrs Bennett? I'm Dr Afolabi, one of the ward doctors. The nurses mentioned dressing and the menu card have been tricky. I'd like to do some short tests of how your brain handles space, numbers and movement. Would that be alright?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:21","w":"P","s":"Are you the one with my scan results? Nobody tells me anything."},{"t":"0:28","w":"D","s":"I can see waiting for that is frustrating. Dr Shah will go through the scan with you tomorrow; I'll make sure she knows you're asking. Today is just these tests. Is that OK?","m":[11,1]},{"t":"0:46","w":"P","s":"Go on then. As long as it's not a spelling test."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Ola, our nurse, will stay with us. I've cleaned my hands. Which hand do you write with? Are your glasses on, and can you hear me clearly?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"Right hand. Glasses on. I hear fine."},{"t":"1:12","w":"D","s":"Any weakness or numbness in your arms or legs?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"No, love. Just my memory."},{"t":"1:22","band":"Fields and neglect","w":"D","s":"Please look at my nose. Cover your left eye. Tell me when you see my finger move.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"There. There. Yes, there."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"Now the other eye. And now, keep looking at my nose: which hand is moving?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"That one. Both of them now."},{"t":"1:49","band":"Gerstmann features","w":"D","s":"Good: both sides seen, even together. Now, show me your ring finger on your left hand.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"This one."},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"And touch your right ear with your left hand.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"Like that?"},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Some sums now. What's 100 take away 7? Then keep taking 7 away.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Ninety-three... eighty... no. I can't. I used to do the books at the Co-op. (Her eyes fill.)"},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"That's really upsetting when it used to come easily. You're doing well to stick with me. Let's leave the sums there.","m":[11]},{"t":"2:41","w":"P","s":"It's like the numbers slide off."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Could you write me a sentence about the weather on this card?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"(Writes 'It is' and stops.) The words won't come out of the pen."},{"t":"3:00","band":"Praxis","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Now some actions. Show me how you'd wave goodbye, then how you'd brush your teeth.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"(Waves; then rubs her finger across her teeth.)"},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Pretend you're holding the toothbrush, rather than using your finger.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"(Tries again, still using her finger.) Like that?"},{"t":"3:27","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Here's a letter and an envelope. Fold it, put it in and seal it, please.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"(Folds, inserts, seals, slowly but correctly.)"},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"This cardigan's inside out. Could you put it right and put it on?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"(Turns it over, pushes her arm into the wrong sleeve, gets stuck.) Oh, this is what the girls laugh about."},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"Nobody's laughing; this is exactly the kind of thing that helps us help you. Let's do it together.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"It's like my arms don't know where the holes are."},{"t":"4:15","band":"Drawing","w":"D","s":"Could you copy these two shapes that overlap, and then this cube?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"(Draws two separate five-sided shapes, then a flat square.) That's not right, is it?"},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Now draw a clock with all the numbers, hands at ten past five.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"(Numbers all around the face, both sides, unevenly spaced; hands at ten and five.)"},{"t":"4:48","band":"Touch, eyes closed","w":"D","s":"Now close your eyes. Tell me what I've just placed in your hand.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"A key. And that's a coin, a ten pence."},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"What number am I drawing on your palm? And is it one point or two touching you now?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:11","w":"P","s":"A three. Two points. Now one."},{"t":"5:16","w":"D","s":"Am I moving your finger up or down? And which hand am I touching now?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:24","w":"P","s":"Up. Down. Both hands."},{"t":"5:28","band":"Awareness and recognition","w":"D","s":"Eyes open. Do you get lost finding your way around the ward? And do you have any problems with your arms or legs?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"I can't find the toilet without the sign. My legs are fine. My memory's the problem."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Who's this in the photo on your locker?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"That's my grandson, Ryan."},{"t":"5:59","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Mrs Bennett, you've worked really hard. I'll clean my hands again. Numbers, writing, dressing and drawing are harder for you, and that helps us plan. Dr Shah will explain the scan tomorrow. Any questions?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:18","w":"P","s":"Will I get my numbers back?"},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"Some people improve, and we can make things easier straight away with practical help. Ola, she'll need help with the menu card and dressing; I'll ask OT to assess her.","m":[12,11]},{"t":"6:38","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thank you, doctor."},{"t":"6:42","w":"P","s":"(Nurse) I'll add dressing prompts and menu help to her care plan."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduced self, explained purpose, gained consent, offered a chaperone and cleaned hands","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checked handedness, glasses, hearing and limb weakness before testing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested visual fields in each eye and visual inattention","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested finger naming and left-right orientation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested calculation and writing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested ideomotor praxis by mime and ideational praxis by a sequence","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested dressing praxis with an inside-out garment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested constructional praxis with overlapping shapes, a cube and a clock","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested stereognosis, graphaesthesia, two-point, position sense and double touch with eyes closed","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checked awareness of deficits, way-finding and face recognition","d":"Content"},{"c":"Responded with empathy to her distress and scan question without false reassurance","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Closed with thanks, explained findings simply and handed over practical next steps","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Covers every parietal domain in order after the basic checks, stays kind when she struggles, and localises the findings into a practical plan.","borderline":"Tests most domains but skips fields or sensation, or cannot say which side is involved.","fail":"Gets stuck on one or two tests, skips consent or basic checks, calls neglect without fields, or discloses the scan without knowing it."}},"c-s-34":{"setup":"Wednesday 14:30, elderly care ward. Ms Pauline Hartley, 68, right-handed, had her first generalised seizure this morning. The medical team asks you to assess her memory and language. Carry out a temporal lobe assessment and say what it shows.","role":{"who":"Ms Pauline Hartley, 68, woman, retired primary teacher, medical inpatient","open":"I suppose. I'm still all over the place. I've never had a fit in my life. Am I going to have more?","manner":"Frightened and a little slowed; cooperative; embarrassed when reading fails because she was a teacher. Settles when reassured that struggling is expected.","free":["Right-handed; wears glasses","Has never had a fit before, as far as she knows","Her son is visiting tonight"],"ifasked":["Months of brief burning-rubber smells and déjà vu","Her sister saw her stare and pick at her sleeve once","Grew up in Preston; first school St Mary's Juniors","Can't remember the ambulance","Cannot see the upper right with either eye","No voices; anxious and a bit low","Planned to drive home on Friday"],"worries":["Am I going to have more fits?","Is this dementia?","Can I still drive?"],"rules":["Registers the address but cannot recall it later; asks the doctor to move on; recognises 'Barnes' and possibly 'Orchard' with cues","Fails to read the written command and stops writing mid-sentence; becomes upset, then settles if validated","Names common objects but not 'buckle'","Mentions the smells and déjà vu only if asked about odd experiences"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and consent","w":"D","s":"Ms Hartley? I'm Dr Nwosu from the liaison psychiatry team. The ward asked us to look at your memory and language after the seizure. I'd like to do some short tests. Is that alright?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:16","w":"P","s":"I suppose. I'm still all over the place. I've never had a fit in my life. Am I going to have more?"},{"t":"0:27","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening, waking up in hospital not knowing what happened. The neurology team will talk with you about seizures. My part is to see how your memory and language are doing. Shall we start?","m":[12,1]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"Alright. Go slowly, though."},{"t":"0:47","w":"D","s":"I will. I've cleaned my hands, and Grace from the ward is staying with us. Have you got your glasses? Which hand do you write with?","m":[1]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Glasses on. Right-handed."},{"t":"1:04","band":"Seizure-like experiences","w":"D","s":"Before the fit, did you have any odd turns? A strange smell or taste, a rising feeling in your tummy, or feeling you'd lived a moment before?","m":[10]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"Now you say it... for a few months I've had funny moments. A smell like burning rubber, and a feeling I've been here before. A few seconds, then gone."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's really useful. Did anyone see you go blank, smack your lips or fiddle with things during those?","m":[10]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"My sister said I stared and picked at my sleeve once. I didn't remember it."},{"t":"1:48","band":"Memory","w":"D","s":"I'm going to give you a name and address. Repeat it back, and I'll ask you for it again later: Harry Barnes, 73 Orchard Street, Kendal.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"Harry Barnes, 73... Orchard Street... Kendal."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Good. What did you eat this morning, and how did you get here?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"Toast, I think. The ambulance brought me, but I don't remember it."},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"Where did you grow up, and what was the name of your first school?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"Preston. St Mary's Juniors."},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"Who was the first woman to be UK Prime Minister? And name me a famous landmark in London.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"Margaret Thatcher. Big Ben."},{"t":"2:42","w":"D","s":"How many animals can you name in one minute? Go.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"Dog, cat, horse, cow, sheep... pig... rabbit... lion... um... duck. That's hard."},{"t":"2:54","band":"Language","w":"D","s":"Nine, that's fine. Now some language tests. Can you tell me a bit about your family?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"I've a sister, Jean, in Lancaster, and my son works in Leeds. He's coming tonight."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Please point to the ceiling, then the door, then touch your nose.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"Ceiling... door... nose."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Say after me: 'The red bus stopped by the old church.'","m":[6]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"The red bus stopped by... the old church."},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"What's this called? And this part? And this?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"A watch. The strap. That's... the thing that does it up. I know it but I can't get the word."},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"That's the buckle. Don't worry, that happens. What about this, and this part?","m":[7,12]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"A pen. The... lid. And the clip."},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"Could you read this card and do what it says?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:03","w":"P","s":"Close... your... (Long pause.) I can't make the words line up. I'm sorry. This is stupid. I was a teacher."},{"t":"4:13","w":"D","s":"It's not stupid at all; it's the seizure and whatever caused it, not you. Thank you for keeping going.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"It's just frightening."},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"I understand. Could you write any sentence you like on this card?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"(Writes 'My son is' and stops.) It's gone."},{"t":"4:38","band":"Vision and recognition","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Now your eyes. Look at my nose and cover one eye. Tell me when you see my finger move, top and bottom on each side.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"Bottom right, yes. Top right... no. Oh, there it is, when you come in."},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"And the other eye?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"Top right again I don't see. The left side is fine."},{"t":"5:08","w":"D","s":"Who is this? It's a picture of a famous face.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:13","w":"P","s":"That's the King."},{"t":"5:17","band":"Mood and psychosis","w":"D","s":"Since all this started, have you heard voices, felt people are against you, or felt your mood has changed?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"No voices. Just frightened and a bit low about it all."},{"t":"5:33","band":"Delayed recall","w":"D","s":"Last one: can you tell me the name and address from earlier?","m":[4]},{"t":"5:39","w":"P","s":"Harry... something. Street. No. I can't. Please move on."},{"t":"5:44","w":"D","s":"Of course. Did it begin with Harry B...? Was it Orchard, Oakwood or Elm Street?","m":[4]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"Barnes? Maybe Orchard. I'm not sure."},{"t":"5:56","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, you've worked hard. You had trouble holding new information, finding some words, reading and writing, and part of your vision on the right. Together that points to the left side of the brain, near where the seizure may start. We'll repeat these tests in a day or two, once you're less tired, and the team will arrange a brain scan and EEG.","m":[5,12]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Can I still drive home on Friday?"},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"Not for now; after a seizure you must stop driving and tell the DVLA. The neurology team will advise how long. Any other questions for me?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:42","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you, doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduced self, explained purpose, gained consent, offered a chaperone and checked handedness and glasses","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Registered a name and address early for later recall","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested recent, remote and semantic memory, including category fluency","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested delayed recall, free and then with cues","d":"Content"},{"c":"Localised the findings to the left temporal region and named postictal effects as a caveat","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tested comprehension with a three-step command and repetition of a sentence","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested naming with common and less common items","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested reading and writing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tested upper and lower visual quadrants in each eye and face recognition","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asked about auras, déjà vu, smells and automatisms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screened for psychotic symptoms and mood change","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Responded empathically to her fear and distress and gave driving advice and next steps","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Covers memory, language, naming, reading, writing, upper fields and seizure experiences, stays kind, and localises with a postictal caveat and plan to repeat.","borderline":"Tests memory and language but skips fields, graded naming or auras, or gives no clear localisation.","fail":"Does a general memory screen only, forgets delayed recall, labels dementia, or ignores her distress and driving."}},"c-t-13":{"setup":"Thursday morning, the neuropsychiatry clinic. Callum Okafor, 27, had a traumatic brain injury in a motorway crash a year ago. His family find him impulsive. Carry out a bedside frontal lobe assessment and summarise what you find.","role":{"who":"Mr Callum Okafor, 27, male, patient","open":"Yep, that's me. Callum.","manner":"Cheerful, laughs a lot, over-familiar and jokey. Cooperative with every task. Does not offer information unless asked. Briefly worried when told some tasks were harder.","free":["Was in a crash about a year ago; does not remember it","Was in hospital for months; now lives with his parents","Feels fine in himself"],"ifasked":["Can hardly smell anything since the crash","Family say he acts without thinking and makes jokes that upset people","Spent about £600 on online games one weekend","Takes sayings literally","Gives the first answer that comes to mind for estimates","Gets stuck repeating a movement when asked to switch","Grips the doctor's fingers if the palm is stroked"],"worries":["Did he fail?","Is his brain damaged for good?"],"rules":["Makes a joke about the doctor at some point; carries on if redirected kindly","Says 'This is stupid' at the proverb; tries if the reason is explained","Performs any other task normally"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and consent","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Can I check you're Callum Okafor?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yep, that's me. Callum."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Callum. I'd like to do some short thinking and movement tasks, some a bit odd, to see how the front of the brain is working since the crash. Is that alright?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Sure, go for it."},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"Some are meant to be tricky, so don't worry about mistakes. Tell me if you need a break. I've cleaned my hands. Do you wear glasses or hearing aids?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Nope. Perfect eyes, me."},{"t":"0:27","band":"Brief history","w":"D","s":"Have you noticed any change in your sense of smell since the crash?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"Now you mention it, I can't really smell much. Mum's cooking is wasted on me."},{"t":"0:36","w":"D","s":"Have your family or friends said you've changed in any way?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Mum says I make bad jokes and do stuff without thinking. I spent a bit on games. Six hundred quid. I'm just a laugh, me."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being straight with me. That's useful. Let's start the tasks.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:52","band":"Verbal fluency","w":"D","s":"In one minute, give me every word you can think of that starts with F. No names of people or places. Go.","m":[4]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"Fish, fun, Frank, food, fork... fish... France, fast. That's it."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"Good. Now as many animals as you can, in one minute.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"Dog, cat, horse, cow, sheep, lion, tiger, bear, pig, rabbit, mouse, fox."},{"t":"3:02","band":"Abstract thinking","w":"D","s":"What does it mean if someone says, 'Don't count your chickens before they hatch'?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"This is stupid. Why are you asking me about chickens?"},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"Fair question; it does sound odd. It shows how the brain handles ideas, which can change after an injury. Just your best guess.","m":[2,5]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Well, you can't count them till they're out of the egg, can you?"},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"In what way are a table and a chair alike?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"They've both got legs. Four each."},{"t":"3:32","band":"Estimates and sequencing","w":"D","s":"Take a guess: how tall is an average woman in this country?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"About eight feet?"},{"t":"3:38","w":"D","s":"Okay. Now watch my hand and don't copy yet.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"Ha, doc, you look like my old PE teacher."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"I'll take that as a compliment. Keep watching: fist, edge, palm, five times. Now you do it, over and over.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"Fist, edge, fist... fist, palm, fist, fist..."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Now copy this zigzag pattern and carry it on to the edge of the page.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"There you go. Oops, I kept doing the same peak."},{"t":"4:16","band":"Response inhibition","w":"D","s":"Next: when I tap once, you tap twice. When I tap twice, you tap once.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"Right... got it, I think. That one's tricky."},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"Well done. New rule: when I tap once, you tap once. When I tap twice, don't tap at all.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"Tap... tap. Oh, I wasn't meant to. Again? Tap. Sorry!"},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"Did I fail that one?"},{"t":"4:59","w":"D","s":"No pass or fail, Callum; some are meant to be hard. I'll explain the overall picture at the end.","m":[2]},{"t":"5:05","band":"Primitive reflexes","w":"D","s":"I'd like to check some reflexes by touching your palm and your lips. Is that okay?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Go on then."},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"Rest your hand open on your knee. Please don't hold my fingers.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:16","w":"P","s":"Ha, I've grabbed you. Didn't mean to."},{"t":"5:19","w":"D","s":"That's fine. Now I'll stroke the base of your thumb and watch your chin. Then close your eyes; I'll tap gently above your lip.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"Okay. That tickled."},{"t":"5:31","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Callum, you worked hard. I've cleaned my hands again. Switching tasks, explaining sayings and stopping yourself were harder for you.","m":[10,1]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"Is my brain damaged for good, then?"},{"t":"5:44","w":"D","s":"That's a really important question. The crash affected the front of your brain, which fits the jokes and spending. Many people keep improving for years, and support makes a real difference.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"5:56","w":"P","s":"Right. What happens now?"},{"t":"5:58","w":"D","s":"A scored test next time, detailed testing with a neuropsychologist, and a smell test. With your okay, I'd like to talk to your parents too. Can you tell me what we're doing next?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"More tests, a smell test, and you talk to Mum and Dad."},{"t":"6:14","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Until then, please don't drive, and ask your parents to check the smoke and gas alarms, as you can't smell burning. Any questions?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"Nope. Cheers, doc."}],"marks":[{"c":"Hand hygiene, confirms identity, gains consent and explains the tasks are odd","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Encourages him warmly, redirects jokes kindly and answers his worries","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about smell and personality changes noticed by others","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests timed letter fluency with rules, and category fluency","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests proverb interpretation and similarities","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks a cognitive estimate","d":"Content"},{"c":"Demonstrates silently, then tests Luria's sequence and an alternating pattern","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests conflicting instructions and go/no-go","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gains consent and tests grasp, palmomental and pout reflexes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Summarises findings in plain words as a pattern","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains next steps and checks his understanding","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets on driving and smoke alarms, and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Fluent, ordered battery with demonstration, clear findings and a plain summary.","borderline":"Covers most tests but misses smell, demonstration or reflexes, or summarises as a list.","fail":"Relies on a memory test, loses structure to his jokes, or touches him without consent."}},"c-t-14":{"setup":"Tuesday afternoon, a medical ward. Gillian Mbatha, 43, has sudden left leg weakness with normal scans; neurology asked liaison psychiatry to see her. The nurse says it is weaker today. Examine both lower limbs and explain as you go.","role":{"who":"Ms Gillian Mbatha, 43, female, patient","open":"Yes. The nurse said you'd come.","manner":"Anxious and tired, worried a stroke was missed. Cooperative with every test. Briefly defensive when she realises you are from psychiatry; relieved by a clear, kind explanation.","free":["The left leg went weak suddenly a week ago while making tea","Doctors first thought it was a stroke","Feels it is even weaker this morning"],"ifasked":["No pain now","No injury, back pain or pins and needles","No bladder or bowel change, no numbness around the bottom","Can hardly lift the left leg when asked, but it moves normally if someone else moves it","Feels light touch and pinprick normally","Can stand and walk, dragging the left leg"],"worries":["Has a stroke been missed?","Does psychiatry mean they think she is making it up?","Will she walk again?"],"rules":["Asks 'So you think I'm making it up?' when psychiatry is mentioned; settles if reassured the weakness is real","Becomes tearful about walking again; settles if validated","Pushes left heel down firmly when asked to lift the right leg"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and consent","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors working with the medical team. Are you Gillian Mbatha?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. The nurse said you'd come. So you think I'm making it up?"},{"t":"0:09","w":"D","s":"No one thinks that. The weakness is real, and neurology asked me to help because we work together on problems like this.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"Okay. It's worse today."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry the leg feels worse today. That must be frightening. I'd like to check your legs carefully, the nerves, muscles and reflexes. Is that alright?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"If it helps, yes."},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"Is anything painful right now? I'll ask a nurse to stay with us as a chaperone. I've cleaned my hands. Could you lie back with the head of the bed raised, and roll your trousers up to above the knee? I'll keep a blanket over you.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"No pain. Just weak."},{"t":"0:44","band":"History and inspection","w":"D","s":"When it started, what were you doing? Any fall, back pain or pins and needles?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"I was making tea and it just went. No fall, no pain, no tingling."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Any change with your bladder or bowels, or numbness around your bottom? And anything new today apart from the weakness?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that. Just weaker."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'm looking at both legs for any wasting, twitching or scars. They look the same on both sides, and there's no walking stick around.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:14","band":"Tone","w":"D","s":"Let your legs go floppy. I'll roll each one side to side, lift behind your knee quickly, and flex your foot up sharply.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"Okay. It moves fine when you do it."},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"Yes, the tone is normal on both sides, and there's no jerking at the ankle.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:33","band":"Power","w":"D","s":"Right leg first. Lift it up and stop me pushing it down. Now push it down into my hand.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"That one's fine."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"Now the left. Lift your leg up off the bed for me.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"I can't. It's barely moving. I'm scared I'll never walk again."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying, and it's hard work. Many people with weakness like this do get back to walking. Bend and straighten your knee against me; now push your foot down and pull it up.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"It goes for a second and then just gives."},{"t":"2:07","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Now push your left heel down into my hand. Keep my hand there, and lift your right leg up against my other hand.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Oh. My left leg just pushed down hard."},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"It did. Now push both knees outwards against my hands, then just the left one.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Both together is fine. Just the left on its own, nothing."},{"t":"2:28","band":"Reflexes","w":"D","s":"I'm going to tap your knees and ankles with this hammer. Grip your hands and pull if I ask. Then I'll run something along each sole.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"That tickles."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Reflexes are equal on both sides, and your toes go down normally.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:45","band":"Coordination","w":"D","s":"Place your right heel below your left knee and run it down the front of the shin. Now the left, as best you can.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"Right's easy. Left I can only just shift."},{"t":"2:57","band":"Sensation","w":"D","s":"Close your eyes. Say yes when you feel this cotton wool, and tell me if this feels sharp or blunt. I'll compare both legs.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"Yes... sharp... blunt... feels the same both sides."},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"Now tell me when the buzzing stops. And is your big toe moving up or down?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"Stopped. Up. Down."},{"t":"3:21","band":"Gait","w":"D","s":"If you feel able, stand and walk a few steps. I'll stay right beside you and the nurse is here.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"I can stand. I just drag it."},{"t":"3:32","w":"D","s":"You're doing well. Let's sit you back down safely.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"3:36","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Gillian. Your nerves, reflexes and feeling work normally, and I found nothing new today. That's reassuring about a stroke.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"So why can't I move it? And why did it push down earlier?"},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"Good question. When you lifted your right leg, your left pushed down strongly by itself. The wiring is there; the trouble is sending the signal on purpose. That's a real problem, and it can be retrained with physiotherapy.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:03","w":"P","s":"So it's not in my head."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"No. It's real, it's common, and it often improves. Can you tell me in your words what we found?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"The connections work, but my brain isn't sending the message properly. It can get better."},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll share this with neurology, and I'd also like to check your arms, face and leg pulses. If you notice numbness, back pain or bladder changes, tell the nurse at once. I've cleaned my hands again. Any questions?","m":[13,1]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you. That's the first time it's made sense."}],"marks":[{"c":"Hand hygiene, identity, consent, chaperone, pain check, exposure with dignity and positioning","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Reassures that the weakness is real and validates her fear","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes a brief history including red flags for cord or cauda equina problems","d":"Content"},{"c":"Inspects both legs for wasting, fasciculation, scars and aids","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests tone at hip, knee and ankle, and clonus","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests power at hip, knee and ankle, comparing sides","d":"Content"},{"c":"Performs Hoover's sign and the hip abductor sign correctly","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests knee and ankle reflexes and plantars","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests heel-shin coordination","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests light touch, pinprick, vibration and joint position","d":"Content"},{"c":"Observes gait safely, standing beside her","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains the findings positively, without 'nothing wrong' or 'all stress'","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding, safety-nets red flags, offers further checks and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Complete, ordered neurological check with Hoover's sign, safe gait check and a kind, positive explanation.","borderline":"Covers most parts but misses Hoover's, the red flags or a modality, or explains awkwardly.","fail":"Dismisses her ('nothing wrong', 'it's stress'), skips consent or chaperone, or walks her unsafely."}},"c-t-15":{"setup":"Friday afternoon, the memory clinic. Margaret Oduya, 78, has agreed that you can talk with her daughter, Claire, about her results. Explain the diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease and its management to Claire.","role":{"who":"Ms Claire Oduya, 50, female, daughter","open":"Yes, Margaret's daughter.","manner":"Anxious and tense. Becomes overwhelmed if given jargon or too much at once and asks the doctor to stop; calms fully when re-explained simply. Practical once she understands.","free":["Has been worried about her mother's memory for about a year","Knows the scan is back","Is nervous about the results"],"ifasked":["Mum burnt a pan last month","Mum has missed paying bills","Mum gave her bank details to a cold caller","Mum still drives to the shops","Claire lives ten minutes away and works full time as a teacher","Mum has not made a lasting power of attorney","A neighbour takes a memory tablet"],"worries":["Can anything slow it down?","Is Mum safe on her own?","Will it get worse, and how fast?"],"rules":["If the doctor uses jargon or a long explanation, says 'Please stop, it's too much'; calms if the doctor pauses and re-explains simply","Becomes tearful when the diagnosis is named; settles if validated","Says her mother insists she is fine at home alone"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the older adults team. Are you Claire Oduya?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes, Margaret's daughter."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Claire. Your mum has said I can talk to you about her results. How has she been, and what do you know so far?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Only that the scan's back. I'm nervous. She's been forgetting things for about a year."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"It's natural to feel nervous. What's worrying you most?","m":[2,1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"That it's dementia. And that she's on her own."},{"t":"0:29","band":"Results","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Your mum scored 46 out of 100 on the memory test, which is well below what we'd expect, and the scan showed changes in several areas, and the blood tests and the mood questionnaire...","m":[3]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"Please stop. It's too much. I can't take it all in."},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"Of course, I'm sorry. Let's pause; take your time.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"Okay. Sorry. Go on, but slowly."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"One bit at a time, then. Her memory score was low. Her scan shows shrinkage in the parts of the brain for memory and finding her way around. Her blood tests were normal, and it doesn't look like depression.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"Shrinkage. Right. So what is it?"},{"t":"1:11","band":"Diagnosis","w":"D","s":"Putting it together, your mum has Alzheimer's disease. Dementia is the umbrella term for memory and thinking problems that affect daily life; Alzheimer's is the commonest type. Hers is at a moderate stage.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"I knew it. I think I knew it."},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to hear, even when you half expected it. Take a moment.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"I should have done something sooner."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"It's common to feel that. These changes creep in slowly and people cover them well. You're here now, and that matters.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"What causes it?"},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Proteins build up in the brain and damage the cells, so it slowly shrinks. It's not her fault and not just ageing. It does get worse over time, though the pace varies.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:57","band":"Medication","w":"P","s":"Is there anything that can slow it down? My neighbour's on a memory tablet."},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"Yes. There's a tablet called donepezil. Alzheimer's lowers a brain chemical used for memory, and donepezil stops it being broken down so fast. It doesn't cure it, but can help her memory and day-to-day life for a while.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"Are there side effects?"},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"Some people get sickness, diarrhoea, odd dreams or a slower pulse, so we check her pulse first and start low, at night, then increase after a month. If it doesn't suit, or later on, there's memantine.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:31","band":"Non-drug help","w":"D","s":"Keeping her active helps too. There's a group called cognitive stimulation therapy, twice a week for seven weeks, and a dementia adviser for routines and memory aids.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"She'd like a group. She loves company."},{"t":"2:45","band":"Living alone and risk","w":"D","s":"You mentioned she's on her own. Have you noticed problems with cooking, medicines, bills or getting lost?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:52","w":"P","s":"She burnt a pan last month. She's missed bills. And she gave her bank details to someone on the phone. She insists she's fine."},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"That's worrying, and it's hard when she says she's fine. An occupational therapist can visit to fit a cooker cut-off, a falls sensor and an alarm; her bank can add fraud protection, and the pharmacy a blister pack.","m":[7,2]},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Does she still drive?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"To the shops. She'd hate to stop."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"I understand; it's a big loss. With these results it isn't safe, and she must tell the DVLA. I'll talk to her about it myself.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:29","band":"Planning ahead","w":"D","s":"While she can still make decisions, it's worth her making lasting powers of attorney, one for health and welfare and one for money.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"I didn't know about that. I work full time, though. I'm ten minutes away."},{"t":"3:43","w":"D","s":"Social services can assess her for help at home as things change, and you're able to ask for a carer's assessment yourself. There are carers' groups too.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"Is it going to get much worse?"},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Over time, yes, but many people live several years with it, and planning now gives her more say. We'll be with you both along the way.","m":[4,2]},{"t":"4:05","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"We've covered a lot. To check I've been clear, what will you tell your mum about today?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Alzheimer's, moderate. A tablet that helps a bit. A group, the OT visit, attorney papers, and she has to stop driving."},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"That's spot on. Here are leaflets from the Alzheimer's Society and our number. If she gets suddenly more confused, falls, or is found lost, call us or her GP.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"Thank you. That's much clearer."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"I'll see your mum with you in four weeks to start the plan. What questions do you have?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"None for now. Thank you for slowing down."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms consent and asks what she knows","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her anxiety and guilt, and pauses when she is overwhelmed","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains results in plain words after re-chunking","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names Alzheimer's, explains dementia, cause and progression","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains donepezil: action, benefit, side effects, pulse check, memantine","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes non-drug help: cognitive stimulation, dementia adviser","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Covers home safety: cooking, scams, medicines, falls and driving","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Discusses LPA, social care and carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives written information and safety-net advice","d":"Close"},{"c":"Agrees follow-up and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Names the diagnosis clearly, explains treatment and safety in plain chunks, responds to her overwhelm, and plans ahead.","borderline":"Covers diagnosis and medication but misses driving, scams or LPA, or keeps using jargon.","fail":"Jargon-heavy, avoids the word Alzheimer's, ignores her distress, or says nothing can be done."}},"c-t-16":{"setup":"Tuesday morning, the emergency department. Angela Thornbury, 63, was strangled by her husband Clive, 65, this morning. He is in police custody. Take a history of the change in Clive's behaviour and assess the risks.","role":{"who":"Mrs Angela Thornbury, 63, female, Clive's wife","open":"It hurts. I just don't understand him any more.","manner":"Tearful and shaky at first, holding her neck. Settles with a calm, patient doctor and then answers clearly. Becomes guilty and protective of Clive when police or prosecution are mentioned.","free":["He grabbed her throat this morning when she would not give him the car keys","He has 'not been himself' for about two years","He has never hurt her before","He makes crude remarks to strangers and doesn't care when she is upset"],"ifasked":["No black-out or incontinence; voice hoarse and swallowing sore","Eats whole packets of biscuits; spent about £4,000 online","Checks the locks over and over; stopped washing daily","Memory, speech and orientation seem fine; no voices or strange beliefs","Father had 'Pick's disease' and died in a care home around 60","Went through a red light last month; she hid the keys because of this","Made a sexual remark to a young woman at the bakery","Takes amlodipine; 2-3 pints a week; no head injury or falls"],"worries":["Is it dementia, when his memory is fine?","Will he go to prison?","Is it her fault for hiding the keys?","Is it safe to go home?"],"rules":["Cannot answer more than one word until the doctor acknowledges her neck and her fear","Blames herself for hiding the keys; relieved if told it was sensible","Wants to go home at first; agrees to stay with Sophie if the doctor explains why gently"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and settle","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors here in A&E. Are you Angela Thornbury?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry about what happened this morning. How is your neck now? Are you comfortable to talk?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"It hurts. I just don't understand him any more."},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"Take all the time you need. Here's some water. I want to understand what's been going on to Clive and make sure you're safe. Everything you say helps with both.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:45","band":"This morning","w":"D","s":"When you're ready, can you take me through what happened, from the start?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"I'd hidden his car keys. He wanted to drive to the shop. He shouted, then he came at me and put both hands round my throat."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"That sounds so frightening. How long were his hands there? Did you black out, wet yourself, or find you couldn't breathe?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Seconds, maybe ten. I couldn't breathe. I didn't black out. The dog was barking and he just let go, like nothing had happened."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Since then, any change in your voice, or trouble swallowing? Did he use or threaten anything else? And where is he now?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"My voice is croaky and it hurts to swallow. Nothing else. The police took him."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll ask the A&E doctors to check your neck again before you leave, because swelling can come on later.","m":[3,12]},{"t":"1:45","band":"The change","w":"D","s":"You said he's not been himself. When did you first notice, and was it sudden or gradual?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"About two years. Slowly. He used to be so gentle. A bank manager, very proper."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"What sort of things has he done that the old Clive wouldn't? Can you give me an example?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"He says crude things to strangers. At the bakery he told the young girl she had a nice figure. I wanted the floor to swallow me."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"And how does he react when you're upset?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"He doesn't. I cried at the table last week and he turned the telly up. He's lost interest in the grandchildren too."},{"t":"2:30","band":"Frontal features","w":"D","s":"That must be lonely. Has his eating changed? Any cravings, sweet things especially?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"He eats whole packets of biscuits. He never had a sweet tooth."},{"t":"2:42","w":"D","s":"Any new habits he repeats? And how is he with money, washing and dressing?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"He checks the locks over and over. He's spent about four thousand pounds online on things we don't need. He doesn't shower unless I nag."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Does he think anything is wrong?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"No. He says I'm the one making a fuss."},{"t":"3:08","band":"Other causes","w":"D","s":"How is his memory for recent things? Does he get lost, or struggle to find words?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"His memory's fine, actually. Better than mine. He talks normally."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Has he seemed low, or heard voices, or believed things that aren't true? Any falls, head injury, weakness, drinking, or new tablets?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"No, none of that. He's on a blood pressure tablet, amlodipine. Two or three pints a week, same as always."},{"t":"3:38","w":"D","s":"Has anyone in his family had a change like this, or dementia at a young age?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"His dad went strange in his fifties. They called it Pick's disease. He died in a home. I never knew what it meant."},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's really important. It's an older name for a type of dementia that affects the front of the brain, and it can run in families.","m":[6,10]},{"t":"4:05","band":"Her risk","w":"D","s":"I need to ask some questions about your safety, which I ask everyone in this situation. Has he ever hurt or threatened you before today?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"Never. Thirty-eight years and never."},{"t":"4:16","w":"D","s":"Are you frightened of what he might do next? Has he ever said he'd kill you? Are there any guns or weapons at home?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"I am frightened now. He's never threatened that. No guns. (pause) It's my fault. I hid the keys. I knew it would set him off."},{"t":"4:34","w":"D","s":"You hid them because you were worried he'd crash. That was sensible. Nothing you did makes it OK for him to hurt you.","m":[2]},{"t":"4:44","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Thank you."},{"t":"4:47","w":"D","s":"How have you been in yourself through all this? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"Exhausted. But no, nothing like that. I've got the grandchildren to think of."},{"t":"4:58","band":"Wider risk","w":"D","s":"Is he still driving? Has he had any near misses?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"He went through a red light last month. That's why I hid the keys."},{"t":"5:07","w":"D","s":"Has he behaved sexually or aggressively towards anyone else, or had any police involvement? And does he look after the grandchildren?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:16","w":"P","s":"Just the remarks. Never in trouble. The children are six and nine; they come at weekends but I'm always there."},{"t":"5:24","w":"D","s":"Has he ever talked about harming himself?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:27","w":"P","s":"No. He doesn't seem bothered by anything."},{"t":"5:32","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Angela. What you're describing, a slow change in his manners, his eating and his feelings for others, with memory still good, can be caused by an illness affecting the front of the brain, sometimes called frontotemporal dementia.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","s":"So it is dementia? Even with his memory fine?"},{"t":"5:52","w":"D","s":"It's possible, and that type often leaves memory alone at first. He'll need blood tests, a brain scan and a specialist assessment to be sure. Does that make sense so far?","m":[10,13]},{"t":"6:02","w":"P","s":"Yes. Will they lock him up?"},{"t":"6:05","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"The police decide that, but I'll tell the custody health team what you've told me, so he's assessed properly. And an illness doesn't make this morning any less dangerous for you.","m":[9,11]},{"t":"6:16","w":"P","s":"I just want to go home."},{"t":"6:19","w":"D","s":"I understand. But he could be released today. Could you stay with Sophie tonight? I'll also refer you to a domestic abuse adviser who'll support you, and Clive mustn't drive.","m":[9,11]},{"t":"6:32","w":"P","s":"Sophie would have me. I'll ring her."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Good. If he comes to the house, or your voice or breathing gets worse, or you have trouble swallowing, ring 999 straight away. What questions do you have for me?","m":[12,14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"None. Thank you for listening."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks identity, and explains the purpose of the conversation","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges her injury and distress and gives time before probing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes a clear account of the assault, including strangulation severity and Clive's whereabouts","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes gradual onset over two years with examples of disinhibition and lost empathy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about eating change, compulsions, money, self-care and insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens memory, language, mood, psychosis, substances, head injury, medication and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses her risk: previous violence, fear, threats, weapons and her own wellbeing","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses wider risks: driving, sexual disinhibition, children, forensic history and self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees she will not be alone with Clive tonight","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains a possible frontal brain illness in plain words and the need for tests","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names safeguarding actions: police, custody team, domestic abuse adviser, no driving","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets for delayed neck injury and further threats","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks her understanding during the explanation","d":"Close"},{"c":"Invites questions and closes warmly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Settles her, gets the assault and a two-year frontal picture with examples, screens other causes and family history, and leaves her safe tonight with safeguarding in place.","borderline":"Gets the behaviour change but skims strangulation detail, family history or driving, or explains well but leaves the plan vague.","fail":"Rushes a crying woman, treats the strangulation as minor, or lets a likely dementia replace a risk assessment and sends her home alone."}},"c-t-17":{"setup":"Thursday evening, the acute medical unit. Harold Brennan, 79, was found by police wandering in pyjamas. You are the first doctor. Take a collateral history from his granddaughter Leah and assess the risks.","role":{"who":"Ms Leah Brennan, 24, female, Harold's granddaughter","open":"Yes. Is he alright?","manner":"Anxious and a bit breathless at first; relieved to hear he is safe. Answers quickly once the pace is set. Becomes tearful near the end about her grandmother.","free":["Police found him on the estate in pyjamas this evening","Her mum is at work, so she came","Her dad found his front door open","Harold lives alone with no carers"],"ifasked":["Unshaven and wearing stained clothes for a few months","Gas hob on last week; he insisted he'd turned it off","Repeats questions and forgot her birthday; old memories fine","No illness, falls, head injury or hospital stays that she knows of","Tablets for blood pressure and cholesterol; Nan used to give them","Drinks only at Christmas; doesn't smoke","Still drives to the shop; a new dent on the car","A man offered to fix his roof for cash last month"],"worries":["Is he alright?","Is it dementia?","Is this the beginning of the end for him?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful about her nan's death two months ago; settles if validated","If not reassured, says she needs to go and be with her grandad","Answers yes/no questions quickly once told why they are short"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Leah, Harold's granddaughter?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. Is he alright?"},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"He's safe and warm on the ward. Thank you for coming in so quickly.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Mum's at work. I just came straight here."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"He's happy for us to talk. I'll ask quite a few short questions so we can work out what happened. Quick answers are fine, and it's OK not to know.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"0:28","band":"Tonight","w":"D","s":"What do you know about tonight, from before he left to when he was found?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"The neighbours rang Mum. The police found him walking round the estate in his pyjamas. Dad went round and the front door was wide open."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"Do you know how long he was out, what time it was, and whether he had shoes or a coat?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"About quarter to nine. Slippers, no coat. Nobody knows how long."},{"t":"0:57","w":"D","s":"Was he hurt at all? And has anything like this happened before?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"The nurse said just cold feet. Never, not that we know."},{"t":"1:07","band":"The months before","w":"D","s":"Over the last few months, have you noticed changes in him?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"He's let himself go. Unshaven, stains on his shirts. He was always smart."},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"Anything left on, or forgotten?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"Last week I went round and the gas hob was burning. He swore blind he'd turned it off."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"How's his memory, for recent things and for the past?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"He asks the same thing twice. Forgot my birthday. But he can tell you every car he ever fixed."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Has it crept up gradually, or did he get suddenly worse? Any change in his personality?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"Slowly, maybe a year. Worse since Nan died. He's still Grandad, just quieter."},{"t":"1:53","band":"Delirium and physical","w":"D","s":"Has he been unwell lately: a cough, fever, water infection, constipation, or been in hospital?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Not that I know of."},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"Any falls, bangs to the head, weakness on one side or slurred speech?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"No. No falls."},{"t":"2:09","w":"D","s":"What tablets does he take, has anything changed, and how much does he drink?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Blood pressure and cholesterol. Nan used to put them out for him. He only drinks at Christmas. No drugs, obviously."},{"t":"2:24","band":"Function and mood","w":"D","s":"Who did the cooking, bills and shopping before? How does he manage now?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Nan did everything. His fridge is nearly empty, just microwave meals. There was a red letter from the energy company."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Does he still drive?","m":[6,8]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"To the shop. There's a new dent on the bumper he can't explain."},{"t":"2:47","w":"D","s":"How has he been in himself since your nan died: mood, sleep, appetite?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"Quiet. He cries sometimes. Up in the night. Not eating much."},{"t":"2:59","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Has he ever said he doesn't want to go on, or that he'd harm himself?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"No. He says he misses her, that's all."},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"Does he smoke? Is he taking his tablets properly now?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"Doesn't smoke. The tablets, I don't know. There were loads left in the box."},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"Is he on his own most of the time? Has anyone come to the door asking for money or offering work?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"A man offered to do the roof for cash last month. Dad sent him off. We visit, but not every day."},{"t":"3:34","band":"Her feelings","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Leah, that's really helpful. How are you doing with all this?","m":[2]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"(starts to cry) Nan died two months ago. Kidney cancer. And now this. Is it the beginning of the end for him?"},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. You've lost your nan and now you're frightened for your grandad. That's a lot to carry. It isn't necessarily the beginning of the end. There may be something treatable going on.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"(wiping eyes) Sorry. Go on."},{"t":"4:08","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Tonight we'll test for things that can make older people suddenly confused, like an infection, dehydration, constipation, or a problem with his tablets. That's called delirium, and it can get better.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"But is it dementia?"},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"It's a fair question. The changes over the past year might be memory problems underneath, but I can't say tonight. Once he's well, he'll have proper memory tests. Does that make sense?","m":[9,13]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"Yes. Will he go home?"},{"t":"4:41","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"Not tonight. It isn't safe for him to be on his own while we find out what's going on. He'll stay with us.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:49","w":"P","s":"He'll hate that."},{"t":"4:51","w":"D","s":"I know. Before he goes home, a social worker will look at what help he needs, and things like a gas safety valve and a door alarm. Until then, could the family keep the car keys?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:03","w":"P","s":"Dad can do that."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"It'd also help to fill in a Herbert Protocol form with the police: a photo and places he might go, in case he ever goes missing again. And talk to your GP if your own grief gets too much.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"OK. Thank you."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Here's the ward number. If you think of anything else tonight, ring us. What questions do you have?","m":[12,14]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"Can I go and sit with him?"},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Of course. A familiar face will help him settle. Thank you, Leah.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Harold's agreement, and sets a quick question pace","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Reassures her early and validates her distress about her grandmother","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes tonight's account: time, door, clothing, injuries, and any previous episode","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes gradual decline with examples, memory pattern and personality","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens illness, falls, head injury, stroke signs, medication and alcohol","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about function, finances, driving, mood and grief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses self-harm, medication, isolation and exploitation risks","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Identifies driving and fire risks","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains delirium versus memory problems in plain words without labelling dementia","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States clearly he will not go home alone tonight","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names practical steps: social worker, gas valve, door alarm, car keys","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers Herbert Protocol, ward number and support for Leah","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding","d":"Close"},{"c":"Invites questions and closes warmly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Sets a quick pace, covers tonight, the course, delirium causes, function and three-part risk, and contains Leah with a clear plan for tonight.","borderline":"Good rapport but misses the physical screen, driving or exploitation, or gives a vague plan.","fail":"Labels it dementia, ignores delirium, discharges him home, or leaves Leah in tears without reassurance."}},"c-t-18":{"setup":"Wednesday afternoon, the older adult clinic. Dr Leonard Okoro, 67, retired pathologist, was referred by his GP after a home visit. He lives alone. Take a history and carry out a mental state assessment.","role":{"who":"Dr Leonard Okoro, 67, male, patient","open":"I am. Rather intrigued why I'm here, to be honest.","manner":"Charming, curious and collegial. Guarded if the doctor seems critical of his journals; opens up with genuine interest. Briefly emotional about his wife.","free":["Lives alone since his wife died eight years ago","Collects medical journals; started with his wife's psychiatry journals","Thinks his daughter is behind the referral","Proud; believes the collection could be valuable"],"ifasked":["Over 1,000 journals, now in every room including the kitchen","A small kitchen fire burnt about 20; no longer uses the gas oven","Has tripped over piles at night","Still buys more online","Took the smoke alarm battery out","Back door blocked by piles","No checking, counting or intrusive thoughts; no special meanings","Mood, sleep, appetite and memory fine; drinks socially; doesn't smoke"],"worries":["Will you make me throw them away?","Is my daughter trying to put me in a home?"],"rules":["Says 'enough' when first asked how many; tells the number if asked again without judgement","Becomes defensive if the house is called dirty or a mess","Agrees to a fire service visit if asked respectfully; undecided about therapy"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Dr Okoro?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"I am. Rather intrigued why I'm here, to be honest."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming. Your GP mentioned some worries about how things are at home.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"Ha, my daughter's doing, no doubt. She fusses."},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"It sounds like she cares a lot. I'd like to hear about life at home in your words.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:23","w":"P","s":"Quiet. I read a great deal. I keep up with the literature, you see. Old habits."},{"t":"0:30","band":"The collection","w":"D","s":"Tell me about your reading. I gather you have quite a collection.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Journals. It started with my wife's. She was a psychiatrist; she wrote for the British journals. After she died I kept them, then kept going. Fascinating how the same ideas come round again."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"That's a lovely way to stay connected to her. How long ago did she die?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","s":"Eight years. (pause) It was hard. I retired early. Couldn't face the lab."},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry. That's a huge loss. Roughly how many journals are there now?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"Enough."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"Fair enough; I'm not counting to judge. It just helps me picture your home. Hundreds, or more?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"(smiles) Over a thousand. Several titles now. Complete runs from the sixties. They're probably worth a fortune."},{"t":"1:22","band":"Impact on the home","w":"D","s":"Where do you keep them all?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"The study, originally. Now, well, everywhere. Landing, dining room, some in the kitchen."},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"Which rooms can you still use for what they're meant for? Can you cook and sit comfortably?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"The bedroom, mostly. A chair in the lounge. I don't use the oven any more."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"What made you stop using the oven?","m":[4,9]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"A small fire. Burnt about twenty. Some very good issues, too."},{"t":"1:53","w":"D","s":"That must have been upsetting. Have you ever tripped or fallen over them?","m":[4,9]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"Once or twice in the dark. Nothing broken."},{"t":"2:02","band":"Discarding and insight","w":"D","s":"How would you feel if some of them were thrown away?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"I couldn't. (bristles) So you want me to throw them all out, I suppose?"},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"No. Nobody's going to clear your house without you. I'm trying to understand what they mean to you and how home is working.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"(settles) Reading them, I feel she's still there. That's all."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"That makes a lot of sense. Are you still adding to the collection?","m":[3,5]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"A few a month. Online."},{"t":"2:32","w":"D","s":"Your daughter sees it differently. What do you make of her worry?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"She exaggerates. Though the kitchen is perhaps a bit much."},{"t":"2:41","band":"Look-alikes","w":"D","s":"Do you ever have to check, count or arrange things a set way, or get unwanted thoughts you can't push away?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"No. I like order, but nothing like that."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"What would happen if someone took the journals? Do they hold any special meaning or message for you?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"I'd be furious. But no messages. They're journals, doctor."},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"Have you had strong interests or routines all your life, or found social situations hard?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"I've always loved my subjects. Socially, fine. My wife was the life and soul, mind."},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been, and your sleep and appetite? Any drink or drugs, or thoughts of not wanting to go on?","m":[6,8]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Mood's fine. I sleep well, eat well. A glass at dinners. Never thought of ending things. I still miss her."},{"t":"3:27","w":"D","s":"How is your memory? Any trouble with names, appointments or finding your way?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"Sharp as ever. I could quote you papers."},{"t":"3:43","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Do you smoke, or use heaters near the piles? Do you have a working smoke alarm, and can you reach both doors easily?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:49","w":"P","s":"Never smoked. A fan heater in the study. I took the alarm battery out; the toast kept setting it off. The back door's a bit blocked."},{"t":"4:03","band":"Mental state","w":"D","s":"Could I ask a few quick memory questions, just for completeness? What's today's date, and where are we?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Wednesday the eleventh of November. Your clinic. Fire away."},{"t":"4:15","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you. What you describe has a name: hoarding disorder. It's common, it's nothing to be ashamed of, and it often begins after a loss.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:26","w":"P","s":"A disorder? I'd have called it scholarship."},{"t":"4:29","w":"D","s":"I can see why. The journals clearly matter. The problem isn't the collecting; it's that the rooms, the oven and the back door can't be used safely. Does that make sense?","m":[10,13]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"I suppose the fire wasn't ideal."},{"t":"4:44","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"The first step is safety. The fire service do free home visits; they'd fit an alarm and help clear a path to both doors. Would you let them come?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"If they don't touch the journals."},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"Agreed. Until then, please keep the oven and the heater off and a torch by your bed.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Very well."},{"t":"5:07","w":"D","s":"There's also a talking therapy that helps people decide what to keep, at their own pace. Some people donate duplicates to medical libraries, so they're still valued. Would you think about it?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"I'll think about it. My daughter mustn't come in with a skip."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Nobody will. With your agreement, I'd like to involve her so she understands the plan. I'll write to your GP and see you again in four weeks. Any questions?","m":[12,14]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"None. You've been very civil about it. Thank you."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, uses his title, and explains the referral without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the collection: what, how it started and its link to his wife","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates grief and pride, and de-escalates when he fears a clear-out","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Maps the impact on rooms, the kitchen, the fire and falls","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores difficulty discarding, ongoing acquiring and insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for OCD, psychosis, autism traits, depression and memory problems","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses fire risk: smoking, heaters, smoke alarm and exits","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Covers suicidal thoughts, substances and a brief cognitive check","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Notices the harm already caused: fire and falls","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains hoarding disorder in plain, non-shaming words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees an immediate safety step: fire service visit, oven and heater off","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers CBT for hoarding and family involvement with consent","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding","d":"Close"},{"c":"Arranges follow-up and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Earns his trust, establishes the scale and impact, rules out the look-alikes, covers fire risk and mental state, and agrees a safe first step.","borderline":"Gets the hoarding history but skips the differentials or the smoke alarm and exits, or explains with a hint of judgement.","fail":"Judgemental opening, never learns the scale, calls it OCD without screening, or misses the fire risk."}},"c-t-29":{"setup":"Monday morning, older adults mental health clinic. Dorothy Pennington, 74, lives alone and believes her neighbours are trying to drive her out. Assess her thinking and perceptions only; a full history isn't needed.","role":{"who":"Mrs Dorothy Pennington, 74, female, retired dinner lady, patient","open":"I am. And I shouldn't be here.","manner":"Irritated and clipped at first. Warms if her stress is acknowledged and nobody argues. Bristles if told she is imagining it. Tearful briefly about her children not believing her.","free":["The neighbours are trying to drive her out of her house","It has been going on for about a year","Her son and daughter made her come","She is not ill; they are the problem"],"ifasked":["It began when they put a satellite dish on the shared wall; she knew at once they wanted her house","They pump a sweet-smelling gas through the bedroom wall","They take her post; she thinks they tamper with her food","At night she hears them through the wall talking about her by name: 'She won't last'","Her tea tastes metallic; she now drinks bottled water and eats only sealed food","Sleeps in the back room with towels under the door; has shouted at them and called the police twice","Has hearing aids but rarely wears them because they whistle"],"worries":["Does the doctor think she is going senile?","If the doctor won't stop the neighbours, what is she supposed to do?","Her children think she's making it up"],"rules":["Becomes curt and says 'I knew you wouldn't believe me' if her beliefs are challenged","Puts her hearing aids in if asked politely","Answers memory questions correctly if asked respectfully"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges her annoyance, and avoids arguing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her distress and fear during the interview","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits what the neighbours do, since when, why, and her conviction","d":"Content"},{"c":"Identifies how it began (probable delusional perception)","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits partition delusion and screens for theft, poisoning, reference and grandiosity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Characterises auditory hallucinations: person, content, timing, location","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens smell, taste, sight and touch hallucinations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens thought insertion, withdrawal, broadcast and passivity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks hearing and sight, and briefly screens orientation and recall","d":"Content"},{"c":"Briefly screens mood and asks about risk to self and neighbours","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses how she has responded to the experiences","d":"Content"},{"c":"Summarises, outlines tests and follow-up, and offers family involvement","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mrs Dorothy Pennington?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"I am. And I shouldn't be here."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming anyway, Mrs Pennington. It sounds like things with your neighbours have been very stressful.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"They have."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Would you mind popping your hearing aids in, so I don't miss anything? Then I want to understand what's been going on, in your words.","m":[1,9]},{"t":"0:23","w":"P","s":"(puts them in) Fine. Though they whistle. It's them next door. They want me out."},{"t":"0:30","band":"The neighbours","w":"D","s":"What have they been doing?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"Everything. Taking my post. Pumping gas into my bedroom. Talking about me all night."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. How long has it been going on?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"A year now."},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"Do you remember the moment you first realised what they were up to?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"They put a satellite dish up on our shared wall. I looked at it and I just knew. They wanted my house."},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"Why do you think they want your house? Is anyone else involved?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"Greed. They've got a big family. I don't think anyone else is in on it."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"How sure are you about this? Could there be any other explanation?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"There's no other explanation. I'm not imagining it."},{"t":"1:16","band":"Other beliefs","w":"D","s":"You mentioned gas. How does it get into your house?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Through the bedroom wall. A sweet smell, sickly. It's worse at night."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"Have you noticed anything else tampered with, like food, belongings or bills?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"My post goes missing. And they've been at my food, I'm sure of it."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"Does anything on the TV, radio or in the papers seem to be about you? Do you feel special or chosen in any way?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"Don't be silly. No."},{"t":"1:45","band":"Hallucinations","w":"D","s":"You said they talk about you all night. Can you tell me more? Where does it come from, and what do they say?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"Through the wall. When it's quiet. They say my name. 'Dorothy won't last much longer.' Laughing."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"That must be horrible to lie awake hearing. Do they talk to you, or to each other about you? Is it as clear as my voice?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"To each other. Clear as day. Never during the daytime, mind."},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"Has your food or drink tasted different lately?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"My tea tastes like metal. So I drink bottled water now."},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"Have you seen anything unusual, or felt anything on your skin, like being touched?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"2:28","band":"Passivity and thought","w":"D","s":"Some people feel thoughts are being put into their head, or taken out, or that others can hear them. Or that someone else controls your movements or feelings?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"Certainly not. My thoughts are my own, and so am I."},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"And your mood, through all of this?","m":[10]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"Angry. Tired. Not depressed, if that's what you mean."},{"t":"2:53","band":"Senses and cognition","w":"D","s":"How's your hearing and eyesight generally? I notice the hearing aids were in your bag.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"I can see fine with my glasses. I don't wear the aids. They whistle."},{"t":"3:04","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone a few quick memory questions. Is that alright? What's today's date, and where are we?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"You think I'm going senile. It's Monday the 9th of November. The clinic on Hill Road."},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"That's spot on, and no, I don't. Could you remember three words for me: apple, table, penny? I'll ask in a moment.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Apple, table, penny."},{"t":"3:28","band":"Risk and coping","w":"D","s":"How have you managed at home with all this? Have you changed where you sleep or what you eat?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"I sleep in the back room with towels under the door. I only eat things in packets."},{"t":"3:41","w":"D","s":"Have you done anything about the neighbours, or thought about getting back at them?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"I've shouted at them. Called the police twice. They did nothing. I'd never hurt anyone."},{"t":"3:53","w":"D","s":"Has it ever got so bad you felt you couldn't go on, or thought of harming yourself?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"No. I'm not giving them the satisfaction. (quieter) My own children think I'm making it up."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"That must feel very lonely. I can see how real and how exhausting this is for you. What were those three words?","m":[2,9]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"Apple, table, penny."},{"t":"4:15","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Thank you, Mrs Pennington. You've had a very hard year, you're not sleeping in your own bed, and you're hearing and smelling things that frighten you at night.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"If you won't stop them, what are you suggesting I do?"},{"t":"4:29","w":"D","s":"I can't promise to change the neighbours, but I can help with how unsettled and unsafe you're feeling at night. I'd like some blood tests, a brain scan and a hearing check, and to see you again in two weeks.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"A brain scan? Well. If it shows I'm right."},{"t":"4:46","w":"D","s":"It helps us check everything properly. Would you be happy for your son and daughter to come next time, so we can all talk together?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"As long as you tell them I'm not making it up."},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"I'll tell them this is very real for you. If you ever feel in danger at home, ring your family, your GP, or 111 and choose option 2; 999 in an emergency. Take care, Mrs Pennington.","m":[2,10,12]}],"global":{"pass":"Engages her without arguing, elicits the persecutory and partition delusions with onset and conviction, screens every sense and thought interference, checks hearing and cognition, and asks about risk.","borderline":"Finds the delusion and voices but misses smell and taste, the onset moment, or the cognitive and sensory check.","fail":"Argues with her beliefs, drifts into a full history, or omits hallucination screening."}},"c-t-31":{"setup":"Memory clinic, Tuesday afternoon. Harold Whitaker, 79, has Alzheimer's and is starting galantamine; he takes bisoprolol and his pulse is 58. His son Neil, 50, wants to talk about the medicine. Explain benefits and side effects and answer his concerns.","role":{"who":"Mr Neil Whitaker, 50, male, son and main family support","open":"Yes, Harold's son. I've got a few questions about this tablet, if that's OK.","manner":"Polite, a little sceptical, tired. Warms when given honest answers rather than reassurance. Becomes briefly emotional when talking about his own exhaustion.","free":["Dad got lost driving home twice and repeats questions","Read online that these drugs 'don't do much'","Dad is on bisoprolol and the GP said his pulse is slow"],"ifasked":["Visits Dad every evening after work and does his shopping","Dad sometimes forgets his tablets","Read that the drug damages the liver","A colleague recommended daffodil extract","Dad is careful with money and will ask about cost","Has not thought about driving or Power of Attorney"],"worries":["Does it actually work?","Will it harm his heart?","Is it addictive?","What else can be done apart from the tablet?"],"rules":["Becomes more doubtful if told the drug will stop the illness","Asks about the heart again if the doctor doesn't raise it by the side-effects stage","Says 'I'm shattered' if asked how he is coping; settles if acknowledged"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the memory clinic. Are you Neil Whitaker?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Yes, Harold's son."},{"t":"0:09","w":"D","s":"Thanks for staying, Neil. Your dad has said he's happy for us to talk about his treatment. What do you already know about galantamine, and what's on your mind?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Not much. I looked it up last night. Mainly whether it actually works. Everything I read says these drugs don't do much."},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"That's a really fair question, and I'll be honest with you. Is there anything else worrying you, so I can cover it all?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"His heart. He's on bisoprolol and the GP said his pulse is slow. And whether it'll make him ill."},{"t":"0:45","band":"How it works","w":"D","s":"Let me start with what's going on. Your dad has Alzheimer's at a mild to moderate stage. Memory and planning are affected, but he still does a lot himself. Does that fit with what you see?","m":[3,11]},{"t":"0:59","w":"P","s":"Yes. He's got lost driving home twice, and he asks me the same thing five times."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"That must be worrying to watch. In Alzheimer's a brain chemical called acetylcholine runs low. It helps nerve cells pass on messages for memory. Galantamine slows its breakdown, so more of it is around.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"So it fixes his memory?"},{"t":"1:25","band":"Honest benefit","w":"D","s":"I'm afraid it doesn't cure Alzheimer's or stop it. What it can do is help symptoms for a while. Some people improve a bit in memory, daily tasks or mood; many stay steadier for some months; some notice no change at all.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"So it's a lottery."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"In a way, yes, and we won't know until he tries it. We'll judge it at about three months. If it's helping and suits him, he carries on long term. If not, we stop it.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"OK. That's more honest than what I read."},{"t":"2:01","band":"How to take it","w":"D","s":"It's one long-acting capsule each morning with breakfast. Food helps stop sickness. He starts on 8 mg for four weeks, then goes up to 16 mg if he's fine. Some people go up to 24 later.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"He forgets tablets sometimes."},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"A weekly pill box or a pharmacy blister pack helps. If he misses more than a few days in a row, ring us, because he'd need to restart on the low dose rather than jump back in.","m":[5,13]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"I can sort his tablets on a Sunday."},{"t":"2:34","band":"Side effects","w":"D","s":"That's great. Now side effects. The common ones are feeling sick, poor appetite, loose stools, some weight loss, dizziness and sometimes vivid dreams. They often settle within a week or two of each step.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"And his heart?"},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"You were right to raise it. Galantamine can slow the heart a little, and bisoprolol already does. So before he starts we'll do a heart tracing, an ECG, and his GP will check his pulse as the dose goes up.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"What if it goes too slow?"},{"t":"3:09","w":"D","s":"If he faints, has falls, feels very dizzy, or his pulse drops below 50, stop the capsule and get him seen that day. Same for any blistering or spreading rash, which is rare but serious.","m":[6,7,13]},{"t":"3:23","w":"P","s":"Right. That's a lot to remember."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"It is, and it's all in the leaflet I'll give you. Can I check: which ones would make you stop it and call?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"Fainting, falls, pulse under 50, a bad rash."},{"t":"3:36","band":"His other questions","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. What else did you read online?","m":[2]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"That it wrecks the liver."},{"t":"3:43","w":"D","s":"It isn't known to damage the liver. We only lower the dose if someone has serious liver disease, and your dad doesn't.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"Is it addictive? I don't want him hooked."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"No, it isn't addictive and he won't crave it. If it's stopped, any benefit fades over a few weeks, so we'd plan stopping together.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"And cost? Dad's careful with money."},{"t":"4:09","w":"D","s":"It's on the NHS, and at his age his prescriptions are free.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"A colleague said daffodil extract does the same thing. Could he just take that?"},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"There is a link: galantamine was first found in snowdrop and daffodil bulbs. But extracts sold as supplements aren't tested, the dose is unknown, and parts of the bulb are poisonous. Please steer him away from them.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"Fair enough."},{"t":"4:36","band":"Monitoring","w":"D","s":"Here's the plan for checking. ECG before he starts. GP checks pulse and weight. We see him in clinic at three months to look at memory, daily life and side effects, then about every six months.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"And if it's not working by then?"},{"t":"4:53","w":"D","s":"Then we talk about stopping it, or other options later on, such as memantine if things move on.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:01","w":"P","s":"Is that it, though? Apart from the tablet, what else can we do for Dad?"},{"t":"5:05","band":"Wider plan","w":"D","s":"Quite a lot. There are memory groups called cognitive stimulation, twice a week. You can have a carers' assessment for support. And it's a good time for him to set up a Lasting Power of Attorney.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:21","w":"P","s":"I'm already shattered, to be honest. Work, then his place every evening."},{"t":"5:26","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting, Neil, and it's a lot to carry on your own. The carers' assessment is exactly for that. I'll pass your details to our dementia adviser today.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"5:38","w":"P","s":"Thanks. That would help."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"One more thing, about driving. With this diagnosis your dad must tell the DVLA. We can help arrange a driving assessment if he wants to keep driving.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:53","w":"P","s":"He'll hate that. But after getting lost, I think he knows."},{"t":"5:57","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Just so I can check I've explained it clearly, could you run the plan back to me?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"ECG first, then 8 mg with breakfast, up to 16 in a month. Watch for sickness and dizziness, stop if he faints or his pulse is under 50. Review at three months."},{"t":"6:16","w":"D","s":"Spot on. Here's the leaflet, the Alzheimer's Society details and our clinic number. If anything worries you, ring us, or the GP or 111 out of hours.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:29","w":"P","s":"Thanks, doctor. I feel better about it now."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. I'll write to his GP today with the plan. Is there anything we haven't covered?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:38","w":"P","s":"No, I think that's everything."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Then I'll see you both in three months. Take care, Neil.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms father's consent, and asks what Neil knows and fears","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds to his doubt, tiredness and worry with empathy before giving information","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Recaps the diagnosis and explains how galantamine works in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States it is not a cure and gives honest, realistic benefit with a review point","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains dosing: XL once daily with food, step-up after four weeks, missed-dose rule","d":"Content"},{"c":"Describes common side effects and the rare serious rash","d":"Content"},{"c":"Addresses slow pulse with bisoprolol: ECG first, pulse checks, clear stop rules","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Answers concerns about liver, addiction, cost and daffodil supplements accurately","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains monitoring and follow-up at three months then six-monthly","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Covers wider support: cognitive stimulation, carers' assessment, LPA and DVLA","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding using teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Summarises, gives leaflet and contacts, and closes warmly","d":"Close"},{"c":"Safety-nets: when to stop and who to call, including out of hours","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Honest about modest benefit, clear dosing and side effects, ECG and pulse plan for bisoprolol, all concerns answered, teach-back and safety net.","borderline":"Accurate information but delivered as a lecture, or misses the heart issue, or no review point.","fail":"Promises a cure, ignores the slow pulse on a beta-blocker, or gives wrong information about addiction or safety."}},"c-t-34":{"setup":"Thursday afternoon, family room on an adult acute ward. Jamal Hussain, 34, has treatment-resistant schizophrenia and is 12 weeks into clozapine. His mother, Mrs Shabnam Hussain, is angry and wants to take him home. Jamal agrees she can be involved. Address her concerns.","role":{"who":"Mrs Shabnam Hussain, 58, female, Jamal's mother","open":"I am, and I'm not happy.","manner":"Angry, holding printouts, interrupts if not listened to. Softens when apologised to and shown his results. Stays firm about complaining.","free":["Read online that clozapine destroys bone marrow","Nobody from the ward has rung her","Jamal looks dirty and wears the same clothes","Wants to take him home now"],"ifasked":["Lives 30 miles away with her husband, who drinks every night","Jamal and her husband argue; Jamal withdraws to his room","Found out last week that Jamal's brother took his benefits every month","Thinks Jamal drank at his brother's","Jamal seemed calmer on Sunday's visit","She is exhausted and not sleeping"],"worries":["Is the drug an experiment?","What happened to his liver tests?","How long until he can leave?","She wants to complain"],"rules":["Gets angrier if interrupted or if told clozapine is 'perfectly safe'","Calms noticeably after a specific apology and hearing his actual results","Will make a complaint whatever happens; respects being given the route"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Let her speak","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mrs Hussain, Jamal's mother?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"I am, and I'm not happy."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"I can see you're upset, and I want to hear why. Jamal has said we can talk with you about his care. Please tell me what's worrying you most, and we'll go through each thing.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"This drug. Clozapine. I looked it up. It destroys bone marrow. You're experimenting on my son."},{"t":"0:32","w":"D","s":"OK. That sounds frightening to read. What else is worrying you?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"Nobody rings me. Three months, and he still smells and wears the same clothes. I can look after him better myself. I'm taking him home."},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"So the medicine, not being told anything, how he looks, and wanting him home. Anything else?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"His blood tests were bad when he came in. Nobody's said what happened."},{"t":"1:03","band":"Acknowledge","w":"D","s":"Thank you. You've been worried sick and you've felt shut out. Nobody rang you, and that shouldn't have happened. I'm sorry.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Sorry doesn't help him."},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"You're right, it doesn't on its own. So from this week, his named nurse will ring you every Friday. And let's go through each worry now.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Fine. Start with the drug."},{"t":"1:33","band":"Clozapine","w":"D","s":"Jamal's illness didn't get better on two other medicines. When that happens, clozapine is the one most likely to help. It's not an experiment; it's the recommended next step.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"But the bone marrow."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"You're right that it can lower the white blood cells that fight infection. It's uncommon, fewer than one in a hundred people, and mostly in the first few months.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"So it could happen to him."},{"t":"2:07","w":"D","s":"It could, and that's the reason his blood is checked weekly. If the count started to drop, we'd stop clozapine straight away. Every single one of his counts has been normal.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"Every week?"},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Weekly for the first eighteen weeks, then fortnightly until a year, then once a month. No normal result, no tablets. Can I check that makes sense?","m":[4,10]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"Weekly, then less often. And they stop it if it's low."},{"t":"2:43","band":"His results","w":"D","s":"Exactly. About his tests on arrival: his liver tests were raised. That was before clozapine, probably from how he'd been living at the flat. They've been normal on every test since.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"Was he drinking? At his brother's?"},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"He's told us he drank some there. Since he's been here, his liver is fine, and we keep checking it.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"Nobody told me that."},{"t":"3:15","band":"Progress and care","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you've had to wait to hear it. Clozapine works slowly. People often keep improving over six months to a year. He's hearing voices less and going to groups now.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"But he's dirty. My son was always clean."},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"That must be really hard to see. The illness takes away motivation. The nurses and occupational therapist work with him every day on washing and dressing, step by step.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"He did seem calmer on Sunday."},{"t":"3:52","band":"Going home","w":"D","s":"That's good to hear. I want him home too, Mrs Hussain. Can I ask about home, so any plan is safe? Who lives with you?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"My husband. He drinks, every night. But I'd manage."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a lot for you to manage already. How does Jamal get on with him when he's been drinking?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"4:18","w":"P","s":"They argue. Jamal goes quiet and stays in his room."},{"t":"4:23","w":"D","s":"And at his brother's, did Jamal ever say anything about his money?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"His brother took his benefits. Every month. I only found out last week."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, that's awful. That's financial abuse, and I'll raise a safeguarding concern today so it's looked into properly.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"Good. Somebody should."},{"t":"4:49","w":"D","s":"There's also the distance. His weekly bloods would need setting up near you, and if he missed clozapine for two days it would have to be restarted slowly.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:03","w":"P","s":"So you're saying no."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"I'm saying not yet, and not without a plan. Leaving now would risk him relapsing. What I'd suggest is graded leave: day visits first, then a night, with his bloods arranged.","m":[7,9]},{"t":"5:21","w":"P","s":"How long until he can leave?"},{"t":"5:23","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I can't give a date today, but his consultant can. Could you come to a family meeting on Monday at two to agree the plan together?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:34","w":"P","s":"I suppose so."},{"t":"5:37","w":"D","s":"We'll also look at whether supported accommodation might suit him better than either flat, and we can arrange a carer's assessment for you.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:49","w":"P","s":"I still want to complain. About the phone calls."},{"t":"5:53","w":"D","s":"That's absolutely your right, and it won't affect his care. Here's the PALS number and the complaints leaflet. I'll pass your concerns to the ward manager today as well.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:07","w":"P","s":"Thank you."},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can I check what you're taking away from today?","m":[10]},{"t":"6:14","w":"P","s":"Weekly blood tests, all fine. Liver's fine. A nurse rings Fridays. Meeting Monday. And you'll look into his brother."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"That's right. If he ever has a fever or sore throat on leave, he needs a blood test that day. Here's a clozapine leaflet and the ward number.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"6:37","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming in and telling us straight. I'll see you on Monday.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Jamal's consent and lets her list all her concerns","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges her anger and distress and apologises specifically for poor contact","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains why clozapine was chosen and that response is slow","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the white cell risk honestly, with frequency","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains monitoring and his normal FBC and liver results","d":"Content"},{"c":"Describes the ward's care for his self-care difficulties","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores home: husband's drinking, distance, bloods, missed-dose risk","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Identifies the brother's financial abuse and raises safeguarding","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains why discharge now is unsafe and offers graded leave and a family meeting","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and gives warning signs for leave","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers weekly contact and the PALS and complaints route without defensiveness","d":"Close"},{"c":"Summarises, gives written information and closes respectfully","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Listens first, apologises specifically, explains clozapine risk and monitoring with his results, discusses home risks and safeguarding, offers a meeting and the complaints route.","borderline":"Accurate on clozapine but defensive, or misses the safeguarding issue, or no plan for discharge.","fail":"Argues with her, says clozapine is completely safe, agrees to discharge to defuse things, or discourages a complaint."}},"c-t-38":{"setup":"Wednesday late morning, a home visit for the older people's mental health team. Reginald Thompson, 65, retired bus driver, referred by his GP for weight loss and low mood. His wife is present. Take a focused history and mental state assessment to reach a diagnosis.","role":{"who":"Mr Reginald (Reg) Thompson, 65, male, retired bus driver, patient; his wife Mrs Pat Thompson, 63, is present","open":"Can't say I've been right.","manner":"Slow, quiet, long pauses; struggles with long open questions, answers short simple ones. Tearful about feeling useless. Pat is worried and chips in once to ask what can help.","free":["Hasn't felt right since retiring last year","Feels useless","Lost weight, not hungry","Wakes early"],"ifasked":["Low every day for 4-5 months, worst in the mornings; wakes at 4am with dread","Stopped going to the allotment; no energy; can't follow the television","Lost about a stone in 3 months; no pain, bowel change, cough or bleeding; ex-smoker; on amlodipine 5 mg","Drinks two cans of bitter most nights, more than before retiring; no other drugs","Worried the pension is smaller, but accepts the house is paid off and they are 'alright really'","Best friend Ken died last year; mother had 'bad nerves'; never ill like this before","Sometimes thinks he would be better off dead; no plan, no attempts; would not do it to Pat or the grandchildren","Still drives to the shops; never after drinking; no guns in the house"],"worries":["'It's cancer, isn't it?'","'Is my memory going?'","(Pat) 'Is there anything that can get him back to himself?'"],"rules":["Answers 'don't know' to long questions; answers well if questions are short and simple","Becomes tearful about feeling useless; settles if validated","Says money will run out until asked to go through the facts, then accepts they are all right","Gets cognitive questions right but slowly and with effort"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Reginald Thompson?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Reg, please."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thank you for having me, Reg. Your GP is worried you've lost weight and felt low. Are you happy for Pat to stay while we talk?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Can't say I've been right. She can stay."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"Would you tell me how things have been for you lately?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:23","w":"P","s":"(long pause) ...Don't know. Just... nothing."},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"That's OK. I'll ask simpler questions, one at a time. Is your mood low most of the day?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"Yes. Every day."},{"t":"0:38","band":"Mood and depression","w":"D","s":"Since when?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Since I retired. Worse since the spring."},{"t":"0:43","w":"D","s":"Are mornings or evenings the worst?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:46","w":"P","s":"Mornings. I wake at four with this dread. Can't get back off."},{"t":"0:51","w":"D","s":"What do you still enjoy? Pat mentioned your allotment.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Haven't been in months. No point. I'm no use to anyone now. Forty years driving and then nothing. (tearful)"},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"That's a huge loss, Reg: the job, the routine, the people. It makes sense you feel lost. How's your energy?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"None. Takes me till lunch to get dressed."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"Can you concentrate on the telly or the paper? And do you blame yourself for anything?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"Can't follow anything. I feel a burden to Pat."},{"t":"1:22","band":"Physical causes","w":"D","s":"How's your appetite? How much weight have you lost, and over how long?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"Not hungry. About a stone, since the summer. It's cancer, isn't it?"},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd worry. Depression often causes weight loss, but we must check properly. Any pain, change in your bowels, blood, or a cough?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"No. None of that. I gave up smoking twenty years ago."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"What tablets do you take? Has the GP done blood tests?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"Blood pressure one. She did bloods, said they were fine."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"And alcohol: how much in a typical week? Anything else, like sleeping tablets or other drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"Two cans of bitter most nights. More than I used to. Nothing else."},{"t":"2:02","band":"Cognition","w":"D","s":"You said you can't follow things. Can I do a few quick memory checks? What day and date is it?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"(slowly) Wednesday... the sixth? November."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"That's right. I'll say three words: apple, table, penny. Please repeat them and keep them in mind.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"Apple, table, penny."},{"t":"2:21","band":"Psychotic features","w":"D","s":"Do you have worries about anything in particular?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Money. We'll run out. The pension's half what I earned."},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"That's a real change. Can we look at it together? What comes in each month, and what goes out?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Well... the house is paid off. Pat's got her pension too. I know we're alright really. It just goes round my head."},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"Do you ever feel something terrible is happening inside your body, or that you deserve to be punished? Any voices when no one's there?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"What were those three words?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"(pause) Apple... table... penny."},{"t":"3:00","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"Well done. Have you ever felt like this before? Anyone in the family with depression?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"Never. Mum had her nerves."},{"t":"3:09","w":"D","s":"What else has changed in the last year or two? Who do you see these days?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"My best mate Ken died last year. I don't see anyone now, just Pat."},{"t":"3:18","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry about Ken. At your lowest, does it ever feel life's not worth carrying on with?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think Pat would be better off without me."},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; that's hard to say. Have you thought of ending your life, or how you'd do it?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"No. I couldn't do that to her. Or the grandkids."},{"t":"3:38","w":"D","s":"Are there tablets saved up anywhere, or any guns in the house?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"No guns. Pat does the tablets."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"Are you eating and drinking enough to keep going? And are you still driving?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"Tea and a bit of toast. I drive to the shops. Never after a drink."},{"t":"3:53","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Reg, from what you've told me, this sounds like depression, and quite a heavy one: low every day, early waking, no enjoyment, weight loss. It's an illness, not a weakness, and it's very treatable at any age.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"Not my memory going, then?"},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Your memory checks were fine; depression slows thinking, and that improves as the mood lifts. I'll ask your GP to check your physical health properly, including the weight loss.","m":[7,12]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"(Pat) Is there anything that can get him back to himself?"},{"t":"4:23","w":"D","s":"Yes. A medicine at night to help sleep and appetite, someone from our team every week, and small daily plans together, like the allotment. Please don't drive for now, Reg, while concentration is poor. Pat, if he stops eating or drinking, talks of ending his life, or says things that seem untrue, call us the same day, or 111 option 2 out of hours. Can you both tell me what we've agreed?","m":[11,13]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"(Reg) Tablet at night, GP tests, no driving, someone coming each week. And Pat rings if I get worse."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the visit and checks consent for his wife to stay","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Adapts to his slowness with short questions and validates his distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits low mood, anhedonia, duration and diurnal variation with early waking","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about energy, concentration, guilt, appetite and weight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores physical causes of weight loss: red flags, medicines, GP tests","d":"Content"},{"c":"Quantifies alcohol and asks about other drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Does a brief cognitive check and interprets it as depression, not dementia","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests money worries against evidence and screens for other psychotic features","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores past episodes, family history, losses and isolation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about death wishes, plans, means and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses eating, drinking and driving","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises and names depression plainly with physical checks","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Involves his wife, agrees next steps and crisis contacts, checks understanding","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Adapts to his slowness, establishes severe depression, excludes psychosis and dementia, checks physical causes and alcohol, covers risk and driving, and explains the diagnosis to both.","borderline":"Gets the depression right but skips testing the money belief, alcohol or driving.","fail":"Persists with open questions he cannot answer, misses suicide risk, or calls it normal retirement sadness or dementia."}},"c-t-40":{"setup":"Friday afternoon, the stroke rehabilitation ward. Harold Fenwick, 68, right-handed, had a stroke three weeks ago with left arm and leg weakness. His family say he ignores his left side and struggles to dress. Assess his parietal lobe function at the bedside.","role":{"who":"Mr Harold (Harry) Fenwick, 68, male, retired joiner, patient","open":"Go on then.","manner":"Polite but a bit impatient; doubts the point of tests. Frustrated when he cannot draw. Cooperates when each test is explained. Unconcerned about his left side.","free":["Right-handed; wears reading glasses","Here because his family asked","His left arm is 'just a bit lazy'"],"ifasked":["No pain; hearing fine","Sees a single finger moving on either side but only the right when both move; same with touch","Marks a line well right of centre; crosses out stars only on the right","Draws pentagons with a distorted left side and a clock with numbers bunched on the right; puts the gown on the right arm only","Thinks he could walk home today","Feels touch in both hands but cannot name a coin or key, or numbers drawn, in the left hand","Finger naming, left and right, sums and writing all correct; writing drifts to the right of the page","Misses finger movements in the lower left quarter with each eye; mime and pen use normal; repeats six digits"],"worries":["'What's the point of all this?'","'Am I going doolally? Is this dementia?'","'Will I be able to drive again?'"],"rules":["Refuses a task if not told why; agrees once given a one-line reason","Says 'I feel like an idiot' after the clock; settles if reassured","Denies anything is really wrong with his left arm if asked directly; does not argue if the doctor does not argue","Reluctant to close his eyes until offered an alternative"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Harold Fenwick?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Harry. Yes."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Harry. Your family wondered if the stroke has affected how you think and notice things.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"So they keep saying."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"I'd like to do some short tests with paper and objects. Is that alright, and are you comfortable?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:19","w":"P","s":"Go on then."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"Which hand do you write with? Have you got your glasses, and is your hearing OK? Any pain? Sister Okafor will stay with us, if that's alright.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Right hand. Glasses are on. No pain. She can stay."},{"t":"0:34","band":"Inattention and neglect","w":"D","s":"Look at my nose and keep looking. Tell me which finger moves.","m":[4]},{"t":"0:39","w":"P","s":"That one. (right) ...That one. (left)"},{"t":"0:43","w":"D","s":"Good. And now?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"That one. (points right only, both moved)"},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Now with your eyes closed, say left, right or both when I touch your hands.","m":[4]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"I'm not closing my eyes. I don't like it."},{"t":"0:56","w":"D","s":"That's fine, we'll do it your way. You can look at the ceiling instead. It tells me whether you notice touch on both sides at once.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"Alright. Right... left... right. (misses left when both touched)"},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"Now mark the middle of this line for me, please. Then cross out every star on this page.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"There. (mark well right of centre) ...Done. (left half of stars untouched)"},{"t":"1:24","band":"Building and dressing","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Could you copy these two overlapping shapes as carefully as you can?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"What's the point of all this? It's my arm that's the problem."},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"Fair question. Your family noticed you miss things on your left, and these show the therapists exactly where to help. Each one takes under a minute.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:42","w":"P","s":"Fine. (draws; left side of the shapes distorted, no overlap)"},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"Now draw me a clock, numbers included, showing ten past eleven.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"(numbers bunched on the right, left side empty) I can't even draw a clock. I feel like an idiot."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"You're not, Harry. This is hard after a stroke on this side, and it's showing me something useful. Could you put this gown on for me?","m":[3,7]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"(puts right arm in; left sleeve left hanging) There."},{"t":"2:12","band":"Awareness","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Is anything wrong with your left arm or leg?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Nothing much. It's just a bit lazy."},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"Do you think you could manage at home today, walking about?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"Course I could. I'd be fine."},{"t":"2:24","band":"Touch and feel","w":"D","s":"OK. Next, some touch tests. First, can you feel me touching each hand? Is your finger going up or down?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"Yes, both. Up. Down... up? (unsure on the left)"},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"I'll cover your hand with this towel so you can't see. What am I putting in your right hand?","m":[3,8]},{"t":"2:41","w":"P","s":"A coin. Now a key."},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"And your left hand?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"Don't know. Something round? No idea."},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"What number am I drawing on your palm? And is this one point or two?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"Right: a three. Two points. Left: no idea. One? Can't tell."},{"t":"2:59","band":"Dominant side","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Which finger am I touching? And put your right hand on your left ear.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Index. (touches left ear correctly)"},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"What's 100 take away 7? And 38 minus 14? Then write me a sentence about the weather.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"93. 24. (writes 'It's raining again', squeezed onto the right side of the page)"},{"t":"3:21","band":"Vision and actions","w":"D","s":"Cover your left eye. Look at my nose and tell me when my finger moves, in each corner. Now the other eye.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Yes... yes... yes... (misses lower left in each eye)"},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"Show me how you'd brush your teeth, and salute. Then take the cap off this pen and write your name.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:42","w":"P","s":"(mimes and salutes correctly; uncaps pen and writes name)"},{"t":"3:47","w":"D","s":"Repeat after me: 'The quick brown fox jumped over the fence.' Now these numbers: 4, 9, 2, 7, 1, 5.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"The quick brown fox jumped over the fence. 4, 9, 2, 7, 1, 5."},{"t":"4:00","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Harry, you've worked hard. Your memory, sums, writing and speech are fine. The stroke has affected the right side of the brain that helps you notice your left side and judge space. That's why the left seems to disappear and dressing is tricky.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"Am I going doolally? Is this dementia?"},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"No. It's the stroke affecting one part of the brain, and it often improves with therapy. I'll share this with your therapists so they can train you to scan to the left, and I'll explain it to your family.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"Will I be able to drive again?"},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"Not for now: with the stroke and the gap in your lower left vision, you mustn't drive and the DVLA needs to know. We'll recheck as you recover. How will you explain today to your family?","m":[13]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"That it's the stroke, not my marbles. And I've got to keep looking left."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, gives the reason for the tests and gains consent","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks handedness, glasses, hearing and pain, and arranges a chaperone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains tests simply and handles refusal and frustration calmly","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Tests visual and sensory inattention, each side alone then both together","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests neglect with line bisection or a cancellation task","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests constructional apraxia with copying and a clock","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests dressing apraxia and awareness of the weakness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Confirms basic touch, then tests stereognosis, graphaesthesia, two-point and position sense","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests finger naming, left-right orientation, calculation and writing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests visual fields by quadrant in each eye","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests ideomotor and ideational praxis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests repetition and working memory","d":"Content"},{"c":"Summarises findings plainly, answers worries, plans team report and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Sets up safely, tests every parietal domain in a logical order with clear instructions, handles his reluctance, and summarises a right parietal pattern plainly.","borderline":"Covers most domains but tests extinction without single stimuli first, or skips cortical sensation or fields.","fail":"Uses a global screen only, gives confusing instructions, argues about his weakness, or runs out of time before half the domains."}},"c-t-43":{"setup":"Adult acute ward, Friday afternoon. Kofi Asante, 18, has fever 41°C, rigidity and CK 1024 after haloperidol, including two intramuscular doses. Explain to his father what is happening, set out the plan and address his concerns. No history needed.","role":{"who":"Mr Kwame Asante, 52, male, Kofi's father","open":"Yes. The nurse said he's unwell. What's going on?","manner":"Anxious and clipped at first, then angry with arms folded once injections are mentioned. Softens with an honest apology and a clear plan; tearful when he blames himself.","free":["He persuaded Kofi to come into hospital","Kofi seemed calmer on Monday when he visited","He wants to know what is wrong right now"],"ifasked":["Nobody told him Kofi had been given injections","Kofi has no allergies and no medical illnesses","Kofi's mother is abroad and he is the main contact","He wants to be with Kofi when he is moved","He works shifts but can come in every evening"],"worries":["Is it an allergy?","Could he die?","Who is responsible, and how does he complain?","How will his psychosis be treated now?"],"rules":["Says 'You've poisoned him' if injections are mentioned without an apology; settles if apology is sincere","Asks 'Could he die?' and becomes more angry if the answer is evasive","Becomes tearful and says 'This is my fault' after the plan is explained; settles if reassured","Accepts future treatment if the precautions are explained clearly"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Kofi's consent, and sets an honest agenda","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges his anger and guilt empathically, without becoming defensive","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the findings and blood tests in plain language","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names NMS, explains it is rare, unpredictable and not an allergy","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains risk factors including doses and injections, and apologises","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains stopping antipsychotics and urgent medical transfer","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Describes fluids, cooling, monitoring, infection checks and possible muscle treatments","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains complications and that it can be fatal, with realistic hope","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Offers consultant meeting, incident review and PALS","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains future antipsychotic rechallenge with precautions","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding with teach-back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Gives contact details, warning signs to report, and closes","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors on the ward. Are you Mr Asante, Kofi's father?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Yes. The nurse said he's unwell. What's going on?"},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"Kofi has agreed I can talk to you. I'll be honest: he's become physically unwell and we're acting quickly.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"May I explain what we've found and what we're doing, then answer your questions?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:21","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"0:23","band":"What we found","w":"D","s":"This afternoon Kofi developed a very high temperature, 41 degrees. His heart is racing and his muscles have gone stiff.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"He was fine on Monday. He was calmer."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"His blood tests show his muscles are breaking down and releasing an enzyme called CK, and his white cells and liver tests are raised.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Is it an infection?"},{"t":"0:48","band":"Likely cause","w":"D","s":"The medical team will check for infection. But we think the most likely cause is a rare, serious reaction to his antipsychotic, haloperidol. It's called neuroleptic malignant syndrome.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"So he's allergic to it?"},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"It's not an allergy. It's an unpredictable reaction that affects temperature and muscle control. It can happen at normal doses, but higher doses, quick increases and injections make it more likely.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"Injections? Nobody told me about injections."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"On Wednesday he became very agitated and wanted to leave, and he was given two injections of haloperidol to calm him.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"I agreed to hospital so he'd get better. Not so you could poison him."},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"I understand why you're angry, and I'm truly sorry this has happened to Kofi. The most important thing now is that we've stopped the cause and he's being treated.","m":[2,5]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"So how are you going to fix it?"},{"t":"1:46","band":"Immediate treatment","w":"D","s":"The haloperidol is stopped, and no similar medicine will be given for now. The medical team are taking over; he's moving to their ward within the hour.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"Why not here?"},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"He needs closer physical care than we can give. They'll give fluids through a drip to protect his kidneys, cool him down, and check his heart, temperature and blood tests regularly.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"They'll also test for infection, and may give medicines to relax his muscles, such as lorazepam, or stronger ones if needed.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Is this dangerous? Could he die?"},{"t":"2:23","band":"Complications","w":"D","s":"I won't pretend: it's serious and can be life-threatening, which is why we're moving fast. The main risks are kidney damage, chest infection and blood clots.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"With quick treatment, most people recover completely, usually within one to two weeks.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:41","w":"P","s":"Most. Not all."},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"Not all, no. I'm sorry; that's frightening to hear. Everything we're doing is aimed at preventing those complications.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"2:52","band":"His concerns","w":"P","s":"Somebody gave him too much. Who's responsible?"},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"You have every right to ask. I've reported it as an incident so it's properly reviewed. His consultant will meet you on Monday to go through every decision.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:07","w":"D","s":"If you want to raise it formally, here are the details for PALS, our patient advice service. That won't affect Kofi's care in any way.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"I persuaded him to come in. This is my fault."},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much you're blaming yourself. Bringing him in was right; he was very unwell. Nobody could have predicted this reaction.","m":[2]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"Thank you. So what about his mind? Does he just stay ill?"},{"t":"3:32","band":"Future medication","w":"D","s":"He'll still get treatment for the psychosis. No antipsychotic until at least two weeks after full recovery.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Then we'd use a different medicine at a low dose, increase it slowly, and check his temperature and bloods closely. We'll record this reaction so he's never given haloperidol again.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"Could it happen again?"},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"It can, which is why we'd be so careful and avoid injections. Most people restart safely with those precautions. Meanwhile, nurses will support him and lorazepam can help if he's distressed.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's something."},{"t":"4:08","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"It's a lot to take in. Can you tell me what you've understood, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"A rare reaction to the haloperidol, not an allergy. It's stopped, he's going to the medical ward for fluids and cooling, and you'll try a different drug later, slowly."},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. Here's the ward number. If you notice he's more confused, stiffer or hotter, tell the staff straight away.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Can I go with him?"},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"Yes, please do. A nurse from our ward will go too. I'll update you again this evening, and the consultant will see you Monday.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"Alright. Thank you for being straight with me."},{"t":"4:49","w":"D","s":"Thank you for listening when you're this worried. Any other questions before we go to him?","m":[2,12]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"No. Let's go."}],"global":{"pass":"Honest, jargon-light explanation of NMS with an early apology, a clear medical plan, a truthful answer about death, complaint routes, and a safe plan for future treatment.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis and plan but defensive about the injections, evasive about mortality, or silent on future treatment.","fail":"Calls it an allergy or a virus, keeps him on the psychiatric ward, avoids apology, or says he can never have antipsychotics again."}},"c-t-51":{"setup":"Tuesday afternoon, memory clinic. Gerald Okafor, 82, has Lewy body dementia on rivastigmine 3 mg twice daily and is worse at home. His grandson Ellis has come in. Discuss his behaviour and the management options, and address Ellis's ideas, concerns and expectations.","role":{"who":"Mr Ellis Okafor, 29, male, grandson","open":"Good, because we're worried sick.","manner":"Anxious, polite and well read; tearful when talking about his grandad speaking to the dead. Relaxes once he hears a clear plan and that antipsychotics will be avoided.","free":["Grandad sits up past midnight chatting to Gran and to his own mum as if they're there","Puts out saucers of milk for a cat nobody else sees; also mentions a fox and children's faces","Fell twice this month; no head injury","One night shouted at Ellis's mum, Grace, thinking she was an intruder"],"ifasked":["Some days he is nearly himself, other days lost and drowsy","Shouts and thrashes in his sleep","GP bloods two weeks ago were fine; bowels normal; no new tablets","Stopped driving last year","Mum puts his tablets out, but he sometimes forgets them when she isn't there","The visions of Gran mostly comfort him; the children's faces sometimes scare him","Mum is exhausted and sleeps over most nights"],"worries":["Is there a tablet to stop the visions?","He read online that antipsychotics could kill his grandad","Will he have to go into a care home?","Will he get worse?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when describing Grandad talking to the dead; settles if validated","Asks 'Why isn't it just Parkinson's?' if the scan is explained","Relieved and thanks the doctor if antipsychotics are clearly ruled out","If the doctor promises a cure, asks sceptically, 'Really? Online it says there isn't one'"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Gerald's consent, and agrees the plan","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates Ellis's distress about the visions of his late relatives","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores Ellis's understanding, worries and hopes for treatment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Clarifies hallucinations, fluctuations, night-time behaviour, falls and aggression","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for infection, constipation, pain and new medicines as causes of decline","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains Lewy bodies, the chemical messengers and the three core features without jargon","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the DaT scan and why this is not Parkinson's disease dementia","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States honestly that it is progressive and not curable","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains rivastigmine increase, its benefits, side effects and pulse or ECG checks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains why levodopa waits and why antipsychotics are avoided","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives non-drug strategies and arranges OT, physio and tablet support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Covers driving, carers' assessment and lasting power of attorney","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding, gives a safety net and a follow-up date, then closes","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Ellis, Gerald's grandson?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's me."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming in. Gerald is happy for me to discuss his care with you, so we can speak openly.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Good, because we're worried sick."},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"I can hear that. Could you tell me what's been happening at home, and then we'll talk about what can help? First, what do you already know about it, and what were you hoping for today?","m":[1,3]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Someone said Lewy body something. Honestly, I hoped there'd be a tablet to make the visions stop."},{"t":"0:37","band":"What he has seen","w":"D","s":"That's a really fair hope. Tell me about the visions.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","s":"He sits up past midnight talking to my gran. And to his mum, who died forty years ago. He puts milk out for a cat. There isn't a cat."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"That must be painful to see, especially hearing him talk to your gran.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"It is. I never know how to answer him. I just end up walking out of the room."},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"That's a very human reaction. Does he seem frightened by what he sees, or comforted?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"Gran, he's happy. But there's a fox, and children's faces at the window. Those scare him."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"Are there days when he's nearly himself, and others when he's very muddled or sleepy?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"Yes. Monday he did the crossword. Tuesday he didn't know who I was."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"What happens at night? And has he been cross with anyone, or fallen?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"He shouts in his sleep, thrashes about. He fell twice this month. And he screamed at my mum, thought she was a burglar."},{"t":"1:41","band":"New causes","w":"D","s":"Has anything changed recently: a water infection, constipation, pain, a new tablet?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"His GP did bloods two weeks ago, all fine. No new tablets. Bowels are OK, Mum checks."},{"t":"1:54","w":"D","s":"And did this come on suddenly over a few days, or creep up?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"Crept up. Over months."},{"t":"2:01","band":"The illness","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that helps. It makes a new infection or medicine problem less likely. Let me explain the illness. Small protein deposits, called Lewy bodies, collect inside brain cells. They upset the chemical messengers for alertness, memory and movement.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"So is that why he sees things?"},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Yes. It typically causes three things: vivid visions of people or animals, alertness that swings from day to day, and stiffness with falls. The shouting in his sleep is part of it too. Nothing anyone did caused it.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"But why isn't it just Parkinson's? He's stiff like a Parkinson's patient."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"They're cousins. His scan looks at the brain cells that use dopamine, and they're working less than they should. That fits Lewy body dementia, and it's usually normal in Alzheimer's. With Parkinson's disease dementia, stiffness and tremor lead by a year or more, and memory follows. In your grandad, memory went first.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"And will he get better with the tablets?"},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, no. It's a progressive illness; it will decline gradually, faster for some people than others. The tablets can help for a while, but they don't cure it.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"Online it said that. I suppose I hoped they were wrong."},{"t":"3:16","band":"Medicines","w":"D","s":"That's a hard thing to hear. Here's what we can do. His rivastigmine boosts the memory messenger and often reduces the visions. His pulse and heart tracing today are fine, so I'd like to raise it to 4.5 milligrams twice a day, and to 6 in two weeks if he copes.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"Any side effects?"},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"Feeling sick, loose stools, poor appetite and weight loss. Less commonly, his heart can slow, making him dizzy or faint. If he falls more or faints, tell us, and the GP checks his pulse before each increase.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"What about Parkinson's tablets for the stiffness?"},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"Levodopa can ease stiffness, but it does nothing for memory and can make the visions worse. Not for now. Should his stiffness become disabling, his neurologist could trial a small dose.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"I read antipsychotics could kill him. Is that true?"},{"t":"4:09","w":"D","s":"You've read right. People with this illness can react badly, even to small doses: severe stiffness, drowsiness, falls, confusion, and it can be fatal. We avoid them. Only in severe distress or danger would a specialist consider a tiny dose of one safer drug, quetiapine, with close checks.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:26","w":"P","s":"Thank God. I was terrified someone would give him one."},{"t":"4:30","band":"Home","w":"D","s":"At home, please don't argue with what he sees. If seeing Gran comforts him, go along gently. If the children's faces scare him, reassure him and move him to another room. Good lighting, night lights, fewer shadows, glasses on.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"We've been telling him Gran's dead. He gets so upset."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"That's understandable, and many families do it. Gentle going along usually works better. I'll ask an occupational therapist to visit for lighting, rails and an alarm, and the falls physio too.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"5:02","band":"Risk and carers","w":"D","s":"Is he driving at all? And who gives him his tablets?","m":[12,11]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"He stopped driving last year. Mum puts them out, but when she's not there he forgets."},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"The pharmacy can do a blister pack, or a daily skin patch can replace the capsules. How are you and your mum coping?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"Mum's exhausted. She's sleeping over most nights. Will he have to go into a home?"},{"t":"5:27","w":"D","s":"Not now. Many people stay home a long time with the right help. Your mum has a right to a carers' assessment, which can bring respite. And while he can still take part, a lasting power of attorney lets him choose who decides for him later.","m":[12,2]},{"t":"5:44","w":"P","s":"I'll talk to Mum about that."},{"t":"5:47","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"What will you tell your mum about today, in your words?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:51","w":"P","s":"It's Lewy body dementia, it'll get worse. Higher dose of his tablet, no antipsychotics, don't argue with him, lights on, OT and physio, help for Mum."},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. If he becomes suddenly more confused, drowsy or unwell, ring the GP that day or 111, as it may be an infection. A fall with a head injury, call 999.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"OK. Who do I ring with questions?"},{"t":"6:18","w":"D","s":"The memory nurse will phone you this week and be your contact. Here's a leaflet from the Lewy Body Society. I'll see him in four weeks.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:28","w":"P","s":"Thank you. That's more than I expected."},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"You're doing a lot for him. Look after yourself too, Ellis.","m":[2,13]}],"global":{"pass":"Hears Ellis's hopes, excludes a new cause, explains the illness and scan clearly, avoids antipsychotics, and leaves a practical, supported plan.","borderline":"Accurate explanation but little exploration of his concerns, or no mention of carers, driving and planning.","fail":"Suggests an antipsychotic or levodopa as the answer, promises improvement, or never checks for delirium."}},"c-t-60":{"setup":"Sunday late morning, the PICU nursing office. You are the on-call registrar. Aisha, the nurse in charge, is worried that Connor Blackwood, 26, in a manic episode, isn't improving. Discuss treatment options, agree a plan, and address her concerns.","role":{"who":"Aisha Rahman, 38, female, nurse in charge","open":"Yes, thanks for coming.","manner":"Stretched and firm; wants lithium today. Crosses her arms if refused without reasons. Accepts a clear explanation; exasperated if offered nothing; warms when given a concrete plan.","free":["Connor isn't improving after three days","He keeps intruding into other patients' space","She wants lithium started today"],"ifasked":["Elated, rapid speech, two to three hours' sleep, barely eating","Drinks a little juice; no injuries","Thinks staff will experiment on his blood; refuses bloods and observations","Olanzapine 10 mg at night for three nights, taken with persuasion","One dose of PRN lorazepam yesterday","Another patient nearly hit him yesterday","Dr Hollis said lithium if olanzapine failed","Detained; admitted after smashing glasses outside a pub"],"worries":["Why can't lithium start today if the consultant said so?","Is someone going to hit him?","Her staff are exhausted","What if he refuses the higher dose?"],"rules":["Mentions the refused bloods only if blood tests are raised","Challenges once with Dr Hollis's words if lithium is declined","Becomes exasperated if no alternative is offered; accepts alternatives that are explained"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and role, acknowledges her concern and asks what she wants","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her and her team's pressure","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gathers presentation: mood, speech, sleep, eating, suspiciousness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gathers treatment so far, adherence and PRN use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about bloods, observations, fluids and injuries","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses risk to Connor and to other patients","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains lithium needs baseline bloods and level monitoring","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains olanzapine has not had an adequate dose or duration","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees olanzapine increase and PRN with doses and limits","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees observations, environment and safeguarding","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Covers physical monitoring and keeps working on bloods","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges consultant handover, documentation and when to call","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors on call. Are you Aisha, the nurse in charge?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Yes, thanks for coming."},{"t":"0:11","w":"D","s":"You're worried about Connor not improving and other patients reacting to him.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"Exactly. And we want him on lithium today."},{"t":"0:23","w":"D","s":"OK, I hear that. Let's go through what's happened so far, then agree a plan together. How is he at the moment?","m":[1,3]},{"t":"0:33","band":"Presentation","w":"P","s":"Elated, talking non-stop, you can barely follow him. Two or three hours' sleep a night. He barely eats."},{"t":"0:47","w":"D","s":"Is he drinking? And any odd or suspicious ideas?","m":[3,5]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"A bit of juice. He thinks we're going to do experiments on his blood."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"What's he like with the other patients?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"In everyone's face. Standing too close, touching their stuff. Yesterday Mr Kerr nearly punched him."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"That sounds really tense for everyone. Has he hurt anyone, or been hurt?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"Not yet. But it's only a matter of time. My staff are exhausted."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"I can see how stretched you are, and you're right to worry. Any self-harm, or attempts to leave?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"1:50","band":"Treatment so far","w":"D","s":"What's he on, and is he taking it?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Olanzapine 10 at night. Three nights now. He takes it, but it's a battle."},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"How much PRN has he needed?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"One lorazepam yesterday after the Mr Kerr thing."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"And what did Dr Hollis say about lithium, exactly?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"That if the olanzapine didn't work, we'd switch to lithium. It isn't working."},{"t":"2:28","band":"Physical health","w":"D","s":"Has he had any bloods or an ECG since admission?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"No. He refuses bloods and obs. That's the blood experiment thing."},{"t":"2:41","w":"D","s":"Any injuries, or does he look dry or unwell physically?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"No injuries. He looks fine, just wired."},{"t":"2:53","band":"Why not lithium today","w":"D","s":"Thanks, that's really clear. I agree he needs more help today. But starting lithium today isn't safe, and I'll explain why.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Before lithium we need kidney, thyroid and calcium tests, and an ECG. Then we check levels in the blood within a week, and regularly after. Without bloods, we can't tell if the level is useless or toxic.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Dr Hollis said lithium if the olanzapine didn't work. Why can't we start it today?"},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"That's a fair challenge. Lithium may well be right later. But he's only had three days, and 10 mg is below the usual starting dose for mania, which is 15. So olanzapine hasn't really had a fair go yet.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"So we just wait while he winds everyone up?"},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"No, you shouldn't just wait. And with him drinking so little, lithium could build up fast. Let's change things today instead.","m":[2,7]},{"t":"4:19","band":"Today's plan","w":"P","s":"Like what?"},{"t":"4:22","w":"D","s":"Olanzapine up to 15 mg tonight, and up to 20 within the week if he needs it. Lorazepam 1 to 2 mg by mouth as needed, up to 4 mg in 24 hours.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"What if he refuses the higher dose?"},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"Offer it calmly with a short explanation. If he refuses and is very agitated, follow the rapid tranquillisation policy, oral first, and ring me.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"And Mr Kerr?"},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"Let's put Connor on within-eyesight observations, nurse him in the quieter lounge, and keep him apart from Mr Kerr. Please log yesterday as a safeguarding incident.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"I'll need another member of staff for that."},{"t":"5:22","w":"D","s":"Fair. Can you ask the bleep holder for an extra member of staff? I'll support that.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:32","w":"P","s":"OK. What about his bloods?"},{"t":"5:36","w":"D","s":"Could his named nurse, someone he trusts, explain each test simply and keep offering daily? Meanwhile a food and fluid chart, and visual observations like breathing rate and colour if he won't allow hands-on.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:56","w":"P","s":"We can do that."},{"t":"5:59","w":"D","s":"And quiet activities and rest periods might help channel his energy.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:07","band":"Hand over and close","w":"P","s":"Can we at least write up the lithium so it's ready?"},{"t":"6:13","w":"D","s":"I'd rather not; it could get given by mistake without bloods. I'll document all this and email Dr Hollis for Monday, so lithium is decided once the tests are possible.","m":[12,7]},{"t":"6:23","w":"P","s":"Fine. That makes sense."},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"Call me if there's any violence, he stops drinking, or you're worried physically. Does this plan work for you?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Yes. Honestly, that's more than I expected. Thanks for explaining it properly."}],"global":{"pass":"Gathers the picture, holds a reasoned line on lithium, and agrees a concrete plan for dose, PRN, observation, environment, physical monitoring and handover.","borderline":"Correctly declines lithium but gives a thin plan or misses physical health or risk from others.","fail":"Prescribes lithium without bloods, refuses only because it's the consultant's call, or offers no alternative."}},"c-t-61":{"setup":"Tuesday afternoon, a home visit for the older adults team. Desmond Clarke, 83, cares alone for his wife who has vascular dementia, and his GP is worried about his mood. Take a history of his symptoms and carry out a thorough risk assessment.","role":{"who":"Mr Desmond (Des) Clarke, 83, male, patient","open":"I am. Des, please.","manner":"Polite, proud and understated; plays things down at first. Becomes tearful talking about Margaret. Opens up about dark thoughts only if asked gently and directly.","free":["It's been a long year since Margaret's diagnosis","He is tired all the time and can't be bothered with things","Margaret needs help washing and dressing","His knees and hands hurt"],"ifasked":["Wakes at 4 am most days for three months; about four hours' sleep","Lost half a stone; has given up his allotment","Feels he is failing Margaret; sometimes cross with her, then guilty","Margaret wanders at night; no paid help, no respite; daughter Karen lives an hour away","Takes over-the-counter ibuprofen and co-codamol; fell in the garden two months ago","Two or three whiskies each night to get to sleep, more than before","Has a tin of about 30 old co-codamol in the kitchen drawer and has thought 'that would do it'","Sometimes thinks they would both be better off gone; no plan; grandchildren Ellie and Sam keep him going"],"worries":["Will they take Margaret away?","Can he have something to help him sleep?","Is he getting dementia too?"],"rules":["Will not discuss Margaret's care freely until he knows she cannot hear","Becomes tearful when asked how he feels about the caring; settles if validated","Admits the joint-death thought only if asked about it directly and kindly","Agrees to hand over the tablets and call Karen if the reason is explained"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists from the older adults team. Are you Mr Desmond Clarke?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"I am. Des, please."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"Thank you for letting me visit, Des. Your GP mentioned things have felt heavy lately.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"It's been a long year."},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"Is there somewhere we can talk privately for a while, and is Margaret settled? I'd like about half an hour, if that's all right.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"She's watching telly, she's fine. We can sit in the kitchen; I'll hear her from there."},{"t":"0:32","band":"Mood","w":"D","s":"Tell me how your mood has been lately.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"Low, I suppose. But anyone would be tired, looking after her."},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"Anyone would be worn out, you're right. I want to check whether it's become something more, because that would be treatable. How long has it felt this low?","m":[2,12]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"It's been creeping up all year, but the last three months it's been much worse."},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"What about sleep, appetite and weight? And the things you used to enjoy?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"I'm awake at four every morning, just lying there. I've lost half a stone. I gave up the allotment in the summer. Couldn't face it."},{"t":"1:14","w":"D","s":"How are your energy and concentration? And how do you see the future?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"No energy. I read the same page three times. The future? I don't see one, to be honest."},{"t":"1:28","band":"Caring","w":"D","s":"Talk me through a typical day and night with Margaret.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"I wash her, dress her, do the meals. She's up two or three times a night, trying the front door. I don't sleep for listening."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"Who helps you with that? When did you last have a proper break?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"Nobody. Our Karen's an hour away and she's got kids. I haven't had a day off in a year."},{"t":"1:56","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about it, deep down?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I promised her I'd never put her in a home. And I'm failing her. Some nights I get cross with her, and then I hate myself."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"That promise shows how much you love her. You haven't failed her. You're doing a job meant for a whole team, on your own, with no sleep. Take your time.","m":[12]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"(pause) Thank you. Nobody's said that."},{"t":"2:26","band":"Physical health","w":"D","s":"How are your knees and hands? What do you take for the pain?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"Agony on the stairs. I take ibuprofen from the chemist and the co-codamol the doctor gives me."},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"Any falls? And how are your hearing, eyesight and memory?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"I went over in the garden in August. Hearing aid in. I forget things now. Am I going the way she did?"},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"That's a natural worry. Low mood very often affects memory and concentration. I'll note it, and we can check it properly once your mood lifts.","m":[4,12]},{"t":"3:02","band":"Psychotic screen","w":"D","s":"When you feel you're failing, do you ever feel you deserve to be punished, or that something terrible is happening to your body or your money that others can't see?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that. Just guilt."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"And any voices when nobody's there, or seeing things others can't?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:19","w":"P","s":"No, love."},{"t":"3:22","band":"Alcohol and medicines","w":"D","s":"What's your drinking like these days, in a typical week?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"A couple of whiskies at night. Three sometimes. Helps me get off."},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"Are there any tablets saved up in the house, yours or Margaret's?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"There's a tin of old co-codamol in the drawer. Thirty-odd."},{"t":"3:40","band":"Suicide risk","w":"D","s":"Des, with everything you've told me, I need to ask: have things felt so bad that you've thought of ending your life?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"(quiet) I've looked at that tin and thought, that'd do it."},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest with me. Have you made a plan, chosen a time, or done anything to prepare, like writing a note or sorting your affairs?","m":[7,12]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"No. No plan. I couldn't do it to the grandchildren, Ellie and Sam."},{"t":"4:19","band":"Risk to Margaret","w":"D","s":"Some people caring for someone they love start to think it would be kinder if they both went. Has anything like that crossed your mind?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:29","w":"P","s":"(long pause) Some nights I wonder if we'd both be better off gone. I wouldn't do anything. I'd never hurt her."},{"t":"4:38","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much it costs you to say that. Have you ever looked into ways for you both to die, or felt like harming her, even for a moment?","m":[8,12]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","s":"No. I shouted at her once at three in the morning. She cried. I felt like dirt."},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"Has anyone in the family raised concerns about how she's being looked after, or has she gone without meals or medicines?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:01","w":"P","s":"No. I do everything for her. It's me that's going without."},{"t":"5:06","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've got this right. For three months you've been waking early, losing weight, lost interest, and feeling guilty and hopeless. You're caring alone through the nights, drinking more, and you've had thoughts of dying, and of you both going, but no plan. Is that fair?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:24","w":"P","s":"That's about it."},{"t":"5:27","w":"D","s":"This sounds like depression, Des, and it's treatable. Can I ask what you understand by that?","m":[9,10]},{"t":"5:33","w":"P","s":"That it's an illness, not me being soft?"},{"t":"5:36","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Today I'd like that tin out of the house, and to ring Karen with your permission. I'll talk to my consultant, and someone from our team will see you again within two days.","m":[11,13]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","s":"Will they take Margaret off me?"},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"No. The aim is to keep you both at home, with help: a carer's assessment for you and support for Margaret at night and in the day. Being honest today makes that more likely.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:04","w":"P","s":"Karen can have the tablets. Ring her if you like."},{"t":"6:08","w":"D","s":"Thank you. If things feel worse before we see you, ring Samaritans on 116 123 or 111 option 2, or 999 in an emergency. I'll write these down. Any questions before I go?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"No. I didn't think anyone could help, to be honest."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks identity, arranges privacy with Margaret settled, explains purpose","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits core and biological symptoms of depression with duration and worsening","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores caring role: tasks, nights, help available, burden and guilt","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about pain, analgesia, falls, senses and memory","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for guilty, nihilistic or poverty delusions and hallucinations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol and saved or prescribed tablets at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses suicidal thoughts, plans, preparations and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about joint-death thoughts, harm and neglect towards Margaret","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises the findings back to him accurately","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names likely depression plainly and checks his understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Plans support for both: carer's assessment and help for Margaret","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates distress and guilt before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Agrees means removal, family involvement, early review and crisis numbers","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gentle, structured history that finds a depressive episode, covers pain, alcohol and tablets, and asks directly about suicide and about Margaret, closing with clear safety steps.","borderline":"Finds depression and asks about suicide but misses saved tablets, alcohol or the joint-death question.","fail":"Accepts 'just tired', omits risk to Margaret, or leaves without addressing means at home."}},"c-t-68":{"setup":"Wednesday afternoon, memory clinic. Stephen Doyle, 52, has phoned about his mother, Mrs Margaret Doyle, 81, who is furious after advice to stop driving. She consents to you talking to him. Explain the findings and agree a plan for her driving.","role":{"who":"Mr Stephen Doyle, 52, male, son","open":"Yes. What's going on with Mum?","manner":"Tense, worried and a bit defensive at first; softens when listened to. Guilty about not noticing sooner. Fears his mother's temper. Practical once he has a plan.","free":["Mum is furious and says the clinic is taking her car","Her memory is much worse than he realised","He lives 20 minutes away and visits on Sundays"],"ifasked":["Months of decline since his dad died two years ago; he put it down to grief","Missed bills, fridge often empty, clothes unwashed","Scraped the car twice this spring; got lost driving to his house once","Drives to the shops and to church","Only learned of the whisky when a neighbour mentioned the bottles","She has a forceful personality and hates being told what to do"],"worries":["Is it just the whisky? Will she get better if she stops?","Can't she take a driving test instead?","What if she ignores everyone and keeps driving?","She'll never forgive me"],"rules":["Mentions the scrapes and getting lost only if asked about driving","Becomes upset and guilty when the score is explained; settles if validated","Pushes once for a driving test as a compromise"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists at the memory clinic. Am I speaking with Stephen Doyle?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Yes. What's going on with Mum?"},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you for calling, Stephen. Your mum has agreed I can talk with you. I can hear this has been upsetting.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"She's livid. Says you're taking her car."},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"Let me explain what we've found and why, then we'll plan together. First, what have you been told, and what's worrying you most?","m":[1,3]},{"t":"0:32","band":"His concerns","w":"P","s":"Just that she's angry about driving. And her memory seems far worse than I thought."},{"t":"0:39","w":"D","s":"How has she been managing at home?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"Not well. Bills missed, fridge empty, same clothes. I thought it was grief since Dad died."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to notice. How has her driving been?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"She scraped the car twice this spring. And once she got lost driving to mine, a route she's done for years."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"Where does she drive now?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"Shops, church. And I'd no idea about the whisky until a neighbour mentioned the bottles."},{"t":"1:18","band":"What we found","w":"D","s":"The nurse did a memory and thinking test called the ACE-III. It's scored out of 100, and your mum scored 50.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"Fifty. That's half."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"It is. Most people without memory problems score above 82 to 88. Her weakest areas were memory and judging space and shapes, which matters for driving. The nurse also noticed she looked unkempt and smelled of alcohol.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"God. I should have noticed. I thought it was just her getting older."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Lots of families think that; changes creep in slowly. You've noticed now and you're acting. That's what matters.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:05","band":"The alcohol","w":"D","s":"Her GP found she gets through half a bottle of whisky or more each day, and has done for months. That's about 14 units, a whole week's limit, every day.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"And the blood tests?"},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"One, GGT, shows heavy drinking. The other, ALT, shows her liver is under strain. One important thing: she mustn't stop suddenly, as that can cause fits. Her GP will start thiamine, a vitamin that protects the brain.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:38","band":"What it may mean","w":"P","s":"Surely it's just the whisky, then? If she stops, she'll be back to normal?"},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd hope so, and drink may play a part. Even so, fifty is very low. It suggests dementia, probably moderate, or damage from alcohol, or both.","m":[6,2]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"How do you find out which?"},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"A brain scan, more bloods, and repeating the test after a couple of months of cutting down. Some may improve; we'll know more then.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:13","band":"Driving now","w":"P","s":"Is she safe to drive?"},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, Stephen. From what we know, she isn't, and she needs to stop now. Her memory, her sense of space, the drinking, and those scrapes all point the same way. Next time someone could be badly hurt.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"Can't she just do a driving test to prove she's fine?"},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"I see why that sounds fair. With these results, she isn't safe to drive while waiting for one. She needs to stop now.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"So what happens with her licence?"},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"By law, she has to tell the DVLA about her memory problems and the drinking. We'll ask her once more to stop and to tell them herself.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"And if she won't?"},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Then we tell her we're informing the DVLA, we do it, and we write to her. The DVLA decides, but they'll very likely stop her licence.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:18","w":"P","s":"What if she ignores them and drives anyway?"},{"t":"4:22","w":"D","s":"Let us know straight away. Driving with no licence is an offence, and as a last resort the police may need to know. Keeping the keys is only for when she's about to drive and it's clearly dangerous.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"She'll never forgive me if I'm the one who stops her."},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"That's a real fear, and you shouldn't carry it alone. Our consultant can meet her with you this week, so she hears it from us, not just you.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"4:57","band":"Longer term","w":"D","s":"What does driving mean to her?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"Freedom. And getting to church and her friends."},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"Those matter. Let's plan what replaces it: lifts from family, a taxi account, a bus pass, community transport. Selling the car can cover taxis for a long time.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"That might work. And the drinking?"},{"t":"5:24","w":"D","s":"An older adults' alcohol worker can help her cut down safely, with her GP. We'll also ask social care to assess help at home, with her agreement, and offer you a carer's assessment.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:39","w":"P","s":"I didn't know I could get anything."},{"t":"5:43","w":"D","s":"You can. And while she can still decide, it's worth talking to her about Lasting Power of Attorney, covering both her health and her money.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:55","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So: the test suggests dementia, the drinking may add to it, and she must stop driving now. If she won't, we'll inform the DVLA. In your words, how will you put this to her this week?","m":[10]},{"t":"6:11","w":"P","s":"That she can't drive now, that you'll tell the DVLA if she won't, and that we'll sort lifts and help with the drink."},{"t":"6:21","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll let you know the meeting time today. Call 999 if she's driving and anyone is at risk. If she gets shaky, sweaty or confused cutting down, call her GP or 111.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","s":"Thank you. That's clearer than I expected."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"You're doing the right thing for her, Stephen. Here's our number if anything changes.","m":[11,2]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms identity and his mother's consent","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges his anger, guilt and fear before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks his concerns, coping at home, driving incidents and where she drives","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains ACE-III score, cut-offs, pattern and the nurse's observations","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains alcohol units, GGT and ALT, and warns against stopping suddenly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains possible dementia, alcohol-related damage or both, and further tests","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States plainly she is unsafe to drive now, with reasons, refusing a test as a way out","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains her DVLA duty, the clinic's step if she refuses, and last resorts","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans joint meeting, transport, alcohol help, social care, carer's assessment and LPA","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises and checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Safety-nets for danger on the road and withdrawal, and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Confirms consent, hears him first, explains results plainly, says clearly she must stop driving, follows the DVLA order, and plans transport and support.","borderline":"Explains well but softens the driving message, or misses the DVLA sequence or the withdrawal warning.","fail":"Blames it all on alcohol, offers a test to keep driving, or leads with police and keys."}},"c-t-69":{"setup":"Monday lunchtime, the ward clinic room. Tomasz Nowak, 45, admitted informally today with depression and somatisation disorder, has four weeks of right arm numbness and tingling. Without taking a history, examine both upper limbs and report any neurological deficit.","role":{"who":"Mr Tomasz Nowak, 45, male, patient","open":"Yes.","manner":"Quiet, low and a little wary; expects to be dismissed. Cooperates with clear instructions. Warms up when told his symptoms are taken seriously.","free":["Right arm numb and tingly for four weeks","Feels doctors think it's in his head","Worried he has MS after reading online"],"ifasked":["Slight ache at the right shoulder when pushed hard","No pain at rest","All sensation feels the same on both sides when tested","Strength feels equal on both sides","Declines a chaperone"],"worries":["Is it MS?","If it's all normal, what is it then?"],"rules":["Every finding is normal and symmetrical","Starts telling his history if asked open questions; stops if redirected kindly","Says 'Everyone thinks it's in my head' early; settles if validated"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Tomasz Nowak?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"I hear your right arm has felt numb and tingly. I'd like to check the nerves and muscles in both arms. It takes about ten minutes. Is that alright, and would you like a chaperone?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:21","w":"P","s":"That's fine. No chaperone. Everyone thinks it's in my head, though."},{"t":"0:27","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry it's felt like that. Your symptoms are real, and that's exactly why I want to check properly. Could you slip down to your vest? Any pain anywhere before I start?","m":[12,1]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"No pain. Just the tingling."},{"t":"0:47","band":"Inspection","w":"D","s":"I'll look at both arms first. Hold them out in front, palms down, fingers spread.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Like this?"},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Your muscles look the same on both sides, no twitching, no tremor, and no scars.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:08","band":"Tone","w":"D","s":"Now let your arm go floppy; I'll do the moving. I'll roll the wrist and bend the elbow, then the other side.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"Okay. It's relaxed."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Tone is normal and the same both sides.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:32","band":"Power","w":"D","s":"Arms out to the sides like a chicken. Don't let me push them down.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"Ow, that's a bit sore near my right shoulder."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. Just push as much as feels comfortable, and tell me if anything hurts.","m":[12,1]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"That's okay now."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"Elbows bent: pull me in towards you. Now push me away.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"Cock your wrists back and hold. Now bend them down. Squeeze my two fingers.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"Both feel the same."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"Straighten your fingers; don't let me bend them. Spread them; don't let me close them. Thumb up to the ceiling and hold it.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Why are you doing the left too? It's the right that's numb."},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"Good question. Comparing with your good arm is how I spot small differences. Your strength is full and equal on both sides.","m":[4,11]},{"t":"2:47","band":"Reflexes","w":"D","s":"Rest your arms on your lap. I'm going to tap at your elbow, the back of the arm and near the wrist.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"That one didn't do much."},{"t":"2:59","w":"D","s":"Clench your teeth for me while I tap again. There, it's there. Now I'll flick the tip of your middle finger and watch your thumb.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"Funny feeling."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Reflexes are normal and the same both sides, and there's no thumb twitch.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:23","band":"Coordination","w":"D","s":"Arms straight out, palms up, eyes closed. Hold still for me.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"3:38","w":"D","s":"Good, no drift. Eyes open now: my finger, then the tip of your nose, quickly, back and forth. Now pat one hand with the other, flipping it over fast.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"Both fine, I think."},{"t":"3:55","band":"Sensation","w":"D","s":"Now feeling. Here's cotton wool on your chest so you know what it feels like. Eyes closed: say yes each time you feel it, and tell me if it's the same both sides.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:11","w":"P","s":"Yes. Yes. Same."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"I'm testing the outer shoulder, thumb, middle finger, little finger and inner elbow on each side. Now with this pin: sharp or blunt?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"Sharp. Blunt. Sharp. Same on both."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"And this metal: does it feel cold, the same on both sides?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"Cold. Same."},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"Now the tuning fork on your chest first. Feel the buzz? Now on your thumb joint; tell me when it stops.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","s":"Now. And now on the left."},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"Last one, eyes closed. I'm moving your thumb: up or down?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"Up. Down. Up."},{"t":"5:14","w":"D","s":"Finally, I'll tap the front of your wrist and then ask you to press the backs of your hands together for a minute. Any tingling?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:27","w":"P","s":"No, nothing more than usual. Is it MS? I read pins and needles can be MS."},{"t":"5:35","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"That's a frightening thing to read. Thank you; you can get dressed. Your strength, reflexes, coordination and feeling are the same on both sides. I haven't found any sign of a nerve problem, and nothing that suggests MS.","m":[9,12]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"So if it's all normal, what is it then?"},{"t":"6:01","w":"D","s":"The nerves are working well, which is reassuring. Tingling can come from tension and anxiety, or from things like low B12, sugar or thyroid. I'll check those bloods today.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:16","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's a relief. But it's still tingling."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"I believe you. If it spreads, you get weak or clumsy, neck pain, or any bladder change, tell the nurses straight away and we'll look again. I'll pass this to the ward round.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","s":"Thank you for actually checking."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you for your patience, Tomasz.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, gains consent, offers chaperone, exposes both arms and asks about pain","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Inspects both arms for wasting, fasciculation, scars and tremor","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests tone at wrist and elbow on both sides","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests power joint by joint (shoulder to thumb), comparing sides","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests biceps, triceps and supinator reflexes, uses reinforcement, checks Hoffmann's","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests pronator drift, finger-nose and rapid alternating movements","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests light touch, pin prick and temperature across C5-T1 dermatomes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests vibration and joint position, plus a carpal tunnel screen","d":"Content"},{"c":"States the findings and absence of deficit in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Plans bloods and safety-nets for new neurological symptoms","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains why both sides are compared","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates his feeling of being dismissed and his fear","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"A complete, ordered, comparative examination of both arms covering every system and sensory pathway, with a clear, respectful result and next steps.","borderline":"Covers most systems but misses a sensory pathway, reinforcement or comparison, or gives a vague result.","fail":"Takes a history instead, examines one arm only, or dismisses the symptom as psychological."}},"c-t-70":{"setup":"Wednesday morning, the ward examination room. Desmond Clarke, 57, has worsening cognition, type 2 diabetes and recent falls. A medical student shadows you. Carry out a cardiovascular examination for signs of vascular dementia and explain what you look for.","role":{"who":"Mr Desmond Clarke, 57, male, patient (with Priya, 21, female, medical student)","open":"Des, yes.","manner":"Friendly but slow to process; needs short instructions one at a time. Anxious about strokes. The student is keen and asks why at each step.","free":["Prefers 'Des'","Has fallen several times, usually when getting up","Worried his memory means he has had a stroke"],"ifasked":["Happy for the student to stay","No chest pain or pain anywhere","Feels dizzy on standing","Feet always feel cold","Smokes 15 cigarettes a day"],"worries":["What's my heart got to do with my memory?","Is this to see if I've had a stroke?"],"rules":["Gets muddled if given two instructions at once; follows single instructions","Becomes dizzy on standing during BP; settles when sat down","Student asks 'Why?' at the BP and carotid steps"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Desmond Clarke?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Des, yes."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Des. I'd like to check your heart, pulse and blood vessels, as they can affect memory. A student is with me today. May we go ahead, and would you like a chaperone?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:19","w":"P","s":"That's fine. She can stay. Is this to see if I've had a stroke?"},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"That's a natural worry. Partly, yes: I'm looking for things that raise the risk of small strokes, so we can treat them. Any pain anywhere? Could you lie back at this angle and slip your top off?","m":[11,1]},{"t":"0:39","w":"P","s":"No pain. There you go."},{"t":"0:43","band":"General look","w":"D","s":"Priya, from the end of the bed I'm checking he's comfortable at rest, not breathless, and looking for self-neglect and insulin sites. He's on tablets, so no injection sites.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"Student: What are the yellow patches by his eyes?"},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Xanthelasma, cholesterol deposits. With a grey ring round the iris before 60, they point to high cholesterol, a vascular risk. No ring here.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"1:12","band":"Hands and pulse","w":"D","s":"Des, can I see your hands? There's some yellow staining on these fingers. Do you smoke?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:19","w":"P","s":"About fifteen a day."},{"t":"1:21","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Priya, I'm feeling the radial pulse for 30 seconds: rate, rhythm and volume. Irregularly irregular would mean atrial fibrillation, which can throw clots to the brain.","m":[3,10]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"Student: Is it regular?"},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Yes, regular, 82. Now both wrists together: a delay on one side would suggest narrowing above it. None.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:51","band":"Blood pressure","w":"D","s":"Des, I'm going to take your blood pressure in both arms while you lie there.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"Student: Why both arms?"},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"A gap over 15 suggests a narrowed artery on one side, and we use the higher arm in future. Right 162 over 96, left 158 over 94: both high.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"2:17","w":"D","s":"Des, now I'd like you to stand up slowly with me. I'll check it again after a minute. Tell me if you feel dizzy.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"Ooh. I'm a bit dizzy standing up."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Sit straight back down; I've got you. That dizziness may be part of why you've been falling, so it's useful to know.","m":[11,4]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"That's better."},{"t":"2:47","w":"D","s":"Priya, it was 138 over 88 at one minute standing: a 24-point drop. Over 20 is postural hypotension, a likely cause of his falls, and we'll check his tablets.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"3:01","band":"Neck","w":"D","s":"Des, lie back again and turn your head slightly to the left. I'm looking at the vein in your neck.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"Like this?"},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"Perfect. The JVP isn't raised. Now I'll feel the pulse in your neck, one side at a time.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"Student: Why one side at a time?"},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"Pressing both can drop the brain's blood flow. Des, take a breath in, out, and hold it for me. I'm listening for a whooshing noise from a narrowed artery.","m":[5,10]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"3:41","w":"D","s":"Breathe normally. No bruits either side. A bruit would suggest carotid narrowing, a source of clots to the brain.","m":[5,10]},{"t":"3:50","band":"Heart","w":"D","s":"Now I'm feeling for the heartbeat on your chest, then any heave or buzz.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"Cold hands!"},{"t":"4:01","w":"D","s":"Sorry about that. Priya, the apex is in the normal place, no heave, no thrill. Now listening in four areas, timing with the neck pulse.","m":[6,10]},{"t":"4:17","w":"P","s":"Student: Anything extra?"},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"Two normal sounds, no murmur. A harsh murmur spreading to the neck could be aortic stenosis, which causes dizziness and falls.","m":[6,10]},{"t":"4:31","band":"Lungs and legs","w":"D","s":"Des, could you sit forward for me? I'm listening to the bottom of your lungs.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"Right you are."},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"Clear. Crackles would suggest fluid from heart failure. Now I'll press your ankles for swelling. None.","m":[7,10]},{"t":"4:51","w":"D","s":"Next your feet: pulses, skin and feeling. Do your feet feel cold?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","s":"Always. My wife complains."},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"Priya, the right foot pulses are present; on the left I can't feel the top one and the inner ankle one is weak. Feeling is reduced on both soles. In diabetes, weak pulses mean arterial disease elsewhere, including the brain.","m":[7,10]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"What's my heart got to do with my memory, then?"},{"t":"5:24","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Good question, Des. The brain needs a steady blood supply. High pressure, smoking and furred arteries can damage small vessels in it, which can affect memory.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"5:38","w":"P","s":"So what did you find?"},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"Your pulse is regular and your heart sounds normal. Your blood pressure is high and drops when you stand, which may explain the falls. The arteries in your left foot are weak. Thank you; you can get dressed.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:58","w":"P","s":"What happens now?"},{"t":"6:01","w":"D","s":"A heart tracing, bloods for cholesterol, sugar and kidneys, a urine test, and a brain scan. We'll review your tablets with the medical team, offer help to stop smoking, and the nurses will measure your blood pressure lying, then standing, every day.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:19","w":"D","s":"Priya, how would you sum up what we found?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:22","w":"P","s":"Student: Hypertension, a postural drop, smoking and weak left foot pulses. Lots of vascular risk, and a reason for the falls."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Des, if you feel dizzy, call a nurse before you stand. Any questions?","m":[12,9]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you, doctor. That made sense."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, gains consent for examination and student, offers chaperone, positions and exposes","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"General inspection: breathlessness, self-neglect, xanthelasma, arcus, nicotine, insulin sites","d":"Content"},{"c":"Pulse rate, rhythm and volume for 30 seconds; radio-radial delay; names AF","d":"Content"},{"c":"BP both arms, then lying and standing, identifying postural drop","d":"Content"},{"c":"JVP, carotids one at a time, and carotid bruits with breath held","d":"Content"},{"c":"Apex, heaves, thrills and auscultation, naming aortic stenosis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Lung bases, ankle oedema, foot pulses and diabetic foot","d":"Content"},{"c":"Summarises findings to the patient in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Plans ECG, bloods, urine, MRI, drug review and smoking help","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains to the student how each step links to vascular dementia or falls","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds to his worries and dizziness with validation","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks the student's understanding and safety-nets and closes with Des","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A complete cardiovascular examination including lying and standing BP, carotids and feet, each step linked aloud to vascular dementia or falls, with Des kept at the centre.","borderline":"A good heart examination but thin links to dementia, or BP lying and standing or feet left out.","fail":"Silent or incomplete examination, no BP or carotids, or Des ignored while teaching."}},"c-u-11":{"setup":"Saturday, 4 a.m., adult ward. Gareth Lloyd, 45, admitted informally last night after a near-lethal hanging attempt, wants to discharge himself. Assess his risk and explain your plan, including his rights, in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Gareth Lloyd, 45, male, informal inpatient","open":"Yes. I just want to go home.","manner":"Flat, tired and restless, coat on. Clipped and irritable at first; softens and becomes tearful when the doctor listens. Reluctant but accepting by the end if treated honestly.","free":["He wants to go home now","His wife left a month ago with their son and daughter, aged 6 and 4","He says he is 'not mentally ill', just that life is not worth living","He thinks coming in was a mistake"],"ifasked":["He planned the hanging for a week and chose a night when no one would call","He left a note for his children saying they would be better off","He is not glad the rope broke and can't say he won't try again","He wants to bring the children home tonight; if they refuse, 'what's the point'","Two months of low mood, tearfulness, no enjoyment, poor concentration and pessimism","No voices, no alcohol, no drugs, no previous self-harm, never in trouble with police","He would never hurt his wife or children and isn't angry with her","There is a rope still in the garage"],"worries":["So are you going to let me leave, or not?","Can you actually keep me here?","Will I get to see my kids?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when asked about the note; settles if the doctor acknowledges it","Gets angry if threatened with being 'sectioned'; calms if the holding powers are explained honestly","Agrees to stay until the morning only if given clear reasons and told what happens next"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists on call tonight. Are you Gareth Lloyd?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. I just want to go home."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"I hear you, Gareth. I'd like to understand why, and talk it through properly before anything is decided.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"There's no point me being here."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Can we sit down for about ten minutes? Tell me what's changed since you arrived.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Nothing's changed. I shouldn't have come. I'm not mentally ill."},{"t":"0:32","band":"Why leave now","w":"D","s":"What's made you want to go right now, in the middle of the night? What would you do first when you got home?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"I'd go and get the kids. Bring them home where they belong."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"How would you get there at four in the morning?","m":[4,11]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"I hadn't really thought about it. A taxi, I suppose."},{"t":"0:58","band":"The attempt","w":"D","s":"Can you take me through what happened the other night, from the start? How long had you been planning it?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"About a week. I picked a night nobody would come round. Tied a rope to the beam in the loft."},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"Did you write anything, or tell anyone what you were going to do?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"I wrote a note to the kids. Told them they'd be better off. Nobody knew."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"That must have been an unbearably dark place to be, writing that to your own children.","m":[12]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"(tearful) Yeah. It was."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"When the rope broke, what went through your mind? And how do you feel about it now?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:53","w":"P","s":"My neck was agony, so I rang for help. Moment of weakness. Honestly, I'm not glad it broke."},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest with me. That tells me how much pain you're in.","m":[12]},{"t":"2:10","band":"Current intent","w":"D","s":"Are you still having thoughts about ending your life, tonight?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"I can't promise I wouldn't. I don't know."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Is there anything at home you'd use? And if the children didn't want to come with you, what then?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"There's more rope in the garage. If they won't come, that's it. It's my last chance."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Mental state","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been over the last couple of months: sleep, enjoying things, concentrating?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"Low, two months or so. I cry a lot. Can't enjoy anything, can't focus. Can't see it getting better."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"Any drink or drugs? And have you heard voices, or felt that people are against you?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"I don't drink. Nothing like that. I'm not mad."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"I don't think you're mad. Two months of that sounds like depression, and depression is treatable.","m":[5,12]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"It's not depression. My life just isn't worth living."},{"t":"3:23","band":"Risk to and from others","w":"D","s":"I have to ask everyone this. Have you had any thoughts of harming anyone else, including your wife or the children?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"God, no. I'd never hurt them. I'm not even angry with her."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Is anyone threatening you or taking advantage of you? Have you ever hurt yourself before, or been in trouble with the police?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"No, no and no."},{"t":"3:53","w":"D","s":"Who's still on your side? What's kept you going until now?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"My brother rings sometimes. The kids, I suppose."},{"t":"4:03","band":"Decision and rights","w":"D","s":"Gareth, I don't think it's safe for you to leave tonight. A day ago you nearly died, you've told me you might try again, and nothing's been treated yet.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"So are you going to let me leave, or not?"},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"I'd like you to stay voluntarily until the consultant sees you at nine. Going home at four, with no plan if the kids say no, worries me a lot.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"And if I just walk out?"},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"I'll be straight with you, not as a threat. A nurse could hold you for up to six hours, and I could hold you for up to 72, so a full Mental Health Act assessment can happen. I'd much rather we agree.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"(pause) Fine. Until nine. But I want to see my kids."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"That matters, and the team will help you plan how to see them properly. That's a strong reason to keep yourself safe.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:58","band":"Ward safety","w":"D","s":"A nurse will stay with you, within sight, for the rest of tonight. We'll go through your things together and take away anything you could hurt yourself with.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"I've only got my coat and my phone charger."},{"t":"5:14","w":"D","s":"The charger cable will go to the office for now. How's your neck: any change in your voice, or trouble swallowing or breathing?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"Sore, but my voice is fine. I can swallow."},{"t":"5:26","w":"D","s":"Good. If that changes at all, tell the nurse straight away and we'll get the medical team.","m":[9,13]},{"t":"5:32","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"So: you'll stay tonight, a nurse is with you, and the consultant sees you at nine. I'm ringing my senior now and writing this up. In your words, what happens between now and nine?","m":[10,14]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"I stay till nine, someone watches me, then I see the boss."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Exactly. What questions do you have before I go?","m":[14]},{"t":"5:54","w":"P","s":"None. Just make sure the consultant actually comes."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges his wish to leave, and agrees to talk first","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the attempt: planning, note, timing, method, survival and regret","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about current suicidal intent, means at home and what happens if the children refuse","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks what he would do on leaving and how he would get there","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for depression, psychosis and substance use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about risk to wife and children, risk from others, past self-harm and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"States he should stay tonight, giving clear reasons","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains Sections 5(4) and 5(2) honestly, without threatening","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Sets continuous observation, a belongings search and a neck check","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names senior escalation, documentation and the 9 a.m. consultant review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Tests whether he has weighed up the risks of leaving now","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his distress, especially about the note and his children","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Safety-nets: tell the nurse at once if voice, swallowing or breathing change","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks his understanding of the plan and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Detailed attempt and current-intent history, risk to the children asked, a clear 'stay' decision with honest holding-power explanation, continuous observation and senior review.","borderline":"Safe decision, but the attempt history is thin, the children are not asked about, or the holding powers are vague.","fail":"Lets him leave, threatens to 'section' him, or never asks about current intent."}},"c-u-13":{"setup":"Thursday afternoon, adult acute ward. Brenda Oyelaran, 63, admitted with anxiety and breathlessness, has chest pain. The healthcare assistant is taking her observations. Review her and carry out a cardiovascular examination in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Brenda Oyelaran, 63, female, inpatient","open":"Yes. My chest, doctor, it's happening again.","manner":"Anxious, hand on her chest, fearful that doctors dismiss her heart. Cooperates readily. Calms visibly when the doctor explains each step and takes the pain seriously.","free":["She has central chest pain that started during a panic attack","It has eased a bit","She is scared it's her heart and feels no one believes her"],"ifasked":["Started about 40 minutes ago; worst 6/10, now 2/10","Tight, central, does not spread; not worse on breathing or moving","No sweating, sickness or fainting; no calf pain, recent surgery or long trips","High blood pressure on amlodipine 5 mg; on sertraline 50 mg","Ex-smoker, 20 years, stopped 5 years ago","Father died of a heart attack at 60","No aspirin allergy; no previous heart problems"],"worries":["Everyone keeps saying it's just my nerves","Is my pulse okay?","Can you hear anything wrong?"],"rules":["Becomes breathless and tearful if the doctor mentions anxiety before examining her; settles with reassurance","Asks why both arms are checked","Relieved and grateful if told a medical doctor will also review"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and safety","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the ward psychiatrists. Are you Brenda Oyelaran?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes. My chest, doctor, it's happening again."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you told the nurses. Is the pain there right now, and are you short of breath at this moment?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"It's eased a bit, but I'm scared. Everyone keeps saying it's just my nerves."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening and frustrating. Chest pain always gets checked properly. Joy, could you do a 12-lead ECG straight after the obs, please?","m":[2,12]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"(HCA) Doing it now. Obs are done: pulse 96, sats 97, NEWS2 is 2."},{"t":"0:33","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Brenda, I'd like to ask a few questions, then examine your heart, with Joy here. Is that alright? I'll wash my hands first.","m":[1,4]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Yes, please. Go ahead."},{"t":"0:43","band":"Pain history","w":"D","s":"When did it start, and where is it? What does it feel like, and does it spread anywhere?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"About forty minutes ago, when I panicked. Tight, right in the middle. Six out of ten, two now. It stays put."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Any sweating, sickness or feeling faint? Does it hurt to breathe in? Any pain in your calves?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"No. None of that."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Any heart problems, blood pressure, diabetes or smoking? Anyone in the family with heart trouble?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"Blood pressure tablets. I smoked for twenty years, stopped five years ago. My dad died of a heart attack at sixty."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"That must make every twinge feel worrying. Thank you, that helps me a lot.","m":[12]},{"t":"1:31","band":"Inspection","w":"D","s":"Could you sit back at about 45 degrees? I'll close the curtain, keep the sheet over you and only uncover what I need.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I didn't want the door open."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"I'm just looking at your breathing and colour, and around the bed for any sprays or oxygen.","m":[5,11]},{"t":"1:50","band":"Hands and pulse","w":"D","s":"Your hands are warm, no changes in the nails. I'm pressing a fingernail to see how fast the colour returns: that's normal.","m":[5,11]},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Now I'm feeling both wrist pulses together. Any pain in your shoulder before I lift your arm?","m":[5,11]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"No. Is my pulse okay?"},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"It's regular, a little quick, which fits with feeling frightened. Joy, could you take the blood pressure in both arms?","m":[5,11]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"(HCA) 148 over 88 right, 146 over 86 left."},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"Why both arms?"},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"Good question. It helps rule out a problem with the main blood vessel from the heart. They're the same, which is good.","m":[5,11]},{"t":"2:31","band":"Face and neck","w":"D","s":"Could you look up for me? I'm checking under your eyelids for paleness. Now turn your head a little to the left and relax.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"I'm looking at the veins in your neck, then feeling the pulse in your neck, one side at a time. That all looks normal.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Heart","w":"D","s":"I'm looking at your chest for any scars, then feeling where your heartbeat is strongest. No scars, and the beat is in the normal place.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"I'll listen in four places now, feeling your neck pulse at the same time. Breathe normally.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Can you hear anything wrong?"},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"Not so far. Roll onto your left side for me. And now lean forward, blow all your breath out and hold. Thank you, that sounds normal.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:30","band":"Lungs and legs","w":"D","s":"I'm listening to the bottom of your lungs at the back. Deep breaths through your mouth. They sound clear.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"Now I'm pressing gently on your lower back and ankles for fluid, and checking your calves. Tell me if anything hurts.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"No, nothing hurts there."},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"(HCA) ECG's done, doctor."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Sinus rhythm, rate 96, no ST changes, QTc 440. Brenda, your heart tracing looks normal.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:03","band":"Summary and escalate","w":"D","s":"So: your tracing, oxygen and examination are all reassuring. Because of your age and your dad's history, I'm asking the medical doctor to see you and check a heart blood test.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"So you don't think it's just my nerves?"},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"Panic can cause real chest pain, but we check the heart first, every time. You were right to tell us.","m":[9,12]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Thank you. That's the first time anyone's said that."},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"I'll ring the medical on-call now with all this. Joy, hourly obs please. If the pain lasts over 15 minutes, the ECG changes or she collapses, call 999 straight away.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"(HCA) Got it."},{"t":"4:42","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Brenda, if the pain comes back or gets worse, press your buzzer at once. Can you tell me what happens next?","m":[13,14]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"A medical doctor comes, a blood test, and I buzz if it's back."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Any questions for me? I'll wash my hands and write this up.","m":[14,4]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you for actually checking."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms identity, gains consent and offers a chaperone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about pain now and requests an ECG and observations at once","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Takes a focused pain history with associated symptoms and cardiac risk factors","d":"Content"},{"c":"Hand hygiene, positioning at 45 degrees and dignified exposure","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Examines hands, capillary refill, radial pulses and blood pressure in both arms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Examines face for pallor, the JVP and the carotid pulses","d":"Content"},{"c":"Inspects and palpates the chest, then listens in four areas with positioning manoeuvres","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks lung bases, sacral and ankle oedema and the calves","d":"Content"},{"c":"Interprets the ECG and explains findings in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Escalates to the medical on-call for troponin and gives named 999 triggers","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains each step of the examination as it happens","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her fear of not being believed","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Safety-nets: buzzer at once if the pain returns or worsens","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks understanding, invites questions and closes with hand hygiene","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"ECG and NEWS2 first, a quick pain history, a complete and explained cardiovascular examination including both-arm BP and calves, and escalation for troponin with clear triggers.","borderline":"Thorough examination but the ECG comes late, the pain history is thin, or escalation is vague.","fail":"Attributes the pain to panic without an ECG, gives sedation first, or examines without consent or explanation."}},"c-u-14":{"setup":"Monday late morning, adult ward. Kerry-Ann Mensah, 39, with bipolar disorder on lithium, has noticed her neck swelling. Take a brief medical history about it and carry out a thyroid examination in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Kerry-Ann Mensah, 39, female, inpatient","open":"Yes, that's me.","manner":"Friendly, a bit self-conscious about her neck and her weight. Cooperative. Worried she will be taken off lithium; relieved when told she can usually continue.","free":["Her neck has looked fuller over the past month","She feels very tired","She has been on lithium for years and it helps her"],"ifasked":["No pain, swallowing or breathing problems, no voice change","Weight gain with the same appetite","Always cold; constipated, last bowel movement four days ago","Dry skin, coarse hair, lower sex drive, heavier periods","No heat intolerance, palpitations or sweating","Six years on lithium; not sure when thyroid was last checked","Mother takes thyroid tablets"],"worries":["Is the lithium doing this? Will I have to come off it?","Could it be cancer?","Could this be why I got so depressed?"],"rules":["Embarrassed if her weight is mentioned bluntly; relaxed if phrased kindly","Asks about stopping lithium; relieved by a clear answer","Follows every examination instruction promptly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and consent","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the ward psychiatrists. Are you Kerry-Ann Mensah?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's me."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"The nurses told me you've noticed some swelling in your neck. I'd like to ask a few questions about it, then have a look and feel. Is that alright? Nurse Ade will stay with us.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:16","w":"P","s":"Yes please. It's been bothering me."},{"t":"0:19","band":"The swelling","w":"D","s":"When did you first notice it, and has it grown?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:23","w":"P","s":"About a month ago. It's slowly got bigger."},{"t":"0:27","w":"D","s":"Is it painful? Any trouble swallowing or breathing, or any change in your voice?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"No pain. I can swallow fine. My voice is the same."},{"t":"0:37","band":"Thyroid symptoms","w":"D","s":"How's your energy? Have you noticed any change in your weight or appetite?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"I'm exhausted all the time. And I've put on weight, but I'm eating the same. I thought I was just getting lazy."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you've been blaming yourself. That kind of tiredness can come straight from the thyroid; it isn't laziness.","m":[11]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"Oh. That's good to hear."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Do you feel the cold more than other people? How are your bowels?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"Freezing all the time. I haven't been for four days, which isn't like me."},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"Any change in your skin or hair, your periods, or your interest in sex?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"Dry skin, hair like straw. Periods heavier. And no, not much interest lately."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Any times you feel hot and sweaty, shaky, or your heart races?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"No, the opposite."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"How long have you taken lithium, and when was your last thyroid blood test? Anyone in the family with thyroid problems?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"Six years. I can't remember the last thyroid test. Mum takes thyroid tablets."},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"Is the lithium doing this? Will I have to come off it?"},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"That's a really fair worry. I'll answer properly once I've examined you, but the short answer is no, people usually stay on it.","m":[11,10]},{"t":"1:50","band":"Inspection","w":"D","s":"Could you sit on this chair and open your collar? Is your neck sore anywhere? I'll wash my hands first.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"Not sore."},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"I'm looking at your hands: they're a little cool and dry, no sweating. Your nails look healthy. Hold your arms out, palms down.","m":[6,12]},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"There's a slight fine tremor, which lithium often causes. Now I'm checking your pulse. It's 58 and regular, a bit slow.","m":[6,12]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"Is a slow pulse bad?"},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"Not dangerous at this rate, and it fits with a slow thyroid. Now follow my finger with your eyes, without moving your head. I'll also look at your eyes from the side.","m":[6,12]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"2:32","band":"Neck","w":"D","s":"Take a sip of water and hold it. Now swallow while I watch. Good. Now stick your tongue out.","m":[7,12]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"It moved up when I swallowed, didn't it?"},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"It did: that tells me it's the thyroid. I'm going to stand behind you and feel gently. Another sip and swallow for me.","m":[7,12]},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"It's smooth, the same on both sides, and I can't feel any separate lumps. Now the glands around your neck, and your windpipe in the middle.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Could it be cancer?"},{"t":"3:07","w":"D","s":"I understand that thought. A smooth swelling on both sides that's grown slowly is far more likely to be the thyroid enlarging. If it ever changes, we'll scan it.","m":[11,10]},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"Your glands are normal and your windpipe is central. I'll tap below your neck, then listen over the swelling.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"3:25","band":"Completing signs","w":"D","s":"No sounds over it. Now I'll tap your ankle reflexes.","m":[9,12]},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"They relax a little slowly, which goes with an underactive thyroid. Could you cross your arms and stand up from the chair? And can I look at your shins?","m":[9,12]},{"t":"3:42","w":"P","s":"There you go. Easy."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Strong legs, and your shins look normal. Thank you, Kerry-Ann, you can do your collar up.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:50","band":"Explain and plan","w":"D","s":"I think your thyroid has become underactive, and lithium is the likely cause. It's common and very treatable.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"Could this be why I got so depressed?"},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"It may well have played a part, so treating it could help your mood too. Today I'll send a thyroid test, calcium, kidney tests, and a lithium level taken 12 hours after your evening dose.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"If it's confirmed, a small thyroid tablet each morning usually sorts it. Please keep taking your lithium. If your voice goes hoarse or swallowing gets hard, tell the nurses straight away.","m":[10,13]},{"t":"4:24","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Let's check I've made sense. What did we find, and what comes next?","m":[14]},{"t":"4:29","w":"P","s":"My thyroid's slow from the lithium, blood tests today, maybe a tablet, and I don't stop lithium."},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. What else would you like to ask? I'll talk to the consultant on the ward round this afternoon.","m":[14]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"Nothing else. Thanks, I feel much better knowing."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms identity, gains consent and has a chaperone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks onset, growth, pain and swallowing, breathing and voice red flags","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks hypothyroid symptoms including bowels, skin and hair, periods and libido, and screens hyperthyroid symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks lithium duration, last thyroid test and family thyroid history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Hand hygiene, positioning and asks about neck pain before touching","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Examines hands, tremor, pulse and eyes, including movements and from the side","d":"Content"},{"c":"Inspects the neck with a swallow and tongue protrusion","d":"Content"},{"c":"Palpates from behind with swallow, checks lymph nodes, trachea, percussion and bruit","d":"Content"},{"c":"Completes with ankle reflexes, proximal power and pretibial skin","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains likely lithium-related hypothyroidism, names blood tests and advises continuing lithium","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her self-blame and fears about cancer and lithium","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains each examination step as it happens","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets: hoarse voice or swallowing difficulty reported at once","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks understanding and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A focused hypothyroid and red-flag history, a complete ordered thyroid examination with swallow and palpation from behind, and a clear link to lithium with tests and advice to continue it.","borderline":"Good examination but a thin symptom history, or no clear explanation and plan.","fail":"Misses the swallow or palpation from behind, says to stop lithium, or examines without consent."}},"c-u-16":{"setup":"02:10, women's psychiatric ward. Sinead Fairweather, 39, on Section 2, started olanzapine three weeks ago. Four days of green phlegm; now breathless with a temperature of 39.6°C. Assess her chest and agree a safe plan in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Sinead Fairweather, 39, female, inpatient on Section 2","open":"Yes. I can't get my breath.","manner":"Breathless, tired and frightened; speaks in short sentences. Briefly suspicious about the new tablet; calms and cooperates when each step is explained. Needs to pause after four deep breaths.","free":["Cough for four days, bringing up green phlegm","Breathless since this evening","Feels hot and shivery","Worried she is seriously ill"],"ifasked":["No pain on breathing in","No blood in the phlegm","No calf pain or swelling","No asthma or past chest infections","Smokes ten cigarettes a day","Water sometimes 'goes down the wrong way' since starting the tablet","No stiffness or muddled feeling"],"worries":["Am I going to die?","Is the new tablet to blame?","Will the other hospital keep me?"],"rules":["Struggles to take more than four deep breaths in a row; needs a pause","Asks 'Am I going to die?' early; settles if the doctor acknowledges the fear and explains","Reluctant to undress until the doctor offers privacy and a female chaperone","Agrees to transfer only once she knows a nurse will go with her"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, the psychiatry doctor on call tonight. Are you Sinead Fairweather? Can I see your wristband?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Yes. I can't get my breath."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you feel so poorly. I've washed my hands. I'd like to ask a few questions and listen to your chest. Is that okay, with Nurse Okafor here too?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Okay. Am I going to die?"},{"t":"0:36","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. You're talking well and your oxygen level is safe right now. I'm going to find out what's going on and get you the right help tonight.","m":[11]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Nurse, what were her latest observations?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Resps 24, sats 95% on air, pulse 106, BP 112 over 70, temp 39.6, alert."},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"Thanks. That's a NEWS2 of 6, so she needs an urgent medical review within the hour. Sinead, can you sit up at an angle? Any pain anywhere before I start?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"No pain. Just the breathing."},{"t":"1:30","band":"Focused questions","w":"D","s":"When did the cough start, and what are you bringing up?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"Four days ago. Green stuff."},{"t":"1:41","w":"D","s":"Any blood in it? Does it hurt when you breathe in deeply?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"No blood. No pain."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"Any pain or swelling in your legs? And have you had asthma or chest problems before?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I smoke, though. Ten a day."},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. Since the new tablet, have drinks ever gone down the wrong way?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Sometimes, with water. It's that tablet, isn't it? You've poisoned me."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"I can see why you'd wonder. The tablet can make chest infections a bit more likely, and I'll tell your consultant. Stopping it suddenly tonight could make you unwell, so let's treat the chest first.","m":[11,10]},{"t":"2:41","w":"P","s":"Fine. Do I have to take my top off? I don't like that."},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"Just me and Nurse Okafor, curtains closed. You can keep your bra on, and I'll uncover one part at a time.","m":[3]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"3:04","band":"Inspect","w":"D","s":"You're working hard to breathe, I can see. Breathe normally while I watch. That cough sounds rough; is this your sputum pot?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"Yes. It's all green."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Rate about 24, using your neck muscles a little, but you're speaking in full sentences. Can I see your hands? Hold them out and cock your wrists back.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"Like this?"},{"t":"3:38","band":"Hands, face and neck","w":"D","s":"Perfect. No clubbing, no blue tinge, no flap. Pulse 106 and regular. Lips and tongue look pink. I'll gently feel your windpipe and the glands in your neck.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Mm-hm."},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"Windpipe's in the middle, no swollen glands, neck veins normal. Now bend and straighten your arm while I hold it. I'm checking your muscles aren't stiff.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"Feels normal."},{"t":"4:15","band":"Front of chest","w":"D","s":"Good, no stiffness. Now my hands on your chest: deep breath in, and out.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"I'll tap on each side now, top to bottom, then listen. Breathe in and out through your mouth, and say 'ninety-nine' when I ask.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:14","w":"P","s":"Ninety-nine... sorry, I need a break."},{"t":"5:19","band":"Back of chest","w":"D","s":"Take your time, you're doing really well. The front sounds fairly clear. Can you lean forward, arms crossed, so I can do your back?","m":[11,8]},{"t":"5:32","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Breathe in deeply... The right side moves less at the bottom. Now tapping... and listening.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"What's that tapping for?"},{"t":"6:44","w":"D","s":"Good question. The sound tells me if the lung below is full of air or something thicker, like infection. Your right side sounds duller at the bottom, and I can hear crackles there.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"7:02","w":"P","s":"Is that bad?"},{"t":"7:05","w":"D","s":"It's treatable. Last thing: I'll press on your calves and ankles. Any tenderness?","m":[7]},{"t":"7:15","w":"P","s":"No, they're fine."},{"t":"7:17","w":"D","s":"Soft and not swollen. Thank you, you can cover up now.","m":[7]},{"t":"7:24","band":"Explain and plan","w":"D","s":"I think you have a chest infection at the bottom of your right lung. You need an X-ray, blood tests and antibiotics tonight, and that's at the main hospital.","m":[9]},{"t":"7:40","w":"P","s":"I'm not going there. They'll keep me forever."},{"t":"7:46","w":"D","s":"That's a fair worry after everything. It's only for your chest. A nurse from here comes with you, and you come back once you're better.","m":[11,10]},{"t":"8:00","w":"P","s":"As long as someone comes."},{"t":"8:04","w":"D","s":"They will. So what's the plan tonight, as you understand it?","m":[9]},{"t":"8:10","w":"P","s":"Chest infection, X-ray, antibiotics, the big hospital, with a nurse."},{"t":"8:17","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Nurse, I'll call the medical registrar and the on-call consultant for Section 17 leave with an escort. Please take bloods now: FBC, U&E, CRP, LFTs, CK, lactate, and two blood cultures, plus a sputum sample.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"8:38","w":"P","s":"Okay. Anything else?"},{"t":"8:40","w":"D","s":"Observations every 30 minutes. If her score reaches 7, sats drop below 94% or she gets drowsy, call 999 and bleep me straight away.","m":[12]},{"t":"8:54","w":"P","s":"Got it."},{"t":"8:56","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Sinead. You did really well. I'm washing my hands now, and I'll come back to tell you when the ambulance is booked.","m":[13]},{"t":"9:10","w":"P","s":"Thanks, Doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Hand hygiene, identity check, consent and a chaperone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Obtains a full set of observations and states the NEWS2 score and urgency","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Positions her at an angle, asks about pain first, and protects her dignity","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about onset, sputum, haemoptysis, pleuritic pain, legs, past chest disease, smoking and swallowing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Inspects, counts the breathing rate and comments on breathlessness and sputum to her","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks hands, pulse, central cyanosis, trachea, neck nodes and JVP","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks muscle tone and calves for NMS and a clot","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks expansion, percussion, breath sounds and vocal resonance, front and back, comparing sides","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains findings and the likely chest infection in plain words and checks understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Plans urgent medical transfer with escort and Section 17 leave; olanzapine flagged, not stopped","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Acknowledges her fear and suspicion and responds calmly","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names bloods with cultures and CK, and sets a NEWS2 or SpO2 trigger for 999","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Thanks her, washes hands and says when they will be back","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A systematic chest check front and back with findings shared with her, NEWS2 stated, NMS and clot screened, and a legal, urgent medical transfer with a clear 999 trigger.","borderline":"Finds the right-sided signs but misses NEWS2, the back of the chest, Section 17 leave, or a clear safety net.","fail":"Disorganised or incomplete check, plans ward treatment overnight, misses sepsis, or ignores her fear and dignity."}},"c-u-17":{"setup":"Thursday late morning, adult inpatient ward. Lewis Marangoni, 45, on clozapine, due for discharge next week, has not opened his bowels for 12 days and has tummy pain. Check his abdomen and agree an urgent plan in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Lewis Marangoni, 45, male, informal inpatient on clozapine","open":"Yeah. My stomach really hurts.","manner":"Uncomfortable and worried, keeps returning to his discharge. Embarrassed about having hidden it. Cooperative once reassured; winces on deep pressure in the lower left tummy.","free":["Has not opened his bowels for 12 days","Pain low in his tummy, about 7 out of 10","Desperate not to lose his discharge date"],"ifasked":["Not passing wind since yesterday","Feels sick but has not vomited","Has not eaten today","Stopped the Laxido sachets at home on leave because of the taste","No missed clozapine doses","Takes a hyoscine tablet at night for drooling","No previous tummy operations","No blood or watery leakage"],"worries":["Will this stop my discharge?","Are you taking my clozapine away?","Can't you just give me a strong laxative?"],"rules":["Declines the rectal check, politely, unless strongly needed","Says 'ow' when the lower left is pressed deeply","Mentions discharge at least twice; settles once told it is delayed, not cancelled","Admits stopping the sachets only if asked which laxatives he actually took"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Lewis Marangoni? Can I check your wristband?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Yeah. My stomach really hurts."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry. The nurses say you haven't been for nearly two weeks. I've washed my hands. I need to check your tummy now. Is that okay, with Nurse Patel here?","m":[1,12]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"Fine. Please don't stop my discharge."},{"t":"0:36","w":"D","s":"I know how much going home matters. Let's make sure you're safe first. Telling us today was the right thing. Where is the pain, out of ten?","m":[12,1]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"Low down, here. About a seven. Comes in waves."},{"t":"0:57","band":"Red flags","w":"D","s":"When did you last open your bowels properly?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"Twelve days ago. Maybe more."},{"t":"1:06","w":"D","s":"Are you passing wind?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"Not since yesterday."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"Have you felt sick or been sick? What have you eaten today?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Bit sick. Haven't eaten anything."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"Any watery leakage, or blood when you wipe?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"Which laxatives did you actually take while you were on leave?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"The senna. I stopped the sachets. They taste awful. I should have said something, I'm an idiot."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"You're not. Lots of people keep quiet when something important is at stake. What matters is you told us. Have you missed any clozapine, and any tummy operations ever?","m":[12,3]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Never missed it. No operations."},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"And you take hyoscine at night for drooling, is that right?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Yeah, one tablet."},{"t":"2:18","band":"Inspect","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Can you lie flat on one pillow and uncover your tummy? Nurse, what's his NEWS2?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"It's 1. Pulse 104, temp 37.6, the rest normal."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"A score of 1, but with this history that doesn't make it safe. Lewis, your tummy looks swollen. No scars. Let me see your hands and feel your pulse.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"2:52","band":"Hands to face","w":"D","s":"Pulse is fast. Look up for me... no yellowing, no paleness. Tongue out... your mouth is dry, so you're a bit dehydrated.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"I've not drunk much."},{"t":"3:10","band":"Palpate","w":"D","s":"I'll start pressing away from the sore part. Tell me if anything hurts; I'll watch your face too.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:42","w":"P","s":"That's okay."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"Now a bit deeper in each area, leaving the low part till last.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Ow, there. On the left."},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"Sorry, I'll stop pressing. Thank you, that helps me find the problem. Does it hurt more when I let go?","m":[12,6]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"No, just when you press."},{"t":"4:44","band":"Percuss and listen","w":"D","s":"Tender low down, worst on the left. No guarding and no lumps. Now I'll tap across your tummy.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"5:04","w":"P","s":"Sounds hollow."},{"t":"5:07","w":"D","s":"It does, like a drum: that's trapped gas. Now I'll listen for a full minute. Stay as still as you can.","m":[7]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"Anything?"},{"t":"6:27","w":"D","s":"Very quiet. Your gut has slowed right down. I'd like to check your groin for hernias, and offer a check inside your back passage with Nurse Patel here. That one's your choice.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"6:44","w":"P","s":"Groin's fine. I'm not having a finger up there."},{"t":"6:50","w":"D","s":"That's completely your choice, and I'll write down that you said no for now. The surgeons may ask again. Groin's clear, thank you.","m":[8]},{"t":"7:16","w":"P","s":"So what is it?"},{"t":"7:19","band":"Explain and plan","w":"D","s":"Clozapine can slow the bowel right down, and I think yours may have stopped moving. That can be serious, so the surgeons at the main hospital need to see you today.","m":[9]},{"t":"7:36","w":"P","s":"Can't you just give me a strong laxative?"},{"t":"7:41","w":"D","s":"Fair question. If the bowel is blocked, pushing it could make it worse. The surgeons check first, then we choose the treatment. Until then, nothing to eat or drink.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"7:57","w":"P","s":"And my clozapine? It's kept me well."},{"t":"8:02","w":"D","s":"I understand that worries you. We'll hold tonight's dose until I've spoken to your consultant and the pharmacist. If it's paused over two days, we restart low and build up again. And we'll stop the hyoscine.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"8:22","w":"P","s":"And my discharge?"},{"t":"8:25","w":"D","s":"It may move a little, but it isn't cancelled. Can you tell me what's happening next, in your words?","m":[9,12]},{"t":"8:36","w":"P","s":"Main hospital today, nothing to eat, and the clozapine might pause."},{"t":"8:43","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Nurse, bloods now please: FBC with neutrophils, U&E, CRP, LFTs, amylase, lactate and a clozapine level. I'll ring the surgical registrar and book an ambulance with an escort.","m":[10]},{"t":"9:00","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"9:02","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"If he vomits or the pain gets worse before then, call 999 and bleep me. I'll tell Dr Ahmed and complete an incident report. Thank you, Lewis. I'm washing my hands now.","m":[10,13]},{"t":"9:20","w":"P","s":"Thanks, Doc."}],"marks":[{"c":"Hand hygiene, identity, consent, chaperone, and pain site and score before touching","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about last bowel action, wind, vomiting, eating and overflow or blood","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about laxatives actually taken, missed clozapine, hyoscine and past surgery","d":"Content"},{"c":"Positions him flat, exposes properly, obtains NEWS2 and says it is not reassuring","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks hands, pulse, eyes and mouth, commenting on dehydration to him","d":"Content"},{"c":"Palpates light then deep, painful area last, watching his face; checks guarding and rebound","d":"Content"},{"c":"Percusses and listens for bowel sounds for a full minute","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks hernias and offers a chaperoned rectal check, respecting refusal","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains suspected clozapine-related obstruction in plain words and checks understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Arranges same-day surgical review, nil by mouth, named bloods and a 999 safety net","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans clozapine: hold, consultant and pharmacist told, re-titration after 48 hours, hyoscine stopped","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Acknowledges his discharge fear and guilt without dismissing them","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Closes: names who will be told, incident report, thanks him, washes hands","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Asks about wind, vomiting and laxatives, checks the abdomen systematically, offers the remaining checks, and sends him to the surgeons today with nil by mouth, bloods and a safe clozapine plan.","borderline":"Thorough check, but treats it as simple constipation, or misses the clozapine restart rule or the hyoscine.","fail":"Prescribes an enema or laxatives and reviews tomorrow, is reassured by NEWS2, or ignores his discharge fear."}},"c-u-18":{"setup":"Monday morning, older adult team base. Tariq Hussain, 68, retired bus driver, referred for memory problems. He has consented. Take a collateral history from his daughter Zainab, consider causes and risks, and share next steps in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Zainab Hussain, 34, female, his daughter","open":"Yes, Tariq's daughter. We've been so worried.","manner":"Warm, articulate and anxious; tends to excuse his lapses as old age. Becomes tearful about her mum's exhaustion; settles when validated and given practical help.","free":["A year of forgetting, starting with missed GP and dentist appointments","Got lost driving to the mosque he has attended for 30 years, last month","Struggles to find words","Beard untrimmed and shirt buttoned wrong, unlike him"],"ifasked":["Steady decline, no sudden drops, no good and bad days","No visions, falls, tremor or stiffness","Personality the same; quieter, not sad","Never drinks alcohol","Takes amlodipine for blood pressure; no stroke, diabetes or head injury","Used to do all the household bills; now red letters, she has taken over","Burnt a pan on the hob last month; paid a phone caller £300 for boiler cover","Still drives to the shops"],"worries":["Is this dementia?","He'll never give up driving.","Mum is exhausted and won't ask for help."],"rules":["Explains lapses as old age unless asked for specific examples","Becomes tearful about her mum; settles if validated","Mentions the scam only if asked about money or cold callers","Asks 'Is this dementia?' near the end"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, a psychiatrist in the older adults team. Are you Zainab Hussain?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Yes, Tariq's daughter. We've been so worried."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming. Your dad has agreed we can talk about him. Before I ask questions, what worries you most?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"That he'll get lost and something will happen. I thought it was just old age. I feel awful we didn't notice sooner."},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"That's so understandable; early changes are easy to explain away. You noticed, and you're here now. What first made you think something had changed?","m":[12,2]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"He started missing appointments. The GP, the dentist."},{"t":"1:14","band":"Timeline","w":"D","s":"When was that, roughly?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"About a year ago."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"Has it crept on gradually, or come in sudden steps? And does it vary from day to day?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:36","w":"P","s":"Gradually. Just slowly worse. No, he's the same every day."},{"t":"1:44","band":"Thinking skills","w":"D","s":"Does he forget recent things, like what you talked about, or repeat himself?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"All the time. He asks when I'm coming three times in one call."},{"t":"2:04","w":"D","s":"Does he struggle to find words, or call things by the wrong name?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Yes, he says 'the thing, the thing' a lot."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"Has he ever got lost somewhere familiar?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"Last month he got lost driving to the mosque. He's been going there thirty years. He rang us from a garage forecourt."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"That must have been frightening. Does he still know who people are, and what day it is?","m":[12,3]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"He knows us all. Days, he gets mixed up."},{"t":"3:05","band":"Daily living","w":"D","s":"How is he with washing, shaving and dressing? And who handles the bills now?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"His beard's a mess, and he buttons his shirt wrong. He used to do all the bills; there were red letters, so I took over."},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"Does he still use the cooker, and has he given up any hobbies?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"He can't follow the cricket on the radio any more. He burnt a pan last month."},{"t":"3:57","band":"Other causes","w":"D","s":"Has his mood been low, or has he lost interest in life?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"He's quieter. Not sad, I don't think."},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"Does he ever see things that aren't there? Any falls, shaking or stiffness?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"Has his personality changed: ruder, more impulsive, saying odd things? Does he drink alcohol?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"No, he's still Dad. He's never drunk."},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"Any stroke, heart trouble, diabetes or head injury? What tablets does he take, including anything from the chemist?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"Just a blood pressure tablet. Amlodipine."},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"Has anyone in the family had memory problems?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:11","w":"P","s":"Not that I know of."},{"t":"5:16","band":"Risks and carer","w":"D","s":"Is he still driving?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"To the shops, yes. He'll never stop. It's his whole life."},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"I know, he drove for a living. But getting lost at the wheel worries me. I'll advise him not to drive until he's assessed, and I'll explain it to him myself.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:51","w":"P","s":"That would help. He won't hear it from me."},{"t":"5:59","w":"D","s":"Has anyone tried to take money from him, by phone or at the door?","m":[7]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"Actually yes. He paid a man £300 for boiler cover we didn't need."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you told me. Let's ask his bank about card limits and a trusted contact. And how is your mum coping?","m":[8,7]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","s":"She's exhausted. She won't ask for help. (tearful) She does everything."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Caring like this is really hard. She's able to ask for a carer's assessment from adult social care, and I'll give you the number today.","m":[12,8]},{"t":"7:06","w":"P","s":"Thank you. So... is this dementia?"},{"t":"7:11","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"What you describe, a steady change over a year starting with memory and words, fits a memory illness such as Alzheimer's disease. We need tests before we can say for sure.","m":[9]},{"t":"7:33","w":"P","s":"What tests?"},{"t":"7:36","w":"D","s":"Blood tests, which he's having now, memory testing in clinic, and a brain scan. Those rule out other causes, like thyroid or vitamin problems.","m":[10]},{"t":"7:54","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"7:57","w":"D","s":"In case he gets lost again, there's a form called the Herbert Protocol: a photo and his usual places, ready for the police. I'll give you a leaflet, and our OT can look at the cooker.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"8:23","w":"P","s":"That's really helpful."},{"t":"8:26","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the next steps, as you understand them?","m":[11]},{"t":"8:35","w":"P","s":"Tests, no driving for now, the form, and help for Mum."},{"t":"8:44","w":"D","s":"Exactly. What other questions do you have? Here's our duty number if things change before clinic. I'll write to the GP today.","m":[13]},{"t":"9:00","w":"P","s":"None for now. Thank you, Doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Tariq's consent and invites her main worry first","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes onset, duration, gradual course and absence of fluctuation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about recent memory, word-finding, getting lost, recognition and orientation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about self-care, bills, cooking and dropped hobbies","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for low mood, visual hallucinations, falls, parkinsonism, personality change and alcohol","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about stroke, vascular risks, head injury, all medicines and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about driving, scams and the carer's wellbeing","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees an action for each risk: driving advice, bank, OT, carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a cautious impression of an Alzheimer's-type illness in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names next steps: bloods, memory testing, brain scan, Herbert Protocol","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding of the next steps","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her guilt, fear and distress about her mum","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Invites questions, gives a contact number and says who will be told","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A structured history that fixes the course, covers each thinking skill and daily task, screens every differential, and turns each risk into an action, with a cautious summary.","borderline":"Good memory history, but misses differentials, driving or carer strain, or gives a diagnosis too firmly.","fail":"Unstructured questioning, no screening for other causes, no risk assessment, or ignores her distress."}},"c-u-19":{"setup":"Wednesday afternoon, medical ward. Gordon Whitfield, 71, admitted two days ago after being found half dressed outside his house; the ward query psychosis. Take a collateral history from his son Rhys to clarify the cause, and explain your impression, in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Rhys Whitfield, 35, male, his son","open":"Yes. What's going on with him?","manner":"Anxious and protective, a little angry at first about not being told much. Becomes tearful about his mum's death. Calms and engages once his worry is acknowledged and he is given a job.","free":["Saw his dad well early last week","Dad took Rhys's two children to the park without trouble","On the ward Dad calls him by his brother Owen's name","Very worried this is dementia"],"ifasked":["Dad had a bad cough with green phlegm; Rhys told him to see the GP","Drowsy at lunch, awake and restless at night","Picks at the air as if seeing things","Took amlodipine and atorvastatin only; nothing new","Drinks two pints at the weekend","Drives, shops, pays bills online, finds new places easily","Mum died last year of breast cancer; since then ready meals, less care over appearance","Tried to leave the ward last night"],"worries":["Is this dementia?","Will he go to a mental hospital?","What can I do?"],"rules":["Initially snaps 'Nobody tells me anything'; settles if acknowledged","Becomes tearful about his mum; settles if validated","Mentions the cough only if asked about recent illness","Asks 'Is this dementia?' at some point"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso from the liaison psychiatry team. Are you Rhys, Gordon's son?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Yes. What's going on with him? Nobody tells me anything."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you've felt left out; that's hard when you're this worried. Your dad has agreed we can talk. I'd really value your view of how he was before this, and then I'll explain what I think.","m":[12,1]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thank you."},{"t":"0:52","band":"Timeline","w":"D","s":"When did you last see him completely himself?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:59","w":"P","s":"Last Tuesday. He took my two kids to the park. Totally normal."},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"And how quickly did things change?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Within a couple of days. Then the neighbour found him outside, half dressed."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"Since he's been here, is he the same all day, or better at some times?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"It's up and down. He's asleep at lunch, then wandering at night."},{"t":"1:48","band":"Features","w":"D","s":"Can he follow a conversation, or does he drift off?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"He loses track mid-sentence. He keeps calling me Owen, my brother."},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"Does he know where he is? Has he seemed to see things that aren't there?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"He asked if he was at work. And he picks at the air, like there's something there."},{"t":"2:32","w":"D","s":"Is he eating and drinking?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"He didn't touch his lunch today."},{"t":"2:43","band":"Physical causes","w":"D","s":"Had he been unwell before this, with a cough, fever or waterworks trouble?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"Actually, yes. A bad cough. Green phlegm. I told him to see the GP. I should have taken him."},{"t":"3:09","w":"D","s":"You did the right thing telling him. Lots of people put the GP off. What you've just said really helps us.","m":[12,4]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"Any new tablets, or anything from the chemist? How much does he drink?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Just his blood pressure and cholesterol ones. Two pints at the weekend, that's all."},{"t":"3:51","w":"D","s":"Any falls or bumps to the head? Any problems with his bowels or passing water?","m":[4]},{"t":"4:03","w":"P","s":"Not that I know of. I don't know about his bowels."},{"t":"4:13","band":"Baseline","w":"D","s":"That's fine, the nurses can chart it. Before this, was he driving, shopping and managing his money?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:26","w":"P","s":"All of it. He pays everything online, drives everywhere, finds his way to new places."},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"Any memory problems over the past year?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"None. He's sharper than me."},{"t":"4:50","band":"Grief and background","w":"D","s":"How has he been since your mum died?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"(tearful) It's been hard. He lives on ready meals. He's let himself go a bit. I should have been there more."},{"t":"5:14","w":"D","s":"Losing your mum has been hard on all of you. You're here for him now. Once he's better, we'll check how he's coping with the loss.","m":[12,6]},{"t":"5:34","w":"P","s":"Thanks."},{"t":"5:37","w":"D","s":"Has he ever seen a psychiatrist, or has anyone in the family had dementia?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:49","w":"P","s":"No and no."},{"t":"5:53","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Has he tried to leave the ward or pull at his drip? And he lives alone and still drives, is that right?","m":[7]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"He tried to leave last night. And yes, he drives."},{"t":"6:19","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Is this the dementia you're worried about?","m":[9]},{"t":"6:27","w":"P","s":"Yes. Is it? The consultant said psychotic."},{"t":"6:33","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I don't think it's either. This looks like delirium: sudden confusion caused by a physical illness, most likely the chest infection.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:50","w":"P","s":"So it's not dementia?"},{"t":"6:55","w":"D","s":"Dementia creeps on over months, with a person awake and alert. Your dad changed in days, and his alertness comes and goes. That points to delirium, and most people get better as the cause is treated.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"7:22","w":"P","s":"How long will it take?"},{"t":"7:27","w":"D","s":"Often days to a couple of weeks; sometimes longer at his age. The ward will treat the infection and check his bloods, bowels and bladder.","m":[10]},{"t":"7:46","w":"P","s":"What can I actually do?"},{"t":"7:51","w":"D","s":"A lot. Visit at regular times, bring his glasses and some family photos, and calmly remind him where he is and why. Familiar faces help.","m":[11]},{"t":"8:10","w":"P","s":"I can do that."},{"t":"8:15","w":"D","s":"One more thing: no driving until he's fully better and his GP has seen him. What's your main takeaway from all this?","m":[10,13]},{"t":"8:32","w":"P","s":"It's delirium from the infection, it should get better, and I can help him get his bearings."},{"t":"8:46","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll speak to the ward consultant now and write in his notes. What questions do you have?","m":[14]},{"t":"9:00","w":"P","s":"None for now. Thank you, that really helps."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms consent and explains the purpose","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes last seen well, acute onset and fluctuation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about attention, orientation, sleep, perceptual changes and eating","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for infection, new medicines, alcohol, falls, bowels and bladder","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes independent baseline function and no prior memory decline","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about bereavement, mood, psychiatric and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about wandering, pulling lines, living alone and driving","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains delirium and its likely cause in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Distinguishes delirium from dementia and psychosis, answering his question","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Outlines the medical plan, including bowels and bladder, and no driving","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives Rhys practical reorientation tasks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his anger, guilt and grief","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks his understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Invites questions and says who will be told","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Fixes last-seen-well, acute onset and fluctuation, hunts causes, confirms an independent baseline, and explains delirium versus dementia clearly while giving Rhys a role.","borderline":"Reaches delirium but misses baseline function, the cause hunt or Rhys's distress.","fail":"Accepts psychosis or dementia without the timeline, suggests regular antipsychotics, or leaves Rhys without an explanation."}},"c-u-20":{"setup":"Saturday afternoon, A&E. Rosalind Hartley, 72, retired teacher, came in with a split thumbnail and says the doctor has a toxic aura. Her son Martin describes a month of changes. Take a history, reach a diagnosis, explain it and assess risk in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Rosalind Hartley, 72, female, retired primary school teacher","open":"I am, and I won't be long, I've far too much to do.","manner":"Fast, loud and charming, then suddenly irritable; talks over the doctor and drifts to crystals. Briefly tearful about her late husband. Sits down and slows a little when treated with respect.","free":["The A&E doctor has a toxic aura","Crystals and homeopathy can heal anything","She has never felt better","Sleeps about three hours and doesn't need more"],"ifasked":["Can heal by touch and read people's energy; no voices","Spent about £2,000 online; one seller wants her bank details","Prednisolone for a COPD flare about five weeks ago","Stopped her inhalers; plans to swallow the right crystal to heal her lungs","Nearly hit a librarian with a collection tin","Depression after her husband died in her fifties","Went to Spain on a whim for five weeks in her sixties and spent her savings","Father was in hospital for his 'moods'"],"worries":["I'm not ill; I've never felt better.","Are you going to lock me up?","My husband would have understood."],"rules":["Talks over the doctor; responds to a calm, brief summary","Gets angry if her beliefs are mocked; settles if the doctor stays curious","Becomes briefly tearful about her husband; settles if validated","Agrees to tests, but not admission, at the end"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, a psychiatrist with the liaison team. Are you Mrs Hartley?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"I am, and I won't be long, I've far too much to do."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"Thank you for sparing me some time. I gather today has been frustrating. Could we use the quiet room, and you tell me what happened?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:39","w":"P","s":"Not that chair. That doctor sat there. He has a toxic aura. He's poisoned the energy."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"Sit wherever feels right. It sounds like nobody here has listened to you.","m":[1,13]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"Exactly! Finally someone with sense."},{"t":"1:07","band":"Mood and energy","w":"D","s":"How have you been feeling in yourself this past month?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Marvellous. Never better. I've discovered crystal frequencies, the library has wonderful books, my pupils always said I was gifted..."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"That's a lot of energy. How many hours are you sleeping? Do you feel tired?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"Three hours. Why would I need more? My mind's buzzing."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"Do your thoughts race? Have people said you're quicker to anger lately?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"They race, yes. And people are irritating. Martin nags. Are you eating my time too?"},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"I'll keep it short. Are you eating properly?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"I forget. My skirts are loose."},{"t":"2:23","band":"Beliefs","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the auras. What can you see or do?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"I read people's energy. I can heal by touch. I'm meant to share it."},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"Any voices talking to you, or visions, when nobody is around?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:52","w":"P","s":"Don't be silly. I just know."},{"t":"2:58","band":"Behaviour and money","w":"D","s":"Martin mentioned the library and some online orders. What's been happening?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"People need cleansing. Some get rude. About two thousand pounds on remedies. One seller wants my bank details to certify me as a healer."},{"t":"3:25","band":"Physical causes","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being open. I'll come back to that seller. Any recent illness or new medicines, like steroid tablets?","m":[11,5]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Steroids for my chest, five weeks ago. I've got COPD. Not for much longer."},{"t":"3:51","w":"D","s":"Any headaches, falls, bumps to the head, or trouble with your memory? Do you drink or take anything?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"No, no, no. My memory's perfect. I don't drink."},{"t":"4:12","band":"Past and family","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had a time like this before: full of energy, spending, or travelling on impulse?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"I once went to Spain for five weeks. Spent all my savings. Never knew why."},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"And any times of depression?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"(tearful) After my husband died. He'd have understood all this. Nobody listens now."},{"t":"4:52","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you've missed him very much. I am listening, and I want to help you feel safe.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:07","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Sorry."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"No need. Has anyone in the family had problems with their moods?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"Father was in hospital for his 'moods'. Nobody spoke about it."},{"t":"5:29","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Now your safety. How are your lungs? Are you using your inhalers?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"I've stopped them. When I find the right crystal I'll swallow it and heal my lungs."},{"t":"5:52","w":"D","s":"I can see how much you want your breathing better. Swallowing a crystal could seriously hurt you, and stopping inhalers could bring on a bad chest.","m":[7,13]},{"t":"6:11","w":"P","s":"Nonsense. You young doctors don't understand energy."},{"t":"6:17","w":"D","s":"You may know more about it than me; I'm not here to argue. Have you come close to hurting anyone?","m":[8]},{"t":"6:32","w":"P","s":"I nearly hit a librarian with the collection tin. She was rude."},{"t":"6:42","w":"D","s":"Any urge to hurt yourself, or to end your life?","m":[7]},{"t":"6:50","w":"P","s":"Never. I'm a healer."},{"t":"6:54","w":"D","s":"How are things at home with Martin? Are you driving?","m":[8]},{"t":"7:03","w":"P","s":"I shout at him. I'd never hurt him. I drive to the library at night."},{"t":"7:14","w":"D","s":"About that online seller: please don't give your bank details to anyone. That's how people get exploited.","m":[9]},{"t":"7:27","w":"P","s":"Hmm. Fine."},{"t":"7:30","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Mrs Hartley, I think your mood and energy have been running far too high for a month: little sleep, racing thoughts, spending, special powers. Doctors call this mania. It's an illness and it can be treated.","m":[10]},{"t":"7:56","w":"P","s":"I'm not ill. I've never felt better."},{"t":"8:02","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you feel well. What worries me is three hours' sleep, weight loss, the money and your chest. The steroids may have set this off, and I'd like blood tests and a brain scan to check your body.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"8:30","w":"P","s":"Are you going to lock me up?"},{"t":"8:36","w":"D","s":"Not today, no. I'd like to talk to my consultant and, with your agreement, to Martin, about how best to help. What do you make of what I've said?","m":[12]},{"t":"8:57","w":"P","s":"I don't agree. But I'll have your tests."},{"t":"9:04","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll arrange them now and come back to you within the hour.","m":[12]},{"t":"9:15","w":"P","s":"Don't be long."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, offers a quiet room, acknowledges frustration and lets her talk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits reduced sleep need, raised energy, racing thoughts, irritability and poor eating","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores grandiose beliefs and screens for hallucinations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about the library behaviour, spending and online sellers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about steroids, head injury, headaches, memory and substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past highs, past depression and family history of mood disorder","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses risk to self: COPD, inhalers, crystal ingestion, suicide","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses risk to others: librarian, Martin, driving","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Addresses exploitation risk from the online seller","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains mania in plain words with her own symptoms","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Links possible steroid trigger and proposes bloods and a brain scan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her response, names who will be told, and says when she'll return","d":"Close"},{"c":"Stays calm and curious, and validates grief and frustration","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Establishes mania with psychotic features, finds the steroid course, past high and family history, maps risks to self, others and from others, and explains mania respectfully.","borderline":"Names mania but misses the physical screen, past history or one major risk such as the crystal or exploitation.","fail":"Argues with her beliefs, loses control of the conversation, misses mania, or asks only about suicide."}},"c-u-21":{"setup":"Tuesday morning on the older adult ward. Mrs Maureen Cartwright, 68, came in overnight under Section 2 after police brought her in. She is furious. In about ten minutes, take a focused history of her beliefs and a targeted mental state, including risk.","role":{"who":"Mrs Maureen Cartwright, 68, female, retired dinner lady, lives alone","open":"Get away from me! I've been dragged here for nothing.","manner":"Loud and hostile at first; settles if spoken to calmly and not argued with. Flares and paces when asked about voices; calms again with an apology. Articulate and logical.","free":["Was brought in by police and has done nothing wrong","A rotten-egg smell in her house for about a year","Her neighbours are behind it and the police laugh at her","Wants to go home"],"ifasked":["Believes the neighbours buried cats in their garden, which causes the smell","Her cat Misty went missing last year; she is sure they took her","Thinks they now plan to poison her because she knows too much","Has written letters to them and the council; 100% certain","Eats only packaged food from a shop in the next town; sleeps badly","No voices, visions, taste or touch changes; no funny turns or blackouts","Too frightened to confront them; no weapons; no thoughts of self-harm","Oriented; wears reading glasses; no medical problems, no alcohol"],"worries":["Are you calling me mad?","When can I go home?","Will you tell the police I'm lying?"],"rules":["Shouts 'I'm not mad!' and stands up if asked about voices; sits again if the doctor apologises and moves on","If the doctor argues with the belief, refuses to say any more","Becomes tearful about Misty; settles if acknowledged"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mrs Cartwright?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Get away from me! I've been dragged here for nothing."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, that sounds really upsetting. I'm not here to make things worse.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"Hmph."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"I'd genuinely like to hear your side. Could we talk for about ten minutes? You choose the chair, and you can stop whenever you like.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"Ten minutes. Then I want to go home."},{"t":"0:28","band":"Her account","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Would you tell me what's been happening at home?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"The smell. Rotten eggs, in my own house, for a year now. It's them next door. I've written to them, I've written to the council, and the police just laugh at me."},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting, not being believed. When did you first notice the smell?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"Last autumn. Around when Misty went."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Misty?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"My cat. She was all I had. They took her. I know they did."},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. Losing her, living on your own, must have been awful. Thank you for telling me.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Nobody else has said sorry."},{"t":"1:13","band":"The belief","w":"D","s":"What do you think the neighbours are doing, and why you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"They've buried cats in their garden. That's the smell. And now they want to poison me, because I know too much."},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"What made you sure it's poison?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Why else would they bury cats? They want me gone before I tell anyone."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"From 0 to 100, how certain are you? And could anything else explain the smell, like the drains or the gas?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"A hundred. It's not the gas. It's them."},{"t":"1:48","band":"Perceptions","w":"D","s":"Does anyone else notice the smell when they visit? And does it come and go, or is it always there?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Nobody visits. It comes in waves, mostly at night."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Has your food tasted strange, or have you felt anything odd on your skin?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"No. I'm careful what I eat."},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"When people are under this much strain, they sometimes hear or see things others don't. Has that happened to you?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"I'm not mad! Why are you asking me that? (stands and paces)"},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, I'm not saying you are. I ask everyone so nothing's missed. Shall we leave that?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"(sits down) No voices. No visions. There."},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"Thank you. One more like it: have you ever had blackouts, funny turns, or a strange feeling rising from your stomach?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"Never. I'm healthy as a horse."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Mood and cognition","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been in yourself?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"Fine. I'm not sad. I'm angry. I want them brought to justice."},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"And sleeping and eating?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"Barely sleep, in case they come in the night. I only eat packets from the big shop in the next town. They've got at the local ones."},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"Just to check I've got things right: what's today's date, and where are we now?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"Tuesday the 3rd. And this is the hospital, the mental one, more's the pity."},{"t":"3:12","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Have you ever thought about going next door to confront them?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"Go round there? I'm terrified of them."},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"Do you keep anything at home to protect yourself, just in case?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:23","w":"P","s":"No. I'd never hurt anyone."},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"Has it ever got so bad you've thought of harming yourself, or wished you weren't here?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"No. I want to see them punished, not me."},{"t":"3:35","band":"Insight","w":"D","s":"Do you think any of this could be your nerves or your health?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"There's nothing wrong with me. They should be in here, not me."},{"t":"3:43","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Thank you for talking to me. I can see how frightened you've been, and how little sleep you've had.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"So you'll give me pills to say I'm mad, will you?"},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"No, I'm not calling you mad. Next we'd like blood tests and a brain scan to check your body, and we'll ask for your house to be checked for gas so we've ruled that out.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"You won't find gas."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"Then we'll know for sure. I'll ask the nurses to bring you sealed, packaged food. Would that help?","m":[7,9]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"Sealed food? Well. That's something."},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"And you have the right to appeal against being kept here and to have an advocate; the nurses will go through that again with you today. The team will see you at eleven. What questions do you have?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"When can I go home?"},{"t":"4:32","w":"D","s":"Not today, I'm afraid, but we'll talk about it at the meeting. I'll write down what you've told me in your words, so it's fair to you.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Make sure you do."},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"To sum up for my notes: you're certain the neighbours mean to poison you, the smell is real to you, you're not sleeping or eating much, and you don't feel unwell. Have I got that right?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"That's the first sensible thing anyone's said."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and offers a choice about how the talk will go","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges her anger and fear, and apologises when a question upsets her","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes content, onset, evidence and conviction of the persecutory belief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens smell, taste, touch, voices and visions without giving up after the outburst","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about blackouts or funny turns because of the smell","d":"Content"},{"c":"Covers mood, sleep, eating and orientation briefly","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about confronting the neighbours, weapons, self-harm and food refusal","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses insight with a direct question","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains next steps plainly: bloods, scan, gas check, sealed food","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Reminds her of her rights and an advocate, and answers her questions honestly","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises the key findings and checks them with her","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Calm, non-confronting; belief, all senses, seizures, mood, orientation, risk and insight covered in ten minutes, with a clear honest close.","borderline":"Gets the belief and risk but stops screening perceptions after the outburst, or misses insight or the gas question.","fail":"Argues with the belief, abandons the interview when she shouts, or never asks about risk."}},"c-u-47":{"setup":"Thursday, older adults ward. Bernard Kowalski, 74, is ready for discharge, but his wife has an injunction and he does not know. Tell him, explore his care needs, and agree a discharge plan in about ten minutes. No history or risk assessment.","role":{"who":"Mr Bernard Kowalski, 74, male, inpatient","open":"Yes. When can I go home?","manner":"Polite and a little foggy, then stunned and defensive at the news. Softens and becomes anxious and practical once he feels the doctor is on his side.","free":["His mood is much better and he is sleeping","He wants to go home","He does not like the idea of a care home"],"ifasked":["Vague about why he came in: 'some trouble at home'","Thinks he had a scan; cannot name his tablets","Admits his memory 'isn't great'","Drinks most days, 'loses it sometimes'","Believes Halina has been seeing someone","Washes and dresses himself; Halina did cooking, tablets, shopping and bills","Does not know his pension amount","Would like his own place with someone popping in"],"worries":["Where am I supposed to go?","Who's going to cook for me?","Can't I go to my son's?","I don't want to end up in a home"],"rules":["Goes silent for a moment after the news, then says 'Forty-eight years, over one argument'","Becomes angry if told he is violent as fact; settles if the doctor says it is not for them to judge","Only mentions the affair belief if asked how things are between him and his wife","Agrees to extra-care housing if it is explained with its benefits"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the ward psychiatrists. Are you Bernard Kowalski?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Yes. When can I go home?"},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"That's exactly what I'd like to talk about, Bernard. You've made real progress with your mood.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"I feel much better. Sleeping too."},{"t":"0:23","w":"D","s":"There's some difficult news about home too. Can we talk about what you'll need and plan it together? It'll take about ten minutes.","m":[1,3]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"What news?"},{"t":"0:37","band":"His understanding","w":"D","s":"I'll explain. First, can you tell me what you understand about why you came into hospital, and what's happened since?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Some trouble at home. The police came. I had a scan, I think. Some tablets."},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"You seem a bit unsure about some of it. Have you noticed problems with your memory?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"It's not what it was. Halina remembers everything for me."},{"t":"1:12","band":"The news","w":"D","s":"I'm afraid what I have to tell you is difficult. Halina has got a court order, which means you can't go back to the house. Tomasz and your daughter support her.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"(silence) A court order? Against me?"},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Yes. She and Tomasz have told us about drinking, and about violence over the years. I'm sorry. This must be a real shock.","m":[3,11]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"Forty-eight years... over one argument? I've never hurt her. Not really."},{"t":"1:57","band":"His reaction","w":"D","s":"I can hear how hard this is to take in. It's not for me to judge what happened; that's for the court. What are your thoughts on it?","m":[4,11]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"I lose it sometimes. When I've had a drink. Everybody does."},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"How much do you usually drink?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"Most days. Four or five cans. More at the weekend."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. How have things been between you and Halina lately?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"She's been seeing someone. I'm sure of it. That's what all this is about."},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"It sounds like that's been on your mind a lot. I'll make sure the team knows, and we can talk about it another time. Today I want to focus on where you'll live.","m":[4,11]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"So where am I supposed to go, then?"},{"t":"3:05","band":"Daily living","w":"D","s":"That's exactly what we'll sort out together. First, what did Halina do for you at home, and what did you do yourself?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"She did everything. Cooking, washing, shopping."},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"Can you wash and dress yourself? And who sorted your tablets?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"I wash and dress myself, always have. Tablets, she put them out for me."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"Have you ever cooked a meal, or done the washing or the shopping yourself?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"Toast. That's about it. I've never used the washing machine."},{"t":"3:51","band":"Money","w":"D","s":"Who looked after the bills and the bank? Do you know roughly what your pension is each month?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:01","w":"P","s":"Halina. I couldn't tell you what comes in. I just get cash when I need it."},{"t":"4:09","band":"His wishes","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you'll need a fair bit of help each day after you leave, with meals, tablets and money. If home isn't possible, where would you like to be?","m":[6,7]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"My own place, I suppose. With someone popping in. I don't want to die in some home."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"That's a really common worry, and no one will force you. What else worries you about leaving?","m":[7,11]},{"t":"4:43","w":"P","s":"Being on my own. Who's going to cook for me? Can't I just go to Tomasz's?"},{"t":"4:53","w":"D","s":"It's natural to want to be with family. Tomasz supports his mum's decision, so that isn't possible right now. I'll make sure you can stay in touch with him if he's happy to.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Right."},{"t":"5:11","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"Let me give you some options. A flat on your own with no help isn't safe yet, because cooking, tablets and money would all fall on you at once.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Fair enough."},{"t":"5:28","w":"D","s":"One option is sheltered or extra-care housing: your own flat and front door, staff on site, and carers for meals and tablets. The barrier is waiting lists, and some providers may hesitate because of what happened at home.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","s":"That sounds better than a home."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"The other is a residential home: the most support and company, and no chores. I know you're not keen, and you don't need nursing care, so it's a fallback. What do you think?","m":[8]},{"t":"6:08","w":"P","s":"The flat with staff. If they'll have me."},{"t":"6:12","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"Then that's what we'll aim for. Our social worker will look at what care you need and refer you to the council's housing team, if you agree. Our OT will see how you get on in the kitchen and with tablets.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:31","w":"P","s":"Yes, all right."},{"t":"6:33","w":"D","s":"We'll also speak to your solicitor and the police, because your bail says you live here, and that has to change first. And the alcohol nurse will come and see you.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:49","w":"P","s":"Do I have to see her? The alcohol one?"},{"t":"6:53","w":"D","s":"It's your choice, but drinking has played a big part in where you are now, so I'd really encourage it. So what happens next, in your words?","m":[9,12]},{"t":"7:07","w":"P","s":"The social worker, the kitchen test, the solicitor. And I can't go home."},{"t":"7:16","w":"D","s":"Exactly. We'll meet again at the discharge meeting next week, and you won't leave until there's a safe place ready. If you're struggling before then, tell the nurses.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"7:30","w":"P","s":"Thank you, doctor. It's a lot to take in."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, praises progress and gives a warning shot before the news","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks his understanding of the admission and enquires about memory","d":"Content"},{"c":"States clearly that a court order stops him returning home","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Elicits his response, drinking and feelings about his wife without a full risk assessment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses washing, dressing, tablets, cooking and chores","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses bills, banking and knowledge of his income","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names his level of need and elicits his wishes and worries","d":"Content"},{"c":"Offers at least two options, each with a benefit and a barrier, and asks his view","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names next steps and owners: social worker, housing, OT, solicitor and police, alcohol nurse","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Reassures he will not leave without a safe plan and gives a review date","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his shock and fears at least twice","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Breaks the news clearly and kindly, maps daily living and money precisely, hears his wishes, and agrees realistic options with named next steps including bail and housing.","borderline":"Breaks the news and offers options, but care needs are vague or options lack reasons, owners or the bail issue.","fail":"Avoids the injunction, turns it into a history or risk assessment, or sends him to a flat alone without support."}},"c-u-51":{"setup":"Thursday afternoon, PICU. Marcus Ferreira, 27, has been secluded for three days, refusing medication, observations and most fluids. Sean, the seclusion nurse, has bleeped you. Take a handover and agree a management plan with him.","role":{"who":"Sean Doyle, 34, male, staff nurse covering seclusion","open":"Yes, thanks for coming quickly.","manner":"Tired and worried; feels the team is stretched and a little guilty. Becomes calmer and more practical once the doctor commits to staffing and a clear plan.","free":["Marcus was admitted three days ago with a relapse of paranoid schizophrenia","He hit a nurse on the day of admission and has been secluded since","He has a headache and a dry mouth today","He refuses medication and observations"],"ifasked":["He stopped clozapine about a week before admission","He came in on Section 136 and is now on Section 3","About 600 mL on day 1, 300 mL on day 2, sips from the tap since","No urine for at least 12 hours","Cracked lips, lying down more, a bit muddled about the day","He will only accept sealed items; believes staff want his DNA","No observations, bloods or ECG this admission","Only two restraint-trained staff on shift"],"worries":["How are we meant to check him when he won't let anyone near?","What signs would tell you he's dehydrated?","If he needs A&E, how do we get him there safely?"],"rules":["Refuses to go in with too few trained staff; settles if the doctor offers to get more","Says he feels the team let Marcus get this bad; calms if validated","If the doctor suggests haloperidol, asks whether that is safe when he's dry"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Sean, covering seclusion?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Yes, thanks for coming quickly."},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"Could you give me the background, then what's worrying you about Marcus right now?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"He's barely drinking and won't let us near him."},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Let's go through it, then decide the plan together.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:28","band":"Admission and seclusion","w":"D","s":"What brought him in, and what's his legal status now?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Relapse of schizophrenia. Police brought him on a 136, and he's on a Section 3 now. He'd stopped his clozapine about a week before."},{"t":"0:53","w":"D","s":"And what led to seclusion?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"He punched a nurse on the first day, during an argument with another patient. It took several of us to get him into the room."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"When was his last medical review, and has the independent review happened?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Medical review this morning. The independent one was yesterday; they said keep going."},{"t":"1:31","w":"D","s":"How has he been since then? Still threatening anyone?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"Every time we open the hatch. He says nobody's taking his DNA without a fight."},{"t":"1:48","band":"Intake and output","w":"D","s":"Let's put numbers on the fluids. How much has he drunk each day?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Roughly 600 mL on day one, then 300, and only sips from the sink since."},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"When did he last pass urine?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Not for at least 12 hours. We can't measure it."},{"t":"2:17","w":"D","s":"And food?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Hardly anything. He won't touch anything that isn't sealed."},{"t":"2:28","band":"Symptoms and signs","w":"D","s":"Apart from the headache and dry mouth, has he seemed dizzy, muddled or sleepier than before?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"He's lying down a lot more. He asked me what day it was earlier, which isn't like him."},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"From the window, how do his lips and eyes look?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Lips are cracked. Eyes look a bit sunken, to be honest."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"Has anyone managed a pulse, blood pressure, bloods or an ECG this admission?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Nothing. He's refused everything."},{"t":"3:16","w":"D","s":"Then we have no physical baseline at all, and with that intake and no urine, he's very likely dehydrated. This is now a medical priority.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"I feel like we've let him get this bad."},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"I can hear that, and it shows you care. You've kept everyone safe and you called at the right time. Let's put it right now.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"But how are we meant to check him when he won't let anyone near?"},{"t":"3:57","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Fair question. First the least restrictive step: sealed bottles of water and packaged food, opened in front of him, by one nurse he knows. Ask him what he'd accept.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"He might take a sealed bottle. But he won't let us do obs."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"So we plan for that too. How many restraint-trained staff are on today?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:26","w":"P","s":"Two. I'm not going in like that."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Quite right, nobody goes in short-staffed. I'll ask the bleep holder for three more trained staff within half an hour.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"Then a planned check: full observations with a NEWS2 score, capillary glucose, bloods for U&E, CK, full blood count and liver tests, and an ECG.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"And if he fights it? Could we give him haloperidol?"},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"Not haloperidol. He's dry, has no ECG and was restrained, so the risk of neuroleptic malignant syndrome and heart rhythm problems is higher. IM lorazepam, 1 to 2 mg.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:22","w":"D","s":"After it, obs every 15 minutes for the first hour, and we do the bloods and ECG while he's settled.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:33","w":"P","s":"Okay. And if the results are bad?"},{"t":"5:37","band":"Transfer","w":"D","s":"If NEWS2 is 5 or more, he's more confused, or his kidneys or CK are off, he goes to A&E for IV fluids.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"How on earth do we get him there safely?"},{"t":"5:54","w":"D","s":"Ambulance, two trained escorts, Section 17 leave from his consultant, his section papers and drug chart. I'll phone the medical team first. Police only if risk is very high.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:11","w":"P","s":"That feels doable if we get the staff."},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Let me check we've got it the same: sealed drinks now, three more staff, then obs, bloods and ECG, with lorazepam if needed. Does that match what you'd planned?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:29","w":"P","s":"Yes. Sealed drinks now, staff by half four, then the check."},{"t":"6:36","w":"D","s":"I'll ring Dr Okafor now and the bleep holder straight after, write it in the seclusion record, and be back at half four for the check.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:50","w":"D","s":"If he becomes drowsy, collapses or fits before then, call 999 and bleep me. Can you start a fluid chart?","m":[12]},{"t":"7:00","w":"P","s":"Will do. Thanks, that's much clearer."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and agrees to focus on the urgent concern first","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes admission, clozapine stopped, legal status and reason for seclusion","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about threats and behaviour since seclusion began","d":"Content"},{"c":"Quantifies daily fluid intake, food and time since last urine","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks for symptoms and signs of dehydration, including confusion","d":"Content"},{"c":"Notes no observations, bloods or ECG and names likely dehydration","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Offers sealed fluids first and asks what he would accept","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans enough trained staff, NEWS2 observations, U&E, CK and ECG","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Chooses IM lorazepam, explains NMS risk and sets 15-minute monitoring","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Sets transfer triggers and a safe route: escorts, Section 17, medical handover","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates the nurse's worry and plans collaboratively","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks shared plan, informs consultant, documents, sets return time and safety-nets","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Numbers on intake and output, likely dehydration named, a staffed plan for checks with lorazepam, clear transfer triggers, and the consultant told.","borderline":"Recognises dehydration but relies on persuasion, or is vague on drug choice, staffing or how transfer would work.","fail":"Keeps encouraging fluids without a plan, gives IM antipsychotics to a dry man with no ECG, or ignores the nurse's staffing concerns."}},"c-u-55":{"setup":"Wednesday morning, memory clinic. Leonard Harper, 79, is being assessed by a colleague next door. His wife Gwen is worried because he has wandered and got lost. Take a collateral history, form an initial impression and explain the next steps.","role":{"who":"Mrs Gwen Harper, 76, female, Leonard's wife","open":"Yes, Leonard's wife.","manner":"Warm, organised and talkative, but tired and close to tears. Becomes tearful when asked about herself; relieved and grateful once given a clear plan.","free":["Leonard has been forgetful for about a year","He got lost recently and neighbours brought him home","She is worried about his safety"],"ifasked":["Gradual change; repeats questions; loses keys; old memories clear","Word-finding problems; can't use the remote; she took over the bills","Lost twice this month, once at 10 pm, heading to his old depot","Left a pan on the gas; still drives to the shop and scraped the gatepost","Nearly gave bank details to a phone caller","Quieter, gave up allotment; no hallucinations, falls or tremor","Amlodipine and a bladder tablet (oxybutynin); two pints on Sunday; his sister had dementia","She is exhausted and up at night listening for the door"],"worries":["So is it dementia?","Should I lock him in when I go out?","Is there a tablet that can help?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when asked about herself; settles with validation","Mentions the bladder tablet only if asked about all his medicines","Says he will not give up driving; accepts it if the doctor offers to raise it with him"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the memory clinic. Are you Mrs Gwen Harper?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Yes, Leonard's wife."},{"t":"0:11","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming, Mrs Harper. Leonard has agreed for us to talk; he's with my colleague now, so we have about ten minutes.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"Good, he'd be cross otherwise."},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"I understand you're worried about him getting lost. Could you tell me when you first noticed changes?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"About a year ago. He started asking the same thing over and over."},{"t":"0:48","band":"Memory","w":"D","s":"Has it come on gradually, or in sudden drops?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","s":"Gradually. A bit worse every few months."},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"Is it recent things he forgets, or things from long ago too?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"Recent. He loses his keys every day. But he can tell you all about his apprenticeship."},{"t":"1:18","band":"Other cognitive areas","w":"D","s":"Does he struggle to find words or name things?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"He calls everything 'the thingy' now."},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"How does he manage gadgets, money, and recognising people?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"Can't work the remote. I took over the bills six months ago. He knows all the family."},{"t":"1:49","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"Talk me through the times he went missing. When, and how was he found?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"Twice this month. He walked towards his old depot. Once it was ten at night. The neighbours brought him back."},{"t":"2:09","w":"D","s":"That must have been frightening. Any problems with cooking or the gas?","m":[4,12]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"He left a pan on once. The smoke alarm went off."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Is he still driving?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"Short trips to the shop. He scraped the gatepost last week. He won't stop; it's the last thing he's got."},{"t":"2:41","w":"D","s":"I hear how hard that would be for him. Any odd phone calls or people at the door?","m":[4,12]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"He nearly gave his bank details to a caller. I grabbed the phone."},{"t":"3:00","band":"Behaviour and mood","w":"D","s":"Has his personality or mood changed?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"He's quieter. Gave up his allotment. Snappy if I correct him."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Has he seen things that aren't there, acted out his dreams, had a tremor or falls?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"3:28","band":"Medical and family","w":"D","s":"Any strokes, heart problems, high blood pressure or diabetes?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"Blood pressure. No strokes."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"What tablets does he take, all of them?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Amlodipine. And one for his waterworks, oxy-something. Since last year."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Oxybutynin, probably. That's useful; it can affect memory, so I'll ask his GP to review it. How much does he drink?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"Two pints on a Sunday."},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"Anyone in the family with memory problems?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:17","w":"P","s":"His sister had dementia in her eighties."},{"t":"4:23","band":"Daily living and carer","w":"D","s":"Can he wash and dress himself? Any problems with continence?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:31","w":"P","s":"If I lay his clothes out. No accidents."},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"And how is all this affecting you?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"I'm so tired. I'm up half the night listening for the door. Should I just lock him in when I pop out?"},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting, and you've been doing it alone. I understand why you'd think of locking the door, but there's a fire risk. A door sensor and a GPS tracker are safer.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"5:13","w":"P","s":"I didn't know you could get those."},{"t":"5:16","band":"Impression and next steps","w":"D","s":"From what you've told me, it sounds like a memory illness, possibly Alzheimer's disease.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"So is it dementia?"},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"It may well be. His tests today, the GP's bloods and a brain scan will help us be sure. We'll see you both in about four weeks for the results.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Is there a tablet that can help?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"If it is Alzheimer's, there are medicines like donepezil that can help memory for a while. We'd discuss them once it's confirmed.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"For now, please fill in a Herbert Protocol form for the police. I'll refer to social care for the sensor, a gas valve and a carer's assessment for you.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:18","w":"D","s":"He shouldn't drive until we're done. I'll raise that with him today, so it isn't left to you.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Thank you. That's a weight off."},{"t":"6:34","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the main things you'll do this week?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"The Herbert form, no driving, and wait for social care to call."},{"t":"6:47","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Here's our number if anything changes. If he goes missing, call 999 straight away. What else would you like to ask?","m":[13]},{"t":"7:00","w":"P","s":"Nothing. That's the first time anyone's asked how I am."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms consent and agrees the focus","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes onset, gradual course, and recent versus remote memory","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens language, gadgets, money and recognition","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about wandering, gas, driving and financial vulnerability","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens personality, mood, hallucinations, dream enactment, tremor and falls","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes vascular, full medicines, alcohol and family history; flags oxybutynin","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about washing, dressing and continence","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about the carer's own wellbeing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives an initial impression of probable Alzheimer's without overstating","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains tests, scan, results appointment and possible medication","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives specific safety steps: Herbert Protocol, telecare, gas, no driving, carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates her worry and exhaustion","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding, gives contact number and safety-nets","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Clear onset and course, other domains and function, all four safety risks, other-dementia screen and medicines, carer's wellbeing, and specific next steps.","borderline":"Good memory history but misses safety risks, medicines or the carer, or gives vague next steps.","fail":"Calls it normal ageing, misses driving and wandering, or gives no impression or plan."}},"c-u-64":{"setup":"Wednesday afternoon, outpatients. Gareth Pugh, 52, has bipolar disorder on lithium 1000 mg at night. His GP reports low T3 and T4 with raised TSH. In about ten minutes, ask about thyroid symptoms, explain the results and examine him. No bipolar history.","role":{"who":"Mr Gareth Pugh, 52, male, patient","open":"That's right.","manner":"Cooperative and quiet; answers what is asked. Worried that lithium will be stopped and that he is becoming depressed again. Relaxes once told it is treatable.","free":["Tired all the time","Wonders if it's the lithium","Worried he'll have to stop lithium"],"ifasked":["Gained about 5 kg in 4 months","Always feels cold; hands cold","Dry skin and hair","Constipated lately","Feels flat; no felt that life isn't worth living","Mum took a thyroid tablet","Takes ibuprofen sometimes for his knee","No neck swelling, swallowing difficulty or voice change"],"worries":["Have I got to come off the lithium?","Is there a lump? Is it cancer?","Is this why I've been so tired?"],"rules":["Follows all instructions during the physical check","Becomes worried if told about a swelling; settles with a clear explanation","Says he was dreading another depression; settles if validated","Findings: pulse 56 regular, dry skin, small smooth goitre, slow-relaxing ankle reflexes; no eye signs"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Gareth Pugh?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"That's right."},{"t":"0:13","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Gareth. Your GP sent us some blood results about your thyroid, so I'd like to ask a few questions and then check your neck. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"Sure. Is it the lithium?"},{"t":"0:36","w":"D","s":"It may well be, and I'll explain shortly. Would you like a chaperone? We have about ten minutes.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's fine."},{"t":"0:52","band":"Symptoms","w":"D","s":"Have you been more tired than usual?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"Shattered. For months."},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"Any change in your weight, or feeling the cold?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"Five kilos on, and I'm not eating more. And I'm always cold."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"Any change in your skin, hair or bowels?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Dry skin. Bit bunged up lately."},{"t":"1:37","w":"D","s":"How has your mood and energy been? Any felt that life isn't worth living?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"Flat. Nothing like that. But I thought I was sliding into depression again. I've been dreading it."},{"t":"2:04","w":"D","s":"That must have been worrying. A slow thyroid can make you feel low and flat, so treating it may lift a lot of that.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"Any swelling in your neck, trouble swallowing or a hoarse voice?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"Any thyroid problems in your family? And any other medicines, including from the chemist?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"Mum took a thyroid tablet. I take ibuprofen sometimes for my knee."},{"t":"2:56","band":"The results","w":"D","s":"Thanks. The thyroid is a small gland at the front of your neck that sets your body's speed. Your bloods show it's running slow.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:11","w":"D","s":"TSH is the brain's signal telling the gland to work harder. It's high because the gland isn't keeping up. Does that make sense?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"So it's underactive. Have I got to come off the lithium?"},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"Lithium commonly causes this, yes. But usually we treat the thyroid with a daily tablet and keep the lithium, because it keeps you well.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"That's a relief."},{"t":"3:55","band":"Hands and pulse","w":"D","s":"Let me examine you now. Can you hold your hands out for me?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"They're always cold now."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"Cool and a bit dry. No tremor. I'm feeling your pulse now. Fifty-six and regular, a little slow.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"I'm just looking at your face and hair too. Your skin's a bit dry and your face slightly puffy.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:43","band":"The neck","w":"D","s":"I'll look at your neck from the front. Take a sip of water and hold it. Now swallow.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"(swallows)"},{"t":"5:02","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Now stick your tongue out for me.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"(does so)"},{"t":"5:13","w":"D","s":"I'll stand behind you to feel the gland. Tip your chin down slightly. Tell me if anything is tender.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"No, that's fine."},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"Swallow again for me, please.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:38","w":"D","s":"Now I'm feeling the glands down each side of your neck, and checking your windpipe is in the middle.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:53","w":"P","s":"Is there a lump? Is it cancer?"},{"t":"5:59","w":"D","s":"I can see why that's frightening. The whole gland is a little enlarged but smooth and even, with no separate lumps. That's typical of lithium.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:18","w":"D","s":"I'll just listen over it with my stethoscope. Nothing unusual there.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:29","band":"Eyes and reflexes","w":"D","s":"Look straight ahead for me. Now follow my finger down slowly, keeping your head still.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:43","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"6:47","w":"D","s":"Eyes look normal. Now kneel up on this chair, feet dangling off the back. I'll tap your ankle tendons.","m":[12]},{"t":"7:00","w":"P","s":"(kneels)"},{"t":"7:04","w":"D","s":"Thank you. And a quick look at your shins. All done, have a seat.","m":[12]},{"t":"7:15","band":"Findings and plan","w":"D","s":"Your pulse is a little slow, the gland is slightly enlarged but smooth, and your ankle reflexes relax slowly. All of that fits a slow thyroid.","m":[13]},{"t":"7:36","w":"P","s":"Is this why I've been so tired?"},{"t":"7:42","w":"D","s":"Very likely. Today I'll check your lithium level, kidneys, calcium and thyroid antibodies, do a heart tracing because of the slow pulse, and write to your GP about a thyroid tablet.","m":[13]},{"t":"8:05","w":"D","s":"Please swap ibuprofen for paracetamol, as ibuprofen raises lithium. If you get sickness, diarrhoea, a shaky hand or feel unsteady or muddled, get help that day.","m":[13]},{"t":"8:26","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what happens next?","m":[13]},{"t":"8:32","w":"P","s":"Bloods and heart test today, GP about a thyroid tablet, stay on lithium, no ibuprofen."},{"t":"8:45","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Thanks, Gareth.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose, gains consent and offers a chaperone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks tiredness, weight, cold intolerance, skin, hair and bowels","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about mood and checks briefly for felt that life isn't worth living","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about neck swelling, swallowing, voice, family history and other medicines","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the results and TSH in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Links it to lithium, says it is treatable, and addresses his fears with empathy","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Examines hands, pulse rate and rhythm, skin and face","d":"Content"},{"c":"Inspects the neck with a swallow and with tongue protrusion","d":"Content"},{"c":"Palpates from behind with a swallow, checks nodes and trachea","d":"Content"},{"c":"Auscultates the thyroid for a bruit","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks eyes for lid retraction, lid lag and eye movements","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests ankle reflexes with him kneeling and inspects shins","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives findings and plan, safety-nets lithium toxicity, and checks understanding","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Focused symptoms, plain-words explanation, an ordered physical check including swallow, palpation from behind and ankle reflexes, and a clear plan keeping lithium.","borderline":"Physical check done but rushed or out of order, or results explained in jargon.","fail":"Runs out of time before the neck or reflexes, takes a bipolar history, or advises stopping lithium."}},"wc-01":{"setup":"Monday 09:00, CMHT outpatient clinic. Your consultant is on leave and you are running seven 30-minute slots alone. Mr Holt, 52, on lithium, has no level for nine months; the 10:00 patient, 22, from supported housing, is not here. Run it safely.","role":{"who":"Mr Gary Holt, 52, man with bipolar disorder on lithium (later on the phone: Ms Linda Grant, supported housing manager for the 10:00 patient, Callum, 22)","open":"Morning. Is Dr Ellis not in? I usually see her.","manner":"Friendly but rushed, keeps checking the time. Becomes upset and a bit cross when he realises his blood tests were missed; settles when the doctor owns it and makes a clear plan. Ms Grant is defensive, then relieved.","free":["Takes Priadel 800 mg every night at about 9 pm and has been well for two years","Works as a warehouse supervisor and needs to be back by 10:30","Has a long-standing slight tremor in his hands","(Ms Grant) Two staff are off sick and nobody was free to bring Callum"],"ifasked":["Last blood test was at the GP surgery 'before Christmas'; nobody rang with a result","Has taken ibuprofen 400 mg most days for three weeks for his back, bought at the supermarket","No diarrhoea, vomiting, unsteadiness or confusion; the tremor is no worse","Drinks plenty of water; no new prescribed tablets","(Ms Grant) Callum was told about today on Friday and said he wanted to come","(Ms Grant) He has been staying in his room and missing meals for two weeks","(Ms Grant) A new 'friend' from outside has been visiting him and staff saw him hand over cash"],"worries":["Why did nobody check my bloods? Is my kidney damaged?","I can't be late back to work.","(Ms Grant) Are you going to report us?"],"rules":["Becomes upset about the missed bloods; calms if the doctor acknowledges the lapse without blaming anyone","Mentions the ibuprofen only if asked about painkillers or shop-bought tablets","Agrees to bloods today once told it takes ten minutes and the timing is right","Ms Grant mentions the cash and the new friend only if asked what has changed lately"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Before we open, I've been through the list. Mr Holt's lithium level is nine months overdue, there are two new referrals this afternoon, and Callum at ten relies on staff to bring him. Who's the duty worker today, and who's my senior cover?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","who":"Admin","s":"Sam Okoro is duty. Dr Patel is covering for Dr Ellis, on her mobile. And Callum's housing aren't picking up yet."},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Could you book Mr Holt a phlebotomy slot this morning? I'll keep ten minutes at the end for letters and calls.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Holt? I'm Dr Shah, one of the psychiatry doctors. Dr Ellis is on leave this week, so you've got me. We have about half an hour. What would you most like us to sort out today?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Morning. Is Dr Ellis not in? I usually see her. Honestly, I just want my repeat sorted. I need to be back at work by half ten."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"That's fine, we'll get you away by then. Repeat prescription is first on the list. I'd also like to check your lithium blood test, as I can't see one recently. Is that okay?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Fine by me."},{"t":"1:25","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"When did you last have a lithium blood test, and where?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"At the GP. Before Christmas, I think. Nobody rang, so I assumed it was fine."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"So that's about nine months. The plan is a check every six months once you're settled, so it's overdue.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Nine months? Hang on. Isn't someone supposed to be checking that? I've done everything I was told."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"You have, and you're right to be annoyed. That's our system letting you down, not you. I'm sorry. Let's put it right today.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Okay. Sorry. It's just my dad had kidney trouble. Is my kidney damaged?"},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"That's a really understandable worry. Most people are fine, and the test will tell us. It checks your kidneys as well as the lithium. How have you been feeling physically?","m":[6,4]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Fine really. Back's been bad."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Any new shakiness, diarrhoea, being sick, feeling unsteady or muddled?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"No. My hands shake a bit, but they always have. No worse."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Good. Are you taking anything new, prescribed or from the shop, like painkillers or water tablets?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Just ibuprofen for my back. Two a day, most days, for about three weeks."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me, that's really important. Ibuprofen can push lithium up to unsafe levels. Any blood pressure tablets or water tablets from the GP?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing else."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"And what time did you take your lithium last night?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Nine o'clock, like always. Eight hundred."},{"t":"3:10","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Then a blood test now is spot on: about twelve hours after the dose. We'll check the lithium level, your kidneys, thyroid and calcium. Admin has booked you in this morning. It takes ten minutes.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"I hate needles, but go on then. When will I know?"},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"I'll look at the result tomorrow afternoon and ring you by Wednesday. If anything needs changing, I'll ring sooner.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:40","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Please stop the ibuprofen from today. Paracetamol is safe for your back. Keep taking your Priadel, eight hundred milligrams every night, no change.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Right. Paracetamol, no ibuprofen. Same lithium."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Lithium can creep up quietly. If you get sickness, diarrhoea, new shakiness, feel unsteady or muddled, go to A&E that day and tell them you're on lithium.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"A&E, not wait for you. Got it."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Just so I can check I've explained it clearly, can you tell me the plan in your words?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Blood test now. No ibuprofen, paracetamol instead. Same dose. You ring Wednesday. A&E if I'm sick or wobbly."},{"t":"4:25","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll write to you with all that, and copy your GP. Your repeat is done. Thanks for your patience today.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Thanks, Doctor. Better than nobody noticing."},{"t":"4:35","band":"Missed patient","w":"D","s":"Hello, is that Linda Grant? I'm Dr Shah at the CMHT. Callum was booked with me at ten and wasn't brought. How is he today?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","who":"Housing manager","s":"He's fine. Look, we're two staff down. Nobody was free. Are you going to report us?"},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds really stretched, and I'm not blaming you. He relies on you to get here, so I record it as was-not-brought, not as a missed choice. Was he told about today?","m":[6,11]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","who":"Housing manager","s":"Friday. He said he wanted to come."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Has anything changed with him lately: eating, sleeping, mood, who he sees?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","who":"Housing manager","s":"He's been in his room, missing meals, about two weeks. And there's a new friend visiting. Staff saw him hand over cash."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's important, and I'm worried about it. Sam Okoro, our duty worker, will ring you this afternoon to rebook within two weeks with a named staff member to bring him. We'll also discuss a safeguarding concern with the local authority. Does that work?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","who":"Housing manager","s":"Yes. Honestly, I'm glad someone's asking."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Letter to Mr Holt, copy to GP. Dear Mr Holt, thank you for coming today. Your lithium blood test was overdue, so we took it this morning. Please stop ibuprofen and use paracetamol instead. Lithium carbonate, Priadel, eight hundred milligrams, 800 mg, each night: no change. Please ask for a lithium level every six months. If you are sick, have diarrhoea, feel unsteady or muddled, go to A&E the same day.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Second note to GP: Callum was not brought today, not a did-not-attend. Duty worker following up today, safeguarding being discussed. Please don't discharge. I'll brief Dr Patel at lunch and Dr Ellis on her return.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","who":"Admin","s":"Sam's on the phone to the housing now. Your next one's here."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Brilliant, thank you. Please ask Sam to put a note in Callum's record when it's done.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Reviews the list before starting, flags the overdue lithium and dependent adult, and confirms senior cover and duty worker","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Introduces self, explains consultant absence, and agrees the patient's top priority in the first two minutes","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes when and where the last level was taken and the timing of last night's dose","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about toxicity: diarrhoea, vomiting, new tremor, unsteadiness and confusion","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about prescribed and shop-bought drugs that raise lithium, including ibuprofen","d":"Content"},{"c":"Acknowledges the missed monitoring and the carer's strain without blaming anyone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Arranges a 12-hour level with U&E/eGFR, thyroid and calcium, and gives a date for the result","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Stops ibuprofen, offers paracetamol, and confirms the lithium dose is unchanged","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking him to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets toxicity signs and same-day A&E","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Records was-not-brought, asks what has changed, and hands same-day follow-up to a named duty worker with safeguarding considered","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Dictates a plain-English letter copied to the GP, with the dose in words and figures","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes each contact with clear next steps and plans to brief the consultants","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Triages first, gets a correctly timed lithium level and full bloods, finds and stops the ibuprofen, safety-nets toxicity, and treats the missed patient as was-not-brought with named same-day follow-up.","borderline":"Safe bloods and follow-up, but misses the ibuprofen, gives no result date, or writes a vague letter.","fail":"Codes the 10:00 patient as DNA and discharges him, skips the lithium level, or overruns so later patients lose their slots."}},"wc-02":{"setup":"Thursday 14:00, CMHT meeting room. You are chairing the care plan review for Daniel Price, 41, with schizophrenia, on a monthly injection since his section 3 ended last year. His care coordinator and his mother, 68, are here. Run the review.","role":{"who":"Mr Daniel Price, 41, man with schizophrenia on a monthly long-acting injection (with his mother, Mrs Margaret Price, 68, and his care coordinator, Jo Reid)","open":"Before we start, I want to say I'm stopping the injection. I want to get a job.","manner":"Direct and a little guarded, expects to be told off. Opens up when his goals are taken seriously. His mother is polite, then tearful when asked how she is coping; she relaxes once offered help.","free":["Wants to stop the injection and get a job, ideally working outdoors","The injection makes him sleepy every morning and he has put on about two stone","Has been well for over a year","(Mother) She is exhausted and not sleeping"],"ifasked":["Stopped his tablets two years ago; within about three months he stopped sleeping, thought neighbours were filming him, and was admitted on section 3","Early signs: sleeping badly, going quiet and not eating, staying in his room (mother notices first)","No thoughts of harming himself or others; no drugs; a few beers at weekends","Smokes 15 cigarettes a day; has not had a physical health check this year","Used to work in a garden centre and would like that sort of work again","(Mother) Nobody has ever offered her a carer's assessment","Happy for his mother and GP to have a copy of the plan"],"worries":["If I tell you about side effects, you'll just put me back in hospital.","Do I have to pay for all this help now I'm off the section?","(Mother) What happens to him when I can't cope any more?"],"rules":["Becomes defensive if told he must stay on the injection; engages if told it is his decision","Agrees to try a change rather than stop if his complaints are taken seriously","Mother becomes tearful when asked how she is coping; settles if validated","Gives the history of the last relapse only if asked what happened when he stopped before"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello everyone, I'm Dr Shah, one of the psychiatry doctors. Daniel, thanks for coming. Mrs Price, Jo, welcome. We've got about forty minutes to review your care plan, Daniel. Are you happy for your mum to stay for all of it?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Yeah, she can stay. Before we start, I want to say I'm stopping the injection. I want to get a job."},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"Thanks for saying it straight away. Both of those matter. Could we do your goals first, then the injection, then the crisis plan, then support for your mum? Does that suit you?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"0:50","band":"His goals","w":"D","s":"What would make the next six months go well for you?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"A proper job. Outdoors. I used to work at a garden centre. I'm sick of sitting at home."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"So in your words: a proper outdoor job, like the garden centre. I'll write that at the top of your plan.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Good. That's a first."},{"t":"1:15","band":"Medication","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the injection. What do you like and dislike about it?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"I'm wiped out every morning. And I've put on two stone. Who's going to hire me like this?"},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Being tired every morning and gaining weight would get anyone down, and I can see why it feels like it's blocking the job. Has it helped with anything?","m":[3,9]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"I suppose I've not been ill. But if I tell you about side effects you'll just put me back in hospital."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"I can see why you'd worry. Side effects are a reason to adjust treatment, not to admit you. You're not on a section or a CTO, so whether you take it is your decision. I want to make sure you have all the facts.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"What happened the last time your medication stopped?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"I stopped the tablets two years ago. About three months later I wasn't sleeping. I thought the neighbours were filming me. That's when I got sectioned."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you. That pattern matters: stopping is the strongest sign of becoming unwell again. So rather than stop outright today, could we try one change first? I'll ask Dr Ellis to review you within two weeks to lower the dose, and we'll check your weight and bloods.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"A lower dose. I'd try that. As long as stopping's still on the table."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"It is. If you still want to stop after that, we'll plan a slow reduction with closer contact, never a sudden stop.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:15","band":"Risk and crisis plan","w":"D","s":"What are the early signs that you're becoming unwell, and who notices first?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Mum does. Ask her."},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"He stops sleeping. Then he goes quiet and stops eating. He stays in his room."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"That's really useful. So: poor sleep, going quiet and off food, staying in his room. If you see those, who should you ring, Daniel?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","who":"Care coordinator","s":"Jo in the day, the crisis line at night. Jo's got the number on my card."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Good. Daniel, any thoughts of self-harm or anyone else? Any drugs or heavy drinking?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"No. A few beers at the weekend. That's it."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Smoking, and when did you last have a physical health check?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Fifteen a day. Not had a check this year."},{"t":"4:15","band":"Work and support","w":"D","s":"We'll do your weight, blood pressure, sugar and cholesterol today, and offer help with smoking. Back to work: would you like an employment specialist? They help you find a real job and support you once you're in it.","m":[6,7]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Yeah. If they can get me something outdoors."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"I'll refer you this week, Jo, if you can follow it up. We'll check your benefits too before you start.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Mrs Price, you've been listening. How are you coping?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Honestly? I'm not sleeping. I'm worn out. And I worry what happens to him when I can't manage any more."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"You've been carrying a lot, and it shows how much you care. Being exhausted after all this isn't a failing. Has anyone offered you a carer's assessment?","m":[9,8]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"No. Nobody's ever asked about me."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"You have your own right to one from social services. With your agreement I'll refer you today and give you the carers' centre number.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","who":"Mother","s":"Thank you. Yes please."},{"t":"5:35","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Daniel, one more thing. Because you were on section 3, your aftercare is free under section 117. That includes Jo, the employment support, and the medication reviews. It continues until health and social care agree with you that you don't need it.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"So I don't pay anything? Good."},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"Could you sum up what we've agreed, so I know it's clear?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Job outdoors, employment person. Dr Ellis looks at a lower dose in two weeks. Mum and Jo watch for sleep and going quiet. Mum gets her own assessment. And it's free."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Spot on. Are you happy for us to share the plan with your mum and GP?","m":[11,1]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Yes."},{"t":"6:30","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"If any of those early signs appear before then, ring Jo or the crisis line the same day. We'll review everything in three months. I'll put the date in today, sooner if anything changes.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you all. Daniel, I'll send you a copy of the plan by next week.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces everyone, agrees the agenda, and checks consent for his mother to stay and for sharing the plan","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Records his main six-month goal in his own words","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores what he likes and dislikes about the injection","d":"Content"},{"c":"Respects his right to decide, asks about the last relapse, and agrees one change to try instead of stopping abruptly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Writes three early warning signs and who to ring into the crisis plan","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about harm to self or others, substances and physical health","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Offers IPS employment support and a benefits check","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks his mother how she is coping and refers her for a carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his frustration with side effects and his mother's exhaustion","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains section 117 aftercare is free and how it ends","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking him to summarise the plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets early signs and closes with a dated review in 3 months","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Puts his goals first, respects his choice while making the relapse history clear, agrees a change to try, a written relapse plan, IPS referral, a carer's assessment and free s117 aftercare, with a dated review.","borderline":"Safe plan, but the meeting is doctor-led, the mother's needs are brushed past, or s117 is not explained.","fail":"Tells him he must stay on the injection, or stops it today without a plan, and ignores work, carer strain or the crisis plan."}},"wc-03":{"setup":"Wednesday 11:30, outside a house. Keith Morgan, 58, recurrent depression, lives alone with a large dog, has missed two appointments and calls. Old alert: verbal aggression when drunk. A community nurse is with you. Visit safely, assess risk and decide next steps.","role":{"who":"Mr Keith Morgan, 58, man with recurrent depression who lives alone (with the community nurse, Priya, and the team base on the phone)","open":"Who is it? I never asked anyone to come.","manner":"Gruff and wary at the door, smells of alcohol but is not drunk. Softens when the doctor is calm and mentions the dog. Tearful about his brother; becomes cooperative once he feels listened to.","free":["Hasn't felt like answering the phone or going out","The dog, Bruno, is 'all I've got'","Has a few cans to help him sleep","His older brother died in the spring"],"ifasked":["Drinks six to eight cans of strong lager a day; no withdrawal shakes in the morning","Eats once a day at most; the fridge has milk and cans","Some days thinks everyone would be better off without him; has thought of taking all his tablets","Has a carrier bag of saved sertraline and paracetamol in the bedside drawer; has not set a date or written a note","Stopped taking his sertraline about a month ago","His grandson, 9, used to visit on Saturdays but has not come for a month","Smokes in the armchair; there are burn marks on it; uses a plug-in heater"],"worries":["You're not taking my tablets.","Are you going to put me in hospital?","Who'll look after Bruno?"],"rules":["Raises his voice if rushed or lectured; settles if the doctor slows down and stays polite","Becomes tearful talking about his brother; settles if validated","Admits the stored tablets only if asked directly about tablets at home","Agrees to hand over the tablets and see the crisis team if Bruno can stay with him"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Before the door","w":"D","s":"Hi, it's Dr Shah. Priya and I are at 14 Elm Road for Keith Morgan. Time in is 11:30. I'll call you by 12:30. If you hear 'can you check the blue file', send help.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","who":"Base","s":"Got it. 14 Elm Road, 11:30, call by 12:30, blue file. Ring if you're worried."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"Priya, I'll sit nearest the door. If voices rise or anyone else turns up, we leave. Okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Agreed. Phones on, keys in pocket."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Morgan? I'm Dr Shah from the mental health team, and this is Priya, a nurse. We were worried because we hadn't heard from you. Can we come in for a few minutes?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Who is it? I never asked anyone to come."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Fair enough, it's a surprise to see us. We came because we care how you are. Ten minutes, and we'll go if you want. Could you pop the dog in the kitchen while we talk?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"He's alright. Bruno, kitchen. Go on. Come in then. He's all I've got, that dog."},{"t":"1:30","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"He's clearly important to you. Thank you. How have things been lately?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Rubbish. I don't answer the phone. Don't go out. What's the point."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"That sounds heavy. Has something happened recently?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"My brother died. In April. He was the one I talked to. Now there's no one. (voice cracks)"},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry about your brother. Losing the person you talked to leaves a real gap. It makes sense things have felt this bad.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"(wipes eyes) Yeah. Sorry."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"No need to be sorry. Take your time. How are you eating and sleeping?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Once a day, maybe. Sleep's rubbish. A few cans help."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"How many cans on a typical day?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Six. Eight on a bad one. Strong ones. I don't shake in the morning, mind."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Alcohol can help you sleep at first but it deepens low mood. Are you still taking your sertraline?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Stopped about a month ago. Didn't see the point."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When people feel this low, they sometimes have thoughts of ending their life. Have you had thoughts like that?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Some days I think everyone would be better off without me."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. Have you thought about how you might do it, or got anything ready?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"I've thought about taking all my tablets."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Do you have tablets stored up at home?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"There's a bag in the bedside drawer. Sertraline. Paracetamol. I haven't set a date or anything. No note."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad you told me. What's kept you going so far?","m":[4,9]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Bruno. And my grandson, though he's not been round for a month. He used to come Saturdays."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Does anyone else live here or stay over? Does your grandson stay?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"No one lives here. He's nine. Just came for his tea."},{"t":"4:05","band":"The home","w":"D","s":"Would you mind Priya having a quick look in the kitchen and bedroom while we talk? We check everyone's home is safe.","m":[5]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Go on. Nothing to see."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I noticed burn marks on the armchair. Do you smoke in that chair?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Sometimes. Fall asleep there with the heater on."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"That worries me, with the drinking. The fire service do free home safety checks and smoke alarms. Can we ask them?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"If it's free."},{"t":"4:45","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'm worried about that bag of tablets while you feel this low. Could Priya take it back to the pharmacy today?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"You're not taking my tablets. Are you putting me in hospital?"},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"No, I'm not planning hospital. And you'll still get the tablets you need, just a week at a time. Bruno stays with you. Could the crisis team visit you this evening and each day this week?","m":[6,8]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"If Bruno stays. Alright. Take the bag."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's a brave step. I'll ask your GP to restart your sertraline with weekly scripts, and to check your bloods. Would you see the alcohol service?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Maybe. One thing at a time."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"That's fair. Can you tell me what's happening today, so I know I've been clear?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Priya takes the bag. Crisis team tonight. GP for tablets. Fire people check the house."},{"t":"5:55","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If the thoughts get stronger before they arrive, ring the crisis line, or 111 and choose option two, or 999. Here's the number on a card.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Okay. On the fridge."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"We'll see ourselves out. Thank you for letting us in, Keith.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Base, it's Dr Shah. We're out, safe, 12:15. Crisis team referral for tonight. Visit complete.","m":[1,10]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","who":"Base","s":"Thanks. Logged."},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Priya, I'll ring the crisis team from the car now. Back at base I'll update his risk record and the alert: stored tablets removed, drinking, fire risk, grandson visits.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"I'll return the tablets and call the fire service."}],"marks":[{"c":"Logs the address, time in, call-back time and code word with base, and agrees exit roles","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Introduces both, explains the worry kindly, and asks for the dog to be shut away","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about mood, losses, eating, sleep, alcohol quantity and medication","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and stored tablets","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks the home for self-neglect, fire risk and dependants or visiting children","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees means restriction with consent and a same-day crisis team plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking him to repeat the plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains he is not being admitted and safety-nets with crisis line, 111 option 2 and 999","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates his grief and his disclosure without rushing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Closes safely, calls base on leaving, and updates the risk record and alert","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Visits safely in a pair with base logged, asks plainly about suicide and stored tablets, sees the self-neglect and fire risk, removes means with consent, and secures same-day crisis follow-up.","borderline":"Safe and kind, but misses the stored tablets, the fire risk or the alcohol quantity, or leaves the follow-up vague.","fail":"Goes alone or without telling base, never asks directly about suicide, or leaves with no same-day plan."}},"wc-04":{"setup":"Tuesday 10:00, adult acute ward. Liam Barnes, 36, admitted informally 18 days ago after an overdose in severe depression, is much better and the consultant wants discharge on Friday. His partner has asked him to move out. Plan a safe discharge with him.","role":{"who":"Mr Liam Barnes, 36, informal inpatient recovering from a severe depressive episode and overdose","open":"Morning. Are we talking about Friday? I'm ready, honestly.","manner":"Keen to leave and upbeat at first. Becomes tearful when talking about his partner; steadier and engaged once he feels there is a real plan.","free":["Feels much better; sleeping and eating again","His partner, Sarah, has asked him to move out of their flat","Wants to get back to work as an electrician"],"ifasked":["Has nowhere fixed to live; his sister Claire has offered her sofa 'for a couple of weeks'","Thoughts of ending his life are now fleeting, maybe once a day, without plans; at admission they were constant","There are packets of paracetamol in the bathroom at the flat","Takes sertraline 100 mg each morning","Main triggers: arguments with Sarah and money worries; has rent arrears","Best friend Dan, and his sister Claire, are the people he trusts","Does not want the team to contact Sarah"],"worries":["Can I just go today? I feel loads better.","Why only a week of tablets?","What if I end up back in here?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when asked where he will live; settles if validated","Refuses contact with Sarah but agrees to Claire and Dan being involved","Mentions the paracetamol at the flat only if asked about tablets at home","Agrees to the housing referral once told it is his choice and helps with a place of his own"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Morning, Liam. I'm Dr Shah. Dr Ellis asked me to plan your discharge with you. Is now okay for about twenty minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Morning. Are we talking about Friday? I'm ready, honestly."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Friday's the aim, and I'm really glad you're feeling better. I'd like to make sure everything is in place first. How do you feel about going on Friday?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Good. Can I just go today? I feel loads better."},{"t":"0:45","band":"His situation","w":"D","s":"I understand. The first days after leaving hospital are when people are most at risk, so I want housing and follow-up sorted first. Where will you live when you leave?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"(pause) Sarah's asked me to move out. So I don't really know. (tearful) Eight years, and that's it."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"That's a huge loss, on top of everything you've been through. It makes sense that it hurts this much.","m":[9]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Sorry. Thought I was past crying."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Please don't apologise. Is there anywhere you could stay for now?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"My sister Claire's offered her sofa. A couple of weeks."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"That helps for Friday. After that you'd be at risk of having nowhere. With your agreement, I'd like our social worker to refer you to the council's housing team today, so they start helping now. Is that okay?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Will that go on some record?"},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"It's your choice, and it only goes to the council you pick. It helps get you a place of your own.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Okay. Do it. The council where Claire lives."},{"t":"2:25","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Do you still have thoughts about ending your life? How strong are they compared with when you came in?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Back then, all the time. Now maybe once a day, a flash. I wouldn't act on it."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Any plans, or anything you've thought about doing?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Are there tablets stored at the flat?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Paracetamol in the bathroom. A few packets."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Could Claire collect your things and leave the paracetamol, or bring it back to a pharmacy?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I'll ask her."},{"t":"3:20","band":"Safety plan","w":"D","s":"What would the warning signs be if things started to slide?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Stopping sleeping. Arguing with Sarah. Worrying about money. Staying in bed."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"And who are two people you could call?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Claire. And my mate Dan."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Good. We'll write those into a safety plan with the crisis line number and 111 option two. Can I contact Claire about the plan?","m":[4,1]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Claire, yes. Dan, yes. But don't tell Sarah anything. It's over."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"That's your choice and I'll respect it. We won't contact Sarah.","m":[1]},{"t":"4:15","band":"Medication","w":"D","s":"You'll carry on sertraline, 100 milligrams each morning. I'll give you seven days to take home, and ask your GP for weekly prescriptions for now.","m":[5]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Why only a week? It's a pain going back."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"I know it's a hassle. After an overdose we keep supplies small for a while to keep you safe. We'll review it once you're steadier.","m":[5,9]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Fair enough."},{"t":"4:50","band":"Follow-up","w":"D","s":"The home treatment team will see you face to face at Claire's on Saturday at 2 pm. Your named nurse is Kerry. Is that okay?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Saturday. Fine. What if I end up back in here?"},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"That fear makes sense. Coming back wouldn't be failure; it would mean you got help. Your plan shows who to ring early, so things are less likely to get that bad.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Okay. That helps."},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"I'll send your GP a summary by Friday afternoon: your medicines, the safety plan, and who's seeing you when. Our social worker will also help with the rent arrears and benefits advice.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:55","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the plan, so I know I've explained it clearly?","m":[8]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Housing referral today. Friday to Claire's. Paracetamol out. Week of tablets, GP weekly. Kerry on Saturday at two. Ring Claire, Dan or the crisis line if it slides."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Perfect. If things get worse before Saturday, ring the crisis line or 111 option two straight away, or 999 if you're in danger. If you miss Saturday's visit, the team will ring you that day.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thanks. Feels real now."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"It does. I'll confirm Friday once the housing referral is in. I'll see you on Thursday's ward round.","m":[10]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose, and respects his consent about who is involved","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks where he will live and makes a duty-to-refer to housing with consent before confirming the date","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Compares current suicidal thoughts with admission and asks about plans and tablets at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Builds a written safety plan with warning signs, two named people and the crisis line","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Limits take-home medication to 7 days with weekly GP prescriptions and explains why","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Books face-to-face follow-up within 72 hours with a named team, date and time","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Commits to the GP discharge summary within 24 hours and arranges social and money support","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking him to repeat the plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates his loss, his frustration and his fear of readmission","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Safety-nets what to do before follow-up and if the visit is missed, and closes clearly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Sorts housing with a consented duty-to-refer, reassesses risk and means at home, writes a safety plan with named people, limits medicines and books named follow-up within 72 hours.","borderline":"Safe plan, but housing is left to chance, the follow-up has no time or named team, or the GP summary is not mentioned.","fail":"Discharges to no fixed abode, skips the suicide reassessment, or gives a month of tablets with no early follow-up."}},"wc-05":{"setup":"Friday 16:00, your educational supervisor's office. You are a CT1, four months in. Clinical supervision has been cancelled four times, you have three WPBAs, ARCP is in six weeks and you want to sit Paper A. Use this meeting to fix all three.","role":{"who":"Dr Helen Ward, 54, consultant psychiatrist and your educational supervisor (later on speakerphone: Dr Amir Hassan, your clinical supervisor)","open":"Come in, sit down. Sorry, it's been one of those weeks. What did you want to cover?","manner":"Warm but distracted and tired. Becomes a little defensive when supervision is raised because her colleague is overstretched; relaxes and turns practical once her pressure is acknowledged. Dr Hassan is apologetic and keen to fix it.","free":["Dr Hassan has covered two wards since the locum left in September","She needs to complete the educational supervisor's report two weeks before ARCP","She has six residents and her own clinics"],"ifasked":["The panel wants WPBAs spread across the post and linked to High Level Outcomes","She will do a CbD with the resident today on a risk case","The medical psychotherapy tutor runs a Balint group starting in January","Mini-PAT needs 10–12 nominated assessors from mixed roles to get enough valid responses","If supervision fails again, tell the college tutor first, then the training programme director","An Outcome 5 means missing evidence, not a fail","(Dr Hassan) Tuesday at 14:00 is free every week"],"worries":["Amir is drowning. Can you not just catch up on the ward?","ARCP is ages away. Don't panic yet.","Are you sure Paper A this early is wise with everything else?"],"rules":["Becomes defensive about Dr Hassan if supervision is raised as a complaint; settles if the resident acknowledges the pressure and asks for a solution","Offers to ring Dr Hassan only if the resident suggests fixing a time today","Gives the escalation route only if asked who to tell if it happens again","Asks about the resident's wellbeing once the plan is written"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Thanks for making time on a Friday. I'd like to fix three things today: my supervision, my evidence for ARCP, and a plan for Paper A. Does that work?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Come in, sit down. Sorry, it's been one of those weeks. Yes, go on."},{"t":"0:25","band":"Supervision","w":"D","s":"My weekly supervision with Dr Hassan has been cancelled four times since August. I've logged the dates. I'm worried it'll show at ARCP.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Amir's covering two wards since the locum left. He's drowning, honestly. Can you not just catch up with him on the ward?"},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard for him, and I know you're stretched too. I'm not looking to blame anyone. I do need a protected hour a week recorded. Could we ring him now and fix a slot?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Fair. I've got six residents and two clinics myself. Let's call him."},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","who":"Clinical supervisor","s":"Amir here. Sorry, I know I've let our sessions slip. It's been chaos."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Dr Hassan, I really value our sessions. Could we hold Tuesday at 14:00 every week? If it has to move, could we rebook in the same week?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","who":"Clinical supervisor","s":"Tuesday at two is free. Same-week rebook, agreed. Send me an invite."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"I'll put it in both diaries tonight. Thank you.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"And if it's cancelled again, who should I tell?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"The college tutor first. If that doesn't fix it, the training programme director. Keep your log going."},{"t":"2:15","band":"Evidence for ARCP","w":"D","s":"What do you need to see for your educational supervisor's report?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"WPBAs spread across the post, linked to the High Level Outcomes. What have you got?"},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Two mini-ACEs and one CbD, plus four reflections. I've mapped them to the HLOs. The gaps are a new-patient assessment, a risk case and a non-clinical skill.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Good, you've done the thinking. So what's the plan?"},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"A full ACE on a new patient with Dr Hassan next Tuesday. A CbD on a risk case with you today, if you have time. And a DONCS chairing Thursday's ward round, with Dr Ellis. All before the end of the month.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"I can do the CbD now. Bring me Monday's self-harm patient."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Could you help me launch the mini-PAT today? Replies take weeks.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Yes. Who are you nominating?"},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Twelve: Dr Hassan, Dr Ellis, a specialty doctor, two residents, the ward manager, two nurses, the OT, the psychologist, the pharmacist and the ward clerk.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Nice mix. Send it before you leave tonight."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Have I left the psychotherapy case too late? I haven't started one.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Not too late, but don't leave it. The tutor's Balint group starts in January."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"I'll email the tutor on Monday to join the group and find a short case.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"ARCP is ages away anyway. Don't panic yet."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Thanks, that's reassuring. Six weeks goes quickly, though. If your report isn't uploaded in time, I'd get an Outcome 5: incomplete evidence, not a fail. I'd then upload what's missing within the time the panel sets.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Exactly. I'll do the report two weeks before."},{"t":"4:50","band":"Paper A","w":"D","s":"Last thing: Paper A. I'd like to aim for the spring sitting. Applications close months ahead, so I'll check the college page this weekend.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Are you sure it's wise this early, with everything else?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"I've thought about it. The revised syllabus applies from January, so I'll study from that. Six hours a week: Tuesday and Thursday evenings and Sunday mornings, starting the week after ARCP.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"That's realistic. Just don't let it eat the portfolio."},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"And how are you, really? You look tired."},{"t":"5:40","band":"Wellbeing","w":"D","s":"Honestly, tired and behind. I've worried I'm failing.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Lots of people feel that in their first post. You're not failing; you spotted the problem and came with a plan. Have you heard of NHS Practitioner Health?"},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"I have. I'll look at it this week, and talk to you or the tutor if it gets worse.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can I check we agree? Tuesday 14:00 supervision, rebooked in-week. ACE, CbD and DONCS by month end. Mini-PAT tonight. Balint from January. Paper A in spring. Tutor then TPD if supervision slips.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Spot on. Write it up and send it to me."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"I'll send the note tonight. Could we review in three weeks?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Put it in my diary. Now, Monday's self-harm patient?"}],"marks":[{"c":"Sets a clear agenda and names the supervision gap with a dated log","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges both supervisors' workload without blaming anyone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Knows the weekly one-hour standard and fixes a protected slot in both diaries","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks what the supervisor's report needs and maps evidence to High Level Outcomes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Lists each WPBA needed with a named assessor and date","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Launches the mini-PAT today with about 12 assessors from mixed roles","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Raises the psychotherapy case and Balint group early","d":"Content"},{"c":"Confirms the escalation route: college tutor, then TPD","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains ARCP Outcome 5 as incomplete evidence and how to fix it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Chooses a Paper A sitting with a realistic weekly study plan and the revised syllabus","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Is honest about wellbeing and agrees a support route","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises the agreed actions and checks shared understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with a written note and a dated review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Raises the supervision gap professionally, fixes a protected weekly slot, leaves with dated WPBAs, a launched mini-PAT, a clear escalation route and a realistic Paper A plan.","borderline":"Gets supervision fixed but leaves the WPBA or study plan vague, or forgets the mini-PAT and psychotherapy case.","fail":"Complains without a solution, blames the supervisor, plans to cram all assessments in the final week, or leaves with no dates."}},"wc-06":{"setup":"Thursday 15:30, CMHT outpatient clinic. Steve Collins, 45, with bipolar disorder, was discharged three weeks ago after a manic episode. He drives a van for work and has been driving since. His wife says he hasn't told the DVLA. Advise him.","role":{"who":"Mr Steve Collins, 45, man with bipolar disorder recovering from a manic episode (with his wife, Mrs Julie Collins, 44)","open":"I feel fine now. I don't see what the fuss is about.","manner":"Breezy at first, then angry and frightened about his job. Calms when his worries are heard and a practical plan forms. Julie is anxious and guilty about raising it.","free":["Drives a Transit van for a plumbing firm every day","Feels completely well now","Has been driving since discharge three weeks ago"],"ifasked":["The van is 3.5 tonnes and he drives it on his ordinary car licence; no lorry or bus licence","Takes lithium and quetiapine 100 mg at night; feels groggy until about 9 am","This is his second manic episode in five years","A couple of pints on Fridays; no drugs","(Julie) He drove the children to school on Monday after hardly sleeping","His boss has a warehouse job he could cover","Happy for Julie to stay and hear everything"],"worries":["So I lose my job because of you?","What if I only drive short trips?","Do you tell the DVLA, or do I?"],"rules":["Becomes angry if told off; settles if his fear about money is acknowledged","Admits the morning grogginess only if asked about side effects","Julie mentions the school run only if asked what she has noticed","Agrees to stop and notify once he hears about the warehouse option and getting the licence back"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Collins, Mrs Collins. I'm Dr Shah, one of the psychiatry doctors. Mr Collins, are you happy for your wife to stay while we talk?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Yeah, Julie stays."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I understand you've been driving since you left hospital. I'd like to talk about driving and the DVLA. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"I feel fine now. I don't see what the fuss is about."},{"t":"0:45","band":"His situation","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you feel well. How's your sleep and mood been since you got home?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Good. Seven hours most nights. Back to normal."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"What medicines are you on, and do any of them make you drowsy?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Lithium. Quetiapine at night. I'm a bit groggy till about nine."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you. That's worth knowing. Do you drink or use anything?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Couple of pints on a Friday. That's it."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the van. What is it, and what licence do you drive it on?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Transit, for a plumbing firm. Three and a half tonnes. Just my normal car licence."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Do you hold a lorry or bus licence as well?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"No. Never had one."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Mrs Collins, what have you noticed about his driving?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","who":"Wife","s":"He drove the kids to school on Monday after hardly any sleep. I was scared. I feel awful saying it in front of him."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That can't have been easy, and you're saying it because you care. Mr Collins, was that a bad night?","m":[7,4]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"One bad night. I was fine."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"After an episode of mania, the DVLA rule is that you stop driving, and you tell them. That applies to all driving, including the van. It's your legal duty to tell them, and you can do it online.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"So I lose my job because of you?"},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"I can hear how frightening that is, with the bills and the family. It's the DVLA rule, not my personal choice. Let's look at work and how you get your licence back.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"What if I just do short trips round town?"},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting to keep some freedom. The rule covers all driving. Most people can drive again after about three months well, and I can support that with a report when they ask.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Three months. That's not forever, I suppose."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"If you keep driving without telling them, you could be fined up to a thousand pounds, and your insurance may not cover you if there's an accident.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Right. My boss has a warehouse job I could cover."},{"t":"4:30","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a good option. Would a fit note or a letter to your boss about non-driving duties help? We can also look at benefits advice if money gets tight.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"A letter would help. Yeah."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"Could you stop driving from today, and tell the DVLA by Monday? Then bring the confirmation to your review in two weeks.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Monday. Julie can do the school run."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I also need to be honest with you, kindly. If you kept driving, I'd have to tell the DVLA's medical adviser myself. I'd talk to my consultant first, tell you before I did it, and write to you afterwards. I'd much rather you did it.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"I'm not going to make you do that. I'll do it."},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"I appreciate that. Even once you're allowed back, please don't drive if the quetiapine makes you groggy, or after drinking.","m":[3,10]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed, so I know it's clear?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"No driving from today. I tell the DVLA online by Monday. Show you the email in two weeks. Back on the road after about three months well."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Mrs Collins, is that your understanding too?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","who":"Wife","s":"Yes. Thank you. I feel better knowing."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'll write this in your notes and in a letter to you, copied to your GP and to Jo, your care coordinator. See you in two weeks.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks consent for his wife to stay, and explains the purpose","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Confirms the van is under 3.5 tonnes (Group 1) and asks about Group 2 entitlement","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks current mental state, sedating medication and alcohol","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks his wife what she has seen and takes the school run disclosure seriously","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains he must stop driving now and that notifying the DVLA is his legal duty","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains relicensing usually follows about three months well","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates his fear about his job and his wife's distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers practical work and money support: employer letter, fit note, benefits advice","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a date to notify and to bring confirmation","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Sets out kindly the steps if he keeps driving, and advises against driving when drowsy","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains the fine and insurance consequences without threat","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding with both of them","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Records the advice and closes with the letter and review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Checks vehicle group, gives the correct rule and three-month expectation, puts the duty on him, agrees a date, and explains kindly what happens if he keeps driving.","borderline":"Correct advice, but misses the vehicle group or sedation, gives no date, or skips the disclosure steps.","fail":"Says the doctor will notify for him, gives wrong time periods, threatens or discloses without warning him, or does not record the advice."}},"wc-07":{"setup":"Tuesday 01:00, A&E liaison bay. Ryan Clarke, 34, wants diazepam and pregabalin, says his tablets were stolen and his GP is away, and threatens to cut himself if he leaves with nothing. Assess him, treat what is real, and decline unsafe prescribing calmly.","role":{"who":"Mr Ryan Clarke, 34, man asking for diazepam and pregabalin in A&E","open":"Finally. Look, I just need my diazepam and my pregabalin. Someone nicked my bag on the bus with all my tablets in it.","manner":"Restless, irritable and testing whether he will be judged. Becomes tearful when he feels written off. Softens and engages if treated with respect and offered a concrete plan, even without tablets.","free":["His bag with his tablets was stolen on the bus on Sunday","His GP is on holiday and the practice 'won't do anything'","Says he takes diazepam 10 mg three times a day and pregabalin 300 mg twice a day","Feels sick and restless and 'can't settle'","Says he will cut himself if he leaves with nothing"],"ifasked":["Smokes heroin a few times a week, not on a methadone or buprenorphine script; last used last night","Buys pregabalin from a friend since his GP stopped prescribing it","Had a couple of cans of lager today","Has never had a fit when stopping tablets or alcohol","Cut his arms several years ago after a relationship ended; nothing since","Sometimes thinks everyone would be better off without him, but has no plan and nothing on him","His daughter Maisie, 7, visits every Saturday and keeps him going","Has not yet reported the theft to the police"],"worries":["So you think I'm making it up?","Then I'll just get it off the street.","Will I get my diazepam in the morning?","The drug team will just put me in groups."],"rules":["Escalates if the refusal sounds personal or moralising; settles if it is framed as a safety rule for everyone","Admits heroin and buying pregabalin only if asked plainly and without judgement","Becomes tearful when talking about being treated 'like a junkie'; calms if this is validated","Agrees to stay until morning if offered symptom relief and a clear next-day plan"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Okoro, one of the psychiatry doctors. Is it Ryan? Thanks for waiting. Tell me what's happened tonight.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"Finally. Look, I just need my diazepam and my pregabalin. Someone nicked my bag on the bus with all my tablets in it."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"That sounds really stressful. When did that happen?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"Sunday. My GP's on holiday and the practice won't do anything till he's back. I've been climbing the walls."},{"t":"0:33","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"Which medicines are you prescribed, and at what doses?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"Diazepam, ten milligrams three times a day. Pregabalin three hundred twice a day."},{"t":"0:44","w":"D","s":"Who prescribes them, and which pharmacy do you collect from?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Dr Hughes at Riverside Surgery. Boots on the high street. I pick up weekly."},{"t":"0:57","w":"D","s":"When did you last collect, and when was your last dose?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"Last week sometime. Last tablet was yesterday morning."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"Any alcohol, or anything else, in the last couple of days?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"Couple of cans. Nothing much."},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"Some people I see also use heroin, or are on methadone or buprenorphine. Is that part of things for you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"(pause) I'm not on a script. I smoke a bit of brown now and then. Not every day."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being straight with me. It genuinely helps me keep you safe. When did you last use?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"Last night."},{"t":"1:46","band":"Withdrawal","w":"D","s":"How's your body feeling right now? Sweating, shaking, feeling sick, diarrhoea?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"Bit sick. I can't settle. My legs won't keep still."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had a fit when you stopped tablets or alcohol?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"2:07","w":"D","s":"Can I check you over? Hold your hands out for me. Your pulse is 78, there's no tremor or sweating, and your pupils are normal. On the CIWA-Ar and COWS scales, your withdrawal scores are low.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"So what, you think I'm making it up?"},{"t":"2:26","w":"D","s":"No. I believe you feel rough. It tells me you're not in dangerous withdrawal right now, and that's good news.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Doesn't feel like good news."},{"t":"2:39","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"You told the nurse you'd cut yourself if you left with nothing. I want to take that seriously. Can you tell me more?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"(voice cracks) I'm just sick of it. Bag gets nicked, nobody helps, and everywhere I go I get treated like a junkie."},{"t":"2:59","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting, and humiliating. I'm sorry it's felt like that. I'm not here to judge you.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"(quieter) Yeah. Well."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Have you hurt yourself before?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"Cut my arms a few years back, when my relationship ended. Not since."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Have you had any thoughts about ending your life, now or recently?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think everyone'd be better off. But I wouldn't do anything. I've got my daughter, Maisie. She's seven. I see her every Saturday."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"She sounds really important. Have you made any plan, or got anything on you to hurt yourself with?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"No. I said it because I was angry. I've got nothing on me."},{"t":"3:52","band":"Check the facts","w":"D","s":"Before I came in, I checked your Summary Care Record. It shows diazepam five milligrams three times a day, last issued six days ago. I can't see pregabalin on it.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"(pause) The GP stopped the pregabalin. I get it off a mate."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. The notes also show you've been to three other A&Es this month. I'm saying that openly, not to catch you out.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","s":"Because nobody sorts it! I keep getting sent away."},{"t":"4:27","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I hear how fed up you are. I can't give diazepam or pregabalin tonight. I can't check what you've already taken, and doubling up can stop your breathing, especially with heroin. That's a rule I follow for everyone.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"This is a joke. Then I'll just get it off the street."},{"t":"4:48","w":"D","s":"I can see you're angry, and I really don't want you buying pills tonight. Pregabalin, diazepam and heroin together is how people stop breathing in their sleep.","m":[7,12]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"So I just suffer?"},{"t":"5:03","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"No. If you're sick or have diarrhoea, the nurse can give you something now. I'd like you to stay till morning, so we can ring Boots and your GP at nine and confirm your dose.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:17","w":"P","s":"And then I get my diazepam?"},{"t":"5:21","w":"D","s":"Then your practice's duty doctor can prescribe what's confirmed. I'll also refer you to the drug and alcohol service for tomorrow, and give you their self-referral number.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:33","w":"P","s":"They'll just put me in groups."},{"t":"5:37","w":"D","s":"They'll ask what you want. They can help with the pregabalin and heroin safely. I'd also like to give you a naloxone kit tonight; it reverses an opioid overdose. Would that be okay?","m":[9,12]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Never had one."},{"t":"5:54","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Can I check I've explained it well? What's the plan, as you understand it?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:01","w":"P","s":"No pills tonight. I stay here, you ring Boots and my GP, the drug team sees me, and I get that naloxone thing."},{"t":"6:11","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll share tonight's notes with your GP and the drug team, because they need the full picture to treat you safely. Is that okay?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"Fine. Whatever helps."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"If you want to leave, tell the nurse first so I can see you. If the urge to cut gets strong, or you feel shaky or confused, tell staff or ring the crisis line. I'll put the number on a written plan.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"Okay. Sorry for kicking off."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"No need. You've had a rotten few days. I'll write down exactly why we've decided this, so the next doctor knows, and I'll check on you before I go.","m":[13,7]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, calm and non-judgemental, and invites his own account of tonight","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Clarifies prescribed drugs, doses, prescriber, pharmacy, last collection and last dose","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol, heroin and opioid substitution, including last use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about withdrawal symptoms, checks signs and scores with CIWA-Ar or COWS","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about previous withdrawal fits","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores the self-harm threat: past self-harm, suicidal thoughts, plan, means and protective people","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his anger and distress without arguing or labelling him","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Declines controlled drugs clearly, giving the safety reason as a rule, not a judgement","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers concrete alternatives: symptom relief, stay till morning, drug service, naloxone","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Shares the record findings and repeat attendances openly and explains sharing with GP and drug team","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding of the plan using his own words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets: overdose risk of mixing, telling staff before leaving, crisis line, written plan","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes warmly and says the reasoning will be documented for the next clinician","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Scores withdrawal objectively, assesses self-harm risk fully, declines controlled drugs calmly with a clear reason, and offers real alternatives and next-day checks.","borderline":"Safe decision, but the risk assessment is thin or the refusal sounds personal, with few concrete alternatives.","fail":"Prescribes unverified controlled drugs, or labels him drug-seeking and refuses without assessing withdrawal or self-harm risk."}},"wc-08":{"setup":"Saturday 02:00, A&E liaison room. Tony Walsh, 52, was evicted yesterday and arrived drunk saying he would 'end it' if sent back onto the street. He is now sober and it is 2 degrees outside. Reassess him and plan tonight.","role":{"who":"Mr Tony Walsh, 52, evicted yesterday, now sober in A&E","open":"Headache. Cold. Bit embarrassed, to be honest.","manner":"Tired, flat and ashamed, with flashes of defensiveness about the council and his drinking. Becomes tearful talking about his son. Warms up and accepts help once he feels he is not being dismissed.","free":["Bailiffs changed the locks yesterday morning; he has only one bag","Lost his job at a depot in the summer and fell behind on rent","Spent the day in the park, then bought cider","Has eaten nothing for two days, 'just cider'"],"ifasked":["Mood low for months: poor sleep, no enjoyment, feels 'what's the point of me' at night","Once thought of walking in front of a lorry; no plan since and no past attempts","Drinks about three litres of white cider most days, more lately; morning shakes until he drinks","Never had a withdrawal fit; last drink about 20:00","Slipped in the park but did not hit his head","Orientated to day and place; no memory problems he has noticed","Has a son, Jordan, 14, who 'is with me most of the time'; says he is at a friend's tonight and avoids saying more"],"worries":["So you're just throwing me back out in the cold?","I don't want the council knowing my business.","I'll be rattling by morning.","I lost the flat because of the drinking. It's all my fault."],"rules":["Plays down the earlier threat at first; admits night-time thoughts if asked again gently","Becomes tearful and self-blaming about his son; settles if this is validated","Agrees to the housing referral only after hearing it needs his consent and what it says","Is vague about where Jordan has been sleeping; does not volunteer it tonight"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Tony, I'm Dr Ahmed, one of the psychiatry doctors. We met earlier when you'd had a drink. How are you feeling now?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Headache. Cold. Bit embarrassed, to be honest."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"No need to be. I'd like to go through things properly now you're clearer. Is that okay? I'll get you a tea and some food too.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"Tea'd be good. Go on then."},{"t":"0:31","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"Tell me about yesterday. What happened?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Bailiffs came at ten and changed the locks. I've been behind on rent since I lost my job at the depot in the summer. Grabbed one bag and that was it."},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"Where did you go after that?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"Park. Then the offy. Then here, I suppose."},{"t":"0:58","band":"Mood and drink","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been over the last few weeks, before yesterday?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Rubbish. Months, really. Since the job went."},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"Are you still enjoying anything? How are your sleep and appetite?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"Nothing, really. I sleep in bits. I've had nothing to eat in two days, just cider."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"How much do you usually drink over a day?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"Three litres of white cider, most days. More lately."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Do you get shakes or sweats in the morning before a drink? Have you ever had a fit coming off it?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"Shakes, yeah, till I've had one. Never had a fit."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"When was your last drink?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"About eight last night."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"Did you fall or bang your head at all yesterday?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"Slipped in the park, but I caught myself. Didn't hit my head."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"And can you tell me what day it is, and where we are?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"Saturday now, isn't it? Early hours. This is the General."},{"t":"2:23","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Earlier you said you'd end it if you were sent back onto the street. Now you're sober, I'd like to ask about that again.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"I didn't mean it. I was drunk."},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you feel steadier. Things said when drunk can still matter. Do those thoughts come at other times?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"(long pause) Sometimes. At night. I think, what's the point of me."},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about how you might do it, or made any plans?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"Thought about walking in front of a lorry once. Never got further than that."},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"Have you ever tried to end your life, or hurt yourself?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"What's kept you going when it's been that bad?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:23","w":"P","s":"My lad. Jordan. He's fourteen."},{"t":"3:27","w":"D","s":"Tell me about Jordan. Where's he staying, and has all this affected him?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"(hesitates) He's with me most of the time. His mum's not around. He's at a mate's tonight. (tearful) I've let him down. I lost the flat because of the drinking. It's all my fault."},{"t":"3:48","w":"D","s":"You sound really hard on yourself. You've lost your job and your home in a few months. Drinking is a health problem, and help does work.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"(wipes eyes) Yeah. Maybe."},{"t":"4:03","w":"D","s":"If Jordan needs somewhere to stay too, tell me. It changes what housing can offer, and it would help him. It's not about getting you in trouble.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"I know. He's alright tonight."},{"t":"4:19","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Can I share what I think is going on? I think you're depressed, and the drink is making it worse. But a psychiatric ward wouldn't help with what you need tonight.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"So you're throwing me back out in the cold?"},{"t":"4:38","w":"D","s":"No. I can hear how scary tonight feels. A ward won't fix housing, but the council can. I've spoken to their out-of-hours team, and they can look at emergency accommodation tonight if I send a referral.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"I don't want the council knowing my business."},{"t":"4:58","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"That's fair; it's your information. I can only send it with your agreement. It gives your name, how to reach you, and that you need housing. Which council would you like it to go to?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:13","w":"P","s":"Eastfield, I suppose. I've lived there twenty years. Go on, send it."},{"t":"5:19","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll send it now and ring them back. You stay here, warm and fed, until they call. With it this cold, they may have emergency beds open.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:32","w":"P","s":"What about the drink? I'll be rattling by morning."},{"t":"5:37","w":"D","s":"Good question. The nurses will check you for withdrawal every few hours and treat it if it starts. I'll also give you thiamine, a vitamin that protects your brain when you've not been eating.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"Right."},{"t":"5:54","w":"D","s":"I'll refer you to the alcohol service, write to your GP, and the crisis team will see you within three days. Would that be okay?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I need to sort it. For Jordan."},{"t":"6:10","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what happens next, so I know I've been clear?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"You send the council thing, I wait here, I get vitamins, then the alcohol people and the crisis team see me."},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"Spot on. If the 'what's the point' thoughts come back strongly, tonight or later, tell staff or ring the crisis line. I'll write the number on a plan for you.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Okay. Thanks, doc."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You did well talking tonight. I'll get the referral off and come back as soon as housing ring.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks he is sober and comfortable, and attends to food and warmth","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the eviction and the events of the day with open questions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for depression: mood, enjoyment, sleep and appetite","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes an alcohol history: amount, last drink, morning shakes and withdrawal fits","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about head injury and checks orientation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Reassesses suicidal thoughts sober: frequency, plan, past attempts and what keeps him going","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks whether any children or dependants are affected and leaves the door open to disclose","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his shame and fear without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains why a psychiatric bed is not the answer and what the council can offer","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gains consent for the duty to refer, explains its content and lets him choose the council","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a plan: housing callback, withdrawal checks, thiamine, alcohol service, GP, crisis team within 72 hours","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding using his own words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets with crisis line and a written plan, and closes clearly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Reassesses risk sober, screens mood, alcohol and head injury, asks about his son, and makes a consented duty-to-refer with a clear plan for tonight.","borderline":"Sound risk review, but the housing route is vague, consent is assumed or dependants are not asked about.","fail":"Discharges on an intoxicated assessment, or admits him as a housing fix without assessing mood, alcohol or children."}},"wc-09":{"setup":"Sunday 15:00, acute adult ward. Leah Turner, 23, detained under section 2, has not spoken, eaten or drunk for two days and stands rigid by the window. The nurse thinks she is being difficult, but her pulse is 112. Assess and act.","role":{"who":"Staff Nurse Grace Mensah, 34, the nurse looking after Leah (Ms Leah Turner, 23, detained under section 2, mute in her room; the consultant later by phone)","open":"She's not said a word since Friday. Won't eat or drink. Stands by the window like a statue. Honestly, I think she's just being difficult. But her pulse is 112.","manner":"Busy and sceptical at first, then worried and a little guilty once she understands. Helpful and quick once given clear jobs. Leah is silent and frightened, and speaks a few words after lorazepam.","free":["Leah has not spoken since Friday and has not eaten or drunk since Friday lunchtime","She stands rigid by the window for hours and resists being moved","Pulse 112 on the last check","Olanzapine 10 mg at night was started five days ago for psychotic depression"],"ifasked":["She held her arm up in the air for a long time this morning","If staff guide her towards the bed she pushes the other way","She grimaces at times; no echoing of words or movements seen","Latest obs: temperature 37.6, BP 145/90, sats 98% on air","Very little urine recorded today","She has been on her feet for hours, then lay on the floor","(Leah, after lorazepam) 'I couldn't move. I was scared.'"],"worries":["She spoke fine on Thursday. Isn't this just behaviour?","Do we stop the olanzapine?","She won't swallow. Can we give it as an injection?","Will the improvement last?"],"rules":["Calls it 'difficult behaviour' until catatonia is explained; then feels guilty and settles if reassured","Gives the posturing and negativism details only if asked directly","Leah does not speak until after lorazepam; then whispers a few words and accepts water if gently invited","The consultant agrees the plan if given a clear, structured summary"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Shah, the on-call doctor. Thanks for bleeping. Tell me what's been happening with Leah.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"She's not said a word since Friday. Won't eat or drink. Stands by the window like a statue. Honestly, I think she's just being difficult. But her pulse is 112."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"Good that you flagged the pulse. When did she last eat or drink?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"A few sips Friday lunchtime. Nothing since. Hardly any urine on the chart today."},{"t":"0:36","w":"D","s":"Any recent changes to her medication?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Olanzapine started five days ago, 10 mg at night, for the psychotic depression."},{"t":"0:47","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Let's see her together.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:51","band":"Look and score","w":"D","s":"Leah, I'm Dr Shah, one of the doctors. You don't have to speak. I'm going to check how you are, and I'll tell you everything I'm doing.","m":[1]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"(no response; stares at the window, arms held stiffly)"},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"Grace, has she held odd postures, or pushed against you when you move her?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"She held her arm up in the air for ages this morning. And if we guide her to the bed, she pushes the other way."},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"Leah, I'm going to move your arm gently. There's stiffness, and her arm stays where I leave it. Has she copied words or movements?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"Not that I've seen. She grimaces sometimes."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"So: mute, staring, posturing, negativism, rigidity and waxy flexibility. This is catatonia. On the Bush-Francis scale she scores 18.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"Catatonia? She was talking on Thursday."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"I can see why it looks like defiance. But she can't come out of this on her own. It's treatable, and it's physically dangerous if we miss it.","m":[10]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"(quietly) I didn't know. I've been a bit short with her today."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"You've been looking after her on a busy ward, and you spotted the pulse. That's what mattered.","m":[11]},{"t":"2:24","band":"Physical risk","w":"D","s":"What are her latest obs?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"Pulse 112, temp 37.6, BP 145 over 90. Sats 98 on air."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Her mouth is very dry. A fast pulse, borderline temperature and new olanzapine make me worry about NMS or malignant catatonia. I'd like CK, U&E, FBC, LFTs, CRP and glucose now, plus an ECG.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"I'll get the bloods kit. Do we stop the olanzapine?"},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Yes. Hold it and all antipsychotics. In catatonia they can tip someone into NMS. I'll write that on the chart.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"3:07","w":"D","s":"I'll also ask the medical team to see her. In a young woman with new catatonia, we need to rule out infection or encephalitis, so they may want a scan or EEG.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:21","band":"Lorazepam","w":"D","s":"Leah, I think your body and mind have got stuck. I'm going to give you a medicine called lorazepam. It often helps quickly. I'll stay close.","m":[1,8]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"(eyes move towards the doctor; no words)"},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"She won't swallow anything. Can it go in as an injection?"},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"Yes. Lorazepam 2 mg IM now. She's on section 2, and this treats her mental disorder, so we can give it without consent. I'll record that, and we'll rescore in 15 to 30 minutes.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"Okay. Giving it now. It's 15:30."},{"t":"4:03","band":"Protect her body","w":"D","s":"While we wait, please start a fluid balance chart and NEWS2 at least four-hourly. I'll ask the medics about IV fluids.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"She won't keep a cannula in, will she?"},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"Maybe not, so let's offer sips the moment she responds. She's immobile and dry, so she needs a VTE assessment; I'll prescribe heparin and stockings unless something rules it out.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:31","w":"P","s":"And her skin. She's been standing for hours, then lying on the floor."},{"t":"4:38","w":"D","s":"Good thinking. Pressure-area checks and a turning chart, please. Keep it calm and quiet, with one familiar nurse.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:47","band":"Rescore","w":"D","s":"Leah, it's been twenty minutes since the injection. How are you feeling?","m":[1,8]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"(slowly, in a whisper) Scared. I couldn't move."},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"That must have been so frightening. You're safe, and things are already easing. Would you try some water?","m":[11,9]},{"t":"5:07","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"(nods and sips) Okay."},{"t":"5:11","w":"D","s":"Grace, she's speaking, drinking and less stiff. She now scores 9 on Bush-Francis. That response supports catatonia.","m":[4,8]},{"t":"5:19","w":"P","s":"That's amazing. Will it last?"},{"t":"5:23","w":"D","s":"It may wear off, so she'll need regular lorazepam. If she doesn't keep improving over a few days, or any malignant signs appear, ECT is the next step.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"5:36","w":"P","s":"Right."},{"t":"5:38","band":"Check and escalate","w":"D","s":"What would make you call me back tonight?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:44","w":"P","s":"Temp 38 or more, pulse or BP swinging, stiffer again, or she stops drinking."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"Exactly. And a high CK when the bloods come back. Any of those, she goes to the medical team urgently.","m":[12,5]},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"Dr Bell, it's Dr Shah on call. Leah Turner, 23, section 2, catatonic, Bush-Francis 18. Pulse 112, temp 37.6, nothing to drink for 48 hours. Olanzapine held, bloods sent, lorazepam 2 mg IM given; she now scores 9.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:16","w":"P","who":"Consultant","s":"Good work. What do you need from me?"},{"t":"6:21","w":"D","s":"Your advice on regular lorazepam and whether she needs medical transfer. If she stops responding, I'd like an early ECT referral, and the MHA office alerted about a SOAD.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:34","w":"P","who":"Consultant","s":"Agreed. Lorazepam 1 mg four times a day for now. Ring me if her temperature or CK rise. I'll review her in the morning."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'll update her family and document it all. Leah, Grace is staying with you, and I'll be back this evening.","m":[1,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and speaks directly to the patient, explaining each step although she is mute","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes a focused history from the nurse: onset, intake, urine output and recent olanzapine start","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits catatonic signs: mutism, staring, posturing, negativism, rigidity, waxy flexibility","d":"Content"},{"c":"Scores on the Bush-Francis scale before and after lorazepam","d":"Content"},{"c":"Reviews vital signs and orders CK, U&E, bloods and ECG for NMS or malignant catatonia","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Considers medical causes, including encephalitis, and involves the medical team","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Holds olanzapine and all antipsychotics, explaining why","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a lorazepam challenge with dose, route and MHA authority, and records the response","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Starts fluid balance, NEWS2, VTE assessment and pressure-area care","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Reframes 'difficult behaviour' as catatonia and explains the next step, including ECT","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates the nurse's guilt and the patient's fear","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks the nurse's understanding of the call-back triggers","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Escalates to the consultant with a structured summary and an ECT and SOAD plan","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Names catatonia with a Bush-Francis score, checks for NMS, holds antipsychotics, gives lorazepam lawfully, protects her body and escalates with an ECT plan.","borderline":"Recognises catatonia and gives lorazepam, but misses fluids, VTE or malignant features, or escalates vaguely.","fail":"Accepts 'just difficult', gives more antipsychotic, or leaves a dehydrated, tachycardic patient without physical checks."}},"wc-10":{"setup":"Saturday 23:40, acute adult ward. Kerry Dawson, 31, detained under section 3, has told the night nurse that a 40-year-old male patient came into her room and touched her breasts and genitals. Make everyone safe, then see her.","role":{"who":"Staff Nurse Priya Nair, nurse in charge tonight (then Ms Kerry Dawson, 31, detained under section 3; the consultant later by phone)","open":"She's in the quiet lounge with Ellie, our HCA. He's in his room on the men's corridor. She says he came into her room and touched her breasts and between her legs.","manner":"The nurse is shaken but practical. Kerry is tearful, shaking and afraid of not being believed; she steadies when believed, given control and not questioned for detail.","free":["Kerry disclosed to the night nurse about 23:30; she says it happened around 23:15","Both patients are currently apart: she is in the lounge with a female HCA, he is in his room","(Kerry) 'I don't want to talk about it again.'","(Kerry) 'I just want a shower. I feel disgusting.'"],"ifasked":["The nurse wrote down some of Kerry's words but not the exact time or who was present","Kerry's room has not been cleaned; she is still in the same clothes","(Kerry) No pain or injury she is aware of; she feels shaky","(Kerry) Wants Ellie to stay with her and does not want her mother told yet","The male patient is asking why a nurse is sitting with him","The nurse was on the corridor and did not see him go in"],"worries":["(Kerry) Nobody's going to believe me. They'll say it's my illness.","(Kerry) What happens if I say no to the police?","(Kerry) Will he be moved?","(Nurse) Should we just call 999?"],"rules":["Kerry becomes tearful and shuts down if asked for details; settles if told she need not explain","Kerry agrees to police only after it is explained and she feels she has a choice","The nurse becomes upset that she missed it; steadies if told she has acted well","The consultant agrees the plan when given a clear summary"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Make it safe","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Kaur, the doctor on tonight. Thanks for bleeping. First, where are they both right now?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"She's in the quiet lounge with Ellie, our HCA. He's in his room on the men's corridor. She says he came into her room and touched her breasts and between her legs."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Safety first. Can someone be with him one-to-one, and keep him on the men's corridor, well away from her?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Yes, I'll put Sam with him now."},{"t":"0:41","w":"D","s":"Please lock her room. No cleaning, and nobody goes in. If she changes, her clothes and bedding go in separate paper bags, not plastic.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"We've got bags in the office. What else?"},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"Please write down exactly what she said to you, in her words, with the time and who was there. Don't add anything.","m":[10]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"I'll do it now. She thinks it happened about quarter past eleven."},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll see her now. Is Ellie okay to stay with us?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Yes. She's asked for Ellie."},{"t":"1:29","band":"With Kerry","w":"D","s":"Kerry, I'm Dr Kaur, the doctor on tonight. Ellie's staying with us. Is it okay if I sit down?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"(shaking) I don't want to talk about it again."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"You don't have to. I won't ask you for details tonight. I just want to make sure you're safe and get you the right help.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"Nobody's going to believe me. They'll say it's my illness."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"I'm taking what you've said seriously. Your diagnosis doesn't change that. He's being kept well away from you, with staff beside him all night.","m":[5,2]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"(starts crying) I thought I was safe in here."},{"t":"2:23","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. You should have been safe, and none of this is your fault. Take your time.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:33","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"(after a pause) Okay."},{"t":"2:37","w":"D","s":"Are you hurt anywhere, or in any pain that needs looking at now?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"No. I just feel shaky. I want a shower. I feel disgusting."},{"t":"2:52","band":"Her choices","w":"D","s":"That makes complete sense. If you can wait a little, it may help the police keep evidence. But it's your choice, and we'll support you either way.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"How long would I have to wait?"},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Until the police or the sexual assault centre advise. It's called a SARC. They support people after an assault, can check you over, and can collect evidence if you want that.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"I don't know about police."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"That's okay. Can I explain what it would mean, so you can decide?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"Go on."},{"t":"3:41","w":"D","s":"I'd ring them now. They'd take a short first account and arrange the SARC. You can stop at any point, and Ellie can be with you.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"And if I say no?"},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"Then I'd respect that as far as I can. But if other women here could be at risk, I may still need to share some information. I'd always tell you first.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"(quietly) Other women... Okay. Call them."},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"Just so I've got it right: you'd like me to call the police, and for them to arrange the SARC?","m":[8,13]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"Yes."},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"Thank you. What would help you feel safer tonight?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"Can Ellie stay with me? And will he be moved?"},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"Ellie will stay. Tonight he has staff with him all the time, and I'm speaking to the consultant about moving him. I'll tell you what's decided.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"Okay."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"Would you like anyone told, like family? That's only with your say-so. I'll come back after I've spoken to the police.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:11","w":"P","who":"Patient","s":"Not my mum. Not yet."},{"t":"5:15","band":"Act and escalate","w":"D","s":"Priya, she agrees to police. I'm ringing 101 now and asking them to arrange the SARC.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"5:24","w":"P","s":"Shouldn't we just call 999?"},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"He's contained and she's safe, so 101 is right. If anything changes, 999. Can you start the incident report? I'll raise the safeguarding concern and ring the on-call manager.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:43","w":"P","s":"Yes. He's asking why Sam's sitting with him."},{"t":"5:49","w":"D","s":"I'll see him next. I'll check his mental state and risk, but I won't discuss the details of the allegation. Please hold his leave for now.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:01","w":"P","s":"(upset) I was on that corridor. I didn't see him go in."},{"t":"6:09","w":"D","s":"That's really hard. You've done everything right since she told you. We'll debrief before the end of the shift.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:19","w":"D","s":"Dr Lowe, it's Dr Kaur. A female patient alleges a male patient touched her sexually tonight. They're separated, he's one-to-one on another corridor, her room's sealed, she agrees to police, and I'm calling 101.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","who":"Consultant","s":"Okay. Keep him on one-to-one. I'll come in early and we'll look at moving him. Make sure safeguarding's raised."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Will do. I'll document everything and update Kerry before I finish.","m":[11,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Gets a brief factual summary from the nurse and checks where both patients are now","d":"Content"},{"c":"Separates them at once: alleged perpetrator on one-to-one on another corridor","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Preserves evidence: room locked, no cleaning, clothing and bedding in paper bags","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Introduces self to her, keeps her chosen female staff present, and asks permission","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Believes and reassures her without asking for details or leading questions","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks only for injury or urgent medical need","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains why waiting to wash helps evidence while leaving the choice with her","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains police and SARC and gains her informed agreement","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Is honest about the limits of confidentiality if others may be at risk","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks the nurse to record her exact words, the time and who was present","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Calls police, raises safeguarding and incident report, and informs consultant and manager","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges support for her, a review for him, and a staff debrief","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks her understanding and tells her what happens next and when she will be updated","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Separates the patients, preserves evidence, believes her without interviewing, gains consent for police and SARC, and raises safeguarding with clear records.","borderline":"Safe and kind, but misses evidence steps, the confidentiality limit or a care plan for the alleged perpetrator.","fail":"Doubts her because of her diagnosis, questions her for details, or lets the room be cleaned and delays police and safeguarding."}},"wc-11":{"setup":"Wednesday 03:10, acute ward in a standalone mental health unit. Crash bleep: Jamie Cole, 29, detained under section 3, was found hanging from his en-suite door with a belt. Staff have cut him down. Lead the resuscitation, then the aftermath.","role":{"who":"Charge Nurse Ben Adeyemi, nurse in charge (with HCA Sam, Staff Nurse Priya as scribe, a frightened patient, and later a paramedic)","open":"He's not responding. He's making odd gasps.","manner":"Fast and frightened at first, needing clear instructions. Steadies once given a job. After the handover he becomes shaken and blames himself; settles when supported.","free":["Jamie was found hanging from the en-suite door at 03:08 and cut down at 03:09","He is unresponsive with occasional gasps","999 has not been called yet","Another patient is in the corridor asking what is happening"],"ifasked":["Jamie was last seen in bed at 02:55 on 15-minute observations","Sam cut the belt near the door hinge, leaving the knot intact","Nobody has started writing down times","The AED, oxygen and bag-valve-mask are in the clinic room","Mark, in bay 2, was close to Jamie and is very upset"],"worries":["Shouldn't we keep his neck still first?","Is he on a section? Can he come with us? (paramedic)","I checked him at 02:55. This is my fault.","What about the other patients?"],"rules":["Staff freeze unless given named, specific jobs","Signs of life return at the second rhythm check if compressions and the shock are done well","The charge nurse breaks down after the handover; steadies if told what he did well and that he will be supported","The paramedic wants a short, timed handover, not a long story"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"First minute","w":"D","s":"I'm Dr Rahman, I'm leading. Is it safe in here? Jamie, can you hear me?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"He's not responding. He's making odd gasps."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"Gasping isn't normal breathing. He's in cardiac arrest. Ben, start compressions now. Has anyone called 999?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"Not yet."},{"t":"0:31","w":"D","s":"Sam, ring 999: adult cardiac arrest after hanging, give our address. Then bring the AED, oxygen and bag-valve-mask.","m":[2,4]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","who":"HCA","s":"On it."},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"Ben, middle of the chest, hard and fast. Five to six centimetres, a hundred to a hundred and twenty a minute.","m":[6]},{"t":"0:59","w":"P","s":"Shouldn't we keep his neck still first?"},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"No delay for that. Keep going. I'll hold his head in line and open his airway.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","who":"HCA","s":"AED and oxygen are here. Ambulance is on its way."},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"Pads on now: one below the right collarbone, one on the left side of his chest. Keep compressing until it tells us to stop.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"It's saying shock advised!"},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"Everyone stand clear. Oxygen away. Ben, hands off. Shocking now.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","who":"HCA","s":"Shock delivered."},{"t":"1:54","w":"D","s":"Straight back on compressions. Who's writing the times?","m":[6,4]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"No one yet."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Priya, you're scribe. Found 03:08, cut down 03:09, CPR 03:10, one shock at 03:12. Write everything from now.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","who":"Scribe","s":"Got it."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Sam, go to the front door and bring the paramedics straight here. Ellie, please take the other patients to the lounge, away from this corridor.","m":[4,11]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","who":"Another patient","s":"(from the corridor) Is he dead? What's happening?"},{"t":"2:42","w":"D","s":"Something serious has happened, and staff are helping him. You're safe. Please go with Ellie to the lounge.","m":[11]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"My arms are going. I need to swap."},{"t":"3:00","band":"Rhythm check","w":"D","s":"Swap now, at the two-minute check. I'll take over compressions; Ben, hold his head in line. Keep the pause short.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"He's moving his arm! He's coughing."},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"Stop compressions. He's breathing, and there's a carotid pulse. Priya, time?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","who":"Scribe","s":"03:16."},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"Onto his side, head kept in line. Oxygen on, sats probe and blood pressure. Leave the pads on in case he arrests again.","m":[7,5]},{"t":"3:43","w":"P","s":"Sats 92. Pulse 110. He's not opening his eyes."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"GCS about 5. He needs hospital urgently even if he wakes. His airway can swell over the next few hours.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:03","band":"Handover","w":"P","who":"Paramedic","s":"Hi, ambulance crew. What've we got?"},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"Jamie, 29. Found hanging with a belt at 03:08, cut down 03:09. Not breathing normally; CPR from 03:10. AED shockable, one shock at 03:12. Output at 03:16, GCS 5, sats 92 on oxygen.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:27","w":"P","who":"Paramedic","s":"Thanks. Is he on a section? Can he come with us?"},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"He's on section 3, and yes, take him now. Priya will go with you with his section papers and drug chart.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","who":"Paramedic","s":"Great. Let's move."},{"t":"4:52","band":"Aftermath","w":"D","s":"Ben, where's the belt?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","s":"On the floor. Sam cut it near the hinge."},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"Good, the knot's intact. Bag it without handling it more. Lock the en-suite and the room, nobody cleans, and print tonight's observation records.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"(voice shaking) I checked him at 02:55. He was in bed. This is my fault."},{"t":"5:28","w":"D","s":"I can see how shaken you are. You did your checks, got him down and started CPR fast. That gave him a chance. You won't be on your own with this.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"(breathes) Okay. Okay."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"I'll ring the consultant, the on-call manager and the police. The consultant will contact his family. Can you start the incident report?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"Yes. What about the others? Mark in bay 2 was close to him."},{"t":"6:12","w":"D","s":"I'll see Mark myself. Let's raise observations for anyone at risk and check every room for ligature points tonight.","m":[13,11]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"Thanks."},{"t":"6:27","w":"D","s":"Before handover at 07:30, let's get everyone together for a short debrief, and make sure night staff are offered support afterwards.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"I'll tell the team."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"You all did really well tonight. I'll update you as soon as I hear from A&E.","m":[11,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Declares leadership, checks safety and recognises gasping as cardiac arrest","d":"Content"},{"c":"Starts CPR at once and has 999 called with clear details","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gets the AED on within the first minute and delivers the shock safely","d":"Content"},{"c":"Allocates named roles: compressions, equipment, scribe, door and other patients","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Manages the airway with the head held in line, without delaying CPR for the neck","d":"Content"},{"c":"Directs good-quality compressions, minimal pauses and swaps at two minutes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Recognises return of circulation and starts post-resuscitation monitoring","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives paramedics a short, timed handover including rhythm, shocks and section status","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Sends a nurse escort with MHA papers and drug chart","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Preserves the ligature with knot intact, the room and the observation records","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Calms and supports distressed staff and patients","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Informs consultant, manager and police, starts the incident report and arranges family contact","d":"Close"},{"c":"Reviews other patients' risk and books a staff debrief before the shift ends","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Starts CPR and the AED within a minute, allocates roles, hands over clearly, preserves the scene, and looks after staff and patients.","borderline":"Effective resuscitation, but roles, timing records or aftermath tasks are patchy.","fail":"Delays CPR (for the neck or a collar), leaves the team leaderless, or neglects the scene, reporting or other patients."}},"wc-12":{"setup":"Thursday 06:15, older adult ward. Derek Lewis, 67, detained under section 3, with COPD and on clozapine, has been found cold and stiff in bed. Confirm death, start the right reporting, then phone his son with the consultant.","role":{"who":"Staff Nurse Jo Hart, nurse in charge (later by phone: Mr Paul Lewis, 42, his son and nearest relative; the consultant sits in on the call)","open":"He was last seen at five on hourly checks, settled in bed. I found him at quarter past six. He's cold and stiff. I didn't start CPR.","manner":"The nurse is calm but guilty. Paul is stunned, then disbelieving, then tearful and briefly angry; he steadies with honest, plain answers and practical next steps.","free":["Mr Lewis was last seen settled at 05:00 on hourly checks","Found at 06:15, cold and stiff; no CPR started","(Paul) 'He was fine yesterday. I spoke to him at teatime.'","(Paul) 'He was meant to be coming home soon.'"],"ifasked":["His nearest relative is his son Paul; the number is on the front sheet","He was on clozapine 400 mg; his notes, drug chart and blood results are on the ward","Other patients are waking and one is asking questions","(Paul) He is at home with his wife","(Paul) The family are Catholic and would want a priest","(Paul) He will ring his sister after the call"],"worries":["(Nurse) Should I call the undertakers?","(Paul) What did he die of? Was it the clozapine?","(Paul) Why wasn't anyone checking on him?","(Paul) Can we see him? When can we have the funeral?"],"rules":["The nurse blames herself for not checking at 05:30; settles if her checks are acknowledged","Paul says 'you've got the wrong person' after the news; accepts it if the doctor stays calm and repeats it kindly","Paul becomes angry about the checks; calms if answered honestly without excuses or speculation","Paul asks about the cause; settles if told plainly it is not yet known and how it will be found out"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Confirm death","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Patel. Thanks for calling me. When was he last seen?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"He was last seen at five on hourly checks, settled in bed. I found him at quarter past six. He's cold and stiff. I didn't start CPR."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"That was right. He's cold, there's stiffness in his jaw and lividity on his back. He died some time ago, so CPR wouldn't help him.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"I keep thinking I should have checked at half five."},{"t":"0:39","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much that's weighing on you. You did his hourly checks. Let's look after him properly now.","m":[6]},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"I'm observing him for five minutes. No central pulse, no heart or breath sounds. Pupils fixed and dilated, no corneal reflex, no response to pressure. I'm confirming death at 06:32.","m":[1]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"06:32. I've written it down."},{"t":"1:09","band":"Report","w":"D","s":"Please leave everything as it is: his body, the bed, anything attached to him. Close the room, and nobody tidies.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:19","w":"P","s":"Should I call the undertakers?"},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"Not yet. He was detained, so this is a death in state detention. It must go to the coroner, and I won't write a death certificate. Out of hours, the police take the report for the coroner.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Right. I didn't know that."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Can you lock away his notes, drug chart, observation records and his latest clozapine bloods? The coroner will need them.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"I'll put them in the office."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"I'll ring the police and the on-call manager. Please leave a message for the MHA office: they need to notify CQC first thing. And could you start the incident report?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"Yes. The other patients are waking up. Pete's asking questions."},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"Let's have someone sit with them. We'll tell them gently once his family know. And a staff debrief at eight.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:31","band":"Prepare","w":"D","s":"Dr Rao's arrived. Who is his nearest relative?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"His son, Paul. The number's on the front sheet."},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"Dr Rao, will you sit in with me? We'll ring from the office, where it's quiet.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:52","w":"P","who":"Consultant","s":"Yes. You lead, I'll be right here."},{"t":"2:57","band":"Tell Paul","w":"D","s":"Hello, is that Paul Lewis? My name's Dr Patel. I'm a doctor at Oakfield Hospital, calling about your father, Derek. Can I just check your date of birth?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","who":"Son","s":"It's Paul. Twelfth of March, 1984. What's happened?"},{"t":"3:16","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Are you somewhere you can talk? Is anyone with you?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:23","w":"P","who":"Son","s":"I'm at home. My wife's here. What's going on?"},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"I'm afraid I have some very sad news. Dr Rao, his consultant, is here with me.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","who":"Son","s":"Oh God."},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"Your father was found this morning not breathing. He had died. I'm so sorry.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","who":"Son","s":"(silence) No. He was fine yesterday. I spoke to him at teatime. You've got the wrong person."},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"(pause) I'm so sorry, Paul. It is your dad, Derek. I know this is a terrible shock.","m":[9,8]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","who":"Son","s":"(crying) He was meant to be coming home soon."},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. Take all the time you need. I'm here.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","who":"Son","s":"(after a while) Was he in pain?"},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"He was seen settled in bed at five and found just after six, lying in bed. There was no sign he'd been in distress.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","who":"Son","s":"What did he die of? Was it the clozapine?"},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"I honestly don't know yet, and I don't want to guess. Because he was detained, a coroner looks into his death, and will usually arrange a post-mortem to find the cause.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","who":"Son","s":"A post-mortem? Why wasn't anyone watching him?"},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"You have every right to ask that. He was checked every hour overnight. The coroner will investigate, and the hospital will review his care openly and share what we find with you.","m":[9,11]},{"t":"5:19","w":"P","who":"Son","s":"Can we see him? We'll need to sort the funeral."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Yes, we'll help you see him. The coroner's officer will ring you, usually today, and can explain timing and any religious needs.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:37","w":"P","who":"Son","s":"We're Catholic. Dad would want a priest."},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"Of course. Our chaplain can come, and we'll tell the coroner's officer it matters to you.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:51","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"I know that's a lot to take in. What will you tell your wife about what happens next?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","who":"Son","s":"Dad's died. The coroner's officer will ring. We can see him, and a priest can come."},{"t":"6:09","w":"D","s":"That's right. Is there anyone else you'd like us to call for you?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:17","w":"P","who":"Son","s":"No, I'll ring my sister."},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","who":"Consultant","s":"Paul, it's Dr Rao. I'm so sorry. When you're ready, I'd like to meet you and your family to answer whatever we can."},{"t":"6:33","w":"D","s":"I'll text you the ward's direct number and my name, Dr Patel. We'll call you again by lunchtime. I'm so sorry for your loss, Paul.","m":[13,9]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","who":"Son","s":"Thank you. Thanks for telling me straight."}],"marks":[{"c":"Confirms death correctly after five minutes' observation and records the time","d":"Content"},{"c":"Recognises irreversible signs and explains why CPR is not started","d":"Content"},{"c":"Leaves the body and room undisturbed and secures notes, drug chart, observations and bloods","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Reports to the coroner via police out of hours and does not issue a death certificate","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks the MHA office to notify CQC and starts the incident report","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Supports the nurse and other patients and books a staff debrief","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Prepares the call: nearest relative, quiet room, a senior present, identity and support checked","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives a warning, then plain news using 'died' and his name","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Pauses and responds to shock, grief and anger with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Answers honestly that the cause is unknown, without guessing","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the coroner, post-mortem, review, viewing and religious needs","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks what he has understood and who will support him","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes with a named contact, a call-back time and an offer to meet","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Confirms death properly, reports to the coroner and CQC route, preserves records, and tells the son clearly and kindly with a senior present.","borderline":"Correct reporting but a rushed or euphemistic call, or the call is right but CQC or record-keeping is missed.","fail":"Writes a death certificate, moves the body or tidies, or breaks the news vaguely, speculates on the cause or ignores the family's grief."}},"wc-13":{"setup":"Saturday 22:40, acute adult ward. Liam Carter, 34, informal, admitted five days ago with depression and heavy drinking, has threatened his ex-partner's new boyfriend and a staff nurse. He is pacing. Assess the threats, keep everyone safe and decide on disclosure.","role":{"who":"Mr Liam Carter, 34, informal inpatient (also Raj Patel, nurse in charge; Amy, staff nurse he threatened; Dr Khan, on-call consultant by phone)","open":"What for? I've got nothing to say to you lot. Everyone's watching me like I'm an animal.","manner":"Tense, loud and defensive, then raw grief about the break-up breaks through. Settles if he feels heard; flares if told off or if confidentiality is raised abruptly. Ends subdued and cooperative.","free":["His partner Sophie left him a month ago after five years and is now with Darren","Lost his flat when they split","Says Darren 'has got what's coming' and he will 'sort him out'","Angry that staff treat him 'like a kid'"],"ifasked":["Knows Darren lives on Ferndale Road and plans to wait outside his work the first night he is out","Not sure if he means a beating 'or worse'","A friend keeps a baseball bat in his car; nothing on the ward (room searched with consent)","Glassed a man in a pub in 2019; community order","Police called once last year during a row with Sophie; says he pushed a door, not her","Last drink five days ago; shakes for two days, now settled","No voices, no belief that Sophie was unfaithful","Passive thoughts that everyone would be better off without him; no plan; his mum keeps him going"],"worries":["You can't tell anyone. That's confidential.","Am I going to get arrested?","So I'm locked up now? I came in voluntarily."],"rules":["Becomes louder if lectured or interrupted; calms if his hurt is named","Gives the plan and the weapon only if asked directly and calmly","Accepts a possible disclosure grudgingly if told openly and in advance","Agrees to stay tonight once the leaving plan is explained without threat"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Make it safe","w":"D","s":"Thanks for bleeping me. Before I see him, is anyone hurt, and could he have anything he could use as a weapon?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","who":"Nurse in charge","s":"No one's hurt. We searched his room with his consent this afternoon, nothing there. He's pacing the corridor. He told Amy she'll get what's coming."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Okay. Will you come in with me? We'll use the lounge, door open, both of us near the exit. Let the response team know, and keep Amy away from him for now.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","who":"Nurse in charge","s":"Will do. I'll come in with you."},{"t":"0:33","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Carter? I'm Dr Okoro, the doctor on call tonight, and this is Raj. Would you sit with us for a few minutes? Sit wherever you like.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"What for? I've got nothing to say to you lot. Everyone's watching me like I'm an animal."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"I can see you're really wound up tonight. I'm not here to tell you off. I want to hear what's going on.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"(sits heavily) She's with him now. Five years we were together, and she's moved on in a month."},{"t":"1:05","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"That's a huge loss, and it sounds very raw. No wonder you're angry.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"(voice cracks) I've got nothing left. No flat, no her. I'm stuck in here and he's in my place."},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful. Thank you for telling me. You said to one of the staff you'd sort him out when you leave. What did you mean?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"Darren. I know where he lives, Ferndale Road. He's got what's coming."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"When you say sort him out, are you thinking of actually hurting him?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"Give him a hiding. Make him think twice. (pause) I don't know. Maybe worse."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about how, or when?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"First night I'm out. Wait for him outside his work."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"Do you have a knife or any weapon, here or at home? Could you get hold of one?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"Not here, they searched. My mate's got a bat in his car. I wouldn't need it."},{"t":"2:06","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Have you ever hurt anyone before? Have the police been involved?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"Glassed a bloke in a pub in 2019. Community order. That was the drink."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being straight with me. Has there been any violence with Sophie, or police called to your home?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Once, last year, we were rowing. I never touched her. I pushed a door."},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"What stops you from acting on it?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:33","w":"P","s":"Her lad. He's eight. I wouldn't want him seeing that. And going back to court, I suppose."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"When did you last have a drink? Any shakes, sweats or feeling sick now?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Not since I came in. Five days. Hands shook the first two days. Fine now."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"Sometimes after a break-up people become sure their partner was cheating, or hear voices, or feel watched. Has anything like that happened?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"She didn't cheat. She just left. I don't hear voices, if that's what you mean. I'm not mad."},{"t":"3:07","w":"D","s":"Okay, thank you. With all this going on, have you had thoughts of self-harm or of not being here?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think everyone'd be better off. I wouldn't do anything. I've got my mum."},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me that. We'll keep an eye on it. Earlier you told Amy she'd get what's coming. What happened?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"3:29","w":"P","s":"She told me to calm down like I was a kid. I didn't mean it. I just lost it."},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"I understand you were wound up, but staff need to feel safe here, and threats aren't okay. Can you keep things calm tonight?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Alright. I'll say sorry to her tomorrow."},{"t":"3:49","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I need to be honest with you. Most of what you tell me stays private. But you've named a man, his road and a plan to hurt him. If someone could be seriously hurt, I may have to share some information to protect them.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"You can't do that! That's confidential. I told you in private."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"I understand why that feels like a betrayal. I'd rather be open now than go behind your back. I'll speak to my consultant first, and if we tell the police, I'll share only what's needed and tell you.","m":[3,9]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"Am I going to get arrested?"},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"I can't promise what the police will do. I won't share your medical history, just enough to keep him safe. You haven't hurt him, and I want it to stay that way.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"(quietly) I just want her back."},{"t":"4:41","w":"D","s":"I know. That grief is real, and it's something we can work on with you here.","m":[3]},{"t":"4:48","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Tonight, staff will check on you more often. If you decide you want to leave, the nurses can hold you for up to six hours and I could hold you for up to 72 while you're assessed under the Mental Health Act. I'd rather it doesn't come to that.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"So I'm locked up now? I came in voluntarily."},{"t":"5:09","w":"D","s":"You're still informal, and I respect that. I'm just being clear about what would happen if you tried to go to Darren's tonight.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"Fine. I'm not going anywhere. Where would I go?"},{"t":"5:22","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back what happens tonight?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"You ring your boss, maybe the police. They watch me more. If I try to go, you stop me."},{"t":"5:33","w":"D","s":"That's right. If the anger builds or those dark thoughts get worse, find Raj straight away. I'll come back and tell you what we've decided.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:43","w":"D","s":"Amy, he's told me what he said to you. Are you okay?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","who":"Staff nurse","s":"Shaken, honestly. He was right in my face. I don't want to work his side tonight."},{"t":"5:54","w":"D","s":"That's completely understandable, and it wasn't acceptable. Raj will swap you over. Please do an incident report, you can report it to the police if you wish, and we'll set up a debrief tomorrow.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:07","w":"D","s":"Dr Khan, it's Dr Okoro. Informal 34-year-old man has named his ex-partner's new partner, knows his road, plans to wait for him on discharge, and has a 2019 assault. Not withdrawing, not psychotic. I think it's a real risk of serious harm and we should tell the police.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","who":"Consultant","s":"Agreed. Minimum necessary: his name, the threat, the named man and the road. Not his diagnosis. Tell him first if it's safe. What if he tries to leave?"},{"t":"6:34","w":"D","s":"Fifteen-minute observations, then section 5(4) and 5(2) and a Mental Health Act assessment if he tries. I'll ring 101, record the reference, and document what I shared and why.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","who":"Consultant","s":"Good. Flag it to the Caldicott Guardian and the ward team on Monday."}],"marks":[{"c":"Agrees immediate safety with the nurse in charge: weapons checked, open room near exits, response team aware","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Introduces self calmly, offers space and a choice of seat, and avoids confrontation","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names and validates his hurt about the break-up without endorsing the threat","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks directly about intent, plan and timing towards the named man and his address","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about weapons on the ward, at home and access once he leaves","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about past violence, police contact, domestic abuse and what stops him acting","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks recent alcohol, withdrawal symptoms and psychotic drivers such as morbid jealousy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts alongside thoughts of harming others","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains the limits of confidentiality honestly and tells him before any disclosure","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Sets limits on threats to staff, checks on the threatened nurse, offers debrief and incident report","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Discusses disclosure with the on-call consultant; shares only minimum facts with police and records it","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans for him leaving: closer observations, s5(4), s5(2) and Mental Health Act assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks his understanding, says how to get help tonight, and closes with next steps","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Sets up safety first, asks plainly about intent, plan, means and past violence, is honest about confidentiality, agrees minimum disclosure with the consultant, and has a clear leaving plan.","borderline":"Safe and respectful, but the risk history is patchy, or disclosure and the leaving plan are vague.","fail":"Sees him alone in a closed room, keeps the threat confidential or shares everything, forgets he is informal, or ignores the threatened nurse."}},"wc-14":{"setup":"Tuesday 03:10, acute adult ward. Desmond Clarke, 52, with schizophrenia and on section 3, has a hot, swollen leg, temperature 38.9C and pulse 118. Medics suspect cellulitis with sepsis. He refuses transfer. Assess his capacity and agree a safe, lawful plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Desmond Clarke, 52, detained under section 3 (also Grace, his ward nurse; Mr Winston Clarke, his brother, by phone; the medical registrar by phone)","open":"It's throbbing. Hot. I told the nurse, I'm not going anywhere. They'll poison me there.","manner":"Feverish, wary and fixed on poisoning, but polite and orientated. Becomes agitated if rushed; calms when his fear is acknowledged. Trusts Grace. Winston is anxious and helpful.","free":["Leg is hot, throbbing and painful; feels shivery and sick","Will not go to A&E because the doctors there will put chemicals in the drip","Will take tablets from Grace, not from 'them'"],"ifasked":["Knows it is Tuesday night and he is on Ashwood Ward; says the months backwards correctly","Can repeat back that the infection may spread to his blood and needs a drip","Believes the leg will settle on its own and the drip is the real danger","No allergies","No advance decision and no power of attorney (patient and brother)","Had his appendix out years ago and went in despite complaining (brother)","When well, he has always wanted to get better (brother)"],"worries":["You can't make me. I know my rights.","Will Grace stay with me?","(Winston) Is he going to be alright?"],"rules":["Raises his voice if he feels unheard; settles if his fear is named","Explains the poisoning belief only when asked what worries him about hospital","Accepts oral antibiotics and bloods from Grace","Goes along with transfer, still unhappy, once told Grace will stay and Winston agrees"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Clarke, I'm Dr Ferreira, the doctor on call. I can see your leg is really sore. Can I sit with you for a few minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"It's throbbing. Hot. I told the nurse, I'm not going anywhere. They'll poison me there."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"I hear that, and I want to understand it. First, how are you feeling in yourself?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"Shivery. Hot and cold. Bit sick."},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"Grace, could you repeat his observations now and keep NEWS2 hourly? Tell me straight away if the score rises.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:39","w":"P","who":"Nurse","s":"Temperature 38.9, pulse 118, blood pressure 104 over 66. NEWS2 is 6."},{"t":"0:44","band":"His thinking","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Mr Clarke, infections can muddle people's thinking, so I need to check that first. Where are we, and what day is it?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Ashwood Ward. Tuesday. Middle of the night."},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"Good. Can you list the months backwards from December, starting with December?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"December, November, October, September, August, July, June, May, April, March, February, January."},{"t":"1:11","w":"D","s":"Spot on. What I need to talk about is one decision: going to A&E now for antibiotics through a drip. Is that okay?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"Go on then."},{"t":"1:24","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"The doctors think you have an infection in your leg called cellulitis, and it may be getting into your blood. That's called sepsis.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"It's just a red leg."},{"t":"1:37","w":"D","s":"It looks that way, but your temperature and fast heart tell us your body is struggling. Sepsis can get worse within hours. Without antibiotics through a drip it could make you very ill, even kill you. With treatment, most people recover.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Die? From my leg?"},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"It's a lot to take in. Can you tell me in your words what I've said?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Infection in my leg. Could go in my blood. Need a drip in the hospital or it gets bad."},{"t":"2:14","band":"Capacity","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. What worries you about going to the hospital?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"The doctors there are in with the people after me. They'll put chemicals in the drip. I've seen it."},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. I can see why you'd want to stay away.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"(voice rising) Nobody listens! You'd do the same if you knew what I know."},{"t":"2:41","w":"D","s":"I am listening. Being scared and unwell at three in the morning is awful. Let's take it slowly.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"(calmer) Okay."},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"If you don't go, what do you think will happen to your leg?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"Nothing. It'll settle. The drip's the danger, not the leg."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"So you're weighing the infection against being poisoned. How sure are you about the poison?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"A hundred percent. I'm not going. That's final."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"Would you take antibiotic tablets here, from Grace, and let her take some blood? Any allergies?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"From Grace, yes. No allergies. Not from them."},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's a real step. Grace, please give the first dose of flucloxacillin and take bloods and cultures now.","m":[2]},{"t":"3:36","band":"Wishes and family","w":"D","s":"Mr Clarke, have you ever written down treatments you wouldn't want, or given anyone legal power over your health?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that. My brother Winston sorts my letters. Ring him."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Mr Clarke, it's Dr Ferreira from Ashwood Ward. Sorry to ring so late. Desmond has a serious leg infection that may be in his blood, and he's refusing A&E because he thinks he'll be poisoned.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","who":"Brother","s":"Oh no. Is he going to be alright? He's always been terrified of hospitals."},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"That's a frightening call to get in the night. With antibiotics he has a good chance. When he's been well, what has he said about medical treatment?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","who":"Brother","s":"He had his appendix out years ago. Moaned, but he went. He'd want to get better."},{"t":"4:32","w":"D","s":"Has he ever signed anything refusing treatment, or given anyone power of attorney for his health?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","who":"Brother","s":"No. Nothing."},{"t":"4:41","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that really helps us decide what's best for him. I'll call you once he's moved.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:49","band":"Decide","w":"D","s":"It's the on-call psychiatry doctor. He's not delirious: orientated, months backwards intact. He understands and repeats the facts, but his belief he'll be poisoned stops him weighing the risk. He lacks capacity for this decision.","m":[3,7]},{"t":"5:04","w":"P","who":"Medical registrar","s":"Fine. He's already sectioned. Can't you just use that for the antibiotics?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"No. Section 63 covers treatment for his mental disorder, and the cellulitis is unrelated. We'll treat him in his best interests using the Mental Capacity Act. No advance decision or LPA, and his brother agrees.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","who":"Medical registrar","s":"Okay. I'll take him in majors and start the sepsis bundle when he arrives."},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"He's detained, so the on-call consultant will authorise section 17 leave. Two nurses he knows will escort him with his papers and my capacity assessment. Persuasion first, and minimal restraint only if truly needed.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:47","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Mr Clarke, I've thought hard about this. I believe your fear of poison is stopping you weigh up the danger to your leg, so we'll take you to hospital for the drip because it's what's best for you.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"So you're forcing me. You're all the same."},{"t":"6:07","w":"D","s":"I understand you're angry, and I'm sorry this is so frightening. Grace will be with you the whole time, and Winston knows and agrees.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"6:18","w":"P","s":"Winston said that? Will Grace stay with me?"},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"Yes. Can you tell me what happens next?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:27","w":"P","s":"Hospital. Grace comes. Drip for the leg."},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"That's right. If you feel worse before you go, more shivery or muddled, tell Grace at once. The hospital doctors will check your thinking again as you improve, and I'll update your consultant.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Okay. But Grace stays."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges his pain and asks how he feels in himself","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks observations, sets hourly NEWS2 with escalation, and starts oral antibiotics and bloods on the ward","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names the specific decision assessed: transfer and IV antibiotics now","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for delirium with orientation and an attention test","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the infection, treatment and risks of refusal in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tests understanding and retention by asking him to repeat back","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the poisoning belief and shows how it prevents him weighing the risks","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his fear and his brother's worry, and offers a trusted nurse","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks for an advance decision or health and welfare LPA","d":"Content"},{"c":"Consults his brother about his past wishes to inform best interests","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Uses the MCA, not section 63, for the infection and explains why","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Arranges section 17 escorted transfer with papers and least-restrictive approach","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Tells him the outcome honestly, checks understanding, safety nets and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Keeps sepsis care moving, excludes delirium, assesses capacity for this one decision, checks advance wishes with family, and uses the MCA with section 17 escort.","borderline":"Reaches the right decision, but the capacity reasoning is thin or the legal route and transfer details are unclear.","fail":"Uses section 63 for the infection, assumes schizophrenia removes capacity, accepts the refusal without assessment, or delays treatment debating the law."}},"wc-15":{"setup":"Friday 23:15, A&E. Jayden, 13, autistic, was brought in by police after hitting his mother and damaging their home. He is shouting and has kicked a nurse. The A&E consultant wants him sedated and sectioned. Make it safe and plan with his mother.","role":{"who":"Mrs Kerry Walsh, 41, Jayden's mother (also Jayden, 13, autistic; and the A&E consultant)","open":"Kerry. I'm sorry. I'm so sorry about the nurse. I don't know what to do any more.","manner":"Exhausted, apologetic and close to tears, then frightened to admit she fears him. Opens up when not blamed. Relieved and practical once a plan forms. Jayden is loud and overwhelmed, then quiet with headphones.","free":["Tonight he threw his tablet, smashed the TV and hit her when she tried to take it","A neighbour called the police","Headphones, low light, no touching and few words calm him","She is bruised and exhausted"],"ifasked":["Moved to a new school six weeks ago after his special unit closed","Barely sleeping for two weeks, up at 3am","Chewing on one side, off his food, won't let her look in his mouth","Bangs his head when frustrated; never talked of dying or self-harm","Siblings aged 6 and 9 are at her mum's tonight; he pushed his sister Lily last week","She alone has parental responsibility; his father is not on the birth certificate","No social worker; on a waiting list for support"],"worries":["Are you going to sedate him? I don't want him drugged.","I can't take him home tonight. Not like this.","Will social services take him off me?"],"rules":["Cries when asked if she is hurt; steadies if told it is not her fault","Admits fear of Jayden only after being validated","Mentions the toothache only if asked about pain or physical illness","Jayden shouts if approached or spoken to at length; settles with headphones and space"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Make it safe","w":"D","s":"I hear he's very distressed. Before anything else, can we move him out of the cubicle into the quiet relatives' room, with fewer people and the lights down?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","who":"A&E consultant","s":"He's kicked one of my nurses. I need him sedated and sectioned. The department's full."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I understand the pressure. He's 13 and autistic, and noise and crowds will be making this worse. Sedation and the Mental Health Act aren't the first step. Let's try a quiet room with his mum while I call CAMHS.","m":[11]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","who":"A&E consultant","s":"Fine. Half an hour. Then I want a plan."},{"t":"0:41","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Mensah from the mental health team. You must be Jayden's mum.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Kerry. I'm sorry. I'm so sorry about the nurse. I don't know what to do any more."},{"t":"0:56","w":"D","s":"You've done really well getting him here safely. What usually helps him when he's this upset?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"His headphones. Low light. Don't talk at him, don't touch him. His weighted blanket, but that's at home."},{"t":"1:12","w":"D","s":"Okay, let's do exactly that. Just you and me with him.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"Jayden, I'm Dr Mensah. It's very noisy here. Quiet room, with Mum?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","who":"Jayden","s":"Go away! I hate it here!"},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"Okay. I'm stepping back. Mum's here. Your headphones are on the chair.","m":[1,3]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"(quietly) He's putting them on. He's coming with me. That's him starting to come down."},{"t":"1:41","band":"What happened","w":"D","s":"He's settling. Can you tell me what happened tonight?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"I asked him to turn his tablet off for bed. He threw it, then smashed the telly. When I tried to take it off him, he hit me."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"That must have been really frightening. Are you hurt?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"Bruised arm. I'm fine. (starts crying) I'm just so tired. I can't do this on my own any more."},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"You've been carrying a huge amount, mostly on your own. This isn't your fault. Has anything changed for him recently?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"New school six weeks ago. His unit closed. And he's barely slept for two weeks. Up at three every morning."},{"t":"2:30","band":"Physical","w":"D","s":"When autistic children are this distressed, there's sometimes pain or illness behind it. Has he been unwell, constipated, off his food, or holding any part of his body?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"Now you say it, he's been chewing on one side. Won't let me look in his mouth. Off his food a bit."},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"That's a really important clue. Toothache can come out as anger. I'll ask the children's doctors to check him once he's calm enough.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"I never thought of that."},{"t":"3:06","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Has he said anything about wanting to hurt himself, or wanting to die?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"No, never. He bangs his head when he's frustrated, but not tonight."},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"Who else lives at home?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"His brother and sister, six and nine. They're at my mum's tonight."},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"Has he ever hurt them, or have they seen this happen?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"He pushed Lily last week. They hide in their room when he kicks off. (pause) I'm scared of him sometimes. Is that terrible?"},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"It's not terrible at all. Thank you for being honest; that takes courage. Your safety and theirs matter too. Is there a social worker involved?","m":[4,8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"No. We're on a waiting list for something."},{"t":"3:59","band":"Law","w":"D","s":"Who has parental responsibility for Jayden? You alone, or his dad too?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"Just me. His dad's not on the birth certificate. Never been around."},{"t":"4:11","w":"D","s":"Thank you. At 13, decisions about his treatment are usually made with you as his parent, unless he understands enough to decide himself. The adult capacity law starts at 16. Sectioning is a last resort, and he doesn't need it tonight.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:29","w":"P","s":"Are you going to sedate him? I don't want him drugged."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"Medication isn't my first step. Quiet, space and your way of calming him are working now. If we ever needed medicine, it would be by mouth first, the lowest dose, agreed with the children's doctors and you.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"Okay. (pause) I can't take him home tonight. Not like this."},{"t":"4:56","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I understand. I suggest the children's doctors check his teeth and see whether he stays on the children's ward tonight. CAMHS will see him within the next day or two.","m":[6,12]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"And then what? It'll just happen again."},{"t":"5:14","w":"D","s":"I want to get you more help, not just tonight. Because of the risk to Lily and her brother and how stretched you are, I'll refer to children's social care. It's about support, not blame.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:29","w":"P","s":"Will they take him off me?"},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"That's a very common worry. The aim is to help you keep him safely at home, with things like short breaks and school support. I'll also ask that he's on the local support register so he isn't sent somewhere unsuitable.","m":[4,12]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Okay. Yes."},{"t":"5:52","w":"D","s":"Let's write a safety plan together: what calms him, who to call, and the crisis line. If anyone's in danger, call 999.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:02","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what's happening next?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:06","w":"P","s":"Children's doctor checks his teeth. Maybe he stays on the ward. CAMHS in a day or two. Social care will help. I've got numbers to ring."},{"t":"6:17","w":"D","s":"That's it exactly. I'll update the A&E consultant now.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:22","w":"P","s":"Please tell the nurse I'm sorry."},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"He's settled with headphones and Mum. Possible dental pain, so paediatrics are coming. CAMHS will follow up within 48 hours and I'm making a safeguarding referral. No sedation or detention needed. Can your nurse do an incident report, and can we offer her a debrief?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","who":"A&E consultant","s":"Good. Better plan than mine. I'll ask the paeds registrar."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self to mother and boy calmly, using few, simple words with him","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Moves him to a quiet, low-stimulus room and reduces the number of staff","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks his mother what calms him and uses her strategies first","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates the mother's exhaustion and fear, and says it is not her fault","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about tonight's trigger and recent changes in school, routine and sleep","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about pain or physical illness and requests paediatric review","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about self-harm and talk of wanting to die","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about siblings, harm to them and any social worker","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Confirms parental responsibility and the legal framework for a 13-year-old (not the MCA)","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains medication is a last resort: oral first, with paediatric input and parental agreement","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tells the A&E consultant that sedation and detention are not first steps, with reasons","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees CAMHS follow-up, safeguarding referral and a written safety plan with the mother","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks the mother's understanding, gives crisis advice and closes with clear next steps","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Calms the environment first, uses his mother's knowledge, looks for pain, applies the right law for a 13-year-old, and leaves with CAMHS, paediatric and safeguarding plans.","borderline":"De-escalates well but misses physical causes, parental responsibility, or the siblings.","fail":"Goes straight to sedation or the MHA, applies the MCA to a 13-year-old, sends him to police custody or an adult unit, or ignores safeguarding."}},"wc-16":{"setup":"Wednesday 14:00, CMHT clinic. Natalie Price, 29, with schizophrenia, has been well on olanzapine 15 mg for eight months but has gained weight and feels exhausted. She wants to stop. Review her side effects and agree a safe plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Natalie Price, 29, CMHT outpatient with schizophrenia","open":"Honestly? I want to stop these tablets. Look at me. Look what they've done.","manner":"Frustrated and determined at first, then tearful about her body image. Embarrassed about sex but relieved when asked plainly. Warms to a shared plan and leaves hopeful.","free":["Has gained nearly two stone and can't fit her clothes","Too tired to work","Wants to stop olanzapine","Mentally she feels well"],"ifasked":["Takes it at 10pm; sleeps 12 hours and feels drugged until lunchtime","Ravenous, especially late at night","No periods for three months; has a boyfriend and uses condoms 'mostly'","No breast discharge, no excess thirst or urination","Low sex drive, causing rows with her boyfriend","Opens bowels every four days, bloated; no severe pain, vomiting or blood","Stopped medication two years ago and was admitted for six weeks believing neighbours were filming her"],"worries":["So I'm stuck with it?","Will the weight come off?","Can I get help getting back to work?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when talking about her appearance; steadies when told it isn't her fault","Mentions the sexual side effects only if asked directly","Admits the past relapse only if asked what happened last time she stopped","Agrees to a switch if her concerns are taken seriously"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Natalie, come in and have a seat. I'm Dr Patel. How have things been?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Honestly? I want to stop these tablets. Look at me. Look what they've done."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you've told me straight away. Tell me what's been happening.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:23","w":"P","s":"I've put on nearly two stone. I'm tired all the time. I can't work. I just want my life back."},{"t":"0:33","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard, and these side effects matter. Let's go through them one by one and see what we can do. Which bothers you most?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"The weight. I can't fit into my clothes. (tearful) I hate looking in the mirror."},{"t":"0:55","band":"Side effects","w":"D","s":"That's really upsetting, and it isn't your fault. Olanzapine increases appetite a lot. Are you hungrier than before?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"Starving. Especially at night. Biscuits at midnight."},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"Have you been very thirsty, or passing more urine than usual?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"No, I don't think so."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the tiredness. What time do you take your tablet, and when are you sleepiest?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"About ten at night. I sleep twelve hours and still feel drugged till lunchtime."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"Have your periods changed at all?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"They've stopped. Three months now."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"Could you be pregnant at the moment?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"I don't think so. I've got a boyfriend. We use condoms... mostly."},{"t":"1:56","w":"D","s":"We'll do a test today to be sure. Any milky discharge from your breasts?","m":[5,10]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"These medicines can affect people's sex life, like interest or arousal. Has that happened for you?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"(pause) Yeah. I just don't feel like it any more. It's causing rows. I didn't know if I could say."},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Lots of people feel this and never mention it. It's a common side effect, and often improves when we change medicine.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"Really? I thought it was just me."},{"t":"2:42","w":"D","s":"How often are you opening your bowels?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"Every four days or so. I'm bloated."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"Any severe tummy pain, swelling, vomiting or blood?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"No. Just uncomfortable."},{"t":"2:59","band":"Her mind","w":"D","s":"How's your mood and thinking? Any voices, or worries about people, like before?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"No. I'm fine in my head. That's why I don't need them."},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"What happened last time you stopped medication?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"(sighs) Two years ago. Six weeks in hospital. I thought the neighbours were filming me."},{"t":"3:26","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"That's really important. Olanzapine has kept you well, but it's causing these problems. Your periods and sex drive may be due to a hormone called prolactin. The appetite and sleepiness come from olanzapine itself. Stopping suddenly could make you unwell again within weeks.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"So I'm stuck with it?"},{"t":"3:51","w":"D","s":"No. There's a middle way. We could switch to aripiprazole, which is much less likely to cause weight gain, sleepiness or hormone changes. We'd bring olanzapine down slowly while building the new one up, over two to four weeks.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"What are the downsides?"},{"t":"4:13","w":"D","s":"Some people feel restless, a bit sick or find it harder to sleep in the first few weeks. You'd take it in the morning. If you feel restless, tell us and we'll adjust it.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Will the weight come off?"},{"t":"4:34","w":"D","s":"Many people lose some weight over the following months, especially with help with food and activity. I can't promise it all goes, but we'll track it together.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","s":"Okay. I'd rather try that than carry on like this."},{"t":"4:53","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Great. Today I'd like a pregnancy test, your weight, waist and blood pressure, and bloods for sugar, cholesterol and prolactin. Is that okay?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Fine. What about the constipation?"},{"t":"5:09","w":"D","s":"We'll start senna at night and a stool softener, with more water and fibre. If you get bad tummy pain, keep vomiting or your tummy swells, go to A&E the same day.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"5:26","w":"D","s":"During the switch, your care coordinator will call weekly. If you notice poor sleep or feel people are watching you, ring the duty team straight away.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Sounds like a lot of people watching me. (laughs) In a good way."},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"I'll write to your GP today. We'll weigh you weekly for six weeks and repeat bloods at 12 weeks. I'll ask the GP to refer you to a weight management service.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"And can I get help getting back to work?"},{"t":"6:08","w":"D","s":"Yes. Once your energy picks up, we'll link you with employment support.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:15","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Just to check I've explained it well, can you tell me the plan?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:22","w":"P","s":"Pregnancy test and bloods today. Switch slowly to the new one, in the morning. Laxative. Ring if I get ill again or have bad tummy pain."},{"t":"6:35","w":"D","s":"Perfect. We'll start the switch once today's test is back. I'll see you in two weeks. Anything else?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks for actually listening."}],"marks":[{"c":"Greets her warmly, invites her concerns and asks which side effect matters most","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her distress about weight and sexual side effects without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about sedation and the timing of her dose","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about appetite, weight gain and symptoms of diabetes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about periods, possible pregnancy and breast discharge","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks plainly about sexual function","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about bowel habit and constipation red flags","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks current mental state and what happened when she last stopped medication","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains likely causes, including prolactin, and the risk of stopping abruptly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Arranges pregnancy test, weight, waist, BP, HbA1c, lipids and prolactin","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a cross-taper to aripiprazole with laxatives and lifestyle support as a shared decision","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety nets relapse signs and constipation red flags, and writes to the GP on monitoring","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding and closes with a review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Asks about each side effect in turn, including sex and bowels, rules out pregnancy, orders metabolic and prolactin tests, and agrees a cross-taper she accepts.","borderline":"Kind and safe, but the side-effect review is patchy or the monitoring plan is vague.","fail":"Dismisses her concerns, insists on no change, stops olanzapine abruptly, or misses pregnancy or sexual side effects."}},"wc-17":{"setup":"Thursday 11:00, acute adult ward. Margaret Lewis, 67, detained under section 3 with psychotic depression, is barely eating or drinking and believes she is dead. Her consultant recommends ECT. Assess her capacity, prepare her safely and speak with her daughter Sarah.","role":{"who":"Mrs Margaret Lewis, 67, detained under section 3 (then her daughter, Ms Sarah Lewis, 40)","open":"(very slow, quiet) If you like. It makes no difference.","manner":"Margaret is slowed, flat and hopeless, tearful at times, never argumentative. Sarah is frightened and sceptical about ECT, then reassured and engaged once the process and safeguards are explained.","free":["Believes she died weeks ago and her insides have rotted","Says dead people don't need food","Has heard of ECT as 'electric shocks like in the films'"],"ifasked":["Had only a sip of tea today","Can repeat back what ECT involves, including possible memory loss","Cannot weigh it: 'You can't treat a dead body'","Hip replacement under anaesthetic four years ago without problems","Has an upper denture; no reflux","No advance decision, LPA or deputy; Sarah only helps with bills","When well, she would want to get better; she loves her grandchildren (Sarah)"],"worries":["(Sarah) Isn't ECT barbaric?","(Sarah) Will it wipe her memory?","(Sarah) Can she say no if she's sectioned?","(Sarah) What if she stops drinking completely?"],"rules":["Margaret cries when she says no one can help a dead woman; settles a little if not argued with","Margaret answers questions briefly and slowly","Sarah becomes tearful when describing her mother's weight loss","Sarah relaxes once she hears an independent doctor must agree"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mrs Lewis, I'm Dr Adeyemi, one of the doctors on the ward. May I sit with you for a little while?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"(very slow, quiet) If you like. It makes no difference."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'd like to talk about a treatment your consultant has suggested. But first, how are you feeling today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"I'm not feeling anything. I'm dead. I died weeks ago. My insides have rotted."},{"t":"0:33","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. I won't argue with you, but I can see how much you're suffering.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","s":"(tearful) Nobody can help a dead woman."},{"t":"0:45","band":"Physical risk","w":"D","s":"The nurses tell me you've hardly eaten or drunk this week. How much have you had today?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","s":"A sip of tea. Dead people don't need food."},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"I'm worried about that. Without enough fluid, your body can become seriously unwell quite quickly.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"It doesn't matter."},{"t":"1:09","band":"Explain ECT","w":"D","s":"Your consultant thinks a treatment called ECT could help. Have you heard of it?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"Electric shocks. Like in the films."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"That's what many people picture. Now you're fully asleep under a short anaesthetic with a muscle relaxant. A small current causes a brief, controlled seizure. It's usually twice weekly, often for six to twelve treatments.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"What would it do?"},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"For depression like yours it often works well, and faster than tablets. Side effects include headache, aching muscles and some confusion afterwards. Some people lose memories from around the treatment time, and a few have longer gaps.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:57","band":"Capacity","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what I've said?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"You put me to sleep and pass electricity through my head. It might help. Memory might go."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"That's right. What do you imagine would happen if you had it, or if you didn't?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Nothing. You can't treat a dead body. There's nothing left to save."},{"t":"2:26","w":"D","s":"So you feel it can't make any difference, because you've already died?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"Yes. It's obvious."},{"t":"2:35","band":"Safety checks","w":"D","s":"Have you had an anaesthetic before? Any problems?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"My hip, four years ago. It was fine."},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"Any loose teeth, dentures or heartburn?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"Top denture."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Have you ever written down treatments you wouldn't want, or given anyone legal power over health decisions?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"No. Sarah does my bills, that's all."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Mrs Lewis. I'll talk with Sarah now and come back later.","m":[1]},{"t":"3:10","band":"Her daughter","w":"D","s":"Hi Sarah, I'm Dr Adeyemi. Thanks for coming in. How are you doing?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","who":"Daughter","s":"Not great. She's fading. She's lost so much weight. I keep thinking I'm going to lose her."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"That's an awful thing to watch, and it's natural to be frightened. She's barely eating or drinking, which is why her consultant thinks ECT is the best option now.","m":[3,10]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","who":"Daughter","s":"ECT? Isn't that barbaric?"},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"I understand that picture. Today it's done under general anaesthetic with a muscle relaxant, so her body barely moves. It's one of the most effective treatments for severe depression with psychotic symptoms.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:57","w":"P","who":"Daughter","s":"Will it wipe her memory?"},{"t":"4:01","w":"D","s":"Some people lose memories from around the treatment period, and a few have longer gaps. We'll test her memory before the first treatment and throughout, and adjust if needed.","m":[4,12]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","who":"Daughter","s":"Can she say no if she's sectioned?"},{"t":"4:19","band":"The law","w":"D","s":"Yes. Being detained doesn't by itself allow ECT. If she can decide and refuses, we can't give it except in an emergency. She understands what ECT is, but her belief that she's dead stops her weighing it, so right now she lacks capacity for this decision.","m":[6,9]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","who":"Daughter","s":"So who decides?"},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"An independent second opinion doctor will review her and talk to us and to you. ECT can only go ahead if they agree and sign a certificate. Did your mum ever sign anything refusing treatment, or give anyone power of attorney?","m":[8,9]},{"t":"5:01","w":"P","who":"Daughter","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"When she was well, what do you think she'd have wanted?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","who":"Daughter","s":"(tearful) She'd want to get better. She adores her grandchildren. This isn't her."},{"t":"5:17","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that really helps, and the second opinion doctor will want to hear it. I'll also make sure she has an independent advocate.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"5:28","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Before the first treatment we'll do blood tests and a heart tracing, the anaesthetist will see her, and we'll do a baseline memory test. We'll review her lorazepam too, as it can make ECT less effective.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","who":"Daughter","s":"And if she stops drinking completely before then?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"We'll keep food and fluid charts and weigh her daily, and the medical team will see her if she gets dehydrated. If her life is at risk, the law allows urgent ECT without waiting.","m":[3,13]},{"t":"6:06","w":"P","who":"Daughter","s":"Okay."},{"t":"6:07","w":"D","s":"Before every treatment we'll check her capacity again. If she improves and can decide, it becomes her choice.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:16","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can I check what you've taken from this?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","who":"Daughter","s":"Tests and the anaesthetist first. An independent doctor has to agree. Memory checked all the way through. You'll check each time if she can decide."},{"t":"6:33","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll ring you after the second opinion visit, and you're welcome at Monday's ward review. If you're worried at any point, call the ward.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","who":"Daughter","s":"Thank you. I feel a bit better knowing what's happening."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self gently, works at her pace and explains the purpose","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her distress without arguing with the delusional belief","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about food and fluid intake and recognises the physical risk","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains ECT simply: anaesthetic, brief seizure, frequency, benefits and memory side effects","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tests understanding, retention and weighing for the specific ECT decision","d":"Content"},{"c":"Concludes she lacks capacity because the belief that she is dead prevents weighing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about previous anaesthetics, dentures and reflux","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks for an advance decision, LPA or deputy covering ECT","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains s58A: detention alone does not allow ECT and a SOAD must certify","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates the daughter's distress and asks about her mother's past wishes","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Plans bloods, ECG, anaesthetic pre-assessment, baseline cognitive test and benzodiazepine review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans capacity checks before each treatment, memory tracking and an advocate","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety nets poor intake, checks the daughter's understanding and closes with follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Assesses capacity for ECT clearly, checks advance wishes, explains the SOAD route to the daughter, and sets up physical, anaesthetic and cognitive checks.","borderline":"Correct legal route, but the capacity assessment or the physical work-up is thin.","fail":"Proceeds because she is detained, uses s62 routinely, skips the capacity assessment, or ignores the daughter."}},"wc-18":{"setup":"Monday 15:30, A&E liaison. Yousif Adam, 24, from Sudan, claimed asylum six months ago and took a paracetamol overdose. His medical treatment is complete. He speaks little English; a video Arabic interpreter is ready. Assess his mental state and risk, and link him to support.","role":{"who":"Mr Yousif Adam, 24, asylum seeker from Sudan (also Hana, professional Arabic interpreter on video; Mark, his support worker)","open":"Yes. It's fine.","manner":"Quiet, polite and wary of authority. Distressed when talking about family and deportation. Relaxes once told the conversation is confidential and free, and that he need not describe his past.","free":["A Home Office letter came; his interview is next week","Didn't sleep for three nights, then bought two boxes of paracetamol and took them all","Called his support worker soon afterwards because he was frightened","Does not want to talk about what happened at home"],"ifasked":["Wanted to die at the time; now regrets it and has no plan","Thoughts of death come sometimes; faith forbids suicide; his mother in Sudan keeps him going","Lives in a hotel room with three men; one shouts at night","Nightmares every night, wakes shouting and sweating","Flashbacks triggered by loud noises, with racing heart and breathlessness","Avoids news; sits with his back to the wall watching the door","Has a solicitor"],"worries":["Will you tell the Home Office about this?","Do I have to pay for this?","If they send me back, I die anyway."],"rules":["Becomes guarded if pushed for details of past trauma; opens up when told he need not share them","Becomes tearful talking about his family still in Sudan","Answers about suicide honestly if asked plainly and calmly","Relaxed once told care is free and confidential"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Thanks for joining, Hana. Are you comfortable with Sudanese Arabic? Please interpret everything exactly, including anything hard to say.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","who":"Interpreter","s":"Yes, I'm from Khartoum. I'll interpret in the first person."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Mr Adam, I'm Dr Rossi, a mental health doctor. This is Hana, a professional interpreter. Are you comfortable with a female interpreter?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"Yes. It's fine."},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"What you tell us is private and used for your care. If I thought you or someone else was in serious danger, I might need to share some information, and I'd tell you first.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Will you tell the Home Office about this?"},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"That's a very understandable worry. No. Your health information isn't passed to the Home Office for your claim. If you ever want a letter for your solicitor, that's your choice.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thank you."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"The medical team tell me your treatment for the tablets is finished and your blood tests are normal now. How does your body feel?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:19","w":"P","s":"Tired. The drip made me sick. Now okay."},{"t":"1:24","band":"What happened","w":"D","s":"Can you talk me through what happened before you took the tablets?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"A letter came. The interview is next week. I didn't sleep three nights. I bought two boxes and took them all in my room."},{"t":"1:41","w":"D","s":"When you took them, did you want to die?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"At that moment, yes. I wanted everything to stop. Then I was frightened and I called Mark."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about it now? Do you still have thoughts about ending your life?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"I'm sorry I did it. Sometimes the thought comes. But no plan. In my religion it is forbidden."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"Have you made any plans or kept any tablets?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing in my room now."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"What keeps you going?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"My faith. Mark. My mother in Sudan. It would destroy her."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"Where are you living? Do you feel safe there?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"A hotel, with three other men. One shouts at night. I don't sleep."},{"t":"2:44","band":"His symptoms","w":"D","s":"You don't need to tell me any details of what happened before you came here. I just want to know how you are now. Is that okay?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I don't want to talk about home."},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"That's completely fine. How are you sleeping? Any bad dreams?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"Every night. I wake up shouting. Sweating."},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"Do memories or pictures come into your mind in the day, as if it's happening again?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Yes. A door bangs and I'm back there. My heart races. I can't breathe."},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"Do you avoid things that remind you? Do you feel on guard or jumpy?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"I don't watch the news. I sit with my back to the wall. Always watching the door."},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"(voice breaks) My family are still there. I shouldn't have left them."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you carry a lot of pain for them. Many people who flee feel this way. It isn't weakness, and we can help with it.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"(long pause) If they send me back, I die anyway."},{"t":"4:09","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying, and it makes sense you feel desperate. I want to keep you safe while your claim continues. Are thoughts of dying there right now?","m":[4,8]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"Not right now. Talking helps a little."},{"t":"4:27","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"The nightmares, the memories coming back and feeling on guard are signs of post-traumatic stress. It's common after frightening experiences, and it's treatable.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"Treatable? How?"},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Mainly with talking therapy focused on trauma, such as trauma-focused CBT or EMDR, with an interpreter. Medication can help with sleep and nightmares too, if you'd like.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"Do I have to pay for this?"},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"No. People who have claimed asylum get NHS care free, including mental health care. You won't get a bill.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:07","w":"P","s":"Really? I was afraid of a bill."},{"t":"5:12","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'll refer you to the trauma service. The crisis team will contact you within 72 hours, always with an interpreter.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:22","w":"D","s":"Mark, could you help him register with a GP, contact Migrant Help about his housing, and make sure his solicitor knows about this before the interview?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","who":"Support worker","s":"Yes. I'll call Migrant Help tomorrow about somewhere quieter. I'll ring his solicitor too."},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'll write to his GP within 24 hours.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"Mr Adam, we'll write a safety plan in Arabic: who to call and what to do if these thoughts return. If you feel you might hurt yourself, come to A&E, call 999, or call 111 and choose the mental health option. Ask for an interpreter.","m":[11,13]},{"t":"6:08","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:14","w":"P","s":"No bill. Crisis team in three days, with interpreter. Trauma service after. If bad thoughts come, I call or come here. Mark helps with the hotel and solicitor."},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. Hana, did anything not translate well, or did he seem unsure of anything?","m":[1,13]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","who":"Interpreter","s":"No, he understood everything."},{"t":"6:39","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Mr Adam. It took courage to talk today.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you, doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Briefs a professional interpreter matched for dialect and checks his gender preference","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Introduces self and explains confidentiality and its limits simply","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Confirms the paracetamol treatment and blood tests are complete","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores events before the overdose, intent at the time, regret and current suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about protective factors and safety where he lives","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for nightmares, flashbacks, avoidance and hyperarousal","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tells him he need not give trauma details and respects his pace","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his distress about family and fear of removal","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains free NHS entitlement and that health records are not shared for his claim","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains post-traumatic stress and treatment options in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Refers to the trauma service with crisis follow-up within 72 hours and a translated safety plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Links practical support: GP registration, Migrant Help, solicitor and GP letter","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding through the interpreter, safety nets and closes warmly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Uses a matched professional interpreter, assesses suicide risk properly, screens for PTSD without digging, reassures about free care, and links him to trauma and practical support.","borderline":"Safe risk assessment, but trauma screening, entitlement or practical links are incomplete.","fail":"Uses the support worker to interpret, pushes for trauma details, misses current suicide risk, or delays care because of his asylum status."}}};
